window.QUIZ_DATA = {"exams":[{"examNumber":1,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":1,"title":"SDL 1 — Embryology of the Foregut","questions":[{"id":"sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"In a study of the embryonic gut tube, an investigator maps which adult structures receive their blood supply from the artery of the midgut. Which of the following structures belongs to this territory?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Upper rectum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The midgut begins just caudal to the liver bud and ends at the junction of the right two-thirds and left one-third of the transverse colon; it forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon, all supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum therefore lies in the midgut territory. The gastric fundus and the pancreatic tail are foregut derivatives supplied by the celiac artery (the pancreatic tail through the splenic artery). The sigmoid colon and upper rectum are hindgut derivatives supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (celiac, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric supply of the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA; the midgut runs from the lower duodenum to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: map visceral pain and ischemia to the gut region and its artery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"The duodenum receives arterial branches from two separate aortic trunks. Which feature of its development explains this?","choices":{"A":"Dual midgut and hindgut origin","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Dual foregut and midgut origin","D":"Secondary retroperitoneal fixation","E":"Origin from the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenum forms from the terminal part of the foregut and the cephalic part of the midgut. Because the foregut is supplied by the celiac artery and the midgut by the superior mesenteric artery, the duodenum receives branches of both: the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the celiac system and the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery from the SMA. The hindgut contributes nothing to the duodenum. Rotation around the SMA describes the midgut loop, not the source of duodenal arteries. Secondary retroperitoneal fixation by Toldt fascia changes position, not blood supply. The hepatic diverticulum gives rise to the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut-midgut junction lies at the ampulla of Vater, where the bile and pancreatic ducts enter (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: pancreaticoduodenal arcades link the celiac and SMA territories as a collateral route.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":1,"stem":"An embryology student traces the boundary between the foregut-derived and midgut-derived parts of the duodenum. Which landmark marks this boundary?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Duodenojejunal flexure","C":"Minor duodenal papilla","D":"Superior duodenal flexure","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The junction between the foregut and midgut lies just distal to the origin of the liver bud, which in the adult corresponds to the entrance of the common bile duct at the major duodenal papilla. Duodenum proximal to the papilla is foregut and celiac supplied; duodenum distal to it is midgut and SMA supplied. The pyloric sphincter and the superior duodenal flexure lie within the foregut portion. The duodenojejunal flexure lies within the midgut. The minor papilla, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains, lies proximal to the major papilla and does not mark the junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (normal gastrointestinal embryology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut runs from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the pancreatic duct and common bile duct insertion (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: obstruction distal to this point produces bilious vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The duodenojejunal flexure lies within the midgut.","A":"The pyloric sphincter lies within the foregut portion.","C":"The minor papilla, where the accessory pancreatic duct drains, lies proximal to the major papilla and does not mark the junction.","D":"The superior duodenal flexure lies within the foregut portion."}},{"id":"sdl01_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"Esophageal atresia with tracheoesophageal fistula most often results from which developmental error?","choices":{"A":"Posterior deviation of the septum","B":"Failure of the lung bud to form","C":"Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","D":"Arrested lengthening of the esophagus","E":"Abnormal migration of neural crest cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Langman attributes esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula to spontaneous posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum or to a mechanical force pushing the dorsal foregut wall anteriorly, so the septum partitions the foregut incompletely. Failure of the lung bud to form causes pulmonary agenesis rather than a fistula. Failed fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds causes congenital diaphragmatic hernia. Arrested lengthening of the esophagus pulls the stomach into the hiatus as a congenital hiatal hernia. Abnormal neural crest migration produces aganglionic segments such as Hirschsprung disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, pathogenesis of esophageal atresia and TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most common variant is esophageal atresia with a distal TEF, which presents with polyhydramnios and choking at the first feed; associated with VACTERL (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an NG tube that will not pass into the stomach confirms the atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"Which variant of esophageal atresia or tracheoesophageal fistula is the most common?","choices":{"A":"Isolated atresia without a fistula","B":"Upper pouch fistula with distal atresia","C":"H-type fistula without atresia","D":"Proximal atresia with distal fistula","E":"Separate fistulas at both segments"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 90% of cases are a blind proximal esophageal pouch with the distal esophagus joined to the trachea just above the bifurcation. Isolated atresia without a fistula and the H-type fistula without atresia each account for about 4%. A proximal fistula with a blind distal segment and a double fistula each account for about 1%. Langman places isolated atresia without a fistula at only about 4% of cases, far behind the 90% blind-pouch-with-distal-fistula form. The H-type fistula without atresia is also only about 4% of cases in Langman Fig. 15.7, so it is not the most common variant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, anatomic variations and frequency); Langman Ch. 15 (Fig. 15.7, variants in order of frequency). First Aid 2025 GI gives 85% for this variant; Langman and the lecture give 90%.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EA with distal TEF is the most common type; air entering the stomach through the fistula produces a prominent gastric bubble, whereas pure EA gives a gasless abdomen (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: aspiration of gastric acid through the distal fistula causes chemical pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A proximal fistula with a blind distal segment accounts for only about 1% of esophageal atresia variants.","E":"Separate fistulas at both segments (a double fistula) account for only about 1% of cases.","A":"Langman places isolated atresia without a fistula at only about 4% of cases.","C":"The H-type fistula without atresia is also only about 4% of cases in Langman Fig. 15.7, so it is not the most common variant."}},{"id":"sdl01_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is esophageal atresia associated with polyhydramnios?","choices":{"A":"Fetal kidneys produce excess urine","B":"Fluid leaks from the fistula into the lungs","C":"Swallowed fluid does not reach the intestine","D":"Amniotic membranes secrete more fluid","E":"Placental transfer of fluid to the mother falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fetus normally swallows amniotic fluid, which is absorbed in the intestine. With esophageal atresia the fluid does not pass the blind pouch into the stomach and gut, so it accumulates in the amniotic sac. Polyhydramnios here is a problem of fetal clearance, not of fetal urine production, which is unchanged. Fluid entering the airway through a fistula is not a route of amniotic clearance. The membranes and placenta are not the site of the defect. Langman attributes the polyhydramnios to blocked passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract, not to any increase in fetal urine output. The excess fluid accumulates because it cannot pass the atretic esophagus into the gut, not because it leaks through the fistula into the lungs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia prevents passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract).","boardPrep":"Step 1: polyhydramnios points to an inability to swallow or absorb amniotic fluid, as in esophageal or duodenal atresia; oligohydramnios points to absent fetal urine (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: polyhydramnios on prenatal ultrasound should prompt evaluation for upper GI atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — muscle type transitions and innervation along the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The amniotic membranes are not the site of the defect.","E":"The placenta is not the site of the defect.","A":"Langman attributes the polyhydramnios to blocked passage of amniotic fluid into the intestinal tract, not to any increase in fetal urine output.","B":"The excess fluid accumulates because it cannot pass the atretic esophagus into the gut, not because it leaks through the fistula into the lungs."}},{"id":"sdl01_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"After the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise around its longitudinal axis, the left vagus nerve innervates which part of the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Posterior wall","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Greater curvature","D":"Anterior wall","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The 90-degree clockwise rotation turns the original left side of the stomach to face anteriorly and the right side to face posteriorly. The left vagus nerve, which ran along the left side, therefore comes to innervate the anterior wall, and the right vagus nerve innervates the posterior wall. The lesser and greater curvatures form from the slower-growing anterior and faster-growing posterior walls, not from vagal territory. The pyloric antrum moves upward and to the right during rotation around the anteroposterior axis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the longitudinal axis); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise; left vagus becomes anterior and right vagus becomes posterior (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: the anterior vagal trunk lies on the front of the distal esophagus during vagotomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"The lesser and greater curvatures form from the slower-growing anterior and faster-growing posterior walls, not from vagal territory.","A":"The posterior wall is innervated by the right vagus, because the original right side of the stomach turns to face posteriorly.","C":"The greater curvature forms from faster growth of the original posterior wall, not from the territory of the left vagus.","E":"The pyloric antrum moves upward and to the right during rotation around the anteroposterior axis."}},{"id":"sdl01_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"The greater curvature of the stomach forms because one of the original walls grows faster than the opposite wall during rotation. Which wall is this?","choices":{"A":"Original anterior wall","B":"Original left lateral wall","C":"Original right lateral wall","D":"Original cranial wall","E":"Original posterior wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"During rotation the original posterior wall of the stomach grows faster than the anterior wall, forming the greater curvature; the slower-growing anterior wall forms the lesser curvature. The original left and right walls become the anterior and posterior surfaces after the 90-degree clockwise rotation and carry the left and right vagal trunks. The original cranial end becomes the cardia and shifts during rotation around the anteroposterior axis; it does not form a curvature. The original anterior wall grows more slowly than the posterior wall and forms the lesser curvature, not the greater. The cephalic end becomes the cardiac portion, which moves left and slightly downward during rotation around the anteroposterior axis rather than forming a curvature. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, differential wall growth and curvatures); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dorsal mesogastrium attaches to the greater curvature and becomes the greater omentum; ventral mesogastrium attaches to the lesser curvature and forms the lesser omentum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: these attachments define the peritoneal ligaments seen in gastric surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The original anterior wall grows more slowly than the posterior wall and forms the lesser curvature, not the greater.","B":"The original left lateral wall becomes an anterior or posterior surface after rotation and carries a vagal trunk; it does not form the greater curvature.","C":"The original right lateral wall becomes an anterior or posterior surface after rotation and carries a vagal trunk; it does not form the greater curvature.","D":"The cephalic end becomes the cardiac portion, which moves left and slightly downward during rotation around the anteroposterior axis rather than forming a curvature."}},{"id":"sdl01_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":1,"stem":"The spleen primordium appears during the fifth week of development between the two leaves of which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ventral mesogastrium","B":"Dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Lesser omentum","D":"Dorsal mesoduodenum","E":"Septum transversum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The spleen primordium arises as a mesodermal proliferation between the leaves of the dorsal mesogastrium. Gastric rotation swings it to the left, and the dorsal mesogastrium between spleen and stomach becomes the gastrosplenic ligament, while the part between spleen and left kidney becomes the splenorenal ligament. The ventral mesogastrium gives rise to the lesser omentum and falciform ligament and contains the developing liver. The dorsal mesoduodenum receives the dorsal pancreatic bud. The septum transversum is the mesoderm into which the liver bud grows. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, development and position of the spleen); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach mesenteries and spleen).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the spleen arises in the dorsal mesogastrium and is mesodermal, yet it has a foregut blood supply through the splenic branch of the celiac trunk (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments are divided during splenectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — distribution of vagal parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Congenital duodenal atresia most often results from which process?","choices":{"A":"Vascular accident with resorption","B":"Abnormal rotation of the ventral bud","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Posterior deviation of the septum","E":"Hypertrophy of circular muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The duodenal lumen is obliterated by epithelial proliferation and then recanalized by vacuolization; when recanalization fails, the lumen stays occluded, producing atresia, and when it is incomplete it produces stenosis. Vascular accidents with resorption of bowel cause jejunal and ileal atresia. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas that encircles the duodenum. Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal atresia. Hypertrophy of circular muscle at the pylorus causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failure of recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (upper duodenal atresias probably from lack of recanalization; Langman attributes atresia from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents, while the lecture applies failed recanalization to the duodenum); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = failure to recanalize from the solid cord stage, double bubble on radiograph, associated with Down syndrome; jejunal and ileal atresia = vascular disruption (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: check infants with trisomy 21 for duodenal atresia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"During normal development of the duodenum, epithelial proliferation briefly converts the lumen into a solid cord. Which event follows this solid stage?","choices":{"A":"Vacuole formation and coalescence","B":"Rotation around the SMA axis","C":"Fusion of the pancreatic buds","D":"Herniation into the umbilical cord","E":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After the solid stage, vacuoles form within the epithelial cord through apoptosis and cleft formation and then coalesce into a continuous lumen, restoring a patent tube. Rotation around the SMA axis and physiologic herniation into the umbilical cord are events of the midgut loop. Fusion of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds follows rotation of the duodenum but does not reopen its lumen. The hepatic diverticulum grows out from the foregut before this stage and forms the liver and biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, normal duodenal recanalization); Langman Ch. 15 (solid stage and cavity formation, Fig. 15.18).","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure of this vacuolization gives duodenal atresia or stenosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the same failure in the esophagus produces esophageal stenosis, usually in the lower third.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"In an infant with congenital duodenal obstruction, the vomitus is bile stained. Where is the obstruction most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Proximal to the major papilla","B":"Distal to the major papilla","C":"At the pyloric sphincter","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile enters the duodenum at the major papilla, so obstruction distal to it produces bilious vomiting; the lecture notes that about 80% to 85% of duodenal obstructions lie distal to the papilla. Obstruction proximal to the papilla, at the pylorus, at the gastroesophageal junction, or in the antrum keeps bile out of the vomitus, which is nonbilious, as in hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, clinical and radiographic presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (entrance of the bile duct at the major papilla); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilious emesis in a neonate signals obstruction distal to the ampulla of Vater, such as duodenal atresia or malrotation with volvulus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bilious vomiting in a newborn is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis results mainly from enlargement of which tissue layer of the pylorus?","choices":{"A":"Circular muscularis externa","B":"Pyloric mucosal epithelium","C":"Submucosal connective tissue","D":"Antral muscularis mucosae","E":"Visceral peritoneal serosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pyloric stenosis occurs when the circular musculature of the pylorus, and to a lesser degree the longitudinal musculature, hypertrophies and narrows the pyloric lumen. The lecture adds that the circular layer of the muscularis propria often thickens by 50% to 100%. The mucosa and submucosa may become edematous and inflamed, but they are secondary changes. The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer within the mucosa that does not form the obstructing mass. The serosa is a covering layer. Langman describes pyloric stenosis as hypertrophy of the circular and, to a lesser degree, longitudinal musculature, not enlargement of the submucosal connective tissue. The visceral peritoneum covering the pylorus is not the hypertrophied layer; the pyloric musculature thickens and narrows the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, underlying developmental abnormality); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (hyperplasia of the pyloric muscularis propria).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the pyloric sphincter muscle produce the palpable olive; treat with pyloromyotomy (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the myotomy divides the hypertrophied muscle down to the submucosa and spares the mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The mucosa and submucosa may become edematous and inflamed, but they are secondary changes.","D":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin layer within the mucosa that does not form the obstructing mass.","C":"Langman describes pyloric stenosis as hypertrophy of the circular and, to a lesser degree, longitudinal musculature, not enlargement of the submucosal connective tissue.","E":"The visceral peritoneum covering the pylorus is not the hypertrophied layer; the pyloric musculature thickens and narrows the lumen."}},{"id":"sdl01_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"An investigator examines pyloric muscle from infants with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and finds few neurons expressing the enzyme that produces the main relaxing transmitter of the sphincter. Which transmitter is deficient?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Substance P","C":"Serotonin","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pyloric relaxation is mediated by nonadrenergic, noncholinergic neurons that release nitric oxide made by neuronal nitric oxide synthase. In hypertrophic pyloric stenosis, neuronal nitric oxide synthase is markedly deficient in the pyloric muscle plexus, so the sphincter fails to relax, and persistent contraction drives work hypertrophy and narrowing. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract GI smooth muscle. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and secretory mediator in the enteric nervous system. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers relaxes gut wall muscle but contracts sphincters. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, nitric oxide synthase deficiency). Among the staged sources this mechanism is stated by the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: loss of NO-mediated relaxation also underlies achalasia, where NO and VIP inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus degenerate at the lower esophageal sphincter (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: think of failed relaxation when a sphincter region thickens in an infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Postnatal exposure to which drug substantially increases the risk of hypertrophic pyloric stenosis?","choices":{"A":"Amoxicillin","B":"Gentamicin","C":"Ceftriaxone","D":"Metronidazole","E":"Erythromycin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Langman notes that treatment with erythromycin in the newborn period substantially increases the risk of pyloric stenosis, suggesting that the defect can develop after birth; Robbins and the lecture extend the association to azithromycin, either given directly or through breast milk in the first 2 weeks of life. Amoxicillin, gentamicin, ceftriaxone, and metronidazole are commonly used in neonates but are not macrolides and are not linked to pyloric stenosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, environmental and medication exposure); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is more common in firstborn males and is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: weigh this risk when choosing pertussis prophylaxis for a young infant.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — pyloric resistance during gastric mixing and emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a bleeding posterior ulcer of the duodenal bulb, and the gastroduodenal artery is surgically ligated. Afterward the duodenum distal to the major papilla remains well perfused. This part of the duodenum is perfused mainly by branches of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Common hepatic artery","C":"Left gastric artery","D":"Right gastroepiploic artery","E":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The duodenum distal to the major papilla is midgut and is supplied by the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery, a branch of the superior mesenteric artery, so it stays perfused after the gastroduodenal artery is tied. The common hepatic artery gives rise to the gastroduodenal artery and supplies the foregut part of the duodenum through the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. The left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. The right gastroepiploic artery is a branch of the ligated gastroduodenal artery. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum supplied by both celiac and superior mesenteric arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac trunk branches are the common hepatic, splenic, and left gastric arteries, and the pancreaticoduodenal arcades join the celiac and SMA territories (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a posterior duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery, and the arcades protect the duodenum when it is ligated.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman develops left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stools after a prolonged episode of hypotension. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from the distal third of the transverse colon through the sigmoid colon, with a normal cecum and ascending colon. The ischemic bowel is derived from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Midgut","B":"Foregut","C":"Pharyngeal gut","D":"Hindgut","E":"Cloaca"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hindgut extends from the distal one-third of the transverse colon to the cloacal membrane and forms the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and upper anal canal, all supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery; hypoperfusion of that territory explains the pattern. The midgut forms the cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, which are normal. The foregut forms the esophagus through the proximal duodenum. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that the urorectal septum divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus; it does not form the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut = IMA, with parasympathetic supply from the pelvic splanchnic nerves rather than the vagus (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: the splenic flexure is a watershed between SMA and IMA territories.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerves rather than the vagus supply parasympathetic fibers to the distal colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The cloaca is the terminal dilation of the hindgut that the urorectal septum divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus; it does not form the colon.","A":"The midgut forms the cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, which are normal here.","B":"The foregut forms the esophagus through the proximal duodenum.","C":"The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx."}},{"id":"sdl01_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has endoscopic removal of an adenoma in the descending duodenum just proximal to the major duodenal papilla. Several hours later he has bleeding from the main artery supplying the resected segment. Which artery is most likely bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery","B":"Right gastroepiploic artery","C":"Short gastric branch of the splenic artery","D":"Ileocolic branch of the SMA","E":"Superior pancreaticoduodenal artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major papilla is foregut and is supplied by the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery, which arises from the gastroduodenal artery of the celiac system. The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery is an SMA branch that supplies the midgut part distal to the papilla. The right gastroepiploic artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach. Short gastric branches of the splenic artery supply the gastric fundus. The ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, dual blood supply of the duodenum); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: posterior duodenal ulcers erode the gastroduodenal artery, the parent of the superior pancreaticoduodenal artery (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: localize duodenal bleeding by its position relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the ventral and dorsal pancreatic buds arise from the duodenum at the foregut–midgut junction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileocolic artery supplies the terminal ileum, cecum, and appendix.","A":"The inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery supplies the midgut duodenum distal to the papilla.","B":"The right gastroepiploic artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach.","C":"Short gastric branches of the splenic artery supply the gastric fundus."}},{"id":"sdl01_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios drools, chokes, and becomes cyanotic during his first feed. A nasogastric tube coils in the upper chest, and a chest radiograph shows a gas-filled stomach. Which additional finding is most likely in this infant?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle","B":"Annular pancreatic tissue","C":"Vertebral segmentation defects","D":"Aganglionic distal colon","E":"Bilateral congenital cataracts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A blind upper esophageal pouch that stops the nasogastric tube, together with air in the stomach, indicates esophageal atresia with a distal tracheoesophageal fistula. Thirty to fifty percent of these infants have other anomalies, most notably the VACTERL association, which includes vertebral defects. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle presents weeks later with projectile nonbilious vomiting. Annular pancreas is associated with trisomy 21 and duodenal obstruction. Aganglionic colon is Hirschsprung disease, which presents with delayed meconium passage. Congenital cataracts point to infections such as rubella. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, clinical features and associated anomalies); Langman Ch. 15 (esophageal abnormalities); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VACTERL = vertebral, anal atresia, cardiac, tracheoesophageal fistula, renal, limb defects; failure to pass an NG tube is the bedside test for EA (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: screen with echocardiography and renal ultrasound once EA is found.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is another foregut anomaly with polyhydramnios and neonatal vomiting","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-month-old girl has had three episodes of pneumonia and coughs and gags during most feeds. A contrast study shows a continuous, patent esophagus with contrast entering the trachea through a short oblique tract in the lower neck. Which developmental error best explains this finding?","choices":{"A":"Failed lengthening of the esophagus during descent","B":"Incomplete recanalization of the esophageal lumen","C":"Loss of ganglion cells in the myenteric plexus","D":"Defective fusion of the pleuroperitoneal folds","E":"Incomplete fusion of tracheoesophageal ridges"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A patent esophagus with a fistula to the trachea is the H-type fistula. It results from incomplete partitioning by the tracheoesophageal septum, which forms when the paired tracheoesophageal ridges grow toward each other and fuse; where fusion is incomplete, a communication persists. Failed lengthening of the esophagus causes congenital hiatal hernia. Incomplete recanalization narrows the lumen as esophageal stenosis without a fistula. Loss of myenteric ganglion cells causes motility disorders such as achalasia. Defective pleuroperitoneal fold fusion causes diaphragmatic hernia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, normal formation of the septum and H-type TEF); Langman Ch. 15 (esophagus and esophageal abnormalities).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H-type fistula resembles the letter H and causes recurrent aspiration pneumonia (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: suspect it in an infant with recurrent pneumonia and choking with feeds despite a passable NG tube.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal stenosis and webs arise from structural narrowing rather than a fistula","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman at 32 weeks of gestation has a fundal height larger than expected. Ultrasound shows an amniotic fluid index of 32 cm (reference range: 5–25 cm) and a fetal stomach that is not visualized on repeated scans. Which finding is most likely at birth?","choices":{"A":"Failure to pass a nasogastric tube","B":"Bilious vomiting after the first feed","C":"Olive-shaped epigastric mass","D":"Delayed passage of meconium","E":"Bowel loops outside the abdomen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Polyhydramnios with an absent fetal stomach bubble means swallowed amniotic fluid is not reaching the stomach, which indicates esophageal atresia; at birth a nasogastric tube will stop in the blind upper pouch. Bilious vomiting with polyhydramnios fits duodenal atresia distal to the papilla, but that lesion produces a distended stomach and duodenum on ultrasound. The olive-shaped mass of pyloric stenosis appears weeks after birth. Delayed meconium passage suggests Hirschsprung disease or meconium ileus. Bowel loops outside the abdomen fit gastroschisis or omphalocele, which are seen directly on ultrasound. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, in utero polyhydramnios and postnatal presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (atresia and polyhydramnios); First Aid 2025 GI (tracheoesophageal anomalies).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure EA gives a gasless abdomen, whereas EA with distal TEF gives a prominent gastric bubble (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a small or absent fetal stomach with polyhydramnios is the prenatal clue to EA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal atresia as the other foregut cause of polyhydramnios (double bubble)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with refractory peptic ulcer disease undergoes truncal vagotomy. The surgeon divides the vagal trunk that lies on the anterior surface of the distal esophagus. The fibers in this trunk come mainly from which nerve?","choices":{"A":"Right vagus nerve","B":"Left phrenic nerve","C":"Left vagus nerve","D":"Greater splanchnic nerve","E":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Before rotation the left and right vagus nerves run along the left and right sides of the stomach. The 90-degree clockwise rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly, where it becomes the anterior vagal trunk innervating the anterior gastric wall. The right vagus becomes the posterior trunk. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion. The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch and supplies the larynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, vagal innervation changes); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotation and vagus nerves).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after the 90-degree clockwise rotation the left vagus lies anterior and the right vagus posterior (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: vagotomy reduces acid secretion but can delay gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the vagus distributes parasympathetic fibers to the stomach and proximal GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The right vagus becomes the posterior trunk.","B":"The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm.","D":"The greater splanchnic nerve carries sympathetic fibers to the celiac ganglion.","E":"The left recurrent laryngeal nerve hooks under the aortic arch and supplies the larynx."}},{"id":"sdl01_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man recovering from acute pancreatitis develops a fluid collection that fills the space directly behind the stomach. This space formed as a result of which effect of gastric rotation?","choices":{"A":"Leftward pull on the ventral mesogastrium","B":"Leftward pull on the dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Rightward swing of the duodenal loop","D":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","E":"Fusion of the transverse mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The space behind the stomach is the omental bursa, or lesser peritoneal sac. As the stomach rotates around its longitudinal axis, it pulls the dorsal mesogastrium to the left and opens this space behind it. The ventral mesogastrium, which becomes the lesser omentum, is pulled to the right rather than the left. The rightward swing of the C-shaped duodenum moves the duodenum and pancreatic head against the posterior wall. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut loop around the SMA positions the intestines. Fusion of the greater omentum with the transverse mesocolon occurs later and closes off the lower part of the bursa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, formation of the omental bursa); Langman Ch. 15 (omental bursa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lesser sac communicates with the greater sac through the epiploic foramen of Winslow (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: pancreatic pseudocysts and posterior gastric ulcer perforations collect in the lesser sac.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 3 — pancreatic pseudocysts are nonneoplastic collections that follow pancreatitis","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl01_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3D model of a 6-week embryo shows the stomach after its first rotation has turned the original left side to face anteriorly. In a second movement, the caudal pyloric end shifts upward and to the right while the cardiac end moves slightly downward and to the left, giving the adult stomach its oblique long axis. Rotation around which axis produces this second movement?","choices":{"A":"Longitudinal axis","B":"Superior mesenteric artery axis","C":"Transverse left-right axis","D":"Anteroposterior axis","E":"Vitelline duct axis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After the 90-degree clockwise turn around its longitudinal axis, the stomach rotates around an anteroposterior axis: the caudal pyloric part moves upward and to the right and the cephalic cardiac part moves slightly downward and to the left, so the adult long axis runs from upper left to lower right. The longitudinal-axis rotation is the earlier turn that brings the original left side forward. The superior mesenteric artery axis is the axis of midgut loop rotation, not stomach rotation. A transverse left-right axis and a vitelline duct axis are not described for the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, rotation around the anteroposterior axis, p5); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach rotates around a longitudinal and an anteroposterior axis, Fig. 15.8).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates around two axes, longitudinal then anteroposterior, which sets the positions of the pylorus and cardia (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dual rotation places the stomach in its adult position with the long axis running from upper left to lower right (lecture SDL 1).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — longitudinal rotation carries the left vagus anteriorly","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The longitudinal-axis rotation is the earlier turn that brings the original left side forward and carries the left vagus anteriorly.","B":"The superior mesenteric artery axis is the axis of midgut loop rotation, not stomach rotation.","C":"A transverse left-right axis is not described for the stomach."}},{"id":"sdl01_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl with upslanting palpebral fissures and a single palmar crease vomits bile after every feed. An abdominal radiograph shows a dilated stomach and proximal duodenum with no gas beyond them. At surgery the pancreas is normal, and a thin membrane occludes the bowel just distal to the major papilla. Which developmental process failed?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","B":"Perfusion of the jejunal mesentery","C":"Rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud","D":"Rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The double bubble with no distal gas, bilious vomiting, and features of Down syndrome indicate duodenal atresia; an occluding membrane distal to the papilla is the result of failed vacuolization and recanalization after the solid stage. Loss of mesenteric perfusion causes jejunal and ileal atresia. Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas, excluded by the normal pancreas at surgery. Malrotation of the midgut causes obstruction by bands or volvulus rather than an intraluminal membrane. Failed pyloric relaxation causes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis with nonbilious vomiting weeks after birth. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, failed recanalization with an occluding membrane, the 80–85% of obstructions distal to the papilla, and associated conditions); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (triple bubble in jejunal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia = double bubble, polyhydramnios, bilious vomiting, Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI; Bootcamp Gastroenterology embryology). COMLEX: look for associated cardiac defects in infants with trisomy 21.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas is the other congenital cause of a double bubble","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Malrotation of the midgut causes obstruction by bands or volvulus rather than an intraluminal membrane.","C":"Abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud creates an annular pancreas, excluded by the normal pancreas at surgery.","B":"Loss of mesenteric perfusion causes jejunal and ileal atresia."}},{"id":"sdl01_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has repeated nonbilious vomiting after feeds. An abdominal radiograph shows a distended stomach and duodenal bulb with a small amount of gas in the distal bowel, and a contrast study shows a narrowed duodenal segment with a perforated web. Where is this lesion most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Distal to the major papilla","B":"At the duodenojejunal flexure","C":"In the terminal ileum","D":"At the gastroesophageal junction","E":"Proximal to the major papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A perforated web with some distal gas indicates duodenal stenosis from incomplete recanalization, and nonbilious vomiting places the narrowing proximal to the major papilla, where bile has not yet entered the lumen. A lesion distal to the papilla or at the duodenojejunal flexure would produce bilious vomiting. Terminal ileal obstruction causes bilious vomiting with diffuse distension rather than a dilated duodenal bulb. Obstruction at the gastroesophageal junction prevents feeds from reaching the stomach, so the stomach and duodenum would not distend. A lesion at the gastroesophageal junction would block feeds before the stomach, so the stomach and duodenal bulb would not distend. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, duodenal stenosis and bilious versus nonbilious vomiting); Langman Ch. 15 (atresias and stenoses of the duodenum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: complete atresia gives no distal gas; stenosis or a perforated web lets some gas pass (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: bile in the vomitus localizes the obstruction relative to the ampulla.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — the bile duct enters at the major papilla as the duodenum and ventral bud rotate","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A lesion distal to the papilla or at the duodenojejunal flexure would produce bilious vomiting.","D":"A lesion at the gastroesophageal junction would block feeds before the stomach, so the stomach and duodenal bulb would not distend."}},{"id":"sdl01_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman at 24 weeks of gestation has an ultrasound showing polyhydramnios and two fluid-filled structures in the fetal upper abdomen that connect through the pylorus. No other fluid-filled bowel is seen. The fetus is most likely to have which additional condition?","choices":{"A":"Trisomy 18","B":"Trisomy 21","C":"Trisomy 13","D":"Turner syndrome","E":"Klinefelter syndrome"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A fluid-filled stomach and proximal duodenum connected through the pylorus is the prenatal double bubble of duodenal atresia, and polyhydramnios reflects swallowed fluid that does not reach the distal bowel. Duodenal atresia is strongly associated with Down syndrome (trisomy 21), present in up to 30% of cases. Trisomy 18 and Turner syndrome carry other congenital anomalies but are not the classic association. Trisomy 13 is associated with midline defects such as holoprosencephaly. Klinefelter syndrome has no characteristic GI atresia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 4, associated conditions); Langman Ch. 15 (duodenal atresia); First Aid 2025 GI (intestinal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Down syndrome GI associations include duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and Hirschsprung disease (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: a prenatal double bubble prompts karyotype counseling.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old firstborn boy has had projectile, nonbilious vomiting after every feed for 5 days and is eager to feed again right after vomiting. A firm, mobile mass is palpable in the epigastrium. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 88 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L), potassium of 3.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate of 33 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The palpable mass consists mainly of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Edematous submucosal connective tissue","B":"Hyperplastic antral mucosal glands","C":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle","D":"Enlarged submucosal lymphoid follicles","E":"Heterotopic pancreatic tissue"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A hungry, firstborn male infant with projectile nonbilious vomiting, an epigastric olive, and hypochloremic, hypokalemic metabolic alkalosis has hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. The olive is the hypertrophied and hyperplastic circular smooth muscle of the pyloric muscularis propria. Mucosal and submucosal edema are secondary findings and do not form the mass. Antral gland hyperplasia and lymphoid follicles are not part of the lesion; lymphoid hyperplasia is a lead point for intussusception. Heterotopic pancreatic tissue can form a small gastric nodule but does not cause this presentation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, developmental abnormality and clinical presentation); Langman Ch. 15 (stomach abnormalities); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis = olive mass, visible peristalsis, projectile nonbilious vomiting at about 3–6 weeks (lecture; First Aid gives 2–6 weeks), hypokalemic hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: correct the fluid and electrolyte deficit before pyloromyotomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — loss of parietal cell HCl and the alkaline tide explain the metabolic alkalosis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal and submucosal edema are secondary findings and do not form the mass.","B":"Antral gland hyperplasia is not part of the pyloric olive.","D":"Lymphoid follicles are not part of the lesion.","E":"Heterotopic pancreatic tissue can form a small gastric nodule but does not cause this presentation."}},{"id":"sdl01_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl whose mother has pertussis was given a 5-day course of a prophylactic antibiotic starting at 6 days of age. She now has projectile, nonbilious vomiting after feeds and an olive-shaped epigastric mass. The prophylactic drug most likely acts by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Binding the 30S ribosomal subunit","B":"Binding the 50S ribosomal subunit","C":"Inhibiting cell wall transpeptidase","D":"Inhibiting DNA gyrase","E":"Inhibiting dihydropteroate synthase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pertussis prophylaxis in a young infant uses a macrolide, and macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks of life is associated with hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Macrolides such as erythromycin and azithromycin bind the 50S ribosomal subunit and block translocation. Binding the 30S subunit is the mechanism of aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. Inhibition of cell wall transpeptidases is the mechanism of beta-lactams. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the mechanism of fluoroquinolones. Inhibition of dihydropteroate synthase is the mechanism of sulfonamides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, macrolide exposure in the first 2 weeks); Langman Ch. 15 (erythromycin in the newborn period); First Aid 2025 GI (macrolide exposure); Katzung Ch. 44 (macrolides bind the 50S ribosomal subunit, Fig. 44–1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypertrophic pyloric stenosis is associated with macrolide exposure (First Aid 2025 GI); erythromycin also acts as a motilin agonist and prokinetic. COMLEX: counsel families about vomiting after neonatal macrolide therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal feedback and pyloric resistance control gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl01_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old girl has forceful vomiting immediately after feeds and remains hungry afterward. Ultrasound shows a thickened, elongated pyloric channel, and her vomitus contains milk but no bile. Which of the following best explains the absence of bile in her vomitus?","choices":{"A":"Absent bile secretion before 3 months of age","B":"Reflux blocked by the sphincter of Oddi","C":"Bile reabsorbed in the gastric antrum","D":"Obstruction proximal to the major papilla","E":"Bile diverted through the cystic duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pylorus lies proximal to the major duodenal papilla, where bile enters the gut, so a pyloric obstruction keeps bile out of the stomach and the vomitus is nonbilious. Infants secrete bile from birth, so age does not explain it. The sphincter of Oddi regulates bile entry into the duodenum but does not prevent bile from reaching the stomach when the obstruction lies distal to the papilla. The gastric antrum does not reabsorb bile; bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum. Diversion through the cystic duct stores bile in the gallbladder but does not eliminate it from the duodenum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, forceful projectile nonbilious vomiting); Robbins Ch. 17 (pyloric stenosis); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonbilious projectile vomiting points to pyloric stenosis; bilious vomiting points to obstruction distal to the ampulla (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: an ultrasound of the pylorus confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — bile enters the duodenum through the sphincter of Oddi under CCK control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. CT shows an embolus in the artery that leaves the aorta just below the celiac trunk, with ischemia from the jejunum to the proximal transverse colon. The ischemic bowel comes from which embryonic gut region?","choices":{"A":"Foregut","B":"Hindgut","C":"Midgut","D":"Cloaca","E":"Pharyngeal gut"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The artery just below the celiac trunk is the superior mesenteric artery, the artery of the midgut, which forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The foregut is supplied by the celiac artery. The hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon onward, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The cloaca is the terminal hindgut that forms the rectum and urogenital sinus. The pharyngeal gut forms the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, four divisions of the gut tube); Langman Ch. 15 (gut divisions and arteries).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut = celiac, midgut = SMA, hindgut = IMA (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: acute mesenteric ischemia gives pain out of proportion to the examination.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the same foregut/midgut/hindgut divisions set the vagal versus pelvic parasympathetic territories","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The hindgut, from the distal third of the transverse colon onward, is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery."}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe formation of the tracheoesophageal septum and explain how abnormal partitioning of the foregut results in esophageal atresia and tracheoesophageal fistula.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-day-old girl born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios has frothy drooling and coughs and turns blue with her first feed. A nasogastric tube cannot be advanced, and a chest radiograph shows its tip coiled in the upper thorax. Her abdomen is distended and tympanitic, with gas filling the stomach and bowel. Which anatomic arrangement best explains the gas in her abdomen?","choices":{"A":"Upper pouch fistula with a blind lower end","B":"Duodenal obstruction trapping swallowed air","C":"Distal esophagus joined to the trachea","D":"Blind upper and lower pouches with no fistula","E":"Patent esophagus with an H type fistula"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The coiled tube marks a blind proximal pouch, so gas can reach the stomach only from the airway through a fistula between the trachea and the distal esophagus, the type C arrangement seen in about 90% of cases, in which each cry or breath pushes air into the stomach. A fistula from the proximal pouch to the trachea with a blind distal segment also gives air no path into the stomach. A duodenal obstruction does not stop a nasogastric tube in the upper chest or explain choking with the first feed. Isolated atresia without a fistula leaves both segments blind, so air has no route into the stomach and the abdomen stays gasless. An H-type fistula leaves the esophagus continuous and patent, so the nasogastric tube would pass into the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 2, anatomic variants and clinical features of esophageal atresia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proximal atresia with a distal fistula is the most common variant and lets air distend the stomach (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 1). COMLEX: a coiled nasogastric tube with a gas-filled abdomen points to a distal fistula and a risk of reflux pneumonitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelium of the esophagus and airway","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A fistula from the proximal pouch to the","B":"A duodenal obstruction does not stop a nasogastric","D":"Isolated atresia without a fistula leaves both segments","E":"An H-type fistula leaves the esophagus continuous and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain stomach rotation and describe its effects on the orientation of the stomach, vagal innervation, and associated mesenteries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has a distal pancreatectomy with splenectomy for a mass in the body of the pancreas. The surgeon frees the spleen by dividing its peritoneal fold to the left kidney, then lifts the pancreatic body off the posterior abdominal wall along a nearly bloodless connective tissue plane. Which developmental event created this plane?","choices":{"A":"Fusion of greater omentum layers over the colon","B":"Clockwise rotation carrying the left vagus forward","C":"Splenic growth between the mesogastric leaves","D":"Fusion of the dorsal mesogastrium to the body wall","E":"Rightward pull of the ventral mesogastrium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As the stomach rotates, the dorsal mesogastrium carrying the pancreatic body and tail is pressed against the posterior wall and fuses with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia, making the pancreas secondarily retroperitoneal; that fused layer is the plane the surgeon dissects. The greater omentum forms when the dorsal mesogastrium bulges down from the greater curvature, and its fused layers hang over the transverse colon rather than pinning the pancreas. Clockwise rotation moves the left vagus onto the anterior gastric wall, which explains vagal trunk position, not fixation of the pancreas. The spleen arises between the leaves of the dorsal mesogastrium, but its growth forms the gastrosplenic and splenorenal ligaments rather than a retroperitoneal fusion plane. The ventral mesogastrium is pulled to the right to form the lesser omentum and does not bind the pancreas to the posterior wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 3, effects of rotation on mesenteries and peritoneal organs).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas becomes secondarily retroperitoneal when the dorsal mesogastrium fuses with the posterior wall (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 1). COMLEX: fusion fascia planes let surgeons mobilize secondarily retroperitoneal organs with little bleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — pancreatic buds in the dorsal and ventral mesentery","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The greater omentum forms when the dorsal mesogastrium","B":"Clockwise rotation moves the left vagus onto the","C":"The spleen arises between the leaves of the","E":"The ventral mesogastrium is pulled to the right"}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe normal duodenal recanalization and explain how failure of this process results in congenital duodenal obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl has had intermittent green vomiting since birth but has passed some stool. She feeds poorly and has mild upper abdominal fullness. An abdominal radiograph shows a gas-filled stomach and proximal duodenum with a small amount of gas scattered through the distal bowel. Which developmental error most likely caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Complete failure of the duodenal recanalization","B":"Partial recanalization leaving a perforated web","C":"Hypertrophy of the pyloric circular muscle","D":"Failed neural crest migration to the colon","E":"Posterior deviation of the foregut septum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bilious vomiting places the block distal to the major papilla, and a double bubble with some distal gas shows a lumen that is narrowed but not closed, the picture of duodenal stenosis from incomplete recanalization of the solid epithelial stage. Complete failure of recanalization produces atresia, which presents within hours of birth with no gas beyond the duodenum. Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis lies proximal to the major papilla, so its vomitus is nonbilious, and it usually appears at 3 to 6 weeks. Failed neural crest migration leaves the distal colon aganglionic and causes colonic obstruction, not a duodenal double bubble. Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal atresia and fistula, which present with drooling and choking rather than bilious vomiting. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, sections 2, 4 and 5); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hirschsprung disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: complete failure of recanalization causes atresia, and partial failure causes stenosis or a perforated web (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 1). COMLEX: a double bubble with some distal gas suggests incomplete duodenal obstruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — annular pancreas as another cause of congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Complete failure of recanalization produces atresia, which presents","C":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis lies proximal to the major","D":"Failed neural crest migration leaves the distal colon","E":"Posterior deviation of the tracheoesophageal septum causes esophageal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the developmental abnormality underlying hypertrophic pyloric stenosis and recognize its characteristic clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy whose father had a pyloromyotomy as an infant has had forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds for 4 days and cries to be fed again afterward. He appears mildly dehydrated. Laboratory studies show a serum chloride of 91 mEq/L (reference range: 95–105 mEq/L) and bicarbonate of 31 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Inspection of his abdomen just before an episode of emesis is most likely to show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Waves moving right to left across the lower abdomen","B":"Waves moving left to right across the epigastrium","C":"A sausage shaped mass in the right upper quadrant","D":"Diffuse distension of the lower abdomen","E":"Bluish periumbilical discoloration"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Forceful nonbilious vomiting right after feeds in a hungry young infant with a family history and hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis indicates hypertrophic pyloric stenosis. Prominent left-to-right peristaltic waves cross the epigastrium just before emesis. Right-to-left waves over the lower abdomen do not follow gastric peristalsis toward the pylorus. A sausage-shaped right-upper-quadrant mass suggests intussusception. Diffuse lower abdominal distension points to a distal bowel obstruction. Bluish periumbilical discoloration reflects retroperitoneal hemorrhage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 5, family history, hungry vomiter, visible gastric peristalsis, and metabolic alkalosis, lines 268–293); First Aid 2025 GI (hypertrophic pyloric stenosis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peristaltic wave in epigastrium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists bottle feeding, nicotine exposure, erythromycin, and azithromycin as risk factors for pyloric stenosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis). COMLEX: First Aid notes that ultrasound shows a thickened and lengthened pylorus (First Aid 2025 GI, hypertrophic pyloric stenosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — pyloric resistance in gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Waves running right to left over the lower abdomen do not follow gastric peristalsis toward the pylorus."}},{"id":"trial_sdl01_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four divisions of the primitive gut tube (pharyngeal gut, foregut, midgut, hindgut) and their corresponding blood supply (celiac, SMA, IMA), including the duodenum's dual foregut/midgut supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has left-sided abdominal pain and bloody stool after a period of hypotension during cardiac surgery. Colonoscopy shows ischemia centered on the splenic flexure. Why is this site vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Celiac and SMA boundary","B":"Portal and caval anastomosis","C":"Hindgut and cloaca junction","D":"Single IMA end artery","E":"SMA and IMA watershed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies near the boundary between the midgut territory of the superior mesenteric artery and the hindgut territory of the inferior mesenteric artery, a watershed area that is vulnerable to low flow. The celiac and SMA territories meet at the duodenum. A portal and caval anastomosis is a venous connection in the esophagus, rectum, or umbilical region and does not explain arterial low flow ischemia. The hindgut and cloaca junction is in the pelvis. A single end artery does not describe the flexure, whose supply comes from the edges of two territories, which is what makes it a watershed. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 1, section 1, midgut and hindgut boundary at the distal third of the transverse colon); First Aid 2025 GI (colonic ischemia at watershed areas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ischemic colitis affects watershed areas such as the splenic flexure (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: pain followed by bloody diarrhea after hypotension suggests colonic ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — the midgut–hindgut boundary near the splenic flexure, where vagal and pelvic parasympathetic territories also meet","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":2,"title":"SDL 2 — Histology of the Foregut part 1","questions":[{"id":"sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between which two parts of the alimentary canal wall?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium and lamina propria","B":"Inner and outer muscle layers","C":"Submucosa and serosa","D":"Mucosa and submucosa","E":"Lamina propria and serosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the mucosa, a thin band of smooth muscle that separates the mucosa from the submucosa beneath it. The epithelium and lamina propria are both above it, inside the mucosa. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers belong to the muscularis externa and are separated by the myenteric plexus. The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae; the submucosa and muscularis externa lie between them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, mucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (the muscularis mucosae forms the boundary between mucosa and submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers from inside out are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (MSMS); erosions stay in the mucosa, while ulcers extend into the submucosa or deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: depth of a lesion relative to the muscularis mucosae separates an erosion from an ulcer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae; the submucosa and muscularis externa lie between them.","A":"The epithelium and lamina propria are both above it, inside the mucosa.","B":"The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers belong to the muscularis externa and are separated by the myenteric plexus.","E":"The serosa is the outermost coat and does not contact the muscularis mucosae."}},{"id":"sdl02_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"The thoracic esophagus is fixed to surrounding mediastinal structures for most of its length. Which coat forms its outermost layer?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Mesentery","D":"Submucosa","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Where the gut tube is bound to adjacent structures, its outer coat is adventitia, connective tissue that blends with the surrounding connective tissue; the thoracic esophagus is the classic example. Serosa covers only the short abdominal segment after the esophagus enters the peritoneal cavity. Mesothelium is the epithelial part of a serosa, so it is absent where there is no serosa. A mesentery suspends intraperitoneal bowel and does not cover the thoracic esophagus. The submucosa lies inside the muscularis externa, not outside it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, serosa or adventitia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal outer layer is adventitia; the abdominal segment is covered by serosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serosa covers intraperitoneal segments and adventitia covers retroperitoneal or fixed segments (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the thoracic esophagus lacks a serosal covering.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serosa, a simple squamous mesothelium on loose connective tissue, covers only the short abdominal segment after the esophagus enters the peritoneal cavity.","B":"Mesothelium is the epithelial part of a serosa, so it is absent where there is no serosa.","C":"A mesentery suspends intraperitoneal bowel and does not cover the thoracic esophagus.","D":"The submucosa lies inside the muscularis externa, not outside it."}},{"id":"sdl02_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa of the small intestine, contraction of which component shortens the gut tube during peristalsis?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Outer longitudinal layer","D":"Inner oblique layer","E":"Lamina propria myofibroblasts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis externa has an inner circular and an outer longitudinal layer of smooth muscle. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the tube, while contraction of the inner circular layer constricts the lumen; together they drive peristalsis and segmentation. The muscularis mucosae lies in the mucosa and makes local folds without moving the whole segment. An inner oblique layer is a regional feature of the stomach, not the small intestine. Myofibroblasts in the lamina propria do not form a muscle layer that shortens the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two layers of the muscularis externa controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the stomach adds an inner oblique layer and the colon condenses its longitudinal layer into teniae coli.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall and location of the enteric plexuses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the human esophagus and resists abrasion by swallowed food?","choices":{"A":"Keratinized stratified squamous","B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous","C":"Simple columnar with goblet cells","D":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","E":"Stratified columnar with microvilli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose multiple cell layers resist abrasion from rough food boluses while staying moist. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium is not the normal human esophageal lining. Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells is the intestinal pattern and appears in the esophagus only as Barrett metaplasia. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airway. Stratified columnar epithelium with microvilli is not a lining of the alimentary canal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; Barrett esophagus replaces it with intestinal columnar epithelium containing goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology and Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic acid reflux drives this metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"The surface mucous cells of the stomach secrete a thick gel that protects the epithelium from luminal acid. This gel is rich in which ions?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen and chloride","B":"Ammonium and phosphate","C":"Calcium and magnesium","D":"Bicarbonate and K+","E":"Sodium and sulfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Surface mucous cells secrete a viscous, visible mucus layer rich in bicarbonate and K+. The bicarbonate trapped in the gel neutralizes acid at the epithelial surface, and the gel slows pepsin, which protects the mucosa from autodigestion. Hydrogen and chloride ions are the components of the hydrochloric acid secreted by parietal cells, the injury the gel guards against. Ammonium is generated by H. pylori urease, not by surface mucous cells. Phosphate, calcium, magnesium, and sulfate are not the defining ions of this barrier. Pawlina states the visible mucus has a high bicarbonate and potassium concentration that keeps it alkaline, not hydrogen and chloride. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins maintain gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion; NSAIDs reduce them and predispose to ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol, a prostaglandin analog, restores this protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ammonium is generated by H. pylori urease, not by surface mucous cells.","A":"Pawlina states the visible mucus has a high bicarbonate and potassium concentration that keeps it alkaline, not hydrogen and chloride."}},{"id":"sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":1,"stem":"Which crypt-base cell type of the small intestine secretes lysozyme and alpha-defensins?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Enteroendocrine cells","C":"Paneth cells","D":"Enterocytes","E":"Tuft cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paneth cells at the base of the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete lysozyme and alpha-defensins, antimicrobial products of innate immunity. Goblet cells secrete mucin for lubrication. Enteroendocrine cells release peptide hormones such as CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin. Enterocytes absorb nutrients through their microvillous brush border. Tuft cells are chemosensory cells and do not secrete these antimicrobial proteins. Goblet cells are unicellular mucin-secreting glands interspersed among the enterocytes, so their product is mucus rather than lysozyme or alpha-defensins. First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not tuft cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, secretion in the small intestine); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (Paneth cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells and Paneth cells that secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: Paneth cells sit at the crypt base, below the stem cells that renew the epithelium.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enteroendocrine cells release peptide hormones such as CCK, secretin, GIP, and motilin.","D":"Enterocytes absorb nutrients through their microvillous brush border.","A":"Goblet cells are unicellular mucin-secreting glands interspersed among the enterocytes, so their product is mucus rather than lysozyme or alpha-defensins.","E":"First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not tuft cells."}},{"id":"sdl02_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the wall of the alimentary canal is the myenteric plexus located?","choices":{"A":"Between the muscle layers","B":"Within the submucosa","C":"Within the lamina propria","D":"Beneath the epithelium","E":"Within the adventitia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus lies in the thin connective tissue between the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, where it innervates both layers. The submucosa holds the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The lamina propria and the region beneath the epithelium carry nerve fibers but not the myenteric ganglia. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat outside the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, anatomic location); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (myenteric plexus between the outer and inner muscle layers).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosa contains the Meissner plexus; muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease shows absent ganglion cells in both plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"In the enteric nervous system, which plexus mainly regulates local secretion, absorption, and blood flow in each gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Hypogastric plexus","D":"Esophageal vagal plexus","E":"Submucosal plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The submucosal (Meissner) plexus innervates mucosal and submucosal glands, local blood vessels, and the muscularis mucosae, controlling secretion, absorption, blood flow, and mucosal folding in each segment. The myenteric (Auerbach) plexus controls motility of the muscularis externa along the length of the gut. The celiac and hypogastric plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. The esophageal vagal plexus carries extrinsic vagal fibers on the esophagus surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, main functional role); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosal plexus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meissner plexus regulates secretion and blood flow; Auerbach plexus regulates motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: both plexuses receive extrinsic vagal and sympathetic modulation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":1,"stem":"Inhibitory neurons of the myenteric plexus relax the lower esophageal sphincter to let a bolus enter the stomach. Which transmitter do these neurons release?","choices":{"A":"Acetylcholine","B":"Nitric oxide","C":"Substance P","D":"Norepinephrine","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Inhibitory peptidergic neurons of the myenteric plexus release nitric oxide, together with VIP or ATP, to relax GI sphincters such as the lower esophageal, pyloric, and ileocecal sphincters. Acetylcholine and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Norepinephrine comes from extrinsic sympathetic postganglionic fibers and inhibits motility rather than mediating enteric sphincter relaxation. Serotonin acts mainly as a sensory and paracrine signal in the mucosa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia is caused by loss of NO and VIP inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: bird beak narrowing on barium swallow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility and secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis externa of the upper third of the esophagus consists of which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Smooth muscle","C":"Mixed striated and smooth","D":"Cardiac muscle","E":"Myoepithelial cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is striated muscle, a continuation of the pharyngeal musculature. Smooth muscle forms the distal third. Mixed and interwoven striated and smooth bundles form the middle third. Cardiac muscle is not part of the gut wall. Myoepithelial cells surround the secretory units of some glands and do not form the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (upper one-third is striated muscle).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 of the esophagus is striated muscle, middle 1/3 mixed, and lower 1/3 smooth muscle (lecture; First Aid 2025 GI groups the middle and lower 2/3 as smooth muscle with some overlap at the transition). COMLEX: upper esophageal dysphagia suggests a skeletal muscle or neuromuscular cause.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"The striated muscle of the proximal esophagus is supplied by somatic motor neurons that travel in the vagus nerve. From which brainstem nucleus do these neurons arise?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus","B":"Hypoglossal nucleus","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Nucleus ambiguus","E":"Facial motor nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Somatic motor neurons to the striated esophageal muscle arise in the nucleus ambiguus and end directly on motor endplates. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus sends preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. The hypoglossal nucleus supplies tongue muscles. The nucleus tractus solitarius receives visceral afferent input. The facial motor nucleus supplies muscles of facial expression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, innervation of striated musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatic motor neurons of the nucleus ambiguus travel in the vagus to the striated muscle of the upper esophagus, whereas the dorsal motor nucleus supplies the smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: a brainstem lesion involving the nucleus ambiguus can cause dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus reach the smooth muscle of the distal esophagus. Where do these fibers synapse?","choices":{"A":"Celiac ganglion neurons","B":"Motor endplates on muscle","C":"Myenteric plexus neurons","D":"Stellate ganglion neurons","E":"Submucosal gland cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal preganglionic fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus synapse on postganglionic enteric neurons whose cell bodies lie in the myenteric plexus of the esophageal wall; these neurons then drive contraction and peristalsis. The celiac and stellate ganglia are sympathetic ganglia. Motor endplates are the targets of somatic fibers from the nucleus ambiguus on striated muscle, not of preganglionic parasympathetic fibers. Submucosal gland cells are effectors, not the site of the ganglionic synapse. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, parasympathetic control of smooth musculature).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long and synapse in ganglia in or near the target organ, within the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal input enhances peristalsis through the enteric neurons.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 1 — neural and muscular coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"The esophageal glands proper are located in which layer of the esophageal wall?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria","B":"Adventitia","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Esophageal glands proper are small compound tubuloalveolar glands in the submucosa, and their ducts cross the muscularis mucosae and mucosa to open into the lumen. The lamina propria holds the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper. The adventitia is the outer connective tissue coat. The epithelium is crossed only by the ducts. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer that the ducts pass through. Pawlina places the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper, in the lamina propria of the mucosa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands are found in the esophagus and in the duodenum as Brunner glands (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: identify gland layer to name the gland type on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer that the ducts pass through.","A":"Pawlina places the esophageal cardiac glands, not the glands proper, in the lamina propria of the mucosa."}},{"id":"sdl02_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"The esophageal cardiac glands near the gastroesophageal junction secrete which product?","choices":{"A":"Neutral mucus","B":"Acidic mucus","C":"Serous fluid","D":"Bicarbonate fluid","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Esophageal cardiac glands secrete neutral mucus that protects the distal esophagus from regurgitated gastric contents. The esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic mucus, which makes acidic mucus the wrong gland. Serous fluid comes from serous glands such as the parotid. Bicarbonate fluid is the product of duodenal Brunner glands. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands produce neutral mucus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: when reflux overwhelms distal protection, GERD can lead to Barrett metaplasia (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin injury of the distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl02_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":1,"stem":"The esophageal glands proper secrete a slightly acidic, viscous mucus. What is the main function of this secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the lumen","C":"Digesting dietary starch","D":"Absorbing swallowed water","E":"Secreting immunoglobulin A"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The slightly acidic mucus of the esophageal glands proper lubricates the luminal surface so boluses pass smoothly and the squamous lining is protected from mechanical injury. Neutralizing refluxed acid is the role of the neutral mucus from esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach. Starch digestion begins with salivary amylase, not esophageal mucus. The esophagus does not absorb swallowed water. IgA is secreted by plasma cells and transported across epithelium, not produced by these glands. Pawlina attributes protection from regurgitated gastric contents to the esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach, not the glands proper. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, secretion type and function); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mucus of glands proper lubricates the luminal wall).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal mucosa is protective, not absorptive (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: impaired lubrication contributes to the sensation of food sticking.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"IgA is secreted by plasma cells and transported across epithelium, not produced by these glands.","A":"Pawlina attributes protection from regurgitated gastric contents to the esophageal cardiac glands near the stomach, not the glands proper."}},{"id":"sdl02_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman who takes daily naproxen for arthritis has epigastric pain. Endoscopic biopsy of a gastric lesion shows that the tissue loss has crossed the muscularis mucosae and ends in dense irregular connective tissue containing large vessels. Which layer forms the base of this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Epithelium","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Adventitia","D":"Mesothelium","E":"Submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large blood and lymphatic vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa. A defect that reaches it is an ulcer rather than an erosion. The epithelium and lamina propria lie above the muscularis mucosae, so a lesion ending there would be an erosion. The adventitia and the mesothelium of the serosa lie outside the muscularis externa, much deeper than this lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, submucosa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (submucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are confined to the mucosa; ulcers extend into the submucosa or muscularis (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers by reducing prostaglandins.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has squamous cell carcinoma of the mid-thoracic esophagus. CT shows the tumor extending directly into the surrounding mediastinal fat. Absence of which layer in this segment of the esophageal wall helps explain this spread?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mucosa","C":"Submucosa","D":"Adventitia","E":"Epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thoracic esophagus has no serosa; its outer coat is adventitia, which blends into the mediastinal connective tissue, so there is no mesothelial boundary between the tumor and the mediastinum. Serosa covers only the short abdominal segment. The mucosa, submucosa, and epithelium are present in the thoracic esophagus, and the tumor arises from its epithelium. Adventitia is present, not absent, and it is the coat the tumor crosses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, adventitia of the thoracic esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal adventitia); First Aid 2025 GI (esophageal cancer: lack of serosa in the esophageal wall allows rapid extension).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma involves the upper 2/3 of the esophagus and adenocarcinoma the lower 1/3 (First Aid 2025 GI esophageal cancer). COMLEX: smoking and alcohol are leading risks for squamous cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — anatomic distribution of esophageal squamous cell carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman undergoes small-bowel biopsy for bloating. The pathologist points to a thin band of smooth muscle at the base of the crypts that separates the mucosa from the submucosa. Contraction of this band has which effect?","choices":{"A":"Propulsive peristalsis of chyme","B":"Shortening of the bowel segment","C":"Local ridges in the inner lining","D":"Closure of the ileocecal valve","E":"Constriction of the full bowel lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The band is the muscularis mucosae. Its local contractions raise folds and ridges of the mucosa that improve contact with chyme for absorption and secretion, independent of whole-organ peristalsis. Propulsive peristalsis, shortening of the segment, and constriction of the whole lumen come from the outer longitudinal and inner circular layers of the muscularis externa. Closure of the ileocecal valve depends on sphincteric circular muscle of the muscularis externa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, muscularis mucosae); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae, local secretion, and blood flow (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: mucosal movement aids contact between chyme and the absorptive surface.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has chronic diarrhea and iron deficiency anemia. Duodenal biopsy shows flattened mucosa with loss of the finger-like projections that contain central lacteals. Loss of these projections impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Acid neutralization","B":"Pathogen sampling","C":"Peristaltic propulsion","D":"Nutrient absorption","E":"Pepsinogen secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The finger-like projections are villi, cores of lamina propria with capillaries and a central lacteal covered by enterocytes. With plicae circulares and microvilli, they amplify absorptive surface area, so villous atrophy impairs nutrient absorption. Acid neutralization in the duodenum depends on Brunner gland and pancreatic bicarbonate. Pathogen sampling is the role of Peyer patches and M cells. Peristaltic propulsion depends on the muscularis externa. Pepsinogen is secreted by gastric chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption and amplification specializations); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (villi).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis, mostly in the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI celiac disease). COMLEX: iron deficiency reflects duodenal malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — loss of specialized epithelial cells causing malabsorption, as parietal-cell loss impairs B12 absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with long-standing heartburn undergoes endoscopy. Biopsy of the distal esophagus shows columnar epithelium with goblet cells replacing the normal lining. The replaced lining was specialized for which function?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing nutrients and water","B":"Secreting acid and pepsinogen","C":"Resisting mechanical abrasion","D":"Sampling luminal antigens","E":"Secreting antimicrobial peptides"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The normal esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium, whose layered cells resist mechanical abrasion from food boluses. Here it has been replaced by intestinal-type columnar epithelium, which is Barrett esophagus. Absorbing nutrients and water is the role of intestinal enterocytes. Secreting acid and pepsinogen is the role of gastric parietal and chief cells. Sampling luminal antigens is done by M cells over Peyer patches. Secreting antimicrobial peptides is the role of Paneth cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, protection, and section 5, cardiac glands and Barrett esophagus); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal epithelium).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells replacing nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the distal esophagus, a precursor of adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: chronic GERD is the driving exposure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — GERD and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman who takes high-dose aspirin develops gastric erosions. The drug reduces prostaglandin-driven secretion by the cells that line the gastric surface and pits. Loss of their secretion impairs which protective mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Killing microbes with lysozyme","B":"Neutralizing acid at the epithelium","C":"Resisting abrasion with keratin","D":"Absorbing acid through tight junctions","E":"Trapping antigens in Peyer patches"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Surface mucous cells line the gastric surface and pits and secrete a viscous mucus rich in bicarbonate that neutralizes acid at the epithelium and slows pepsin. Prostaglandins sustain this secretion, so aspirin weakens the barrier. Killing microbes with lysozyme is a Paneth cell function in the intestine. Gastric epithelium is columnar and not keratinized. Tight junctions seal the epithelium against back-diffusion of acid rather than absorbing it. Peyer patches are lymphoid aggregates of the ileum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, surface mucous cells and alkaline mucus layer); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs inhibit COX and reduce prostaglandin-dependent mucus and bicarbonate secretion, causing erosions and ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: misoprostol is used to prevent NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has abdominal distension. Full-thickness rectal biopsy shows no ganglion cells between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers or in the submucosa. Loss of the ganglia between the muscle layers impairs which function?","choices":{"A":"Local secretion by mucosal glands","B":"Regulation of submucosal blood flow","C":"Coordinated propulsive motility","D":"Absorption of nutrients by enterocytes","E":"Antigen sampling by Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the circular and longitudinal layers form the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus, which controls tone, rhythmic contraction, and conduction of peristaltic waves and relaxes sphincters. Without it the aganglionic segment stays contracted and does not propel stool. Local secretion and submucosal blood flow are mainly regulated by the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. Absorption by enterocytes is an epithelial function. Antigen sampling is a lymphoid function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus function); First Aid 2025 GI (Hirschsprung disease; suction biopsy, which samples mucosa and submucosa, is the usual first test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease is absent ganglion cells in both plexuses of the distal colon from failed neural crest migration; diagnosed by rectal suction biopsy; associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: empty rectum with explosive stool on rectal exam.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes part in a study of a toxin that selectively destroys the enteric motor neurons, lying between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers, that relax gut smooth muscle. Failed opening of which of the following would be expected?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Upper esophageal sphincter","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Puborectalis sling","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptidergic relaxing neurons of the Auerbach plexus open the smooth muscle sphincters of the gut, including the pyloric and ileocecal sphincters, so their loss leaves the ileocecal sphincter contracted; their transmitters are nitric oxide, VIP, and ATP. The external anal sphincter and the puborectalis sling are skeletal muscle under somatic control. The upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle driven by vagal motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus. The diaphragmatic crura are skeletal muscle that reinforce the lower esophageal sphincter from outside. The submucosal plexus has only a minimal role in sphincter tone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, sphincter control by the myenteric plexus, p13; section 4, striated muscle innervation, p14); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: myenteric inhibitory neurons that release NO and VIP relax the LES, pylorus, and ileocecal sphincter; achalasia is loss of these neurons in the distal esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls secretion and blood flow and has little effect on sphincter tone (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — inhibitory enteric neurons release VIP, nitric oxide, and ATP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man receives a small-bowel transplant, which severs all extrinsic vagal and sympathetic nerves to the graft. Within days the graft shows coordinated peristalsis and local secretion. Which explains this function?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic enteric plexuses remain","B":"Sympathetic fibers regenerate","C":"Vagal fibers regrow into the graft","D":"Smooth muscle needs no neurons","E":"Hormones replace neural control"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The enteric nervous system is an intrinsic network of neurons and glia in two plexuses inside the gut wall. The myenteric plexus coordinates peristalsis and the submucosal plexus controls local secretion, and both work without extrinsic input, which only modulates them. Severed sympathetic and vagal fibers do not regenerate into a graft within days. Smooth muscle pacing alone does not coordinate peristalsis. Hormones modulate gut function but do not replace neural reflex circuits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation, and section 4, intrinsic autonomy); Guyton Ch. 63 (enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system can function independently of extrinsic nerves, which modulate it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic input increases and sympathetic input decreases peristalsis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — extrinsic modulation of the enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has a brainstem stroke with hoarseness and difficulty initiating swallowing. Manometry shows weak contractions in the upper esophagus with normal peristalsis in the distal esophagus. Which tissue in the upper esophagus explains this selective weakness?","choices":{"A":"Visceral smooth muscle","B":"Abundant myenteric neurons","C":"Serosal covering","D":"Striated skeletal muscle","E":"Cardiac gland mucosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The upper third of the esophagus is striated skeletal muscle supplied by somatic motor neurons from the nucleus ambiguus, which a brainstem stroke can damage. The distal esophagus is smooth muscle driven by the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus and the myenteric plexus, which can keep running on its own, so distal peristalsis is preserved. Visceral smooth muscle is the distal tissue, the one spared. Myenteric neurons are not selectively abundant in the upper third. The thoracic esophagus has adventitia, not serosa. Cardiac glands are mucosal glands and do not contract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions and innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: upper 1/3 striated, middle 1/3 mixed, lower 1/3 smooth muscle (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 2, section 4; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to striated muscle or its brainstem control.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — neural coordination of swallowing","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with Raynaud phenomenon and sclerodactyly has heartburn and dysphagia. Manometry shows absent contractions in the distal esophagus with low lower sphincter pressure and normal proximal contractions. Which tissue is affected?","choices":{"A":"Striated muscle","B":"Pharyngeal constrictors","C":"Diaphragmatic crura","D":"Cricopharyngeus","E":"Smooth muscle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Scleroderma causes atrophy and fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle, which makes up the distal esophagus and the lower sphincter, so distal peristalsis and sphincter tone fail. Striated muscle of the proximal third is spared, which fits the normal proximal contractions. The pharyngeal constrictors and cricopharyngeus are striated muscles above or at the upper esophagus. The diaphragmatic crura reinforce the sphincter externally but are skeletal muscle and not the site of disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions); First Aid 2025 GI (scleroderma esophageal dysmotility).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma causes esophageal smooth muscle atrophy with decreased LES pressure and distal dysmotility, leading to reflux and dysphagia; part of CREST (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: GERD from scleroderma can cause strictures and Barrett esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has injury to both vagal trunks during a thoracic operation. Afterward, balloon distension of the distal esophagus still triggers peristaltic waves in that segment. What mediates these waves?","choices":{"A":"Nucleus ambiguus output","B":"Myenteric plexus reflexes","C":"Glossopharyngeal afferents","D":"Phrenic nerve reflexes","E":"Sympathetic celiac outflow"},"correct":"B","explanation":"When vagal fibers to the distal esophagus are interrupted, the intrinsic myenteric plexus can still start secondary peristaltic waves in response to local distension. The nucleus ambiguus sends its output through the vagus, which is injured, and it supplies striated muscle. Glossopharyngeal afferents trigger the pharyngeal phase of swallowing, not local esophageal reflexes. The phrenic nerve supplies the diaphragm. Sympathetic celiac outflow suppresses esophageal motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, intrinsic autonomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and begins at the point of distension (Costanzo Ch. 8; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6, secondary peristalsis). COMLEX: intrinsic reflexes keep some motility after vagal injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — primary versus secondary esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl02_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man with occasional heartburn has biopsies taken just above the gastroesophageal junction. The pathologist notes branched tubular mucous glands in the lamina propria that resemble gastric cardiac glands. What is the main function of these glands?","choices":{"A":"Protecting against refluxed acid","B":"Lubricating the upper esophagus","C":"Secreting acid into the lumen","D":"Secreting pepsinogen for digestion","E":"Absorbing fluid from the bolus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These are esophageal cardiac glands. Their neutral mucus protects the distal esophageal mucosa from regurgitated acidic gastric juice; when reflux overwhelms them, heartburn and GERD follow. Lubricating the upper esophagus is the role of the esophageal glands proper in the submucosa, which are concentrated in the upper half. Acid is secreted by gastric parietal cells and pepsinogen by chief cells, not by these mucous glands. The esophagus does not absorb fluid from the bolus. Pawlina assigns luminal lubrication to the slightly acidic mucus of the submucosal glands proper, not the cardiac glands near the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal cardiac glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophageal cardiac glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD complications include erosive esophagitis, strictures, and Barrett esophagus (First Aid 2025 GI GERD). COMLEX: heartburn reflects acid and pepsin contact with distal mucosa.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury of the distal esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Acid is secreted by gastric parietal cells and pepsinogen by chief cells, not by these mucous glands.","B":"Pawlina assigns luminal lubrication to the slightly acidic mucus of the submucosal glands proper, not the cardiac glands near the stomach."}},{"id":"sdl02_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist counts submucosal mucous glands with dilated ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium in sections taken at several levels of an autopsy esophagus. Where are these glands most densely concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment below the hiatus","B":"Upper half of the esophagus","C":"Region just above the cardia","D":"Evenly along its length","E":"Distal third of smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Submucosal tubuloalveolar glands with dilated squamous-lined ducts are esophageal glands proper. They are scattered along the whole esophagus but are most densely concentrated in its upper half, where their viscous mucus lubricates the lumen. The region just above the cardia is where esophageal cardiac glands, which lie in the lamina propria, are found. The abdominal segment and the distal smooth-muscle third are not where glands proper are densest. Their distribution is not even, because they cluster in the upper half. Pawlina places the esophageal cardiac glands, not the submucosal glands proper, in the terminal part of the esophagus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper concentrated in the upper half, p15); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, comparison table, p20).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal glands proper lie in the submucosa and esophageal cardiac glands in the lamina propria (lecture SDL 2). COMLEX: cardiac glands sit near the gastroesophageal junction, where their neutral mucus meets refluxed acid (lecture SDL 2).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 5 — esophageal versus gastric glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Their distribution is not even, because they cluster in the upper half.","C":"Pawlina places the esophageal cardiac glands, not the submucosal glands proper, in the terminal part of the esophagus."}},{"id":"sdl02_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endoscopy for intermittent dysphagia. Small cysts are found in the upper esophagus, and biopsy shows dilated submucosal ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium and filled with mucus. Which property of the secretion favors these cysts?","choices":{"A":"High bicarbonate content","B":"Enzymatic digestion of ducts","C":"Neutral pH of the mucus","D":"High viscosity of the mucus","E":"Serous watery composition"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ducts belong to esophageal glands proper, which secrete a viscous, slightly acidic mucus. Its viscosity favors transient cysts in the ducts. High bicarbonate content is typical of Brunner gland and pancreatic secretion. These glands make mucus, not enzymes that digest ducts. Neutral mucus is the product of the cardiac glands, not the glands proper. A serous watery secretion would flow freely and is not what these glands make. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands proper); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (viscous secretion; transient cysts in the ducts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal epithelium is nonkeratinized stratified squamous (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: esophageal glands proper are most concentrated in the upper half of the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — structural causes of dysphagia","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four histologic layers of the alimentary canal from lumen outward.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with long-standing Barrett esophagus has endoscopic resection of a small nodule. Pathology shows adenocarcinoma that has broken through the muscularis mucosae into the underlying connective tissue layer but not into muscle. His team now recommends esophagectomy with lymph node dissection, which they would not have advised had the tumor stayed above the muscularis mucosae. Which feature of the newly invaded layer best explains this change?","choices":{"A":"It contains the myenteric nerve plexus","B":"It is made of longitudinal smooth muscle","C":"It holds the larger lymph and blood vessels","D":"It is covered by a simple squamous mesothelium","E":"It holds the lymphoid nodules of the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The layer beneath the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa, dense irregular connective tissue that holds the larger blood and lymphatic vessels, so invasion into it gives tumor cells access to channels that drain toward regional nodes. The myenteric plexus lies between the circular and longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa, which the tumor has not reached. Longitudinal smooth muscle forms the outer muscularis externa, and the tumor stopped short of muscle. A simple squamous mesothelium covers the serosa of intraperitoneal organs, and the thoracic esophagus has an adventitia instead. The lamina propria holds GALT nodules, capillaries and small nerve fibers, and it lies above the muscularis mucosae, where the tumor had already been. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 1, four histologic layers; SDL 8, section 3, adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosa is dense irregular connective tissue holding large vessels, lymphatics and the Meissner plexus (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 2). COMLEX: tumor depth past the muscularis mucosae raises concern for nodal spread.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The myenteric plexus lies between the circular and","B":"Longitudinal smooth muscle forms the outer muscularis externa,","D":"A simple squamous mesothelium covers the serosa of","E":"The lamina propria holds GALT nodules, capillaries and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has rectal bleeding and a firm, ulcerated mass in the lowest third of her anal canal, below the transition zone. Biopsy is taken from the edge of the mass. Based on the epithelium that normally lines this site, which tumor pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lymphoid cells invading mucosal glands","B":"Nests of squamous cells with keratin","C":"Mucin producing glands of columnar cells","D":"Uniform cells with salt and pepper nuclei","E":"Spindle cells in fascicles staining for KIT"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lower third of the anal canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium that protects against abrasion, and anal carcinomas recapitulate the epithelium they arise from, so a tumor there usually shows squamous differentiation. Lymphoid cells invading glands form the lymphoepithelial lesions of MALT lymphoma in glandular mucosa such as the stomach. Gland-forming adenocarcinoma recapitulates the columnar rectal epithelium of the upper anal canal, not the lowest third. Salt-and-pepper chromatin marks neuroendocrine tumors, which arise from diffuse neuroendocrine cells rather than the surface lining. Spindle cells positive for KIT describe a stromal tumor arising from interstitial cells of Cajal in the gut wall. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (anal canal tumors); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2; SDL 9, section 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lower anal canal is lined by stratified squamous epithelium, so its carcinomas are usually squamous (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: tumors of the anal canal below the pectinate line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue, the source of gastric MALT lymphoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lymphoid cells invading glands form the lymphoepithelial lesions","C":"Gland-forming adenocarcinoma recapitulates the columnar rectal epithelium of","D":"Salt-and-pepper chromatin marks neuroendocrine tumors, which arise from","E":"Spindle cells positive for KIT describe a stromal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses by location and function.","batch":3,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from rural Brazil has progressive dysphagia, and serology is positive for Trypanosoma cruzi. Biopsy shows destruction of the neurons between the muscle layers of the distal esophagus. Which function is lost?","choices":{"A":"LES relaxation","B":"Gland secretion","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Bolus lubrication","E":"Gastric acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chagas disease destroys neurons of the myenteric plexus, including the inhibitory NO and VIP neurons that relax the lower esophageal sphincter, producing secondary achalasia. Gland secretion and mucosal blood flow are mainly controlled by the submucosal plexus. Bolus lubrication comes from the esophageal glands proper. Gastric acid output is controlled at the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 3, myenteric plexus and sphincter control); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia and Chagas disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary achalasia can result from Chagas disease (First Aid 2025 GI achalasia). COMLEX: barium swallow shows bird beak narrowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — achalasia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the muscle type transitions and their innervation along the esophagus.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has drooping eyelids and double vision that worsen as the day goes on. She now feels solid food stick at the base of her neck soon after swallowing, mostly late in meals. Serum antibodies against the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor are positive. Weakness of which part of the esophagus best explains her dysphagia?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis mucosae of the esophagus","B":"Lower esophageal sphincter muscle","C":"Myenteric neurons of the middle third","D":"Smooth muscle of the distal third","E":"Upper third of the esophageal wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Antibodies against the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor impair skeletal neuromuscular junctions, and the upper third of the esophageal muscularis externa is striated muscle driven by somatic motor neurons of the vagus from the nucleus ambiguus. The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer that moves mucosal folds and does not propel the bolus. The lower esophageal sphincter is smooth muscle controlled through the myenteric plexus, so it has no skeletal endplates for these antibodies. Myenteric neurons relay vagal preganglionic input to smooth muscle and are not part of the skeletal neuromuscular junction. The distal third is purely smooth muscle driven by enteric neurons, which these antibodies do not target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 4, muscle type transitions and innervation); First Aid 2025 (myasthenia gravis autoantibody).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle supplied from the nucleus ambiguus, and the lower third is smooth muscle controlled through the myenteric plexus (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 2). COMLEX: disorders of skeletal muscle or its junction cause high dysphagia, while scleroderma weakens the distal smooth muscle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — swallowing coordination","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle","B":"The lower esophageal sphincter is smooth muscle controlled","C":"Myenteric neurons relay vagal preganglionic input to smooth","D":"The distal third is purely smooth muscle driven"}},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Locate and explain the functions of esophageal glands proper and esophageal cardiac glands.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with occasional heartburn has endoscopy that shows no columnar mucosa above the gastroesophageal junction. A biopsy taken 1 cm above the junction shows intact stratified squamous epithelium over a lamina propria that contains branched tubular glands of columnar cells secreting neutral mucus. No goblet cells are present. What is the best interpretation of these glands?","choices":{"A":"Ectopic fundic mucosa with parietal cells","B":"Normal glands native to this level","C":"Submucosal esophageal glands proper","D":"Invasive well differentiated adenocarcinoma","E":"Barrett intestinal metaplasia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Branched tubular mucous glands in the lamina propria of the terminal esophagus are the esophageal cardiac glands, a normal structure whose neutral mucus protects the distal mucosa from refluxed acid. Fundic mucosa contains parietal and chief cells, whereas these glands are made of mucous cells. Esophageal glands proper are tubuloalveolar glands of the submucosa, most dense in the upper half, with ducts lined by stratified squamous epithelium. An adenocarcinoma would show atypical mucin-producing glands invading the stroma, not orderly glands under intact squamous mucosa. Barrett esophagus requires columnar surface epithelium with goblet cells, and here the surface is squamous with no goblet cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 5, esophageal glands; SDL 8, section 3, Barrett morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal cardiac glands sit in the lamina propria near the gastroesophageal junction and secrete neutral mucus, whereas glands proper sit in the submucosa (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 2). COMLEX: Barrett esophagus requires goblet cells on biopsy, so mucous glands under squamous mucosa do not qualify.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — cardiac-gland mucus in the reflux barrier","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Fundic mucosa contains parietal and chief cells, whereas","C":"Esophageal glands proper are tubuloalveolar glands of the","D":"An adenocarcinoma would show atypical mucin-producing glands invading","E":"Barrett esophagus requires columnar surface epithelium with goblet"}},{"id":"trial_sdl02_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Correlate epithelial types and mucosal specializations with protection, absorption, and secretion in the GI tract.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has a total colectomy with end ileostomy for ulcerative colitis. In the first weeks she passes 1.5 L per day of watery effluent and becomes lightheaded on standing, with a serum sodium of 131 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Loss of which epithelial function best explains her fluid and salt losses?","choices":{"A":"Gastric secretion of bicarbonate rich mucus","B":"Paneth cell secretion of lysozyme","C":"Colonic resorption of water and electrolytes","D":"Digestion by small bowel brush border enzymes","E":"Squamous protection of the lower anal canal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The simple columnar epithelium of the large intestine mainly resorbs water and electrolytes, so removing the colon leaves ileal effluent unconcentrated and causes fluid and sodium loss. The bicarbonate-rich gastric mucus gel protects the stomach surface and is untouched by colectomy. Paneth cells at the crypt base secrete lysozyme and defensins for innate immunity, not fluid transport. The small bowel brush border remains in place, and its enzymes digest nutrients rather than reclaim the last of the fluid. The stratified squamous lining of the lower anal canal protects against abrasion and does not reabsorb fluid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 2, section 2, absorption and protection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel enterocytes absorb nutrients through a microvillus brush border, while colonic columnar cells mainly resorb water and electrolytes (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 2). COMLEX: a new ileostomy can cause dehydration and hyponatremia from high output.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 1 — gallbladder wall histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The bicarbonate-rich gastric mucus gel protects the stomach","B":"Paneth cells at the crypt base secrete lysozyme","D":"The small bowel brush border remains in place,","E":"The stratified squamous lining of the lower anal"}}]},{"sdlNumber":3,"title":"SDL 3 — Histology of the Foregut part 2","questions":[{"id":"sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"In which region of the stomach do the gastric pits extend through about half the thickness of the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac region","B":"Fundic region","C":"Esophagogastric junction","D":"Pyloric region","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pyloric region has deep gastric pits that occupy about half of the mucosal thickness, opening onto branched, coiled pyloric glands with wide lumina. The cardiac region has relatively shallow pits over tubular mucous glands. The fundic region has shallow pits, about one-fourth to one-third of the mucosal thickness, with long fundic glands below. The esophagogastric junction is lined by squamous epithelium on its esophageal side and lacks gastric pits there. The duodenal bulb has villi and crypts rather than gastric pits. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands empty into deep gastric pits that occupy about half the thickness of the mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells in the antrum and duodenum secrete gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: pit depth and gland shape identify the gastric region on a slide.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"A histology student examines glands that surround the esophageal orifice of the stomach. Which cell type predominates in these glands?","choices":{"A":"Mucous gland cells","B":"Zymogenic chief cells","C":"Parietal cells","D":"Enteroendocrine G cells","E":"Paneth cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Glands around the esophageal orifice are cardiac glands, tubular glands made up mostly of mucus-secreting cells. Chief cells predominate in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck of fundic glands. G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands. Paneth cells sit at the base of intestinal crypts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, cardiac region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (cardiac glands are composed of mucus-secreting cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett metaplasia arises in the distal esophagus just above the gastroesophageal junction (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: mucous cardiac glands resemble the esophageal cardiac glands.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands.","B":"Chief cells predominate in the base of fundic glands.","C":"Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck of fundic glands.","E":"Paneth cells sit at the base of intestinal crypts."}},{"id":"sdl03_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell type predominates in the deep base of the fundic glands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Surface mucous cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chief cells are concentrated in the base of fundic glands. Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck and upper part of the gland. Mucous neck cells are localized to the neck, among the parietal cells. Surface mucous cells line the surface and the gastric pits. Stem cells lie in the isthmus, between the pit and the gland. Pawlina places mucous neck cells in the neck region of the gastric gland, not its deep base. Pawlina notes surface mucous cells line the inner surface of the stomach and the gastric pits, not the base of the fundic glands. Pawlina describes stem cells in the isthmus of the fundic gland as replicating and differentiating to renew the epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, which is activated to pepsin by acid (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: chief cells are basophilic on H&E.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — gastrin, histamine, and somatostatin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells are most numerous in the neck and upper part of the gland.","B":"Pawlina places mucous neck cells in the neck region of the gastric gland, not its deep base.","D":"Pawlina notes surface mucous cells line the inner surface of the stomach and the gastric pits, not the base of the fundic glands.","E":"Pawlina describes stem cells in the isthmus of the fundic gland as replicating and differentiating to renew the epithelium."}},{"id":"sdl03_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric epithelial cell secretes intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Chief cells","B":"Mucous neck cells","C":"Surface mucous cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Parietal cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal (oxyntic) cells secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor, a 44-kDa glycoprotein that binds vitamin B12 for absorption in the distal ileum. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and gastric lipase. Mucous neck cells secrete a soluble mucus that lubricates the gland lumen. Surface mucous cells secrete the insoluble alkaline mucus coat. ECL cells release histamine that stimulates parietal cells. In Pawlina, chief cells secrete pepsinogen and a weak lipase. Pawlina describes mucous neck cells as secreting a less alkaline soluble mucus. Surface mucous cells produce the high-alkaline insoluble mucus in Pawlina. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (intrinsic factor is a 44-kDa glycoprotein).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells make gastric acid and intrinsic factor; autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor and an intact terminal ileum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"ECL cells release histamine that stimulates parietal cells.","A":"In Pawlina, chief cells secrete pepsinogen and a weak lipase.","B":"Pawlina describes mucous neck cells as secreting a less alkaline soluble mucus.","C":"Surface mucous cells produce the high-alkaline insoluble mucus in Pawlina."}},{"id":"sdl03_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"In H&E sections of fundic glands, chief cells show strongly basophilic basal cytoplasm. Which cellular feature produces this staining?","choices":{"A":"Abundant rough ER","B":"Intracellular canaliculi","C":"Apical mucin granules","D":"Abundant mitochondria","E":"Tubulovesicular membranes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Chief cells have the morphology of protein-secreting cells: a basal nucleus, basal cytoplasm packed with rough endoplasmic reticulum whose ribosomal RNA stains basophilic, and apical zymogen granules. Abundant mitochondria, intracellular canaliculi, and a tubulovesicular membrane system are features of parietal cells and produce their eosinophilic staining. Apical mucin granules belong to surface mucous cells and appear pale on H&E. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (chief cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic (pink) and chief cells are basophilic (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: staining pattern reflects the dominant organelle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen as an inactive proenzyme. Which condition in the gastric lumen converts it to active pepsin?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin cleavage","B":"Enterokinase action","C":"Acidic pH below 5","D":"Bile salt binding","E":"Alkaline pH above 8"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen undergoes autocatalytic cleavage to pepsin on contact with acidic gastric juice at a pH below 5, with an optimum around pH 1.8 to 3.5; pepsin is an endopeptidase that starts protein digestion. Trypsin cleaves pancreatic zymogens in the duodenum, and enterokinase activates trypsinogen to trypsin. Bile salts emulsify fat rather than activate proteases. An alkaline pH inactivates pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, chief cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pepsinogen and pepsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pepsinogen is converted to pepsin in the presence of H+ (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: acid suppression reduces gastric pepsin activity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion through the H+/K+-ATPase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"In autoimmune atrophic gastritis, what is the principal reason that even crystalline oral vitamin B12 is malabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric acid","B":"Loss of intrinsic factor","C":"Loss of bile secretion","D":"Loss of ileal receptors","E":"Loss of pancreatic proteases"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parietal cells are the gastric source of intrinsic factor. When autoimmune destruction removes them, B12 does not form the B12 and intrinsic factor complex needed for receptor-mediated uptake in the distal ileum, which produces pernicious anemia. Crystalline B12 is not bound to food protein, so loss of gastric acid, which with pepsin frees dietary B12, does not explain its malabsorption. Bile secretion is unaffected by gastritis and does not carry B12 to the ileum. The ileal receptors are intact in this disease. Pancreatic proteases are not affected by gastritis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pernicious anemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and to intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and raises the risk of pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: macrocytic anemia with neurologic signs suggests B12 deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis causes achlorhydria and markedly elevated serum gastrin. What causes the gastrin elevation?","choices":{"A":"Direct antibody stimulation of G cells","B":"Increased vagal drive to G cells","C":"Reduced renal gastrin clearance","D":"Gastrin release by ECL cells","E":"Loss of acid feedback on G cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Luminal acid normally provides negative feedback on antral G cells. With parietal cells destroyed, the antral lumen is no longer acidic, the feedback is lost, and G cells secrete gastrin continuously, which leads to G cell and ECL cell hyperplasia. The autoantibodies target parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, not G cells. Vagal drive is not the mechanism of the elevation. Renal clearance is not reduced in this disease. ECL cells release histamine, not gastrin. Costanzo describes ECL cells as releasing histamine onto parietal cells in response to gastrin, while gastrin itself comes from G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis causes hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia, with risk of gastric cancer (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia with achlorhydria points away from gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The autoantibodies target parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, not G cells.","D":"Costanzo describes ECL cells as releasing histamine onto parietal cells in response to gastrin, while gastrin itself comes from G cells."}},{"id":"sdl03_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with chronic atrophic gastritis has lost gastric pepsin activity, yet protein digestion and absorption remain adequate. What explains this preserved digestion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Salivary proteases","C":"Gastric lipase activity","D":"Colonic bacterial enzymes","E":"Intestinal pepsin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases, including trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases, work at neutral to alkaline pH in the duodenum and hydrolyze dietary protein into absorbable peptides and amino acids, so loss of gastric pepsin is compensated. Saliva contains amylase and lingual lipase rather than significant proteases. Gastric lipase digests fat. Colonic bacteria act after the main site of protein absorption. The intestine does not secrete pepsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, chief cell dysfunction and protein digestion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsinogen is activated by enterokinase and then activates the other pancreatic zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: protein malabsorption suggests pancreatic insufficiency rather than gastric loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis and pernicious anemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"The unstirred layer beneath the gastric mucus gel stays near pH 7 while the lumen is near pH 1. Which component chiefly creates this alkaline microenvironment?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction sealing of cells","B":"Prostaglandin vasodilation","C":"Epithelial bicarbonate secretion","D":"Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel","E":"Rapid epithelial restitution"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Surface epithelial cells secrete bicarbonate into the fluid trapped beneath the mucus gel, creating an unstirred alkaline layer with a surface pH near 7 that neutralizes back-diffusing hydrogen ions and inactivates pepsin. Tight junctions prevent back-diffusion into the lamina propria but do not alkalinize the surface. Prostaglandins stimulate mucus and bicarbonate and maintain blood flow, but the alkaline layer itself is made by bicarbonate. Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel is a result of the alkaline layer, not its cause. Restitution repairs defects. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial bicarbonate secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PGE2 increases mucus and bicarbonate secretion and decreases acid; NSAIDs remove this protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol restores prostaglandin protection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Pepsin inactivation by the mucus gel is a result of the alkaline layer, not its cause."}},{"id":"sdl03_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Nonselective NSAIDs injure the gastric mucosa mainly by inhibiting cyclooxygenase. Which change follows?","choices":{"A":"Increased gastrin release","B":"Reduced PGE2 synthesis","C":"Urease ammonia production","D":"Blocked H2 receptors","E":"Direct vagal stimulation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cyclooxygenase inhibition suppresses PGE2 synthesis, which decreases bicarbonate and mucus secretion, reduces mucosal blood flow, and impairs restitution. Increased gastrin release follows loss of acid feedback, not COX inhibition. Urease ammonia production is a mechanism of H. pylori. Blocked H2 receptors would reduce acid and protect the mucosa. Direct vagal stimulation of acid occurs with brain injury in Cushing ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2, decreasing gastric mucosal protection; Curling ulcer follows burns and Cushing ulcer follows brain injury (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: co-prescribe misoprostol or a PPI for high-risk NSAID users.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":1,"stem":"Within minutes of a superficial gastric epithelial injury, the defect is sealed without new cell division. Which process accomplishes this?","choices":{"A":"Migration of neck cells","B":"Rapid stem cell mitosis","C":"Fibroblast scar formation","D":"Mucus plug secretion","E":"Platelet clot formation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Restitution seals small epithelial defects as gland neck cells migrate along the basal lamina to restore continuity, without requiring immediate mitosis. Stem cell mitosis renews surface cells over days, not minutes. Fibroblast scarring follows deeper injury such as ulcers. Mucus slows pepsin and abrasion but does not close the epithelial gap. Platelet clotting stops bleeding but does not restore epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, epithelial restitution and continuous cell renewal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: erosions are limited to the mucosa and heal without scarring; ulcers extend deeper (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: rapid restitution limits superficial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"The nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the esophagus changes abruptly at the esophagogastric junction to which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Stratified columnar","B":"Pseudostratified columnar","C":"Simple cuboidal","D":"Simple columnar","E":"Transitional"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction the stratified squamous lining changes abruptly to the simple columnar epithelium of the stomach, made up of surface mucous cells. Stratified columnar epithelium lines some large ducts. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airway. Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the thyroid follicles. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, lining epithelium); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (esophagogastric junction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus replaces squamous epithelium with intestinal columnar epithelium and goblet cells (First Aid 2025 GI Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the squamocolumnar junction is the endoscopic landmark for metaplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — Barrett metaplasia at the esophagogastric junction","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Stratified columnar epithelium lines some large ducts.","B":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airway.","C":"Simple cuboidal epithelium lines small ducts and the thyroid follicles.","E":"Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."}},{"id":"sdl03_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"The muscularis externa of the stomach differs from that of the esophagus by an additional layer. Which layer is this?","choices":{"A":"Outer circular layer","B":"Inner oblique layer","C":"Striated upper layer","D":"Middle longitudinal layer","E":"Submucosal muscle layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The stomach has three smooth muscle layers: an innermost oblique layer deep to the circular layer, an inner circular layer, and an outer longitudinal layer. The esophagus has two layers, inner circular and outer longitudinal. The outer layer of both organs is longitudinal, not circular. The striated muscle of the upper esophagus is absent from the stomach. There is no middle longitudinal layer or submucosal muscle layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, muscularis externa); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (stomach muscularis externa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach adds an innermost oblique layer deep to the inner circular and outer longitudinal layers (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 5; Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: oblique fibers aid churning of chyme.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — muscle type transitions along the esophagus","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The outer layer of both organs is longitudinal, not circular.","C":"The striated muscle of the upper esophagus is absent from the stomach.","D":"There is no middle longitudinal layer.","E":"There is no submucosal muscle layer."}},{"id":"sdl03_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":1,"stem":"Unlike the esophagus, the stomach has no submucosal glands. In which layer do all gastric glands lie?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Adventitia","D":"Muscularis externa","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric glands are mucosal, confined to the lamina propria and opening into the bases of gastric pits; they include cardiac, fundic, and pyloric glands. The submucosa holds esophageal glands proper and duodenal Brunner glands, not gastric glands. The muscularis mucosae lies beneath the gland bases. The adventitia and muscularis externa are outer layers without glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, glands); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (gastric mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: submucosal glands identify the esophagus and the duodenum (Brunner glands) on a slide (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: gland layer separates esophageal from gastric biopsies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal glands proper and cardiac glands","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The submucosa holds esophageal glands proper and duodenal Brunner glands, not gastric glands.","B":"The muscularis mucosae lies beneath the gland bases.","C":"The adventitia is an outer layer and does not contain gastric glands.","D":"The muscularis externa is an outer layer and does not contain gastric glands."}},{"id":"sdl03_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with dyspepsia has an endoscopic biopsy labeled gastric body. The section shows deep pits occupying half of the mucosa, coiled glands with wide lumina lined by mucous cells, and many gastrin-producing cells. Where was the biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Gastric fundus","B":"Gastric body","C":"Cardiac region","D":"Pyloric antrum","E":"Duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Deep pits occupying about half of the mucosa, branched coiled glands with wide lumina, mucous lining cells, and abundant G cells define the pyloric region in the antrum. The fundus and body share fundic glands with shallow pits and many parietal and chief cells. The cardiac region has shallow pits over tubular mucous glands without concentrated G cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and submucosal Brunner glands. Pawlina limits cardiac glands to the narrow cardia around the esophageal orifice, whereas deep pits over half the mucosa with coiled wide-lumen glands mark the pyloric antrum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyloric glands are lined mainly by mucus secreting cells (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: H. pylori gastritis affects the antrum first.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — source and release triggers of gastrin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The fundus and body share fundic glands with shallow pits and many parietal and chief cells.","C":"Pawlina limits cardiac glands to the narrow cardia around the esophageal orifice, whereas deep pits over half the mucosa with coiled wide-lumen glands mark the pyloric antrum."}},{"id":"sdl03_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with vitiligo has fatigue and macrocytic anemia. Serum antibodies against the parietal cell proton pump are present. Which gastric region is the main target of this destruction?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and body","B":"Antrum and pylorus","C":"Cardia and esophagus","D":"Duodenal bulb","E":"Lesser curvature"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Parietal cells are concentrated in the fundic glands of the fundus and body, so autoimmune gastritis targets these regions. The antrum and pylorus contain pyloric glands with few parietal cells and are spared; H. pylori gastritis begins there instead. The cardia has mucous cardiac glands, and the esophagus has squamous mucosa. The duodenal bulb is intestinal mucosa. The lesser curvature is an anatomic margin, not a gland region. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, fundic region, and section 3, parietal cell loss); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis affects the body and fundus; H. pylori affects the antrum first and spreads to the body (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: autoimmune disease clusters, including vitiligo and thyroiditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis anatomic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man undergoes antrectomy for a bleeding ulcer. Afterward his serum gastrin falls well below his preoperative value. Removal of which cells explains this change?","choices":{"A":"Fundic ECL cells","B":"Pyloric G cells","C":"Cardiac mucous cells","D":"Fundic chief cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastrin-producing G cells are concentrated in the pyloric glands of the antrum, so antrectomy removes the main source of gastrin. Fundic ECL cells release histamine and remain after antrectomy. Cardiac mucous cells secrete mucus. Fundic chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Duodenal S cells secrete secretin, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, pyloric region, and section 2, enteroendocrine cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (pyloric glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released by G cells in the antrum and duodenum in response to peptides, amino acids, and gastric distension (First Aid 2025 GI GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: antrectomy reduces gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — gastrin regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy. In the neck of the fundic glands are large triangular cells with central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm full of mitochondria and canaliculi. What do these cells secrete into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsinogen and lipase","B":"Soluble neck mucus","C":"Histamine","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Hydrochloric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large triangular eosinophilic cells with central nuclei, abundant mitochondria, and intracellular canaliculi in the gland neck are parietal cells. Their apical H+/K+-ATPase secretes hydrochloric acid, and they also secrete intrinsic factor. Pepsinogen and lipase come from basophilic chief cells in the gland base. Soluble neck mucus comes from mucous neck cells. Histamine is a paracrine product of ECL cells, and somatostatin of D cells, released toward the lamina propria rather than the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, parietal cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (parietal cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid, stimulated by histamine, ACh, and gastrin and inhibited by somatostatin (First Aid 2025 GI GI secretory products). COMLEX: PPIs block the H+/K+-ATPase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — cellular mechanism of HCl secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old healthy volunteer in a physiology study receives a gastrin infusion, which raises gastric acid output. Pretreatment with an H2 receptor blocker abolishes most of this rise. Gastrin acts mainly through which paracrine product?","choices":{"A":"D cell somatostatin","B":"Chief cell pepsinogen","C":"Mucous neck cell mucus","D":"ECL cell histamine","E":"Parietal cell intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin raises acid mainly by stimulating enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells in the fundic glands to release histamine, which acts on parietal cell H2 receptors; that is why H2 blockade abolishes most of the response. D cell somatostatin inhibits acid and gastrin release. Chief cell pepsinogen is a digestive proenzyme, not a paracrine signal. Mucous neck cell mucus lubricates the gland. Parietal cell intrinsic factor binds B12. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells); First Aid 2025 GI (gastrin acts mainly through ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases acid secretion primarily through its effects on ECL cells, leading to histamine release, rather than through its direct effect on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce gastrin-driven acid.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — histamine and somatostatin control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old woman has a gastric biopsy stained with PAS. Tall columnar cells lining the surface and pits have large apical cups that stain intensely. Their secretion contributes which component of gastric defense?","choices":{"A":"Soluble lubricating gland mucus","B":"Acid into the gland lumen","C":"Insoluble alkaline gel coat","D":"Pepsinogen for protein digestion","E":"Intrinsic factor for B12"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall columnar cells with PAS-positive apical mucous cups lining the surface and pits are surface mucous cells. They secrete an insoluble visible mucus rich in bicarbonate and potassium that forms a protective alkaline gel. Soluble lubricating mucus in the gland lumen comes from mucous neck cells, which lack prominent apical cups. Acid and intrinsic factor come from parietal cells. Pepsinogen comes from chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (surface mucous cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: prostaglandins increase gastric mucus and bicarbonate secretion (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: the mucus layer limits pepsin contact with the epithelium.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man had a total gastrectomy for cancer 6 years ago and takes no supplements. He now has numbness in both feet and decreased vibration sense, hemoglobin 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and mean corpuscular volume 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which explains his findings?","choices":{"A":"Absent gastric pepsin","B":"Ileal bacterial overgrowth","C":"Reduced folate absorption","D":"Pancreatic protease loss","E":"Absent intrinsic factor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Total gastrectomy removes all parietal cells, the gastric source of intrinsic factor. Without it, B12 is not absorbed in the distal ileum, and liver stores are depleted over several years, producing megaloblastic anemia and posterior and lateral column demyelination. Absent pepsin reduces release of B12 from food but is not the main cause. Ileal bacterial overgrowth is not the main cause of this delayed, stores-depleted pattern. Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis without neurologic signs. Pancreatic proteases are preserved. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss and vitamin B12 malabsorption).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 absorption requires intrinsic factor; deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with subacute combined degeneration (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: give parenteral B12 after total gastrectomy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — pernicious anemia from loss of parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absent pepsin reduces release of B12 from food but is not the main cause.","D":"Pancreatic proteases only move B12 from R proteins onto intrinsic factor.","B":"Ileal bacterial overgrowth is not the main cause of this delayed pattern.","C":"Folate deficiency causes macrocytosis without neurologic signs."}},{"id":"sdl03_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman with antibodies to intrinsic factor has paresthesias, ataxia, and loss of position sense in both feet. Her vitamin B12 deficiency has gone untreated. Demyelination in which spinal cord region explains her neurologic signs?","choices":{"A":"Posterior and lateral columns","B":"Anterior horn motor neurons","C":"Anterior spinothalamic tracts","D":"Cerebellar vermis and flocculus","E":"Ventral nerve roots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe B12 deficiency causes demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord, producing sensory loss, ataxia, and weakness. Loss of position sense reflects the posterior columns, and the lateral columns carry the corticospinal tracts. Anterior horn neurons are lost in motor neuron diseases. Anterior spinothalamic tracts carry crude touch and are not the classic target. The cerebellar vermis and flocculus are brain structures, not spinal cord regions. Ventral roots carry motor fibers and are not the primary site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, hematologic and neurologic impairment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration (Bootcamp Gastroenterology), with demyelination of the posterior and lateral columns of the spinal cord (Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 3, section 3). COMLEX: neurologic damage may persist after treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — complications of autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long-standing autoimmune atrophic gastritis has a serum gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: 0–100 pg/mL). Endoscopy shows small nodules in the gastric body, and biopsy shows proliferating small uniform cells with neuroendocrine granules. Which cell type is most likely proliferating?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"ECL cells","C":"D cells","D":"EC cells","E":"Ghrelin cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Loss of acid removes negative feedback on antral G cells, and the resulting hypergastrinemia drives hyperplasia of the gastrin-responsive ECL cells in the body, which can form small nodules. G cells also become hyperplastic, but they lie in the antrum, not in body nodules. D cells secrete somatostatin and are not driven by gastrin. EC cells release serotonin. Ghrelin cells of the fundus modulate appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, enteroendocrine cells, and section 3, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic atrophic gastritis leads to hypochlorhydria and hypergastrinemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hypergastrinemia from achlorhydria is distinguished from gastrinoma by gastric pH.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 5 — gastric neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"G cells also become hyperplastic, but they lie in the antrum, not in body nodules.","C":"D cells secrete somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin and acid, and are not driven by gastrin.","E":"Ghrelin cells of the fundus modulate appetite.","D":"EC cells release serotonin and are not the gastrin-responsive population named in the lecture."}},{"id":"sdl03_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old man with epigastric pain has a positive stool antigen test. Biopsy shows curved rods within the mucus over the surface foveolar cells. Which bacterial mechanism lets the organism survive at this acidic site?","choices":{"A":"Bicarbonate secretion","B":"Proton pump inhibition","C":"Gastrin receptor blockade","D":"Capsule blocking acid entry","E":"Urease generating ammonia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"H. pylori uses urease to convert urea into an ammonia cloud that buffers local acid, the basis of the urea breath test, and uses flagella to move through mucus. Bicarbonate is secreted by the host epithelium, not the bacterium. The organism does not inhibit the proton pump. The organism does not block gastrin receptors. A capsule is not its mechanism of acid survival. Its cytotoxins, such as CagA, then damage tight junctions and the mucus gel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, Helicobacter pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is the most common cause of chronic gastritis, increases risk of peptic ulcer disease and MALT lymphoma, and affects the antrum first (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: urea breath test and stool antigen confirm infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A capsule is not its mechanism of acid survival.","A":"Bicarbonate is secreted by the host epithelium, not the bacterium.","B":"The organism does not inhibit the proton pump.","C":"The organism does not block gastrin receptors."}},{"id":"sdl03_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with burns over 40% of his body surface area develops upper GI bleeding on day 3. Endoscopy shows multiple superficial gastric erosions. Failure of which barrier component is most responsible?","choices":{"A":"Tight junction proteins","B":"Surface cell turnover","C":"Mucosal blood flow","D":"Mucus gel thickness","E":"Parietal cell number"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Severe burns cause hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia. Reduced blood flow lowers oxygen and bicarbonate delivery and fails to wash away back-diffused hydrogen ions, leading to local acidosis, cell death, and erosions (Curling ulcer). Tight junctions are disrupted by H. pylori toxins and aspirin. Surface cell turnover and mucus thickness fall secondarily but are not the initiating failure. Parietal cell number is unchanged. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, ischemia, shock, alcohol, and stress); First Aid 2025 GI (Curling ulcer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Curling ulcer follows burns from hypovolemia and mucosal ischemia; Cushing ulcer follows brain injury from vagal stimulation and increased acid (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: give stress ulcer prophylaxis to critically ill patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute stress gastropathy","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman takes high-dose aspirin for headaches and develops gastric erosions. Apart from inhibiting cyclooxygenase, aspirin injures the gastric surface by which direct action?","choices":{"A":"Damages hydrophobic membranes","B":"Stimulates gastrin release","C":"Blocks bicarbonate channels","D":"Activates pepsinogen in cells","E":"Raises vagal acid output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aspirin directly damages the hydrophobic apical cell membranes, allowing acid to leak down its electrochemical gradient into the tissue, in addition to suppressing PGE2. It does not stimulate gastrin release. Its effect on bicarbonate comes through loss of prostaglandins, not channel blockade. Pepsinogen is activated in the lumen, not inside cells. Vagal acid output rises in Cushing ulcer after brain injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, NSAIDs and aspirin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs reduce PGE2 and gastric mucosal protection (First Aid 2025 GI acute gastritis). COMLEX: misoprostol prevents NSAID-induced ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID-associated ulcer prevention","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl03_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has a biopsy labeled distal esophagus. It shows simple columnar surface cells, gastric pits, and glands packed with numerous parietal and chief cells in the lamina propria, with no glands in the submucosa. Where was the tissue taken?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophagus","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Duodenal bulb","D":"Gastric body","E":"Pyloric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Simple columnar epithelium, gastric pits, and mucosal glands rich in parietal and chief cells, without submucosal glands, define fundic mucosa of the gastric body. The distal esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and submucosal glands proper. The cardia has mucous glands with few parietal cells. The duodenal bulb has villi and Brunner glands in the submucosa. The pyloric antrum has deep pits over mucous glands with G cells. Pawlina describes cardiac glands as composed mainly of mucus-secreting cells, so glands packed with parietal and chief cells are not cardiac mucosa. Pyloric antrum glands in Pawlina are coiled and lined by mucous-like cells with only occasional parietal cells, not packed with parietal and chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, histologic comparison); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (fundic glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophagus has nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium; stomach has gastric glands with parietal and chief cells (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract histology). COMLEX: a mislabeled specimen is identified by its epithelium and glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types along the GI tract","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The distal esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and submucosal glands proper.","B":"Pawlina describes cardiac glands as composed mainly of mucus-secreting cells, so glands packed with parietal and chief cells are not cardiac mucosa.","E":"Pyloric antrum glands in Pawlina are coiled and lined by mucous-like cells with only occasional parietal cells, not packed with parietal and chief cells."}},{"id":"sdl03_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has leg edema and an albumin of 2.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Endoscopy shows giant folds in the body of the stomach resembling brain gyri, and biopsy shows foveolar hyperplasia with loss of parietal cells. Which normal structure is enlarged?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal villi","B":"Gastric rugae","C":"Plicae circulares","D":"Colonic haustra","E":"Esophageal papillae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric rugae are longitudinal folds of mucosa and submucosa that flatten when the stomach fills; in Ménétrier disease they become hypertrophied, with excess mucus production and protein loss. Intestinal villi are mucosal projections of the small intestine. Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds of the small intestine. Colonic haustra are sacculations of the colon. Esophageal papillae are connective tissue projections into the esophageal epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization); First Aid 2025 GI (Ménétrier disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ménétrier disease is gastric mucosal hyperplasia with hypertrophied rugae, excess mucus, protein loss, and parietal cell atrophy with reduced acid (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: protein-losing gastropathy presents with edema.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — hypertrophic gastropathy (Ménétrier disease)","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl03_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":2,"stem":"A pathologist compares the luminal surfaces of the esophagus and stomach with a hand lens. Only the gastric surface is divided by shallow grooves into small, slightly bulging, cobblestone-like patches that contain mucosa alone. What are these patches called?","choices":{"A":"Gastric rugae","B":"Plicae circulares","C":"Mamillated areas","D":"Intestinal villi","E":"Haustral folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Shallow grooves divide the gastric surface into slightly bulging patches called mamillated areas, which consist of mucosa only and slightly increase the surface area for secretion. The esophagus has a smooth surface with temporary longitudinal folds and no such areas. Gastric rugae are larger longitudinal folds that include submucosa and flatten as the stomach fills. Plicae circulares are permanent folds of the small intestine. Intestinal villi are finger-like mucosal projections of the small bowel. Haustral folds belong to the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 5, mucosal surface organization, p20); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Digestive System II) (mamillated areas consist only of mucosa).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach mucosa shows rugae, mamillated areas, and gastric pits, while the esophagus lacks gastric pits (lecture SDL 3). COMLEX: rugae flatten when the stomach is distended (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — gastric pits and regional glands","isHighYield":false},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions of the stomach based on their characteristic gastric pits, glands, and predominant cell types.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman is evaluated for a low vitamin B12 level. A single biopsy taken just distal to the gastroesophageal junction shows shallow gastric pits over tubular, slightly tortuous glands made almost entirely of pale mucous cells with flattened basal nuclei, and a few parietal cells appear at one edge. A trainee reports the findings as loss of acid-secreting glands. What is the best interpretation?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosa of the fundic region","B":"Autoimmune loss of oxyntic glands","C":"Intestinal metaplasia with goblet cells","D":"Normal mucosa of the pyloric region","E":"Normal mucosa of the cardiac region"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Shallow pits over tortuous mucous glands with only occasional parietal cells near the fundic border describe the cardiac region, so the biopsy site, not disease, explains the scarcity of parietal cells, and the body must be sampled to judge atrophy. Fundic glands are packed with parietal cells in the neck and chief cells in the base rather than mucous cells. Autoimmune gastritis destroys parietal and chief cells in the body and fundus, and this cannot be judged from a sample of the cardia. Intestinal metaplasia would show goblet and absorptive cells, which are absent. Pyloric mucosa has deep pits occupying about half the mucosal thickness over coiled glands and lies at the far end of the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 1, gastric regions; SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune gastritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac glands are mucous, fundic glands hold parietal and chief cells, and pyloric glands have deep pits with G and D cells (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 3). COMLEX: suspected atrophic gastritis needs biopsies from the gastric body because the biopsy site changes the normal cell mix.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 5 — esophageal cardiac glands","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fundic glands are packed with parietal cells in","B":"Autoimmune gastritis destroys parietal and chief cells in","C":"Intestinal metaplasia would show goblet and absorptive cells,","D":"Pyloric mucosa has deep pits occupying about half"}},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the major gastric epithelial cell types (surface mucous, mucous neck, parietal, chief, and enteroendocrine cells) and correlate their location and histologic appearance with their secretory products and functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has refractory duodenal and jejunal ulcers, watery diarrhea, and a fasting serum gastrin of 1,100 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL). Biopsy of the gastric body shows a thickened oxyntic mucosa crowded with large, pear-shaped cells that have central nuclei and intensely eosinophilic cytoplasm. Which feature of these multiplied cells best accounts for their staining?","choices":{"A":"Apical microvilli that sense the lumen","B":"Apical cups packed with mucinogen","C":"Dense core granules at the cell base","D":"Abundant mitochondria for the pumps","E":"Dense rough ER for zymogen synthesis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin from a gastrinoma drives parietal cell hyperplasia, and parietal cells are large triangular cells whose cytoplasm is intensely eosinophilic because it is packed with mitochondria, canaliculi and tubulovesicles that power and house the H+/K+-ATPase. Apical microvilli that act as chemoreceptors mark open-type enteroendocrine cells that reach the lumen. Mucinogen-filled apical cups belong to surface mucous cells and look pale or empty on H&E. Basal dense-core granules identify enteroendocrine cells, which often look clear on H&E. Dense rough endoplasmic reticulum makes the basal cytoplasm of chief cells basophilic, the opposite staining pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 2, gastric cell types; SDL 5, section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells are eosinophilic and mitochondria-rich, while chief cells are basophilic from rough ER (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 3). COMLEX: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome thickens the oxyntic mucosa through gastrin-driven parietal cell hyperplasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — proton pump inhibitors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Apical microvilli that act as chemoreceptors mark open-type","B":"Mucinogen-filled apical cups belong to surface mucous cells","C":"Basal dense-core granules identify enteroendocrine cells, which often","E":"Dense rough endoplasmic reticulum makes the basal cytoplasm"}},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman with vitiligo has fatigue, a smooth red tongue, and numbness in both feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12–16 g/dL) with an MCV of 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum antibodies against parietal cells are present. Which pair of laboratory results is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High serum gastrin with high acid output","B":"Normal methylmalonic acid, high homocysteine","C":"Low methylmalonic acid, normal homocysteine","D":"Low serum gastrin and low pepsinogen activity","E":"High methylmalonic acid and high homocysteine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal cell destruction in autoimmune gastritis removes intrinsic factor, so dietary B12 cannot be absorbed in the distal ileum, and B12 deficiency raises both methylmalonic acid and homocysteine while causing megaloblastic anemia and dorsal column damage. High gastrin with high acid output describes a gastrinoma, whereas her destroyed parietal cells cannot make acid. High homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid is the pattern of folate deficiency, which does not cause neurologic signs. Low methylmalonic acid with normal homocysteine does not fit B12 deficiency, which raises both metabolites. Low gastrin would be unexpected, because lost acid feedback lets antral G cells release more gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss); First Aid 2025 (vitamin B12 and folate deficiency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and homocysteine, while folate deficiency raises homocysteine alone (First Aid 2025). COMLEX: pernicious anemia with neurologic signs needs parenteral B12 because intrinsic factor is gone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — autoimmune gastritis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High gastrin with high acid output describes a","B":"High homocysteine with normal methylmalonic acid is the","C":"Low methylmalonic acid with normal homocysteine does not","D":"Low gastrin would be unexpected, because lost acid"}},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis and a bleeding gastric ulcer last year must stay on naproxen. Her physician adds a synthetic analog of a mucosal lipid mediator; it gives her crampy abdominal pain and diarrhea and must be avoided in pregnancy. Which effect of the added drug most directly protects her gastric mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Neutralization of acid in the lumen","B":"Irreversible block of the proton pump","C":"Competitive block of H2 receptors","D":"Killing of urease positive bacteria","E":"More mucus and bicarbonate secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The added drug is misoprostol, a PGE1 analog that replaces the prostaglandin signal lost to COX inhibition and directly stimulates mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, keeping the surface layer near neutral. Antacids neutralize luminal acid directly for only 1 to 2 hours and do not restore mucosal defenses. Irreversible proton pump block is the action of omeprazole and related drugs, which do not cause crampy diarrhea or carry an abortifacient warning. Competitive H2 receptor blockers such as famotidine reduce histamine-driven acid but are not prostaglandin analogs. Eradication regimens kill H. pylori, but her injury comes from prostaglandin loss due to naproxen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, mucosal barrier; SDL 10, section 2, NSAID ulcer prevention).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs cut PGE2 and with it mucus, bicarbonate and mucosal blood flow, which misoprostol restores (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 3 and SDL 10). COMLEX: misoprostol is an abortifacient and is contraindicated in pregnancy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — acute gastropathy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antacids neutralize luminal acid directly for only 1","B":"Irreversible proton pump block is the action of","C":"Competitive H2 receptor blockers such as famotidine reduce","D":"Eradication regimens kill H. pylori, but her injury"}},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the normal histologic features of the stomach from those of the esophagus, including epithelial type, mucosal organization, glands, and muscularis externa.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has heartburn after large meals. Endoscopy shows erosions in the distal esophagus, while the gastric mucosa, bathed in the same acid every day, is normal. Which feature of the esophageal mucosa best explains why it is injured while the stomach is not?","choices":{"A":"Adventitia in place of a serosal coat","B":"Absence of glands anywhere in its wall","C":"Thinner muscularis externa with two layers","D":"Squamous lining with no mucus gel coat","E":"Absent muscularis mucosae under the lining"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The stomach is lined by simple columnar surface mucous cells that secrete an insoluble bicarbonate-rich gel keeping the cell surface near pH 7, whereas the esophagus is lined by nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium built to resist abrasion and protected against acid mainly by the neutral mucus of its cardiac glands. An adventitia in place of a serosa affects how disease spreads outward, not how the lining resists acid. The esophagus has glands proper in the submucosa and cardiac glands in the lamina propria, so it is not glandless. The number of muscle layers affects motility, not the chemical resistance of the lining. The esophagus does have a muscularis mucosae of longitudinal smooth muscle, so its absence is not the problem. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, sections 4 and 5; SDL 2, section 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the esophagus has stratified squamous epithelium and a two-layer muscularis externa, while the stomach has columnar mucous cells, gastric pits and three muscle layers (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 3). COMLEX: chronic reflux injury can drive columnar metaplasia of the distal esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"An adventitia in place of a serosa affects","B":"The esophagus has glands proper in the submucosa","C":"The number of muscle layers affects motility, not","E":"The esophagus does have a muscularis mucosae of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl03_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the histologic components of the gastric mucosal barrier and explain how disruption of this barrier contributes to gastric mucosal injury and ulceration.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has epigastric pain, and an antral biopsy shows curved rods attached to the surface foveolar cells with neutrophils in the pits. Electron microscopy shows that the junctional seals between neighboring surface cells are disrupted where the organisms attach. Which consequence follows most directly from this finding?","choices":{"A":"Loss of acid output by parietal cells","B":"Back-diffusion of H+ into the tissue","C":"Lower PGE2 synthesis from COX blockade","D":"Ammonia buffering of the luminal acid","E":"Failure of pepsinogen to become pepsin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Tight junctions between surface cells normally block H+ and enzymes from leaking into the tissue, and when H. pylori cytotoxins such as CagA destroy them, acid diffuses back into the lamina propria, injuring cells and, when defenses are overwhelmed, producing ulceration. Antral infection does not destroy parietal cells, and antral-predominant gastritis has normal to increased acid output. Reduced PGE2 synthesis follows cyclooxygenase inhibition by NSAIDs, not loss of junctional seals. Urease-derived ammonia buffers acid around the bacteria, a survival tactic separate from junction damage. Pepsinogen activation needs luminal acid below pH 5, which persists with this infection. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 4, barrier disruption; SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori weakens the barrier with urease, adhesins and cytotoxins such as CagA that break tight junctions (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 3). COMLEX: H. pylori gastritis is a treatable cause of gastric and duodenal ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antral infection does not destroy parietal cells, and","C":"Reduced PGE2 synthesis follows cyclooxygenase inhibition by NSAIDs,","D":"Urease-derived ammonia buffers acid around the bacteria, a","E":"Pepsinogen activation needs luminal acid below pH 5,"}},{"id":"grid_sdl03_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate loss or dysfunction of specific gastric cell types to clinically important consequences, including impaired vitamin B12 absorption and protein digestion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with Hashimoto thyroiditis and vitiligo has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, and numbness in both feet. Biopsy of the gastric body shows thin mucosa with few glands, while the antrum looks normal. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Serum B12"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↓","↓"],"C":["↔","↔","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells in the body and fundus, so the pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) is correct. Acid falls, gastrin from the spared antrum rises, and serum B12 falls, which fits the glossitis and neuropathy. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is a gastrinoma (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome), in which tumor gastrin drives intact parietal cells to secrete excess acid; intrinsic factor is still made, so B12 is typically normal, and although very low duodenal pH can inactivate the pancreatic proteases that cleave R-binder and free B12 to bind intrinsic factor (inferred: Costanzo describes low duodenal pH inactivating pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and pancreatic proteases degrading R proteins), the high acid level excludes this row whatever the B12 value. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ serum B12) is folate deficiency, which causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia but leaves gastric secretion and B12 unchanged and does not explain the numbness in her feet. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows total gastrectomy, which removes the antral G cells, so gastrin falls, and removes the parietal cells, so acid and intrinsic factor are lost and B12 is not absorbed; the low gastrin separates this row from the key. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↓ serum B12) follows resection of the distal ileum, where the B12 and intrinsic factor complex is absorbed, with normal gastric function. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, section 3, parietal cell loss, achlorhydria and hypergastrinemia); Pawlina Ch. 17 (pernicious anemia); Guyton supplemental (folic acid deficiency causes a macrocytic anemia almost identical to pernicious anemia); First Aid 2025 GI (autoimmune gastritis; Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; gastrectomy removes the source of intrinsic factor, so B12 must be injected); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 3, total or partial gastrectomy causes B12 deficiency); the low gastrin in row D is inferred from removal of the antrum, and the B12 value in row A is inferred from preserved intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis has antibodies to the parietal cell H+/K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor, affects the body and fundus, and causes hypochlorhydria, hypergastrinemia, and pernicious anemia (First Aid 2025 GI chronic gastritis). COMLEX: a high gastrin level with low acid points to atrophic gastritis or acid suppression rather than gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":4,"title":"SDL 4 — Physiology - GI Innervation","questions":[{"id":"sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Within the muscularis externa, contraction of the inner circular layer has which effect on a gut segment?","choices":{"A":"Narrows the lumen","B":"Shortens the segment","C":"Folds the mucosa","D":"Widens the lumen","E":"Lengthens the segment"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Contraction of the inner circular layer decreases the luminal diameter. Contraction of the outer longitudinal layer shortens the segment. Folding of the mucosa is produced by the muscularis mucosae. Widening of the lumen follows relaxation of circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and lengthening follows relaxation of longitudinal muscle; neither is produced by circular contraction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, inner circular muscle layer); Costanzo Ch. 8 (circular muscle decreases diameter, longitudinal muscle decreases length).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the myenteric (Auerbach) plexus between the two muscle layers controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: circular and longitudinal layers are reciprocally innervated during peristalsis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"Which layer of the gut wall alters the folding and surface area of the inner lining to aid local absorption and secretion?","choices":{"A":"Inner circular layer","B":"Outer longitudinal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Lamina propria","E":"Serosal layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The muscularis mucosae is a thin smooth muscle layer at the base of the mucosa whose local contractions change mucosal shape and surface area. The inner circular and outer longitudinal layers of the muscularis externa move the whole segment. The lamina propria is loose connective tissue with vessels and immune cells. The serosa is not the layer described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, muscularis mucosae).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gut wall layers are mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and serosa or adventitia (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus controls the muscularis mucosae.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":1,"stem":"During peristalsis, when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, what does the longitudinal muscle of that segment do?","choices":{"A":"Contracts","B":"Relaxes","C":"Is unchanged","D":"Fatigues","E":"Depolarizes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Circular and longitudinal muscle are reciprocally innervated, so when the circular muscle of a segment contracts, its longitudinal muscle relaxes, and the segment narrows and lengthens behind the bolus. Simultaneous contraction is what reciprocal innervation prevents. The longitudinal layer changes its tone rather than staying unchanged. Fatigue is not part of the reflex. Depolarization would lead to contraction, not relaxation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (reciprocal innervation and peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts; ahead of it, circular muscle relaxes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the segment ahead of the bolus relaxes so the bolus can advance (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Simultaneous contraction is what reciprocal innervation prevents.","E":"Depolarization would lead to contraction, not relaxation."}},{"id":"sdl04_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Preganglionic sympathetic fibers to the gastrointestinal tract synapse in which location?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus","B":"Submucosal plexus","C":"Dorsal root ganglia","D":"Inferior vagal ganglion","E":"Prevertebral ganglia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers are short and synapse outside the gut in four prevertebral ganglia: celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric. The myenteric and submucosal plexuses are where parasympathetic preganglionic fibers synapse and where sympathetic postganglionic fibers end. Dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic synapse sites. The inferior vagal ganglion is not a sympathetic synapse site. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic splanchnic supply is T5 to T9 for the foregut, T10 to T12 for the midgut, and L1 to L2 for the hindgut (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture also lists paravertebral chain ganglia in the sympathetic pathway.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which description fits the parasympathetic preganglionic fibers that supply the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Short with prevertebral ganglia","B":"Short with intramural ganglia","C":"Long with chain ganglia","D":"Long with intramural ganglia","E":"Short with chain ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic preganglionic fibers are long, running from the brainstem or sacral cord to the gut, and synapse in ganglia within the wall, in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses, so the postganglionic fibers are short. Short preganglionic fibers synapsing in prevertebral ganglia describe the sympathetic supply. Short fibers ending in the wall describe no autonomic division. Chain ganglia are sympathetic paravertebral ganglia. Paravertebral chain ganglia receive preganglionic fibers that leave the thoracolumbar spinal cord through the ventral roots and white rami, the sympathetic outflow. Sympathetic preganglionic neurons leave the thoracolumbar cord and project to paravertebral chain or prevertebral ganglia, the sympathetic rather than the parasympathetic pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic ganglia lie in or near the target organ; sympathetic ganglia lie outside it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagal preganglionic fibers synapse in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Short fibers ending in the wall describe no autonomic division.","E":"Chain ganglia are sympathetic paravertebral ganglia.","C":"Paravertebral chain ganglia receive preganglionic fibers that leave the thoracolumbar spinal cord through the ventral roots and white rami, the sympathetic outflow.","A":"Sympathetic preganglionic neurons leave the thoracolumbar cord and project to paravertebral chain or prevertebral ganglia, the sympathetic rather than the parasympathetic pattern."}},{"id":"sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Activation of sympathetic postganglionic fibers to the gut produces which effect?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of sphincters","B":"Relaxation of sphincters","C":"Increased gland secretion","D":"Increased wall motility","E":"Mucosal vasodilation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sympathetic activity generally inhibits gut function: it relaxes the smooth muscle of the walls, contracts GI sphincters, decreases motility, inhibits secretion, and constricts GI vessels. Relaxation of sphincters, increased gland secretion, and increased wall motility are parasympathetic effects. Mucosal vasodilation accompanies parasympathetic stimulation during digestion, whereas sympathetic activity causes vasoconstriction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic stimulation inhibits activity in the gut).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norepinephrine inhibits gut motility, mostly through inhibition of enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: intense sympathetic activity can stop movement of food through the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"The pelvic nerve supplies parasympathetic fibers to which part of the gastrointestinal tract?","choices":{"A":"Stomach and proximal duodenum","B":"Jejunum and terminal ileum","C":"Cecum and ascending colon","D":"Upper third of esophagus","E":"Descending and sigmoid colon"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The pelvic nerve, from sacral segments S2 to S4, supplies the lower GI tract: the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal. The stomach, duodenum, jejunum, ileum, cecum, and ascending colon receive vagal parasympathetic fibers. The upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle supplied by vagal fibers from the nucleus ambiguus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation; Costanzo lists the transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, and the lecture adds the rectum and anal canal).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hindgut parasympathetic supply is from S2 to S4 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers coordinate defecation reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Besides coordinating defecation reflexes, which colonic function does parasympathetic outflow through the pelvic nerve stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Mucus secretion","B":"Water absorption","C":"Bile acid uptake","D":"Motility inhibition","E":"Sphincter contraction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pelvic nerve fibers from S2 to S4 are heavily concentrated in the sigmoid, rectal, and anal regions, where they coordinate defecation reflexes and stimulate colonic mucus secretion. Water absorption by the colonic epithelium is not the function the lecture assigns to pelvic outflow. Bile acid uptake occurs mainly in the terminal ileum, which is in vagal territory. Parasympathetic activity increases rather than inhibits colonic motility. Sphincter contraction is a sympathetic effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve distribution and functions, lines 843–850; section 2, sympathetic contraction of sphincters); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that parasympathetic activity increases peristalsis and secretions while lowering sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach physiology). COMLEX: sacral cord lesions impair defecation reflexes and colonic mucus secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most distal part of the gastrointestinal tract that receives vagal parasympathetic fibers?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Proximal transverse colon","C":"Proximal descending colon","D":"Proximal sigmoid colon","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The vagus supplies the esophagus, stomach, pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid region are supplied by the pelvic nerve. This boundary matches the midgut and hindgut junction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus nerve distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut parasympathetic supply is the vagus; hindgut supply is S2 to S4 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the distal third of the transverse colon is hindgut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — vagal innervation of the rotated stomach","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectosigmoid region are supplied by the pelvic nerve."}},{"id":"sdl04_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Gastric distension triggers receptive relaxation of the proximal stomach through a vagovagal reflex. What defines a reflex as vagovagal?","choices":{"A":"Its afferents reach the spinal dorsal horn","B":"Its efferents synapse in prevertebral ganglia","C":"Its arc is confined to the enteric plexus","D":"Both its limbs travel in the vagus nerve","E":"Its afferents run in the splanchnic nerves"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most vagal fibers are afferent, carrying mechanoreceptor and chemoreceptor signals from the gut to the brainstem, and the motor limb returns in the same nerve, so a reflex with both afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus is called vagovagal. Afferents ending in the spinal dorsal horn belong to spinal visceral pathways, not the vagus. Synapses in prevertebral ganglia are a feature of sympathetic outflow. A reflex confined to the enteric plexus is a local reflex that needs no extrinsic nerve. Splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers and their accompanying pain afferents. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, parasympathetic innervation; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a vagovagal reflex has both its afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus, as in receptive relaxation of the stomach (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: truncal vagotomy abolishes vagovagal reflexes such as receptive relaxation, causing early satiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Afferents ending in the spinal dorsal horn","B":"Synapses in prevertebral ganglia are a feature","C":"A reflex confined to the enteric plexus","E":"Splanchnic nerves carry sympathetic fibers and their"}},{"id":"sdl04_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"In a vagovagal reflex, the vagal afferent fibers from the gut terminate in which brainstem nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","B":"Nucleus ambiguus of medulla","C":"Nucleus tractus solitarius","D":"Spinal trigeminal nucleus","E":"Hypoglossal motor nucleus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vagal afferents from the gut end in the nucleus tractus solitarius of the medulla. The dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus is the origin of the efferent limb. The nucleus ambiguus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and hypoglossal nucleus are not the termination site of vagal afferents in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent and efferent pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in vagovagal reflexes both limbs travel in the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: receptive relaxation and distension-driven acid secretion are vagovagal reflexes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The nucleus ambiguus, spinal trigeminal nucleus, and hypoglossal nucleus are not the termination site of vagal afferents in the lecture."}},{"id":"sdl04_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":1,"stem":"Distension of the orad stomach triggers a vagovagal reflex that relaxes the gastric wall. Which transmitters do the postganglionic neurons release?","choices":{"A":"ACh and substance P","B":"VIP and nitric oxide","C":"Gastrin and CCK","D":"Secretin and somatostatin","E":"GRP and histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which peptidergic postganglionic neurons release VIP and nitric oxide, relaxing the orad stomach so it can hold up to 1.5 L without a rise in pressure. ACh and substance P are excitatory transmitters that contract smooth muscle. Gastrin and CCK are hormones, not reflex transmitters. Secretin and somatostatin are a hormone and a paracrine inhibitor. GRP stimulates G cells, and histamine is a paracrine stimulant of parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (receptive relaxation; the neurotransmitter is VIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"In the peristaltic reflex, intrinsic primary afferent neurons in the gut wall receive chemical activation from which signal?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers","B":"ACh from vagal efferent fibers","C":"Gastrin from antral G endocrine cells","D":"Histamine from mucosal mast cells","E":"Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bolus stimulates enterochromaffin cells in the mucosa to release serotonin, which binds intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) and starts the peristaltic reflex in that segment. Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers suppresses enteric activity. ACh from vagal efferent fibers is extrinsic modulation, not the local trigger. Gastrin from antral G cells is a hormone acting on parietal cells. Histamine from mast cells is not the trigger described in the lecture. IPANs also respond directly to stretch. Histamine is secreted by endocrine-type cells of the gastric mucosa and acts with gastrin and ACh to stimulate parietal cell H+ secretion, not to activate IPANs in the peristaltic reflex. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sensorimotor architecture); Costanzo Ch. 8 (5-HT binds IPANs to initiate the peristaltic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system contains more than 100 million neurons (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: local reflexes work without central processing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Histamine is secreted by endocrine-type cells of the gastric mucosa and acts with gastrin and ACh to stimulate parietal cell H+ secretion, not to activate IPANs in the peristaltic reflex.","A":"Norepinephrine from sympathetic fibers suppresses enteric activity.","B":"ACh from vagal efferent fibers is extrinsic modulation, not the local trigger.","C":"Gastrin from antral G cells is a hormone acting on parietal cells."}},{"id":"sdl04_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"During the peristaltic reflex, excitatory enteric neurons contract the circular muscle behind the bolus. Which transmitters do they release?","choices":{"A":"VIP and nitric oxide","B":"Norepinephrine and dopamine","C":"Secretin and CCK","D":"ACh and substance P","E":"Gastrin and histamine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Excitatory enteric neurons release acetylcholine and substance P to contract muscle behind the bolus. VIP and nitric oxide are released by inhibitory neurons that relax muscle ahead of the bolus and relax sphincters. Norepinephrine is released by extrinsic sympathetic fibers and inhibits motility. Secretin and CCK are hormones. Gastrin and histamine regulate acid secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, motility control via the myenteric plexus); Costanzo Ch. 8 (peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity, and norepinephrine usually inhibits it (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs slow gut motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":1,"stem":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers suppress gut motility and secretion. Where do most of these fibers end?","choices":{"A":"On smooth muscle cells","B":"On mucosal epithelium","C":"On enteric neurons","D":"On vagal ganglia","E":"On dorsal root ganglia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on neurons of the enteric nervous system, where norepinephrine inhibits transmitter release; only a small share acts directly on smooth muscle and vessels. Smooth muscle cells receive a minor direct input. Mucosal epithelium is controlled mainly through enteric neurons. Vagal ganglia and dorsal root ganglia are not sympathetic targets. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (sympathetic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic stimulation inhibits gut activity mainly through the enteric nervous system (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: sympathetic activity also contracts sphincters and constricts gut vessels (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 2 — enteric control of secondary peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth muscle cells receive a minor direct input.","B":"Mucosal epithelium is controlled mainly through enteric neurons."}},{"id":"sdl04_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has a full-thickness colon biopsy for chronic constipation. Ganglion cells are seen between the dense connective tissue of the submucosa and the inner circular muscle layer. Which function does this network mainly control?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa contraction","B":"Relaxation of the GI sphincters","C":"Local secretion and blood flow","D":"Initiation of the swallow reflex","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ganglia between the submucosal connective tissue and the inner circular layer form the submucosal (Meissner) plexus, which controls local fluid and electrolyte secretion, absorption, local blood flow, and movement of the muscularis mucosae. Contraction of the muscularis externa and relaxation of sphincters are controlled by the myenteric plexus. The swallow reflex is initiated by the medullary swallowing center. Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex of the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosal plexus location, and section 5, submucosal plexus control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosal plexus integrates epithelial sensory signals to control fluid secretion and local vasodilation (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: the submucosal plexus also controls the muscularis mucosae (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 3 — submucosal versus myenteric plexus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman has an endoscopic submucosal injection before removal of a colon polyp. The needle tip lies in dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels, just deep to the muscularis mucosae. Which neural network lies in this layer?","choices":{"A":"Auerbach plexus","B":"Celiac plexus","C":"Vagal trunk plexus","D":"Meissner plexus","E":"Pelvic plexus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dense irregular connective tissue with large vessels just deep to the muscularis mucosae is the submucosa, which contains the submucosal (Meissner) plexus. The Auerbach (myenteric) plexus lies deeper, between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. The celiac and pelvic plexuses are extrinsic autonomic plexuses outside the gut wall. Vagal trunks lie on the esophagus and stomach, outside the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, submucosa and submucosal plexus); First Aid 2025 GI (digestive tract anatomy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the submucosa contains the Meissner plexus and vessels; the muscularis externa contains the Auerbach plexus (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: the submucosa carries the larger vessels of the gut wall (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman in a pharmacology study receives an agent that blocks synaptic transmission in the ganglia between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers. Which function is reduced?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic wave velocity","B":"Mucosal fluid secretion","C":"Muscularis mucosae folding","D":"Local mucosal vasodilation","E":"Epithelial absorption"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ganglia between the two muscle layers form the myenteric plexus, which sets smooth muscle tone, the rate and intensity of rhythmic contractions, and the conduction velocity of peristaltic waves. Mucosal fluid secretion, muscularis mucosae folding, and local vasodilation are controlled by the submucosal plexus. Epithelial absorption depends on enterocytes and submucosal control, not on the myenteric plexus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1, myenteric plexus location, and section 5, motility control).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Auerbach plexus controls motility (First Aid 2025 GI digestive tract anatomy). COMLEX: loss of myenteric neurons causes achalasia in the esophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — enteric control of esophageal peristalsis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has episodes of severe hypertension, headaches, and sweating, and imaging shows an adrenal mass. She also reports constipation. Which gastrointestinal effect of her excess catecholamine most likely contributes to the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Faster transit with relaxed sphincters","B":"Slow transit and tight sphincters","C":"Increased glandular fluid secretion","D":"Mucosal vasodilation and hyperemia","E":"Destruction of myenteric neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norepinephrine, the sympathetic postganglionic transmitter, relaxes the smooth muscle of the gut wall, contracts sphincters, decreases motility and transit, and inhibits secretion, which together most likely favor constipation; Robbins does not list constipation among pheochromocytoma features, so the clinical outcome is inferred from the mechanism. Faster transit with relaxed sphincters and increased glandular secretion are parasympathetic effects. Sympathetic activity constricts, rather than dilates, gut vessels. Norepinephrine inhibits enteric neurons but does not destroy them. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Guyton Ch. 63 (norepinephrine usually inhibits GI activity).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity inhibits gut motility and secretion and contracts sphincters (Costanzo Ch. 8; Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: norepinephrine acts mainly through enteric neurons (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — sympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Sympathetic activity constricts, rather than dilates, gut vessels.","E":"Norepinephrine inhibits enteric neurons but does not destroy them."}},{"id":"sdl04_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman takes an overdose of a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. She has a dry mouth, flushed skin, and absent bowel sounds. Which normal parasympathetic action has been blocked?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscle","B":"Vasoconstriction of gut vessels","C":"Inhibition of glandular secretion","D":"Contraction of gut smooth muscle","E":"Release of norepinephrine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic cholinergic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic receptors to increase smooth muscle contraction and motility, relax sphincters, and increase glandular secretion. Blocking muscarinic receptors removes these effects, causing ileus and dry mouth. Relaxation of gut smooth muscle, vasoconstriction, and inhibition of secretion are sympathetic effects. Norepinephrine is released by sympathetic, not parasympathetic, fibers. Norepinephrine is released by adrenergic sympathetic postganglionic neurons. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, neurotransmitters and effects on GI function).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetylcholine most often excites GI activity (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: parasympathetic activity relaxes GI sphincters (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 2 — parasympathetic control of salivary secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Relaxation of gut smooth muscle is a sympathetic effect, not the parasympathetic action blocked here.","B":"Vasoconstriction of gut vessels is a sympathetic effect.","C":"Inhibition of glandular secretion is a sympathetic effect.","E":"Norepinephrine is released by adrenergic sympathetic postganglionic neurons."}},{"id":"sdl04_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with pancreatic cancer has severe abdominal pain and receives a celiac plexus block. The injected ganglion lies outside the gut wall in front of the aorta. Which neurons have cell bodies in this ganglion?","choices":{"A":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons","B":"Vagal preganglionic neurons","C":"Sympathetic postganglionic neurons","D":"Enteric interneurons of the wall","E":"Dorsal root sensory neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral sympathetic ganglion where short preganglionic fibers synapse on postganglionic neurons whose long adrenergic fibers run to the gut. Parasympathetic postganglionic cell bodies lie in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses within the gut wall. Vagal preganglionic cell bodies lie in the dorsal motor nucleus of the brainstem, and vagal fibers pass through the celiac plexus without synapsing. Enteric interneurons lie in the gut wall. Dorsal root sensory neurons do not lie in the celiac ganglion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, ganglion location and fiber length); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: foregut sympathetic supply runs through the T5 to T9 splanchnic nerves (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, embryology). COMLEX: the lecture lists the celiac, superior mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and hypogastric ganglia as prevertebral.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"Which of the following digestive organs receives its parasympathetic supply from the cranial division rather than from the S2 to S4 sacral division?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Rectum","D":"Anal canal","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The vagus nerve, the cranial division, supplies parasympathetic fibers to the esophagus, stomach, exocrine pancreas, liver and gallbladder, small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The pelvic nerve from S2 to S4 supplies the distal transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon, the rectum, and the anal canal, so the sigmoid colon, rectum, anal canal, and descending colon all depend on the sacral outflow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, p24); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal supply ends at the proximal transverse colon, and S2 to S4 supply begins at the distal transverse colon (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: pelvic nerve outflow is concentrated in the sigmoid, rectum, and anus to coordinate defecation (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 3 — vagal acetylcholine drives the cephalic phase","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman undergoes truncal vagotomy, with both vagal trunks divided at the esophageal hiatus, during surgery for recurrent bleeding from a duodenal ulcer. Her sacral spinal cord and pelvic nerves are intact. Which segment of the gut loses its parasympathetic supply?","choices":{"A":"Distal transverse colon","B":"Ascending colon","C":"Descending colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dividing both vagal trunks removes parasympathetic supply from the whole vagal territory, which runs from the esophagus and stomach through the small intestine, cecum, appendix, ascending colon, and proximal transverse colon. The distal transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and anal canal receive parasympathetic fibers from the pelvic nerve, which arises from S2 to S4 and is intact in this patient. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, vagus and pelvic nerve distribution, lines 835–850); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid gives the vagus as the parasympathetic nerve of the foregut and of the midgut, which extends to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: after truncal vagotomy, the left colon still receives sacral parasympathetic outflow (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — anterior and posterior vagal trunks","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the distribution of parasympathetic innervation to the GI tract by the vagus and pelvic nerves. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old woman with a sacral myelomeningocele has absent function of the S2 to S4 cord segments. Which gastrointestinal function is most affected?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid secretion","B":"Gallbladder contraction","C":"Pancreatic enzyme secretion","D":"Small bowel peristalsis","E":"Rectal defecation reflexes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pelvic nerve innervation from S2 to S4 is concentrated in the sigmoid colon, rectum, and anal canal, where it coordinates defecation reflexes and stimulates colonic mucus secretion. Gastric acid secretion, gallbladder contraction, and pancreatic enzyme secretion receive vagal parasympathetic input. Small bowel peristalsis is driven by the myenteric plexus with vagal modulation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 3, pelvic nerve).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pelvic nerve arises from the second, third, and fourth sacral cord segments (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: neurogenic bowel follows sacral cord injury.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — hindgut derivatives","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old man has early satiety and epigastric fullness after small meals since a truncal vagotomy. Loss of which reflex best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Peristaltic reflex","E":"Defecation reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex in which distension of the orad stomach relaxes the gastric wall so it can hold a meal without a rise in pressure; vagotomy eliminates it, causing early fullness. The gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility after a meal. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying in response to duodenal contents, and its loss would not cause early satiety. The peristaltic reflex is a local enteric reflex that survives vagotomy. The defecation reflex is sacral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, receptive relaxation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (vagotomy eliminates receptive relaxation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: receptive relaxation is a vagovagal reflex mediated by VIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired gastric accommodation causes early satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — receptive relaxation of the orad stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman in a physiology study has a balloon inflated in her stomach, and her serum gastrin rises. Postganglionic vagal neurons release which transmitter onto the antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"VIP","B":"ACh","C":"CCK","D":"NO","E":"GRP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric distension initiates vagovagal reflexes in which postganglionic neurons release ACh onto parietal cells and gastrin-releasing peptide (GRP) onto antral G cells, increasing acid secretion. VIP and nitric oxide relax the orad stomach in receptive relaxation. ACh is the transmitter acting on parietal cells rather than on G cells. CCK is a hormone from duodenal I cells. Costanzo notes atropine does not block vagal gastrin release because the neurotransmitter at the G cell synapse is GRP, not ACh. Costanzo states vagus nerves innervate G cells and release GRP as the neurotransmitter, not VIP. Costanzo states vagus nerves innervate G cells and release GRP as the neurotransmitter, not CCK. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastric secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation releases GRP to increase gastrin (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: atropine does not block vagal stimulation of gastrin because GRP is not cholinergic (First Aid 2025 GI acid secretion diagram: atropine acts on the ACh limb).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — neural regulation of acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"VIP and nitric oxide relax the orad stomach in receptive relaxation.","B":"Costanzo notes atropine does not block vagal gastrin release because the neurotransmitter at the G cell synapse is GRP, not ACh.","A":"Costanzo states vagus nerves innervate G cells and release GRP as the neurotransmitter, not VIP.","C":"CCK is a hormone from duodenal I cells."}},{"id":"sdl04_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman notices an urge to defecate within minutes after each large meal. Which reflex is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Receptive relaxation","B":"Gastrocolic reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Rectosphincteric reflex","E":"Vomiting reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastric distension triggers the gastrocolic reflex, a long arc reflex that increases colonic motility and starts mass movements. The lecture describes it as a long arc vagovagal reflex, which is the exam convention; Costanzo instead places the afferent limb in parasympathetic nerves and the efferent limb in CCK and gastrin. Receptive relaxation relaxes the stomach itself. The enterogastric reflex slows gastric emptying. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter when the rectum fills, after mass movements arrive. The vomiting reflex is coordinated in the medulla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, gastrocolic reflex); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrocolic reflex).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex increases colonic motility and mass movements after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: mass movements carry colonic contents toward the rectum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has a segment of jejunum studied after all of its extrinsic nerves are cut. Stroking the mucosa still triggers a local peristaltic reflex. Where are the cell bodies of the sensory neurons that detect this stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Dorsal root ganglia","B":"Nodose ganglion","C":"Prevertebral ganglia","D":"Within the gut submucosa","E":"Nucleus tractus solitarius"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intrinsic primary afferent neurons (IPANs) are embedded in the submucosa. They detect stretch or chemical stimuli, often after enterochromaffin cells release serotonin, and signal enteric interneurons that coordinate motor, secretory, and endocrine responses without central processing, so the reflex survives extrinsic denervation. Dorsal root ganglia and the nodose ganglion hold the cell bodies of extrinsic spinal and vagal afferents, which were cut. Prevertebral ganglia hold sympathetic postganglionic neurons. The nucleus tractus solitarius is the medullary site where vagal afferents end. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, IPANs embedded in the submucosa, p25; section 4, vagal afferents end in the nucleus tractus solitarius, p24).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the enteric nervous system has over 100 million intrinsic neurons and can run local reflexes on its own (lecture SDL 4). COMLEX: extrinsic sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers modulate, but are not needed for, intrinsic reflexes (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents in the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl04_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has paralytic ileus after a long abdominal operation with intense sympathetic activation. Norepinephrine suppresses his motility mainly through which effect on the enteric nervous system?","choices":{"A":"Increased vagal outflow","B":"Loss of enteric neurons","C":"Blocked nicotinic receptors","D":"Increased VIP and NO release","E":"Reduced enteric ACh release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers end mainly on enteric neurons, and norepinephrine binds presynaptic receptors that inhibit release of excitatory transmitters such as ACh, suppressing motility. Increased vagal outflow would increase motility. Enteric neurons are inhibited, not lost. Norepinephrine does not block nicotinic receptors. Increased VIP and NO release would relax muscle by a different pathway and is not the sympathetic mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, sympathetic input); Guyton Ch. 63 (inhibitory effect of norepinephrine on enteric neurons).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strong sympathetic stimulation can inhibit gut movement enough to block passage of food (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes also use prevertebral sympathetic ganglia (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — sympathetic limb of the enterogastric reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enteric neurons are inhibited, not lost."}},{"id":"sdl04_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman is studied after her extrinsic gut nerves are blocked pharmacologically. Luminal peptides still increase gastrin release from her antral G cells. Which neural element can still modulate the G cells?","choices":{"A":"Vagal afferent fibers","B":"Prevertebral ganglia","C":"Enteric interneurons","D":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers","E":"Spinal motor neurons"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enteric interneurons synapse with and modulate enteroendocrine cells, regulating the release of local hormones such as gastrin, CCK, secretin, and somatostatin in response to luminal conditions, even without extrinsic input. Vagal afferents, prevertebral sympathetic ganglia, and pelvic splanchnic fibers are extrinsic and are blocked. Spinal motor neurons supply skeletal muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, endocrine modulation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: G cells release gastrin in response to small peptides and amino acids (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: the enteric nervous system functions independently of extrinsic nerves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — gastrin release triggers","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the layers of the GI tract wall and locate the submucosal (Meissner) and myenteric (Auerbach) plexuses. (Fig. 8.1)","batch":3,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with years of crampy right lower quadrant pain and diarrhea has surgery for bowel obstruction. The terminal ileum is thickened, mesenteric fat wraps over its surface, and it is joined to an adjacent loop by a fistula. Histology shows granulomatous inflammation extending from the mucosa through the full thickness of the wall. Inflammation must cross which layer for the ileum to fuse with and fistulize into a neighboring loop?","choices":{"A":"Serosal mesothelium","B":"Submucosal connective tissue","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Outer longitudinal muscle","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ileum is intraperitoneal, so its outermost coat is a serosa of simple squamous mesothelium, and transmural inflammation that reaches it produces serosal disease and creeping fat and lets the bowel stick to and fistulize into neighboring loops. Submucosal connective tissue lies just beneath the muscularis mucosae, far from the surface that touches other loops. Muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the mucosa, so inflammation there has not left the inner wall. Outer longitudinal muscle is the last muscle layer, and inflammation must still pass the serosa beyond it. Lamina propria is the loose connective tissue of the mucosa, the first layer involved, not the last. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 1; SDL 2, section 1); Robbins Ch. 17 (Crohn disease morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: from the lumen outward the wall is mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa and serosa or adventitia (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 4). COMLEX: transmural Crohn disease causes fistulas and strictures, whereas ulcerative colitis spares the serosa.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Submucosal connective tissue lies just beneath the muscularis","C":"Muscularis mucosae is the deepest part of the","D":"Outer longitudinal muscle is the last muscle layer,","E":"Lamina propria is the loose connective tissue of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man in cardiogenic shock after a large myocardial infarction is started on a high-dose vasoconstrictor infusion. Two days later he has abdominal pain and bloody stools. CT angiography shows patent mesenteric arteries with diffusely thickened, poorly enhancing bowel. Which autonomic action best explains the bowel injury?","choices":{"A":"Release of nitric oxide by enteric neurons","B":"More mucosal blood flow from vagal input","C":"Norepinephrine constricting gut arterioles","D":"Contraction of gut wall muscle by acetylcholine","E":"Relaxation of gut sphincters by vagal fibers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine that constricts GI blood vessels, and in shock this splanchnic vasoconstriction, amplified by a vasoconstrictive drug, can cause ischemic bowel injury with open arteries. Nitric oxide from inhibitory enteric neurons relaxes smooth muscle and would favor perfusion. Parasympathetic activity increases mucosal blood flow during digestion, the opposite of what injured this bowel. Acetylcholine contracts gut wall muscle, which does not starve the mucosa of blood. Vagal parasympathetic fibers relax sphincters and increase motility but do not cut the blood supply. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 2, effects on GI function); Robbins Ch. 17 (ischemic bowel disease, hypoperfusion without obstruction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic outflow inhibits motility and secretion, contracts sphincters and constricts GI vessels (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 4). COMLEX: shock and vasoconstrictive drugs can cause mesenteric ischemia without arterial occlusion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nitric oxide from inhibitory enteric neurons relaxes smooth","B":"Parasympathetic activity increases mucosal blood flow during digestion,","D":"Acetylcholine contracts gut wall muscle, which does not","E":"Vagal parasympathetic fibers relax sphincters and increase motility"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a truncal vagotomy for recurrent ulcers. Months later, a balloon inflated in his stomach raises his acid output much less than it did before surgery, although the response does not disappear. Which explanation best fits these results?","choices":{"A":"Antral G cells were removed with the vagus","B":"Stretch receptors in the gastric wall were lost","C":"Both vagal limbs of the long reflex were cut","D":"Intramural enteric reflexes were abolished","E":"Parietal cell H2 receptors were downregulated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In a vagovagal reflex both the afferent and efferent fibers run in the vagus: distension signals travel to the solitary nucleus and return as efferents that release ACh on parietal cells and GRP on G cells, so vagotomy removes this long reflex and leaves the local intramural reflexes. Vagotomy does not remove G cells, which still respond to luminal peptides and local reflexes. Vagotomy cuts nerve fibers but leaves the receptors in the wall, which still drive local reflexes and explain the remaining response. Intramural enteric reflexes run within the gut wall and survive vagotomy, which is why some acid response remains. Parietal cell H2 receptor numbers are not the target of vagotomy, and histamine signaling persists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, vagovagal reflex; SDL 7, section 5, gastric phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a vagovagal reflex has its afferent and efferent limbs in the vagus and is integrated in the medulla (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 4). COMLEX: vagotomy blunts acid secretion but also impairs receptive relaxation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — phases of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vagotomy does not remove G cells, which still","B":"Vagotomy cuts nerve fibers but leaves the receptors","D":"Intramural enteric reflexes run within the gut wall","E":"Parietal cell H2 receptor numbers are not the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the afferent and efferent pathways of a vagovagal reflex and their role in regulating GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has a small medullary infarct. In a study afterward, stomach distension produces normal vagal afferent signals that reach the medulla, yet no reflex discharge returns along the vagus: his fundus no longer relaxes to accommodate a meal, and distension no longer raises acid output. Which structure is most likely damaged?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric plexus of the fundus","B":"Dorsal motor nucleus of vagus","C":"Nucleus ambiguus of the medulla","D":"Celiac prevertebral ganglion","E":"Inferior vagal sensory ganglion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Afferents deliver the signal to the nucleus tractus solitarius, and the efferent limb leaves as preganglionic vagal fibers from the dorsal motor nucleus, so loss of that nucleus abolishes receptive relaxation and reflex acid secretion despite normal sensory input. The myenteric plexus sits in the gut wall, and its neurons are the postganglionic targets of the efferent limb. The nucleus ambiguus sends somatic motor fibers to striated muscle of the pharynx and upper esophagus, not to gastric smooth muscle and glands. The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic relay outside the brainstem and would not be damaged by a medullary infarct. The inferior vagal ganglion holds afferent cell bodies, and signals reaching the medulla show that limb is intact. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 4, afferent and efferent pathways; SDL 2, section 4).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagovagal afferents end in the nucleus tractus solitarius, and efferents arise in the dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 4). COMLEX: brainstem lesions can disturb gastric accommodation and secretion as well as swallowing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 1 — medullary swallowing center","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The myenteric plexus sits in the gut wall,","C":"The nucleus ambiguus sends somatic motor fibers to","D":"The celiac ganglion is a sympathetic relay outside","E":"The inferior vagal ganglion holds afferent cell bodies,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain how the enteric nervous system independently regulates GI motility, secretion, and endocrine function and how extrinsic autonomic input modulates its activity. (Fig. 8.3)","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has a distended abdomen. A contrast enema shows a narrow rectum and sigmoid colon with dilated colon above, and a rectal suction biopsy shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa. Why is the narrow segment tonically contracted rather than flaccid?","choices":{"A":"Excess vagal cholinergic drive to the rectum","B":"Sympathetic overactivity in the segment","C":"No inhibitory neurons are there to relax it","D":"Hypertrophy of the circular smooth muscle","E":"Loss of interstitial cells of Cajal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failed neural crest migration leaves the distal colon without myenteric and submucosal ganglion cells, including the inhibitory motor neurons that release VIP, nitric oxide and ATP to relax muscle ahead of a bolus, so the aganglionic segment stays contracted and the bowel above it dilates. The rectum gets parasympathetic supply from the pelvic nerves rather than the vagus, and extrinsic drive is not the defect. Sympathetic overactivity is not the lesion, which is a missing population of neurons within the wall. Hypertrophy of circular muscle defines pyloric stenosis, and the dilated proximal colon here is secondary to distal obstruction. Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves, and their loss would weaken rhythmic contraction rather than cause tonic spasm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, section 5, enteric nervous system); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hirschsprung disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric inhibitory motor neurons release VIP, NO and ATP to relax muscle ahead of the bolus, while excitatory neurons release ACh and substance P behind it (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 4). COMLEX: delayed meconium with a narrow distal segment calls for rectal suction biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — LES relaxation ahead of the bolus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectum gets parasympathetic supply from the pelvic","B":"Sympathetic overactivity is not the lesion, which is","D":"Hypertrophy of circular muscle defines pyloric stenosis, and","E":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves, and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl04_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation of the GI tract, including ganglion location, fiber length, neurotransmitters, and effects on GI function. (Fig. 8.2)","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has 8 hours of dull, poorly localized pain around her umbilicus, then nausea, and now sharp pain that has moved to the right lower quadrant. At surgery her appendix is obstructed and inflamed. Why was her early pain felt around the umbilicus?","choices":{"A":"Phrenic afferents refer pain from the diaphragm","B":"Parietal peritoneum is irritated by the appendix","C":"Vagal afferents relay it to the solitary nucleus","D":"Pelvic splanchnic afferents enter at S2 to S4","E":"Visceral afferents enter the cord at T8 to T10"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Visceral pain fibers from the midgut appendix travel with the sympathetic splanchnic nerves, have cell bodies in dorsal root ganglia, and enter the cord at T8 to T10, so distension of the obstructed appendix is referred to the periumbilical dermatomes. Phrenic afferents refer diaphragmatic irritation to the shoulder, as with free air under the diaphragm. Parietal peritoneal irritation comes later, when inflammation reaches the serosa, and it produces the sharp localized right lower quadrant pain. Vagal afferents ending in the solitary nucleus drive reflexes such as receptive relaxation and acid secretion rather than referred dermatomal pain. Pelvic splanchnic fibers run with sacral parasympathetic outflow to the distal colon and rectum, not the midgut appendix. Sources: First Aid 2025 (appendicitis); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 4, sections 2 and 3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an obstructed appendix stimulates visceral afferents at T8 to T10, giving periumbilical pain before right lower quadrant pain (First Aid 2025). COMLEX: migration of pain to McBurney point signals parietal peritoneal irritation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Phrenic afferents refer diaphragmatic irritation to the shoulder,","B":"Parietal peritoneal irritation comes later, when inflammation reaches","C":"Vagal afferents ending in the solitary nucleus drive","D":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers run with sacral parasympathetic outflow"}}]},{"sdlNumber":5,"title":"SDL 5 — Physiology - GI Hormones, Paracrines, & Appetite","questions":[{"id":"sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"How does a GI paracrine reach its target cell?","choices":{"A":"Portal then systemic circulation","B":"Release into a synaptic cleft","C":"Local interstitial diffusion","D":"Secretion into the gut lumen","E":"Retrograde axonal transport"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Paracrines are secreted by endocrine cells of the GI mucosa and act locally, diffusing short distances through interstitial fluid to adjacent target cells. Travel through the portal and then systemic circulation describes hormones. Release into a synaptic cleft after an action potential describes neurocrines. Secretion into the lumen describes exocrine products such as enzymes. Retrograde axonal transport carries signals within neurons, not between cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (classification of GI peptides).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin and histamine are the classic GI paracrines (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: H2 blockers interrupt a paracrine signal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"Which GI peptide acts as a neurocrine released by enteric neurons?","choices":{"A":"GIP","B":"VIP","C":"CCK","D":"PYY","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is synthesized in enteric neurons and released at synapses, so it is a neurocrine. GIP, CCK, and gastrin are official GI hormones secreted by K, I, and G cells into the blood. Peptide YY is a candidate hormone from L cells of the ileum and colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors). COMLEX: VIP relaxes GI smooth muscle and sphincters.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Peptide YY is a candidate hormone from L cells of the ileum and colon.","A":"GIP is not part of that answer.","C":"CCK is not part of that answer.","E":"Gastrin is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl05_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":1,"stem":"Which paracrine is released by enterochromaffin-like cells in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Histamine","B":"Somatostatin","C":"Gastrin","D":"Secretin","E":"Ghrelin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"ECL cells in the oxyntic region release histamine, which diffuses to neighboring parietal cells and binds H2 receptors. Somatostatin is a paracrine, but it comes from D cells. Gastrin is a hormone from antral G cells. Secretin is a hormone from duodenal S cells. Ghrelin is a hormone from gastric oxyntic cells that acts on the hypothalamus. Costanzo describes somatostatin as a D-cell paracrine released in response to low luminal pH that inhibits gastric H+ secretion. Costanzo states secretin is secreted by the S cells of the duodenum, not by ECL cells in the oxyntic mucosa. Guyton identifies the enterochromaffin-like cells of the oxyntic glands as the cells that secrete histamine, not ghrelin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines, and section 6, ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells through a Gs cascade (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 receptor antagonists reduce acid by blocking this paracrine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric neurons and their neurocrines","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastrin is a hormone from antral G cells.","B":"Costanzo describes somatostatin as a D-cell paracrine released in response to low luminal pH that inhibits gastric H+ secretion.","D":"Costanzo states secretin is secreted by the S cells of the duodenum, not by ECL cells in the oxyntic mucosa.","E":"Guyton identifies the enterochromaffin-like cells of the oxyntic glands as the cells that secrete histamine, not ghrelin."}},{"id":"sdl05_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"S cells release secretin in response to which stimulus?","choices":{"A":"Amino acids in the stomach","B":"Glucose in the jejunum","C":"Distension of the antrum","D":"Vagal release of GRP","E":"Acid in the duodenum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when acidic chyme below pH 4.5 enters the duodenum, and fatty acids also stimulate it. Amino acids in the stomach, antral distension, and vagal GRP release are stimuli for gastrin. Glucose in the small intestine stimulates GIP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- secretion and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin also inhibits gastrin release.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"Fatty acids entering the duodenum release a hormone from I cells that contracts the gallbladder. Which hormone is this?","choices":{"A":"CCK","B":"PYY","C":"VIP","D":"GIP","E":"Gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is secreted by I cells of the duodenum and jejunum in response to monoglycerides, fatty acids, small peptides, and amino acids; it contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion. GIP comes from K cells and stimulates insulin release. PYY comes from L cells and decreases appetite. VIP is a neurocrine. Gastrin comes from G cells and stimulates acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases pancreatic secretion and gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals provoke biliary colic through CCK.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":1,"stem":"One GI hormone is secreted in response to all three nutrient types: glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"I cells","B":"S cells","C":"K cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GIP is secreted by K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa and, unlike the other GI hormones, is released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids. I cells secrete CCK in response to fats and protein products. S cells secrete secretin in response to acid and fatty acids. G cells secrete gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids. L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and decreases gastric acid secretion (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP is an incretin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"I cells secrete CCK in response to fats and protein products.","B":"S cells secrete secretin in response to acid and fatty acids.","D":"G cells secrete gastrin in response to peptides and amino acids.","E":"L cells secrete GLP-1 and PYY."}},{"id":"sdl05_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome can cause steatorrhea. Which mechanism explains it?","choices":{"A":"Loss of bile acid secretion","B":"Acid inactivation of lipase","C":"Gastrin block of CCK release","D":"Villous atrophy from gluten","E":"Bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, excess acid acidifies the intestinal lumen and inactivates pancreatic lipase, so dietary fat is not digested and appears in the stool. Bile acid secretion is not reduced by gastrin. Gastrin does not block CCK release. Villous atrophy from gluten is celiac disease. Bacterial overgrowth causes malabsorption by a different mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Zollinger-Ellison syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrinoma presents with refractory ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastrinoma is associated with MEN1.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"In Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, chronically high gastrin levels change the gastric oxyntic mucosa in which way?","choices":{"A":"Atrophy and parietal loss","B":"Intestinal type metaplasia","C":"Fibrosis and scarring of glands","D":"Necrosis and mucosal sloughing","E":"Hypertrophy and hyperplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastrin has a trophic effect, so chronically elevated gastrin causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa with marked parietal cell hyperplasia. Atrophy with parietal cell loss occurs after antrectomy or in autoimmune gastritis. Intestinal metaplasia follows chronic gastritis. Fibrosis and necrosis are not effects of gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin increases growth of the gastric mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: thickened gastric folds with refractory ulcers suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptic ulcer location most strongly suggests Zollinger-Ellison syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Lesser curvature","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal jejunum","E":"Gastric cardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Zollinger-Ellison syndrome causes severe recurrent ulcers in the duodenum and stomach and frequently in unusual distal sites such as the jejunum, because excess unbuffered acid reaches the small intestine. The rectum is not exposed to gastric acid, so it is not a peptic ulcer site in this syndrome. Lesser curvature and antral ulcers are typical gastric ulcers. The cardia is an uncommon ulcer site with no link to gastrinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, ulcer location).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are most common in gastrinoma, but jejunal ulcers are the most specific clue (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: ulcers that do not respond to therapy suggest gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lesser curvature and antral ulcers are typical gastric ulcers.","B":"Lesser curvature is typical gastric ulcers.","E":"The cardia is an uncommon ulcer site with no link to gastrinoma.","A":"The rectum is not exposed to gastric acid."}},{"id":"sdl05_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Oral glucose releases more insulin than an intravenous glucose load that produces the same blood glucose concentration. Which hormone is mainly responsible?","choices":{"A":"GIP from K cells","B":"CCK from I cells","C":"Secretin from S cells","D":"Gastrin from G cells","E":"Ghrelin from the stomach"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Oral glucose stimulates GIP release from K cells, and GIP binds receptors on pancreatic beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion; GLP-1 from L cells contributes. Intravenous glucose bypasses the gut and relies on direct beta cell sensing. CCK, secretin, and gastrin are not incretins in the lecture. Ghrelin increases appetite and is suppressed by food. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP increases insulin release and is stimulated by oral glucose (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: oral glucose produces a larger insulin response than intravenous glucose.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"The greater insulin response to oral glucose than to an equivalent intravenous glucose load has which name?","choices":{"A":"Somogyi effect","B":"Incretin effect","C":"Dawn phenomenon","D":"Glucose toxicity","E":"Insulin resistance"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The larger insulin release after oral glucose is the incretin effect, mediated by GIP and GLP-1. The Somogyi effect is rebound hyperglycemia after nocturnal hypoglycemia. The dawn phenomenon is early morning hyperglycemia from counterregulatory hormones. Glucose toxicity is beta cell impairment from chronic hyperglycemia. Insulin resistance is a reduced tissue response to insulin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are incretins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":1,"stem":"Besides GIP, the incretin GLP-1 amplifies insulin release. Which cells secrete GLP-1?","choices":{"A":"K cells of duodenum and jejunum","B":"I cells of duodenum and jejunum","C":"S cells of the duodenum","D":"L cells of ileum and colon","E":"G cells of the gastric antrum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GLP-1 is cleaved from proglucagon and secreted by L cells of the distal ileum and colon in response to carbohydrates and fats. K cells secrete GIP. I cells secrete CCK. S cells secrete secretin. Antral G cells secrete gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin action); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 increases insulin secretion, decreases gastric emptying, and decreases appetite (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs promote weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — GIP and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"K cells secrete GIP.","B":"I cells secrete CCK.","C":"S cells secrete secretin.","E":"Antral G cells secrete gastrin"}},{"id":"sdl05_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Somatostatin from antral D cells diffuses to neighboring G cells. What effect does it have on them?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastrin release","B":"Inhibits gastrin release","C":"Stimulates histamine release","D":"Blocks gastrin receptors","E":"Destroys G cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Somatostatin binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin synthesis and release. Stimulation of gastrin release is produced by peptides, distension, and vagal GRP. Somatostatin also inhibits, rather than stimulates, histamine release. It acts on G cells, not on gastrin receptors of parietal cells. It inhibits G cells without destroying them. Costanzo states that somatostatin acts by a paracrine mechanism to inhibit, not stimulate, gastrin release from G cells. Costanzo states that paracrine somatostatin inhibits histamine release from ECL cells rather than stimulating it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin decreases gastric acid and pepsinogen secretion and many hormones (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide is a somatostatin analog.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"It acts on G cells, not on gastrin receptors of parietal cells.","A":"Costanzo states that somatostatin acts by a paracrine mechanism to inhibit, not stimulate, gastrin release from G cells.","C":"Costanzo states that paracrine somatostatin inhibits histamine release from ECL cells rather than stimulating it."}},{"id":"sdl05_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"D cells release somatostatin in response to which luminal condition?","choices":{"A":"High gastric pH","B":"Gastric distension","C":"Low gastric pH","D":"Luminal amino acids","E":"Vagal GRP release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"D cells release somatostatin in response to low luminal pH, providing negative feedback that suppresses gastrin and acid. A high gastric pH, distension, luminal amino acids, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release instead. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (low pH stimulates somatostatin, which inhibits parietal and G cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin is increased by acid and decreased by vagal stimulation (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: this feedback is lost in gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Histamine from ECL cells increases parietal cell acid secretion through which receptor and second messenger?","choices":{"A":"H2 receptor and cAMP","B":"H1 receptor and IP3","C":"CCK2 receptor and calcium","D":"M3 receptor and calcium","E":"SST receptor and Gi"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors on parietal cells, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cAMP and drive the H+/K+-ATPase. H1 receptors act through IP3 in other tissues. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors through calcium. ACh acts on M3 receptors through calcium. A somatostatin receptor coupled to Gi lowers cAMP, which is how somatostatin inhibits acid secretion. Costanzo states histamine binds parietal cell H2 receptors coupled by Gs to adenylyl cyclase, so its second messenger is cAMP rather than IP3. Costanzo assigns CCK2 receptors with IP3/Ca2+ signaling to gastrin, not to histamine from ECL cells. Costanzo assigns muscarinic M3 receptors with IP3/Ca2+ signaling to vagal ACh, not to histamine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect on parietal cell acid secretion); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (ECL cell histamine and D cell somatostatin pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: histamine potentiates the acid stimulating effects of ACh and gastrin (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce acid stimulated by all three secretagogues.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 4 — somatostatin, histamine, and acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A somatostatin receptor coupled to Gi lowers cAMP, which is how somatostatin inhibits acid secretion.","B":"Costanzo states histamine binds parietal cell H2 receptors coupled by Gs to adenylyl cyclase, so its second messenger is cAMP rather than IP3.","C":"Costanzo assigns CCK2 receptors with IP3/Ca2+ signaling to gastrin, not to histamine from ECL cells.","D":"Costanzo assigns muscarinic M3 receptors with IP3/Ca2+ signaling to vagal ACh, not to histamine."}},{"id":"sdl05_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Which peptide rises before meals and during fasting to increase food intake?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Serotonin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric oxyntic cells before meals and during fasting and is orexigenic. Leptin from adipocytes, PYY from L cells, insulin from beta cells, and serotonin are anorexigenic in the lecture. Leptin from adipose tissue stimulates anorexigenic neurons and inhibits orexigenic neurons, so it decreases rather than increases food intake. Insulin, like leptin, stimulates anorexigenic neurons and inhibits orexigenic neurons, acutely decreasing appetite. Serotonin is not the named fasting orexigenic signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, hormones and peptides influencing appetite).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in the fasting state and in Prader-Willi syndrome and decreased after gastric bypass (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin also increases gastric motility.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Leptin from adipocytes, PYY from L cells, insulin from beta cells, and serotonin are anorexigenic in the lecture.","A":"Leptin from adipose tissue stimulates anorexigenic neurons and inhibits orexigenic neurons, so it decreases rather than increases food intake.","D":"Insulin, like leptin, stimulates anorexigenic neurons and inhibits orexigenic neurons, acutely decreasing appetite.","E":"Serotonin is not the named fasting orexigenic signal."}},{"id":"sdl05_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"Leptin is an anorexigenic signal secreted in proportion to body fat mass. Which cells secrete it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes","B":"Enterocytes","C":"Beta cells","D":"Myocytes","E":"Adipocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin is secreted by adipocytes in proportion to fat mass and activates POMC neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons. Hepatocytes and myocytes are not listed sources of appetite peptides. Enterocytes do not secrete leptin, although L and I cells of the intestine secrete PYY, GLP-1, and CCK. Beta cells secrete insulin, another anorexigenic signal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin and insulin stimulate the anorexigenic pathway (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: leptin rises with fat mass.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":1,"stem":"In the orexigenic pathway, AGRP released from arcuate neurons acts at melanocortin-4 receptors in the paraventricular nucleus. How does it act there?","choices":{"A":"Competitive antagonist","B":"Partial receptor agonist","C":"Full receptor agonist","D":"Positive allosteric enhancer","E":"Irreversible agonist"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AGRP is a competitive antagonist at MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus, blocking satiety signaling while NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus. Partial, full, and irreversible agonists would activate MC4R and reduce appetite, which is the role of alpha-MSH. A positive allosteric enhancer would increase MC4R signaling. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MC4R defects cause hyperphagia and early onset obesity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: AGRP and NPY increase appetite.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with reflux takes a drug that blocks receptors for a substance released by ECL cells. Which mode of signaling does the drug interrupt?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine diffusion","B":"Endocrine circulation","C":"Neurocrine release","D":"Autocrine feedback","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine from ECL cells diffuses through interstitial fluid to adjacent parietal cells, which is paracrine signaling. Endocrine signaling uses the portal and systemic circulation. Neurocrine signaling is release by neurons into a synapse. Autocrine signaling acts on the releasing cell itself. Luminal secretion releases products into the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, paracrines); Costanzo Ch. 8 (paracrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptor antagonists block histamine on parietal cells (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: H2 blockers are used for reflux and peptic ulcer disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old woman eats a fatty meal. A peptide from duodenal I cells contracts her gallbladder. How is this peptide classified?","choices":{"A":"Paracrine","B":"Autocrine","C":"Neurocrine","D":"GI hormone","E":"Exocrine enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CCK is secreted by I cells into the portal circulation, passes through the liver into the systemic circulation, and acts on distant targets, so it is a GI hormone. Paracrines act locally by diffusion. Neurocrines are released by neurons at synapses. Autocrines act on the cell that secretes them. Exocrine enzymes are secreted into the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrointestinal hormones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the four official GI hormones are gastrin, CCK, secretin, and GIP (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: CCK release explains postprandial biliary colic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and duodenal control of the stomach","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old man in a physiology study has electrical stimulation of the vagus nerve, and his serum gastrin rises even after atropine. The mediator is a peptide released by an action potential. How is this mediator classified?","choices":{"A":"Circulating hormone","B":"Paracrine peptide","C":"Neurocrine peptide","D":"Autocrine signal","E":"Luminal secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastrin-releasing peptide is released by postganglionic vagal and enteric neurons onto G cells after an action potential, so it is a neurocrine; it is not cholinergic, which is why atropine does not block it. Circulating hormones travel in the blood. Paracrines are released by endocrine cells and diffuse locally. Autocrine signals act on the releasing cell. Luminal secretions enter the gut lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, neurocrines).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vagal stimulation increases gastrin through GRP (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GRP is also called bombesin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 43-year-old man has a study in which acidic chyme with a pH of 4 is infused into his duodenum. His pancreatic juice volume and bicarbonate concentration rise. Which hormone is responsible?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin","B":"Motilin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Secretin","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acid below pH 4.5 in the duodenum releases secretin from S cells, which stimulates pancreatic ductal and biliary bicarbonate secretion to neutralize the acid. Gastrin release is inhibited by acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives migrating motor complexes. Ghrelin increases appetite. Somatostatin is also released by acid but inhibits pancreatic secretion. Gastrin is secreted by antral G cells to promote H+ secretion by parietal cells, not pancreatic bicarbonate secretion. Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting to initiate the migrating complexes, not in response to duodenal acid. Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells before a meal to increase appetite and does not stimulate pancreatic bicarbonate secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, secretin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secretin, motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic HCO3- and decreases gastric acid (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: secretin is secreted by S cells in the duodenal mucosa (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — secretin and duodenal acid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin is also released by acid but inhibits pancreatic secretion.","A":"Gastrin is secreted by antral G cells to promote H+ secretion by parietal cells, not pancreatic bicarbonate secretion.","B":"Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting to initiate the migrating complexes, not in response to duodenal acid.","C":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells before a meal to increase appetite and does not stimulate pancreatic bicarbonate secretion."}},{"id":"sdl05_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has hepatobiliary scintigraphy and receives an analog of the duodenal hormone that contracts the gallbladder. Which additional effect does this hormone produce?","choices":{"A":"Faster gastric emptying","B":"Increased gastric acid","C":"Inhibition of enzyme release","D":"Contraction of Oddi sphincter","E":"Slower gastric emptying"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK contracts the gallbladder, relaxes the sphincter of Oddi, stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion, and delays gastric emptying so that fat enters the duodenum slowly. Faster emptying is the opposite effect. Increased gastric acid is an action of gastrin. CCK relaxes rather than contracts the sphincter of Oddi. CCK stimulates, rather than inhibits, enzyme release. Costanzo states that CCK inhibits or slows gastric emptying and increases gastric emptying time. Costanzo lists secretion of pancreatic enzymes among the major actions of CCK, so it stimulates rather than inhibits enzyme release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, cholecystokinin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: CCK analogs are used to measure gallbladder ejection fraction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"CCK relaxes rather than contracts the sphincter of Oddi.","A":"Costanzo states that CCK inhibits or slows gastric emptying and increases gastric emptying time.","C":"Costanzo lists secretion of pancreatic enzymes among the major actions of CCK, so it stimulates rather than inhibits enzyme release."}},{"id":"sdl05_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man eats a steak dinner, and his serum gastrin rises within minutes. Which luminal component stimulates his antral G cells?","choices":{"A":"Intact dietary triglycerides","B":"Small peptides and amino acids","C":"Free fatty acids and monoglycerides","D":"Luminal acid below pH 3","E":"Glucose and galactose"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Small peptides and amino acids in the stomach, distension, and vagal GRP stimulate gastrin release. Intact triglycerides do not release gastrin. Fatty acids and monoglycerides in the duodenum release CCK. Acid below pH 3 inhibits gastrin release. Glucose stimulates GIP rather than gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is increased by distension, alkalinization, amino acids, peptides, and vagal GRP, and decreased by gastric acid; the lecture uses pH below 3, while First Aid gives pH below 1.5 (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: protein meals raise gastrin.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — gastric phase of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has ulcers in the distal duodenum, chronic diarrhea, and weight loss. His fasting serum gastrin is markedly elevated while his gastric pH is low. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastrinoma","B":"Insulinoma","C":"VIPoma","D":"Somatostatinoma","E":"Glucagonoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Jejunal ulcers with high gastrin and low gastric pH indicate Zollinger-Ellison syndrome, caused by a gastrinoma. An insulinoma causes hypoglycemia. A VIPoma causes watery diarrhea with achlorhydria, so gastric pH would be high. A somatostatinoma is a delta cell tumor that does not raise gastrin. A glucagonoma causes diabetes and necrolytic migratory erythema. Insulinomas, the most common pancreatic endocrine neoplasms, present with fasting hypoglycemic episodes promptly relieved by glucose, not with gastrin-driven ulcers. Glucagonomas raise serum glucagon and cause mild diabetes mellitus, necrolytic migratory erythema, and anemia, mostly in perimenopausal and postmenopausal women, rather than acid hypersecretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, responsible tumor); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma and pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a gastrinoma is a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: gastrinomas often lie in the gastrinoma triangle.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A VIPoma causes watery diarrhea with achlorhydria, so gastric pH would be high.","D":"A somatostatinoma is a delta cell tumor that does not raise gastrin.","B":"Insulinomas, the most common pancreatic endocrine neoplasms, present with fasting hypoglycemic episodes promptly relieved by glucose, not with gastrin-driven ulcers.","E":"Glucagonomas raise serum glucagon and cause mild diabetes mellitus, necrolytic migratory erythema, and anemia, mostly in perimenopausal and postmenopausal women, rather than acid hypersecretion."}},{"id":"sdl05_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 65-year-old man has a gastrinoma with a gastric pH near 1. In a healthy stomach, this acidity would sharply reduce gastrin release. Why does his gastrin stay high?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin excess","B":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","C":"Loss of histamine receptors","D":"Tumor ignores pH feedback","E":"Vagal hyperactivity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin from the tumor is not subject to negative feedback by low gastric pH, so acid secretion becomes massive and autonomous. Somatostatin excess would lower gastrin. Parietal cell autoimmunity raises gastrin by removing acid, which is the opposite pH. Loss of histamine receptors would reduce acid. Vagal hyperactivity increases gastrin through GRP but would still be subject to acid feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, H+ secretion effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is decreased by gastric acid in normal physiology (lecture pH below 3; First Aid pH below 1.5) and increased in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: treatment uses proton pump inhibitors and tumor resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — feedback control of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers despite treatment. Endoscopy shows prominent thickened folds in the gastric body, and his serum gastrin is markedly elevated. Which mechanism explains the thickened folds?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin induced edema","B":"Trophic effect of gastrin","C":"Lymphocytic infiltration","D":"Foveolar mucous hyperplasia","E":"Submucosal fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronically elevated gastrin from a gastrinoma has a trophic effect that causes hypertrophy and hyperplasia of the oxyntic mucosa, including parietal cell hyperplasia, which thickens the folds. Gastrin acts by growth of the mucosa, not by edema. Lymphocytic infiltration is a feature of gastritis or lymphoma. Foveolar mucous hyperplasia without high gastrin is Ménétrier disease. Submucosal fibrosis follows ulcer healing, not gastrin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, mucosal effect); Costanzo Ch. 8 (trophic effect of gastrin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin stimulates trophic growth of the gastric oxyntic mucosa (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: refractory ulcers with thick folds warrant a fasting gastrin level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a fasting study has a normal plasma glucose. He receives an intravenous infusion of GIP with no glucose. How does his insulin secretion respond?","choices":{"A":"Large rise causing hypoglycemia","B":"Fall below the fasting level","C":"Rise matching an oral glucose load","D":"Little change from fasting level","E":"Delayed rise after several hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"GIP stimulates insulin secretion from beta cells in a glucose-dependent manner: it amplifies glucose-induced insulin release, so with normal fasting glucose and no glucose load, insulin changes little. A large rise with hypoglycemia would require insulin release independent of glucose. GIP does not suppress insulin below fasting levels. A rise matching an oral glucose load needs both the glucose stimulus and the incretins. A delayed rise after several hours is not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP stimulates insulin in a glucose-dependent manner, p29; section 4, incretins amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion, p29).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP is the only GI hormone released by glucose, amino acids, and fatty acids (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 act through G-protein coupled receptors on beta cells (lecture SDL 5).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GIP release and actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 51-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts a weekly injected analog of the incretin secreted by ileal L cells. Besides improving insulin secretion, which effect helps her lose weight?","choices":{"A":"Increased ghrelin","B":"Increased glucagon release","C":"Reduced appetite","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower leptin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"GLP-1 analogs amplify glucose-dependent insulin release, slow gastric emptying, and are anorexigenic, reducing appetite. Increased ghrelin would promote weight gain. GLP-1 inhibits rather than increases glucagon release. GLP-1 slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. Lower leptin would increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4 and 6, GLP-1); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GLP-1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs treat type 2 diabetes and promote weight loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nausea from slowed gastric emptying is a common adverse effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormones that slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman eats a mixed meal, and her duodenal K cells release a hormone that amplifies her insulin secretion. Which other action does this hormone have?","choices":{"A":"Stimulates gastric acid","B":"Contracts the gallbladder","C":"Speeds gastric emptying","D":"Increases appetite","E":"Inhibits gastric acid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"GIP from K cells stimulates insulin release and also inhibits gastric H+ secretion, inhibits gastrin, and slows gastric motility. Stimulation of acid is an action of gastrin. Gallbladder contraction is an action of CCK. GIP slows rather than speeds gastric emptying. GIP is listed as anorexigenic, so it does not increase appetite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, GIP, and section 6, anorexigenic hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8 (GIP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP decreases gastric H+ secretion and increases insulin release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: GIP was first named gastric inhibitory peptide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — inhibitors of acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman with acromegaly starts octreotide, an analog of a D cell paracrine. Her serum gastrin falls. Which mechanism explains the fall?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of G cells","B":"Inhibition of parietal cells","C":"Blockade of H2 receptors","D":"Rise in gastric pH","E":"Loss of vagal GRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Octreotide mimics somatostatin, which binds receptors on G cells and suppresses gastrin release. Inhibition of parietal cells lowers acid but would raise, not lower, gastrin through loss of acid feedback. Blockade of H2 receptors is the action of H2 antagonists. A rise in gastric pH would increase gastrin. Octreotide does not remove vagal GRP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin effect on gastrin secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is a somatostatin analog used for acromegaly, carcinoid syndrome, VIPoma, and variceal bleeding (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide can cause gallstones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — somatostatin","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl05_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman takes a drug that blocks histamine receptors on parietal cells, and her acid secretion falls. Which second messenger falls in her parietal cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Diacylglycerol","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Cyclic AMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Histamine binds H2 receptors, activating Gs and adenylyl cyclase to raise cyclic AMP and protein kinase A activity, which drives the H+/K+-ATPase; blocking H2 receptors lowers cyclic AMP. IP3 and calcium mediate ACh and gastrin. Cyclic GMP and nitric oxide mediate smooth muscle relaxation. Diacylglycerol accompanies IP3 in the calcium pathway. Costanzo describes diacylglycerol as a product of phospholipase C in ACh signaling, whereas H2 receptor blockade lowers cAMP. Costanzo states the second messenger for histamine is cAMP, since H2 receptors couple through Gs to adenylyl cyclase, not cyclic GMP. Costanzo describes histamine acting through Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, not nitric oxide, at parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, histamine effect).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 receptors use a Gs cascade to increase H+/K+-ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acid secretion pathways). COMLEX: H2 blockers reduce nocturnal acid secretion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — H2 receptor antagonists","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IP3 and calcium mediate ACh and gastrin.","C":"Costanzo describes diacylglycerol as a product of phospholipase C in ACh signaling, whereas H2 receptor blockade lowers cAMP.","B":"Costanzo states the second messenger for histamine is cAMP, since H2 receptors couple through Gs to adenylyl cyclase, not cyclic GMP.","D":"Costanzo describes histamine acting through Gs-coupled adenylyl cyclase and cAMP, not nitric oxide, at parietal cells."}},{"id":"sdl05_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man has antral H. pylori gastritis and a duodenal ulcer. His serum gastrin is elevated and his gastric acid output is high. Loss or suppression of which gastric cell best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"G cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"D cells","D":"ECL cells","E":"Chief cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antral gastritis causes loss or suppression of D cell somatostatin (Bootcamp describes D cell destruction; Costanzo describes inhibited somatostatin secretion), which removes inhibition of G cells and parietal cells and raises gastrin and acid. Loss of G cells would lower gastrin. Loss of parietal cells would lower acid. Loss of ECL cells would lower histamine and acid. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and do not control gastrin. Costanzo states H. pylori inhibits somatostatin secretion from antral D cells, and this removal of inhibition raises gastrin and H+ secretion, not loss of ECL cells. Costanzo states H. pylori inhibits somatostatin secretion from antral D cells, and this removal of inhibition raises gastrin and H+ secretion, not loss of chief cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (destruction of antral D cells lowers somatostatin and raises acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are associated with H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: eradicating H. pylori lowers ulcer recurrence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of G cells would lower gastrin.","B":"Loss of parietal cells would lower acid.","D":"Loss of ECL cells would lower histamine and acid.","E":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and do not control gastrin."}},{"id":"sdl05_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has severe hyperphagia and obesity, and his serum leptin is undetectable because of a gene mutation. Which change in his arcuate nucleus explains his hunger?","choices":{"A":"Less POMC neuron activity","B":"Less AGRP neuron activity","C":"Less NPY neuron activity","D":"More MC4R activation","E":"Lower ghrelin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin activates POMC and CART neurons and inhibits NPY and AGRP neurons. Without leptin, POMC activity falls, alpha-MSH no longer activates MC4R, and NPY and AGRP activity rises, increasing appetite. Less AGRP activity, less NPY activity, and more MC4R activation would reduce appetite. Lower ghrelin would also reduce hunger. Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal and stimulates orexigenic neurons while inhibiting anorexigenic neurons, so less ghrelin would curb hyperphagia. Costanzo states leptin inhibits orexigenic neurons, so its absence increases rather than decreases NPY neuron activity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (leptin stimulates the anorexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin activates POMC and CART neurons while inhibiting NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: leptin acts on the arcuate nucleus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Less AGRP neuron activity would reduce appetite.","D":"More MC4R activation would reduce appetite.","E":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal and stimulates orexigenic neurons while inhibiting anorexigenic neurons, so less ghrelin would curb hyperphagia.","C":"Costanzo states leptin inhibits orexigenic neurons, so its absence increases rather than decreases NPY neuron activity."}},{"id":"sdl05_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl with Prader-Willi syndrome has constant hunger, and her plasma ghrelin is markedly elevated. Which arcuate neurons does ghrelin activate to increase her food intake?","choices":{"A":"POMC and CART neurons","B":"NPY and AGRP neurons","C":"Lateral hypothalamic neurons","D":"Paraventricular MC4R neurons","E":"Brainstem NTS neurons"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ghrelin stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons in the arcuate nucleus; NPY activates the lateral hypothalamus and AGRP blocks MC4R in the paraventricular nucleus. POMC and CART neurons are anorexigenic and are activated by leptin and insulin. The lateral hypothalamus and paraventricular nucleus are downstream targets, not arcuate neurons. The NTS receives MC4R projections in the satiety pathway. Costanzo describes POMC neurons as anorexigenic and stimulated by leptin and insulin, whereas ghrelin stimulates the orexigenic pathway. Guyton places the NPY-AGRP neurons that increase food intake, and that are targets of ghrelin, in the arcuate nuclei rather than the lateral hypothalamus. Guyton places the NPY-AGRP neurons that increase food intake, and that are targets of ghrelin, in the hypothalamic arcuate nuclei rather than the brainstem NTS. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, orexigenic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: ghrelin rises with fasting and sleep deprivation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, stomach).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The lateral hypothalamus and paraventricular nucleus are downstream targets, not arcuate neurons.","A":"Costanzo describes POMC neurons as anorexigenic and stimulated by leptin and insulin, whereas ghrelin stimulates the orexigenic pathway.","C":"The lateral hypothalamus is a downstream target of arcuate NPY neurons, not the arcuate population itself.","E":"The brainstem NTS is a satiety relay, not the arcuate population ghrelin activates."}},{"id":"sdl05_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman reports much less hunger after a gastric bypass operation. Which hormonal change most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Higher orexin release","B":"Lower PYY levels","C":"Higher NPY release","D":"Reduced leptin action","E":"Lower ghrelin levels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass, reducing stimulation of NPY and AGRP neurons and lowering hunger. Higher orexin release, higher NPY release, and reduced leptin action would increase hunger, because orexins and NPY are orexigenic. PYY is anorexigenic, so lower PYY would also increase hunger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, appetite hormones); First Aid 2025 GI (ghrelin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is decreased after gastric bypass surgery (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: PYY decreases appetite by inhibiting ghrelin secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — For each of the three categories- hormone, paracrine, neurocrine- list examples of each, where each is released from, and how it travels to reach its target cell. Fig 8.4","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has had about 3 L per day of watery diarrhea for two months, and it continues while she fasts. Serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), gastric analysis shows very low acid output, and CT shows a mass in the pancreatic tail. In healthy people, how does the peptide this tumor secretes normally reach its target cells?","choices":{"A":"Local diffusion from mucosal D cells","B":"Secretion into the lumen by goblet cells","C":"Portal blood from duodenal endocrine cells","D":"Diffusion from enterochromaffin like cells","E":"Release by enteric neurons at a synapse"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Watery diarrhea, hypokalemia and achlorhydria from a pancreatic tumor point to a VIPoma, and VIP is normally a neurocrine, made in enteric neurons and released into the synaptic cleft to act on nearby muscle, gland and vascular cells. Local diffusion from D cells describes somatostatin, a paracrine. Goblet cells secrete mucin into the lumen for lubrication and do not release signaling peptides. Portal and then systemic blood carries true GI hormones such as CCK, secretin and GIP from endocrine cells. Enterochromaffin-like cells release histamine, a paracrine that diffuses to nearby parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 1, categories of GI peptides); First Aid 2025 (VIPoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GI hormones travel in blood, paracrines diffuse locally, and neurocrines such as VIP are released by enteric neurons (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 5). COMLEX: a VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia and achlorhydria and can be treated with octreotide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric enteroendocrine cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Local diffusion from D cells describes somatostatin, a","B":"Goblet cells secrete mucin into the lumen for","C":"Portal and then systemic blood carries true GI","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells release histamine, a paracrine that diffuses"}},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — For each of the 4 GI hormones: explain where each is secreted from, what triggers its release, and its main action(s). Table 8.2","batch":3,"stem":"In a physiology study, a healthy volunteer's duodenum is perfused on one day with a triglyceride emulsion mixed with a drug that blocks pancreatic lipase, and on another day with an equal load of the corresponding free fatty acids. Ultrasound shows only weak gallbladder contraction during the first perfusion and brisk contraction during the second. Which explanation best fits this difference?","choices":{"A":"Fatty acids inhibit I cell secretion","B":"Gastrin occupies the CCK receptors","C":"Secretin relaxes the gallbladder wall","D":"Too few fatty acids form to release CCK","E":"Intact triglycerides strongly release CCK"},"correct":"D","explanation":"CCK from duodenal and jejunal I cells is released by fatty acids and monoglycerides but not by intact triglycerides, so when lipase is blocked the triglyceride stays undigested, little CCK is released, and the gallbladder barely contracts, whereas the free fatty acids release CCK directly. Fatty acids are a main stimulus of I cells, so they do not inhibit CCK secretion. Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors of parietal and ECL cells, and nothing in either perfusion raises it. Secretin stimulates pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion and does not relax the gallbladder. Intact triglycerides are not a CCK stimulus, which is why the lipase-blocked perfusion gave a weak response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 2, characteristics of the four GI hormones); Costanzo Ch. 8, cholecystokinin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK from I cells is released by fatty acids, monoglycerides, small peptides and amino acids, not intact triglycerides, and contracts the gallbladder (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 5). COMLEX: lipase must act before dietary fat can signal the gallbladder and pancreas through CCK.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — CCK and secretin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fatty acids are a main stimulus of I cells","B":"Gastrin acts on CCK2 receptors of parietal","C":"Secretin stimulates pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion","E":"Intact triglycerides are not a CCK stimulus"}},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Name the tumor responsible for Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and list its three consequences (H+ secretion, mucosal effect, ulcer location).","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has hypercalcemia from parathyroid hyperplasia and peptic ulcers that do not heal with treatment. His fasting gastrin is high and his gastric pH is low. Which test best confirms the suspected tumor?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucose tolerance test","B":"Urea breath test","C":"Serum glucagon level","D":"Secretin stimulation test","E":"Dexamethasone suppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Refractory ulcers with high gastrin, low gastric pH, and MEN1 features suggest a gastrinoma, and the secretin stimulation test follows fasting gastrin and gastric pH in the workup. An oral glucose tolerance test evaluates diabetes. A urea breath test detects H. pylori. A serum glucagon level is used for glucagonoma. Dexamethasone suppression evaluates Cushing syndrome. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastrinoma diagnostics: fasting serum gastrin and gastric pH, then secretin stimulation test).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison syndrome is caused by a gastrin secreting neuroendocrine tumor in the pancreas or duodenum (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: screen for MEN1 in a patient with gastrinoma and hypercalcemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand which route of glucose administration (oral vs. IV) causes more insulin release for the same blood glucose level, and name the hormone responsible.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with type 2 diabetes had two episodes of hypoglycemia on glipizide and is switched to daily liraglutide. His fasting glucose falls from 168 to 112 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL), and over six months he has no further hypoglycemia. Why does the new drug rarely cause hypoglycemia when used without insulin or a sulfonylurea?","choices":{"A":"It acts through nuclear receptors in fat","B":"It is cleared within minutes of each meal","C":"It closes beta cell potassium channels","D":"Its insulin release depends on high glucose","E":"It blocks glucose reabsorption in the kidney"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Liraglutide is a GLP-1 analog, and like GIP, GLP-1 acts on G protein coupled receptors on beta cells to amplify glucose-induced insulin secretion, so its effect fades as glucose approaches normal. Nuclear receptor activation in fat is the action of pioglitazone, not of incretin analogs. Rapid clearance describes native GLP-1, which DPP-4 inactivates, whereas analogs are built to last much longer. Closing beta cell potassium channels is how sulfonylureas trigger insulin release regardless of glucose, which is why they cause hypoglycemia. Blocking renal glucose reabsorption is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 4, incretin effect); First Aid 2025 (diabetes drugs).","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral glucose releases more insulin than intravenous glucose because GIP and GLP-1 amplify glucose-dependent insulin secretion (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 5). COMLEX: GLP-1 analogs cause nausea and weight loss and rarely cause hypoglycemia without insulin or a sulfonylurea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — GIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nuclear receptor activation in fat is the action","B":"Rapid clearance describes native GLP-1, which DPP-4 inactivates,","C":"Closing beta cell potassium channels is how sulfonylureas","E":"Blocking renal glucose reabsorption is the action of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has taken high-dose omeprazole for 6 years. His fasting serum gastrin is 380 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), and his gastric pH at the time is 6.2. Imaging shows no pancreatic or duodenal mass. Which mechanism best explains his gastrin level?","choices":{"A":"Autonomous gastrin release from a tumor","B":"Histamine from ECL cells stimulating G cells","C":"Less somatostatin from antral D cells","D":"Destruction of antral G cells by antibodies","E":"Blockade of vagal GRP release onto G cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antral D cells release somatostatin when luminal pH falls, and it diffuses to G cells to suppress gastrin, so when omeprazole keeps the pH high this paracrine brake is lost and gastrin rises. A gastrinoma secretes gastrin despite low pH, so its gastric pH is very acidic rather than near 6. Histamine from ECL cells acts on parietal cell H2 receptors and does not drive gastrin release. Destruction of G cells would lower gastrin, and autoimmune gastritis spares the antrum. Blocking vagal GRP would reduce gastrin rather than raise it, and omeprazole does not act on vagal fibers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin and gastrin; SDL 7, section 4).","boardPrep":"Step 1: low antral pH releases somatostatin, which inhibits gastrin release from G cells (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 5). COMLEX: check gastric pH when gastrin is high, since acid suppression raises gastrin while a gastrinoma keeps the pH low.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A gastrinoma secretes gastrin despite low pH, so","B":"Histamine from ECL cells acts on parietal cell","D":"Destruction of G cells would lower gastrin, and","E":"Blocking vagal GRP would reduce gastrin rather than"}},{"id":"trial_sdl05_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — List which hormones/peptides increase appetite and which decrease it, then explain the orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways. Table 8.3, Fig 8.7","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has early onset obesity and hyperphagia from a loss of function mutation in melanocortin-4 receptors. Besides increased appetite, which change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sympathetic tone","B":"Lower ghrelin secretion","C":"Higher leptin sensitivity","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Lower energy expenditure"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MC4R activation in the paraventricular nucleus decreases food intake and increases energy expenditure through projections to the NTS and increased sympathetic activity. Loss of MC4R function therefore lowers energy expenditure and sympathetic tone. Lower ghrelin would reduce appetite. MC4R loss does not increase leptin sensitivity, since leptin acts upstream of MC4R. Gastric emptying is not an MC4R effect in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 6, anorexigenic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin from the gastric fundus is orexigenic and stimulates NPY and AGRP neurons (lecture SDL 5). COMLEX: alpha-MSH acts at MC4R to promote satiety.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — obesity and metabolic syndrome as the drivers of MASLD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of MC4R function therefore lowers energy expenditure and sympathetic tone."}},{"id":"grid_sdl05_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain what somatostatin does to gastrin secretion, and what histamine does to parietal cell acid secretion.","batch":4,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has new diabetes and gallstones and no prior abdominal surgery. CT shows a mass in the pancreas, and she has lost weight. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Serum gastrin","Gastric acid","Fecal fat"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↓","↑"],"C":["↓","↓","↔"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"A somatostatinoma releases excess somatostatin from tumor delta cells, so the pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is correct. Somatostatin suppresses gastrin release from G cells and inhibits parietal cell acid secretion, causing hypochlorhydria, and it inhibits pancreatic secretion, causing steatorrhea; the diabetes and gallstones reflect inhibition of insulin release and gallbladder contraction. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↑ gastric acid, ↑ fecal fat) is a gastrinoma, which raises gastrin and acid, and the acid entering the duodenum inactivates pancreatic lipase and causes steatorrhea. The pattern (↓ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) follows resection of the gastric antrum, which removes the G cells so gastrin and acid fall. The pattern (↔ serum gastrin, ↔ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is a glucagonoma, which causes diabetes, weight loss, and necrolytic migratory erythema without changing gastric secretion. The pattern (↑ serum gastrin, ↓ gastric acid, ↔ fecal fat) is autoimmune atrophic gastritis, in which loss of parietal cells lowers acid and removes feedback on G cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 5, section 5, somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion; section 3, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Costanzo Ch. 8 (antral resection decreases gastrin and H+ secretion; acid inactivates pancreatic lipase in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome); Robbins (SDL 19 pathology text: somatostatinomas cause diabetes, cholelithiasis, steatorrhea, and hypochlorhydria); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors: gastrinoma steatorrhea, glucagonoma, and somatostatinoma); First Aid 2025 GI (somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin release). Rows C, D, and E list ↔ for analytes their sources do not describe as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin inhibits gastric acid, pancreatic secretion, gallbladder contraction, and insulin and glucagon release (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: octreotide, a somatostatin analog, can cause gallstones and steatorrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":6,"title":"SDL 6 — Physiology - GI Motility: Chewing, Swallowing, Esophageal and Gastric Motility","questions":[{"id":"sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which sensory receptors detect the bolus and initiate the involuntary swallowing reflex?","choices":{"A":"Gastric stretch receptors","B":"Carotid sinus baroreceptors","C":"Laryngeal chemoreceptors","D":"Esophageal pain receptors","E":"Tonsillar pillar receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tactile receptors around the pharyngeal opening, especially on the tonsillar pillars, detect the bolus pushed back by the tongue and start the automatic swallowing reflex. Gastric stretch receptors drive gastric reflexes. Carotid sinus baroreceptors sense blood pressure. Laryngeal chemoreceptors trigger cough, not swallowing. Esophageal pain receptors do not start swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, voluntary stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (oral phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the oral phase of swallowing is voluntary and the pharyngeal and esophageal phases are reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oropharyngeal dysphagia points to neural or striated muscle disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cranial nerves carry the afferent limb of the swallowing reflex from the pharynx to the medulla?","choices":{"A":"CN VII and CN XII","B":"CN X and CN XI","C":"CN III and CN IV","D":"CN V and CN IX","E":"CN VI and CN XII"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sensory impulses from pharyngeal tactile receptors travel in the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves to the tractus solitarius in the medullary swallowing center. The other nerve pairs are not the afferent limb in the lecture. The lecture gives the pharyngeal afferents as the trigeminal (CN V) and glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerves, and Costanzo gives the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves; neither names CN VII or CN XII. CN XI is not named by either source: the lecture gives CN V and CN IX as the pharyngeal afferents, and Costanzo gives the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves. Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN III and CN IV as the afferent limb of swallowing. Neither the lecture (CN V and IX) nor Costanzo (glossopharyngeal and vagus) names CN VI and CN XII as the afferent limb of swallowing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, involuntary pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center lies in the medulla (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: brainstem strokes impair swallowing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lecture gives the pharyngeal afferents.","B":"CN XI is not named by either source: the lecture gives CN V and CN IX as the pharyngeal afferents, and Costanzo gives the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves.","C":"CN III and CN IV are not the afferent limb of swallowing.","E":"CN VI and CN XII are not the afferent limb of swallowing."}},{"id":"sdl06_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":1,"stem":"During the pharyngeal stage of swallowing, which event prevents food from refluxing into the nasal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Epiglottis tilting back","B":"Soft palate elevation","C":"Vocal cord closure","D":"Upper sphincter relaxation","E":"Tongue base retraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The soft palate elevates to close the posterior nares and prevent reflux into the nasal cavity. The epiglottis swings back and the vocal cords approximate to protect the airway, not the nose. Relaxation of the upper esophageal sphincter opens the esophagus. Tongue movement pushes the bolus back in the voluntary stage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nasal regurgitation suggests palatal weakness.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 4 — striated and smooth muscle of the esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary esophageal peristalsis is initiated by which event?","choices":{"A":"Esophageal distension","B":"Refluxed gastric acid","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Duodenal fat delivery","E":"A voluntary swallow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary peristalsis begins with the voluntary act of swallowing as a continuation of the pharyngeal peristaltic wave, coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Esophageal distension and refluxed gastric contents initiate secondary peristalsis. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response to the bolus. Duodenal fat slows gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, primary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the LES opens and the orad stomach relaxes as the primary wave travels down (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: primary waves propel the swallowed bolus.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"The upper third of the esophagus is striated muscle. What controls the primary peristaltic wave in this region?","choices":{"A":"Glossopharyngeal and vagal motor impulses","B":"Slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal","C":"Myenteric reflexes triggered by distension","D":"Sympathetic fibers from the stellate ganglion","E":"Hypoglossal and facial nerve motor output"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Because the pharyngeal wall and upper third of the esophagus are striated muscle, the primary peristaltic wave there is driven by skeletal nerve impulses carried in the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves as part of the swallowing reflex. Slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal pace smooth muscle, not striated muscle. Myenteric reflexes triggered by distension are the basis of secondary peristalsis. Sympathetic fibers from the stellate ganglion do not drive esophageal peristalsis. Hypoglossal and facial nerve output moves the tongue and face during the oral stage, not the esophagus. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64, esophageal stage of swallowing; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary peristalsis continues the pharyngeal wave and is centrally driven through CN IX and X in the striated upper esophagus (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: brainstem strokes impair swallowing because the central swallowing program drives primary peristalsis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Slow waves from interstitial cells of Cajal","C":"Myenteric reflexes triggered by distension are the","D":"Sympathetic fibers from the stellate ganglion do","E":"Hypoglossal and facial nerve output moves the"}},{"id":"sdl06_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":1,"stem":"Secondary esophageal peristalsis can still occur after the vagal supply is interrupted. Which mechanism allows this?","choices":{"A":"Medullary swallowing center","B":"Intrinsic myenteric circuits","C":"Somatic motor reflex arcs","D":"Sympathetic chain ganglia input","E":"Voluntary cortical input"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Intrinsic myenteric networks can drive secondary peristaltic waves on their own after a brief period of adaptation, even when vagal supply is interrupted. The medullary swallowing center acts through the vagus and is cut off. Somatic motor reflexes do not drive smooth muscle peristalsis. Sympathetic input inhibits motility. Voluntary cortical input starts primary peristalsis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis); Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary wave is mediated by the enteric nervous system).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis clears residual food and refluxed contents (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired clearance prolongs acid exposure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"Interstitial cells of Cajal in the caudad stomach generate slow waves at about what rate?","choices":{"A":"12 per minute","B":"8 to 10 per minute","C":"1 per minute","D":"3 to 5 per minute","E":"20 to 30 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate slow waves of about 3 to 5 per minute in the caudad stomach. About 12 per minute is the duodenal rate in Costanzo. Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 per minute match no gastric source. Twelve per minute is the duodenal slow wave rate, the highest in the GI tract, not the rate in the caudad stomach. Eight to 10 per minute is near the ileal rate of about 9, faster than the 3 to 5 per minute of the caudad stomach. Twenty to 30 per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm); Costanzo Ch. 8 (slow wave frequency).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials; action potentials on top of them cause phasic contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastric pacing disorders slow emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Eight to 10, 1, and 20 to 30 per minute match no gastric source.","A":"Twelve per minute is the duodenal slow wave rate, the highest in the GI tract, not the rate in the caudad stomach.","B":"Eight to 10 per minute is near the ileal rate of about 9, faster than the 3 to 5 per minute of the caudad stomach.","E":"Twenty to 30 per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves."}},{"id":"sdl06_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the term retropulsion describe in gastric motility?","choices":{"A":"Antral contents jetting backward","B":"Chyme entering the duodenum","C":"Bile refluxing into the stomach","D":"Fundic receptive relaxation","E":"Duodenal acid inhibiting emptying"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As a peristaltic ring nears the closed pylorus, most antral contents are squirted backward through the ring toward the body, mixing them with acid. Chyme entering the duodenum is emptying. Bile reflux is retrograde duodenal flow. Receptive relaxation is fundic accommodation. Duodenal acid acts through enterogastric feedback. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach mixes and grinds, and the orad stomach receives the bolus (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: solids leave the stomach more slowly than liquids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Duodenal acid acts through enterogastric feedback.","B":"Chyme entering the duodenum is emptying.","C":"Bile reflux is retrograde duodenal flow.","D":"Receptive relaxation is fundic accommodation."}},{"id":"sdl06_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":1,"stem":"A larger meal stretches the stomach wall and promotes emptying through local myenteric reflexes. What do these reflexes do at the pylorus and antrum?","choices":{"A":"Close the pylorus and slow the antrum","B":"Release secretin from duodenal cells","C":"Boost pyloric pump, relax pylorus","D":"Raise intragastric storage pressure","E":"Start retropulsion back into the fundus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Stretch of the stomach wall elicits local myenteric reflexes that strongly accentuate the pyloric pump and at the same time inhibit the thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter, so more chyme passes. Closing the pylorus and slowing the antrum is what duodenal enterogastric reflexes do. Secretin release from duodenal cells is triggered by acid in the duodenum, not by gastric stretch. Raised intragastric storage pressure is not the cause, because normal increases in volume barely change gastric pressure. Retropulsion is the backward jetting of antral contents against a closed pylorus, which mixes rather than empties. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64, role of the pylorus and gastric factors that promote emptying.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric distension speeds emptying by boosting the pyloric pump and relaxing the pylorus, while duodenal signals slow it (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: pyloric resistance is the balance point that duodenal feedback adjusts after a meal.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 1 — muscle layers of the gut wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Closing the pylorus and slowing the antrum","B":"Secretin release from duodenal cells is triggered","D":"Raised intragastric storage pressure is not the","E":"Retropulsion is the backward jetting of antral"}},{"id":"sdl06_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Besides slowing antral propulsion, which effect does it produce?","choices":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion","B":"Faster fundic contraction","C":"Relaxed pyloric sphincter","D":"Increased gastric acid","E":"Increased pyloric tone"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CCK slows antral propulsion and increases pyloric sphincter tone, giving time for bile emulsification and pancreatic digestion of fat. Increased antral propulsion and faster fundic contraction would speed emptying. A relaxed pyloric sphincter would let chyme pass faster. CCK does not act by raising gastric acid. Guyton states that duodenal hormones inhibit the pyloric pump while increasing the strength of pyloric sphincter contraction, so they do not speed fundic contraction. Guyton states that duodenal hormones increase the strength of pyloric sphincter contraction, the opposite of relaxing it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (CCK inhibits gastric emptying).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: fatty meals stay in the stomach longer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased antral propulsion and faster fundic contraction would speed emptying.","B":"Guyton states that duodenal hormones inhibit the pyloric pump while increasing the strength of pyloric sphincter contraction, so they do not speed fundic contraction.","C":"Guyton states that duodenal hormones increase the strength of pyloric sphincter contraction, the opposite of relaxing it."}},{"id":"sdl06_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"Enterogastric reflexes can switch on within seconds of chyme entering the duodenum. To which duodenal contents are they especially sensitive?","choices":{"A":"Glucose in isotonic solution","B":"Neutral chyme of small volume","C":"Irritants and acid in the chyme","D":"Distension of the gastric antrum","E":"An empty duodenal lumen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Guyton states that enterogastric reflexes are especially sensitive to irritants and acid in duodenal chyme and can become strongly active very quickly, blocking further gastric emptying until the acid is neutralized. Glucose in isotonic solution is neither irritant nor acidic and is a weak trigger. Neutral chyme of small volume gives little stretch, acid, or irritation to the duodenal wall. Distension of the gastric antrum is a gastric signal that promotes emptying rather than an enterogastric trigger. An empty duodenal lumen provides no stimulus, so emptying proceeds. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64, enterogastric nervous reflexes; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal acid, irritants, distension, osmolality, and protein products trigger enterogastric reflexes that slow emptying (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slowing emptying gives pancreatic bicarbonate time to neutralize duodenal acid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose in isotonic solution is neither irritant","B":"Neutral chyme of small volume gives little","D":"Distension of the gastric antrum is a gastric","E":"An empty duodenal lumen provides no stimulus"}},{"id":"sdl06_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":1,"stem":"Acid that lowers duodenal pH below 4.5 releases a hormone that inhibits gastric motility and acid secretion. Which hormone and source is this?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin from EC cells","B":"GIP from K cells","C":"Gastrin from G cells","D":"Secretin from S cells","E":"Motilin from M cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Duodenal S cells release secretin in response to acid below pH 4.5; secretin inhibits gastrin release, gastric acid secretion, and gastric motility until bicarbonate neutralizes the chyme. Serotonin from enterochromaffin cells activates enteric sensory neurons rather than acting as this duodenal hormone. GIP is released by fat, amino acids, and carbohydrate. Gastrin stimulates acid. Motilin is released during fasting and drives interdigestive motility. GIP from duodenal, jejunal, and ileal K cells mainly stimulates insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells, so it is classed as an incretin rather than an acid-triggered brake. Guyton states that secretin is released from the small intestinal mucosa when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 to 5.0, so the hormone is secretin, not serotonin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP exerts a mild inhibitory effect on gastric motility and emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: duodenal acid slows gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastrin stimulates acid.","E":"Motilin is released during fasting and drives interdigestive motility.","B":"GIP from duodenal, jejunal, and ileal K cells mainly stimulates insulin secretion by pancreatic beta cells, so it is classed as an incretin rather than an acid-triggered brake.","A":"Guyton states that secretin is released from the small intestinal mucosa when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 to 5.0, so the hormone is secretin, not serotonin."}},{"id":"sdl06_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"When a swallowed bolus moves down the esophagus, the lower esophageal sphincter opens for the whole passage. What mediates this relaxation?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic release of norepinephrine","B":"Gastrin released from antral G cells","C":"Muscarinic excitation by acetylcholine","D":"Rising intra-abdominal pressure","E":"Vagal fibers releasing VIP and NO"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opening of the lower esophageal sphincter during a swallow is mediated by peptidergic vagal fibers that release VIP and nitric oxide, which relax its smooth muscle; once the bolus passes, the sphincter returns to its high resting tone. Sympathetic release of norepinephrine does not produce the swallow-linked relaxation. Gastrin from antral G cells raises sphincter tone rather than relaxing it. Muscarinic excitation by acetylcholine contracts smooth muscle and supports tone. Rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses the distal esophagus and helps keep the junction closed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, esophageal motility; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 6.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES relaxation is mediated by vagal VIP and NO neurons, and loss of these inhibitory neurons causes achalasia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reflux follows when the LES relaxes inappropriately or its resting tone is lost.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sympathetic release of norepinephrine does not produce","B":"Gastrin from antral G cells raises sphincter","C":"Muscarinic excitation by acetylcholine contracts smooth muscle","D":"Rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses the distal esophagus"}},{"id":"sdl06_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides extrinsic pinch valve reinforcement of the lower esophageal sphincter during coughing or straining?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal rectus muscles","B":"Diaphragmatic crura","C":"Upper esophageal sphincter","D":"Intercostal muscles","E":"Pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Skeletal muscle of the diaphragmatic crura surrounds the LES at the esophageal hiatus and reinforces it during inspiration, coughing, and straining. Rectus muscles raise abdominal pressure but do not encircle the LES. The upper esophageal sphincter keeps air out of the upper esophagus. Intercostal muscles move the chest wall. The pyloric sphincter controls gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, diaphragmatic crural reinforcement).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a sliding hiatal hernia moves the gastroesophageal junction into the mediastinum and raises GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased intra-abdominal pressure helps prevent reflux by compressing which structure?","choices":{"A":"Distal esophageal segment","B":"Thoracic esophageal body","C":"Wall of the gastric fundus","D":"Upper esophageal sphincter","E":"Pyloric sphincter muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A 1 to 2 cm segment of distal esophagus lies in the abdomen, so rising intra-abdominal pressure compresses it inward, creating a flap valve closure. The thoracic esophagus is exposed to intrathoracic pressure. Compression of the fundus would push contents upward. The upper esophageal sphincter and pylorus are not compressed by intra-abdominal pressure in this mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal esophageal segment).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the 1 to 2 cm abdominal esophagus is compressed by intra-abdominal pressure (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: chronically raised intra-abdominal pressure from obesity or pregnancy and hiatal hernia still promote GERD (lecture SDL 8; Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The upper esophageal sphincter and pylorus are not compressed by intra-abdominal pressure in this mechanism."}},{"id":"sdl06_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 73-year-old man has a stroke in the medulla, and liquids enter his trachea during swallowing. Failure of which pharyngeal stage event best explains the aspiration?","choices":{"A":"Soft palate elevation","B":"Lower sphincter relaxation","C":"Secondary esophageal waves","D":"Gastric receptive relaxation","E":"Airway closure by larynx"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the pharyngeal stage the vocal cords approximate, the larynx moves up and forward, and the epiglottis covers the laryngeal opening to prevent aspiration; this sequence is coordinated by the medullary swallowing center. Soft palate elevation closes the posterior nares and prevents nasal reflux. LES relaxation, secondary peristalsis, and receptive relaxation occur after the bolus leaves the pharynx. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center coordinates CN V, IX, X, and XII (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: aspiration risk is high after brainstem stroke.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — brainstem nuclei of vagal reflexes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"LES relaxation, secondary peristalsis, and receptive relaxation occur after the bolus leaves the pharynx.","A":"The soft palate is pulled upward to close the posterior nares, preventing nasal reflux.","C":"Secondary peristalsis occurs after the bolus leaves the pharynx.","D":"Receptive relaxation occurs after the bolus leaves the pharynx."}},{"id":"sdl06_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has anterior neck surgery that tethers the hyoid, and food sticks at the top of his esophagus. Which normal event opens the upper esophageal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"VIP release at the LES","B":"Laryngeal stretch","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Secondary peristalsis","E":"Epiglottic closure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upward laryngeal movement stretches and relaxes the upper esophageal sphincter, opening the entrance to the esophagus. VIP release relaxes the lower, not upper, sphincter. Secondary peristalsis begins in the esophagus after the bolus enters. Receptive relaxation occurs in the orad stomach. Epiglottic closure protects the airway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (upper esophageal sphincter relaxes in the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the upper esophageal sphincter is striated muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired laryngeal elevation causes oropharyngeal dysphagia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 4 — striated muscle of the upper esophagus","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has a videofluoroscopic swallow study, and his breathing pauses briefly with each swallow. Which mechanism causes the pause?","choices":{"A":"Voluntary cortical breath holding","B":"Reflex vocal cord paralysis","C":"Medullary respiratory inhibition","D":"Fatigue of the phrenic nerve","E":"Nasal block by the epiglottis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Medullary signals temporarily inhibit respiration throughout the pharyngeal stage, which lasts under 2 seconds. The pause is reflex, not voluntary. The vocal cords approximate actively rather than becoming paralyzed. Phrenic nerve fatigue would not cycle with each swallow. The soft palate, not the epiglottis, closes the nasal passage. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, pharyngeal stage); Costanzo Ch. 8 (breathing is inhibited during the pharyngeal phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pharyngeal phase is a reflex controlled in the medulla (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired coordination of breathing and swallowing raises aspiration risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old woman has nighttime reflux, and esophageal manometry records a peristaltic wave that begins in the midesophagus without a swallow. What started this wave?","choices":{"A":"A voluntary pharyngeal swallow","B":"Distension of the wall","C":"Gastric receptive relaxation","D":"Cortical motor command","E":"Duodenal acid reflex"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secondary peristalsis is started locally by distension of the esophageal wall from residual food or refluxed gastric contents and does not require a swallow. A voluntary swallow starts primary peristalsis in the pharynx. Receptive relaxation is a gastric response. A cortical command is part of the voluntary stage. Duodenal acid acts on gastric emptying. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary esophageal peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated by the enteric nervous system and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6; Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: nocturnal reflux is cleared by secondary waves.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — intrinsic enteric circuits","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man swallows a large bolus that the primary wave does not fully clear. A second wave clears it without another swallow. Where does this second wave begin?","choices":{"A":"In the upper pharyngeal wall","B":"At the lower sphincter","C":"In the gastric fundus","D":"At the site of distension","E":"At the cricopharyngeus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distension and travels downward until the lumen is cleared. Waves starting in the pharynx are primary waves. The lower sphincter relaxes rather than starting the wave. The gastric fundus is beyond the esophagus. The cricopharyngeus is part of the upper esophageal sphincter. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (secondary peristaltic contraction begins at the point of distention); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary peristalsis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: impaired secondary peristalsis worsens reflux esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — the peristaltic reflex","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Waves starting in the pharynx are primary waves.","B":"The lower sphincter relaxes rather than starting the wave.","C":"The gastric fundus is beyond the esophagus.","E":"Sits, not at the cricopharyngeus."}},{"id":"sdl06_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a 24-hour pH and manometry study. During one reflux episode, refluxed acid distends his midesophagus while he is not swallowing. Which pattern of esophageal motor activity follows?","choices":{"A":"A single wave followed by quiescence","B":"Simultaneous contraction of the whole body","C":"Retrograde waves toward the pharynx","D":"Tonic contraction of the esophageal body","E":"Repeated waves until the lumen clears"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distension from refluxed contents starts secondary peristalsis without a swallow, and secondary waves repeat until the esophageal lumen is fully cleared. A single wave followed by quiescence would leave residual refluxate when one wave fails to clear it. Simultaneous contraction of the whole body is not a clearing pattern. Peristaltic waves travel caudally toward the stomach, not toward the pharynx. A tonic contraction of the esophageal body would not propel the refluxate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 2, secondary waves repeat until the lumen is cleared, p33; section 5, secondary clearance of reflux, p35).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary peristalsis is mediated partly by myenteric circuits and partly by vagovagal reflexes (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: local distension during reflux triggers secondary waves that return fluid to the stomach (lecture SDL 6).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl06_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man has a gastric emptying study. Solid food leaves his stomach only after it has been broken into particles smaller than 1 mm. Which mechanism grinds the solids?","choices":{"A":"Fundic relaxation","B":"Duodenal segmentation","C":"Antral retropulsion","D":"Migrating complexes","E":"Pyloric relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Strong antral rings push contents against a nearly closed pylorus, so most chyme is squirted back toward the body, shearing solids into particles under 1 mm. Fundic relaxation stores the meal. Duodenal segmentation mixes chyme after it leaves the stomach. Migrating motor complexes clear the fasting stomach. Pyloric relaxation would let solids leave without grinding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, retropulsion and mechanical grinding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudad stomach has the thickest muscle and produces the strongest contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: impaired antral grinding delays solid emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — circular and longitudinal muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has a gastric emptying study. Liquid leaves her stomach quickly, while solid food stays until it is mixed into chyme. Which feature explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Tonic pyloric contraction","B":"Weak tone in the fundus","C":"Faster gastric slow waves","D":"Acidic duodenal contents","E":"Absent antral peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter lets fluids pass easily but restricts solids until they are mixed into fluid chyme. Weak fundic tone would affect storage, not particle size. Faster slow waves would increase contraction rate for both phases. Duodenal acid slows emptying of all contents. Absent antral peristalsis would slow both liquids and solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, pyloric resistance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric sphincter is circular smooth muscle (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: gastric emptying studies use radiolabeled solid meals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 5 — the pyloric circular muscle","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 32-year-old man has antral manometry after a meal. For about one fifth of the recording, powerful antral contractions generate 50 to 70 cm H2O of pressure. What is this pattern called?","choices":{"A":"Mixing waves","B":"Pyloric pump","C":"Mass movement","D":"Segmentation","E":"Receptive relaxation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"For about 20% of the time after a meal, gastric peristalsis turns into powerful contractions called the pyloric pump, generating 50 to 70 cm H2O and driving emptying. Mixing waves are weak constrictor waves that do not empty the stomach. Mass movements occur in the colon. Segmentation mixes small intestinal contents. Receptive relaxation lowers orad gastric pressure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3, basic electrical rhythm and propulsion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pyloric pump generates 50 to 70 cm H2O and drives emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: enterogastric reflexes inhibit the pyloric pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman feels full for hours after fatty meals but not after meals of fruit and bread. Which response in her duodenum most slows emptying after the fatty meal?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release by peptides","B":"GIP release by glucose","C":"CCK release by lipids","D":"Motilin release by fasting","E":"Ghrelin release by fasting"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fatty acids and monoglycerides release CCK from I cells, and CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying. Gastrin is released in the stomach and stimulates acid. GIP has only a mild inhibitory effect on emptying. Motilin and ghrelin rise during fasting. Motilin is released during fasting to initiate the migrating complexes that clear the stomach, so it does not slow emptying after a fatty meal. Ghrelin is secreted before a meal to increase appetite, and it is not the duodenal signal that slows emptying of fat. Guyton names CCK, released in response to fatty acids, as one of the most potent inhibitors of gastric emptying, whereas GIP has only a weak effect on motility. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, hormonal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and decreases gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI regulatory substances). COMLEX: low fat meals help patients with delayed emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — CCK","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin is released in the stomach and stimulates acid.","D":"Motilin is released during fasting to initiate the migrating complexes that clear the stomach, so it does not slow emptying after a fatty meal.","E":"Ghrelin is secreted before a meal to increase appetite, and it is not the duodenal signal that slows emptying of fat.","B":"Guyton names CCK, released in response to fatty acids, as one of the most potent inhibitors of gastric emptying, whereas GIP has only a weak effect on motility."}},{"id":"sdl06_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man drinks a hyperosmolar sports drink, and it empties from his stomach more slowly than the same volume of water. Which mechanism slows emptying of the drink?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal osmoreceptor reflex","B":"Gastric acid hypersecretion","C":"Pyloric sphincter relaxation","D":"Faster antral slow waves","E":"Vagal receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Duodenal receptors monitor osmolality, and hypertonic chyme triggers enterogastric reflexes that inhibit the pyloric pump and increase pyloric tone. Gastric acid secretion does not depend on drink osmolality. Pyloric relaxation and faster antral waves would speed emptying. Receptive relaxation accommodates both drinks equally. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, duodenal stimuli and enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal distension, acid, osmolality, and fat and protein products slow gastric emptying (lecture SDL 6). COMLEX: isotonic fluids empty faster than hypertonic ones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — reflex control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pyloric relaxation and faster antral waves would speed emptying.","B":"Gastric acid secretion does not depend on drink osmolality.","D":"Faster antral waves would speed emptying.","E":"Receptive relaxation accommodates both drinks equally."}},{"id":"sdl06_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":2,"stem":"A 77-year-old man has a vagotomy, and duodenal distension still inhibits his antral contractions within seconds. Which routes of the enterogastric reflex remain?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent pathways","B":"Circulating gastrin release","C":"Pelvic splanchnic reflexes","D":"Somatic spinal reflexes","E":"Myenteric and sympathetic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterogastric reflexes act through intrinsic myenteric circuits, sympathetic nerves via prevertebral ganglia, and the vagus; after vagotomy the myenteric and sympathetic routes remain. Vagal efferents are cut. Gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric activity. Pelvic splanchnic fibers supply the distal colon. Somatic reflexes do not control gastric smooth muscle. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic activity decreases peristalsis and increases sphincter tone (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, general principles). COMLEX: vagotomy also removes receptive relaxation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic effects on motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastrin stimulates rather than inhibits gastric activity.","A":"Vagal efferent pathways are the route a vagotomy removes.","C":"Pelvic splanchnic fibers supply the distal colon.","D":"Somatic reflexes do not control gastric smooth muscle."}},{"id":"sdl06_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a sliding hiatal hernia has daily heartburn. Her gastroesophageal junction lies above the diaphragm. Which antireflux mechanism has she lost?","choices":{"A":"Abdominal segment compression","B":"Esophageal cardiac gland mucus","C":"Secondary peristaltic clearance","D":"Upper sphincter tone","E":"Gastric receptive relaxation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"When the gastroesophageal junction moves into the mediastinum, the distal esophagus no longer lies in the abdomen, so intra-abdominal pressure no longer compresses it into a flap valve, and crural reinforcement is lost. Cardiac gland mucus and secondary peristalsis are separate clearance and buffering defenses. Upper sphincter tone keeps air out of the esophagus. Receptive relaxation is gastric accommodation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, intra-abdominal segment and crural reinforcement); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (sliding hiatal hernia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sliding hiatal hernia is the most common type and increases GERD risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, sliding hiatal hernia). COMLEX: obesity and pregnancy are risk factors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — sliding hiatal hernia in GERD pathogenesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has heartburn and regurgitation. Manometry shows normal resting sphincter pressure, and pH monitoring shows acid exposure episodes that coincide with periods of complete sphincter relaxation without a preceding swallow. Which mechanism explains his reflux?","choices":{"A":"Excess LES resting tone","B":"Absent gastric acid","C":"Strong crural contraction","D":"Transient LES relaxations","E":"Rapid gastric emptying"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Abnormal transient relaxations of the LES permit gastric reflux even when resting tone is normal. Excess resting tone would oppose reflux and suggests achalasia. Absent acid would reduce injury. Strong crural contraction reinforces the barrier. Rapid gastric emptying would lower the volume available to reflux. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (GERD is inappropriate relaxation of the LES).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD presents with burning retrosternal pain, regurgitation, and cough (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are first line.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl06_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old woman in a manometry study has a zone at the gastroesophageal junction with pressure higher than in both the esophagus and the stomach. Which property produces this zone?","choices":{"A":"Cholinergic mucosal secretion","B":"Tonic circular contraction","C":"Longitudinal muscle shortening","D":"Submucosal venous engorgement","E":"Constant VIP release"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The LES is circular smooth muscle at the distal esophagus that stays tonically contracted at rest, creating a pressure barrier above both intrathoracic esophageal and gastric pressure. Mucosal secretion does not generate sphincter pressure. Longitudinal shortening does not create a sphincter zone. Venous engorgement is not the mechanism. VIP relaxes the LES. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone); Costanzo Ch. 8 (LES resting pressure exceeds esophageal and gastric pressure).","boardPrep":"Step 1: LES resting pressure exceeds intrathoracic and gastric pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: low LES pressure predisposes to GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the neural and muscular mechanisms that coordinate the three stages of swallowing.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has a small medullary stroke. His tongue deviates to the left on protrusion, and the left side of his tongue is wasted. At meals, chewed food collects in his mouth and he cannot push it back toward his pharynx, but once food reaches the pharynx he swallows it without coughing. Which stage of swallowing is impaired?","choices":{"A":"The voluntary oral stage","B":"Secondary esophageal peristalsis","C":"Primary esophageal peristalsis","D":"Lower sphincter relaxation","E":"Involuntary pharyngeal stage"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The tongue deviates toward a hypoglossal lesion, and in the voluntary stage the tongue rolls the bolus up and back against the hard palate into the pharynx, while the involuntary reflex works once the bolus touches pharyngeal receptors. Secondary esophageal peristalsis is triggered by distension from residue in the esophagus, not by food left in the mouth. Primary esophageal peristalsis begins after the bolus passes the upper sphincter, which happens normally once food reaches the pharynx. Lower sphincter relaxation occurs ahead of the esophageal wave and has no role in moving food out of the mouth. Involuntary pharyngeal stage function is intact, since food that reaches the pharynx is swallowed without coughing. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 1, three stages of swallowing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the swallowing center coordinates output through cranial nerves V, IX, X and XII, and the tongue drives the voluntary stage (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 6). COMLEX: a deviated, wasted tongue points to a hypoglossal lesion on that side.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — medullary nuclei","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Secondary esophageal peristalsis is triggered by distension from","C":"Primary esophageal peristalsis begins after the bolus passes","D":"Lower sphincter relaxation occurs ahead of the esophageal","E":"Involuntary pharyngeal stage function is intact, since food"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate between primary and secondary esophageal peristalsis, including their initiation and functional roles.","batch":3,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Esophageal manometry shows that each swallow triggers a normal-amplitude peristaltic wave that reaches the distal esophagus, but lower esophageal sphincter pressure stays near 35 mm Hg throughout the swallow instead of falling. Which normal component of primary peristalsis is missing?","choices":{"A":"Pharyngeal initiation of the peristaltic wave","B":"Distension triggered secondary waves","C":"Closure of the upper sphincter after entry","D":"Relaxation of the sphincter ahead of the wave","E":"Gravity assisted transit while upright"},"correct":"D","explanation":"As the primary wave travels down, peptidergic inhibitory myenteric neurons relax the lower esophageal sphincter and orad stomach ahead of the bolus, and here the wave is normal but that relaxation is lost, the defect produced by loss of nitric oxide neurons. The wave starts normally with each swallow, so pharyngeal initiation is intact. Secondary waves arise from distension to clear residue and are not what opens the sphincter during a swallow. The upper sphincter closes after the bolus enters to prevent pharyngeal reflux and does not affect the lower sphincter. Gravity shortens transit to 5 to 8 seconds upright but cannot open a sphincter that fails to relax. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, sections 1 and 2; SDL 8, section 1, achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary peristalsis follows a swallow and relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter ahead of the bolus through VIP and NO neurons (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 6). COMLEX: a sphincter that does not relax with swallowing is the core manometric sign of achalasia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — vagal transmitters","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The wave starts normally with each swallow, so","B":"Secondary waves arise from distension to clear residue","C":"The upper sphincter closes after the bolus enters","E":"Gravity shortens transit to 5 to 8 seconds"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a pyloroplasty, which widens the pyloric channel, during surgery for a scarred pyloric ulcer. Which change in his gastric emptying is most likely afterward?","choices":{"A":"Liquids leave the stomach more slowly","B":"Retropulsion grinds food into finer bits","C":"Larger solid particles reach the duodenum","D":"Duodenal fat stops slowing gastric emptying","E":"Antral slow waves become more frequent"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thick, tonically contracted pyloric sphincter lets fluid pass but holds back solids until retropulsion grinds them to particles under 1 mm, so widening the channel removes much of this resistance and larger particles escape into the duodenum. Liquids already pass the pylorus with ease, and a wider channel would not slow them. Retropulsion depends on a restricted outlet, so a wider channel weakens it rather than improving grinding. Duodenal fat still releases CCK and triggers enterogastric reflexes after the operation. Slow waves of 3 to 5 per minute are set by interstitial cells of Cajal and are not changed by widening the pylorus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, sections 3 and 4, gastric mixing and duodenal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyloric resistance and retropulsion grind solids to particles smaller than 1 mm before they leave the stomach (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 6). COMLEX: operations on the pylorus change how fast and in what form food reaches the duodenum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — regions of the stomach","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Liquids already pass the pylorus with ease, and","B":"Retropulsion depends on a restricted outlet, so a","D":"Duodenal fat still releases CCK and triggers enterogastric","E":"Slow waves of 3 to 5 per minute"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the roles of enterogastric reflexes, CCK, secretin, and GIP, and how duodenal stimuli (e.g., fat, acid, osmolality) modulate pyloric function.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has early satiety, nausea, and fullness for hours after meals, and scintigraphy confirms delayed gastric emptying. Her dietitian uses the duodenal signals that slow emptying to plan her meals. Which meal would her stomach empty most readily?","choices":{"A":"A large high fat milkshake","B":"An acidic juice taken with fried food","C":"A concentrated sugary fruit syrup","D":"A small low fat isotonic drink","E":"A large portion of fried meat and cheese"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Duodenal receptors slow emptying in response to distension, acid, hypertonic or hypotonic chyme, fat and protein products, and irritation, acting through enterogastric reflexes and hormones such as CCK and secretin, so a small, low fat, isotonic liquid triggers the least feedback. Fat releases CCK, the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying, and a large volume adds duodenal distension. Acid releases secretin and fat releases CCK, and both slow gastric emptying. Hypertonic chyme stimulates duodenal osmoreceptor reflexes that slow emptying. Large volume, fat and protein products all trigger enterogastric reflexes and CCK release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 4, enterogastric reflexes and hormonal feedback).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK is the most potent hormonal inhibitor of gastric emptying, and duodenal acid, osmolality and distension also slow it (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 6). COMLEX: small, low fat meals are a practical step for patients with delayed gastric emptying.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — duodenal hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fat releases CCK, the most potent hormonal inhibitor","B":"Acid releases secretin and fat releases CCK, and","C":"Hypertonic chyme stimulates duodenal osmoreceptor reflexes that slow","E":"Large volume, fat and protein products all trigger"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has heartburn and regurgitation most nights when she lies down after dinner. A barium study shows the gastroesophageal junction in normal position below the diaphragm. Manometry shows normal peristalsis but a resting lower esophageal sphincter pressure of 6 mm Hg (normal: about 30 mm Hg). Which failure of the antireflux barrier best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Excess neutral mucus from cardiac glands","B":"Weak resting tone of the sphincter","C":"Absent secondary peristaltic clearance","D":"Loss of the diaphragmatic crural pinch","E":"Upward shift of the abdominal segment"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The lower esophageal sphincter normally holds a positive pressure near 30 mm Hg through tonic contraction of its circular smooth muscle, enough to overcome negative intrathoracic pressure, so at 6 mm Hg the barrier is too weak and lying flat lets gastric contents flow back. Neutral mucus from cardiac glands buffers residual acid and would protect rather than cause reflux. Her peristalsis is normal, so clearance of refluxed acid is preserved. Crural reinforcement is lost when the junction slides above the hiatus, but her junction sits in normal position. The abdominal segment is still below the diaphragm, so intra-abdominal pressure can still compress it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, mechanisms preventing reflux).","boardPrep":"Step 1: reflux is prevented by LES tone near 30 mm Hg, crural pinch, intra-abdominal compression of the distal segment and secondary clearance (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 6). COMLEX: test results that exclude a hiatal hernia and dysmotility point to a weak sphincter as the cause of reflux.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 2 — reflux esophagitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutral mucus from cardiac glands buffers residual acid","C":"Her peristalsis is normal, so clearance of refluxed","D":"Crural reinforcement is lost when the junction slides","E":"The abdominal segment is still below the diaphragm,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl06_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the process of gastric mixing and propulsion, including the roles of peristaltic waves, retropulsion, and pyloric resistance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes and delayed gastric emptying starts a drug that blocks D2 receptors and increases acetylcholine release from enteric neurons. Antral manometry shows stronger contractions after the drug, but even at the highest dose they never exceed about 3 per minute. What sets this ceiling?","choices":{"A":"Tone of the fundic smooth muscle","B":"Level of duodenal CCK feedback","C":"Refractory period of vagal motor fibers","D":"Interstitial cell slow wave frequency","E":"Resistance of the pyloric sphincter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Interstitial cells of Cajal generate gastric slow waves at about 3 per minute, and action potentials and contractions occur only when a slow wave brings the membrane to threshold, so a drug can strengthen each contraction but cannot make them more frequent than the slow waves. Fundic tone and receptive relaxation govern storage and do not generate the antral rhythm. Duodenal CCK slows antral propulsion and raises pyloric tone, which slows emptying rather than setting the maximum rate. Vagal input changes the force of contraction through enteric neurons but does not set the rhythm. Pyloric resistance governs how much chyme leaves with each wave, not how often waves occur. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (slow waves set the frequency of contractions); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 3; SDL 10, section 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency, set by interstitial cells of Cajal, caps the rate of gastric contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: prokinetics such as metoclopramide strengthen contractions but carry extrapyramidal risks.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric pacemaking","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fundic tone and receptive relaxation govern storage and","B":"Duodenal CCK slows antral propulsion and raises pyloric","C":"Vagal input changes the force of contraction through","E":"Pyloric resistance governs how much chyme leaves with"}},{"id":"grid_sdl06_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the mechanisms preventing gastroesophageal reflux, including the role of the lower esophageal sphincter and intra-abdominal pressure.","batch":4,"stem":"A 73-year-old woman has Raynaud phenomenon, tight thickened skin over her fingers, heartburn, and dysphagia to both solids and liquids. Esophageal manometry is performed. Which of the following sets of manometry findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["LES resting pressure","LES relaxation","Distal contractions"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↓"],"B":["↔","↔","↑"],"C":["↓","↔","↓"],"D":["↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis causes smooth muscle atrophy and fibrosis of the distal esophagus, so the pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is correct. The weak sphincter loses the positive pressure barrier that normally prevents reflux, and the weak distal smooth muscle clears refluxed acid poorly, producing heartburn and dysphagia. The pattern (↑ LES resting pressure, ↓ LES relaxation, ↓ distal contractions) is achalasia, from loss of inhibitory myenteric neurons. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↑ distal contractions) is distal esophageal spasm, with uncoordinated high amplitude contractions. The pattern (↓ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is reflux from LES incompetence with intact peristalsis. The pattern (↔ LES resting pressure, ↔ LES relaxation, ↔ distal contractions) is cardiovascular dysphagia from external compression by an enlarged left atrium, which has normal manometry. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 6, section 5, LES resting tone and LES incompetence); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal disorders of motility: achalasia, distal esophageal spasm, cardiovascular dysphagia, scleroderma esophageal dysmotility; GERD); First Aid 2025 GI (achalasia). Cells shown as ↔ are values the sources do not describe as changed; LES resting pressure in row D is inferred from the lecture term incompetence.","boardPrep":"Step 1: scleroderma esophageal dysmotility shows ↓ LES resting pressure and ↓ esophageal contractions, and achalasia shows ↑ LES resting pressure with ↓ contractions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal disorders of motility). COMLEX: scleroderma is a risk factor for GERD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 1 — esophageal motility disorders","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":7,"title":"SDL 7 — Physiology - Salivary and Gastric Secretion","questions":[{"id":"sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the tonicity of the initial saliva secreted by acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"Hypotonic to plasma","B":"Hypertonic to plasma","C":"Varies with the diet","D":"Twice plasma osmolarity","E":"Isotonic to plasma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete an initial saliva that is isotonic, with Na+, K+, Cl−, and HCO3− concentrations similar to plasma. Saliva becomes hypotonic later, in the ducts. It does not become hypertonic or reach twice plasma osmolarity, and diet does not set its tonicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, acinar cell secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous salivary secretions contain alpha amylase and lipase (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands). COMLEX: the parotid gland is primarily serous.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which property of salivary ductal cells allows the final saliva to become hypotonic?","choices":{"A":"Active water secretion","B":"Potassium reabsorption","C":"Rapid acinar flow","D":"Low water permeability","E":"Bicarbonate absorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ductal cells absorb more NaCl than they secrete KHCO3, and because they are relatively impermeable to water, water does not follow the absorbed solute, so the saliva becomes hypotonic. Ducts do not secrete water. They secrete rather than reabsorb potassium and bicarbonate. Rapid flow makes saliva closer to isotonic. Costanzo states salivary ductal cells secrete K+ into saliva rather than reabsorbing it, so K+ ends up higher than in plasma. Costanzo states salivary ductal cells secrete HCO3- rather than absorbing it, and it is ductal water impermeability that makes saliva hypotonic. Costanzo notes that at the highest flow rates the final saliva most closely resembles plasma, so rapid flow makes it less modified rather than hypotonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification); Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: final saliva is hypotonic with lower Na+ and Cl− and higher K+ and HCO3− than plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular gland makes most of the saliva in the mouth (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ducts do not secrete water.","B":"Salivary ductal cells secrete potassium into saliva rather than reabsorbing it.","E":"Salivary ductal cells secrete bicarbonate rather than absorbing it.","C":"Costanzo notes that at the highest flow rates the final saliva most closely resembles plasma, so rapid flow makes it less modified rather than hypotonic."}},{"id":"sdl07_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":1,"stem":"What does contraction of salivary myoepithelial cells accomplish?","choices":{"A":"Secretes salivary amylase","B":"Expels stored saliva","C":"Reabsorbs ductal sodium","D":"Dilates gland arterioles","E":"Lowers saliva tonicity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Myoepithelial (basket) cells lie between the basal membranes of acinar and ductal cells and the basal lamina; on neural stimulation they contract, compressing the acini and shortening the ducts to eject saliva. Acinar cells secrete amylase. Ductal cells reabsorb sodium and lower tonicity. Bradykinin from kallikrein dilates the arterioles. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, myoepithelial cell function); Costanzo Ch. 8 (structure of the salivary glands).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasympathetic stimulation contracts myoepithelial cells (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: the sublingual gland is primarily mucous (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which second messenger pathway mediates parasympathetic stimulation of salivary acinar cells?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and Ca2+","B":"Cyclic AMP","C":"Cyclic GMP","D":"Nitric oxide","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Postganglionic parasympathetic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors, which act through IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ to produce a large volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva. Cyclic AMP is the sympathetic beta receptor messenger. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and tyrosine kinase are not the salivary parasympathetic pathway. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parasympathetic input to the salivary glands travels in CN VII and CN IX (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: anticholinergic drugs cause dry mouth.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands synapse?","choices":{"A":"Submandibular ganglion","B":"Pterygopalatine ganglion","C":"Inferior cervical ganglion","D":"Geniculate ganglion","E":"Superior cervical ganglion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sympathetic preganglionic fibers arise from thoracic segments T1 to T3 and synapse in the superior cervical ganglion; postganglionic fibers release norepinephrine onto the glands. The submandibular ganglion is a parasympathetic relay for the submandibular gland. The pterygopalatine, inferior cervical, and geniculate ganglia are not the salivary sympathetic relay in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: both parasympathetic and sympathetic stimulation increase saliva, and parasympathetic control is dominant (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is regulated by autonomic nerves rather than GI hormones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which factor decreases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands?","choices":{"A":"Oral tactile input","B":"Dehydration","C":"Sour taste","D":"Smell of food","E":"Conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands is decreased by fear, sleep, and dehydration. It is increased by food, taste (especially sour stimuli), smell, tactile oral stimuli, nausea, and conditioned reflexes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (factors that modulate parasympathetic input).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary stasis from dehydration is associated with salivary duct stones (First Aid 2025 GI, sialolithiasis). COMLEX: Pavlov conditioned reflexes increase salivation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia and transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme in the parietal cell cytoplasm generates the H+ that is secreted into the gastric lumen?","choices":{"A":"Adenylyl cyclase","B":"Phospholipase C","C":"Protein kinase A","D":"Carbonic anhydrase","E":"Histidine decarboxylase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase combines CO2 and H2O into H2CO3, which dissociates into H+ for secretion and HCO3− for absorption into the blood. Adenylyl cyclase, phospholipase C, and protein kinase A are signaling enzymes that regulate the pump. Histidine decarboxylase makes histamine. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, intracellular generation of H+ and HCO3−); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parietal cells secrete gastric acid and intrinsic factor (First Aid 2025 GI, gastrointestinal secretory products). COMLEX: gastric pH is normally between 1 and 2 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Through which apical pathway does chloride enter the gastric lumen during HCl secretion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","B":"Sodium chloride cotransporter","C":"Chloride ATPase pump","D":"Potassium chloride symport","E":"Passive chloride channels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cl− diffuses passively into the canalicular lumen through apical Cl− channels and combines with secreted H+ to form HCl. The chloride bicarbonate exchanger sits on the basolateral membrane and brings Cl− into the cell. Sodium chloride and potassium chloride cotransport and a chloride ATPase are not part of the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell apical membrane contains the H+/K+ ATPase and Cl− channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vomiting gastric acid causes hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (First Aid 2025 GI).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the bicarbonate generated in parietal cells go during acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Into the gastric lumen","B":"Into the canaliculus","C":"Into gastric venous blood","D":"Into parietal cell vesicles","E":"Into the mucus gel layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HCO3− made by carbonic anhydrase leaves across the basolateral membrane into the blood, producing the alkaline tide. It is not secreted into the lumen or canaliculus, where H+ goes. It is not stored in vesicles. Bicarbonate trapped in surface mucus comes from surface epithelial cells, not parietal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport and the alkaline tide); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 3).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the alkaline tide raises blood pH after gastric acid secretion, such as after meals or vomiting (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: absorbed HCO3− is later secreted back into the gut in pancreatic juice (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"What explains potentiation among the gastric acid secretagogues?","choices":{"A":"Different second messengers","B":"Shared receptor binding","C":"Common G protein coupling","D":"Competitive receptor antagonism","E":"Increased gastrin clearance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Histamine acts through cAMP while ACh and gastrin act through IP3/Ca2+, and ACh and gastrin also release histamine from ECL cells, so combined stimuli give more acid than the sum of their separate effects. Each agent has its own receptor, so they do not share binding or one G protein. Antagonism and gastrin clearance reduce rather than amplify the response. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, potentiation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase in parietal cells supplies the H+ that the apical pump secretes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: combined secretagogues produce more acid than expected.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"Gastrin from antral G cells binds its receptors on which two cell types in the oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Chief and ECL cells","B":"Parietal and D cells","C":"Chief and mucous cells","D":"Parietal and ECL cells","E":"Mucous and D cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin binds its receptors on both parietal cells and ECL cells, so it can stimulate parietal cells directly and also act through ECL cells. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and are not the gastrin targets named in the lecture. D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits acid. Mucous cells secrete mucus. Chief cells have one secretory product, pepsinogen, which the low gastric pH converts to active pepsin to begin protein digestion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastrin acts on parietal cells and ECL cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastrin is released from G cells of the gastric antrum (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: PPIs are used to treat Zollinger-Ellison syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, proton pump inhibitors).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"D cells release somatostatin, which inhibits acid.","C":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen and are not the gastrin targets named in the lecture.","A":"Chief cells have one secretory product, pepsinogen, which the low gastric pH converts to active pepsin to begin protein digestion."}},{"id":"sdl07_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptor mechanism does somatostatin use directly on parietal cells to reduce acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Gq protein and IP3","B":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","C":"Inhibitory G protein","D":"Ligand gated channel","E":"Nuclear receptor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Somatostatin binds Gi coupled receptors on parietal cells, inhibiting adenylyl cyclase and lowering cAMP, which antagonizes histamine. Gq and IP3 are the ACh and gastrin pathway. Tyrosine kinase receptors, ligand gated channels, and nuclear receptors are not the somatostatin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways); Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D cells release somatostatin, which acts through a Gi cascade to reduce H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: lower cAMP reduces protein kinase A activity in the parietal cell (lecture SDL 7).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 5 — somatostatin and histamine actions on acid secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"In the gastric phase of acid secretion, which luminal products act directly on antral G cells to release gastrin?","choices":{"A":"Amino acids and small peptides","B":"Emulsified fats and fatty acids","C":"Glucose and other simple sugars","D":"Bile salts refluxed from the duodenum","E":"Hydrogen ions from parietal cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the gastric phase, distension and protein breakdown products drive secretion, and amino acids and small peptides act directly on antral G cells to release gastrin. Emulsified fats and fatty acids act in the duodenum, where they release CCK and inhibit acid. Glucose and other simple sugars are not G cell stimuli. Bile salts refluxed from the duodenum injure the mucosa but do not drive physiologic gastrin release. Hydrogen ions from parietal cells inhibit gastrin release through somatostatin, the main negative feedback. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, phases of HCl secretion; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 7.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase is driven by distension and by amino acids and small peptides acting on G cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: a protein meal is a strong stimulus of gastrin and acid.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Emulsified fats and fatty acids act in","C":"Glucose and other simple sugars are not","D":"Bile salts refluxed from the duodenum injure","E":"Hydrogen ions from parietal cells inhibit gastrin"}},{"id":"sdl07_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric mucosal defense rapidly seals small erosions?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin secretion","B":"Mucosal fibrosis","C":"Cell restitution","D":"Parietal hyperplasia","E":"Gastrin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid epithelial cell restitution seals small erosions, alongside mucus and trapped HCO3−, tight junctions, and mucosal blood flow. Pepsin is a damaging factor. Fibrosis is scarring rather than a rapid defense. Parietal cell hyperplasia and gastrin increase acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and give physical protection from acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: peptic ulcers reflect an imbalance of protective and damaging factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Parietal cell hyperplasia and gastrin increase acid."}},{"id":"sdl07_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which H. pylori enzyme lets the organism survive in gastric acid?","choices":{"A":"Catalase","B":"Urease","C":"Lipase","D":"Protease","E":"Mucinase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"H. pylori urease converts urea into ammonia and CO2, forming an alkaline ammonia cloud that protects it from luminal acid. Catalase, lipase, protease, and mucinase are not the lecture mechanism for acid survival. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (urease hydrolyzes urea to form ammonia, which neutralizes gastric acid).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the urea breath test detects H. pylori urease activity (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: H. pylori is treated with triple or quadruple therapy that includes a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 81-year-old woman is dehydrated, and her salivary flow falls below 1 mL/min. Compared with her saliva at a high flow rate, which change in her final saliva is expected?","choices":{"A":"Higher sodium concentration","B":"Higher chloride concentration","C":"Higher bicarbonate concentration","D":"Higher potassium concentration","E":"Isotonic osmolarity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low flow rates, saliva spends more time in contact with ductal cells, so more Na+ and Cl− are reabsorbed and more K+ is secreted; final saliva is most hypotonic with the highest K+. Sodium and chloride are lowest at low flow. Bicarbonate is lowest at low flow because its secretion is selectively stimulated during active salivation. Isotonic saliva is seen at high flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (effect of flow rate on composition of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at the highest flow rates saliva most closely resembles plasma (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: dehydration reduces parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy chews sour candy, and his salivary flow rises to about 4 mL/min. His salivary bicarbonate is higher than at rest even though the saliva spends less time in the ducts. Which mechanism explains this?","choices":{"A":"Ductal bicarbonate reabsorption","B":"Higher plasma bicarbonate","C":"Water reabsorption by ducts","D":"Slower acinar fluid secretion","E":"Stimulated ductal secretion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCO3− is the exception to the contact time rule: its ductal secretion is selectively stimulated when saliva production is stimulated, so its concentration rises with flow rate. Ducts secrete rather than reabsorb bicarbonate. Plasma bicarbonate does not change with a sour stimulus. Ductal cells are relatively impermeable to water. Acinar secretion speeds up, not slows, during stimulation. Costanzo explains that ductal cells secrete HCO3- and that this secretion is selectively stimulated as flow rises, so it is not reabsorbed. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on electrolyte composition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (HCO3− is not described by the contact time explanation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sour taste increases parasympathetic drive to the salivary glands (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary bicarbonate buffers ingested food.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ductal cells are relatively impermeable to water.","D":"Acinar secretion speeds up, not slows, during stimulation.","A":"Costanzo explains that ductal cells secrete HCO3- and that this secretion is selectively stimulated as flow rises, so it is not reabsorbed."}},{"id":"sdl07_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman in a physiology study has saliva collected at a low flow rate, and its Na+ concentration is far below that of plasma. Through which luminal membrane transporter do her ductal cells take up this Na+?","choices":{"A":"Sodium potassium pumps","B":"Sodium glucose carriers","C":"Epithelial sodium channels","D":"Sodium hydrogen exchangers","E":"Sodium phosphate carriers"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The luminal membrane of salivary ductal cells contains epithelial Na+ channels (ENaC), CFTR Cl− channels, Cl−/HCO3− exchange, and H+/K+ exchange; Na+ enters through ENaC and is pumped out across the basolateral membrane. The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane, not the luminal one. Sodium glucose, sodium hydrogen, and sodium phosphate carriers are not the ductal luminal transporters listed. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (formation of saliva, step 2: ductal luminal and basolateral transporters); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, ductal cell modification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CFTR is the luminal Cl− channel of salivary ductal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: saliva is not a simple ultrafiltrate of plasma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The Na+/K+ ATPase sits on the basolateral membrane, not the luminal one."}},{"id":"sdl07_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 75-year-old man starts an anticholinergic drug for bladder spasm and now has a dry mouth during meals. Blockade of which receptors on his salivary cells best explains the loss of watery saliva?","choices":{"A":"Beta adrenergic receptors","B":"Alpha adrenergic receptors","C":"Histamine H2 receptors","D":"Muscarinic M3 receptors","E":"Dopamine D2 receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Parasympathetic postganglionic neurons release ACh onto muscarinic M3 receptors on acinar and ductal cells, and the IP3/Ca2+ cascade produces a high volume of watery, enzyme rich saliva; blocking M3 removes this dominant drive. Beta and alpha adrenergic receptors mediate the smaller sympathetic response. H2 and D2 receptors are not the salivary parasympathetic receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (parasympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: atropine blocks muscarinic receptors (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: parasympathetic control of saliva is dominant.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man gives a stressful speech and notices that his saliva becomes thick and scant. Activation of which pathway in his salivary cells produces this protein rich secretion?","choices":{"A":"M3 receptors raising IP3","B":"Nicotinic receptors on acini","C":"H2 receptors raising cAMP","D":"Gi receptors lowering cAMP","E":"Beta receptors raising cAMP"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers from the superior cervical ganglion release norepinephrine onto beta adrenergic receptors, which activate adenylyl cyclase and raise cAMP, producing a smaller volume of viscous, protein and mucin rich saliva. M3 and IP3 mediate the watery parasympathetic response. Nicotinic receptors are on ganglion cells, not acini. H2 receptors are on parietal cells. A Gi pathway would lower cAMP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (sympathetic innervation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from T1 to T3 (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: sympathetic alpha receptors are also present on acinar cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man in a study has parasympathetic stimulation of his submandibular gland, and gland blood flow rises sharply along with secretion. Which acinar product mediates this vasodilation?","choices":{"A":"Alpha amylase","B":"Lingual lipase","C":"Kallikrein","D":"Mucin glycoprotein","E":"Secretory IgA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During high salivary activity, acinar cells secrete kallikrein, which cleaves high molecular weight kininogen into bradykinin, a potent local vasodilator that accounts for the high salivary blood flow. Alpha amylase and lingual lipase begin carbohydrate and lipid digestion. Mucin lubricates food. Secretory IgA is an immune product. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, parasympathetic pathway); Costanzo Ch. 8 (organic constituents of saliva).","boardPrep":"Step 1: maximal salivary blood flow is more than 10 times that of exercising muscle when corrected for size (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the submandibular duct is Wharton duct (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, salivary glands).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl07_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has parietal cells studied during acid secretion. As each HCO3− ion leaves across the basolateral membrane, which ion enters the cell in exchange?","choices":{"A":"Sodium","B":"Chloride","C":"Potassium","D":"Hydrogen","E":"Phosphate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The basolateral membrane of the parietal cell carries a Cl−/HCO3− exchanger, so HCO3− leaves for the blood as Cl− enters; that Cl− then diffuses into the lumen through apical Cl− channels to accompany the secreted H+. Na+ and K+ are moved by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase, not by this exchanger. H+ is pumped into the lumen. Phosphate is not part of HCl secretion. Costanzo states that HCO3- leaves the parietal cell across the basolateral membrane via a Cl−-HCO3− exchanger, so the ion entering is chloride, not phosphate. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion: basolateral Cl−/HCO3− exchangers, apical Cl− channels); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, basolateral transport). The direction of Cl− entry is inferred from the exchanger swapping Cl− for HCO3−.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the parietal cell basolateral membrane also contains the Na+/K+ ATPase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: net HCl secretion is paired with net HCO3− absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium is moved by the basolateral sodium-potassium pump, not by this exchanger.","C":"Potassium is moved by the basolateral sodium-potassium pump, not by this exchanger.","D":"H+ is pumped into the lumen.","E":"Costanzo states that HCO3- leaves the parietal cell across the basolateral membrane via a Cl−-HCO3− exchanger, so the ion entering is chloride, not phosphate."}},{"id":"sdl07_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has gastric glands studied after surgery. Blocking the apical K+ channels of her parietal cells lowers acid output even though the pump is intact. Why?","choices":{"A":"Carbonic anhydrase is inhibited","B":"Chloride channels close","C":"Pump loses its exchange ion","D":"Histamine release falls","E":"Bicarbonate exits apically"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The H+/K+ ATPase secretes H+ in exchange for luminal K+, and K+ recycles back into the canaliculus through apical K+ channels; without that recycling, little K+ is available for exchange and H+ secretion falls. Carbonic anhydrase, chloride channels, and histamine release are not directly affected. Bicarbonate exits across the basolateral membrane. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 3, apical active transport and Cl− channels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the H+/K+ ATPase is a primary active transporter that moves H+ and K+ against their gradients (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors block this pump.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the cellular mechanisms of gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells, including the functions of carbonic anhydrase, H⁺/K⁺-ATPase, Cl⁻ channels, and the alkaline tide.","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man takes omeprazole for a gastric ulcer, and his acid output falls in response to every stimulus, including histamine and gastrin. Which step does the drug block?","choices":{"A":"Apical proton pump","B":"Parietal H2 receptors","C":"Parietal M3 receptors","D":"CCK2 receptors","E":"Carbonic anhydrase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole irreversibly block the apical H+/K+ ATPase, the final step of acid secretion shared by every secretagogue. H2, M3, and CCK2 receptor blockade would each remove only one stimulus. Carbonic anhydrase is not the drug target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cellular mechanism of HCl secretion, step 2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs can cause malabsorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"H2 blockade would remove only the histamine stimulus.","C":"M3 blockade would remove only the cholinergic stimulus.","D":"CCK2 blockade would remove only the gastrin stimulus.","E":"Carbonic anhydrase is not the drug target."}},{"id":"sdl07_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old woman takes cimetidine, and her acid response to vagal stimulation and to gastrin falls much more than expected from blocking one secretagogue. Which mechanism explains this larger effect?","choices":{"A":"Direct M3 receptor blockade","B":"Lost histamine potentiation","C":"CCK2 receptor blockade","D":"Proton pump inhibition","E":"Gastrin release inhibition"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Histamine potentiates the actions of ACh and gastrin, so an H2 blocker removes both the direct histamine effect and the histamine-potentiated effects of ACh and gastrin. Cimetidine does not block M3 or CCK2 receptors, inhibit the proton pump, or stop gastrin release. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition); Costanzo Ch. 8 (potentiation and H2 receptor blocking agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine inhibits cytochrome P450 and has antiandrogenic effects (First Aid 2025 GI, H2 blockers). COMLEX: famotidine and nizatidine are other H2 antagonists.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — acid suppressing drug classes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has gastric pH recorded through a meal. The pH stays relatively high while food is in his stomach but drops sharply once the meal empties into the duodenum. What explains the drop?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin secretion rises","B":"Somatostatin release falls","C":"Vagal tone increases","D":"Pepsin activity increases","E":"Less food buffer remains"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Food buffers secreted H+, so gastric contents are less acidic while the meal is present; once chyme moves into the small intestine, buffering falls and continued H+ secretion lowers pH, which then inhibits gastrin and triggers somatostatin release to slow acid secretion. Gastrin falls and somatostatin rises as pH drops. Vagal tone falls rather than rises as the meal leaves, and pepsin activity does not lower pH. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (inhibition of HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, inhibitory pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distension of the stomach drives gastric phase acid secretion through vagovagal and local reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the cephalic phase is mediated by the vagus (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin falls and somatostatin rises as pH drops."}},{"id":"sdl07_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":2,"stem":"An 84-year-old woman in a study has her vagus nerves stimulated, and ACh binds muscarinic receptors on her parietal cells. Which enzyme does this receptor activate to drive acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase C","B":"Adenylyl cyclase","C":"Guanylyl cyclase","D":"Tyrosine kinase","E":"Lipoxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M3 receptors activate phospholipase C, which liberates diacylglycerol and IP3; IP3 releases intracellular Ca2+, and Ca2+ and diacylglycerol activate protein kinases that drive H+ secretion. Adenylyl cyclase is the histamine H2 pathway. Guanylyl cyclase, tyrosine kinase, and lipoxygenase are not the parietal M3 pathway. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (substances that alter HCl secretion); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues and receptor pathways).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACh acts on M3 receptors through a Gq cascade to increase H+/K+ ATPase activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, parietal cell physiology). COMLEX: atropine blocks direct vagal stimulation of parietal cells.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 85-year-old man has H. pylori infection and a duodenal ulcer. The infection also spreads to the duodenum. Which duodenal defense does it impair?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic enzyme output","B":"Bile salt secretion","C":"Duodenal villus height","D":"Local bicarbonate output","E":"Duodenal mucosal IgA"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Spread of H. pylori to the duodenum inhibits duodenal HCO3− secretion, so acid delivered from the stomach is poorly neutralized and erodes the mucosa. Pancreatic enzymes, bile salts, villus height, and mucosal IgA are not the described mechanism. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (duodenal ulcers); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, peptic ulcer disease and H. pylori infection).","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal ulcers are caused by H. pylori and gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: H. pylori is diagnosed by urea breath test, fecal antigen, or biopsy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl07_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"An 80-year-old woman has an H. pylori gastric ulcer. Her measured net acid secretion is lower than normal, and her serum gastrin is raised. What explains the low net acid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cell autoimmunity","B":"Back leak into the mucosa","C":"Gastrin receptor loss","D":"Excess somatostatin release","E":"Vagal nerve injury"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gastric ulcers the mucosal barrier is defective, so some secreted H+ leaks back into the damaged mucosa and net H+ secretion is lower than normal; the reduced net acid removes feedback on G cells and raises gastrin. Autoimmunity against parietal cells causes atrophic gastritis. Gastrin receptor loss, excess somatostatin, and vagal injury are not the described mechanism. Somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion, so excess somatostatin would not fit her raised serum gastrin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric ulcers and Table 8.5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori releases CagA toxin after attaching to epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic NSAID use is a cause of gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion, so excess somatostatin would not fit her raised serum gastrin."}},{"id":"sdl07_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman in a study smells and chews food and then spits it out, and her acid output rises before food reaches her stomach. Which vagal actions drive this response?","choices":{"A":"ACh and GRP release","B":"Gastrin and CCK release","C":"Secretin and GIP release","D":"Motilin and GIP release","E":"Histamine and somatostatin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, vagal efferents release ACh onto parietal cells and GRP onto antral G cells, so acid rises directly and through gastrin. Gastrin is released secondarily via GRP rather than by the vagus itself, and CCK is not part of the cephalic phase. Secretin, GIP, and motilin are not vagal transmitters. Histamine comes from ECL cells, and somatostatin inhibits acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, phases of gastric acid secretion); Costanzo Ch. 8 (cephalic phase).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic phase accounts for about 30% of meal stimulated acid secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: smell, taste, chewing, and conditioned reflexes start the cephalic phase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes and gastric secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the formation and modification of saliva, including the roles of acinar cells, ductal cells, myoepithelial cells, and how salivary flow rate affects electrolyte composition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with an autoimmune disorder that has reduced her salivary secretion starts oral pilocarpine, and her salivary flow rises from 0.3 to 3.5 mL/min. Compared with her saliva before treatment, which change in composition is expected?","choices":{"A":"Hypertonic fluid relative to plasma","B":"Composition unchanged by flow rate","C":"Lower bicarbonate concentration","D":"Higher potassium concentration","E":"Higher sodium and chloride"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pilocarpine is a muscarinic agonist that increases salivary secretion, and at high flow the isotonic acinar fluid passes through the ducts too quickly for full NaCl reabsorption, so the final saliva has more Na+ and Cl-, less K+, and moves closer to plasma tonicity. Saliva never becomes hypertonic, because the ducts are relatively impermeable to water and reabsorb more solute than they secrete. Flow rate does change composition, because it sets the time available for ductal modification. Bicarbonate rises at high flow because ductal bicarbonate secretion is stimulated during active salivation. Potassium is highest at low flow, when saliva spends the longest time in contact with ductal cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 1, effect of flow rate on composition); First Aid 2025 (pilocarpine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at high flow saliva has more Na+, Cl- and HCO3- and less K+, while at low flow it is most hypotonic with the most K+ (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 7). COMLEX: muscarinic agonists such as pilocarpine can relieve dry mouth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial specializations for secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Saliva never becomes hypertonic, because the ducts are","B":"Flow rate does change composition, because it sets","C":"Bicarbonate rises at high flow because ductal bicarbonate","D":"Potassium is highest at low flow, when saliva"}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare parasympathetic and sympathetic regulation of salivary secretion, including neurotransmitters, receptors, intracellular signaling pathways, and factors that increase or decrease salivary secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy is badly frightened before a school play, and his sympathetic activity is high. His mouth is dry even though sympathetic input stimulates his acinar cells. Which sympathetic effect also limits his fluid output?","choices":{"A":"Beta receptor blockade","B":"Ductal water absorption","C":"Reduced gland blood flow","D":"Myoepithelial relaxation","E":"Acinar cell apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic stimulation raises cAMP and produces a small volume of viscous saliva, but concurrent sympathetic vasoconstriction reduces local blood supply and blunts overall fluid output; fear also lowers parasympathetic drive. Norepinephrine activates rather than blocks beta receptors. Ductal water absorption is not a sympathetic effect. Stimulation contracts rather than relaxes myoepithelial cells. Fear does not kill acinar cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 2, sympathetic pathway and parasympathetic pathway).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sympathetic preganglionic fibers to the salivary glands arise from spinal segments T1 to T3 (lecture SDL 7). COMLEX: salivary glands have a very high blood flow that rises when saliva production is stimulated (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic transmitters","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"E":"Fear does not kill acinar cells."}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q03","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with a duodenal gastrinoma is awaiting surgery, and his fasting gastrin is 950 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL). On high-dose famotidine his measured acid output falls substantially, even though the tumor's hormone does not bind histamine receptors. Which mechanism best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Famotidine blocks CCK2 receptors on parietal cells","B":"Histamine drives gastrin release from the tumor","C":"Famotidine lowers vagal acetylcholine output","D":"Gastrin acts largely by releasing ECL histamine","E":"Famotidine inhibits the apical proton pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastrin binds CCK2 receptors on parietal and ECL cells, but in humans its main effect on acid comes indirectly through histamine released from ECL cells, which acts on parietal H2 receptors via cAMP, so blocking H2 receptors removes much of the gastrin response and its potentiation. Famotidine is a competitive H2 receptor antagonist and does not occupy CCK2 receptors. The tumor releases gastrin autonomously, so histamine is not what drives it. H2 blockers do not change vagal output, but they blunt the potentiated effect of ACh at the parietal cell. Direct pump inhibition is the action of proton pump inhibitors, not H2 blockers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, secretagogues, potentiation and pharmacologic inhibition).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ACh and gastrin act through IP3 and Ca2+ while histamine acts through cAMP, and gastrin works mainly through ECL histamine (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 7). COMLEX: ulcers in the jejunum or ulcers refractory to therapy should prompt a fasting gastrin level.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Famotidine is a competitive H2 receptor antagonist and","B":"The tumor releases gastrin autonomously, so histamine is","C":"H2 blockers do not change vagal output, but","E":"Direct pump inhibition is the action of proton"}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of gastric acid secretion by acetylcholine, gastrin, histamine, somatostatin, and prostaglandins, including receptor signaling pathways, potentiation, and mechanisms of pharmacologic inhibition.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with erosive esophagitis still has erosions after 8 weeks of twice-daily famotidine. She is switched to omeprazole taken before breakfast, and her erosions heal. Why does omeprazole suppress meal-stimulated acid more completely than famotidine?","choices":{"A":"It neutralizes acid already in the lumen","B":"It blocks the step shared by every secretagogue","C":"It increases somatostatin release from D cells","D":"It reversibly competes with histamine at H2 receptors","E":"It also blocks muscarinic receptors on parietal cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Omeprazole covalently and irreversibly inhibits the apical H+/K+-ATPase, the final common pathway used by ACh, gastrin and histamine, whereas an H2 blocker removes only the histamine input and its potentiation. Neutralizing acid already in the lumen is what antacids do, with a short duration of action. Raising gastric pH lowers somatostatin release from D cells, so this would not suppress acid. Reversible competition at H2 receptors describes famotidine, which leaves direct ACh and gastrin stimulation partly active. Muscarinic blockade is the action of drugs such as atropine, and omeprazole leaves M3 receptors untouched. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, pharmacologic inhibition; SDL 10, section 1, GERD drugs).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the H+/K+-ATPase, while H2 blockers competitively block histamine receptors (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 7). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors heal erosive esophagitis far more often than H2 blockers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin inhibits gastrin secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutralizing acid already in the lumen is what","C":"Raising gastric pH lowers somatostatin release from D","D":"Reversible competition at H2 receptors describes famotidine, which","E":"Muscarinic blockade is the action of drugs such"}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man in the ICU with septic shock needs vasopressors to keep his mean arterial pressure near 60 mm Hg. On day 3 coffee-ground material returns from his nasogastric tube, and endoscopy shows many shallow erosions scattered throughout the stomach. He takes no NSAIDs, tests negative for H. pylori, and has no increase in acid secretion. Failure of which mucosal defense best explains these lesions?","choices":{"A":"Blood flow that clears back-diffused H+","B":"Prostaglandin synthesis blocked by drugs","C":"Junction seals broken by a bacterial toxin","D":"Somatostatin feedback on acid output","E":"Mucus gel dissolved by refluxed bile"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Shock and vasoconstrictors reduce mucosal microvascular flow, so oxygen and bicarbonate delivery fall and back-diffused H+ is no longer washed away, producing local acidosis and multiple stress erosions even with normal acid output. Prostaglandin synthesis is blocked by NSAIDs, which he does not take. Junction seals broken by CagA require H. pylori, which is absent. Somatostatin feedback controls acid output, and his acid secretion is not increased. Refluxed bile causes reactive gastropathy after gastric surgery, and nothing suggests bile reflux. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, gastric mucosal barrier; SDL 3, section 4); Robbins Ch. 17 (stress-related mucosal injury).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucus, bicarbonate, tight junctions, restitution, blood flow and PGE2 protect the gastric mucosa (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 7). COMLEX: critically ill patients in shock develop stress erosions from mucosal ischemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Prostaglandin synthesis is blocked by NSAIDs, which he","C":"Junction seals broken by CagA require H. pylori,","D":"Somatostatin feedback controls acid output, and his acid","E":"Refluxed bile causes reactive gastropathy after gastric surgery,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl07_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has a duodenal ulcer and a positive urea breath test. Before treatment his fasting serum gastrin is mildly elevated and his stimulated acid output is high. Two months after successful eradication therapy, both values are normal. Restored activity of which cell best explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal I cells releasing CCK","B":"Antral D cells releasing somatostatin","C":"Fundic ECL cells releasing histamine","D":"Parietal cells releasing intrinsic factor","E":"Duodenal S cells releasing secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antral H. pylori inflammation inhibits somatostatin release from D cells, removing the paracrine brake on G cells so gastrin and acid rise and duodenal ulcers form, and eradication restores somatostatin release and returns gastrin and acid toward normal. CCK from I cells regulates the gallbladder and pancreas and is not the brake that the infection removed. ECL histamine drives acid but lies downstream of gastrin, so its fall is a result, not the restored brake. Intrinsic factor binds B12 and has no role in regulating gastrin or acid. Secretin from duodenal S cells inhibits gastrin, but the infection acted on antral D cells, not S cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 5, H. pylori; SDL 9, section 2, role of H. pylori in PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antral H. pylori lowers somatostatin, raising gastrin and acid delivery to the duodenum (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 7). COMLEX: confirm eradication with a urea breath test at least 4 weeks after antibiotics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CCK from I cells regulates the gallbladder and","C":"ECL histamine drives acid but lies downstream of","D":"Intrinsic factor binds B12 and has no role","E":"Secretin from duodenal S cells inhibits gastrin, but"}},{"id":"grid_sdl07_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the physiologic phases of gastric acid secretion and the mechanisms that protect the gastric mucosa, including the pathophysiology of peptic ulcer disease, H. pylori infection, and Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":4,"stem":"An 83-year-old woman with osteoarthritis has taken a nonprescription anti-inflammatory pill at high dose every day for several months and now has burning epigastric pain. Endoscopy shows multiple gastric erosions. Which of the following sets of gastric mucosal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Acid secretion","Bicarbonate secretion","Mucosal blood flow"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↑","↓","↔"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower PGE2, so the pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is correct. PGE2 normally acts through Gi receptors on parietal cells to oppose histamine, stimulates mucus and HCO3− secretion, and maintains mucosal blood flow; losing it tips the balance toward damaging factors. In the barrier-loss view, which Robbins emphasizes, prostaglandins support nearly every mucosal defense (mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution), so the injury comes mainly from weakened protection rather than from extra acid; Robbins notes that prostaglandins also reduce acid secretion, so their loss fits the ↑ acid that Bootcamp gives. The pattern (↓ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is an H2 receptor antagonist such as famotidine, which reversibly blocks parietal cell H2 receptors and lowers acid secretion. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is a Cushing ulcer, in which brain injury and raised intracranial pressure increase vagal stimulation of parietal cells. The pattern (↔ acid secretion, ↔ bicarbonate secretion, ↓ mucosal blood flow) is a Curling ulcer, in which burns or trauma cause hypovolemia and reduced gastric blood flow. The pattern (↑ acid secretion, ↓ bicarbonate secretion, ↔ mucosal blood flow) is antral H. pylori infection in duodenal ulcer disease, in which loss of D cell somatostatin raises gastrin and acid delivery while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 7, section 4, lines 1368–1370, prostaglandins bind Gi receptors on parietal cells; section 5, PGE2 preserves the mucosal barrier; SDL 9, section 2, lines 1715–1719, antral H. pylori in duodenal ulcer disease; SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonists); Costanzo Ch. 8 (PGE2 stimulates HCO3− and mucus secretion and increases mucosal blood flow); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (line 1044, NSAIDs lower PGE2, raising H+ secretion and lowering bicarbonate secretion and gastric blood flow; lines 1045–1046, Cushing and Curling ulcers); Robbins Ch. 17 (NSAIDs remove prostaglandin support for mucus, bicarbonate, blood flow, and restitution, and prostaglandins reduce acid secretion). The ↔ cells in rows A, B, D, and E are inferred because the sources do not describe those values as changed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol is used for NSAID ulcer prophylaxis and is contraindicated in pregnancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: Curling ulcers follow burns and hypovolemia, and Cushing ulcers follow brain injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — NSAID associated ulcer treatment and prevention","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":8,"title":"SDL 8 — Pathology of the Esophagus","questions":[{"id":"sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"Loss of which neurons causes primary achalasia?","choices":{"A":"Excitatory cholinergic neurons","B":"Vagal sensory neurons","C":"Sympathetic adrenergic neurons","D":"Interstitial pacemaker cells","E":"Nitric oxide neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Primary achalasia results from idiopathic degeneration or autoimmune loss of nitric oxide producing inhibitory enteric neurons in the myenteric plexus, so the LES fails to relax and the distal esophagus loses peristalsis. Loss of excitatory cholinergic neurons would weaken contraction. Vagal sensory, sympathetic, and pacemaker cells are not the lesion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: achalasia shows a bird beak sign on barium swallow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, achalasia). COMLEX: achalasia raises the risk of esophageal cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does a B ring of the distal esophagus lie?","choices":{"A":"In the upper cervical esophagus","B":"At the squamocolumnar junction","C":"At the cricopharyngeal muscle","D":"In the middle thoracic esophagus","E":"Within the gastric antrum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction and have gastric cardia mucosa on their undersurface, whereas A rings lie above the gastroesophageal junction and are covered by squamous mucosa. Upper esophageal webs occur in Plummer Vinson syndrome. The cricopharyngeus, midesophagus, and antrum are not ring sites. The cricopharyngeal muscle forms the upper esophageal sphincter. Robbins places B rings at the squamocolumnar junction of the lower esophagus, not in the upper cervical esophagus. Robbins places B rings at the squamocolumnar junction of the lower esophagus, above the stomach rather than within the gastric antrum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Schatzki ring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki rings are associated with hiatal hernia and GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal ring). COMLEX: rings cause intermittent solid food dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The middle thoracic esophagus is not where a B ring lies.","C":"The cricopharyngeal muscle forms the upper esophageal sphincter.","A":"A B ring does not lie in the upper cervical esophagus; webs there are a different lesion.","E":"The cricopharyngeus, midesophagus, and antrum are not ring sites."}},{"id":"sdl08_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":1,"stem":"What dysphagia pattern does progressive esophageal stenosis typically produce?","choices":{"A":"Painful swallowing of liquids","B":"Solids and liquids from the onset","C":"Liquids worse than solids","D":"Solids first and later liquids","E":"Episodic dysphagia for meat"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stenosis narrows the lumen progressively, so mechanical dysphagia begins with solids and later involves liquids as the caliber narrows, with appetite and weight usually preserved unless the lesion is malignant. Dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset is the motor pattern of achalasia. Episodic meat impaction fits rings. Painful swallowing is odynophagia; the lecture describes esophageal stenosis as progressive mechanical dysphagia, first to solids and later to liquids. Liquids worse than solids is not the stenosis pattern; as the lumen narrows, liquids are affected only later, after solids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: strictures follow GERD, caustic ingestion, or radiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: barium swallow shows narrowing at the stricture.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Painful swallowing is odynophagia; the lecture describes esophageal stenosis as progressive mechanical dysphagia, first to solids and later to liquids.","C":"Liquids worse than solids is not the stenosis pattern; as the lumen narrows, liquids are affected only later, after solids."}},{"id":"sdl08_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What failure during severe retching causes a Mallory Weiss tear?","choices":{"A":"Pylorus fails to close","B":"Vagal tone is lost","C":"Gastric acid is excessive","D":"Diaphragm fails to contract","E":"LES fails to relax first"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mallory Weiss tears occur when reflex relaxation of the LES fails to precede antiperistaltic gastric expulsion, so the wall near the gastroesophageal junction is overstretched and lacerated. Pyloric closure, vagal tone, excess acid, and diaphragm contraction are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Mallory Weiss syndrome is associated with alcohol use and bulimia nervosa (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: these tears account for up to 10% of upper GI bleeding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Failure of the pylorus to close is not how a Mallory-Weiss tear is produced.","C":"Pyloric closure, vagal tone, excess acid, and diaphragm contraction are not the lecture mechanism.","B":"Loss of vagal tone is not how a Mallory-Weiss tear is produced.","D":"Failure of the diaphragm to contract is not how a Mallory-Weiss tear is produced."}},{"id":"sdl08_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What does Candida esophagitis look like at endoscopy?","choices":{"A":"Punched out volcanic ulcers","B":"Stacked circular mucosal rings","C":"Salmon pink mucosal tongues","D":"Gray white pseudomembranes","E":"Dilated submucosal veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Candida forms adherent gray white pseudomembranes of matted hyphae, pseudohyphae, and inflammatory cells over eroded mucosa. Punched out ulcers, stacked rings, salmon pink tongues, and dilated veins are not the Candida appearance. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (esophageal candidiasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Candida histology shows pseudohyphae and budding yeast (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal candidiasis). COMLEX: infectious esophagitis causes prominent odynophagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the minimum eosinophil count on esophageal biopsy that supports eosinophilic esophagitis?","choices":{"A":"At least 2 per HPF","B":"At least 15 per HPF","C":"At least 5 per HPF","D":"At least 10 per HPF","E":"At least 50 per HPF"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis shows dense intraepithelial eosinophils, at least 15 per high power field, forming superficial clusters with spongiosis. Reflux esophagitis has only scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, so counts of 2, 5, or 10 per field are below the cutoff, and 50 per field exceeds the minimum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophilic esophagitis is driven by Th2 inflammation to food antigens (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: it is associated with atopic conditions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium and protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two eosinophils per high-power field is below the biopsy cutoff used for eosinophilic esophagitis.","C":"Five eosinophils per high-power field is still below the biopsy cutoff used for eosinophilic esophagitis."}},{"id":"sdl08_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell on biopsy is the histologic hallmark of Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Chief cells","D":"Paneth cells","E":"Keratinocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Barrett esophagus is simple columnar epithelium containing distinctive goblet cells with pale blue mucin vacuoles. Parietal and chief cells belong to gastric glands. Paneth cells are not the lecture hallmark. Keratinocytes would indicate squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus the Z line extends proximally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett mucosa may undergo malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"What process replaces esophageal squamous epithelium in Barrett esophagus?","choices":{"A":"Squamous hyperplasia","B":"Gastric glandular atrophy","C":"Intestinal metaplasia","D":"Fibrous scarring","E":"Epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Persistent acid injury reprograms esophageal stem cells toward a columnar fate, so intestinal metaplasia replaces the squamous lining with acid resistant columnar epithelium. Hyperplasia keeps squamous epithelium. Atrophy, scarring, and apoptosis do not produce columnar epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of Barrett esophagus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus is a complication of chronic GERD (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: endoscopy shows salmon pink mucosa above the junction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene is a key step in progression of Barrett dysplasia to adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"TP53","C":"BRCA1","D":"RB1","E":"VHL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"TP53 mutation is among the genetic alterations that drive Barrett dysplasia to invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma in the lecture. APC, BRCA1, RB1, and VHL are not listed for this progression. APC mutations underlie familial adenomatous polyposis and colorectal neoplasia, not Barrett progression. BRCA1 is a DNA repair gene linked to hereditary breast and ovarian cancer, not to Barrett progression. RB loss is the classic driver of retinoblastoma, not of Barrett dysplasia progression. VHL loss-of-function mutations underlie hereditary and many sporadic renal cell carcinomas, not Barrett progression. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma; section 5, pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: esophageal adenocarcinoma arises in the distal third (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: GERD and obesity are adenocarcinoma risk factors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations underlie familial adenomatous polyposis and colorectal neoplasia, not Barrett progression.","C":"BRCA1 is a DNA repair gene linked to hereditary breast and ovarian cancer, not to Barrett progression.","D":"RB loss is the classic driver of retinoblastoma, not of Barrett dysplasia progression.","E":"VHL loss-of-function mutations underlie hereditary and many sporadic renal cell carcinomas, not Barrett progression."}},{"id":"sdl08_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do esophageal veins drain the blood shunted to them from the portal circulation?","choices":{"A":"Splenic vein","B":"Inferior mesenteric vein","C":"Hepatic veins","D":"Renal veins","E":"Azygos system"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, portal blood shunted at the junction enters esophageal veins that drain into the azygos system. The splenic and inferior mesenteric veins feed the portal system. Hepatic veins drain the liver. Renal veins drain the kidneys. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices are dilated submucosal veins at a portocaval anastomosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding causes hematemesis and shock.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"In portal hypertension, collaterals form where portal and systemic veins share capillary beds. Besides the esophagogastric junction, which site forms such collaterals?","choices":{"A":"Great saphenous vein of the leg","B":"Pulmonary veins at the lung bases","C":"Periumbilical abdominal veins","D":"Dural venous sinuses of the skull","E":"Internal jugular vein of the neck"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Portosystemic shunts open wherever portal and systemic circulations share capillary beds: around the rectum, at the esophagogastric junction, in the retroperitoneum, and along the falciform ligament, where periumbilical abdominal wall veins form caput medusae. The great saphenous vein of the leg drains to the femoral vein and has no portal connection. Pulmonary veins at the lung bases carry oxygenated blood to the left atrium and do not join portal flow. Dural venous sinuses of the skull drain the brain into the jugular system. The internal jugular vein of the neck is a systemic vein with no portal anastomosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, portal hypertension and portosystemic shunts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portosystemic collaterals form at the esophagogastric junction, rectum, retroperitoneum, and periumbilical veins (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: caput medusae on exam signals portal hypertension.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The great saphenous vein of the leg","B":"Pulmonary veins at the lung bases carry","D":"Dural venous sinuses of the skull drain","E":"The internal jugular vein of the neck"}},{"id":"sdl08_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":1,"stem":"Many patients with cirrhosis have esophageal varices that never bleed. Which feature of a varix most raises the risk of life-threatening hemorrhage?","choices":{"A":"Large size of the dilated vein","B":"Location in the upper esophagus","C":"Overlying Barrett metaplasia","D":"Drainage into the azygos vein","E":"A coexisting sliding hiatal hernia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes that most small varices never bleed, whereas rupture of large varices can cause exsanguination, so size is the key risk feature. Location in the upper esophagus is wrong because varices form in the distal esophagus and proximal stomach. Overlying Barrett metaplasia is a reflux change that does not drive variceal rupture. Drainage into the azygos vein is shared by all esophageal varices and does not separate high-risk ones. A coexisting sliding hiatal hernia causes reflux, not variceal bleeding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17, esophageal varices.","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices form in the distal esophagus from portal hypertension, and large varices carry the risk of fatal rupture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: sudden massive hematemesis in cirrhosis is variceal bleeding until proven otherwise.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — layers of the esophageal wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Location in the upper esophagus is wrong","C":"Overlying Barrett metaplasia is a reflux change","D":"Drainage into the azygos vein is shared","E":"A coexisting sliding hiatal hernia causes reflux"}},{"id":"sdl08_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the esophagus does squamous cell carcinoma most often arise?","choices":{"A":"Distal third","B":"Gastric cardia","C":"Middle third","D":"Upper sphincter","E":"Abdominal segment"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises mainly in the middle third (about 50%) and upper third, whereas adenocarcinoma lies in the distal third and often invades the gastric cardia. The upper sphincter and abdominal segment are not the usual sites. Adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, is exclusively in the distal third; squamous cell carcinoma occurs primarily in the proximal esophagus, with half in the middle third. Distal-third adenocarcinoma frequently invades the gastric cardia; squamous cell carcinoma occurs primarily in the proximal esophagus, with half in the middle third. Robbins notes that adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, usually occurs in the distal esophagus, while about half of squamous cell carcinomas arise in the middle third. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, anatomic distribution); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma: proximal two thirds).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: barium swallow may show an apple core lesion.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, is exclusively in the distal third.","B":"Distal-third adenocarcinoma frequently invades the gastric cardia; squamous cell carcinoma occurs primarily in the proximal esophagus, with half in the middle third.","E":"Robbins notes that adenocarcinoma, not squamous cell carcinoma, usually occurs in the distal esophagus, while about half of squamous cell carcinomas arise in the middle third."}},{"id":"sdl08_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Human papillomavirus","B":"Epstein Barr virus","C":"Cytomegalovirus","D":"Helicobacter pylori","E":"Herpes simplex virus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture notes that H. pylori infection is associated with a reduced risk of esophageal adenocarcinoma. Human papillomavirus is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor. Epstein Barr virus, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus are not described as protective. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, major risk factors); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma risk factors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer in the US (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: adenocarcinoma shows a strong male predominance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Epstein Barr virus is linked to a subtype of gastric adenocarcinoma.","C":"Cytomegalovirus is not described as protective.","E":"Herpes simplex virus is not described as protective.","A":"Human papillomavirus is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor."}},{"id":"sdl08_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic feature identifies esophageal squamous cell carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Keratin pearls","B":"Goblet cell glands","C":"Signet ring cells","D":"Mucin vacuoles","E":"Cribriform glands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma shows nests of malignant squamous cells with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges. Goblet cells, signet ring cells, mucin vacuoles, and cribriform glands are glandular features rather than squamous ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (squamous cell carcinoma histopathology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma risk factors include smoking, alcohol, and hot beverages (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both esophageal cancers present with progressive dysphagia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman who grew up in rural Brazil has dysphagia to both solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food at night, and a bird beak narrowing on barium swallow. Which agent most likely caused her disease?","choices":{"A":"Trypanosoma cruzi","B":"Candida albicans","C":"Herpes simplex virus","D":"Cytomegalovirus","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secondary achalasia arises from Chagas disease, in which Trypanosoma cruzi destroys the myenteric plexus; it can also follow diabetic neuropathy, amyloidosis, sarcoidosis, or malignancy. Candida, herpes simplex virus, and cytomegalovirus cause infectious esophagitis with odynophagia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, achalasia); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Chagas disease secondary achalasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: T. cruzi is shed in the feces of the reduviid bug (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Chagas disease). COMLEX: Chagas disease can cause megaesophagus.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has had episodes over several years of a steak bolus sticking low in his chest, each resolving without trouble swallowing liquids. Which lesion best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Primary achalasia","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Scleroderma dysmotility","D":"Esophageal varices","E":"Distal esophageal ring"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rings cause episodic, nonprogressive mechanical dysphagia triggered by poorly chewed solid boluses, the steakhouse syndrome. Achalasia causes dysphagia to solids and liquids from onset. Cancer causes progressive dysphagia with weight loss. Scleroderma causes motor dysphagia with reflux. Varices bleed rather than obstruct. First Aid describes esophageal varices as dilated submucosal veins from portal hypertension that may cause life-threatening hematemesis, not episodic solid-food bolus impaction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schatzki B rings lie at the squamocolumnar junction (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: rings present with dysphagia and regurgitation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cancer causes progressive dysphagia with weight loss.","D":"First Aid describes esophageal varices as dilated submucosal veins from portal hypertension that may cause life-threatening hematemesis, not episodic solid-food bolus impaction."}},{"id":"sdl08_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has fatigue, a sore smooth tongue, cracks at the corners of her mouth, and dysphagia high in her throat. Her blood count shows a microcytic anemia. Which syndrome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pharyngeal Zenker diverticulum","B":"Plummer Vinson syndrome","C":"Mallory Weiss syndrome","D":"Boerhaave syndrome","E":"Barrett esophagus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Upper esophageal webs with iron deficiency anemia, glossitis, and cheilosis make up Plummer Vinson syndrome. Zenker diverticulum is a pouch with halitosis and regurgitation. Mallory Weiss and Boerhaave syndromes follow vomiting. Barrett esophagus is distal metaplasia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal webs and rings); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Plummer Vinson syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plummer Vinson syndrome raises the risk of squamous cell carcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Plummer Vinson syndrome). COMLEX: webs occur most often in women over 40.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 21-year-old man vomits repeatedly after a night of binge drinking and then vomits bright red blood. His vital signs are stable, and the bleeding stops on its own. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Ruptured submucosal vein","B":"Bleeding gastric ulcer","C":"Longitudinal mucosal tear","D":"Ulcerated squamous carcinoma","E":"Candida pseudomembranes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Mallory Weiss tear is a longitudinal mucosal laceration crossing the gastroesophageal junction after severe retching, presenting as painful hematemesis that usually heals quickly. Variceal rupture needs portal hypertension. A gastric ulcer is not linked to the retching sequence. Carcinoma and candidiasis do not fit the sudden onset after vomiting. A ruptured submucosal vein describes variceal bleeding. Candida esophagitis forms adherent gray-white pseudomembranes of matted fungal hyphae, not bleeding after forceful retching. First Aid describes Mallory-Weiss syndrome as longitudinal mucosal lacerations at the gastroesophageal junction from severe vomiting, presenting with hematemesis in patients with alcohol use disorder, not a bleeding gastric ulcer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Mallory Weiss syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy shows longitudinal tears near the gastroesophageal junction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Mallory Weiss syndrome). COMLEX: prolonged coughing is also an association.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric propulsion and retropulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Carcinoma and candidiasis do not fit the sudden onset after vomiting.","A":"A ruptured submucosal vein describes variceal bleeding.","E":"Candida esophagitis forms adherent gray-white pseudomembranes of matted fungal hyphae, not bleeding after forceful retching.","B":"First Aid describes Mallory-Weiss syndrome as longitudinal mucosal lacerations at the gastroesophageal junction from severe vomiting, presenting with hematemesis in patients with alcohol use disorder, not a bleeding gastric ulcer."}},{"id":"sdl08_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-year-old man who is otherwise healthy has severe pain on swallowing. Endoscopy shows punched out ulcers, and biopsy of the ulcer edge shows multinucleated squamous cells with nuclear molding. Which organism is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Herpes simplex virus","B":"Cytomegalovirus","C":"Candida albicans yeast","D":"Trypanosoma cruzi","E":"Helicobacter pylori"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Herpes simplex virus causes punched out, volcanic ulcers with multinucleated squamous cells, Cowdry A inclusions, and nuclear molding at the ulcer edge, and it can occur in healthy hosts. Cytomegalovirus causes shallow linear ulcers. Candida forms pseudomembranes. T. cruzi causes achalasia. H. pylori infects the stomach. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (herpes esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: herpes esophagitis is usually caused by HSV 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, herpes esophagitis). COMLEX: odynophagia is prominent in infectious esophagitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 13-year-old boy with asthma and eczema has repeated food impaction. Endoscopy shows stacked circular rings and linear furrows, and his symptoms persist after 8 weeks of a PPI. Which condition is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acid reflux esophagitis","B":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","C":"Herpes simplex esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Multiple Schatzki rings"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilic esophagitis is a Th2 driven allergic disorder linked to atopy that causes food impaction, a ringed feline esophagus with furrows and exudates, and symptoms that are refractory to PPI therapy alone. Reflux esophagitis responds to acid suppression. Herpes and Candida cause odynophagia with ulcers or plaques. Schatzki rings are distal and nonprogressive and do not cause furrows, atopy, or PPI refractory symptoms. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, eosinophilic esophagitis); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (eosinophilic esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endoscopy may show trachealization of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, eosinophilic esophagitis). COMLEX: infants present with feeding intolerance and vomiting.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has heartburn that worsens when she lies down after meals. Endoscopy shows hyperemia, and biopsy of the lower esophagus shows elongated lamina propria papillae. Which other finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell metaplasia","B":"Keratin pearl formation","C":"Cowdry A inclusions","D":"Basal zone hyperplasia","E":"Fungal pseudohyphae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Reflux esophagitis shows scattered intraepithelial eosinophils, basal zone hyperplasia above 20% of epithelial thickness, and papillae extending into the upper third of the epithelium. Goblet cells, keratin pearls, Cowdry A inclusions, and pseudohyphae are not features of reflux injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, reflux esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GERD risk factors include obesity, pregnancy, hiatal hernia, and smoking (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GERD). COMLEX: GERD is treated with lifestyle change and a PPI.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — mechanisms preventing reflux","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing Barrett esophagus has surveillance biopsies. The glands show rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of nuclear polarity, atypical mitoses, and gland within gland patterns, but the basement membrane is intact. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Low grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"High grade dysplasia","D":"Reflux esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"High grade dysplasia shows marked atypia, rounded hyperchromatic nuclei, loss of polarity, atypical mitoses, and cribriform architecture, while the basement membrane is not breached. Low grade dysplasia has elongated nuclei limited to the basal aspect of glands. Invasion of the lamina propria defines carcinoma. Reflux esophagitis is inflammatory. Squamous dysplasia arises in squamous epithelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, progression to dysplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic reflux causes intestinal metaplasia with simple columnar epithelium (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: Barrett esophagus needs endoscopic surveillance.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 3 — precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with Barrett esophagus has a new nodule in the distal esophagus. Biopsy shows atypical glands that have breached the basement membrane and invaded the lamina propria and submucosa. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"High grade dysplasia","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma","C":"Squamous cell carcinoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Leiomyoma of the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Once dysplastic Barrett cells breach the basement membrane and invade the lamina propria and submucosa, the lesion is invasive esophageal adenocarcinoma. High grade dysplasia lacks invasion. Squamous cell carcinoma forms squamous nests. Stromal tumors and leiomyomas are mesenchymal. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, development of esophageal adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma histology shows glandular tumor with intestinal differentiation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: esophageal cancer presents with progressive dysphagia for solids then liquids and rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman with alcoholic cirrhosis suddenly vomits a large amount of blood and becomes hypotensive. Which lesion is the most likely source?","choices":{"A":"Mallory Weiss mucosal tear","B":"Ulcerated Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Ruptured esophageal varices","D":"Candida pseudomembranes","E":"Eosinophilic esophagitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cirrhosis raises portal pressure and creates varices beneath thin squamous mucosa that rupture with massive, life threatening hematemesis. A Mallory Weiss tear needs prior retching and is usually self limited. Carcinoma, Candida, and eosinophilic esophagitis rarely cause sudden massive bleeding. Robbins describes Mallory-Weiss lacerations as superficial tears that heal rapidly without surgery, whereas rupture of large varices can cause exsanguination. First Aid describes Candida esophagitis as white pseudomembranes associated with immunosuppression. First Aid describes eosinophilic esophagitis as an atopy-associated condition with esophageal rings and linear furrows, whereas esophageal varices in cirrhosis may be a source of life-threatening hematemesis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: variceal bleeding can cause melena or hematochezia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: survivors have a high risk of rebleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Carcinoma, Candida, and eosinophilic esophagitis rarely cause sudden massive bleeding.","A":"Robbins describes Mallory-Weiss lacerations as superficial tears that heal rapidly without surgery, whereas rupture of large varices can cause exsanguination.","D":"First Aid describes Candida esophagitis as white pseudomembranes associated with immunosuppression.","E":"First Aid describes eosinophilic esophagitis as an atopy-associated condition with esophageal rings and linear furrows, whereas esophageal varices in cirrhosis may be a source of life-threatening hematemesis."}},{"id":"sdl08_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has cirrhosis and portal hypertension. Which vein carries portal blood into his esophageal collaterals at the esophagogastric junction?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal vein","B":"Paraumbilical vein","C":"Inferior vena cava","D":"Right renal vein","E":"Left gastric vein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"At the esophagogastric junction, blood shunts from the left gastric (coronary) vein into the esophageal veins. The superior rectal and paraumbilical veins form other portosystemic collaterals. The inferior vena cava and renal veins are systemic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portosystemic collateral vessels).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices form at portocaval anastomoses (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: varices also form in the proximal stomach.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — management of variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis has large varices in her distal esophagus. Which anatomic feature makes these vessels especially prone to rupture?","choices":{"A":"Thick muscular venous walls","B":"Low pressure within the veins","C":"Deep location in the adventitia","D":"Thin overlying squamous mucosa","E":"Dense surrounding fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Varices lie immediately beneath thin squamous mucosa under high hydrostatic pressure, so they rupture easily and bleed massively. Their walls are thin, pressure is high, and they lie in the subepithelial and submucosal plexuses rather than the adventitia. Fibrosis does not protect them. Robbins explains that varices form because of portal hypertension, which raises portal venous pressure, so the veins are not under low pressure. Robbins places esophageal varices within the mucosa and submucosa of the distal esophagus, not deep in the adventitia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, development of collaterals and clinical significance).","boardPrep":"Step 1: varices lie in the subepithelial and submucosal venous plexuses of the distal esophagus (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: varices develop in many patients with cirrhosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Their walls are thin, pressure is high, and they lie in the subepithelial and submucosal plexuses rather than the adventitia.","E":"Fibrosis does not protect them","B":"Robbins explains that varices form because of portal hypertension, which raises portal venous pressure, so the veins are not under low pressure.","C":"Robbins places esophageal varices within the mucosa and submucosa of the distal esophagus, not deep in the adventitia."}},{"id":"sdl08_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman who grew up in northern Iran drinking very hot tea has progressive dysphagia and weight loss. Endoscopy shows an ulcerated esophageal mass. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma","C":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","D":"Esophageal leiomyoma","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma is the most common esophageal cancer worldwide, concentrated in belts such as Iran and central China, and linked to very hot beverages. Adenocarcinoma arises from Barrett mucosa. Stromal tumors, leiomyomas, and neuroendocrine tumors are not linked to hot tea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, epidemiology, risk factors, and anatomic distribution).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zenker diverticulum is listed as a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, squamous cell carcinoma). COMLEX: both cancers have poor five year survival.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with long-standing reflux and obesity has a mass in her distal esophagus that extends into the gastric cardia. Which histology is expected on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Nests with keratin pearls","B":"Spindle cells with KIT","C":"Small round blue cells","D":"Malignant mucin glands","E":"Sheets of lymphoid cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma, linked to GERD, Barrett esophagus, and obesity, arises in the distal third and shows mucin producing malignant glands or signet ring cells. Keratin pearls, spindle cells, small round cells, and lymphoid sheets are not the expected pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, morphologic features).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenocarcinoma risk factors include Barrett esophagus, smoking, GERD, and obesity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: endoscopy with biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl08_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has a midesophageal lesion in which atypical cells replace the full thickness of the surface lining but do not invade. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Barrett metaplasia","B":"Basal zone hyperplasia","C":"Reflux esophagitis","D":"Candida esophagitis","E":"Squamous dysplasia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Squamous cell carcinoma arises from squamous dysplasia, also called carcinoma in situ, driven by cell cycle gene mutations. Barrett metaplasia is columnar. Basal zone hyperplasia and reflux esophagitis are reactive. Candida esophagitis forms pseudomembranes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 5, pathogenesis and precursor lesions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tylosis is a squamous cell carcinoma risk factor (lecture SDL 8). COMLEX: early lesions appear as gray white plaques.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathogenesis, morphologic features, and clinical manifestations of esophageal obstructive and motility disorders, including stenosis, webs & rings, and achalasia, and correlate the underlying structural or neuromuscular abnormality with patterns of dysphagia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman swallowed lye as a child and now has slowly worsening dysphagia. Endoscopy shows a localized circumferential narrowing. Which change in the wall causes the narrowing?","choices":{"A":"Myenteric neuron loss","B":"Submucosal fibrosis","C":"Mucosal fold duplication","D":"Muscularis hypertrophy","E":"Venous plexus dilation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Esophageal stenosis is incomplete luminal opening from fibrous thickening of the submucosa with atrophy of the muscularis propria and mucosal damage; acquired forms follow caustic ingestion, GERD, radiation, or systemic sclerosis. Myenteric neuron loss causes achalasia. Fold duplication is not a stenosis mechanism. The muscularis atrophies rather than hypertrophies. Dilated venous plexuses are varices. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 1, esophageal stenosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: caustic ingestion is a risk factor for esophageal stricture (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal stricture). COMLEX: caustic strictures also raise squamous cell carcinoma risk (lecture SDL 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has sudden severe chest pain after forceful vomiting. He is tachypneic and hypotensive, with crepitus in his neck and air around the mediastinum on CT. Which injury explains the air?","choices":{"A":"Full thickness wall tear","B":"Superficial mucosal tear","C":"Variceal wall rupture","D":"Candida mucosal erosion","E":"Eosinophilic microabscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Boerhaave syndrome is a transmural esophageal rupture from a sudden rise in intraesophageal pressure during vomiting or straining, so air escapes and causes pneumomediastinum, mediastinitis, and shock. A superficial tear is confined to the mucosa and does not perforate the esophageal wall, so air does not enter the mediastinum. Variceal rupture, Candida erosions, and eosinophilic microabscesses do not breach the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, mucosal lacerations); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Boerhaave syndrome: transmural perforation, subcutaneous or mediastinal emphysema).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Boerhaave syndrome usually involves the distal third of the esophagus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome). COMLEX: barium for contrast esophagography may cause mediastinitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Boerhaave syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 1 — layers of the alimentary canal","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the major forms of esophageal injury and inflammation-including mucosal lacerations, reflux espophagitis, infectious esophagitis, and eosinophilic esophagitis-based on their etiology, pathogenesis, clinical presentation and characteristic morphologic findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man takes tacrolimus and prednisone after a kidney transplant and now has severe pain on swallowing. Endoscopy shows several shallow, linear ulcers in the mid and distal esophagus without plaques or pseudomembranes. Biopsy of which site is most likely to show diagnostic viral inclusions?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cells at the edge of the ulcer","B":"Surface plaques of matted fungal hyphae","C":"Normal squamous mucosa away from ulcers","D":"Endothelial cells in the ulcer base","E":"Columnar cells of the gastric cardia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Shallow linear ulcers in an immunosuppressed patient suggest cytomegalovirus, which infects endothelial cells and stromal fibroblasts, so its nuclear and cytoplasmic inclusions are found in the ulcer base. Multinucleated squamous cells with Cowdry A inclusions at the ulcer edge are the target in herpes simplex esophagitis, which forms punched-out ulcers. Matted fungal hyphae form the gray-white pseudomembranes of Candida, and none are present. Normal squamous mucosa away from the ulcers is unlikely to contain infected cells. The ulcers are esophageal, and the virus is sought in the ulcer bed itself, not the cardia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 2, infectious esophagitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CMV ulcers are shallow and linear with inclusions in endothelium of the ulcer base, while HSV ulcers are punched out with inclusions at the edge (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 8). COMLEX: odynophagia after transplant calls for endoscopy with biopsies of both the base and edge of ulcers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — esophageal epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Multinucleated squamous cells with Cowdry A inclusions at","B":"Matted fungal hyphae form the gray-white pseudomembranes of","C":"Normal squamous mucosa away from the ulcers is","E":"The ulcers are esophageal, and the virus is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the pathogenesis and morphologic features of gastroesophageal reflux disease and correlate chronic reflux injury with the development of Barrett esophagus, dysplasia and esophagel adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with obesity and 20 years of heartburn has surveillance endoscopy that shows salmon-pink mucosa extending 4 cm above the gastroesophageal junction. Biopsies show columnar epithelium with goblet cells, and the gland nuclei are elongated, hyperchromatic, and crowded but stay in the basal part of the cells. The glands are simple, and the lamina propria is not invaded. Which interpretation is best supported?","choices":{"A":"High grade dysplasia with cribriform glands","B":"Adenocarcinoma invading the lamina propria","C":"Metaplasia without any nuclear atypia","D":"Squamous reflux injury with basal hyperplasia","E":"Low grade dysplasia in Barrett mucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Goblet cells confirm Barrett intestinal metaplasia, and nuclear elongation, hyperchromasia and crowding confined to the basal part of the cells without architectural complexity define low-grade dysplasia. High-grade dysplasia shows rounded nuclei, loss of polarity, atypical mitoses and gland-within-gland patterns, none of which are described. Invasive adenocarcinoma requires a breach of the basement membrane into the lamina propria, which is absent. Metaplasia without atypia lacks the nuclear elongation, hyperchromasia and crowding seen. Squamous reflux esophagitis keeps a squamous lining with basal zone hyperplasia and no goblet cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 3, Barrett esophagus and dysplasia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Barrett esophagus progresses from intestinal metaplasia to low-grade dysplasia, high-grade dysplasia and adenocarcinoma, driven by TP53 and CDKN2A changes (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 8). COMLEX: obesity and long-standing reflux are key risk factors for Barrett esophagus and adenocarcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia shows rounded nuclei, loss of polarity,","B":"Invasive adenocarcinoma requires a breach of the basement","C":"Metaplasia without atypia lacks the nuclear elongation, hyperchromasia","D":"Squamous reflux esophagitis keeps a squamous lining with"}},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathogenesis and clinical significance of esophageal varices by relating portal hypertension and cirrhosis to the development of portosystemic collateral vessels and the risk of life-threatening upper GI hemorrhage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with alcohol-associated cirrhosis has ascites, splenomegaly, and large esophageal varices. Besides the fibrosis and active constriction inside his liver, which change in his splanchnic circulation adds to his portal hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Lower flow through the left gastric vein","B":"Reduced endothelin in hepatic sinusoids","C":"Sinusoidal dilation from excess nitric oxide","D":"Constriction of the mesenteric arterioles","E":"Arteriolar dilation raising portal inflow"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Portal hypertension in cirrhosis comes from increased intrahepatic resistance, both fixed fibrosis and active sinusoidal constriction, plus splanchnic arterial vasodilation driven by excess NO, which increases portal venous inflow. Flow through the left gastric vein increases as portal blood shunts into the esophageal veins. Endothelin-1 is elevated in cirrhosis and adds to active sinusoidal constriction. Inside the liver endothelial NO is decreased, so the sinusoids constrict rather than dilate. Constriction of the mesenteric arterioles would reduce portal inflow and is the goal of octreotide and vasopressin therapy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, section 4, portal hypertension; SDL 10, section 4).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal pressure rises from intrahepatic resistance plus NO-driven splanchnic vasodilation that increases portal inflow (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 8). COMLEX: splanchnic vasoconstrictors and nonselective beta blockers lower portal inflow to treat or prevent variceal bleeding.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 4 — vasoactive agents for variceal hemorrhage","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flow through the left gastric vein increases as","B":"Endothelin-1 is elevated in cirrhosis and adds to","C":"Inside the liver endothelial NO is decreased, so","D":"Constriction of the mesenteric arterioles would reduce portal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl08_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare esophageal squamous cell carcinoma and adenocarcinoma with respect to epidemology, major risk factors, pathoogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical presentation and precursor lesions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has had dysphagia to solids and liquids with regurgitation of undigested food since her thirties, and a barium study years ago showed a dilated esophagus with a bird-beak narrowing. She does not smoke, drink alcohol, or have heartburn. She now has rapid weight loss and worsening dysphagia. Which tumor type and location are most likely?","choices":{"A":"Stromal tumor of the gastric cardia","B":"Signet ring carcinoma of the gastric body","C":"Squamous carcinoma of the middle third","D":"Neuroendocrine tumor of the cardia","E":"Adenocarcinoma of the distal third"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Long-standing achalasia is a risk factor for esophageal squamous cell carcinoma, which arises mainly in the middle third, about 50% of cases. Stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal and are not linked to achalasia. Diffuse-type signet ring carcinoma has no known precursor lesion and is not a complication of achalasia. Gastric neuroendocrine tumors arise from ECL cells driven by hypergastrinemia, not from achalasia. Adenocarcinoma of the distal third arises from Barrett mucosa after long-standing reflux, which she lacks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 8, sections 1 and 5; SDL 9, sections 4 and 5).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma risk factors include alcohol, tobacco, very hot beverages, achalasia, Plummer-Vinson syndrome and caustic strictures (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 8). COMLEX: new progressive dysphagia with weight loss in long-standing achalasia needs endoscopy to exclude carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — squamous and columnar epithelium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Diffuse-type signet ring carcinoma has no known precursor","D":"Gastric neuroendocrine tumors arise from ECL cells driven","E":"Adenocarcinoma of the distal third arises from Barrett"}}]},{"sdlNumber":9,"title":"SDL 9 — Pathology of the Stomach","questions":[{"id":"sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature of the inflammatory infiltrate typifies acute reactive gastropathy?","choices":{"A":"Few or absent neutrophils","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Dense plasma cell band","D":"Gland centered lymphocytes","E":"Eosinophil microabscesses"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive gastropathy from NSAIDs, alcohol, bile reflux, or ischemia shows edema, vascular congestion, erosions, and foveolar hyperplasia with a notable paucity or absence of neutrophils. Pit abscesses and a superficial plasma-cell infiltrate are features of H. pylori gastritis. A deep gland-centered lymphocytic infiltrate is autoimmune gastritis. Eosinophil microabscesses are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, and physiologic stress cause gastritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastritis general principles). COMLEX: gastritis is evaluated by H. pylori testing and upper endoscopy with biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis, often an allergic disorder, not reactive gastropathy.","B":"Robbins describes neutrophils crossing the basement membrane and collecting in gastric pits as pit abscesses in H. pylori gastritis, not in reactive gastropathy.","C":"Clusters or sheets of plasma cells in the superficial lamina propria are the infiltrate of H. pylori gastritis, not reactive gastropathy.","D":"Inflammation that is deeper and centered on the gastric glands marks autoimmune atrophic gastritis of the body and fundus."}},{"id":"sdl09_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are H. pylori organisms concentrated in a gastric biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Deep oxyntic glands","B":"Submucosal lymphatics","C":"Muscularis propria","D":"Lamina propria capillaries","E":"Superficial mucus layer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Spiral bacilli of H. pylori are concentrated in the superficial mucus layer and are seen on H&E or silver and immunostains. They do not live in the deep glands, submucosal lymphatics, muscularis propria, or lamina propria vessels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is a gram negative motile spiral bacillus that may be seen on silver stain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: chronic H. pylori infection raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis destroys parietal cells in the gastric body. Why is serum gastrin high in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies stimulate gastrin receptors","B":"A pancreatic gastrinoma develops","C":"Absent acid stimulates antral G cells","D":"Low vitamin B12 drives gastrin release","E":"Renal clearance of gastrin falls"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of parietal cells removes acid production, and the absence of acid stimulates gastrin release, causing hypergastrinemia and hyperplasia of the spared antral G cells. Antibodies in this disease target parietal cell components and intrinsic factor, not gastrin receptors. A pancreatic gastrinoma is a separate tumor that raises gastrin with high, not absent, acid. Low vitamin B12 is a consequence of intrinsic factor loss and does not drive gastrin. Renal clearance of gastrin is not reduced by autoimmune gastritis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17, autoimmune atrophic gastritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune gastritis causes achlorhydria, hypergastrinemia with antral G cell hyperplasia, and B12 deficiency (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: chronic hypergastrinemia predisposes these patients to gastric neuroendocrine tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 4 — the gastric mucosal barrier","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antibodies in this disease target parietal cell","B":"A pancreatic gastrinoma is a separate tumor","D":"Low vitamin B12 is a consequence of","E":"Renal clearance of gastrin is not reduced"}},{"id":"sdl09_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"Which zone forms the deepest layer of a chronic peptic ulcer base?","choices":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris","B":"Neutrophilic infiltrate","C":"Granulation tissue","D":"Dense fibrous scar","E":"Foveolar hyperplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A peptic ulcer shows four zones from the surface down: fibrinoid necrotic debris, an active neutrophilic infiltrate, vascularized granulation tissue, and deep dense fibrous scar. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive gastropathy feature, not an ulcer zone. Fibrinoid necrotic debris is the most superficial zone of a peptic ulcer, not the deepest. The active neutrophilic infiltrate is the second zone from the surface, above granulation tissue and the scar. Vascularized granulation tissue is the third zone, lying above the deep dense fibrous scar. Robbins describes the ulcer base as fibrinoid debris, a neutrophilic infiltrate, granulation tissue, and finally a fibrous or collagenous scar, with no foveolar hyperplasia layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric ulcers most often lie on the lesser curvature (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: gastric ulcers carry a higher malignancy risk than duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fibrinoid necrotic debris is the most superficial zone of a peptic ulcer, not the deepest.","B":"The active neutrophilic infiltrate is the second zone from the surface, above granulation tissue and the scar.","C":"Vascularized granulation tissue is the third zone, lying above the deep dense fibrous scar.","E":"Robbins describes the ulcer base as fibrinoid debris, a neutrophilic infiltrate, granulation tissue, and finally a fibrous or collagenous scar, with no foveolar hyperplasia layer."}},{"id":"sdl09_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"When does peptic ulcer pain classically occur?","choices":{"A":"Within minutes of swallowing","B":"Mostly on waking each morning","C":"One to three hours after eating","D":"Steady through day and night","E":"Mainly during strenuous exercise"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peptic ulcer pain is epigastric burning or aching that often occurs 1 to 3 hours after meals or at night and is relieved by food or antacids. Pain within minutes of swallowing suggests an esophageal cause. Pain mainly on waking, steady day and night, or with exertion does not fit, because ulcer pain follows meals by 1 to 3 hours, is worse at night (usually 11 p.m. to 2 a.m.), and is relieved by food or alkali. Robbins notes ulcer pain is worst at night, usually between 11 p.m. and 2 a.m., rather than mainly on waking. Ulcer pain is relieved by food or alkali, so it does not stay steady through the day and night. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, clinical features of PUD); Robbins Ch. 17 (peptic ulcer pain timing).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori infection and NSAIDs are the main causes of peptic ulcer disease (lecture SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric ulcers lead to weight loss and duodenal ulcers to weight gain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Pain mainly on waking, steady day and night, or with exertion does not fit, because ulcer pain follows meals by 1 to 3 hours, is worse at night (usually 11 p.m. to 2 a.m.), and is relieved by food or alkali.","B":"Robbins notes ulcer pain is worst at night, usually between 11 p.m. and 2 a.m., rather than mainly on waking.","D":"Ulcer pain is relieved by food or alkali, so it does not stay steady through the day and night."}},{"id":"sdl09_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"How does antral H. pylori infection lead to duodenal ulcer disease?","choices":{"A":"Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis","B":"Autonomous tumor gastrin release","C":"Autoimmune parietal cell loss","D":"Increased duodenal bicarbonate output","E":"Reduced D cell somatostatin release"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In duodenal ulcer disease, antral H. pylori inflammation inhibits D cell somatostatin secretion, removing the brake on G cells; the resulting uninhibited gastrin release causes parietal cell hyperplasia and greater acid delivery to the duodenum, while duodenal bicarbonate secretion falls. Reduced mucosal prostaglandin synthesis is the mechanism of NSAIDs, which inhibit cyclooxygenase and lower mucus, bicarbonate, and blood flow. Autonomous tumor gastrin release is Zollinger-Ellison syndrome from a gastrinoma. Autoimmune parietal cell loss causes atrophic gastritis with low acid rather than duodenal ulcers. Duodenal bicarbonate output falls rather than rises in H. pylori duodenal ulcer disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, roles of H. pylori and NSAIDs in PUD and ZES mechanism, lines 1714–1752); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (peptic ulcer disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists H. pylori in about 90 percent of duodenal ulcers and about 70 percent of gastric ulcers (First Aid 2025 GI, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that NSAIDs inhibit cyclooxygenase, lowering PGE2, bicarbonate secretion, and gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 5 — mucosal protection and peptic ulcer pathophysiology","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gastric polyps account for up to 75 percent of all gastric polyps?","choices":{"A":"Fundic gland polyps","B":"Gastric adenomas","C":"Hyperplastic polyps","D":"Hamartomatous polyps","E":"Neuroendocrine polyps"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps account for up to 75% of gastric polyps and arise as reactive responses to chronic mucosal injury, most often chronic H. pylori gastritis. Fundic gland polyps are less common. Adenomas are up to 10% of gastric polyps. Hamartomatous and neuroendocrine polyps are not the majority. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, inflammatory and hyperplastic polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic gastritis can cause gastric mucosal atrophy with loss of glands and metaplasia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic gastritis). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps peak at ages 50 to 60 (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic finding characterizes a fundic gland polyp?","choices":{"A":"Cystic oxyntic glands","B":"Corkscrew foveolar glands","C":"Dysplastic tubular glands","D":"Sheets of signet ring cells","E":"Lymphoepithelial lesions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fundic gland polyps are composed of cystically dilated glands lined by parietal, chief, and foveolar cells with minimal inflammation. Elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an inflamed lamina propria are hyperplastic polyps. Dysplastic glands define adenomas. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Lymphoepithelial lesions are MALT lymphoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric adenocarcinoma is the most common gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps lie in the body and fundus (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":1,"stem":"Gastric adenomas carry a greater cancer risk than colonic adenomas. Which feature of a gastric adenoma most correlates with adenocarcinoma inside it?","choices":{"A":"Location in the gastric fundus","B":"Larger diameter of the polyp","C":"Cystically dilated oxyntic glands","D":"Onset during proton pump therapy","E":"A smooth, pale mucosal surface"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The risk of adenocarcinoma within a gastric adenoma correlates with size and is particularly elevated in large lesions, which is why these polyps are resected. Location in the gastric fundus is not the risk factor described. Cystically dilated oxyntic glands describe fundic gland polyps, which have little neoplastic risk. Onset during proton pump therapy is linked to fundic gland polyps, not to dysplastic adenomas. A smooth, pale mucosal surface does not mark malignant change. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17, gastric adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric adenomas arise on chronic atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia, and cancer risk rises with size (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gastric adenomas need complete endoscopic removal and surveillance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Location in the gastric fundus is not","C":"Cystically dilated oxyntic glands describe fundic gland","D":"Onset during proton pump therapy is linked","E":"A smooth, pale mucosal surface does not"}},{"id":"sdl09_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Which molecular subtype of intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma features TP53 mutations and RTK RAS amplifications?","choices":{"A":"Microsatellite instability","B":"Epstein Barr virus type","C":"Genomically stable type","D":"Hypermutated POLE type","E":"Chromosomal instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The chromosomal instability subtype is characterized by TP53 mutations, Wnt and SMAD4 mutations, and RTK-RAS pathway amplifications such as ERBB2, KRAS, EGFR, and MET. Microsatellite instability and Epstein Barr virus association are separate subtypes. Genomically stable tumors correspond mainly to diffuse-type gastric cancer. A hypermutated POLE type is not described in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, pathogenesis and molecular alterations).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EBV is a risk factor for gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intestinal type tumors form glands (tubular or papillary) (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Genomically stable tumors correspond mainly to diffuse-type gastric cancer, not intestinal type.","A":"Microsatellite-unstable gastric cancers favor the gastric body, show MLH1 silencing, and only rarely carry TP53 or KRAS mutations.","B":"Epstein Barr virus association is a separate subtype.","D":"A hypermutated POLE type is not described in the lecture."}},{"id":"sdl09_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Spread of gastric adenocarcinoma to the left supraclavicular lymph node produces which named finding?","choices":{"A":"Krukenberg tumor","B":"Virchow node","C":"Sister Mary Joseph nodule","D":"Irish node","E":"Blumer shelf"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The left supraclavicular sentinel node is the Virchow node. A Krukenberg tumor is ovarian metastasis, a Sister Mary Joseph nodule is a periumbilical node, an Irish node is the left axillary node, and a Blumer shelf is a deposit in the pouch of Douglas. A Krukenberg tumor is gastric adenocarcinoma metastatic to the ovaries, a separate common metastatic site from the left supraclavicular node. A Sister Mary Joseph nodule is gastric cancer spread to the periumbilical lymph nodes, not to the left supraclavicular node. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Virchow node, Sister Mary Joseph nodule, Krukenberg tumor). Irish node and Blumer shelf names are not used in the lecture; their sites are.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy may present with the Leser Trélat sign or acanthosis nigricans (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: gastric cancer is difficult to detect early (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Irish node is not used in the lecture.","E":"Blumer shelf names are not used in the lecture.","A":"A Krukenberg tumor is gastric adenocarcinoma metastatic to the ovaries, a separate common metastatic site from the left supraclavicular node.","C":"A Sister Mary Joseph nodule is gastric cancer spread to the periumbilical lymph nodes, not to the left supraclavicular node."}},{"id":"sdl09_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the stomach do intestinal type adenocarcinomas mainly arise?","choices":{"A":"Fundus and greater curve","B":"Cardia near the junction","C":"Body along the greater curvature","D":"Antrum or lesser curvature","E":"Pylorus and duodenal bulb"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma is located predominantly in the antrum or lesser curvature and forms bulky exophytic or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. Diffuse type tumors infiltrate the wall without a discrete mass. Robbins adds that the lesser curvature is involved more often than the greater curvature, so the fundus, greater curve, and cardia are not the main sites, and the duodenal bulb lies outside the stomach. Robbins states that most distal gastric adenocarcinomas occur in the antrum and involve the lesser curvature more often than the greater curvature. First Aid states intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma is commonly on the lesser curvature, not the pylorus and duodenal bulb. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (intestinal type: lesser curvature); Robbins Ch. 17 (most distal tumors in the antrum, lesser curvature more than greater).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at an older age than diffuse type (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric malignancy can cause gastric outlet obstruction and upper GI bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cardia is not the main site, and the duodenal bulb lies outside the stomach.","C":"Greater curve is not the main site, and the duodenal bulb lies outside the stomach.","A":"Robbins states that most distal gastric adenocarcinomas occur in the antrum and involve the lesser curvature more often than the greater curvature.","E":"First Aid states intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma is commonly on the lesser curvature, not the pylorus and duodenal bulb."}},{"id":"sdl09_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"Which marker pattern fits the cells of gastric MALT lymphoma?","choices":{"A":"CD20 positive CD5 negative","B":"CD5 and CD23 positive","C":"CD10 and BCL6 positive","D":"CD3 positive CD20 negative","E":"CD117 and DOG1 positive"},"correct":"A","explanation":"MALT lymphoma cells express mature B cell markers CD19 and CD20, are negative for CD5 and CD10, are CD43 positive in about 25% of cases, and show light chain restriction. CD5 positivity and CD10 positivity are excluded. CD3 marks T cells. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. Robbins states that MALT lymphomas do not express CD10, so a CD10-positive pattern argues against it. MALT lymphomas are mature B-cell tumors that express CD19 and CD20, so a CD3-positive, CD20-negative T-cell pattern does not fit. KIT (CD117) is the most useful marker of GIST and DOG1 is positive in 95% of GISTs, so this pattern points to a stromal tumor rather than a lymphoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MALT lymphoma occurs at an older age and follows persistent H. pylori inflammation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MALT lymphoma). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma is about 5% of gastric malignancies (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — mucosal specializations and MALT","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CD5 positivity and CD10 positivity are excluded.","C":"Robbins states that MALT lymphomas do not express CD10, so a CD10-positive pattern argues against it.","D":"MALT lymphomas are mature B-cell tumors that express CD19 and CD20, so a CD3-positive, CD20-negative T-cell pattern does not fit.","E":"KIT (CD117) is the most useful marker of GIST and DOG1 is positive in 95% of GISTs, so this pattern points to a stromal tumor rather than a lymphoma."}},{"id":"sdl09_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumors arise from which cells?","choices":{"A":"Smooth muscle myocytes","B":"Cajal pacemaker cells","C":"Enterochromaffin cells","D":"Peripheral Schwann cells","E":"Stromal myofibroblasts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GISTs are the most common abdominal mesenchymal tumor and arise from the interstitial cells of Cajal, the pacemaker cells of the gut. Enterochromaffin type cells give rise to neuroendocrine tumors. Smooth muscle cells, Schwann cells, and myofibroblasts are not the described origin. Gastric mesenchymal tumors of smooth muscle origin are leiomyomas or leiomyosarcomas. Tumors of nerve sheath origin in the stomach are schwannomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIST is most common in the stomach but can arise elsewhere in the GI tract (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST occurs at an older age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric mesenchymal tumors of smooth muscle origin are leiomyomas or leiomyosarcomas, not GISTs.","D":"Tumors of nerve sheath origin in the stomach are schwannomas, not GISTs.","C":"Enterochromaffin cells give rise to neuroendocrine tumors.","E":"Stromal myofibroblasts are a different cell; the stromal name is historical."}},{"id":"sdl09_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":1,"stem":"Which staining result supports the diagnosis of a gastric neuroendocrine tumor?","choices":{"A":"CD117 and DOG1 staining","B":"CD20 and CD19 staining","C":"Desmin and actin staining","D":"Chromogranin A staining","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastric neuroendocrine tumors express synaptophysin and chromogranin A, and the diagnosis is made by histology with this positivity. CD117 and DOG1 mark GIST. CD20 and CD19 mark B cell lymphoma. Desmin, actin, S100, and SOX10 are not the described markers. Desmin and actin staining marks smooth muscle tumors such as leiomyoma, not neuroendocrine tumors. S100 and SOX10 staining marks Schwann cell tumors such as schwannoma, not neuroendocrine tumors. KIT (CD117) is detectable in normal Cajal cells and about 95% of gastric GISTs, and DOG1 is positive in about 95% of GISTs including epithelioid types, so this pair marks a stromal tumor. CD19 and CD20 are pan B-cell markers found on pre-B and mature B cells but not plasma cells, so they identify B-cell lineage rather than endocrine granules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome causes watery diarrhea, flushing, and bronchospasm (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: urinary 5 HIAA is used to diagnose carcinoid tumors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Desmin and actin staining marks smooth muscle tumors such as leiomyoma, not neuroendocrine tumors.","E":"S100 and SOX10 staining marks Schwann cell tumors such as schwannoma, not neuroendocrine tumors.","A":"KIT (CD117) is detectable in normal Cajal cells and about 95% of gastric GISTs, and DOG1 is positive in about 95% of GISTs including epithelioid types, so this pair marks a stromal tumor.","B":"CD19 and CD20 are pan B-cell markers found on pre-B and mature B cells but not plasma cells, so they identify B-cell lineage rather than endocrine granules."}},{"id":"sdl09_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man had a partial gastrectomy with a gastrojejunal anastomosis decades ago and has chronic bile reflux. A gastric biopsy shows mucosal edema, vascular congestion, and corkscrew foveolar hyperplasia. Which diagnosis fits best?","choices":{"A":"Helicobacter gastritis","B":"Reactive gastropathy","C":"Autoimmune gastritis","D":"Menetrier disease","E":"MALT lymphoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile reflux injures the epithelium directly, and the reactive gastropathy pattern is edema, congestion, foveolar hyperplasia with corkscrew profiles, and few or no neutrophils. H. pylori gastritis has neutrophils and plasma cells. Autoimmune gastritis has a deep lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in the body. Ménétrier disease causes giant folds with protein loss. MALT lymphoma is a dense lymphoid infiltrate. Autoimmune atrophic gastritis damages oxyntic mucosa of the body and fundus with a deep gland-centered infiltrate, sparing the antrum, rather than producing edema and congestion from bile. Ménétrier disease shows irregular enlargement of the rugae in the body and fundus with glandular atrophy and excess TGF-α, not a bile-reflux injury pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, acute or reactive gastropathy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: previous gastric surgery raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: management of gastritis includes avoiding NSAIDs, tobacco, and alcohol (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 4 — gastric mucosal barrier and its disruption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"H. pylori gastritis has neutrophils and plasma cells.","C":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis damages oxyntic mucosa of the body and fundus with a deep gland-centered infiltrate, sparing the antrum, rather than producing edema and congestion from bile.","D":"Ménétrier disease shows irregular enlargement of the rugae in the body and fundus with glandular atrophy and excess TGF-α, not a bile-reflux injury pattern."}},{"id":"sdl09_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man has chronic dyspepsia, and a silver stain of his antral biopsy shows curved rods. Which inflammatory finding marks active disease in this gastritis?","choices":{"A":"Eosinophilic microabscesses","B":"Noncaseating granulomas","C":"Deep lymphoid gland injury","D":"Mast cell infiltrates","E":"Intraepithelial neutrophils"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In H. pylori gastritis, intraepithelial and lamina propria neutrophils form pit abscesses, alongside a superficial infiltrate of plasma cells, lymphocytes, and macrophages. Eosinophilic microabscesses and granulomas are not the described pattern. Deep gland centered lymphocytic injury is autoimmune gastritis. Mast cell infiltrates are not described. Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis, often an allergic disorder, not active H. pylori gastritis. Granulomatous gastritis is most often caused by Crohn disease, followed by sarcoidosis, and granulomas do not mark H. pylori activity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, H. pylori gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori is treated with triple therapy (amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI) or quadruple therapy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: H. pylori infection usually starts in the antrum and spreads to the body (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dense eosinophilic infiltrates define eosinophilic gastritis, often an allergic disorder, not active H. pylori gastritis.","B":"Granulomatous gastritis is most often caused by Crohn disease, followed by sarcoidosis, and granulomas do not mark H. pylori activity."}},{"id":"sdl09_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman with type 1 diabetes has a macrocytic anemia. Biopsy of the gastric body shows a deep gland centered infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells without prominent neutrophils, with goblet and Paneth cells in the mucosa. Which other change is expected in her oxyntic mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Foveolar hyperplasia","B":"Neutrophilic pit abscesses","C":"Spiral surface bacilli","D":"Loss of chief cells","E":"Signet ring cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune atrophic gastritis shows thinning of the body and fundic mucosa, a deep gland centered lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate, extensive loss of parietal and chief cells, intestinal metaplasia, and neuroendocrine cell hyperplasia. Foveolar hyperplasia is a reactive or Ménétrier change. Pit abscesses and spiral bacilli are H. pylori gastritis. Signet ring cells are diffuse carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 1, autoimmune atrophic gastritis morphology).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia raises the risk of gastric adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is linked to HLA predisposition (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a known posterior duodenal ulcer now has constant epigastric pain radiating to his back, and his serum lipase is raised. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Penetration into pancreas","B":"Free perforation","C":"Gastric outlet obstruction","D":"Acute ulcer hemorrhage","E":"Malignant transformation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Penetration of an ulcer into an adjacent structure such as the pancreas causes constant pain radiating to the back and can raise pancreatic enzymes. Free perforation causes peritonitis and free air. Outlet obstruction causes vomiting. Hemorrhage causes melena or hematemesis. Duodenal ulcers are usually benign. Perforation into the peritoneal cavity is a surgical emergency marked by free air under the diaphragm, whereas penetrating ulcers cause pain referred to the back. Bleeding is the most frequent ulcer complication, but it presents with blood loss rather than constant back pain and a raised lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (duodenal ulcers usually benign).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a posterior duodenal ulcer can bleed from the gastroduodenal artery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, ulcer hemorrhage). COMLEX: H. pylori and gastrinoma cause duodenal ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Duodenal ulcers are usually benign.","B":"Perforation into the peritoneal cavity is a surgical emergency marked by free air under the diaphragm, whereas penetrating ulcers cause pain referred to the back.","D":"Bleeding is the most frequent ulcer complication, but it presents with blood loss rather than constant back pain and a raised lipase."}},{"id":"sdl09_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has endoscopy for epigastric pain. A 1.5 cm round gastric lesion has clean, flat, nonelevated borders and a smooth base, and biopsy shows necrotic debris over granulation tissue and scar. Which lesion is this?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal type carcinoma","B":"Ulcerated stromal tumor","C":"Gastric MALT lymphoma","D":"Linitis plastica","E":"Benign peptic ulcer"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A peptic ulcer is classically a solitary, small, round to oval punched out defect with clean nonelevated borders and a smooth flat base, with zones of necrotic debris, neutrophils, granulation tissue, and scar. Intestinal type carcinoma forms bulky or ulcerated masses of malignant glands. A stromal tumor is a submucosal mass. MALT lymphoma is a lymphoid infiltrate. Linitis plastica thickens the whole wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, morphologic features of PUD; section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NSAIDs and H. pylori cause gastric ulcers (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, peptic ulcer disease). COMLEX: PUD is managed with a PPI or H2 antagonist (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute erosive gastritis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with years of ulcer disease near the pylorus now vomits undigested food several hours after meals, and a succussion splash is heard. Which change most likely causes her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","B":"Vagal motor neuron loss","C":"Twisting of the stomach","D":"Pyloric channel scarring","E":"Swallowed bezoar formation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Peptic ulcers can cause gastric outlet obstruction from edema or fibrous scarring. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is a congenital infant disorder. Vagal motor neuron loss, gastric volvulus, and bezoars are not ulcer complications in the lecture. First Aid describes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis as the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in infants at about 2 to 6 weeks old, not in an adult with long-standing peri-pyloric ulcer disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric outlet obstruction is a complication of gastric malignancy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: intractable pain is also a complication of PUD (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing, propulsion, and pyloric resistance","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Vagal motor neuron loss, gastric volvulus, and bezoars are not ulcer complications in the lecture.","A":"First Aid describes hypertrophic pyloric stenosis as the most common cause of gastric outlet obstruction in infants at about 2 to 6 weeks old, not in an adult with long-standing peri-pyloric ulcer disease."}},{"id":"sdl09_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old girl whose father had colectomy for hundreds of colon adenomas has many small polyps in her gastric body and fundus made of cystically dilated glands. Which gene is most likely mutated in her germline?","choices":{"A":"CDH1","B":"STK11","C":"APC","D":"SMAD4","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Syndromic fundic gland polyps arise with germline APC mutations in familial adenomatous polyposis, which is dominant and fits her father; they can develop dysplasia. CDH1 causes hereditary diffuse gastric cancer. STK11 and SMAD4 cause hamartomatous polyposis syndromes. KRAS is a somatic oncogene. Germline loss of CDH1, which encodes the adherens junction protein E-cadherin, accounts for up to about 30% of hereditary diffuse gastric cancers rather than fundic gland polyposis with colonic adenomas. Robbins links syndromic fundic gland polyps to germline APC (familial adenomatous polyposis) or MUTYH mutations, not KRAS. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps). The STK11 and SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture; Robbins links STK11 to Peutz-Jeghers syndrome and SMAD4 to juvenile polyposis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are the most common colonic polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, colonic polyps). COMLEX: fundic gland polyps can also arise with MUTYH mutations (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 1 — cardiac, fundic, and pyloric regions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The STK11 and SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture.","D":"SMAD4 syndromes are not in the lecture.","A":"Germline loss of CDH1, which encodes the adherens junction protein E-cadherin, accounts for up to about 30% of hereditary diffuse gastric cancers rather than fundic gland polyposis with colonic adenomas.","E":"Robbins links syndromic fundic gland polyps to germline APC (familial adenomatous polyposis) or MUTYH mutations, not KRAS."}},{"id":"sdl09_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has taken omeprazole for ten years. Endoscopy shows several small smooth polyps in the gastric body, and biopsy confirms the polyp type linked to his acid suppression. What is their malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Negligible cancer risk","B":"Risk similar to adenomas","C":"High risk needing gastrectomy","D":"Risk rising after 1 cm","E":"Frequent HER2 amplification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic and PPI associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk of malignant transformation, unlike syndromic polyps in FAP. Adenomas carry a high risk. Hyperplastic polyp risk rises in pedunculated polyps over 1 cm. HER2 amplification is a carcinoma change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, fundic gland polyps; hyperplastic polyps; gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPIs are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sporadic fundic gland polyps have increased with widespread PPI use (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 1 — drug classes for acid suppression","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic atrophic gastritis. Endoscopy shows a solitary 2.5 cm velvety antral polyp, and biopsy shows enlarged elongated hyperchromatic nuclei with pseudostratification and crowded glands. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Repeat biopsy in five years","B":"Complete endoscopic resection","C":"PPI therapy without resection","D":"Reassurance and no follow up","E":"Antibiotics without resection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is a gastric adenoma, a true neoplastic polyp with epithelial dysplasia that arises on chronic gastritis with atrophy and metaplasia; its risk of harboring or progressing to invasive carcinoma requires complete endoscopic resection and surveillance. Delayed biopsy, acid suppression, reassurance, or antibiotics leave the dysplastic lesion in place. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric adenomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric malignancy is diagnosed by endoscopy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: high grade dysplasia carries a substantial risk of progression to invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — Barrett esophagus and dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repeating the biopsy in five years leaves the dysplastic lesion in place.","C":"Delayed biopsy, acid suppression, reassurance, or antibiotics leave the dysplastic lesion in place."}},{"id":"sdl09_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has early satiety and weight loss. Imaging shows a diffusely thickened, rigid stomach wall without a discrete mass, and biopsy shows single discohesive cells infiltrating the wall. What mainly stiffens the wall?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal edema","B":"Smooth muscle hypertrophy","C":"Amyloid deposition","D":"Desmoplastic stroma","E":"Dense lymphoid infiltrate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma infiltrates as individual mucin filled cells and elicits an intense desmoplastic stromal reaction that stiffens and thickens the wall without a mass, giving the leather bottle appearance of linitis plastica. Edema, muscle hypertrophy, amyloid, and lymphoid infiltrates are not the described cause. Lymphoid aggregates in chronic H. pylori gastritis are induced MALT that can transform into lymphoma, a different process from the desmoplastic stroma of diffuse-type carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, morphologic patterns).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma has obscure borders and poorly cohesive cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: early satiety is especially common in diffuse type cancer (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Lymphoid aggregates in chronic H. pylori gastritis are induced MALT that can transform into lymphoma, a different process from the desmoplastic stroma of diffuse-type carcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl09_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man grew up eating salt preserved and smoked fish and has smoked for decades. He now has a bulky antral adenocarcinoma of the intestinal type. Which dietary carcinogen class is most implicated?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Acrylamide","C":"Nitrosamines","D":"Benzene","E":"Vinyl chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intestinal type gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors include chronic H. pylori gastritis, a high salt diet, N nitroso compounds or nitrosamines, and smoking. Aflatoxin is linked to liver cancer. Acrylamide, benzene, and vinyl chloride are not the listed gastric risk factors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, risk factors and precursor lesions); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (nitrosamines, smoked and salt preserved foods).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric cancer metastasizes to the periumbilical node as a Sister Mary Joseph nodule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric malignancy). COMLEX: tobacco use raises gastric cancer risk (Bootcamp Gastroenterology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal cancer risk factors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has bilateral solid ovarian masses, and the ovarian tissue contains mucin filled cells with nuclei pushed to the periphery. Her stomach wall is thickened. Which term describes the ovarian lesions?","choices":{"A":"Brenner tumor","B":"Krukenberg tumor","C":"Dysgerminoma","D":"Mature teratoma","E":"Granulosa cell tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metastasis of gastric signet ring cell carcinoma to both ovaries is a Krukenberg tumor. Brenner tumors, dysgerminomas, teratomas, and granulosa cell tumors are primary ovarian tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, metastatic patterns); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (Krukenberg tumor). The primary ovarian tumor types are not in the lecture.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma occurs at a younger age (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, diffuse type gastric adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: gastric cancer can spread to the pouch of Douglas (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 5 — esophageal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman has early stage gastric marginal zone B cell lymphoma arising in mucosa infected with H. pylori. Which first treatment can produce complete remission?","choices":{"A":"Trastuzumab therapy","B":"Total gastrectomy","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Octreotide therapy","E":"Radiation therapy first"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Early stage H. pylori dependent MALT lymphomas frequently achieve complete remission after antibiotic eradication of H. pylori, because chronic antigen stimulation drives them. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Gastrectomy is not first line. Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for neuroendocrine tumors. Radiation is reserved for resistant tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (MALT lymphoma: eradication therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: quadruple therapy for H. pylori uses metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori infection). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma presents with dyspepsia, epigastric pain, or GI bleeding (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with autoimmune gastritis has several small yellow submucosal nodules in the gastric body. Biopsy shows uniform cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and staining confirms neuroendocrine origin. Which finding best predicts an indolent course?","choices":{"A":"Low Ki 67 index","B":"TP53 and RB1 loss","C":"High mitotic count","D":"Liver metastases","E":"KIT gene mutation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Well differentiated neuroendocrine tumors have a low Ki 67 proliferative index, and small gastric NETs associated with autoimmune gastritis have an indolent course managed by endoscopic resection. High Ki 67 with TP53 and RB1 mutations marks poorly differentiated carcinoma. Liver metastases signal aggressive disease and carcinoid syndrome. KIT mutations drive GIST. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about one third of carcinoid tumors metastasize, usually to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor). COMLEX: carcinoid tumors are managed with resection or a somatostatin analog such as octreotide (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, carcinoid tumor).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl09_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has melena. CT shows a 9 cm gastric submucosal mass with liver lesions, and biopsy shows spindle cells that stain strongly for CD117 and DOG1. Which drug is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab","B":"Trastuzumab","C":"Octreotide","D":"Cisplatin","E":"Imatinib"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Unresectable or metastatic GISTs with KIT or PDGFRA mutations are treated with tyrosine kinase inhibitors such as imatinib. Rituximab targets CD20 on B cells. Trastuzumab targets HER2. Octreotide treats neuroendocrine tumors. Cisplatin is not the described therapy. Cisplatin is a platinum analog that damages DNA like an alkylating agent, whereas imatinib targets the c-KIT tyrosine kinase expressed by GISTs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastrointestinal stromal tumors).","boardPrep":"Step 1: c KIT and PDGFRA mutations cause aberrant receptor tyrosine kinase activity in GIST (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GIST). COMLEX: GIST malignant risk correlates with size, mitotic count, and location (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cisplatin is a platinum analog that damages DNA like an alkylating agent, whereas imatinib targets the c-KIT tyrosine kinase expressed by GISTs.","A":"Rituximab is an anti-CD20 agent.","B":"Trastuzumab is a HER2 antibody.","C":"Octreotide is a somatostatin analog."}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate acute/reactive gastropathy, Helicobacter Pylori gastritis, and autoimmune gastritis based on their etiologies, pathogenesis, anatomic distribution, morphologic features, clinical manifestations, and major complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man who grew up where H. pylori infection is common has had dyspepsia for decades. Biopsies from both the antrum and body show chronic inflammation with lymphoid aggregates, glandular atrophy, and patches of goblet and Paneth cells. Antiparietal cell antibodies are absent, and serum B12 is normal. Which neoplasm is he at greatest risk of developing from this mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Fundic gland polyp with dysplasia","B":"Intestinal type adenocarcinoma","C":"Diffuse type signet ring carcinoma","D":"Squamous cell carcinoma of the cardia","E":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long-standing H. pylori infection that spreads from the antrum to the body produces multifocal atrophic gastritis with intestinal metaplasia, the precursor sequence for intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma. Fundic gland polyps are linked to PPI use or FAP and have no relation to atrophic gastritis. Diffuse-type carcinoma has no identified precursor lesion and arises de novo, often with CDH1 loss. The stomach has no squamous lining, and squamous carcinoma arises in the esophagus. Stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal in the wall and are not linked to mucosal atrophy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, sections 1, 3 and 4).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori gastritis begins in the antrum and can progress to pangastritis with atrophy and intestinal metaplasia, raising adenocarcinoma and MALT lymphoma risk (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 9). COMLEX: autoimmune gastritis is distinguished by body-restricted atrophy, antiparietal antibodies and B12 deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — gastric epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fundic gland polyps are linked to PPI use","C":"Diffuse-type carcinoma has no identified precursor lesion and","D":"The stomach has no squamous lining, and squamous","E":"Stromal tumors arise from interstitial cells of Cajal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pathogenesis, clinical and morphological features, and complications of gastritis, including the roles of H. pylori, and NSAIDs in PUD, and gastric acid hypersecretion in Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 49-year-old man who smokes and takes ibuprofen daily for back pain has sudden, severe epigastric pain that spreads across his abdomen and to his right shoulder. His abdomen is rigid, and an upright chest radiograph shows air under the diaphragm. Which ulcer location most likely explains this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Narrow channel of the pylorus","B":"Mucosa of the proximal jejunum","C":"Lesser curvature of the gastric body","D":"Anterior wall of the duodenal bulb","E":"Posterior duodenum near the pancreas"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Air under the diaphragm means perforation into the peritoneal cavity, and anterior duodenal ulcers are the ones that perforate into the abdominal cavity, with shoulder pain referred from diaphragmatic irritation. Pyloric channel ulcers typically cause gastric outlet obstruction from edema and scarring. Jejunal ulcers suggest Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and are not the usual source of perforation in an NSAID user. Lesser curvature ulcers characteristically bleed from the left gastric artery. Posterior duodenal ulcers tend to bleed from the gastroduodenal artery or penetrate the pancreas rather than release free air. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, complications of PUD); First Aid 2025 (ulcer complications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anterior duodenal ulcers perforate, posterior duodenal ulcers bleed from the gastroduodenal artery, and lesser curvature ulcers bleed from the left gastric artery (First Aid 2025). COMLEX: free air with a rigid abdomen is a surgical emergency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, gastrinoma, glucagonoma, and VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pyloric channel ulcers typically cause gastric outlet obstruction","B":"Jejunal ulcers suggest Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and are not","C":"Lesser curvature ulcers characteristically bleed from the left","E":"Posterior duodenal ulcers tend to bleed from the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish the major types of gastric polyps and pecursor epithelial lesions based on their pathogenesis, morphologic features, clinical associations, and risk for malignant transformation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with untreated H. pylori gastritis who has also taken omeprazole for 6 years has endoscopy showing a 1.6 cm pedunculated antral polyp and a few 3 mm smooth polyps in the gastric body. The antral polyp shows elongated corkscrew foveolar glands in an edematous, inflamed lamina propria. The body polyps show cystically dilated glands lined by parietal and chief cells with no inflammation. Which lesion carries the greater risk of dysplasia?","choices":{"A":"The body polyps, because there are several","B":"The antral polyp, given its size and stalk","C":"Neither, because both are reactive lesions","D":"Both equally, since both arose from gastritis","E":"The body polyps, because she takes omeprazole"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The antral lesion is a hyperplastic polyp, whose dysplasia risk rises significantly when it is pedunculated and larger than 1 cm, while the body lesions are fundic gland polyps related to PPI use, which carry no significant risk of malignant change. Being several in number does not raise the risk of these PPI-associated fundic gland polyps. Hyperplastic polyps are reactive, but their dysplasia risk of 1% to 20% rises in pedunculated polyps over 1 cm. Both did not arise from gastritis, since the body polyps reflect acid suppression rather than inflammation. PPI-associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk of malignant transformation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 3, gastric polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps make up to 75% of gastric polyps and their risk rises with size over 1 cm, while sporadic fundic gland polyps carry little risk (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 9). COMLEX: gastric adenomas carry the highest cancer risk and need complete resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 10 Obj 2 — H. pylori eradication regimens","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Being several in number does not raise the","C":"Hyperplastic polyps are reactive, but their dysplasia risk","D":"Both did not arise from gastritis, since the","E":"PPI-associated fundic gland polyps carry no significant risk"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, precursor lesions, morphologic patterns, and clinical features of gastric adenocarcinoma, including the relationship of chronic gastritis, intestinal metaplasia, dysplasia and injerited or acquired molecular alteration to tumor development.","batch":3,"stem":"A 32-year-old man whose mother and maternal uncle died of stomach cancer before age 45 has early satiety and weight loss. Endoscopy shows a stiff gastric wall that barely distends with air and no discrete mass. Biopsy shows scattered single cells with large mucin vacuoles pushing their nuclei aside, infiltrating a fibrotic wall. Loss of which function best explains how these cells spread one by one?","choices":{"A":"Cell to cell adhesion via E-cadherin","B":"Cell cycle arrest controlled by TP53","C":"Viral control of epithelial growth","D":"DNA mismatch repair by MLH1","E":"Wnt suppression by the APC protein"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Signet ring cells infiltrating a rigid, linitis plastica stomach in a young patient with this family history point to hereditary diffuse gastric cancer from loss of CDH1, which encodes the adherens junction protein E-cadherin, so the cells lose cohesion and invade singly. TP53 mutations characterize the chromosomal instability subtype of intestinal-type carcinoma. Viral control of growth describes EBV-associated carcinoma, a separate intestinal-type molecular subtype. DNA mismatch repair loss underlies the microsatellite unstable subtype of intestinal-type carcinoma. APC and other Wnt pathway mutations belong to the chromosomal instability subtype of intestinal-type carcinoma, which forms cohesive glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 4, gastric adenocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diffuse gastric cancer shows signet ring cells and linitis plastica from CDH1 loss, while intestinal-type cancer forms glands after atrophy and metaplasia (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 9). COMLEX: early satiety with a rigid stomach that will not distend suggests diffuse-type gastric cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — molecular progression in Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"TP53 mutations characterize the chromosomal instability subtype of","C":"Viral control of growth describes EBV-associated carcinoma, a","D":"DNA mismatch repair loss underlies the microsatellite unstable","E":"APC and other Wnt pathway mutations belong to"}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with dyspepsia has gastric biopsies showing a dense infiltrate of small B cells, positive for CD20 and negative for CD5 and CD10, that invade glands to form lymphoepithelial lesions. H. pylori is present. Eight months after the organism has been eradicated, repeat biopsies show the same lymphoma, and cytogenetics show t(11;18)(q21;q21). Which conclusion is best supported?","choices":{"A":"The translocation forms a BCR ABL1 fusion","B":"It has become a gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Imatinib will target the driving kinase","D":"A second antibiotic course will clear it","E":"Chemotherapy or radiation will be needed"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This is gastric MALT lymphoma, and early H. pylori dependent tumors often regress after eradication, but tumors carrying t(11;18), which forms the API2::MALT1 fusion protein, persist and need chemotherapy or radiation. BCR::ABL1 comes from t(9;22) in chronic myeloid leukemia, whereas t(11;18) forms API2::MALT1. The cells are still B cells forming lymphoepithelial lesions, not malignant glands. Imatinib targets the KIT and PDGFRA kinases of stromal tumors and does not treat this lymphoma. Lymphomas with t(11;18) no longer depend on H. pylori antigen stimulation, so more antibiotics are unlikely to help. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, gastric lymphomas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric MALT lymphoma is CD20 positive and CD5 and CD10 negative, and t(11;18) forms API2::MALT1 (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 9). COMLEX: MALT lymphoma that persists after H. pylori eradication needs chemotherapy or radiation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — mucosal lymphoid tissue","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BCR::ABL1 comes from t(9;22) in chronic myeloid leukemia,","B":"The cells are still B cells forming lymphoepithelial","C":"Imatinib targets the KIT and PDGFRA kinases of","D":"Lymphomas with t(11;18) no longer depend on H."}},{"id":"trial_sdl09_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate gastric lymphomas, neuroendocrine tumors, and gastrointestinal stromal tumors based on their pathogenesis, molecular or immunophenotypic features, morphology, clinical associations and principles of diagnosis and management.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has kidney stones from parathyroid hyperplasia and recurrent jejunal ulcers, and his fasting gastrin is 1,400 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL). Endoscopy shows several small yellow-tan nodules in the oxyntic mucosa. Biopsy shows uniform cells in nests and trabeculae with salt-and-pepper chromatin that stain for chromogranin A. What most likely drives the growth of these nodules?","choices":{"A":"Activating KIT mutation in Cajal cells","B":"Excess somatostatin from D cells","C":"Gastrin stimulating ECL cell growth","D":"Loss of E-cadherin in signet ring cells","E":"Chronic H. pylori antigen stimulation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperparathyroidism with a gastrinoma suggests MEN1, and the very high gastrin is trophic to the oxyntic mucosa, so gastric neuroendocrine tumors arise from ECL cells under hypergastrinemia and show salt-and-pepper chromatin and chromogranin staining. Activating KIT mutations drive stromal tumors made of spindle or epithelioid cells, not chromogranin-positive nests. Excess somatostatin inhibits gastrin and would not drive ECL cell growth. Loss of E-cadherin produces diffuse-type carcinoma of discohesive signet ring cells. Chronic H. pylori antigen stimulation drives MALT lymphoma, a B-cell tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 5, neuroendocrine tumors; SDL 5, section 3); First Aid 2025 (MEN1).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric neuroendocrine tumors arise from ECL cells driven by hypergastrinemia from autoimmune gastritis, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome in MEN1, or long-term PPI use (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 9). COMLEX: a gastrinoma with hyperparathyroidism should prompt screening for MEN1.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric pacemaking and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Activating KIT mutations drive stromal tumors made of","B":"Excess somatostatin inhibits gastrin and would not drive","D":"Loss of E-cadherin produces diffuse-type carcinoma of discohesive","E":"Chronic H. pylori antigen stimulation drives MALT lymphoma,"}}]},{"sdlNumber":10,"title":"SDL 10 — Pharmacology of acid-peptic diseases, antiemetics, and variceal hemorrhage","questions":[{"id":"sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"How long does acid suppression from a single proton pump inhibitor dose last despite a plasma half life of about 1.5 hours?","choices":{"A":"About 90 minutes","B":"About 6 hours","C":"About 3 days","D":"About 2 weeks","E":"About 24 hours"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Proton pump inhibitors have a short plasma half life of about 1.5 hours but act for about 24 hours because they bind the H+/K+ ATPase covalently and irreversibly, so acid secretion returns only as new pumps are made. The shorter and longer durations do not match the lecture. About 90 minutes is close to the plasma half life of 1.5 hours, but acid suppression lasts about 24 hours because the PPI binds the pump irreversibly. About 6 hours is too short. About 3 days is too long. About 2 weeks is far too long. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly inhibit the H+/K+ ATPase on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"About 90 minutes is close to the plasma half life of 1.5 hours, but acid suppression lasts about 24 hours because the PPI binds the pump irreversibly.","B":"About 6 hours is too short.","C":"About 3 days is too long.","D":"About 2 weeks is far too long."}},{"id":"sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antisecretory drug class requires a dose reduction in renal insufficiency?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Alginate raft formers","C":"H2 receptor antagonists","D":"Calcium carbonate antacids","E":"Dopamine D2 antagonists"},"correct":"C","explanation":"H2 receptor antagonists are cleared by hepatic metabolism and renal secretion, so their dose is reduced in renal insufficiency. Proton pump inhibitors are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal adjustment. Alginates and antacids act locally, although magnesium and aluminum accumulate in renal failure. Dopamine D2 antagonists are prokinetics rather than antisecretory drugs. Katzung notes PPIs have negligible renal clearance so dose reduction is not needed in renal insufficiency, whereas H2 antagonists require it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist pharmacokinetics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H2 antagonists reversibly inhibit H2 receptors on parietal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 antagonists are used for gastritis, PUD, GERD, and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Alginates and antacids act locally, although magnesium and aluminum accumulate in renal failure.","A":"Katzung notes PPIs have negligible renal clearance so dose reduction is not needed in renal insufficiency, whereas H2 antagonists require it."}},{"id":"sdl10_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":1,"stem":"How far apart should antacid doses be spaced from tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, or iron?","choices":{"A":"Ten minutes","B":"Six hours","C":"Twelve hours","D":"Two hours","E":"One full day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antacids bind other drugs such as tetracyclines, fluoroquinolones, and iron, so the lecture calls for a 2 hour separation. Ten minutes falls short of that separation, and six hours, twelve hours, or a full day are longer than the lecture requires. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aluminum hydroxide causes constipation and hypophosphatemia, while magnesium hydroxide causes diarrhea (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: calcium carbonate can cause milk alkali syndrome and rebound acidity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is bismuth quadruple therapy now recommended as first line H. pylori treatment?","choices":{"A":"Clarithromycin resistance","B":"Amoxicillin allergy rates","C":"Shorter treatment course","D":"Fewer daily pill doses","E":"Metronidazole resistance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bismuth quadruple therapy is first line because clarithromycin resistance is rising, and triple therapy is now limited to regions where resistance is below 15 percent. The quadruple regimen lasts 10 to 14 days with several daily doses, so it is not shorter or simpler, and it contains metronidazole. Amoxicillin allergy is not the stated reason, and the bismuth regimen (PPI, bismuth, metronidazole, tetracycline) contains no amoxicillin. Katzung states quadruple therapy is now first-line because of treatment failures from rising clarithromycin resistance, not amoxicillin allergy rates. Katzung lists both the older triple regimen and the current quadruple regimens as 14-day courses, so quadruple therapy is not shorter. Katzung gives the bismuth quadruple regimen with each agent four times daily, so it does not reduce daily pill doses. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, H. pylori eradication regimens).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori quadruple therapy combines metronidazole, tetracycline, bismuth, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: bismuth also treats traveler diarrhea and can darken the stool (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antidiarrheal medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 1 — H. pylori gastritis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The quadruple regimen lasts 10 to 14 days with several daily doses, so it is not shorter or simpler, and it contains metronidazole.","B":"Katzung states quadruple therapy is now first-line because of treatment failures from rising clarithromycin resistance, not amoxicillin allergy rates.","C":"Katzung lists both the older triple regimen and the current quadruple regimens as 14-day courses, so quadruple therapy is not shorter.","D":"Katzung gives the bismuth quadruple regimen with each agent four times daily, so it does not reduce daily pill doses."}},{"id":"sdl10_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"How long must a proton pump inhibitor be held before a test that confirms H. pylori eradication?","choices":{"A":"Two days","B":"Six hours","C":"Six months","D":"Two weeks","E":"Twelve hours"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PPIs must be held for 2 weeks before a urea breath test, fecal antigen test, or biopsy to avoid false negative results, and testing is done at least 4 weeks after the antibiotics end. Holds of six hours, twelve hours, or two days are too short to avoid false negative results, and six months is far longer than the lecture's 2 weeks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bismuth and sucralfate bind the gastric mucosa and raise bicarbonate secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: sucralfate requires an acidic environment to work (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Holds of six hours, twelve hours, or two days are too short.","B":"Holds of six hours are too short.","C":"Six months is far longer than the 2 weeks PPI therapy should be stopped before eradication testing.","E":"Twelve hours are too short."}},{"id":"sdl10_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":1,"stem":"What sustained intragastric pH threshold prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clot formation?","choices":{"A":"Below pH 2","B":"Above pH 6","C":"Near pH 3","D":"Near pH 4","E":"Above pH 10"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sustained elevation of intragastric pH above 6.0 prevents acid inactivation of pepsin and preserves clotting by optimizing platelet aggregation and coagulation. Lower values still allow clot breakdown, and a pH above 10 is not a lecture target. Katzung states that an intragastric pH higher than 6 is believed to enhance coagulation and platelet aggregation in bleeding ulcers, not a threshold of pH 10. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, reduction of recurrent ulcer bleeding risk).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors raise the risk of C. difficile infection and aspiration pneumonia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: proton pump inhibitors are used for GI bleeding and stress ulcer prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — peptic ulcer disease and its complications","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A sustained pH below 2 still lets pepsin break clot down.","C":"A sustained pH near 3 still allows clot breakdown.","D":"A sustained pH near 4 still allows clot breakdown.","E":"Katzung states that an intragastric pH higher than 6 is believed to enhance coagulation and platelet aggregation in bleeding ulcers, not a threshold of pH 10."}},{"id":"sdl10_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptors in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and area postrema do aprepitant and fosaprepitant block?","choices":{"A":"Substance P receptors","B":"Dopamine D2 receptors","C":"Muscarinic M1 receptors","D":"Histamine H1 receptors","E":"Cannabinoid CB1 receptors"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aprepitant and fosaprepitant cross the blood brain barrier and block central NK1 receptors, the receptors for substance P. Dopamine D2 receptors are blocked by prochlorperazine and metoclopramide. M1 and H1 receptors are blocked by scopolamine and the antihistamines. Dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: NK1 antagonists such as aprepitant act centrally and treat chemotherapy induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: NK1 antagonists are combined with a 5 HT3 antagonist and dexamethasone for highly emetogenic chemotherapy (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"What property makes palonosetron better than older agents for delayed chemotherapy induced emesis?","choices":{"A":"Extra NK1 blockade","B":"Central CB1 agonism","C":"Long 40 hour half life","D":"Added D2 receptor blockade","E":"Peripheral restriction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Palonosetron is a second generation 5 HT3 antagonist with higher receptor affinity and a 40 hour half life, which makes it superior for delayed CINV. It does not block NK1 or D2 receptors or act at CB1 receptors, and it acts both peripherally and centrally. Katzung credits palonosetron with greater 5-HT3 receptor affinity and a 40-hour half-life versus 4 to 9 hours for first-generation agents, not added NK1 blockade. Katzung credits palonosetron with greater 5-HT3 receptor affinity and a 40-hour half-life versus 4 to 9 hours for first-generation agents, not added D2 receptor blockade. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, serotonin 5 HT3 receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5 HT3 antagonists cause QT prolongation, headache, and serotonin syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: 5 HT3 antagonists are used for chemotherapy, postoperative, and radiation induced emesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"It does not block NK1 or D2 receptors or act at CB1 receptors, and it acts both peripherally and centrally.","A":"Palonosetron does not add NK1-receptor blockade.","D":"Katzung credits palonosetron with greater 5-HT3 receptor affinity and a 40-hour half-life versus 4 to 9 hours for first-generation agents, not added D2 receptor blockade."}},{"id":"sdl10_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":1,"stem":"Aprepitant inhibits CYP3A4. What change is required in the dexamethasone given with it?","choices":{"A":"Double the dose","B":"Reduce dose by half","C":"Give it intravenously","D":"Stop dexamethasone","E":"Give it at bedtime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Aprepitant and netupitant inhibit CYP3A4 and raise dexamethasone levels, so the dexamethasone dose is reduced by 50 percent. Raising the dose, changing the route or timing, and stopping the steroid are not what the lecture describes, because dexamethasone potentiates NK1 antagonists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, NK1 adverse effects and interactions).","boardPrep":"Step 1: aprepitant also lowers the warfarin INR (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: dexamethasone used as an antiemetic can cause insomnia and hyperglycemia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Double the dose is not part of that answer.","C":"Give it intravenously is not part of that answer.","D":"Stopping dexamethasone is not the adjustment used for the CYP3A4 interaction with aprepitant.","E":"Give it at bedtime is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl10_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"How does octreotide reduce portal inflow in acute variceal bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Inhibits glucagon release","B":"Activates V1a receptors","C":"Blocks beta 1 receptors","D":"Releases nitric oxide","E":"Directly constricts arterioles"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Somatostatin and octreotide cause selective splanchnic vasoconstriction by inhibiting the release of glucagon and other vasodilatory peptides, which lowers portal inflow without systemic vasoconstriction. Vasopressin and terlipressin activate V1a receptors. Beta 1 blockade is how nonselective beta blockers reduce cardiac output. Nitric oxide is a splanchnic vasodilator that worsens portal hypertension. Octreotide's vasoconstriction is indirect; direct arteriolar constriction through vascular smooth muscle receptors is the vasopressin and terlipressin mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, somatostatin and octreotide mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension dilates submucosal portocaval veins that form esophageal varices (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices). COMLEX: variceal bleeding begins abruptly with hematemesis or melena and can cause hemorrhagic shock (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophageal varices).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug must be given with vasopressin in variceal bleeding to offset its systemic and coronary effects?","choices":{"A":"Phenylephrine","B":"Nitroglycerin","C":"Propranolol","D":"Terlipressin","E":"Norepinephrine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vasopressin causes severe systemic and coronary vasoconstriction with hypertension and myocardial ischemia, so it requires co administration of IV nitroglycerin. Phenylephrine and norepinephrine add vasoconstriction. Propranolol is contraindicated during active bleeding. Terlipressin is a V1 agonist with greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects, not a drug that offsets vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vasopressin also causes free water retention and hyponatremia (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: terlipressin has greater V1 selectivity and fewer side effects than vasopressin (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":1,"stem":"To what resting heart rate is propranolol titrated in chronic variceal management?","choices":{"A":"40 to 45 bpm","B":"70 to 75 bpm","C":"80 to 85 bpm","D":"90 to 95 bpm","E":"55 to 60 bpm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dose is titrated to reduce the resting heart rate by 25 percent or to 55 to 60 bpm. Lower targets risk symptomatic bradycardia, and higher ones give too little portal pressure reduction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TIPS is a management option for portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: lactulose and rifaximin treat hepatic encephalopathy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A resting heart rate of 40 to 45 bpm risks symptomatic bradycardia.","B":"70 to 75 bpm gives too little portal-pressure reduction.","C":"80 to 85 bpm gives too little portal-pressure reduction.","D":"90 to 95 bpm gives too little portal-pressure reduction."}},{"id":"sdl10_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has heartburn four times a week for six months. Endoscopy shows erosive esophagitis without Barrett mucosa. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Famotidine as needed","B":"Metoclopramide daily","C":"Antacids as needed","D":"Alginate after meals","E":"Daily omeprazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Frequent heartburn on 3 or more days a week and erosive esophagitis call for a proton pump inhibitor, which heals 85 to 90 percent of erosive esophagitis when taken 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast. H2 blockers heal less than half and suit mild infrequent symptoms. Metoclopramide is not recommended as monotherapy. Antacids and alginates give only short temporary relief. Katzung states that long-term daily full- or half-dose PPI maintenance is usually needed for erosive esophagitis, PPIs being the most effective agents for erosive reflux disease, rather than daily metoclopramide. Katzung states that long-term daily full- or half-dose PPI maintenance is usually needed for erosive esophagitis, PPIs being the most effective agents for erosive reflux disease, rather than as-needed antacids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI and H2 blocker indications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors reduce absorption of vitamin B12, iron, calcium, and magnesium and can cause acute interstitial nephritis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: GERD is managed with lifestyle changes and a proton pump inhibitor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, esophagus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 3 — reflux injury and Barrett esophagus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"H2 blockers heal less than half and suit mild infrequent symptoms.","D":"Alginate after meals gives only brief relief and does not heal erosive esophagitis.","B":"Metoclopramide is not recommended as monotherapy.","C":"Antacids and alginates give only short temporary relief."}},{"id":"sdl10_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man takes an over the counter drug for heartburn. Over three months he develops tender breast enlargement, and his warfarin effect rises. Which drug is he most likely taking?","choices":{"A":"Pantoprazole","B":"Famotidine","C":"Cimetidine","D":"Sucralfate","E":"Nizatidine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cimetidine inhibits androgen binding and raises prolactin, causing gynecomastia, impotence, and galactorrhea, and it inhibits CYP1A2, 2C9, 2D6, and 3A4, which raises warfarin levels. Famotidine and nizatidine lack these effects. Pantoprazole is a PPI without antiandrogen effects. Sucralfate is a local mucosal protectant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, H2 receptor antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cimetidine causes CYP450 inhibition, antiandrogenic effects, and CNS effects (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: H2 blockers can cause confusion in elderly or ICU patients with renal or hepatic impairment (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with long standing diabetes has persistent regurgitation and delayed gastric emptying despite a proton pump inhibitor. An adjunct drug is added that blocks central and peripheral dopamine receptors. Which adverse effect most limits its long term use?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic diarrhea","B":"Tardive dyskinesia","C":"Milk alkali syndrome","D":"Interstitial nephritis","E":"Hypomagnesemia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist that crosses the blood brain barrier, and central D2 blockade causes extrapyramidal effects including dystonia, parkinsonism, and tardive dyskinesia. Osmotic diarrhea is from magnesium antacids. Milk alkali syndrome is from excess calcium. Interstitial nephritis and hypomagnesemia are long term PPI risks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, prokinetic agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide raises LES tone and gastric contractions and causes hyperprolactinemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide is contraindicated in small bowel obstruction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 5 — the lower esophageal sphincter","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman who is 12 weeks pregnant has rheumatoid arthritis and a past bleeding ulcer. She must continue an NSAID and needs ulcer prophylaxis. Which drug should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Omeprazole","B":"Aluminum hydroxide","C":"Pantoprazole","D":"Misoprostol","E":"Sucralfate"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Misoprostol is a synthetic PGE1 analog that raises mucus and bicarbonate secretion and mucosal blood flow, but it is an abortifacient and is contraindicated in pregnancy, and it also causes dose dependent cramping and diarrhea. A once daily PPI is the alternative cotherapy for high risk NSAID users. Sucralfate acts on the mucosa without abortifacient effects. Aluminum hydroxide is an antacid that neutralizes gastric acid locally and is not an abortifacient, so it does not need to be avoided in pregnancy. Sucralfate is not absorbed and is virtually devoid of systemic adverse effects; constipation from its aluminum salt occurs in about 2% of patients. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, NSAID ulcer prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: misoprostol raises bicarbonate and mucus, lowers parietal cell acid secretion, and raises gastric blood flow (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: misoprostol is used for NSAID induced PUD prophylaxis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Omeprazole is a once-daily proton-pump inhibitor used as NSAID cotherapy, not a drug that must be avoided in pregnancy.","C":"Pantoprazole is a once-daily proton-pump inhibitor used as NSAID cotherapy, not a drug that must be avoided in pregnancy.","E":"Sucralfate is not absorbed and is virtually devoid of systemic adverse effects; constipation from its aluminum salt occurs in about 2% of patients.","B":"Aluminum hydroxide is an antacid that neutralizes gastric acid locally and is not an abortifacient, so it does not need to be avoided in pregnancy."}},{"id":"sdl10_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a duodenal ulcer and a positive urea breath test starts a regimen of two antimicrobials, bismuth, and a twice daily acid suppressant. How does the acid suppressant improve eradication?","choices":{"A":"Antibiotic stability","B":"Thicker protective mucus","C":"Bacterial urease blockade","D":"Faster gastric emptying","E":"Stronger IgA secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PPIs suppress acid and raise gastric pH, which increases antibiotic stability and lowers the minimal inhibitory concentration against H. pylori. They do not thicken mucus, block urease directly, speed emptying, or raise IgA. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, role of PPI in eradication).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H. pylori triple therapy combines amoxicillin, clarithromycin, and a PPI (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, H. pylori). COMLEX: after the antibiotics, the PPI is continued once daily for 4 to 6 weeks to heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — H. pylori and NSAIDs in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has hematemesis. Endoscopy shows a duodenal ulcer with a visible vessel that is treated endoscopically. Which drug regimen best lowers his risk of rebleeding?","choices":{"A":"Oral famotidine at bedtime","B":"IV PPI bolus and infusion","C":"IV octreotide infusion","D":"Oral sucralfate slurry","E":"IV vasopressin infusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ulcers with high risk stigmata such as active bleeding, a visible vessel, or an adherent clot are treated with an IV PPI bolus followed by a continuous infusion for 72 hours, or high dose oral PPI. Bedtime famotidine and sucralfate give too little acid suppression. Octreotide and vasopressin are vasoactive drugs for variceal bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, high dose or IV PPI therapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peptic ulcer disease is a cause of upper GI bleeding along with varices and Mallory Weiss tears (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, upper GI bleeding). COMLEX: a duodenal ulcer can erode the gastroduodenal artery (lecture SDL 9).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 9 Obj 2 — complications of peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who is 10 weeks pregnant has nausea and vomiting most mornings. Diet changes have not helped. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Meclizine by mouth","B":"Dronabinol by mouth","C":"Prochlorperazine suppository","D":"Aprepitant with dexamethasone","E":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Doxylamine with pyridoxine is the H1 antihistamine combination the lecture names for nausea and vomiting of pregnancy. Meclizine is for motion sickness and vertigo. Dronabinol is second line for refractory CINV. Prochlorperazine is a broad spectrum D2 antagonist with extrapyramidal effects. Aprepitant with dexamethasone is for highly emetogenic CINV. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics cause sedation, dry mouth, blurred vision, and urinary retention (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: prochlorperazine blocks D2, muscarinic, and histamine receptors and causes extrapyramidal symptoms (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is leaving on a week long sailing trip and became very nauseated on his last boat trip. Which option best prevents his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Scopolamine patch","B":"Ondansetron tablets","C":"Aprepitant capsules","D":"Dexamethasone tablets","E":"Dronabinol capsules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Motion sickness is prevented by blocking M1 and H1 receptors in the vestibular apparatus and solitary nucleus, as with a transdermal scopolamine patch, meclizine, or dimenhydrinate. 5 HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are ineffective for motion sickness. Aprepitant is for CINV, and dexamethasone potentiates other antiemetics in CINV and PONV. Dronabinol is for refractory CINV and AIDS cachexia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, H1 antihistamines and anticholinergics).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dronabinol and nabilone are CB1 agonists that can cause euphoria, sedation, and orthostatic hypotension (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: cannabinoids also stimulate appetite in AIDS cachexia (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic innervation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man taking sertraline for depression receives repeated doses of ondansetron after surgery. He becomes agitated with tremor, clonus, sweating, and a temperature of 39.2 °C. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonia","B":"Serotonin syndrome","C":"Neuroleptic malignant syndrome","D":"Anticholinergic toxicity","E":"Thyroid storm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"5 HT3 antagonists carry a risk of serotonin syndrome when combined with SSRIs or SNRIs, and clonus, tremor, agitation, and fever fit it. Acute dystonia is an extrapyramidal effect of D2 blockers. Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows dopamine blockade and causes rigidity rather than clonus. Anticholinergic toxicity causes dry skin rather than sweating. Thyroid storm is not linked to these drugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, 5 HT3 antagonist adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide blocks D2 and 5 HT3 receptors centrally and stimulates 5 HT4 receptors peripherally (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications). COMLEX: metoclopramide can cause neuroleptic malignant syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, antiemetic medications).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 1 — GI hormones, paracrines, and neurocrines","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with alcohol related cirrhosis has hematemesis, a pulse of 118/min, and a blood pressure of 88/54 mm Hg. An infusion of octreotide is started before endoscopy. Why is his usual propranolol held?","choices":{"A":"Blunts compensatory reflexes","B":"Raises portal vein inflow","C":"Worsens hepatic encephalopathy","D":"Interacts with octreotide","E":"Constricts coronary arteries"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers are contraindicated in acute active variceal bleeding because they blunt the compensatory cardiovascular reflexes needed during hemorrhagic shock. They lower rather than raise portal inflow. Encephalopathy and an octreotide interaction are not the reason given. Coronary vasoconstriction is a vasopressin effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, contraindications and adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the esophageal portocaval anastomosis joins the left gastric vein to the distal esophageal veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: octreotide is given immediately for suspected variceal bleeding and continued for 3 to 5 days (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with cirrhosis and medium varices that have never bled starts nadolol. Besides lowering cardiac output, which effect of the drug reduces portal inflow?","choices":{"A":"Direct V1a agonism","B":"Reduced glucagon release","C":"Unopposed alpha 1 tone","D":"More nitric oxide release","E":"Stellate cell relaxation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers lower portal pressure by beta 1 blockade, which cuts cardiac output, and by beta 2 blockade of vasodilatory receptors, which leaves alpha 1 receptors unopposed to constrict the splanchnic bed. V1a agonism is the vasopressin mechanism. Reduced glucagon release is the octreotide mechanism. Nitric oxide dilates the splanchnic bed. Stellate cell relaxation is not the described mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, nonselective beta blocker mechanism).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: beta blockers are contraindicated in severe asthma or COPD because of bronchospasm (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices and portal hypertension","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl10_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with cirrhosis survived a variceal bleed two months ago and takes propranolol. What should be combined with it to prevent another bleed?","choices":{"A":"Chronic octreotide infusion","B":"Daily oral metoprolol","C":"Monthly vasopressin doses","D":"Endoscopic band ligation","E":"Balloon tamponade"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secondary prophylaxis combines a nonselective beta blocker with endoscopic variceal band ligation. Octreotide and vasopressin are short term acute therapies. Metoprolol is cardioselective and far less effective. Balloon tamponade is a temporary rescue measure for active bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, clinical role in chronic settings).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a hepatic venous pressure gradient measurement assesses portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: primary prophylaxis is for medium or large varices that have not bled (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on hemodialysis takes an antacid several times a day for heartburn and has had loose stools since starting it. He develops weakness, hyporeflexia, and hypotension. Which ion has most likely accumulated?","choices":{"A":"Calcium","B":"Aluminum","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Phosphate","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Magnesium and aluminum from antacids accumulate in renal failure, and magnesium excess causes hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure. Calcium excess follows calcium carbonate use and can cause milk alkali syndrome. Aluminum also accumulates in renal failure but causes constipation rather than loose stools. Aluminum hydroxide lowers phosphate. Bicarbonate excess follows sodium bicarbonate use. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, antacid adverse effects); Bootcamp Gastroenterology (gastric pharmacology, lines 1203–1209).","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium hydroxide can cause hyporeflexia, hypotension, cardiac effects, and respiratory failure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastric pharmacology). COMLEX: antacids give rapid relief within 5 to 15 minutes that lasts only 1 to 2 hours (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and their products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Aluminum also accumulates in renal failure but causes constipation rather than loose stools."}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Compare and contrast the mechanisms of action, pharmacokinetics, adverse effects, and clinical indications of the major drug classes used to treat gastroesophageal reflux disease, and select appropriate therapy based on clinical presentation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has taken a proton pump inhibitor daily for eight years and now has a low trauma hip fracture. Which drug effect best explains her fracture risk?","choices":{"A":"Reduced vitamin D synthesis","B":"Reduced calcium absorption","C":"Increased renal calcium loss","D":"Direct osteoclast activation","E":"Low estrogen production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Long term PPI use decreases absorption of calcium, non heme iron, and vitamin B12 and raises the risk of osteoporotic hip and wrist fractures. PPIs do not block vitamin D synthesis, raise renal calcium loss, activate osteoclasts directly, or lower estrogen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 1, PPI adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: long term PPI use also causes benign fundic gland polyps (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: PPIs are cleared by hepatic CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 and need no renal dose change (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 3 — consequences of gastric cell loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the pharmacologic principles underlying the treatment and prevention of peptic ulcer disease, including H. pylori eradication, NSAID-associated ulcer treatment and prevention, and pharmacologic strategies to reduce the risk of recurrent bleeding.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old woman with severe hip osteoarthritis had a bleeding gastric ulcer last year. She needs long term anti inflammatory treatment. Which regimen carries the lowest risk of another ulcer?","choices":{"A":"Celecoxib with a PPI","B":"Naproxen with famotidine","C":"Ibuprofen with sucralfate","D":"Celecoxib with antacids","E":"Aspirin with misoprostol"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Selective COX 2 inhibitors combined with a PPI carry the lowest ulcer risk in high risk patients. Famotidine and sucralfate give less protection than a PPI with nonselective NSAIDs. Antacids give only brief relief. Misoprostol protects but aspirin remains a nonselective COX inhibitor, and misoprostol causes cramping and diarrhea. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 2, prevention in high risk patients).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for NSAID ulcers include prior ulcer or bleeding, advanced age, and anticoagulant, antiplatelet, or corticosteroid use (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: if NSAIDs are stopped, H2 blockers or PPIs for 4 to 8 weeks heal the ulcer (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — prostaglandins in mucosal protection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Famotidine with a nonselective NSAID protects less than a PPI does.","C":"Famotidine and sucralfate give less protection than a PPI with nonselective NSAIDs."}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q04","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and major adverse effects of antiemetic drug classes, and select appropriate therapy for nausea and vomiting in common clinical settings, including chemotherapy-induced and postoperative nausea and vomiting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman becomes nauseated and vomits on long car rides over winding mountain roads. Before her last trip she took ondansetron, which had worked well for her nausea after surgery, but it gave no relief during the drive. Which explanation best fits this failure?","choices":{"A":"Substance P acting at NK1 receptors drives it","B":"Vestibular input uses H1 and M1 receptors","C":"Dopamine at the trigger zone drives it","D":"Serotonin from gut cells triggers it","E":"Cannabinoid receptors drive this nausea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ondansetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone and works for postoperative and chemotherapy nausea, but it is ineffective for motion sickness, which arises from the vestibular apparatus and responds to H1 and M1 blockers such as scopolamine or meclizine. Substance P at NK1 receptors in the trigger zone is the target of aprepitant in chemotherapy nausea, not the vestibular pathway. D2 blockers act at the chemoreceptor trigger zone and are used for postoperative and uremic nausea rather than motion sickness. Serotonin released from the gut drives chemotherapy-induced nausea, which is why ondansetron works for that and not here. Cannabinoid agonists are second-line drugs for refractory chemotherapy nausea and appetite loss, not standard care for motion sickness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 3, antiemetic drug classes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists treat chemotherapy, postoperative and radiation emesis but not motion sickness, which responds to H1 and muscarinic blockers (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 10). COMLEX: a scopolamine patch or meclizine before travel helps prevent motion sickness.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagal afferents and the vagovagal reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Substance P at NK1 receptors in the trigger","C":"D2 blockers act at the chemoreceptor trigger zone","D":"Serotonin released from the gut drives chemotherapy-induced nausea,","E":"Cannabinoid agonists are second-line drugs for refractory chemotherapy"}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with cirrhosis and large varices that have never bled starts propranolol, titrated to a resting heart rate of 58/min. He also has type 1 diabetes treated with insulin. Two weeks later his wife finds him confused with a blood glucose of 38 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL); he says he felt none of the shakiness or pounding heartbeat that usually warns him. Which drug effect best explains the missing warning symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Inhibited glucagon release by alpha cells","B":"Bronchospasm limiting oxygen delivery","C":"Blunted adrenergic signs of low glucose","D":"Increased insulin release from islets","E":"Unopposed alpha tone in gut vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Nonselective beta blockers mask the adrenergic warning symptoms of hypoglycemia, such as tremor and palpitations, a listed caution when propranolol or nadolol is used in patients with diabetes. Inhibited glucagon release is an action of octreotide in variceal bleeding, not of propranolol. Bronchospasm is a beta-2 blocking effect in asthma or COPD and does not hide hypoglycemia. He has type 1 diabetes, so his symptoms cannot be explained by insulin release from his own islets. Unopposed alpha-1 tone in the splanchnic bed is how propranolol lowers portal inflow, and it does not affect hypoglycemia warnings. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, nonselective beta blockers and vasoactive agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: nonselective beta blockers lower portal pressure by beta-1 blockade of cardiac output and beta-2 blockade that leaves splanchnic alpha-1 constriction unopposed (Exam 1 lecture notes SDL 10). COMLEX: warn insulin-treated patients that propranolol can hide the early warning signs of hypoglycemia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — esophageal varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibited glucagon release is an action of octreotide","B":"Bronchospasm is a beta-2 blocking effect in asthma","D":"He has type 1 diabetes, so his symptoms","E":"Unopposed alpha-1 tone in the splanchnic bed is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl10_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the mechanisms of action and clinical roles of vasoactive agents and nonselective β-blockers in the management of esophageal variceal hemorrhage, distinguishing their use in acute and chronic settings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with cirrhosis is given a vasopressin infusion for variceal bleeding. Two days later he is confused, and his serum sodium is 124 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Renal sodium wasting","B":"Adrenal insufficiency","C":"Osmotic diuresis","D":"Free water retention","E":"Gastrointestinal sodium loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vasopressin causes free water retention that produces hyponatremia, along with systemic and coronary vasoconstriction. Renal sodium wasting, adrenal insufficiency, osmotic diuresis, and GI sodium loss are not the described effects of vasopressin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 10, section 4, vasopressin adverse effects).","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is given as a 50 mcg bolus then 50 mcg per hour (lecture SDL 10). COMLEX: long term octreotide can cause gallstones (lecture SDL 10).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":11,"title":"SDL 11 — Embryology of the liver, gallbladder and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"In the middle of the third week, the hepatic diverticulum buds from which site?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum mesoderm","B":"Dorsal mesentery of the stomach","C":"Midgut endoderm at the vitelline duct","D":"Ventral endoderm of the distal foregut","E":"Surface ectoderm of the body wall"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hepatic diverticulum appears about day 25 as an endodermal bud from the ventral wall of the distal foregut, at the site of the future proximal duodenum. The septum transversum is the mesodermal plate that this bud grows into after it forms. The dorsal mesentery suspends the stomach and holds the dorsal pancreatic bud. The vitelline duct is the midgut's open connection to the yolk sac, caudal to the foregut bud. Surface ectoderm covers the outside of the embryo. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The septum transversum is the mesodermal plate that this bud grows into after it forms.","B":"The dorsal mesentery suspends the stomach and holds the dorsal pancreatic bud.","C":"The vitelline duct is the midgut's open connection to the yolk sac, caudal to the foregut bud.","E":"Surface ectoderm covers the outside of the embryo."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the liver bud is an endodermal outgrowth of the distal foregut in the middle of the third week (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: that bud is the hepatic diverticulum at the future proximal duodenum (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"Proliferating cells of the hepatic diverticulum next invade which structure?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum","B":"Dorsal mesogastrium","C":"Neural tube","D":"Intermediate mesoderm of the urogenital ridge","E":"Vitelline duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hepatic diverticulum grows into the septum transversum, the mesodermal plate between the pericardial cavity and the stalk of the yolk sac. The dorsal mesogastrium is the mesentery behind the stomach and is the site of the spleen. The neural tube is the ectodermal precursor of the brain and spinal cord. Intermediate mesoderm forms the urogenital ridge, lateral to the gut. The vitelline duct is a narrow endodermal tube from the midgut to the yolk sac. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"The dorsal mesogastrium is the mesentery behind the stomach and is the site of the spleen.","C":"The neural tube is the ectodermal precursor of the brain and spinal cord.","D":"Intermediate mesoderm forms the urogenital ridge, lateral to the gut.","E":"The vitelline duct is a narrow endodermal tube from the midgut to the yolk sac."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: liver cords penetrate the septum transversum while the stalk to the duodenum narrows into the bile duct (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the septum transversum lies between the pericardial cavity and the yolk-sac stalk (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":1,"stem":"Hepatocytes in the liver cords arise from which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Septum transversum mesoderm","B":"Vitelline vein endothelium","C":"Cardiac mesoderm","D":"Neural crest","E":"Foregut endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatocytes, the parenchymal cells of the liver cords, differentiate from foregut endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum. Septum transversum mesoderm forms Glisson capsule, perisinusoidal connective tissue, and the visceral peritoneum. Endothelium of the vitelline veins is remodeled into the lining of the hepatic sinusoids. Cardiac mesoderm lies beside the foregut and forms the myocardium. Neural crest cells migrate into the gut wall as enteric neurons. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Septum transversum mesoderm forms Glisson capsule, perisinusoidal connective tissue, and the visceral peritoneum.","B":"Endothelium of the vitelline veins is remodeled into the lining of the hepatic sinusoids.","C":"Cardiac mesoderm lies beside the foregut and forms the myocardium.","D":"Neural crest cells migrate into the gut wall as enteric neurons."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: liver cords from the endodermal bud become the parenchyma and the biliary lining (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: hepatocytes are foregut endoderm, while hepatic connective tissue is septum transversum mesoderm (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia is produced by failure of which step?","choices":{"A":"Vacuolation and recanalization of the solid extrahepatic ducts","B":"Outgrowth of the cystic diverticulum","C":"Shift of the bile-duct entrance to the back of the duodenum","D":"Fusion of the ventral and dorsal pancreatic ducts","E":"Invasion of the septum transversum by liver cords"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The extrahepatic ducts pass through a temporary solid epithelial phase, and developmental biliary atresia is failure of those cords to recanalize, so the lumen is partly or completely obliterated. The cystic diverticulum is the ventral outgrowth that forms the gallbladder and the cystic duct. Duodenal rotation moves an already forming bile-duct entrance from anterior to posterior. Fusion of the ventral duct with the distal dorsal duct creates the main pancreatic duct. Invasion of the septum transversum is how the endodermal cords build the liver. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"The cystic diverticulum is the ventral outgrowth that forms the gallbladder and the cystic duct.","C":"Duodenal rotation moves an already forming bile-duct entrance from anterior to posterior.","D":"Fusion of the ventral duct with the distal dorsal duct creates the main pancreatic duct.","E":"Invasion of the septum transversum is how the endodermal cords build the liver."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: extrahepatic biliary atresia from failed recanalization occurs in about 1 in 15,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the fetal form is failed recanalization, while the more common perinatal form is later inflammatory scarring of ducts that had opened (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Why are the stools clay-colored when the extrahepatic bile duct is completely blocked?","choices":{"A":"Conjugated bilirubin is filtered into the urine","B":"Bilirubin never reaches the gut to become stercobilin","C":"Hepatocytes fail to conjugate bilirubin","D":"Heme is no longer broken down to bilirubin","E":"The gallbladder cannot concentrate bile"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With the extrahepatic duct closed, conjugated bilirubin cannot enter the intestine, so gut bacteria never convert it to stercobilin and the stool loses its color. Conjugated bilirubin filtered at the glomerulus is the pigment that darkens the urine. Hepatocytes continue to conjugate bilirubin when the duct is blocked. Macrophages continue to break heme down to bilirubin proximal to the obstruction. The gallbladder concentrates bile that has already been delivered to it. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Conjugated bilirubin filtered at the glomerulus is the pigment that darkens the urine.","C":"Hepatocytes continue to conjugate bilirubin when the duct is blocked.","D":"Macrophages continue to break heme down to bilirubin proximal to the obstruction.","E":"The gallbladder concentrates bile that has already been delivered to it."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: acholic stools mean conjugated bilirubin never reached the bowel to become stercobilin (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: the same block sends conjugated bilirubin backward into the blood.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Dark, tea-colored urine in complete extrahepatic biliary obstruction comes from glomerular filtration of which substance?","choices":{"A":"Unconjugated bilirubin","B":"Stercobilin","C":"Urobilinogen in increased amounts","D":"Conjugated bilirubin","E":"Intact heme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conjugated bilirubin is water-soluble, so when it backs up into the blood the glomeruli filter it and the urine turns dark. Unconjugated bilirubin travels bound to albumin and remains in the plasma. Stercobilin is the pigment bacteria make from bilirubin inside the intestinal lumen. Urobilinogen appears in the urine after intestinal bacteria have processed bilirubin, and that urinary pigment is low when bile never enters the gut. Heme is split to bilirubin in macrophages before the liver excretes it. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Unconjugated bilirubin travels bound to albumin and remains in the plasma.","B":"Stercobilin is the pigment bacteria make from bilirubin inside the intestinal lumen.","C":"Urobilinogen appears in the urine after intestinal bacteria have processed bilirubin, and that urinary pigment is low when bile never enters the gut.","E":"Heme is split to bilirubin in macrophages before the liver excretes it."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: conjugated bilirubin is filtered by the kidney, which is bilirubinuria (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: unconjugated bilirubin is albumin-bound and stays in the plasma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q07","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":1,"stem":"Which part of the pancreas forms from the ventral bud?","choices":{"A":"Tail of pancreas","B":"Body of pancreas","C":"Neck of pancreas","D":"Uncinate process","E":"Accessory duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ventral pancreatic bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior, posterior part of the head. The tail of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud. The body of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud. The neck of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud. The accessory duct is the proximal portion of the dorsal pancreatic duct. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The tail of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud.","B":"The body of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud.","C":"The neck of the pancreas is formed by the dorsal bud.","E":"The accessory duct is the proximal portion of the dorsal pancreatic duct."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the dorsal pancreatic bud lies in the dorsal mesentery, and the ventral bud lies next to the bile duct (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral mesoderm around the pancreatic buds forms the pancreatic connective tissue (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q08","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":1,"stem":"Endocrine progenitors that express both PAX4 and PAX6 become which islet cells?","choices":{"A":"Alpha cells only","B":"Acinar cells and centroacinar cells","C":"Beta, delta, and gamma (PP) cells","D":"Alpha cells and beta cells together","E":"Duct cells only"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Progenitors that express both PAX4 and PAX6 become beta cells, which secrete insulin, delta cells, which secrete somatostatin, and gamma (PP) cells, which secrete pancreatic polypeptide. Alpha cells develop from progenitors that express PAX6 alone and secrete glucagon. Acinar cells and centroacinar cells make up the exocrine pancreas. An option that pairs alpha cells with beta cells joins the PAX6-only lineage to a second lineage. Duct cells line the exocrine outflow tract of the pancreas. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Alpha cells develop from progenitors that express PAX6 alone and secrete glucagon.","B":"Acinar cells and centroacinar cells make up the exocrine pancreas.","D":"An option that pairs alpha cells with beta cells joins the PAX6-only lineage to a second lineage.","E":"Duct cells line the exocrine outflow tract of the pancreas."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: PAX4 plus PAX6 specifies beta, delta, and gamma cells; PAX6 alone specifies alpha cells (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: insulin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide are the hormones of the coexpressing lineages (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q09","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":1,"stem":"The accessory pancreatic duct of Santorini derives from which duct segment?","choices":{"A":"Distal dorsal duct","B":"Proximal dorsal duct","C":"Entire ventral duct","D":"Proximal ventral duct","E":"Common hepatic duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The accessory duct of Santorini is the proximal portion of the dorsal pancreatic duct, and it either disappears or opens at the minor duodenal papilla. The distal dorsal duct is the segment that joins the ventral duct. The ventral duct runs its full length into the main pancreatic duct. Any segment of the ventral duct opens with the bile duct at the major papilla. The common hepatic duct drains bile from the liver into the biliary tree. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The distal dorsal duct is the segment that joins the ventral duct.","C":"The ventral duct runs its full length into the main pancreatic duct.","D":"Any segment of the ventral duct opens with the bile duct at the major papilla.","E":"The common hepatic duct drains bile from the liver into the biliary tree."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the main pancreatic duct and the bile duct enter the duodenum together at the major papilla (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the accessory duct, when it persists, drains at the minor papilla (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal development and the major papilla","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q10","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":1,"stem":"As the hepatic diverticulum invades the septum transversum, the narrow connection between the diverticulum and the duodenum becomes which structure?","choices":{"A":"Cystic duct","B":"Main pancreatic duct","C":"Accessory pancreatic duct","D":"Common hepatic duct","E":"Common bile duct"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrowed connection between the hepatic diverticulum and the duodenum becomes the common bile duct, and its epithelium is foregut endoderm. The cystic duct is the channel of a later ventral outgrowth that also forms the gallbladder. The main pancreatic duct is the joined ventral and distal dorsal pancreatic ducts. The accessory pancreatic duct is the proximal portion of the dorsal pancreatic duct. The common hepatic duct is formed by union of the right and left hepatic ducts. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The cystic duct is the channel of a later ventral outgrowth that also forms the gallbladder.","B":"The main pancreatic duct is the joined ventral and distal dorsal pancreatic ducts.","C":"The accessory pancreatic duct is the proximal portion of the dorsal pancreatic duct.","D":"The common hepatic duct is formed by union of the right and left hepatic ducts."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the stalk of the hepatic diverticulum narrows into the bile duct (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: that duct and its lining are foregut endoderm (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q11","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":1,"stem":"The gallbladder and the cystic duct arise from which embryonic outgrowth?","choices":{"A":"A ventral outgrowth of the bile duct","B":"The dorsal pancreatic bud","C":"The vitelline duct","D":"The hepatic sinusoids","E":"The dorsal mesogastrium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A small ventral outgrowth of the bile duct, the cystic diverticulum, forms the gallbladder and the cystic duct, and its lining is foregut endoderm. The dorsal pancreatic bud forms the neck, body, tail, and superior part of the head of the pancreas. The vitelline duct connects the midgut loop to the yolk sac. Hepatic sinusoids are vascular channels made as liver cords meet the vitelline and umbilical veins. The dorsal mesogastrium is the mesentery that holds the spleen and the dorsal pancreatic bud. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"The dorsal pancreatic bud forms the neck, body, tail, and superior part of the head of the pancreas.","C":"The vitelline duct connects the midgut loop to the yolk sac.","D":"Hepatic sinusoids are vascular channels made as liver cords meet the vitelline and umbilical veins.","E":"The dorsal mesogastrium is the mesentery that holds the spleen and the dorsal pancreatic bud."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the gallbladder and cystic duct come from a ventral outgrowth of the bile duct (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: their epithelium, like the rest of the biliary lining, is foregut endoderm (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q12","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":1,"stem":"Union of the cystic duct with the common hepatic duct produces which channel?","choices":{"A":"Main pancreatic duct","B":"Accessory pancreatic duct","C":"Hepatic portal vein","D":"Definitive bile duct","E":"Left umbilical vein"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cystic duct joins the common hepatic duct to form the definitive bile duct. The main pancreatic duct is an anastomosis of the ventral and distal dorsal pancreatic ducts and meets the bile duct at the ampulla. The accessory pancreatic duct is the proximal dorsal pancreatic duct and opens at the minor papilla when it persists. The hepatic portal vein is a vessel formed during remodeling of the vitelline veins. The left umbilical vein is an embryonic vessel that carries oxygenated blood from the placenta. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The main pancreatic duct is an anastomosis of the ventral and distal dorsal pancreatic ducts and meets the bile duct at the ampulla.","B":"The accessory pancreatic duct is the proximal dorsal pancreatic duct and opens at the minor papilla when it persists.","C":"The hepatic portal vein is a vessel formed during remodeling of the vitelline veins.","E":"The left umbilical vein is an embryonic vessel that carries oxygenated blood from the placenta."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: once the cystic duct has joined the hepatic duct, bile can enter the intestine (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the definitive bile duct is the cystic duct joined to the common hepatic duct (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q13","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Annular pancreas","B":"Ectopic pancreas","C":"Pancreatic agenesis","D":"Pancreatic hypoplasia","E":"Pancreas divisum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is the most common congenital anomaly of the pancreas and is present in about 3% to 10% of people. Annular pancreas is a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum. Ectopic pancreas is pancreatic tissue in the wall of the gut, often in the stomach or a Meckel diverticulum. Pancreatic agenesis is complete failure of the gland to form. Pancreatic hypoplasia is an undergrown gland. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Annular pancreas is a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum.","B":"Ectopic pancreas is pancreatic tissue in the wall of the gut, often in the stomach or a Meckel diverticulum.","C":"Pancreatic agenesis is complete failure of the gland to form.","D":"Pancreatic hypoplasia is an undergrown gland."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral bud duct networks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies). COMLEX: in pancreas divisum the ventral and dorsal parts fail to fuse at 7 weeks (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":1,"stem":"Which islet cells develop from progenitors that express PAX6 alone?","choices":{"A":"Beta cells","B":"Alpha cells","C":"Delta cells","D":"Gamma (PP) cells","E":"All four endocrine cell types"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Progenitors that express PAX6 alone become alpha cells and secrete glucagon. Beta cells secrete insulin and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6. Delta cells secrete somatostatin and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6. Gamma cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6. Beta cells, delta cells, and gamma cells require PAX4 coexpressed with PAX6. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Beta cells secrete insulin and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6.","C":"Delta cells secrete somatostatin and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6.","D":"Gamma cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide and are specified when PAX4 is expressed together with PAX6.","E":"Beta cells, delta cells, and gamma cells require PAX4 coexpressed with PAX6."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: PAX6 without PAX4 restricts the progenitor to the alpha-cell lineage (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: alpha cells secrete glucagon (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q15","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":1,"stem":"A second gallbladder that empties through its own cystic duct, or through a cystic duct shared with the other gallbladder, results from which event?","choices":{"A":"Folding of the fundus over the body","B":"A septum dividing a single lumen","C":"An extra outgrowth of the bile duct","D":"Failure of ductal recanalization","E":"Embedding of one gallbladder in the liver"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gallbladder duplication is an extra developmental outgrowth that creates a second gallbladder draining by its own cystic duct or by a duct shared with the other gallbladder. Folding of the fundus over the body is a Phrygian cap, a change in the shape of one gallbladder. An internal septum produces a bilobed gallbladder, which is still a single organ. Failure of recanalization obliterates a duct lumen and is developmental biliary atresia. An intrahepatic gallbladder is a single gallbladder buried partly or completely in the liver, found in about 5% to 10% of people. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Folding of the fundus over the body is a Phrygian cap, a change in the shape of one gallbladder.","B":"An internal septum produces a bilobed gallbladder, which is still a single organ.","D":"Failure of recanalization obliterates a duct lumen and is developmental biliary atresia.","E":"An intrahepatic gallbladder is a single gallbladder buried partly or completely in the liver, found in about 5% to 10% of people."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder duplication is usually silent until gallstones or cholecystitis develop (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: duplication and accessory hepatic ducts become important under pathologic conditions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — foregut divisions and the liver bud","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"A histologist traces the lining of the canals of Hering and the intrahepatic bile ductules back to its germ layer. Which origin is correct?","choices":{"A":"Foregut endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum","B":"Septum transversum mesoderm","C":"Vitelline vein endothelium","D":"Neural crest","E":"Yolk-sac mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Biliary epithelium, including the lining of the canals of Hering and of the intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts, differentiates from foregut endoderm of the hepatic diverticulum. Septum transversum mesoderm forms the connective-tissue stroma, Kupffer cells, and hematopoietic islands. Vitelline vein endothelium is taken into the walls of the hepatic sinusoids. Neural crest cells supply the enteric ganglia of the gut wall. Yolk-sac mesoderm supplies macrophage and blood-cell progenitors in the fetal liver. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"Septum transversum mesoderm forms the connective-tissue stroma, Kupffer cells, and hematopoietic islands.","C":"Vitelline vein endothelium is taken into the walls of the hepatic sinusoids.","D":"Neural crest cells supply the enteric ganglia of the gut wall.","E":"Yolk-sac mesoderm supplies macrophage and blood-cell progenitors in the fetal liver."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the same endodermal cords that make hepatocytes also make the biliary epithelium (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the lining of the canals of Hering, ductules, and extrahepatic ducts is foregut endoderm (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"Which set of structures is derived from mesoderm of the septum transversum?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes and intrahepatic biliary epithelium","B":"Gallbladder epithelium and the cystic-duct lining","C":"The dorsal pancreatic bud and the spleen","D":"Enteric neurons in the gut wall","E":"Glisson capsule, the falciform ligament, and the lesser omentum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mesoderm of the septum transversum forms hepatic connective tissue, including Glisson capsule, and the ventral mesentery that becomes the lesser omentum and the falciform ligament. Hepatocytes and intrahepatic biliary epithelium differentiate from foregut endoderm. The lining of the gallbladder and cystic duct is foregut endoderm of the cystic diverticulum. The spleen forms in the dorsal mesogastrium, and the dorsal pancreatic bud arises in the dorsal mesentery. Enteric neurons reach the gut by migrating from the neural crest. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Hepatocytes and intrahepatic biliary epithelium differentiate from foregut endoderm.","B":"The lining of the gallbladder and cystic duct is foregut endoderm of the cystic diverticulum.","C":"The spleen forms in the dorsal mesogastrium, and the dorsal pancreatic bud arises in the dorsal mesentery.","D":"Enteric neurons reach the gut by migrating from the neural crest."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: connective tissue of the liver comes from septum transversum mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the bare area is the cranial surface against the central tendon of the diaphragm, which never gains peritoneum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":2,"stem":"Kupffer cells and the blood-forming islands of the fetal liver are derived from which source?","choices":{"A":"Foregut endoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Septum transversum mesoderm","E":"Cardiac mesoderm"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Kupffer cells, arising from yolk-sac mesodermal progenitors, and the transient hematopoietic islands of the fetal liver are mesoderm of the septum transversum. Foregut endoderm forms hepatocytes and the biliary epithelium. Neural crest cells become the neurons of the enteric plexus. Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis. Cardiac mesoderm forms the myocardium beside the cranial foregut. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Foregut endoderm forms hepatocytes and the biliary epithelium.","B":"Neural crest cells become the neurons of the enteric plexus.","C":"Surface ectoderm forms the epidermis.","E":"Cardiac mesoderm forms the myocardium beside the cranial foregut."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: hematopoietic cells, Kupffer cells, and connective tissue cells come from septum transversum mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: fetal hematopoietic islands between the liver cells shrink late in pregnancy and are small by birth (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-week-old boy has jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and hepatomegaly. Direct bilirubin and GGT are high. Ultrasound shows no visible gallbladder. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Physiologic jaundice","B":"Breast milk jaundice","C":"Dubin Johnson syndrome","D":"Biliary atresia","E":"Crigler Najjar syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conjugated jaundice, dark urine, clay-colored stools, and hepatomegaly in the first weeks of life are the findings of extrahepatic biliary atresia, because bile cannot enter the intestine. Physiologic jaundice is an unconjugated rise in the first week of life, and the stools stay yellow. Breast-milk jaundice is a prolonged unconjugated rise, and the stools stay pigmented. Dubin-Johnson syndrome stores a dark pigment in an otherwise patent liver, and bile still reaches the bowel. Crigler-Najjar syndrome is a failure of bilirubin conjugation, so the pigment that accumulates is unconjugated. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Physiologic jaundice is an unconjugated rise in the first week of life, and the stools stay yellow.","B":"Breast-milk jaundice is a prolonged unconjugated rise, and the stools stay pigmented.","C":"Dubin-Johnson syndrome stores a dark pigment in an otherwise patent liver, and bile still reaches the bowel.","E":"Crigler-Najjar syndrome is a failure of bilirubin conjugation, so the pigment that accumulates is unconjugated."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: biliary atresia presents in early infancy with conjugated jaundice, dark urine, and acholic stools (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: uncorrected obstruction progresses to biliary cirrhosis within 3 to 6 months (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — conjugated versus unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-week-old boy with extrahepatic biliary atresia has greasy stools and a coagulopathy that corrects after vitamin K is given. Both findings follow the absence of which substance from the intestinal lumen?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase","B":"Bile salts","C":"Intrinsic factor","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Pepsin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile salts emulsify dietary fat and form micelles, so their absence from the lumen causes steatorrhea and malabsorption of the fat-soluble vitamins. Pancreatic lipase is still secreted when the only defect is a blocked bile duct. Intrinsic factor is a gastric glycoprotein required for absorption of vitamin B12. Lactase on the brush border splits lactose. Pepsin is a gastric protease. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase is still secreted when the only defect is a blocked bile duct.","C":"Intrinsic factor is a gastric glycoprotein required for absorption of vitamin B12.","D":"Lactase on the brush border splits lactose.","E":"Pepsin is a gastric protease."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: missing luminal bile salts block micelle formation and uptake of vitamins A, D, E, and K (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: bile salts retained in the blood cause severe pruritus (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-week-old boy has rising direct bilirubin, acholic stools, and hepatomegaly, and ultrasound shows an irregular gallbladder. A liver biopsy is taken before surgery. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal ground glass cells","B":"Portal ductular reaction","C":"Centrilobular necrosis","D":"Interface plasma cells","E":"Copper laden hepatocytes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile stasis in extrahepatic biliary atresia produces portal tract edema, a ductular reaction, and feathery degeneration of swollen hepatocytes. Ground-glass hepatocytes are the cytoplasmic inclusion of hepatitis B surface antigen. Centrilobular necrosis is the pattern of ischemic or toxic injury around the central vein. Plasma cells crowded along the limiting plate are the infiltrate of autoimmune hepatitis. Copper stored in hepatocytes is the metal accumulation of Wilson disease. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Ground-glass hepatocytes are the cytoplasmic inclusion of hepatitis B surface antigen.","C":"Centrilobular necrosis is the pattern of ischemic or toxic injury around the central vein.","D":"Plasma cells crowded along the limiting plate are the infiltrate of autoimmune hepatitis.","E":"Copper stored in hepatocytes is the metal accumulation of Wilson disease."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructed bile flow causes portal edema, ductular reaction, and feathery degeneration (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: the liver enlarges as bile and bile acids accumulate (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 5 — bile flow through canaliculi and ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a pancreaticoduodenectomy, and the surgeon notes that the common bile duct runs behind the duodenum. Which developmental event explains this position?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal rotation","B":"Liver bud regression","C":"Gallbladder descent","D":"Ventral mesentery loss","E":"Splenic migration"},"correct":"A","explanation":"As the duodenum rotates to the right and becomes C-shaped, the entrance of the bile duct shifts from an anterior position to a posterior one, so the common bile duct passes behind the duodenum. The liver bud persists and enlarges into the liver. The gallbladder's final position is set by its own outgrowth from the bile duct. The ventral mesentery remains in the adult as the lesser omentum and the falciform ligament. The spleen develops in the dorsal mesogastrium. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"The liver bud persists and enlarges into the liver.","C":"The gallbladder's final position is set by its own outgrowth from the bile duct.","D":"The ventral mesentery remains in the adult as the lesser omentum and the falciform ligament.","E":"The spleen develops in the dorsal mesogastrium."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal rotation carries the bile-duct entrance from anterior to posterior (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the ventral pancreatic bud follows that same path and comes to lie below and behind the dorsal bud (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation and the mesenteries","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has ERCP, and contrast injected at the major papilla fills the main pancreatic duct to the tail. Which embryonic ducts formed this channel?","choices":{"A":"Ventral and proximal dorsal ducts","B":"Dorsal duct and cystic duct","C":"Ventral duct and bile duct","D":"Ventral and distal dorsal ducts","E":"Proximal and distal dorsal ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The main pancreatic duct of Wirsung is the entire ventral pancreatic duct joined to the distal part of the dorsal pancreatic duct, and it opens with the bile duct at the major papilla. The proximal dorsal duct is the accessory duct of Santorini. The cystic duct drains the gallbladder into the biliary tree. The bile duct reaches the ampulla alongside the pancreatic duct and is a biliary channel. The proximal and distal pieces of the dorsal duct are two segments of the dorsal bud's own channel. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The proximal dorsal duct is the accessory duct of Santorini.","B":"The cystic duct drains the gallbladder into the biliary tree.","C":"The bile duct reaches the ampulla alongside the pancreatic duct and is a biliary channel.","E":"The proximal and distal pieces of the dorsal duct are two segments of the dorsal bud's own channel."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the ventral bud forms the uncinate process and the inferior head (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal bud forms the neck, body, tail, and superior part of the head (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — exocrine pancreas and its ducts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has a first trimester ultrasound, and her physician explains how the fetal pancreas forms. After the ventral bud moves around the duodenum, where does it come to lie relative to the dorsal bud?","choices":{"A":"Above and in front","B":"Directly anterior to it","C":"To the left of it","D":"Behind the stomach","E":"Below and behind it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"After the duodenum rotates to the right, the ventral pancreatic bud lies immediately below and behind the dorsal bud. Above and in front of the dorsal bud is still the anterior side of the duodenum, where the bile duct starts. Directly anterior to the dorsal bud is the ventral bud's relation while the bile-duct entrance is still on the front of the duodenum. The pancreas extending left from the head toward the spleen is body and tail, which come from the dorsal bud. The region behind the stomach is the dorsal mesogastrium, where the dorsal bud begins. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Above and in front of the dorsal bud is still the anterior side of the duodenum, where the bile duct starts.","B":"Directly anterior to the dorsal bud is the ventral bud's relation while the bile-duct entrance is still on the front of the duodenum.","C":"The pancreas extending left from the head toward the spleen is body and tail, which come from the dorsal bud.","D":"The region behind the stomach is the dorsal mesogastrium, where the dorsal bud begins."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the pancreas forms from a dorsal and a ventral endodermal bud of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the ventral bud starts beside the bile duct and finishes below and behind the dorsal bud (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy has a loss of function variant in a transcription factor gene, leaving only one major islet lineage able to form. Which islet hormone can still be produced normally?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon release","B":"Insulin release","C":"Somatostatin release","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide release","E":"Amylin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of PAX4 leaves the lineage that expresses PAX6 alone, the alpha cell, which continues to secrete glucagon. Insulin is the hormone of beta cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6. Somatostatin is the hormone of delta cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6. Pancreatic polypeptide is the hormone of gamma cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6. Amylin is cosecreted by beta cells, the same PAX4-and-PAX6 lineage that secretes insulin. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"Insulin is the hormone of beta cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6.","C":"Somatostatin is the hormone of delta cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6.","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide is the hormone of gamma cells, which are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6.","E":"Amylin is cosecreted by beta cells, the same PAX4-and-PAX6 lineage that secretes insulin."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make insulin, delta cells make somatostatin, and gamma cells make pancreatic polypeptide (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: alpha cells make glucagon (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":2,"stem":"Which congenital variation is the most common anomaly of the gallbladder and is formed when the fundus folds inward over the body?","choices":{"A":"Gallbladder duplication","B":"Bilobed gallbladder","C":"Phrygian cap","D":"Gallbladder agenesis","E":"Intrahepatic gallbladder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A Phrygian cap, the most common congenital variation of the gallbladder, is an inward fold of the fundus over the body. Gallbladder duplication is a second gallbladder grown from an extra outgrowth of the bile duct. A bilobed gallbladder is one gallbladder divided by a longitudinal or transverse septum. Gallbladder agenesis is complete absence of the gallbladder. An intrahepatic gallbladder lies partly or wholly inside the liver parenchyma. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Gallbladder duplication is a second gallbladder grown from an extra outgrowth of the bile duct.","B":"A bilobed gallbladder is one gallbladder divided by a longitudinal or transverse septum.","D":"Gallbladder agenesis is complete absence of the gallbladder.","E":"An intrahepatic gallbladder lies partly or wholly inside the liver parenchyma."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the Phrygian cap is the most common congenital variation of the gallbladder (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: partial or complete embedding of the gallbladder in the liver is seen in about 5% to 10% of people (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the formation of the extrahepatic bile duct, gallbladder, and cystic duct from foregut endoderm, and identify gallbladder duplication and a congenital variation.","batch":2,"stem":"An extra duct that drains part of the liver in addition to the normal hepatic ducts is which variation?","choices":{"A":"Phrygian cap","B":"Accessory hepatic duct","C":"Gallbladder duplication","D":"Pancreas divisum","E":"Annular pancreas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An accessory hepatic duct is an additional duct leaving the liver beside the normal hepatic ducts. A Phrygian cap is a fold of the gallbladder fundus over the body. Gallbladder duplication is a second gallbladder, grown from an extra outgrowth of the bile duct. Pancreas divisum is persistence of separate dorsal and ventral pancreatic duct systems. Annular pancreas is a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum. Sources: Langman Ch. 15.","whyNot":{"A":"A Phrygian cap is a fold of the gallbladder fundus over the body.","C":"Gallbladder duplication is a second gallbladder, grown from an extra outgrowth of the bile duct.","D":"Pancreas divisum is persistence of separate dorsal and ventral pancreatic duct systems.","E":"Annular pancreas is a ring of pancreatic tissue around the second part of the duodenum."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: accessory hepatic ducts and gallbladder duplication are common, usually asymptomatic, and important once the biliary tree is diseased or operated on (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an accessory hepatic duct is an extra duct from the liver, distinct from a second gallbladder.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has recurrent pancreatitis without alcohol use or gallstones. MRCP shows most of the gland draining through a narrow minor papilla, and a small duct drains only part of the head. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Abnormal ventral bud rotation","B":"Failed ductal fusion","C":"Ectopic pancreatic rests","D":"Absent ventral bud","E":"Duodenal recanalization failure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreas divisum is persistence of separate dorsal and ventral duct systems after they fail to fuse, so the bulk of the gland drains through the narrow minor papilla by the duct of Santorini, and about 5% of affected people develop pancreatitis. Migration of a split ventral bud in two directions produces a ring of pancreas around the duodenum. An ectopic pancreatic rest is a nodule of pancreas in the gut wall. Absence of the ventral bud would remove the uncinate process and the duct of Wirsung. Failure of duodenal recanalization leaves a web or an atresia in the bowel wall. Sources: course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Migration of a split ventral bud in two directions produces a ring of pancreas around the duodenum.","C":"An ectopic pancreatic rest is a nodule of pancreas in the gut wall.","D":"Absence of the ventral bud would remove the uncinate process and the duct of Wirsung.","E":"Failure of duodenal recanalization leaves a web or an atresia in the bowel wall."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: drainage of most of the gland through the minor papilla predisposes to pancreatitis (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: most people with pancreas divisum remain asymptomatic (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old girl vomits with every feed. Imaging shows a double bubble, and surgery finds a ring of normal pancreatic tissue around the duodenum. Which developmental error explains this?","choices":{"A":"Failed ductal fusion","B":"Failed duodenal recanalization","C":"Split ventral bud rotation","D":"Pyloric muscle hypertrophy","E":"Incomplete midgut rotation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A bifid ventral bud sends its right portion along the usual posterior path and its left portion the opposite way, and the two arms meet as a ring of pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. Failure of the dorsal and ventral ducts to fuse leaves the body and tail draining at the minor papilla. A duodenum that fails to recanalize keeps a solid segment or a mucosal web in its own wall. Hypertrophy of pyloric circular muscle narrows the pyloric channel. Incomplete rotation of the midgut leaves the intestine malpositioned, with peritoneal bands. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Failure of the dorsal and ventral ducts to fuse leaves the body and tail draining at the minor papilla.","B":"A duodenum that fails to recanalize keeps a solid segment or a mucosal web in its own wall.","D":"Hypertrophy of pyloric circular muscle narrows the pyloric channel.","E":"Incomplete rotation of the midgut leaves the intestine malpositioned, with peritoneal bands."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: annular pancreas can narrow or obstruct the second part of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about one third of cases are asymptomatic, and infants may present with vomiting after polyhydramnios (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl11_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":2,"stem":"A laboratory isolates islet cells that coexpress PAX4 and PAX6. Which hormones can this population secrete?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon only","B":"Insulin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide","C":"Glucagon and insulin","D":"Digestive enzymes","E":"Glucagon, insulin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells that coexpress PAX4 and PAX6 are beta cells secreting insulin, delta cells secreting somatostatin, and gamma cells secreting pancreatic polypeptide. Glucagon is the hormone of alpha cells, which express PAX6 alone. Glucagon is an alpha-cell hormone, and alpha cells express PAX6 without PAX4. Digestive enzymes are secreted by acinar cells of the exocrine pancreas. Glucagon in that list is the product of alpha cells, which express PAX6 and lack PAX4. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Glucagon is the hormone of alpha cells, which express PAX6 alone.","C":"Glucagon is an alpha-cell hormone, and alpha cells express PAX6 without PAX4.","D":"Digestive enzymes are secreted by acinar cells of the exocrine pancreas.","E":"Glucagon in that list is the product of alpha cells, which express PAX6 and lack PAX4."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: the coexpressing lineages secrete insulin, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide (course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: glucagon is the hormone of the PAX6-only alpha cell (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain how the hepatic diverticulum develops from foregut endoderm and grows into the septum transversum, distinguishing the origins of hepatocytes and biliary epithelium from hepatic connective tissue.","batch":3,"stem":"Cords of the hepatic diverticulum are spreading through the septum transversum and breaking a pair of venous systems into a capillary-like network. Which vessels are remodeled into the hepatic sinusoids?","choices":{"A":"Common cardinal veins","B":"Dorsal aortae","C":"Vitelline and umbilical veins","D":"Vitelline arteries","E":"Umbilical arteries"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Endodermal liver cords intermingle with the vitelline and umbilical veins, and those veins are remodeled into the hepatic sinusoids. The common cardinal veins return systemic blood to the sinus venosus. The dorsal aortae are the arterial trunks that fuse as the descending aorta. Vitelline arteries leave the aorta for the gut and become the celiac and mesenteric arteries. The umbilical arteries carry blood from the embryo to the placenta. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"The common cardinal veins return systemic blood to the sinus venosus.","B":"The dorsal aortae are the arterial trunks that fuse as the descending aorta.","D":"Vitelline arteries leave the aorta for the gut and become the celiac and mesenteric arteries.","E":"The umbilical arteries carry blood from the embryo to the placenta."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids form where liver cords meet the vitelline and umbilical veins (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: bile secretion begins near the 12th week, after the gallbladder and cystic duct have joined the hepatic duct (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — foregut, midgut, and hindgut","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Relate failure of bile duct recanalization to developmental extrahepatic biliary atresia and recognize the resulting impairment of bile flow from clinical findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-week-old girl has had pale stools since the first day of life, with jaundice and dark urine. Direct bilirubin is 6.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.0–0.3 mg/dL). Operative cholangiography shows the extrahepatic bile duct replaced by a solid fibrous cord. Which embryonic failure accounts for that cord?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of a temporarily solid extrahepatic duct","B":"Inflammatory destruction of a duct that had already opened after birth","C":"Cystic dilation of a patent common bile duct","D":"Plugging of small ducts by inspissated mucus","E":"Loss of intrahepatic ducts after a fetal infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A solid fibrous cord in place of the extrahepatic duct, with pale stools from birth, is developmental biliary atresia from failure of the solid ductal cords to recanalize. Postnatal inflammatory scarring is the perinatal form, and those infants pass pigmented stools before the ducts close. A choledochal cyst is a dilated, patent segment of the bile duct. Inspissated mucus fills small ducts in cystic fibrosis and leaves the extrahepatic duct as a tube. Paucity of intrahepatic ducts is a rare lesion inside the liver, sometimes after fetal infection, and the extrahepatic duct remains a duct. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"Postnatal inflammatory scarring is the perinatal form, and those infants pass pigmented stools before the ducts close.","C":"A choledochal cyst is a dilated, patent segment of the bile duct.","D":"Inspissated mucus fills small ducts in cystic fibrosis and leaves the extrahepatic duct as a tube.","E":"Paucity of intrahepatic ducts is a rare lesion inside the liver, sometimes after fetal infection, and the extrahepatic duct remains a duct."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: failure of the solid extrahepatic ducts to recanalize is the fetal form of biliary atresia, about 1 in 15,000 births (Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11). COMLEX: among children with extrahepatic biliary atresia, 15% to 20% have patent proximal ducts and a correctable lesion (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q03","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":3,"stem":"Pancreatic polypeptide, which slows pancreatic enzyme and bicarbonate secretion, is released by which cells?","choices":{"A":"Alpha cells that express PAX6 alone","B":"Beta cells that express PAX6 alone","C":"Delta cells that express PAX6 alone","D":"Gamma cells that express PAX4 and PAX6","E":"Acinar cells, which express neither PAX gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gamma (PP) cells secrete pancreatic polypeptide and are specified by coexpression of PAX4 and PAX6. Alpha cells secrete glucagon and are the lineage that expresses PAX6 alone. Beta cells secrete insulin and are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6. Delta cells secrete somatostatin and are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6. Acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes and stand outside the endocrine PAX split. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Alpha cells secrete glucagon and are the lineage that expresses PAX6 alone.","B":"Beta cells secrete insulin and are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6.","C":"Delta cells secrete somatostatin and are specified by PAX4 together with PAX6.","E":"Acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes and stand outside the endocrine PAX split."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: gamma cells make pancreatic polypeptide and require both PAX4 and PAX6 (course notes, SDL 11; Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: pancreatic polypeptide inhibits pancreatic exocrine secretion and modulates gut motility (course notes, SDL 11).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q04","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Distinguish the endocrine pancreatic cell lineages associated with PAX4/PAX6 expression from those associated with PAX6 alone, and identify their hormone products.","batch":3,"stem":"A newborn girl has persistent hyperglycemia from the first day of life. Ultrasound shows a pancreas of normal size, and her stools are normal with no excess fat on stool testing. Serum insulin, glucagon, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide are all undetectable. A loss of function in which gene best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"PAX4","B":"PDX1","C":"PAX6","D":"HNF4","E":"SHH"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PAX6 is required in every endocrine lineage, so its loss removes insulin, glucagon, somatostatin, and pancreatic polypeptide while acini and ducts still form. Loss of PAX4 removes beta, delta, and gamma cells and leaves glucagon secretion from alpha cells. Loss of PDX1 stops the pancreas from forming, so the gland is absent and fat appears in the stool. HNF4 directs liver-bud cells toward hepatocytes and biliary epithelium. Sonic hedgehog is a secreted foregut signal, separate from the paired-box genes that assign islet lineages. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Loss of PAX4 removes beta, delta, and gamma cells and leaves glucagon secretion from alpha cells.","B":"Loss of PDX1 stops the pancreas from forming, so the gland is absent and fat appears in the stool.","D":"HNF4 directs liver-bud cells toward hepatocytes and biliary epithelium.","E":"Sonic hedgehog is a secreted foregut signal, separate from the paired-box genes that assign islet lineages."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: PAX4 with PAX6 specifies beta, delta, and PP cells, and PAX6 alone specifies alpha cells (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: neonatal diabetes with a normal-sized pancreas and no steatorrhea points to an islet-lineage defect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — islet cell types and their hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q05","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman who does not drink alcohol has had three attacks of acute pancreatitis. Ultrasound shows no gallstones, and triglycerides and calcium are normal. MRCP shows a dominant duct from the tail and body crossing the head to open at a small papilla above the major papilla, and a separate short duct from the lower head that joins the bile duct. Failure of which developmental event best explains her anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Fusion of the dorsal and ventral ducts","B":"Migration of a bifid ventral bud around the gut","C":"Regression of the dorsal pancreatic bud","D":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","E":"Outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral ducts leaves pancreas divisum, in which the body, tail, and superior head drain by the duct of Santorini at the minor papilla, and the inferior head and uncinate process drain by the duct of Wirsung at the major papilla. A bifid ventral bud that migrates both ways forms an annular pancreas around the second part of the duodenum. Regression of the dorsal bud would remove the body and the tail. Failed recanalization of the duodenum produces stenosis or atresia of the bowel lumen. The hepatic diverticulum is the endodermal bud that forms the liver and the bile ducts. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"B":"A bifid ventral bud that migrates both ways forms an annular pancreas around the second part of the duodenum.","C":"Regression of the dorsal bud would remove the body and the tail.","D":"Failed recanalization of the duodenum produces stenosis or atresia of the bowel lumen.","E":"The hepatic diverticulum is the endodermal bud that forms the liver and the bile ducts."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral duct systems, so most of the gland drains through the minor papilla via the duct of Santorini (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: Consider divisum in idiopathic recurrent pancreatitis, especially with a coexisting genetic risk factor.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 1 — congenital pancreatic anomalies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl11_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain normal ventral pancreatic duct fusion, and distinguish the developmental abnormalities underlying annular pancreas and persistence of separate pancreatic duct systems.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy born after a pregnancy complicated by polyhydramnios vomits after every feed. An abdominal radiograph shows a double bubble with a little distal gas. At surgery, a flat band of tissue completely encircles the second part of the duodenum and narrows it. A biopsy of the band would most likely show which tissue?","choices":{"A":"Dense fibrous peritoneal tissue","B":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle","C":"A mucosal web with submucosa","D":"Acini, ducts, and islets","E":"Gastric glands with parietal cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The band encircling the second part of the duodenum is an annular pancreas, a ring of ordinary pancreatic tissue, so a biopsy shows acini, ducts, and islets. Dense fibrous peritoneal tissue is the makeup of Ladd bands, which cross the duodenum in malrotation. Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle is the wall lesion of pyloric stenosis. A mucosal web with submucosa is the intrinsic diaphragm of duodenal atresia. Gastric glands with parietal cells are heterotopic gastric mucosa in the bowel lining. Sources: Langman Ch. 15; course notes, SDL 11.","whyNot":{"A":"Dense fibrous peritoneal tissue is the makeup of Ladd bands, which cross the duodenum in malrotation.","B":"Hypertrophied circular smooth muscle is the wall lesion of pyloric stenosis.","C":"A mucosal web with submucosa is the intrinsic diaphragm of duodenal atresia.","E":"Gastric glands with parietal cells are heterotopic gastric mucosa in the bowel lining."},"boardPrep":"Step 1: Annular pancreas is a bifid ventral bud ringing the second part of the duodenum with normal pancreatic tissue, and it is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid p. 367). COMLEX: A neonatal double bubble has a differential of duodenal atresia, annular pancreas, and malrotation with Ladd bands.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":12,"title":"SDL 12 — Histology of the Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"The portal vein supplies most of the blood reaching the liver. From which organs does this blood come?","choices":{"A":"Gut, pancreas, and spleen","B":"Kidneys, adrenals, and gonads","C":"Lungs, heart, and thoracic aorta","D":"Pelvis, perineum, and lower limbs","E":"Head, neck, and upper limbs"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The hepatic portal vein brings oxygen-poor blood from the digestive tract and major abdominal organs such as the pancreas and spleen, so it carries absorbed nutrients and toxins, breakdown products of blood cells, and pancreatic and enteroendocrine hormones. Kidneys, adrenals, and gonads drain into the systemic veins and inferior vena cava. Lungs, heart, and thoracic aorta are not part of portal drainage. Pelvis, perineum, and lower limbs return blood through iliac veins to the cava. Head, neck, and upper limbs drain to the superior vena cava. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18, blood supply of the liver.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood carries gut-absorbed nutrients and toxins, splenic blood products, and pancreatic hormones to the liver (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: first-pass hepatic metabolism of oral drugs follows from this portal drainage.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kidneys, adrenals, and gonads drain into the","C":"Lungs, heart, and thoracic aorta are not","D":"Pelvis, perineum, and lower limbs return blood","E":"Head, neck, and upper limbs drain to"}},{"id":"sdl12_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"How thick are normal adult hepatocyte plates?","choices":{"A":"Two cells thick","B":"Three cells thick","C":"Five cells thick","D":"Ten cells thick","E":"One cell thick"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Adult hepatocytes form branching, anastomosing plates that are normally one cell thick and radiate from the central vein, with sinusoids between neighboring plates. Thicker plates are not the adult pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatocyte plates and sinusoids, lines 2294–2297).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes have a central round nucleus with prominent nucleoli and stain PAS positive from glycogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: hepatocytes carry out metabolism, detoxification, and protein synthesis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern.","B":"Three-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern.","C":"Five-cell-thick plates are not the adult pattern.","D":"Ten-cell-thick plates do not occur normally."}},{"id":"sdl12_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":1,"stem":"Into which vessels does the central vein drain first?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic veins","B":"Portal venules","C":"Interlobular veins","D":"Sublobular veins","E":"Splenic veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The central vein empties into sublobular veins, which join the hepatic veins and then the inferior vena cava. Portal and interlobular veins bring blood into the lobule rather than draining it, and the splenic vein feeds the portal vein. Hepatic veins receive blood from the sublobular veins; the central vein drains first into sublobular veins. Pawlina notes interlobular veins receive capillary blood from the larger portal canals before emptying into the sinusoids, not from the central vein. Pawlina states the terminal hepatic venule (central vein) empties into the sublobular veins, the tributaries of the hepatic vein, not portal venules. Pawlina states the terminal hepatic venule (central vein) empties into the sublobular veins, the tributaries of the hepatic vein, not splenic veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, centripetal flow and venous drainage, lines 2303–2308).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal vein blood passes through interlobular veins and sinusoids to the central vein and hepatic vein, a summary that skips the sublobular veins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: Budd Chiari syndrome causes ischemic zone 3 injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic veins receive blood from the sublobular veins; the central vein drains first into sublobular veins.","C":"Pawlina notes interlobular veins receive capillary blood from the larger portal canals before emptying into the sinusoids, not from the central vein.","B":"Pawlina states the terminal hepatic venule (central vein) empties into the sublobular veins, the tributaries of the hepatic vein, not portal venules.","E":"Pawlina states the terminal hepatic venule (central vein) empties into the sublobular veins, the tributaries of the hepatic vein, not splenic veins."}},{"id":"sdl12_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"What forms the short axis of the liver acinus?","choices":{"A":"Line joining two central veins","B":"Sublobular vein branches","C":"Interlobular bile ducts","D":"Bile canaliculi network","E":"Distributing portal vessels"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The short axis of the acinus is formed by the penetrating distributing vessels from portal triads between two classic lobules, and the long axis runs between the two nearest central veins. Sublobular veins drain the lobule, and bile ducts and canaliculi carry bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, lines 2312–2316).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 1 is the first zone to meet incoming blood and has the highest oxygen and metabolic activity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is affected first by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins such as cocaine (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The line joining two central veins is the long axis of the acinus.","B":"Sublobular veins drain the lobule.","C":"Interlobular bile ducts carry bile.","D":"Bile canaliculi carry bile."}},{"id":"sdl12_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"What gross appearance results from chronic passive congestion of the liver?","choices":{"A":"Hobnail liver","B":"Nutmeg liver","C":"Bronze liver","D":"Green liver","E":"Zahn infarct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ischemic or congestive injury causes centrilobular necrosis and a mottled gross appearance termed nutmeg liver. A hobnail surface fits cirrhosis, bronze color fits iron overload, green color fits cholestasis, and a Zahn infarct is not the described pattern. Lines of Zahn are laminations of thrombi formed in flowing blood, not a feature of hepatic congestion. First Aid ties the bronze diabetes triad of cirrhosis, diabetes, and skin pigmentation to hemochromatosis, whereas nutmeg liver reflects chronic passive congestion. First Aid describes nutmeg liver as the mottled gross appearance of chronic passive congestion from right heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome, not a hobnail liver. First Aid describes nutmeg liver as the mottled gross appearance of chronic passive congestion from right heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome, not a green liver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2333).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is affected first by ischemia because it is least oxygenated, as in congestive hepatopathy (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and chronic alcohol use as zone 3 injuries (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Lines of Zahn are laminations of thrombi formed in flowing blood, not a feature of hepatic congestion.","C":"First Aid ties the bronze diabetes triad of cirrhosis, diabetes, and skin pigmentation to hemochromatosis, whereas nutmeg liver reflects chronic passive congestion.","A":"First Aid describes nutmeg liver as the mottled gross appearance of chronic passive congestion from right heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome, not a hobnail liver.","D":"First Aid describes nutmeg liver as the mottled gross appearance of chronic passive congestion from right heart failure or Budd-Chiari syndrome, not a green liver."}},{"id":"sdl12_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":1,"stem":"What overall shape does the liver acinus have?","choices":{"A":"Diamond shaped","B":"Hexagonal prism","C":"Spherical cluster","D":"Cylindrical tube","E":"Rectangular block"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The liver acinus is a diamond or lozenge shaped functional unit defined along microvascular lines. The hexagonal prism is the classic lobule. Spherical, cylindrical, and rectangular shapes are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, structure of the liver acinus, line 2312).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 2 has intermediate oxygen tension and is the zone affected by yellow fever (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: the classic lobule is a hexagon with portal tracts at its six angles (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"Hepatic sinusoids differ from other sinusoids because a second cell type regularly forms part of the vessel lining. Which cell is it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Vascular pericytes","D":"Kupffer cells","E":"Smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Besides the thin, discontinuous endothelium with large fenestrae, the hepatic sinusoid lining regularly includes Kupffer cells, resident macrophages whose processes may span the lumen. Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space of Disse outside the lining. Cholangiocytes line bile ductules and ducts, not blood vessels. Vascular pericytes are not described as part of the sinusoid lining. Smooth muscle cells are absent from sinusoid walls. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18, hepatic sinusoids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids are lined by discontinuous fenestrated endothelium plus Kupffer cells, over a perisinusoidal space (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: this open lining lets plasma proteins and lipoproteins move freely between hepatocytes and blood.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells sit in the space","B":"Cholangiocytes line bile ductules and ducts, not","C":"Vascular pericytes are not described as part","E":"Smooth muscle cells are absent from sinusoid"}},{"id":"sdl12_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"Hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse. What is their main role?","choices":{"A":"Carrying bile into the canaliculi","B":"Anchoring stellate cells to the wall","C":"Widening the plasma exchange area","D":"Trapping bacteria from portal blood","E":"Moving lymph into the space of Mall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Microvilli from the basal surface of hepatocytes enlarge the surface available for exchange between hepatocytes and plasma, and because the endothelium has gaps and no continuous basal lamina, plasma reaches the hepatocyte membrane almost freely. Carrying bile into the canaliculi is a function of the apical canalicular membrane, not the sinusoidal microvilli. Anchoring stellate cells to the wall is not a microvillus function. Trapping bacteria from portal blood is the job of Kupffer cells. Moving lymph into the space of Mall happens by fluid flow, not microvillus action. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18, perisinusoidal space.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the space of Disse is the site of exchange between blood and hepatocytes, aided by hepatocyte microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: collagen deposition in this space in cirrhosis impairs that exchange.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Carrying bile into the canaliculi is a","B":"Anchoring stellate cells to the wall is","D":"Trapping bacteria from portal blood is the","E":"Moving lymph into the space of Mall"}},{"id":"sdl12_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":1,"stem":"Excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into which space to form hepatic lymph?","choices":{"A":"Space of Mall","B":"Space of Retzius","C":"Bile canaliculi","D":"Central vein lumen","E":"Canals of Hering"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess fluid in the space of Disse drains into the periportal spaces of Mall and forms hepatic lymph. The space of Retzius is retropubic. Bile canaliculi and the canals of Hering carry bile. The central vein carries blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2362–2363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocytes take up glucose, amino acids, lipids, and toxins from plasma in the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"Hepatic sinusoids differ from sinusoids elsewhere because a second cell type, besides the fenestrated endothelium, forms a regular part of the vessel lining. Which cell is it?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cell","B":"Kupffer cell","C":"Cholangiocyte","D":"Hepatoblast","E":"Mast cell"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic sinusoids differ from other sinusoids in that the stellate sinusoidal macrophage, or Kupffer cell, is a regular part of the vessel lining, where it phagocytoses particles and aged cells from the blood. Hepatic stellate cells lie in the perisinusoidal space of Disse, outside the lining, where they store vitamin A in lipid droplets. Cholangiocytes line the bile ducts, not the blood sinusoids. Hepatoblasts are the embryonic precursors of hepatocytes and of the cells lining the bile ducts. Mast cells sit in connective tissue and are not part of the sinusoidal wall. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18 (hepatic sinusoids; stellate sinusoidal macrophages; hepatic stellate cells); Langman Ch. 15 (liver bud).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells are the resident macrophages lining the hepatic sinusoids and remove particles and aged blood cells from portal blood (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: in chronic liver injury it is the hepatic stellate cell in the space of Disse, not the Kupffer cell, that becomes a collagen-producing myofibroblast.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells lie in the perisinusoidal space","C":"Cholangiocytes line the bile ducts, not the","D":"Hepatoblasts are the embryonic precursors of hepatocytes","E":"Mast cells sit in connective tissue and"}},{"id":"sdl12_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"In what chemical form is dietary vitamin A stored in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Free retinol","C":"Beta carotene","D":"Retinyl esters","E":"Retinal aldehyde"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Quiescent hepatic stellate cells take up and esterify dietary vitamin A and store it as retinyl esters in cytoplasmic lipid droplets. Retinol is released bound to retinol binding protein when blood levels fall. Retinal acts in the retina, where 11-cis-retinal binds opsin, and neither it nor beta carotene is the esterified form held in stellate cell lipid droplets. Retinoic acid is the active vitamin A form that regulates gene transcription through nuclear retinoid receptors, not the stored form. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, vitamin A storage, lines 2384–2388); Pawlina Ch. 18 (hepatic stellate cells: retinyl esters; 11-cis-retinal binds opsin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells store vitamin A when quiescent and produce extracellular matrix when activated (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: activated stellate cells are responsible for hepatic fibrosis (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Retinoic acid is the active vitamin A form that regulates gene transcription through nuclear retinoid receptors, not the stored form.","B":"Retinol is released bound to retinol binding protein when blood levels fall.","C":"Neither it nor beta carotene is the esterified form held in stellate cell lipid droplets.","E":"Retinal acts in the retina, where 11-cis-retinal binds opsin, and neither it nor beta carotene is the esterified form held in stellate cell lipid droplets."}},{"id":"sdl12_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which two cytokines drive fibrogenic activation in chronic liver injury?","choices":{"A":"IL 2 and IL 4","B":"IFN gamma and IL 12","C":"TGF beta and PDGF","D":"IL 5 and IL 13","E":"GM CSF and IL 3"},"correct":"C","explanation":"TGF beta and PDGF released by activated Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes turn stellate cells into proliferative, fibrogenic myofibroblasts. The other pairs are lymphocyte or hematopoietic cytokines not described here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2393).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells release IL 1, IL 6, TNF alpha, and IL 8 in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: zone 3 is usually affected first in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IL 2 and IL 4 are not named.","B":"IFN gamma and IL 12 are not named.","D":"IL 5 and IL 13 are not named.","E":"GM CSF and IL 3 are not named."}},{"id":"sdl12_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"Bile leaves the lobule through interlobular ducts that enlarge toward the porta hepatis. How does their lining change along the way?","choices":{"A":"Ciliated cells appear on the surface","B":"Cuboidal cells become columnar","C":"Stratified squamous cells appear","D":"The cells flatten to squamous","E":"Transitional epithelium appears"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Interlobular bile ducts in the portal triads are lined by cholangiocytes that are cuboidal near the lobules and gradually become columnar as the ducts approach the porta hepatis, with well-developed microvilli. Ciliated cells appearing on the surface is not a feature of bile ducts. Stratified squamous cells line the esophagus and skin, not the biliary tree. Cells flattening to squamous would be the reverse of the real change. Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18, intrahepatic bile ducts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile flows from canaliculi to canals of Hering to interlobular ducts, whose cholangiocytes go from cuboidal to columnar (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: diseases such as PBC target the small interlobular ducts in portal tracts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ciliated cells appearing on the surface is","C":"Stratified squamous cells line the esophagus and","D":"Cells flattening to squamous would be the","E":"Transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract."}},{"id":"sdl12_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"Which short channels at the lobule margin are lined by both hepatocytes and cuboidal cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi","B":"Interlobular ducts","C":"Canals of Hering","D":"Spaces of Mall","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Canaliculi empty into canals of Hering at the lobule margin, which are uniquely lined by both hepatocytes and cholangiocytes and have contractile activity. Canaliculi are lined only by hepatocyte membranes. Interlobular ducts are fully cholangiocyte lined. The spaces of Mall hold lymph, and sublobular veins carry blood. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, intrahepatic biliary pathway, lines 2409–2412).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile ductules collect bile from the canals of Hering and are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: ductules pass into the periportal space before joining interlobular ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do the right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Gallbladder neck","C":"Minor papilla","D":"Caudate lobe","E":"Porta hepatis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right and left hepatic ducts unite at the porta hepatis to form the common hepatic duct. The ampulla of Vater and minor papilla open into the duodenum, the gallbladder neck leads to the cystic duct, and the caudate lobe is a liver lobe. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, major hepatic ducts, lines 2417–2420).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the portal triad of the lobule holds a hepatic arteriole, an interlobular vein, and a bile ductule (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture). COMLEX: tight junctions on hepatocytes prevent mixing of bile with sinusoidal blood (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic microarchitecture).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a liver biopsy. At one angle of a hexagonal lobule, the pathologist identifies branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery next to an interlobular bile duct. Which other structures normally run with these?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoids and Kupffer cells","B":"Lymphatics and nerves","C":"Lymph nodes and ganglia","D":"Hepatic venules","E":"Smooth muscle bundles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A portal triad holds terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery and an interlobular bile duct, along with lymphatic vessels and nerve fibers. Sinusoids and Kupffer cells lie between the plates. Lymph nodes and ganglia are not in portal tracts. Hepatic venules lie at the lobule center. Smooth muscle bundles are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, portal triad components, lines 2291–2293).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the portal vein, hepatic artery, bile ducts, and lymphatics in the portal triad (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: the classic lobule is about 1 to 2 mm across (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 37-year-old woman has a liver transplant, and her hepatic artery thromboses while portal flow continues. Which feature of hepatic inflow has she mainly lost?","choices":{"A":"Nutrient rich blood","B":"Gut derived toxins","C":"Splenic venous return","D":"Oxygen rich blood","E":"Most of total flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The hepatic artery brings oxygen rich blood and 25 to 30 percent of flow, while the portal vein brings most of the flow as deoxygenated, nutrient rich blood from the GI tract and spleen. Losing the artery removes mainly the oxygen rich supply. Gut derived blood and splenic venous return both travel in the portal vein, which still flows. The portal vein, which still flows, carries most of total flow (70 to 75 percent); the hepatic artery supplies only 25 to 30 percent. Pawlina states gut-absorbed toxic materials reach the liver in hepatic portal vein blood, whereas the hepatic artery supplies the oxygenated blood lost here. Pawlina states splenic and other abdominal venous blood reaches the liver through the hepatic portal vein, whereas the hepatic artery supplies the oxygenated blood lost here. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow pathway, lines 2299–2303).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic artery blood passes through hepatic arterioles and sinusoids to the central vein (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, flow of blood and bile). COMLEX: blood from both inflow vessels opens into sinusoids at the lobule periphery (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gut derived blood and splenic venous return both travel in the portal vein, which still flows.","E":"The portal vein, which still flows, carries most of total flow (70 to 75 percent); the hepatic artery supplies only 25 to 30 percent.","B":"Pawlina states gut-absorbed toxic materials reach the liver in hepatic portal vein blood, whereas the hepatic artery supplies the oxygenated blood lost here.","C":"Pawlina states splenic and other abdominal venous blood reaches the liver through the hepatic portal vein, whereas the hepatic artery supplies the oxygenated blood lost here."}},{"id":"sdl12_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has contrast injected into a small portal vein branch during angiography. Where does the contrast first enter the hepatic sinusoids?","choices":{"A":"Lobule center","B":"Space of Disse","C":"Lobule periphery","D":"Bile canaliculi","E":"Sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Terminal branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery open into the sinusoids at the periphery of the lobule, and blood then flows centripetally toward the central vein. The center is where blood leaves. The space of Disse holds plasma outside the sinusoid, canaliculi carry bile, and sublobular veins drain the lobule. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 1, hepatic blood flow and centripetal flow, lines 2302–2306).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed blood flows centripetally from the portal triads toward the central vein (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: the hepatic veins drain into the inferior vena cava (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portocaval collaterals","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has cardiogenic shock for six hours. Two days later his aminotransferases are markedly raised. A liver biopsy is taken. Which pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Periportal necrosis","B":"Midzonal necrosis","C":"Portal tract fibrosis","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Pericentral necrosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Zone 3 hepatocytes receive blood already depleted of oxygen, so shock and hypotension cause centrilobular or pericentral necrosis. Periportal injury fits direct toxins or viral hepatitis. Midzonal necrosis fits yellow fever. Fibrosis and interface hepatitis are chronic patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, ischemic susceptibility, lines 2327–2331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ischemic injury such as Budd Chiari syndrome among zone 3 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: ischemic hepatitis follows hypotension, shock, or heart failure (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow and ischemic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has acute viral hepatitis, and a liver biopsy shows the most intense inflammation in one part of the acinus. Which part is it most likely?","choices":{"A":"Subcapsular region","B":"Midzonal zone 2","C":"Central vein wall","D":"Periportal zone 1","E":"Sublobular septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Direct acting toxins and viral hepatitis often injure the periportal zone 1, which meets incoming blood first. Zone 2 is the yellow fever pattern. The subcapsular region, the central vein wall, and sublobular septa are not described sites. The pericentral zone 3 around the central vein is the least oxygenated region and the classic site of ischemic and acetaminophen necrosis, not viral periportal injury. First Aid states the periportal zone I is affected first by viral hepatitis, not the subcapsular region. First Aid states the periportal zone I is affected first by viral hepatitis, not the sublobular septa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2338–2340).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists autoimmune hepatitis and alpha 1 antitrypsin deficiency among zone 1 lesions (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: zone 1 is best oxygenated and most resistant to circulatory compromise (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Zone 2 is the yellow fever pattern.","C":"The pericentral zone 3 around the central vein is the least oxygenated region and the classic site of ischemic and acetaminophen necrosis, not viral periportal injury.","A":"The subcapsular region is not described sites.","E":"Sublobular septa is not described sites."}},{"id":"sdl12_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man is exposed to carbon tetrachloride at work, and a liver biopsy shows necrosis of the hepatocytes around the central veins. Which property of these hepatocytes best explains their injury?","choices":{"A":"High P450 enzyme content","B":"Highest oxygen tension","C":"Absent Kupffer cells","D":"Thin sinusoidal flow","E":"Densest bile canaliculi network"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zone 3 has high concentrations of cytochrome P450 enzymes, so toxins that need bioactivation, such as carbon tetrachloride and acetaminophen, form reactive metabolites there and cause centrilobular necrosis. Zone 3 has the lowest, not highest, oxygen. Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow, and canaliculi do not explain the zone. Pawlina notes zone 1 cells are the first to receive oxygen from sinusoidal blood, so the highest oxygen tension is periportal rather than centrilobular. First Aid states pericentral zone III hepatocytes have the highest cytochrome P-450 concentration and are most sensitive to metabolic toxins such as CCl4, not an absence of Kupffer cells. First Aid states pericentral zone III hepatocytes have the highest cytochrome P-450 concentration and are most sensitive to metabolic toxins such as CCl4, not thin sinusoidal flow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 2, toxic susceptibility, lines 2334–2338).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zone 3 is the main site of drug detoxification by CYP450 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists halothane, CCl4, and rifampin as zone 3 toxins (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatic zones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Kupffer cells, sinusoidal flow, and canaliculi do not explain the zone.","B":"Pawlina notes zone 1 cells are the first to receive oxygen from sinusoidal blood, so the highest oxygen tension is periportal rather than centrilobular.","C":"Kupffer cells do not explain the zone.","D":"Sinusoidal flow do not explain the zone."}},{"id":"sdl12_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has normal liver function, and his hepatocytes make albumin continuously. By which route does newly made albumin reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Bile canaliculi to ducts","B":"Lymph to thoracic duct","C":"Canals of Hering to hepatic veins","D":"Space of Disse to sinusoid","E":"Central vein directly"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes release albumin, clotting factors, and lipoproteins into the space of Disse, and these enter sinusoidal blood through the fenestrated, gapped endothelium. Canaliculi and canals of Hering carry bile, lymph carries only excess Disse fluid, and hepatocytes do not secrete straight into the central vein. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2362).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the basolateral surface of hepatocytes faces the sinusoids (First Aid 2025 GI liver tissue architecture). COMLEX: large macromolecules and plasma proteins pass freely from the sinusoid into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has a research liver study showing that large lipoproteins pass freely between sinusoidal plasma and hepatocytes. Besides large fenestrae and wide cell gaps, which feature allows this?","choices":{"A":"Tight endothelial junctions","B":"Thick glycocalyx layer","C":"Diaphragm covered pores","D":"Pericyte wrapped capillaries","E":"No continuous basal lamina"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The sinusoidal endothelium has large fenestrae without diaphragms, wide intercellular gaps, and no continuous basal lamina, so even large macromolecules pass freely into the space of Disse. Tight junctions, a thick glycocalyx, diaphragms, and pericyte coverage would restrict exchange. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, discontinuous endothelium and exchange, lines 2345–2358).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic sinusoids are discontinuous capillaries (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: hepatocyte microvilli project into the space of Disse (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Tight junctions, a thick glycocalyx, diaphragms, and pericyte coverage would restrict exchange.","A":"Tight junctions would restrict exchange.","B":"A thick glycocalyx would restrict exchange.","D":"Pericyte coverage would restrict exchange."}},{"id":"sdl12_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a biopsy showing dense collagen in the perisinusoidal space and sinusoids that have lost their fenestrae. Which hepatocyte function is most directly impaired by this change?","choices":{"A":"Bile acid conjugation","B":"Plasma exchange","C":"Glycogen synthesis","D":"Drug glucuronidation","E":"Ammonia to urea"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Collagen in the space of Disse with loss of fenestrae, called sinusoidal capillarization, blocks the free passage of plasma between sinusoids and hepatocytes. Conjugation, glycogen synthesis, glucuronidation, and the urea cycle are intracellular functions, not the exchange step this structure provides. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 3, plasma hepatocyte exchange, lines 2355–2358); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, sinusoidal capillarization, lines 2394–2396).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cirrhosis features bridging fibrosis from activated stellate cells and regenerative nodules (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis). COMLEX: activated stellate cells contract sinusoids and contribute to portal hypertension (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — fibrosis in chronic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bile-acid conjugation is an intracellular hepatocyte function, not the exchange lost when sinusoids lose their fenestrae.","E":"The urea cycle is an intracellular hepatocyte function, not the exchange lost when sinusoids lose their fenestrae."}},{"id":"sdl12_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has a gut barrier defect that lets bacteria and endotoxin into his portal blood. Which liver cells are the first line of defense against them?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Endothelial cells","D":"Pit cells","E":"Kupffer cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kupffer cells are resident sinusoidal macrophages that act as the first line of defense against gut bacteria, endotoxin, and particles in portal blood, recognizing PAMPs with pattern recognition receptors. Stellate cells store vitamin A and make collagen. Cholangiocytes line ducts. Endothelial cells line sinusoids. Pit cells are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, phagocytosis and innate defense, lines 2371–2375).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells make up 80 to 90 percent of the tissue macrophages in the body (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: Kupffer cells recognize endotoxin with pattern recognition receptors (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with chronic hepatitis has a biopsy in which perisinusoidal cells have lost their lipid droplets, express smooth muscle actin, and lay down type I and type III collagen. Which cell type is this?","choices":{"A":"Kupffer cells","B":"Cholangiocytes","C":"Stellate cells","D":"Endothelial cells","E":"Pit cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Activated hepatic stellate cells lose their vitamin A lipid droplets, become myofibroblasts with alpha smooth muscle actin, and deposit type I and type III collagen in the space of Disse, causing fibrosis. Kupffer cells release the activating cytokines but do not make this matrix. Cholangiocytes, endothelial cells, and pit cells are not the fibrogenic cell. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, activation and collagen deposition, lines 2389–2398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stellate cells sit in the space of Disse between hepatocytes and endothelial cells (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: chronic liver injury, reactive oxygen species, and cytokines activate stellate cells (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman has chronic hemolysis, and her Kupffer cells take up many senescent red cells. After hemoglobin breakdown, how is the released iron sent back to the blood?","choices":{"A":"Through ferroportin","B":"Through DMT1 channels","C":"Through hepcidin pores","D":"Through hemopexin","E":"Through haptoglobin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Kupffer cells break down hemoglobin and store iron in ferritin or export it through ferroportin to bind transferrin for erythropoiesis. DMT1 is an apical iron importer, hepcidin regulates ferroportin rather than forming pores, and hemopexin and haptoglobin bind heme and hemoglobin in plasma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 4, iron recycling, lines 2376–2380).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Kupffer cells store iron safely in ferritin (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: exported iron binds transferrin for systemic erythropoiesis (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — hemochromatosis and iron overload","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"DMT1 is an apical iron importer, not the route that returns Kupffer-cell iron to the blood.","C":"Hepcidin does not form pores that export iron from Kupffer cells.","D":"Hemopexin binds heme in plasma; it does not return iron from Kupffer cells to the blood.","E":"Haptoglobin binds hemoglobin in plasma; it does not return iron from Kupffer cells to the blood."}},{"id":"sdl12_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has a liver biopsy, and the histology instructor traces bile flow within a classic lobule. In which direction does bile move?","choices":{"A":"Toward central veins","B":"Into sinusoids","C":"Toward portal triads","D":"Into space of Disse","E":"Into sublobular veins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile flows centrifugally, starting near the central vein and moving outward to the portal triads, opposite to the centripetal flow of blood toward the central vein. Bile does not enter sinusoids, the space of Disse, or sublobular veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, directional contrast, lines 2421–2425).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile and blood flow in opposite directions in the lobule (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: interlobular ducts converge into larger intrahepatic ducts (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl12_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has an autoimmune disease that destroys the small interlobular bile ducts. Where are these ducts located in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Around central veins","B":"Within portal triads","C":"In space of Disse","D":"Between hepatocyte plates","E":"In sublobular septa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bile ductules join interlobular bile ducts within the portal triads of the portal canals. The central veins carry blood, the space of Disse holds plasma, canaliculi rather than ducts run between hepatocytes, and sublobular septa are not described. Central veins drain sinusoidal blood at the lobule center and do not carry interlobular bile ducts. The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium and contains no bile ducts. Between hepatocyte plates run bile canaliculi, not interlobular bile ducts. Pawlina notes sublobular and hepatic veins travel alone, separate from the portal triad members. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, interlobular bile ducts, lines 2415–2416).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interlobular bile ducts run in the portal canals with the portal vein and hepatic artery branches (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: intrahepatic bile ductules are lined completely by cholangiocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary cholangitis and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Central veins drain sinusoidal blood at the lobule center and do not carry interlobular bile ducts.","C":"The space of Disse lies between hepatocytes and sinusoidal endothelium and contains no bile ducts.","D":"Between hepatocyte plates run bile canaliculi, not interlobular bile ducts.","E":"Pawlina notes sublobular and hepatic veins travel alone, separate from the portal triad members."}},{"id":"sdl12_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a cholestatic drug injury in which bile leaks from canaliculi into the blood. Which structures normally seal bile canaliculi from the vascular space?","choices":{"A":"Tight junctions","B":"Desmosomes","C":"Gap junctions","D":"Hemidesmosomes","E":"Adherens junctions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between hepatocytes and are sealed from the vascular space by tight junctions. Desmosomes and adherens junctions anchor cells, gap junctions allow small molecule passage, and hemidesmosomes anchor cells to the basal lamina. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 12, section 5, bile synthesis and canaliculi, lines 2403–2407).","boardPrep":"Step 1: canals of Hering have contractile activity that propels bile (lecture SDL 12). COMLEX: canaliculi are formed by apposed grooves between adjacent hepatocytes (lecture SDL 12).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with polycythemia vera has 2 weeks of right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, and new ascites. Liver biopsy shows marked sinusoidal congestion and hepatocyte loss around the central veins, while the parenchyma around the portal tracts looks normal. Doppler shows no flow in the hepatic veins. Obstruction at which other site would produce the same biopsy pattern?","choices":{"A":"Main portal vein trunk","B":"Common hepatic artery","C":"Suprahepatic vena cava","D":"Splenic vein at the hilum","E":"Superior mesenteric vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Blood leaves the sinusoids through the central vein, then the sublobular veins, the hepatic veins, and the inferior vena cava, so a block in the vena cava above the hepatic vein openings backs blood into the central veins and zone 3 sinusoids just as hepatic vein thrombosis does. The main portal vein trunk carries inflow, so its occlusion causes portal hypertension without centrilobular congestion. The common hepatic artery supplies only about a quarter of inflow, and its loss does not congest the central veins. Splenic vein thrombosis raises pressure in splenic and gastric veins upstream of the liver and spares the hepatic sinusoids. Superior mesenteric vein occlusion blocks gut venous return before the liver and threatens the bowel rather than congesting zone 3. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 1 and SDL 17 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hepatic blood flows from portal triad to sinusoid to central vein to sublobular and hepatic veins to the IVC, so any outflow block congests zone 3 (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Budd-Chiari syndrome presents with tender hepatomegaly and ascites, and a hypercoagulable state such as polycythemia vera should be sought.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — hepatic venous outflow obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The main portal vein trunk carries inflow,","B":"The common hepatic artery supplies only about","D":"Splenic vein thrombosis raises pressure in splenic","E":"Superior mesenteric vein occlusion blocks gut venous"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare zones 1, 2, and 3 of the liver acinus based on blood supply and oxygenation, and predict their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury, including centrilobular (zone 3) injury.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old man returns from a jungle trip in the Amazon basin, for which he received no pretravel vaccines. He has high fever, backache, jaundice, bleeding gums, and has vomited black material. AST is 2,400 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Liver biopsy shows eosinophilic apoptotic hepatocyte bodies. In which part of the hepatic acinus is the injury most concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Periportal zone 1","B":"Pericentral zone 3","C":"Portal tract bile ducts","D":"Whole lobule evenly","E":"Midzonal zone 2"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fever, jaundice, hemorrhage, and black vomit after unvaccinated travel to the Amazon point to yellow fever, a mosquito-borne flavivirus whose liver injury, marked by Councilman bodies, falls mainly on the intermediate zone 2 of the acinus. Periportal zone 1 is hit first by viral hepatitis and by ingested toxins such as cocaine. Pericentral zone 3 is the site of ischemic injury and of P-450 activated toxins such as acetaminophen. Portal tract bile ducts are the target of PBC and PSC, which cause cholestasis rather than hemorrhagic hepatitis. Even injury of the whole lobule describes massive necrosis in acute liver failure, not the zonal pattern of this infection. Sources: First Aid 2025 pp. 168, 374; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 2.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zone 1 is injured by viral hepatitis and ingested toxins, zone 2 by yellow fever, and zone 3 by ischemia and P-450 activated toxins (First Aid p. 374). COMLEX: Yellow fever is prevented by a live attenuated vaccine recommended before travel to endemic areas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — hepatic injury, scarring, repair, and liver failure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Periportal zone 1 is hit first by","B":"Pericentral zone 3 is the site of","C":"Portal tract bile ducts are the target","D":"Even injury of the whole lobule describes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the structure of hepatic sinusoids and the space of Disse and explain how the discontinuous sinusoidal endothelium facilitates exchange between plasma and hepatocytes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with constrictive pericarditis has jugular venous distention, tender hepatomegaly, and ascites. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and plasma total protein is 6.8 g/dL (reference range: 6.0–8.3 g/dL). Paracentesis shows ascitic fluid total protein of 5.0 g/dL. Which feature of his liver best explains why the ascitic fluid is so rich in protein?","choices":{"A":"Portal capillaries leak from high pressure","B":"Sinusoidal lining is freely permeable","C":"Peritoneal mesothelium secretes protein","D":"Hepatic lymphatics are blocked by scar","E":"Plasma oncotic pressure is very low"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic sinusoids have large fenestrae without diaphragms, wide gaps, and no continuous basal lamina, so plasma proteins pass freely into the space of Disse and hepatic lymph carries about 6 g/dL of protein. When hepatic venous pressure rises, this nearly plasma-like fluid weeps from the liver surface into the peritoneal cavity. Portal capillaries leaking from high pressure would give a protein-poor transudate, because ordinary gut capillaries hold back most plasma protein. Peritoneal mesothelium does not secrete protein; it lines the cavity that receives the fluid. Hepatic lymphatics blocked by scar are not a feature of pericardial constriction, and his hepatic lymph flow is increased. Very low plasma oncotic pressure would require low albumin, but his serum albumin is normal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 71 (hepatic lymph and ascites); Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 3.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hepatic sinusoids are discontinuous, with large undiaphragmed fenestrae and no basal lamina, so hepatic lymph is nearly as protein rich as plasma (Guyton Ch. 71). COMLEX: Protein-rich ascites with a congested liver points to raised hepatic venous pressure, as in heart failure or constrictive pericarditis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 2 Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal capillaries leaking from high pressure would","C":"Peritoneal mesothelium does not secrete protein; it","D":"Hepatic lymphatics blocked by scar are not","E":"Very low plasma oncotic pressure would require"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the major functions of Kupffer cells and hepatic stellate (Ito) cells, including their roles in phagocytosis, iron recycling, vitamin A storage, and collagen deposition during hepatic fibrosis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C has esophageal varices and splenomegaly. Liver biopsy shows bridging fibrosis, and the perisinusoidal cells in the space of Disse have lost their vitamin A lipid droplets and taken on a myofibroblast appearance. Besides laying down collagen, which action of these cells adds directly to his portal hypertension?","choices":{"A":"Phagocytosis of portal endotoxin","B":"Release of stored retinol into blood","C":"Export of iron through ferroportin","D":"Contraction that narrows sinusoids","E":"Secretion of albumin into Disse"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activated hepatic stellate cells become myofibroblasts with contractile alpha smooth muscle actin, and their contraction constricts the sinusoids, raising intrahepatic resistance on top of the collagen they deposit in the space of Disse. Phagocytosis of portal endotoxin is the job of Kupffer cells, which line the sinusoid lumen. Release of stored retinol bound to retinol binding protein is a function of quiescent stellate cells, which these activated cells no longer perform. Export of iron through ferroportin is how Kupffer cells return recycled red cell iron to transferrin. Secretion of albumin into the space of Disse is a hepatocyte function and does not change sinusoidal resistance. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 4 and SDL 15 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Activated stellate cells lose vitamin A, deposit type I and III collagen in the space of Disse, and contract to narrow sinusoids (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Portal hypertension in cirrhosis has both a fixed fibrotic part and a dynamic contractile part.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile salt micelles needed to absorb fat-soluble vitamins such as A and D","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Phagocytosis of portal endotoxin is the job","B":"Release of stored retinol bound to retinol","C":"Export of iron through ferroportin is how","E":"Secretion of albumin into the space of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Trace bile flow from hepatocytes through bile canaliculi and the intrahepatic bile ducts, and contrast the direction of bile flow with hepatic blood flow.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old bodybuilder who injects anabolic steroids has 3 weeks of itching and jaundice. ALP is 410 U/L (reference range: 40–120 U/L) and ALT is 60 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). Liver biopsy shows bland cholestasis with bile plugs in zone 3 and very little inflammation. The channels holding these plugs have walls formed by what?","choices":{"A":"Cuboidal cholangiocyte epithelium","B":"Hepatocytes mixed with cholangiocytes","C":"Grooves of adjacent hepatocytes","D":"Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelium","E":"Stellate cell processes in Disse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anabolic steroids cause bland cholestasis with canalicular bile plugs in zone 3, and bile canaliculi have no lining of their own: their walls are apposed grooves in neighboring hepatocytes sealed by tight junctions. Cuboidal cholangiocyte epithelium lines ductules and interlobular ducts, where the ductular plugs of sepsis sit. Hepatocytes mixed with cholangiocytes form the canals of Hering at the lobule margin, not the channels between zone 3 hepatocytes. Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelium lines blood channels that carry blood toward the central vein and never hold bile. Stellate cell processes lie in the space of Disse and store vitamin A rather than bounding any bile channel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 5 and SDL 15 section 5; Pawlina Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bile canaliculi are intercellular channels formed by apposed hepatocyte membranes sealed by tight junctions, draining to canals of Hering and portal ducts (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: Oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids cause bland canalicular cholestasis with itching and a high ALP.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation and composition of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cuboidal cholangiocyte epithelium lines ductules and interlobular","B":"Hepatocytes mixed with cholangiocytes form the canals","D":"Fenestrated sinusoidal endothelium lines blood channels that","E":"Stellate cell processes lie in the space"}},{"id":"trial_sdl12_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the organization of the classic hepatic lobule and trace hepatic blood flow, including the portal triad, hepatic sinusoids, hepatocyte plates, and central vein.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with a JAK2-positive myeloproliferative neoplasm vomits blood from esophageal varices. Imaging shows chronic thrombosis of the extrahepatic portal vein and a normal-appearing liver. Albumin is 4.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), INR is 1.0 (reference range: 0.8–1.2), and ALT is 28 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). Which feature best explains why his hepatocytes remain healthy?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic artery carries most inflow","B":"Hepatic veins reverse flow into lobules","C":"Splenic vein blood enters the sinusoids","D":"Hepatocytes switch to anaerobic metabolism","E":"Arterial blood still reaches sinusoids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The liver has a dual inflow: the hepatic artery brings about 25% to 30% of flow as oxygen-rich blood, and its terminal branches open into the same peripheral sinusoids as the portal vein, so arterial flow keeps hepatocytes alive while the blocked portal vein causes varices. Hepatic artery dominance is a misconception, because the portal vein normally supplies about 70% to 75% of inflow. The hepatic veins do not reverse flow into lobules; they carry blood away from the central veins to the vena cava. Splenic vein blood joins the portal vein before the liver, so the same thrombus blocks it. Anaerobic metabolism cannot sustain hepatocytes, which depend on oxygen delivered through the sinusoids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 12 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18 (portal vein obstruction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The portal vein gives about 70% to 75% of hepatic inflow and the hepatic artery about 25% to 30%, and both empty into sinusoids at the lobule periphery (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Extrahepatic portal vein thrombosis presents with varices and portal hypertension rather than liver failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic artery dominance is a misconception, because","B":"The hepatic veins do not reverse flow","C":"Splenic vein blood joins the portal vein","D":"Anaerobic metabolism cannot sustain hepatocytes, which depend"}}]},{"sdlNumber":13,"title":"SDL 13 — Histology of the Gallbladder, and pancreas","questions":[{"id":"sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"What type of epithelium lines the gallbladder mucosa?","choices":{"A":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","B":"Stratified squamous epithelium","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium","D":"Simple columnar epithelium","E":"Transitional epithelium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The gallbladder is lined by a single layer of tall columnar absorptive cells with short apical microvilli, apical tight junctions, and abundant mitochondria. Cuboidal, stratified squamous, pseudostratified ciliated, and transitional linings are not described. Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and ends at the esophagogastric junction, not the gallbladder. Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi, not the gallbladder. Transitional epithelium (urothelium) lines the urinary passages from the calyces to the bladder, not the gallbladder. Cuboidal cholangiocytes line the small interlobular bile ducts near the lobules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, simple columnar epithelium, lines 2428–2431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have apical tight junctions that seal the lumen (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the gallbladder has a fundus, body, infundibulum, and neck (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary anatomy).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium lines the esophagus and ends at the esophagogastric junction, not the gallbladder.","C":"Pseudostratified ciliated epithelium lines the trachea and bronchi, not the gallbladder.","E":"Transitional epithelium (urothelium) lines the urinary passages from the calyces to the bladder, not the gallbladder.","A":"Cuboidal cholangiocytes line the small interlobular bile ducts near the lobules."}},{"id":"sdl13_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"Which two layers are missing from the gallbladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis externa and serosa","B":"Muscularis mucosae and submucosa","C":"Lamina propria and adventitia","D":"Epithelium and lamina propria","E":"Adventitia and muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder lacks both a muscularis mucosae and a submucosa, so the lamina propria rests directly on the muscularis externa. It has an epithelium, a lamina propria, a muscularis externa, and an adventitia on the liver side with serosa on the free surface. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, absence of muscularis mucosae and submucosa, lines 2438–2440).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lamina propria of the gallbladder rests directly upon the muscularis externa (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the free surface of the gallbladder is covered by visceral peritoneum (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the gallbladder lamina propria noticeably lack?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic vessels","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Plasma cells","D":"Resident lymphocytes","E":"Small venules"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The gallbladder lamina propria is cellular loose connective tissue rich in fenestrated capillaries, small venules, lymphocytes, and plasma cells, but it noticeably lacks lymphatic vessels. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, lamina propria, lines 2435–2437).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the empty gallbladder mucosa forms deep temporary folds that flatten as the organ fills (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: calculous cholecystitis follows gallstone impaction in the cystic duct and thickens the gallbladder wall (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fenestrated capillaries are not part of that answer.","C":"Plasma cells are not part of that answer.","D":"Resident lymphocytes are not part of that answer.","E":"Small venules are not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl13_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pump on the lateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells drives active electrolyte transport?","choices":{"A":"Hydrogen potassium ATPase","B":"Calcium ATPase pumps","C":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Sodium potassium ATPase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lateral membrane Na+/K+ ATPase pumps drive transport of Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular space. The hydrogen potassium ATPase is the gastric proton pump. Calcium pumps, sodium glucose cotransport, and a chloride bicarbonate exchanger are not named as the driver. The Na+-glucose cotransporter SGLT1 absorbs glucose across the small intestinal apical membrane and is not the gallbladder electrolyte driver. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, active electrolyte transport, lines 2456–2459).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder cells move Na+, Cl- and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular compartment (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: solute build up in the narrow lateral space sets up an osmotic gradient with the lumen (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+-glucose cotransporter SGLT1 absorbs glucose across the small intestinal apical membrane and is not the gallbladder electrolyte driver."}},{"id":"sdl13_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Which membrane water channels carry water across gallbladder epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Aquaporins 1 and 8","B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7","E":"Connexin channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Water crosses the apical and basolateral membranes of gallbladder epithelial cells through aquaporin 1 and aquaporin 8. The other aquaporin pairs are not described for the gallbladder, and connexins form gap junctions. Pawlina states gallbladder epithelial cells express two aquaporin water channels, AQP1 and AQP8, that facilitate rapid passive water movement, not connexin channels. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, aquaporin mediated water absorption, lines 2464–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water movement in the gallbladder is passive and follows the osmotic gradient (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: bile is stored within the gallbladder between meals (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary physiology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Aquaporins 2 and 3 are not the gallbladder pair.","C":"Aquaporins 4 and 5 are not the gallbladder pair.","D":"Aquaporins 0 and 7 are not the gallbladder pair.","E":"Pawlina states gallbladder epithelial cells express two aquaporin water channels, AQP1 and AQP8, that facilitate rapid passive water movement, not connexin channels."}},{"id":"sdl13_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"As the gallbladder concentrates stored bile, which constituents become most concentrated?","choices":{"A":"Sodium, chloride, and water","B":"Bile salts and cholesterol","C":"Bicarbonate, chloride, and water","D":"Sodium and bicarbonate ions","E":"Chloride and bicarbonate ions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder actively absorbs sodium, followed by chloride, water, and most small electrolytes, while bile salts, cholesterol, lecithin, and bilirubin are not reabsorbed and so become highly concentrated. Sodium, chloride, and water are the main substances the mucosa removes. Bicarbonate, chloride, and water are absorbed, so bicarbonate falls in gallbladder bile. Sodium and bicarbonate ions are also absorbed during concentration. Chloride and bicarbonate ions both fall as they are reabsorbed. Sources: Guyton Ch. 65, the gallbladder stores and concentrates bile.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder concentration removes water and electrolytes and leaves bile salts, cholesterol, and lecithin highly concentrated (Guyton Ch. 65). COMLEX: an imbalance of these concentrated components sets the stage for cholesterol stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium, chloride, and water are the main","C":"Bicarbonate, chloride, and water are absorbed, so","D":"Sodium and bicarbonate ions are also absorbed","E":"Chloride and bicarbonate ions both fall as"}},{"id":"sdl13_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"How is the exocrine pancreas classified as a gland?","choices":{"A":"Compound tubuloacinar serous gland","B":"Simple branched tubular mucous gland","C":"Compound tubular mixed mucous gland","D":"Simple coiled tubular gland","E":"Compound alveolar seromucous gland"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The exocrine pancreas is a compound tubuloacinar serous gland divided into ill defined lobules by thin septa from its capsule. Simple branched, simple coiled, mucous, and seromucous patterns describe other glands. The exocrine pancreas is a serous gland whose acinar or tubuloacinar units are built of pyramidal serous cells, so it is not a mucous gland. Pawlina calls the exocrine pancreas a compound tubuloacinar gland with a branching duct network, not a simple coiled tubular gland. Its secretory units are formed only by pyramidal serous cells, and it closely resembles the parotid, so it is a serous rather than a seromucous alveolar gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, overall organization, lines 2478–2479).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the exocrine pancreas makes up the majority of pancreatic mass (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: thin septa from the capsule divide the pancreas into ill defined lobules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Simple branched, simple coiled, mucous, and seromucous patterns describe other glands.","B":"The exocrine pancreas is a serous gland whose acinar or tubuloacinar units are built of pyramidal serous cells, so it is not a mucous gland.","D":"Pawlina calls the exocrine pancreas a compound tubuloacinar gland with a branching duct network, not a simple coiled tubular gland.","E":"Its secretory units are formed only by pyramidal serous cells, and it closely resembles the parotid, so it is a serous rather than a seromucous alveolar gland."}},{"id":"sdl13_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"A pathologist must decide whether a section of serous gland came from the pancreas or the parotid. Which feature found within the acini identifies the pancreas?","choices":{"A":"Basophilic basal cytoplasm","B":"Apical zymogen granules","C":"Pale centroacinar cells","D":"Serous demilunes on mucous cells","E":"Pyramidal secretory cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pale, flattened centroacinar cells at the center of the acinus are the intra-acinar start of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Basophilic basal cytoplasm, apical zymogen granules, and pyramidal secretory cells are shared by serous acini such as those of the parotid. Serous demilunes capping mucous acini belong to mixed glands such as the submandibular gland. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells are a unique feature of the pancreas, p71); Pawlina (serous demilunes of the submandibular gland).","boardPrep":"Step 1: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and secrete an isotonic sodium bicarbonate fluid (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: secretin acts on duct and centroacinar cells through cAMP (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — duct and centroacinar bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":1,"stem":"Pale centroacinar cells mark the intra-acinar start of which ducts?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular ducts","B":"Striated ducts","C":"Main pancreatic duct","D":"Intercalated ducts","E":"Intralobular ducts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Centroacinar cells are the intra-acinar origin of the intercalated ducts, a feature unique to the pancreas. Intralobular and interlobular ducts lie farther downstream, striated ducts belong to salivary glands, and the main duct is the final collecting channel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2491).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic secretions pass from acini to centroacinar cells, intercalated ducts, and pancreatic ducts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: centroacinar cells lack zymogen granules and ergastoplasm and stain faintly (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells release secretin when acid enters the small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Duodenal I cells","B":"Duodenal S cells","C":"Jejunal K cells","D":"Gastric G cells","E":"Ileal L cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells when gastric acid lowers duodenal pH below about 4.5. I cells release CCK, K cells release GIP, G cells release gastrin, and L cells release GLP 1 and PYY. Antral G cells secrete gastrin after a meal in response to small peptides and amino acids, gastric distention, and vagal stimulation, not to acid entering the duodenum. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin source and trigger, lines 2507–2508).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin increases pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and bile secretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: secretin also decreases parietal cell H+ secretion and gastric motility (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Duodenal I cells release CCK when fat and protein enter the bowel, not secretin.","C":"Jejunal K cells release GIP, not secretin.","E":"Ileal L cells release GLP-1 and PYY, not secretin.","D":"Antral G cells secrete gastrin after a meal in response to small peptides and amino acids, gastric distention, and vagal stimulation, not to acid entering the duodenum."}},{"id":"sdl13_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"CCK1 receptors on the pancreatic target cells of CCK activate which signaling pathway?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"IP3 and calcium","D":"JAK STAT signaling","E":"Tyrosine kinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and activates the IP3 and intracellular Ca2+ pathway, causing zymogen granule exocytosis. Cyclic AMP is the secretin pathway in ductal cells. Cyclic GMP, JAK STAT, and tyrosine kinase signaling are not described. Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of the CCK1 receptor. JAK-STAT signaling follows hormone binding to tyrosine kinase-associated receptors such as the growth hormone receptor, not the CCK1 receptor. Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling is the mechanism of insulin and insulin-like growth factors, not of CCK at CCK1 receptors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, CCK target cell and pathway, lines 2519–2520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK increases gallbladder contraction and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, GI hormones). COMLEX: CCK also slows gastric emptying (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of the CCK1 receptor.","D":"JAK-STAT signaling follows hormone binding to tyrosine kinase-associated receptors such as the growth hormone receptor, not the CCK1 receptor.","E":"Receptor tyrosine kinase signaling is the mechanism of insulin and insulin-like growth factors, not of CCK at CCK1 receptors."}},{"id":"sdl13_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which phase accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Cephalic phase","B":"Gastric phase","C":"Interdigestive phase","D":"Oral phase","E":"Intestinal phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Secretin, CCK, and vagal ACh act together during the intestinal phase, which accounts for about 80 percent of total pancreatic secretion. The cephalic and gastric phases contribute less, and the interdigestive and oral phases are not the main period. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, coordinated action, lines 2524–2527).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin and CCK act synergistically with vagal ACh to deliver an alkaline enzyme rich juice (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits the exocrine pancreas (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"The endocrine pancreas is a small part of the gland. How do islets of Langerhans look in an H&E section?","choices":{"A":"Basophilic acini with zymogen granules","B":"Ducts lined by low cuboidal cells","C":"Pale clusters among darker acini","D":"Dense bands of fibrous septa","E":"Large vacuolated fat cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In H&E sections, islets of Langerhans appear as clusters of pale-staining cells surrounded by more intensely staining pancreatic acini, and their cell types cannot be told apart without special methods. Basophilic acini with zymogen granules are the exocrine tissue around the islets. Ducts lined by low cuboidal cells are intercalated or intralobular ducts. Dense bands of fibrous septa divide lobules and are not endocrine. Large vacuolated fat cells are adipose tissue, not islets. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 18, islets of Langerhans.","boardPrep":"Step 1: islets are pale-staining endocrine clusters scattered among exocrine acini, most numerous in the tail (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: islet cell types need immunostaining, which matters when classifying islet tumors.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Basophilic acini with zymogen granules are the","B":"Ducts lined by low cuboidal cells are","D":"Dense bands of fibrous septa divide lobules","E":"Large vacuolated fat cells are adipose tissue"}},{"id":"sdl13_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"Where in a human islet are beta cells mainly located?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Capsule surface","C":"Acinar lumen","D":"Central core","E":"Duct walls"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta cells, about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells, lie mainly in the central core of the islet. Alpha and delta cells lie mainly at the periphery. Capsules, acinar lumens, and duct walls are not islet locations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2541).","boardPrep":"Step 1: beta cells make up about 60 to 70 percent of islet cells and produce insulin (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: islet cells form irregular cords invested by fenestrated capillaries (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":1,"stem":"Which electron microscopic feature marks the 325 nm granules of islet delta cells?","choices":{"A":"Crystalline polyhedral core","B":"Round eccentric core","C":"Clear peripheral halo","D":"Lamellar body","E":"Homogeneous matrix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Delta cell granules are about 325 nm with a homogeneous matrix. Beta cell granules have a crystalline polyhedral core with a clear halo, and alpha cell granules have a dense round eccentric core. Lamellar bodies are not islet granules. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delta cells make up about 5 to 10 percent of islet cells and sit at the islet periphery (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: PP cells make pancreatic polypeptide, which inhibits intestinal motility and pancreatic secretion (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with gallstones and chronic cholecystitis has a cholecystectomy. Histology shows deep mucosal outpouchings that extend through the lamina propria into the muscularis externa. What are these structures?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal crypts of Lieberkuhn","B":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","C":"Submucosal Brunner glands","D":"Periportal canals of Hering","E":"Pancreatic acinar rests"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are deep mucosal invaginations that extend into or through the muscularis externa. They arise from epithelial hyperplasia and herniation under luminal pressure. Crypts of Lieberkuhn and Brunner glands belong to the intestine, canals of Hering belong to the liver, and acinar rests are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, lines 2448–2452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile stagnation in mucosal outpouchings promotes chronic gallbladder inflammation and gallstones (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: a porcelain gallbladder is a calcified wall from chronic cholecystitis (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 4 — chronic cholecystitis and porcelain gallbladder","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Brunner glands are submucosal glands of the duodenum, not gallbladder structures.","D":"Canals of Hering lie at the periphery of hepatic lobules, not in the gallbladder wall.","A":"Crypts of Lieberkühn are simple tubular intestinal glands that reach only down to the muscularis mucosae, whereas these gallbladder outpouchings extend into the muscularis externa.","E":"Pancreatic acinar rests are not described in the gallbladder wall."}},{"id":"sdl13_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and the surgeon dissects the gallbladder off the liver bed. Which layer covers the surface that was attached to the liver?","choices":{"A":"Visceral serosa","B":"Submucosal layer","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Fibrous adventitia","E":"Parietal peritoneum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The attached surface is covered by a connective tissue adventitia carrying large vessels, lymphatics, and nerves, while the free surface has a smooth serosa of visceral peritoneum. The gallbladder lacks a submucosa and a muscularis mucosae, and parietal peritoneum lines the body wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, adventitia versus serosa, lines 2444–2447).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the adventitia on the liver side carries large blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: acalculous cholecystitis is due to stasis, hypoperfusion, or infection in critically ill patients (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his gallbladder squeezes bile into the cystic duct. Which wall component produces this contraction?","choices":{"A":"Interlacing smooth muscle","B":"Circular skeletal muscle","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Myoepithelial cells","E":"Longitudinal skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscularis externa is made of randomly oriented interlacing smooth muscle bundles with elastic and collagen fibers, and its contraction reduces luminal volume to expel bile. The gallbladder has no skeletal muscle or muscularis mucosae, and myoepithelial cells belong to glands such as salivary glands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 1, muscularis externa, lines 2441–2443).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after a fatty meal, CCK triggers gallbladder contraction that can force a stone into the cystic duct (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology). COMLEX: biliary colic worsens after meals because CCK drives gallbladder contraction (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, biliary colic).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her gallbladder changes the bile it stores. By about how much are bile salts concentrated during storage?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"100 to 200 times","C":"4 to 5 times","D":"50 to 60 times","E":"10 to 20 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Removal of about 90 percent of the water concentrates bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin up to 10 to 20 times. The smaller figures understate, and the larger figures overstate, the effect. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, net bile concentration, lines 2472–2474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: most gallstones form within the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: raised cholesterol, reduced bile salts, and gallbladder stasis all cause sludge or stones (First Aid 2025 GI biliary pathology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — formation and composition of gallstones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Concentrating bile salts only 2 to 3 times understates gallbladder storage.","B":"Concentrating bile salts 100 to 200 times overstates gallbladder storage.","C":"Concentrating bile salts only 4 to 5 times understates gallbladder storage.","D":"Concentrating bile salts 50 to 60 times overstates gallbladder storage."}},{"id":"sdl13_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man has a cholecystectomy, and the pathologist explains how the gallbladder absorbs fluid. After isotonic fluid crosses the basal lamina into the lamina propria, where is it taken up?","choices":{"A":"Lymphatic capillaries","B":"Fenestrated capillaries","C":"Lumen of the cystic duct","D":"Bile in the gallbladder lumen","E":"Muscularis externa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hydrostatic pressure in the lateral spaces pushes isotonic fluid across the basal lamina, where fenestrated capillaries rapidly absorb it. The lamina propria noticeably lacks lymphatics, fluid does not return to the lumen or cystic duct, and the muscularis does not absorb it. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, hydrostatic fluid movement, lines 2468–2471).","boardPrep":"Step 1: water build up distends the lateral intercellular space and raises local hydrostatic pressure (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the lamina propria of the gallbladder has fenestrated capillaries and small venules (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"The lamina propria noticeably lacks lymphatics, fluid does not return to the lumen or cystic duct, and the muscularis does not absorb it.","A":"Lymphatic capillaries are not part of that answer.","C":"Lumen of the cystic duct is not part of that answer.","E":"Muscularis externa is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl13_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy, and her surgeon explains how a living gallbladder concentrates bile. Water moves from the lumen through membrane water channels into the lateral intercellular space. What drives this water movement?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping","B":"Bile salt uptake","C":"Osmotic gradient","D":"Vesicular transport","E":"Ciliary beating"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Actively pumped electrolytes collect in the lateral space and create an osmotic gradient, and water follows passively through aquaporins. Water is not pumped actively, bile salts are concentrated rather than absorbed, and vesicles and cilia are not described. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 2, osmotic gradient and water absorption, lines 2460–2467).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder epithelial cells have abundant apical and basal mitochondria for active transport (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the striated border of gallbladder cells is formed by short apical microvilli (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"D":"Vesicular transport is not how water leaves the gallbladder lumen during concentration.","E":"Ciliary beating is not the mechanism that concentrates stored bile.","A":"Water is not pumped actively out of the gallbladder lumen.","B":"Bile salts are concentrated in the lumen rather than taken up to move water."}},{"id":"sdl13_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has recurrent pneumonia, poor weight gain, greasy stools, and a raised sweat chloride. Which apical protein of his pancreatic duct cells is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"ENaC sodium channel","B":"Aquaporin water channel","C":"Voltage gated K channel","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"CFTR chloride channel"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ductal cells secrete Na+ and HCO3- rich fluid through CFTR Cl- channels working with apical Cl-/HCO3- exchangers. A defective CFTR reduces this watery output. The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the partner of CFTR but is not the defective protein in cystic fibrosis. ENaC, aquaporins, and potassium channels are not the described apical secretory step. ENaC absorbs luminal sodium and is normally inhibited by CFTR, so in cystic fibrosis its activity rises rather than being the defective protein. Costanzo states the apical membrane of pancreatic ductal cells contains a Cl−-HCO3− exchanger and CFTR Cl− channels, so the defect raising sweat chloride is CFTR, not an aquaporin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, ductal cell secretory function, lines 2500–2503).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ductal secretion brings duodenal pH to about 8.0 for pancreatic enzyme activity (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: pancreatic fluid is isotonic with water, bicarbonate, and other electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"ENaC, aquaporins, and potassium channels are not the described apical secretory step.","D":"The Cl-/HCO3- exchanger is the partner of CFTR but is not the defective protein in cystic fibrosis.","A":"ENaC absorbs luminal sodium and is normally inhibited by CFTR, so in cystic fibrosis its activity rises rather than being the defective protein.","B":"Costanzo states the apical membrane of pancreatic ductal cells contains a Cl−-HCO3− exchanger and CFTR Cl− channels, so the defect raising sweat chloride is CFTR, not an aquaporin."}},{"id":"sdl13_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a pancreatic biopsy. In H&E sections, pale flattened cells sit in the center of each acinus and lack zymogen granules. What do these cells mainly secrete?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bicarbonate fluid","B":"Trypsinogen and proelastase","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide","D":"Mucin and lysozyme","E":"Pancreatic amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Centroacinar cells secrete the initial isotonic aqueous fluid rich in sodium and bicarbonate. Acinar cells secrete proenzymes and amylase, islet cells secrete insulin and pancreatic polypeptide, and mucin and lysozyme are not described. Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide come from islet beta and PP cells, not duct-type cells. Lysozyme is an antimicrobial product of Paneth cells, not pancreatic duct cells. Pancreatic amylase is stored in zymogen granules of acinar cells, not secreted by pale duct-type cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, centroacinar cells, lines 2487–2493).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intercalated ducts secrete bicarbonate (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: at high postprandial flow, the bicarbonate to chloride ratio of pancreatic fluid rises (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acinar cells secrete proenzymes and amylase, islet cells secrete insulin and pancreatic polypeptide, and mucin and lysozyme are not described.","C":"Insulin and pancreatic polypeptide come from islet beta and PP cells, not duct-type cells.","D":"Lysozyme is an antimicrobial product of Paneth cells, not pancreatic duct cells.","E":"Pancreatic amylase is stored in zymogen granules of acinar cells, not secreted by pale duct-type cells."}},{"id":"sdl13_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has acute pancreatitis with raised serum enzymes. Which pancreatic enzymes do acinar cells secrete in active form rather than as proenzymes?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase","B":"Chymotrypsin and trypsin","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase","D":"Amylase and lipase","E":"Enteropeptidase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acinar cells secrete proteases as proenzymes such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, procarboxypeptidase, and proelastase, but release pancreatic amylase and lipases as active enzymes. Enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than an acinar product. Trypsin and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes trypsinogen and proelastase, not in active form. Carboxypeptidase and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes procarboxypeptidase and proelastase, not in active form. Trypsin and chymotrypsin are secreted as the inactive precursors trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen and are activated only in the duodenal lumen. Costanzo describes enterokinase as a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, not an acinar secretion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 3, acinar cell secretory function, lines 2484–2486).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic fluid contains zymogens such as trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: pancreatic proteases are secreted as proenzymes also called zymogens (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes and autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes trypsinogen and proelastase, not in active form.","C":"Carboxypeptidase and elastase are secreted as the proenzymes procarboxypeptidase and proelastase, not in active form.","B":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin are secreted as the inactive precursors trypsinogen and chymotrypsinogen and are activated only in the duodenal lumen.","E":"Costanzo describes enterokinase as a brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, not an acinar secretion."}},{"id":"sdl13_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man eats a meal, and acidic chyme drops his duodenal pH below 4.5. Which pancreatic secretion does the resulting hormone mainly stimulate?","choices":{"A":"Low volume enzyme rich juice","B":"Mucus rich interlobular secretion","C":"Insulin rich islet output","D":"Chloride rich low flow fluid","E":"High volume bicarbonate fluid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acid releases secretin from S cells, which acts on ductal and centroacinar cells to produce a copious watery fluid rich in sodium bicarbonate with little enzyme. Low volume enzyme rich juice is the CCK response, and chloride rich fluid is the low flow basal pattern. Interlobular goblet cells add little mucus, and islet insulin is not an exocrine output of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin, lines 2506–2514).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released by acid and fatty acids in the duodenal lumen (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones). COMLEX: S cells that release secretin are found in the duodenum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, enteroendocrine cells).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman eats a meal rich in fat and protein, and fatty acids and phenylalanine stimulate duodenal I cells. Which pancreatic cells are the main target of the released hormone?","choices":{"A":"Ductal cells","B":"Centroacinar cells","C":"Acinar cells","D":"Beta cells","E":"Stellate cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"I cells release CCK, which binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells and triggers zymogen granule exocytosis for a low volume enzyme rich secretion. Ductal and centroacinar cells answer mainly to secretin, beta cells are endocrine, and stellate cells are not the target. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, cholecystokinin, lines 2515–2523).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK stimulates zymogen release from acinar cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas). COMLEX: First Aid places CCK producing I cells in the duodenum and jejunum (First Aid 2025 GI GI hormones).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with suspected gastrinoma has a secretin stimulation test. In the pancreas, secretin binds ductal cell receptors that raise which intracellular messenger?","choices":{"A":"IP3 and calcium","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Cyclic AMP","E":"Diacylglycerol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretin binds receptors on ductal and centroacinar cells and stimulates adenylyl cyclase to raise intracellular cAMP. IP3 and calcium are the CCK pathway in acinar cells. Cyclic GMP, nitric oxide, and diacylglycerol are not described. Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of secretin in ductal cells. Nitric oxide from endothelial cells acts through cGMP to cause vasodilation and is not the second messenger of secretin. Diacylglycerol is released with IP3 by phospholipase C and, with Ca2+, activates protein kinase C, the muscarinic ACh pathway rather than the cAMP pathway of secretin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 4, secretin target cell and pathway, lines 2509–2511).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger Ellison workup uses fasting gastrin and gastric pH, then a secretin stimulation test (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma). COMLEX: secretin stimulates bicarbonate release from ductal cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is generated by guanylyl cyclase, the mechanism of atrial natriuretic peptide and nitric oxide, not of secretin in ductal cells.","C":"Nitric oxide from endothelial cells acts through cGMP to cause vasodilation and is not the second messenger of secretin.","E":"Diacylglycerol is released with IP3 by phospholipase C and, with Ca2+, activates protein kinase C, the muscarinic ACh pathway rather than the cAMP pathway of secretin."}},{"id":"sdl13_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has new type 1 diabetes from autoimmune loss of insulin producing islet cells. On electron microscopy, which granule appearance marked the destroyed cells?","choices":{"A":"Round eccentric core","B":"Polyhedral core with halo","C":"Homogeneous granule matrix","D":"Large zymogen type granule","E":"Lamellated concentric layers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Beta cell granules are about 300 nm with a dense crystalline polyhedral core surrounded by a clear halo. A round eccentric core marks alpha cells, a homogeneous matrix marks delta cells, large zymogen granules belong to acinar cells, and lamellated layers are not described. Large zymogen granules belong to pancreatic acinar cells, not insulin-producing beta cells. A dense spherical core surrounded by a clear area marks the glucagon-secreting A cell, which is not the cell destroyed in type 1 diabetes. Larger 300-350 nm granules of low to medium electron density mark the somatostatin-secreting D cells, not the insulin-producing cells. Pawlina describes insulin-secreting B cell granules as having a dense polyhedral core (crystallized insulin) in a pale matrix, not lamellated concentric layers. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, beta cells, lines 2540–2543).","boardPrep":"Step 1: islets are pale staining, highly vascular clusters scattered among dark serous acini (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: the pancreas holds about 1 to 3 million islets of Langerhans (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — insulinoma, the beta cell tumor, contrasted with beta cell loss in type 1 diabetes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Large zymogen granules belong to pancreatic acinar cells, not insulin-producing beta cells.","A":"A dense spherical core surrounded by a clear area marks the glucagon-secreting A cell, which is not the cell destroyed in type 1 diabetes.","C":"Larger 300-350 nm granules of low to medium electron density mark the somatostatin-secreting D cells, not the insulin-producing cells.","E":"Pawlina describes insulin-secreting B cell granules as having a dense polyhedral core (crystallized insulin) in a pale matrix, not lamellated concentric layers."}},{"id":"sdl13_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has a pancreatic islet tumor that secretes excess glucagon. In a normal human islet, where do the cells that make this hormone mainly sit?","choices":{"A":"Islet periphery","B":"Central islet core","C":"Duct epithelium","D":"Acinar lumen","E":"Capsular septa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha cells, about 15 to 20 percent of islet cells, sit primarily at the islet periphery in humans and make glucagon, which stimulates glycogenolysis and raises blood glucose. Beta cells occupy the central core. Ducts, acinar lumens, and septa are not islet sites. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, alpha cells, lines 2544–2547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: alpha cell granules are about 250 nm with a dense round eccentric core (lecture SDL 13). COMLEX: glucagon raises blood glucose by stimulating glycogenolysis (lecture SDL 13).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl13_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with a delta cell tumor of the pancreas has new hyperglycemia and fatty stools. Inhibition of which outputs by the excess hormone explains both findings?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon and bicarbonate output","B":"Amylin and mucin secretion","C":"Insulin and enzyme secretion","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide output","E":"Gastrin and secretin release"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Delta cells make somatostatin, which suppresses insulin and glucagon release as well as exocrine secretion. Less insulin raises glucose, and less enzyme output causes fatty stools. Less glucagon would lower rather than raise glucose, and amylin, mucin, pancreatic polypeptide, gastrin, and secretin do not explain both findings. Somatostatin does suppress glucagon, but less glucagon would lower glucose, and the fatty stools come from suppressed exocrine enzyme secretion rather than bicarbonate alone. Pancreatic polypeptide itself inhibits exocrine secretion, so suppressing its output would not cause fatty stools or raise glucose. Pawlina states somatostatin from D cells inhibits insulin and glucagon secretion and suppresses pancreatic exocrine secretion, which explains hyperglycemia and steatorrhea rather than amylin and mucin loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 13, section 5, delta cells, lines 2548–2551).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatinomas arise from delta cells with autonomous somatostatin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatinoma). COMLEX: somatostatin decreases insulin, glucagon, CCK, and secretin release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 5 — somatostatin actions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Less glucagon would lower rather than raise glucose, and amylin, mucin, pancreatic polypeptide, gastrin, and secretin do not explain both findings.","A":"Somatostatin does suppress glucagon, but less glucagon would lower glucose, and the fatty stools come from suppressed exocrine enzyme secretion rather than bicarbonate alone.","D":"Pancreatic polypeptide itself inhibits exocrine secretion, so suppressing its output would not cause fatty stools or raise glucose.","B":"Pawlina states somatostatin from D cells inhibits insulin and glucagon secretion and suppresses pancreatic exocrine secretion, which explains hyperglycemia and steatorrhea rather than amylin and mucin loss."}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Identify the key histologic features of the gallbladder wall, including its simple columnar epithelium, mucosal folds, muscularis externa, absence of a muscularis mucosae and submucosa, and Rokitansky- Aschoff sinuses.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long-standing gallstones has a cholecystectomy. Histology shows an adenocarcinoma whose invasive front has just passed through the lamina propria on the free, peritoneal side of the gallbladder. Which layer does the tumor enter next?","choices":{"A":"Submucosal layer","B":"Muscularis externa","C":"Muscularis mucosae","D":"Visceral serosa","E":"Fibrous adventitia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The gallbladder wall lacks both a muscularis mucosae and a submucosa, so the lamina propria rests directly on the muscularis externa, which is the next layer any invading tumor meets. A submucosal layer is absent from the gallbladder, unlike most of the gut tube. The muscularis mucosae is also absent, which is why the mucosa sits directly on the muscle coat. Visceral serosa covers the free surface but lies outside the muscularis externa, so the tumor reaches it later. Fibrous adventitia covers the surface attached to the liver bed and also lies outside the muscle coat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 1; Pawlina Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The gallbladder wall has a mucosa of simple columnar epithelium and lamina propria, a muscularis externa, and a serosa or adventitia, with no muscularis mucosae or submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: Gallbladder carcinoma often presents late with direct invasion of the liver.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder storage and concentration","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A submucosal layer is absent from the","C":"The muscularis mucosae is also absent, which","D":"Visceral serosa covers the free surface but","E":"Fibrous adventitia covers the surface attached to"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how gallbladder epithelial cells concentrate bile through active electrolyte transport and passive, aquaporin-mediated water absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"During ERCP after an overnight fast, a 50-year-old woman has bile sampled first from the common hepatic duct and then from the gallbladder. Compared with the hepatic duct sample, which finding is expected in the gallbladder sample?","choices":{"A":"Far more bile salt and higher osmolality","B":"More sodium chloride than plasma","C":"Less bile salt and more water","D":"Far more bile salt, same osmolality","E":"Much lower osmolality than plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gallbladder epithelium pumps Na+, Cl-, and HCO3- into the lateral intercellular spaces with Na+/K+-ATPase, and water follows through aquaporins, so the fluid removed is isosmotic. Bile salts, cholesterol, and bilirubin are left behind and become 10 to 20 times more concentrated while osmolality stays equal to plasma. A higher osmolality would require solute to be concentrated faster than water is removed, but gallbladder absorption is isosmotic. More sodium chloride than plasma is wrong because NaCl is the solute being absorbed, not concentrated. Less bile salt and more water is the reverse of storage, since about 90% of the incoming water is removed. A much lower osmolality than plasma would need water to stay behind while solute leaves, which does not happen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 2; Costanzo Ch. 8 (concentration of bile).","boardPrep":"Step 1: The gallbladder concentrates bile by isosmotic NaCl and water absorption driven by lateral Na+/K+-ATPase, concentrating bile salts 10 to 20 times (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Prolonged storage and stasis of concentrated bile favor crystal accumulation and gallstones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — gallbladder function and enterohepatic circulation","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"A higher osmolality would require solute to","B":"More sodium chloride than plasma is wrong","C":"Less bile salt and more water is","E":"A much lower osmolality than plasma would"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the organization of the exocrine pancreas and distinguish acinar, centroacinar, and ductal cells by their histologic features and secretory functions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pancreatic mass, tender red nodules on his legs from subcutaneous fat necrosis, and polyarthralgia. Serum lipase is 9,800 U/L (reference range: 10–140 U/L). The resected tumor forms small acinar structures, and its cells contain zymogen granules that stain for trypsin. Normal cells of the type this tumor resembles have which histologic feature?","choices":{"A":"Basophilic basal rough ER","B":"Pale cytoplasm without granules","C":"Apical CFTR chloride channels","D":"Location at the islet core","E":"Goblet cells among columnar cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zymogen granules with trypsin and lipase production mark acinar differentiation, and lipase released into the blood explains the fat necrosis. Normal acinar cells are pyramidal serous cells with strongly basophilic basal cytoplasm full of rough ER and apical acidophilic zymogen granules. Pale cytoplasm without granules describes centroacinar cells, which start the intercalated ducts and secrete bicarbonate-rich fluid. Apical CFTR chloride channels belong to duct cells, which make bicarbonate-rich fluid rather than enzymes. Location at the islet core is typical of insulin-producing beta cells, which are endocrine rather than zymogen cells. Goblet cells among columnar cells line the larger interlobular ducts, not the enzyme-secreting acini. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 3; Robbins Ch. 19 (acinar cell carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acinar cells show basal basophilia from rough ER and apical zymogen granules, while centroacinar and duct cells stain pale and secrete bicarbonate (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: A pancreatic tumor with very high lipase and subcutaneous fat necrosis suggests acinar cell carcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pale cytoplasm without granules describes centroacinar cells,","C":"Apical CFTR chloride channels belong to duct","D":"Location at the islet core is typical","E":"Goblet cells among columnar cells line the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the regulation of pancreatic exocrine secretion by secretin and CCK, relating each hormone to its target pancreatic cell type and secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man in an early-phase drug trial receives a drug that blocks CCK1 receptors and then eats a meal rich in fat and protein. Compared with his control meal day, which change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Less bicarbonate with normal enzyme output","B":"More enzyme output from vagal input","C":"Less enzyme release and bile ejection","D":"Normal enzymes but no bile salt secretion","E":"Loss of secretin release by S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"CCK from duodenal and jejunal I cells binds CCK1 receptors on acinar cells to drive zymogen exocytosis, and it also contracts the gallbladder while relaxing the sphincter of Oddi, so blocking the receptor cuts both enzyme output and bile ejection. Less bicarbonate with normal enzymes would follow a block of secretin, whose cAMP signal in duct cells drives bicarbonate output. More enzyme output is the opposite of what losing the main hormonal enzyme stimulus does during the intestinal phase. Normal enzymes with no bile salt secretion is wrong on both counts, because hepatocytes keep secreting bile salts and enzyme output falls. Loss of secretin release does not occur, because duodenal acid rather than CCK triggers the S cells. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 4 and SDL 14 section 5; Costanzo Ch. 8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK from I cells, released by fatty acids and amino acids, stimulates acinar enzyme secretion through CCK1 receptors and IP3 and calcium, contracts the gallbladder, and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Fatty meals provoke biliary colic because CCK contracts a gallbladder whose outlet is blocked by a stone.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Less bicarbonate with normal enzymes would follow","B":"More enzyme output is the opposite of","D":"Normal enzymes with no bile salt secretion","E":"Loss of secretin release does not occur,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has a distal pancreatectomy removing the body and tail after a bicycle handlebar injury. Over the next year his fasting glucose rises to 160 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). His surgeon notes that removing the same mass of pancreatic head would have been less likely to cause diabetes. Which feature of the gland explains this difference?","choices":{"A":"Beta cells lie mainly in the head","B":"Tail acini secrete insulin into ducts","C":"Head islets are mostly alpha cells","D":"Tail islets lack somatostatin cells","E":"Islets are densest in the tail"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Islets make up about 1% to 2% of the pancreas and are scattered among the acini but are most concentrated in the tail, so a distal resection removes a larger share of beta cells than an equal mass of head. Beta cells lying mainly in the head is the reverse of the islet distribution, which favors the tail. Tail acini secrete digestive enzymes, not insulin, which comes from beta cells in the islet core. Head islets are not mostly alpha cells; beta cells make up about 60% to 70% of islet cells. Tail islets do contain somatostatin-producing delta cells at their periphery, and losing them would not cause diabetes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 5; Pawlina Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Islets are 1% to 2% of the pancreas and densest in the tail, with central beta cells and peripheral alpha and delta cells (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: Monitor glucose after distal pancreatectomy, since the resected tail holds a large share of the islets.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Beta cells lying mainly in the head","B":"Tail acini secrete digestive enzymes, not insulin,","C":"Head islets are not mostly alpha cells;","D":"Tail islets do contain somatostatin-producing delta cells"}},{"id":"trial_sdl13_q06","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the endocrine and exocrine pancreas histologically and identify the major islet cell types and their hormone products","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with 20 years of heavy alcohol use has steatorrhea and weight loss, and his fasting glucose is 88 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). A pancreatic biopsy shows dense fibrosis with few remaining dark-staining acini, while pale clusters of cells laced with capillaries persist within the scar. Which hormone do most of the cells in these clusters secrete?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon","B":"Insulin","C":"Somatostatin","D":"Gastrin","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The pale, highly vascular clusters spared within the scar are islets of Langerhans, which survive until late in chronic pancreatitis, and beta cells in the islet core make up about 60% to 70% of islet cells and secrete insulin. Glucagon comes from alpha cells, which make up about 15% to 20% of islet cells and sit at the islet periphery. Somatostatin comes from delta cells, about 5% to 10% of islet cells, which inhibit insulin, glucagon, and exocrine secretion. Gastrin is made by gastric G cells and by gastrinomas rather than by the cells of normal islets. Secretin is released by duodenal S cells in response to acid and is not an islet hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 13 section 5 and SDL 19 section 2; Pawlina Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Islets appear as pale, capillary-rich clusters among dark-staining acini, and beta cells are their most numerous cell (Pawlina Ch. 18). COMLEX: In chronic pancreatitis, islets are preserved until late, so exocrine failure usually precedes diabetes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — pancreatic neuroendocrine neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucagon comes from alpha cells, which make","C":"Somatostatin comes from delta cells, about 5%","D":"Gastrin is made by gastric G cells","E":"Secretin is released by duodenal S cells"}}]},{"sdlNumber":14,"title":"SDL 14 — Physiology - Pancreas, Biliary Secretion, and Liver","questions":[{"id":"sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"Which brush border enzyme of duodenal epithelial cells first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Carboxypeptidase B","B":"Enteropeptidase","C":"Phospholipase A2","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Aminopeptidase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enteropeptidase, also called enterokinase, sits in the brush border glycocalyx of duodenal cells and cleaves a hexapeptide from trypsinogen to form trypsin. Carboxypeptidase B, phospholipase A2, and chymotrypsin are activated by trypsin afterward, and aminopeptidase is not the trigger. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, enteropeptidase mediated trypsin activation, lines 2565–2569).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteropeptidase is a brush border enzyme on duodenal and jejunal mucosa (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: activation of pancreatic proteases begins in the duodenal lumen rather than in the gland (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic enzyme group is secreted already active rather than as a zymogen?","choices":{"A":"Phospholipase A2","B":"Carboxypeptidase A","C":"Elastase","D":"Chymotrypsin","E":"Nucleases"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases are secreted only as inactive proenzymes, while alpha amylase, lipases, cholesterol ester hydrolase, and nucleases are secreted active. Phospholipase A2, carboxypeptidase A, elastase, and chymotrypsin act only after their zymogens are cleaved by trypsin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, synthesis and secretion of pancreatic enzymes, lines 2558–2564).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic alpha amylase is secreted in active form (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic enzymes are made on rough ER, pass through the Golgi, and are stored in apical zymogen granules (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":1,"stem":"Which trypsin inhibitor do acinar and ductal cells package directly into zymogen granules?","choices":{"A":"SPINK1","B":"PRSS1","C":"CTRC","D":"SERPINA1","E":"MRP2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acinar and ductal cells co-package serine protease inhibitor Kazal type 1, SPINK1, into zymogen granules, where it inactivates up to 20 percent of prematurely formed trypsin. PRSS1 is listed with SPINK1 as a pancreatitis gene but is not the co-packaged inhibitor, SERPINA1 is not described here, CTRC aids trypsin self degradation, and MRP2 moves conjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin inhibitors, lines 2600–2603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: PRSS1 and SPINK1 mutations are causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: chronic pancreatitis is linked to alcohol use, tobacco use, cystic fibrosis, and hypertriglyceridemia (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"PRSS1 is a pancreatitis-associated trypsinogen gene, not the inhibitor packaged in zymogen granules.","C":"CTRC helps trypsin degrade itself; it is not the inhibitor packaged in zymogen granules.","D":"SERPINA1 is not the trypsin inhibitor packaged in these zymogen granules.","E":"MRP2 transports conjugated bilirubin; it is not a trypsin inhibitor in zymogen granules."}},{"id":"sdl14_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"Stimulated pancreatic juice is very rich in bicarbonate. What is the main purpose of this bicarbonate?","choices":{"A":"Activating trypsinogen within the ducts","B":"Emulsifying dietary fat into droplets","C":"Neutralizing acid so lipases can work","D":"Dissolving cholesterol in stored bile","E":"Shielding acini from autodigestion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secretin stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate to neutralize the H+ delivered from the stomach, which is essential for fat digestion because pancreatic lipases work at pH 6 to 8 and are inactivated at low pH. Activating trypsinogen within the ducts would cause pancreatitis; trypsinogen is activated by duodenal enteropeptidase. Emulsifying dietary fat into droplets is the job of bile salts. Dissolving cholesterol in stored bile depends on bile salts and lecithin. Shielding acini from autodigestion relies on zymogen storage and trypsin inhibitors such as SPINK1. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, secretin and pancreatic secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin drives pancreatic bicarbonate to neutralize duodenal acid so pancreatic enzymes, especially lipase, can act (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acid hypersecretion in gastrinoma inactivates lipase and causes steatorrhea.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Activating trypsinogen within the ducts would cause","B":"Emulsifying dietary fat into droplets is the","D":"Dissolving cholesterol in stored bile depends on","E":"Shielding acini from autodigestion relies on zymogen"}},{"id":"sdl14_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic duct cells move HCO3- across their apical membrane into the duct lumen. By which route do Na+ and water follow it to make an isotonic juice?","choices":{"A":"Through apical CFTR chloride channels","B":"Between cells through tight junctions","C":"Through apical sodium-hydrogen exchangers","D":"By apical sodium-potassium pumping","E":"By transcytotic vesicle transport"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Na+ and water follow HCO3- paracellularly across the tight junctions, down electrical and osmotic gradients, which makes pancreatic juice isotonic and alkaline. Apical CFTR channels carry Cl- back to the lumen to keep the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger running. The Na+/H+ exchanger and the Na+/K+-ATPase sit on the basolateral membrane, where they extrude H+ and acidify pancreatic venous blood. Transcytotic vesicle transport is not described. Guyton states that the negative luminal voltage pulls Na+ across the tight junctions between duct cells, not by transcytotic vesicles. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion and paracellular fluid secretion, p75).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice stay equal to plasma at all flow rates (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: at high flow, HCO3- rises toward 145 mEq/L while Cl- falls (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — centroacinar and duct cells of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The sodium-hydrogen exchanger of the duct cell sits on the basolateral membrane, not the apical membrane.","D":"The duct-cell sodium-potassium pump sits on the basolateral membrane, not the apical membrane.","E":"Guyton states that the negative luminal voltage pulls Na+ across the tight junctions between duct cells, not by transcytotic vesicles."}},{"id":"sdl14_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Na+ and K+ concentrations in pancreatic juice behave as flow rate changes?","choices":{"A":"Rise with flow","B":"Halve at low flow","C":"Exceed plasma twofold","D":"Stay equal to plasma","E":"Track chloride levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Na+ and K+ in pancreatic juice stay constant and equal to plasma at all flow rates, while HCO3- and Cl- change reciprocally. They do not rise, halve, double, or follow chloride. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2639).","boardPrep":"Step 1: at low basal flow, pancreatic juice is lower in HCO3 and higher in Cl- (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that low flow between meals lowers the HCO3 to Cl- ratio (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, exocrine pancreas).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — ductal and centroacinar cells as the source of the bicarbonate-rich fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"They do not rise, halve, double, or follow chloride."}},{"id":"sdl14_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"The cephalic phase makes a small contribution to the pancreatic enzyme response. Which stimuli start this phase?","choices":{"A":"Distension of the gastric wall","B":"Fatty acids in the duodenum","C":"Acid entering the duodenum","D":"Peptides in the gastric antrum","E":"Smell, taste, and conditioning"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion is initiated by smell, taste, and conditioning and is mediated by vagal acetylcholine acting on acinar cells. Distension of the gastric wall starts the gastric phase through vagovagal reflexes. Fatty acids in the duodenum release CCK in the intestinal phase. Acid entering the duodenum releases secretin, also in the intestinal phase. Peptides in the gastric antrum are a gastric phase stimulus for gastrin, not a cephalic trigger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, regulation of pancreatic secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic secretion has cephalic, gastric, and intestinal phases, and the intestinal phase is the most important (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: vagotomy blunts the cephalic and gastric phases but spares hormonal intestinal control.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"Distension of the gastric wall starts the","B":"Fatty acids in the duodenum release CCK","C":"Acid entering the duodenum releases secretin, also","D":"Peptides in the gastric antrum are a"}},{"id":"sdl14_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary stimulus of the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Sight and smell of food","B":"Acidic duodenal chyme","C":"Stomach wall distention","D":"Duodenal fatty acids","E":"Rising blood glucose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mechanical distention of the stomach wall by food drives the gastric phase through long vagovagal reflexes and enteric circuits. Sight and smell drive the cephalic phase, acidic chyme and fatty acids drive the intestinal phase, and blood glucose is not described. During the cephalic phase, the brain signals that drive gastric secretion also release ACh from vagal endings in the pancreas, so sight and smell of food drive the cephalic, not the gastric, phase. Acid in the small intestine stimulates secretin release from duodenal S cells, an intestinal phase stimulus. Monoglycerides and fatty acids in the duodenum stimulate CCK release from I cells, an intestinal phase stimulus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase, lines 2668–2673).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase maintains a low volume, enzyme rich acinar secretion (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: gastric phase reflexes release ACh onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"During the cephalic phase, the brain signals that drive gastric secretion also release ACh from vagal endings in the pancreas, so sight and smell of food drive the cephalic, not the gastric, phase.","B":"Acid in the small intestine stimulates secretin release from duodenal S cells, an intestinal phase stimulus.","D":"Monoglycerides and fatty acids in the duodenum stimulate CCK release from I cells, an intestinal phase stimulus."}},{"id":"sdl14_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":1,"stem":"What mediator acts directly on acinar cells during the cephalic phase of pancreatic secretion?","choices":{"A":"Vagal acetylcholine","B":"Sympathetic norepinephrine","C":"Circulating secretin","D":"Circulating CCK","E":"Enteric VIP release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the cephalic phase, central impulses travel in the vagus nerve and release ACh from postganglionic vagal endings directly onto acinar cells. Secretin and CCK are released later in the intestinal phase, and norepinephrine and VIP are not described here. Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so norepinephrine does not mediate cephalic pancreatic enzyme secretion. CCK is secreted by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response to fatty acids and amino acids once food reaches the intestine, so it is not the cephalic phase mediator. Guyton states that cephalic phase pancreatic enzyme secretion is caused by acetylcholine released from vagal nerve endings in the pancreas, not enteric VIP. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase pathways, lines 2661–2663).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cephalic stimulation yields a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK acts on acinar CCK1 receptors and through vagovagal reflexes in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"C":"Secretin and CCK are released later in the intestinal phase, and norepinephrine and VIP are not described here.","B":"Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so norepinephrine does not mediate cephalic pancreatic enzyme secretion.","D":"CCK is secreted by duodenal and jejunal I cells in response to fatty acids and amino acids once food reaches the intestine, so it is not the cephalic phase mediator.","E":"Guyton states that cephalic phase pancreatic enzyme secretion is caused by acetylcholine released from vagal nerve endings in the pancreas, not enteric VIP."}},{"id":"sdl14_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Bile carries cholesterol into the duodenum. Which bile constituents keep this cholesterol in solution?","choices":{"A":"Bilirubin and urobilinogen","B":"Calcium and bicarbonate","C":"Bile salts and lecithin","D":"Mucin and immunoglobulin","E":"Sodium and potassium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts are amphipathic and, together with phospholipids such as lecithin, form micelles that hold cholesterol in solution; when cholesterol exceeds this capacity, it can precipitate as stones. Bilirubin and urobilinogen are pigments that do not solubilize cholesterol. Calcium and bicarbonate do not form micelles, and calcium salts contribute to pigment stones. Mucin and immunoglobulin are gallbladder secretions that can promote crystal nucleation rather than solubility. Sodium and potassium are electrolytes with no micelle-forming role. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, composition of bile; Robbins Ch. 18, cholelithiasis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol is solubilized in bile by micelles of bile salts and lecithin, and excess cholesterol relative to these causes cholesterol stones (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: anything lowering bile salt secretion, such as ileal disease, favors gallstones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — the bile salt, phospholipid, and cholesterol balance behind cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bilirubin and urobilinogen are pigments that do","B":"Calcium and bicarbonate do not form micelles","D":"Mucin and immunoglobulin are gallbladder secretions that","E":"Sodium and potassium are electrolytes with no"}},{"id":"sdl14_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hepatocyte enzyme converts cholesterol into the primary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"HMG CoA reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Lipoprotein lipase","D":"7alpha hydroxylase","E":"Acyl CoA synthetase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatocytes make cholic and chenodeoxycholic acid from cholesterol through 7alpha hydroxylase, the rate limiting enzyme. HMG CoA reductase makes cholesterol, heme oxygenase breaks down heme, lipoprotein lipase acts on plasma triglycerides, and acyl CoA synthetase activates fatty acids. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, primary bile acids, lines 2702–2703).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7alpha hydroxylase catalyzes the rate limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: the primary bile acids are cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants and cholesterol conversion into bile acids","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What change do intestinal bacteria make to primary bile acids to form secondary bile acids?","choices":{"A":"Dehydroxylation at C7","B":"Conjugation with glycine","C":"Glucuronidation at C3","D":"Oxidation at C12","E":"Sulfation at C24"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal bacteria dehydroxylate primary bile acids at C7, turning cholic acid into deoxycholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid into lithocholic acid. Conjugation with glycine or taurine happens in the liver. Glucuronidation is how the liver conjugates bilirubin, and glucuronidation, C12 oxidation, and C24 sulfation would all add groups, whereas deoxycholic and lithocholic acid each lack the C7 hydroxyl of their parent acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, secondary bile acids, lines 2704–2706).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the secondary bile acids are deoxycholic acid and lithocholic acid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: bacteria in the ileum and colon deconjugate and modify bile salts to form secondary bile acids (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — ursodiol, a secondary bile acid used as a bile acid agent","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"After bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum, how do most of them return to the liver?","choices":{"A":"Through intestinal lymph lacteals","B":"Through hepatic artery branches","C":"Through the portal vein","D":"Through the common bile duct","E":"Through the thoracic duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the enterohepatic circulation, bile salts are absorbed from the ileum into the portal circulation, carried back to the liver, and extracted from portal blood by hepatocytes for resecretion. Intestinal lymph lacteals carry chylomicrons, not recycled bile salts. Hepatic artery branches bring systemic arterial blood, not ileal venous return. The common bile duct carries bile from the liver to the duodenum, the opposite direction. The thoracic duct drains lymph to the left subclavian vein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, enterohepatic circulation of bile salts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are absorbed in the terminal ileum and return to hepatocytes through portal blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: ileal resection breaks this circuit and causes bile acid diarrhea and fat malabsorption.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intestinal lymph lacteals carry chylomicrons, not recycled","B":"Hepatic artery branches bring systemic arterial blood","D":"The common bile duct carries bile from","E":"The thoracic duct drains lymph to the"}},{"id":"sdl14_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which carrier moves bile salts from the ileal lumen into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Sodium bile salt cotransporter","B":"Sodium glucose cotransporter","C":"Proton peptide cotransporter","D":"Chloride bicarbonate exchanger","E":"Multidrug resistance protein 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal enterocytes take up bile salts actively through Na+ bile salt cotransporters, called ASBT. Sodium glucose and proton peptide cotransport carry sugars and peptides, the chloride bicarbonate exchanger moves anions, and MRP2 exports conjugated bilirubin into bile. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane on the Na+ gradient; it does not transport bile salts. Separate H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into enterocytes, whereas ileal bile salts enter on Na+-bile salt cotransport. Apical Cl–HCO3- exchange in the ileum yields NaCl absorption and does not carry bile salts into the enterocyte. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, terminal ileal reabsorption, lines 2760–2761).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces reuptake at the terminal ileum into the portal circulation and back to the liver (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: hepatocytes extract returning bile salts efficiently through basolateral carriers (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Sodium glucose and proton peptide cotransport carry sugars and peptides, the chloride bicarbonate exchanger moves anions, and MRP2 exports conjugated bilirubin into bile.","B":"SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane on the Na+ gradient; it does not transport bile salts.","C":"Separate H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into enterocytes, whereas ileal bile salts enter on Na+-bile salt cotransport.","D":"Apical Cl–HCO3- exchange in the ileum yields NaCl absorption and does not carry bile salts into the enterocyte."}},{"id":"sdl14_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":1,"stem":"The bile salt pool is small and is recycled, but a small fraction is lost in the feces each day. How is this loss replaced?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary bile salts","B":"Hepatic synthesis from cholesterol","C":"Synthesis by colonic gut bacteria","D":"Synthesis by gallbladder epithelium","E":"Reabsorption from the urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because bile salts recirculate to the liver, demand for new synthesis is low, and the liver replaces the small fraction lost in feces by making primary bile acids from cholesterol and conjugating them. Absorption of dietary bile salts is not a source, since bile salts are not a dietary nutrient. Colonic gut bacteria only convert primary to secondary bile acids by dehydroxylation. Gallbladder epithelium stores and concentrates bile but does not synthesize bile salts. Reabsorption from the urine is not a meaningful route, because bile salts are cleared by the liver. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8, enterohepatic circulation and bile salts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hepatic 7alpha-hydroxylase makes new bile acids from cholesterol to replace fecal loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid sequestrants increase fecal loss, so the liver uses more cholesterol and LDL falls.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 20 → Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absorption of dietary bile salts is not","C":"Colonic gut bacteria only convert primary to","D":"Gallbladder epithelium stores and concentrates bile but","E":"Reabsorption from the urine is not a"}},{"id":"sdl14_q16","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"Most bilirubin comes from breakdown of aging red cells. Which source produces most of the remainder?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial breakdown of bile acids","B":"Oxidation of hepatic cholesterol","C":"Degradation of plasma albumin","D":"Hydrolysis of hepatic glycogen","E":"Turnover of hepatic heme proteins"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins states that most bilirubin comes from macrophage breakdown of effete red cells, and the rest comes from turnover of hepatic heme-containing proteins such as cytochrome P450 enzymes, since bilirubin is the end product of heme degradation. Bacterial breakdown of bile acids yields secondary bile acids, not bilirubin. Oxidation of hepatic cholesterol makes bile acids, not pigment. Degradation of plasma albumin yields amino acids, and albumin merely carries bilirubin. Hydrolysis of hepatic glycogen releases glucose. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, bilirubin and bile formation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bilirubin derives from heme, mostly red cell hemoglobin and partly hepatic heme proteins such as P450 (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: hemolysis raises the heme load and causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bacterial breakdown of bile acids yields secondary","B":"Oxidation of hepatic cholesterol makes bile acids","C":"Degradation of plasma albumin yields amino acids","D":"Hydrolysis of hepatic glycogen releases glucose."}},{"id":"sdl14_q17","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"Which macrophage enzyme converts heme to biliverdin?","choices":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase","B":"Heme oxygenase","C":"Glucuronyl transferase","D":"Beta glucuronidase","E":"Ferrochelatase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Macrophage heme oxygenase turns heme into biliverdin, which biliverdin reductase then reduces to unconjugated bilirubin. Glucuronyl transferase conjugates bilirubin in hepatocytes, bacterial beta glucuronidase deconjugates it in the gut, and ferrochelatase builds heme. Biliverdin reductase acts after heme oxygenase, reducing biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin; it does not convert heme itself. UDP-glucuronyl transferase works in hepatic microsomes to conjugate bilirubin after it reaches the liver bound to albumin, not to break down heme. Bacterial enzymes deconjugate bilirubin in the terminal ileum and colon, a step far downstream of heme breakdown. First Aid states heme is metabolized by heme oxygenase to biliverdin, so ferrochelatase, a heme synthesis enzyme, is not the converting step. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, bilirubin production, lines 2784–2786).","boardPrep":"Step 1: biliverdin is green and bilirubin is yellow brown (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin). COMLEX: indirect bilirubin is unconjugated and water insoluble (First Aid 2025 GI bilirubin).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biliverdin reductase acts after heme oxygenase, reducing biliverdin to unconjugated bilirubin; it does not convert heme itself.","C":"UDP-glucuronyl transferase works in hepatic microsomes to conjugate bilirubin after it reaches the liver bound to albumin, not to break down heme.","D":"Bacterial enzymes deconjugate bilirubin in the terminal ileum and colon, a step far downstream of heme breakdown.","E":"First Aid states heme is metabolized by heme oxygenase to biliverdin, so ferrochelatase, a heme synthesis enzyme, is not the converting step."}},{"id":"sdl14_q18","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporter moves conjugated bilirubin across the canalicular membrane into bile?","choices":{"A":"MRP2","B":"ASBT","C":"OATP","D":"CFTR","E":"NTCP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Conjugated bilirubin is actively pumped across the canalicular membrane by MRP2, multidrug resistant protein 2. ASBT takes up bile salts in the ileum, CFTR is a chloride channel, and OATP and NTCP are basolateral uptake carriers not described for this step. ASBT absorbs bile salts across ileal enterocytes, not bilirubin into canaliculi. OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 mediate hepatic uptake from blood, not canalicular export. NTCP is the sinusoidal sodium taurocholate cotransporter (also the HBV entry receptor), not a canalicular bilirubin exporter. CFTR is an apical chloride channel of pancreatic ductal cells that works with the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger to secrete bicarbonate, not a canalicular bilirubin pump. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and excretion, lines 2797–2799).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dubin Johnson syndrome is a defective MRP2 transporter with reduced excretion of conjugated bilirubin (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: Dubin Johnson and Rotor syndromes are excretion defects (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"ASBT absorbs bile salts across ileal enterocytes, not bilirubin into canaliculi.","C":"OATP1B1 and OATP1B3 mediate hepatic uptake from blood, not canalicular export.","E":"NTCP is the sinusoidal sodium taurocholate cotransporter (also the HBV entry receptor), not a canalicular bilirubin exporter.","D":"CFTR is an apical chloride channel of pancreatic ductal cells that works with the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger to secrete bicarbonate, not a canalicular bilirubin pump."}},{"id":"sdl14_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has acute pancreatitis after a small amount of trypsin is activated inside the gland. Which built in property of trypsin normally limits this damage?","choices":{"A":"Autolysis aided by CTRC","B":"Acinar cell apoptosis","C":"Bicarbonate neutralization","D":"Lysosomal colocalization","E":"Bile salt inhibition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trypsinogen and trypsin carry cleavage sites that allow autocatalytic self degradation, helped by chymotrypsin C or caldecrin, if trypsin is activated early. Apoptosis, bicarbonate, and bile salts do not inactivate trypsin, and lysosomal colocalization is not described as protective. Aberrant delivery of zymogens to lysosomes lets lysosomal hydrolases activate pancreatic proenzymes, so colocalization drives pancreatitis rather than limiting it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, trypsin self inactivation, lines 2604–2607).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zymogens stay unactivated until they leave the ducts and meet enteropeptidase in the small intestine (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: chronic alcohol use and tobacco use are listed causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Aberrant delivery of zymogens to lysosomes lets lysosomal hydrolases activate pancreatic proenzymes, so colocalization drives pancreatitis rather than limiting it."}},{"id":"sdl14_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis asks why her enzyme capsules need more than lipase. Her physician explains that duodenal bile salts inhibit lipase on its own. Which cofactor, activated by trypsin, binds lipase so it can still work?","choices":{"A":"Elastase","B":"Lecithin","C":"Albumin","D":"Colipase","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Trypsin converts procolipase to colipase, which binds pancreatic lipase and lets it digest lipid in the presence of inhibitory bile salts. Elastase is a protease, lecithin is a phospholipid, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and secretin is a hormone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, cascade activation by trypsin, lines 2578–2580).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglycerides into 2 monoglycerides and free fatty acids (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: trypsin also activates prophospholipase A2 (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with chronic pancreatitis takes enzyme capsules with meals. Which capsule protease removes single amino acids from the C terminal end of peptides?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic trypsin","B":"Pancreatic chymotrypsin","C":"Pancreatic elastase","D":"Brush border aminopeptidase","E":"Carboxypeptidase B"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Carboxypeptidases A and B are exopeptidases that remove single amino acids one at a time from the C terminal end. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase are endopeptidases that cut internal bonds, and aminopeptidase is a brush border enzyme rather than a pancreatic capsule protease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 1, digestive functions of activated enzymes, lines 2582–2585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trypsin also cleaves more trypsinogen into active trypsin in a positive feedback loop (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic enzymes). COMLEX: pancreatic alpha amylase produces disaccharides and trisaccharides from starch and glycogen (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase for exocrine insufficiency","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has poor pancreatic bicarbonate output. Normally, how does her defective apical channel support bicarbonate secretion?","choices":{"A":"Pumping bicarbonate into blood","B":"Secreting enzymes into acini","C":"Absorbing sodium from the lumen","D":"Recycling chloride to the lumen","E":"Generating carbonic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The apical CFTR Cl- channel lets Cl- recycle back into the lumen, keeping luminal Cl- high enough for continuous Cl-/HCO3- exchange. CFTR does not pump bicarbonate into blood, secrete enzymes, absorb sodium, or make carbonic acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, role of CFTR Cl- channels, lines 2623–2627).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis is a listed cause of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis etiology). COMLEX: secretin raises duct cell cAMP to open CFTR and drive Cl-/HCO3 exchange (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as a cause of chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with suspected chronic pancreatitis has a secretin stimulation test. As his duct cells secrete bicarbonate, how does the venous blood leaving his pancreas change?","choices":{"A":"It becomes more alkaline","B":"It stays unchanged","C":"It loses chloride","D":"It gains bile salts","E":"It becomes more acidic"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The H+ formed with each HCO3- is pumped across the basolateral membrane by Na+/H+ exchange into capillaries, acidifying pancreatic venous blood. It does not become alkaline or stay unchanged, and chloride and bile salts are not the described change. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, basolateral H+ extrusion, lines 2628–2631).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sodium and water follow HCO3 paracellularly across tight junctions into the duct lumen (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes pancreatic fluid as isotonic, with water, bicarbonate, and electrolytes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic fluid).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin and CCK regulation of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q24","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has pancreatic juice collected during a secretin test, and her flow rises sharply. How does the anion content of the juice change?","choices":{"A":"Chloride up and bicarbonate down","B":"Bicarbonate up and chloride down","C":"Both anions rise together","D":"Neither anion changes","E":"Bicarbonate down and chloride flat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"At high flow, centroacinar and ductal cells secrete large volumes with bicarbonate rising to about 140 to 145 mEq/L and a reciprocal fall in chloride. Low flow shows the opposite pattern, and the two anions change in opposite directions rather than together, not at all, or with one held flat. Guyton states that secretin-driven pancreatic fluid has a high HCO3- (up to 145 mEq/L) but a low Cl- concentration, so both anions do not rise together. Guyton states that secretin-driven pancreatic fluid has a high HCO3- (up to 145 mEq/L) but a low Cl- concentration, so bicarbonate rises rather than falls. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 2, flow rate and ionic composition, lines 2635–2644).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretin is released when duodenal pH falls below 4.5 (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: ACh and CCK potentiate secretin driven bicarbonate secretion through IP3 and Ca2+ (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 5 Obj 2 — GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chloride rising while bicarbonate falls is the low-flow pattern, not the change when secretin raises flow.","D":"Both anions change when pancreatic flow rises; neither stays flat.","C":"Guyton states that secretin-driven pancreatic fluid has a high HCO3- (up to 145 mEq/L) but a low Cl- concentration, so both anions do not rise together.","E":"Guyton states that secretin-driven pancreatic fluid has a high HCO3- (up to 145 mEq/L) but a low Cl- concentration, so bicarbonate rises rather than falls."}},{"id":"sdl14_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with a prior truncal vagotomy joins a pancreatic function study. A balloon distends his stomach while no chyme is allowed to enter the duodenum, and his pancreatic enzyme output still rises slightly. Which pathway most likely carries this signal?","choices":{"A":"Vagal efferent fibers to acinar cells","B":"Secretin from duodenal S cells","C":"Local enteric neural circuits","D":"Sympathetic splanchnic fibers","E":"Cortical conditioned reflexes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gastric distension drives the gastric phase of pancreatic secretion through two routes: long vagovagal reflexes and local enteric neural circuits, both of which release ACh onto acinar cells. With the vagal trunks divided, the local enteric circuits are the route left to carry the signal. Vagal efferent fibers were cut by the vagotomy. Secretin release needs acid chyme below pH 4.5 in the duodenum, which was prevented. Sympathetic fibers do not drive acinar enzyme secretion. Cortical conditioned reflexes belong to the cephalic phase and travel in the vagus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, gastric phase pathways, p76).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastric phase makes a low-volume, enzyme-rich juice and provides about 5 to 10 percent of the enzyme response (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: sympathetic input generally inhibits GI secretion (lecture SDL 4).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman smells lunch cooking, and her pancreas secretes enzymes that mostly stay in the duct system until later. Why do they stay there?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition by somatostatin","B":"Very low fluid volume","C":"Ductal reabsorption of enzymes","D":"Absent zymogen granules","E":"Low enzyme synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cephalic response is a moderate enzyme rich secretion with very little water or electrolyte volume, so most enzymes stay stored in the ducts until fluid flow increases later. Somatostatin, ductal enzyme reabsorption, absent granules, and low synthesis are not the described reason. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, cephalic phase secretory response, lines 2664–2667).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the cephalic phase, ACh is released from postganglionic vagal endings onto acinar cells (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: somatostatin inhibits secretin and CCK release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, somatostatin).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagal parasympathetic innervation","isHighYield":false},{"id":"sdl14_q27","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man eats a large meal, and chyme distends his duodenum and excites stretch and chemical receptors in the duodenal wall. Which pathway then amplifies the effects of secretin and CCK?","choices":{"A":"Vagovagal reflexes","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Gastrocolic reflex","D":"Sympathetic reflexes","E":"Myenteric peristalsis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the intestinal phase, distention and chemical stimuli excite vagal afferents and trigger efferent ACh release, a vagovagal reflex that potentiates both secretin and CCK. The enterogastric and gastrocolic reflexes, sympathetic reflexes, and peristalsis are not described here. Enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall inhibit gastric emptying rather than drive pancreatic secretion. The gastrocolic reflex is triggered by gastric distention and acts on the colon, not the pancreas. Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so it does not amplify secretin and CCK. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 3, intestinal phase mechanisms, lines 2686–2688).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal phase stimuli include acidic chyme and fat and protein digestion products (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: CCK stimulates massive zymogen exocytosis from acinar cells in the intestinal phase (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterogastric reflexes from the duodenal wall inhibit gastric emptying rather than drive pancreatic secretion.","C":"The gastrocolic reflex is triggered by gastric distention and acts on the colon, not the pancreas.","D":"Sympathetic stimulation usually inhibits gastrointestinal tract activity, so it does not amplify secretin and CCK."}},{"id":"sdl14_q28","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with gallstones asks how her bile salts can work in the duodenum. They stay ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH. Which feature explains this?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial dehydroxylation","B":"Albumin binding in bile","C":"Low pKa after conjugation","D":"Micelle core packing","E":"Bicarbonate buffering"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers the pKa from about 7 to between 1 and 4, so bile salts are fully ionized and water soluble at duodenal pH (3 to 5 in the lecture). Bacterial dehydroxylation makes secondary bile acids, albumin carries bilirubin in blood, and micelles and buffering do not set the pKa. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, conjugation into bile salts, lines 2707–2711).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acids are conjugated in the liver with glycine or taurine to form amphipathic bile salts (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acids). COMLEX: conjugation with glycine or taurine makes bile acids water soluble (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl14_q29","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man eats a fatty meal, and his bile salts break large fat globules into tiny emulsified droplets. By up to about how much does the lipid surface area increase?","choices":{"A":"Up to 10 times","B":"Up to 2 times","C":"Up to 100 times","D":"Up to 1 million times","E":"Up to 1000 times"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Bile salts lower interfacial tension, and intestinal mixing breaks fat into droplets under 1 µm, raising surface area up to 1000 fold for pancreatic lipase. Their negative charges repel and keep the droplets from merging. Two, 10, or 100 times understates the gain, and 1 million times overstates it. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in emulsification, lines 2716–2721).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states that emulsification raises surface area for pancreatic enzymes and increases fat digestion and absorption (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bile acid function). COMLEX: bile salts have a hydrophobic steroid nucleus and hydrophilic negatively charged groups (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase restoring fat digestion when lipase is deficient","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Two, 10, or 100 times understates the gain, and 1 million times overstates it."}},{"id":"sdl14_q30","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman digests a fatty meal, and mixed micelles carry lipids across the unstirred water layer to her enterocytes. Where do the bile salts end up once the lipids enter the cells?","choices":{"A":"In the gut lumen","B":"Inside the enterocyte","C":"In the portal blood","D":"In the lymph lacteals","E":"In the brush border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"At the brush border, monoglycerides and fatty acids leave the micelle and diffuse into enterocytes, while bile salts are left behind in the lumen for later ileal reabsorption. They do not enter the jejunal enterocyte, portal blood, lacteals, or brush border with the lipids. Bile salts reach the portal blood only later, after ileal absorption returns them to the liver in the enterohepatic circulation. Lacteals take up chylomicrons made from lipids re-esterified inside the enterocyte, while the bile salts stay behind in the lumen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 4, role of bile salts in micelle formation, lines 2730–2733).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile functions include absorption of lipids and fat soluble vitamins (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: bile is the main route for eliminating cholesterol and bilirubin (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation returns bile salts from the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"They do not enter the jejunal enterocyte, portal blood, lacteals, or brush border with the lipids.","E":"Bile salts do not remain in the brush border after the lipids enter the enterocyte.","C":"Bile salts reach the portal blood only later, after ileal absorption returns them to the liver in the enterohepatic circulation.","D":"Lacteals take up chylomicrons made from lipids re-esterified inside the enterocyte, while the bile salts stay behind in the lumen."}},{"id":"sdl14_q31","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease has his terminal ileum resected and develops steatorrhea even though his liver now makes more bile acids. Why does steatorrhea occur?","choices":{"A":"Synthesis is shut off","B":"Synthesis cannot match losses","C":"Lipase is destroyed in bile","D":"Micelles form too easily","E":"Gallbladder stops storing bile"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Less bile salt returns to the liver, which relieves inhibition and raises synthesis, but even maximal synthesis falls behind heavy fecal loss. The pool shrinks, lipid solubilization fails, and steatorrhea follows. Synthesis rises rather than stopping; micelles form too poorly, not too easily, and the cause is the shrunken bile salt pool rather than lipase destruction or loss of gallbladder storage. After ileal resection, Costanzo attributes steatorrhea to a smaller bile salt pool, because synthesis cannot keep pace with fecal loss, not to destruction of lipase. Decreased bile salt content of bile impairs lipid absorption, so micelles form too poorly rather than too easily. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2770–2774).","boardPrep":"Step 1: malabsorption of bile salts at the distal ileum, as in Crohn disease, prevents normal fat absorption (First Aid 2025 GI bile). COMLEX: ileal bile salt loss lets dietary calcium bind fat, so free oxalate is absorbed and oxalate stones form (First Aid 2025 GI bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Synthesis rises rather than stopping; micelles form too poorly, not too easily, and the cause is the shrunken bile salt pool rather than lipase destruction or loss of gallbladder storage.","C":"After ileal resection, Costanzo attributes steatorrhea to a smaller bile salt pool, because synthesis cannot keep pace with fecal loss, not to destruction of lipase.","D":"Decreased bile salt content of bile impairs lipid absorption, so micelles form too poorly rather than too easily."}},{"id":"sdl14_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman fasts overnight, and her liver bile flows into her gallbladder for storage. What are the states of her sphincter of Oddi and gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Open with gallbladder contracted","B":"Open with gallbladder relaxed","C":"Closed with gallbladder relaxed","D":"Closed with gallbladder contracted","E":"Spasming with ductal reflux"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Between meals the sphincter of Oddi is closed and the gallbladder is relaxed, so continuously made bile flows into the gallbladder. After a meal CCK reverses both, contracting the gallbladder and relaxing the sphincter. Spasm with ductal reflux is not the fasting state; the closed sphincter simply diverts bile into the relaxed gallbladder. An open sphincter would not divert bile into the gallbladder for storage. The sphincter is closed, but the gallbladder is relaxed, not contracted; CCK after a meal contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, gallbladder storage, lines 2738–2740).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CCK contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter of Oddi so bile is ejected in pulsatile spurts (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: vagal ACh adds to CCK in stimulating gallbladder emptying (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After a meal CCK reverses both, contracting the gallbladder and relaxing the sphincter.","B":"An open sphincter would not divert bile into the gallbladder for storage.","D":"The sphincter is closed, but the gallbladder is relaxed, not contracted; CCK after a meal contracts the gallbladder and relaxes the sphincter."}},{"id":"sdl14_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man eats three meals a day. About how many times does his bile salt pool recirculate with each meal?","choices":{"A":"2 to 3 times","B":"10 to 12 times","C":"Once per day","D":"5 to 6 times","E":"20 to 30 times"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The bile salt pool of about 2.5 to 3.0 g recirculates 2 to 3 times per meal, for 12 to 30 g per day. Once per day is too few, and the larger counts are too many. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 5, regulation of bile acid synthesis, lines 2766–2767).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 95 percent of bile salts reach the terminal ileum for reuptake (lecture SDL 14). COMLEX: portal blood carries returning bile salts back to hepatocytes (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 2 — bile acid sequestrants interrupting the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ten to 12 recirculations is too many.","D":"Five to 6 recirculations is too many.","E":"Twenty to 30 recirculations is too many."}},{"id":"sdl14_q34","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 25-year-old man with sickle cell disease has jaundice during a hemolytic crisis. Which finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Strongly positive urine bilirubin","B":"Absent urine urobilinogen","C":"Pale clay colored stools","D":"Negative urine bilirubin","E":"Raised conjugated bilirubin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hemolytic jaundice raises mainly unconjugated bilirubin, which is albumin bound and is not filtered by glomeruli, so urine bilirubin is negative. Urine urobilinogen rises and stools are dark. Strong urine bilirubin, absent urobilinogen, and pale stools fit obstruction, and conjugated bilirubin stays normal. Dark urine from high urinary conjugated bilirubin is the obstructive pattern, whereas hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin. Clay-colored stools reflect reduced fecal stercobilin from bile duct obstruction, not increased red cell destruction. Hemolytic jaundice comes from increased production of unconjugated bilirubin; conjugated bilirubin enters the blood when ducts are obstructed or the liver is diseased. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, prehepatic jaundice, lines 2813–2823).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hemolysis is a cause of unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid 2025 GI jaundice). COMLEX: jaundice appears when total serum bilirubin exceeds about 2.0 to 2.5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 14).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Urine urobilinogen rises and stools are dark.","A":"Dark urine from high urinary conjugated bilirubin is the obstructive pattern, whereas hemolysis raises unconjugated bilirubin.","C":"Clay-colored stools reflect reduced fecal stercobilin from bile duct obstruction, not increased red cell destruction.","E":"Hemolytic jaundice comes from increased production of unconjugated bilirubin; conjugated bilirubin enters the blood when ducts are obstructed or the liver is diseased."}},{"id":"sdl14_q35","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a pancreatic head mass that completely blocks her common bile duct. Which stool and urine pattern is expected?","choices":{"A":"Dark stools with clear urine","B":"Dark stools and raised urobilinogen","C":"Pale stools and dark urine","D":"Normal stools and normal urine","E":"Green stools and red urine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut, so stools are pale and urine urobilinogen is absent, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin spills into urine and makes it dark and tea colored. Dark stools and raised urobilinogen fit hemolysis. Complete bile duct obstruction keeps bile pigment out of the gut, so stools are pale, not dark, and conjugated bilirubin spills into urine to darken it. Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut and spills water-soluble conjugated bilirubin into urine, so stools are pale and urine dark, not normal. Complete obstruction gives pale stools and dark, tea-colored urine, not green stools and red urine. Gut bacteria make urobilinogen from bilirubin that reaches the ileum and colon in bile, so complete duct obstruction cannot raise it or darken the stool. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, posthepatic jaundice, lines 2835–2846).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obstructive jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder is the Courvoisier sign (First Aid 2025 GI biliary structures). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that about 80 percent of urobilinogen becomes stercobilin for fecal excretion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, bilirubin metabolism).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 4 — pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Complete bile duct obstruction keeps bile pigment out of the gut, so stools are pale, not dark, and conjugated bilirubin spills into urine to darken it.","D":"Complete obstruction keeps bile out of the gut and spills water-soluble conjugated bilirubin into urine, so stools are pale and urine dark, not normal.","E":"Complete obstruction gives pale stools and dark, tea-colored urine, not green stools and red urine.","B":"Gut bacteria make urobilinogen from bilirubin that reaches the ileum and colon in bile, so complete duct obstruction cannot raise it or darken the stool."}},{"id":"sdl14_q36","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has mild unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia that appears during fasting. Which hepatocyte enzyme of the smooth ER is mildly deficient?","choices":{"A":"HMOX1","B":"UGT1A1","C":"BLVRA","D":"CYP7A1","E":"CYP2E1"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome is an inherited mild UGT1A1 deficiency that lowers bilirubin glucuronidation in the smooth ER and raises unconjugated bilirubin. HMOX1 makes biliverdin, BLVRA makes bilirubin, CYP7A1 is the rate limiting 7 alpha hydroxylase of bile acid synthesis, and CYP2E1 is induced by alcohol, so none of them conjugates bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 14, section 6, hepatic conjugation and hepatic jaundice, lines 2793–2796, 2824–2827).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crigler Najjar type I has severely reduced UGT1A1 activity and type II less severe loss (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: phenobarbital raises UGT1A1 expression and may help Crigler Najjar type II (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 3 — hereditary hyperbilirubinemias","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion, including zymogen secretion, enteropeptidase-mediated trypsin activation, and activation of other pancreatic proteases by trypsin.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-month-old boy has chronic diarrhea, poor weight gain, and generalized edema with serum albumin of 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Duodenal fluid collected after a feed has a normal trypsinogen content but no trypsin activity, and adding a small amount of purified trypsin to the sample restores all protease activity. Which other enzyme activity is expected to be low in his untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic amylase","B":"Ribonuclease","C":"Cholesterol esterase","D":"Phospholipase A2","E":"Deoxyribonuclease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal trypsinogen with no trypsin activity that is rescued by a little trypsin points to missing duodenal enteropeptidase, the brush border enzyme that makes the first trypsin. Trypsin then activates every other zymogen, including prophospholipase A2 and procolipase, so phospholipase A2 activity is also missing. Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form and does not need trypsin. Ribonuclease, like the other nucleases, is secreted already active. Cholesterol esterase, also called cholesterol ester hydrolase, is secreted active and works without trypsin. Deoxyribonuclease is a nuclease secreted in active form, so its activity is preserved. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 1; Costanzo Ch. 8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enteropeptidase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates the other proteases, prophospholipase A2, and procolipase, while amylase, lipase, cholesterol ester hydrolase, and nucleases are secreted active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Protein maldigestion in infancy with hypoalbuminemic edema suggests failed protease activation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — pancrelipase replacing lipase and other enzymes in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form","B":"Ribonuclease, like the other nucleases, is secreted","C":"Cholesterol esterase, also called cholesterol ester hydrolase,","E":"Deoxyribonuclease is a nuclease secreted in active"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion, including the roles of secretin, carbonic anhydrase, CFTR, and Cl⁻/HCO₃⁻ exchange.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man takes a drug for glaucoma that has also caused tingling in his fingers and a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis with serum bicarbonate of 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). During a research secretin stimulation test, his peak pancreatic juice bicarbonate is lower than expected. Which step of duct cell bicarbonate secretion does his drug most directly impair?","choices":{"A":"Chloride recycling through CFTR","B":"Secretin receptor cAMP signaling","C":"Hydration of CO2 inside duct cells","D":"Paracellular sodium and water flow","E":"Basolateral proton extrusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Glaucoma therapy with paresthesias and acidosis points to a carbonic anhydrase inhibitor such as acetazolamide. Duct cells make most of their bicarbonate by carbonic anhydrase catalyzed combination of CO2 and water into carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ and HCO3-, so inhibiting the enzyme cuts the bicarbonate supply for apical exchange. Chloride recycling through CFTR keeps luminal chloride available for exchange and does not depend on carbonic anhydrase. Secretin receptor cAMP signaling is intact, because the drug acts on bicarbonate generation downstream of the receptor. Paracellular sodium and water flow follows bicarbonate secretion and is not a target of the drug. Basolateral proton extrusion by the Na+/H+ exchanger removes the H+ produced and is not the step the drug blocks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 2; First Aid 2025 p. 626 (acetazolamide).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic duct cells make HCO3- with carbonic anhydrase, take up more by basolateral Na+-HCO3- cotransport, and secrete it by apical Cl-/HCO3- exchange supported by CFTR (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: The secretin stimulation test measures duct bicarbonate output to assess exocrine pancreatic function.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 4 — secretin acting on ductal cells to drive bicarbonate-rich pancreatic secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chloride recycling through CFTR keeps luminal chloride","B":"Secretin receptor cAMP signaling is intact, because","D":"Paracellular sodium and water flow follows bicarbonate","E":"Basolateral proton extrusion by the Na+/H+ exchanger"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the phases of pancreatic secretion and their primary neural and hormonal stimuli.","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man had a truncal vagotomy for refractory ulcers years ago. In a pancreatic function study, sham feeding with chewed and spat-out food produces no rise in his enzyme output. When he later eats a full meal, which part of his pancreatic response should remain the largest?","choices":{"A":"Cephalic phase driven by smell","B":"Gastric phase from antral distention","C":"Basal interdigestive secretion","D":"Vagovagal reflex from the duodenum","E":"Hormonal intestinal phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Truncal vagotomy removes vagal acetylcholine, so the cephalic response to sham feeding disappears, but in the intestinal phase acid and digestion products in the duodenum release secretin and CCK into the blood, and that phase normally supplies about 70% to 80% of pancreatic secretion. The cephalic phase depends on vagal ACh acting on acinar cells, which is why his sham feeding produced nothing. The gastric phase supplies 5% to 10% of the response and partly uses vagovagal reflexes that vagotomy cuts. Basal interdigestive secretion is small and is not the main response to a meal. Vagovagal reflexes from the duodenum travel in the vagus and are lost after truncal vagotomy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 3; Costanzo Ch. 8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The cephalic (about 20%) and gastric (5% to 10%) phases act mainly through vagal ACh, while the intestinal phase (70% to 80%) is driven by secretin and CCK plus vagovagal reflexes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: After vagotomy, hormonal stimulation keeps pancreatic digestion largely intact.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 4 — vagovagal reflexes","isHighYield":false,"whyNot":{"A":"The cephalic phase depends on vagal ACh","B":"The gastric phase supplies 5% to 10%","C":"Basal interdigestive secretion is small and is","D":"Vagovagal reflexes from the duodenum travel in"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in lipid emulsification and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with a large jejunal diverticulum has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A glucose hydrogen breath test is positive for small bowel bacterial overgrowth, and her pancreatic function is normal. By which mechanism do the bacteria most directly impair her fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Inactivating pancreatic lipase directly","B":"Deconjugating her bile salts","C":"Blocking hepatic 7alpha hydroxylase","D":"Blocking ileal bile salt uptake","E":"Consuming dietary fat in the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Overgrowing bacteria remove glycine and taurine from bile salts, turning them back into bile acids whose pKa is above intestinal pH. These nonionized bile acids are lipid soluble and absorbed passively, so too few remain in the lumen to form micelles. Inactivating pancreatic lipase is not the bacterial effect, since her pancreatic function is normal and the problem is micelle formation. Blocking hepatic 7alpha hydroxylase would require more bile salts returning to the liver, not bacterial action in the gut. Blocking ileal bile salt uptake occurs with ileal disease or resection, which she does not have. Consuming dietary fat in the lumen is not how bacteria cause steatorrhea; they act on the bile salts that solubilize fat. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (bacterial overgrowth and bile salts); Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 4.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Conjugation with glycine or taurine lowers bile acid pKa from about 7 to between 1 and 4, keeping bile salts ionized in the lumen to form micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Steatorrhea with a blind loop or diverticulum and normal pancreatic function suggests bacterial overgrowth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 2 — gallbladder concentration of bile before it reaches the duodenum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inactivating pancreatic lipase is not the bacterial","C":"Blocking hepatic 7alpha hydroxylase would require more","D":"Blocking ileal bile salt uptake occurs with","E":"Consuming dietary fat in the lumen is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Explain gallbladder function and the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts, including bile storage and concentration, CCK-mediated gallbladder contraction and sphincter of Oddi relaxation, terminal ileal reabsorption, hepatic recycling, and regulation of bile acid synthesis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 60-year-old man has an external biliary drain placed after bile duct surgery, and for 2 weeks all of his bile flows into a collection bag instead of the duodenum. Which change in his hepatocytes is expected?","choices":{"A":"Lower bile acid synthesis rate","B":"More bile salt uptake from portal blood","C":"Larger total bile salt pool","D":"Higher 7alpha hydroxylase activity","E":"Less cholesterol conversion to bile acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bile salts returning in portal blood normally inhibit 7alpha-hydroxylase, the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis, so diverting all bile outside removes that feedback and hepatocytes convert more cholesterol into new bile acids. A lower bile acid synthesis rate would follow more bile salt return, the opposite of external drainage. More bile salt uptake from portal blood cannot occur, because no bile salts reach the ileum to be reabsorbed. A larger total bile salt pool is wrong because synthesis cannot keep pace with complete loss, so the pool shrinks. Less cholesterol conversion to bile acid is the reverse of what loss of feedback causes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 5; Costanzo Ch. 8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95% of bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by Na+-bile salt cotransport and return to inhibit 7alpha-hydroxylase (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Interrupted enterohepatic circulation, as after ileal resection, shrinks the bile salt pool and causes fat malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A lower bile acid synthesis rate would","B":"More bile salt uptake from portal blood","C":"A larger total bile salt pool is","E":"Less cholesterol conversion to bile acid is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl14_q06","objective":6,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 6 — Trace bilirubin production, transport, hepatic conjugation, intestinal metabolism, and excretion, and predict changes in bilirubin and its metabolites in major causes of jaundice.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has taken several weeks of broad-spectrum oral antibiotics and notices that his stools have become pale. Serum total bilirubin is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL), and his liver enzymes are normal. Which urinalysis finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Excess urobilinogen without bilirubin","B":"Strongly positive bilirubin with dark urine","C":"Little urobilinogen and no bilirubin","D":"Bilirubin present and urobilinogen high","E":"Hemoglobin with hemosiderin granules"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gut bacteria deconjugate bilirubin and reduce it to urobilinogen, a small part of which is absorbed and excreted in urine, while the rest is oxidized to the stercobilin that browns stool. Suppressing the flora stops urobilinogen formation, so urine urobilinogen falls, and with a normal serum bilirubin the urine has no bilirubin. Excess urobilinogen without bilirubin is the hemolysis pattern, in which extra bilirubin reaches the gut and more urobilinogen forms. Strongly positive urine bilirubin needs conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, as in bile duct obstruction, but his serum bilirubin is normal. Bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the hepatocellular pattern, which his normal liver enzymes argue against. Hemoglobin with hemosiderin marks intravascular hemolysis, which would also raise unconjugated bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 14 section 6; Costanzo Ch. 8.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut bacteria convert bilirubin to urobilinogen, which becomes stercobilin in stool and urobilin in urine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Urine urobilinogen is high in hemolysis, variable in hepatitis, and absent when bilirubin cannot reach or be converted in the gut.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Excess urobilinogen without bilirubin is the hemolysis","B":"Strongly positive urine bilirubin needs conjugated hyperbilirubinemia,","D":"Bilirubin with high urobilinogen is the hepatocellular","E":"Hemoglobin with hemosiderin marks intravascular hemolysis, which"}}]},{"sdlNumber":15,"title":"SDL 15 — Pathology - Infectious and Inflammatory Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"In acute and chronic hepatitis, what does Robbins call the pyknotic eosinophilic remnants of apoptotic hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Mallory hyaline","B":"Acidophil bodies","C":"Russell bodies","D":"Ground glass cells","E":"Psammoma bodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Irreversible hepatocyte injury by apoptosis leaves pyknotic, eosinophilic acidophil bodies ringed by mononuclear cells. Robbins notes that these were first described in yellow fever and named Councilman bodies, and by convention keeps that eponym for yellow fever. Mallory hyaline is clumped intermediate filament in ballooned cells, ground glass cells mark chronic hepatitis B, and Russell and psammoma bodies are not liver apoptosis findings. Sources: Robbins ch 18, hepatocyte apoptosis (SDL 15–19 course reading, p. 6); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatocyte responses to injury, lines 2850–2856).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp and the lecture use Councilman bodies more loosely, for the eosinophilic inclusions of acute viral hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute viral hepatitis); expect either name. COMLEX: confluent necrosis is zonal loss of contiguous hepatocytes, such as zone 3 perivenular necrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cell is mainly responsible for collagen deposition and scar formation in the injured liver?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic stellate cells","B":"Resident Kupffer cells","C":"Portal tract fibroblasts","D":"Ductal cholangiocytes","E":"Sinusoidal endothelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatic stellate or Ito cells are the main collagen depositing cells. Cytokines such as TNF alpha and TGF beta from Kupffer cells and injured hepatocytes activate them, and they become myofibroblasts that lay down type I and III collagen in the space of Disse. Kupffer cells signal but do not make the scar. Portal fibroblasts may also contribute in some disorders, but stellate cells are the principal source; sinusoidal endothelium loses its fenestrations as the collagen is laid down rather than making it, and cholangiocytes are not named as scar forming cells. Robbins describes sinusoidal endothelial cells losing their fenestrations (sinusoidal capillarization) while scar is laid down in the space of Disse; the activated stellate cell, not the endothelium, deposits the collagen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, scar formation and hepatic stellate cells, lines 2857–2865); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal fibroblasts may also play a role in scarring).","boardPrep":"Step 1: collagen in the space of Disse causes loss of endothelial fenestrations, called sinusoidal capillarization (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: PDGF drives proliferation of activated stellate cells (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kupffer cells signal but do not make the scar.","C":"Portal fibroblasts may also contribute in some disorders, but stellate cells are the principal source.","D":"Portal fibroblasts may also contribute in some disorders, but stellate cells are the principal source; sinusoidal endothelium loses its fenestrations as the collagen is laid down rather than making it, and cholangiocytes are not named as scar forming cells.","E":"Robbins describes sinusoidal endothelial cells losing their fenestrations (sinusoidal capillarization) while scar is laid down in the space of Disse; the activated stellate cell, not the endothelium, deposits the collagen."}},{"id":"sdl15_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":1,"stem":"Acute liver failure is defined as encephalopathy and coagulopathy arising within what interval after the first injury?","choices":{"A":"Within 2 weeks","B":"Within 52 weeks","C":"Within 6 weeks","D":"Within 26 weeks","E":"Within 5 years"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute liver failure is acute liver illness with encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of the first injury in a patient without preexisting liver disease, and it shows massive necrosis with a small shrunken liver. 2 weeks, 6 weeks, 52 weeks, and 5 years are not that window. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, acute liver failure, lines 2873–2876).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in acute liver failure, portal hypertension develops over days to weeks and shows as ascites (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid lists drug injury such as acetaminophen, ischemic hepatitis, viral hepatitis, and autoimmune hepatitis as causes of very high aminotransferases (First Aid 2025 GI liver markers).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 4 — Kupffer and stellate cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"2 weeks is too short for the defined window.","B":"52 weeks is too long for the defined window.","C":"6 weeks is too short for the defined window.","E":"5 years is too long for the defined window."}},{"id":"sdl15_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"What genome and structure does hepatitis A virus have?","choices":{"A":"Enveloped partially double stranded DNA","B":"Enveloped positive sense RNA","C":"Nonenveloped positive sense RNA","D":"Enveloped circular defective RNA","E":"Nonenveloped negative sense RNA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HAV is a small nonenveloped single stranded positive sense RNA picornavirus. Partially double stranded DNA is HBV, enveloped positive sense RNA is HCV, enveloped circular defective RNA is HDV, and no nonenveloped hepatotropic virus is negative sense. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis A virus, lines 2898–2901).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgG anti HAV persists for life and confers long term immunity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: HAV spreads by the fecal oral route and does not cause chronic liver disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"Anti-HCV antibodies can stay positive after the virus clears. Which test confirms active hepatitis C infection?","choices":{"A":"Anti-HCV antibody titer","B":"Serum HCV RNA level","C":"Serum ALT activity","D":"Serum AFP level","E":"Anti-HBc IgM level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-HCV antibody is a screening test that may be negative early and may remain positive after spontaneous or treated clearance, so detectable serum HCV RNA is the marker of active infection, and its persistence beyond several months defines chronic infection. Anti-HCV antibody titer cannot separate current from past infection. Serum ALT activity fluctuates and is not specific for HCV. Serum AFP level is used in screening for hepatocellular carcinoma. Anti-HBc IgM level marks acute hepatitis B, a different virus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, hepatitis C virus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCV RNA appears first and confirms active infection, while anti-HCV marks exposure (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: everyone with detectable HCV RNA is a candidate for antiviral therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Anti-HCV antibody titer cannot separate current from","C":"Serum ALT activity fluctuates and is not","D":"Serum AFP level is used in screening","E":"Anti-HBc IgM level marks acute hepatitis B"}},{"id":"sdl15_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":1,"stem":"Which marker is crucial for diagnosing acute hepatitis B after HBsAg clears but before anti HBs appears?","choices":{"A":"IgG anti HBs","B":"IgG anti HBc","C":"IgM anti HAV","D":"IgG anti HDV","E":"IgM anti HBc"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In the window period after HBsAg clears and before anti HBs appears, IgM anti HBc identifies acute infection. IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark past or resolved infection, and the HAV and HDV antibodies belong to other viruses. Robbins notes anti-HBs rises only after HBsAg disappears and may be delayed for weeks to months, which is exactly the gap IgM anti-HBc fills; anti-HBs then persists for life as protection. Anti-HDV antibodies mark delta co-infection or superinfection, which requires HBsAg; Robbins names IgM anti-HBc, not an HDV antibody, for diagnosis when anti-HBs is delayed. First Aid states IgG anti-HBc indicates prior exposure or chronic infection, whereas IgM anti-HBc marks acute or recent infection and may be the sole positive marker in the window period. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2921–2926).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBeAg and HBV DNA indicate active replication and high infectivity (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HBs rises after resolution and is the basis of the hepatitis B vaccine (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgG anti HBc and anti HBs mark past or resolved infection, and the HAV and HDV antibodies belong to other viruses.","A":"Robbins notes anti-HBs rises only after HBsAg disappears and may be delayed for weeks to months, which is exactly the gap IgM anti-HBc fills; anti-HBs then persists for life as protection.","D":"Anti-HDV antibodies mark delta co-infection or superinfection, which requires HBsAg; Robbins names IgM anti-HBc, not an HDV antibody, for diagnosis when anti-HBs is delayed.","B":"First Aid states IgG anti-HBc indicates prior exposure or chronic infection, whereas IgM anti-HBc marks acute or recent infection and may be the sole positive marker in the window period."}},{"id":"sdl15_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"Which organisms most commonly ascend the biliary tree to cause cholangitis and pyogenic liver abscess?","choices":{"A":"Enteric coliforms","B":"Staphylococcus aureus","C":"Group A streptococci","D":"Anaerobic clostridia","E":"Pseudomonas species"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteric bacteria, most commonly coliforms such as E. coli along with enterococci, ascend the obstructed biliary tree and can extend into the liver to form pyogenic abscesses. Staphylococcus aureus, group A streptococci, clostridia, and Pseudomonas are not the gut organisms the lecture names (E. coli, enterococci, Klebsiella) as ascending the obstructed tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis pathogenesis, lines 2989–2992).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Charcot triad of cholangitis is jaundice, fever, and right upper quadrant pain (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis). COMLEX: the Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock to the Charcot triad (First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"What morphologic finding characterizes cholestasis of sepsis?","choices":{"A":"Zone 3 necrosis","B":"Interface hepatitis","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Ductular bile plugs","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cholestasis of sepsis shows ductular or cholangiolar cholestasis, with dark bile plugs in dilated canals of Hering and ductules at the portal parenchymal interface and little inflammation. Zone 3 necrosis, interface hepatitis, ground glass cells, and onion skin fibrosis are other patterns. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, sepsis and cholestasis of sepsis, lines 2998–3005).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sepsis injures the liver by direct bacterial action, hypoperfusion, or the host response to circulating endotoxin (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: complications of ascending cholangitis include sepsis and intrahepatic abscess (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":1,"stem":"Which imaging feature is characteristic of hydatid cysts in the liver?","choices":{"A":"Air fluid level","B":"Central stellate scar","C":"Peripheral target sign","D":"Septated hemorrhage","E":"Calcified cyst wall"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Echinococcus larval tapeworms form large hydatid cysts that characteristically show calcification of the cyst wall on imaging. A central stellate scar suggests focal nodular hyperplasia. An air fluid level, a peripheral target sign, and septated hemorrhage are not described for hydatid disease; Robbins notes the wall calcification is what may enable radiologic diagnosis, and First Aid calls it eggshell calcification. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, echinococcosis, lines 3016–3019); Robbins Ch. 18 (hydatid cyst wall calcification); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Echinococcus: eggshell calcification).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hydatid cysts carry a risk of rupture, anaphylaxis, and daughter cyst spread (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: liver flukes inhabit intrahepatic bile ducts and cause hepatolithiasis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"Which autoantibodies characterize type 1 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"LKM1 and LC1","B":"AMA and gp210","C":"ANA and ASMA","D":"ANCA and MPO","E":"Anti CCP and RF"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows antinuclear and anti smooth muscle actin antibodies, sometimes with anti SLA or LPA, and high IgG. LKM1 and LC1 mark type 2. AMA points to primary biliary cholangitis, and ANCA and anti CCP fit other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3048–3052).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists hypergammaglobulinemia with raised IgG in autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 1 autoimmune hepatitis is linked to thyroiditis, type 1 diabetes, and celiac disease (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme is the target of LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis?","choices":{"A":"CYP2D6","B":"CYP2E1","C":"CYP3A4","D":"CYP1A2","E":"CYP2C9"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti LKM1 antibodies in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis are directed against cytochrome P450 CYP2D6. CYP2E1 converts acetaminophen to its reactive toxic metabolite, CYP3A4 is the most abundant hepatic P450 and a major drug-metabolizing enzyme, CYP1A2 metabolizes caffeine and acetaminophen, and CYP2C9 accounts for most pharmacokinetic warfarin interactions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3056–3058).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that autoimmune hepatitis is often positive for anti smooth muscle or anti LKM1 antibodies (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: type 2 autoimmune hepatitis mainly affects children and adolescents (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CYP2E1 converts acetaminophen to its reactive toxic metabolite.","C":"CYP3A4 is the most abundant hepatic P450, about 30% of liver P450 content, and is a major drug-metabolizing enzyme.","D":"CYP1A2 is a drug-metabolizing isoform whose substrates include caffeine and acetaminophen.","E":"CYP2C9 is the isoform behind most pharmacokinetic warfarin interactions."}},{"id":"sdl15_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":1,"stem":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows a female predominance, HLA associations, and autoantibodies. Which treatment response also helps define the disease?","choices":{"A":"Response to antiviral therapy","B":"Response to copper chelation","C":"Response to phlebotomy","D":"Response to immunosuppression","E":"Response to ursodeoxycholic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins defines autoimmune hepatitis as a chronic progressive hepatitis with genetic predisposition, autoantibodies, and a therapeutic response to immunosuppression, consistent with a T cell driven attack on hepatocytes. Response to antiviral therapy points to viral hepatitis, which must be excluded. Response to copper chelation identifies Wilson disease. Response to phlebotomy is the treatment of hereditary hemochromatosis. Response to ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line effect sought in primary biliary cholangitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, autoimmune hepatitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune hepatitis features ANA and ASMA in type 1, anti-LKM1 in type 2, high IgG, plasma cells, and response to immunosuppression (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: corticosteroids are the mainstay for a woman with chronic hepatitis, high IgG, and autoantibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Response to antiviral therapy points to viral","B":"Response to copper chelation identifies Wilson disease.","C":"Response to phlebotomy is the treatment of","E":"Response to ursodeoxycholic acid is the first-line"}},{"id":"sdl15_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cytochrome converts excess acetaminophen into its toxic metabolite?","choices":{"A":"CYP3A4","B":"CYP2D6","C":"CYP2E1","D":"CYP2C9","E":"CYP1A2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"After an overdose saturates glucuronidation and sulfation, acetaminophen is shunted to CYP2E1, which forms NAPQI. CYP3A4, CYP2D6, CYP2C9, and CYP1A2 are not the isoform the lecture names for NAPQI formation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3086).","boardPrep":"Step 1: induction of CYP2E1 by chronic alcohol or anticonvulsants increases acetaminophen susceptibility (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists zone 3 centrilobular necrosis in acetaminophen toxicity (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature describes idiosyncratic drug induced hepatotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Highly predictable","B":"Not dose dependent","C":"Reproducible in animals","D":"Immediate after exposure","E":"Seen in most users"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Idiosyncratic hepatotoxicity is unpredictable, not dose dependent, occurs in only a small minority of exposed people, and has variable latency. Predictable, reproducible, rapid injury is direct intrinsic toxicity. Injury seen in most users would be direct intrinsic toxicity. Robbins classifies drug-induced liver injury as idiosyncratic (unpredictable) or dose-dependent (predictable); a highly predictable injury is the dose-dependent type. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, general pathogenesis and categories, lines 3067–3074).","boardPrep":"Step 1: idiosyncratic susceptibility comes from polymorphisms in drug metabolizing enzymes, transporters, or HLA alleles (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: direct hepatotoxicity is predictable, dose dependent, and occurs shortly after exposure (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Predictable, reproducible, rapid injury is direct intrinsic toxicity.","E":"Injury seen in most users would be direct intrinsic toxicity.","A":"Robbins classifies drug-induced liver injury as idiosyncratic (unpredictable) or dose-dependent (predictable); a highly predictable injury is the dose-dependent type."}},{"id":"sdl15_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is listed as a cause of microvesicular steatosis from mitochondrial toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Amiodarone","B":"Methotrexate","C":"Isoniazid","D":"Oxaliplatin","E":"Valproate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, and nucleoside analogs such as zidovudine cause microvesicular steatosis. Amiodarone causes steatohepatitis, methotrexate fibrosis, isoniazid acute hepatitis, and oxaliplatin sinusoidal obstruction. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3120).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvesicular steatosis presents with liver dysfunction, hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and encephalopathy without overt necrosis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: methotrexate, retinoids, and enalapril are listed causes of periportal or pericellular fibrosis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with cirrhosis has new confusion and a nonrhythmic flapping of his outstretched wrists. Which mechanism best explains these signs?","choices":{"A":"Low serum albumin","B":"Raised blood ammonia","C":"High serum bilirubin","D":"Low platelet count","E":"High portal pressure"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatic encephalopathy, with confusion and asterixis, comes from raised blood ammonia when hepatic urea cycle detoxification fails. Low albumin, high bilirubin, low platelets, and portal pressure do not cause asterixis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, hepatic encephalopathy, lines 2877–2881).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists hepatic encephalopathy and asterixis as neurologic signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ammonia with high PT or INR and low albumin in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis diagnostics).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with chronic liver failure from long standing cholestasis has severe, constant itching. What most likely causes her itching?","choices":{"A":"Histamine from mast cells","B":"High serum ammonia","C":"Excess estrogen","D":"Low clotting factors","E":"Retained bile salts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent cholestasis in chronic liver failure causes profound pruritus from systemic buildup of retained bile salts and solutes. Ammonia causes encephalopathy, excess estrogen causes spider angiomas and gynecomastia, low clotting factors cause bleeding, and mast cell histamine is not the described cause. Elevated ammonia, produced mainly in the gut, is believed to cause hepatic encephalopathy, not cholestatic pruritus. Hyperestrogenemia from impaired estrogen metabolism causes palmar erythema and spider angiomas, not pruritus. Loss of hepatic clotting factor synthesis causes coagulopathy with easy bruising and bleeding, not itching. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure and pruritus, lines 2889–2892).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, conjunctival icterus, pruritus, and fetor hepaticus in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists jaundice among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Elevated ammonia, produced mainly in the gut, is believed to cause hepatic encephalopathy, not cholestatic pruritus.","C":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired estrogen metabolism causes palmar erythema and spider angiomas, not pruritus.","D":"Loss of hepatic clotting factor synthesis causes coagulopathy with easy bruising and bleeding, not itching."}},{"id":"sdl15_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with cirrhosis has gynecomastia, palmar erythema, and spider angiomas on his chest. Which change best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Excess testosterone output","B":"Raised serum ammonia levels","C":"Impaired estrogen clearance","D":"Portal venous congestion","E":"Reduced clotting factors"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperestrogenemia from impaired hepatic estrogen clearance causes palmar erythema, spider angiomas, hypogonadism, and gynecomastia. Testosterone is not raised, ammonia causes encephalopathy, portal congestion causes ascites and varices, and low clotting factors cause bleeding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 1, chronic liver failure, lines 2892–2894).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists gynecomastia, hypogonadism, anovulation, and palmar erythema in cirrhosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cirrhosis presentation). COMLEX: First Aid lists spider angiomas and palmar erythema among the integumentary signs of liver disease (First Aid 2025 GI cirrhosis signs).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old man still has detectable HBsAg 8 months after an acute hepatitis illness. Which state does this finding define?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis window period","B":"Resolved infection","C":"Acute HDV coinfection","D":"Chronic hepatitis B","E":"Prior HBV vaccination"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Persistence of HBsAg beyond 6 months defines chronic HBV infection. The window period has no HBsAg, resolved infection and vaccination show anti HBs without HBsAg, and HDV coinfection is usually a self limited acute illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis B serological markers, lines 2927–2929).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, about 5 to 10 percent of HBV infections progress to chronic hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: perinatal HBV transmission leads to chronic infection in about 90 percent of infants (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman who is 30 weeks pregnant develops severe acute hepatitis during a waterborne outbreak in South Asia. Which virus is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Hepatitis E","B":"Hepatitis A","C":"Hepatitis B","D":"Hepatitis C","E":"Hepatitis D"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HEV spreads by the fecal oral route in waterborne epidemics, and acute HEV in pregnant women carries a mortality approaching 20 percent. HAV is also fecal oral, but the lecture ties this pregnancy mortality to HEV, and HBV, HCV, and HDV are parenteral. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis E virus, lines 2975–2978).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HEV is a nonenveloped positive sense RNA virus in the Hepeviridae family (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: chronic HEV occurs only in immunocompromised hosts such as organ transplant recipients (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 2 — liver disease in pregnancy","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hepatitis A spreads by the fecal-oral route, but it is not the virus tied to severe hepatitis in late pregnancy.","C":"Hepatitis B spreads parenterally, not in a waterborne outbreak.","D":"Hepatitis C spreads parenterally, not in a waterborne outbreak.","E":"Hepatitis D spreads parenterally with hepatitis B, not in a waterborne outbreak."}},{"id":"sdl15_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old man who is a long standing HBsAg carrier and injects drugs develops a new acute hepatitis, and IgM anti HDV is positive. What outcome is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Rapid HBsAg clearance","B":"Lifelong HDV immunity","C":"Mild self limited illness","D":"Clearance of both viruses","E":"Chronic HDV infection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HDV superinfection of a chronic HBsAg carrier leads to chronic HDV in more than 80 percent of cases, with rapid progression to severe hepatitis, cirrhosis, and HCC. Coinfection with HBV and HDV at the same time is the usually self-limited pattern. Lifelong immunity and clearance of both viruses are unlikely while the carrier's HBsAg persists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 2, hepatitis D virus, lines 2960–2964).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HDV encodes a single internal polypeptide, the delta antigen (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: anti HDV and HBsAg persist in chronic delta hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rapid HBsAg clearance is unlikely while the carrier's HBsAg persists.","B":"Antibody that persists with HBsAg marks ongoing infection, not lifelong immunity.","C":"A self-limited illness fits simultaneous HBV and HDV coinfection.","D":"Clearance of both viruses usually follows coinfection."}},{"id":"sdl15_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman has fever, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice, and enteric bacteria grow from her bile. Which condition sets the stage for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis","B":"Biliary obstruction","C":"Right heart failure","D":"Alcohol overuse","E":"Hepatic artery spasm"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extrahepatic or intrahepatic biliary obstruction from stones, strictures, or tumors creates stagnant bile that lets bacteria multiply and ascend, causing ascending cholangitis. Portal thrombosis, heart failure, alcohol, and arterial spasm are not the described setup. Portal vein thrombosis causes portal hypertension. Right heart failure causes passive hepatic congestion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, ascending cholangitis cause and risk factors, lines 2985–2988).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp explains that biliary obstruction impairs the mechanisms that keep bacteria out of the biliary tree (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists conjunctival icterus, pale stools, and dark urine in acute cholangitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — large bile duct obstruction and cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis obstructs portal inflow and causes portal hypertension with congestive splenomegaly, not infection of stagnant bile.","C":"Right heart failure causes passive hepatic congestion with centrilobular necrosis, not ascending bacterial infection of bile.","D":"Alcohol is not the described setup.","E":"Arterial spasm is not the described setup."}},{"id":"sdl15_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old man from Southeast Asia often eats undercooked freshwater fish and has flukes in his intrahepatic bile ducts. Which tumor is he at greatly increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Gallbladder adenoma","C":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","D":"Pancreatic cystadenoma","E":"Cholangiocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opisthorchis and Clonorchis flukes live in intrahepatic bile ducts and cause chronic biliary inflammation, hepatolithiasis, and a dramatically increased risk of cholangiocarcinoma. Flukes raise the risk of bile duct cancer rather than hepatocellular carcinoma, gallbladder adenoma, or pancreatic cystadenoma, and hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, liver flukes, lines 3012–3015); Robbins Ch. 18 (angiosarcoma: vinyl chloride, arsenic, Thorotrast).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Clonorchis sinensis infection as a risk factor for cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid lists liver fluke infection such as Clonorchis as a cholangiocarcinoma risk factor (First Aid 2025 GI biliary tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — HCC versus other hepatic malignancies","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has bloody dysentery and weeks later a liver abscess that drains thick anchovy paste fluid. Which organism most likely caused it?","choices":{"A":"Entamoeba histolytica","B":"Echinococcus granulosus","C":"Schistosoma mansoni","D":"Clonorchis sinensis","E":"Giardia lamblia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica trophozoites breach the colonic mucosa, causing amebic dysentery, and travel by portal blood to form liver abscesses full of necrotic anchovy paste debris. Echinococcus forms hydatid cysts, Schistosoma causes portal fibrosis, Clonorchis lives in bile ducts, and Giardia does not invade. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 3, amebiasis, lines 3020–3023).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebiasis spreads by the fecal oral route (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: bacteria can also reach the liver by portal hematogenous spread or direct extension (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — portal venous flow that carries gut organisms to the liver","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with thyroiditis has chronic liver inflammation with high IgG and circulating autoantibodies. Which histologic feature is most characteristic of her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Onion skin fibrosis","B":"Florid duct lesion","C":"Ground glass hepatocytes","D":"Interface hepatitis","E":"Zone 3 necrosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis shows prominent interface hepatitis, dense lymphoplasmacytic infiltrates with plasma cell clusters, emperipolesis, and hepatocyte rosettes. Onion skin fibrosis fits PSC, the florid duct lesion fits PBC, ground glass cells fit chronic HBV, and zone 3 necrosis fits toxic or ischemic injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, morphologic features, lines 3036–3042).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emperipolesis is intact lymphocytes or plasma cells engulfed within hepatocytes (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: First Aid describes a portal and periportal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate in autoimmune hepatitis (First Aid 2025 GI autoimmune hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old girl has autoimmune hepatitis with anti LKM1 antibodies and no ANA. Which other autoantibody may also be present?","choices":{"A":"Anti mitochondrial","B":"Anti liver cytosol 1","C":"Anti smooth muscle","D":"Anti neutrophil cytoplasm","E":"Anti tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Type 2 autoimmune hepatitis shows anti LKM1 and or anti liver cytosol 1 antibodies, with ANA and smooth muscle antibodies typically absent. Anti mitochondrial antibodies fit PBC, anti smooth muscle fits type 1, and the other antibodies fit vasculitis and celiac disease. Antimitochondrial antibody against the E2 component of pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDC-E2) is the most characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis and appears only uncommonly in autoimmune hepatitis. Anti-smooth muscle actin antibodies travel with ANA in type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, the more common type, which does not fit this ANA-negative child. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, lines 3054–3059).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists ANA and anti smooth muscle in type 1 and anti LKM1 in type 2 autoimmune hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, autoimmune hepatitis). COMLEX: serum IgG is elevated in both types of autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies fit vasculitis, not anti-LKM1 autoimmune hepatitis.","E":"Tissue transglutaminase antibodies fit celiac disease, not anti-LKM1 autoimmune hepatitis.","A":"Antimitochondrial antibody against the E2 component of pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDC-E2) is the most characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis and appears only uncommonly in autoimmune hepatitis.","C":"Anti-smooth muscle actin antibodies travel with ANA in type 1 autoimmune hepatitis, the more common type, which does not fit this ANA-negative child."}},{"id":"sdl15_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman of European descent develops autoimmune hepatitis after a course of minocycline. Which HLA allele is most linked to her disease?","choices":{"A":"HLA B27","B":"HLA DQ2","C":"HLA DR3","D":"HLA B51","E":"HLA DR2"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Autoimmune hepatitis is linked to HLA DR3 in Europeans, HLA DR4 in Japanese, and HLA DRB1 in South Americans, and drugs such as minocycline are proposed triggers. HLA B27, DQ2, B51, and DR2 are tied to other diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 4, pathogenesis and triggers, lines 3029–3033).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proposed triggers of autoimmune hepatitis include viral infections, drugs, and vaccinations (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: CD4 helper T cells drive B cells to make autoantibodies in autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions and characteristic serum antibodies of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl15_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman takes a large acetaminophen overdose and two days later has very high aminotransferases. Depletion of which protective molecule allows the toxic metabolite to injure her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Glutathione","B":"Glucuronic acid","C":"Sulfate stores","D":"Cytochrome P450","E":"Bilirubin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Excess NAPQI depletes glutathione and then binds cellular proteins, damaging mitochondria and killing zone 3 hepatocytes. Glucuronidation and sulfation are the normal pathways that are saturated, cytochrome P450 makes NAPQI, and bilirubin is not protective here. Most acetaminophen is excreted as glucuronate or sulfate conjugates. Robbins notes that cytochrome P-450 generates the reactive metabolites that mediate most drug-induced liver injury, so it produces NAPQI rather than protecting against it. First Aid states the acetaminophen metabolite NAPQI depletes glutathione and N-acetylcysteine regenerates it, not bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute necrosis pathogenesis, lines 3083–3089).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acetaminophen is the most common cause of acute liver failure requiring transplantation in the United States (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that depleted glutathione raises NAPQI and causes hepatocyte necrosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acetaminophen toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — acinar zones and their injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Glucuronidation and sulfation are the normal pathways that are saturated, cytochrome P450 makes NAPQI, and bilirubin is not protective here.","B":"Most acetaminophen is excreted as glucuronate or sulfate conjugates.","D":"Robbins notes that cytochrome P-450 generates the reactive metabolites that mediate most drug-induced liver injury, so it produces NAPQI rather than protecting against it.","E":"First Aid states the acetaminophen metabolite NAPQI depletes glutathione and N-acetylcysteine regenerates it, not bilirubin."}},{"id":"sdl15_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man had high dose chemotherapy for a bone marrow transplant and now has tender hepatomegaly, weight gain, ascites, and rising bilirubin. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Budd Chiari syndrome","B":"Portal vein thrombosis","C":"Veno occlusive disease","D":"Acute viral hepatitis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, or veno-occlusive disease, follows high-dose cytotoxic drugs, with toxic sinusoidal endothelial injury and fibrous obliteration of terminal hepatic venules. Budd-Chiari syndrome involves the large hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis mainly causes abdominal pain and portal hypertension with varices. Drug-induced bland cholestasis presents with pruritus and a high alkaline phosphatase. Acute viral hepatitis does not follow high-dose chemotherapy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, lines 3137–3146); Robbins Ch. 18 (portal vein obstruction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrrolizidine alkaloids in bush tea also cause sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: azathioprine, oxaliplatin, and cyclophosphamide are listed causes of sinusoidal obstruction syndrome (lecture SDL 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Acute viral hepatitis does not follow high-dose chemotherapy.","A":"Budd-Chiari syndrome obstructs the large hepatic veins.","B":"Robbins notes portal vein occlusion typically produces abdominal pain and portal hypertension with esophageal varices, not tender hepatomegaly and weight gain after conditioning chemotherapy."}},{"id":"sdl15_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old man taking isoniazid for latent tuberculosis develops fever, rash, eosinophilia, and jaundice with ALT more than 5 times normal. Which injury pattern is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Microvesicular steatosis","B":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","C":"Bland canalicular cholestasis","D":"Acute inflammatory hepatitis","E":"Sinusoidal endothelial injury"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Isoniazid, nitrofurantoin, phenytoin, methyldopa, and checkpoint inhibitors cause idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis with lymphoplasmacytic inflammation, fever, rash, eosinophilia, and ALT at least 5 times normal. Microvesicular steatosis follows valproate, tetracycline, high dose aspirin, or zidovudine; bland canalicular cholestasis follows oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids; sinusoidal endothelial injury follows high dose cytotoxic chemotherapy; and zone 3 confluent necrosis is the acetaminophen pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, acute inflammatory hepatitis, lines 3108–3116).","boardPrep":"Step 1: immune checkpoint inhibitors are listed causes of idiosyncratic acute inflammatory hepatitis (lecture SDL 15). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes eosinophilia with halothane induced liver injury (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, inhaled anesthetic toxicity).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with no prior liver disease develops jaundice and confusion 3 weeks after starting a new herbal supplement. She has asterixis and easy bruising. INR is 3.4 (reference range: 0.8–1.2) and platelets are 190,000/µL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/µL). After 10 mg of intravenous vitamin K, her INR is unchanged the next day. Which mechanism best explains her coagulopathy?","choices":{"A":"Failed hepatic synthesis of factors","B":"Poor vitamin K absorption from cholestasis","C":"Splenic pooling of platelets","D":"Antibody destruction of platelets","E":"Collagen defect from vitamin C lack"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of injury in a previously normal liver define acute liver failure, and the coagulopathy comes from failed hepatocyte synthesis of clotting factors such as II, V, VII, IX, and X. Because parenteral vitamin K did not correct the INR, the problem is synthesis rather than vitamin K supply. Poor vitamin K absorption from cholestasis would correct with intravenous vitamin K, which bypasses the gut. Splenic pooling of platelets lowers the platelet count and does not prolong the INR, and her count is normal. Antibody destruction of platelets, as in immune thrombocytopenia, would lower her normal platelet count. A collagen defect from vitamin C deficiency causes capillary fragility and gum bleeding with a normal INR. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute liver failure is encephalopathy and coagulopathy within 26 weeks of injury without prior liver disease, and its coagulopathy reflects failed clotting factor synthesis (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: An INR that does not correct with parenteral vitamin K points to hepatic synthetic failure rather than vitamin K deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — complications of alcohol-associated liver disease and MASLD","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Poor vitamin K absorption from cholestasis would","C":"Splenic pooling of platelets lowers the platelet","D":"Antibody destruction of platelets, as in immune","E":"A collagen defect from vitamin C deficiency"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the serological markers and their timing, viral proteins and genome types (RNA vs DNA, positive vs negative sense, envelope), and clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses (Hepatitis A, B, C, D, and E).","batch":3,"stem":"A 23-year-old man who injects drugs had an episode of acute hepatitis 8 months ago. He now has anti-HCV antibodies and persistently detectable HCV RNA. Which viral feature best explains why his antibody response failed to clear the infection?","choices":{"A":"Integration into host chromosomes","B":"Latency in sensory ganglia","C":"Dependence on HBV envelope","D":"Reverse transcription of its genome","E":"Error prone NS5B polymerase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Persistent HCV RNA beyond 6 months confirms chronic infection, which follows in 80% to 90% of cases. The NS5B RNA-dependent RNA polymerase makes frequent copying errors, generating genotypes and quasispecies within one host that escape the antibody response. Integration into host chromosomes does not occur with HCV, a positive sense RNA flavivirus without a DNA stage. Latency in sensory ganglia is how herpesviruses persist, not hepatitis viruses. Dependence on an HBV envelope describes hepatitis D, which needs HBsAg to complete its life cycle. Reverse transcription of the genome is a feature of HBV, whose polymerase also acts as a reverse transcriptase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 2; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCV is an enveloped positive sense RNA flavivirus whose error-prone NS5B polymerase creates quasispecies, explaining chronic infection in 80% to 90% of cases (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Anti-HCV antibody does not mean immunity, so HCV RNA testing separates cleared from chronic infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Integration into host chromosomes does not occur","B":"Latency in sensory ganglia is how herpesviruses","C":"Dependence on an HBV envelope describes hepatitis","D":"Reverse transcription of the genome is a"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the complications (liver abscess, sepsis, cholangiocarcinoma, hydatid cysts), risk factors (biliary obstruction), clinical manifestations, and underlying causes (ascending cholangitis, vectors) related to bacterial and parasitic liver infections.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who grew up near Lake Malawi and swam there often vomits blood from esophageal varices and has splenomegaly. Albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL) and INR is 1.0 (reference range: 0.8–1.2). Liver biopsy shows granulomas around eggs in the portal tracts with dense portal fibrosis and no regenerative nodules. At which level is blood flow into her liver obstructed?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoids around central veins","B":"Intrahepatic portal venules","C":"Terminal hepatic venules","D":"Two or more hepatic veins","E":"Inferior vena cava above liver"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Freshwater exposure in Africa with egg granulomas points to schistosomiasis: worms in the mesenteric veins release eggs that lodge in portal venules, and the granulomatous reaction and portal fibrosis block inflow before the sinusoids, giving noncirrhotic portal hypertension with preserved liver function. Sinusoids around central veins are congested in outflow disorders, not in egg-induced portal disease. Terminal hepatic venules are obliterated in sinusoidal obstruction syndrome after high-dose chemotherapy, not by egg granulomas. Two or more hepatic veins are occluded in Budd-Chiari syndrome, which causes centrilobular congestion. Inferior vena cava obstruction above the liver mimics Budd-Chiari outflow block and would not produce portal granulomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 3; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schistosoma eggs lodge in portal venules and cause granulomatous portal fibrosis with presinusoidal, noncirrhotic portal hypertension (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Varices with normal synthetic function in someone raised near African fresh water should prompt evaluation for schistosomiasis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 8 Obj 4 — portal hypertension and the portosystemic collaterals that form varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sinusoids around central veins are congested in","C":"Terminal hepatic venules are obliterated in sinusoidal","D":"Two or more hepatic veins are occluded","E":"Inferior vena cava obstruction above the liver"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis, risk factors, and characteristic serological markers of autoimmune hepatitis type 1 and 2.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl has 2 months of fatigue and jaundice. ALT is 640 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L), IgG is 2,400 mg/dL (reference range: 700–1,600 mg/dL), ceruloplasmin is 32 mg/dL (reference range: 20–40 mg/dL), and viral hepatitis serologies are negative. Anti-liver kidney microsome 1 antibodies are positive, and ANA is negative. Which process mainly destroys her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Antibody-driven complement lysis","B":"Copper-driven free radical injury","C":"Viral cytopathic replication","D":"Cytotoxic T cell attack","E":"Misfolded protein ER stress"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High IgG, anti-LKM1 antibodies, and negative viral tests in a teenager point to type 2 autoimmune hepatitis. CD4+ helper T cells drive the response, and CD8+ cytotoxic T cells working with them kill hepatocytes at the interface between portal tracts and lobule. Antibody-driven complement lysis is not the described mechanism; CD4+ T cells help B cells make autoantibodies that serve as diagnostic markers. Copper-driven free radical injury is Wilson disease, which her normal ceruloplasmin argues against. Viral cytopathic replication is excluded by her negative viral serologies. Misfolded protein ER stress is the hepatocyte injury of alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, which does not raise IgG or anti-LKM1. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 4; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis has ANA and anti-smooth muscle antibodies, type 2 has anti-LKM1 against CYP2D6 and affects children and teens, and both show interface hepatitis with plasma cells (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Check ceruloplasmin and viral serologies in a young patient with hepatitis before settling on autoimmune hepatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Antibody-driven complement lysis is not the described","B":"Copper-driven free radical injury is Wilson disease,","C":"Viral cytopathic replication is excluded by her","E":"Misfolded protein ER stress is the hepatocyte"}},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the morphological finding, clinical manifestation, associated agent, and idiosyncratic genetic susceptibility of drug and toxin-induced liver injuries.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy is given aspirin for influenza and days later has vomiting, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. Which liver finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Macrovesicular steatosis","B":"Microvesicular steatosis","C":"Zone 3 confluent necrosis","D":"Portal interface hepatitis","E":"Pericellular bridging fibrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High dose aspirin in children causes Reye syndrome, a mitochondrial toxicity with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus. Macrovesicular fat pushes the nucleus aside, zone 3 necrosis fits acetaminophen, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune disease, and bridging fibrosis is chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 15, section 5, microvesicular steatosis, lines 3117–3126).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes Reye syndrome as mitochondrial injury with hyperammonemia, encephalopathy, and cerebral edema (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Reye syndrome). COMLEX: First Aid links Reye syndrome to aspirin given for viral infections such as VZV and influenza (First Aid 2025 GI Reye syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — alcohol associated and steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl15_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair and identify the clinical features of acute (hepatic encephalopathy, coagulopathy, portal hypertension) and chronic (pruritus) liver failure.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with long-standing hepatitis C had a liver biopsy 6 years ago showing fibrous septa that encircle nodules of hepatocytes. After antiviral therapy cleared the virus, a repeat biopsy shows thin, fragmented septa and partly reconnected parenchyma. Which process most directly explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocytes engulfing the collagen","B":"Septa calcifying and contracting","C":"Metalloproteinase breakdown of scar","D":"Type III collagen maturing to type I","E":"Bile ductules replacing the septa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cirrhosis is fibrous septa encircling regenerative nodules after chronic injury, and when the injury stops, metalloproteinases can degrade the fibrous bands, giving partial scar regression known as incomplete septal cirrhosis, although vascular remodeling often persists. Hepatocytes engulfing collagen is not a recognized repair mechanism; hepatocytes restore mass but do not remove scar. Septa calcifying and contracting would not thin and fragment them. Type III collagen maturing to type I describes scar maturation, not the loss of septa seen on the repeat biopsy. Bile ductules replacing the septa would be a ductular reaction, which does not dissolve fibrous bands. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Stellate cells activated by TGF-beta and PDGF deposit type I and III collagen, and cirrhosis is diffuse fibrous septa around regenerative nodules (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Removing the cause of injury, such as curing HCV, can let fibrous septa partly regress.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatocytes engulfing collagen is not a recognized","B":"Septa calcifying and contracting would not thin","D":"Type III collagen maturing to type I","E":"Bile ductules replacing the septa would be"}}]},{"sdlNumber":16,"title":"SDL 16 — Pathology - Steatotic, Inherited, and Cholestatic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"About how much daily alcohol intake for 10 to 20 years is the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease?","choices":{"A":"At least 8 g per day","B":"At least 20 g per day","C":"At least 200 g per day","D":"At least 80 g per day","E":"At least 40 g per day"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The lecture gives 80 g or more of ethanol per day for 10 to 20 years as the usual threshold for alcohol associated liver disease, with women more susceptible at lower intake. Robbins (SDL 15-19 reading) puts it slightly differently: risk of severe injury becomes significant at 80 g or more per day, and 160 g or more per day for 10 to 20 years is frequently associated with severe injury. The other values are not that figure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, risk factors, lines 3149–3154).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists macrovesicular steatosis, neutrophils, ballooning, and Mallory Denk bodies in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds pericellular and sinusoidal fibrosis in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"At least 8 g per day is far below the threshold.","B":"At least 20 g per day is below the lecture's threshold.","C":"At least 200 g per day is above the lecture's usual threshold.","E":"At least 40 g per day is below the lecture's threshold."}},{"id":"sdl16_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"Which proteins make up the Mallory hyaline seen in ballooned hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Keratin 8 and 18","B":"Desmin and vimentin","C":"Actin and myosin","D":"Tau and amyloid beta","E":"Collagen types I and III"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mallory hyaline, or Mallory Denk bodies, are tangled clumps of the intermediate filaments keratin 8 and 18 in ballooned hepatocytes. Desmin, vimentin, actin, myosin, tau, amyloid beta, and collagen are not its components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives an AST to ALT ratio above 1.5 to 1 with values under 500 U/L in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised GGT, bilirubin, and ferritin with leukocytosis in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":1,"stem":"What AST to ALT ratio is typical of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease?","choices":{"A":"2 or greater","B":"About 1.5","C":"Less than 1","D":"Above 5","E":"Between 3 and 4"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In MASLD the ALT usually exceeds the AST, so the AST to ALT ratio is less than 1. A ratio of 2 or more points to alcohol associated disease. Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, or above 5 all exceed 1, and the last two fall in the alcohol range. Robbins notes that serum ALT tends to exceed AST in chronic liver diseases other than alcohol-associated disease, so a ratio above 5 does not fit. Robbins notes that serum ALT tends to exceed AST in chronic liver diseases other than alcohol-associated disease, so a ratio of 3 to 4 does not fit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp gives MASLD aminotransferases under 500 U/L with an AST to ALT ratio below 1 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists central obesity, type 2 diabetes, hypertension, high triglycerides, and low HDL as MASLD causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A ratio of 2 or more points to alcohol associated disease.","B":"An AST to ALT ratio of about 1.5 is too high for metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease.","D":"Ratios of about 1.5, 3 to 4, or above 5 all exceed 1, and the last two fall in the alcohol range.","E":"Robbins notes that serum ALT tends to exceed AST in chronic liver diseases other than alcohol-associated disease, so a ratio of 3 to 4 does not fit."}},{"id":"sdl16_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"Which HFE mutation is the most frequent cause of hereditary hemochromatosis?","choices":{"A":"H63D","B":"C282Y","C":"G551D","D":"F508del","E":"V600E"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hereditary hemochromatosis is most often an autosomal recessive HFE mutation on chromosome 6, most frequently the C282Y cysteine to tyrosine change. H63D is a less frequent HFE variant, and the others are CFTR and BRAF mutations. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hereditary hemochromatosis, lines 3185–3190).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Vibrio vulnificus, Yersinia enterocolitica, and Listeria as infections favored by iron overload (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists therapeutic phlebotomy, deferoxamine, deferasirox, and deferiprone as treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"G551D is a CFTR mutation.","D":"F508del is a CFTR mutation.","E":"V600E is a BRAF mutation.","A":"H63D is another HFE variant, less often the one found in diagnosed patients."}},{"id":"sdl16_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene and chromosome are mutated in Wilson disease?","choices":{"A":"HFE on chromosome 6","B":"ATP7A on the X chromosome","C":"SERPINA1 on chromosome 14","D":"HAMP on chromosome 19","E":"ATP7B on chromosome 13"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Wilson disease is an autosomal recessive loss of function mutation in ATP7B on chromosome 13, a hepatocyte copper transporting ATPase. HFE causes hemochromatosis, SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency, HAMP encodes hepcidin, and ATP7A is a different copper transporter. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3213–3217).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists Fanconi syndrome as a complication of Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists a low copper diet, penicillamine, trientine, and zinc salts as management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":1,"stem":"PiZZ alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes very low circulating enzyme inhibitor. Why does it lead to emphysema?","choices":{"A":"Unchecked neutrophil elastase activity","B":"Copper deposits in alveolar walls","C":"Iron overload of alveolar macrophages","D":"Misfolded protein trapped in lung cells","E":"Excess surfactant made by type II cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Alpha-1 antitrypsin normally inhibits proteases released by neutrophils, especially neutrophil elastase, so its deficiency allows unchecked elastase activity that destroys alveolar walls and causes emphysema. Copper deposits in alveolar walls are not part of this disease; copper overload is Wilson disease. Iron overload of alveolar macrophages is not the mechanism. Misfolded protein trapped in cells explains the liver disease, because the retained protein accumulates in hepatocytes, not lung cells. Excess surfactant made by type II cells does not cause emphysema. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AAT deficiency causes emphysema by loss of elastase inhibition and liver disease by toxic accumulation of misfolded protein in hepatocytes (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: panacinar emphysema in a young nonsmoker with liver disease suggests AAT deficiency.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Copper deposits in alveolar walls are not","C":"Iron overload of alveolar macrophages is not","D":"Misfolded protein trapped in cells explains the","E":"Excess surfactant made by type II cells"}},{"id":"sdl16_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"What serum bilirubin level is typical of Crigler Najjar type I?","choices":{"A":"Below 3 mg/dL","B":"Above 20 mg/dL","C":"About 5 mg/dL","D":"Below 1 mg/dL","E":"About 10 mg/dL"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type I causes severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, typically above 20 mg/dL. Gilbert syndrome is usually below 3 mg/dL, and type II is usually below 20 mg/dL. Values of about 5 or 10 mg/dL fit the moderate, usually below 20 mg/dL range of type II. Below 1 mg/dL is not hyperbilirubinemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type I, lines 3258–3266).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes that phototherapy converts unconjugated bilirubin into a water soluble form excreted in urine and bile (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp states that liver transplant is curative in Crigler Najjar syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Below 1 mg/dL is not hyperbilirubinemia."}},{"id":"sdl16_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"Gilbert syndrome leaves partial UGT1A1 activity. Why does this raise unconjugated rather than conjugated bilirubin?","choices":{"A":"Canalicular MRP2 export is defective","B":"Hepatic uptake transporters are absent","C":"Bilirubin glucuronidation is reduced","D":"Heme oxygenase activity is increased","E":"Red cell destruction is accelerated"},"correct":"C","explanation":"UGT1A1 conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid in the hepatocyte endoplasmic reticulum, so residual activity in Gilbert syndrome means less bilirubin is glucuronidated and unconjugated bilirubin rises mildly. Defective canalicular MRP2 export is Dubin-Johnson syndrome, which raises conjugated bilirubin. Absent hepatic uptake transporters describe Rotor syndrome, also a conjugated pattern. Increased heme oxygenase activity is not the defect in Gilbert syndrome. Accelerated red cell destruction is hemolysis, which Gilbert syndrome lacks. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, hereditary hyperbilirubinemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UGT1A1 deficiency causes unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, severe in Crigler-Najjar type I and mild in Gilbert syndrome (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Gilbert syndrome is benign and needs reassurance, not treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Defective canalicular MRP2 export is Dubin-Johnson syndrome","B":"Absent hepatic uptake transporters describe Rotor syndrome","D":"Increased heme oxygenase activity is not the","E":"Accelerated red cell destruction is hemolysis, which"}},{"id":"sdl16_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":1,"stem":"Which transporters are deficient in Rotor syndrome?","choices":{"A":"MRP2 transporter","B":"UGT1A1 enzyme","C":"ATP7B transporter","D":"BSEP transporter","E":"OATP1B1 and 1B3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotor syndrome is an autosomal recessive deficiency of OATP1B1 and OATP1B3, impairing hepatic reuptake and storage of conjugated bilirubin. MRP2 is defective in Dubin Johnson syndrome, and UGT1A1 in Gilbert and Crigler Najjar syndromes. ATP7B is the Wilson disease copper transporter, and BSEP exports bile salts rather than bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Rotor syndrome, lines 3294–3302); Robbins Ch. 18 (canalicular transporters: BSEP for bile salts).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised total urinary coproporphyrin in Rotor syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Rotor syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that some conjugated bilirubin returns to the sinusoids and is taken up by downstream hepatocytes via OATP1B1 and 1B3 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which laboratory pattern accompanies large bile duct obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Isolated raised AST","B":"Raised ALP and GGT","C":"Raised lipase and amylase","D":"Raised indirect bilirubin","E":"Raised CK and aldolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Large bile duct obstruction raises serum alkaline phosphatase, GGT, and conjugated bilirubin, with jaundice, pruritus, dark urine, and pale stools. Indirect bilirubin, isolated AST, lipase, and muscle enzymes do not define it. Robbins names elevated serum alkaline phosphatase and GGT, canalicular and bile duct enzymes, as the characteristic laboratory findings of cholestatic disease, not an isolated AST rise. Lipase and amylase are pancreatic enzymes. First Aid lists biliary tract obstruction as a cause of conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia, not raised indirect bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes choledocholithiasis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp adds that the pain may radiate to the back or right scapula with nausea and diaphoresis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Indirect bilirubin, isolated AST, lipase, and muscle enzymes do not define it.","A":"Robbins names elevated serum alkaline phosphatase and GGT, canalicular and bile duct enzymes, as the characteristic laboratory findings of cholestatic disease, not an isolated AST rise.","C":"Lipase and amylase are pancreatic enzymes.","D":"First Aid lists biliary tract obstruction as a cause of conjugated (direct) hyperbilirubinemia, not raised indirect bilirubin."}},{"id":"sdl16_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nodule pattern marks biliary cirrhosis after uncorrected choledocholithiasis?","choices":{"A":"Garland shaped nodules","B":"Hobnail surface nodules","C":"Nutmeg mottled pattern","D":"Jigsaw puzzle nodules","E":"Onion skin layering"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Uncorrected choledocholithiasis causes progressive periductal fibrosis and biliary cirrhosis with irregular jigsaw puzzle shaped regenerative nodules. Garland shaped cirrhosis is linked to PBC in the lecture, nutmeg liver is passive congestion, onion skin fibrosis is PSC, and a hobnail surface is not the lecture pattern. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists surgical or ischemic strictures and PSC as adult causes of large duct obstruction (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists pancreatic head adenocarcinoma and cholangiocarcinoma as malignant adult causes (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the morphologic hallmark of ascending cholangitis?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas around ducts","B":"Concentric periductal fibrosis","C":"Plasma cell interface hepatitis","D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates","E":"Neutrophils in duct epithelium"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ascending cholangitis shows neutrophilic infiltration within bile duct epithelium and lumens. Granulomatous duct injury fits PBC, concentric fibrosis fits PSC, and plasma cell interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis. Eosinophilic duct infiltrates are not the hallmark of ascending cholangitis, which shows neutrophils in the duct epithelium and lumens. Poorly formed epithelioid granulomas centered on bile ducts (the florid duct lesion) are Robbins's characteristic lesion of PBC. Circumferential onion-skin periductal fibrosis around an atrophic duct lumen is the PSC lesion in Robbins. Interface injury with numerous clustered plasma cells is typical of autoimmune hepatitis in Robbins, not of bacterial cholangitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331).","boardPrep":"Step 1: severe ascending cholangitis can progress to pyogenic liver abscesses and Gram negative sepsis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture lists acute on chronic liver failure and death as outcomes of severe cases (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Eosinophilic duct infiltrates are not the hallmark of ascending cholangitis, which shows neutrophils in the duct epithelium and lumens.","A":"Poorly formed epithelioid granulomas centered on bile ducts (the florid duct lesion) are Robbins's characteristic lesion of PBC.","B":"Circumferential onion-skin periductal fibrosis around an atrophic duct lumen is the PSC lesion in Robbins.","C":"Interface injury with numerous clustered plasma cells is typical of autoimmune hepatitis in Robbins, not of bacterial cholangitis."}},{"id":"sdl16_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Primary biliary cholangitis is an autoimmune attack on bile ducts. Which ducts does it mainly destroy?","choices":{"A":"Extrahepatic and large hilar ducts","B":"Cystic duct and gallbladder neck","C":"Common bile duct at the ampulla","D":"Small intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Main pancreatic duct and papilla"},"correct":"D","explanation":"PBC is a T cell mediated inflammatory destruction of small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, while large intrahepatic ducts and the extrahepatic tree are spared. Extrahepatic and large hilar ducts are the targets of primary sclerosing cholangitis. The cystic duct and gallbladder neck are sites of stone impaction, not PBC. The common bile duct at the ampulla is involved by stones and tumors. The main pancreatic duct and papilla are not affected by PBC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, primary biliary cholangitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PBC destroys small intrahepatic ducts with florid duct lesions, while PSC beads large intrahepatic and extrahepatic ducts (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: a middle-aged woman with itching, high ALP, and AMA has PBC.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Extrahepatic and large hilar ducts are the","B":"The cystic duct and gallbladder neck are","C":"The common bile duct at the ampulla","E":"The main pancreatic duct and papilla are"}},{"id":"sdl16_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antigen do the antimitochondrial antibodies of PBC target?","choices":{"A":"PDC E2 subunit","B":"CYP2D6 enzyme","C":"Thyroid peroxidase","D":"Tissue transglutaminase","E":"Proteinase 3"},"correct":"A","explanation":"AMA, present in 95 percent of PBC, targets the PDC E2 subunit of pyruvate dehydrogenase. CYP2D6 is the LKM1 target, and thyroid peroxidase, tissue transglutaminase, and proteinase 3 belong to other autoimmune diseases. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists antimitochondrial antibodies with hypergammaglobulinemia in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a dense lymphocytic portal infiltrate with granulomas on PBC biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Chronic inflammation of the bile ducts in primary sclerosing cholangitis can lead to biliary intraepithelial neoplasia. Which malignancy is the major long-term risk?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","C":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Hepatoblastoma","E":"Gastric MALT lymphoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In PSC, chronic inflammatory injury of the large bile ducts can produce biliary intraepithelial neoplasia that progresses to cholangiocarcinoma, so these patients need surveillance. Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign tumor linked to oral contraceptives. Hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to vinyl chloride and arsenic exposure. Hepatoblastoma is a liver tumor of young children. Gastric MALT lymphoma arises in H. pylori gastritis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18, primary sclerosing cholangitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PSC, often with ulcerative colitis and pANCA, predisposes to cholangiocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: new jaundice or a dominant stricture in PSC raises concern for cholangiocarcinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign tumor linked","B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is linked to vinyl chloride","D":"Hepatoblastoma is a liver tumor of young","E":"Gastric MALT lymphoma arises in H. pylori"}},{"id":"sdl16_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks a fifth of whiskey daily has AST 240 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L) and ALT 100 U/L (reference 10–40 U/L), and his CK is normal. Which mechanism best explains why his AST exceeds his ALT?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal AST clearance","B":"Bile duct leakage of ALT","C":"Selective hepatic ALT degradation","D":"Low hepatic pyridoxal phosphate","E":"Skeletal muscle AST release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In alcohol associated disease the AST to ALT ratio is usually 2 or more because ethanol depletes hepatic pyridoxal 5 phosphate, and because ethanol injures hepatocyte mitochondria. Renal clearance, ductal leak, selective ALT degradation, and muscle release, which a normal CK argues against, are not the lecture mechanism. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, AST to ALT ratio, lines 3176–3181).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised PT or INR, low albumin, and low platelets in advanced alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists acute liver failure, cirrhosis, and HCC as complications (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Renal clearance, ductal leak, selective ALT degradation, and muscle release, which a normal CK argues against, are not the lecture mechanism."}},{"id":"sdl16_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with central obesity and type 2 diabetes who does not drink has hepatomegaly and fatty liver on imaging. Which mechanism best explains the fat in her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Increased VLDL export","B":"More peripheral lipolysis","C":"Excess NADH from ethanol","D":"Reduced insulin resistance","E":"Copper driven radicals"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In MASLD, insulin resistance increases lipolysis in adipose tissue, raising free fatty acid delivery to the liver, where triglyceride synthesis causes steatosis. VLDL export removes fat, ethanol derived NADH is the alcohol mechanism, and copper radicals fit Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, pathogenesis, lines 3155–3162).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes triglyceride and free fatty acid buildup causing ROS, mitochondrial dysfunction, and inflammation that progress to MASH (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that MASLD is often asymptomatic apart from hepatomegaly (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, MASLD).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has a liver biopsy compared with that of a patient with MASH. Which finding is more prominent in his biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Mainly microvesicular fat","B":"Granulomatous duct injury","C":"Periportal iron granules","D":"Lymphoplasmacytic rosettes","E":"Abundant Mallory hyaline"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Both diseases show steatosis, ballooning, and chicken wire pericellular fibrosis, but Mallory hyaline and neutrophilic infiltrates are more prominent in alcoholic hepatitis than in MASH. Fat is mainly macrovesicular, and the other findings belong to PBC, hemochromatosis, and autoimmune hepatitis. Robbins states that macrovesicular steatosis is the predominant form in alcohol-associated liver disease, with microvesicular fat only in the unusual alcoholic foamy degeneration. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 1, histologic findings, lines 3163–3171).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, tender hepatomegaly, anorexia, and fever in alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists hepatic encephalopathy in severe alcohol induced hepatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, alcohol induced hepatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Granulomatous bile-duct injury fits primary biliary cholangitis, not the alcohol-related biopsy.","C":"Periportal iron granules fit hemochromatosis, not the alcohol-related biopsy.","D":"Lymphoplasmacytic rosettes fit autoimmune hepatitis, not the alcohol-related biopsy.","A":"Robbins states that macrovesicular steatosis is the predominant form in alcohol-associated liver disease, with microvesicular fat only in the unusual alcoholic foamy degeneration."}},{"id":"sdl16_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has diabetes, slate gray skin, cardiomyopathy, and cirrhosis. His transferrin saturation is very high. Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Excess dietary heme iron","B":"Defective ceruloplasmin","C":"Blocked biliary iron excretion","D":"Low hepcidin synthesis","E":"Reduced ferroportin activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In hereditary hemochromatosis, HFE mutations impair hepatic hepcidin synthesis. Low hepcidin leaves ferroportin uninhibited in enterocytes and macrophages, so iron is hyperabsorbed. Reduced ferroportin activity would lower absorption, and ceruloplasmin defects fit Wilson disease. Hemochromatosis is not caused by excess dietary heme iron. Iron overload in hemochromatosis comes from increased absorption due to low hepcidin, not blocked biliary iron excretion. Failure to incorporate copper into ceruloplasmin results from ATP7B loss in Wilson disease, which accumulates toxic copper in the liver, brain, and eye rather than iron. Reduced ferroportin activity would do the opposite. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, hemochromatosis pathogenesis, lines 3191–3196).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised iron, ferritin, and transferrin saturation with low TIBC in hereditary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic transfusions and chronic liver disease as causes of secondary hemochromatosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hemochromatosis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hemochromatosis is not caused by excess dietary heme iron.","C":"Iron overload in hemochromatosis comes from increased absorption due to low hepcidin, not blocked biliary iron excretion.","B":"Failure to incorporate copper into ceruloplasmin results from ATP7B loss in Wilson disease, which accumulates toxic copper in the liver, brain, and eye rather than iron.","E":"Reduced ferroportin activity would do the opposite."}},{"id":"sdl16_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old boy has a new tremor, depression, and Coombs negative hemolytic anemia. His serum ceruloplasmin is low. Which eye finding is most likely on slit lamp exam?","choices":{"A":"Kayser Fleischer rings","B":"Cherry red macular spot","C":"Upward lens dislocation","D":"Blue tinted sclerae","E":"Conjunctival Bitot spots"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Wilson disease leaks free copper into the blood, which deposits in Descemet membrane at the corneal limbus as green to brown Kayser Fleischer rings. Cherry red spots, lens dislocation, blue sclerae, and Bitot spots fit other disorders. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, Wilson disease, lines 3223–3236).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists low haptoglobin and raised indirect bilirubin with a negative direct Coombs test in Wilson disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low ceruloplasmin, raised 24 hour urine copper, and raised free serum copper (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Wilson disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has cholestatic hepatitis. His biopsy shows round cytoplasmic globules in periportal hepatocytes that stay pink after diastase digestion with PAS. Which mechanism best explains his liver injury?","choices":{"A":"Unchecked elastase in liver","B":"Copper buildup in cells","C":"Iron overload of cells","D":"Viral infection of cells","E":"Misfolded protein in ER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In AAT deficiency the PiZ protein misfolds and is retained in the endoplasmic reticulum, a toxic gain of function that triggers ER stress and hepatocyte death. Unchecked elastase injures the lungs, not the liver. Copper and iron fit Wilson disease and hemochromatosis. Viral infection does not produce PAS positive, diastase resistant globules; those are retained AAT. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 2, AAT deficiency, lines 3241–3254).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links abnormal AAT in hepatocytes to cell stress, inflammation, cirrhosis, and HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, AAT deficiency). COMLEX: the lecture gives a 2 to 3 percent HCC risk in adult AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl has unconjugated bilirubin of 28 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) that persists despite phototherapy. Her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal, and her direct Coombs test is negative. Which defect is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Absent UGT1A1 activity","B":"Mildly reduced UGT1A1","C":"Defective MRP2 export","D":"Absent OATP1B1 reuptake","E":"Excess heme oxygenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Profound unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia above 20 mg/dL with hemolysis excluded fits Crigler Najjar type I, a complete loss of UGT1A1 activity that risks kernicterus. Type II has residual activity that phenobarbital can induce. MRP2 and OATP defects raise conjugated bilirubin. Overproduction of bilirubin from heme, as in hemolysis, is excluded by the normal hemoglobin and reticulocyte count and the negative Coombs test. MRP2 mutation causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a clinically innocuous conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with brown-black pigment deposited in hepatocytes. Robbins attributes Crigler-Najjar type 1 to severe UGT1A1 deficiency, a conjugation defect rather than excess heme oxygenase activity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar syndromes, lines 3258–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes persistent neonatal jaundice progressing to kernicterus in type I disease (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes type II is less likely to cause neonatal jaundice (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Crigler Najjar syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Type II has residual activity that phenobarbital can induce.","D":"MRP2 and OATP defects raise conjugated bilirubin.","C":"MRP2 mutation causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a clinically innocuous conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with brown-black pigment deposited in hepatocytes.","E":"Robbins attributes Crigler-Najjar type 1 to severe UGT1A1 deficiency, a conjugation defect rather than excess heme oxygenase activity."}},{"id":"sdl16_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has recurrent mild jaundice with conjugated bilirubin in her urine. Her liver biopsy shows coarse dark pigment within hepatocyte lysosomes. Which hepatocyte transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Sinusoidal OATP1B1","B":"Basolateral NTCP","C":"Canalicular MRP2","D":"Canalicular BSEP","E":"Hepatocyte ATP7B"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Benign conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with dark lysosomal pigment and a grossly black liver is Dubin Johnson syndrome, a loss of function of the canalicular MRP2 transporter. OATP1B1 loss causes Rotor syndrome with a normal looking liver, BSEP exports bile salts, NTCP takes up bile salts, and ATP7B defects cause Wilson disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Dubin Johnson syndrome, lines 3284–3302).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists over 50 percent direct bilirubin with a mild course in Dubin Johnson syndrome (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes dark granular pigment in hepatic lysosomes on biopsy (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, Dubin Johnson syndrome).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy notices mild yellow eyes after fasting and exam stress. His indirect bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and other tests are normal. Which genetic change most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Coding region frameshift","B":"Complete UGT1A1 deletion","C":"MRP2 nonsense mutation","D":"TATA box promoter mutation","E":"Biallelic OATP1B3 deletion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gilbert syndrome causes mild, fluctuating unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia under 3 mg/dL with stress or fasting, and is most often due to a TATA box promoter mutation that lowers UGT1A1 expression. Complete loss fits Crigler Najjar type I, and MRP2 or OATP changes raise conjugated bilirubin. A coding region frameshift that destroys the enzyme would fit complete loss of activity rather than Gilbert's reduced expression. MRP2 mutation causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with brown-black hepatocyte pigment, whereas Gilbert syndrome keeps residual UGT1A1 activity with unconjugated bilirubin. First Aid describes Gilbert syndrome as mildly decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase conjugation, whereas absent enzyme activity defines Crigler-Najjar type I, so a destructive coding frameshift does not fit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Gilbert syndrome, lines 3275–3283).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes some Gilbert syndrome variants are exceptions to autosomal recessive inheritance (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives Gilbert syndrome a normal life expectancy (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"MRP2 mutation causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a conjugated hyperbilirubinemia with brown-black hepatocyte pigment, whereas Gilbert syndrome keeps residual UGT1A1 activity with unconjugated bilirubin.","E":"Complete loss fits Crigler Najjar type I, and MRP2 or OATP changes raise conjugated bilirubin.","C":"An MRP2 nonsense mutation causes Dubin-Johnson syndrome, a conjugated hyperbilirubinemia, not this mild unconjugated rise.","A":"First Aid describes Gilbert syndrome as mildly decreased UDP-glucuronosyltransferase conjugation, whereas absent enzyme activity defines Crigler-Najjar type I, so a destructive coding frameshift does not fit."}},{"id":"sdl16_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman has jaundice, itching, tea colored urine, and clay colored stools from a blocked common bile duct. What best explains the color of her stools?","choices":{"A":"Raised fecal urobilinogen","B":"Excess biliverdin in gut","C":"Colonic bile salt deficiency","D":"Bacterial overgrowth in colon","E":"Absent intestinal stercobilin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"With large duct obstruction, conjugated bilirubin fails to reach the gut, so no stercobilin forms and stools turn pale or acholic, while water soluble conjugated bilirubin darkens the urine. Fecal urobilinogen falls rather than rises, biliverdin is reduced to bilirubin in macrophages long before bile, and the brown color comes from bacterial stercobilin, so neither bile salt lack nor bacterial overgrowth explains pale stools. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, clinical features, lines 3313–3318).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists jaundice, icterus, pale stools, dark urine, and pruritus in choledocholithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis). COMLEX: the lecture explains that pruritus comes from systemic retention of bile salts (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman with a stone in her common bile duct develops epigastric pain radiating to the back and a lipase five times normal. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Acute cholecystitis","B":"Acute pancreatitis","C":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","D":"Hepatic abscess","E":"Ascending cholangitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impacted ductal stones can cause secondary acute pancreatitis, shown here by back radiating epigastric pain and a high lipase. Cholangitis, abscess, cholecystitis, and ulcer perforation do not explain the lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, choledocholithiasis, lines 3320–3324).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists pressure necrosis and biliary colic from impacted ductal stones (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes choledocholithiasis as common bile duct obstruction by a gallstone with severe RUQ or epigastric pain (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, choledocholithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 3 — acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with known gallstones has fever, chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice. Which named sign cluster does she show?","choices":{"A":"Beck triad","B":"Virchow triad","C":"Charcot triad","D":"Whipple triad","E":"Reynolds pentad"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fever with chills, right upper quadrant pain, and jaundice form the Charcot triad of ascending cholangitis, usually from enteric coliforms such as E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella. Reynolds pentad adds altered mental status and shock, which she lacks. Beck triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds) is cardiac tamponade, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice. Virchow triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability) predicts thrombosis, not fever, RUQ pain, and jaundice. Whipple triad (episodic hypoglycemia relieved by glucose) is a pancreatic islet tumor presentation, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice. First Aid defines Reynolds pentad as the Charcot triad plus altered mental status and shock, which this patient lacks. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 4, ascending cholangitis, lines 3325–3331); First Aid 2025 GI acute cholangitis (fa_gastro_cache 2267–2268).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture names E coli, Enterococcus, and Klebsiella as the usual organisms of ascending cholangitis (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes cholangitis as infection of a stagnant, obstructed biliary tree (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Beck triad (hypotension, distended neck veins, distant heart sounds) is cardiac tamponade, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice.","B":"Virchow triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability) predicts thrombosis, not fever, RUQ pain, and jaundice.","D":"Whipple triad (episodic hypoglycemia relieved by glucose) is a pancreatic islet tumor presentation, not fever with RUQ pain and jaundice.","E":"First Aid defines Reynolds pentad as the Charcot triad plus altered mental status and shock, which this patient lacks."}},{"id":"sdl16_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with autoimmune thyroiditis has fatigue, intense itching, and a high ALP. Her antimitochondrial antibody is positive. Which finding is most likely on liver biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Florid duct lesion","B":"Onion skin fibrosis","C":"Interface hepatitis","D":"Bile duct beading","E":"Ground glass cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"PBC is T cell mediated destruction of small and medium intrahepatic bile ducts, with the florid duct lesion of granulomatous, lymphoplasmacytic duct destruction. Onion skin fibrosis and beading fit PSC, interface hepatitis fits autoimmune hepatitis (lecture SDL 15, line 3036), and ground glass cells are not a PBC finding. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, morphology and duct lesions, lines 3345–3351).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised cholesterol and triglycerides with markedly raised ALP in PBC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp links PBC to autoimmune conditions such as Hashimoto thyroiditis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl16_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with ulcerative colitis has a raised ALP on routine labs, and MRCP shows strictures alternating with dilations of his bile ducts. Which antibody is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Positive AMA","B":"Positive pANCA","C":"Positive ASMA","D":"Positive anti LKM1","E":"Positive anti dsDNA"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PSC, linked to ulcerative colitis and showing a beaded biliary tree, is pANCA positive in about 65 percent, while AMA is characteristically negative. Anti smooth muscle and anti LKM1 fit autoimmune hepatitis, and anti dsDNA fits lupus. AMA is characteristically negative in PSC; a positive AMA marks PBC. Anti-LKM1 antibodies, directed against CYP2D6, characterize type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, which is more common in children. Antibodies to double-stranded DNA and the Smith antigen are virtually diagnostic of systemic lupus erythematosus. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, characteristic serum antibodies, lines 3358–3363).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes concentric periductal onion skin fibrosis on PSC histopathology (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes that ERCP shows similar findings to MRCP and allows stent placement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary sclerosing cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"AMA is characteristically negative in PSC; a positive AMA marks PBC.","C":"Anti smooth muscle and anti LKM1 fit autoimmune hepatitis, and anti dsDNA fits lupus.","D":"Anti-LKM1 antibodies, directed against CYP2D6, characterize type 2 autoimmune hepatitis, which is more common in children.","E":"Antibodies to double-stranded DNA and the Smith antigen are virtually diagnostic of systemic lupus erythematosus."}},{"id":"sdl16_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with PBC for many years now has high serum cholesterol. Which skin finding is she most likely to develop?","choices":{"A":"Pretibial myxedema","B":"Malar rash","C":"Xanthelasmas","D":"Erythema nodosum","E":"Heliotrope rash"},"correct":"C","explanation":"PBC progresses over years to hypercholesterolemia, xanthelasmas, jaundice, steatorrhea, osteomalacia, and biliary cirrhosis. Pretibial myxedema fits Graves disease, the malar rash fits lupus, erythema nodosum fits sarcoidosis or IBD, and the heliotrope rash fits dermatomyositis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 5, clinical presentations and risks, lines 3364–3367).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture lists osteomalacia and steatorrhea late in PBC (lecture SDL 16). COMLEX: the lecture describes PSC as often found by a raised ALP during ulcerative colitis screening (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate alcohol-associated liver disease from metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on risk factors, pathogenesis, histologic findings (chicken wire, Mallory hyaline), complications, and AST:ALT ratio.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with obesity and type 2 diabetes says she does not drink alcohol. AST is 210 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L) and ALT is 95 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). Liver biopsy shows macrovesicular steatosis, many ballooned hepatocytes with abundant Mallory hyaline, clusters of neutrophils around injured cells, and cholestasis. Which conclusion do these findings best support?","choices":{"A":"Typical metabolic steatohepatitis","B":"Wilson disease copper toxicity","C":"Acetaminophen zone 3 necrosis","D":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis","E":"Hidden heavy alcohol use"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An AST to ALT ratio above 2 together with prominent ballooning, abundant Mallory hyaline, neutrophil clusters, and cholestasis favors alcohol-associated steatohepatitis over MASH, even in a patient with metabolic risk factors who denies drinking. Typical metabolic steatohepatitis usually has ALT above AST and fewer Mallory bodies and neutrophils. Wilson disease usually presents in children and young adults with neuropsychiatric signs and low ceruloplasmin. Acetaminophen zone 3 necrosis gives confluent necrosis with very high aminotransferases and minimal inflammation, not steatohepatitis. Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows interface hepatitis with plasma cells and high IgG rather than steatosis with Mallory hyaline. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 16 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: An AST to ALT ratio of 2 or more and abundant Mallory-Denk bodies with neutrophils favor alcohol, while ALT above AST favors MASLD (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Revisit the alcohol history when labs and biopsy point to alcohol despite the patient's denial.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Typical metabolic steatohepatitis usually has ALT above","B":"Wilson disease usually presents in children and","C":"Acetaminophen zone 3 necrosis gives confluent necrosis","D":"Type 1 autoimmune hepatitis shows interface hepatitis"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 50-year-old man has diabetes, slate gray skin, cirrhosis, and painful knees with calcium pyrophosphate crystals. He reports erectile dysfunction. Testosterone is 150 ng/dL (reference range: 300–1,000 ng/dL), LH is 1.0 IU/L (reference range: 1.5–9.3 IU/L), and transferrin saturation is 85% (reference range: 20%–50%). Deposition in which tissue best explains his low testosterone?","choices":{"A":"Testicular Leydig cells","B":"Anterior pituitary","C":"Adrenal zona reticularis","D":"Penile arterial walls","E":"Pancreatic beta cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Diabetes, skin pigmentation, cirrhosis, pseudogout, and very high transferrin saturation point to hereditary hemochromatosis, and its hypogonadism comes from iron deposition in the pituitary, which fits a low testosterone with a low rather than high LH. Testicular Leydig cell damage would cause primary hypogonadism with a high LH. The adrenal zona reticularis makes weak androgens and does not set adult male testosterone levels. Penile arterial disease can cause erectile dysfunction but would not lower testosterone or LH. Pancreatic beta cell iron explains his diabetes, not his low testosterone. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 16 section 2; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HFE hemochromatosis lowers hepcidin, causing iron overload of the liver, pancreas, heart, joints, skin, and pituitary, with a 200-fold HCC risk (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism with diabetes and arthropathy in a middle-aged man should prompt iron studies.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Testicular Leydig cell damage would cause primary","C":"The adrenal zona reticularis makes weak androgens","D":"Penile arterial disease can cause erectile dysfunction","E":"Pancreatic beta cell iron explains his diabetes,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate hereditary unconjugated and conjugated hyperbilirubinemias based on the underlying defect, bilirubin pattern, and characteristic clinical features (Crigler-Najjar, Dubin-Johnson, and Rotor syndromes).","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl has lifelong unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia near 12 mg/dL (reference 0.1–1.0 mg/dL total) and residual UGT1A1 activity under 10 percent of normal. Which drug most likely lowers her bilirubin?","choices":{"A":"Penicillamine","B":"Cholestyramine","C":"Phenobarbital","D":"Phlebotomy","E":"Ursodeoxycholic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Crigler Najjar type II keeps residual UGT1A1 activity, and its jaundice responds to phenobarbital, which induces the residual enzyme. Penicillamine treats Wilson disease, phlebotomy treats hemochromatosis, and Bootcamp lists cholestyramine and ursodeoxycholic acid as PBC treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 16, section 3, Crigler Najjar type II, lines 3267–3274).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes conjugated bilirubin enters the bile canaliculi through MRP2 (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hereditary hyperbilirubinemias). COMLEX: the lecture gives type II a milder course with low kernicterus risk (lecture SDL 16).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the etiologies, clinical features, and complications (choledocholithiasis and ascending cholangitis) of large bile duct obstructions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl has had three episodes of jaundice with right upper quadrant pain over the past year. Examination shows scleral icterus and a palpable right upper quadrant mass. Total bilirubin is 4.6 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL) and direct bilirubin is 3.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.0–0.3 mg/dL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and a large fusiform cystic dilation of the common bile duct. If the lesion is left in place, she is at increased long-term risk of which malignancy?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic angiosarcoma","B":"Hepatocellular adenoma","C":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","D":"Bile duct adenocarcinoma","E":"Pancreatic acinar carcinoma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A fusiform cystic dilation of the common bile duct with recurrent pain, jaundice, and a right upper quadrant mass in a young child is a choledochal cyst, a congenital dilation that predisposes to stones, strictures, pancreatitis, cholangitis, and adenocarcinoma of the biliary tree. Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelium and is linked to vinyl chloride, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign tumor tied to oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, and obesity. Fibrolamellar carcinoma affects young adults without cirrhosis and is driven by a DNAJB1-PRKACA fusion, not biliary stasis. Pancreatic acinar carcinoma is a rare tumor with acinar differentiation that is not linked to bile duct cysts. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18 (choledochal cyst); Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 16 section 4 and SDL 17 sections 3 to 5.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Choledochal cysts are congenital common bile duct dilations, more common in girls, that predispose to stones, strictures, pancreatitis, and biliary adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Childhood jaundice with recurrent pain and a right upper quadrant mass is the classic picture, though about 20% present first in adulthood.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelium and","B":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign tumor tied","C":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma affects young adults without cirrhosis","E":"Pancreatic acinar carcinoma is a rare tumor"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated conditions, morphology (portal inflammation, intralobular bile duct destruction, beading, onion-skin fibrosis), characteristic serum antibodies, and clinical presentations of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has had fatigue and intense itching for 8 years. ALP is 520 U/L (reference range: 40–120 U/L), and antimitochondrial antibody is positive. She now has diffuse bone pain and greasy stools, and 25-hydroxyvitamin D is 9 ng/mL (reference range: 30–100 ng/mL). Which mechanism best explains her bone disease?","choices":{"A":"Renal loss of 1 alpha hydroxylase","B":"Autoantibodies against osteoblasts","C":"Poor bile salt delivery to the gut","D":"Copper deposited in bone matrix","E":"High estrogen from poor clearance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Itching, high ALP, and a positive antimitochondrial antibody in a middle-aged woman indicate primary biliary cholangitis, which destroys small intrahepatic bile ducts. As cholestasis progresses, too little bile salt reaches the gut to form micelles, so fat and fat-soluble vitamin D are malabsorbed, causing steatorrhea and osteomalacia. Renal loss of 1 alpha hydroxylase would lower calcitriol rather than 25-hydroxyvitamin D, and her kidneys are not involved. Autoantibodies against osteoblasts are not part of PBC, whose antimitochondrial antibodies target the PDC-E2 subunit. Copper deposited in bone matrix is not a cause of osteomalacia. High estrogen from poor hepatic clearance causes spider angiomas and palmar erythema, not vitamin D deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 16 section 5 and SDL 14 section 4; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PBC is antimitochondrial antibody-positive, T cell-mediated destruction of small interlobular bile ducts with florid duct lesions, mainly in middle-aged women (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Long-standing PBC brings steatorrhea, osteomalacia, xanthelasmas, and hypercholesterolemia, so fat-soluble vitamins should be checked.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Renal loss of 1 alpha hydroxylase would","B":"Autoantibodies against osteoblasts are not part of","D":"Copper deposited in bone matrix is not","E":"High estrogen from poor hepatic clearance causes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl16_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the genetic mutations, pathogenesis, morphological hallmarks, and clinical features of inherited metabolic liver diseases (hemochromatosis, Wilson disease, and α-antitrypsin deficiency).","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man who has never smoked has basal panacinar emphysema and cirrhosis. His liver biopsy shows periportal hepatocytes containing round globules that stay PAS positive after diastase digestion. He begins weekly intravenous infusions of the purified normal serum protein that he lacks. Which outcome is most expected?","choices":{"A":"Liver globules clear, lungs unchanged","B":"Both lung and liver disease regress","C":"Own protein secreted, liver heals","D":"Liver injury worsens, lungs helped","E":"Lung loss slows, liver unchanged"},"correct":"E","explanation":"PAS-positive, diastase-resistant globules with panacinar emphysema in a nonsmoker point to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. Infused normal AAT restores antielastase activity in the lung, which protects the remaining alveolar walls, but the liver disease is a toxic gain of function from misfolded Z protein retained in the hepatocyte ER, which the infusion cannot remove. Clearance of liver globules would require stopping retention of the misfolded protein, and infused protein does not change how his own hepatocytes fold it. Regression of both lung and liver disease is not expected, because destroyed alveolar walls do not regrow and the liver injury is not caused by a lack of circulating protein. Secretion of his own protein cannot be restored, because the mutation that causes misfolding remains in his hepatocytes. Worsening of liver injury from the added protein is not expected, since the infused protein circulates in plasma and is not the retained misfolded form. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 16 section 2; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: AAT deficiency, from codominant SERPINA1 alleles such as PiZ, causes emphysema by loss of function and liver disease by ER retention of misfolded protein (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Panacinar basal emphysema in a young nonsmoker, or neonatal cholestatic hepatitis, should prompt AAT testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 4 — hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Clearance of liver globules would require stopping","B":"Regression of both lung and liver disease","C":"Secretion of his own protein cannot be","D":"Worsening of liver injury from the added"}}]},{"sdlNumber":17,"title":"SDL 17 — Pathology - Circulatory, Pregnancy-Related, and Neoplastic Liver Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"Budd Chiari syndrome is caused by thrombosis or obstruction of which veins?","choices":{"A":"One minor hepatic vein","B":"A single left hepatic vein","C":"The main portal vein","D":"Two or more major veins","E":"Scattered sublobular veins"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome is hepatic venous outflow obstruction from thrombosis or obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins. Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic problem, and a single small vein does not produce the syndrome. Scattered sublobular veins and a single small vein do not produce the syndrome. Robbins notes that thrombosis of a single main hepatic vein is clinically silent; Budd-Chiari requires obstruction of the major hepatic veins. Robbins treats portal vein obstruction as a separate prehepatic entity that causes abdominal pain and portal hypertension with varices; Budd-Chiari is obstruction of the major hepatic veins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome causes congestive liver disease with hepatomegaly, ascites, varices, and abdominal pain but no JVD (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists Budd Chiari syndrome and cardiac causes as posthepatic causes of portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal vein thrombosis is a prehepatic problem, and a single small vein does not produce the syndrome.","E":"Scattered sublobular veins and a single small vein do not produce the syndrome.","B":"Robbins notes that thrombosis of a single main hepatic vein is clinically silent; Budd-Chiari requires obstruction of the major hepatic veins.","C":"Robbins treats portal vein obstruction as a separate prehepatic entity that causes abdominal pain and portal hypertension with varices; Budd-Chiari is obstruction of the major hepatic veins."}},{"id":"sdl17_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"Which disorder is a primary cause of hepatic vein thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Hemophilia A","B":"Polycythemia vera","C":"Von Willebrand disease","D":"Immune thrombocytopenia","E":"Vitamin K deficiency"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Primary causes of Budd Chiari syndrome include myeloproliferative neoplasms such as polycythemia vera, inherited thrombophilias, antiphospholipid syndrome, PNH, HCC invading hepatic veins, pregnancy, and oral contraceptives. The other choices are bleeding disorders, not thrombotic ones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari syndrome, lines 3376–3383).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links Budd Chiari syndrome to hypercoagulable states, the postpartum state, and HCC (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: the lecture notes a swollen red purple liver with a tense capsule in Budd Chiari syndrome (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hemophilia A is a bleeding disorder.","C":"Von Willebrand disease is a bleeding disorder.","D":"Immune thrombocytopenia is a bleeding disorder.","E":"Vitamin K deficiency is a bleeding disorder."}},{"id":"sdl17_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the pericellular centrilobular fibrosis of long standing right heart failure called?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac sclerosis","B":"Bridging necrosis","C":"Hepatoportal sclerosis","D":"Chicken wire fibrosis","E":"Onion skin fibrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long standing right heart failure leads to pericellular centrilobular fibrosis termed cardiac sclerosis. Bridging necrosis links lobules in severe hepatitis, hepatoportal sclerosis is a portal venous lesion, chicken wire fibrosis is the perisinusoidal pattern of steatohepatitis, and onion skin fibrosis surrounds ducts in PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3400).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists congestive hepatopathy and nutmeg liver with centrilobular necrosis as posthepatic findings (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists nonselective beta blockers such as carvedilol, nadolol, and propranolol to prevent variceal bleeding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 12 → Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and which other feature?","choices":{"A":"Jaundice","B":"Seizures","C":"Hypoglycemia","D":"Hematuria","E":"Hypothermia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Preeclampsia is maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects, and eclampsia is preeclampsia plus hyperreflexia and seizures. Hypoglycemia is a feature of acute fatty liver of pregnancy. Mild jaundice can occur in preeclampsia but does not define eclampsia, and hematuria and hypothermia are not listed features. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia and eclampsia, lines 3405–3411).","boardPrep":"Step 1: preeclampsia can present as HELLP syndrome, with hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: the lecture explains preeclamptic liver injury by endothelial dysfunction, microvascular thrombosis, and vasospasm (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the characteristic histology of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Macrovesicular fat with ballooning","C":"Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis","D":"Portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate","E":"Diffuse microvesicular steatosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"AFLP shows diffuse microvesicular steatosis, with hepatocytes packed with tiny fat droplets around a central nucleus that is not displaced. Periportal fibrin deposits fit preeclampsia, and ballooning with macrovesicular fat fits steatohepatitis. Centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis is the ischemic or congestive zone 3 pattern, and a portal lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate fits idiosyncratic drug induced hepatitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3428–3431).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture gives AFLP an incidence of about 1 in 16,000 pregnancies (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe AFLP shows lobular disarray, hepatocyte dropout, and cholestasis (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which fetal enzyme defect underlies a subset of acute fatty liver of pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"LCHAD deficiency","B":"MCAD deficiency","C":"G6PD deficiency","D":"HGPRT deficiency","E":"CPT2 deficiency"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In a subset of AFLP cases, homozygous fetal long chain 3 hydroxyacyl CoA dehydrogenase deficiency lets toxic fatty acid intermediates cross into the mother and overwhelm maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. The other enzyme defects are not the described cause. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3427).","boardPrep":"Step 1: AFLP requires urgent termination of pregnancy (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP presents late in pregnancy with bleeding, nausea, vomiting, jaundice, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"MCAD deficiency is a different enzyme.","C":"G6PD deficiency is a different enzyme.","D":"HGPRT deficiency is a different enzyme.","E":"CPT2 deficiency is a different enzyme."}},{"id":"sdl17_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunostaining pattern is pathognomonic of focal nodular hyperplasia?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear beta catenin","B":"Loss of LFABP staining","C":"Map like GS staining","D":"CRP and SAA staining","E":"Membranous CD31 staining"},"correct":"C","explanation":"FNH shows a pathognomonic map like pattern of glutamine synthetase, or GS, staining. Nuclear beta catenin, LFABP loss, and CRP or SAA overexpression mark hepatocellular adenoma subtypes, and CD31 marks endothelium. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3444–3450).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls FNH the second most common benign liver tumor, mostly in women aged 35 to 50 years (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes FNH becomes isodense to the surrounding liver in the portal venous phase after arterial hyperenhancement (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"Which exposure is most strongly associated with hepatocellular adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Vinyl chloride exposure","B":"Chronic hepatitis B","C":"Aflatoxin ingestion","D":"Liver fluke infection","E":"Oral contraceptive use"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is a benign epithelial neoplasm strongly associated with oral contraceptives, anabolic steroids, and metabolic syndrome or obesity. Vinyl chloride fits angiosarcoma, HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC, and liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma. Angiosarcoma, a malignant vascular liver tumor, is the lesion historically tied to vinyl chloride, arsenic, or thorotrast exposure, and it has become rare as those exposures fell. Aflatoxin, an Aspergillus mycotoxin that contaminates crops in Africa and Asia, acts synergistically with alcohol and hepatitis B to raise the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3456).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes a hepatic adenoma may regress spontaneously or rupture with abdominal pain and shock (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp ties adenoma rupture to a rich arterial supply and lack of stroma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Vinyl chloride fits angiosarcoma, HBV and aflatoxin fit HCC, and liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma.","D":"A malignant vascular liver tumor is the lesion historically tied to vinyl chloride, arsenic, or thorotrast exposure, and it has become rare as those exposures fell.","A":"Angiosarcoma, a malignant vascular liver tumor, is the lesion historically tied to vinyl chloride, arsenic, or thorotrast exposure, and it has become rare as those exposures fell.","C":"Aflatoxin, an Aspergillus mycotoxin that contaminates crops in Africa and Asia, acts synergistically with alcohol and hepatitis B to raise the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl17_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":1,"stem":"Which hepatocellular adenoma subtype carries a high risk of transformation to HCC?","choices":{"A":"HNF1A inactivated","B":"Inflammatory subtype","C":"Sonic hedgehog subtype","D":"Beta catenin activated","E":"Fibrolamellar subtype"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta catenin activated adenomas carry activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations, are common in males and anabolic steroid users, and have a high risk of HCC. HNF1A inactivated adenomas have minimal risk, inflammatory adenomas mild risk, and sonic hedgehog adenomas a high bleeding risk. Fibrolamellar is an HCC variant. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgical resection is indicated for adenomas in males, beta catenin mutants, or tumors 5 cm or larger (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: sonic hedgehog adenomas carry GLI1 fusions and a high risk of intraperitoneal hemorrhage (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which precursor lesions give rise to HCC in cirrhotic livers?","choices":{"A":"Mallory bodies","B":"Dysplastic nodules","C":"Councilman bodies","D":"Hyperplastic polyps","E":"Florid duct lesions"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HCC arises from precursor lesions such as small cell change, large cell change, and dysplastic nodules in cirrhotic livers. Mallory and Councilman bodies are injury findings, florid duct lesions fit PBC, and hyperplastic polyps are colonic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology and precursor lesions, lines 3482–3485).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid calls HCC the most common primary malignant liver tumor in adults and notes hematogenous spread (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists tumor seeding during liver biopsy as an HCC complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene fusion drives fibrolamellar HCC?","choices":{"A":"TMPRSS2 ERG","B":"KIAA1549 BRAF","C":"DNAJB1 PRKACA","D":"ETV6 NTRK3","E":"RUNX1 RUNX1T1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The fibrolamellar variant occurs in young adults without cirrhosis, is driven by a DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. The other fusions drive prostate cancer, pilocytic astrocytoma, infantile fibrosarcoma, and acute myeloid leukemia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3489).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCC has a strong propensity for vascular invasion into the portal and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be green from cholestasis or yellow from fat (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TMPRSS2::ERG is a prostate-cancer fusion, not the driver of fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma.","B":"KIAA1549::BRAF drives pilocytic astrocytoma, not fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma.","D":"ETV6::NTRK3 drives infantile fibrosarcoma, not fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma.","E":"RUNX1::RUNX1T1 drives acute myeloid leukemia, not fibrolamellar hepatocellular carcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl17_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which serum tumor marker is raised in advanced HCC?","choices":{"A":"Carcinoembryonic antigen","B":"Cancer antigen 125","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Chromogranin A","E":"Alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Serum alpha fetoprotein is raised in advanced HCC, although it is unhelpful for early screening and negative in fibrolamellar HCC. CEA, CA 125, PSA, and chromogranin A mark other tumors. CA-125 is the serum marker followed for ovarian tumors, not for advanced hepatocellular carcinoma. Chromogranin A is an endocrine granule marker, stained alongside synaptophysin in neuroendocrine tumors, not a hepatocyte product raised in HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3495–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that HCC recurrence is monitored with serum AFP (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss, hepatomegaly, and abdominal pain in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Carcinoembryonic antigen marks other tumors, not advanced hepatocellular carcinoma.","C":"Prostate-specific antigen marks prostate cancer, not advanced hepatocellular carcinoma.","B":"CA-125 is the serum marker followed for ovarian tumors, not for advanced hepatocellular carcinoma.","D":"Chromogranin A is an endocrine granule marker, stained alongside synaptophysin in neuroendocrine tumors, not a hepatocyte product raised in HCC."}},{"id":"sdl17_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which exposure is historically associated with hepatic angiosarcoma?","choices":{"A":"Aflatoxin B1","B":"Liver flukes","C":"Oral contraceptives","D":"Vinyl chloride","E":"Asbestos fibers"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is historically associated with occupational vinyl chloride monomer, arsenic, and Thorotrast. Aflatoxin fits HCC, liver flukes fit cholangiocarcinoma, and oral contraceptives fit adenoma. Asbestos is linked to bronchogenic carcinoma and mesothelioma rather than liver tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3527–3529); First Aid 2025 Pathology (carcinogens: asbestos, lung).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatic angiosarcoma is often metastatic at diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PECAM 1, or CD31, positivity in angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"Which immunostains are positive in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma?","choices":{"A":"CK7 and CK19","B":"HepPar1 and AFP","C":"CD31 and CD34","D":"S100 and HMB45","E":"Desmin and actin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma is positive for mucin and CK7 and CK19, and negative for AFP and HepPar1. CD31 and CD34 mark angiosarcoma, S100 and HMB45 mark melanoma, and desmin and actin mark muscle. HepPar1 and AFP are negative in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, which is positive for mucin, CK7, and CK19. Robbins uses the endothelial marker CD31 to show the endothelial nature of angiosarcoma, not a bile duct adenocarcinoma. HMB-45 is the melanocytic marker Robbins uses to identify melanoma cells, not a marker of biliary epithelium. Smooth muscle actin and desmin mark smooth muscle tumors such as leiomyosarcoma in Robbins, not gland-forming cholangiocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3519–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists raised CA 19 9 and CEA in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid places the most common cholangiocarcinoma at the convergence of the right and left hepatic ducts (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"HepPar1 and AFP are negative in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, which is positive for mucin, CK7, and CK19.","C":"Robbins uses the endothelial marker CD31 to show the endothelial nature of angiosarcoma, not a bile duct adenocarcinoma.","D":"HMB-45 is the melanocytic marker Robbins uses to identify melanoma cells, not a marker of biliary epithelium.","E":"Smooth muscle actin and desmin mark smooth muscle tumors such as leiomyosarcoma in Robbins, not gland-forming cholangiocarcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl17_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":1,"stem":"From which cells does hepatic angiosarcoma arise?","choices":{"A":"Intrahepatic biliary epithelium","B":"Perisinusoidal stellate cells","C":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells","D":"Polygonal hepatocytes","E":"Resident Kupffer macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hepatic angiosarcoma is a rare, aggressive mesenchymal tumor of sinusoidal endothelial cells that forms hemorrhagic red purple masses. Biliary epithelium gives cholangiocarcinoma, and hepatocytes give HCC. Kupffer macrophages are not the cell of origin. Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelial cells, not perisinusoidal stellate cells. Robbins defines intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma as adenocarcinoma arising from the intrahepatic biliary tree. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3526).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiosarcoma cells line and dissect along preexisting sinusoidal spaces (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: angiosarcoma stains for CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hepatic angiosarcoma arises from sinusoidal endothelial cells, not perisinusoidal stellate cells.","A":"Robbins defines intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma as adenocarcinoma arising from the intrahepatic biliary tree."}},{"id":"sdl17_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with antiphospholipid syndrome has abdominal pain, tender hepatomegaly, and new ascites. Doppler shows thrombi in three major hepatic veins. Which liver finding is most likely on biopsy?","choices":{"A":"Periportal fibrin deposits","B":"Portal egg granulomas","C":"Microvesicular steatosis","D":"Periductal onion skin","E":"Pericentral congestion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budd Chiari syndrome causes marked pericentral or centrilobular sinusoidal congestion with hepatocellular necrosis, and chronic cases develop pericentral sinusoidal fibrosis. Periportal fibrin fits preeclampsia, egg granulomas fit schistosomiasis, microvesicular fat fits AFLP, and onion skin fibrosis fits PSC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, Budd Chiari morphology, lines 3384–3388).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes Budd Chiari syndrome can produce a mottled nutmeg liver (First Aid 2025 GI Budd-Chiari syndrome). COMLEX: Bootcamp links posthepatic obstruction to raised sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man with long standing right sided heart failure dies, and his liver is examined at autopsy. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Chocolate brown liver","B":"Diffusely green liver","C":"Nutmeg mottled liver","D":"Diffusely yellow liver","E":"Jigsaw nodular liver"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Passive congestion from right heart failure gives a nutmeg liver, a mottled red tan pattern of centrilobular congestion alternating with lighter periportal tissue. Chocolate brown fits hemochromatosis, green fits cholestasis, yellow fits steatosis, and jigsaw nodules fit biliary cirrhosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3389–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in passive congestion, raised central venous pressure is transmitted back through the IVC and hepatic veins (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: centrilobular sinusoids are dilated and filled with red cells (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with severe cardiac decompensation has centrilobular hepatocyte necrosis on biopsy. Which hepatocytes are most likely to remain intact?","choices":{"A":"Zones 1 and 2","B":"Central zone 3","C":"Zones 2 and 3","D":"Zones 1 and 3","E":"Midzonal zone 2"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In passive congestion, centrilobular zone 3 hepatocytes undergo ischemic atrophy and necrosis while periportal zone 1 and 2 hepatocytes remain intact. Zone 3 is the necrotic zone, so any choice that includes zone 3 is wrong. Midzonal zone 2 does survive, but so does periportal zone 1; Robbins places the hemorrhagic necrosis of hypoperfusion with congestion in the centrilobular region only. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 1, passive congestion, lines 3395–3398).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists paracentesis with a raised serum ascites albumin gradient in portal hypertension (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists TIPS as a portal hypertension treatment (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, portal hypertension).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Zone 3 is the necrotic zone.","C":"Zones 2 and 3 include the centrilobular zone that necroses, so they are not the hepatocytes that remain intact.","D":"Zones 1 and 3 include the centrilobular zone that necroses, so they are not the hepatocytes that remain intact.","E":"Midzonal zone 2 does survive, but so does periportal zone 1; Robbins places the hemorrhagic necrosis of hypoperfusion with congestion in the centrilobular region only."}},{"id":"sdl17_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman at 36 weeks of gestation has blood pressure of 162/110 mm Hg, proteinuria, edema, and right upper quadrant pain. Her AST is 310 U/L (reference 12–38 U/L). Which liver finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse microvesicular fat","B":"Pericentral congestion","C":"Portal plasma cell infiltrate","D":"Periportal fibrin deposits","E":"Bridging fibrous septa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In preeclampsia, periportal sinusoids contain fibrin deposits with hemorrhage into the space of Disse, leading to periportal coagulative necrosis. Microvesicular fat fits AFLP, pericentral congestion fits venous outflow problems, plasma cells fit autoimmune hepatitis, and septa fit chronic fibrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3412–3414).","boardPrep":"Step 1: patients with preeclamptic liver injury have raised transaminases and mild jaundice (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: severe coagulopathy marks advanced disease that needs prompt delivery (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman at 37 weeks of gestation with eclampsia suddenly develops severe right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. Which complication is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic vein thrombosis","B":"Ruptured liver hematoma","C":"Acute cholecystitis","D":"Perforated peptic ulcer","E":"Ruptured ectopic pregnancy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In eclampsia, high pressure bleeding forms intrahepatic hematomas that can dissect beneath Glisson capsule into a subcapsular hematoma with a risk of catastrophic rupture. An ectopic pregnancy is not possible at 37 weeks of an intrauterine pregnancy. Hepatic vein thrombosis produces liver enlargement, pain, and ascites, acute cholecystitis begins with progressive pain lasting more than 6 hours with mild fever, and a perforated ulcer causes peritonitis with free air, so none fits sudden pain with shock in eclampsia. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, preeclampsia morphology, lines 3414–3417); Robbins Ch. 18 (Budd-Chiari syndrome; acute calculous cholecystitis clinical features); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 9, section 2, perforation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lecture defines HELLP as hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: preeclampsia combines maternal hypertension, proteinuria, edema, and coagulation defects (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman at 35 weeks of gestation has nausea, vomiting, jaundice, and confusion. Her glucose is 45 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), and viral serologies are negative. Which mechanism best explains her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Maternal autoimmune hepatitis","B":"Acute hepatitis A infection","C":"Hepatic microvascular thrombosis","D":"Canalicular bile salt retention","E":"Overwhelmed beta oxidation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute fatty liver of pregnancy presents in the third trimester with incipient liver failure, hypoglycemia, and encephalopathy. In a subset, fetal LCHAD deficiency sends toxic fatty acid intermediates into the mother, overwhelming maternal mitochondrial beta oxidation. Microvascular thrombosis is the preeclampsia mechanism. Canalicular bile salt retention is cholestasis of pregnancy, which brings pruritus and a high alkaline phosphatase rather than hypoglycemia and encephalopathy. Autoimmune hepatitis is a different disease. Severe disease can mimic viral hepatitis morphologically, but the lesion is diffuse microvesicular steatosis and viral serologies are negative. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 2, acute fatty liver of pregnancy, lines 3421–3435).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in AFLP, tiny fat droplets surround a central nucleus without displacing it (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: AFLP is a rare, life threatening metabolic emergency of the third trimester (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Autoimmune hepatitis is a different liver disease.","B":"Robbins notes that the morphology of severe acute fatty liver of pregnancy can mimic viral hepatitis, but the diagnosis rests on diffuse microvesicular steatosis, and her viral serologies are negative.","C":"Microvascular thrombosis is the preeclampsia mechanism.","D":"Canalicular cholestasis with pruritus and greatly raised bile salts is intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy, which Robbins says resolves within 2 to 3 weeks of delivery."}},{"id":"sdl17_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has a 4 cm liver mass found incidentally on imaging. It is unencapsulated with a central stellate scar and radiating fibrous septa. Which approach is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Urgent surgical resection","B":"Conservative management","C":"Hepatic artery embolization","D":"Systemic chemotherapy","E":"Liver transplantation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A circumscribed unencapsulated nodule with a central stellate scar is focal nodular hyperplasia, a benign hyperplastic lesion with no risk of malignant transformation or spontaneous rupture, so it is usually managed conservatively. Resection, embolization, chemotherapy, and transplant are reserved for lesions such as adenomas 5 cm or larger or malignancy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, focal nodular hyperplasia, lines 3440–3452).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FNH septa contain thick walled dystrophic arteries with a ductular reaction but no interlobular bile ducts (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: First Aid notes FNH is usually asymptomatic and found incidentally (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman taking oral contraceptives has a liver adenoma. Its biopsy shows marked steatosis and loss of LFABP expression. Which molecular change is most likely?","choices":{"A":"CTNNB1 exon 3 activation","B":"IL6ST activation","C":"HNF1A inactivation","D":"GLI1 gene fusion","E":"DNAJB1 PRKACA fusion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"HNF1A inactivated adenomas show loss of LFABP expression, marked steatosis, and minimal malignant risk. CTNNB1 mutations define beta catenin adenomas, gp130 activation defines inflammatory adenomas, GLI1 fusions define sonic hedgehog adenomas, and DNAJB1 PRKACA drives fibrolamellar HCC. Activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations define the β-catenin-activated adenoma, linked to anabolic steroids and at high risk of malignant transformation, not to steatosis with LFABP loss. GLI1 fusion genes drive the sonic hedgehog adenoma subtype, which is prone to hemorrhage, not the steatotic LFABP-negative subtype. The DNAJB1::PRKACA fusion is the driver of fibrolamellar HCC in adolescents and young adults, not of hepatocellular adenoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, adenoma subtypes, lines 3457–3469).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory adenomas overexpress CRP and SAA and are associated with obesity (lecture SDL 17). COMLEX: adenomas are sheets of normal appearing hepatocytes with arterial supply but no portal tracts or bile ducts (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 1 — risk factors of metabolic dysfunction associated steatotic liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"CTNNB1 mutations define beta catenin adenomas, gp130 activation defines inflammatory adenomas, GLI1 fusions define sonic hedgehog adenomas, and DNAJB1 PRKACA drives fibrolamellar HCC.","A":"Activating CTNNB1 exon 3 mutations define the β-catenin-activated adenoma, linked to anabolic steroids and at high risk of malignant transformation, not to steatosis with LFABP loss.","D":"GLI1 fusion genes drive the sonic hedgehog adenoma subtype, which is prone to hemorrhage, not the steatotic LFABP-negative subtype.","E":"The DNAJB1::PRKACA fusion is the driver of fibrolamellar HCC in adolescents and young adults, not of hepatocellular adenoma."}},{"id":"sdl17_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman who takes oral contraceptives has sudden right upper quadrant pain and hypotension. CT shows hemoperitoneum from a bleeding liver mass. Which lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","C":"Simple hepatic cyst","D":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","E":"Amebic liver abscess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hepatocellular adenoma is linked to oral contraceptives and is prone to spontaneous intraperitoneal hemorrhage or rupture, a surgical emergency. FNH carries no rupture risk, fibrolamellar HCC is malignant without this exposure, a simple cyst does not bleed in this way, and an abscess would bring fever. A hemorrhaging mass in a young woman on oral contraceptives fits hepatocellular adenoma, which is strongly associated with these drugs; a simple cyst is not. Fibrolamellar carcinoma arises in adolescents and young adults without liver disease and shows oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen; it is not linked to oral contraceptives. Robbins notes liver abscesses, including amebic ones, present with fever, right upper quadrant pain, and tender hepatomegaly, not spontaneous bleeding from a mass in an afebrile woman on oral contraceptives. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 3, hepatocellular adenoma, lines 3453–3474).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes hepatocellular adenoma carries a raised risk of malignant transformation (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses). COMLEX: transformation to HCC is most likely with the beta catenin subtype or a size of 5 cm or more (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"FNH carries no rupture risk, fibrolamellar HCC is malignant without this exposure, a simple cyst does not bleed in this way, and an abscess would bring fever.","C":"A hemorrhaging mass in a young woman on oral contraceptives fits hepatocellular adenoma, which is strongly associated with these drugs; a simple cyst is not.","D":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma arises in adolescents and young adults without liver disease and shows oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen; it is not linked to oral contraceptives.","E":"Robbins notes liver abscesses, including amebic ones, present with fever, right upper quadrant pain, and tender hepatomegaly, not spontaneous bleeding from a mass in an afebrile woman on oral contraceptives."}},{"id":"sdl17_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with chronic hepatitis B and cirrhosis has weight loss and a liver mass invading the portal vein. Biopsy shows polygonal cells in thick trabeculae with bile plugs. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","B":"Cavernous hemangioma","C":"Cholangiocarcinoma","D":"Colorectal metastasis","E":"Hepatocellular carcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"HCC cells resemble hepatocytes growing in thickened trabeculae or pseudoacinar structures with bile plugs, and HCC invades the portal and hepatic veins. Cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile, FNH and hemangioma are benign, and metastases, though the most common malignant liver tumors, are rarely solitary and would not fit HBV cirrhosis with bile plugs as well. Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, a small red-blue subcapsular nodule of dilated thin-walled vascular channels, not trabeculae of hepatocytes with bile plugs. Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma usually occurs in noncirrhotic liver as a firm gray-white mass, whereas trabeculae of hepatocyte-like cells with bile plugs are HCC. Robbins calls the liver the most common site of metastases from colon, lung, and breast primaries, but metastases do not form trabeculae of hepatocyte-like cells with bile plugs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, morphology, lines 3478–3490).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes aflatoxin B1 forming DNA adducts that cause a TP53 G to T transversion (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes metastases are the most common malignant liver tumors overall (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile, FNH and hemangioma are benign, and metastases, though the most common malignant liver tumors, are rarely solitary and would not fit HBV cirrhosis with bile plugs as well.","B":"Cavernous hemangioma is the most common benign liver tumor, a small red-blue subcapsular nodule of dilated thin-walled vascular channels, not trabeculae of hepatocytes with bile plugs.","C":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma usually occurs in noncirrhotic liver as a firm gray-white mass, whereas trabeculae of hepatocyte-like cells with bile plugs are HCC.","D":"Robbins calls the liver the most common site of metastases from colon, lung, and breast primaries, but metastases do not form trabeculae of hepatocyte-like cells with bile plugs."}},{"id":"sdl17_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man without liver disease has a large liver mass. His serum tumor markers are unremarkable, and biopsy shows large oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen bundles. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular adenoma","B":"Fibrolamellar carcinoma","C":"Focal nodular hyperplasia","D":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma","E":"Hepatic angiosarcoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fibrolamellar HCC arises in young adults without cirrhosis, is AFP negative, and shows oncocytic cells in dense lamellar collagen. FNH has a central stellate scar rather than lamellar collagen around oncocytic cells, adenoma lacks stroma, cholangiocarcinoma forms glands in desmoplastic stroma, and angiosarcoma is vascular. Hepatocellular adenoma typically occurs in young women and is strongly associated with oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids, not oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen in a young man. Angiosarcoma is a malignant vascular tumor tied to vinyl chloride, arsenic, or thorotrast exposure, not a tumor of oncocytic cells in dense collagen lamellae. Robbins notes intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma usually arises in noncirrhotic liver as a firm gray-white mass, not as large oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, fibrolamellar variant, lines 3487–3497).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes contrast CT or MRI confirms HCC after ultrasound screening (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes HCC on ultrasound as a heterogeneous, hypervascular lesion with irregular borders (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — pathophysiology of hepatic injury, scarring, and repair","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"FNH has a central stellate scar rather than lamellar collagen around oncocytic cells, adenoma lacks stroma, cholangiocarcinoma forms glands in desmoplastic stroma, and angiosarcoma is vascular.","A":"Hepatocellular adenoma typically occurs in young women and is strongly associated with oral contraceptives and anabolic steroids, not oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen in a young man.","E":"Angiosarcoma is a malignant vascular tumor tied to vinyl chloride, arsenic, or thorotrast exposure, not a tumor of oncocytic cells in dense collagen lamellae.","D":"Robbins notes intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma usually arises in noncirrhotic liver as a firm gray-white mass, not as large oncocytic cells in lamellar collagen."}},{"id":"sdl17_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old farmer from rural West Africa without cirrhosis develops HCC. He ate mold contaminated stored peanuts for decades. Which molecular change most likely links this exposure to his tumor?","choices":{"A":"KRAS activating mutation","B":"APC truncating mutation","C":"BRAF V600E mutation","D":"TP53 point mutation","E":"MYCN amplification"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The dietary mycotoxin from Aspergillus on stored crops forms DNA adducts that cause a TP53 inactivating G to T transversion, lowering p53 function. Robbins notes aflatoxin acts synergistically with HBV. KRAS, APC, BRAF, and MYCN changes are not the described route. BRAF mutations are described in bile duct lesions and craniopharyngiomas, not as the aflatoxin signature in HCC. MYCN amplification is a prognostic marker in neuroblastoma, not a feature of aflatoxin-driven HCC. APC loss is the lineage-specific driver of colon carcinoma, whereas aflatoxin leaves a codon 249 TP53 signature mutation in HCC. Robbins describes KRAS mutations as common in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma and extrahepatic biliary adenocarcinoma, not as the aflatoxin route to HCC. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, risk factors, lines 3478–3481); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists HBV with or without cirrhosis, and all other causes of cirrhosis, as HCC associations (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: HCC risk factors include cirrhosis from hemochromatosis and AAT deficiency (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"BRAF mutations are described in bile duct lesions and craniopharyngiomas, not as the aflatoxin signature in HCC.","E":"MYCN amplification is a prognostic marker in neuroblastoma, not a feature of aflatoxin-driven HCC.","B":"APC loss is the lineage-specific driver of colon carcinoma, whereas aflatoxin leaves a codon 249 TP53 signature mutation in HCC.","A":"Robbins describes KRAS mutations as common in intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma and extrahepatic biliary adenocarcinoma, not as the aflatoxin route to HCC."}},{"id":"sdl17_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with primary sclerosing cholangitis and ulcerative colitis has a firm gray white sclerotic liver mass. Which finding best separates it from HCC?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive glands","B":"Bile plugs in tumor","C":"AFP positive cells","D":"HepPar1 positive cells","E":"CD31 positive channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, linked to PSC, forms malignant glands in desmoplastic stroma that are mucin positive and do not make bile. Bile plugs, AFP, and HepPar1 favor HCC, and CD31 marks angiosarcoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, lines 3510–3520).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists the Courvoisier sign in cholangiocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 5 — associated conditions of primary biliary and sclerosing cholangitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl17_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who worked for decades at a PVC plastics plant has an ill defined hemorrhagic red purple liver mass. Which immunostains are most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Glypican 3 and AFP","B":"CK7 and CK19","C":"CD31 and ERG","D":"Chromogranin and CD56","E":"CD117 and DOG1"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vinyl chloride exposure and a hemorrhagic red purple mass point to angiosarcoma, which is positive for the endothelial markers CD31, CD34, ERG, and Factor VIII related antigen. Glypican 3 and AFP fit HCC, CK7 and CK19 fit cholangiocarcinoma, and the others fit neuroendocrine and stromal tumors. Elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein is a frequent finding in advanced hepatocellular carcinoma, the hepatocyte tumor, not in a vascular tumor. First Aid describes hepatic angiosarcoma as a malignant tumor of endothelial origin linked to vinyl chloride exposure, so biliary keratins CK7 and CK19 do not fit. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, hepatic angiosarcoma, lines 3521–3531).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links hepatic angiosarcoma to arsenic and vinyl chloride (First Aid 2025 GI liver tumors). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists thorium dioxide exposure for angiosarcoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, additional liver masses).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 5 — morphological findings of drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Chromogranin and CD56 mark neuroendocrine tumors, not a vinyl-chloride-associated vascular liver mass.","E":"CD117 and DOG1 mark gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not this hemorrhagic hepatic mass.","A":"Elevated serum alpha-fetoprotein is a frequent finding in advanced hepatocellular carcinoma, the hepatocyte tumor, not in a vascular tumor.","B":"First Aid describes hepatic angiosarcoma as a malignant tumor of endothelial origin linked to vinyl chloride exposure, so biliary keratins CK7 and CK19 do not fit."}},{"id":"sdl17_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with chronic hepatitis C has a liver mass. Biopsy shows polygonal tumor cells in trabeculae with bile production. Which stain is most likely positive?","choices":{"A":"Mucin positive","B":"ERG positive","C":"Chromogranin positive","D":"HepPar1 positive","E":"Desmin positive"},"correct":"D","explanation":"HCC is positive for AFP, HepPar1, and glypican 3 and negative for mucin. Mucin fits cholangiocarcinoma, ERG fits angiosarcoma, and chromogranin and desmin fit neuroendocrine and muscle tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, HCC diagnostic markers, lines 3502–3509).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists cellular atypia, pleomorphic hyperchromatic nuclei, and a trabecular pattern in HCC (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: the lecture ties HCC to chronic HBV, HCV, cirrhosis, alcohol, MASLD, and aflatoxin (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the morphology and underlying causes of circulatory disorders of the liver, including hepatic venous outflow obstruction (Budd-Chiari syndrome) and passive congestion (cardiac failure).","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria has 3 weeks of right upper quadrant pain, tender hepatomegaly, and ascites. Jugular venous pressure and an echocardiogram are normal, and she has had no chemotherapy or herbal tea exposure. Liver biopsy shows congestion and hepatocyte necrosis around the central veins. Where is the obstruction most likely?","choices":{"A":"Right heart and pericardium","B":"Major hepatic veins","C":"Small portal venules","D":"Terminal hepatic venules","E":"Extrahepatic portal vein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Centrilobular congestion means outflow is blocked downstream of the sinusoids, and a normal jugular venous pressure and echocardiogram rule out the heart. In a patient with PNH, a thrombotic state, the likely site is thrombosis of two or more major hepatic veins, which is Budd-Chiari syndrome. Right heart and pericardial disease would raise the jugular venous pressure, which is normal in her. Small portal venules are blocked by schistosome eggs, causing presinusoidal portal hypertension without centrilobular congestion. Terminal hepatic venules are obliterated in sinusoidal obstruction syndrome, which follows high-dose chemotherapy or pyrrolizidine alkaloids that she has not had. The extrahepatic portal vein is an inflow vessel, and its thrombosis causes varices without centrilobular congestion. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 17 section 1 and SDL 15 sections 3 and 5; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Budd-Chiari syndrome is obstruction of two or more major hepatic veins, caused by myeloproliferative neoplasms, thrombophilias, PNH, antiphospholipid syndrome, HCC, pregnancy, or oral contraceptives (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Tender hepatomegaly and ascites with a normal jugular venous pressure point to hepatic venous outflow obstruction rather than heart failure.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 2 — zones of the liver acinus and their susceptibility to ischemic and toxic injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Right heart and pericardial disease would raise","C":"Small portal venules are blocked by schistosome","D":"Terminal hepatic venules are obliterated in sinusoidal","E":"The extrahepatic portal vein is an inflow"}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the pathology, clinical presentation, and morphology of preeclampsia, eclampsia, and acute fatty liver of pregnancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman at 34 weeks of gestation has headache, right upper quadrant pain, blood pressure of 168/112 mm Hg, and 3+ proteinuria. AST is 420 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L), platelets are 68,000/µL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/µL), a blood smear shows schistocytes, and INR is 2.1 (reference range: 0.8–1.2). Which step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Prompt delivery of the fetus","B":"Plasma exchange therapy","C":"High dose oral corticosteroids","D":"Urgent liver transplantation","E":"Continuous heparin infusion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypertension, proteinuria, hemolysis with schistocytes, raised aminotransferases, and low platelets in the third trimester are preeclampsia with HELLP syndrome, and a severe coagulopathy marks advanced disease that calls for prompt delivery. Plasma exchange therapy is used for thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, not for a disorder of pregnancy treated by ending the pregnancy. High dose oral corticosteroids do not treat the endothelial dysfunction driving her liver injury. Urgent liver transplantation is not the indicated step, since severe hepatic involvement in preeclampsia is managed by delivery. Continuous heparin infusion would add bleeding risk to her low platelets and raised INR and does not treat the disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 17 section 2; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Preeclampsia injures the liver through endothelial dysfunction with periportal fibrin deposits and hemorrhage, and HELLP is hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Severe coagulopathy or HELLP in pregnancy is an obstetric emergency managed by delivery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 1 — clinical features of acute liver failure","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Plasma exchange therapy is used for thrombotic","C":"High dose oral corticosteroids do not treat","D":"Urgent liver transplantation is not the indicated","E":"Continuous heparin infusion would add bleeding risk"}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate the complications, risk factors, underlying causes, and morphology of nonneoplastic mass lesions (focal nodular hyperplasia) and benign neoplasms of the liver (hepatocellular adenoma).","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man who has used anabolic steroids for bodybuilding for years has a 3.5 cm liver mass found on imaging for abdominal discomfort. Biopsy shows sheets of benign-appearing hepatocytes with no portal tracts or bile ducts, and the tumor cells show nuclear beta-catenin staining. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Stop steroids and repeat imaging","B":"Reassurance since it is benign","C":"Listing for liver transplant","D":"Surgical resection of the mass","E":"Systemic chemotherapy for HCC"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Benign hepatocyte sheets lacking portal tracts and bile ducts are a hepatocellular adenoma, and nuclear beta-catenin in a man using anabolic steroids marks the beta-catenin activated subtype with a high risk of transformation to HCC. Resection is indicated for men, beta-catenin mutants, or tumors 5 cm or larger. Stopping steroids and repeating imaging is too passive for a beta-catenin activated adenoma in a man. Reassurance because the tumor is benign ignores the high malignant risk of this subtype. Listing for liver transplant is not needed for a solitary resectable benign tumor. Systemic chemotherapy treats advanced malignancy, while the biopsy shows a benign neoplasm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 17 section 3; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hepatocellular adenoma subtypes are HNF1A-inactivated, inflammatory, beta-catenin activated with high HCC risk, and sonic hedgehog with high bleeding risk (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Resect adenomas in men, in beta-catenin mutants, or when they reach 5 cm.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 12 Obj 1 — organization of the classic hepatic lobule and hepatic blood flow","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stopping steroids and repeating imaging is too","B":"Reassurance because the tumor is benign ignores","C":"Listing for liver transplant is not needed","E":"Systemic chemotherapy treats advanced malignancy, while the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old man with cirrhosis has a liver mass that enhances brightly in the early contrast phase and washes out in the delayed phase on triphasic CT. What explains this enhancement pattern?","choices":{"A":"Portal venous supply","B":"Venous outflow block","C":"Arterial tumor supply","D":"Intratumoral fat content","E":"Central fibrous scar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Arterial phase hyperenhancement with venous or delayed washout is the hallmark of HCC and reflects a predominant arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. The tumor is fed by arteries rather than the portal vein, so contrast arrives early and leaves early, and a central scar fits FNH. Intratumoral fat does not explain early arterial enhancement with washout. Venous outflow block does not explain early arterial hyperenhancement; HCC enhances brightly because of arterial blood supply from tumor neovascularization. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 4, characteristic image finding, lines 3491–3494).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes multiphase imaging with early arterial hyperenhancement and rapid washout in portal and delayed phases (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, hepatocellular carcinoma). COMLEX: HCC grossly may be a unifocal mass, multifocal nodules, or a diffusely infiltrative tumor (lecture SDL 17).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 2 — clinical outcomes of the major hepatotropic viruses","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Venous outflow block does not explain early arterial hyperenhancement."}},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate hepatocellular carcinoma from other hepatic malignancies (cholangiocarcinoma, hepatic angiosarcoma) by morphology, exposure associations, and associated conditions.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with recurrent intrahepatic duct stones has weight loss and dull right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a solitary firm mass in the left hepatic lobe with dilated ducts upstream of it. Needle biopsy shows malignant glands embedded in abundant desmoplastic stroma. Which additional histologic finding is most likely in this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Bile production by tumor cells","B":"Prominent perineural invasion","C":"Tumor cells dissecting sinusoids","D":"Polygonal cells in trabeculae","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hepatolithiasis is a risk factor for intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, a sclerotic adenocarcinoma of biliary epithelium with malignant glands in desmoplastic stroma and prominent perineural invasion. Bile production by tumor cells is a feature of hepatocellular carcinoma; cholangiocarcinoma does not produce bile. Malignant endothelial cells dissecting along sinusoids are the pattern of hepatic angiosarcoma. Polygonal cells in trabeculae are the growth pattern of hepatocellular carcinoma. Ovarian-type stroma defines mucinous cystic neoplasms, which are cystic rather than this infiltrating adenocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 17, section 5, intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, HCC, and angiosarcoma morphology, lines 3501–3528); First Aid 2025 GI (cholangiocarcinoma histology); Robbins Ch. 18 (mucinous cystic neoplasms as less common precursors, lines 4511–4512).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes cholangiocarcinoma histology as infiltrating neoplastic glands with desmoplastic stroma (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma). COMLEX: First Aid notes that cholangiocarcinoma usually presents late with fatigue, weight loss, abdominal pain, and jaundice (First Aid 2025 GI, cholangiocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl17_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Identify the risk factors, morphology, characteristic image finding, and tumor markers associated with hepatocellular carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from East Asia who acquired hepatitis B at birth develops a liver mass. Biopsy of the mass shows hepatocellular carcinoma, and the surrounding liver shows chronic hepatitis. Which viral product is most directly linked to his tumor?","choices":{"A":"Large HBsAg envelope protein","B":"HBeAg from the precore gene","C":"HBx transcriptional activator","D":"Hepatitis delta antigen","E":"NS5B RNA dependent polymerase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Chronic HBV raises the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma, and the HBV product linked to HCC is HBx, a viral transcriptional activator. The large HBsAg envelope protein binds the NTCP receptor for entry and marks infection, but it is not the oncogenic product. HBeAg from the precore transcript marks active replication and high infectivity. Hepatitis delta antigen belongs to HDV, which is not part of his history. NS5B RNA-dependent polymerase is an HCV protein, not part of HBV. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 15 section 2 and SDL 17 section 4; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HBV is a partially double-stranded DNA hepadnavirus whose HBx transcriptional activator is linked to HCC (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Perinatal HBV becomes chronic in about 90% of infants, which is why birth-dose vaccination matters.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 2 — inherited metabolic liver diseases and their clinical features","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The large HBsAg envelope protein binds the","B":"HBeAg from the precore transcript marks active","D":"Hepatitis delta antigen belongs to HDV, which","E":"NS5B RNA-dependent polymerase is an HCV protein,"}}]},{"sdlNumber":18,"title":"SDL 18 — Pathology of the Gallbladder","questions":[{"id":"sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"Which genetic variants explain a cholesterol gallstone prevalence above 75 percent in Pima, Hopi, and Navajo groups?","choices":{"A":"ABCG8 variants","B":"HFE variants","C":"PKD1 variants","D":"CFTR variants","E":"ATP7B variants"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Native American groups such as the Pima, Hopi, and Navajo exceed 75 percent prevalence of cholesterol gallstones because of ABCG8 transporter variants. HFE and ATP7B variants cause hemochromatosis and Wilson disease (lecture SDL 16), CFTR variants cause cystic fibrosis (lecture SDL 19), and PKD1 variants cause polycystic kidney disease (Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt lines 4457–4462). Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, ethnicity and genetics, lines 3544–3547).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists the 8 Fs of gallstones, including fair, feeds (TPN), fasting, and fibrates (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes cholesterol stones are the majority of gallstones and are generally radiolucent (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"What is cholesterolosis, with foam cells filling the lamina propria, also called?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Strawberry gallbladder","C":"Hydrops of gallbladder","D":"Courvoisier gallbladder","E":"Phrygian cap gallbladder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholesterol stones are associated with cholesterolosis, or strawberry gallbladder, in which the lamina propria contains abundant cholesterol laden foam cells. Porcelain gallbladder is wall calcification, and hydrops is a dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid. Courvoisier sign is a palpably enlarged, nontender gallbladder with painless jaundice, not cholesterolosis. A phrygian cap is a congenital fold of the gallbladder fundus, not cholesterol-laden foam cells in the lamina propria. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic calcification of the gallbladder wall in chronic cholecystitis and carries an increased incidence of gallbladder carcinoma. Hydrops of the gallbladder is an atrophic, chronically obstructed, often dilated gallbladder that contains only clear secretions, with no foam cells in the lamina propria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstone morphology, lines 3551–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pure cholesterol stones are pale yellow with a radiating crystalline palisade on cross section (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: fibrate therapy and multiparity are conditions associated with cholesterol stones (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and conjugation and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Courvoisier sign is a palpably enlarged, nontender gallbladder with painless jaundice, not cholesterolosis.","E":"A phrygian cap is a congenital fold of the gallbladder fundus, not cholesterol-laden foam cells in the lamina propria.","A":"Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic calcification of the gallbladder wall in chronic cholecystitis and carries an increased incidence of gallbladder carcinoma.","C":"Hydrops of the gallbladder is an atrophic, chronically obstructed, often dilated gallbladder that contains only clear secretions, with no foam cells in the lamina propria."}},{"id":"sdl18_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do brown pigment gallstones form?","choices":{"A":"Sterile gallbladder bile","B":"Main pancreatic duct lumen","C":"Hepatocyte bile canaliculi","D":"Gallbladder mucosal crypts","E":"Infected large bile ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and are soft, laminated, and greasy. Black pigment stones form in sterile gallbladder bile. The main pancreatic duct, hepatocyte canaliculi, and mucosal crypts are not where they form, because brown stones need infection of the large bile ducts. Black pigment stones are the ones found in sterile gallbladder bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, black versus brown stones, lines 3567–3577).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp links brown pigment stones to Clonorchis sinensis and E coli infection of the biliary system (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: First Aid lists black pigment stones as radiopaque and brown pigment stones as radiolucent (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The main pancreatic duct is not where they form.","C":"Hepatocyte canaliculi is not where they form.","D":"Mucosal crypts are not where they form.","A":"Black pigment stones are the ones found in sterile gallbladder bile, whereas brown."}},{"id":"sdl18_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"What keeps cholesterol in colloidal solution in bile?","choices":{"A":"Albumin and globulins","B":"Mucin and calcium","C":"Bilirubin and mucin","D":"Bile salts and lecithin","E":"Bicarbonate and chloride"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Water insoluble cholesterol must be incorporated into mixed micelles with bile salts and phospholipids such as lecithin to stay in colloidal solution. Mucin and calcium instead help stones grow. Albumin and globulins and the electrolytes bicarbonate and chloride cannot form micelles. Robbins credits mucus hypersecretion with trapping nucleated crystals so stones can grow. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, solubilization requirement, lines 3583–3585).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists high biliary cholesterol, low bile acids or lecithin, and biliary stasis as general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: supersaturation occurs when hepatocytes hypersecrete cholesterol or bile salt and lecithin secretion drops (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albumin and globulins do not form the micelles that keep cholesterol dissolved in bile.","C":"Mucin and calcium instead help stones grow.","E":"Bicarbonate and chloride do not form the micelles that keep cholesterol dissolved in bile.","B":"Robbins credits mucus hypersecretion with trapping nucleated crystals so stones can grow."}},{"id":"sdl18_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"How do estrogens, as in pregnancy or oral contraceptive use, promote cholesterol gallstone formation?","choices":{"A":"They block gallbladder mucin secretion","B":"They raise hepatic bile salt synthesis","C":"They increase biliary lecithin output","D":"They boost bilirubin deconjugation","E":"They raise liver cholesterol synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Estrogens increase hepatic lipoprotein receptor expression and stimulate HMG-CoA reductase, so the liver takes up and makes more cholesterol and secretes the excess into bile, which then becomes supersaturated. Blocking gallbladder mucin secretion would protect against stones, because mucus hypersecretion is what traps nucleated crystals. Raising hepatic bile salt synthesis would improve micellar solubilization of cholesterol and oppose stone formation. Increasing biliary lecithin output would also keep more cholesterol in micelles rather than promoting crystals. Boosting bilirubin deconjugation drives pigment stones, not the cholesterol supersaturation behind estrogen-related stones. Sources: Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, risk factors and pathogenesis of cholesterol stones; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Estrogen raises hepatic cholesterol uptake and HMG-CoA reductase activity, causing excess biliary cholesterol secretion (Robbins ch 18). COMLEX: Ask about pregnancy and oral contraceptive use when a woman presents with biliary colic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking gallbladder mucin secretion would protect against","B":"Raising hepatic bile salt synthesis would improve","C":"Increasing biliary lecithin output would also keep","D":"Boosting bilirubin deconjugation drives pigment stones, not"}},{"id":"sdl18_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":1,"stem":"Black pigment gallstones, as in chronic hemolysis, are made mainly of which material?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol monohydrate crystals","B":"Calcium soaps of palmitate","C":"Calcium salts of free bilirubin","D":"Calcium oxalate crystals","E":"Soluble bilirubin glucuronides"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Black stones contain oxidized polymers of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin, which forms when excess conjugated bilirubin in bile is partly deconjugated and precipitates with calcium. Cholesterol monohydrate crystals are the main component of cholesterol stones, appearing only in small amounts in black stones. Calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate characterize brown stones formed in infected bile ducts. Calcium oxalate crystals are a kidney stone component and are not described as a gallstone constituent. Soluble bilirubin glucuronides stay dissolved in bile; it is the deconjugated, insoluble form that precipitates. Sources: Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, pathogenesis and morphology of pigment stones.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Black pigment stones are calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin and form in sterile gallbladder bile in chronic hemolysis (Robbins ch 18). COMLEX: Think of black stones in a young patient with sickle cell disease or hereditary spherocytosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholesterol monohydrate crystals are the main component","B":"Calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate characterize","D":"Calcium oxalate crystals are a kidney stone","E":"Soluble bilirubin glucuronides stay dissolved in bile;"}},{"id":"sdl18_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"What initiates most cases of acute calculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Primary infection of stagnant bile","B":"Ischemia of the cystic artery","C":"A stone impacted in the ileum","D":"A stone blocking the cystic duct","E":"A stone in the common bile duct"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute calculous cholecystitis is precipitated by a stone obstructing the gallbladder neck or cystic duct; trapped bile then injures the mucosa chemically through lysolecithin and bile salts. Primary infection of stagnant bile is not the trigger; bacterial infection is superimposed later in the course. Ischemia of the cystic artery is the proposed mechanism of acute acalculous cholecystitis in severely ill patients. A stone impacted in the ileum causes gallstone ileus, a bowel obstruction after a fistula forms. A stone in the common bile duct obstructs the biliary tree and causes jaundice or cholangitis, not gallbladder-neck obstruction. Sources: Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, acute cholecystitis pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute calculous cholecystitis begins with stone obstruction of the gallbladder neck or cystic duct, followed by chemical injury (Robbins ch 18). COMLEX: Right upper quadrant pain, fever, and a positive Murphy sign after a fatty meal suggest it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Primary infection of stagnant bile is not","B":"Ischemia of the cystic artery is the","C":"A stone impacted in the ileum causes","E":"A stone in the common bile duct"}},{"id":"sdl18_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Into which toxic product do mucosal phospholipases convert luminal lecithin in acute calculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Sphingomyelin","B":"Lysolecithin","C":"Phosphatidylserine","D":"Cholesteryl ester","E":"Ceramide"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mucosal phospholipases hydrolyze luminal lecithin into toxic lysolecithin, which breaks down the protective mucus and exposes the epithelium to bile salt detergent action. Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylserine, cholesteryl esters, and ceramide are not what phospholipase hydrolysis of lecithin produces. Robbins describes mucosal phospholipases hydrolyzing luminal lecithins to toxic lysolecithins, not to sphingomyelin. Phospholipase action on lecithin yields lysolecithin, not cholesteryl ester; cholesterol in bile is kept in solution by bile salts and lecithin. Ceramide is not a product of lecithin hydrolysis; Robbins names lysolecithin as the toxic product that disrupts the protective mucus layer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous cholecystitis, lines 3621–3623).","boardPrep":"Step 1: distention compresses mucosal blood flow and causes ischemia with prostaglandin mediated inflammation (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: E coli and Enterococcus may superinfect the inflamed gallbladder (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sphingomyelin, phosphatidylserine, cholesteryl esters, and ceramide are not what phospholipase hydrolysis of lecithin produces.","A":"Robbins describes mucosal phospholipases hydrolyzing luminal lecithins to toxic lysolecithins, not to sphingomyelin.","D":"Phospholipase action on lecithin yields lysolecithin, not cholesteryl ester; cholesterol in bile is kept in solution by bile salts and lecithin.","E":"Ceramide is not a product of lecithin hydrolysis."}},{"id":"sdl18_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary driver of acute acalculous cholecystitis?","choices":{"A":"Impacted gallstone","B":"Clonorchis infestation","C":"Cystic artery ischemia","D":"Sphincter of Oddi spasm","E":"Biliary atresia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis is driven mainly by ischemia of the cystic artery, an end artery without collaterals, compounded by stasis, viscous bile, and sludge. An impacted stone defines calculous disease. Clonorchis is a risk for pigment stones and cholangiocarcinoma, sphincter of Oddi spasm is not a listed driver, and biliary atresia is an infant duct disease rather than a complication of critical illness. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3637–3642).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes acalculous cholecystitis is seen in critically ill patients and can follow CMV infection (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp attributes acalculous cholecystitis to reduced perfusion, biliary stasis, and infection (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a cholecystectomy for long-standing gallstones. The resected wall shows dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa, with very little epithelium left. Which variant is this?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain gallbladder","B":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","C":"Hydrops of the gallbladder","D":"Cholesterolosis of the mucosa","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis is a variant of chronic cholecystitis with dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall and mucosa and minimal remaining epithelium; it carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic calcification of the wall. Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis is a shrunken, nodular wall full of foamy macrophages from ruptured Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Hydrops is a dilated gallbladder filled with clear mucoid fluid. Cholesterolosis is lipid-laden macrophages in the lamina propria, the strawberry gallbladder. Sources: course SDL 18 reading (Robbins, Liver and gallbladder, chronic cholecystitis variants); Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, morphology and variants, p103).","boardPrep":"Step 1: porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, and long-standing gallstones raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gallstones are present in more than 90 percent of chronic cholecystitis (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 5 — risk factors for gallbladder carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"What defines porcelain gallbladder?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic calcification","B":"Foamy macrophage infiltrate","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Clear mucoid distention","E":"Transmural wall necrosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Porcelain gallbladder is a rare form of chronic cholecystitis with extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall. Foamy macrophages fit xanthogranulomatous disease, hyaline fibrosis fits hyalinizing cholecystitis, and mucoid distention is hydrops. Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall, not necrosis. Foamy lipid-laden macrophages (xanthoma cells) define xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, triggered by rupture of Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Complete replacement of the wall and mucosa by dense fibrosis is hyalinizing cholecystitis, which may occur with or without calcification. An atrophic, chronically obstructed gallbladder containing only clear secretions is hydrops of the gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, porcelain gallbladder, lines 3667–3668).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes porcelain gallbladder is usually found incidentally on imaging (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder). COMLEX: First Aid generally recommends prophylactic cholecystectomy because of cancer risk (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Porcelain gallbladder is extensive dystrophic intramural calcification of the wall, not necrosis.","B":"Foamy lipid-laden macrophages (xanthoma cells) define xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, triggered by rupture of Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses.","C":"Complete replacement of the wall and mucosa by dense fibrosis is hyalinizing cholecystitis, which may occur with or without calcification.","D":"An atrophic, chronically obstructed gallbladder containing only clear secretions is hydrops of the gallbladder."}},{"id":"sdl18_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does gallstone ileus usually obstruct the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Ligament of Treitz","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Ileocecal valve","E":"Rectosigmoid junction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A large stone eroding through a cholecystenteric fistula can cause gallstone ileus, with obstruction at the ileocecal valve. First Aid adds that the ileocecal valve is the narrowest point, so the stone lodges there rather than at the ligament of Treitz or in the colon. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3684–3686); First Aid 2025 GI (gallstone ileus: ileocecal valve, narrowest point).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Rigler triad of pneumobilia, small bowel obstruction, and an ectopic gallstone (First Aid 2025 GI gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists large gallstones, acute cholecystitis, and malignancy as fistula risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ligament of Treitz is a different point along the bowel.","B":"The sigmoid colon is a different point along the bowel.","C":"The splenic flexure is a different point along the bowel.","E":"The rectosigmoid junction is a different point along the bowel."}},{"id":"sdl18_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which process is thought to link long-standing gallstones to gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Inherited APC mutation","B":"Hepatitis B integration","C":"Chronic hemolysis","D":"Bile salt deficiency","E":"Chronic inflammation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins names chronic inflammation as the common thread tying gallstones and chronic biliary infections to gallbladder cancer, and porcelain gallbladder from chronic cholecystitis also raises the risk. An inherited APC mutation underlies familial adenomatous polyposis and colorectal cancer, not stone-related gallbladder cancer. Hepatitis B integration is linked to hepatocellular carcinoma rather than to gallbladder carcinoma. Chronic hemolysis causes black pigment stones but is not the process that turns gallbladder epithelium malignant. Bile salt deficiency promotes cholesterol supersaturation and stone formation, not malignant transformation. Sources: Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, gallbladder carcinoma pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gallstones and chronic biliary infection lead to gallbladder carcinoma through chronic inflammation (Robbins ch 18). COMLEX: A porcelain gallbladder on imaging warrants discussion of cholecystectomy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An inherited APC mutation underlies familial adenomatous","B":"Hepatitis B integration is linked to hepatocellular","C":"Chronic hemolysis causes black pigment stones but","D":"Bile salt deficiency promotes cholesterol supersaturation and"}},{"id":"sdl18_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which lesion is a precursor of gallbladder carcinoma?","choices":{"A":"Hyperplastic polyp","B":"Flat dysplasia","C":"Adenomyomatosis","D":"Cholesterol polyp","E":"Rokitansky sinus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma progresses through flat dysplasia or in situ lesions, intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms, and intestinal metaplasia. Hyperplastic and cholesterol polyps, adenomyomatosis, and Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are not on the lecture's precursor list; Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are the mucosal outpouchings of chronic cholecystitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, precursor lesions, lines 3704–3705).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mucosal irritation drives persistent epithelial injury and regeneration in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: gain of function mutations in EGF receptor family members, including HER2, occur in gallbladder carcinoma (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"The early symptoms of gallbladder carcinoma most often resemble those of which condition?","choices":{"A":"Symptomatic gallstones","B":"Acute viral hepatitis","C":"Peptic ulcer perforation","D":"Acute pancreatitis","E":"Small bowel obstruction"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Gallbladder carcinoma presents insidiously with abdominal pain, jaundice, anorexia, and nausea that are typically indistinguishable from cholelithiasis, so it is rarely diagnosed before surgery and is usually advanced when found. Acute viral hepatitis causes a prodrome and marked aminotransferase elevation, which is not the usual picture of this tumor. Peptic ulcer perforation produces sudden severe pain and peritonitis rather than an insidious course. Acute pancreatitis presents abruptly with epigastric pain radiating to the back and high lipase. Small bowel obstruction causes distention, vomiting, and constipation, not the biliary-type symptoms of this cancer. Sources: Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, gallbladder carcinoma clinical features.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gallbladder carcinoma mimics cholelithiasis clinically, so most cases are detected late (Robbins ch 18). COMLEX: Weight loss or a firm mass in an older woman with biliary symptoms should prompt imaging for cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acute viral hepatitis causes a prodrome and","C":"Peptic ulcer perforation produces sudden severe pain","D":"Acute pancreatitis presents abruptly with epigastric pain","E":"Small bowel obstruction causes distention, vomiting, and"}},{"id":"sdl18_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with obesity, three prior pregnancies, and oral contraceptive use has gallstones on ultrasound that do not show on a plain radiograph. What are her stones most likely made of?","choices":{"A":"Calcium bilirubinate polymer","B":"Calcium carbonate salts","C":"Calcium oxalate crystals","D":"Cholesterol monohydrate","E":"Uric acid crystals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Female sex hormones, multiparity, oral contraceptives, and obesity raise cholesterol secretion into bile, and cholesterol stones are typically radiolucent, with only 10 to 20 percent opaque. Calcium bilirubinate stones are black pigment stones, many of them radiopaque. Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stones. Calcium oxalate and uric acid are not gallstone types in the lecture. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, cholesterol gallstones, lines 3535–3557).","boardPrep":"Step 1: estrogen raises hepatic LDL receptor expression and HMG CoA reductase activity (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: biliary cholesterol saturation increases progressively with age (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stones.","C":"Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture (cholesterol, black pigment, brown pigment).","A":"Calcium bilirubinate polymer makes black pigment stones.","E":"Uric acid is not a gallstone type in the lecture."}},{"id":"sdl18_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with hereditary spherocytosis has small, friable, dark, spiculated stones in his gallbladder. Which stone type is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Brown pigment stones","B":"Black pigment stones","C":"Cholesterol stones","D":"Calcium carbonate stones","E":"Calcium oxalate stones"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chronic hemolytic anemias such as hereditary spherocytosis and sickle cell anemia cause black pigment stones, which are small, multiple, friable, and dark with molded or spiculated contours. Brown stones form in infected ducts. Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and add cholesterol and calcium palmitate and stearate to the bilirubin salts, whereas black stones form in sterile gallbladder bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, pigment gallstones, lines 3558–3573).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mechanical prosthetic heart valves are also associated with pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic hemolysis and liver disease as risk factors for black pigment stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Cholesterol stones follow female sex hormones, obesity, and multiparity.","D":"Calcium carbonate is only a component of black pigment stones.","E":"Calcium oxalate is not a gallstone class in the lecture.","A":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large bile ducts and add cholesterol and calcium palmitate and stearate to the bilirubin salts, whereas black stones form in sterile gallbladder bile."}},{"id":"sdl18_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man from rural Southeast Asia with Clonorchis infection has soft, laminated, greasy stones in his large bile ducts. What are these stones mainly composed of?","choices":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin gel","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid","D":"Cystine and xanthine crystals","E":"Calcium soaps and bilirubinate"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brown pigment stones form in infected large ducts and are made of calcium salts of unconjugated bilirubin, cholesterol, and calcium soaps of palmitate and stearate. They are characteristically radiolucent. Pure cholesterol monohydrate makes cholesterol stones. Calcium phosphate and mucin are components of black pigment stones. Calcium oxalate, uric acid, cystine, and xanthine are not gallstone components. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1, brown pigment stones, lines 3574–3577); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (cystinuria: cystine stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp states brown pigment stones usually form outside the gallbladder (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis). COMLEX: pigment stones are more common in rural populations and non Western countries (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 15 Obj 3 — underlying causes and vectors of bacterial and parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pure cholesterol monohydrate makes cholesterol stones.","B":"Calcium phosphate and mucin are components of black pigment stones.","C":"Calcium oxalate and uric acid are not gallstone components in the lecture.","D":"Cystine and xanthine are urinary stones, not gallstone components."}},{"id":"sdl18_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman with sickle cell anemia forms black gallstones. Her hepatic bilirubin conjugation is normal. Why does free unconjugated bilirubin rise in her bile?","choices":{"A":"Absent hepatic conjugation","B":"Bacterial infection of the bile","C":"Lower biliary calcium levels","D":"Deconjugation of excess output","E":"Cholesterol supersaturated bile"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic hemolysis the liver secretes excessive conjugated bilirubin into bile. About 1 percent of it deconjugates, so chronic hypersecretion yields high absolute levels of free unconjugated bilirubin that binds calcium. Infection drives brown stones, and supersaturation drives cholesterol stones. Her hepatic conjugation is normal; the free bilirubin comes from the liver secreting excessive conjugated bilirubin, about 1 percent of which deconjugates in bile. Biliary infection raises free bilirubin through microbial beta-glucuronidases, the brown-stone mechanism in Robbins; in hemolysis the rise comes from deconjugation of excess secreted conjugated bilirubin. Robbins links cholesterol supersaturation to nucleation of cholesterol monohydrate crystals and cholesterol stones, not to calcium bilirubinate black stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, pathogenesis of black pigment stones, lines 3598–3610).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid links pigment stones to Crohn disease, chronic hemolysis, alcoholic cirrhosis, and TPN (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: black stones contain calcium bilirubinate, carbonate, phosphate, mucin, and little cholesterol (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 6 — bilirubin production, conjugation, and causes of jaundice","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Her hepatic conjugation is normal; the free bilirubin comes from the liver secreting excessive conjugated bilirubin, about 1 percent of which deconjugates in bile.","B":"Biliary infection raises free bilirubin through microbial beta-glucuronidases, the brown-stone mechanism in Robbins; in hemolysis the rise comes from deconjugation of excess secreted conjugated bilirubin.","E":"Robbins links cholesterol supersaturation to nucleation of cholesterol monohydrate crystals and cholesterol stones, not to calcium bilirubinate black stones."}},{"id":"sdl18_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman loses 30 kg in the 6 months after bariatric surgery and develops gallstones. Which change most directly lets cholesterol crystals accumulate into stones?","choices":{"A":"Biliary bacterial infection","B":"Excess bilirubin secretion","C":"Gallbladder hypomotility","D":"Low cholesterol secretion","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Rapid weight loss and gallbladder stasis are major risk factors. Supersaturated cholesterol precipitates as monohydrate crystals, and gallbladder hypomotility lets the crystals accumulate. More lecithin and less cholesterol would protect against stones. Biliary infection gives brown pigment stones in the ducts, and excess bilirubin secretion gives black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 1 and 2, risk factors and nucleation and stasis, lines 3547, 3589–3592).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid lists fasting or rapid weight loss among the 8 Fs of gallstones (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists biliary stasis among the general causes of gallstones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"More lecithin and less cholesterol would protect against stones.","A":"Biliary infection gives brown pigment stones in the ducts.","B":"Excess bilirubin secretion, as in hemolysis, gives black pigment stones.","E":"More lecithin would keep cholesterol in micelles."}},{"id":"sdl18_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with obesity has cholesterol supersaturated bile containing crystals. What lets the nucleated crystals grow into macroscopic stones?","choices":{"A":"Mucin glycoprotein gel","B":"Calcium phosphate shell","C":"Fibrin clot matrix","D":"Collagen fiber scaffold","E":"Bacterial biofilm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Epithelial hypersecretion of mucin glycoproteins forms a gel matrix that traps nucleated crystals and allows progressive accretion into macroscopic stones. A calcium phosphate shell, fibrin clot, collagen scaffold, or bacterial biofilm is not the matrix; cholesterol stones have a mucin glycoprotein scaffold, and calcium phosphate belongs to black pigment stones. Calcium phosphate is a minor component of black pigment stones; Robbins names mucin glycoproteins as the scaffolding and interparticle cement of all stone types. Bacterial infection is the setting of brown stones in large ducts, but the scaffold that holds crystals together in all stones is mucin glycoprotein, not a biofilm. Robbins names hypersecretion of gallbladder mucus, not a fibrin clot, as the trap that holds nucleated crystals and lets them accrete into macroscopic stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, mucus secretion and accretion, lines 3593–3595).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol is water insoluble and needs mixed micelles with bile salts and lecithin (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists malabsorption syndromes among the classic risk factors for cholesterol stones (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"A calcium phosphate shell, fibrin clot, collagen scaffold, or bacterial biofilm is not the matrix; cholesterol stones have a mucin glycoprotein scaffold, and calcium phosphate belongs to black pigment stones.","B":"Calcium phosphate is a minor component of black pigment stones; Robbins names mucin glycoproteins as the scaffolding and interparticle cement of all stone types.","E":"Bacterial infection is the setting of brown stones in large ducts, but the scaffold that holds crystals together in all stones is mucin glycoprotein, not a biofilm.","C":"Robbins names hypersecretion of gallbladder mucus, not a fibrin clot, as the trap that holds nucleated crystals and lets them accrete into macroscopic stones."}},{"id":"sdl18_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has 8 hours of right upper quadrant pain, fever, and inspiratory arrest on palpation. Ultrasound shows a stone in the gallbladder neck. Which histologic change is most likely early on?","choices":{"A":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses","B":"Edema and neutrophils","C":"Dense hyaline fibrosis","D":"Foamy xanthoma cells","E":"Dystrophic wall calcium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Early acute calculous cholecystitis shows edema, congestion, mucosal erosions, and early neutrophilic infiltration. Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses, hyaline fibrosis, xanthoma cells, and calcification are features of chronic disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3 and 4, acute calculous morphology and chronic variants, lines 3629–3636, 3664–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the Murphy sign as inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation, with pain that may radiate to the right shoulder (First Aid 2025 GI cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists gallbladder wall thickening, edema, and pericholecystic fluid on ultrasound (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dystrophic wall calcium is not part of that answer.","A":"Rokitansky Aschoff sinuses are not part of that answer.","C":"Hyaline fibrosis is feature of chronic disease.","D":"Foamy xanthoma cells are not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl18_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man in the ICU after severe burns develops fever and right upper quadrant tenderness. Ultrasound shows a distended gallbladder with sludge and no stones. Which outcome is more frequent in his condition than in the stone related form?","choices":{"A":"Porcelain wall calcification","B":"Common duct stone impaction","C":"Gallbladder adenocarcinoma","D":"Cholecystenteric fistula ileus","E":"Gangrene and perforation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis in critically ill patients is recognized late, so gangrene and perforation occur at a significantly higher frequency than in calculous disease. Porcelain calcification and adenocarcinoma follow long standing chronic cholecystitis, and common duct impaction and fistula ileus need stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute acalculous cholecystitis, lines 3643–3650).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acalculous disease include sepsis with hypotension, trauma, burns, cardiac surgery, diabetes, and vasculitis (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised CRP and leukocytosis in acute cholecystitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with diabetes has acute gallbladder inflammation, and CT shows gas within the gallbladder wall. Which form of the disease is this?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous cholecystitis","B":"Gangrenous cholecystitis","C":"Gallbladder empyema","D":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis","E":"Hyalinizing cholecystitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Severe acute cholecystitis can show gas forming bacterial infection, called emphysematous cholecystitis. Gangrenous disease is transmural necrosis, empyema is a gallbladder full of pus, and xanthogranulomatous and hyalinizing forms are chronic. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 3, acute calculous morphology, lines 3629–3636).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes cholecystitis pain usually lasts more than 4 hours with localized guarding (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp states a HIDA scan is positive when the gallbladder is not seen on delayed or morphine augmented imaging (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has a porcelain gallbladder on imaging. Which malignancy of that organ is she at greatest risk of?","choices":{"A":"Angiosarcoma","B":"MALT lymphoma","C":"Carcinoid tumor","D":"Hepatoblastoma","E":"Adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic cholecystitis carries a markedly increased risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma, particularly with porcelain gallbladder, hyalinizing cholecystitis, or long standing stones. Angiosarcoma, MALT lymphoma, and carcinoid tumor are not linked to porcelain gallbladder, and hepatoblastoma is a rare childhood liver tumor. MALT lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma arising from mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue induced by H. pylori gastritis in the stomach. The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common tumor of the appendix and is almost always benign, not the cancer of porcelain gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, complications, lines 3677–3680); Robbins Ch. 18 (hepatoblastoma occurs in children).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp traces chronic cholecystitis with porcelain gallbladder to gallbladder adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute cholecystitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes the cancer risk is mostly adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI porcelain gallbladder).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Angiosarcoma is not linked to porcelain gallbladder, and hepatoblastoma is a rare childhood liver tumor.","D":"Angiosarcoma, MALT lymphoma, and carcinoid tumor are not linked to porcelain gallbladder, and hepatoblastoma is a rare childhood liver tumor.","B":"MALT lymphoma is a B-cell lymphoma arising from mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue induced by H. pylori gastritis in the stomach.","C":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common tumor of the appendix and is almost always benign, not the cancer of porcelain gallbladder."}},{"id":"sdl18_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with recurrent cholecystitis has vomiting and abdominal distention. Radiographs show air in the biliary tree, dilated small bowel, and an ectopic calcified stone in the bowel. Which mechanism explains her presentation?","choices":{"A":"Emphysematous infection","B":"Choledochal cyst rupture","C":"Cholecystenteric fistula","D":"Duodenal ulcer perforation","E":"Mesenteric ischemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A large stone can erode from the gallbladder into the duodenum or colon through a cholecystenteric fistula, letting air and bacteria into the biliary tree and causing gallstone ileus. Emphysematous infection is a gas forming infection of the gallbladder itself, not a stone in the bowel. Choledochal cyst rupture, duodenal ulcer perforation, and mesenteric ischemia would not put air in the biliary tree with a calcified stone in the bowel. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, biliary enteric fistula, lines 3684–3686).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallstone ileus raises the risk of acute cholangitis and often needs surgery (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes episodic small bowel obstruction with intermittent diffuse pain in gallstone ileus (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallstone ileus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with chronic cholecystitis has a shrunken, nodular, markedly thickened gallbladder wall filled with lipid laden foamy macrophages. What process most likely produced these cells?","choices":{"A":"Dystrophic wall calcification","B":"Cystic artery thrombosis","C":"Clostridial gas infection","D":"Ruptured Rokitansky sinuses","E":"Hemolytic bile pigment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis forms when ruptured Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses spill bile into the wall, drawing abundant foamy, lipid-laden xanthoma cells. Calcification makes porcelain gallbladder, and gas infection is emphysematous disease. Cystic artery ischemia drives acute acalculous cholecystitis. Hemolytic bile pigment is the basis of black pigment stones. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 4, xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis, lines 3671–3674).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyalinizing cholecystitis is dense, uniform fibrous replacement of the wall with little epithelium (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: hydrops is a chronically obstructed, dilated gallbladder of clear mucoid fluid (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Hemolytic bile pigment is the basis of black pigment stones.","A":"Extensive dystrophic calcification of the wall produces porcelain gallbladder, not a wall full of xanthoma cells.","B":"Robbins attributes acute acalculous cholecystitis to ischemia, because the cystic artery is an end artery without collaterals; that process does not produce xanthoma cells.","C":"Gas-forming organisms such as clostridia and coliforms cause acute emphysematous cholecystitis, not xanthogranulomatous disease."}},{"id":"sdl18_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with long standing gallstones has right upper quadrant pain, weight loss, and a firm thickened fundic wall. She then becomes jaundiced. What does her jaundice most likely indicate?","choices":{"A":"Early localized disease","B":"Porta hepatis invasion","C":"Benign stone passage","D":"Paraneoplastic hemolysis","E":"Drug induced cholestasis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In gallbladder carcinoma, jaundice indicates direct invasion or metastasis into the porta hepatis or common bile duct, an advanced stage. Jaundice therefore does not mean early localized disease, and benign stone passage, paraneoplastic hemolysis, and drug cholestasis do not fit a firm, infiltrating fundic mass with weight loss. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, jaundice and advanced stage, lines 3716–3717).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gallbladder carcinoma has a female to male ratio above 2 to 1 and high incidence in Native American, Hispanic, Chilean, Bolivian, and Northern Indian groups (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists PSC and Clonorchis infection as gallbladder adenocarcinoma risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has a resected gallbladder carcinoma with marked aneuploidy. Which tumor suppressor change is found in about half of these tumors?","choices":{"A":"TP53 loss of function","B":"APC loss of function","C":"VHL loss of function","D":"SMAD4 loss of function","E":"BRCA1 loss of function"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of function TP53 mutations occur in about 50 percent of gallbladder carcinomas and cause marked aneuploidy, alongside gain of function changes in EGF receptor family members and RAS signaling. APC, VHL, SMAD4, and BRCA1 are not among the drivers the lecture lists for this tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, molecular driver alterations, lines 3706–3710).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic S typhi carriage and parasitic infection are chronic inflammatory risk factors (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms are precursor lesions (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl18_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old woman with symptoms like chronic cholecystitis is found to have gallbladder carcinoma with direct liver invasion. What most explains the poor outlook of this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Chemotherapy induced toxicity","B":"Frequent bone metastasis","C":"Advanced stage at diagnosis","D":"Hormone driven growth","E":"Early brain metastasis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Symptoms mimic gallstones or chronic cholecystitis, so most tumors present at an advanced, unresectable stage with liver invasion, peritoneal spread, or nodal metastasis. Chemotherapy toxicity and hormone driven growth are not the reason, and the spread is to liver, peritoneum, and nodes rather than bone or brain; fewer than 20% are diagnosed before surgery, and 5 year survival is under 10%. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 5, clinical presentation and prognosis, lines 3711–3723).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes gallbladder adenocarcinoma is initially asymptomatic before an RUQ mass and pain appear (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gallbladder adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: right upper quadrant pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and weight loss are the presenting symptoms (lecture SDL 18).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the distinct epidemiological risk factors (4 Fs), associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with obesity and very high triglycerides has taken gemfibrozil for several years and now has recurrent biliary colic. Which description best fits the stones expected at cholecystectomy?","choices":{"A":"Small, black, friable, often radiopaque","B":"Soft, brown, greasy, and laminated","C":"Biliary sludge with no discrete stones","D":"Single large stone of pure bilirubin","E":"Pale yellow, faceted, radiolucent"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity and fibrate therapy are associated with cholesterol stones, which form in the gallbladder as pale yellow, often multiple and faceted stones with a radiating crystalline cut surface; only 10% to 20% contain enough calcium carbonate to be radiopaque. Small, black, friable stones form in sterile gallbladder bile with chronic hemolysis or ileal disease, and 50% to 75% are radiopaque. Soft, brown, greasy, laminated stones form in infected large bile ducts, as with E. coli, Ascaris, or Clonorchis. Biliary sludge with no discrete stones does not match the stones being sought, and her bile favors crystalline cholesterol stones. A single large stone of pure bilirubin is not a recognized type, since pigment stones contain calcium bilirubinate with other calcium salts. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18 section 1; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cholesterol stone risks include female sex, estrogen, age, obesity, rapid weight loss, stasis, fibrates, and Native American ancestry, and most of these stones are radiolucent (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Pigment stones point instead toward hemolysis, ileal disease, or biliary infection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the role of bile salts in micelle formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Small, black, friable stones form in sterile","B":"Soft, brown, greasy, laminated stones form in","C":"Biliary sludge with no discrete stones does","D":"A single large stone of pure bilirubin"}},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the underlying biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones.","batch":3,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with Crohn disease had his terminal ileum resected and now has black gallstones. How does his ileal disease promote these stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess cholesterol absorption","B":"Biliary bacterial growth","C":"Reduced bilirubin output","D":"Lost bile salt reabsorption","E":"Increased lecithin output"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In ileal disease, impaired bile salt reabsorption leads the liver to secrete excess conjugated bilirubin into bile, and the deconjugated fraction precipitates with calcium as black stones. Bacterial growth drives brown stones. More lecithin would help keep cholesterol in solution and plays no part in black stones, which come from excess bilirubin. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 18, section 2, hemolysis and ileal disease, lines 3601–3603).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal resection or bypass is an associated condition of pigment stones (lecture SDL 18). COMLEX: First Aid lists Crohn disease among pigment stone associations (First Aid 2025 GI cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has 10 hours of constant right upper quadrant pain after a fatty meal, a temperature of 38.3 °C, and inspiratory arrest on palpation. Ultrasound shows a stone impacted in the gallbladder neck, a thickened wall, and pericholecystic fluid. Bile cultured at early surgery is sterile. Which mechanism most directly initiated her mucosal injury?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial invasion from the duodenum","B":"Lysolecithin and bile salt attack","C":"Cystic artery ischemia from shock","D":"Autoimmune attack on epithelium","E":"Gas forming bacterial infection"},"correct":"B","explanation":"With the outlet blocked, mucosal phospholipases convert luminal lecithin to toxic lysolecithin, which strips the protective mucus and exposes the epithelium to the detergent action of bile salts. Distention then compresses mucosal blood flow, and bacteria such as E. coli may invade later. Bacterial invasion from the duodenum is a later superinfection, and her early bile culture is sterile. Cystic artery ischemia from shock drives acalculous cholecystitis in critically ill patients, not stone obstruction in a stable patient. Autoimmune attack on the epithelium is not part of calculous cholecystitis. Gas forming bacterial infection produces emphysematous cholecystitis, a severe complication with gas in the wall. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18 section 3; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute calculous cholecystitis begins as chemical injury from lysolecithin and bile salts behind an impacted stone, followed by ischemia and prostaglandin-mediated inflammation, with bacteria joining later (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Fever and focal right upper quadrant tenderness with inspiratory arrest after a fatty meal suggest acute cholecystitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bacterial invasion from the duodenum is a","C":"Cystic artery ischemia from shock drives acalculous","D":"Autoimmune attack on the epithelium is not","E":"Gas forming bacterial infection produces emphysematous cholecystitis,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the associated conditions, morphology (porcelain gallbladder), and complications of chronic cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has had 4 years of recurrent right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals that resolves within a few hours. Ultrasound shows multiple stones and a thick-walled, contracted gallbladder. She has an elective cholecystectomy. Which microscopic finding is most expected in the gallbladder wall?","choices":{"A":"Transmural neutrophils and necrosis","B":"Gas filled cysts within the wall","C":"Granulomas around parasite eggs","D":"Mural fibrosis with lymphocytes","E":"Mucin cysts with ovarian stroma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Years of recurrent biliary colic with stones and a thick, contracted gallbladder indicate chronic cholecystitis, which shows lymphocytes, plasma cells, and macrophages with variable mural fibrosis, often with Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses. Transmural neutrophils and necrosis describe acute gangrenous cholecystitis, not years of recurrent colic. Gas filled cysts within the wall indicate emphysematous cholecystitis from gas forming bacteria. Granulomas around parasite eggs are the portal tract lesion of schistosomiasis, not gallstone disease. Mucin cysts with ovarian stroma define a pancreatic mucinous cystic neoplasm. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18 sections 3 and 4; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chronic cholecystitis shows mononuclear inflammation, mural fibrosis, and Rokitansky-Aschoff sinuses, with variants such as porcelain, hyalinizing, and xanthogranulomatous cholecystitis (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Porcelain and hyalinizing variants raise the risk of gallbladder adenocarcinoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Transmural neutrophils and necrosis describe acute gangrenous","B":"Gas filled cysts within the wall indicate","C":"Granulomas around parasite eggs are the portal","E":"Mucin cysts with ovarian stroma define a"}},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Identify the risk factors, underlying pathogenesis, and clinical presentation of gallbladder carcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old Bolivian woman with 20 years of gallstones has weight loss, right upper quadrant pain, and a firm, diffusely thickened fundic wall on CT. Resection shows invasive adenocarcinoma, and the nearby mucosa has intestinal metaplasia and flat dysplasia. Which sequence best explains how her tumor arose?","choices":{"A":"APC loss in a polyp, then cancer","B":"Viral DNA integration in epithelium","C":"Irritation driving dysplasia to cancer","D":"Germline mismatch repair loss","E":"Bacterial toxin causing direct mutation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Gallstones are present in about 95% of gallbladder carcinomas, and chronic mechanical or infectious irritation causes repeated epithelial injury and regeneration that progresses through flat dysplasia and intestinal metaplasia to invasive carcinoma, helped by EGF receptor family activation and TP53 loss. APC loss in a polyp followed by cancer is the colorectal adenoma-carcinoma sequence, not the usual route of gallstone-associated gallbladder cancer. Viral DNA integration in epithelium is how HBV contributes to HCC, not gallbladder cancer. Germline mismatch repair loss is an inherited cancer mechanism with no link to gallstones. A bacterial toxin causing direct mutation is not the described mechanism, since chronic Salmonella typhi infection acts through ongoing inflammation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18 section 5; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gallbladder carcinoma arises through chronic inflammation, flat dysplasia, intestinal metaplasia, and intracholecystic papillary tubular neoplasms, with gallstones in about 95% of cases (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Suspect it in older women from high-incidence groups with long-standing gallstones, weight loss, and a thickened wall.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 17 Obj 5 — cholangiocarcinoma and its associated conditions versus hepatocellular carcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC loss in a polyp followed by","B":"Viral DNA integration in epithelium is how","D":"Germline mismatch repair loss is an inherited","E":"A bacterial toxin causing direct mutation is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl18_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the pathology, morphology, and risk factors of acute calculous and acalculous cholecystitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man is on day 9 in the ICU after cardiac surgery complicated by septic shock requiring vasopressors. He has had nothing by mouth and is fed by total parenteral nutrition. He develops fever and right upper quadrant tenderness, and ultrasound shows a distended, thick-walled gallbladder full of sludge with no stones. Which mechanism best explains his disease?","choices":{"A":"Stone impacted in the cystic duct","B":"Excess CCK driven gallbladder spasm","C":"Autoimmune vasculitis of the wall","D":"Ascending infection through the ampulla","E":"Cystic artery ischemia and stasis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acalculous cholecystitis in a critically ill patient comes mainly from ischemia of the cystic artery, an end artery without collaterals, worsened by hypotension, together with stasis and viscous sludge in a gallbladder that is not emptying because fasting gives no CCK stimulus. A stone impacted in the cystic duct causes calculous cholecystitis, but his ultrasound shows no stones. Excess CCK driven spasm is the opposite of his state, since fasting and parenteral feeding remove the meal signal for CCK release. Autoimmune vasculitis is a rare risk factor, but his septic shock and fasting explain the picture without it. Ascending infection through the ampulla describes cholangitis of the bile ducts rather than the primary cause of acalculous gallbladder injury. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 18 section 3; Robbins Ch. 18.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acalculous cholecystitis, about 10% of cases, results from cystic artery ischemia with stasis and sludge in sepsis, trauma, burns, or after cardiac surgery (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: Suspect it in a febrile ICU patient with right upper quadrant tenderness even when no stones are seen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A stone impacted in the cystic duct","B":"Excess CCK driven spasm is the opposite","C":"Autoimmune vasculitis is a rare risk factor,","D":"Ascending infection through the ampulla describes cholangitis"}}]},{"sdlNumber":19,"title":"SDL 19 — Pathology of the Pancreas and Pancreatic Neuroendocrine Tumors","questions":[{"id":"sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"What developmental error causes pancreas divisum?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete ventral bud rotation","B":"Absence of the dorsal pancreatic bud","C":"Two duct systems fail to fuse","D":"Pancreatic rests left in the stomach","E":"Failed duodenal recanalization"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancreas divisum results when the duct systems of the dorsal and ventral pancreatic primordia fail to fuse, so most of the gland drains separately through the small minor papilla. Incomplete ventral bud rotation produces an annular pancreas that encircles the second part of the duodenum. Absence of the dorsal pancreatic bud would leave the body and tail missing, not two separately draining ducts. Pancreatic rests left in the stomach describe ectopic pancreas, a different congenital anomaly. Failed duodenal recanalization causes duodenal atresia rather than a ductal variant of the pancreas. Sources: Robbins ch 19, congenital anomalies; Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 19.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreas divisum is failed fusion of the dorsal and ventral ducts and is the most common congenital pancreatic anomaly (Robbins ch 19). COMLEX: Consider it in recurrent idiopathic pancreatitis, especially with a coexisting genetic risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Incomplete ventral bud rotation produces an annular","B":"Absence of the dorsal pancreatic bud would","D":"Pancreatic rests left in the stomach describe","E":"Failed duodenal recanalization causes duodenal atresia rather"}},{"id":"sdl19_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"Through which route does the bulk of the gland drain in pancreas divisum?","choices":{"A":"Major papilla via Wirsung","B":"Ampulla via common bile duct","C":"Minor papilla via Wirsung","D":"Major papilla via Santorini","E":"Minor papilla via Santorini"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In pancreas divisum the dorsal and ventral duct systems fail to fuse, so the bulk of the gland, from the dorsal primordium, drains through the small minor papilla via the duct of Santorini. The duct of Wirsung drains only part of the head through the major papilla. The ampulla is not that pancreatic route. The duct of Wirsung does not open at the minor papilla, and the duct of Santorini does not open at the major papilla. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum, lines 3726–3734).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 5 percent of people with pancreas divisum develop recurrent acute or chronic pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the fetal duct systems normally fuse between the 5th and 8th gestational weeks (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The ampulla and common bile duct are the biliary entrance, not the pancreatic duct path in this anomaly.","A":"This pairs the major papilla with the duct of Wirsung.","C":"The duct of Wirsung does not open at the minor papilla.","D":"The duct of Santorini does not open at the major papilla."}},{"id":"sdl19_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":1,"stem":"Where is ectopic pancreatic tissue most often found?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum and ileum","B":"Stomach and duodenum","C":"Liver and gallbladder","D":"Sigmoid colon and rectum","E":"Spleen and its hilum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The favored sites of ectopic pancreas are the stomach and duodenum, followed by the jejunum, Meckel diverticula, and ileum; these rests are usually incidental but can cause pain or bleeding. The jejunum and ileum are less frequent sites that come after the stomach and duodenum. The liver and gallbladder are not among the favored sites listed for pancreatic ectopia. The sigmoid colon and rectum are not listed as usual sites of ectopic pancreas. The spleen and its hilum are the classic site of accessory splenic tissue, not of pancreatic rests. Sources: Robbins ch 19, ectopic pancreas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ectopic pancreas favors the stomach and duodenum and is made of normal acini, ducts, and sometimes islets (Robbins ch 19). COMLEX: A submucosal gastric nodule with a central dimple on endoscopy can be a pancreatic rest.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 11 → Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The jejunum and ileum are less frequent","C":"The liver and gallbladder are not among","D":"The sigmoid colon and rectum are not","E":"The spleen and its hilum are the"}},{"id":"sdl19_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which event initiates acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cell destruction","B":"Stellate cell collagen deposition","C":"IgG4 plasma cell infiltration","D":"Trypsin activation in gland","E":"Biliary epithelial apoptosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute pancreatitis begins with inappropriate intrapancreatic activation of trypsinogen to trypsin, which activates other zymogens and drives autodigestion and fat necrosis. Stellate cell collagen deposition drives chronic pancreatitis. Islet loss is a late chronic change, IgG4 plasma cells mark autoimmune type 1 chronic pancreatitis, and biliary epithelial apoptosis is not the initiating event. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3778–3782).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists didanosine, stavudine, furosemide, hydrochlorothiazide, glucocorticoids, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid gives the I GET SMASHED mnemonic for causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which serum marker is the most sensitive and specific for acute pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Serum lipase","B":"Serum amylase","C":"Serum ALT","D":"Serum calcium","E":"Serum CRP"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Serum lipase is the most sensitive and specific marker. It rises within 4 to 12 hours and stays raised for 8 to 14 days, whereas amylase rises quickly but returns to baseline within 3 to 5 days. Serum amylase rises early but has a short half-life and may return to normal in 3 to 5 days, so it is less sensitive and specific than lipase. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3804–3808).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid diagnoses acute pancreatitis by 2 of 3 criteria, including enzymes at 3 times the upper limit of normal (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists raised ESR, CRP, and LDH in pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Serum ALT is not the marker named here.","D":"Serum calcium is not the marker named here.","E":"Serum CRP is not the marker named here.","B":"Serum amylase rises early but has a short half-life and may return to normal in 3 to 5 days, so it is less sensitive and specific than lipase."}},{"id":"sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells deposit collagen in chronic pancreatitis after activation by TGF beta and PDGF?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells","B":"Centroacinar duct cells","C":"Pancreatic stellate cells","D":"Resident tissue mast cells","E":"Ductal epithelial cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Repeated acinar injury stimulates local TGF beta and PDGF, which activate pancreatic stellate cells, the periacinar myofibroblasts, to deposit collagen and cause progressive fibrosis and acinar loss. Islet alpha cells secrete glucagon. Centroacinar cells secrete bicarbonate. Mast cells and ductal epithelium are not the collagen source. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, pathogenesis, lines 3782–3785).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists dystrophic calcifications and parenchymal atrophy in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists low fecal elastase 1 in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Islet alpha cells are glucagon-secreting endocrine cells.","B":"Centroacinar cells are duct cells that secrete bicarbonate.","D":"Mast cells are not the collagen-depositing cells.","E":"Ductal epithelial cells are not the collagen source."}},{"id":"sdl19_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"What makes up the wall of a pancreatic pseudocyst?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous granulation tissue","B":"Glycogen rich cuboidal cells","C":"Tall mucinous columnar cells","D":"Dense ovarian type stroma","E":"Papillary ductal epithelium"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A pseudocyst lacks a true epithelial lining. Its wall is fibrous tissue, granulation tissue, and necrotic debris with saponified fat. Glycogen rich cuboidal cells line serous cystadenomas, and mucinous epithelium with ovarian stroma lines MCNs. Papillary ductal epithelium would be a true epithelial lining. Glycogen-rich cuboidal cells line serous cystic neoplasms in Robbins, a true epithelial lining that a pseudocyst lacks. Columnar mucin-producing epithelium lines mucinous cystic neoplasms. Dense ovarian-type stroma beneath mucinous epithelium defines the mucinous cystic neoplasm in Robbins, not a walled-off collection of fat necrosis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3826–3829).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pseudocysts account for about 75 percent of all pancreatic cysts (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes a pseudocyst as a fibrous capsule surrounding pancreatic fluid (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Papillary ductal epithelium would be a true epithelial lining.","B":"Glycogen-rich cuboidal cells line serous cystic neoplasms in Robbins, a true epithelial lining that a pseudocyst lacks.","C":"Columnar mucin-producing epithelium lines mucinous cystic neoplasms.","D":"Dense ovarian-type stroma beneath mucinous epithelium defines the mucinous cystic neoplasm in Robbins, not a walled-off collection of fat necrosis."}},{"id":"sdl19_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene and chromosome are tied to serous cystic neoplasms?","choices":{"A":"KRAS on 12p","B":"VHL on 3p","C":"GNAS on 20q","D":"RNF43 on 17q","E":"SMAD4 on 18q"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Serous cystadenomas are strongly associated with loss of function of the VHL tumor suppressor on chromosome 3p. KRAS and GNAS mark mucinous lesions, and SMAD4 marks invasive progression. RNF43 loss marks mucinous cystic neoplasms and IPMNs. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, serous cystic neoplasms, lines 3841–3845).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serous cystadenomas occur twice as often in females, peak in the 6th to 7th decades, and favor the tail (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: serous cystadenomas carry virtually no risk of malignant transformation (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":1,"stem":"Which feature is pathognomonic of a mucinous cystic neoplasm?","choices":{"A":"Glycogen rich lining","B":"Main duct papillae","C":"Granulation tissue wall","D":"Honeycomb microcysts","E":"Ovarian type stroma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MCNs are lined by tall mucin producing columnar epithelium backed by a pathognomonic dense ovarian type stroma that expresses estrogen and progesterone receptors and inhibin. Main duct papillae fit IPMN, and honeycomb microcysts with glycogen rich cells fit serous cystadenoma. A wall of granulation and fibrous tissue without epithelium is a pseudocyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3853).","boardPrep":"Step 1: MCNs are driven by KRAS mutations in about 50 percent and RNF43 loss in about 33 percent (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: up to one third of MCNs harbor an invasive adenocarcinoma (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Why are carcinomas of the pancreatic body and tail often large and widespread at diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"They usually arise from islet cells","B":"They do not block the bile duct","C":"They rarely invade nerves or vessels","D":"They are mostly cystic neoplasms","E":"They lack a desmoplastic stroma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Head tumors obstruct the distal common bile duct and come to attention through jaundice, whereas body and tail tumors do not impinge on the biliary tract and stay silent until they are large and disseminated. Ductal adenocarcinoma arises from ductal epithelium in every region, not from islet cells. Perineural and vascular invasion are common in pancreatic cancer regardless of location. Body and tail ductal carcinomas are solid infiltrating tumors, not mainly cystic lesions. Intense desmoplasia is seen in pancreatic carcinomas of the head, body, and tail alike. Sources: Robbins ch 19, carcinoma of the pancreas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic head carcinoma causes obstructive jaundice and the Courvoisier sign, while body and tail tumors present late (Robbins ch 19). COMLEX: New back pain and weight loss without jaundice can still mean a body or tail tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ductal adenocarcinoma arises from ductal epithelium in","C":"Perineural and vascular invasion are common in","D":"Body and tail ductal carcinomas are solid","E":"Intense desmoplasia is seen in pancreatic carcinomas"}},{"id":"sdl19_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"Which alteration is the earliest driver in low grade PanIN 1 lesions?","choices":{"A":"TP53 inactivation","B":"SMAD4 inactivation","C":"CDKN2A inactivation","D":"KRAS activation","E":"BRCA2 inactivation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Activating KRAS point mutations on 12p occur in more than 90 percent of cases and are the earliest driver in PanIN 1. CDKN2A loss appears at PanIN 2, TP53 and SMAD4 loss at PanIN 3, and BRCA2 is a germline predisposition gene. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, key mutations, lines 3894–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: CDKN2A is inactivated in about 30 percent by mutation and up to 90 percent with promoter hypermethylation (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: First Aid names a KRAS activating mutation as the most common genomic abnormality (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":1,"stem":"What causes the Trousseau sign in pancreatic cancer?","choices":{"A":"Tumor procoagulant mucins","B":"Platelet antibody consumption","C":"Antithrombin gene mutation","D":"Factor V Leiden variant","E":"Estrogen driven clotting"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The Trousseau sign, migratory thrombophlebitis or recurrent venous thrombosis, is caused by tumor derived procoagulant mucins. Platelet antibodies, antithrombin or factor V Leiden variants, and estrogen are not the mechanism, because the clotting comes from the tumor itself. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, systemic presentations, lines 3885–3888).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes migratory thrombophlebitis as redness and tenderness on palpation of the extremities (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp links a hypercoagulable state to superficial thrombophlebitis in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"From which protein is the amyloid in insulinomas derived?","choices":{"A":"Proinsulin","B":"Calcitonin","C":"Light chains","D":"Transthyretin","E":"Islet amylin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Insulinomas show monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with characteristic amyloid derived from amylin. Calcitonin amyloid fits medullary thyroid carcinoma, light chains fit AL amyloid, and transthyretin fits senile amyloid. First Aid also lists islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), not proinsulin, as the islet amyloid protein. Robbins notes amyloid derived from calcitonin is the stromal deposit of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid, not of insulinoma. Immunoglobulin light chains from a monoclonal plasma cell population form AL amyloid in Robbins, a plasma cell dyscrasia product rather than an islet tumor deposit. Robbins describes transthyretin amyloid as wild-type TTR deposits mainly in the heart of older men or variant TTR in heart and nerves, not in insulinomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinoma morphology, lines 3911–3915); First Aid 2025 Pathology (islet amyloid polypeptide from amylin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 90 percent of insulinomas are benign, solitary, encapsulated nodules under 2 cm (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp states insulinomas arise from beta cells with autonomous insulin production (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"First Aid also lists islet amyloid polypeptide (amylin), not proinsulin, as the islet amyloid protein.","B":"Robbins notes amyloid derived from calcitonin is the stromal deposit of medullary carcinoma of the thyroid, not of insulinoma.","C":"Immunoglobulin light chains from a monoclonal plasma cell population form AL amyloid in Robbins, a plasma cell dyscrasia product rather than an islet tumor deposit.","D":"Robbins describes transthyretin amyloid as wild-type TTR deposits mainly in the heart of older men or variant TTR in heart and nerves, not in insulinomas."}},{"id":"sdl19_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"A patient with gastrinoma is found to have MEN1. Which disorder is the most common manifestation of this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma","B":"Adrenal pheochromocytoma","C":"Primary hyperparathyroidism","D":"Pituitary prolactinoma","E":"Multiple mucosal neuromas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Primary hyperparathyroidism from parathyroid hyperplasia or adenomas is the most common and usually the first manifestation of MEN1, ahead of the pancreatic and pituitary tumors. Medullary thyroid carcinoma belongs to MEN2A and MEN2B, not MEN1. Adrenal pheochromocytoma is part of MEN2 rather than MEN1. Pituitary prolactinoma is the usual pituitary tumor of MEN1 but is less common than parathyroid disease. Multiple mucosal neuromas are a feature of MEN2B and are not seen in MEN1. Sources: Robbins ch 24, multiple endocrine neoplasia; Robbins ch 24, Zollinger-Ellison syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MEN1 is parathyroid, pancreas, and pituitary, and hyperparathyroidism is its most common feature (Robbins ch 24). COMLEX: Check serum calcium in any patient with gastrinoma.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Medullary thyroid carcinoma belongs to MEN2A and","B":"Adrenal pheochromocytoma is part of MEN2 rather","D":"Pituitary prolactinoma is the usual pituitary tumor","E":"Multiple mucosal neuromas are a feature of"}},{"id":"sdl19_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":1,"stem":"Which skin rash is characteristic of a glucagonoma?","choices":{"A":"Painful erythema nodosum","B":"Violaceous livedo reticularis","C":"Velvety acanthosis nigricans","D":"Necrolytic migratory erythema","E":"Itchy dermatitis herpetiformis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucagonoma causes mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and deep vein thrombosis. The other rashes fit inflammatory bowel disease, vasculopathy, insulin resistance, and celiac disease. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas are most common in perimenopausal or postmenopausal women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss and thrombosis in glucagonoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 13 → Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Painful erythema nodosum fits inflammatory bowel disease, not a glucagonoma.","B":"Violaceous livedo reticularis fits vasculopathy, not a glucagonoma.","C":"Velvety acanthosis nigricans fits insulin resistance, not a glucagonoma.","E":"Itchy dermatitis herpetiformis fits celiac disease, not a glucagonoma."}},{"id":"sdl19_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man who does not drink alcohol has had four episodes of acute pancreatitis since age 17. He takes no medications, ultrasound shows no gallstones, and his serum calcium and triglycerides are normal. His younger sister has also had pancreatitis, and neither parent has. MRCP shows that his dorsal and ventral pancreatic ducts never joined. Genetic testing is most likely to show a variant in which gene?","choices":{"A":"ATP7B","B":"UGT1A1","C":"ABCB11","D":"CFTR","E":"HNF1A"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Recurrent pancreatitis from adolescence, with an affected sibling and unaffected parents, plus pancreas divisum, points to a recessive susceptibility gene. CFTR is one of the genes named in hereditary pancreatitis, along with PRSS1 and SPINK1. ATP7B mutations cause Wilson disease. UGT1A1 variants cause Gilbert or Crigler-Najjar syndrome. ABCB11 defects cause familial intrahepatic cholestasis. HNF1A variants cause monogenic diabetes and hepatocellular adenomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, pancreas divisum and predisposing genetic defects, lines 3726–3738); Robbins Ch. 19 (pancreas divisum; PRSS1, SPINK1, and CFTR in hereditary pancreatitis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that pancreas divisum is a common anomaly, mostly asymptomatic, that may cause chronic abdominal pain or pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists an increased risk of pancreatitis and abdominal pain with pancreas divisum (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic anomalies).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — cystic fibrosis as an etiology of pancreatitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has had 8 months of postprandial epigastric fullness and intermittent vomiting. She does not use NSAIDs, and upper endoscopy shows no ulcer or mass. CT shows a band of tissue with the same density and enhancement as the pancreatic head wrapped around the duodenum, with a dilated stomach and duodenal bulb proximal to it. Which process best explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophy of pyloric muscle","B":"Peptic scarring of the pylorus","C":"Extrinsic duodenal constriction","D":"Intraluminal duodenal mucosal web","E":"Failure of gastric smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A ring of pancreatic tissue around the duodenum is an annular pancreas, formed when a split ventral bud migrates around the duodenal C loop. About one third of cases are incidental, but in symptomatic patients the ring constricts the duodenum from outside and causes stenosis or obstruction, presenting in neonates or in adults with persistent vomiting and distention. Pyloric muscle hypertrophy is an infant lesion at the pylorus and would not dilate the duodenal bulb. Peptic scarring of the pylorus requires ulcer disease, and endoscopy showed none. An intraluminal duodenal web is a recanalization defect inside the lumen, not an encircling band of pancreas. Gastric smooth muscle failure would leave the duodenal bulb undilated and would show no encircling tissue. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, annular pancreas, lines 3739–3748); Robbins Ch. 19 (annular pancreas); First Aid 2025 GI (annular pancreas).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes that annular pancreas may cause duodenal narrowing and vomiting and is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid 2025 GI, pancreas and spleen embryology). COMLEX: an annular pancreas can first cause symptoms in adulthood (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — congenital duodenal obstruction","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man has epigastric pain and occult blood in his stool. Endoscopy shows a small submucosal gastric nodule, and biopsy shows normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets. Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Gastric carcinoid","B":"Gastric lipoma","C":"Gastric leiomyoma","D":"Gastric lymphoma","E":"Ectopic pancreas"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ectopic pancreas forms subepithelial rests of normal acini, ducts, and sometimes islets, most often in the stomach and duodenum. Inflammation can cause epigastric pain, and ulceration can cause occult bleeding. A carcinoid, lipoma, leiomyoma, or lymphoma would not show normal appearing acini, ducts, and islets on biopsy. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 1, ectopic pancreas, lines 3749–3759).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ectopic pancreas is found in about 2 percent of postmortem examinations (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: ectopic pancreas in a Meckel diverticulum can cause peptic injury (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 3 — how the pancreas forms from two buds and rotates around the duodenum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has epigastric pain radiating to his back, and CT shows peripancreatic fat necrosis. His serum calcium is 7.2 mg/dL (reference 8.4–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism explains his low calcium?","choices":{"A":"Fat saponification","B":"Renal calcium wasting","C":"PTH gland failure","D":"Calcitonin surge","E":"Bone mineral uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In acute pancreatitis, released lipases break down fat, and the fatty acids bind calcium to form calcium soaps, so fat saponification causes hypocalcemia. Renal wasting, parathyroid failure, a calcitonin surge, and bone uptake are not the mechanism; calcium is consumed as soaps in the necrotic fat. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, morphology and complications, lines 3794–3797, 3812–3816).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes hypocalcemia in acute pancreatitis to precipitation of calcium soaps (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp notes acute pancreatitis pain radiates to the back and improves with leaning forward (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman is admitted with acute pancreatitis. Which admission finding counts toward her Ranson score?","choices":{"A":"AST 150 U/L","B":"Age 45 years","C":"Glucose 120 mg/dL","D":"LDH 200 U/L","E":"LDH 400 U/L"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ranson admission criteria include age over 55, WBC over 16,000 per microliter, glucose over 200 mg/dL, AST over 250 U/L, and LDH over 350 U/L. Only an LDH of 400 U/L meets a threshold here. The other values are not that figure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, Ranson criteria concept, lines 3786–3792).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ranson criteria at 48 hours include a hematocrit fall over 10 percent and a BUN rise over 5 mg/dL (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: the 48 hour criteria include calcium under 8 mg/dL and PaO2 under 60 mm Hg (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes and the mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An AST of 150 U/L is below its cutoff.","B":"Age 45 is below its cutoff.","C":"A glucose of 120 mg/dL is below its cutoff.","D":"An LDH of 200 U/L is below its cutoff."}},{"id":"sdl19_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man with long standing alcohol use has steatorrhea, weight loss, and pancreatic calcifications on CT. During a painful attack his pancreatic enzymes are barely raised. Which explains this?","choices":{"A":"Rapid renal enzyme loss","B":"Enzymes bound by albumin","C":"Lab assay interference","D":"Acinar cell dropout","E":"Islet cell destruction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In chronic pancreatitis, amylase and lipase may be normal or only mildly raised during attacks because extensive acinar dropout leaves too little functional tissue to release enzymes. Islets are preserved until late and do not make these enzymes. The low values reflect real loss of enzyme producing tissue rather than assay interference. Albumin binding is not the explanation. Robbins ties destruction of the endocrine pancreas to diabetes in chronic pancreatitis, whereas the missing amylase rise reflects acinar cell dropout, since islets do not make these enzymes. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, serum markers, lines 3809–3811).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp lists fat soluble vitamin A, D, E, and K deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears below 10 percent function (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Albumin binding is not the explanation.","E":"Robbins ties destruction of the endocrine pancreas to diabetes in chronic pancreatitis, whereas the missing amylase rise reflects acinar cell dropout, since islets do not make these enzymes."}},{"id":"sdl19_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with chronic alcoholic pancreatitis had an acute attack 6 weeks ago and now has a walled off peripancreatic fluid collection with debris. Which complication is this lesion prone to?","choices":{"A":"Malignant transformation","B":"Islet amyloid deposits","C":"Abscess formation","D":"GNAS driven carcinoma","E":"Tuberculous granulomas"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A pseudocyst arises after acute pancreatitis, especially on chronic alcoholic disease, when walled off fat necrosis liquefies. Its complications are secondary infection forming an abscess, erosion into vessels or viscera, and rupture. It is not a neoplasm, so malignant change and GNAS carcinoma do not apply, islet amyloid belongs to type 2 diabetes, and tuberculous granulomas are not among its listed complications; its infectious risk is abscess. Amyloid deposition in islets is a pathologic feature of type 2 diabetes, not a pseudocyst complication. GNAS mutations are present in about two-thirds of IPMNs but not in other pancreatic cysts, so they do not arise in a non-neoplastic pseudocyst. First Aid lists pancreatic pseudocyst, abscess, necrosis, hemorrhage, and infection as complications of pancreatitis, not tuberculous granulomas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, pseudocysts, lines 3830–3836).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp notes pseudocysts raise the risk of extrinsic compression of local structures (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic pseudocyst). COMLEX: First Aid describes a pseudocyst as lined by granulation tissue, not epithelium (First Aid 2025 GI pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is not a neoplasm, so malignant change and GNAS carcinoma do not apply, islet amyloid belongs to type 2 diabetes, and tuberculous granulomas are not among its listed complications.","B":"Amyloid deposition in islets is a pathologic feature of type 2 diabetes, not a pseudocyst complication.","D":"GNAS mutations are present in about two-thirds of IPMNs but not in other pancreatic cysts, so they do not arise in a non-neoplastic pseudocyst.","E":"First Aid lists pancreatic pseudocyst, abscess, necrosis, hemorrhage, and infection as complications of pancreatitis, not tuberculous granulomas."}},{"id":"sdl19_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 42-year-old woman has a slow growing, painless, 6 cm multiloculated cyst in her pancreatic tail filled with thick, tenacious mucin. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Endoscopic drainage","B":"Percutaneous drainage","C":"Antibiotic therapy","D":"Early resection","E":"Somatostatin analog"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A mucin filled multiloculated tail cyst in a woman is a mucinous cystic neoplasm, a precancerous lesion in which up to one third harbor invasive adenocarcinoma, so early surgical resection is mandatory. Drainage suits pseudocysts, not neoplasms. Antibiotics treat an infected pseudocyst or abscess, not a neoplasm, and a somatostatin analog does not remove a precancerous cyst. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, mucinous cystic neoplasms, lines 3849–3861).","boardPrep":"Step 1: About 95 percent of MCNs arise in women (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: TP53 and SMAD4 mutations mark the transition of MCN to invasive cancer (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a cystic lesion of the pancreatic head that arises within a dilated main duct and contains mucin producing papillae without ovarian stroma. Which mutation best distinguishes this lesion from other pancreatic cysts?","choices":{"A":"VHL inactivation","B":"GNAS activation","C":"BRAF activation","D":"HNF1A inactivation","E":"CTNNB1 activation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"An IPMN arises in the larger ducts, favors the head in men, and lacks ovarian stroma. Its oncogenic GNAS mutations, in about two thirds, are unique among pancreatic cysts. VHL marks serous cystadenoma. BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are not among the cyst mutations the lecture lists (KRAS, RNF43, GNAS, VHL). CTNNB1 activating mutations are found in nearly all solid pseudopapillary neoplasms, not intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms. BRAF alterations occur in only about 10% of IPMNs, so they do not distinguish this lesion the way GNAS mutations do. VHL inactivation is the most common genetic abnormality in serous cystic neoplasms, not in mucin-producing duct lesions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 3, IPMNs, lines 3862–3873).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 20 percent of IPMNs are multifocal (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: IPMNs also carry KRAS mutations in about 80 percent and RNF43 mutations in about 50 percent (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"BRAF, HNF1A, and CTNNB1 are not among the cyst mutations the lecture lists (KRAS, RNF43, GNAS, VHL).","E":"CTNNB1 activating mutations are found in nearly all solid pseudopapillary neoplasms, not intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms.","C":"BRAF alterations occur in only about 10% of IPMNs, so they do not distinguish this lesion the way GNAS mutations do.","A":"VHL inactivation is the most common genetic abnormality in serous cystic neoplasms, not in mucin-producing duct lesions."}},{"id":"sdl19_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has mild, progressive painless jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and a palpable nontender gallbladder. Where is his tumor most likely located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic head","B":"Pancreatic body","C":"Pancreatic tail","D":"Gallbladder fundus","E":"Proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tumors of the pancreatic head obstruct the distal common bile duct and cause obstructive jaundice with the Courvoisier sign, a palpable nontender gallbladder. Body and tail tumors present late with pain and weight loss. A jejunal mass does not obstruct the distal common bile duct. A gallbladder fundus cancer forms a firm, thickened wall rather than a distended nontender gallbladder. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, location dependent presentation, lines 3877–3884).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes a double duct sign on imaging in pancreatic adenocarcinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists endoscopic ultrasound with tissue sampling for diagnosis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — clinical features of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A jejunal mass does not obstruct the distal common bile duct.","B":"Body tumors present late with pain and weight loss.","C":"Tail tumors present late with pain and spread.","D":"A gallbladder fundus cancer forms a firm thickened wall rather than a distended nontender gallbladder."}},{"id":"sdl19_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman developed diabetes a year ago and now has back pain, weight loss, and recurrent superficial vein clots. CT shows a mass in her pancreatic body. Which serum marker is most useful to track her response to treatment?","choices":{"A":"Cancer antigen 125 level","B":"Carbohydrate antigen 19 9","C":"Prostate specific antigen","D":"Neuron specific enolase","E":"Serum alpha fetoprotein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"CA 19 9 and CEA are often raised in pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma. They lack the sensitivity and specificity for screening but are useful to track response to treatment and detect recurrence. CA 125, PSA, neuron specific enolase, and alpha fetoprotein, an HCC marker, are not the pancreatic adenocarcinoma markers the lecture names. Serum alpha-fetoprotein is raised in advanced hepatocellular carcinoma and is not a pancreatic adenocarcinoma marker. First Aid lists neuron-specific enolase as a neuroendocrine cell marker, whereas CA 19-9 is the tumor marker for pancreatic adenocarcinoma. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, tumor markers, lines 3889–3893).","boardPrep":"Step 1: new onset diabetes appears in up to 50 percent of patients up to 2 years before diagnosis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists metastasis to the liver as a complication (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 16 Obj 4 — etiologies, clinical features, and complications of large bile duct obstructions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CA 125 is not the pancreatic adenocarcinoma markers the lecture names.","C":"CA 125, PSA, neuron specific enolase, and alpha fetoprotein, an HCC marker, are not the pancreatic adenocarcinoma markers the lecture names.","E":"Serum alpha-fetoprotein is raised in advanced hepatocellular carcinoma and is not a pancreatic adenocarcinoma marker.","D":"First Aid lists neuron-specific enolase as a neuroendocrine cell marker, whereas CA 19-9 is the tumor marker for pancreatic adenocarcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl19_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma, and several relatives have had melanoma and atypical moles. Which germline gene is most likely mutated in his family?","choices":{"A":"BRCA2 gene","B":"PALB2 gene","C":"PRSS1 gene","D":"SERPINA1 gene","E":"CDKN2A gene"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Germline CDKN2A mutation causes FAMMM syndrome, with familial atypical moles, melanoma, and pancreatic cancer. BRCA2, PALB2, and PRSS1 are other pancreatic cancer genes without this melanoma pattern, and SERPINA1 causes AAT deficiency. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 4, germline predisposition, lines 3905–3907).","boardPrep":"Step 1: about 10 percent of patients carry germline mutations in cancer genes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists HNPCC, neurofibromatosis type 1, and hereditary breast and ovarian cancer syndrome as inherited risks (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, pancreatic adenocarcinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has episodes of confusion during fasting that resolve when she eats. During an episode her glucose is 40 mg/dL (reference 70–100 mg/dL), with high insulin and C peptide. Which histology is most likely in her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian type stroma","B":"Cords with amyloid","C":"Glycogen rich clear cells","D":"Signet ring cells","E":"Desmoplastic glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Fasting hypoglycemia relieved by food with high insulin and C peptide is the Whipple triad of insulinoma, which shows monotonous cords of well differentiated beta cells with amylin derived amyloid. Ovarian type stroma marks mucinous cystic neoplasms, glycogen rich clear cells mark serous cystadenoma, desmoplastic glands mark ductal adenocarcinoma, and signet ring cells mark diffuse gastric cancer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, insulinomas, lines 3911–3925); Robbins Ch. 19 (ductal adenocarcinoma desmoplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (diffuse gastric carcinoma signet ring cells).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp defines the Whipple triad as hypoglycemic symptoms, hypoglycemia during the episode, and relief with glucose (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, insulinoma). COMLEX: congenital hyperinsulinism occurs in infants of diabetic mothers and Beckwith Wiedemann syndrome (lecture SDL 19).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl19_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with MEN1 has severe, refractory ulcers in his duodenum and jejunum and chronic diarrhea. Where are his gastrinomas most likely found?","choices":{"A":"Single pancreatic tail mass","B":"Single pancreatic head mass","C":"Multiple duodenal tumors","D":"Gastric antral polyps","E":"Primary hepatic tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"MEN1 associated gastrinomas are frequently multifocal and located in the duodenum, whereas sporadic gastrinomas are typically single. A single pancreatic mass, in the tail or the head, fits that sporadic pattern. Gastrinomas arise in the pancreas or the duodenal gastrinoma triangle, not as antral polyps and not as primary liver tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, gastrinomas, lines 3926–3938).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea in Zollinger Ellison syndrome comes from acid inactivation of pancreatic enzymes (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists refractory peptic ulcers, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss in gastrinoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, gastrinoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation of gastric acid secretion by gastrin","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A single pancreatic tail mass is one tumor.","B":"A single pancreatic head mass is one tumor.","D":"Gastric antral polyps lie outside the pancreas and duodenal gastrinoma triangle.","E":"A primary hepatic tumor lies outside the pancreas and duodenum."}},{"id":"sdl19_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea of 4 liters a day, potassium of 2.6 mEq/L (reference 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and achlorhydria. CT shows a large mass in his pancreatic tail. Which mechanism explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP secretion","B":"Excess gastrin secretion","C":"Excess serotonin secretion","D":"Excess glucagon secretion","E":"Excess calcitonin secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A VIPoma, typically a large solitary tail tumor, causes WDHA or Verner Morrison syndrome: watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria. Gastrin excess raises acid, the opposite of achlorhydria. A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP; serotonin is not its product. Excess glucagon (glucagonoma) gives mild diabetes, migrating rash, anemia, and glossitis, not watery diarrhea with hypokalemia and achlorhydria. A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP, not calcitonin. Gastrinoma causes acid hypersecretion with severe peptic ulceration in 90% to 95% of patients, the opposite of achlorhydria. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, VIPomas, lines 3948–3957).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bootcamp describes VIP driving bicarbonate and chloride secretion by intestinal epithelial cells (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists weight loss in VIPoma (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, VIPoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Serotonin is not its product.","D":"Excess glucagon (glucagonoma) gives mild diabetes, migrating rash, anemia, and glossitis, not watery diarrhea with hypokalemia and achlorhydria.","E":"A VIPoma, a large solitary pancreatic tail tumor, causes WDHA syndrome (watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, achlorhydria) through VIP, not calcitonin.","B":"Gastrinoma causes acid hypersecretion with severe peptic ulceration in 90% to 95% of patients, the opposite of achlorhydria."}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the presentation and pathogenesis of congenital pancreatic anomalies (pancreas divisum, annular pancreas, and ectopic pancreas)","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had four attacks of acute pancreatitis without alcohol use, gallstones, or hypertriglyceridemia. At ERCP, contrast injected through the major papilla fills the bile duct and a short duct confined to the lower pancreatic head, but the ducts of the body and tail do not fill. Cannulation of which opening would most likely fill the duct draining her body and tail?","choices":{"A":"Cystic duct orifice","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Common hepatic duct","D":"Hepatopancreatic ampulla","E":"Accessory hepatic duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Filling of a short lower head duct through the major papilla with no filling of the body and tail is the ERCP picture of pancreas divisum: the dorsal and ventral duct systems never fused, so the dorsal bud, which forms the body and tail, drains through the duct of Santorini into the minor papilla. The cystic duct orifice connects the gallbladder to the bile duct and carries no pancreatic juice. The common hepatic duct drains the liver above the cystic duct junction. The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the major papilla already injected, which drains the ventral duct and bile duct. An accessory hepatic duct is a biliary variant that drains liver rather than pancreas. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 19 section 1 and SDL 11 section 3; Robbins Ch. 19.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In pancreas divisum the ventral duct of Wirsung drains a small part of the head through the major papilla, and the bulk of the gland drains through the duct of Santorini and the minor papilla (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: About 5% of people with divisum develop recurrent or chronic pancreatitis, often with a coexisting genetic risk.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 5 — common congenital anomalies of the pancreas, like annular pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cystic duct orifice connects the gallbladder","C":"The common hepatic duct drains the liver","D":"The hepatopancreatic ampulla is the major papilla","E":"An accessory hepatic duct is a biliary"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man with Crohn disease develops acute epigastric pain radiating to his back a few weeks after starting a new maintenance drug. He does not drink and has no gallstones. Which drug most likely caused his pancreatitis?","choices":{"A":"Azathioprine","B":"Amoxicillin","C":"Metformin","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Levothyroxine"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The lecture lists azathioprine, statins, and GLP 1 agonists among drug causes of acute pancreatitis, beside gallstones, alcohol, hypertriglyceridemia, hypercalcemia, trauma, ERCP, infections, and genetic mutations. Amoxicillin, metformin, acetaminophen, and levothyroxine are not among the drug causes it lists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 2, etiology, lines 3763–3769).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mumps and coxsackievirus are infectious causes of acute pancreatitis (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists post ERCP injury among traumatic or iatrogenic causes (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, acute pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — mechanisms that prevent pancreatic autodigestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Differentiate nonneoplastic cysts (pseudocysts) from cystic neoplasms (serous, mucinous, and intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms) by their histology, underlying cause, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has an incidental 4 cm cystic mass in the pancreatic tail. Resection shows many small cysts lined by glycogen-rich cuboidal cells and filled with thin, clear, straw-colored fluid. Which statement about this lesion is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Up to a third contain invasive cancer","B":"It arises within the main pancreatic duct","C":"It resolves as inflammation subsides","D":"It almost never becomes malignant","E":"It often secretes excess insulin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Small cysts lined by glycogen-rich cuboidal cells with thin, clear fluid in the tail of an older woman are a serous cystadenoma, a benign VHL-associated neoplasm with virtually no risk of malignant change, for which resection is curative when mass effect causes symptoms. Up to a third harboring invasive cancer describes mucinous cystic neoplasms, which hold thick mucin and have ovarian-type stroma. Arising within the main pancreatic duct is the feature of intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms, which make mucin. Resolving as inflammation subsides fits a pseudocyst, which has no epithelial lining. Secreting excess insulin is a feature of insulinomas, which are solid islet cell tumors. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 19 section 3; Robbins Ch. 19.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serous cystadenomas are microcystic, glycogen-rich, VHL-associated, and benign, while mucinous cystic neoplasms and IPMNs are precancerous mucin-producing lesions (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: Thin clear cyst fluid favors a serous lesion, while thick mucin points to a neoplasm that needs resection.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Up to a third harboring invasive cancer","B":"Arising within the main pancreatic duct is","C":"Resolving as inflammation subsides fits a pseudocyst,","E":"Secreting excess insulin is a feature of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe location-dependent clinical presentation (Courvoisier and Trousseau sign), markers (CA19-9), and mutations of pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man had painless jaundice, dark urine, clay colored stools, and a palpable nontender gallbladder. He underwent resection of the responsible pancreatic tumor, and his CA19-9 fell from 1,100 U/mL before surgery to 30 U/mL (reference range: <37 U/mL) afterward. How is this marker best used in his further care?","choices":{"A":"Tracking recurrence after treatment","B":"Screening his siblings for cancer","C":"Proving the tumor's tissue type","D":"Separating cancer from pancreatitis","E":"Finding early precursor lesions"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Painless jaundice with a palpable nontender gallbladder, the Courvoisier sign, points to a pancreatic head adenocarcinoma, and CA19-9 and CEA lack the sensitivity and specificity for screening but are useful for tracking a patient's response to therapy and detecting recurrence. Screening his siblings with CA19-9 is not useful, because the marker lacks the sensitivity and specificity needed for screening. Proving the tumor's tissue type requires a biopsy, because a serum marker lacks the needed specificity. Separating cancer from pancreatitis is unreliable with this marker for the same reason of low specificity. Finding early precursor lesions such as PanIN is beyond the marker's sensitivity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 19 section 4; Robbins Ch. 19.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic head adenocarcinoma causes obstructive jaundice and the Courvoisier sign, body and tail tumors present late with pain and weight loss, and Trousseau sign reflects procoagulant mucins (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: Follow CA19-9 after resection or chemotherapy to monitor for recurrence, not to screen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 3 — organization of the exocrine pancreas","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Screening his siblings with CA19-9 is not","C":"Proving the tumor's tissue type requires a","D":"Separating cancer from pancreatitis is unreliable with","E":"Finding early precursor lesions such as PanIN"}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the morphology, associated conditions, serum markers, and clinical presentations of insulinomas, gastrinomas, glucagonomas, and VIPomas.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old postmenopausal woman has mild diabetes, glossitis, anemia, a deep vein thrombosis, and a painful, migrating, blistering red rash. Which cells most likely gave rise to her tumor?","choices":{"A":"Islet beta cells","B":"Islet delta cells","C":"Islet alpha cells","D":"Duodenal G cells","E":"Islet PP cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mild diabetes, a migrating blistering rash, anemia, glossitis, and DVT in a postmenopausal woman fit a glucagonoma, an alpha cell tumor with extremely high plasma glucagon. Beta cells form insulinomas and G cells form gastrinomas. Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a delta cell tumor. Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a PP cell tumor. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 19, section 5, glucagonomas, lines 3939–3947).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucagonomas show monotonous cells with salt and pepper chromatin, and a high proportion are malignant (lecture SDL 19). COMLEX: Bootcamp describes glucagonomas as arising from alpha cells with autonomous glucagon release (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, glucagonoma).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 13 Obj 5 — major islet cell types and their hormone products","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mild diabetes, necrolytic migratory erythema, anemia, glossitis, and DVT describe a glucagonoma, which the lecture calls an alpha cell tumor, not a delta cell tumor.","D":"Beta cells form insulinomas and G cells form gastrinomas.","E":"Islet PP cells are not the alpha-cell source of a glucagonoma."}},{"id":"trial_sdl19_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism), diagnostic criteria (Ranson criteria concept), morphology, serum markers, and complications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with heavy alcohol use has had 5 days of constant epigastric pain radiating to his back with vomiting before coming to the emergency department. Serum amylase is 95 U/L (reference range: 30–110 U/L), and serum lipase is 980 U/L (reference range: 10–140 U/L). CT shows an edematous pancreas with peripancreatic fat stranding. Which explanation best accounts for the normal amylase?","choices":{"A":"Chronic acinar loss stops enzyme release","B":"Lipase is made outside the pancreas","C":"Amylase falls back sooner than lipase","D":"Alcohol directly inhibits amylase","E":"Amylase is secreted as a zymogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both enzymes rise in acute pancreatitis, but amylase rises quickly and returns to baseline within 3 to 5 days, while lipase stays elevated for 8 to 14 days, so a patient seen 5 days into an attack can have a normal amylase with a high lipase. Chronic acinar loss can blunt enzyme release in chronic pancreatitis, but lipase would then also be low, and his CT shows acute edema. Lipase is not mainly made outside the pancreas; acinar cells secrete it in active form along with amylase. Direct inhibition of amylase by alcohol is not the explanation, since the discordance reflects different times to normalize. Amylase is not secreted as a zymogen, because like lipase it leaves acinar cells already active. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 19 section 2; Robbins Ch. 19.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Serum lipase rises within 4 to 12 hours and stays up for 8 to 14 days, whereas amylase normalizes in 3 to 5 days, making lipase the more sensitive and specific marker (Robbins Ch. 19). COMLEX: When a patient presents days after the onset of pancreatic-type pain, rely on lipase and imaging rather than amylase.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 20 Obj 1 — clinical use of pancrelipase in exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chronic acinar loss can blunt enzyme release","B":"Lipase is not mainly made outside the","D":"Direct inhibition of amylase by alcohol is","E":"Amylase is not secreted as a zymogen,"}}]},{"sdlNumber":20,"title":"SDL 20 — Pharmacology of bile acid agents and pancreatic enzyme supplements","questions":[{"id":"sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Below what level of normal enzyme secretion does exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occur?","choices":{"A":"Below 10 percent","B":"Below 50 percent","C":"Below 30 percent","D":"Below 75 percent","E":"Below 90 percent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when pancreatic enzyme secretion falls below 10 percent of normal. It is most often caused by cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis, or surgical resection. The other values are not that figure. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, etiology and pathophysiology of EPI, lines 3960–3963).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid notes pancreatic insufficiency typically appears at less than 10 percent pancreatic function (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists decreased fecal elastase 1 among chronic pancreatitis diagnostics (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Below 50 percent is too high a threshold.","C":"Below 30 percent is too high a threshold.","D":"Below 75 percent is far too high.","E":"Below 90 percent is far too high."}},{"id":"sdl20_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the source of the enzyme extracts used in pancrelipase?","choices":{"A":"Recombinant yeast","B":"Porcine pancreas","C":"Bovine stomach","D":"Fungal fermentation","E":"Human donor plasma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Replacement therapy uses animal derived porcine pancreatic extracts containing lipase, amylase, and proteases. Pancrelipase gives about 12 times the lipolytic activity of older pancreatin extracts. Recombinant yeast, bovine stomach, fungal fermentation, and human donor plasma are not the source the lecture gives. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, therapeutic rationale, lines 3967–3972).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancrelipase gives more than 4 times the proteolytic activity per weight of pancreatin (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists steatorrhea and fat soluble vitamin deficiency in chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"When should pancrelipase be taken?","choices":{"A":"Once daily at bedtime","B":"Before breakfast on waking","C":"With each meal and snack","D":"Twice daily between meals","E":"Weekly with a fatty meal"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pancrelipase is given orally with every meal and snack, with the dose individualized by body weight, severity of insufficiency, and dietary fat intake. Once daily, on waking, between meals, or weekly dosing would leave most meals without enzyme. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, clinical uses and dosing, lines 3984–3989).","boardPrep":"Step 1: therapy starts at 60,000 to 90,000 USP units of lipase per meal (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cystic fibrosis among causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Once daily would leave most meals without enzyme.","B":"On waking would leave most meals without enzyme.","D":"Twice daily between meals would leave most meals without enzyme.","E":"Weekly dosing would leave most meals without enzyme."}},{"id":"sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Which receptor does obeticholic acid activate?","choices":{"A":"Farnesoid X receptor","B":"Pregnane X receptor","C":"Liver X receptor alpha","D":"PPAR gamma receptor","E":"Vitamin D receptor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is a synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative and a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, or FXR, which downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism, lines 4029–4035).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid is given at 5 to 10 mg per day for PBC (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis (First Aid 2025 GI primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pregnane X receptor is not part of that answer.","C":"Liver X receptor alpha is not part of that answer.","D":"Obeticholic acid is a potent agonist of the nuclear farnesoid X receptor, not PPAR gamma.","E":"Vitamin D receptor is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl20_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does long-term oral ursodiol come to make up a large part of the circulating bile acid pool?","choices":{"A":"It is not absorbed from the gut","B":"It is stored in adipose tissue","C":"It is converted into cholic acid","D":"It is excreted unchanged in urine","E":"It recirculates enterohepatically"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol is absorbed, conjugated in the liver with glycine or taurine, and secreted into bile, and conjugated ursodiol then undergoes extensive enterohepatic recirculation, so it accumulates in the bile acid pool. If ursodiol were not absorbed from the gut, it could not enter the liver and bile at all. Ursodiol is a bile acid handled by the liver and intestine, not a drug stored in adipose tissue. Ursodiol is conjugated with glycine or taurine, not converted into cholic acid. Urinary excretion would remove the drug from the pool, whereas ursodiol is secreted in bile and recycled. Sources: Katzung ch 62, drugs that promote gallstone dissolution; Exam 1 pharmacology notes, SDL 20.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ursodiol is conjugated in the liver and recirculates enterohepatically, lowering biliary cholesterol secretion (Katzung ch 62). COMLEX: It dissolves small radiolucent cholesterol stones in patients who decline surgery.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"If ursodiol were not absorbed from the","B":"Ursodiol is a bile acid handled by","C":"Ursodiol is conjugated with glycine or taurine,","D":"Urinary excretion would remove the drug from"}},{"id":"sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Which adverse effect of obeticholic acid can lead to stopping treatment?","choices":{"A":"Severe hepatotoxicity","B":"Uric acid kidney stones","C":"Oropharyngeal mucositis","D":"Dose dependent pruritus","E":"Fibrosing colonopathy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Obeticholic acid causes severe, dose dependent pruritus in up to 25 percent of patients, leading to stopping in up to 10 percent. Uric acid stones, mucositis, and colonopathy are pancrelipase effects, and hepatotoxicity is the problem the lecture ties to the older agent chenodeoxycholate. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid adverse effects, lines 4043–4045; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028).","boardPrep":"Step 1: obeticholic acid has been studied in MASLD, with improved liver fibrosis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholestyramine with ursodeoxycholic acid in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man with alcohol related chronic pancreatitis takes non enteric coated pancrelipase tablets with meals, but his greasy stools persist. Which drug should be added to improve his response?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine resin","B":"Loperamide with meals","C":"Metronidazole course","D":"Ondansetron before meals","E":"Proton pump inhibitor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Gastric acid rapidly and permanently inactivates pancrelipase, so non enteric coated tablets such as Viokace must be given with acid suppression, a PPI or H2 blocker. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, loperamide only slows transit, metronidazole is a nitroimidazole with potent activity against anaerobes, and ondansetron is a 5 HT3 antagonist antiemetic; none protects the enzymes from acid. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, pharmacodynamics and formulations, lines 3973–3983); Katzung Ch 50, metronidazole, lines 22–27; Katzung Ch 62, serotonin 5 HT3 antagonists, lines 1802–1806.","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteric coated capsules dissolve in alkaline duodenal pH, so they need no routine acid suppression (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists chronic alcohol use and tobacco use as causes of chronic pancreatitis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis has taken high dose pancrelipase for years. She now has crampy abdominal pain and distension, and imaging shows a long stricture of her ascending colon. Which complication best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Meconium plug ileus","B":"Ileocolic intussusception","C":"Sigmoid colon volvulus","D":"Fibrosing colonopathy","E":"Toxic colonic dilation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High doses with high lipase activity were linked to fibrosing colonopathy, with colonic strictures, in patients with cystic fibrosis. Meconium ileus affects newborns, volvulus and intussusception cause acute obstruction without a long stricture, and toxic dilation follows severe colitis. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excessive pancrelipase doses can cause diarrhea and abdominal pain (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid lists cystic fibrosis among genetic risk factors for chronic pancreatitis (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl20_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man on high dose pancrelipase after a Whipple resection develops flank pain and hematuria. CT shows a renal stone that was not seen on plain film. Which property of his therapy best explains this?","choices":{"A":"High purine content","B":"High oxalate content","C":"High calcium content","D":"High cystine content","E":"High phosphate load"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic extracts contain high purine levels, so high doses can cause hyperuricosuria and renal uric acid stones, which are radiolucent, unlike radiopaque calcium stones such as calcium oxalate. Phosphate is not a constituent the lecture flags in the extracts, and cystine stones come from the inborn error cystinuria. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 1, adverse effects and precautions, lines 3990–3996); Robbins Ch 20, library/text/Robbins Patho/Ch.Robbins20.pdf.txt, lines 4907–4908 (cystinuria) and 4953 (radiolucent uric acid stones).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capsules must be swallowed whole, since chewing them can cause oropharyngeal mucositis (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: First Aid notes chronic pancreatitis can cause diabetes mellitus from pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid 2025 GI chronic pancreatitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"High oxalate content is not described.","C":"High calcium content is not described."}},{"id":"sdl20_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with symptomatic gallstones refuses surgery. Ultrasound shows several 4 mm stones, and plain film shows no calcified stones. Which mechanism explains how the drug chosen for her will dissolve them?","choices":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the gut","B":"Lower biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Activating hepatic FXR signaling","D":"Raising bilirubin conjugation rate","E":"Stimulating gallbladder contraction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol dissolves small, radiolucent, noncalcified cholesterol stones by suppressing hepatic cholesterol secretion and lowering the cholesterol content of bile, with dissolution in up to 50 percent over 12 to 24 months. Sequestrants bind bile acids, and FXR agonism is obeticholic acid. Raising bilirubin conjugation and stimulating gallbladder contraction are not ursodiol actions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol mechanism and uses, lines 4008–4018).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol also prevents gallstones in obese patients during rapid weight loss (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists cholesterol supersaturation of bile among causes of cholelithiasis (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Sequestrants bind bile acids, and FXR agonism is obeticholic acid."}},{"id":"sdl20_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman with antimitochondrial antibodies has had a high alkaline phosphatase for a year despite full dose ursodiol. Which drug should be added?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Chenodeoxycholate","C":"Obeticholic acid","D":"Oral prednisone","E":"Oral pancrelipase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obeticholic acid is approved for PBC at 5 to 10 mg per day with ursodiol when the response to ursodiol alone is inadequate, or alone if ursodiol is not tolerated. Chenodeoxycholate is hepatotoxic, and pancrelipase treats exocrine insufficiency. Cholestyramine, a bile acid binding resin, can ease cholestatic pruritus but does not treat the disease, and prednisone is not among the lecture's PBC drugs. Cholestyramine is a bile acid-binding resin that removes bile acids from the gut, not a second-line PBC therapy for persistent alkaline phosphatase. Chenodeoxycholate itself was associated with hepatotoxicity. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid clinical uses, lines 4037–4042; ursodiol adverse effects, lines 4027–4028); Katzung Ch. 35 (bile acid binding resins relieve pruritus in cholestasis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day is first line for early PBC and delays transplant (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists liver transplant in PBC management (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and regulation of bile acid synthesis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cholestyramine is a bile acid-binding resin that removes bile acids from the gut, not a second-line PBC therapy for persistent alkaline phosphatase.","B":"Chenodeoxycholate itself was associated with hepatotoxicity."}},{"id":"sdl20_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with primary biliary cholangitis stopped his first-line bile acid therapy after 3 months because he could not tolerate it. His serum alkaline phosphatase is 410 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L) and total bilirubin is 1.1 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL). He starts once-daily oral monotherapy with a synthetic derivative of chenodeoxycholic acid. Which hepatic effect of this drug most directly protects his hepatocytes and cholangiocytes?","choices":{"A":"Reduced bile acid synthesis","B":"Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion","C":"Binding of bile acids in the gut","D":"Blocked ileal bile acid uptake","E":"Inhibited hepatic cholesterol synthesis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A synthetic chenodeoxycholate derivative used when first-line therapy for primary biliary cholangitis is not tolerated is obeticholic acid. It is a potent FXR agonist, and FXR activation downregulates hepatic bile acid synthesis, lowering the load of toxic endogenous bile acids. Reduced biliary cholesterol secretion is the action of ursodiol. Binding bile acids in the gut describes bile acid sequestrants. Blocking ileal uptake acts in the gut, not on a hepatic nuclear receptor. Inhibiting hepatic cholesterol synthesis is the action of statins. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, obeticholic acid mechanism and clinical uses, lines 4029–4046; ursodiol mechanism, lines 4008–4013); First Aid 2025 GI (bile, rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase catalyzes the rate-limiting step of bile acid synthesis (First Aid 2025 GI, bile). COMLEX: First Aid gives ursodiol as the treatment of primary biliary cholangitis, the setting for add-on or substitute bile acid agents (First Aid 2025 GI, primary biliary cholangitis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile acid synthesis and the composition of bile","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy with cystic fibrosis has been chewing his enteric coated pancrelipase capsules because they are hard to swallow. He now has painful erosions on his tongue and buccal mucosa, without fever, vesicles, or white plaques. Which mechanism best explains the lesions?","choices":{"A":"Allergy to porcine pancreatic protein","B":"Oral thrush from his inhaled drugs","C":"Vitamin B12 loss from malabsorption","D":"Uric acid crystals from purine load","E":"Active enzymes digesting the mucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pancrelipase capsules must be swallowed whole, because chewing them releases active lipase, proteases, and amylase in the mouth, where the enzymes digest the mucosa and cause oropharyngeal mucositis. Allergy to porcine pancreatic protein is not the listed reaction, and the lesions followed chewing rather than starting the drug. Oral thrush from inhaled drugs would give white plaques, which he does not have. Vitamin B12 loss from malabsorption causes glossitis that develops slowly and is not tied to chewing capsules. Uric acid from the purine load causes hyperuricosuria and kidney stones, not mouth lesions. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 20 section 1; Katzung Ch. 62.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancrelipase adverse effects are oropharyngeal mucositis if chewed, diarrhea and pain with excess dose, hyperuricosuria from purines, and fibrosing colonopathy with very high doses in cystic fibrosis (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Teach patients to swallow capsules whole with every meal and snack.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — differentiate acute and chronic pancreatitis based on etiology (cystic fibrosis, alcoholism)","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Allergy to porcine pancreatic protein is not","B":"Oral thrush from inhaled drugs would give","C":"Vitamin B12 loss from malabsorption causes glossitis","D":"Uric acid from the purine load causes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 is starting a very low calorie diet for rapid weight loss before bariatric surgery. Her physician wants to lower her risk of forming gallstones during this period. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Obeticholic acid","C":"Orlistat","D":"Gemfibrozil","E":"Ursodiol"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ursodiol prevents gallstone formation in obese patients undergoing rapid weight loss by lowering the cholesterol content of bile. Cholestyramine binds bile acids, fibrates raise gallstone risk, orlistat is a GI lipase inhibitor that reduces lipid absorption for weight loss rather than a stone preventive, and obeticholic acid is a second line PBC drug. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes (SDL 20, section 2, ursodiol clinical uses, lines 4008–4020); Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis; Katzung Ch 16, anti obesity drugs, lines 1781–1785.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ursodiol is first line for early PBC at 13 to 15 mg/kg/day (lecture SDL 20). COMLEX: Bootcamp lists obesity, female sex, fibrates, and rapid weight loss as gallstone risk factors (Bootcamp Gastroenterology, cholelithiasis).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 1 — epidemiological risk factors, associated conditions, and morphology of cholesterol and pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Cholestyramine binds bile acids, fibrates raise gallstone risk, orlistat is a GI lipase inhibitor that reduces lipid absorption for weight loss rather than a stone preventive, and obeticholic acid is a second line PBC drug."}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 59-year-old man with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency from chronic pancreatitis has steatorrhea and weight loss. He had recurrent Clostridioides difficile infection while taking a proton pump inhibitor, and his gastroenterologist wants to avoid acid suppression. Which pancrelipase formulation best fits?","choices":{"A":"Non enteric coated tablets with water","B":"Delayed release coated capsules","C":"Older pancreatin extract tablets","D":"Chewable uncoated enzyme tablets","E":"Uncoated powder taken at bedtime"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancrelipase is rapidly and permanently inactivated by gastric acid, and enteric coated, delayed release capsules have an acid-resistant coat that dissolves at the alkaline pH of the duodenum, so they do not need routine acid suppression. Non enteric coated tablets must be given with a proton pump inhibitor or H2 antagonist to protect the enzymes from gastric acid. Older pancreatin extract has much less lipolytic activity per weight and is no longer in common use. Chewable uncoated enzyme tablets would be destroyed by acid and could cause oropharyngeal mucositis. Uncoated powder taken at bedtime would be inactivated by acid and would miss the meals it must accompany. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 20 section 1; Katzung Ch. 62.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non enteric coated pancrelipase needs a PPI or H2 blocker, while enteric coated delayed release capsules dissolve in the duodenum and do not (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: PPIs raise the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection, so formulation choice can spare patients acid suppression.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — mechanism and regulation of pancreatic bicarbonate rich secretion, including the role of CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Non enteric coated tablets must be given","C":"Older pancreatin extract has much less lipolytic","D":"Chewable uncoated enzyme tablets would be destroyed","E":"Uncoated powder taken at bedtime would be"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 56-year-old woman has fatigue, itching, ALP of 390 U/L (reference range: 40–120 U/L), and a positive antimitochondrial antibody. She starts the standard first-line oral bile acid therapy for her condition, and her ALP falls by half over 6 months. Which mechanism best explains how this drug protects her hepatocytes?","choices":{"A":"Activating the farnesoid X receptor","B":"Binding bile acids in the gut lumen","C":"Suppressing T cells that attack ducts","D":"Replacing hydrophobic bile acids","E":"Blocking ileal bile acid reabsorption"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Itching, high ALP, and antimitochondrial antibody indicate primary biliary cholangitis, for which ursodiol is first-line therapy. Ursodiol is a hydrophilic bile acid that, with long-term use, makes up 30% to 50% of the pool, replacing toxic endogenous hydrophobic bile acids and stabilizing canalicular membranes. Activating the farnesoid X receptor is the action of obeticholic acid, the add-on for an inadequate response to first-line therapy. Binding bile acids in the gut lumen is how resins such as cholestyramine work. Suppressing T cells is not how this drug works, even though it has mild anti-inflammatory effects. Blocking ileal bile acid reabsorption would shrink the pool, whereas this drug undergoes extensive enterohepatic recirculation. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 20 section 2; Katzung Ch. 62.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ursodiol is a hydrophilic bile acid that lowers biliary cholesterol and displaces toxic hydrophobic bile acids, while obeticholic acid is an FXR agonist (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Ursodiol is first-line for PBC, and obeticholic acid is added when the ALP response is inadequate.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — formation, composition, and digestive functions of bile, including bile acid synthesis and conjugation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Activating the farnesoid X receptor is the","B":"Binding bile acids in the gut lumen","C":"Suppressing T cells is not how this","E":"Blocking ileal bile acid reabsorption would shrink"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the therapeutic rationale and clinical use of pancrelipase in the treatment of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a distal pancreatectomy removing about half of his gland for a benign cystic neoplasm. Three months later he has formed stools and stable weight, and 72-hour fecal fat is 4 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). He asks whether he needs pancrelipase capsules with meals. Which reasoning best applies?","choices":{"A":"Needed after any pancreatic resection","B":"Needed to prevent new diabetes","C":"Not needed above 10% of normal output","D":"Needed because the tail makes most lipase","E":"Not needed if he avoids all dietary fat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency appears only when enzyme secretion falls below about 10% of normal, so losing half of the gland leaves enough output, as his normal stools, weight, and fecal fat show, and replacement is reserved for steatorrhea, azotorrhea, and weight loss from true insufficiency. Pancrelipase after any pancreatic resection is not required, because it treats insufficiency, which he does not have. Preventing new diabetes is not a role of pancrelipase, which replaces exocrine enzymes and has no effect on islet hormones. The tail making most lipase is not true, since acinar tissue fills the whole gland while the tail is where islets are densest. Avoiding all dietary fat is not the basis for skipping enzymes, since dosing is matched to dietary fat when insufficiency exists. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 20 section 1; Katzung Ch. 62.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency occurs when enzyme secretion falls below 10% of normal, most often from cystic fibrosis, chronic pancreatitis, or resection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Start pancrelipase for steatorrhea, azotorrhea, fat-soluble vitamin loss, or weight loss, dosed to body weight and dietary fat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion, activation, and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancrelipase after any pancreatic resection is not","B":"Preventing new diabetes is not a role","D":"The tail making most lipase is not","E":"Avoiding all dietary fat is not the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl20_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the pharmacodynamics, mechanisms of action, and clinical uses of bile acid agents in the management of gastrointestinal and hepatobiliary disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with severe heart failure has recurrent biliary colic and is not a surgical candidate. Ultrasound shows several 6 mm gallstones in a functioning gallbladder, and a plain abdominal radiograph shows the same stones as dense opacities. Which feature of his stones makes oral ursodiol unlikely to dissolve them?","choices":{"A":"Size of 6 mm or smaller","B":"Calcification on radiograph","C":"Location in the gallbladder","D":"Presence of several stones","E":"A functioning gallbladder"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ursodiol lowers biliary cholesterol and dissolves only small, radiolucent, noncalcified cholesterol stones, so stones dense enough to show on a plain radiograph contain calcium salts that it cannot dissolve. A size of 6 mm is within the small range, under 5 to 10 mm, in which ursodiol can work. Location in the gallbladder is where ursodiol-enriched bile acts on cholesterol stones. The presence of several stones does not prevent dissolution if they are small cholesterol stones. A functioning gallbladder does not prevent dissolution and fills with the ursodiol-enriched bile. Sources: Exam 1 lecture notes, SDL 20 section 2; Katzung Ch. 62.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ursodiol dissolves small radiolucent cholesterol stones over 12 to 24 months, prevents stones during rapid weight loss, and is first-line for PBC (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Calcified stones will not dissolve with ursodiol, so nonsurgical patients with them need other options.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 18 Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of the formation and composition of cholesterol and black pigment gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A size of 6 mm is within","C":"Location in the gallbladder is where ursodiol-enriched","D":"The presence of several stones does not","E":"A functioning gallbladder does not prevent dissolution"}}]}]},{"examNumber":2,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":21,"title":"SDL 21 — Embryology of the Small Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Viewed from the front, how far does the primary intestinal loop normally rotate in total?","choices":{"A":"About 90 degrees clockwise","B":"About 180 degrees clockwise","C":"About 90 degrees counterclockwise","D":"About 270 degrees counterclockwise","E":"About 360 degrees counterclockwise"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The loop rotates about 270 degrees counterclockwise: about 90 degrees during herniation and the remaining 180 degrees during return to the abdomen. Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase, and clockwise rotation is abnormal. A full 360 degrees overshoots that total. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the stomach rotates 90 degrees clockwise during the 4th to 6th weeks, so the left vagus comes to lie anteriorly (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the jejunum and ileum coil during rotation, whereas the large intestine lengthens without coiling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"A full 360 degrees overshoots the normal total.","A":"Langman calls a 90-degree clockwise turn reversed rotation, which places the transverse colon behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery.","C":"Ninety degrees counterclockwise is only the herniation phase.","B":"Clockwise rotation is abnormal."}},{"id":"sdl21_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms the axis of rotation of the primary intestinal loop?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Vitelline duct and yolk stalk","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Left umbilical vein remnant"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The superior mesenteric artery forms the axis about which the primary intestinal loop rotates. The celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery supply the foregut and hindgut, and the vitelline duct connects the loop apex to the yolk sac. The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop. The celiac artery supplies the foregut and is not the axis of the primary intestinal loop. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, whereas the midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery. Moore states the returning midgut rotates 270 degrees around the axis of the SMA, while the omphaloenteric duct only attaches it to the umbilical vesicle. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cephalic limb of the primary loop forms the distal duodenum, jejunum, and part of the ileum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the dorsal mesentery twists around the origin of this artery as the caudal limb moves to the right (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The umbilical vein runs in the cord to the liver and is not the pivot of the midgut loop.","A":"The celiac artery supplies the foregut and is not the axis of the primary intestinal loop.","D":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the hindgut, whereas the midgut loop rotates around the superior mesenteric artery.","B":"Moore states the returning midgut rotates 270 degrees around the axis of the SMA, while the omphaloenteric duct only attaches it to the umbilical vesicle."}},{"id":"sdl21_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do the herniated intestinal loops begin to return to the abdominal cavity?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Sixth week","C":"Eighth week","D":"Sixteenth week","E":"Tenth week"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Herniated loops begin to return during the 10th week, aided by regression of the mesonephros, slower liver growth, and abdominal cavity expansion. Herniation begins in the 6th week, and the return is finished by about the 12th week. In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week. In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week. By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week. The 6th week is when the loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord, not when they begin to return. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Rotation of the midgut; Retraction of herniated loops; Gut rotation defects, Figs. 15.24–15.27, 15.33). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal jejunum returns first and settles on the left side (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud returns last and then descends into the right iliac fossa (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"In the fourth week the loops have not yet herniated; physiologic herniation does not begin until the 6th week.","C":"In the eighth week the loops are still herniated in the cord; retraction into the abdomen starts at the 10th week.","D":"By the sixteenth week the loops have long since returned, since retraction is complete by the 10th to 12th week.","B":"The 6th week is when the loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord, not when they begin to return."}},{"id":"sdl21_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Which covering surrounds the herniated viscera in an omphalocele?","choices":{"A":"Skin","B":"Dura","C":"Amnion","D":"Chorion","E":"Pleura"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In omphalocele, the viscera herniate through an enlarged umbilical ring and are covered by a membrane of amnion and peritoneum separated by Wharton's jelly, which gives the sac a smooth, spherical look on ultrasound. Skin covers an umbilical hernia, and gastroschisis has no covering membrane. Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly. Skin does not cover an omphalocele; Langman describes the herniated viscera as covered by amnion. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid describes the omphalocele sac as peritoneum plus amnion, light gray and shiny (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele occurs in about 2.5 per 10,000 births with about 25% mortality (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dura is a meningeal covering of the central nervous system and plays no part in the omphalocele sac, which is amnion and peritoneum with Wharton's jelly.","D":"Chorion is not part of the omphalocele sac.","E":"Pleura lines the thoracic cavity, whereas the omphalocele membrane.","A":"Skin does not cover an omphalocele."}},{"id":"sdl21_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"During which week do midgut loops normally enter the umbilical cord as physiologic herniation?","choices":{"A":"Third week","B":"Fourth week","C":"Twelfth week","D":"Sixth week","E":"Twentieth week"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rapid elongation of the loop and growth of the liver make the abdomen temporarily too small, so loops enter the extraembryonic cavity of the cord during the 6th week. They return beginning in the 10th week. The third week is too early; herniation into the cord is driven by midgut elongation and liver growth beginning in the 6th week. The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week. By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord. By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: coiling of the small intestinal loops and formation of the cecum occur while the gut is herniated (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: physiologic herniation persists until about the 10th week, so bowel in the cord base before then is normal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The third week is too early.","B":"The fourth week precedes physiologic herniation, which begins in the 6th week.","C":"By the twelfth week the loops have finished returning to the abdomen rather than entering the cord.","E":"By the twentieth week bowel in the cord is abnormal, because loops normally return by the 10th to 12th week."}},{"id":"sdl21_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the abdominal wall defect of gastroschisis usually located?","choices":{"A":"Through the enlarged umbilical ring itself","B":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the right","C":"Lateral to the umbilicus on the left side","D":"In the upper epigastric midline above it","E":"Just above the pubic symphysis in midline"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Gastroschisis occurs lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, from abnormal body wall closure around the connecting stalk. Herniation through the umbilical ring itself is omphalocele. A suprapubic defect suggests bladder exstrophy. Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left. Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect. Herniation through an enlarged umbilical ring is omphalocele, whereas gastroschisis protrudes through the body wall lateral to the umbilicus. Langman attributes a midline suprapubic defect to bladder exstrophy from failed body wall closure in the pelvic region, whereas gastroschisis lies right of the umbilicus. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Physiologic herniation; Body wall defects, Figs. 15.26, 15.31). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is a lateral fold closure defect, while bladder exstrophy is a caudal fold defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis is increasing in frequency, especially in babies of mothers under 20 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Gastroschisis is lateral to the umbilicus but usually on the right, not the left.","D":"Gastroschisis is a defect beside the umbilicus, not an upper epigastric midline defect.","A":"Herniation through an enlarged umbilical ring is omphalocele, whereas gastroschisis protrudes through the body wall lateral to the umbilicus.","E":"Langman attributes a midline suprapubic defect to bladder exstrophy from failed body wall closure in the pelvic region, whereas gastroschisis lies right of the umbilicus."}},{"id":"sdl21_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Meckel diverticulum results from persistence of which embryonic structure?","choices":{"A":"Urachal remnant","B":"Allantoic stalk","C":"Vitelline duct","D":"Cloacal membrane","E":"Umbilical vein"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A small persisting portion of the vitelline duct forms an outpocketing of the ileum, Meckel diverticulum. A persisting urachus comes from the allantois and drains urine. The cloacal membrane and umbilical vein are not related to the ileum. The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching. The cloacal membrane marks the caudal end of the hindgut and does not form an outpocketing of the ileum. Langman describes the urachus as the obliterated allantois linking the bladder apex to the umbilicus, and a patent lumen drains urine. Moore describes the ileal (Meckel) diverticulum as a remnant of the proximal embryonic omphaloenteric duct (yolk stalk), not the umbilical vein. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is the most common congenital anomaly of the GI tract (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: the diverticulum usually causes no symptoms in adults (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The allantois gives rise to the urachus and bladder region, not to an ileal outpouching.","D":"The cloacal membrane marks the caudal end of the hindgut and does not form an outpocketing of the ileum.","A":"Langman describes the urachus as the obliterated allantois linking the bladder apex to the umbilicus, and a patent lumen drains urine.","E":"Moore describes the ileal (Meckel) diverticulum as a remnant of the proximal embryonic omphaloenteric duct (yolk stalk), not the umbilical vein."}},{"id":"sdl21_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"Along which border of the ileum does Meckel diverticulum arise?","choices":{"A":"Antimesenteric border","B":"Mesenteric border","C":"Duodenojejunal border","D":"Retroperitoneal border","E":"Ileocecal valve border"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Meckel diverticulum lies on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve. It does not arise from the mesenteric border, the duodenojejunal flexure, or the ileocecal valve itself. The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one. Langman places Meckel diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, not the mesenteric border. Langman locates Meckel diverticulum on the ileum about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve, far from the duodenojejunal junction. Meckel diverticulum lies about 40 to 60 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve in Langman, not at the valve itself. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the rule of 2s lists 2 feet from the ileocecal valve and 2 inches long (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum is twice as likely in males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The ileum bearing a Meckel diverticulum is intraperitoneal on its mesentery; the diverticulum arises on the antimesenteric border, not a retroperitoneal one.","B":"Langman places Meckel diverticulum on the antimesenteric border of the ileum, not the mesenteric border.","C":"Langman locates Meckel diverticulum on the ileum about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve, far from the duodenojejunal junction.","E":"Meckel diverticulum lies about 40 to 60 cm proximal to the ileocecal valve in Langman, not at the valve itself."}},{"id":"sdl21_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":1,"stem":"What anomaly results when the omphalomesenteric duct stays patent along its entire length?","choices":{"A":"Vitelline cyst","B":"Urachal sinus tract","C":"Duplication cyst","D":"Omphalocele sac","E":"Vitelline fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A duct that stays patent throughout forms a vitelline, or umbilical, fistula connecting the ileal lumen with the umbilicus, so feces may drain at the umbilicus. A vitelline cyst forms when only the middle part persists between two fibrous cords. A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum. Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct. An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring, not a patent vitelline duct. A vitelline cyst forms when both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and only the middle persists, so it cannot drain feces to the umbilicus. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Vitelline duct abnormalities, Fig. 15.32). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: umbilical drainage of feces points to a patent omphalomesenteric duct, whereas urine drainage points to a patent urachus (Langman Ch. 16). COMLEX: the vitelline duct connects the apex of the primary loop with the yolk sac (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A urachal sinus is an allantoic remnant draining toward the bladder, not a patent omphalomesenteric duct reaching the ileum.","C":"Duplication cysts are abnormal proliferations of gut parenchyma, most often near the ileum, unrelated to a patent vitelline duct.","D":"An omphalocele sac is failed return of herniated loops through an enlarged umbilical ring.","A":"A vitelline cyst forms when both ends of the duct become fibrous cords and only the middle persists, so it cannot drain feces to the umbilicus."}},{"id":"sdl21_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":5,"stem":"In which intestinal segment do most atresias and stenoses occur?","choices":{"A":"Jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Appendix","D":"Duodenum","E":"Colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Most atresias and stenoses occur in the duodenum, the fewest occur in the colon, and the jejunum and ileum have equal numbers (about 1 in 1500 births). The appendix is not a usual atresia site. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: duodenal atresia is associated with Down syndrome and shows a double bubble on x-ray (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: atresias account for 95% of these defects and stenoses for only 5% (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mechanism most likely causes atresia of the upper duodenum, just distal to the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Lack of recanalization","B":"Vascular accident","C":"Midgut volvulus","D":"Amniotic fluid injury","E":"Persistent vitelline cord"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Upper duodenal atresias are probably due to failure of the solid duodenal lumen to recanalize. Distal to the duodenum, atresias have been attributed to vascular accidents, which can follow volvulus, malrotation, or abdominal wall defects. Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia. A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia. Langman attributes atresias from the distal duodenum caudally, not the upper duodenum, to vascular accidents that compromise blood flow. Langman attributes atresias from the distal duodenum caudally to vascular accidents that may follow volvulus, whereas upper duodenal atresias reflect a lack of recanalization. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intestinal atresia presents with bilious vomiting and distension within the first 1 to 2 days of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: the duodenal lumen is temporarily obliterated by epithelial proliferation before recanalizing (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Exposure of bowel to amniotic fluid injures bare loops in gastroschisis but does not cause upper duodenal atresia.","E":"A persistent vitelline cord causes obstruction by loops twisting around it, not upper duodenal atresia.","B":"A vascular accident accounts for atresias caudal to the upper duodenum, not atresia just distal to the stomach.","C":"Midgut volvulus is not the developmental mechanism of atresia of the upper duodenum."}},{"id":"sdl21_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Misexpression of which gene families supports a non-vascular, differentiation-based cause of intestinal atresia?","choices":{"A":"RET and GDNF family receptor genes","B":"HOX genes and FGF family genes","C":"SHH and patched receptor genes","D":"PAX and SOX transcription factor genes","E":"WNT and BMP signaling pathway genes"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Misexpression of HOX genes and of FGF family ligands and receptors disrupts epithelial-mesenchymal signaling and causes gut atresias, showing that faulty gut differentiation, not only vascular accidents, can produce these defects. RET mutations cause aganglionic megacolon. SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression. SOX2 and PAX genes specify regions and cell types (SOX2 the esophagus and stomach, PAX4/PAX6 pancreatic endocrine cells); gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression instead. WNT and BMP4 signals are named for somite and muscle patterning (together they activate MyoD), whereas gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression. RET mutations affect neural crest migration and cause aganglionic megacolon, not gut atresias. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Gut atresias and stenoses, Figs. 15.34–15.35). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: RET mutations impair neural crest migration and cause Hirschsprung disease (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: in 50% of atresias a region of bowel is lost, and in 20% a fibrous cord remains (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"SHH from endoderm induces HOX expression in mesoderm for craniocaudal patterning; the notes tie atresia specifically to HOX and FGF misexpression.","D":"SOX2 and PAX genes specify regions and cell types (SOX2 the esophagus and stomach, PAX4/PAX6 pancreatic endocrine cells); gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression instead.","E":"WNT and BMP4 signals are named for somite and muscle patterning (together they activate MyoD), whereas gut atresia is tied to HOX and FGF misexpression.","A":"RET mutations affect neural crest migration and cause aganglionic megacolon, not gut atresias."}},{"id":"sdl21_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which germ layer forms the epithelium lining the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Ectoderm","B":"Neural crest","C":"Somatic layer","D":"Paraxial layer","E":"Endoderm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The epithelium of the digestive system and the parenchyma of its derivatives originate in endoderm. Connective tissue, muscle, and peritoneal components come from mesoderm. Ectoderm forms only the caudal anal canal. Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm. Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm. Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal. Ectoderm contributes only the lower third of the anal canal, so it is not the source of the gut epithelium. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the caudal third of the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: gut differentiation depends on reciprocal epithelial-mesenchymal interactions (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Neural crest forms enteric ganglia, not the mucosal epithelium, which derives from gut tube endoderm.","C":"Somatic mesoderm forms the body wall; gut wall mesoderm is the splanchnic layer, and the epithelium itself is endoderm.","D":"Paraxial mesoderm forms somites, not the intestinal epithelium, which is endodermal.","A":"Ectoderm contributes only the lower third of the anal canal, so it is not the source of the gut epithelium."}},{"id":"sdl21_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which embryonic tissue forms the smooth muscle of the small intestinal wall?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm","B":"Gut tube endoderm","C":"Surface ectoderm","D":"Paraxial mesoderm","E":"Somatic body wall mesoderm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The muscular and connective tissue components of the gut come from mesoderm, specifically the splanchnic (visceral) layer surrounding the endodermal tube. Endoderm forms the epithelium. Somatic and paraxial mesoderm form body wall and somite derivatives. Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle. Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and gland parenchyma, while muscle, connective tissue, and peritoneal components of the gut wall come from visceral mesoderm. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 21 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: endodermal sonic hedgehog induces HOX gene expression in the surrounding gut mesoderm (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: visceral peritoneum covers the gut while parietal peritoneum lines the body wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Surface ectoderm forms the skin epidermis and only the caudal anal canal, not intestinal smooth muscle.","B":"Endoderm forms the epithelial lining and gland parenchyma, while muscle, connective tissue, and peritoneal components of the gut wall come from visceral mesoderm."}},{"id":"sdl21_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"Which structure is the most distal derivative of the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Distal third of the transverse colon and flexure","B":"Proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon","C":"Descending colon at the splenic flexure region","D":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve junction","E":"Upper anal canal above the pectinate line"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut ends at the junction of the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon with the distal third. The distal third of the transverse colon, the descending colon, and the upper anal canal are hindgut. The terminal ileum is proximal to the end. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Summary; Table 15.1). Textbook enrichment (Langman) — detail not in SDL 21 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut receives vagal parasympathetic innervation and lies at the L1 vertebral level of its artery (First Aid Gastrointestinal, blood supply and innervation). COMLEX: the caudal limb of the primary loop forms the lower ileum, cecum, appendix, and ascending colon (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman"},{"id":"sdl21_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has sudden bilious vomiting after initially passing meconium. The pregnancy was uneventful and he was born at term. The abdomen is mildly distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and the extremities are warm with brisk capillary refill. Serum lactate is 1.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). An upper gastrointestinal series shows the small bowel on the right, and a contrast enema shows the cecum and colon on the left with the ileum entering the cecum from the right. Which developmental error best explains this anatomy?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of the primary loop","B":"Complete rotation with failed colonic fixation","C":"Failure of the loops to leave the abdomen","D":"Persistent attachment of the omphalomesenteric duct","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When rotation stops near 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, and later loops move to the right. Right-sided small bowel, a left-sided colon, and an ileum entering the cecum from the right match this incomplete turn. Clockwise rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum. Failed colonic fixation leaves a mobile colon in its usual right-sided place. Loops that never leave the abdomen skip herniation and cannot produce this left-sided colon. Persistent omphalomesenteric duct attachment produces an ileal diverticulum or umbilical fistula.","boardPrep":"Step 1: malrotation leaves the small bowel clumped on the right and the colon on the left, with fibrous Ladd bands (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malrotation). COMLEX: malrotation predisposes to volvulus with compromised blood supply (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Clockwise rotation places the transverse colon behind the duodenum","B":"Failed colonic fixation leaves a mobile colon in its usual right-sided place","C":"Loops that never leave the abdomen skip herniation and cannot produce this left-sided colon","D":"Persistent omphalomesenteric duct attachment produces an ileal diverticulum or umbilical fistula"}},{"id":"sdl21_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old girl suddenly develops bilious vomiting, abdominal distension, and bloody stools. An upper gastrointestinal study in the first week of life showed the duodenojejunal junction to the right of the spine. She is lethargic, the abdomen is distended and tender, and the extremities are cool. Heart rate is 190/min, blood pressure is 58/32 mm Hg, and capillary refill is 4 seconds. Serum lactate is 6.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Which complication most likely threatens her bowel?","choices":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum","B":"Twisting that compromises blood flow","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The right-sided duodenojejunal junction marks malrotation, and the sudden shock, bloody stools, and lactate of 6.2 mmol/L mean the bowel has twisted and lost its blood flow. Telescoping of ileum into the cecum causes episodic pain and a right-sided mass. Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment is the mechanism of Hirschsprung disease. Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa causes painless bleeding in a well-appearing child. Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium occurs in newborns with cystic fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: midgut volvulus is more common in infants, whereas sigmoid volvulus is more common in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: in gastroschisis, volvulus with compromised blood supply may kill large regions of bowel (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Telescoping of ileum into the cecum causes episodic pain and a right-sided mass","C":"Spasm of an aganglionic colonic segment is the mechanism of Hirschsprung disease","D":"Ulceration of ectopic gastric mucosa causes painless bleeding in a well-appearing child","E":"Plugging of the ileum by thick meconium occurs in newborns with cystic fibrosis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has left flank pain and one episode of gross hematuria. She has never had abdominal surgery. The abdomen is soft, with left costovertebral angle tenderness and normal bowel sounds. CT shows a 4 mm left ureteral stone and, incidentally, the transverse colon passing posterior to the duodenum and behind the superior mesenteric artery. Which embryologic event best explains the colonic position?","choices":{"A":"Clockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","B":"Counterclockwise rotation of about 90 degrees","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud from the liver","E":"Return of the colon before the proximal jejunum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reversed rotation is a clockwise turn of about 90 degrees that places the transverse colon behind the duodenum and the superior mesenteric artery. The incidental CT course of the colon posterior to both structures is that event. A short counterclockwise turn leaves the colon and cecum on the left side. Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon leaves that colon mobile. Arrested descent of the cecal bud leaves the cecum high under the liver. Colon returning before jejunum is the pattern of a left-sided colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after normal rotation, the transverse colon passes in front of the duodenum (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cecal bud lies temporarily in the right upper quadrant below the liver before descending (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A short counterclockwise turn leaves the colon and cecum on the left side","C":"Failed posterior attachment of the ascending colon leaves that colon mobile","D":"Arrested descent of the cecal bud leaves the cecum high under the liver","E":"Colon returning before jejunum is the pattern of a left-sided colon"}},{"id":"sdl21_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 20 weeks of gestation. She takes a prenatal vitamin and her fundal height matches dates. Examination is otherwise unremarkable. Ultrasound shows a smooth membrane-bound midline sac at the cord insertion that contains both bowel and liver. Targeted views of the fetal heart have not yet been obtained. Which associated finding should next be sought?","choices":{"A":"Renal agenesis","B":"Cardiac anomaly","C":"Hip dysplasia","D":"Cleft palate","E":"Clubfoot deformity"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Omphalocele is a smooth membrane-bound sac of bowel and liver at the cord insertion, and it is associated with cardiac anomalies in about half of live-born infants. Neural tube defects and chromosomal anomalies are the other classic companions, so the heart is the finding to seek next. Renal agenesis is not part of the anomaly cluster linked to this midline sac. Hip dysplasia is a mechanical joint problem and is unrelated to this cord sac. Cleft palate results from failed palatal fusion and is a craniofacial event. Clubfoot is a positional foot deformity and is unrelated to this membrane sac.","boardPrep":"Step 1: omphalocele is associated with trisomies 13 and 18 and Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: omphalocele arises when bowel fails to return from physiologic herniation during weeks 6 to 10 (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Renal agenesis is not part of the anomaly cluster linked to this midline sac","C":"Hip dysplasia is a mechanical joint problem and is unrelated to this cord sac","D":"Cleft palate results from failed palatal fusion and is a craniofacial event","E":"Clubfoot is a positional foot deformity and is unrelated to this membrane sac"}},{"id":"sdl21_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy, born to an 18-year-old mother with limited prenatal care, has bowel outside the abdomen. She used cocaine in the second trimester. Thickened, uncovered loops protrude through a small defect just to the right of a normally inserted cord, and no other anomalies are found. The loops are matted and edematous. Which developmental error most likely caused this defect?","choices":{"A":"Failure of loops to return from the umbilical cord","B":"Persistence of a patent omphalomesenteric duct","C":"Delayed closure of the umbilical ring after birth","D":"Abnormal closure of the body wall around the stalk","E":"Failed recanalization of the solid gut lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis is caused by abnormal closure of the body wall around the connecting stalk, leaving bowel exposed to amniotic fluid. A right paraumbilical defect, a normal cord, no covering, matted loops, and no other anomalies match that error. Failure of loops to return leaves viscera inside a membrane at the cord. A patent omphalomesenteric duct leaks fecal fluid from ileum onto the umbilicus. Delayed umbilical ring closure after birth leaves a hernia covered by skin. Failed recanalization blocks the upper duodenal lumen and does not open the body wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastroschisis is not commonly associated with chromosomal abnormalities and has a good prognosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, ventral wall defects). COMLEX: gastroschisis occurs in about 1 per 10,000 births (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Failure of loops to return leaves viscera inside a membrane at the cord","B":"A patent omphalomesenteric duct leaks fecal fluid from ileum onto the umbilicus","C":"Delayed umbilical ring closure after birth leaves a hernia covered by skin","E":"Failed recanalization blocks the upper duodenal lumen and does not open the body wall"}},{"id":"sdl21_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman has a dating ultrasound at 9 weeks of gestation. She has had first-trimester nausea but no vaginal bleeding. Examination and fundal size are appropriate for dates. Several bowel loops lie within the base of the umbilical cord. The fetal heart, spine, and limbs look normal. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Omphalocele with amnion covering","B":"Gastroschisis with uncovered loops","C":"Physiologic midgut herniation","D":"Congenital umbilical hernia","E":"Vitelline cyst at the umbilicus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bowel within the umbilical cord at 9 weeks is physiologic midgut herniation, which is present from the 6th week until return begins in the 10th week. A normal fetal heart, spine, and limbs fit this expected stage of development. An omphalocele is a pathologic sac diagnosed after bowel should already have returned. Gastroschisis loops lie bare beside the umbilicus rather than inside the cord. A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and appears after birth. A vitelline cyst is a dilated middle duct segment between fibrous cords.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a congenital umbilical hernia is covered by skin and often closes spontaneously (First Aid Gastrointestinal, congenital umbilical hernia). COMLEX: herniation occurs because liver growth makes the abdomen temporarily too small (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An omphalocele is a pathologic sac diagnosed after bowel should already have returned","B":"Gastroschisis loops lie bare beside the umbilicus rather than inside the cord","D":"A congenital umbilical hernia is skin-covered and appears after birth","E":"A vitelline cyst is a dilated middle duct segment between fibrous cords"}},{"id":"sdl21_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy suddenly passes a large amount of maroon blood per rectum and then seems comfortable. He has never had abdominal surgery. He is pale, the abdomen is soft and nontender, and capillary refill is brisk. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL) and the heart rate is 130/min. A technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. What most likely caused his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Acid secretion by ectopic gastric mucosa","B":"Ischemia from an ileocolic intussusception","C":"Angiodysplasia within the cecal mucosal wall","D":"Hyperplasia of the ileal lymphoid follicles","E":"Erosion by a swallowed coin or button battery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Painless maroon bleeding with a pertechnetate-avid right lower quadrant focus is a Meckel diverticulum in which ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid and ulcerates adjacent ileum. Anemia with a soft nontender abdomen fits that mucosal source of bleeding. Ileocolic intussusception causes crampy pain and usually a palpable abdominal mass. Angiodysplasia of the cecum is a degenerative lesion seen in older adults. Ileal lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up the tracer used in this scan. A swallowed button battery causes focal caustic injury and is not tracer avid.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pertechnetate scan is called a Meckel scan because it detects heterotopic gastric mucosa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: heterotopic pancreatic tissue in the diverticulum can also cause ulceration (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ileocolic intussusception causes crampy pain and usually a palpable abdominal mass","C":"Angiodysplasia of the cecum is a degenerative lesion seen in older adults","D":"Ileal lymphoid hyperplasia does not take up the tracer used in this scan","E":"A swallowed button battery causes focal caustic injury and is not tracer avid"}},{"id":"sdl21_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and abdominal distension. She has never had abdominal surgery, and a sweat test in infancy was normal. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and perfusion is preserved. Serum lactate is 1.0 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). At surgery a cyst lies between the ileum and the umbilicus, fixed by fibrous cords, with a loop of ileum wrapped around one cord. Which mechanism caused her obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with free perforation","B":"Volvulus of loops on the vitelline ligaments","C":"Telescoping of the ileum into the ascending colon","D":"Aganglionic spasm of a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium filling the ileal lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A cyst between the ileum and the umbilicus, tethered by fibrous cords with a loop wrapped on a cord, is a vitelline cyst causing volvulus on those ligaments. Colicky pain, bilious vomiting, and distension with a normal lactate are mechanical obstruction from that wrap. Mucosal ulceration with perforation follows acid from ectopic tissue in a diverticulum. Telescoping into the ascending colon is intussusception driven by an intraluminal lead point. Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease and affects a distal colonic segment. Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with cystic fibrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: a vitelline cyst is also called an enterocystoma (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — parietal cell HCl secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal ulceration with perforation follows acid from ectopic tissue in a diverticulum","C":"Telescoping into the ascending colon is intussusception driven by an intraluminal lead point","D":"Aganglionic spasm is Hirschsprung disease and affects a distal colonic segment","E":"Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with cystic fibrosis"}},{"id":"sdl21_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 12 hours of right lower quadrant pain and fever. He has never had abdominal surgery. Temperature is 38.2°C and there is focal right lower quadrant tenderness. The leukocyte count is 14,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). Laparoscopy shows a normal appendix and an inflamed 5 cm outpouching of ileum about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve, and the wall contains mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria. How is this outpouching best classified?","choices":{"A":"Duplication cyst","B":"False diverticulum","C":"Pulsion pseudodiverticulum","D":"Pancreatic rest","E":"True diverticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"An outpouching that contains mucosa, submucosa, and muscularis propria is a true diverticulum and is a persistent vitelline duct remnant. The inflamed 5 cm ileal lesion about 50 cm from the ileocecal valve has every one of those wall layers. A duplication cyst is a separate tubular or cystic structure alongside the bowel. A false diverticulum contains only mucosa and submucosa pushed through a wall defect. A pulsion pseudodiverticulum is acquired and lacks a muscular coat. A pancreatic rest is a nodule of ectopic pancreas embedded in the wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum commonly presents in the first 2 years of life (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: about 2% of people have a Meckel diverticulum (lecture notes; Langman gives 2% to 4%).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A duplication cyst is a separate tubular or cystic structure alongside the bowel","B":"A false diverticulum contains only mucosa and submucosa pushed through a wall defect","C":"A pulsion pseudodiverticulum is acquired and lacks a muscular coat","D":"A pancreatic rest is a nodule of ectopic pancreas embedded in the wall"}},{"id":"sdl21_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. Her mother used cocaine throughout pregnancy. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and capillary refill is brisk. Serum lactate is 1.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). A radiograph shows several dilated proximal loops and a gasless pelvis, and surgery shows a blind proximal jejunum separated from distal bowel by a V-shaped mesenteric gap. Which mechanism best explains this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Ischemic necrosis of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization of the lumen","C":"Traction by a persistent vitelline fibrous cord","D":"Exposure of loops to amniotic fluid through a defect","E":"Rotation that stopped near 90 degrees of turning"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A blind proximal jejunum separated by a V-shaped mesenteric gap is ischemic necrosis of a fetal bowel segment after a vascular accident. Maternal cocaine use, bilious vomiting, and dilated proximal loops with a gasless pelvis fit that in utero loss of a segment. Failure of solid-cord recanalization explains a web in the upper duodenum. A persistent vitelline cord obstructs when loops twist around the tether. Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, and no wall defect is present. Rotation that stops near 90 degrees leaves the colon on the left side.","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal and ileal atresia are associated with cystic fibrosis and gastroschisis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia). COMLEX: maternal tobacco smoking is a risk factor for jejunal and ileal atresia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal atresia).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Failure of solid-cord recanalization explains a web in the upper duodenum","C":"A persistent vitelline cord obstructs when loops twist around the tether","D":"Amniotic exposure damages bare loops in gastroschisis, and no wall defect is present","E":"Rotation that stops near 90 degrees leaves the colon on the left side"}},{"id":"sdl21_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy born at 32 weeks, weighing 1500 g, has bilious vomiting. The pregnancy was not complicated by vasoconstrictor exposure. The abdomen is distended and bowel sounds are tinkling. A radiograph shows a few dilated proximal loops and a gasless pelvis. At surgery the proximal jejunum ends blindly and the short distal small bowel is coiled around a thin mesenteric remnant. Which defect is present?","choices":{"A":"Membranous duodenal web","B":"Annular pancreas","C":"Apple peel atresia","D":"Multiple segmental atresias","E":"Midgut duplication cyst"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Apple peel atresia accounts for about 10 percent of atresias and consists of a blind proximal jejunum with shortened distal bowel coiled around a mesenteric remnant. Prematurity, low birth weight, and this helical distal bowel on a thin mesentery are that defect. A membranous web is a thin diaphragm inside bowel that remains in continuity. Annular pancreas is a ring of ventral bud tissue constricting the duodenum. Multiple segmental atresias are separate gaps rather than one helical distal bowel. A duplication cyst is a separate structure, most often near the ileum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: effects of an atresia depend on how much bowel is damaged and where it lies (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a thin diaphragm separating larger and smaller pieces of bowel accounts for about 20% of atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A membranous web is a thin diaphragm inside bowel that remains in continuity","B":"Annular pancreas is a ring of ventral bud tissue constricting the duodenum","D":"Multiple segmental atresias are separate gaps rather than one helical distal bowel","E":"A duplication cyst is a separate structure, most often near the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl21_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has bilious vomiting. An older sister required surgery for the same intestinal defect, and the pregnancy included no cocaine or other vasoconstrictors. The abdomen is distended and bowel sounds are high-pitched. At surgery there are several separate ileal atresias, the mesenteric vessels are intact, and there is no malrotation or volvulus. Which mechanism most likely explains his atresias?","choices":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus with segment infarction","B":"Failed recanalization of the solid lumen","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drug exposure","D":"Faulty gene-directed gut differentiation","E":"Amniotic injury through an abdominal wall defect"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Several ileal atresias with intact vessels, no volvulus, and an affected sibling are due to faulty gene-directed gut differentiation, including misexpression of developmental genes and growth factor receptors. The sister's similar history and the intact mesenteric vessels are the clues in this newborn. Intrauterine volvulus infarcts bowel only after mesenteric vessels are occluded. Failed recanalization is the mechanism of atresia confined to the upper duodenum. Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs produce atresia by interrupting mesenteric blood flow. Amniotic injury requires bare bowel outside an abdominal wall defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stenoses and multiple atresias each account for about 5% of these defects (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about 33% of intestinal duplications are associated with other defects such as atresias (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrauterine volvulus infarcts bowel only after mesenteric vessels are occluded","B":"Failed recanalization is the mechanism of atresia confined to the upper duodenum","C":"Maternal vasoconstrictive drugs produce atresia by interrupting mesenteric blood flow","E":"Amniotic injury requires bare bowel outside an abdominal wall defect"}},{"id":"sdl21_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation has sudden severe periumbilical pain. He stopped warfarin three days ago after a fall. The rhythm is irregularly irregular, the abdomen is soft, and the pain far exceeds the tenderness. Serum lactate is 5.4 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the superior mesenteric artery just beyond its origin. Which bowel segment is most likely to be spared from ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Descending colon","D":"Cecum and appendix","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The descending colon is spared because it is a hindgut derivative supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery. The embolus blocks the superior mesenteric artery, and pain out of proportion with a lactate of 5.4 mmol/L reflects ischemia in the territory of that vessel. The proximal jejunum lies inside the territory of the occluded arterial trunk. The terminal ileum is perfused by ileal branches of the occluded trunk. The cecum and appendix depend on the ileocolic branch of the occluded trunk. The ascending colon is fed by right colic branches of the occluded trunk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the hindgut extends from the distal third of the transverse colon to the anal canal above the pectinate line (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the left third of the transverse colon is a hindgut derivative (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"The proximal jejunum lies inside the territory of the occluded arterial trunk","B":"The terminal ileum is perfused by ileal branches of the occluded trunk","D":"The cecum and appendix depend on the ileocolic branch of the occluded trunk","E":"The ascending colon is fed by right colic branches of the occluded trunk"}},{"id":"sdl21_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has postprandial epigastric pain and a 6 kg weight loss. She has a 40 pack-year smoking history and widespread atherosclerotic disease. The abdomen is soft and nontender, with an epigastric bruit. Angiography shows a high-grade celiac trunk stenosis and a patent superior mesenteric artery, and the distal duodenum remains well perfused. Which landmark marks the embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut territory in her duodenum?","choices":{"A":"Pyloric sphincter","B":"Minor duodenal papilla","C":"Duodenojejunal flexure","D":"First-part duodenal bulb","E":"Major duodenal papilla"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The embryologic boundary between foregut and midgut in the duodenum is the major duodenal papilla, just distal to the entrance of the bile duct. Celiac stenosis with a patent superior mesenteric artery leaves the duodenum past that landmark well perfused. The pyloric sphincter is the gastric outlet and lies in proximal foregut territory. The minor papilla opens the accessory pancreatic duct and is not the regional boundary. The duodenojejunal flexure lies far downstream within established midgut territory. The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum and stays under celiac arterial supply.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the foregut extends from the esophagus to the duodenum at the level of the ampulla of Vater (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the liver, pancreas, and biliary apparatus grow out of the upper duodenal endoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The pyloric sphincter is the gastric outlet and lies in proximal foregut territory","B":"The minor papilla opens the accessory pancreatic duct and is not the regional boundary","C":"The duodenojejunal flexure lies far downstream within established midgut territory","D":"The duodenal bulb is proximal duodenum and stays under celiac arterial supply"}},{"id":"sdl21_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":5,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy feeds poorly and has a single median orbit. A loss-of-function mutation is found in a secreted signaling gene, and his mother did not have diabetes. The abdomen is soft and the abdominal wall is intact. Imaging also shows malformed intestinal rotation. In the developing gut, which signal from the endoderm patterned the surrounding mesoderm along the craniocaudal axis?","choices":{"A":"Retinoic acid","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4","D":"Sonic hedgehog","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gut endoderm secretes sonic hedgehog, which induces HOX genes in the surrounding mesoderm and sets the craniocaudal organization of the gut. Holoprosencephaly with malformed intestinal rotation after loss of a secreted signal fits failure of that endodermal cue. Retinoic acid is a regional gradient and is not secreted by gut endoderm. WNT proteins come from neural tube and limb ectoderm, not from gut endoderm. Bone morphogenetic protein 4 ventralizes mesoderm and blocks neural induction. Fibroblast growth factor 10 acts during budding of the limb and the lung.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HOX genes in gut mesoderm, induced by endodermal signaling, regulate craniocaudal organization of the gut and its derivatives (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: an RA gradient causes region-specific transcription factors along the gut tube (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Langman","whyNot":{"A":"Retinoic acid is a regional gradient and is not secreted by gut endoderm","B":"WNT proteins come from neural tube and limb ectoderm, not from gut endoderm","C":"Bone morphogenetic protein 4 ventralizes mesoderm and blocks neural induction","E":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 acts during budding of the limb and the lung"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-week-old boy has had intermittent bilious vomiting for 10 days but feeds eagerly between episodes. He passed meconium on the first day of life. He is afebrile, the abdomen is soft and flat, and capillary refill is brisk. Serum lactate is 1.0 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). An upper gastrointestinal series shows the duodenojejunal junction and all small-bowel loops to the right of the midline, and laparotomy shows pink, untwisted bowel with the cecum high in the abdomen. Which finding most likely explains his vomiting?","choices":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric circular muscle","B":"Peritoneal bands crossing the duodenum","C":"A fibrous vitelline cord to the umbilicus","D":"A solid duodenal plug never recanalized","E":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided small bowel and a high cecum indicate malrotation from an incomplete 270 degree counterclockwise turn, and peritoneal bands from the cecum cross and compress the duodenum. Pink untwisted bowel, a normal lactate, and intermittent bilious vomiting fit duodenal compression by those bands. Hypertrophied pyloric muscle blocks the stomach outlet above the entry of bile. A fibrous vitelline cord tethers ileum and would leave the bowel twisted and ischemic. A never-recanalized duodenal plug causes complete obstruction from the first feed. Absent rectal ganglion cells cause distal obstruction with distension and delayed meconium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Malrotation leaves the small bowel on the right and the colon on the left, and its Ladd bands can obstruct the duodenum (First Aid 2025 GI pathology). COMLEX: Bilious vomiting in an infant is treated as malrotation with possible volvulus until imaging proves otherwise.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hypertrophied pyloric muscle blocks the stomach outlet above the entry of bile","C":"A fibrous vitelline cord tethers ileum and would leave the bowel twisted and ischemic","D":"A never-recanalized duodenal plug causes complete obstruction from the first feed","E":"Absent rectal ganglion cells cause distal obstruction with distension and delayed meconium"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate physiologic umbilical herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis using developmental timing, defect location, coverings of the viscera, and associated abnormalities.","batch":3,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has a routine ultrasound at 18 weeks. She takes a prenatal vitamin, and fundal height matches dates. Free-floating bowel with a ragged outline protrudes through a small defect just to the right of a normally inserted cord, and no membrane surrounds it. The fetal heart and spine look normal. At delivery the protruding loops are thick, edematous, and matted. What most likely caused these bowel wall changes?","choices":{"A":"Pressure from a covering amnion","B":"A chromosomal defect of gut mesoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the lumen","D":"Direct exposure to amniotic fluid","E":"Torsion around a vitelline cord"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gastroschisis leaves bare loops beside a normal cord, and the thick matted wall comes from direct exposure to amniotic fluid. The fetal heart and spine are normal, which fits a defect that is usually isolated. A covering amnion would enclose the viscera in a smooth sac at the cord. Chromosomal defects of mesoderm accompany membrane-covered sacs and not this isolated defect. Failed recanalization narrows the upper duodenal lumen and does not thicken exteriorized bowel. Torsion around a vitelline cord twists loops that remain inside the abdomen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gastroschisis lies lateral to the umbilicus, usually on the right, with no covering membrane, and the bowel may be damaged by amniotic fluid (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Unlike omphalocele, gastroschisis is rarely linked to chromosomal defects, so survival is excellent.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A covering amnion would enclose the viscera in a smooth sac at the cord","B":"Chromosomal defects of mesoderm accompany membrane-covered sacs and not this isolated defect","C":"Failed recanalization narrows the upper duodenal lumen and does not thicken exteriorized bowel","E":"Torsion around a vitelline cord twists loops that remain inside the abdomen"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Relate the persistence of different portions of the vitelline duct to Meckel diverticulum, vitelline cysts, and vitelline fistulas and explain their associated bleeding or obstruction.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has a persistently moist umbilicus. He feeds well and voids clear urine in a normal stream. He is afebrile, a small opening sits at the base of the umbilicus, and the abdomen is soft. Yellow-green, stool-like material repeatedly stains the diaper at that opening, and a thin catheter passes easily into it. Contrast injected through the catheter would most likely fill which structure?","choices":{"A":"Urinary bladder","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Transverse colon","D":"Sigmoid colon","E":"Distal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Yellow-green stool at the umbilicus is a vitelline fistula from a duct patent along its entire length, and that duct opens into the distal ileum. Contrast injected at the umbilicus therefore fills the distal ileum, about 40 to 60 cm from the ileocecal valve on the antimesenteric border. A urinary bladder connection is urachal and would drain urine rather than stool. The proximal jejunum has no embryonic channel running out to the umbilicus. The transverse colon never communicates with the umbilicus through a yolk-sac duct. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and develops from the cloaca.","boardPrep":"Step 1: A vitelline fistula is a fully patent vitelline duct that links the ileum to the umbilicus and can discharge feces there (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Fecal drainage from an infant's umbilicus should prompt imaging of a tract to the ileum.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A urinary bladder connection is urachal and would drain urine rather than stool","B":"The proximal jejunum has no embryonic channel running out to the umbilicus","C":"The transverse colon never communicates with the umbilicus through a yolk-sac duct","D":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and develops from the cloaca"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl vomits after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. The pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios, and her mother did not use cocaine. The abdomen is scaphoid and bowel sounds are quiet. A radiograph shows two large gas bubbles in the upper abdomen and no gas beyond them. Surgery finds a complete mucosal diaphragm in the upper duodenum just proximal to the major papilla, with a normal wall and normal mesenteric vessels. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Perfusion of a fetal intestinal segment","B":"Recanalization of the duodenal lumen","C":"Migration of enteric neural crest","D":"Fusion of the dorsal mesentery","E":"Regression of the vitelline duct"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A complete mucosal diaphragm in the upper duodenum just proximal to the major papilla, with a normal wall and vessels, means recanalization of the duodenal lumen failed. Nonbilious vomiting and two upper-abdominal gas bubbles with no distal gas place the block above the entry of bile. Loss of fetal perfusion destroys a segment of bowel and its mesentery. Failed enteric neural crest migration causes aganglionosis of the distal colon. Fusion of the dorsal mesentery to the posterior wall only fixes bowel position. Regression of the vitelline duct concerns the ileum and the umbilicus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Upper duodenal atresia most likely reflects failed recanalization, whereas atresias from the distal duodenum onward are attributed to vascular accidents or faulty gut differentiation (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: A newborn with a double bubble and no distal gas has complete duodenal obstruction needing surgery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 4 — duodenal recanalization","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of fetal perfusion destroys a segment of bowel and its mesentery","C":"Failed enteric neural crest migration causes aganglionosis of the distal colon","D":"Fusion of the dorsal mesentery to the posterior wall only fixes bowel position","E":"Regression of the vitelline duct concerns the ileum and the umbilicus"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has intermittent melena and fatigue. She does not take anticoagulants. She is pale, the abdomen is soft, and the heart rate is 96/min. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). A resected jejunal mass is a circumscribed proliferation of bland smooth muscle in the muscularis externa, with intact villi and crypts over it. The tumor's tissue of origin shares its embryonic source with which other component of the jejunal wall?","choices":{"A":"Absorptive cells of the villi","B":"Paneth cells in the crypts","C":"Visceral peritoneum on the loop","D":"Neurons of the myenteric plexus","E":"Epithelium over Peyer patches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Smooth muscle of the muscularis externa arises from splanchnic mesoderm, which also forms the visceral peritoneum and the connective tissue of the gut wall. This jejunal muscle tumor therefore shares its embryonic source with the visceral peritoneum on the loop. Absorptive cells of the villi are endodermal epithelium of the gut lining. Paneth cells in the crypts are endodermal secretory cells of the epithelium. Neurons of the myenteric plexus migrate in from the neural crest. Epithelium over Peyer patches is endoderm covering lymphoid tissue in the mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut epithelium comes from endoderm, smooth muscle, connective tissue, and visceral peritoneum from splanchnic mesoderm, and enteric ganglia from neural crest (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: A bleeding small bowel mass can arise from the muscle wall while the mucosa over it stays intact.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 11 Obj 1 — signals triggering liver development","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absorptive cells of the villi are endodermal epithelium of the gut lining","B":"Paneth cells in the crypts are endodermal secretory cells of the epithelium","D":"Neurons of the myenteric plexus migrate in from the neural crest","E":"Epithelium over Peyer patches is endoderm covering lymphoid tissue in the mucosa"}},{"id":"trial_sdl21_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Apply normal midgut rotation and intestinal positioning to distinguish incomplete rotation from reversed rotation, including timeline and explain their potential consequences.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 18 hours of anorexia and pain that began at the umbilicus and then moved to the left lower quadrant. She has never had abdominal surgery. Temperature is 38.4°C, there is left lower quadrant tenderness with rebound, and bowel sounds are present. The leukocyte count is 16,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows an inflamed appendix arising from a cecum in the left lower abdomen, the entire colon to the left of midline, small bowel on the right, and the ileum entering the cecum from the right. During her embryonic development, which part of the gut most likely reentered the abdominal cavity first?","choices":{"A":"Proximal jejunum","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Colon and cecum","D":"Middle jejunal loops","E":"Hindgut segments"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A left-sided colon, right-sided small bowel, and an ileum entering from the right occur when the primary loop rotates only about 90 degrees, so the colon and cecum reenter the abdomen first and settle on the left. Left lower quadrant appendicitis in this adult is that same rotational pattern. The proximal jejunum returns first only after a completed normal rotation. The distal ileum returns late and does not decide which side the colon occupies. Middle jejunal loops follow the proximal bowel during ordinary return. Hindgut segments are not part of the herniated midgut loop.","boardPrep":"Step 1: When the primary loop rotates only 90 degrees, the colon and cecum return first and settle on the left, producing a left-sided colon (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Appendicitis in a patient with malrotation can cause left-sided pain, so imaging guides the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 3 — stomach rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal jejunum returns first only after a completed normal rotation","B":"The distal ileum returns late and does not decide which side the colon occupies","D":"Middle jejunal loops follow the proximal bowel during ordinary return","E":"Hindgut segments are not part of the herniated midgut loop"}},{"id":"sdl21_q31","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Distinguish intestinal obstruction caused by lack of recanalization from atresia following vascular compromise, and recognize abnormal gut differentiation as another mechanism of atresia.","batch":1,"stem":"Intrauterine vascular compromise causes intestinal atresia in which region?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum, just beyond the stomach","B":"Appendix alone","C":"Bowel distal to the duodenal-jejunal junction","D":"Thoracic esophagus","E":"Body of the stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An intrauterine vascular accident causes atresia distal to the duodenal-jejunal junction. Failed recanalization, not focal ischemia, is the mechanism that closes the proximal duodenum. The appendix is not the segment these vascular atresias occupy. The esophagus is foregut and is outside the bowel segments lost after a fetal vascular accident. The stomach is not the site of atresia from an intrauterine vascular accident.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Failed recanalization, not focal ischemia, is the mechanism that closes the proximal duodenum","B":"The appendix is not the segment these vascular atresias occupy","D":"The esophagus is foregut and is outside the bowel segments lost after a fetal vascular accident","E":"The stomach is not the site of atresia from an intrauterine vascular accident"}},{"id":"sdl21_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the ileum?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Celiac trunk","C":"Inferior mesenteric artery","D":"Internal pudendal artery","E":"Proper hepatic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ileum is midgut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The celiac trunk supplies the proximal duodenum above the major duodenal papilla, not the ileum. The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, not the ileum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the ileum. The proper hepatic artery stays in the celiac distribution and does not feed the ileum.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The celiac trunk supplies the proximal duodenum above the major duodenal papilla, not the ileum","C":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies hindgut derivatives, not the ileum","D":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the ileum","E":"The proper hepatic artery stays in the celiac distribution and does not feed the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl21_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-day-old boy has bilious vomiting and a mildly distended abdomen. Prenatal ultrasound showed polyhydramnios, and a contrast study stops in the third portion of the duodenum, past the ampulla. He is afebrile, the abdominal wall is intact, and there is no peritoneal sign. Serum sodium is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and bicarbonate is 23 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The atretic segment lies in the territory of which artery?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal pudendal artery","C":"Left colic artery","D":"Celiac trunk","E":"Superior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Duodenum distal to the major duodenal papilla is midgut and is supplied by the superior mesenteric artery. The inferior mesenteric artery feeds hindgut, which begins farther distal than the third portion of the duodenum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line. The left colic artery is an inferior mesenteric branch to the descending colon. The celiac trunk supplies only the duodenum above the major duodenal papilla.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The inferior mesenteric artery feeds hindgut, which begins farther distal than the third portion of the duodenum","B":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line","C":"The left colic artery is an inferior mesenteric branch to the descending colon","D":"The celiac trunk supplies only the duodenum above the major duodenal papilla"}},{"id":"sdl21_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Apply developmental origins and arterial supply of the small intestine to distinguish its epithelial, muscular and vascular components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl vomits after every feed, and the vomitus contains no bile. Pregnancy was complicated by polyhydramnios. The abdomen is soft and not distended, and the abdominal wall is closed. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Imaging shows the blockage proximal to the ampulla. Which artery supplied that duodenal segment in the embryo?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Celiac trunk","C":"Inferior mesenteric artery","D":"Ileocolic artery","E":"Superior rectal artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Duodenum proximal to the major duodenal papilla is caudal foregut and is supplied by the celiac trunk. The superior mesenteric artery supplies duodenum only distal to the major duodenal papilla. The inferior mesenteric artery is the hindgut vessel and does not supply the proximal duodenum. The ileocolic artery is a superior mesenteric branch to the ileocecal region. The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery to the upper rectum.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies duodenum only distal to the major duodenal papilla","C":"The inferior mesenteric artery is the hindgut vessel and does not supply the proximal duodenum","D":"The ileocolic artery is a superior mesenteric branch to the ileocecal region","E":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery to the upper rectum"}}]},{"sdlNumber":22,"title":"SDL 22 — Embryology of the Large Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which artery supplies the cecum and ascending colon?","choices":{"A":"Celiac arterial trunk","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Superior mesenteric artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Splenic artery"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The cecum and ascending colon are midgut derivatives and are supplied by the superior mesenteric artery through its ileocolic and right colic branches. The celiac trunk and splenic artery supply foregut structures, and the internal iliac and median sacral arteries supply pelvic structures rather than midgut colon. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the midgut ends where the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon meet the distal third (First Aid Gastrointestinal, normal embryology). COMLEX: the ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic branches serve the colonic part of this territory (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Where along the transverse colon does midgut territory end and hindgut territory begin?","choices":{"A":"Junction of its proximal two-thirds and distal third","B":"Junction of its proximal third and distal two-thirds","C":"Midpoint between the two colic flexures","D":"Hepatic flexure at the right colic angle","E":"Splenic flexure at the left colic angle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The midgut extends to the junction of the proximal two-thirds and distal third of the transverse colon; the distal third belongs to the hindgut. The proximal-third split reverses the proportions, the midpoint is not the boundary, the hepatic flexure lies well within midgut territory, and the boundary lies just before the splenic flexure rather than at it. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: this boundary is why the splenic flexure region is a watershed between superior and inferior mesenteric supply (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI blood supply). COMLEX: hindgut derivatives continue caudally to the pectinate line of the anal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":1,"stem":"Which of the following large intestine structures develops from the midgut?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Vermiform appendix","C":"Descending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Upper anal canal"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The midgut forms the cecum, vermiform appendix, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. The sigmoid colon, descending colon, rectum, and upper anal canal all come from the hindgut and receive inferior mesenteric supply. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (midgut and hindgut contributions); Langman Ch. 15 (Midgut; Hindgut; Table 15.1). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix forms from the distal end of the cecal bud (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: about half of appendices lie retrocecal or retrocolic because the appendix grows during colonic descent (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which connective tissue layer anchors the ascending and descending colon to the posterior abdominal wall?","choices":{"A":"Scarpa fascia","B":"Camper fascia","C":"Toldt fascia","D":"Denonvilliers fascia","E":"Transversalis fascia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the ascending and descending colon settle against the posterior wall, their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum through Toldt fascia. Scarpa and Camper fascia are layers of the anterior abdominal subcutaneous tissue, Denonvilliers fascia lies between rectum and prostate, and transversalis fascia lines the inner abdominal wall. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: surgeons mobilize the colon along the white line of Toldt during colectomy (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the mesentery of the sigmoid colon persists, so this segment stays intraperitoneal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"Once their dorsal mesenteries fuse with the parietal peritoneum, the ascending and descending colon are classified as which type of structure?","choices":{"A":"Primarily retroperitoneal","B":"Intraperitoneal on a mesentery","C":"Infraperitoneal in the pelvis","D":"Extraperitoneal in the wall","E":"Secondarily retroperitoneal"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The colon first hangs on a dorsal mesentery and becomes secondarily retroperitoneal after fusion with the posterior wall. Primary retroperitoneal organs, such as the kidneys, develop without a mesentery; intraperitoneal describes segments that keep a mesentery, such as the transverse and sigmoid colon; infraperitoneal and extraperitoneal describe pelvic or body wall positions. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum except its first part and the pancreas except its tail are also secondarily retroperitoneal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, retroperitoneal structures). COMLEX: the transverse mesocolon fuses with the greater omentum rather than the posterior wall (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":1,"stem":"After midgut rotation, which process carries the cecum from the right upper quadrant to the right lower quadrant?","choices":{"A":"Rotation of the stomach","B":"Elongation of the colon","C":"Enlargement of the liver","D":"Regression of the mesonephros","E":"Fusion of the mesocolon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The cecal bud lies first in the right upper quadrant below the liver, and lengthening of the colon carries the cecum and appendix caudally into the right iliac fossa. Gastric rotation shapes the foregut, liver growth and mesonephric regression influence the earlier return of herniated loops, and mesocolic fusion fixes the colon after it is in place. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (cecal descent and mesenteric attachment); Langman Ch. 15 (Retraction of herniated loops; Mesenteries of the intestinal loops; Abnormalities of the mesenteries). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cecal bud is the last part of the herniated loop to reenter the abdomen (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: a cecum that stays high produces a subhepatic appendix with atypical right upper quadrant pain (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure forms between the anal and urogenital openings after the cloaca is divided?","choices":{"A":"Genital tubercle","B":"Urogenital fold","C":"Anal column","D":"Perineal body","E":"Labioscrotal swelling"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the cloacal membrane ruptures, the tip of the dividing mesodermal wedge remains between the anal and urogenital openings as the perineal body (Langman-only detail; not in SDL 22 lecture notes). The genital tubercle, urogenital folds, and labioscrotal swellings are external genital primordia, and anal columns are mucosal folds of the upper anal canal. Sources: Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; tip of urorectal septum → perineal body, Figs. 15.36–15.37); SDL 22 lecture notes (urorectal septum context). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the perineal body anchors the perineal muscles and can be torn during vaginal delivery (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the allantois enters the anterior cloaca and the hindgut enters the posterior cloaca (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"At the end of which week does the anal membrane (posterior cloacal membrane) break down?","choices":{"A":"Fourth week","B":"Seventh week","C":"Fifth week","D":"Tenth week","E":"Twelfth week"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The anal membrane (the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane) degenerates at the end of the seventh week, opening communication between the anal pit and the hindgut. The fourth and fifth weeks are too early because the cloaca is still being partitioned, and the tenth and twelfth weeks correspond to return of the herniated midgut. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (imperforate anus / anal membrane); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas occur in about 1 in 5,000 live births (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: these defects likely reflect misexpression of genes during epithelial–mesenchymal signaling (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":1,"stem":"If the urorectal septum fails to extend far enough caudally, how is the hindgut opening displaced?","choices":{"A":"Posterior shift of the hindgut opening","B":"Lateral shift of the hindgut opening","C":"Cranial shift of the hindgut opening","D":"Duplication of the hindgut opening","E":"Anterior shift of the hindgut opening"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A small cloaca or a urorectal septum that stops short causes the hindgut opening to shift anteriorly, so it can empty into the urethra, vagina, or perineum. Posterior, lateral, and cranial displacement are not described, and duplication is a separate anomaly unrelated to septal growth. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (anorectal malformations vs imperforate anus); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut; Hindgut abnormalities, Figs. 15.36–15.37). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: an anterior opening explains meconium in the urine of a boy with a rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: some variants end in rectal atresia or a fibrous cord to the perineum (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 2 — foregut partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelium lines the anal canal just below the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium","B":"Transitional epithelium","C":"Stratified squamous epithelium","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the ectodermal mucosa is stratified squamous epithelium that continues to the perianal skin. Pseudostratified columnar epithelium lines the airways, transitional epithelium lines the urinary tract, simple cuboidal epithelium lines ducts and tubules, and stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger gland ducts. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line are typically squamous cell carcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: an anal transitional zone lies at the junction of the two epithelial types (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of which vessel?","choices":{"A":"Inferior mesenteric artery","B":"Internal iliac artery","C":"Internal pudendal artery","D":"Median sacral artery","E":"Left colic artery"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The superior rectal artery continues the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and supplies the anal canal above the pectinate line. The internal iliac and internal pudendal arteries supply the region below the line, the median sacral artery is a small midline branch of the aorta, and the left colic artery is a separate, more proximal branch. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids above the line drain to the superior rectal vein and portal system (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: sigmoidal branches arise from the same parent vessel between the left colic and terminal branch (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal landmark lies immediately above the pectinate line?","choices":{"A":"Anal verge","B":"Perianal skin","C":"Intersphincteric groove","D":"Anal columns","E":"Levator ani"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The pectinate line, which marks the junction of endodermal and ectodermal parts of the anal canal, lies just below the anal columns. The anal verge and perianal skin lie below it at the outer margin, the intersphincteric groove lies between the sphincters below the line, and the levator ani is pelvic floor muscle rather than a mucosal landmark. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (dual origin of the anal canal); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut, Fig. 15.36). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: visceral pain fibers serve the canal above the line, and somatic fibers serve it below (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the cranial two-thirds of the anal canal come from the primitive anorectal canal (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — hemorrhoids and anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Parasympathetic ganglion cells of the enteric nervous system derive from which embryonic cells?","choices":{"A":"Splanchnic mesoderm cells","B":"Paraxial mesoderm cells","C":"Surface ectoderm cells","D":"Neural tube cells","E":"Neural crest cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enteric parasympathetic ganglion cells come from neural crest cells that migrate from the neural folds into the bowel wall. Splanchnic mesoderm forms the gut muscle and connective tissue, paraxial mesoderm forms somites, surface ectoderm forms epidermis, and the neural tube forms the central nervous system. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neural crest derivatives also include adrenal medulla, melanocytes, and Schwann cells (First Aid Embryology, germ layer derivatives). COMLEX: the endothelin-3 and EDNRB pathway also supports these migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"The RET gene that is mutated in congenital megacolon encodes which type of protein?","choices":{"A":"Ligand-gated ion channel","B":"G protein-coupled receptor","C":"Nuclear hormone receptor","D":"Receptor tyrosine kinase","E":"Serine threonine kinase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase required for proliferation, survival, and directed migration of enteric neural crest precursors. Ligand-gated channels, G protein-coupled receptors, nuclear hormone receptors, and serine threonine kinases are other signaling classes not encoded by RET. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: gain-of-function RET mutations cause MEN 2A and 2B, the opposite of the loss of function in megacolon (First Aid Endocrinology, MEN syndromes). COMLEX: RET is discussed further with neural crest development in Langman Ch. 18.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":1,"stem":"Which bowel segment is involved in nearly every case of aganglionic megacolon?","choices":{"A":"Rectum","B":"Cecum","C":"Appendix","D":"Jejunum","E":"Ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Aganglionosis begins distally and extends proximally for a variable distance, so the rectum is involved in nearly all cases. The cecum and appendix are affected only in long-segment or total colonic disease, and the jejunum and ileum only in rare extended forms. Sources: SDL 22 lecture notes (neural crest, RET, congenital megacolon); Langman Ch. 15 (Hindgut abnormalities). Aligned with SDL 22 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: suction rectal biopsy is the diagnostic test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: in about 80% the defect extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid, and in about 3% the whole colon is affected (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl22_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has sudden severe periumbilical pain. He has atrial fibrillation and stopped apixaban four days ago after a fall. The abdomen is soft and much less tender than the pain, the pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum lactate is 5.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L), and CT angiography shows an embolus occluding the first anterior aortic branch below the celiac trunk, just past its origin. Which colonic segment is at greatest risk of infarction?","choices":{"A":"Descending colon","B":"Sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Rectum","E":"Distal transverse colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occluded vessel is the superior mesenteric artery, so the ascending colon, a midgut segment, is at greatest risk of infarction. Pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen, atrial fibrillation, and a lactate of 5.1 mmol/L indicate acute mesenteric ischemia from loss of midgut flow. The descending colon is hindgut and remains perfused by the inferior mesenteric artery after this embolus. The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives inferior mesenteric blood that this occlusion does not interrupt. The rectum is supplied by the superior rectal artery, a terminal branch of the inferior mesenteric artery. The distal transverse colon lies in hindgut territory and is reached by the left colic artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute mesenteric ischemia classically follows embolism from atrial fibrillation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: pain out of proportion to examination is the early bedside clue (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The descending colon is hindgut and remains perfused by the","B":"The sigmoid colon is hindgut and receives inferior mesenteric blood","D":"The rectum is supplied by the superior rectal artery, a","E":"The distal transverse colon lies in hindgut territory and is"}},{"id":"sdl22_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has left lower quadrant pain and bloody diarrhea two days after open aortic aneurysm repair. During that operation the anterior aortic branch arising at L3 was ligated, and she had no prior colitis. The left lower quadrant is tender, the pulse is 98/min, and blood pressure is 128/74 mm Hg. Colonoscopy shows ischemic mucosa from just beyond the splenic flexure through the rectosigmoid. Which other structure normally depends on the same arterial territory?","choices":{"A":"Lower third of the anal canal","B":"Cecum and vermiform appendix","C":"Proximal two-thirds of transverse colon","D":"Upper two-thirds of the anal canal","E":"Terminal ileum and ileocecal valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The ligated vessel is the inferior mesenteric artery, the artery of the hindgut, and that territory includes the upper two-thirds of the anal canal along with the distal colon and rectum. Ischemia beginning just beyond the splenic flexure and extending through the rectosigmoid shows loss of this hindgut supply. The lower third of the anal canal is surface ectoderm supplied by the inferior rectal arteries. The cecum and vermiform appendix are midgut structures supplied by the ileocolic artery. The proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon receive middle colic flow from the superior mesenteric artery. The terminal ileum and ileocecal valve lie in superior mesenteric territory and keep their own arterial trunk.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia after aortic surgery reflects loss of inferior mesenteric inflow (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: the left colic, sigmoidal, and superior rectal arteries are the named branches of this vessel (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 1 — gut divisions and blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The lower third of the anal canal is surface ectoderm","B":"The cecum and vermiform appendix are midgut structures supplied by","C":"The proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon receive middle colic","E":"The terminal ileum and ileocecal valve lie in superior mesenteric"}},{"id":"sdl22_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has crampy left upper quadrant pain and maroon stool two days after septic shock. His blood pressure remained 78/42 mm Hg for several hours, and he has no known embolic source. The left upper quadrant is tender and the pulse is 104/min. Serum lactate is 2.8 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L), and colonoscopy shows a short segment of ischemic mucosa centered on the splenic flexure. The ischemic region lies between the terminal branches of which arteries?","choices":{"A":"Ileocolic and right colic arteries","B":"Middle colic and left colic arteries","C":"Right colic and middle colic arteries","D":"Left colic and sigmoidal arteries","E":"Sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Low-flow ischemia centered on the splenic flexure is a watershed infarct at the midgut-hindgut boundary, where terminal branches of the middle colic artery meet those of the left colic artery. Prolonged hypotension with a short ischemic segment at that flexure selects the junction where collateral flow is poorest. The ileocolic and right colic arteries are both superior mesenteric branches that meet along the ascending colon. The right colic and middle colic arteries are both superior mesenteric branches and anastomose within the proximal colon. The left colic and sigmoidal arteries are both inferior mesenteric branches supplying the descending and sigmoid colon. The sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries share an inferior mesenteric origin and meet in the distal colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction are the classic watershed sites for colonic ischemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: hypotension rather than occlusion is the usual cause of this pattern (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ileocolic and right colic arteries are both superior mesenteric","C":"The right colic and middle colic arteries are both superior","D":"The left colic and sigmoidal arteries are both inferior mesenteric","E":"The sigmoidal and superior rectal arteries share an inferior mesenteric"}},{"id":"sdl22_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has 12 hours of colicky pain, distension, and vomiting. She has had two similar milder episodes and has never undergone abdominal surgery. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, and the pulse is 108/min. The leukocyte count is 12,400/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows a dilated cecum in the left upper quadrant with a whirl of mesenteric vessels at its base. Which anatomic feature of her colon most likely allowed this twist?","choices":{"A":"Elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon","B":"Narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve","C":"Persistent free mesentery of the ascending colon","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum","E":"Thickened teniae coli of the cecum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Failure of the ascending colon to fuse with the posterior wall leaves a free mesentery, allowing cecal volvulus around the vascular pedicle. Recurrent colic, a cecum displaced into the left upper quadrant, and a mesenteric whirl are the clues to that closed-loop twist. An elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon allows the sigmoid to twist while the cecum stays in the right iliac fossa. A narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve can slow emptying but does not swing the cecum into the left upper quadrant. Absent ganglion cells in the rectum cause constipation from a narrow distal segment and do not rotate the cecum into the left upper quadrant. Thickened teniae coli change the shape of the cecum but do not create a pedicle that can twist.","boardPrep":"Step 1: volvulus of the cecum and colon can progress to ischemic gangrene (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: volvulus is one of the four major causes of intestinal obstruction, most often in the sigmoid colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — intestinal obstruction","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An elongated mesentery of the sigmoid colon allows the sigmoid","B":"A narrowed lumen at the ileocecal valve can slow emptying","D":"Absent ganglion cells in the rectum cause constipation from a","E":"Thickened teniae coli change the shape of the cecum but"}},{"id":"sdl22_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has two days of cramping pain and bilious vomiting. He has never had abdominal surgery and has no groin or ventral hernia. The abdomen is mildly distended and the pulse is 96/min. CT shows dilated small-bowel loops lying behind the right colon inside an abnormal peritoneal recess. Which developmental error most likely produced this recess?","choices":{"A":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon","B":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incomplete fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the posterior abdominal wall leaves retrocolic pockets that can trap and obstruct small intestine. Bilious vomiting and dilated loops behind a right-sided colon, without prior surgery, point to that congenital recess. A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus forms a diverticulum, sinus, or fistula rather than a pocket behind the colon. Failed return of the herniated midgut leaves bowel outside the abdomen within a sac at the umbilicus. Reversed rotation of the midgut loop places the transverse colon behind the duodenum instead of forming a right-sided recess. Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon would misplace the left colon and would not open a recess behind the right colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hernias and adhesions are leading causes of small bowel obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: internal hernias can occur without surgical history when mesenteric fusion is abnormal (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 1 — midgut rotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus forms a diverticulum,","C":"Failed return of the herniated midgut leaves bowel outside the","D":"Reversed rotation of the midgut loop places the transverse colon","E":"Arrested descent of the sigmoid colon would misplace the left"}},{"id":"sdl22_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has 18 hours of fever, anorexia, and right upper quadrant pain. He has had no cough or jaundice. Temperature is 38.4°C, the pulse is 110/min, and tenderness is greatest just below the right costal margin. The leukocyte count is 15,800/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). Ultrasound shows a normal gallbladder and an inflamed appendix lying beneath the right hepatic lobe at the end of a high-riding colon. Which developmental process most likely failed?","choices":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut","B":"Caudal descent of the cecal bud","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After rotation the cecal bud lies in the right upper quadrant and normally descends into the right iliac fossa, so failed caudal descent leaves a subhepatic appendix. Fever, leukocytosis, and an inflamed appendix beside a normal gallbladder explain the right upper quadrant pain. Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut is what places the colon on the right, and its failure would leave the colon on the left. Fusion of the descending mesocolon attaches the left colon to the posterior wall and does not set cecal height. Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen is not a step in forming the appendix, which is not laid down as a solid cord. Return of loops from the umbilical cord brings the bowel back into the abdomen, and failure leaves an umbilical wall defect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: periumbilical pain migrating to McBurney point is the classic presentation when the appendix is in its usual position (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: atypical appendix positions delay diagnosis and raise perforation risk (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Counterclockwise rotation of the midgut is what places the colon","C":"Fusion of the descending mesocolon attaches the left colon to","D":"Recanalization of the appendiceal lumen is not a step in","E":"Return of loops from the umbilical cord brings the bowel"}},{"id":"sdl22_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has a flat perineum and has passed no stool. He has fed poorly since an otherwise uncomplicated delivery. There is no anal opening, and the abdomen is becoming distended. Over the next day, green particulate material appears in the urine. Contrast imaging shows a fistula from the rectum into the urethra. Which embryologic error most likely caused this malformation?","choices":{"A":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum","B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum","E":"Persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Arrested caudal growth of the urorectal septum leaves the hindgut opening in the urethra, producing a rectourinary fistula. A flat perineum without an anal opening, followed by meconium in the urine, is the result of that incomplete cloacal separation. Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm would disturb the anal pit and would not open the rectum into the urinary tract. Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen is not how the rectum forms, and it would not create a fistula into the urethra. Absent neural crest cells in the rectum produce a narrow distal segment with a patent anus and no urinary fistula. A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus connects the ileum to the umbilicus rather than the rectum to the urethra.","boardPrep":"Step 1: meconium in the urine of a newborn boy signals a rectourinary tract communication (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: anorectal malformations often occur with vertebral, cardiac, renal, and limb defects in VACTERL association (First Aid Genetics, VACTERL).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Failed proliferation of proctodeal ectoderm would disturb the anal pit","C":"Failed recanalization of the rectal lumen is not how the","D":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum produce a narrow","E":"A persistent vitelline duct at the umbilicus connects the ileum"}},{"id":"sdl22_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has passed no stool and has mild abdominal distension. Prenatal ultrasound had been reported as normal. There is no anal opening in the usual position, and meconium is present at the vaginal introitus. Vital signs are stable, and a radiograph shows several dilated bowel loops without free air. Abnormal partitioning between which two embryonic compartments most likely caused this finding?","choices":{"A":"Allantoic duct and yolk sac","B":"Midgut and hindgut loops","C":"Urogenital sinus and anorectal canal","D":"Mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts","E":"Bladder and the urachus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The urorectal septum normally separates the urogenital sinus from the anorectal canal, and incomplete partitioning leaves a rectovaginal fistula. Meconium at the vaginal introitus with no normally placed anus shows that the hindgut still opens into the urogenital compartment. The allantoic duct and yolk sac are extraembryonic structures and do not partition the perineum. The midgut and hindgut loops meet near the transverse colon, far from the vaginal opening. The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form internal reproductive ducts and do not separate the rectum from the vagina. The bladder and the urachus meet along the anterior abdominal wall, and a defect there leaks urine at the umbilicus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectovaginal fistula is the female counterpart of the rectourethral fistula (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: the cloaca is endoderm-lined and bounded ventrally by surface ectoderm (Langman Ch. 15).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The allantoic duct and yolk sac are extraembryonic structures and","B":"The midgut and hindgut loops meet near the transverse colon,","D":"The mesonephric and paramesonephric ducts form internal reproductive ducts and","E":"The bladder and the urachus meet along the anterior abdominal"}},{"id":"sdl22_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":2,"stem":"A 1-day-old boy has not passed meconium, and his abdomen is distended. He was born at term and his urine has stayed clear. A normally placed anal dimple is present, and a thin bluish membrane bulges at its center. No meconium appears in the urine or on the perineum, and imaging shows no fistula. The persistent tissue separates the hindgut from which structure?","choices":{"A":"Allantois","B":"Urachus","C":"Phallus","D":"Bladder","E":"Anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"This form of imperforate anus is persistence of the anal membrane, which keeps the hindgut closed off from the anal pit, the ectodermal depression of the proctodeum. A normally placed dimple with a bulging membrane and no fistula shows that the barrier is the membrane rather than a misplaced rectal opening. The allantois runs from the cloaca toward the umbilicus and does not face the hindgut across this membrane. The urachus is the fibrous remnant of the allantois between the bladder dome and the umbilicus. The phallus is formed by the genital tubercle, anterior to the anal region, and is not sealed by this membrane. The bladder develops from the urogenital sinus, anterior to the partition that divides the cloaca.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an isolated membrane without a fistula distinguishes this lesion from septal defects (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: the anal membrane is the posterior portion of the cloacal membrane (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 4 — intestinal atresia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The allantois runs from the cloaca toward the umbilicus and","B":"The urachus is the fibrous remnant of the allantois between","C":"The phallus is formed by the genital tubercle, anterior to","D":"The bladder develops from the urogenital sinus, anterior to the"}},{"id":"sdl22_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has a painful, bleeding perianal ulcer for three months. He has longstanding HIV infection and has not been taking antiretroviral therapy. The pulse is 82/min and blood pressure is 126/78 mm Hg, and a tender ulcer is visible at the anal margin. The CD4 count is 180/µL (reference range: 500–1,500/µL). Anoscopy shows an ulcerated mass entirely below the pectinate line, and biopsy shows invasive squamous cells. Which artery is the main arterial supply to the region of this tumor?","choices":{"A":"Superior rectal artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Sigmoidal artery","D":"Inferior rectal artery","E":"Median sacral artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the anal canal is ectodermal and is supplied by the inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal artery. A painful squamous carcinoma confined below that line therefore lies in inferior rectal territory. The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal anal canal above the pectinate line. The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that supplies the transverse colon. The sigmoidal artery supplies the sigmoid colon from the inferior mesenteric artery. The median sacral artery descends on the anterior sacrum and is not the main supply of the lower anal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tumors below the line drain to the superficial inguinal nodes (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: HIV and HPV infection raise the risk of anal squamous cancer (general knowledge; no First Aid anal-cancer entry verified).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior rectal artery supplies the endodermal anal canal above","B":"The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that","C":"The sigmoidal artery supplies the sigmoid colon from the inferior","E":"The median sacral artery descends on the anterior sacrum and"}},{"id":"sdl22_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has painless bright red blood coating the stool for two weeks. She has had two vaginal deliveries and has no pain with defecation. The pulse is 76/min, blood pressure is 118/72 mm Hg, and no external anal lesion is seen. Hemoglobin is 13.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Anoscopy shows engorged vascular cushions arising above the pectinate line. Biopsy of the mucosa overlying these cushions would most likely show which epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium","B":"Simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium with ducts"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Above the pectinate line the mucosa is lined by simple columnar epithelium with goblet cells. Painless bleeding from cushions above the line, with a normal hemoglobin, samples that glandular surface. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airways, including the trachea and bronchi. Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells lines the urinary tract from the renal pelvis through much of the urethra. Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli lines small ducts and tubules rather than colorectal mucosa. Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger glandular ducts, such as the ducts of sweat glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids are painless because the region above the line has visceral innervation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hemorrhoids). COMLEX: tumors above the line are typically adenocarcinomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — hemorrhoids","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines the airways, including the trachea","C":"Transitional epithelium with umbrella cells lines the urinary tract from","D":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with microvilli lines small ducts and tubules","E":"Stratified cuboidal epithelium lines larger glandular ducts, such as the"}},{"id":"sdl22_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has sharp anal pain with every bowel movement and streaks of blood on the paper for three weeks. He has hard stools and has never been told he has inflammatory bowel disease. The pulse is 74/min and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. Examination shows a posterior midline tear at the anal verge. The tear runs from the outer margin to just below the pectinate line. The epithelium at the site of this tear derives from which embryonic tissue?","choices":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum","C":"Endoderm of the allantois","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube","E":"Ectoderm of the anal pit"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Below the pectinate line the tear is an anal fissure in ectoderm of the anal pit. Sharp pain on defecation and a posterior midline tear place the injury in that somatically sensitive segment. Endoderm of the hindgut lines the canal above the pectinate line, where the epithelium is columnar. Mesoderm of the urorectal septum contributes to the perineal body and does not form this surface lining. Endoderm of the allantois contributes to the bladder and urachus rather than the anal lining. Ectoderm of the neural tube forms the central nervous system and does not cover the anal canal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anal fissures are painful because they lie below the line in somatically innervated tissue (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anal fissure). COMLEX: most fissures occur in the posterior midline (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal disorders","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Endoderm of the hindgut lines the canal above the pectinate","B":"Mesoderm of the urorectal septum contributes to the perineal body","C":"Endoderm of the allantois contributes to the bladder and urachus","D":"Ectoderm of the neural tube forms the central nervous system"}},{"id":"sdl22_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-day-old boy has not passed meconium and now has abdominal distension and bilious vomiting. He was born at term and has passed urine normally. The abdomen is distended and the pulse is 150/min. Rectal examination produces an explosive release of stool. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectosigmoid with the colon proximal to it measuring 4 cm in diameter. Which mechanism best explains the dilation of the proximal colon?","choices":{"A":"Tonic contraction of the distal aganglionic segment","B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment","E":"Fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hirschsprung disease leaves the distal bowel without ganglia that normally signal relaxation, so that segment stays tonically contracted and the innervated colon above it dilates. Delayed meconium, explosive stool after rectal examination, and a narrow rectosigmoid with proximal dilation show this functional obstruction. Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment would narrow that segment, but the enema shows it is the wide portion. Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium plugs the terminal ileum and does not leave a narrow rectosigmoid transition. Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment would not produce a narrow rectosigmoid with a dilated colon above it. A fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury is an acquired scar and would not be present in the first days of life.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the narrow segment is the diseased one, and the dilated bowel above it is normal (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: Hirschsprung disease is associated with Down syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — defecation reflex","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Absent ganglia in the dilated proximal segment would narrow that","C":"Mechanical obstruction by inspissated meconium plugs the terminal ileum and","D":"Excess ganglion cells in the distal segment would not produce","E":"A fibrotic stricture from ischemic injury is an acquired scar"}},{"id":"sdl22_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-week-old girl has chronic constipation and abdominal distension. Her older brother required a colonic resection in infancy for a similar obstruction. The abdomen is distended and vital signs are normal. Contrast enema shows a narrow rectum with a transition zone 3 cm above the anal verge. Genetic testing shows a heterozygous loss-of-function variant in RET. Which developmental process was most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop","E":"Craniocaudal migration of enteric precursors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of RET signaling stops the craniocaudal migration of neural crest enteric precursors, leaving the distal bowel without ganglia. A narrow rectum, a transition zone, and an affected sibling match Hirschsprung disease from that failed migration. Recanalization of the colonic lumen, if it failed, would narrow or occlude the lumen itself rather than the nerve supply. Partitioning of the cloaca, when incomplete, opens the rectum into the urinary tract or the vagina. Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle is a mesodermal process, and smooth muscle is still present in the narrow distal segment. Rotation of the primary intestinal loop sets where the bowel lies and how its mesentery is fixed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the most distal bowel is affected because it is reached last by migrating precursors (SDL 22 notes). COMLEX: in about 80% of cases the aganglionic segment extends to the midpoint of the sigmoid (Langman Ch. 15); EDNRB–EDN3 also supports enteric crest migration (SDL 22 notes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Recanalization of the colonic lumen, if it failed, would narrow","B":"Partitioning of the cloaca, when incomplete, opens the rectum into","C":"Differentiation of colonic smooth muscle is a mesodermal process, and","D":"Rotation of the primary intestinal loop sets where the bowel"}},{"id":"sdl22_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-day-old boy has delayed passage of meconium and abdominal distension. He was born at 39 weeks and has had no fever. The abdomen is soft but distended, and the anus is patent. A suction biopsy is taken from the rectum 2 cm above the pectinate line. Absence of ganglion cells in which layers of the bowel wall would confirm the suspected diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and lamina propria","B":"Muscularis mucosae and serosa","C":"Serosa and mesenteric fat","D":"Submucosal and myenteric plexuses","E":"Celiac and mesenteric ganglia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hirschsprung disease is confirmed when neural-crest-derived ganglion cells are absent from the submucosal and myenteric plexuses on a rectal biopsy. Delayed meconium and distension, with the sample taken 2 cm above the pectinate line, target the distal segment these cells fail to reach. Mucosal crypts and the lamina propria hold epithelium and immune cells, not the enteric ganglion cell bodies. The muscularis mucosae and serosa are structural layers and do not contain the parasympathetic ganglia of the bowel. The serosa and mesenteric fat lie outside the intramural nerve plexuses and do not hold these neuron cell bodies. The celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia that sit outside the bowel wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosed by absence of ganglion cells on rectal suction biopsy; RET mutation in the REcTum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: biopsies are taken ≥2 cm above the pectinate line to avoid the normal hypoganglionic zone (general knowledge).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — GI wall layers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal crypts and the lamina propria hold epithelium and immune","B":"The muscularis mucosae and serosa are structural layers and do","C":"The serosa and mesenteric fat lie outside the intramural nerve","E":"The celiac and mesenteric ganglia are prevertebral sympathetic ganglia that"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has three months of fatigue and a change in bowel habit, with a 4 kg weight loss. He has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. The abdomen is soft and the pulse is 88/min. Colonoscopy shows an ulcerated mass in the descending colon 8 cm distal to the splenic flexure. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 14 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which vessel is the main arterial trunk that the surgeon plans to divide at its origin to control the branches feeding this segment?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric artery","B":"Middle colic artery","C":"Superior rectal artery","D":"Inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Internal pudendal artery"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The descending colon is hindgut, and the trunk divided at its origin to devascularize it is the inferior mesenteric artery, by way of the left colic branch. An ulcerated carcinoma with anemia and a carcinoembryonic antigen of 14 ng/mL sits in that hindgut segment. The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, and its territory ends in the transverse colon near the splenic flexure. The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that supplies the transverse colon. The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery and supplies the rectum. The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the pectinate line by way of the inferior rectal artery.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The hindgut runs from the distal third of the transverse colon to the upper anal canal and is supplied by the inferior mesenteric artery (First Aid 2025 GI embryology). COMLEX: Surgical anatomy gives most of the transverse colon to the middle colic artery, while the descending colon depends on the left colic branch of the IMA (Moore Ch. 5).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 2 — colonic ischemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the midgut, and its territory","B":"The middle colic artery is a superior mesenteric branch that","C":"The superior rectal artery is the terminal continuation of the","E":"The internal pudendal artery supplies the anal canal below the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Apply normal cecal descent and mesenteric attachment to explain abnormal colonic positioning, increased mobility, and intestinal entrapment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is undergoing laparoscopic right colectomy for a cecal mass found after a positive fecal immunochemical test. He has had two months of fatigue and has never had abdominal surgery. At operation the ascending colon is lifted medially off the posterior abdominal wall along a nearly bloodless fascial plane, and the mesentery is kept intact. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 8 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which developmental event created this plane?","choices":{"A":"Mesocolon fusing with the posterior wall","B":"Persistence of a free mesentery for the colon","C":"Rotation of the gut loop around its artery","D":"Closure of the umbilical ring after return","E":"Elongation of the colon during cecal descent"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Fusion of the ascending mesocolon with the peritoneum of the posterior body wall creates the bloodless fascial plane used to lift the colon while the mesentery stays intact. The surgeon can separate the ascending colon from the wall along that plane and still keep the mesentery, with its vessels, intact. A persistent free mesentery leaves the colon mobile on a pedicle, so there is no bloodless attachment to the wall. Rotation of the gut loop around its artery sets the position of the bowel but does not attach the colon to the wall. Closure of the umbilical ring completes return of the intestine and is related to defects of the body wall. Elongation of the colon during cecal descent carries the cecum toward the right iliac fossa without forming this fascial layer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The ascending and descending colon become secondarily retroperitoneal when their mesentery fuses to the posterior wall with Toldt fascia between the layers (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: The Toldt plane lets surgeons mobilize the colon without disrupting its mesentery.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 1 — volvulus","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A persistent free mesentery leaves the colon mobile on a","C":"Rotation of the gut loop around its artery sets the","D":"Closure of the umbilical ring completes return of the intestine","E":"Elongation of the colon during cecal descent carries the cecum"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Distinguish anorectal malformations caused by abnormal cloacal or urorectal septum development from imperforate anus caused by failure of anal membrane breakdown.","batch":3,"stem":"A 1-day-old girl has not passed meconium, and her abdomen is distended. No meconium has appeared at the vagina or the urethra. A shallow anal dimple lies in the normal position, and a perineal body separates the urogenital openings. Imaging shows a rectum that ends blindly 3 cm above the pelvic floor and is joined to the perineal skin by a thin fibrous cord. Which mechanism best explains this defect?","choices":{"A":"Persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit","B":"Urorectal septum stopping short caudally","C":"Faulty epithelial-mesenchymal gene signaling","D":"A cloaca too small to divide normally","E":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A rectum ending blindly above the pelvic floor and joined to the perineum by a fibrous cord, with no fistula and a normal perineal body, is rectoanal atresia caused by faulty epithelial-mesenchymal gene signaling. The normally placed dimple and the absence of meconium in the urine or vagina show that the rectal end never reached the skin. A persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit would leave the rectum ending on that membrane, not on a fibrous cord above the pelvic floor. A urorectal septum stopping short caudally shifts the hindgut opening forward into the urethra or the vagina. A cloaca too small to divide normally shifts the hindgut opening forward into the urethra or vagina and produces a fistula. Absent neural crest cells in the rectum cause a narrow distal segment with a patent anal opening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rectoanal atresias reflect faulty epithelial-mesenchymal signaling, rectourethral and rectovaginal fistulas reflect cloacal or urorectal septum defects, and imperforate anus reflects a persistent anal membrane (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: The level and presence of a fistula guide the surgical plan for anorectal malformations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 1 Obj 2 — partitioning by a septum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A persistent membrane at the level of the anal pit","B":"A urorectal septum stopping short caudally shifts the hindgut opening","D":"A cloaca too small to divide normally shifts the hindgut","E":"Absent neural crest cells in the rectum cause a narrow"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Apply the dual embryologic origin of the anal canal to explain the epithelial transition at the pectinate line and the arterial supplies above and below it.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has painless bright red bleeding with defecation for six weeks. He has no pain on stool passage. The pulse is 80/min, blood pressure is 134/78 mm Hg, and no mass is felt at the anal verge. Anoscopy shows a 2 cm mass in the anal canal. Biopsy shows invasive glands of simple columnar epithelium, with no stratified squamous epithelium in any sample, and carcinoembryonic antigen is 7 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which artery most likely provides the main blood supply to the mucosa at this site?","choices":{"A":"Inferior rectal artery","B":"Superior rectal artery","C":"Middle colic artery","D":"Left colic artery","E":"Inferior epigastric artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Simple columnar glandular mucosa marks the anal canal above the pectinate line, a hindgut segment supplied by the superior rectal artery, the continuation of the inferior mesenteric artery. Painless bleeding from columnar mucosa without squamous epithelium places the mass in that endodermal segment. The inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal artery, supplies the stratified squamous canal below the pectinate line. The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon and does not reach the anal mucosa. The left colic artery supplies the descending colon, well above this anal mass. The inferior epigastric artery runs in the anterior abdominal wall and does not supply intestinal mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Above the pectinate line the canal is endoderm-derived columnar mucosa supplied by the superior rectal artery, and below it is ectoderm-derived squamous mucosa supplied by the inferior rectal artery (First Aid 2025 GI anatomy). COMLEX: Adenocarcinoma arises above the pectinate line and squamous cell carcinoma below it.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 5 — anal canal tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The inferior rectal artery, a branch of the internal pudendal","C":"The middle colic artery supplies the transverse colon and does","D":"The left colic artery supplies the descending colon, well above","E":"The inferior epigastric artery runs in the anterior abdominal wall"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Relate neural crest cell migration and RET function to the development of intestinal parasympathetic ganglia and congenital megacolon.","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has progressive abdominal distension. He was born at term and has been feeding poorly. The abdomen is distended and the pulse is 152/min. Digital rectal examination is followed by an explosive release of stool. A suction biopsy 2 cm above the anal verge shows no ganglion cells in the submucosa, and sequencing finds no variant in RET but a loss-of-function variant in a G protein-coupled receptor for a vasoconstrictor peptide. Which gene is most likely affected?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"SPINK1","C":"APC","D":"EDNRB","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent distal ganglion cells with explosive stool after rectal examination indicate Hirschsprung disease, and the mutated G protein-coupled receptor for a vasoconstrictor peptide is EDNRB, an endothelin receptor required for neural crest colonization of the bowel. The usual receptor tyrosine kinase, RET, is not the gene altered here. CFTR defects cause meconium ileus from inspissated meconium, and ganglion cells are still present in the bowel wall. SPINK1 encodes a pancreatic trypsin inhibitor linked to pancreatitis and has no role in enteric neuron migration. APC is a tumor suppressor gene whose loss causes colorectal adenomatous polyposis rather than absent ganglia. FOXP3 defects cause IPEX through loss of regulatory T cells and do not remove enteric ganglion cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration, most often from loss-of-function RET variants, with EDNRB and EDN3 as other causes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Rectal suction biopsy showing absent ganglion cells confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR defects cause meconium ileus from inspissated meconium, and ganglion","B":"SPINK1 encodes a pancreatic trypsin inhibitor linked to pancreatitis and","C":"APC is a tumor suppressor gene whose loss causes colorectal","E":"FOXP3 defects cause IPEX through loss of regulatory T cells"}},{"id":"trial_sdl22_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Relate the midgut and hindgut contributions to the large intestine to their boundaries, epithelial origin and arterial supply.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a polyp removed at screening colonoscopy. She has no rectal bleeding or weight loss. The abdomen is soft. The lesion is a 12 mm tubular adenoma of the descending colon, and histology shows dysplastic columnar epithelium arising from the colonic crypts. The same embryonic gut region that produced this epithelium also forms the epithelial lining of which organ?","choices":{"A":"Vermiform appendix","B":"Distal ileum","C":"Lower anal canal","D":"Gallbladder","E":"Urinary bladder"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The descending colon is a hindgut derivative, and hindgut endoderm also forms the epithelial lining of the urinary bladder and the urethra. Dysplastic columnar epithelium in a descending-colon adenoma therefore shares its embryonic source with the bladder lining. The vermiform appendix develops from the midgut, with the cecum, and does not share the hindgut epithelial source. The distal ileum is a midgut derivative formed with the small intestine, not with the descending colon. The lower anal canal is lined by epithelium derived from the ectoderm of the anal pit. The gallbladder grows from the foregut as an outgrowth of the hepatic diverticulum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hindgut endoderm lines the distal colon, rectum, and upper anal canal and also forms the lining of the bladder and urethra (Langman Ch. 15). COMLEX: Shared hindgut origin explains why anorectal malformations often involve the urinary tract.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 21 Obj 5 — origins of gut components","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The vermiform appendix develops from the midgut, with the cecum,","B":"The distal ileum is a midgut derivative formed with the","C":"The lower anal canal is lined by epithelium derived from","D":"The gallbladder grows from the foregut as an outgrowth of"}}]},{"sdlNumber":23,"title":"SDL 23 — Histology of the Small Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"In which part of the small intestine are goblet cells most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum","B":"Distal duodenum","C":"Proximal jejunum","D":"Terminal ileum","E":"Middle jejunum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Goblet cells increase in number from the duodenum to the terminal ileum. The duodenum and jejunum have fewer of them. The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum. The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum. The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal. The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23); First Aid Gastrointestinal (GI histology). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: on routine H&E, the mucinogen-filled apex of a goblet cell looks empty because the mucinogen is lost during preparation (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal duodenum is marked by Brunner glands, not peak goblet cell density, which is reached toward the distal ileum.","B":"The distal duodenum still has relatively few goblet cells; their density rises steadily to its highest in the ileum.","C":"The proximal jejunum is specialized for absorption with tall plicae and villi, not for maximal goblet cell density, which is ileal.","E":"The middle jejunum has fewer goblet cells than the ileum, where mucus-secreting cells reach their highest density."}},{"id":"sdl23_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":5,"stem":"What portion of the small intestine does the jejunum make up?","choices":{"A":"Lower three-fifths of the small intestine","B":"Middle one-fifth of the small intestine","C":"Upper two-fifths of the small intestine","D":"Upper one-tenth of the small intestine","E":"Lower one-fifth of the small intestine"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The jejunum is about 2.5 m long and makes up the upper two-fifths of the small intestine. The ileum is the lower three-fifths, and the duodenum is about 25 cm long. The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine; it is the upper two-fifths, about 2.5 m long. One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum. The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment. The jejunum is the upper two-fifths. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the small intestine is the longest part of the digestive tract, at more than 6 m (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the ileum ends at the ileocecal junction, where the distal ileum joins the cecum (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The jejunum is not one-fifth of the small intestine.","D":"One-tenth better approximates the short duodenum (about 25 cm), not the 2.5-m jejunum.","E":"The lower part of the small intestine is the ileum, which is three-fifths, not a one-fifth jejunal segment.","A":"The jejunum is the upper two-fifths."}},{"id":"sdl23_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are plicae circulares most numerous?","choices":{"A":"Proximal duodenum next to the pylorus","B":"Middle and distal ileum segments","C":"Terminal ileum at the ileocecal valve","D":"Cecum and proximal ascending colon","E":"Distal duodenum and proximal jejunum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Plicae circulares are most numerous in the distal duodenum and the beginning of the jejunum. They become smaller and fewer by the middle of the ileum; the lecture notes likewise describe the jejunum as having the tallest, most numerous plicae and the distal ileum as markedly reduced. Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them. At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency. The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine. Plicae circulares become reduced in size and frequency in the middle of the ileum rather than reaching their peak there. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on oral contrast, the jejunum has a feathered look because its plicae circulares are taller and more numerous than the ileum's (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: these circular folds are permanent ridges that extend one-half to two-thirds of the way around the lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares only begin about 5 to 6 cm distal to the pylorus, so the pylorus-adjacent duodenum lacks them.","C":"At the terminal ileum plicae are markedly reduced in height and frequency.","D":"The cecum and colon lack plicae circulares and villi altogether; these folds belong to the small intestine.","B":"Plicae circulares become reduced in size and frequency in the middle of the ileum rather than reaching their peak there."}},{"id":"sdl23_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which structure provides the major amplification of the small intestinal luminal surface?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares folds","B":"Mucosal intestinal villi","C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn","D":"Enterocyte microvilli","E":"Lateral cell plications"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Microvilli of the enterocytes provide the largest amplification of the luminal surface (20- to 600-fold), with several thousand per cell. Plicae and villi amplify it less, and lateral plications enlarge the lateral membrane. Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in a malabsorption workup, steatorrhea can be screened for with a Sudan stain for fecal fat (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: under the light microscope, the closely packed microvilli form a striated border on the apex of the enterocyte (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Crypts of Lieberkühn are glands housing stem cells and Paneth cells.","A":"Plicae circulares are the circular folds of the intestinal wall.","B":"Mucosal villi are the finger-like projections of the mucosa.","E":"Lateral plications increase the lateral membrane for transport enzymes."}},{"id":"sdl23_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tissue layer forms the core of a plica circularis?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Lamina propria","C":"Epithelium","D":"Muscularis mucosae","E":"Subserosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Plicae circulares are permanent transverse folds with a core of submucosa, so they are permanent folds that also carry the villi on their surface. Lamina propria forms the core of each villus, a smaller mucosal projection that sits on the folds. Epithelium covers the surface of the folds and villi but does not form their core. Muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa and does not form the core of a plica. Subserosa sits beneath the serosa on the outside of the wall, far from the luminal folds. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, small intestine, plicae circulares.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Plicae circulares are submucosal folds, villi have a lamina propria core, and microvilli are apical cell projections (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Plicae make the valvulae conniventes that cross the full width of dilated small bowel on radiographs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lamina propria forms the core of each","C":"Epithelium covers the surface of the folds","D":"Muscularis mucosae belongs to the mucosa and","E":"Subserosa sits beneath the serosa on the"}},{"id":"sdl23_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":5,"stem":"Which protein anchors the actin core at the tip of each microvillus?","choices":{"A":"Myosin I","B":"Spectrin","C":"Villin","D":"Dystrophin","E":"Titin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The actin microfilaments of the microvillus core are anchored to villin at the tip. Myosin I attaches them to the plasma membrane along the sides. Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip. Dystrophin links actin to laminin in skeletal muscle and is lost in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, not a microvillus tip protein. Titin anchors myosin thick filaments to the Z line of the sarcomere, not the actin core of microvilli. Myosin I attaches the actin core laterally to the microvillus plasma membrane; villin anchors it at the tip. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Enterocytes, Fig. 17.21). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tight junctions in the ileum are relatively impermeable, so solutes must be moved across by active transport (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: elongated mitochondria cluster in the apical enterocyte cytoplasm, between the terminal web and the nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"Spectrin is a terminal web component, not the protein anchoring actin at the microvillus tip.","D":"Dystrophin links actin to laminin in skeletal muscle and is lost in Duchenne muscular dystrophy, not a microvillus tip protein.","E":"Titin anchors myosin thick filaments to the Z line of the sarcomere, not the actin core of microvilli.","A":"Myosin I attaches the actin core laterally to the microvillus plasma membrane; villin anchors it at the tip."}},{"id":"sdl23_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibacterial products are found in Paneth cell secretory vesicles?","choices":{"A":"Lysozyme and α-defensins","B":"Mucinogen and trefoil factors","C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase","D":"Secretin and cholecystokinin","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Paneth cell vesicles contain lysozyme, α-defensins, other glycoproteins, an arginine-rich protein, and zinc. Mucinogen comes from goblet cells, and the hormones come from enteroendocrine cells. Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents. Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules. First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not mucinogen and trefoil factors. First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not secretin and cholecystokinin. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lysozyme digests the cell walls of certain groups of bacteria (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells may occasionally be found in small numbers in the normal colon, and their number may rise in some diseases (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Pepsinogen and gastric lipase are gastric chief cell products, not Paneth cell contents.","E":"Intrinsic factor and gastrin are gastric parietal cell and G-cell products, not Paneth cell antimicrobial granules.","B":"First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not mucinogen and trefoil factors.","D":"First Aid states Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF, not secretin and cholecystokinin."}},{"id":"sdl23_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is the basal portion of a goblet cell intensely basophilic?","choices":{"A":"Abundant smooth ER with lipid droplets","B":"Dense packing of acidic mucinogen granules","C":"Stacked apical mitochondria with actin","D":"Large glycogen stores with many lysosomes","E":"Heterochromatic nucleus with extensive rER"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The narrow basal stem holds a heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes, which make it intensely basophilic. The mucinogen granules sit at the apex. Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem. Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E. Mitochondria do gather in the basal cytoplasm, but the intense basophilia comes from the heterochromatic nucleus, extensive rER, and free ribosomes. The narrow basal stem of a goblet cell is filled with nucleus, rER, and free ribosomes, not glycogen stores and lysosomes. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a large Golgi apparatus forms a wide cup around newly formed mucinogen granules above the goblet cell nucleus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: microvilli are more obvious on immature goblet cells in the deep half of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Smooth ER with lipid droplets characterizes lipid-processing enterocytes, not the basophilic goblet cell stem.","B":"Mucinogen granules fill the apical cup, not the basal stem, and are lost on routine H&E.","C":"Mitochondria do gather in the basal cytoplasm, but the intense basophilia comes from.","D":"The narrow basal stem of a goblet cell is filled with nucleus, rER, and free ribosomes, not glycogen stores and lysosomes."}},{"id":"sdl23_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":5,"stem":"What activates the apical receptors of open-type intestinal enteroendocrine cells?","choices":{"A":"Stretch of the muscularis","B":"Taste receptor stimulation","C":"Vagal acetylcholine release","D":"Circulating thyroid hormone","E":"Lamina propria histamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Open enteroendocrine cells have taste receptors on their apical membrane, and activating them triggers a G-protein cascade and peptide release. Closed cells lie mainly in the lower gland. Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells. Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors. Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Goblet, Paneth, enteroendocrine and M cells, Figs. 17.22–17.23). Textbook enrichment (Pawlina) — detail not in SDL 23 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP released in the small intestine stimulates insulin release from the pancreas (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: somatostatin and histamine from enteroendocrine cells act as paracrine hormones that do not circulate (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Muscularis stretch is a mechanical stimulus to the enteric nervous system, not the activator of apical receptors on open enteroendocrine cells.","C":"Vagal acetylcholine acts from the basal side, whereas open-type cells sense lumen contents through apical taste receptors.","E":"Lamina propria histamine acts from below, not on apical luminal receptors of open-type cells."}},{"id":"sdl23_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is the small intestinal stem cell niche located?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the intestinal villi","B":"Middle third of each villus surface","C":"Within the muscularis mucosae layer","D":"Lower half of the intestinal gland","E":"Within the duodenal submucosal glands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the base of the intestinal gland, and the niche is restricted to the lower half of the gland. Cells then migrate upward onto the villus and are shed at the tip. Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live. The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt. The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche. Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in celiac disease, histology shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: the niche is also called the zone of cell replication (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus tips are where aged cells undergo apoptosis and are shed, not where stem cells live.","B":"The mid-villus holds migrating differentiated cells; proliferation is confined to the crypt.","C":"The muscularis mucosae is a smooth muscle layer, not an epithelial stem cell niche.","E":"Duodenal submucosal (Brunner) glands secrete alkaline mucus and do not host the intestinal stem cell niche."}},{"id":"sdl23_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do aging absorptive cells of the small intestine undergo apoptosis and slough off?","choices":{"A":"Tips of the villi","B":"Base of the crypts","C":"Lower half of the gland","D":"Over Peyer patch domes","E":"Between gland openings"},"correct":"A","explanation":"New cells arise from stem cells at the base of the intestinal glands and migrate upward onto the villi, reaching the villus tips, where they undergo apoptosis and are shed into the lumen. The base of the crypts is where Paneth cells, which migrate downward, reside. The lower half of the gland is the stem cell niche where new cells are produced. The domes over Peyer patches hold M cells for antigen sampling, not the usual extrusion zone. Shedding between gland openings is the pattern described for the large intestine, which has no villi. Sources: Pawlina ch 5 and ch 17, epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Small intestinal cells move from crypt stem cells up to the villus tips, while Paneth cells move down (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Rapid epithelial turnover explains mucositis and diarrhea after chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The base of the crypts is where","C":"The lower half of the gland is","D":"The domes over Peyer patches hold M","E":"Shedding between gland openings is the pattern"}},{"id":"sdl23_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Increased expression of which transcription factor commits crypt progenitor cells to the secretory lineage?","choices":{"A":"Notch1","B":"Math1","C":"Cdx2","D":"Pdx1","E":"Ptf1a"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cells committed to the secretory lineage (goblet, enteroendocrine, and Paneth cells) have increased Math1 expression. When Math1 is inhibited, cells follow the default pathway and become enterocytes. Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duodenum during gut tube patterning and is upregulated in the developing pancreatic buds; it does not choose the crypt secretory lineage. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the small intestine). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enteroendocrine cells appear to divide only once before differentiating (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: enteroendocrine cells migrate with absorptive and goblet cells, but more slowly (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Pdx1 (pancreatic and duodenal homeobox 1) specifies the duodenum during gut tube patterning and is upregulated in the developing pancreatic buds; it does not choose the crypt secretory lineage."}},{"id":"sdl23_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Where are Peyer patches preferentially located in the ileal wall?","choices":{"A":"Along the mesenteric border itself","B":"Within the inner muscularis externa","C":"Opposite the mesenteric attachment","D":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium","E":"Within the myenteric nerve plexus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Peyer patches are large and numerous in the ileum and lie preferentially on the side of the intestine opposite the mesenteric attachment. They sit in the lamina propria and often extend into the submucosa. Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border. Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa. Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa. The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Small intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.17–17.19). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches are unencapsulated lymphoid tissue in the lamina propria and submucosa of the ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: in gross specimens, these aggregated nodules look like clusters of white specks (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches favor the antimesenteric wall, not the mesenteric border.","B":"Peyer patches sit in the lamina propria and submucosa, not within the muscularis externa.","D":"Peyer patches lie in the lamina propria and submucosa, not beneath the serosa.","E":"The myenteric plexus is a nerve network between muscle layers, not the site of Peyer patches."}},{"id":"sdl23_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"From what is the secretory component of secretory IgA derived?","choices":{"A":"Plasma cell J chain peptide","B":"Cleaved polymeric Ig receptor","C":"Goblet cell mucin glycoprotein","D":"Paneth cell defensin precursor","E":"Hepatocyte bile acid carrier"},"correct":"B","explanation":"After the receptor-dIgA complex reaches the apical surface, the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor is cleaved. Its bound extracellular portion becomes the secretory component. The J chain comes from the plasma cell and links the IgA dimer. Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA. Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component. The secretory component is the cleaved extracellular part of the enterocyte pIgR that stays bound to the IgA dimer, not a hepatocyte bile acid carrier. Pawlina describes the J chain as a plasma-cell product within dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the polymeric Ig receptor carries dIgA across the enterocyte by transcytosis; only at the apical surface is it cleaved, and the retained fragment is the secretory component (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the receptor is a 75-kDa transmembrane glycoprotein synthesized by enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Goblet cell mucin forms the protective mucus gel, not the secretory component of sIgA.","D":"Paneth cell defensins are innate antimicrobial peptides, unrelated to the secretory component.","E":"The secretory component is the cleaved extracellular part of the enterocyte pIgR that stays bound to the IgA dimer, not a hepatocyte bile acid carrier.","A":"Pawlina describes the J chain as a plasma-cell product within dimeric IgA."}},{"id":"sdl23_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody do most plasma cells in the intestinal lamina propria secrete?","choices":{"A":"Monomeric IgG","B":"Pentameric IgM","C":"Monomeric IgE","D":"Monomeric IgD","E":"Dimeric IgA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Most lamina propria plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA rather than IgG. Some make pentameric IgM or IgE, and IgM can use a similar transcytotic route. IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA. IgD is a membrane B-cell receptor on mature naive B cells alongside IgM, not the antibody lamina propria plasma cells secrete. IgG is the more common antibody elsewhere, but most lamina propria plasma cells of the intestine secrete dimeric IgA. Some intestinal plasma cells produce pentameric IgM, but most secrete dimeric IgA. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (GALT; Immunoglobulin-mediated responses, Fig. 17.24; Folder 17.5). Aligned with SDL 23 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B cells stimulated in Peyer patch germinal centers become IgA-secreting plasma cells that settle in the lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: some IgE binds lamina propria mast cells and sensitizes them to luminal antigens (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"IgE is made by only some lamina propria plasma cells; most make dimeric IgA.","D":"IgD is a membrane B-cell receptor on mature naive B cells alongside IgM, not the antibody lamina propria plasma cells secrete.","A":"IgG is the more common antibody elsewhere, but most lamina propria plasma cells of the intestine secrete dimeric IgA.","B":"Some intestinal plasma cells produce pentameric IgM, but most secrete dimeric IgA."}},{"id":"sdl23_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has burning epigastric pain that starts about an hour after meals and is partly relieved by food. He has taken ibuprofen daily for knee osteoarthritis for two years. The abdomen is soft, with mild epigastric tenderness and no peritoneal signs. Biopsy just distal to the pylorus shows branched tubular glands in the submucosa, and fluid from those glands has a bicarbonate concentration of 70 mEq/L. Besides protecting the mucosa, what does this secretion do?","choices":{"A":"Secretes lysozyme and defensins into the crypt lumens","B":"Brings chyme near the pancreatic enzyme pH optimum","C":"Absorbs conjugated bile acids from the passing chyme","D":"Produces intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 uptake","E":"Concentrates dimeric IgA for transport to the lumen"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These bicarbonate-rich submucosal glands raise the pH of acidic gastric chyme into the range where pancreatic enzymes work best. A biopsy just beyond the pylorus showing branched tubular glands identifies that secretion. Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at crypt bases, not from these alkaline submucosal glands. Conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by ileal enterocytes, far distal to this biopsy site. Intrinsic factor is produced by gastric parietal cells, which are not in this duodenal sample. Dimeric IgA reaches the lumen by enterocyte transcytosis, not by secretion from these glands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bicarbonate from Brunner glands neutralizes acid, and pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion is stimulated by secretin (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI secretory products). COMLEX: the duct of each Brunner gland opens into the lumen of an intestinal crypt (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lysozyme and defensins come from Paneth cells at crypt","C":"Conjugated bile acids are reclaimed by ileal enterocytes, far","D":"Intrinsic factor is produced by gastric parietal cells, which","E":"Dimeric IgA reaches the lumen by enterocyte transcytosis, not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a segment of small bowel removed after a stab wound. She had been well, with no chronic diarrhea or weight loss. At laparotomy the injured loop lay free on a mesentery in the left upper abdomen. The specimen has tall villi and tall, closely spaced circular folds, with no submucosal glands and no large lymphoid aggregates. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Ileum","C":"Jejunum","D":"Appendix","E":"Rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Tall villi with tall, closely spaced circular folds, and neither submucosal glands nor large lymphoid aggregates, identify jejunum. Trauma resection of that pattern is a mid-small-bowel segment rather than duodenum or ileum. Submucosal alkaline glands are the landmark of the proximal duodenum, and this specimen has none. Large aggregated lymphoid nodules are characteristic of ileum, and this specimen lacks them. The appendix is a colonic outpouching and does not bear tall circular folds of this kind. Rectal mucosa is flat, without villi or plicae circulares.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileum contains Peyer patches, and its plicae circulares are limited to its proximal part (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the jejunum gradually changes its morphology as it becomes the ileum, with no sharp boundary (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Submucosal alkaline glands are the landmark of the proximal","B":"Large aggregated lymphoid nodules are characteristic of ileum, and","D":"The appendix is a colonic outpouching and does not","E":"Rectal mucosa is flat, without villi or plicae"}},{"id":"sdl23_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":5,"stem":"A 39-year-old man is stabbed in the posterior abdomen. He is tender in the right upper quadrant, the pulse is 108/min, and blood pressure is 104/68 mm Hg. CT shows the injury confined to the portion of duodenum fixed against the posterior abdominal wall. Which layer forms the outer covering of this segment?","choices":{"A":"Serosa","B":"Mesothelium","C":"Submucosa","D":"Muscularis","E":"Adventitia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The retroperitoneal duodenum is fixed to the posterior wall and is covered by adventitia rather than a free serosa. The stab wound is limited to that fixed segment. A serosal coat covers intraperitoneal bowel, which this fixed duodenal segment is not. Mesothelium lines a peritoneal surface, and this segment has lost that free surface. Submucosa lies deep to the mucosa and is not the outer covering of the wall. Muscularis is an inner muscle coat and is not the outermost layer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the duodenum is about 25 cm long and is the first, shortest, and widest part of the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the myenteric plexus between the two muscularis externa layers contains postsynaptic parasympathetic neuron cell bodies (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"A serosal coat covers intraperitoneal bowel, which this fixed","B":"Mesothelium lines a peritoneal surface, and this segment has","C":"Submucosa lies deep to the mucosa and is not","D":"Muscularis is an inner muscle coat and is not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has months of diarrhea and fatigue. A sister has autoimmune thyroid disease, and she has eaten wheat daily. She is pale and the abdomen is soft, without a rash. Hemoglobin is 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL) and IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 96 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL). Duodenal biopsy shows a flat mucosa, crypt hyperplasia, and many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which surface-amplifying structure is lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae","B":"Villi","C":"Crypts","D":"Teniae","E":"Rugae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This is celiac disease, and the flat mucosa means the villi have been lost while crypts hypertrophy. Iron deficiency, a high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, and intraepithelial lymphocytes support that injury. Plicae are the circular folds of submucosa, and a flat mucosal biopsy does not describe their loss. Crypts are hyperplastic in this biopsy, so they have not been lost. Teniae are longitudinal muscle bands of the colon and are not a small-bowel surface amplifier. Rugae are gastric folds and are not present in a duodenal biopsy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and/or proximal jejunum and is linked to HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac serology includes IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase and anti-endomysial antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae are the circular folds of submucosa, and a","C":"Crypts are hyperplastic in this biopsy, so they have","D":"Teniae are longitudinal muscle bands of the colon and","E":"Rugae are gastric folds and are not present in"}},{"id":"sdl23_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has leg swelling and pale, bulky stools. He has never had abdominal surgery, and there is no jaundice. He has pitting edema to the knees and a soft abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Small-bowel biopsy shows dilated central lymphatic capillaries in the villi. Why do absorbed lipoprotein particles normally enter these vessels rather than villus blood capillaries?","choices":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries lack endothelial fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries actively pump in triglyceride","C":"Portal venous blood rapidly degrades fat particles","D":"Lipoproteins cross the tight junctions into lymph","E":"Particles are too large for the blood capillaries"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chylomicrons are too large to enter villus blood capillaries, so they pass into the central lacteal. Edema, low albumin, fatty stools, and dilated villus lymphatics show that this route is blocked. Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, so lack of fenestrations is not why fat particles avoid them. Lymphatic capillaries do not actively pump triglyceride into their lumen. Portal blood does not rapidly degrade chylomicrons, which normally bypass the portal vein. Lipoproteins do not cross tight junctions to reach lymph.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an enterocyte packages resynthesized lipid as chylomicrons and discharges it into the intercellular space (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: smooth muscle from the muscularis mucosae runs alongside each lacteal and may squeeze lymph out of it (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus blood capillaries are fenestrated, so lack of fenestrations","B":"Lymphatic capillaries do not actively pump triglyceride into their","C":"Portal blood does not rapidly degrade chylomicrons, which normally","D":"Lipoproteins do not cross tight junctions to reach"}},{"id":"sdl23_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old healthy man in a nutrition study has a jejunal biopsy taken while he is absorbing a mixed meal. He has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is soft and vital signs are normal. Electron microscopy shows widened spaces between the lateral surfaces of adjacent enterocytes. What structural change explains these widened spaces?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of the apical tight junction protein strands","B":"Loss of the underlying enterocyte basal lamina sheet","C":"Separation of interdigitating cell-surface folds","D":"Shedding of aged enterocytes at the villus tips","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules at the cell apex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"During absorption the interdigitating lateral folds of neighboring enterocytes separate, opening the intercellular space as fluid and solute leave the cell. The biopsy was taken while absorption was active, which is when those spaces widen. The apical tight-junction strands are not ruptured by ordinary absorption. The enterocyte basal lamina remains intact during this widening. Shedding of aged cells occurs at the villus tip and does not open lateral spaces along the villus. Fusion of mucinogen granules is a goblet-cell event and does not widen lateral spaces.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the duodenum and jejunum, hypotonic contents allow water and small solutes to cross leaky tight junctions by solvent drag (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: contraction of the terminal web spreads the microvilli apart and exposes more surface area (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The apical tight-junction strands are not ruptured by ordinary","B":"The enterocyte basal lamina remains intact during this","D":"Shedding of aged cells occurs at the villus tip","E":"Fusion of mucinogen granules is a goblet-cell event and"}},{"id":"sdl23_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has had watery diarrhea for four months, without fever or blood. Stool cultures and a test for Clostridium difficile toxin have been negative. She is not volume depleted, and the abdomen is soft and nontender. A jejunal biopsy shows, at the base of the glands, cells with basophilic basal cytoplasm and large eosinophilic refractile apical vesicles. What probably causes that eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes","B":"Glycogen granule stores","C":"Neutral mucin polymer","D":"Arginine-rich protein","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Paneth-cell granules are eosinophilic because they are packed with arginine-rich antimicrobial proteins. Basal basophilia and apical refractile vesicles at the gland base identify those cells. Heme-rich cytochromes are not the material stored in these apical vesicles. Glycogen does not produce large refractile eosinophilic apical granules. Neutral mucin is pale on routine stains and belongs to goblet cells, not these basal cells. Iron-ferritin deposits are not the content of these apical secretory vesicles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Paneth cells in the crypts of Lieberkühn secrete defensins, lysozyme, and TNF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: zinc is also stored in these refractile apical vesicles (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heme-rich cytochromes are not the material stored in these apical","B":"Glycogen does not produce large refractile eosinophilic apical","C":"Neutral mucin is pale on routine stains and belongs","E":"Iron-ferritin deposits are not the content of these apical secretory"}},{"id":"sdl23_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 55-year-old man has chronic loose stools without bleeding or weight loss. He does not use laxatives. An ileal biopsy stained with hematoxylin and eosin shows many epithelial cells with pale, empty-looking apical cups and narrow basal stems. Why does the apex of these cells look empty?","choices":{"A":"Lipid droplets dissolve in alcohol","B":"Mucinogen is lost during preparation","C":"Glycogen leaches out during fixation","D":"Apical vacuoles are filled with water","E":"Dying cells lose their organelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"These are goblet cells, and their mucinogen is extracted during routine preparation, leaving an empty-looking cup. The narrow basal stem and pale apex are the usual appearance after that loss. Lipid droplets are not the material that normally fills these apical cups. Glycogen is not stored as a large apical cup in these cells. The apical cup is not a water-filled vacuole in the living cell. These pale cells are not dying cells that have lost their organelles.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in Barrett esophagus, the squamous epithelium is replaced by nonciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Barrett esophagus). COMLEX: the apical mucinogen mass in a goblet cell pushes on and distorts the shape of neighboring cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Lipid droplets are not the material that normally fills","C":"Glycogen is not stored as a large apical cup","D":"The apical cup is not a water-filled vacuole in","E":"These pale cells are not dying cells that have"}},{"id":"sdl23_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old woman has episodic flushing and watery diarrhea that continues while she is fasting. A proton-pump inhibitor started for presumed reflux has not helped. The face is flushed and the abdomen is soft, without a mass. Chromogranin A is 280 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). A duodenal biopsy shows nests of uniform cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin that stain for synaptophysin. From which epithelial cell type does this tumor most likely arise?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Absorptive enterocytes","C":"Intermediate goblet cells","D":"Microfold epithelial cells","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Nests of uniform duodenal cells with salt-and-pepper chromatin and synaptophysin staining are enteroendocrine cells, and the elevated chromogranin A marks that same lineage. Flushing and fasting watery diarrhea fit hormone release from those cells. Absorptive enterocytes give rise to ordinary adenomas and carcinomas, not this neuroendocrine tumor. Goblet cells form mucin-producing tumors rather than a chromogranin-positive neuroendocrine tumor. Microfold cells sample antigen over lymphoid nodules and are not the source of this tumor. Myoepithelial basket cells are not a normal epithelial population of the duodenal mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin from the small intestine initiates gastric and intestinal motility (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: VIP, bombesin, and enkephalins are neurocrine peptides secreted by submucosal and myenteric nerve cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 2 — the four GI hormones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Absorptive enterocytes give rise to ordinary adenomas and carcinomas,","C":"Goblet cells form mucin-producing tumors rather than a chromogranin-positive neuroendocrine","D":"Microfold cells sample antigen over lymphoid nodules and are","E":"Myoepithelial basket cells are not a normal epithelial population"}},{"id":"sdl23_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman develops watery diarrhea five days after starting cytotoxic chemotherapy for lymphoma. She has had no sick contacts. She is afebrile, the abdomen is soft, and bowel sounds are active. The leukocyte count is 2,200/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL), and biopsy shows short, denuded villi. Why is the small-intestinal epithelium damaged so quickly?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells migrating upward onto the villi","B":"Slow turnover of the mature villus enterocyte layer","C":"Continuous replacement from dividing crypt cells","D":"Stem cells concentrated near the outer villus tips","E":"Mature enterocytes dividing on the villus tips"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Villi are continuously replaced by cells born in the crypts, so killing those dividing cells strips the surface within days. Diarrhea a few days after cytotoxic chemotherapy, with short denuded villi, follows that rapid turnover. Paneth cells migrate downward, not upward onto the villi. Mature villus enterocytes turn over in days, so their replacement is not slow. The stem-cell niche sits in the crypt, not near the villus tips. Mature enterocytes on the villus tips do not divide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells in the niche usually undergo one or two divisions before committing to absorptive or goblet fates (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: cells shed at the villus tip are replaced by cells migrating upward from the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth cells migrate downward, not upward onto the","B":"Mature villus enterocytes turn over in days, so their","D":"The stem-cell niche sits in the crypt, not near","E":"Mature enterocytes on the villus tips do not"}},{"id":"sdl23_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old healthy volunteer has crypt cells radiolabeled. He has no diarrhea. Days later most labeled cells are on the villi. One labeled population has moved down to the gland base, survives about four weeks, and no longer divides. Which cells are these?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enterocytes","D":"Stem cells","E":"Plasma cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The cells that migrate downward, stop dividing, and live for weeks at the gland base are Paneth cells. The other labeled cohort has moved up onto the villi, which is the path of the shorter-lived lineages. Goblet cells migrate up the villus and are shed, rather than settling at the gland base for weeks. Enterocytes migrate onto the villus and are shed within days. Stem cells remain in the niche and continue to divide. Plasma cells reside in the lamina propria and are not labeled crypt epithelial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dead Paneth cells are replaced by differentiation of a nearby committed cell in the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: stem cells in the crypt base give rise to every mature cell type of the intestinal epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Goblet cells migrate up the villus and are shed,","C":"Enterocytes migrate onto the villus and are shed within","D":"Stem cells remain in the niche and continue to","E":"Plasma cells reside in the lamina propria and are"}},{"id":"sdl23_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has had loose stools since infancy. A genetic variant blocks the transcription factor that commits intestinal stem cells to a secretory fate. Growth is normal and the abdomen is soft. A crypt-cell assay confirms failure of that secretory program. Which cells would differentiating crypt cells mainly become?","choices":{"A":"Mucus-secreting goblet cells","B":"Antimicrobial Paneth cells","C":"Hormone-secreting endocrine cells","D":"Absorptive enterocytes","E":"Antigen-sampling microfold cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"When the secretory-fate factor is blocked, crypt daughters default to absorptive enterocytes. Loose stools from infancy fit loss of the secretory lineages that this factor normally produces. Goblet cells are a secretory lineage and are not produced when that fate is blocked. Paneth cells are secretory cells and depend on the same blocked program. Enteroendocrine cells are part of the secretory lineage and are not the default. Microfold cells are a specialized secretory-related population and are not the default product.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intermediate cells committed to the absorptive fate lose their secretory granules and gather mitochondria, rER, and ribosomes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: intermediate cells committed to the goblet fate develop a small rounded cluster of granules under the apical membrane (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Goblet cells are a secretory lineage and are not","B":"Paneth cells are secretory cells and depend on the","C":"Enteroendocrine cells are part of the secretory lineage and","E":"Microfold cells are a specialized secretory-related population and are"}},{"id":"sdl23_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 23-year-old man develops fever and abdominal pain a week after returning from South Asia, where he did not take antibiotic prophylaxis. Temperature is 39.1°C, the pulse is 104/min, and the abdomen is tender. Blood culture grows Salmonella. The organisms crossed ileal epithelium where it covers large lymphatic nodules. Which route did they most likely use?","choices":{"A":"Microfold cell transcytosis","B":"Paracellular tight junctions","C":"Goblet cell mucus exocytosis","D":"Paneth cell phagocytosis","E":"Lacteal lymphatic uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Salmonella crosses the epithelium over Peyer patches by transcytosis through microfold cells. Fever after travel and bacteremia from that ileal site match this entry path. Paracellular tight junctions are not the route these organisms use over the lymphoid nodules. Goblet-cell mucus release does not carry bacteria across the epithelium. Paneth cells secrete antimicrobials and do not phagocytose bacteria as the entry path. Lacteals receive chylomicrons and are not the epithelial crossing used here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peyer patches contain microfold cells that sample antigens and present them to immune cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Peyer patches). COMLEX: M cells have microfolds and a thin glycocalyx on their apical surface, not microvilli (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Paracellular tight junctions are not the route these organisms","C":"Goblet-cell mucus release does not carry bacteria across the","D":"Paneth cells secrete antimicrobials and do not phagocytose bacteria","E":"Lacteals receive chylomicrons and are not the epithelial crossing used"}},{"id":"sdl23_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has had three episodes of giardiasis in two years. She is not taking immunosuppressants. She is afebrile and the abdomen is soft, without lymphadenopathy. Serum IgA is undetectable (reference range: 70–400 mg/dL), IgG is 1,100 mg/dL (reference range: 700–1,600 mg/dL), and IgM is normal. Which mucosal defense is mainly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Lysis of luminal bacteria by Paneth cell enzymes","B":"Sensitization of the lamina propria mast cells","C":"Opsonization of invading bacteria for neutrophils","D":"Blocking of microbial adhesion to the epithelium","E":"Activation of the classical complement pathway"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretory IgA blocks microbes from sticking to the epithelium, and its absence leaves that barrier down. Undetectable IgA with preserved IgG and IgM, plus repeated Giardia, localizes the defect to that adhesive block. Paneth-cell enzymes are intact in isolated IgA deficiency and are not the missing defense. Mast-cell sensitization is an IgE event and is not the function lost with absent IgA. Neutrophil opsonization is mainly an IgG function, and IgG is normal here. Classical complement activation depends on other isotypes, not on secretory IgA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secretory IgA binds an HIV envelope glycoprotein and prevents viral attachment and internalization (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the GALT works as an immunologic barrier along the whole length of the gastrointestinal tract (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Paneth-cell enzymes are intact in isolated IgA deficiency and","B":"Mast-cell sensitization is an IgE event and is not","C":"Neutrophil opsonization is mainly an IgG function, and IgG","E":"Classical complement activation depends on other isotypes, not on secretory"}},{"id":"sdl23_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has recurrent bacterial enteritis and grows poorly. She is not taking immunosuppressants. She is small for her age and the abdomen is soft. Lamina propria plasma cells secrete normal J-chain-linked IgA dimers, but intestinal secretions contain no IgA. Serum IgG is 900 mg/dL (reference range: 700–1,600 mg/dL). Which step most likely fails?","choices":{"A":"Class switching of mucosal B cells to the IgA isotype","B":"Linking of IgA monomers together by the J chain","C":"Homing of IgA plasma cells to the lamina propria","D":"Antigen uptake by the Peyer patch dendritic cells","E":"Receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IgA dimers are made normally but never reach the lumen, so receptor-mediated transcytosis across enterocytes has failed. Recurrent enteric infection with absent secretory IgA and intact plasma-cell dimers localizes the block to that transport step. Class switching has already produced IgA plasma cells, so that step succeeded. The J chain is linking the dimers, so monomer assembly is intact. IgA plasma cells are already in the lamina propria, so homing occurred. Antigen uptake by dendritic cells is upstream of plasma-cell formation, which is intact here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IgM molecules use a similar receptor-mediated transcytosis pathway to reach the mucosal surface (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: dendritic cells and lymphocytes in the M-cell recess sample luminal antigen and can stimulate specific antibody production (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Class switching has already produced IgA plasma cells, so","B":"The J chain is linking the dimers, so monomer","C":"IgA plasma cells are already in the lamina propria,","D":"Antigen uptake by dendritic cells is upstream of plasma-cell"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has chronic diarrhea and a small-bowel stricture. He is thin and the abdomen is soft. A 60 cm resection shows, at the uninvolved margins, short villi, many goblet cells, no submucosal glands, and large lymphoid nodules opposite the mesenteric attachment. Loss of this segment most directly risks deficiency of which nutrient?","choices":{"A":"Folic acid","B":"Ferrous iron","C":"Dietary calcium","D":"Vitamin C","E":"Vitamin B12"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Short villi, abundant goblet cells, and lymphoid aggregates opposite the mesentery identify ileum, the site of vitamin B12 absorption. Resection of that segment removes the uptake of the intrinsic-factor complex. Folate is absorbed in the proximal small bowel, which this ileal pattern is not. Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, a segment that has submucosal glands this specimen lacks. Calcium absorption is not confined to the ileal segment marked by these lymphoid nodules. Vitamin C is not selectively dependent on this distal ileal segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, folate in the duodenum and jejunum, and vitamin B12 with bile salts in the terminal ileum (First Aid 2025 GI physiology). COMLEX: Patients after ileal resection need lifelong vitamin B12 replacement that bypasses the gut.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Folate is absorbed in the proximal small bowel, which","B":"Iron is absorbed in the duodenum, a segment that","C":"Calcium absorption is not confined to the ileal segment","D":"Vitamin C is not selectively dependent on this distal ileal"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has watery diarrhea, bloating, and gas after milk and sweetened juice, beginning three weeks after a viral gastroenteritis. He tolerated those drinks before the illness. He is afebrile, well hydrated, and the abdomen is soft and mildly distended. The stool osmotic gap is 140 mOsm/kg (values above 100 mOsm/kg indicate an osmotic diarrhea). Villi and crypts are normal in height, but electron microscopy shows short, sparse, irregular microvilli. Which loss best explains his symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Lacteal transport of chylomicrons","B":"Brush-border disaccharidase activity","C":"Paneth cell antimicrobial secretion","D":"Crypt chloride and water secretion","E":"Goblet cell release of surface mucus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Short sparse microvilli after a viral illness remove brush-border disaccharidases while the villus architecture stays normal. Osmotic diarrhea triggered by milk and sweetened juice follows that enzyme loss. Lacteal transport of chylomicrons is a fat pathway and does not explain sugar-triggered osmotic diarrhea. Paneth-cell secretion is antimicrobial and is not the function housed on these damaged microvilli. Crypt chloride secretion would cause secretory diarrhea, not an osmotic gap of 140 mOsm/kg. Goblet-cell mucus release does not digest milk sugar or juice sugars.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brush-border disaccharidases and peptidases sit on the microvillus membrane, bringing digestion close to absorption (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Transient sugar intolerance after viral gastroenteritis reflects brush-border injury and usually resolves.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion and lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lacteal transport of chylomicrons is a fat pathway and","C":"Paneth-cell secretion is antimicrobial and is not the function","D":"Crypt chloride secretion would cause secretory diarrhea, not an","E":"Goblet-cell mucus release does not digest milk sugar or juice"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has dyspepsia without weight loss. The abdomen is soft. Duodenal biopsies show scattered epithelial cells that reach the lumen, with receptors on a narrow apex and small granules packed under the nucleus against the basal lamina. After a fatty meal, release from some of these cells most directly produces which response?","choices":{"A":"Increased acid secretion and faster emptying","B":"Lysozyme discharge into the crypt lumen","C":"Mucus discharge onto the villus surface","D":"Gallbladder contraction and enzyme secretion","E":"Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These open enteroendocrine cells sense luminal fat and release a signal that contracts the gallbladder and drives pancreatic enzyme secretion. Their open apex and basal granules match that sensory cell. Increased acid secretion and faster emptying are not the response driven by these fat-sensing cells. Lysozyme discharge is a Paneth-cell action, and these cells are not confined to the crypt base. Mucus discharge is a goblet-cell action, and these cells have basal granules rather than an apical mucin cup. Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes is a microfold-cell function over lymphoid tissue.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Duodenal I cells release cholecystokinin in response to fat and protein products, which contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Fatty meals provoke biliary colic because cholecystokinin contracts a gallbladder holding stones.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 2 — large-intestine absorptive and goblet cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased acid secretion and faster emptying are not the","B":"Lysozyme discharge is a Paneth-cell action, and these cells","C":"Mucus discharge is a goblet-cell action, and these cells","E":"Antigen transfer to dome lymphocytes is a microfold-cell function"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Outline the process of epithelial renewal in the small intestine, including intestinal crypt stem cells, differentiation pathways, and migration patterns of absorptive and secretory cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has bloating, loose stools, and fatigue. A cousin has type 1 diabetes, and she eats wheat daily. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 140 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. Duodenal biopsy shows flat villi, many intraepithelial lymphocytes, and long crypts full of mitotic figures. Which mechanism best explains the crypt changes?","choices":{"A":"Arrested migration of cells out of crypts","B":"Neoplastic transformation of stem cells","C":"Conversion of crypt cells to Paneth cells","D":"Slower shedding of cells at the villus tips","E":"Faster villus loss driving crypt output"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Celiac disease destroys villus cells quickly, and the crypts increase their output to replace them. A high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, endomysial antibody, flat villi, and intraepithelial lymphocytes define that injury. Cells are not arrested inside the crypts; mitoses are increased because output has risen. The crowded crypt mitoses are regenerative, not a neoplastic transformation of stem cells. The crypts are not converting into Paneth cells. Shedding at the villus tip is faster, not slower, in this injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In celiac disease, epithelial turnover is accelerated, villi shorten, and crypts become hyperplastic as a compensatory response (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and intraepithelial lymphocytes on duodenal biopsy confirms the diagnosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cells are not arrested inside the crypts; mitoses are","B":"The crowded crypt mitoses are regenerative, not a neoplastic","C":"The crypts are not converting into Paneth","D":"Shedding at the villus tip is faster, not slower,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the structure and immune functions of intestinal gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT), including Peyer patches, M-cell antigen transport, and the production and epithelial transport of secretory IgA.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl, born at term, receives an oral live-attenuated rotavirus vaccine and continues to feed well. Examination is normal. Weeks later her stool contains rotavirus-specific antibody that blocks viral attachment to enterocytes, while serum antibody remains low. Stool IgA is present and serum IgG is 450 mg/dL (reference range for age: 200–600 mg/dL). Which sequence best describes how this luminal antibody was generated?","choices":{"A":"M cell uptake, follicle activation, dimeric IgA, pIgR transport","B":"Enterocyte uptake, splenic activation, IgG, paracellular leak","C":"Paneth cell uptake, follicle activation, IgM, lacteal transport","D":"Goblet cell uptake, thymic activation, IgE, mast cell release","E":"M cell uptake, nodal activation, IgG, Fc receptor transport"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Luminal IgA after an oral vaccine is generated by microfold-cell uptake, lymphoid-follicle activation, dimeric IgA, and polymeric-immunoglobulin-receptor transport. Low serum antibody with antibody in the stool fits that mucosal path. Enterocyte uptake with splenic IgG and a paracellular leak would raise serum antibody, which is low. Paneth cells do not take up vaccine virus, and lacteals do not deliver the luminal antibody. Goblet cells and thymic IgE are not the route that puts blocking antibody in the stool. Nodal IgG carried by Fc receptors would appear in serum, which stays low here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intestinal plasma cells secrete dimeric IgA that is carried across enterocytes by the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor and released as secretory IgA (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Oral vaccines work by inducing mucosal secretory IgA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — lamina propria and GALT of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterocyte uptake with splenic IgG and a paracellular leak","C":"Paneth cells do not take up vaccine virus, and","D":"Goblet cells and thymic IgE are not the route","E":"Nodal IgG carried by Fc receptors would appear in"}},{"id":"trial_sdl23_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman remains anemic despite correctly taken oral ferrous sulfate. She has heavy menses. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and serum folate and vitamin B12 are normal. Biopsies show disease confined to the single small-bowel segment where dietary iron is normally absorbed. Which histologic feature would the normal wall of that segment display?","choices":{"A":"Peyer patches opposite the mesentery","B":"Teniae coli in the muscularis","C":"Mucous glands in the submucosa","D":"Tall folds with no submucosal glands","E":"Parietal cells in deep glands"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dietary iron is absorbed in the duodenum, whose wall contains mucous glands in the submucosa. Disease limited to that segment, with folate and B12 spared, points to this proximal site. Peyer patches opposite the mesentery mark ileum, which is not the iron-absorbing segment. Teniae coli are a large-bowel muscle feature and are absent from small bowel. Tall folds without submucosal glands describe jejunum, distal to the iron-absorbing segment. Parietal cells belong to the stomach, not to the small-bowel segment that absorbs iron.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Brunner glands in the submucosa identify the duodenum, which is also the main site of iron absorption (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Proximal mucosal disease such as celiac disease often presents with iron deficiency that resists oral iron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peyer patches opposite the mesentery mark ileum, which is","B":"Teniae coli are a large-bowel muscle feature and are","D":"Tall folds without submucosal glands describe jejunum, distal to","E":"Parietal cells belong to the stomach, not to the"}},{"id":"sdl23_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunal submucosa is distinguished by the absence of which pair of structures?","choices":{"A":"Villi and microvilli","B":"Plicae circulares and enterocytes","C":"Goblet cells and Paneth cells","D":"Brunner glands and Peyer patches","E":"Crypts of Lieberkühn and enteroendocrine cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Jejunal submucosa lacks both Brunner glands and aggregated lymphatic nodules. Villi and microvilli are prominent in the jejunum and are not the structures missing from its submucosa. Plicae circulares are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and enterocytes line them. Goblet cells and Paneth cells are epithelial, and Paneth cells sit in jejunal crypts. Crypts and enteroendocrine cells are part of jejunal mucosa.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villi and microvilli are prominent in the jejunum and are not the structures missing from its submucosa","B":"Plicae circulares are tallest and most numerous in the jejunum, and enterocytes line them","C":"Goblet cells and Paneth cells are epithelial, and Paneth cells sit in jejunal crypts","E":"Crypts and enteroendocrine cells are part of jejunal mucosa"}},{"id":"sdl23_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the duodenum, jejunum, and ileum based on their characteristic histologic features, including Brunner glands, plicae circulares, villi, and Peyer patches.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old man has right-lower-quadrant pain and a nonbloody loose stool for three months. He has lost 4 kg and has a draining perianal tract. Temperature is 37.8°C, and the right lower quadrant is tender without rebound. Hemoglobin is 12.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and albumin is 3.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Resection of the distal small bowel shows large lymphoid aggregates on the antimesenteric wall that reach the submucosa. Which segment was removed?","choices":{"A":"Ileum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"First portion of the duodenum","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Appendix"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peyer patches, aggregated lymphoid nodules on the antimesenteric wall that often enter the submucosa, mark the ileum. Proximal jejunum has the tallest plicae and no aggregated lymphatic nodules in the submucosa. The first portion of the duodenum is identified by submucosal Brunner glands, not Peyer patches. The gastric antrum has gastric pits and glands, not antimesenteric Peyer patches. The appendix is large intestine and is not the antimesenteric small-bowel segment that bears Peyer patches.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Proximal jejunum has the tallest plicae and no aggregated lymphatic nodules in the submucosa","C":"The first portion of the duodenum is identified by submucosal Brunner glands, not Peyer patches","D":"The gastric antrum has gastric pits and glands, not antimesenteric Peyer patches","E":"The appendix is large intestine and is not the antimesenteric small-bowel segment that bears Peyer patches"}},{"id":"sdl23_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the structural specializations of the small intestine that facilitate nutrient absorption, including plicae circulares, villi, enterocyte microvilli, and lacteals, and explain their roles in absorptive surface area and lipid transport.","batch":1,"stem":"Which tissue forms the core of an intestinal villus?","choices":{"A":"Submucosa","B":"Muscularis externa","C":"Serosa","D":"A thick band of circular muscle","E":"Lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A villus is a mucosal projection whose core is lamina propria covered by simple columnar epithelium. Submucosa is the core of a plica circularis, not of a villus. Muscularis externa is the deep muscle coat and does not form the villus core. Serosa is the outer peritoneal coat, far from the villus. Circular muscle belongs to the muscularis externa, not the villus core.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Submucosa is the core of a plica circularis, not of a villus","B":"Muscularis externa is the deep muscle coat and does not form the villus core","C":"Serosa is the outer peritoneal coat, far from the villus","D":"Circular muscle belongs to the muscularis externa, not the villus core"}},{"id":"sdl23_q34","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical specialization do M cells have in place of a brush border?","choices":{"A":"Motile cilia","B":"Microfolds","C":"Stereocilia","D":"A thick keratin layer","E":"Kinocilia"},"correct":"B","explanation":"M cells overlying Peyer patches have apical microfolds instead of microvilli. Motile cilia are not the apical surface of intestinal M cells. Stereocilia are long actin processes of other epithelia, not the M-cell surface. A keratin layer is a feature of stratified squamous surfaces, not M cells. Kinocilia are microtubule-based processes and are not the M-cell apex.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Motile cilia are not the apical surface of intestinal M cells","C":"Stereocilia are long actin processes of other epithelia, not the M-cell surface","D":"A keratin layer is a feature of stratified squamous surfaces, not M cells","E":"Kinocilia are microtubule-based processes and are not the M-cell apex"}},{"id":"sdl23_q35","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify the major epithelial cell types of the small intestine and explain their histologic features and functions, including enterocytes, goblet cells, Paneth cells, enteroendocrine cells, and M cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has fever and watery diarrhea two days after a preschool picnic. He is drinking, the abdomen is soft, and there is no blood in the stool. Temperature is 38.2°C. Sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and potassium is 3.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). A research biopsy over an ileal lymphoid nodule shows epithelial cells whose basolateral recess holds lymphocytes and dendritic cells. Which cell carried intact luminal antigen to those immune cells?","choices":{"A":"M cell","B":"Paneth cell","C":"Goblet cell","D":"Chief cell","E":"Parietal cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"M cells endocytose intact luminal antigen and deliver it by transcytosis into a deep basolateral pocket that holds lymphocytes and dendritic cells. Paneth cells sit at the crypt base and release antimicrobial granules, and they do not ferry antigen into a lymphocyte pocket. Goblet cells secrete mucin and do not transcytose intact antigen to dendritic cells. Chief cells are gastric and secrete pepsinogen. Parietal cells are gastric and secrete acid and intrinsic factor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Paneth cells sit at the crypt base and release antimicrobial granules, and they do not ferry antigen into a lymphocyte pocket","C":"Goblet cells secrete mucin and do not transcytose intact antigen to dendritic cells","D":"Chief cells are gastric and secrete pepsinogen","E":"Parietal cells are gastric and secrete acid and intrinsic factor"}}]},{"sdlNumber":24,"title":"SDL 24 — Histology of the Large Intestine","questions":[{"id":"sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"How many teniae coli run along the cecum and colon?","choices":{"A":"Two opposing bands","B":"Four paired bands","C":"Five irregular bands","D":"Three equal bands","E":"Six spiral bands"},"correct":"D","explanation":"There are three narrowed, thickened, equally spaced teniae coli, formed from the outer longitudinal layer of the muscularis externa. Two bands is too few; the outer longitudinal muscle condenses into three equally spaced teniae. There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands. The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones. The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: between the teniae, the outer longitudinal layer of the colon is an extremely thin sheet (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: omental appendices are small fat-filled peritoneal projections on the outer colonic surface (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Two bands is too few.","B":"There are three teniae coli, not four paired bands.","C":"The teniae are three equally spaced bands, not five irregular ones.","E":"The teniae are three straight, equally spaced bands, not six spiral bands."}},{"id":"sdl24_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"In which parts of the large intestine are teniae coli absent?","choices":{"A":"Cecum, ascending, and transverse colon","B":"Transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon","C":"Ascending colon, cecum, and appendix","D":"Descending colon, sigmoid, and rectum","E":"Rectum, anal canal, and appendix"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teniae coli are seen mainly in the cecum and colon. In the rectum, anal canal, and vermiform appendix, the outer longitudinal muscle is a uniformly thick layer, as in the small intestine. The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen. The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli. The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them. The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the appendix differs from the cecum by having a uniform layer of longitudinal muscle in its muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the anal canal averages 4 cm and runs from the upper pelvic diaphragm to the anus (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum and ascending and transverse colon are exactly where teniae coli are seen.","B":"The transverse, descending, and sigmoid colon all carry teniae coli.","C":"The cecum and ascending colon have teniae; only the appendix of this group lacks them.","D":"The descending and sigmoid colon have teniae; only the rectum of this group lacks them."}},{"id":"sdl24_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":1,"stem":"What are omental appendices of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Fatty projections of the serosa","B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules","C":"Submucosal outpouchings of the wall","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle","E":"Sacculations between muscle bands"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Omental appendices are small fatty projections of the serosa on the outer colonic surface. Teniae are muscle bands, and haustra are sacculations between them. Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface. Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices. Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle are the teniae coli, thickenings of the outer muscularis externa, not omental appendices. Sacculations between the muscle bands are the haustra coli, not the fatty serosal omental appendices. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; gross features, Fig. 17.28; Muscularis externa). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustra coli are the visible sacculations between the teniae on the external surface of the cecum and colon (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the colon is subdivided into ascending, transverse, descending, and sigmoid parts by anatomic location (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucosal folds over lymphoid nodules are internal features, whereas omental appendices hang from the outer serosal surface.","C":"Submucosal outpouchings describe diverticula, not the fatty serosal tags called omental appendices.","D":"Bands of longitudinal smooth muscle are the teniae coli, thickenings of the outer muscularis externa, not omental appendices.","E":"Sacculations between the muscle bands are the haustra coli, not the fatty serosal omental appendices."}},{"id":"sdl24_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorbing most dietary amino acids","B":"Secreting brush-border disaccharidases","C":"Reabsorbing water and electrolytes","D":"Transporting dimeric IgA to the lumen","E":"Producing antimicrobial defensins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The primary function of colonic columnar absorptive cells is reabsorbing water and electrolytes. They use the same Na+/K+-ATPase-driven system as small intestinal enterocytes. Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption. Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes. Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine. IgA transcytosis is not the function of these absorptive cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Mucosa, Figs. 17.29–17.30). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic absorptive cells are essentially identical in morphology to small intestinal enterocytes (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the principal functions of the large intestine are electrolyte and water reabsorption and elimination of undigested waste (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Most dietary amino acids are absorbed in the small intestine, not by colonocytes, whose role is water and electrolyte reabsorption.","B":"Colonocytes do not express digestive enzymes in their glycocalyx, unlike small intestinal enterocytes.","E":"Defensins come from Paneth cells, which are normally absent from the human large intestine.","D":"IgA transcytosis is not the function of these absorptive cells."}},{"id":"sdl24_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which epithelial cell type of the small intestine is normally absent from the human colon?","choices":{"A":"Paneth cells","B":"Goblet cells","C":"Enteroendocrine cells","D":"Absorptive cells","E":"Stem cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The colonic epithelium has the same cell types as the small intestine except Paneth cells, which are normally absent in humans. Goblet cells are more numerous in the colon than in the small intestine and lubricate firm contents. Enteroendocrine cells are present in colonic glands and are renewed from the same stem cells. Absorptive cells are the predominant colonic cell and reabsorb water and electrolytes. Stem cells sit in the lower third of the colonic gland and renew every lineage. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, large intestine mucosa.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon has absorptive, goblet, enteroendocrine, and stem cells but no Paneth cells (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Paneth cells in a left colon biopsy suggest metaplasia from chronic injury such as IBD.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Goblet cells are more numerous in the","C":"Enteroendocrine cells are present in colonic glands","D":"Absorptive cells are the predominant colonic cell","E":"Stem cells sit in the lower third"}},{"id":"sdl24_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":1,"stem":"How does the glycocalyx of colonic absorptive cells differ from that of small intestinal enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"It is absent from the cell surface","B":"It is renewed much more slowly","C":"It is made by the goblet cells","D":"It lacks carbohydrate side chains","E":"It has no proven digestive enzymes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic absorptive cells secrete glycocalyx at a rapid rate, but unlike the small intestinal brush border it has not been shown to contain digestive enzymes, fitting a segment that absorbs water and salt rather than digesting nutrients. Colonic absorptive cells do have a glycocalyx, which they secrete rapidly. The colonic glycocalyx turns over rapidly, so it is not renewed more slowly. The glycocalyx is secreted by the absorptive cells themselves, while goblet cells make mucus. A glycocalyx is by definition a carbohydrate-rich surface coat, so it does not lack carbohydrate. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, large intestine absorptive cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic absorptive cells reabsorb water and electrolytes using lateral Na/K-ATPase, and their glycocalyx lacks shown digestive enzymes (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Nutrient digestion and absorption failures point to small bowel disease, not colonic disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic absorptive cells do have a glycocalyx,","B":"The colonic glycocalyx turns over rapidly, so","C":"The glycocalyx is secreted by the absorptive","D":"A glycocalyx is by definition a carbohydrate-rich"}},{"id":"sdl24_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"What part of the colonic intestinal gland forms the stem cell niche?","choices":{"A":"Upper third near the surface","B":"Lower third of the gland","C":"Middle third of the gland","D":"Entire length of the gland","E":"Surface cuff between glands"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Stem cells sit at the bottom of the intestinal gland, and the lower third of the gland is the stem cell niche. New cells then migrate up to the luminal surface. The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche. The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third. Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length. The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: all mucosal epithelial cells of the large intestine come from stem cells at the bottom of the intestinal gland (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the intermediate cell types in the lower third of the colonic gland are identical to those in the small intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The upper gland near the surface is where cells migrate toward shedding, not the niche.","C":"The middle third holds migrating cells; the niche and amplifying zone are in the lower third.","D":"Stem cells are restricted to the gland base, not spread along its whole length.","E":"The surface cuff between glands is where senile cells are shed, not where they are generated."}},{"id":"sdl24_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"From which source are colonic enteroendocrine cells renewed?","choices":{"A":"Migrating neural crest cells","B":"Pericryptal fibroblast sheath","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Circulating marrow precursors","E":"Stem cells of the gland base"},"correct":"E","explanation":"All colonic epithelial cells, enteroendocrine cells included, arise from the single stem cell population at the base of the intestinal glands; enteroendocrine cells are not neural crest derivatives despite making amine and peptide products. Migrating neural crest cells give rise to APUD cells elsewhere, and Pawlina warns not to confuse them with gut enteroendocrine cells. The pericryptal fibroblast sheath migrates beside the epithelium and may become lamina propria macrophages. Submucosal plexus neurons are enteric nervous system cells and do not produce epithelial cells. Circulating marrow precursors supply blood and immune cells, not the epithelial lining of the glands. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, enteroendocrine cells and epithelial renewal in the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut enteroendocrine cells are endodermal and come from crypt stem cells, not neural crest (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Neuroendocrine tumors of the gut arise from these epithelial enteroendocrine cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Migrating neural crest cells give rise to","B":"The pericryptal fibroblast sheath migrates beside the","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons are enteric nervous system","D":"Circulating marrow precursors supply blood and immune"}},{"id":"sdl24_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":1,"stem":"Where on the colonic surface are senile epithelial cells shed?","choices":{"A":"Base of each intestinal gland","B":"Rim of each gland opening","C":"Crests of the semilunar folds","D":"Between two adjacent glands","E":"Tips of the colonic villi"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Senile colonic epithelial cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed at the midpoint between two adjacent intestinal glands. The colon has no villi. The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed. Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening. Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site. The colonic mucosa has a smooth surface with neither plicae circulares nor villi. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: on scanning EM, the colonic surface is divided by clefts into territories of gland openings (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic gland openings on the luminal surface are arranged in an orderly pattern (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gland base is the stem cell niche, where cells are made rather than shed.","B":"Cells are shed midway between gland openings, not at the rim of each opening.","C":"Semilunar folds are contraction-dependent folds between haustra, not the defined shedding site.","E":"The colonic mucosa has a smooth surface with neither plicae circulares nor villi."}},{"id":"sdl24_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"Where does the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer of the colon lie?","choices":{"A":"Between muscularis mucosae and submucosa","B":"Between the teniae and the circular muscle","C":"Under the epithelial basal lamina","D":"Within the core of each lymphatic nodule","E":"Beneath the serosal mesothelium layer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The collagen table is a thick layer of collagen and proteoglycans between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries. The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae. The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table. Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer. The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the collagen table participates in regulating water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the absorptive venous capillaries under the colonic epithelium are fenestrated (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The collagen table lies directly under the surface epithelium, not deep at the muscularis mucosae.","B":"The teniae and circular muscle are muscularis externa, far from the subepithelial collagen table.","D":"Lymphatic nodule cores are GALT, not the site of the subepithelial collagen–proteoglycan layer.","E":"The collagen table is subepithelial in the mucosa, not beneath the serosa."}},{"id":"sdl24_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the function of the subepithelial collagen table of the colon?","choices":{"A":"Regulating water and salt transport","B":"Anchoring the teniae coli to the mucosa","C":"Holding the lymphatic capillary network","D":"Producing the secretory component of IgA","E":"Storing mucinogen for the goblet cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The collagen table lies between the epithelial basal lamina and that of the fenestrated absorptive venous capillaries, and it helps regulate water and electrolyte transport from the epithelium to the vascular compartment. Anchoring the teniae coli is not its role, since the teniae are bands of the outer muscularis externa. The lamina propria near the surface has no lymphatic vessels, so the layer holds no lymphatic network. Secretory component comes from the polymeric Ig receptor of epithelial cells, not from this layer. Mucinogen is stored inside goblet cell granules, not in the subepithelial connective tissue. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, lamina propria of the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colonic collagen table regulates water and electrolyte movement to the capillaries (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: A thickened subepithelial collagen band with watery diarrhea and normal colonoscopy suggests collagenous colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Anchoring the teniae coli is not its","C":"The lamina propria near the surface has","D":"Secretory component comes from the polymeric Ig","E":"Mucinogen is stored inside goblet cell granules,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":1,"stem":"From what may macrophages in the core of the colonic lamina propria arise?","choices":{"A":"Circulating neutrophils","B":"Pericryptal fibroblasts","C":"Exhausted goblet cells","D":"Epithelial niche cells","E":"Muscularis mucosae cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pericryptal fibroblasts divide beneath the gland base and migrate upward with the epithelium. Near the surface most take on macrophage features, and lamina propria macrophages may be their terminal stage. Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors. Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts. Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from circulating neutrophils. Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from muscularis mucosae cells. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pericryptal fibroblast sheath exists in both the large and small intestines (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: pericryptal fibroblasts divide adjacent to the epithelial stem cells at the bottom of the gland (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Exhausted goblet cells are epithelial cells shed at the surface, not macrophage precursors.","D":"Epithelial niche cells generate epithelium, whereas lamina propria macrophages come from pericryptal fibroblasts.","A":"Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from circulating neutrophils.","E":"Pawlina notes that pericryptal fibroblasts migrate upward and some evidence suggests the macrophages of the colonic lamina propria core arise as their terminal differentiation, not from muscularis mucosae cells."}},{"id":"sdl24_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Where do colonic lymphatics from the gland bases first drain?","choices":{"A":"Lacteals in the lamina propria core","B":"Vessels reaching the luminal surface","C":"Plexus beneath the serosal surface","D":"Network within the muscularis mucosae","E":"Sinusoids of the regional lymph nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Lymphatics are generally absent from the lamina propria core, but occasional small vessels at the gland bases drain into a network in the muscularis mucosae. Lymph then passes to submucosal and muscularis externa plexuses and on to regional nodes. The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface. Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases. Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus. Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Large intestine; Lamina propria; Cecum and appendix); Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: no lymphatic vessels extend toward the luminal surface of the large intestine (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: lymph leaves the colonic wall only after passing through plexuses in the submucosa and muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The colon has no villi and no lacteals; lamina propria lymphatics are absent near the surface.","B":"Lymphatics do not reach the luminal surface; they are confined to gland bases.","C":"Gland-base lymphatics drain first to the muscularis mucosae, not directly to a subserosal plexus.","E":"Regional nodes come last, after the muscularis mucosae, submucosal, and muscularis externa plexuses."}},{"id":"sdl24_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody marker is used to identify lymphatic endothelium in colonic lamina propria?","choices":{"A":"CD117","B":"CD34","C":"D2-40","D":"CK20","E":"S-100"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The monoclonal antibody D2-40 reacts with a 40-kDa O-linked sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium and is used to map lamina propria lymphatics. CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and most gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not lymphatic endothelium. CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloid blasts, not a specific lymphatic endothelial marker. S-100 is a Schwann cell calcium-binding protein marker, not a lymphatic endothelial marker. Pawlina describes D2-40 as the monoclonal antibody that reacts with a 40-kDa sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium, which CK20 does not mark. Sources: Pawlina Ch. 17 (Lamina propria lymphatic vessels; Folder 17.6). Aligned with SDL 24 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the absence of lamina propria lymphatics was first found with light and electron microscopy of biopsy samples (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: D2-40 recognizes an O-linked sialoglycoprotein of about 40 kDa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CD117 (KIT) marks normal interstitial cells of Cajal and most gastrointestinal stromal tumors, not lymphatic endothelium.","B":"CD34 is a marker of hematopoietic progenitors such as myeloid blasts, not a specific lymphatic endothelial marker.","E":"S-100 is a Schwann cell calcium-binding protein marker, not a lymphatic endothelial marker.","D":"Pawlina describes D2-40 as the monoclonal antibody that reacts with a 40-kDa sialoglycoprotein on lymphatic endothelium, which CK20 does not mark."}},{"id":"sdl24_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":1,"stem":"Many adenomatous polyps of the large intestine lie in the rectum and sigmoid colon. Which procedure can therefore detect a large share of them?","choices":{"A":"Capsule endoscopy","B":"Rectosigmoidoscopy","C":"Upper endoscopy","D":"Barium swallow study","E":"Abdominal ultrasound"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because almost half of colonic adenomas sit in the rectum and sigmoid colon, rectosigmoidoscopy can find many of them, and removal is adequate while the lesion stays in the mucosa above the lymphatics of the muscularis mucosae. Capsule endoscopy is mainly used to image the small intestine, not to sample the rectosigmoid. Upper endoscopy examines the esophagus, stomach, and duodenum, not the distal colon. A barium swallow study outlines the esophagus and cannot show rectal or sigmoid polyps. Abdominal ultrasound cannot reliably image small mucosal polyps inside the colon. Sources: Pawlina ch 17, Folder 17.6, lymph vessel distribution and diseases of the large intestine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic adenomas confined to the mucosa rarely metastasize because lamina propria lymphatics are absent (Pawlina ch 17). COMLEX: Flexible sigmoidoscopy is an accepted alternative screening test, though full colonoscopy reaches the proximal colon.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Capsule endoscopy is mainly used to image","C":"Upper endoscopy examines the esophagus, stomach, and","D":"A barium swallow study outlines the esophagus","E":"Abdominal ultrasound cannot reliably image small mucosal"}},{"id":"sdl24_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man is having his first screening colonoscopy. He has never had rectal bleeding or a change in stool caliber. The examination is unremarkable. A transverse-colon biopsy is histologically normal, and an ileal biopsy taken at the same procedure shows villi. Compared with the ileal biopsy, which mucosal feature distinguishes the colonic sample?","choices":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells","B":"Lamina propria rich in lymphocytes","C":"Muscularis mucosae deep to the glands","D":"Smooth surface without villi","E":"Goblet cells among absorptive cells"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Normal colonic mucosa has a smooth surface without villi, unlike the villous ileal mucosa sampled at the same procedure. Both sites have glands, lymphocytes, a muscularis mucosae, and goblet cells, so those features do not separate them. Glands lined by simple columnar cells are present in both the colon and the ileum. A lymphocyte-rich lamina propria is found in both biopsies. A muscularis mucosae lies under the glands in both the colon and the ileum. Goblet cells sit among absorptive cells in both the colon and the ileum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the colon has crypts of Lieberkühn with abundant goblet cells but no villi (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI histology). COMLEX: the smooth colonic mucosa shows semilunar folds formed by contractions of the muscularis externa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glands lined by simple columnar cells are present in","B":"A lymphocyte-rich lamina propria is found in both","C":"A muscularis mucosae lies under the glands in both","E":"Goblet cells sit among absorptive cells in both the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had bloody diarrhea for eight years and takes mesalamine. A cousin has autoimmune thyroid disease. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Barium enema shows a featureless lead-pipe colon. p-ANCA is positive, ASCA is negative, and hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which normal arrangement produces the sacculations she has lost?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares anchored in the submucosa","B":"Teniae bundles crossing circular muscle","C":"Fat lobules pushing out the serosal surface","D":"Lymphoid nodules distorting the gland rows","E":"Uniform longitudinal muscle around the wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Haustra are made where teniae cross the circular muscle, and their loss produces the lead-pipe contour. Years of bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA identify the colitis in which those sacculations disappear. Plicae circulares are small-bowel folds anchored in submucosa and do not form colonic haustra. Fat lobules on the serosa are the omental appendices and do not create the haustra. Lymphoid nodules distort glands locally and do not form the regular sacculations. A uniform longitudinal muscle coat would not pinch the wall into haustra the way teniae do.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is continuous colonic inflammation that involves the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: ulcerative colitis causes friable mucosa with superficial or deep ulcerations and crypt abscesses (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are small-bowel folds anchored in submucosa and","C":"Fat lobules on the serosa are the omental appendices","D":"Lymphoid nodules distort glands locally and do not form","E":"A uniform longitudinal muscle coat would not pinch the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":5,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has a screening flexible sigmoidoscopy. He has no bleeding, pain, or history of colitis. In the upper rectum the endoscopist sees several shelf-like mucosal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Colonic semilunar folds","B":"Longitudinal anal columns","C":"Transverse rectal folds","D":"Plicae circulares folds","E":"Gastric rugae-type folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The shelf-like folds in the upper rectum are the transverse rectal folds. They are a normal feature of that segment and are not the semilunar folds of the colon or the columns of the anal canal. Semilunar folds are the haustral shelves of the colon, not these rectal shelves. Longitudinal anal columns lie in the anal canal, distal to the upper rectum. Plicae circulares are circular folds of the small intestine. Rugae are gastric folds and are not seen in the rectum.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pectinate line forms where hindgut endoderm meets ectoderm (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pectinate line). COMLEX: the depressions between the anal columns are called anal sinuses (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Semilunar folds are the haustral shelves of the colon,","B":"Longitudinal anal columns lie in the anal canal, distal","D":"Plicae circulares are circular folds of the small","E":"Rugae are gastric folds and are not seen in the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy during screening colonoscopy. She does not use laxatives and has no diarrhea. At the luminal surface many tall, thin goblet cells hold only a few mucinogen granules in the central apical cytoplasm. What explains their appearance?","choices":{"A":"Secretion outpacing mucin synthesis","B":"Mucin loss during tissue processing","C":"Immature cells still in the niche","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells","E":"Conversion into absorptive cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Surface goblet cells look depleted because secretion of mucus has outrun new mucin synthesis. Their tall thin shape and few remaining central granules fit cells that have recently discharged. Mucin loss during processing empties the whole cup, which is not the picture of a few retained central granules. Cells still in the stem niche sit deep in the gland, not on the luminal surface. Apoptosis of aging goblet cells sheds them rather than leaving a few apical granules. These cells have not converted into absorptive cells, which lack mucinogen granules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: caveolated tuft cells described in the colonic epithelium may be a form of exhausted goblet cell (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: colonic goblet cells may mature deep in the gland, even within the replicative zone (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mucin loss during processing empties the whole cup, which","C":"Cells still in the stem niche sit deep in","D":"Apoptosis of aging goblet cells sheds them rather than","E":"These cells have not converted into absorptive cells, which"}},{"id":"sdl24_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has biopsies from several sites at colonoscopy. He has no bleeding or chronic diarrhea. One site shows absorptive cells and goblet cells in a ratio close to 1 to 1. Where was that biopsy most likely taken?","choices":{"A":"Proximal cecum","B":"Lower rectum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Transverse colon","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A near-equal mix of absorptive cells and goblet cells is reached in the lower rectum, where goblet cells increase. Elsewhere absorptive cells outnumber goblet cells about four to one. The proximal cecum keeps the usual excess of absorptive cells, about four for each goblet cell. The ascending colon keeps that absorptive majority rather than an even mix. The transverse colon has the usual excess of absorptive cells. The descending colon still has more absorptive cells than goblet cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectal mucosa resembles distal colonic mucosa, with straight tubular glands rich in goblet cells (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: the rectum is the dilated distal portion of the alimentary canal (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The proximal cecum keeps the usual excess of absorptive","C":"The ascending colon keeps that absorptive majority rather than","D":"The transverse colon has the usual excess of absorptive","E":"The descending colon still has more absorptive cells than goblet"}},{"id":"sdl24_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has passed hard stools only twice a week for years. The abdomen is soft, and stool is palpable in the left colon. A biopsy shows abundant goblet cells packing the colonic glands. What is the main role of their continuous secretion?","choices":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid in chyme","B":"Digesting the residual disaccharides","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids","E":"Easing the passage of firm contents"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Colonic goblet cells continuously secrete mucus that lubricates the mucosa and eases passage of firm contents. Constipation with a goblet-cell-rich biopsy asks for that lubricating function. Neutralizing gastric acid is a duodenal task and is not the role of colonic goblet mucus. Digesting residual disaccharides is a brush-border enzyme function, not a mucus function. Killing bacteria with lysozyme is a Paneth-cell role, and the colon lacks those cells. Absorbing short-chain fatty acids is the work of colonocytes, not of goblet mucus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is associated with low-fiber diets and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticulosis). COMLEX: elimination of semisolid to solid waste is aided by the large amount of mucus in the colonic glands (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 2 — epithelial types and mucosal specializations","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutralizing gastric acid is a duodenal task and is","B":"Digesting residual disaccharides is a brush-border enzyme function, not","C":"Killing bacteria with lysozyme is a Paneth-cell role, and","D":"Absorbing short-chain fatty acids is the work of colonocytes,"}},{"id":"sdl24_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old healthy man volunteers for a labeling study of colonic epithelium. He has one formed stool each day. Newly generated crypt cells are tagged as they leave the stem-cell zone and are followed toward the lumen. The labeled cells later appear in shed surface epithelium. About how long after they begin migrating are these cells shed?","choices":{"A":"About 5 hours","B":"About 5 weeks","C":"About 5 days","D":"About 5 months","E":"About 1 year"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Colonic epithelial cells migrate from the crypt and are shed at the surface after about 5 days. The labeling study times that interval directly. Five hours is far shorter than the colonic epithelial lifespan. Five weeks would leave cells in place long after they have been shed. Five months greatly exceeds the turnover of this epithelium. A year is not the survival time of a colonocyte.","boardPrep":"Step 1: as new colonic cells begin their upward migration, they continue dividing (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the stomach, surface and pit mucous cells turn over in 3–5 days (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Five hours is far shorter than the colonic epithelial","B":"Five weeks would leave cells in place long after","D":"Five months greatly exceeds the turnover of this","E":"A year is not the survival time of a"}},{"id":"sdl24_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 40-year-old woman has a colonic biopsy examined by electron microscopy during a research protocol. She has no diarrhea or family history of polyposis. Deep in a gland, two cells that still hold apical mucinogen granules are in mitosis. What does this show?","choices":{"A":"Mature goblet cells can still divide","B":"Mucinogen granules form during mitosis","C":"Exhausted surface cells reenter the cycle","D":"Paneth cells give rise to new goblets","E":"Absorptive cells convert into goblets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Goblet cells that still contain mucinogen granules can divide, so differentiation and proliferation overlap in the gland. Mitoses in deep cells that already hold granules demonstrate that overlap. The granules are not formed by the mitotic apparatus itself. Exhausted surface cells are shed and do not reenter the cell cycle deep in the gland. Paneth cells are absent from the human colon and do not give rise to these goblet cells. Absorptive cells do not convert into goblet cells during these mitoses.","boardPrep":"Step 1: dividing intestinal cells typically move away from the basal lamina toward the gland lumen (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: chromosomes of dividing goblet cells lack a surrounding nuclear membrane on EM (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"B":"The granules are not formed by the mitotic apparatus","C":"Exhausted surface cells are shed and do not reenter","D":"Paneth cells are absent from the human colon and","E":"Absorptive cells do not convert into goblet cells during these"}},{"id":"sdl24_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":5,"stem":"A 24-year-old man is found to have hundreds of colonic adenomas at colonoscopy. His father had a colectomy at age 35 for the same problem. The abdomen is soft and there is no mass. Which initial epithelial event leads to these small polyps?","choices":{"A":"Mutation of the KRAS proto-oncogene","B":"Deletion of the TP53 suppressor gene","C":"Methylation of the MLH1 repair gene","D":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","E":"Gain of the RET tyrosine kinase gene"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The first epithelial event in these familial adenomas is loss of the APC suppressor gene. Hundreds of polyps at a young age, with an affected father, match that earliest step rather than a later mutation. KRAS mutation occurs later in the adenoma sequence and does not initiate the small polyp. TP53 deletion is a late event associated with carcinoma, not the first step. MLH1 methylation is a mismatch-repair pathway and is not the start of this polyposis. Gain of RET is related to enteric neurons, not to the start of an adenoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: familial adenomatous polyposis is an autosomal dominant APC mutation on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, familial adenomatous polyposis). COMLEX: about 98% of colorectal cancers are adenocarcinomas arising from glandular epithelium (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"KRAS mutation occurs later in the adenoma sequence and","B":"TP53 deletion is a late event associated with carcinoma,","C":"MLH1 methylation is a mismatch-repair pathway and is not","E":"Gain of RET is related to enteric neurons, not"}},{"id":"sdl24_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a small hyperplastic polyp removed at colonoscopy. He has no chronic diarrhea or weight loss. Under the surface epithelium a layer of fibrillar protein and proteoglycans measures about 15 µm, several times the usual few micrometers. Which structure is thickened?","choices":{"A":"Basal lamina","B":"Muscularis mucosae","C":"Pericryptal sheath","D":"Submucosa","E":"Collagen table"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The fibrillar layer immediately under the surface epithelium is the collagen table, and 15 µm is thicker than its usual few micrometers. The polyp finding asks which named layer has that thickness. The basal lamina is a thin sheet and is not this 15-µm fibrillar layer. The muscularis mucosae is a muscle band deep to the glands, not a subepithelial fibrillar layer. The pericryptal sheath surrounds glands and is not the subepithelial table. The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae and is far thicker than 15 µm.","boardPrep":"Step 1: mucosal polyps are usually small, look like normal mucosa, and are clinically insignificant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: submucosal polyps may include lipomas, leiomyomas, and fibromas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basal lamina is a thin sheet and is","B":"The muscularis mucosae is a muscle band deep to","C":"The pericryptal sheath surrounds glands and is not the subepithelial","D":"The submucosa lies below the muscularis mucosae and is"}},{"id":"sdl24_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has mild cramping and a colonic biopsy. She is afebrile and the abdomen is soft. Large lymphatic nodules distort the spacing of the glands and extend into the submucosa. What best explains this extensive lymphoid development?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens","B":"Thin goblet-cell mucus layer","C":"Absence of epithelial renewal","D":"Abundant luminal microbes","E":"Drainage of lacteal lymph flow"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Colonic lymphoid tissue is extensive because the lumen holds abundant microbes. Nodules that reach the submucosa are the expected response to that microbial load. Absorption of dietary antigens is a small-bowel function and does not drive these colonic nodules. A thin mucus layer is not the reason the colon develops large lymphoid nodules. The epithelium renews continuously, so absence of renewal is not the explanation. Lacteal lymph flow belongs to small-bowel villi, which the colon does not have.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease microscopically shows noncaseating granulomas and lymphoid aggregates (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: large colonic lymphatic nodules extend from the lamina propria into the submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Absorption of dietary antigens is a small-bowel function and","B":"A thin mucus layer is not the reason the","C":"The epithelium renews continuously, so absence of renewal is","E":"Lacteal lymph flow belongs to small-bowel villi, which the"}},{"id":"sdl24_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":5,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy has fever and right lower quadrant pain. He has never had abdominal surgery, and there is no family history of cystic fibrosis. Temperature is 38.3°C and the right lower quadrant is tender. The leukocyte count is 16,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). No fecalith is found, and the appendix shows markedly enlarged lymphatic nodules with germinal centers. What is the most likely cause of the obstruction?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous scar tissue","B":"Lymphoid hyperplasia","C":"Carcinoid tumor mass","D":"Inspissated mucus plug","E":"Spasm of the teniae"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Without a fecalith, the lumen is closed by lymphoid hyperplasia of the enlarged nodules. Fever, leukocytosis, and germinal centers in the appendix wall support that swelling as the obstruction. Fibrous scar is not the acute obstructing lesion in a first attack without prior surgery. A carcinoid mass is not the typical obstructing lesion of acute appendicitis in a child. An inspissated mucus plug is the cystic-fibrosis pattern, and there is no such history. Spasm of the teniae is not how an appendix without a fecalith becomes obstructed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendiceal obstruction causes early diffuse periumbilical pain through T8-T10 visceral afferents (First Aid Gastrointestinal, appendicitis). COMLEX: the appendix is a common site of carcinoid, a tumor of enteroendocrine cells (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Pawlina","whyNot":{"A":"Fibrous scar is not the acute obstructing lesion in","C":"A carcinoid mass is not the typical obstructing lesion","D":"An inspissated mucus plug is the cystic-fibrosis pattern, and","E":"Spasm of the teniae is not how an appendix"}},{"id":"sdl24_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has a pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed at his first screening colonoscopy. The abdomen is soft. The polyp contains carcinoma confined to the mucosa above the muscularis mucosae. Why is endoscopic removal considered adequate?","choices":{"A":"Polyp stalks contain no blood vessels","B":"Basal lamina halts carcinoma cell spread","C":"Serosa blocks spread to the regional nodes","D":"Mucosal cancer cells stop dividing early","E":"No lymphatics in the lamina propria"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphatics are absent from the colonic lamina propria, so a carcinoma that has not crossed the muscularis mucosae cannot yet reach nodes. Confinement above that line is why polypectomy is enough. Polyp stalks do contain blood vessels, so absence of vessels is not the reason. The basal lamina is not the barrier that keeps this carcinoma from nodal spread. The serosa is far deep to a mucosal carcinoma and is not what makes polypectomy adequate. Mucosal cancer cells do not stop dividing early as the reason for cure.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous adenomas have more malignant potential than tubular ones ('villous histology is villainous') (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic polyps). COMLEX: the polyp stalk is continuous with the colonic submucosa (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polyp stalks do contain blood vessels, so absence of","B":"The basal lamina is not the barrier that keeps","C":"The serosa is far deep to a mucosal carcinoma","D":"Mucosal cancer cells do not stop dividing early as"}},{"id":"sdl24_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has bloody diarrhea. She has not had radiation. The abdomen is mildly tender. p-ANCA is positive, ASCA is negative, and hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). A colonic biopsy shows many lymphatic vessels in the lamina propria between the glands. What does this finding indicate?","choices":{"A":"Active inflammation","B":"Healed remission phase","C":"Normal colonic mucosa","D":"Early adenoma growth","E":"Ischemic mucosal injury"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Numerous lymphatics between the glands indicate active inflammation, which induces vessels the normal lamina propria lacks. Bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA is the colitis in which this appears. Healed mucosa does not show this new lymphatic network in the lamina propria. Normal colonic mucosa has essentially no lymphatics in the lamina propria. Early adenoma growth does not fill the lamina propria with new lymphatics. Ischemic injury thins the mucosa and does not produce this lymphatic proliferation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis complications include toxic megacolon and colorectal cancer, with risk rising in pancolitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: treatment progress in ulcerative colitis can be monitored by biopsies showing lymphatics disappearing (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Healed mucosa does not show this new lymphatic network","C":"Normal colonic mucosa has essentially no lymphatics in the lamina","D":"Early adenoma growth does not fill the lamina propria","E":"Ischemic injury thins the mucosa and does not produce"}},{"id":"sdl24_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, and biopsies show new lymphatic vessels forming in the colonic lamina propria. He has not had radiation. The abdomen is mildly tender. p-ANCA is positive and ASCA is negative. Expression of which factor is linked to this lymphangiogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met","C":"Vascular endothelial growth factor","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 signaling","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 axis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The new lamina-propria lymphatics of active colitis are linked to vascular endothelial growth factor. Bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA and a negative ASCA is the setting in which that factor drives the vessels. Transforming growth factor alpha is not the factor tied to these new lymphatics. Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met are not the drivers named for this lymphangiogenesis. Fibroblast growth factor 10 is a developmental signal and is not the factor linked here. Insulin-like growth factor 1 is not the factor linked to these new mucosal lymphatics.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: in ulcerative colitis, granulation tissue forms with proliferating blood and lymphatic vessels (Pawlina Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Transforming growth factor alpha is not the factor tied","B":"Hepatocyte growth factor and c-Met are not the drivers","D":"Fibroblast growth factor 10 is a developmental signal and","E":"Insulin-like growth factor 1 is not the factor linked"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has mild left lower quadrant discomfort. He has eaten a low-fiber diet for decades and is not taking antibiotics. The abdomen is soft, without peritonitis. CT shows many small outpouchings of the sigmoid wall without inflammation. In a resection specimen each outpouching is mucosa and submucosa only partly covered by muscle. Where in the wall do these lesions form?","choices":{"A":"Directly beneath a tenia coli band","B":"At gaps in the muscularis mucosae","C":"At vessel entry gaps in circular muscle","D":"Through every layer of the bowel wall","E":"Where lymphoid nodules enlarge the wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"These are false diverticula that herniate where vessels pierce gaps in the circular muscle. A low-fiber diet, sigmoid outpouchings, and a wall of mucosa plus submucosa without a full muscle coat mark that site. They do not form directly beneath a tenia, which is where the muscle is reinforced. Gaps in the muscularis mucosae are not the penetration site of these outpouchings. A through-and-through outpouching of every layer would be a true diverticulum, which this is not. Lymphoid nodules are not the point where these vascular gaps let mucosa herniate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic diverticula are pseudodiverticula of mucosa and submucosa at sites where vasa recta penetrate the circular muscle between the teniae (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Diverticulosis is common in older adults on low-fiber diets and can bleed or become inflamed.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"They do not form directly beneath a tenia, which","B":"Gaps in the muscularis mucosae are not the penetration","D":"A through-and-through outpouching of every layer would be a","E":"Lymphoid nodules are not the point where these vascular"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the main epithelial cell types of the large intestine (columnar absorptive cells and goblet cells) and explain their roles in water/electrolyte reabsorption and mucus secretion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had his colon removed and an end ileostomy after years of bloody diarrhea. p-ANCA had been positive and ASCA negative. Months later the stoma puts out a large volume of loose fluid, and on hot days he becomes lightheaded. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Loss of which colonic epithelial function best explains the larger fluid losses?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cells secreting lubricating mucus","B":"Absorptive cells reclaiming water and salt","C":"Enteroendocrine cells releasing hormones","D":"Paneth cells releasing antibacterial enzymes","E":"M cells moving antigens to lymphoid tissue"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colonocytes reclaim water and salt, so their removal leaves a high-volume ileostomy effluent and volume loss in the heat. The prior bloody diarrhea with p-ANCA was the reason the colon was removed. Goblet-cell mucus lubricates stool and does not account for the liters of fluid now lost. Enteroendocrine hormone release does not reclaim the water the stoma is now putting out. Paneth cells are not a normal colonic population and do not reclaim salt and water. M cells move antigen and do not absorb the fluid lost after colectomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic columnar absorptive cells reabsorb water and electrolytes, while goblet cells secrete lubricating mucus (Pawlina Ch. 17). COMLEX: Patients with an ileostomy are prone to dehydration and need higher fluid and salt intake.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 2 — electrolyte absorption in jejunum, ileum, and colon","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Goblet-cell mucus lubricates stool and does not account for","C":"Enteroendocrine hormone release does not reclaim the water the","D":"Paneth cells are not a normal colonic population and","E":"M cells move antigen and do not absorb the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man has a 4 mm rectosigmoid polyp removed at screening colonoscopy, which is otherwise normal. He has had no bleeding or change in bowel habit. Crypts and surface epithelium have a saw-tooth outline of crowded goblet and absorptive cells. The nuclei are normal and there is no dysplasia. Which alteration in epithelial renewal most likely produced this architecture?","choices":{"A":"Stem cells moved to the gland surface","B":"Loss of the APC suppressor gene","C":"Faster migration of cells off the surface","D":"Arrest of division in the crypt base","E":"Delayed shedding of surface cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A hyperplastic polyp with a saw-tooth surface and no dysplasia comes from delayed shedding of surface cells. Normal nuclei exclude the dysplastic sequence that begins with loss of a suppressor gene. Stem cells have not moved to the surface; the crowded cells are the ones that failed to shed. Loss of APC starts an adenoma, and this polyp has no dysplasia. Faster migration off the surface would thin the epithelium rather than crowd it. Division in the crypt base is not arrested in this lesion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperplastic polyps arise from decreased turnover and delayed shedding of surface cells and have no malignant potential (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: They must be distinguished from sessile serrated lesions, which look similar but can progress.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stem cells have not moved to the surface; the","B":"Loss of APC starts an adenoma, and this polyp","C":"Faster migration off the surface would thin the epithelium","D":"Division in the crypt base is not arrested in this"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has four months of watery diarrhea without blood or weight loss. She does not use laxatives. The abdomen is soft and colonoscopy looks normal. Biopsies show a collagen band 25 µm thick, directly under the surface basal lamina, where this layer is normally only about 5 µm, plus increased intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which normal function of this layer is most likely disturbed?","choices":{"A":"Anchoring the teniae coli muscle","B":"Supplying macrophages to the core","C":"Moving absorbed fluid to capillaries","D":"Housing the crypt stem cell niche","E":"Draining lymph away from the gland bases"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The thickened subepithelial collagen table normally moves absorbed fluid toward capillaries, and a 25-µm band blocks that path. Chronic watery diarrhea with a normal-looking colonoscopy fits that disturbed function. Anchoring the teniae is not the job of this thin subepithelial layer. Supplying macrophages is not the role of the collagen table. The crypt stem-cell niche sits at the gland base, not in this subepithelial band. Lymph leaves from deeper vessels, and this layer is not the lymphatic drain of the gland base.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Collagenous colitis shows a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm with intraepithelial lymphocytes and causes chronic watery diarrhea with a normal endoscopy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Older women with chronic watery diarrhea and normal colonoscopy need random biopsies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Anchoring the teniae is not the job of this","B":"Supplying macrophages is not the role of the collagen","D":"The crypt stem-cell niche sits at the gland base,","E":"Lymph leaves from deeper vessels, and this layer is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the lymphatic drainage pattern of the large intestine and explain how the absence of lamina propria lymphatic vessels impacts disease processes such as adenomatous polyp progression and ulcerative colitis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old man has lost 4 kg and has a sigmoid mass that has grown through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa and muscularis. He has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 16 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). The surgeon removes the regional lymph nodes with the specimen. Which named node group receives lymph from this segment?","choices":{"A":"Superior mesenteric nodes","B":"Superficial inguinal nodes","C":"Internal iliac nodes","D":"Inferior mesenteric nodes","E":"Para-aortic nodes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The sigmoid colon drains to inferior mesenteric nodes. A carcinoma that has entered the submucosa, with an elevated carcinoembryonic antigen, is the lesion whose regional nodes are that group. Superior mesenteric nodes drain midgut colon, not the sigmoid. Superficial inguinal nodes drain the anal canal below the pectinate line, not the sigmoid. Internal iliac nodes are not the primary group for the sigmoid colon. Para-aortic nodes are a later station, not the named regional group removed with this segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon from the splenic flexure to the upper rectum drains to inferior mesenteric nodes, and the lower rectum to internal iliac nodes (First Aid 2025 Anatomy). COMLEX: Colon cancer staging depends on whether the tumor has reached the lymphatic-bearing layers and regional nodes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Superior mesenteric nodes drain midgut colon, not the","B":"Superficial inguinal nodes drain the anal canal below the","C":"Internal iliac nodes are not the primary group for","E":"Para-aortic nodes are a later station, not the named"}},{"id":"trial_sdl24_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile rectosigmoid mass. She has no history of colitis or polyposis. Biopsy shows long, slender, finger-like projections lined by dysplastic columnar epithelium, a surface pattern never seen in normal colonic mucosa. Compared with a polyp built of dysplastic tubular glands, which feature is this lesion most likely to have?","choices":{"A":"No malignant potential","B":"Origin from submucosal fat","C":"Hamartomatous growth of normal tissue","D":"Greater malignant potential","E":"Regenerating mucosa after colitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Finger-like dysplastic projections are a villous adenoma, which has greater malignant potential than a tubular adenoma. The sessile rectosigmoid mass with that surface pattern is the higher-risk lesion. This dysplastic villous architecture is not free of malignant potential. The projections are mucosal epithelium, not growth from submucosal fat. Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized normal tissue and are not lined by dysplastic epithelium. Regenerating mucosa after colitis is not a mass of dysplastic finger-like projections.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Normal colonic mucosa has no villi, and villous adenomas have the greatest malignant potential of the adenomatous polyps (Bootcamp Gastroenterology). COMLEX: Large sessile villous lesions need complete removal and close surveillance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"This dysplastic villous architecture is not free of malignant","B":"The projections are mucosal epithelium, not growth from submucosal","C":"Hamartomatous polyps are disorganized normal tissue and are not","E":"Regenerating mucosa after colitis is not a mass of"}},{"id":"sdl24_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the distinctive gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli, haustra coli, omental appendices, and the smooth mucosal surface without villi or plicae circulares.","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man is having a screening colonoscopy. He has never had bleeding or a change in stool caliber. The examination is easy, the mucosa is pale pink, and there is no mass. Hemoglobin is 14.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Between the outward sacculations of the colon, the endoscopist sees crescent-shaped internal folds. Which structures are these?","choices":{"A":"Plicae circulares","B":"Semilunar folds","C":"Rugae of the stomach","D":"Spiral valves of the cystic duct","E":"Circular folds of the jejunum only"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Semilunar folds are the internal shelves that form between haustra when the muscularis externa contracts. Plicae circulares are permanent submucosal folds of the small intestine, which this colon does not have. Gastric rugae are folds of the stomach, not shelves between colonic haustra. Spiral valves are mucosal folds of the cystic duct. Circular folds of the jejunum are plicae circulares, absent from colonic mucosa.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Plicae circulares are permanent submucosal folds of the small intestine, which this colon does not have","C":"Gastric rugae are folds of the stomach, not shelves between colonic haustra","D":"Spiral valves are mucosal folds of the cystic duct","E":"Circular folds of the jejunum are plicae circulares, absent from colonic mucosa"}},{"id":"sdl24_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 33-year-old healthy man volunteers for a colonic epithelial labeling study. He has one formed stool a day and takes no laxatives. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 15.1 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Three weeks later a few labeled cells are still on the surface, long after the absorptive cells have been shed. Which cell type accounts for those persistent labels?","choices":{"A":"Enteroendocrine cells","B":"Surface absorptive cells that normally live for months","C":"Mature goblet cells that never leave the surface","D":"Parietal cells that migrated from the stomach","E":"Hepatocytes shed into the crypt"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colonic enteroendocrine cells migrate more slowly than absorptive and goblet cells and can live up to four weeks. Surface absorptive cells turn over in about six days, so a three-week label is not an ordinary colonocyte. Goblet cells are shed on the same short timetable as absorptive cells, not after three weeks. Parietal cells are gastric and do not colonize colonic crypts. Hepatocytes are liver cells and are not the long-lived labeled cells of colonic epithelium.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Surface absorptive cells turn over in about six days, so a three-week label is not an ordinary colonocyte","C":"Goblet cells are shed on the same short timetable as absorptive cells, not after three weeks","D":"Parietal cells are gastric and do not colonize colonic crypts","E":"Hepatocytes are liver cells and are not the long-lived labeled cells of colonic epithelium"}},{"id":"sdl24_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Outline the process of epithelial cell renewal in the large intestine, including the stem cell location at the base of intestinal glands and turnover times for absorptive and goblet cells.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman joins a study of colonic cell division. She has regular bowel habits and no family history of polyposis. The abdomen is soft and colonoscopy is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Newly generated crypt cells are seen dividing two or three more times as they move up the gland. What does that finding show?","choices":{"A":"Surface cells re-enter the cell cycle after they are shed","B":"Paneth cells are the only dividing cells in the colon","C":"Stem cells are confined to the luminal surface","D":"The amplifying compartment keeps dividing during upward migration","E":"Enteroendocrine cells divide every few hours for months"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intermediate cells in the lower third of the colonic gland undergo two or three further divisions as they migrate upward. Cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed, and they are not the population still dividing in the gland. Paneth cells are normally absent from human colonic epithelium. The stem-cell niche is at the base of the gland, not on the luminal surface. Enteroendocrine cells migrate slowly and are not the cells adding two or three divisions on the way up the gland.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cells that reach the surface undergo apoptosis and are shed, and they are not the population still dividing in the gland","B":"Paneth cells are normally absent from human colonic epithelium","C":"The stem-cell niche is at the base of the gland, not on the luminal surface","E":"Enteroendocrine cells migrate slowly and are not the cells adding two or three divisions on the way up the gland"}},{"id":"sdl24_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Summarize the specializations of the lamina propria in the large intestine, including the collagen table, pericryptal fibroblast sheath, and the development of GALT.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has mild cramping, and a colonic biopsy is taken. She is afebrile and has no weight loss. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 13.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and the leukocyte count is 7,200/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). The biopsy shows large lymphoid nodules that push glands apart and cross the muscularis mucosae. How far do these nodules normally extend?","choices":{"A":"They stay inside a single surface enterocyte","B":"They are limited to the serosa and never reach mucosa","C":"They replace the teniae coli","D":"They fill the lumen of the inferior mesenteric artery","E":"Through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Large colonic lymphoid nodules distort gland spacing and extend through the muscularis mucosae into the submucosa. These nodules are lymphoid tissue in the wall, not inclusions inside one enterocyte. The nodules begin in the mucosa and lamina propria, not as a serosal-only collection. Teniae coli are bands of the outer longitudinal muscle and are not lymphoid nodules. The nodules are intramural lymphoid tissue and do not occupy the inferior mesenteric artery.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"These nodules are lymphoid tissue in the wall, not inclusions inside one enterocyte","B":"The nodules begin in the mucosa and lamina propria, not as a serosal-only collection","C":"Teniae coli are bands of the outer longitudinal muscle and are not lymphoid nodules","D":"The nodules are intramural lymphoid tissue and do not occupy the inferior mesenteric artery"}}]},{"sdlNumber":25,"title":"SDL 25 — Physiology - Small & Large Intestinal Motility","questions":[{"id":"sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Slow waves in intestinal smooth muscle originate in which cells?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Myenteric ganglion neurons","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells","E":"Longitudinal smooth muscle cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Costanzo describes the interstitial cells of Cajal, abundant in the myenteric plexus, as the pacemaker of gastrointestinal smooth muscle; their spontaneous depolarizations spread to smooth muscle through gap junctions. Myenteric and submucosal neurons modulate action potentials and force but do not set slow wave rhythm. Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it. Enterochromaffin-like cells are gastric mucosal cells that release histamine to stimulate parietal cell acid secretion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Slow waves, origin of slow waves); Guyton Ch. 63 (slow waves). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: interstitial cells of Cajal act like the sinoatrial node of the gut and couple to smooth muscle by gap junctions (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: unitary smooth muscle lets action potentials spread rapidly from cell to cell (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Myenteric neurons modulate action potentials and force; they do not set slow-wave rhythm.","E":"Smooth muscle cells follow the pacemaker rather than generate it.","C":"Submucosal plexus neurons do not set slow-wave rhythm.","D":"Enterochromaffin-like cells release histamine to stimulate parietal cell acid secretion."}},{"id":"sdl25_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the intrinsic slow wave frequency of the duodenum?","choices":{"A":"3 per minute","B":"6 per minute","C":"9 per minute","D":"12 per minute","E":"20 per minute"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Costanzo gives slow wave frequencies from 3 to 12 per minute along the tract, with the duodenum highest at 12 per minute. Three per minute is the stomach, the lowest rate. Nine per minute is the ileum. Six per minute is not a characteristic value given, and 20 per minute exceeds the stated range. Three slow waves per minute is the stomach rate, the lowest in the GI tract. Nine slow waves per minute is the ileal rate. Six slow waves per minute is not a characteristic gut rate. Twenty per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves, whose maximum is the duodenal rate of 12. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; Small intestinal motility); Guyton Ch. 64 (segmentation maximum near 12 per minute in duodenum, 8 to 9 in terminal ileum). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow wave frequency sets the maximum contraction frequency, so segmentation in the proximal small intestine reaches at most about 12 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow wave frequency falls from duodenum to terminal ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Three slow waves per minute is the stomach rate, the lowest in the GI tract.","C":"Nine slow waves per minute is the ileal rate.","B":"Six slow waves per minute is not a characteristic gut rate.","E":"Twenty per minute exceeds the 3 to 12 per minute range of gastrointestinal slow waves, whose maximum is the duodenal rate of 12."}},{"id":"sdl25_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"How does parasympathetic stimulation change the electrical activity of intestinal smooth muscle?","choices":{"A":"Higher slow wave frequency","B":"More spikes per slow wave","C":"Larger slow wave amplitude","D":"Shorter slow wave plateau","E":"Longer repolarization phase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Neural and hormonal inputs do not alter the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation increases the number of action potentials on the slow wave plateau and so strengthens contraction. A higher slow wave frequency is the classic trap because that rhythm is set by the pacemaker. Slow wave amplitude, plateau, and repolarization are set by pacemaker calcium and potassium currents and are not described as vagal targets; sympathetic input instead decreases spikes. Neural input does not change the intrinsic slow wave frequency; parasympathetic stimulation adds action potentials on top of each slow wave. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Frequency of slow waves; gastric and small intestinal motility). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the more action potentials ride on a slow wave, the larger the phasic contraction (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: acetylcholine depolarizes gut smooth muscle and norepinephrine hyperpolarizes it (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 2 — parasympathetic and sympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neural input does not change the intrinsic slow wave frequency."}},{"id":"sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main role of segmentation contractions in the small intestine?","choices":{"A":"Propelling chyme toward the cecum","B":"Clearing residue during fasting","C":"Emptying stomach into duodenum","D":"Moving feces into the rectum","E":"Mixing chyme with secretions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Segmentation splits a bolus orad and caudad and then lets it recombine, mixing chyme with pancreatic enzymes and exposing it to the mucosa with no forward propulsion. Propulsion toward the cecum is peristalsis. Fasting clearance is the migrating complex. Gastric emptying and rectal filling by mass movements are other patterns. Propulsion toward the large intestine is the job of peristaltic contractions; segmentation produces no forward movement. Clearing residual chyme during fasting is done by the migrating myoelectric complexes every 90 minutes. Moving feces into the rectum is done by colonic mass movements, not small intestinal segmentation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Segmentation contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: segmentation mixes and peristalsis propels, both coordinated by the enteric nervous system (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: segmentation movements may still travel about 1 cm anally, so the two patterns overlap (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Propulsion toward the large intestine is the job of peristaltic contractions; segmentation produces no forward movement.","B":"Clearing residual chyme during fasting is done by the migrating myoelectric complexes every 90 minutes.","D":"Moving feces into the rectum is done by colonic mass movements, not small intestinal segmentation."}},{"id":"sdl25_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"During small intestinal peristalsis, what happens to the segment orad to the bolus?","choices":{"A":"It narrows and lengthens","B":"It widens and shortens","C":"It narrows and shortens","D":"It widens and lengthens","E":"It keeps a fixed diameter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts while longitudinal muscle relaxes, so the segment narrows and lengthens. Widening and shortening is the caudad segment, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Narrowing with shortening or widening with lengthening would need both layers to contract or relax together, which reciprocal innervation prevents. A fixed diameter does not propel the bolus. Widening and shortening happens in front of (caudad to) the bolus, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts. Behind the bolus, longitudinal muscle relaxes while circular muscle contracts, so the segment lengthens rather than shortens. Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts, so the segment narrows rather than widens. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions, Fig. 8.12). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: excitatory transmitters such as ACh and substance P act behind the bolus, inhibitory VIP and NO ahead of it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: contraction of circular muscle narrows a segment and longitudinal contraction shortens it (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Widening and shortening happens in front of (caudad to) the bolus, where circular muscle relaxes and longitudinal muscle contracts.","C":"Behind the bolus, longitudinal muscle relaxes while circular muscle contracts, so the segment lengthens rather than shortens.","D":"Behind the bolus, circular muscle contracts, so the segment narrows rather than widens."}},{"id":"sdl25_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which mucosal mediator activates intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex?","choices":{"A":"Somatostatin","B":"Serotonin","C":"Histamine","D":"Glucagon","E":"Secretin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the bolus is sensed by mucosal enterochromaffin (EC) cells, which release serotonin onto intrinsic primary afferent neurons to start the peristaltic reflex. Somatostatin is an inhibitory paracrine. Histamine is a gastric acid secretagogue. Glucagon and secretin inhibit small intestinal motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Peristaltic contractions). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT from mucosal cells activates IPANs that initiate peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrin, CCK, insulin, motilin, and serotonin enhance small intestinal motility, while secretin and glucagon inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"At what interval do migrating myoelectric complexes recur during fasting?","choices":{"A":"At 5 minute intervals","B":"At 30 minute intervals","C":"At 90 minute intervals","D":"At 4 hour intervals","E":"At 12 hour intervals"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo states that migrating myoelectric complexes recur about every 90 minutes during fasting to clear residual chyme from the stomach and small intestine. The shorter and longer intervals do not match that fasting cycle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (gastric motility; Small intestinal motility; motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the interdigestive complex sweeps from stomach through small intestine every 90 minutes while fasting (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: fasting complexes clear the stomach of residual chyme from the previous meal (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Every 5 minutes is far too frequent.","B":"A 30-minute interval is too short.","D":"A 4-hour interval is too long.","E":"A 12-hour interval would allow only one or two complexes per fast."}},{"id":"sdl25_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone initiates the interdigestive migrating complexes?","choices":{"A":"Cholecystokinin","B":"Secretin","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Motilin","E":"Peptide YY"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, initiates the interdigestive myoelectric complexes. CCK and secretin are fed-state hormones that slow gastric emptying. Ghrelin is a gastric hunger hormone, not the initiator of fasting complexes. Peptide YY from ileum and colon inhibits secretion and appetite. CCK, with gastrin, mediates the efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex; motilin initiates the interdigestive complexes. Secretin decreases action potential frequency and contraction force, so it does not trigger the fasting complexes. Peptide YY from the ileum and colon inhibits gastric acid and pancreatic secretion and decreases appetite; it does not start the complexes. Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal to increase appetite. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (candidate hormones, motilin). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: motilin is a 22 amino acid candidate hormone outside the gastrin-CCK and secretin families (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin receptor agonists such as erythromycin are used as prokinetics (First Aid Gastrointestinal, GI hormones).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CCK, with gastrin, mediates the efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex; motilin initiates the interdigestive complexes.","B":"Secretin decreases action potential frequency and contraction force, so it does not trigger the fasting complexes.","E":"Peptide YY from the ileum and colon inhibits gastric acid and pancreatic secretion and decreases appetite; it does not start the complexes.","C":"Ghrelin is secreted by gastric cells just before a meal to increase appetite."}},{"id":"sdl25_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":1,"stem":"How does eating a meal affect motilin secretion?","choices":{"A":"It inhibits secretion","B":"It stimulates secretion","C":"It doubles the cycle length","D":"It shifts release to the ileum","E":"It has no measurable effect"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guyton states that motilin is released cyclically during fasting and that its secretion is inhibited after ingestion of food, which is why the fasting complexes stop in the fed state. Stimulation is the opposite effect. Cycle length doubling and an ileal shift are not described; motilin comes from stomach and upper duodenum. Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, so a meal does not stimulate its release. Costanzo states motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, not shifted to the ileum by a meal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 63 (motilin); Costanzo Ch. 8 (motilin secreted during fasting). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: feeding replaces fasting complexes with segmentation and peristalsis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: motilin is secreted by the stomach and upper duodenum, not the ileum (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, so a meal does not stimulate its release.","D":"Costanzo states motilin is secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting, not shifted to the ileum by a meal."}},{"id":"sdl25_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"How often do colonic mass movements occur?","choices":{"A":"Ten to twelve times per minute","B":"Three to five times per hour","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day","D":"Once or twice per week","E":"One to three times per day"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo states that mass movements move colonic contents long distances, such as transverse to sigmoid colon, one to three times per day. Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm. The hourly and frequent daily options are too often, and once or twice per week too rare. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Mass movements); Guyton Ch. 64 (series of mass movements lasting 10 to 30 minutes). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a final mass movement propels feces into the rectum and produces the urge to defecate (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: during a mass movement the colon loses haustrations and contracts as a unit (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten to twelve per minute resembles duodenal slow wave rhythm.","B":"Three to five times per hour is far more often than colonic mass movements.","C":"Twenty to thirty times per day is far more often than colonic mass movements.","D":"Once or twice per week is less often than colonic mass movements."}},{"id":"sdl25_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"The efferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is mediated by which hormones?","choices":{"A":"Secretin and GIP","B":"Motilin and ghrelin","C":"CCK and gastrin","D":"Somatostatin and PYY","E":"VIP and nitric oxide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Costanzo describes gastric distention as the afferent limb, carried by the parasympathetic nervous system, and CCK and gastrin as the efferent limb that increases colonic motility and mass movements. Secretin and GIP decrease contraction force. Motilin and ghrelin are fasting-state peptides; motilin drives migrating complexes and ghrelin stimulates appetite. Somatostatin inhibits release of the other GI hormones, and peptide YY inhibits gastric and pancreatic secretion. VIP and nitric oxide are inhibitory neurocrines that relax gastrointestinal smooth muscle. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Gastrocolic reflex). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the gastrocolic reflex explains the urge to defecate soon after a meal (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: gastrocolic and duodenocolic reflexes nearly disappear when extrinsic autonomic nerves to the colon are removed (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Secretin and GIP decrease contraction force.","B":"Motilin and ghrelin are fasting-state peptides; motilin drives migrating complexes and ghrelin stimulates appetite.","D":"Somatostatin inhibits release of the other GI hormones, and peptide YY inhibits gastric and pancreatic secretion.","E":"VIP and nitric oxide are inhibitory neurocrines that relax gastrointestinal smooth muscle."}},{"id":"sdl25_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":1,"stem":"Which combined contraction forms colonic haustrations?","choices":{"A":"Circular and teniae coli relaxation","B":"Muscularis mucosae fold contraction","C":"External anal sphincter contraction","D":"Circular and teniae coli contraction","E":"Ileocecal sphincter tonic contraction"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Guyton describes about 2.5 cm of circular muscle constricting while the teniae coli also contract, so the unstimulated wall bulges into baglike haustrations. Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs. The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa, and the anal and ileocecal sphincters are not involved. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Mixing movements, haustrations); Costanzo Ch. 8 (segmentation in the cecum and proximal colon). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: haustral contractions peak in about 30 seconds and fade over the next 60 seconds (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: slow haustral mixing takes 8 to 15 hours to move chyme from the ileocecal valve through the colon (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The external anal sphincter is striated muscle for continence.","A":"Relaxation of both layers would not form sacs.","E":"The ileocecal sphincter contracts to prevent reflux into the ileum.","B":"The muscularis mucosae folds the mucosa."}},{"id":"sdl25_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"What does rectal distention cause in the rectosphincteric reflex?","choices":{"A":"Relaxation of the external anal sphincter","B":"Contraction of the internal anal sphincter","C":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter","D":"Relaxation of the ileocecal sphincter","E":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"C","explanation":"As the rectum fills, its smooth muscle contracts and the internal anal sphincter relaxes in the rectosphincteric reflex. The external sphincter stays tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily. Internal sphincter contraction is the opposite response. Ileocecal sphincter contraction follows entry of chyme into the cecum and is unrelated to rectal filling. The external anal sphincter stays tonically contracted during the rectosphincteric reflex and relaxes only voluntarily. Rectal distention relaxes, not contracts, the internal anal sphincter in the rectosphincteric reflex. Rectal distention relaxes the internal anal sphincter, not the ileocecal sphincter, which acts at the junction of ileum and cecum. The ileocecal sphincter contracts after chyme enters the cecum to prevent reflux into the ileum, a response unrelated to rectal distention. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation is delayed after internal sphincter relaxation because the striated external sphincter remains contracted (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the internal anal sphincter is a tonic smooth muscle sphincter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The external anal sphincter stays tonically contracted during the rectosphincteric reflex and relaxes only voluntarily.","B":"Rectal distention relaxes, not contracts, the internal anal sphincter in the rectosphincteric reflex.","D":"Rectal distention relaxes the internal anal sphincter, not the ileocecal sphincter, which acts at the junction of ileum and cecum.","E":"The ileocecal sphincter contracts after chyme enters the cecum to prevent reflux into the ileum, a response unrelated to rectal distention."}},{"id":"sdl25_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does defecation not occur as soon as rectal filling relaxes the internal anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Rectal smooth muscle stays fully relaxed","B":"External sphincter stays contracted","C":"Pelvic nerve activity stops completely","D":"Internal sphincter recontracts at once","E":"Gastrocolic reflex input is suppressed"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal anal sphincter, but the striated external anal sphincter is under voluntary control and remains tonically contracted until it is relaxed voluntarily when defecation is appropriate. The rectal smooth muscle actually contracts as the rectum fills, so it does not stay relaxed. Pelvic parasympathetic nerves carry the rectal reflex; their activity does not stop with filling. The internal sphincter relaxes in the reflex, and its recontraction is not what holds stool back. The gastrocolic reflex speeds colonic mass movements and is not what keeps the anus closed. Sources: Costanzo ch 8, defecation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Defecation needs internal sphincter relaxation by reflex plus voluntary external sphincter relaxation, with rectal contraction and Valsalva (Costanzo ch 8). COMLEX: External sphincter or pudendal injury causes urge incontinence despite normal rectal sensation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectal smooth muscle actually contracts as","C":"Pelvic parasympathetic nerves carry the rectal reflex;","D":"The internal sphincter relaxes in the reflex,","E":"The gastrocolic reflex speeds colonic mass movements"}},{"id":"sdl25_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nerve controls the external anal sphincter?","choices":{"A":"Pelvic splanchnic nerves","B":"Hypogastric nerves","C":"Vagus nerve","D":"Inferior mesenteric nerves","E":"Pudendal nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Guyton states that the external anal sphincter, striated voluntary muscle, is controlled by the somatic pudendal nerve. Pelvic splanchnic nerves carry sacral parasympathetic fibers that reinforce the defecation reflex. Hypogastric and inferior mesenteric nerves are sympathetic. The vagus does not reach the anal canal. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation); Costanzo Ch. 8 (external anal sphincter is striated). Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the external anal sphincter is striated voluntary muscle, unlike the smooth muscle internal sphincter (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic outflow comes from cord segments S2 to S4 (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has abdominal distention and has passed no flatus for two days after an open colectomy. Opioids were stopped yesterday, and imaging shows an intact anastomosis without peritonitis. Temperature is 37.2°C, the pulse is 96/min, blood pressure is 134/82 mm Hg, and bowel sounds are absent. Serum potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Heightened sympathetic outflow is suspected. Which change in intestinal smooth-muscle electrical activity is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Faster pacemaker slow wave rhythm","B":"Slower pacemaker slow wave rhythm","C":"Fewer spikes on each slow wave","D":"More spikes on each slow wave","E":"Loss of gap junction coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sympathetic outflow reduces the spikes that ride on each slow wave, so contractions fade after surgery. A soft ileus with normal potassium and absent bowel sounds fits fewer spikes rather than a change in pacemaker rate. Sympathetic input does not speed the pacemaker slow-wave rhythm. The slow-wave rhythm itself is not slowed by this sympathetic surge. More spikes would increase contractions, the opposite of this silent abdomen. Gap-junction coupling of the muscle is not what sympathetic outflow removes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal irritation inhibits excitatory enteric nerves and can cause intestinal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sympathetic fibers to the small intestine arise from the celiac and superior mesenteric ganglia (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — sympathetic and parasympathetic effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sympathetic input does not speed the pacemaker slow-wave","B":"The slow-wave rhythm itself is not slowed by this sympathetic","D":"More spikes would increase contractions, the opposite of this silent","E":"Gap-junction coupling of the muscle is not what sympathetic outflow"}},{"id":"sdl25_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman develops constipation and a dry mouth three weeks after starting oxybutynin for urge incontinence. She is not taking opioids. The abdomen is soft and bowel sounds are present. Serum calcium is 9.4 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Reduced action of which transmitter on intestinal smooth muscle best explains the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Norepinephrine","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide","C":"Nitric oxide","D":"Somatostatin","E":"Acetylcholine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A drug that blocks muscarinic receptors for urge incontinence also blocks acetylcholine on gut smooth muscle, slowing motility. Dry mouth with new constipation is the cholinergic pair of effects. Norepinephrine is a sympathetic transmitter and is not the signal this bladder drug blocks. Vasoactive intestinal peptide relaxes muscle and is not the transmitter removed here. Nitric oxide mediates inhibitory relaxation and is not the blocked excitatory transmitter. Somatostatin is not the muscarinic transmitter this incontinence drug interrupts.","boardPrep":"Step 1: anticholinergic adverse effects include constipation, dry mouth, urinary retention, and mydriasis (First Aid Pharmacology, muscarinic antagonists). COMLEX: postganglionic parasympathetic neurons sit in the myenteric and submucosal plexuses (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 2 — parasympathetic neurotransmitters and effects on GI function","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Norepinephrine is a sympathetic transmitter and is not the","B":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide relaxes muscle and is not the","C":"Nitric oxide mediates inhibitory relaxation and is not the","D":"Somatostatin is not the muscarinic transmitter this incontinence drug"}},{"id":"sdl25_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had bloating, vomiting, and distention for two years, and imaging shows no mechanical blockage. She does not have diabetes and is not taking anticholinergic drugs. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, with quiet bowel sounds. A full-thickness jejunal biopsy shows loss of the pacemaker cells around the myenteric plexus, and serum TSH is 2.1 µU/mL (reference range: 0.4–4.5 µU/mL). Loss of which function most likely explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release","B":"Pacemaker slow wave generation","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output","E":"Somatic sphincter control"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The missing cells around the myenteric plexus are the pacemakers that generate slow waves. Without them the bowel cannot organize contractions, which matches a dilated gut with no mechanical obstruction. Mucosal serotonin release starts reflexes but is not the pacemaker function that was lost. Inhibitory nitrergic signaling is a nerve function, not the role of these pacemaker cells. Sympathetic ganglion output is outside the gut wall and is not the missing cell population. Somatic sphincter control uses skeletal muscle and is unrelated to these jejunal pacemakers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: slow waves are not action potentials but oscillations in membrane potential set by pacemaker cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: slow wave depolarization is due to inward calcium current and repolarization to outward potassium current (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system regulation of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucosal serotonin release starts reflexes but is not the","C":"Inhibitory nitrergic signaling is a nerve function, not the","D":"Sympathetic ganglion output is outside the gut wall and","E":"Somatic sphincter control uses skeletal muscle and is unrelated"}},{"id":"sdl25_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":5,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has profuse watery diarrhea and cramps 12 hours after eating undercooked chicken. Temperature is 38.3°C, the pulse is 104/min, and the abdomen is soft with hyperactive bowel sounds. Barium given earlier for another study reached the colon from the jejunum within minutes. Which motility pattern best explains this rapid transit?","choices":{"A":"Peristaltic rush","B":"Segmentation contractions","C":"Migrating motor complex","D":"Haustral contractions","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A powerful peristaltic rush sweeps contents from jejunum to colon within minutes during this infectious diarrhea. Cramping, fever, and that rapid barium transit distinguish it from mixing or a fasting complex. Segmentation mixes contents and does not race barium to the colon in minutes. A migrating motor complex is a fasting housekeeping wave, not this fed infectious rush. Haustral contractions are slow colonic mixing movements. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum after a meal and does not flush the whole small bowel.","boardPrep":"Step 1: normal small intestinal peristalsis moves chyme only about 1 cm/min, taking 3 to 5 hours from pylorus to ileocecal valve (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: peristaltic rush is driven by autonomic and brainstem reflexes plus intrinsic myenteric reflexes (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"B":"Segmentation mixes contents and does not race barium to","C":"A migrating motor complex is a fasting housekeeping wave,","D":"Haustral contractions are slow colonic mixing","E":"The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum after a meal"}},{"id":"sdl25_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman in a drug trial receives an agent that inhibits neuronal nitric oxide synthase in the gut plexuses. Over the next week she has cramps. The abdomen is mildly tender and blood pressure is 128/80 mm Hg. Transit of a radiopaque marker is slowed. Failure of which step of the peristaltic reflex best explains the slowing?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of circular muscle behind the bolus","B":"Contraction of longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus","C":"Relaxation of circular muscle ahead of the bolus","D":"Release of serotonin from mucosal cells","E":"Slow wave generation in pacemaker cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"VIP and nitric oxide relax circular muscle ahead of a bolus, and blocking nitric oxide synthase removes that receiving relaxation. Cramping with slowed transit follows when the bowel cannot open in front of the contents. Contraction behind the bolus is the excitatory cholinergic step, not the nitric-oxide step that was blocked. Longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus is not the nitric-oxide relaxation that failed. Serotonin release from mucosal cells starts the reflex and is not the inhibited enzyme. Slow-wave generation by pacemaker cells continues and is not nitric-oxide synthase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP and NO are inhibitory enteric neurocrines that relax gut smooth muscle (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: reciprocal innervation keeps circular and longitudinal layers from contracting together (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 2 — esophageal peristalsis and its neural control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Contraction behind the bolus is the excitatory cholinergic step,","B":"Longitudinal muscle ahead of the bolus is not the","D":"Serotonin release from mucosal cells starts the reflex and","E":"Slow-wave generation by pacemaker cells continues and is not nitric-oxide"}},{"id":"sdl25_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 60-year-old man with abdominal cramps takes a drug that blocks muscarinic receptors. Imaging shows no mechanical obstruction. Jejunal manometry before the dose shows ring contractions about 11 times per minute; afterward the rings fade while the slow-wave frequency is unchanged. Serum magnesium is 2.0 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). This change shows that his mixing contractions depend on which input?","choices":{"A":"Circulating motilin from the duodenum","B":"Excitation from the myenteric plexus","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion","D":"Voluntary outflow from the sacral cord","E":"Stretch afferents of the colonic wall"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The mixing rings fade when muscarinic excitation from the myenteric plexus is blocked, while the slow-wave frequency stays the same. That split shows the contractions need neural excitation on top of the pacemaker rhythm. Circulating motilin drives fasting complexes, not these fed mixing rings. Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion inhibits the gut and is not the muscarinic drive that was blocked. Voluntary sacral outflow does not generate jejunal mixing rings. Stretch afferents of the colon are not the muscarinic input removed by this drug.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the terminal ileum the maximum segmentation rate is about 8 to 9 per minute (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: segmentation exposes chyme to pancreatic enzymes and the mucosa for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and extrinsic modulation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Circulating motilin drives fasting complexes, not these fed mixing","C":"Sympathetic outflow from the celiac ganglion inhibits the gut","D":"Voluntary sacral outflow does not generate jejunal mixing","E":"Stretch afferents of the colon are not the muscarinic"}},{"id":"sdl25_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":5,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with long-standing type 1 diabetes has early satiety and vomiting after meals. He takes insulin and has never had gastric surgery. The abdomen is soft. A gastric-emptying study shows marked delay, and a low dose of a macrolide antibiotic improves the symptoms. Hemoglobin A1c is 8.2% (reference range: <5.7%). Which drug action most likely explains the improvement?","choices":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism","B":"Muscarinic receptor agonism","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism","D":"Mu opioid receptor agonism","E":"Motilin receptor agonism"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Low-dose erythromycin speeds emptying by agonism at motilin receptors. Delayed emptying in long-standing diabetes that improves on that macrolide fits this prokinetic action. Dopamine D2 antagonism is the action of a different prokinetic, not of this macrolide. Direct muscarinic agonism is not how a low-dose macrolide improves emptying. Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism reduces nausea and does not provide this prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism slows the gut and would worsen the delay.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist prokinetic that can cause tardive dyskinesia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, metoclopramide). COMLEX: motilin is released cyclically during fasting and suppressed by eating (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 3 — gastric mixing and propulsion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Dopamine D2 antagonism is the action of a different","B":"Direct muscarinic agonism is not how a low-dose macrolide improves","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 antagonism reduces nausea and does not provide","D":"Mu opioid agonism slows the gut and would worsen the"}},{"id":"sdl25_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with tight skin over the fingers has chronic bloating and loose stools. She is not taking opioids and has had no intestinal surgery. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. A lactulose breath test shows an early hydrogen rise, and fasting antroduodenal recordings show no phase III activity over six hours. Serum albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Loss of which function most likely contributed?","choices":{"A":"Postprandial mixing of jejunal chyme","B":"Rectal storage of formed feces","C":"Colonic absorption of luminal water","D":"Interdigestive clearance of residue","E":"Gastrocolic reflex after meals"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Absent phase III activity means the migrating motor complex is lost, so residue is no longer cleared between meals. An early hydrogen rise and loose stools follow bacterial overgrowth when that sweep is gone. Postprandial mixing is a fed pattern and is not the missing fasting phase III. Rectal storage does not clear small-bowel residue during fasting. Colonic water absorption does not produce the absent phase III complexes. The gastrocolic reflex follows meals and is not the fasting sweep that failed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small intestinal bacterial overgrowth causes bloating, diarrhea, and a positive hydrogen breath test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: the interdigestive complex moves through the stomach and small intestine in a fasting person (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — pathogenesis of malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Postprandial mixing is a fed pattern and is not","B":"Rectal storage does not clear small-bowel residue during","C":"Colonic water absorption does not produce the absent phase III","E":"The gastrocolic reflex follows meals and is not the"}},{"id":"sdl25_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old healthy woman in a fasting motility study has bands of intense contractions that start in the stomach and travel down the small intestine. She is not taking prokinetic drugs and does not have diabetes. Blood glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL) before breakfast, and the complexes stop when she eats. Which change in the recording is most likely after the meal?","choices":{"A":"Shortening of the complex interval","B":"Spread of complexes to the colon","C":"Interruption of the complex cycle","D":"Increase in phase III force","E":"Doubling of slow wave frequency"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Food inhibits motilin secretion and interrupts the migrating complex cycle, so the fasting bands stop. A normal fasting glucose and cessation when she eats show that interruption rather than a change in slow-wave rate. The interval is not merely shortened; the cycle is interrupted by the meal. These complexes do not spread into the colon after breakfast. Phase III force does not increase as the explanation for the complexes stopping. Slow-wave frequency does not double when the meal arrives.","boardPrep":"Step 1: peristalsis rises after a meal through duodenal stretch and the gastroenteric reflex (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: motilin is a candidate hormone secreted from the upper duodenum during fasting (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — hormonal control of gastric and duodenal motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The interval is not merely shortened; the cycle is","B":"These complexes do not spread into the colon after","D":"Phase III force does not increase as the explanation","E":"Slow-wave frequency does not double when the meal"}},{"id":"sdl25_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman needs to pass stool within 20 minutes after almost every breakfast. She has no weight loss, no blood in the stool, and no nocturnal diarrhea. Examination is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which reflex best explains the timing of the urge?","choices":{"A":"Gastroileal reflex","B":"Rectosphincteric reflex","C":"Enterogastric reflex","D":"Gastrocolic reflex","E":"Peritoneointestinal reflex"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The urge soon after breakfast is the gastrocolic reflex, in which a meal prompts colonic mass movement. Absence of bleeding, weight loss, and night symptoms keeps this a physiologic response. The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum and does not produce the call to stool. The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter when the rectum fills. The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach when the intestine is full. The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits the gut when the peritoneum is irritated.","boardPrep":"Step 1: an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex contributes to postprandial urgency in irritable bowel syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: the afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex is parasympathetic (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — enterogastric reflexes and duodenal stimuli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The gastroileal reflex empties the ileum and does not","B":"The rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter when the rectum","C":"The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach when the intestine is","E":"The peritoneointestinal reflex inhibits the gut when the peritoneum is"}},{"id":"sdl25_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":5,"stem":"A 31-year-old man is passing 8 to 10 bloody stools a day during a flare of the same illness he has had for years. Temperature is 37.9°C and the pulse is 102/min. Hemoglobin is 11.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), C-reactive protein is 48 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L), p-ANCA is positive, and ASCA is negative. Which colonic motility pattern is most likely increased?","choices":{"A":"Haustral mixing","B":"Mass movements","C":"Migrating complexes","D":"Jejunal segmentation","E":"Reverse peristalsis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"During this colitis flare, colonic mass movements are persistently increased and drive the frequent bloody stools. Fever, a high C-reactive protein, and p-ANCA with a negative ASCA mark the inflammatory drive. Haustral mixing is not the pattern that produces many urgent bloody stools. Migrating complexes are a fasting small-bowel pattern, not this colonic flare. Jejunal segmentation mixes small-bowel contents and does not cause this colitis pattern. Reverse peristalsis would move content orad and does not explain frequent defecation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally in continuous fashion (First Aid Gastrointestinal, inflammatory bowel disease). COMLEX: a series of mass movements lasts 10 to 30 minutes and may recur half a day later (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 5 — lymphatic drainage and ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Haustral mixing is not the pattern that produces many","C":"Migrating complexes are a fasting small-bowel pattern, not this colonic","D":"Jejunal segmentation mixes small-bowel contents and does not cause","E":"Reverse peristalsis would move content orad and does not"}},{"id":"sdl25_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man with mild constipation has colonic contractions that shift contents back and forth over a few minutes. Thyroid studies are normal and there is no mass. Serum calcium is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which function do these contractions mainly serve?","choices":{"A":"Mixing contents for water absorption","B":"Propelling feces into the rectum","C":"Clearing residue during fasting","D":"Relaxing the internal anal sphincter","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"These nonpropulsive contractions mix contents so water can be absorbed. Normal calcium and thyroid studies, and the lack of a mass, leave this as ordinary haustral mixing. Propelling feces into the rectum is the job of mass movements, not these short to-and-fro contractions. Clearing residue during fasting is the migrating complex, which is not this fed mixing pattern. Relaxing the internal sphincter is a rectoanal reflex, not a colonic mixing contraction. Preventing backflow into the ileum is the ileocecal sphincter, not these colonic rings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: teniae coli are three longitudinal muscle bands that create haustra (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: water absorption in the distal colon makes feces semisolid and harder to move (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 24 Obj 1 — teniae coli and haustra coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Propelling feces into the rectum is the job of","C":"Clearing residue during fasting is the migrating complex, which","D":"Relaxing the internal sphincter is a rectoanal reflex, not","E":"Preventing backflow into the ileum is the ileocecal sphincter,"}},{"id":"sdl25_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":5,"stem":"A 4-day-old boy has not passed meconium and has progressive abdominal distension. He was born at term. The abdomen is distended, and digital rectal examination releases an explosive gush of stool. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Absence of which cells in the distal colon most likely explains this?","choices":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal","B":"Striated sphincter muscle fibers","C":"Enterochromaffin cells of the mucosa","D":"Sacral preganglionic parasympathetic neurons","E":"Ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Failure to pass meconium with an explosive release of stool means ganglion cells of the myenteric plexus are absent from the distal colon. A normal serum sodium excludes a metabolic cause of the ileus. Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow waves and are not the cells missing in this distal functional obstruction. Striated sphincter fibers are skeletal muscle and are not absent in this newborn pattern. Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin and are not the missing neurons. Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not in the distal bowel wall that lacks ganglia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hirschsprung disease results from failed neural crest migration and is associated with Down syndrome and RET mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease). COMLEX: rectal suction biopsy is diagnostic, showing absent ganglion cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Hirschsprung disease).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 1 — myenteric and submucosal plexuses","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"Interstitial cells of Cajal set slow waves and are","B":"Striated sphincter fibers are skeletal muscle and are not","C":"Enterochromaffin cells release serotonin and are not the missing","D":"Sacral preganglionic neurons lie in the cord, not in"}},{"id":"sdl25_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman leaks stool when she coughs and cannot voluntarily squeeze the anus. She had a difficult vaginal delivery and has no back pain. Limb strength is normal. Anal manometry shows reduced squeeze pressure with preserved resting tone. Dysfunction of which structure most likely explains the leakage?","choices":{"A":"External anal sphincter","B":"Internal anal sphincter","C":"Rectal circular muscle","D":"Sigmoid colon teniae","E":"Ileocecal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of voluntary squeeze with preserved resting tone localizes to the external anal sphincter. A difficult delivery is the usual reason that striated sphincter fails while the internal sphincter still holds resting pressure. The internal sphincter provides resting tone, which is preserved here. Rectal circular muscle does not provide the voluntary squeeze she has lost. Sigmoid teniae organize haustra and do not mediate voluntary continence. The ileocecal sphincter is far from the anus and does not control cough leakage.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the pudendal nerve can be blocked at the ischial spine for vaginal delivery (First Aid Reproductive, pudendal nerve). COMLEX: the external sphincter is held contracted subconsciously unless conscious signals inhibit it (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — vagus and pelvic nerve distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The internal sphincter provides resting tone, which is preserved","C":"Rectal circular muscle does not provide the voluntary squeeze","D":"Sigmoid teniae organize haustra and do not mediate voluntary","E":"The ileocecal sphincter is far from the anus and"}},{"id":"sdl25_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has weak rectal emptying after pelvic surgery that divided the nerves beside the rectum. He is not taking opioids and has no spinal cord injury. Soft stool sits in the vault, and voluntary squeeze is intact. Serum potassium is 4.3 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Loss of which pathway most likely explains the weak propulsion?","choices":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves","B":"Vagal fibers supplying the proximal colon","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers in the rectum","D":"Parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall muscles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Rectal propulsion uses parasympathetic fibers in the pelvic nerves, which pelvic surgery can divide. Intact voluntary squeeze and soft stool in the vault show that the somatic sphincter and the stool consistency are not the problem. Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves are inhibitory and are not the propulsive pathway that was cut. Vagal fibers reach the proximal colon and do not drive the rectum. Intrinsic myenteric fibers remain in the wall and are not the pathway divided outside the rectum. Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall raise pressure but are not the rectal propulsive nerves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: defecation signals entering the cord also trigger a deep breath, glottic closure, and abdominal muscle contraction (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: sacral parasympathetic fibers via the pelvic nerves supply the distal colon, rectum, and anus (Guyton Ch. 63).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sympathetic fibers in the hypogastric nerves are inhibitory and","B":"Vagal fibers reach the proximal colon and do not","C":"Intrinsic myenteric fibers remain in the wall and are","E":"Somatic fibers to the abdominal wall raise pressure but"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the relationship between slow waves and intestinal smooth muscle contraction and the effects of parasympathetic and sympathetic input on intestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man starts a calcium-channel blocker for hypertension and develops constipation. He is not taking opioids, and imaging shows no obstruction. The abdomen is soft, the pulse is 60/min, and blood pressure is 128/76 mm Hg. The same drug slows atrioventricular conduction and relaxes vascular smooth muscle. Which current underlying intestinal slow-wave depolarization does this drug most directly reduce?","choices":{"A":"Inward sodium current","B":"Inward calcium current","C":"Outward potassium current","D":"Outward chloride current","E":"Inward magnesium current"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The upstroke of intestinal slow waves depends on inward calcium current, which this blocker reduces. Constipation together with slowed atrioventricular conduction and vasodilation is the same calcium-channel effect. The slow-wave upstroke in gut muscle is not an inward sodium current. Outward potassium current repolarizes the cell and is not the current this drug blocks. Outward chloride current is not the depolarizing current reduced here. Inward magnesium current does not generate the slow-wave upstroke.","boardPrep":"Step 1: constipation is a common adverse effect of nondihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as verapamil (First Aid Cardiovascular, calcium channel blockers). COMLEX: even subthreshold slow waves produce weak basal contractions (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The slow-wave upstroke in gut muscle is not an","C":"Outward potassium current repolarizes the cell and is not","D":"Outward chloride current is not the depolarizing current reduced","E":"Inward magnesium current does not generate the slow-wave"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman has constipation with infrequent pellet-like stools. She is not taking anticholinergic drugs, and thyroid studies are normal. The abdomen is soft, with stool in the left colon. Manometry shows mostly nonpropagating ring contractions, few propagated waves, and no mass movements. Serum calcium is 9.3 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.5 mg/dL). Which property of the dominant contraction pattern best explains the constipation?","choices":{"A":"Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents","B":"Raising the frequency of mass movements","C":"Narrowing the segment behind the bolus","D":"Speeding transit toward the rectum","E":"Mixing without net forward movement"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The dominant rings mix contents without net forward movement, so stool is not propelled. Few propagated waves and absent mass movements leave mixing as the pattern that explains the pellets. Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents would speed transit, which is not happening. Raising the frequency of mass movements would relieve constipation, and those movements are absent. Narrowing the segment behind a bolus is the propulsive step she lacks. Speeding transit toward the rectum is the opposite of this recording.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Segmentation mixes chyme without forward movement, whereas peristalsis contracts behind and relaxes ahead of the bolus to propel it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Opioid-induced constipation is expected and should be anticipated with a bowel regimen.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Relaxing circular muscle ahead of contents would speed transit,","B":"Raising the frequency of mass movements would relieve constipation,","C":"Narrowing the segment behind a bolus is the propulsive","D":"Speeding transit toward the rectum is the opposite of this"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy man in a fasting study has intense contractions that sweep from the stomach through the small intestine about every 90 minutes while he is fasting. The complexes disappear for several hours after each meal. Blood glucose before the meal is 90 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the missing pattern after he eats?","choices":{"A":"Gastrin release halts the slow waves","B":"Food keeps motilin release suppressed","C":"Vagal input silences Cajal pacemakers","D":"Mucosal serotonin stores are depleted","E":"Duodenal cells oversecrete motilin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Food suppresses motilin release, so the fasting complexes that depend on motilin stop after a meal. A healthy subject whose 90-minute sweeps vanish only after eating shows that suppression. Gastrin does not halt the slow waves when a meal arrives. Vagal input does not silence the pacemaker cells as the reason the complexes stop. Mucosal serotonin stores are not depleted by an ordinary meal. Duodenal cells do not oversecrete motilin after eating; release falls.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Motilin released cyclically during fasting drives migrating motor complexes every 90 minutes, and eating inhibits its secretion (Guyton Ch. 63). COMLEX: Loss of the fasting clearance pattern predisposes to stasis and bacterial overgrowth.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastrin does not halt the slow waves when a meal","C":"Vagal input does not silence the pacemaker cells as","D":"Mucosal serotonin stores are not depleted by an ordinary","E":"Duodenal cells do not oversecrete motilin after eating; release"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":5,"stem":"A 54-year-old man with an end ileostomy after colectomy notices that stoma output rises sharply within minutes of each meal. The stoma is pink and there is no obstruction. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which reflex best explains the increase?","choices":{"A":"Gastrocolic reflex","B":"Enterogastric reflex","C":"Rectosphincteric reflex","D":"Renointestinal reflex","E":"Gastroileal reflex"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A meal increases ileal emptying through the gastroileal reflex, which raises ileostomy output within minutes. A pink stoma and no obstruction keep this a reflex rather than a mechanical problem. The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been removed. The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach and would not increase stoma output. The rectosphincteric reflex concerns the anus, which is not in continuity here. The renointestinal reflex is a response to renal irritation, not to a meal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ileocecal sphincter normally holds chyme in the ileum, prolonging absorption (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: about 1500 to 2000 mL of chyme empties into the cecum each day (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"A":"The gastrocolic reflex acts on the colon, which has been","B":"The enterogastric reflex slows the stomach and would not","C":"The rectosphincteric reflex concerns the anus, which is not","D":"The renointestinal reflex is a response to renal irritation,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman strains and cannot empty the rectum. She has no back injury and is not taking opioids. When the rectum is distended, the internal sphincter relaxes, but anal-canal pressure then rises and electromyography shows increased activity in the striated sphincter. Serum potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which component of defecation is abnormal?","choices":{"A":"Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter","B":"Sensory detection of rectal filling","C":"Voluntary external sphincter relaxation","D":"Generation of intraabdominal pressure","E":"Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The internal sphincter relaxes as it should, but the external sphincter contracts instead of relaxing, so voluntary external-sphincter relaxation has failed. Rising anal pressure and increased striated-muscle activity during attempted defecation show that paradox. Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter is intact on this recording. She senses the distention, so detection of rectal filling is present. Generation of intra-abdominal pressure is not the component that shows increased sphincter EMG. Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall are working, because the internal sphincter still relaxes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In defecation the rectosphincteric reflex relaxes the internal sphincter, and the external sphincter must be relaxed voluntarily while rectal and abdominal pressure rise (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Paradoxical contraction of the striated sphincter while straining is a treatable cause of chronic constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Reflex relaxation of the internal sphincter is intact on this","B":"She senses the distention, so detection of rectal filling is","D":"Generation of intra-abdominal pressure is not the component that","E":"Enteric ganglia in the rectal wall are working, because"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":5,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has marked cecal distention from an obstructing sigmoid mass. He has lost 5 kg and has had chronic constipation. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, and the cecum is tender. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and carcinoembryonic antigen is 22 ng/mL (reference range: <3.0 ng/mL). Which reflex response is expected where the small intestine joins the large intestine?","choices":{"A":"Contraction of the ileocecal sphincter","B":"Intensified peristalsis in the ileum","C":"Faster emptying of ileal chyme","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation","E":"Relaxation of the internal sphincter"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cecal distention from a distal obstruction reflexly contracts the ileocecal sphincter, limiting further inflow. A sigmoid tumor with an elevated carcinoembryonic antigen is the blockage that distends the cecum. Intensified ileal peristalsis would push more content into an already distended cecum and is not the protective reflex. Faster ileal emptying is the opposite of the response to cecal distention. Increased jejunal segmentation does not guard the distended cecum. Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter is a rectal reflex and does not answer cecal distention.","boardPrep":"Step 1: large bowel obstruction is most often caused by colorectal cancer in older adults (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intestinal obstruction). COMLEX: an inflamed appendix can cause ileocecal sphincter spasm and partial ileal paralysis (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 25 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Guyton","whyNot":{"B":"Intensified ileal peristalsis would push more content into an","C":"Faster ileal emptying is the opposite of the response","D":"Increased jejunal segmentation does not guard the distended","E":"Relaxation of the internal anal sphincter is a rectal"}},{"id":"grid_sdl25_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has episodes of reflex bowel emptying at unpredictable times. A motorcycle crash one year ago completely transected the spinal cord at T6, and spinal shock has resolved. He is not taking anticholinergic drugs. Examination shows a T6 sensory level, no perianal sensation, and preserved knee jerks. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which of the following sets of anorectal findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Reflex IAS relaxation","Voluntary EAS squeeze","Rectal fullness sensation"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↔","↔"],"B":["↔","↓","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↓"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is correct. The intrinsic myenteric defecation reflex and the sacral parasympathetic reflex through the pelvic nerves lie below the T6 lesion, so rectal distention still relaxes the internal anal sphincter, and Guyton notes that people with transected cords have automatic emptying of the lower bowel. Conscious control of the external anal sphincter through the pudendal nerve needs descending cortical input, and the sensation of rectal fullness needs ascending tracts, so both are lost. Choice A (↓ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is Hirschsprung disease, in which aganglionic bowel fails to relax the internal sphincter. Choice B (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↓ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is pudendal nerve injury, as after a difficult vaginal delivery. Choice D (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↔ rectal fullness sensation) is normal anorectal function, as in functional constipation. Choice E (↔ reflex IAS relaxation, ↔ voluntary EAS squeeze, ↓ rectal fullness sensation) is a rectal sensory neuropathy, as in long-standing diabetes. Sources: Guyton Ch. 64 (Defecation; Defecation reflexes; transected spinal cords); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Defecation, rectosphincteric reflex); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Hirschsprung disease). The ↔ cells and the diabetic sensory row are inferred because the sources do not describe them as changed. Aligned with SDL 25 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the sacral parasympathetic defecation reflex runs from rectal afferents to cord segments S2 to S4 and back through the pelvic nerves (Guyton Ch. 64). COMLEX: spinal cord lesions above the sacral segments leave reflex bowel emptying intact but remove voluntary sphincter control (Guyton Ch. 64).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 3 — pelvic nerve parasympathetic distribution","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl25_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has crampy pain and bloating after meals. He is not taking opioids, and thyroid studies are normal. The abdomen is soft, with rushes of bowel sounds but no mass. Hemoglobin is 14.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Jejunal manometry shows the lumen failing to narrow behind a bolus, and a drug that blocks substance P receptors reproduces the finding. Which transmitter pair normally constricts circular muscle behind the bolus?","choices":{"A":"Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide","B":"Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide","C":"Acetylcholine and substance P","D":"Norepinephrine and somatostatin","E":"Dopamine and motilin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Behind a bolus, circular muscle contracts under acetylcholine and substance P from the myenteric plexus. Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide relax circular muscle ahead of the bolus. Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide are duodenal hormones that slow gastric emptying, not the orad circular-muscle pair. Norepinephrine is the sympathetic brake on motility, and somatostatin is not the orad excitatory pair. Dopamine inhibits gut motility, and motilin starts the fasting complex rather than the orad squeeze.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nitric oxide and vasoactive intestinal peptide relax circular muscle ahead of the bolus","B":"Secretin and gastric inhibitory peptide are duodenal hormones that slow gastric emptying, not the orad circular-muscle pair","D":"Norepinephrine is the sympathetic brake on motility, and somatostatin is not the orad excitatory pair","E":"Dopamine inhibits gut motility, and motilin starts the fasting complex rather than the orad squeeze"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate segmentation from peristalsis based on their mechanisms and roles in mixing versus propulsion of intestinal contents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman has bloating without weight loss. She is not taking anticholinergic drugs. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Thyroid-stimulating hormone is 2.1 mIU/L (reference range: 0.4–4.0 mIU/L) and hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Manometry shows mixing contractions at 12 per minute in the duodenum and 8 per minute in the ileum. What sets that ceiling?","choices":{"A":"The local slow-wave frequency","B":"The number of meals eaten that day","C":"Voluntary contraction of the external anal sphincter","D":"Portal venous pressure","E":"The titer of anti-tissue transglutaminase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The maximum rate of segmentation is limited by the local slow-wave frequency, about 12 per minute in the duodenum and 8 to 9 per minute in the ileum. Meal timing replaces the fasting complex with fed motility, but it does not set the 12-per-minute duodenal ceiling. The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle of the anal canal and does not pace duodenal segmentation. Portal pressure does not set the frequency of intestinal slow waves. Anti-tissue transglutaminase is a celiac marker and does not determine slow-wave rate.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meal timing replaces the fasting complex with fed motility, but it does not set the 12-per-minute duodenal ceiling","C":"The external anal sphincter is skeletal muscle of the anal canal and does not pace duodenal segmentation","D":"Portal pressure does not set the frequency of intestinal slow waves","E":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase is a celiac marker and does not determine slow-wave rate"}},{"id":"sdl25_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the migrating motor complex and explain its role in small-intestinal motility during fasting.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old healthy woman is in a fasting motility study. She has not eaten since midnight and takes no prokinetic drugs. The abdomen is soft. Glucose is 86 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL) and potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Intense contraction bands sweep down the small bowel about every 90 minutes. Which cells release the hormone that starts those bands?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells of the stomach","B":"Hepatocytes","C":"Pancreatic islet beta cells","D":"M cells of the upper duodenum","E":"Chief cells of the gastric fundus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Motilin, which initiates the interdigestive migrating complex, is released from M cells of the upper duodenum during fasting. Parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor, not the hormone that starts the fasting complex. Hepatocytes make bile and plasma proteins, not motilin. Beta cells secrete insulin in response to glucose, and a meal abolishes the fasting complex. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, not the peptide that starts migrating complexes.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor, not the hormone that starts the fasting complex","B":"Hepatocytes make bile and plasma proteins, not motilin","C":"Beta cells secrete insulin in response to glucose, and a meal abolishes the fasting complex","E":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, not the peptide that starts migrating complexes"}},{"id":"sdl25_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman needs to pass stool within 20 minutes of breakfast on most days. She has no bleeding, no weight loss, and no nocturnal stools. The abdomen is soft and the rectal exam is normal. Hemoglobin is 13.5 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 2 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Stretch receptors that start this meal-linked colonic response sit in which organ, and which nerve carries their afferents?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, carried by the pudendal nerve","B":"Cecum, carried by the phrenic nerve","C":"Duodenum, carried by the pelvic splanchnic nerves","D":"Sigmoid colon, carried only by sympathetic hypogastric nerves","E":"Stomach, carried by the vagus nerve"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The afferent limb of the gastrocolic reflex begins in gastric mechanoreceptors and travels in the vagus. Rectal stretch and the pudendal nerve belong to defecation and continence, not the meal-triggered colonic reflex. The cecum is not the organ whose filling starts this reflex, and the phrenic nerve is the diaphragm's nerve. The duodenum is not the source of the afferents, and pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic efferents. The sigmoid is a destination of mass movements, not the site of the stretch receptors that start the reflex.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rectal stretch and the pudendal nerve belong to defecation and continence, not the meal-triggered colonic reflex","B":"The cecum is not the organ whose filling starts this reflex, and the phrenic nerve is the diaphragm's nerve","C":"The duodenum is not the source of the afferents, and pelvic splanchnics are sacral parasympathetic efferents","D":"The sigmoid is a destination of mass movements, not the site of the stretch receptors that start the reflex"}},{"id":"trial_sdl25_q08","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain colonic motility, including haustral contractions, mass movements, and the gastrocolic reflex.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has an urge to stool about 40 minutes after breakfast. He has no bleeding and has not lost weight. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). During the urge, a long segment of colon distal to a constricting ring flattens its sacculations and shortens as a single unit. What has happened to that segment?","choices":{"A":"It is only mixing contents back and forth over a few centimeters","B":"It has lost its haustra and is contracting as one segment","C":"Its external sphincter has been cut by the pudendal nerve","D":"Its slow waves have stopped, so the muscle cannot contract","E":"It is secreting bicarbonate into a closed diverticulum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In a mass movement the colon distal to the constricting ring loses its haustrations and contracts as one segment. Back-and-forth mixing over a short distance is haustration, not this long unified contraction. The pudendal nerve supplies the external anal sphincter and does not divide it during a colonic propulsive wave. The segment is contracting, so its muscle has not lost the ability to fire. Bicarbonate secretion into a diverticulum is not the motor event that clears haustra.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Back-and-forth mixing over a short distance is haustration, not this long unified contraction","C":"The pudendal nerve supplies the external anal sphincter and does not divide it during a colonic propulsive wave","D":"The segment is contracting, so its muscle has not lost the ability to fire","E":"Bicarbonate secretion into a diverticulum is not the motor event that clears haustra"}},{"id":"sdl25_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Describe the defecation reflex and differentiate the neural control and functions of the internal and external anal sphincters.","batch":2,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has weak rectal emptying after pelvic surgery, but a strong urge still appears when the rectum fills. He is not taking opioids. The abdomen is soft, anal wink is reduced, and there is no spinal tenderness. Sodium is 140 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and glucose is 92 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). The parasympathetic reflex that intensifies rectal contraction returns from which spinal levels?","choices":{"A":"S2 to S4","B":"C3 to C5","C":"T1 to T4","D":"L1 only","E":"The midbrain vagal nuclei"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The parasympathetic defecation reflex arcs through sacral cord segments S2 to S4 and returns in the pelvic nerves. C3 to C5 is the phrenic motor pool for the diaphragm, not the sacral defecation reflex. T1 to T4 is sympathetic outflow, not the sacral parasympathetic arc. L1 is not the sacral segment that returns the defecation reflex. Vagal nuclei are cranial and do not provide the sacral boost to rectal contraction.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"C3 to C5 is the phrenic motor pool for the diaphragm, not the sacral defecation reflex","C":"T1 to T4 is sympathetic outflow, not the sacral parasympathetic arc","D":"L1 is not the sacral segment that returns the defecation reflex","E":"Vagal nuclei are cranial and do not provide the sacral boost to rectal contraction"}}]},{"sdlNumber":26,"title":"SDL 26 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 1 (Carbs, Proteins)","questions":[{"id":"sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic α-amylase digests starch into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and free fructose units","B":"Maltose, maltotriose, α-limit dextrins","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose units","D":"Glycogen, amylopectin, and cellulose","E":"Galactose, maltose, and sucrose units"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds in starch and yields three oligosaccharides: α-limit dextrins, maltose, and maltotriose. Brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase then produce glucose. Monosaccharides are the brush-border end products, not amylase products. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are dietary disaccharides. Glycogen and amylopectin are substrates, and cellulose is not digested. Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharides made by brush-border enzymes. Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharides that skip the amylase step, not products of starch digestion. In Costanzo, galactose comes from lactase digestion of lactose and sucrose is a dietary disaccharide split by sucrase, so neither is a product of amylase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: salivary amylase starts starch digestion but is inactivated by low gastric pH (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: human amylase does not hydrolyze the β-1,4 bonds of cellulose (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose, galactose, and fructose are the final monosaccharides made by brush-border enzymes.","C":"Sucrose, lactose, and trehalose are the dietary disaccharides that skip the amylase step, not products of starch digestion.","E":"In Costanzo, galactose comes from lactase digestion of lactose and sucrose is a dietary disaccharide split by sucrase, so neither is a product of amylase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Brush-border lactase digests lactose into which monosaccharides?","choices":{"A":"Glucose and fructose","B":"Two glucose molecules","C":"Glucose and galactose","D":"Galactose and fructose","E":"Glucose and maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lactase splits each lactose into one glucose and one galactose. Glucose plus fructose is the sucrase product, and two glucose molecules come from trehalose or maltose. Galactose with fructose is not a dietary disaccharide product, and maltose is a disaccharide. Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on sucrose, not of lactase. Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalose, not from lactase acting on lactose. Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from sucrose, so no single brush-border disaccharidase yields galactose plus fructose. Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glucose, so it is not a product of lactose digestion. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: sucrase splits sucrose into glucose and fructose, and trehalase splits trehalose into two glucose molecules (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: about 80 percent of carbohydrate end product is glucose, with galactose and fructose making up the rest (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose and fructose are the products of sucrase acting on sucrose, not of lactase.","B":"Two glucose molecules come from trehalase acting on trehalose, not from lactase acting on lactose.","D":"Galactose comes only from lactose and fructose only from sucrose, so no single brush-border disaccharidase yields galactose plus fructose.","E":"Maltose is itself a disaccharide that maltase digests to glucose, so it is not a product of lactose digestion."}},{"id":"sdl26_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":1,"stem":"Undigested lactose in lactase deficiency causes diarrhea by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Active chloride secretion by crypts","B":"Faster colonic motility from gastrin","C":"Mucosal inflammation and blood loss","D":"Bile acid irritation of colonocytes","E":"Osmotic water retention in the lumen"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Nonabsorbable lactose stays in the lumen and holds an equivalent amount of water to keep contents isosmotic, which causes osmotic diarrhea. Crypt chloride secretion drives secretory diarrhea. Gastrin and motility changes are not the mechanism. Inflammation with blood loss suggests colitis. Bile acid diarrhea follows ileal disease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption, Box 8.2). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic bacteria ferment undigested lactose and produce gas, causing bloating and flatulence (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: lactose intolerance is managed by avoiding milk products or adding lactase (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical transporter moves glucose and galactose into intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"GLUT5","B":"GLUT2","C":"GLUT4","D":"SGLT1","E":"NHE3"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SGLT1, the Na⁺-glucose cotransporter, carries glucose and galactose across the apical membrane against an electrochemical gradient. GLUT5 is the apical fructose transporter. GLUT2 moves monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane. GLUT4 is the insulin-responsive carrier of muscle and fat. NHE3 is an apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion transporter, not the carrier for glucose and galactose. GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosaccharides exit into blood by facilitated diffusion; it is not the apical uptake step. Costanzo describes GLUT4 as the insulin-directed glucose transporter inserted into muscle and adipose cell membranes, not the apical intestinal carrier. First Aid states glucose and galactose are taken up into enterocytes by Na+-dependent SGLT1, not NHE3. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.28). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 in intestine and SGLT2 in the proximal tubule both use the sodium gradient for glucose uptake (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: glucose and galactose absorption resembles transport in the early proximal convoluted tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT5 is the apical fructose-specific facilitated-diffusion transporter, not the carrier for glucose and galactose.","B":"GLUT2 sits on the basolateral membrane and lets monosaccharides exit into blood by facilitated diffusion; it is not the apical uptake step.","C":"Costanzo describes GLUT4 as the insulin-directed glucose transporter inserted into muscle and adipose cell membranes, not the apical intestinal carrier.","E":"First Aid states glucose and galactose are taken up into enterocytes by Na+-dependent SGLT1, not NHE3."}},{"id":"sdl26_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"What supplies the energy for glucose uptake by SGLT1?","choices":{"A":"Direct ATP hydrolysis by the SGLT1 protein","B":"Sodium gradient set by the Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","C":"Proton gradient set by apical exchange","D":"Glucose gradient into the enterocyte","E":"Potassium gradient through apical channels"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that the energy for SGLT1 does not come directly from ATP but from the Na⁺ gradient across the apical membrane, which the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase creates and maintains. This is secondary active transport. Proton gradients drive peptide uptake. Glucose moves against its gradient on SGLT1. Potassium gradients do not drive it. SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself. An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptide and tripeptide uptake, not glucose entry on SGLT1. SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemical gradient, so a glucose gradient cannot supply the energy. Costanzo states the energy for SGLT1 uptake comes from the apical Na+ gradient kept by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, not a potassium gradient. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary active transport couples uphill solute movement to downhill Na⁺ movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Na⁺-glucose cotransport in the small intestine resembles transport in the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 does not hydrolyze ATP itself.","C":"An H+ gradient set by apical Na+-H+ exchange drives dipeptide and tripeptide uptake, not glucose entry on SGLT1.","D":"SGLT1 moves glucose into the cell against its electrochemical gradient, so a glucose gradient cannot supply the energy.","E":"Costanzo states the energy for SGLT1 uptake comes from the apical Na+ gradient kept by the basolateral Na+-K+ ATPase, not a potassium gradient."}},{"id":"sdl26_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":1,"stem":"How does fructose cross the apical membrane of intestinal epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport via SGLT1","B":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT4","C":"Proton cotransport via PepT1","D":"Primary active ATP pumping","E":"Facilitated diffusion via GLUT5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane by facilitated diffusion on the fructose-specific transporter GLUT5 and leaves across the basolateral membrane on GLUT2. No cotransporter or energy-requiring step is involved, so fructose moves down its gradient. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose. GLUT4 is insulin regulated in muscle and fat. PepT1 carries small peptides. SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptake involves no cotransporter or energy-requiring step. Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it has no energy-requiring step and cannot move against its gradient. H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the enterocyte, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion on GLUT5. GLUT4 is the transporter insulin inserts into muscle and adipose membranes in Costanzo, not the apical route for intestinal fructose. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of carbohydrates). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion does not proceed against an electrochemical gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: glucose and galactose exit the basolateral membrane on GLUT2, as fructose does (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"SGLT1 carries glucose and galactose with Na⁺; fructose uptake involves no cotransporter or energy-requiring step.","D":"Fructose absorption uses only facilitated diffusion, so it has no energy-requiring step and cannot move against its gradient.","C":"H+-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the enterocyte, whereas fructose enters by facilitated diffusion on GLUT5.","B":"GLUT4 is the transporter insulin inserts into muscle and adipose membranes in Costanzo, not the apical route for intestinal fructose."}},{"id":"sdl26_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protease begins protein digestion in the stomach?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Elastase","C":"Renin","D":"Pepsin","E":"Thrombin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Protein digestion begins with pepsin, formed when pepsinogen from chief cells meets low gastric pH. Trypsin and elastase are pancreatic proteases that act in the small intestine. Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease. Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small intestine after enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not in the stomach. Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is activated by trypsin in the small intestine, so it does not begin protein digestion in the stomach. First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not renin. First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not thrombin. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chief cells secrete pepsinogen, and parietal cells secrete H⁺ that activates it (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin is an endopeptidase that digests collagen in meat (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin is a pancreatic protease that works in the small intestine after enterokinase activates trypsinogen, not in the stomach.","B":"Elastase is secreted by the pancreas as proelastase and is activated by trypsin in the small intestine, so it does not begin protein digestion in the stomach.","C":"Renin is a renal enzyme, and thrombin is a coagulation protease.","E":"First Aid lists pepsin from gastric chief cells as the stomach protease for protein digestion, not thrombin."}},{"id":"sdl26_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"Above what pH is pepsin denatured and inactivated?","choices":{"A":"Above pH 5","B":"Above pH 2","C":"Above pH 3","D":"Above pH 9","E":"Above pH 11"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The three pepsin isozymes have pH optima between 1 and 3, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so it stops acting once chyme is neutralized by duodenal bicarbonate. pH 2 and 3 fall in the optimum range. pH 9 and 11 overshoot the textbook threshold. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is active at pH 2, not denatured. Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is still active at pH 3, not denatured. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past the point of inactivation. Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past the point of inactivation. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 26 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic HCO₃⁻ neutralizes duodenal chyme and inactivates pepsin (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: pepsin handles about 10 to 20 percent of total protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 7 → Obj 3 — gastric HCl secretion by parietal cells","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is active at pH 2, not denatured.","C":"Pepsin isozymes have optimum activity between pH 1 and 3, so pepsin is still active at pH 3, not denatured.","D":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 9 is far past the point of inactivation.","E":"Pepsin is already denatured above pH 5, so pH 11 is far past the point of inactivation."}},{"id":"sdl26_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Which pancreatic proteases are exopeptidases?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin","B":"Pepsin and elastase","C":"Carboxypeptidase A and B","D":"Elastase and trypsin","E":"Enterokinase and pepsin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Exopeptidases hydrolyze one amino acid at a time from the C-terminal end; carboxypeptidases A and B are the pancreatic exopeptidases. Trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and pepsin are endopeptidases that cleave interior bonds. Enterokinase is a brush-border activating enzyme. Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Pepsin and elastase are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases. Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze interior peptide bonds. Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, so neither is a pancreatic exopeptidase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brush-border aminopeptidases and dipeptidases finish oligopeptide digestion to absorbable units (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: elastase digests the elastin fibers that hold meat together (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Trypsin and chymotrypsin are endopeptidases that cut interior peptide bonds, not exopeptidases.","B":"Pepsin is a gastric endopeptidase and enterokinase is the brush-border enzyme that activates trypsinogen, so neither is a pancreatic exopeptidase.","D":"Elastase and trypsin are both endopeptidases that hydrolyze interior peptide bonds; only carboxypeptidases A and B remove.","E":"Enterokinase is a brush-border activator of trypsinogen, not a pancreatic exopeptidase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme first converts trypsinogen to trypsin?","choices":{"A":"Aminopeptidase","B":"Chymotrypsin","C":"Elastase","D":"Cathepsin B","E":"Enterokinase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin; trypsin then activates the remaining zymogens and more trypsinogen. Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides. Chymotrypsin and elastase are themselves products of trypsin activation. Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins, Fig. 8.29). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: enterokinase is also called enteropeptidase (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions). COMLEX: trypsin activates trypsinogen in an autocatalytic step after the first activation (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Chymotrypsin is made from chymotrypsinogen by trypsin, so it acts downstream of trypsin rather than starting the cascade.","A":"Aminopeptidase is a brush-border exopeptidase that trims oligopeptides.","C":"Elastase is itself a product of trypsin activation.","D":"Cathepsin B is a lysosomal protease."}},{"id":"sdl26_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is enterokinase located?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic zymogen granules","B":"Gastric chief cells","C":"Intestinal brush border","D":"Pancreatic ductal cells","E":"Hepatic bile canaliculi"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Enterokinase is a brush-border enzyme of the small intestinal epithelium, so trypsinogen is first activated in the intestinal lumen, not in the pancreas. Zymogen granules hold inactive precursors. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen. Ductal cells secrete HCO₃⁻-rich fluid. Bile canaliculi carry bile. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pepsin, not enterokinase. Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive precursors inside acinar cells, and trypsinogen is activated only after secretion, by enterokinase on the intestinal brush border. Pancreatic ductal and centroacinar cells secrete the aqueous HCO₃⁻-rich component of pancreatic juice, not enterokinase. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Exocrine pancreas; Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: separating zymogen storage from the activating enzyme protects the pancreas from autodigestion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin from the first activation step converts further trypsinogen to trypsin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the precursor of pepsin, not enterokinase.","A":"Zymogen granules store the pancreatic peptidases as inactive precursors inside acinar cells, and trypsinogen is activated only after secretion, by enterokinase on the intestinal brush border.","D":"Pancreatic ductal and centroacinar cells secrete the aqueous HCO₃⁻-rich component of pancreatic juice, not enterokinase."}},{"id":"sdl26_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":1,"stem":"Which enzyme converts chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases to active forms?","choices":{"A":"Trypsin","B":"Enterokinase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Lipase","E":"Amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Once a small amount of trypsin is made by enterokinase, trypsin catalyzes the conversion of the other inactive precursors to chymotrypsin, elastase, and carboxypeptidases A and B. Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen. Pepsin works in the stomach. Lipase and amylase digest fat and starch and do not activate proteases. Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinogen; trypsin then converts the other inactive proteases. Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above pH 5, so its action ends in the duodenum and it does not activate the pancreatic zymogens. Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, not the peptide bonds that convert protease precursors to active enzymes. Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydrolyzes triglyceride; it does not activate the protease precursors. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of trypsin alone looks like loss of the pancreatic proteases because trypsin activates each precursor (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: trypsin also activates procolipase and prophospholipase A₂ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic secretions).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — zymogen secretion and enteropeptidase activation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase only starts the cascade by activating trypsinogen.","C":"Pepsin works only at low gastric pH and is denatured above pH 5, so its action ends in the duodenum and it does not activate the pancreatic zymogens.","E":"Pancreatic amylase hydrolyzes interior 1,4-glycosidic bonds of starch, not the peptide bonds that convert protease precursors to active enzymes.","D":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted as the active enzyme and hydrolyzes triglyceride; it does not activate the protease precursors."}},{"id":"sdl26_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"Dipeptides and tripeptides cross the apical membrane by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Sodium cotransport","B":"Facilitated diffusion","C":"Primary active transport","D":"Proton cotransport","E":"Receptor endocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Separate H⁺-dependent cotransporters carry dipeptides and tripeptides into the cell, using an H⁺ gradient created by the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger. Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids. Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids. Primary active transport and endocytosis are not the described routes. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins, Fig. 8.31). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide carrier is PepT1 (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: peptide carriers allow protein nutrition despite single amino acid transporter defects (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium cotransport carries free L-amino acids.","B":"Facilitated diffusion is the basolateral exit route for amino acids.","C":"Primary active transport is not the described apical route.","E":"Endocytosis is not the described apical route."}},{"id":"sdl26_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"In which form is most ingested protein absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Free single amino acids","B":"Dipeptides and tripeptides","C":"Intact dietary protein molecules","D":"Large oligopeptides","E":"Long polypeptide chains"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Costanzo states that most ingested protein is absorbed in the dipeptide and tripeptide forms rather than as free amino acids. Intact proteins and larger oligopeptides are not absorbable and must be broken down by brush-border peptidases. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: proteins are absorbable in larger units than carbohydrates, which must reach monosaccharides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: oligopeptides are hydrolyzed by brush-border proteases before absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Free amino acids are absorbed on their own carriers.","D":"Oligopeptides are not absorbable until brush-border peptidases shorten them.","C":"Intact dietary protein is not absorbable until proteases cut it.","E":"Long polypeptide chains are not absorbable until brush-border peptidases shorten them."}},{"id":"sdl26_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to most dipeptides and tripeptides after they enter the epithelial cell?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis by cytoplasmic peptidases","B":"Export back out through apical PepT1","C":"Re-esterification in the smooth ER","D":"Packaging into nascent chylomicrons","E":"Conversion to cellular glycogen"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Inside the cell, most dipeptides and tripeptides are hydrolyzed to amino acids by cytoplasmic peptidases, and the amino acids exit across the basolateral membrane by facilitated diffusion; a few peptides are absorbed unchanged. PepT1 imports peptides. Re-esterification and chylomicrons belong to lipid absorption. Glycogen storage is unrelated. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Absorption of proteins). Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal blood receives mostly free amino acids after protein absorption (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: basolateral amino acid exit uses separate carriers for neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has bloating, gas, and watery diarrhea within two hours of drinking milk. Symptoms began in adolescence and stop when he avoids dairy, without fever or weight loss. Temperature is 36.8°C and the abdomen is soft. Breath hydrogen rises by 30 ppm after lactose (a rise above 20 ppm is abnormal), and stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5). Which stool finding is most likely while he is symptomatic?","choices":{"A":"High stool sodium concentration","B":"Fecal leukocytes and blood","C":"Fat globules on Sudan stain","D":"Diarrhea persisting during fasting","E":"Increased stool osmotic gap"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lactase deficiency leaves milk sugar unabsorbed, so unmeasured osmoles widen the stool osmotic gap and diarrhea stops when lactose is withdrawn. A positive lactose hydrogen breath test, acid stool, and absent blood or leukocytes match that pattern. A high stool sodium with a narrow gap is the pattern of secretory diarrhea. Leukocytes and blood in stool indicate inflammatory or invasive mucosal disease. Sudan-positive fat globules indicate malabsorption of dietary triglyceride. Diarrhea that continues throughout fasting indicates a secretory process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial fermentation of lactose raises breath hydrogen and lowers stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the other brush-border enzymes are preserved in isolated lactase deficiency (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A high stool sodium with a narrow gap is the pattern of secretory diarrhea","B":"Leukocytes and blood in stool indicate inflammatory or invasive mucosal disease","C":"Sudan-positive fat globules indicate malabsorption of dietary triglyceride","D":"Diarrhea that continues throughout fasting indicates a secretory process"}},{"id":"sdl26_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-month-old girl has had watery diarrhea with every lactose-containing formula feeding for three weeks since rotavirus. She tolerated the same formula before the illness, and there is no blood in the stool. She is mildly dehydrated and the abdomen is soft. Stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5), and stool reducing substances are positive. Which change in her small intestine best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic amylase output","B":"Loss of brush-border lactase","C":"Defective apical glucose cotransport","D":"Absent enterokinase activity","E":"Deficient bile salt secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus injures villus-tip enterocytes and temporarily removes brush-border lactase, so formula lactose reaches the colon and is fermented. Acid stool, positive reducing sugars, and tolerance of the same formula before the illness fit that reversible loss. Pancreatic amylase digests starch, and lactose never requires an amylase step. A congenital glucose-transport defect would have been present from the first feeds. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and has no role in digesting milk sugar. Bile salts form lipid micelles and are not required for lactose digestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: secondary lactase deficiency follows mucosal injury from gastroenteritis or celiac disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: lactase sits on the villus brush border, where mucosal injury removes it first (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic amylase digests starch, and lactose never requires an amylase step","C":"A congenital glucose-transport defect would have been present from the first feeds","D":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and has no role in digesting milk sugar","E":"Bile salts form lipid micelles and are not required for lactose digestion"}},{"id":"sdl26_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes starts acarbose with each meal. Two weeks later she has flatulence and loose stools that are worse after bread and pasta. The abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.8% (reference range: <5.7%). Inhibition of which digestive step most directly explains her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border oligosaccharide hydrolysis","B":"Pancreatic triglyceride ester hydrolysis","C":"Basolateral monosaccharide exit","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransport","E":"Gastric pepsin protein hydrolysis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Acarbose inhibits brush-border α-glucosidases, so maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins are not cleaved to glucose and are fermented in the colon. Gas and loose stools after bread and pasta follow that block of oligosaccharide hydrolysis. Triglyceride hydrolysis is lipid digestion and is blocked by a different medication. Basolateral sugar exit moves free monosaccharides and is not a digestive cleavage. Apical sodium-glucose uptake transports free glucose and does not split oligosaccharides. Gastric pepsin hydrolyzes protein and does not digest dietary carbohydrate.","boardPrep":"Step 1: α-glucosidase inhibitors lower postprandial glucose by delaying carbohydrate digestion (First Aid Endocrine, diabetes drugs). COMLEX: oligosaccharides that escape digestion produce osmotic diarrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triglyceride hydrolysis is lipid digestion and is blocked by a different medication","C":"Basolateral sugar exit moves free monosaccharides and is not a digestive cleavage","D":"Apical sodium-glucose uptake transports free glucose and does not split oligosaccharides","E":"Gastric pepsin hydrolyzes protein and does not digest dietary carbohydrate"}},{"id":"sdl26_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-week-old boy has severe watery diarrhea on breast milk and on standard infant formula. Diarrhea stops when the only carbohydrate in the formula is fructose. He is below birth weight, with a sunken fontanelle and a pulse of 162/min. Serum urea nitrogen is 28 mg/dL (reference range: 7–18 mg/dL), and stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5). Which transporter is most likely defective?","choices":{"A":"Apical fructose-specific uniporter","B":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","C":"Brush-border lactase hydrolase","D":"Apical sodium-glucose cotransporter","E":"Basolateral monosaccharide uniporter"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose-galactose malabsorption is loss of apical SGLT1, so glucose and galactose stay in the lumen, while fructose still enters on GLUT5. Relief on a fructose-only formula, acid stool, and an elevated urea nitrogen fit dehydrated sugar malabsorption from birth. Loss of the fructose carrier would leave glucose and galactose absorption intact. Failure of the basolateral sodium pump would not spare a fructose-only feeding. Isolated lactase loss would still permit absorption of a glucose-based formula. A shared basolateral exit block would trap fructose inside the cell too.","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucose-galactose malabsorption is an autosomal recessive SGLT1 defect (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: fructose is absorbed independently of sodium (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — solute-coupled water absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of the fructose carrier would leave glucose and galactose absorption intact","B":"Failure of the basolateral sodium pump would not spare a fructose-only feeding","C":"Isolated lactase loss would still permit absorption of a glucose-based formula","E":"A shared basolateral exit block would trap fructose inside the cell too"}},{"id":"sdl26_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has profuse rice-water diarrhea for 12 hours after drinking untreated water during an outbreak of rice-water diarrhea. Plain water has not stayed down. The pulse is 112/min, blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and the mucous membranes are dry. Serum bicarbonate is 17 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Why does an oral rehydration solution correct his volume deficit?","choices":{"A":"Glucose blocks crypt chloride secretion","B":"Sodium enters through the apical GLUT5","C":"Glucose stimulates pancreatic fluid outflow","D":"Sodium uptake no longer requires ATP use","E":"Glucose-coupled sodium uptake is preserved"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera toxin keeps crypt chloride channels open, but apical SGLT1 still couples glucose entry to sodium entry, and water follows the absorbed sodium. Metabolic acidosis with tachycardia shows volume loss that this preserved cotransport can replace. Glucose does not close the chloride channels that the toxin holds open. The apical fructose carrier transports fructose and carries no sodium ion. Stimulating pancreatic fluid secretion would add luminal volume, not restore it. The driving sodium gradient still depends on ATP use by the basolateral pump.","boardPrep":"Step 1: oral rehydration solution uses SGLT1 cotransport of sodium and glucose (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: cholera toxin raises cAMP in crypt cells and opens CFTR chloride channels (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — cholera toxin and secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucose does not close the chloride channels that the toxin holds open","B":"The apical fructose carrier transports fructose and carries no sodium ion","C":"Stimulating pancreatic fluid secretion would add luminal volume, not restore it","D":"The driving sodium gradient still depends on ATP use by the basolateral pump"}},{"id":"sdl26_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old woman training for a marathon tolerates more carbohydrate each hour from a gel that mixes glucose and fructose than from glucose alone. She has no diarrhea or weight change on long runs. The pulse is 58/min and blood pressure is 112/70 mm Hg. Fasting glucose is 84 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which property of intestinal sugar absorption best explains the higher tolerable dose?","choices":{"A":"Fructose activates the glucose cotransporter","B":"Fructose uses a separate apical carrier","C":"Glucose stimulates fructose cotransport","D":"Fructose enters against its gradient","E":"Glucose exits through apical GLUT5"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Glucose uptake is limited by apical SGLT1, whereas fructose enters on a separate carrier, GLUT5, by facilitated diffusion, so the sugars add absorptive capacity. Better tolerance of the mixed gel with a normal fasting glucose fits that dual apical route. Fructose does not turn on or accelerate the sodium-glucose cotransporter. Glucose does not drive a coupled fructose transporter at the apical membrane. Fructose can move only down its gradient by facilitated diffusion. Glucose does not leave enterocytes through the apical fructose carrier.","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: fructose absorption is passive and depends on its concentration gradient (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — monosaccharide transporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose does not turn on or accelerate the sodium-glucose cotransporter","C":"Glucose does not drive a coupled fructose transporter at the apical membrane","D":"Fructose can move only down its gradient by facilitated diffusion","E":"Glucose does not leave enterocytes through the apical fructose carrier"}},{"id":"sdl26_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"A 63-year-old man is seen one year after total gastrectomy for a gastric mass. He eats regular meals, has no diarrhea, and his weight has been stable for months. There is a healed midline scar and the abdomen is soft. Serum albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and fecal nitrogen is 1.4 g/day (reference range: 1–2 g/day). Which statement best describes his protein digestion?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal proteases complete digestion","B":"Protein digestion falls by about half","C":"Free amino acid uptake becomes defective","D":"Trypsinogen activation fails in duodenum","E":"Undigested protein reaches the colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases and brush-border peptidases finish protein digestion without pepsin, so absorption stays normal after total gastrectomy. Stable weight, normal albumin, and normal fecal nitrogen show that dietary protein is still assimilated. A one-half reduction overstates how much the gastric protease contributes. Intestinal carriers for free amino acids do not require an intact stomach. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid. Fecal nitrogen would rise if large amounts of protein were left undigested.","boardPrep":"Step 1: after total gastrectomy, vitamin B₁₂ deficiency follows loss of intrinsic factor (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin accounts for a minor share of protein digestion (Guyton Ch. 66).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — intrinsic factor and vitamin B12","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"A one-half reduction overstates how much the gastric protease contributes","C":"Intestinal carriers for free amino acids do not require an intact stomach","D":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase, not by gastric acid","E":"Fecal nitrogen would rise if large amounts of protein were left undigested"}},{"id":"sdl26_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with years of heavy alcohol use has bulky, floating stools and weight loss. He has recurrent epigastric pain. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g), and fecal nitrogen is 5 g/day (reference range: 1–2 g/day). Which digestive step is most impaired for dietary protein?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of lactose to monosaccharides","B":"Hydrolysis of proteins to oligopeptides","C":"Activation of pepsinogen in the stomach","D":"Apical uptake of small peptides","E":"Basolateral exit of amino acids"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic insufficiency removes trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and the carboxypeptidases, so protein is not hydrolyzed to absorbable oligopeptides. Low fecal elastase and high fecal nitrogen mark that missing luminal step. Lactose is split by brush-border lactase, which does not come from the pancreas. Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid and does not require the pancreas. Apical proton-coupled peptide uptake is an epithelial step spared by pancreatic failure. Basolateral amino acid exit is epithelial facilitated diffusion, not a pancreatic enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low fecal elastase is a marker of exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic pancreatitis). COMLEX: pancreatic enzyme replacement treats both steatorrhea and creatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is split by brush-border lactase, which does not come from the pancreas","C":"Pepsinogen activation depends on gastric acid and does not require the pancreas","D":"Apical proton-coupled peptide uptake is an epithelial step spared by pancreatic failure","E":"Basolateral amino acid exit is epithelial facilitated diffusion, not a pancreatic enzyme"}},{"id":"sdl26_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":5,"stem":"A 39-year-old woman has taken high-dose omeprazole for two years for reflux. Heartburn is controlled, and she has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is nontender. Intragastric pH during monitoring is 6.0 (reference range: 1.5–3.5). Which change in gastric protein digestion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin activity rises in the stomach","B":"Trypsinogen stays inactive in the duodenum","C":"Pepsinogen stays inactive in the stomach","D":"Protein absorption ceases in the jejunum","E":"Chief cells stop secreting pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsinogen converts to pepsin only at low pH, and pepsin is denatured above pH 5, so a gastric pH of 6.0 leaves pepsinogen inactive. Absent diarrhea and stable weight fit continued protein digestion by pancreatic and brush-border proteases. Activity of the gastric protease falls, rather than rises, as luminal pH climbs. Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by enterokinase, independent of gastric acid. Jejunal protein uptake does not cease when gastric acid is pharmacologically suppressed. Chief cells keep secreting pepsinogen under vagal drive despite the higher pH.","boardPrep":"Step 1: proton pump inhibitors irreversibly block the parietal cell H⁺-K⁺ ATPase (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: pepsin isozymes have optimal activity between pH 1 and 3 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — regulation and pharmacologic inhibition of gastric acid secretion","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Activity of the gastric protease falls, rather than rises, as luminal pH climbs","B":"Trypsinogen is activated in the duodenum by enterokinase, independent of gastric acid","D":"Jejunal protein uptake does not cease when gastric acid is pharmacologically suppressed","E":"Chief cells keep secreting pepsinogen under vagal drive despite the higher pH"}},{"id":"sdl26_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and generalized edema. He has been formula-fed and has had no respiratory infections. He has anasarca and a soft abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), sweat chloride is 15 mEq/L (reference range: <30 mEq/L), duodenal trypsinogen protein is normal, and adding a brush-border extract restores proteolysis. Which finding was most likely in the untreated duodenal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Low pancreatic trypsinogen secretion","B":"Low brush-border lactase activity","C":"Low pancreatic α-amylase activity","D":"Low trypsin and elastase activity","E":"Low gastric pepsin enzyme activity"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital enterokinase deficiency leaves trypsinogen unactivated, so trypsin, chymotrypsin, elastase, and the carboxypeptidases stay inactive. Normal precursor protein, normal sweat chloride, and rescue by a brush-border extract place the defect on that activator. The precursor protein is present, so failure of trypsinogen secretion is not the problem. Lactase digests lactose and plays no part in activating pancreatic proteases. Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form and does not await trypsin. Pepsin is switched on by gastric acid and does not need an intestinal activator.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypoproteinemic edema reflects low oncotic pressure from protein malabsorption (First Aid Cardiovascular, edema). COMLEX: absence of trypsin mimics loss of the pancreatic proteases (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — pancreatic proteases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The precursor protein is present, so failure of trypsinogen secretion is not the problem","B":"Lactase digests lactose and plays no part in activating pancreatic proteases","C":"Pancreatic amylase is secreted in active form and does not await trypsin","E":"Pepsin is switched on by gastric acid and does not need an intestinal activator"}},{"id":"sdl26_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 48-year-old man has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back after a weekend of heavy drinking. He has vomited twice and is not jaundiced. Temperature is 38.2°C, the pulse is 116/min, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg, with marked epigastric tenderness. Serum lipase is 1,450 U/L (reference range: 0–160 U/L). Premature activation of which zymogen inside the pancreas most directly starts this autodigestive cascade?","choices":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen","B":"Proelastase","C":"Trypsinogen","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Premature trypsinogen activation lets trypsin turn on itself and the other pancreatic zymogens, which starts autodigestion. Severe pain after heavy drinking and a lipase many times the reference limit fit that intrapancreatic cascade. Chymotrypsinogen is cleaved later and cannot ignite the remaining zymogens. Proelastase is a downstream precursor and does not activate its companion enzymes. Procarboxypeptidase A is activated later and does not start the enzyme cascade. Pepsinogen is produced by gastric chief cells and is not a pancreatic precursor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alcohol and gallstones are the leading causes of acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: lipase is more specific than amylase for acute pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chymotrypsinogen is cleaved later and cannot ignite the remaining zymogens","B":"Proelastase is a downstream precursor and does not activate its companion enzymes","D":"Procarboxypeptidase A is activated later and does not start the enzyme cascade","E":"Pepsinogen is produced by gastric chief cells and is not a pancreatic precursor"}},{"id":"sdl26_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":5,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had three episodes of acute pancreatitis. She does not drink alcohol, and ultrasound shows no gallstones. She recovers fully between episodes. Serum triglycerides are 110 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). Genetic testing shows loss of a protein stored with the digestive zymogens in acinar cells. Which protective mechanism is most likely lost?","choices":{"A":"Blocking of early active trypsin","B":"Enterokinase storage in acinar cells","C":"Bicarbonate secretion by duct cells","D":"Secretin release from duodenal cells","E":"Bile reflux into the pancreatic duct"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic trypsin inhibitor is packaged in acinar granules and blocks trypsin that forms too early, so its loss allows recurrent autodigestion. Normal triglycerides and no alcohol or gallstones point away from the common metabolic and obstructive triggers. Enterokinase resides on the intestinal brush border and is not an acinar granule protein. Ductal bicarbonate neutralizes acidic chyme and is not stored inside acinar granules. Secretin comes from duodenal S cells and is not packaged with acinar zymogens. Bile reflux can provoke pancreatitis, but it is not a protective granule protein.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SPINK1 and PRSS1 mutations cause hereditary pancreatitis (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: zymogen granules are impermeable to proteins, keeping enzymes out of the cytoplasm (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Enterokinase resides on the intestinal brush border and is not an acinar granule protein","C":"Ductal bicarbonate neutralizes acidic chyme and is not stored inside acinar granules","D":"Secretin comes from duodenal S cells and is not packaged with acinar zymogens","E":"Bile reflux can provoke pancreatitis, but it is not a protective granule protein"}},{"id":"sdl26_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 12-year-old boy has a photosensitive scaly rash and episodes of ataxia that flare when his diet is low in protein. Growth and school performance are otherwise normal. There is a rash on sun-exposed skin and no edema. Urine contains excess neutral amino acids, and serum albumin is 4.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains his normal protein nutrition despite this transport defect?","choices":{"A":"Absorption on basolateral GLUT2 carriers","B":"Paracellular leak of free amino acids","C":"Pinocytosis of intact dietary protein","D":"Colonic absorption of amino acids","E":"Absorption as dipeptides and tripeptides"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hartnup disease disables the sodium-dependent transporter for neutral amino acids, but dipeptides and tripeptides still enter on a proton-dependent carrier and preserve nutrition. Normal albumin, a photosensitive rash, and neutral aminoaciduria fit tryptophan loss with intact peptide uptake. GLUT2 exports monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane and ignores amino acids. Paracellular leakage is not a meaningful absorptive route for dietary amino acids. Intact dietary protein cannot cross the mucosa in nutritionally useful amounts. Colonic mucosa does not absorb enough amino acids to maintain protein stores.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hartnup disease causes pellagra-like findings from tryptophan loss (First Aid Biochemistry). COMLEX: neutral, acidic, basic, and imino amino acids use separate Na⁺ cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — amino acid cotransporters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLUT2 exports monosaccharides across the basolateral membrane and ignores amino acids","B":"Paracellular leakage is not a meaningful absorptive route for dietary amino acids","C":"Intact dietary protein cannot cross the mucosa in nutritionally useful amounts","D":"Colonic mucosa does not absorb enough amino acids to maintain protein stores"}},{"id":"sdl26_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"Jejunal mucosa from a 41-year-old man is studied in vitro after bariatric surgery. He has had no diarrhea or protein deficiency and does not take acid-suppressing drugs. The tissue is viable, with a stable potential difference. A selective block of apical sodium-hydrogen exchange drops glycylglycine uptake sharply, while brush-border peptidase activity is unchanged. What best explains the fall in dipeptide uptake?","choices":{"A":"Loss of the luminal sodium gradient","B":"Inhibition of brush-border peptidases","C":"Block of basolateral amino acid exit","D":"Loss of the luminal proton gradient","E":"Reduced pancreatic protease release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Apical dipeptide cotransport uses a proton gradient created by sodium-hydrogen exchange, so blocking that exchanger removes the driving force. Sharp loss of glycylglycine uptake, with peptidase activity left intact, ties the result to that gradient. The sodium gradient is what drives apical uptake of free amino acids. Surface peptidase activity was unchanged, so enzyme loss does not explain the fall. Basolateral amino acid exit does not control how fast apical peptide entry occurs. An isolated mucosal sheet no longer receives proteases secreted by the pancreas.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the apical peptide transporter uses H⁺ cotransport (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: free amino acid uptake mirrors glucose uptake by Na⁺ cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sodium gradient is what drives apical uptake of free amino acids","B":"Surface peptidase activity was unchanged, so enzyme loss does not explain the fall","C":"Basolateral amino acid exit does not control how fast apical peptide entry occurs","E":"An isolated mucosal sheet no longer receives proteases secreted by the pancreas"}},{"id":"sdl26_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old man has recurrent flank pain and passes hexagonal urinary crystals. He has had two prior stones and has no diarrhea or growth failure. There is left costovertebral angle tenderness. Serum creatinine is 0.9 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and the urine contains excess cystine, lysine, arginine, and ornithine. The same carrier fails in the intestine. Which mechanism normally moves these amino acids into enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport","B":"Primary active ATP pumping","C":"Sodium-dependent cotransport","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis","E":"Paracellular solvent drag"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cystinuria is loss of the sodium-dependent cotransporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine, and arginine in kidney and intestine. Hexagonal crystals, dibasic aminoaciduria, and a normal creatinine identify that shared sodium-coupled carrier. Proton-dependent cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides. These free amino acids are not pumped by a carrier that hydrolyzes ATP directly. Receptor-mediated endocytosis is not how free dibasic amino acids enter enterocytes. Paracellular solvent drag is not the membrane route described for these amino acids.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystinuria causes hexagonal cystine stones with a positive cyanide-nitroprusside test (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: unabsorbed dibasic amino acids are excreted in feces (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption by enterocytes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Proton-dependent cotransport is the apical route for dipeptides and tripeptides","B":"These free amino acids are not pumped by a carrier that hydrolyzes ATP directly","D":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis is not how free dibasic amino acids enter enterocytes","E":"Paracellular solvent drag is not the membrane route described for these amino acids"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 6-year-old boy has bloating and watery diarrhea after juice sweetened with cane sugar. He tolerates milk without symptoms, and the pattern has been present since infancy. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. After a sucrose load, stool pH is 5.0 (reference range: 6.5–7.5) and breath hydrogen rises by 28 ppm (a rise above 20 ppm is abnormal). Deficiency of which brush-border enzyme is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Lactase","B":"Trehalase","C":"Amylase","D":"Lipase","E":"Sucrase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Brush-border sucrase, the sucrose-splitting activity of sucrase-isomaltase, releases glucose and fructose, so its loss leaves cane sugar in the lumen. Acid stool and a positive sucrose hydrogen breath test after cane sugar, with continued tolerance of milk, fit unabsorbed sucrose. Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms after milk, which this child tolerates. Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms and does not digest cane sugar. Amylase acts on starch within the lumen and is not a brush-border disaccharidase. Lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and has no action on dietary sucrose.","boardPrep":"Step 1: congenital sucrase-isomaltase deficiency presents when sucrose enters the diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption). COMLEX: sucrase also contributes to digestion of starch oligosaccharides to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactase deficiency would cause symptoms after milk, which this child tolerates","B":"Trehalase splits trehalose from mushrooms and does not digest cane sugar","C":"Amylase acts on starch within the lumen and is not a brush-border disaccharidase","D":"Lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and has no action on dietary sucrose"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the intestinal absorption of glucose, galactose, and fructose, including the roles of SGLT1, GLUT5, GLUT2, and the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase.","batch":3,"stem":"Jejunal mucosa from a 42-year-old woman is studied immediately after bariatric surgery. She has no history of sugar intolerance. The mucosa is mounted in a bath containing glucose, galactose, and fructose, and baseline serosal appearance of glucose is measurable. A compound that selectively blocks the basolateral GLUT2 transporter is then added on the serosal side. Which result is expected?","choices":{"A":"Fructose exit continues through basolateral GLUT5","B":"Glucose exit shifts onto basolateral SGLT1 carriers","C":"Galactose exit continues by sodium cotransport","D":"Exit of all three sugars to the serosa falls","E":"Apical sugar entry rises to offset the block"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glucose and galactose enter on apical SGLT1 and fructose enters on apical GLUT5, but all three monosaccharides leave through basolateral GLUT2. Blocking that shared exit therefore cuts serosal delivery of every sugar in the bath. Fructose does not exit across a basolateral GLUT5 protein. The sodium-glucose carrier faces the lumen and cannot serve as a basolateral exit. Galactose is not driven out of the cell by sodium cotransport. Sugar trapped in the cytosol reduces, rather than raises, further apical entry.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SGLT1 and GLUT5 bring sugars across the apical membrane, while GLUT2 carries glucose, galactose, and fructose out across the basolateral membrane (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Oral rehydration solutions rely on the intact SGLT1 pathway to drive sodium and water uptake.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fructose does not exit across a basolateral GLUT5 protein","B":"The sodium-glucose carrier faces the lumen and cannot serve as a basolateral exit","C":"Galactose is not driven out of the cell by sodium cotransport","E":"Sugar trapped in the cytosol reduces, rather than raises, further apical entry"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":3,"stem":"A 31-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and leg swelling for six months. He has an itchy blistering rash on the elbows and does not drink heavily. There is pitting ankle edema and a tympanitic abdomen. Serum albumin is 2.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is positive, and fecal elastase is 400 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Duodenal biopsy shows flattened villi with many intraepithelial lymphocytes. Which step of protein digestion is most impaired?","choices":{"A":"Activation of pepsinogen by gastric acid","B":"Secretion of pancreatic endopeptidases","C":"C-terminal cleavage by carboxypeptidase","D":"Acid denaturation of dietary protein","E":"Brush-border oligopeptide hydrolysis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Celiac disease, shown by transglutaminase antibody and flat villi, removes brush-border peptidases that finish oligopeptides made by pancreatic proteases. Hypoalbuminemia with leg edema fits loss of that mucosal digestive step. Pepsinogen activation by acid occurs in the stomach and is not essential here. Normal fecal elastase shows that pancreatic endopeptidase secretion is preserved. Carboxypeptidase cleavage is a pancreatic function, and pancreatic output is intact. Acid denaturation of dietary protein occurs in the stomach, upstream of this injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic proteases yield amino acids, dipeptides, tripeptides, and oligopeptides, and brush-border proteases finish the oligopeptides (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Mucosal disease can cause protein loss and edema even when pancreatic function is normal.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsinogen activation by acid occurs in the stomach and is not essential here","B":"Normal fecal elastase shows that pancreatic endopeptidase secretion is preserved","C":"Carboxypeptidase cleavage is a pancreatic function, and pancreatic output is intact","D":"Acid denaturation of dietary protein occurs in the stomach, upstream of this injury"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-month-old boy has failure to thrive, diarrhea, and edema on standard formula. He has had no respiratory infections. Duodenal aspirate contains normal amounts of trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and proelastase protein but almost no protease activity. Serum albumin is 2.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Addition of a small amount of purified active trypsin sharply raises trypsin, chymotrypsin, and elastase activities. Which property of trypsin explains this result?","choices":{"A":"It replaces gastric pepsin in digestion","B":"It activates trypsinogen and other zymogens","C":"It shields the zymogens from acid denaturation","D":"It binds colipase to restore lipase action","E":"It is the sole enzyme able to digest elastin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The zymogens are present but inactive until a little trypsin autocatalyzes more trypsinogen and activates chymotrypsinogen, proelastase, and the procarboxypeptidases. Hypoalbuminemia with absent activity that returns after trypsin is added fits a failed activation step. Pepsin is unnecessary for protein digestion and does not activate these zymogens. These precursors are activated in alkaline duodenal fluid, not shielded from acid. Colipase anchors lipase to fat droplets and does not switch on proteases. Elastin is cleaved by elastase, so trypsin is not the sole elastin-cleaving enzyme.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterokinase activates trypsinogen, and trypsin then activates more trypsinogen and the other pancreatic zymogens (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Premature trypsin activation inside the pancreas sets off the autodigestion of acute pancreatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin is unnecessary for protein digestion and does not activate these zymogens","C":"These precursors are activated in alkaline duodenal fluid, not shielded from acid","D":"Colipase anchors lipase to fat droplets and does not switch on proteases","E":"Elastin is cleaved by elastase, so trypsin is not the sole elastin-cleaving enzyme"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of amino acids, dipeptides, and tripeptides, including Na⁺- and H⁺-dependent cotransport.","batch":3,"stem":"A strip of jejunal mucosa from a 36-year-old man is mounted between two chambers at surgery. He had no diarrhea beforehand. The luminal chamber contains free leucine, and the tissue potential is stable. Ouabain is added on the serosal side to inhibit the basolateral sodium-potassium pump. Which transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-coupled uptake of free leucine","B":"Basolateral exit by facilitated diffusion","C":"Cytosolic hydrolysis of absorbed dipeptides","D":"Brush-border cleavage of oligopeptides","E":"Apical fructose entry through GLUT5"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Free amino acids enter on apical sodium-coupled carriers driven by the sodium gradient that the basolateral sodium-potassium ATPase maintains. Serosal ouabain dissipates that gradient, so uptake of luminal leucine falls first. Basolateral amino acid exit is facilitated diffusion and does not require sodium. Cytosolic peptidases split dipeptides with no dependence on the sodium gradient. Brush-border proteases cleave luminal peptides and are not sodium-driven transporters. Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by sodium-independent diffusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Free amino acids enter by Na⁺-dependent cotransport and leave by facilitated diffusion, while dipeptides and tripeptides enter by H⁺-dependent cotransport (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Defects in specific amino acid cotransporters cause disorders such as Hartnup disease and cystinuria.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Basolateral amino acid exit is facilitated diffusion and does not require sodium","C":"Cytosolic peptidases split dipeptides with no dependence on the sodium gradient","D":"Brush-border proteases cleave luminal peptides and are not sodium-driven transporters","E":"Fructose crosses the apical membrane on GLUT5 by sodium-independent diffusion"}},{"id":"trial_sdl26_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with long-standing heavy alcohol use has recurrent epigastric pain radiating to the back, bulky foul-smelling stools, and weight loss. He has not started enzyme capsules. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. CT shows calcifications throughout the pancreas, and fecal elastase is 40 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Digestion of which dietary carbohydrate is most impaired?","choices":{"A":"Lactose","B":"Sucrose","C":"Starch","D":"Trehalose","E":"Maltose"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Calcific chronic pancreatitis with low fecal elastase removes pancreatic amylase, so starch is not split into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins. Salivary amylase adds little because gastric acid inactivates it before the intestine. Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase and never needs pancreatic amylase. Sucrose is split by brush-border sucrase and does not require pancreatic amylase. Trehalose is cleaved by brush-border trehalase without any pancreatic amylase step. Maltose is split by mucosal maltase, which remains on an intact brush border.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Pancreatic amylase digests starch to oligosaccharides that brush-border enzymes then convert to glucose, while dietary disaccharides need only brush-border enzymes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Pancreatic enzyme replacement is the treatment for maldigestion in chronic pancreatitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is hydrolyzed by brush-border lactase and never needs pancreatic amylase","B":"Sucrose is split by brush-border sucrase and does not require pancreatic amylase","D":"Trehalose is cleaved by brush-border trehalase without any pancreatic amylase step","E":"Maltose is split by mucosal maltase, which remains on an intact brush border"}},{"id":"grid_sdl26_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe carbohydrate digestion by pancreatic amylase and intestinal brush-border enzymes, and explain the physiologic consequences of lactase deficiency.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has bulky stools and weight loss. Six months ago she had a total pancreatectomy for an intraductal tumor, and she stopped her enzyme capsules a month ago. The abdomen is soft, without tenderness. Hemoglobin A1c is 7.9% (reference range: <5.7%), and fecal fat is 18 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which of the following sets of carbohydrate digestion findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Lactose digestion","Sucrose digestion","Starch digestion"]},"choices":{"A":["↔","↔","↓"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↔","↓","↔"],"D":["↓","↓","↓"],"E":["↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is correct. Without the pancreas there is no pancreatic α-amylase to split starch into maltose, maltotriose, and α-limit dextrins, so starch digestion falls; salivary amylase is inactivated by gastric acid and gives little help. Lactose and sucrose are already disaccharides and are split by brush-border lactase and sucrase, which are intact. Choice B (↓ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is lactase deficiency. Choice C (↔ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, in which sucrose goes undigested while pancreatic amylase and maltase still handle most starch. Choice D (↓ lactose digestion, ↓ sucrose digestion, ↓ starch digestion) is diffuse villous injury, as in celiac disease, which removes brush-border enzymes. Choice E (↔ lactose digestion, ↔ sucrose digestion, ↔ starch digestion) is normal digestion, as with adequate enzyme replacement. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of carbohydrates, Fig. 8.27; Disorders of carbohydrate digestion and absorption); First Aid Gastrointestinal (malabsorption syndromes). The ↔ cells, including starch in sucrase-isomaltase deficiency, are inferred because the sources do not grade them. Aligned with SDL 26 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diabetes after total pancreatectomy reflects loss of islet insulin and glucagon (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: brush-border α-dextrinase, maltase, and sucrase finish starch digestion to glucose (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — secretion and functions of pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl26_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":1,"stem":"Pepsin cleaves dietary protein at which sites?","choices":{"A":"Interior peptide bonds","B":"Only the C-terminal amino acid","C":"Only the N-terminal amino acid","D":"Glycosidic bonds in starch","E":"Ester bonds in triglyceride"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pepsin is an endopeptidase that hydrolyzes interior peptide bonds, yielding large polypeptides, proteoses, and peptones. Removing the C-terminal amino acid is the action of carboxypeptidases, not pepsin. N-terminal trimming is an exopeptidase action, not pepsin's. Glycosidic bonds in starch are hydrolyzed by amylase. Triglyceride ester bonds are the substrate of lipases.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Removing the C-terminal amino acid is the action of carboxypeptidases, not pepsin","C":"N-terminal trimming is an exopeptidase action, not pepsin's","D":"Glycosidic bonds in starch are hydrolyzed by amylase","E":"Triglyceride ester bonds are the substrate of lipases"}},{"id":"sdl26_q32","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old man has a sample of gastric juice taken an hour after a protein meal, during a research study. He has no diarrhea, no weight loss, and takes no acid-suppressing drug. The abdomen is soft. Intragastric pH is 1.8 (reference range: 1.5–3.5) and albumin is 4.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). The protein fragments in the sample are large polypeptides rather than free amino acids. Which products has pepsin mainly released?","choices":{"A":"Free amino acids ready for portal absorption","B":"Glucose and galactose","C":"Proteoses and peptones","D":"Micelles of monoglyceride","E":"Intact triglyceride droplets"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pepsin's endopeptidase action releases large polypeptides, proteoses, and peptones, not a pool of free amino acids. Free amino acids are a later product of pancreatic and brush-border peptidases, not the main gastric yield. Glucose and galactose are carbohydrate products and are not released by pepsin. Monoglyceride micelles are the result of lipid digestion, not of pepsin. Intact triglyceride is fat that pepsin does not hydrolyze.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Free amino acids are a later product of pancreatic and brush-border peptidases, not the main gastric yield","B":"Glucose and galactose are carbohydrate products and are not released by pepsin","D":"Monoglyceride micelles are the result of lipid digestion, not of pepsin","E":"Intact triglyceride is fat that pepsin does not hydrolyze"}},{"id":"sdl26_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe protein digestion by gastric, pancreatic, and intestinal brush-border proteases.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old woman has epigastric burning that improves on an acid-suppressing drug. She has no diarrhea or weight loss. The abdomen is soft and there is no mass. Hemoglobin is 13.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and albumin is 4.2 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). A gastric biopsy highlights the cells that package the inactive precursor of the gastric protease. Which cells are they?","choices":{"A":"Parietal cells","B":"Chief cells","C":"Goblet cells of the colon","D":"Hepatocytes","E":"Pancreatic duct cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, the inactive precursor that acid converts to pepsin. Parietal cells secrete the acid that activates pepsinogen, and they do not package that precursor. Colonic goblet cells secrete mucin, not pepsinogen. Hepatocytes do not synthesize the gastric protease precursor. Pancreatic duct cells secrete bicarbonate, not pepsinogen.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Parietal cells secrete the acid that activates pepsinogen, and they do not package that precursor","C":"Colonic goblet cells secrete mucin, not pepsinogen","D":"Hepatocytes do not synthesize the gastric protease precursor","E":"Pancreatic duct cells secrete bicarbonate, not pepsinogen"}},{"id":"sdl26_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the activation of pancreatic proteases, including enterokinase-mediated activation of trypsin and subsequent activation of other pancreatic zymogens.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old man has duodenal juice sampled in a research protocol an hour after a meal. He has no abdominal pain and does not drink alcohol. The abdomen is soft. Serum lipase is 28 U/L (reference range: 10–140 U/L) and triglycerides are 90 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL). A small amount of trypsin appears, and then more trypsinogen converts even though no further enterokinase is added. What converts that remaining trypsinogen?","choices":{"A":"Pepsin spilling back from the stomach","B":"Bile salts acting as proteases","C":"Salivary amylase","D":"Trypsin already present","E":"Gastric intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Once a little trypsin is present, it autocatalyzes activation of the remaining trypsinogen. Pepsin is denatured once duodenal pH rises, and it is not the enzyme that continues the trypsinogen cascade. Bile salts emulsify fat and are not proteases. Salivary amylase digests starch and does not cleave trypsinogen. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and has no protease activity.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin is denatured once duodenal pH rises, and it is not the enzyme that continues the trypsinogen cascade","B":"Bile salts emulsify fat and are not proteases","C":"Salivary amylase digests starch and does not cleave trypsinogen","E":"Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and has no protease activity"}}]},{"sdlNumber":27,"title":"SDL 27 — Physiology - Digestion & Absorption of the Small Intestine Part 2 (Lipids, Vitamins)","questions":[{"id":"sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes each triglyceride molecule into which products?","choices":{"A":"One monoglyceride and two fatty acids","B":"Glycerol and three free fatty acids","C":"Two monoglycerides and one fatty acid","D":"Cholesterol and two free fatty acids","E":"Lysolecithin and one free fatty acid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Pancreatic lipase, secreted in active form, splits a triglyceride into one monoglyceride and two fatty acids. Pancreatic lipase does not release free glycerol, and two monoglycerides would need a second glycerol backbone. Cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids, and lysolecithin comes from phospholipids. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving triglyceride ester bonds. Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids, not of lipase acting on triglyceride. Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from each triglyceride. Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters, not lipase acting on triglyceride. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lingual and gastric lipases hydrolyze about 10 percent of ingested triglyceride before the chyme reaches the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the optimum pH for pancreatic lipase is about 6, so duodenal acid must be neutralized by bicarbonate (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase cleaving triglyceride ester bonds; pancreatic lipase yields.","E":"Lysolecithin plus a fatty acid is the product of phospholipase A2 acting on phospholipids, not of lipase acting on triglyceride.","C":"Pancreatic lipase yields one monoglyceride, not two, from each triglyceride.","D":"Free cholesterol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol esters, not lipase acting on triglyceride."}},{"id":"sdl27_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the main function of colipase in lipid digestion?","choices":{"A":"Emulsifying lipid droplets in the gastric lumen","B":"Activating trypsinogen in the duodenal lumen","C":"Displacing bile salts from lipase","D":"Hydrolyzing cholesterol esters to free cholesterol","E":"Packaging lipids into chylomicrons in enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts displace pancreatic lipase from emulsified droplets. Colipase, activated from procolipase by trypsin, displaces the bile salts and binds lipase so digestion can proceed. Dietary proteins emulsify fat in the stomach, enterokinase activates trypsinogen, cholesterol ester hydrolase acts on cholesterol esters, and enterocytes assemble chylomicrons. Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colipase is instead activated from procolipase by trypsin. In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary proteins; colipase acts in the small intestine. Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes cholesterol esters. Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipase in the lumen. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat and fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: CCK released by fat in the duodenum slows gastric emptying and gives pancreatic enzymes time to act (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Trypsinogen is activated by brush-border enterokinase; colipase is instead activated from procolipase by trypsin.","A":"In the stomach, lipid droplets are emulsified by dietary proteins; colipase acts in the small intestine.","D":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase, not colipase, hydrolyzes cholesterol esters.","E":"Chylomicrons are assembled inside enterocytes, not by colipase in the lumen."}},{"id":"sdl27_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":1,"stem":"Pancreatic phospholipase A2 hydrolyzes dietary phospholipids into which products?","choices":{"A":"Glycerol and fatty acids","B":"Cholesterol and fatty acids","C":"Monoglyceride and fatty acids","D":"Lysolecithin and fatty acids","E":"Bile acids and free glycine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Phospholipase A2, secreted as a proenzyme and activated by trypsin, converts phospholipids to lysolecithin and fatty acids. Lysolecithin then joins bile salts in emulsifying fat. The other pairs come from triglyceride or cholesterol ester hydrolysis, and bile acids with glycine come from deconjugation. Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol ester, not by phospholipase A2. A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase makes from triglyceride, not phospholipase A2 products. Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on triglyceride ester bonds. Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation, not phospholipase A2 action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile salts are amphipathic, so the hydrophilic side faces water and the hydrophobic side faces the lipid core (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the end products of lipid digestion, other than glycerol, are insoluble in water and must be carried in micelles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Free cholesterol and fatty acid are made by cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on cholesterol ester, not by phospholipase A2.","C":"A monoglyceride and fatty acids are what pancreatic lipase makes from triglyceride, not phospholipase A2 products.","A":"Glycerol comes from cholesterol ester hydrolase acting on triglyceride ester bonds.","E":"Removing glycine from bile salts is bacterial deconjugation, not phospholipase A2 action."}},{"id":"sdl27_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"In which organelle do enterocytes re-esterify absorbed lipid digestion products?","choices":{"A":"Rough endoplasmic reticulum","B":"Golgi secretory apparatus","C":"Apical brush-border membrane","D":"Lysosomal digestive compartment","E":"Smooth endoplasmic reticulum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inside the enterocyte, monoglycerides, fatty acids, cholesterol, and lysolecithin are re-esterified on the smooth endoplasmic reticulum to triglycerides, cholesterol esters, and phospholipids. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward. Lipids cross the brush border by diffusion, and lysosomes digest heme, not lipids. Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasmic reticulum. The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles after re-esterification. Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification happens inside the cell. Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they do not re-esterify lipids. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chylomicrons carry dietary triglyceride and are cleared by lipoprotein lipase in peripheral capillaries (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: lipid crosses the apical membrane by diffusion, so absorption depends on mucosal surface area (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Re-esterification occurs on the smooth, not rough, endoplasmic reticulum.","B":"The Golgi packages chylomicrons into secretory vesicles afterward.","C":"Lipids diffuse across the brush border; re-esterification happens inside the cell.","D":"Lysosomal enzymes digest heme iron inside enterocytes; they do not re-esterify lipids."}},{"id":"sdl27_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"Chylomicrons leave the intestinal villus by entering which vessels?","choices":{"A":"Portal venules","B":"Villus blood capillaries","C":"Hepatic sinusoids","D":"Lymphatic lacteals","E":"Mesenteric arterioles"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Chylomicrons are about 1000 Å across, too large to enter vascular capillaries, so they move between endothelial cells into lacteals and reach the blood through the thoracic duct. Portal venules and villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients. Hepatic sinusoids and mesenteric arterioles are not the exit route. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: injury to the thoracic duct leaks chylomicron-rich lymph, giving milky fluid high in triglyceride (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: glycerol is water soluble and does not need micelles for absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal venules carry water-soluble nutrients.","B":"Villus capillaries carry water-soluble nutrients.","C":"Hepatic sinusoids are not the exit route from the villus.","E":"Mesenteric arterioles carry blood toward the gut, not absorbed lipid away from it."}},{"id":"sdl27_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":1,"stem":"What happens to the bile salts of a micelle when its lipids are absorbed at the brush border?","choices":{"A":"They enter the enterocyte with the lipids","B":"They are packaged into chylomicrons","C":"They remain in the intestinal lumen","D":"They are re-esterified in the cytosol","E":"They are degraded by brush-border enzymes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Micelles release lipids at the apical membrane, but the micelles themselves do not enter the cell. The bile salts stay in the lumen and are absorbed downstream in the ileum. They are not part of chylomicrons, not re-esterified, and not degraded at the brush border. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mixed micelles are disks about 50 Å across with bile salts lining the outside (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile salt reuse through the enterohepatic circulation keeps the pool large without heavy hepatic synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Micelles do not enter the cell; only their lipids diffuse in.","B":"Chylomicrons contain re-esterified lipids and apoproteins.","D":"Re-esterification is a step for absorbed lipid products, not for bile salts.","E":"Brush-border enzymes do not degrade the bile salts."}},{"id":"sdl27_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"In which intestinal segment are conjugated bile salts actively reabsorbed?","choices":{"A":"Duodenum","B":"Proximal jejunum","C":"Ascending colon","D":"Gastric antrum","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the ileum and returned to the liver in portal blood, the enterohepatic circulation. About 95 percent of secreted bile acids return this way. The duodenum and jejunum are where bile salts work on lipids. The colon and stomach do not reabsorb them. The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recirculation step is in the terminal ileum. Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum because reabsorption is located in the terminal ileum. The recirculation step is located in the terminal small intestine, not the colon. The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of bile salt reabsorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 7α-hydroxylase is the rate-limiting enzyme of bile acid synthesis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: Crohn disease of the terminal ileum can interrupt bile salt reabsorption and cause fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The duodenum is where bile salts work on lipids; their recirculation step is in the terminal ileum.","B":"Bile salts stay in high concentration through the jejunum because reabsorption is located in the terminal ileum.","C":"The recirculation step is located in the terminal small intestine, not the colon.","D":"The gastric antrum contains pyloric glands with G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of bile salt reabsorption."}},{"id":"sdl27_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does surgical removal of the intestinal segment that reabsorbs bile salts shrink the total bile salt pool?","choices":{"A":"Hepatic synthesis stops after resection","B":"Hepatic synthesis lags behind fecal loss","C":"Bile salts are deconjugated in the bile","D":"Gallbladder storage is lost to the colon","E":"Micelles enter the ileal wall intact"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Resecting the reabsorptive segment interrupts the enterohepatic circulation, so bile salts go out in feces. The liver increases synthesis, but synthesis falls short of the fecal loss and the pool shrinks. Synthesis rises rather than stopping. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism. Gallbladder storage is unaffected, and micelles do not enter cells. After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimulated rather than stopped. Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a result of ileal loss. Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipids diffuse in. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption from short bowel or Crohn disease can cause fat malabsorption and bile acid diarrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: a smaller bile salt pool limits micelle formation and causes steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"After ileal resection, bile acid synthesis is strongly stimulated rather than stopped.","C":"Deconjugation is a bacterial overgrowth mechanism, not a result of ileal loss.","E":"Micelles do not enter the intestinal cells; only their lipids diffuse in."}},{"id":"sdl27_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"By which intestinal level are most ingested lipids absorbed?","choices":{"A":"Mid-jejunum","B":"Terminal ileum","C":"Gastric antrum","D":"Proximal colon","E":"Rectosigmoid"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most dietary lipid is absorbed by the mid-jejunum, so the work of bile salts is done long before they reach the terminal ileum for reabsorption. The ileum recycles bile salts rather than absorbing most fat, and the stomach and colon absorb little lipid. The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of dietary lipid absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Digestion of lipids; Absorption of lipids). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 27 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: short bowel syndrome follows extensive small bowel resection and causes malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, short bowel syndrome). COMLEX: bile salts left in the lumen after lipid uptake travel on to the ileum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"The terminal ileum reabsorbs bile salts rather than most dietary fat.","D":"The proximal colon absorbs little lipid.","E":"The rectosigmoid absorbs little lipid.","C":"The gastric antrum contains G cells and mucous cells that secrete gastrin and mucus, and it is not a site of dietary lipid absorption."}},{"id":"sdl27_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells secrete intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Gastric chief cells","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells","C":"Gastric parietal cells","D":"Salivary gland cells","E":"Duodenal S cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Intrinsic factor is a glycoprotein secreted by gastric parietal cells, the same cells that secrete H+. Chief cells secrete pepsinogen, acinar cells secrete pancreatic enzymes, salivary glands secrete R proteins, and S cells secrete secretin. Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells. Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes. Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ and fatty acids in the lumen, not intrinsic factor. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoimmune destruction of parietal cells causes chronic gastritis and pernicious anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: after gastrectomy, oral B12 is not absorbed and B12 must be given by injection (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric chief cells secrete pepsinogen; intrinsic factor comes from parietal cells.","B":"Pancreatic acinar cells secrete digestive enzymes.","D":"Salivary juices supply R proteins, not intrinsic factor.","E":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin in response to H+ and fatty acids in the lumen, not intrinsic factor."}},{"id":"sdl27_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"Free vitamin B12 released in the stomach first binds to which carrier?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"R protein","C":"Transferrin","D":"Apoferritin","E":"Calbindin"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Free B12 binds R proteins from salivary secretions. In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins and B12 moves to intrinsic factor. Transferrin and apoferritin carry iron, and calbindin binds calcium. Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum, after pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins it first binds. Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed iron, not a vitamin B12 binder. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does not carry vitamin B12. Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protein of intestinal cells, not a vitamin B12 carrier. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency can impair B12 absorption as well as fat absorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: most water-soluble vitamins use sodium-dependent cotransport, unlike B12 (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin B12 moves onto intrinsic factor only in the duodenum, after pancreatic proteases degrade the R proteins it first binds.","C":"Transferrin is the plasma β-globulin that carries absorbed iron, not a vitamin B12 binder.","D":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it does not carry vitamin B12.","E":"Calbindin D-28K is the vitamin D-induced Ca²⁺-binding protein of intestinal cells, not a vitamin B12 carrier."}},{"id":"sdl27_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":1,"stem":"What event in the duodenum allows vitamin B12 to bind intrinsic factor?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic proteases","B":"Gastric hydrochloric acid","C":"Conjugated bile salts","D":"Brush-border lactase","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In the duodenum, pancreatic proteases degrade R proteins, so B12 transfers to intrinsic factor. The B12-intrinsic factor complex resists these proteases and travels to the ileum. Acid and pepsin act earlier in the stomach, and bile salts, lactase, and amylase do not free B12. Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its transfer to intrinsic factor requires pancreatic proteases degrading the R proteins in the duodenum. Bile salts do not free B12. Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer requires protease degradation of R proteins. Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancreatic protease degradation of R proteins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Water-soluble vitamins). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia with neurologic findings (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 3 → Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Gastric pepsin releases vitamin B12 from food, but its transfer to intrinsic factor requires pancreatic proteases degrading the R proteins in the duodenum.","C":"Bile salts do not free B12.","D":"Lactase is a brush-border carbohydrate enzyme; B12 transfer requires protease degradation of R proteins.","E":"Salivary amylase digests starch; B12 transfer requires pancreatic protease degradation of R proteins."}},{"id":"sdl27_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption mainly by inducing which protein?","choices":{"A":"Apoferritin storage protein","B":"Transferrin carrier","C":"Pancreatic colipase","D":"Calbindin D-28K","E":"Intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol induces the vitamin D-dependent calcium-binding protein calbindin D-28K in enterocytes, which promotes calcium absorption. Apoferritin and transferrin handle iron, colipase helps lipase, and intrinsic factor binds B12. Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is not the vitamin D-induced calcium-binding protein. Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the protein that vitamin D induces for calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed for ileal vitamin B12 absorption, not for calcium. Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the vitamin D-induced protein. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inadequate calcium absorption causes rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: 25-hydroxycholecalciferol is the main circulating but inactive form of vitamin D (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Apoferritin binds free iron inside intestinal cells; it is not the vitamin D-induced calcium-binding protein.","B":"Transferrin is the plasma carrier of absorbed iron, not the protein that vitamin D induces for calcium absorption.","E":"Intrinsic factor is the parietal-cell glycoprotein needed for ileal vitamin B12 absorption, not for calcium.","C":"Colipase displaces bile salts to help lipase; it is not the vitamin D-induced protein."}},{"id":"sdl27_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How do absorbed vitamins A, D, E, and K leave the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Bound to albumin in portal blood","B":"By sodium cotransport into blood","C":"In micelles across the basal membrane","D":"Bound to transferrin in portal blood","E":"In chylomicrons into lymph"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat-soluble vitamins are handled like dietary lipids. They travel in micelles, diffuse into the cell, are incorporated into chylomicrons, and are extruded into lymph. Sodium cotransport handles most water-soluble vitamins, micelles do not enter the cell, and transferrin carries iron. Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bound to albumin in portal blood. Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamins, not vitamins A, D, E, and K. Micelles do not enter the cell. Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble vitamins. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat inhibits gastric and pancreatic lipase and decreases absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: vitamin K deficiency prolongs the prothrombin time (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Fat-soluble vitamins leave in chylomicrons via lymph, not bound to albumin in portal blood.","B":"Na+-dependent cotransport absorbs most water-soluble vitamins, not vitamins A, D, E, and K.","C":"Micelles do not enter the cell.","D":"Transferrin carries iron in the circulation, not fat-soluble vitamins."}},{"id":"sdl27_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":1,"stem":"How is heme iron converted to free iron inside the enterocyte?","choices":{"A":"Digestion by pancreatic proteases","B":"Digestion by lysosomal enzymes","C":"Hydrolysis by brush-border peptidases","D":"Cleavage by gastric pepsin","E":"Release by bile salt micelles"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heme iron crosses the apical membrane intact. Inside the enterocyte, lysosomal enzymes digest heme and release free iron, which binds apoferritin and then crosses the basolateral membrane. The other enzymes act in the lumen, and micelles carry lipids. Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not luminal pancreatic proteases. Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not by brush-border peptidases. Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes, not by gastric pepsin. Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Vitamins; Calcium; Iron). Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis increases intestinal iron absorption through low hepcidin (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: transferrin carries absorbed iron to the liver and bone marrow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — enterocyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heme crosses the apical membrane intact and is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not luminal pancreatic proteases.","C":"Heme is digested inside the cell by lysosomal enzymes, not by brush-border peptidases.","D":"Heme is digested inside the enterocyte by lysosomal enzymes, not by gastric pepsin.","E":"Micelles carry lipid digestion products, not heme iron."}},{"id":"sdl27_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman starts a weight-loss drug that inhibits the enzymes splitting triglyceride in the stomach and duodenum. She has never had pancreatitis. One month later she has oily, foul-smelling stools and a soft abdomen. Stool fat is 14 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day). Which digestive step is most directly impaired?","choices":{"A":"Hydrolysis of dietary triglyceride","B":"Exocytosis of chylomicrons into lymph","C":"Synthesis of apolipoprotein B in enterocytes","D":"Reabsorption of bile salts in the ileum","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin enzyme"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Blocking lipase leaves dietary triglyceride unhydrolyzed, so fat appears in the stool. A normal abdomen and no pancreatic history keep the defect at that luminal hydrolysis step. Chylomicron export into lymph happens only after triglyceride has already been split and absorbed. Apoprotein synthesis inside enterocytes is downstream of the hydrolysis this drug blocks. Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is not the enzyme this weight-loss drug inhibits. Activation of procolipase by trypsin is a separate step and is not the drug target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: orlistat causes steatorrhea and decreased absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (First Aid Gastrointestinal, orlistat). COMLEX: lingual and gastric lipases normally digest only about 10 percent of triglyceride (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Chylomicron export into lymph happens only after triglyceride has","C":"Apoprotein synthesis inside enterocytes is downstream of the hydrolysis","D":"Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is not the enzyme this weight-loss drug","E":"Activation of procolipase by trypsin is a separate step"}},{"id":"sdl27_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":5,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has recurrent duodenal ulcers, diarrhea, and greasy stools. Serum alkaline phosphatase is 78 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L), and ultrasound shows no biliary dilation. The abdomen is soft, with mild epigastric tenderness. Fasting gastrin is 1,200 pg/mL (reference range: <100 pg/mL), duodenal aspirate pH is 3 (reference range: 6–7), and measured exocrine enzyme output is normal. Which mechanism best explains the fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Loss of hepatic bile salt secretion","B":"Defective chylomicron assembly in enterocytes","C":"Excess colipase release from acini","D":"Reduced ileal absorptive surface","E":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Excess gastrin keeps duodenal pH near 3, and that acid inactivates pancreatic lipase even though enzyme output is normal. Ulcers plus a gastrin of 1,200 pg/mL identify the acid load. Hepatic bile-salt secretion is intact, and the problem is the low duodenal pH. Enterocytes assemble chylomicrons normally once lipid products are formed. Colipase helps lipase work and is not being released in excess as the cause. The ileal surface is intact, and enzyme output was measured as normal.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Zollinger-Ellison syndrome is a gastrin-secreting tumor, often of the pancreas or duodenum (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: a proton pump inhibitor reduces the H+ load delivered to the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 5 — Zollinger-Ellison syndrome and gastrin","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic bile-salt secretion is intact, and the problem is","B":"Enterocytes assemble chylomicrons normally once lipid products are","C":"Colipase helps lipase work and is not being released","D":"The ileal surface is intact, and enzyme output was"}},{"id":"sdl27_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has painless jaundice, dark urine, pale bulky stools, and a 6 kg weight loss. He has never had pancreatitis. The abdomen is soft, and the gallbladder is palpable and nontender. A mass blocks the common bile duct. Total bilirubin is 9.2 mg/dL (reference range: 0.2–1.2 mg/dL), CA 19-9 is 240 U/mL (reference range: <37 U/mL), and pancreatic enzyme secretion is normal. Which mechanism best explains the steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of pancreatic lipase activity in the duodenal lumen","B":"Failure of trypsin to activate procolipase","C":"Failure of emulsification and micelle formation","D":"Defective apoprotein synthesis in enterocytes","E":"Obstruction of intestinal lymphatic drainage"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Bile salts fail to form micelles because bile never reaches the duodenum, while pancreatic enzyme secretion stays normal. Jaundice, a bile-duct mass, and an elevated CA 19-9 mark that block. Pancreatic lipase output was measured as normal, so lipase activity is not the missing piece. Trypsin is still available to activate procolipase, because enzyme secretion is intact. Enterocyte apoprotein synthesis is not interrupted by a bile-duct mass. Intestinal lymphatics are open, and the missing factor is bile, not lymph flow.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pale stools and dark urine point to obstructed bile flow with conjugated hyperbilirubinemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal). COMLEX: bile salts are amphipathic, which lets them line micelles in the aqueous lumen (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 4 — bile formation and micelles","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase output was measured as normal, so lipase","B":"Trypsin is still available to activate procolipase, because enzyme","D":"Enterocyte apoprotein synthesis is not interrupted by a bile-duct","E":"Intestinal lymphatics are open, and the missing factor is"}},{"id":"sdl27_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-month-old boy, born at term and breastfed, has poor growth and pale, foul-smelling stools. There is no jaundice. He is small, with generalized edema and a soft abdomen. The blood smear shows acanthocytes, fasting triglyceride is 8 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL), and duodenal enterocytes are filled with lipid droplets. Which defect best explains these findings?","choices":{"A":"Failure of micelle formation","B":"Inactive pancreatic lipase","C":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly","E":"Deficient brush-border lactase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterocytes full of lipid with acanthocytes and a triglyceride of 8 mg/dL mean chylomicrons cannot be assembled for export. Fat is absorbed into the cell and then trapped there. Micelles are forming, because lipid has already entered the enterocytes. Pancreatic lipase is working, or the cells would not be packed with re-esterified lipid. Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is intact in this mucosal assembly defect. Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from milk sugar, not lipid-laden enterocytes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia presents with acanthocytes, steatorrhea, and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: apoproteins cover about 20 percent of the chylomicron surface (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 5 — abetalipoproteinemia","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Micelles are forming, because lipid has already entered the","B":"Pancreatic lipase is working, or the cells would not","C":"Ileal bile-salt reabsorption is intact in this mucosal assembly","E":"Lactase deficiency causes osmotic diarrhea from milk sugar, not lipid-laden"}},{"id":"sdl27_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man develops a milky right pleural effusion five days after esophagectomy, once he resumes a regular diet. He is afebrile, and breath sounds are reduced at the right base. Pleural-fluid triglyceride is 420 mg/dL (reference range: <110 mg/dL). He is placed on medium-chain triglycerides in place of long-chain fats. Why does this diet reduce the leak?","choices":{"A":"Medium-chain fats enter portal blood directly","B":"Medium-chain fats bypass bile salt emulsification","C":"Medium-chain fats increase lacteal reabsorption","D":"Medium-chain fats block chylomicron uptake by tissues","E":"Medium-chain fats seal the thoracic duct wall"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Medium-chain fats are water soluble enough to enter portal blood directly, so less lipid travels through the injured thoracic duct. A milky effusion with a triglyceride of 420 mg/dL is chyle from that duct. Medium-chain fats still interact with bile salts, and bypassing emulsification is not why the leak falls. They do not increase lacteal uptake, which would send more fat into the injured duct. They do not block tissue uptake of chylomicrons as the reason the pleural leak slows. They do not seal the duct wall.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the thoracic duct carries intestinal lymph and chylomicrons into the left subclavian vein (Guyton Ch. 66). COMLEX: chylomicrons are too large to enter vascular capillaries (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus lacteals","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Medium-chain fats still interact with bile salts, and bypassing","C":"They do not increase lacteal uptake, which would send","D":"They do not block tissue uptake of chylomicrons as","E":"They do not seal the duct"}},{"id":"sdl27_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools. A sister has type 1 diabetes, and she eats wheat daily. She is thin, without jaundice. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 110 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. Duodenal biopsy shows villous atrophy, and pancreatic function is normal. Which mechanism best explains the impaired fat absorption?","choices":{"A":"Absent pancreatic lipase secretion into the duodenum","B":"Reduced mucosal surface for lipid diffusion","C":"Deficient apoprotein B synthesis in enterocytes","D":"Loss of bile salt conjugation","E":"Blocked lacteal drainage by tumor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villous atrophy shrinks the mucosal surface across which lipid products diffuse. A high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer, endomysial antibody, and normal pancreatic function place the defect on that lost surface. Pancreatic lipase secretion is normal, so enzyme absence is not the mechanism. Apoprotein B synthesis fails in a different disease, with lipid trapped in enterocytes rather than flat villi. Bile-salt conjugation is a hepatic step and is intact here. No lymphatic tumor is blocking lacteal drainage in this mucosal injury.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease mainly affects the distal duodenum and proximal jejunum and is linked to iron deficiency anemia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease gives an abnormal D-xylose test, unlike pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase secretion is normal, so enzyme absence is","C":"Apoprotein B synthesis fails in a different disease, with","D":"Bile-salt conjugation is a hepatic step and is intact","E":"No lymphatic tumor is blocking lacteal drainage in this mucosal"}},{"id":"sdl27_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman had 100 cm of terminal ileum removed for chronic stricturing ileitis with transmural inflammation. She now has greasy stools and flank pain. IgA ASCA is positive and p-ANCA is negative. Stone analysis shows calcium oxalate, and serum calcium is 9.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the kidney stone?","choices":{"A":"Increased colonic oxalate absorption","B":"Increased hepatic bile acid synthesis","C":"Decreased renal calcium filtration","D":"Increased intestinal calcium uptake","E":"Decreased colonic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loss of the terminal ileum lets unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium, so free oxalate is absorbed in the colon and forms a calcium oxalate stone. Positive ASCA with a negative p-ANCA fits the ileitis that required the resection. The liver does increase bile-acid synthesis after ileal loss, but that does not precipitate the oxalate stone. Renal calcium filtration is not reduced, and the serum calcium is normal. Intestinal calcium uptake is not increased; calcium is trapped in the lumen by fatty acids. Bacterial overgrowth is not decreased by this resection, and that is not the stone mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: fat malabsorption from ileal bile salt loss causes calcium to bind fat, so oxalate is absorbed and forms calcium oxalate stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: about 95 percent of secreted bile acids normally return to the liver (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"The liver does increase bile-acid synthesis after ileal loss,","C":"Renal calcium filtration is not reduced, and the serum","D":"Intestinal calcium uptake is not increased; calcium is trapped","E":"Bacterial overgrowth is not decreased by this resection, and"}},{"id":"sdl27_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had 30 cm of terminal ileum removed after a penetrating injury. He now has watery diarrhea several times a day without weight loss, and it improves when he takes a bile-acid binder. Stool fat is 6 g/day (reference range: <7 g/day), and serum albumin is 4.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Undigested lactose holding water in the lumen","B":"Gastrin-driven acid overload of the duodenum","C":"Bile acids stimulating colonic secretion","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum","E":"Loss of colonic surface for water uptake"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A limited ileal resection lets bile acids spill into the colon, where they stimulate secretion, and a binder stops the diarrhea. Normal stool fat shows this is not fat maldigestion. Lactose is not the unabsorbed solute, and the diarrhea responds to a bile-acid binder. Gastrin-driven acid is not produced by loss of a short ileal segment. Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum causes steatorrhea, and stool fat here is normal. The colonic surface is intact, and the stimulus is bile acid reaching it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ileal bile salt malabsorption can cause bile acid diarrhea as well as fat malabsorption (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile). COMLEX: longer ileal resections deplete the pool faster than the liver can replace it, adding steatorrhea (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactose is not the unabsorbed solute, and the diarrhea","B":"Gastrin-driven acid is not produced by loss of a","D":"Bacterial deconjugation in the jejunum causes steatorrhea, and stool","E":"The colonic surface is intact, and the stimulus is"}},{"id":"sdl27_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":5,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has bloating, weight loss, and greasy stools years after surgery that left a blind loop of small bowel. The abdomen is mildly distended. A hydrogen breath test rises soon after glucose, and vitamin B12 is 160 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL). Which mechanism best explains the fat malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Acid inactivation of pancreatic lipase","B":"Loss of the ileal bile salt carrier","C":"Failure of chylomicron export to lymph","D":"Bacterial deconjugation of bile salts","E":"Autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bacteria in the blind loop strip glycine and taurine from bile salts, so the salts are absorbed too early and micelles fail. An early hydrogen rise and a low B12 are the bacterial clues. Acid inactivation of lipase is the gastrinoma mechanism, and there is no ulcer history. The ileal bile-salt carrier itself is present; bacteria are destroying the salts. Chylomicron export is intact once micelles deliver lipid to the mucosa. Intrinsic factor is not lost in a blind-loop syndrome, though bacteria consume B12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bacterial overgrowth can cause B12 deficiency because bacteria consume the vitamin (Guyton Ch. 72). COMLEX: low luminal pH also promotes early bile acid absorption by converting bile acids to the nonionized form (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Acid inactivation of lipase is the gastrinoma mechanism, and","B":"The ileal bile-salt carrier itself is present; bacteria are","C":"Chylomicron export is intact once micelles deliver lipid to the","E":"Intrinsic factor is not lost in a blind-loop syndrome,"}},{"id":"sdl27_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has fatigue and numbness in both feet. She eats a mixed diet and has had no ileal surgery. She is pale, and vibration sense is reduced at the ankles. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and antibodies to parietal cells are positive. Which route of vitamin B12 replacement is most reliable?","choices":{"A":"Oral tablets taken with food","B":"Oral tablets with antacid","C":"Sublingual tablets at bedtime","D":"Oral liquid with pancreatic enzymes","E":"Intramuscular injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Parietal-cell antibodies remove intrinsic factor, so B12 taken by mouth is not absorbed and must be injected. A macrocytic anemia with numb feet and those antibodies defines the need for that route. Oral tablets with food still require intrinsic factor, which she no longer makes. An antacid does not replace the missing intrinsic factor. A sublingual tablet is still an enteral dose and is not reliable without intrinsic factor. Pancreatic enzymes do not restore gastric intrinsic factor.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pernicious anemia reflects autoimmune loss of intrinsic factor from parietal cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: B12 deficiency also causes neurologic findings, which folate does not correct (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral tablets with food still require intrinsic factor, which","B":"An antacid does not replace the missing intrinsic","C":"A sublingual tablet is still an enteral dose and","D":"Pancreatic enzymes do not restore gastric intrinsic"}},{"id":"sdl27_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum removed four years ago for chronic transmural ileitis. He now has fatigue and paresthesias and has taken no B12. He is pale, ankle vibration is reduced, and a gastric biopsy is normal. Mean corpuscular volume is 114 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), serum B12 is 110 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), IgA ASCA is positive, and p-ANCA is negative. Which step of B12 absorption is lost?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin","B":"Ileal uptake of the B12-intrinsic factor complex","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins","D":"Gastric secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","E":"Protease cleavage of R proteins in the duodenum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The resected ileum is where the B12-intrinsic factor complex is taken up, and that step is gone. A normal gastric biopsy and positive ASCA keep the defect at the ileum rather than at intrinsic factor. Pepsin release of B12 from food is a gastric step, and the stomach was normal. Binding of free B12 to R proteins still occurs in the stomach. Parietal cells still secrete intrinsic factor, as the gastric biopsy is normal. Proteases still cleave R proteins in the duodenum, which was not resected.","boardPrep":"Step 1: intrinsic factor is needed for B12 uptake in the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, secretory products). COMLEX: body B12 stores last years, so deficiency appears long after resection (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — parietal cells and intrinsic factor","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pepsin release of B12 from food is a gastric","C":"Binding of free B12 to R proteins still occurs","D":"Parietal cells still secrete intrinsic factor, as the gastric","E":"Proteases still cleave R proteins in the duodenum, which"}},{"id":"sdl27_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with years of heavy alcohol use has greasy stools and weight loss. He has had no gastric surgery and is not taking enzyme capsules. He is thin, with mild epigastric tenderness. Mean corpuscular volume is 106 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), serum B12 is 170 pg/mL (reference range: 200–900 pg/mL), and a gastric biopsy is normal. Which step of B12 absorption is most likely impaired?","choices":{"A":"Release of B12 from food in the stomach","B":"Secretion of intrinsic factor by parietal cells","C":"Uptake of the complex in the terminal ileum","D":"Degradation of R proteins in the duodenum","E":"Binding of B12 to transferrin in plasma"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Pancreatic proteases normally degrade R proteins so B12 can transfer to intrinsic factor, and that duodenal step fails when enzyme output is lost. A normal gastric biopsy excludes loss of intrinsic factor. Release of B12 from food by pepsin is a gastric step, and the stomach is normal. Parietal cells are present on the normal gastric biopsy. The terminal ileum is intact and would take up the complex if it were formed. Transferrin carries iron in plasma and does not bind B12.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption of fat, fat-soluble vitamins, and vitamin B12 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal D-xylose test because the mucosa is intact (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 1 — pancreatic digestive enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Release of B12 from food by pepsin is a","B":"Parietal cells are present on the normal gastric","C":"The terminal ileum is intact and would take up","E":"Transferrin carries iron in plasma and does not bind"}},{"id":"sdl27_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 62-year-old man on hemodialysis has bone pain and muscle cramps. He eats a normal diet and has had no intestinal surgery. The shins are tender. Serum calcium is 7.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal, and creatinine is 8.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the low intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Deficient hepatic 25-hydroxylation","B":"Deficient renal 1α-hydroxylation","C":"Excess intestinal transferrin synthesis","D":"Loss of intrinsic factor secretion","E":"Impaired chylomicron formation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Failed kidneys cannot perform 1-alpha hydroxylation, so active vitamin D is low even though 25-hydroxyvitamin D is normal. Bone pain on hemodialysis with a creatinine of 8.4 mg/dL localizes the block to the kidney. Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact, because that metabolite is normal. Intestinal transferrin synthesis does not control calcium absorption. Intrinsic factor is for B12, not for calcium. Chylomicron formation is unrelated to this renal vitamin D step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in adults, inadequate calcium absorption causes osteomalacia (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: active vitamin D acts on intestine, kidney, and bone (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — calcium absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatic 25-hydroxylation is intact, because that metabolite is","C":"Intestinal transferrin synthesis does not control calcium","D":"Intrinsic factor is for B12, not for","E":"Chylomicron formation is unrelated to this renal vitamin D"}},{"id":"sdl27_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has fatigue, joint pain, and darkening skin. He has never received a transfusion. The liver is enlarged and the skin is bronze. Transferrin saturation is 78% (reference range: 20–50%), ferritin is 1,900 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL), and he is homozygous for an HFE mutation. Which change best explains the iron overload?","choices":{"A":"High hepcidin increasing iron absorption","B":"Loss of lysosomal heme digestion","C":"Excess intrinsic factor secretion","D":"Excess vitamin D-induced calbindin","E":"Low hepcidin increasing iron absorption"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The HFE mutation lowers hepcidin, and low hepcidin lets intestinal iron absorption run unchecked. Bronze skin, a saturation of 78%, and a ferritin of 1,900 ng/mL are the result. High hepcidin would decrease iron absorption, which is the opposite of this overload. Lysosomal heme digestion is not the step driven by the HFE mutation. Intrinsic factor does not regulate iron absorption. Calbindin is a vitamin D-related calcium protein and does not cause this iron load.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hereditary hemochromatosis is linked to HFE mutation on chromosome 6 (First Aid Hematology, hemochromatosis). COMLEX: iron enters enterocytes as Fe2+ or as heme iron (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — iron absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High hepcidin would decrease iron absorption, which is the","B":"Lysosomal heme digestion is not the step driven by","C":"Intrinsic factor does not regulate iron","D":"Calbindin is a vitamin D-related calcium protein and does"}},{"id":"sdl27_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl with recurrent respiratory infections and thick airway secretions has stopped her pancreatic enzyme capsules. She now bruises easily and has greasy stools. She is not taking warfarin. Scattered ecchymoses are present. The prothrombin time is 19 seconds (reference range: 11–13.5 seconds), and the platelet count is 250,000/µL (reference range: 150,000–400,000/µL). Deficiency of which vitamin best explains the bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin C","B":"Vitamin B12","C":"Vitamin K","D":"Vitamin B1","E":"Folic acid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Missing pancreatic enzymes cause fat maldigestion and loss of vitamin K, which prolongs the prothrombin time with a normal platelet count. Easy bruising after she stopped the capsules fits that fat-soluble vitamin. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not prolong the prothrombin time. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia and neuropathy, not this coagulopathy. Vitamin B1 deficiency causes beriberi and is not the cause of the long prothrombin time. Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and does not drop the vitamin K-dependent factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency from cystic fibrosis causes malabsorption of vitamins A, D, E, and K (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: vitamin K is needed to make clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X (Guyton Ch. 72).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 4 — malabsorption and pancreatic insufficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not prolong","B":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia and neuropathy, not this","D":"Vitamin B1 deficiency causes beriberi and is not the","E":"Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia and does not drop"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-month-old girl has diarrhea, poor weight gain, and edema. She was born at term and has had no respiratory infection. Duodenal fluid after a feed contains normal lipase and bile salts, but trypsin activity is almost absent because of a congenital brush-border enzyme defect. Serum albumin is 2.1 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL), and the stools contain excess undigested triglyceride. Which step most directly explains the failed lipolysis?","choices":{"A":"Lipase is secreted as an inactive zymogen","B":"Bile salts cannot emulsify fat without trypsin","C":"Lingual lipase is destroyed in the duodenum","D":"Procolipase is not activated to colipase","E":"Micelles fail to form without phospholipase"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Without enterokinase, trypsinogen is not activated, and trypsin is what converts procolipase to colipase. Normal lipase protein with absent trypsin and undigested triglyceride localizes the failure to that activation. Pancreatic lipase is secreted already active, so it is not waiting as a zymogen. Bile salts are present and can emulsify fat, but they are not a substitute for trypsin. Lingual lipase is not the enzyme destroyed by this brush-border defect. Micelles can still form, and the missing piece is active colipase.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colipase is secreted as procolipase, activated by trypsin, and displaces bile salts so pancreatic lipase can act (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Defects that block trypsin activation impair both protein and fat digestion.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase is secreted already active, so it is","B":"Bile salts are present and can emulsify fat, but","C":"Lingual lipase is not the enzyme destroyed by this brush-border","E":"Micelles can still form, and the missing piece is active"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the absorption and intracellular processing of lipids, including uptake from micelles, re-esterification, chylomicron formation, and transport through the lymphatic system.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old healthy man has blood drawn two hours after a large fatty breakfast. He takes no lipid-lowering drugs. Examination is normal. The plasma is milky, a fasting sample a week later is clear, and triglycerides in the fed sample are 280 mg/dL (fasting reference range: <150 mg/dL). By which route did the particles that clouded the plasma reach the blood?","choices":{"A":"Villus capillaries, then the portal vein","B":"Lacteals, then thoracic duct to veins","C":"Hepatic sinusoids after hepatocyte uptake","D":"Paracellular passage into submucosal veins","E":"Direct release into the azygos system"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Chylomicrons from the meal enter lacteals and reach the veins through the thoracic duct, which is why the plasma is milky only after the fatty breakfast. A clear fasting sample shows the particles are dietary, not a fasting lipoprotein. Villus capillaries and the portal vein carry medium-chain fats and portal nutrients, not these meal chylomicrons. Hepatic sinusoids are not the first stop for chylomicrons, which bypass the portal vein. Paracellular passage into submucosal veins is not how these large particles leave the mucosa. The azygos system is not the route that delivers chylomicrons to the blood.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Chylomicrons enter lacteals because they are too large for blood capillaries, then reach the blood through the thoracic duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Thoracic duct injury after chest surgery causes chylothorax, which worsens with long-chain dietary fat.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus capillaries and the portal vein carry medium-chain fats","C":"Hepatic sinusoids are not the first stop for chylomicrons,","D":"Paracellular passage into submucosal veins is not how these","E":"The azygos system is not the route that delivers"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old man had 120 cm of distal ileum removed three years ago for chronic transmural ileitis. He now has right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals. Ultrasound shows gallbladder stones without wall thickening. IgA ASCA is positive and p-ANCA is negative. Which mechanism links the surgery to the stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess estrogen raising biliary cholesterol","B":"Reduced hepatic 7α-hydroxylase activity","C":"Lost ileal reabsorption of bile acids","D":"Bacterial infection of the biliary tree","E":"Oxalate crystals forming in the gallbladder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Loss of ileal reabsorption spills bile acids, shrinks the bile-acid pool, and lets cholesterol precipitate as stones. Positive ASCA and a long distal resection identify the missing reabsorptive segment. Estrogen is not raised by an ileal resection. Hepatic 7-alpha hydroxylase increases when bile acids are lost, rather than falling. The biliary tree is not infected as the link between the resection and the stones. Oxalate stones form in the kidney after ileal loss, not in the gallbladder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ileal disease or resection interrupts bile acid recycling and raises gallstone risk; Robbins lists it under pigment stones, while First Aid lists Crohn disease under both cholesterol and pigment stones (Robbins Ch. 18; First Aid 2025 GI pathology). COMLEX: Patients after extensive ileal resection also need monitoring for vitamin B12 deficiency and kidney stones.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Estrogen is not raised by an ileal","B":"Hepatic 7-alpha hydroxylase increases when bile acids are lost,","D":"The biliary tree is not infected as the link","E":"Oxalate stones form in the kidney after ileal loss,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain vitamin B₁₂ absorption, including the roles of intrinsic factor and the terminal ileum, and predict the consequences of impaired absorption in pernicious anemia and terminal ileal disease or resection.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman had a total gastrectomy for a gastric mass seven years ago and has taken no supplements. She now has fatigue and numb feet. She is pale, and ankle vibration sense is reduced. Hemoglobin is 8.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), and the mean corpuscular volume is 112 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which serum finding would best distinguish this anemia from folate deficiency?","choices":{"A":"Elevated serum homocysteine","B":"Low serum ferritin level","C":"Elevated serum gastrin","D":"Elevated methylmalonic acid","E":"Low transferrin saturation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"After total gastrectomy she lacks intrinsic factor, and B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid while folate deficiency does not. Numb feet years after the operation point to that B12-specific metabolite. Homocysteine rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, so it does not separate them. Ferritin is an iron marker and is not how these two megaloblastic anemias are split. Gastrin may be high after gastric surgery but does not distinguish the anemia from folate deficiency. Transferrin saturation reports iron, not the B12-versus-folate distinction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vitamin B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and homocysteine and can cause subacute combined degeneration, whereas folate deficiency leaves methylmalonic acid normal (First Aid 2025 Biochemistry). COMLEX: Patients after total gastrectomy need lifelong parenteral vitamin B12.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Homocysteine rises in both B12 and folate deficiency, so","B":"Ferritin is an iron marker and is not how","C":"Gastrin may be high after gastric surgery but does","E":"Transferrin saturation reports iron, not the B12-versus-folate"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium, including the major factors required for their normal absorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 79-year-old woman who rarely leaves her apartment has bone pain and proximal weakness. She eats poorly and has no kidney disease. The thighs are tender. Serum calcium is 8.1 mg/dL (reference range: 8.5–10.2 mg/dL), 25-hydroxyvitamin D is very low, PTH is elevated, and creatinine is normal. Which change most directly reduces intestinal calcium absorption?","choices":{"A":"Lack of intrinsic factor for calcium uptake","B":"Failed renal 1α-hydroxylation of vitamin D","C":"Excess hepcidin blocking calcium export","D":"Calcium bound to transferrin in the gut","E":"Less vitamin D-induced calbindin synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Without vitamin D she makes less calbindin, so enterocytes absorb less calcium. A shut-in patient with a very low 25-hydroxyvitamin D, a high PTH, and a normal creatinine has dietary and sunlight deficiency rather than renal failure. Intrinsic factor is required for B12, not for calcium uptake. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylation is intact, because the creatinine is normal and the missing metabolite is the 25-hydroxy form. Hepcidin regulates iron export and does not block calcium. Transferrin binds iron, not calcium in the gut lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active vitamin D increases calcium absorption by inducing calbindin D-28K in intestinal cells (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Homebound older adults are at high risk for vitamin D deficiency and osteomalacia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrinsic factor is required for B12, not for calcium","B":"Renal 1-alpha hydroxylation is intact, because the creatinine is","C":"Hepcidin regulates iron export and does not block","D":"Transferrin binds iron, not calcium in the gut"}},{"id":"trial_sdl27_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":3,"stem":"A 57-year-old man had a distal gastrectomy with gastrojejunostomy for ulcer disease. He now has mild steatorrhea. Fecal elastase is normal. A gastric-emptying study shows fat leaving the remnant within minutes of a meal. Which mechanism best explains the steatorrhea?","choices":{"A":"Loss of gastric lipase removes most lipolysis","B":"Bacteria deconjugate the bile salts","C":"Excess acid inactivates pancreatic lipase","D":"Villous atrophy reduces lipid uptake","E":"Fat arrives faster than enzymes can digest it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Fat leaves the small gastric remnant so quickly that it reaches the intestine before pancreatic enzymes can digest it. Normal fecal elastase shows the pancreas can still make enzymes. Gastric lipase is a minor contributor, and its loss is not most of normal lipolysis. Bile salts are not being deconjugated, and there is no blind-loop history. Duodenal acid is reduced after a distal gastrectomy, not increased. The villi are intact, and emptying, not mucosal area, is the problem.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The stomach aids lipid digestion mainly by emptying slowly, a rate that CCK further slows when fat enters the duodenum (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Rapid gastric emptying after gastric surgery can cause diarrhea and mild malabsorption.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric lipase is a minor contributor, and its loss","B":"Bile salts are not being deconjugated, and there is","C":"Duodenal acid is reduced after a distal gastrectomy, not","D":"The villi are intact, and emptying, not mucosal area,"}},{"id":"grid_sdl27_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":4,"stem":"A 56-year-old man with a high LDL cholesterol starts a resin that binds bile acids in the intestinal lumen. He has no liver disease and has had no intestinal surgery. Months later he has mild bloating and a soft abdomen, without jaundice. LDL cholesterol was 182 mg/dL (reference range: <130 mg/dL) before treatment. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely after treatment begins?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal bile acids","Hepatic bile acid synthesis","Vitamin K absorption"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↔","↔","↓"],"E":["↓","↓","↑"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (↑ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) is correct. Bound bile acids escape ileal reabsorption and leave in stool, interrupting the enterohepatic circulation. The liver raises synthesis to replace the loss, and a smaller luminal bile acid pool reduces micelle formation, so absorption of fat-soluble vitamin K falls. Choice A (↓ fecal bile acids, ↑ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) has the fecal loss backward; a binder increases, not decreases, stool bile acids. Choice C (↑ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) ignores the hepatic response to bile acid loss, in which reduced FXR feedback derepresses synthesis. Choice D (↔ fecal bile acids, ↔ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↓ vitamin K absorption) fits a lipase inhibitor such as orlistat rather than a bile acid binder. Choice E (↓ fecal bile acids, ↓ hepatic bile acid synthesis, ↑ vitamin K absorption) reverses every effect, as if bile acids were supplemented. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Bile; Disorders of lipid digestion and absorption; Fat-soluble vitamins). The vitamin cell is inferred from micelle dependence; the sources do not grade it for this drug. Aligned with SDL 27 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: after ileal resection, hepatic synthesis rises but does not keep pace with fecal bile salt loss (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: bile acid loss in Crohn disease of the ileum can cause calcium oxalate kidney stones (First Aid Gastrointestinal, bile).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 5 — enterohepatic circulation of bile salts","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl27_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":1,"stem":"Bile salts returning in portal blood slow further bile acid synthesis by inhibiting which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase","B":"Pancreatic lipase","C":"Enterokinase","D":"Pepsin","E":"Salivary amylase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Bile salts reabsorbed in the terminal ileum return in portal blood and inhibit cholesterol 7α-hydroxylase, matching new synthesis to fecal loss. Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and is not the feedback target of returning bile salts. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen on the duodenal brush border. Pepsin is the gastric endopeptidase and is not regulated by portal bile salts. Salivary amylase starts starch digestion and is unrelated to bile acid synthesis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pancreatic lipase hydrolyzes triglyceride and is not the feedback target of returning bile salts","C":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen on the duodenal brush border","D":"Pepsin is the gastric endopeptidase and is not regulated by portal bile salts","E":"Salivary amylase starts starch digestion and is unrelated to bile acid synthesis"}},{"id":"sdl27_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the digestion and emulsification of dietary lipids, including the roles of bile salts, lecithin, pancreatic lipase, colipase, and micelle formation.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man has greasy stools and a 5 kg weight loss. He has no ulcer history and does not drink alcohol. The abdomen is soft, and there is no jaundice. Fecal elastase is 80 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g) and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Triglyceride digestion is only partly reduced, but cholesterol esters in the lumen stay intact. Which enzyme is missing?","choices":{"A":"Salivary amylase","B":"Cholesterol ester hydrolase","C":"Pepsin","D":"Enterokinase","E":"Gastric intrinsic factor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pancreatic cholesterol ester hydrolase splits cholesterol esters into free cholesterol and fatty acids. Salivary amylase digests starch and does not hydrolyze cholesterol esters. Pepsin is a gastric protease and does not cleave cholesterol esters. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and is not the enzyme that frees cholesterol from its esters. Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and is not a lipase.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Salivary amylase digests starch and does not hydrolyze cholesterol esters","C":"Pepsin is a gastric protease and does not cleave cholesterol esters","D":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen and is not the enzyme that frees cholesterol from its esters","E":"Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12 and is not a lipase"}},{"id":"sdl27_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman had 80 cm of terminal ileum removed for stricturing disease with transmural inflammation. She now has watery diarrhea after meals and pale stools. IgA anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibody is positive. Serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 3.3 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Which apical transporter was removed with that bowel?","choices":{"A":"Epithelial sodium channel of the colon","B":"GLUT5 fructose carrier","C":"Ferroportin on the basolateral membrane of the duodenum","D":"The sodium–bile salt cotransporter","E":"PepT1 for dipeptides"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Conjugated bile salts are reabsorbed in the terminal ileum by the apical sodium–bile salt cotransporter. The epithelial sodium channel is the colonic route for sodium, not the ileal bile-salt carrier. GLUT5 carries fructose across the brush border and does not reclaim bile salts. Ferroportin exports iron from duodenal enterocytes. PepT1 carries dipeptides and tripeptides, not conjugated bile salts.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The epithelial sodium channel is the colonic route for sodium, not the ileal bile-salt carrier","B":"GLUT5 carries fructose across the brush border and does not reclaim bile salts","C":"Ferroportin exports iron from duodenal enterocytes","E":"PepT1 carries dipeptides and tripeptides, not conjugated bile salts"}},{"id":"sdl27_q34","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the enterohepatic circulation of bile salts and predict the consequences of terminal ileal disease or resection, including bile salt loss, steatorrhea, and bile acid–induced secretory diarrhea.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 100 cm of terminal ileum removed and then developed watery postprandial diarrhea without fever. A bile acid–binding resin stops the watery stools, but the greasy stools continue. He is afebrile and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Why do the greasy stools persist on the resin?","choices":{"A":"The resin increases pancreatic lipase secretion","B":"The resin restores the ileal vitamin B12 receptor","C":"The resin blocks gastric acid so pepsin stays active in the ileum","D":"The resin adds conjugated bile salts to the lumen","E":"The circulating bile salt pool is still too small for normal micelles"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A bile acid–binding resin neutralizes colonic bile acids and eases secretory diarrhea, but steatorrhea continues because the bile salt pool stays depleted. The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase output. The resin does not replace the ileal receptor for the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex. Pepsin is not kept active in the ileum by a bile acid resin, and that is not why fat malabsorption remains. The resin binds bile acids and does not replenish them.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase output","B":"The resin does not replace the ileal receptor for the intrinsic-factor–B12 complex","C":"Pepsin is not kept active in the ileum by a bile acid resin, and that is not why fat malabsorption remains","D":"The resin binds bile acids and does not replenish them"}}]},{"sdlNumber":28,"title":"SDL 28 — Physiology - Intestinal Fluid & Electrolyte Transport","questions":[{"id":"sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"Why can a disturbance of intestinal fluid absorption deplete body fluid so rapidly?","choices":{"A":"Diarrheal fluid is hypertonic to plasma","B":"Oral intake exceeds all gut secretions","C":"Normal feces hold liters of water","D":"Daily absorbed fluid nears ECF volume","E":"Kidneys fail to conserve sodium"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Each day the intestines absorb the fluid from the diet plus the much larger volume of salivary, gastric, pancreatic, biliary, and intestinal secretions, an amount almost equal to the entire extracellular fluid volume, so failed absorption threatens body water quickly. Intestinal fluid is absorbed isosmotically, and diarrheal fluid is not described as hypertonic to plasma. Oral intake is the smaller share; gut secretions supply most of the fluid that enters the lumen. Normal feces carry a small volume of water, not liters. The kidneys respond to volume loss by activating the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and conserving sodium. Sources: Costanzo ch 8, intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The gut absorbs a fluid volume close to the whole ECF volume each day, mostly from secretions (Costanzo ch 8). COMLEX: Severe diarrhea calls for prompt oral or IV rehydration before shock develops.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intestinal fluid is absorbed isosmotically, and diarrheal","B":"Oral intake is the smaller share; gut","C":"Normal feces carry a small volume of","E":"The kidneys respond to volume loss by"}},{"id":"sdl28_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the osmolarity of the fluid absorbed by the intestine compared with plasma?","choices":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma","B":"Isosmotic to plasma","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma","D":"Variable with meal tonicity","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The absorbate is isosmotic: solute and water are absorbed in proportion, as in the renal proximal tubule. It is not hyposmotic or hyperosmotic, and it does not vary with meal tonicity or segment. The absorbate is isosmotic in every segment; the jejunum differs from the ileum and colon only in which solutes it absorbs. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: jejunal transport mechanisms resemble those of the early renal proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the small remaining volume of 100–200 mL is lost in feces each day (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyposmotic to plasma is a different tonicity.","C":"Hyperosmotic to plasma is a different tonicity.","D":"Variable with meal tonicity is a different relationship.","E":"Hyperosmotic in the jejunum is a different pattern."}},{"id":"sdl28_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first step in intestinal fluid absorption by villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Absorption of solute","B":"Absorption of water","C":"Secretion of chloride","D":"Secretion of potassium","E":"Secretion of mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Villus cells first absorb solute, and water follows osmotically so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Water does not move first. Chloride secretion is a crypt function, potassium secretion occurs in the colon, and mucus does not drive fluid uptake. Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption so the absorbate stays isosmotic. Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes. Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the colon, not as the first step in villus absorption. Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the villus enterocytes and does not drive absorption, which begins with solute uptake followed by water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal fluid and electrolyte transport; Intestinal absorption, Fig. 8.34). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the cell type determines direction, with villus cells absorbing and crypt cells secreting fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: disease that reduces absorptive surface area, such as small intestinal infection, reduces fluid absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — Na⁺-coupled solute absorption and Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Water absorption comes second, following solute absorption so the absorbate stays isosmotic.","C":"Chloride secretion is a crypt-cell function; villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes.","D":"Potassium secretion occurs through apical K+ channels in the colon, not as the first step in villus absorption.","E":"Mucus is secreted by goblet cells interspersed among the villus enterocytes and does not drive absorption, which begins with solute uptake followed by water."}},{"id":"sdl28_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"The jejunum mainly produces net absorption of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Potassium chloride","C":"Sodium bicarbonate","D":"Potassium bicarbonate","E":"Calcium chloride"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In the jejunum, H+ leaves on apical Na+-H+ exchange and HCO3- made by carbonic anhydrase is absorbed into blood, giving net NaHCO3 absorption. The ileum adds Cl--HCO3- exchange and absorbs net NaCl. The colon absorbs Na+ and secretes K+. Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels. Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum; H⁺ is exchanged for Na⁺, so the cation absorbed with HCO₃⁻ is sodium. Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- exchange is added; the jejunum absorbs NaHCO3. Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-induced calbindin, not the Na+-H+ exchange that gives jejunal NaHCO3 absorption. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the jejunum is the major site of Na+ absorption in the small intestine (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: jejunal Na+ also enters on Na+-glucose, Na+-galactose, and Na+-amino acid cotransporters (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Potassium chloride is not the jejunal absorbate; the colon handles K⁺, secreting it through apical K⁺ channels.","D":"Potassium bicarbonate is not absorbed in the jejunum.","A":"Net NaCl absorption is the ileal pattern, where Cl--HCO3- exchange is added; the jejunum absorbs NaHCO3.","E":"Ca2+ absorption in the small intestine depends on vitamin D-induced calbindin, not the Na+-H+ exchange that gives jejunal NaHCO3 absorption."}},{"id":"sdl28_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which hormone induces synthesis of apical Na⁺ channels in colonic epithelial cells?","choices":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone","B":"Natriuretic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Aldosterone","E":"Somatostatin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Like renal principal cells, colonocytes have apical Na+ and K+ channels, and aldosterone induces Na+ channel synthesis. This increases Na+ absorption and, secondarily, K+ secretion. ADH acts on renal water channels, ANP promotes natriuresis, and CCK and secretin regulate pancreatic and biliary secretion. Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels. CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; induction of colonic Na+ channel synthesis is the action of aldosterone. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic cells resemble the principal cells of the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: increased Na+ entry raises basolateral pumping, which drives K+ into the cell for secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Somatostatin from D cells is inhibitory, suppressing other GI hormones and gastric H+ secretion, and does not induce colonic Na+ channels.","C":"CCK contracts the gallbladder and stimulates pancreatic enzyme secretion; induction of colonic Na+ channel synthesis is the action of aldosterone."}},{"id":"sdl28_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":1,"stem":"Which apical exchanger is present in the ileum but not the jejunum?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"H⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The ileum has the jejunal mechanisms plus apical Cl--HCO3- exchange and a basolateral Cl- transporter. Combined with Na+-H+ exchange, this gives net NaCl absorption. Na+-H+ exchange and Na+-glucose cotransport are present in both segments. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is basolateral in crypt cells, and H+-K+ ATPase is gastric. Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum. Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, and the ileum contains the same mechanisms. The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells, not the ileal apical membrane. H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal cells that secretes H+ into the stomach, not an ileal anion exchanger. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Jejunum; Ileum; Colon, Figs. 8.35–8.36). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: in the ileum, HCO3- is secreted into the lumen rather than absorbed into blood (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the ileal basolateral membrane has a Cl- transporter instead of an HCO3- transporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — SGLT1 Na⁺ cotransport and basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Na+-H+ exchange is present in both the jejunum and the ileum","C":"Na+-glucose cotransport is on the jejunal apical membrane, and the ileum contains the same mechanisms.","D":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter is on the basolateral membrane of crypt cells, not the ileal apical membrane.","E":"H+-K+ ATPase is the apical proton pump of gastric parietal cells that secretes H+ into the stomach, not an ileal anion exchanger."}},{"id":"sdl28_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal cells secrete chloride and water into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus tip enterocytes","B":"Goblet mucus cells","C":"Paneth defensin cells","D":"Enteroendocrine I cells","E":"Crypt epithelial cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Epithelial cells lining the crypts secrete fluid and electrolytes, while cells lining the villi absorb them. Goblet cells secrete mucus, Paneth cells secrete defensins, and I cells secrete CCK. Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are the secretory population. Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus. I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in response to fatty acids and amino acids, not Cl- and water. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: crypts of Lieberkühn contain stem cells that replace enterocytes and goblet cells (First Aid Gastrointestinal, digestive tract histology). COMLEX: normally villus cells reabsorb what crypt cells secrete (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells absorb fluid and electrolytes; crypt cells are the secretory population.","B":"Goblet cells on the villi secrete mucus.","D":"I cells of the duodenal and jejunal mucosa secrete CCK in response to fatty acids and amino acids, not Cl- and water."}},{"id":"sdl28_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which basolateral transporter brings chloride from the blood into chloride-secreting intestinal cells?","choices":{"A":"Na⁺-glucose cotransporter","B":"Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchanger","C":"Apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger protein","D":"Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ cotransporter","E":"Basolateral K⁺ leak channel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crypt cells have a basolateral Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter like the one in the thick ascending limb. Cl- enters on it and exits through apical Cl- channels. Na+-glucose cotransport and Na+-H+ exchange are apical absorptive carriers, and Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal mechanism. The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells. Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of villus cells, not a basolateral Cl- entry route. Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, not the basolateral Cl- loader in secreting cells. Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carries no Cl-. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the same three-ion cotransporter in the thick ascending limb is the target of loop diuretics (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: the Na+-K+ ATPase on the basolateral membrane keeps intracellular Na+ low for the cotransporter (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"The basolateral K⁺ leak channel only recycles K⁺ that NKCC1 brings in; it is not the carrier that loads Cl⁻ into crypt cells.","A":"Na+-glucose cotransport is an apical absorptive carrier of villus cells, not a basolateral Cl- entry route.","B":"Cl--HCO3- exchange is an apical ileal absorptive mechanism, not the basolateral Cl- loader in secreting cells.","C":"Na+-H+ exchange is an apical absorptive transporter and carries no Cl-."}},{"id":"sdl28_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"How does sodium reach the lumen during intestinal chloride secretion?","choices":{"A":"Passively by the paracellular route","B":"Through apical Na⁺ channels into the lumen","C":"On the basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase pump","D":"By apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","E":"By vesicular transcytosis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cl- is secreted through apical channels, and Na+ follows passively by the paracellular route between cells. Water then follows the NaCl. Apical Na+ channels and Na+-H+ exchange move Na+ into cells, and the Na+-K+ ATPase is basolateral. The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane toward blood, not into the lumen. Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cell, the opposite direction. Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ from the lumen into the cell; during Cl- secretion Na+ reaches the lumen by moving between the cells. Costanzo states that in crypt Cl- secretion Na+ follows passively by moving between the cells, not by vesicles. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leaky small-intestinal tight junctions permit substantial paracellular movement (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon has high-resistance tight junctions that limit paracellular flow (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 1 — neurocrines including VIP","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The Na+-K+ ATPase pumps Na+ across the basolateral membrane toward blood, not into the lumen.","D":"Apical Na+-H+ exchange moves Na+ from the lumen into the cell, the opposite direction.","B":"Apical Na+ channels are a colonic feature that absorbs Na+ from the lumen into the cell; during Cl- secretion Na+ reaches the lumen by moving between the cells.","E":"Costanzo states that in crypt Cl- secretion Na+ follows passively by moving between the cells, not by vesicles."}},{"id":"sdl28_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Which covalent change does cholera toxin make to the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine phosphorylation","B":"Glycosylation","C":"Ubiquitination","D":"Methylation","E":"ADP-ribosylation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The A subunit of cholera toxin catalyzes ADP-ribosylation of the αs subunit of the Gs protein. The other modifications are not the toxin's action. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin ADP-ribosylates the αs subunit so Gs stays GTP-bound and adenylyl cyclase stays active (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts on Gs at the basolateral side (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"What is the direct effect of cholera toxin on the αs subunit of Gs?","choices":{"A":"Loss of its GTP binding","B":"Faster GDP rebinding","C":"Loss of its GTPase activity","D":"Faster receptor desensitizing","E":"Gain of Gi-type coupling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"ADP-ribosylation inhibits the GTPase activity of αs, so GTP stays bound and the subunit stays active. GTP binding is kept, not lost. Faster GDP rebinding would end the signal, and the toxin does not change receptor desensitization or coupling. GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the modified αs subunit. The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so no GDP rebinding occurs. Costanzo describes cholera toxin ADP-ribosylating αs so GTP stays bound and adenylyl cyclase is permanently activated, not switching to Gi coupling. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: with GTP permanently bound, cAMP stays high and apical Cl- channels stay open (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the secreted fluid volume can reach 10 to 12 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GTP binding is preserved; GTP stays permanently bound to the modified αs subunit.","B":"The toxin prevents GTP from being converted back to GDP, so no GDP rebinding occurs.","E":"Costanzo describes cholera toxin ADP-ribosylating αs so GTP stays bound and adenylyl cyclase is permanently activated, not switching to Gi coupling."}},{"id":"sdl28_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":1,"stem":"Why does cholera cause massive diarrhea despite intact villus cells?","choices":{"A":"Villus cells switch over to net fluid secretion","B":"Crypt secretion exceeds villus absorption","C":"Colonic water absorption is abolished","D":"Toxin destroys the villus epithelium","E":"Bacteria invade the submucosal layer"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholera stimulates crypt Cl- and fluid secretion so strongly that it overwhelms the absorptive capacity of villus cells and the colon. Villus cells keep absorbing, which is why glucose-coupled Na+ absorption still works. The toxin does not destroy the epithelium or cause invasion. Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply overwhelms their absorptive capacity. Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by the volume secreted into the lumen. Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells rather than destroying the villus epithelium, and the villar cells keep absorbing but are overwhelmed. Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts through cAMP, so the diarrhea comes from toxin-driven secretion, not bacterial invasion of the submucosa. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Intestinal secretion, Fig. 8.37); Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Vibrio cholerae and some Escherichia coli strains cause secretory diarrhea through toxins (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: the colon can reabsorb a maximum of about 6 to 8 L per day (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 2 — sodium-coupled glucose absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus cells keep absorbing; the crypt secretion simply overwhelms their absorptive capacity.","C":"Colonic absorption is not abolished; it is overwhelmed by the volume secreted into the lumen.","D":"Cholera toxin acts by ADP-ribosylating Gs inside crypt cells rather than destroying the villus epithelium, and the villar cells keep absorbing but are overwhelmed.","E":"Cholera toxin enters crypt cells across the apical membrane and acts through cAMP, so the diarrhea comes from toxin-driven secretion, not bacterial invasion of the submucosa."}},{"id":"sdl28_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"Which laxative class includes lactulose and polyethylene glycol?","choices":{"A":"Osmotic laxatives","B":"Stimulant laxatives","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives","D":"Emollient laxatives","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Osmotic laxatives provide a nonabsorbable osmotic load that draws water into the lumen. Lactulose and polyethylene glycol act that way. Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves, bulk-forming agents are soluble fibers, emollients lower stool surface tension, and opioid agents slow motility. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea); First Aid Gastrointestinal (anticonstipation drugs). Textbook enrichment (Costanzo) — detail not in SDL 28 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactulose also treats hepatic encephalopathy because gut bacteria acidify the colon and trap NH4+ (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: bacteria degrade some nonabsorbed solutes into more osmotically active particles (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"B":"Stimulant laxatives act on enteric nerves.","C":"Bulk-forming laxatives such as psyllium and methylcellulose are indigestible hydrophilic colloids that form a gel distending the colon, not lactulose or polyethylene glycol.","D":"Stool surfactants such as docusate and glycerin soften stool by letting water and lipids penetrate, and they do not include lactulose or polyethylene glycol.","E":"Opioid antidiarrheals such as loperamide slow colonic transit and increase fecal water absorption, the opposite of a laxative."}},{"id":"sdl28_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major cause of secretory diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border lactase deficiency","B":"Exocrine pancreatic insufficiency","C":"Nonabsorbable laxative ingestion","D":"Loss of absorptive mucosal area","E":"Enteropathic bacterial overgrowth"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Costanzo names overgrowth of enteropathic bacteria such as Vibrio cholerae or Escherichia coli as the major cause of secretory diarrhea. Lactase deficiency and laxatives cause osmotic diarrhea, pancreatic insufficiency causes malabsorption, and reduced mucosal area is a separate category. Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it retains water and causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea. Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea. Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is its own diarrhea category, separate from secretory diarrhea. Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes steatorrhea, whereas secretory diarrhea is excessive crypt fluid secretion, mostly from enteropathic bacteria. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: enteritis increases mucosal secretion and motility to flush the infectious agent (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactase deficiency leaves lactose in the lumen, where it retains water and causes osmotic, not secretory, diarrhea.","C":"Nonabsorbable solutes cause osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea.","D":"Loss of absorptive area from infection or inflammation is its own diarrhea category, separate from secretory diarrhea.","B":"Pancreatic insufficiency impairs fat digestion and causes steatorrhea, whereas secretory diarrhea is excessive crypt fluid secretion, mostly from enteropathic bacteria."}},{"id":"sdl28_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Diarrheal fluid is rich in which anion because intestinal secretions contain it?","choices":{"A":"Chloride ion","B":"Phosphate ion","C":"Bicarbonate","D":"Sulfate ion","E":"Lactate ion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Salivary, pancreatic, and intestinal juices have a high HCO3- content, so diarrheal fluid does too. Loss of HCO3- relative to Cl- causes hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate. Phosphate, sulfate, and lactate are not the named anion. Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why diarrhea causes hyperchloremic acidosis. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Diarrhea; Osmotic diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal fluid is also high in K+ from flow-dependent colonic secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — lactase deficiency","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Chloride is retained relative to bicarbonate, which is why diarrhea causes hyperchloremic acidosis."}},{"id":"sdl28_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old man has a high-output jejunostomy after extensive bowel resection. He drinks about 2 L a day. The stoma is pink, blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg, and the mucous membranes are dry. Stoma output is 3.5 L a day, and the blood urea nitrogen is 31 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). Which best explains why output can exceed what he drinks?","choices":{"A":"Upper gut secretions add to dietary fluid","B":"Excess colonic secretion of potassium-rich fluid","C":"Renal water loss into the bowel","D":"Bacterial fermentation of dietary starch","E":"Stoma mucosa secreting mucus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Saliva, gastric juice, bile, and pancreatic juice add a large volume to what he drinks, and a jejunostomy vents that fluid before the bowel can reabsorb it. An output of 3.5 L from 2 L of intake is that added secretion. The colon, which secretes some potassium-rich fluid, is downstream of a jejunostomy and is not the source of this excess. The kidneys are not pouring water into the bowel. Bacterial fermentation of starch produces gas, not liters of stoma fluid. Mucus from the stoma is a small volume and cannot account for an extra 1.5 L.","boardPrep":"Step 1: a high-output stoma can cause dehydration and prerenal azotemia because proximal secretions are not reabsorbed (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: diarrheal loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate-rich secretion adds to luminal fluid","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The colon, which secretes some potassium-rich fluid, is downstream","C":"The kidneys are not pouring water into the","D":"Bacterial fermentation of starch produces gas, not liters of stoma","E":"Mucus from the stoma is a small volume and"}},{"id":"sdl28_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man receives a hypertonic enteral feed directly into the jejunum and within the hour develops cramps and watery output. He has no fever. Serum sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which process best explains the early increase in luminal fluid?","choices":{"A":"Active water pumping by crypt epithelial cells","B":"Closure of jejunal tight junctions","C":"Water entry until contents are isosmotic","D":"Toxin-driven chloride channel opening","E":"Aldosterone-driven colonic secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Water enters the lumen until the hypertonic feed is brought to the osmolality of plasma. Cramps and watery output right after a hypertonic jejunal feed, with a normal serum sodium, are that osmotic equilibration. Crypt cells are not actively pumping water in response to a hypertonic meal. Jejunal tight junctions do not close and thereby add fluid. No toxin has opened chloride channels in this feeding complication. Aldosterone acts on the colon and does not cause this immediate jejunal response.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperosmotic luminal contents draw water into the lumen until they become isosmotic (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intestinal absorbate is isosmotic, much like proximal tubular fluid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 6 Obj 4 — duodenal osmolality and gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Crypt cells are not actively pumping water in response","B":"Jejunal tight junctions do not close and thereby add","D":"No toxin has opened chloride channels in this feeding","E":"Aldosterone acts on the colon and does not cause"}},{"id":"sdl28_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has an end ileostomy after colectomy for refractory colitis. The stoma is pink and blood pressure is 108/68 mm Hg. Effluent sodium stays near her plasma sodium of 140 mEq/L, whereas formed stool sodium is typically near 30 mEq/L. Which feature of the small intestine explains the failure to lower effluent sodium?","choices":{"A":"Absent basolateral Na⁺-K⁺ ATPase","B":"Resistance of enterocytes to aldosterone","C":"High colonic potassium secretion","D":"Bile salt-driven crypt secretion","E":"Leaky low-resistance tight junctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Small-bowel tight junctions are leaky and low-resistance, so the lumen cannot hold a large sodium gradient the way the colon can. Ileostomy fluid therefore leaves with a sodium close to plasma. The basolateral sodium pump is present and working in this healthy stoma. Enterocytes respond to aldosterone, but the colon that would finish sodium salvage is gone. High colonic potassium secretion is a colonic event and does not set ileostomy sodium. Bile-salt-driven secretion is not why a healthy ileostomy fails to lower sodium.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aldosterone increases colonic Na+ absorption through apical Na+ channels (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: loss of the colon removes the high-resistance segment that finishes salt and water absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — enterocyte apical and basolateral membranes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The basolateral sodium pump is present and working in","B":"Enterocytes respond to aldosterone, but the colon that would","C":"High colonic potassium secretion is a colonic event and","D":"Bile-salt-driven secretion is not why a healthy ileostomy fails"}},{"id":"sdl28_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":5,"stem":"A 71-year-old woman has three days of profuse watery diarrhea from norovirus. She is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. She is weak, the abdomen is soft, and blood pressure is 104/66 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which mechanism most directly explains the low potassium?","choices":{"A":"Renal K⁺ wasting from loop diuretics","B":"Shift of K⁺ into cells from alkalosis","C":"Reduced dietary potassium intake","D":"Flow-dependent colonic K⁺ secretion","E":"Crypt-cell K⁺ secretion via channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High flow through the colon increases potassium secretion, which is why the serum potassium has fallen to 2.8 mEq/L. Watery diarrhea without diuretics or vomiting points to that flow-dependent colonic loss. She is not taking a loop diuretic, so renal wasting from that drug is not the cause. There is no alkalosis described that would shift potassium into cells. Intake has not been the limiting factor over only 3 days of diarrhea. A separate crypt-cell potassium channel is not the flow-dependent colonic secretion that dominates here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hypokalemia can cause muscle weakness and flattened T waves (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: aldosterone also increases colonic K+ secretion through Na+ channel synthesis (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"She is not taking a loop diuretic, so renal","B":"There is no alkalosis described that would shift potassium into","C":"Intake has not been the limiting factor over only","E":"A separate crypt-cell potassium channel is not the flow-dependent"}},{"id":"sdl28_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 49-year-old man has hypertension and muscle cramps. He is not taking diuretics and has no diarrhea. Blood pressure is 168/100 mm Hg. Serum potassium is 2.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), plasma renin is suppressed, and CT shows an adrenal adenoma. Which change in his colonic epithelium is expected?","choices":{"A":"Reduced colonic K⁺ secretion","B":"Increased opening of crypt Cl⁻ channels by cAMP","C":"Increased Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange activity","D":"Decreased basolateral K⁺ uptake","E":"Increased apical Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The adenoma secretes aldosterone, which increases synthesis of apical sodium channels in the colon. Hypertension, a potassium of 2.8 mEq/L, and suppressed renin are that mineralocorticoid excess. Colonic potassium secretion increases with aldosterone, rather than falling. Crypt chloride channels opened by cyclic AMP are the secretory pathway, not this aldosterone effect. Chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the colonic change driven by this adenoma. Basolateral potassium uptake is not decreased by aldosterone.","boardPrep":"Step 1: primary hyperaldosteronism causes hypertension, hypokalemia, and low renin (First Aid Endocrine). COMLEX: the colonic transport pattern mirrors the late distal tubule and collecting duct (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — colonic electrolyte transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic potassium secretion increases with aldosterone, rather than","B":"Crypt chloride channels opened by cyclic AMP are the","C":"Chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the colonic change driven by this","D":"Basolateral potassium uptake is not decreased by"}},{"id":"sdl28_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman takes acetazolamide for glaucoma. She has no diarrhea. Serum bicarbonate is 19 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which jejunal transport step is most directly reduced?","choices":{"A":"Apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport by SGLT1 carriers","B":"Supply of H⁺ for apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchange","C":"Basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Apical Na⁺ channel insertion by aldosterone","E":"Paracellular water absorption"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Carbonic anhydrase supplies the hydrogen ion that the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger secretes, and inhibiting the enzyme cuts that supply. A bicarbonate of 19 mEq/L is the systemic marker of the same enzyme block. Sodium-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic anhydrase. Basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride uptake is a crypt secretory step and is not the jejunal step cut here. Aldosterone-driven sodium channels are colonic and are not the target of this glaucoma drug. Paracellular water absorption follows solute and is not the step the enzyme directly feeds.","boardPrep":"Step 1: carbonic anhydrase inhibitors cause metabolic acidosis by reducing proximal tubular HCO3- reabsorption (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: jejunal electrolyte transport is identical to that of the early proximal tubule (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — carbonic anhydrase and H⁺/HCO₃⁻ generation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Sodium-glucose cotransport does not depend on carbonic","C":"Basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride uptake is a crypt secretory step and","D":"Aldosterone-driven sodium channels are colonic and are not the","E":"Paracellular water absorption follows solute and is not the"}},{"id":"sdl28_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A newborn has abdominal distension and has not passed meconium. The anus is patent. Contrast enema shows a microcolon with inspissated meconium in the distal ileum. Sweat chloride is 78 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L). Which defect best explains the thick intestinal contents?","choices":{"A":"Impaired apical Cl⁻ secretion by crypt cells","B":"Excess Na⁺ absorption by colonic channels","C":"Loss of basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ uptake","D":"Absent Na⁺-glucose cotransport on villus enterocytes","E":"Overactive apical anion exchange"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A defective cystic fibrosis channel impairs apical chloride secretion by crypt cells, so the lumen stays dry and meconium inspissates. A sweat chloride of 78 mmol/L confirms that channel defect. Colonic sodium absorption is not excessive as the cause of this thick meconium. The basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter is intact, and the missing piece is the apical channel. Sodium-glucose cotransport on villus cells is preserved and is why oral rehydration still works in this disease. Anion exchange is not overactive, and the lumen is too dry rather than too alkaline from excess exchange.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cystic fibrosis causes exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes). COMLEX: Na+ follows secreted Cl- passively between cells, and water follows NaCl (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 14 Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Colonic sodium absorption is not excessive as the cause","C":"The basolateral sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter is intact, and the missing","D":"Sodium-glucose cotransport on villus cells is preserved and is","E":"Anion exchange is not overactive, and the lumen is"}},{"id":"sdl28_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman passes about 3 L of watery stool a day, and the diarrhea continues while she fasts. She is not taking laxatives. She is weak and the abdomen is soft. Serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), gastric acid output is low, and chromogranin A is 340 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). CT shows a mass in the pancreatic tail. Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Nonabsorbed solute holding water","B":"Bile acid stimulation of colonic epithelium","C":"Peptide-driven crypt-cell cAMP excess","D":"Mucosal invasion by bacteria","E":"Rapid transit from gastrin excess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A peptide from the pancreatic mass drives crypt-cell cyclic AMP, opening chloride secretion that continues during fasting. Low acid output, hypokalemia, and an elevated chromogranin A fit that neuroendocrine peptide. An unabsorbed solute would stop causing diarrhea once she is fasting, and this diarrhea continues. Bile acids are not the stimulus, and there is no ileal disease. There is no mucosal invasion or fever to support an invasive bacterial diarrhea. Gastrin excess would raise gastric acid, which is low here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIPoma causes watery diarrhea, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria (WDHA syndrome) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, VIP). COMLEX: octreotide is used to treat VIPoma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 19 Obj 5 — VIPoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed solute would stop causing diarrhea once she","B":"Bile acids are not the stimulus, and there is","D":"There is no mucosal invasion or fever to support","E":"Gastrin excess would raise gastric acid, which is low"}},{"id":"sdl28_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old farmworker has vomiting, drooling, pinpoint pupils, and profuse watery diarrhea after spraying an organophosphate. Heart rate is 48/min and bowel sounds are hyperactive. Plasma cholinesterase is 900 U/L (reference range: 3,000–8,000 U/L). Which mechanism best explains the increased fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Excess VIP release from a pancreatic islet tumor","B":"Excess acetylcholine at crypt cell receptors","C":"Toxin-mediated Gs activation in crypt cells","D":"Nonabsorbed pesticide holding water","E":"Loss of colonic Na⁺ channel synthesis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Organophosphate poisoning leaves excess acetylcholine at crypt-cell receptors, which drives secretion. Bradycardia, small pupils, drooling, and a cholinesterase of 900 U/L are the same cholinergic excess. A VIP-secreting islet tumor is not acquired by spraying a pesticide. Toxin-mediated activation of Gs is the cholera mechanism, not this cholinergic poisoning. The pesticide is not an unabsorbed osmotic solute holding water in the lumen. Colonic sodium-channel synthesis is an aldosterone effect and is not acutely increased here.","boardPrep":"Step 1: organophosphates irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase and cause a cholinergic toxidrome (First Aid Pharmacology). COMLEX: atropine blocks muscarinic effects and pralidoxime regenerates acetylcholinesterase (First Aid Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 4 Obj 5 — enteric nervous system and autonomic input","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A VIP-secreting islet tumor is not acquired by spraying a","C":"Toxin-mediated activation of Gs is the cholera mechanism, not","D":"The pesticide is not an unabsorbed osmotic solute holding","E":"Colonic sodium-channel synthesis is an aldosterone effect and is"}},{"id":"sdl28_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has profuse rice-water stools two days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 92/58 mm Hg and the pulse is 120/min. The abdomen is soft. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod, and serum potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Which intracellular messenger remains persistently high in his crypt cells?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cholera toxin keeps cyclic AMP high in crypt cells, so chloride channels stay open. Rice-water stools, hypotension, and a comma-shaped gram-negative rod identify that locked messenger. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of the heat-stable toxin of E coli, not of cholera toxin. Inositol trisphosphate is not the messenger cholera toxin holds high. Diacylglycerol is not the persistent messenger in these crypt cells. Nitric oxide is not the messenger that cholera toxin elevates.","boardPrep":"Step 1: cholera toxin causes secretory diarrhea by permanently activating Gs (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous replacement of fluid and electrolytes is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 7 Obj 4 — Gs/cAMP receptor signaling pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is the messenger of the heat-stable toxin","C":"Inositol trisphosphate is not the messenger cholera toxin holds","D":"Diacylglycerol is not the persistent messenger in these crypt","E":"Nitric oxide is not the messenger that cholera toxin"}},{"id":"sdl28_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman returns from Mexico with four days of watery diarrhea from toxigenic Escherichia coli. She is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. She is thirsty and the abdomen is soft. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), chloride is 114 mEq/L (reference range: 98–106 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorder is most likely?","choices":{"A":"High anion gap metabolic acidosis","B":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis","C":"Acute respiratory acidosis","D":"Hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis","E":"Acute respiratory alkalosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Loss of bicarbonate in diarrheal fluid leaves a hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis, with chloride 114 mEq/L and bicarbonate 15 mEq/L. A normal sodium and no vomiting exclude the alkalosis of gastric loss. The anion gap is not widened, because the fall in bicarbonate is matched by the rise in chloride. Hypochloremic alkalosis follows vomiting, and her chloride is high rather than low. There is no CO2 retention to support an acute respiratory acidosis. Respiratory alkalosis would lower PCO2 and is not described by this electrolyte pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea is a classic cause of normal anion gap metabolic acidosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Escherichia coli enterotoxins cause secretory diarrhea like cholera toxin (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The anion gap is not widened, because the fall","B":"Hypochloremic alkalosis follows vomiting, and her chloride is high","C":"There is no CO2 retention to support an acute respiratory","E":"Respiratory alkalosis would lower PCO2 and is not described"}},{"id":"sdl28_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy in a flood-relief camp has had 12 watery stools in a day. He has not vomited. He is lethargic, the eyes are sunken, the heart rate is 168/min, and blood pressure is 70/40 mm Hg. Capillary refill is 4 seconds, and serum sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which consequence of the diarrhea most directly threatens his life?","choices":{"A":"Hyperkalemic cardiac arrhythmia","B":"Loss of extracellular fluid volume","C":"Hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis from vomiting","D":"Bacteremic septic shock","E":"Hypernatremic seizures"},"correct":"B","explanation":"He has lost extracellular fluid volume, which is why the pressure is 70/40 mm Hg and the heart rate is 168/min. Sunken eyes and delayed refill are that volume loss, not a primary arrhythmia or alkalosis. Hyperkalemia is not the threat, and secretory diarrhea tends to lower potassium. He has not been vomiting, so a hypochloremic alkalosis is not the life-threatening piece. There is no fever or source to make bacteremic shock the direct consequence of the stool loss. The sodium is normal, so hypernatremic seizures are not the immediate threat.","boardPrep":"Step 1: without fluid replacement, up to 50 percent of patients with cholera die (Guyton Ch. 67). COMLEX: antibiotics can shorten the duration and volume of cholera diarrhea (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hyperkalemia is not the threat, and secretory diarrhea tends","C":"He has not been vomiting, so a hypochloremic alkalosis","D":"There is no fever or source to make bacteremic","E":"The sodium is normal, so hypernatremic seizures are not"}},{"id":"sdl28_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man starts milk of magnesia daily for constipation and develops watery diarrhea. The diarrhea stops within a day when he is kept fasting for a procedure. The abdomen is soft. Serum magnesium is 2.8 mg/dL (reference range: 1.7–2.2 mg/dL). Which mechanism best explains the diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt Cl⁻ secretion","B":"Toxin-driven Gs activation in crypt cells","C":"Inflammatory loss of villus absorptive surface","D":"Unabsorbed cations retaining luminal water","E":"Bile acid stimulation of colonocytes"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Unabsorbed magnesium holds water in the lumen, an osmotic diarrhea that stops when intake stops. A serum magnesium above the reference range shows the salt is being absorbed in part and still left in the lumen. Peptide-driven crypt secretion would continue during fasting, and this diarrhea stops. Gs activation by a toxin does not follow a magnesium laxative and would not stop so promptly. There is no villus injury or inflammatory diarrhea in this story. Bile acids are not the osmotic cation in milk of magnesia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic laxatives such as magnesium salts may be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, anticonstipation drugs). COMLEX: secretory diarrhea results from excess crypt-cell secretion rather than inadequate absorption (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 26 Obj 1 — lactase deficiency as osmotic diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peptide-driven crypt secretion would continue during fasting, and this diarrhea","B":"Gs activation by a toxin does not follow a","C":"There is no villus injury or inflammatory diarrhea in this","E":"Bile acids are not the osmotic cation in milk of"}},{"id":"sdl28_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"An 18-month-old girl has four days of vomiting and watery diarrhea during a rotavirus outbreak. She is not taking laxatives. Heart rate is 150/min and capillary refill is 3 seconds. A biopsy in a similar case shows blunted villi. Which category of diarrhea best describes the primary mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion","B":"Peptide hormone-driven secretion","C":"Nonabsorbable osmotic laxative load","D":"Aldosterone-driven colonic K⁺ loss","E":"Decreased absorptive surface area"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Blunted villi reduce the absorptive surface, so fluid that would have been absorbed stays in the lumen. An outbreak illness with that histologic pattern is loss of surface rather than a secretagogue. A toxin-driven crypt secretion is the cholera pattern and is not the villus lesion described. No peptide hormone is driving secretion in this viral illness. She is not taking a nonabsorbable osmotic laxative. Aldosterone-driven potassium loss is a consequence of volume depletion, not the primary diarrheal mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rotavirus is a leading cause of watery diarrhea in young children and is preventable by vaccine (First Aid Microbiology). COMLEX: viral injury to villus tips can cause secondary lactase deficiency (First Aid Gastrointestinal, malabsorption syndromes).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 23 Obj 2 — villus structure and brush border","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"A toxin-driven crypt secretion is the cholera pattern and","B":"No peptide hormone is driving secretion in this viral","C":"She is not taking a nonabsorbable osmotic","D":"Aldosterone-driven potassium loss is a consequence of volume depletion,"}},{"id":"sdl28_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman has had five days of profuse watery diarrhea and feels dizzy on standing. She is not taking diuretics. Blood pressure is 96/60 mm Hg, and the pulse rises when she stands. Urine sodium is 8 mEq/L (reference range: 40–220 mEq/L). Which compensatory response is most likely active?","choices":{"A":"Suppressed antidiuretic hormone release","B":"Increased atrial natriuretic peptide","C":"Activated renin-angiotensin-aldosterone","D":"Reduced sympathetic outflow","E":"Suppressed baroreceptor reflex activity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Volume loss has activated the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone axis, which is why the urine sodium is only 8 mEq/L. Dizziness, a pressure of 96/60 mm Hg, and a rise in pulse on standing are the volume stimulus. Antidiuretic hormone is released, not suppressed, when volume is low. Atrial natriuretic peptide falls when the atria are underfilled, rather than rising. Sympathetic outflow increases with hypotension and is not reduced. Baroreceptor reflexes are driving the tachycardia and are not suppressed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: low urine sodium in hypovolemia reflects avid renal sodium retention (First Aid Renal). COMLEX: compensation fails if diarrheal fluid loss is too large or too rapid (Costanzo Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Antidiuretic hormone is released, not suppressed, when volume is","B":"Atrial natriuretic peptide falls when the atria are underfilled,","D":"Sympathetic outflow increases with hypotension and is not","E":"Baroreceptor reflexes are driving the tachycardia and are not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A healthy 29-year-old volunteer has her jejunum perfused with two solutions, each isosmotic to plasma. She has no diarrhea. Solution 1 contains sodium chloride and glucose and produces net water absorption. Solution 2 replaces those solutes with mannitol, which is not absorbed, and net water absorption falls to about zero. Which principle best explains the difference?","choices":{"A":"Mannitol closes apical water channels","B":"Water absorption follows absorbed solute","C":"Glucose itself carries water molecules","D":"Isotonic fluid moves by hydrostatic force","E":"Plasma osmolality falls during perfusion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Water absorption follows the solute that the mucosa absorbs, and mannitol is not absorbed, so water stays in the lumen. Both solutions start isosmotic, which removes a simple osmotic gradient as the explanation. Mannitol does not close apical water channels. Glucose does not itself carry water molecules across the membrane. Hydrostatic force is not what differs between these two isosmotic perfusions. Plasma osmolality does not fall during the short perfusion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intestinal fluid absorption is isosmotic, with solute absorbed first and water following (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Nonabsorbable solutes such as mannitol or lactulose retain water in the lumen and cause osmotic diarrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mannitol does not close apical water","C":"Glucose does not itself carry water molecules across the","D":"Hydrostatic force is not what differs between these two isosmotic","E":"Plasma osmolality does not fall during the short"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure starts high-dose spironolactone. He has no diarrhea. Weeks later he has mild weakness. Serum potassium is 5.8 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). Besides the kidney, which intestinal change is expected?","choices":{"A":"Less jejunal Na⁺-glucose cotransport","B":"Less ileal apical Cl⁻-HCO₃⁻ exchange","C":"Less colonic Na⁺ uptake, K⁺ secretion","D":"More crypt Cl⁻ secretion through CFTR","E":"More apical jejunal Na⁺-H⁺ exchange"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Spironolactone blocks aldosterone, so the colon takes up less sodium and secretes less potassium. A potassium of 5.8 mEq/L is the renal result, and the colon changes in the same direction. Jejunal sodium-glucose cotransport is not an aldosterone effect. Ileal chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the transport this drug reduces. Crypt chloride secretion through the apical anion channel is not increased by aldosterone blockade. Apical jejunal sodium-hydrogen exchange is not the aldosterone-dependent step.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The colon resembles the renal principal cell, with aldosterone-induced apical Na⁺ channels that drive Na⁺ absorption and K⁺ secretion (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Aldosterone antagonists cause hyperkalemia, so potassium must be monitored.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 2 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Jejunal sodium-glucose cotransport is not an aldosterone","B":"Ileal chloride-bicarbonate exchange is not the transport this drug","D":"Crypt chloride secretion through the apical anion channel is","E":"Apical jejunal sodium-hydrogen exchange is not the aldosterone-dependent"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanisms of intestinal Cl⁻ and water secretion, including the roles of CFTR channels, cAMP, and VIP. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with constipation starts a minimally absorbed oral peptide that binds the same enterocyte receptor as the heat-stable toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli. Within days the stools become soft and frequent. The abdomen is soft and vital signs are normal. Through which apical channel does the extra chloride secretion occur?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2","B":"ENaC","C":"NKCC1","D":"CFTR","E":"Aquaporin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The peptide, like heat-stable toxin, opens apical CFTR so chloride is secreted. Soft frequent stools after a luminally acting peptide, with a normal examination, are that channel opening. ClC-2 is not the apical channel this toxin receptor opens. ENaC absorbs sodium in the colon and is not the secretory chloride channel. NKCC1 is the basolateral entry step, not the apical channel. Aquaporin conducts water and does not secrete chloride.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Linaclotide activates guanylate cyclase-C, raising cGMP and opening CFTR, while lubiprostone activates ClC-2 (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: Both drugs treat constipation by increasing intestinal fluid secretion, and diarrhea is their main side effect.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"ClC-2 is not the apical channel this toxin receptor","B":"ENaC absorbs sodium in the colon and is not","C":"NKCC1 is the basolateral entry step, not the apical","E":"Aquaporin conducts water and does not secrete"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker has passed 15 L of rice-water stool in two days after drinking untreated water. Blood pressure is 88/54 mm Hg and the pulse is 124/min. Culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod. In a model of this illness, which drug action on crypt cells would most reduce the fluid secretion?","choices":{"A":"Blocking the apical Na⁺-H⁺ exchanger","B":"Blocking apical Na⁺-glucose cotransport","C":"Activating Gs to raise cyclic AMP","D":"Stimulating basolateral Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase","E":"Blocking basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ entry"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Cholera secretion needs chloride entry across the basolateral membrane on the sodium-potassium-2-chloride cotransporter, and blocking that entry dries up the lumen. Rice-water volume and a comma-shaped rod identify the toxin-driven secretion. Blocking the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger would impair absorption, not stop this secretion. Blocking sodium-glucose cotransport would remove a useful absorptive path and would not cut the toxin-driven secretion. Activating Gs would raise cyclic AMP further and increase secretion. Stimulating the basolateral sodium pump would support secretion rather than reduce it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crypt chloride secretion uses basolateral Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ entry and apical chloride channels opened by cAMP, with Na⁺ and water following paracellularly (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Cholera kills by rapid volume loss, so rehydration is the priority.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking the apical sodium-hydrogen exchanger would impair absorption, not","B":"Blocking sodium-glucose cotransport would remove a useful absorptive path","C":"Activating Gs would raise cyclic AMP further and increase","D":"Stimulating the basolateral sodium pump would support secretion rather"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":5,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman has large-volume watery diarrhea that continues despite fasting. She is not taking laxatives. She is weak, and serum potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). CT shows a pancreatic mass, and chromogranin A is 360 ng/mL (reference range: <93 ng/mL). Which drug reduces the tumor's hormone secretion?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Lactulose","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Psyllium"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide suppresses hormone release from this neuroendocrine tumor and thereby slows the secretory diarrhea. Fasting diarrhea, a pancreatic mass, and an elevated chromogranin A identify the hormone-driven illness. Loperamide slows motility and does not reduce the tumor hormone output. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative and would worsen the diarrhea. Bisacodyl stimulates secretion and is not a treatment for this tumor. Psyllium adds bulk and does not suppress tumor hormone release.","boardPrep":"Step 1: octreotide is also used for acute variceal bleeds, acromegaly, and carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide). COMLEX: octreotide can cause steatorrhea and cholelithiasis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, octreotide).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Costanzo","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide slows motility and does not reduce the tumor hormone","C":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative and would worsen the","D":"Bisacodyl stimulates secretion and is not a treatment for this","E":"Psyllium adds bulk and does not suppress tumor hormone"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption, including the roles of solute transport, osmosis, and epithelial tight junctions. (Fig. 8.34)","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man is lightheaded during two days of watery diarrhea from viral gastroenteritis. He is not taking diuretics. Standing blood pressure is 98/60 mm Hg and the pulse is 108/min. His family asks why a glucose-and-salt solution is better than plain water. Which mechanism makes the solution more effective?","choices":{"A":"Coupled Na⁺-glucose uptake pulls in water","B":"Glucose closes the crypt chloride channels","C":"Salt induces apical Na⁺ channels in the jejunum","D":"Hypertonic glucose draws water into the blood","E":"Sodium blocks flow-driven colonic K⁺ secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sodium-glucose cotransport pulls water in with the solute, so the solution replaces volume that plain water does not. Standing hypotension during viral diarrhea is the deficit that cotransport corrects. Glucose does not close the crypt chloride channels. Salt does not induce colonic sodium channels in the jejunum as the reason the solution works. A hypertonic glucose drink would hold water in the lumen rather than draw it into the blood. Sodium in the solution does not act by blocking colonic potassium secretion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oral rehydration solution uses Na⁺-glucose cotransport to stimulate absorption of fluid and electrolytes (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: Oral rehydration is first-line therapy for mild to moderate dehydration from diarrhea.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 1 — notes-aligned trial item","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glucose does not close the crypt chloride","C":"Salt does not induce colonic sodium channels in the","D":"A hypertonic glucose drink would hold water in the","E":"Sodium in the solution does not act by blocking"}},{"id":"grid_sdl28_q01","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the pathophysiology of secretory diarrhea, including cholera toxin–mediated increases in cAMP and subsequent electrolyte and water loss. (Fig. 8.37)","batch":4,"stem":"A 30-year-old man has had two days of profuse rice-water stools during an outbreak of rice-water diarrhea. He is not taking diuretics and has not been vomiting. He has not yet received intravenous fluid. Blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, the heart rate is 124/min, and the abdomen is soft. Stool culture grows a comma-shaped gram-negative rod. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely before treatment?","grid":{"headers":["Plasma aldosterone","Serum K⁺","Serum HCO₃⁻"]},"choices":{"A":["↓","↓","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↓"],"C":["↑","↓","↓"],"D":["↑","↔","↑"],"E":["↓","↑","↑"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) is correct. Loss of extracellular-type fluid lowers arterial pressure and activates the renin-angiotensin II-aldosterone system. Diarrheal fluid is high in K⁺ from flow-dependent colonic secretion, which aldosterone adds to, so hypokalemia follows. Diarrheal fluid is also rich in HCO₃⁻ from intestinal secretions, causing hyperchloremic metabolic acidosis. Choice A (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↓ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) misses the volume response. Choice B (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↓ serum HCO₃⁻) ignores fecal K⁺ loss. Choice D (↑ plasma aldosterone, ↔ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) fits vomiting more than diarrhea, with loss of gastric H⁺ raising HCO₃⁻. Choice E (↓ plasma aldosterone, ↑ serum K⁺, ↑ serum HCO₃⁻) reverses each change. Sources: Costanzo Ch. 8 (Colon; Diarrhea; Secretory diarrhea). Aligned with SDL 28 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea causes normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, whereas vomiting causes metabolic alkalosis (Costanzo Ch. 8). COMLEX: intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement is the main basis of therapy in severe cholera (Guyton Ch. 67).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 5 — electrolyte effects of diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl28_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare electrolyte absorption in the jejunum, ileum, and colon, emphasizing Na⁺, Cl⁻, and HCO₃⁻ transport mechanisms. (Figs. 8.35- 8.36)","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man has an end ileostomy after colectomy for a perforation. Output is watery but he is eating, and the stoma is pink. He is not taking diuretics. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), potassium is 4.0 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 24 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). Before the colectomy, net absorption in the ileum was mainly of which salt?","choices":{"A":"Sodium chloride","B":"Sodium bicarbonate, with no chloride transport","C":"Potassium chloride secreted as the only net flux","D":"Calcium phosphate","E":"Magnesium sulfate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The ileum has both sodium–hydrogen and chloride–bicarbonate exchange, so its net absorption is sodium chloride. Net sodium bicarbonate absorption is the jejunal pattern, which lacks the ileal chloride–bicarbonate exchanger. Potassium secretion is a colonic feature and is not the ileum's net salt absorption. Calcium phosphate is not the salt the ileum absorbs in net fashion. Magnesium sulfate is an osmotic load, not the ileum's net absorbate.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Net sodium bicarbonate absorption is the jejunal pattern, which lacks the ileal chloride–bicarbonate exchanger","C":"Potassium secretion is a colonic feature and is not the ileum's net salt absorption","D":"Calcium phosphate is not the salt the ileum absorbs in net fashion","E":"Magnesium sulfate is an osmotic load, not the ileum's net absorbate"}},{"id":"sdl28_q32","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":1,"stem":"Which diarrhea continues while the patient is fasting?","choices":{"A":"Diarrhea from an unabsorbed sugar","B":"Diarrhea that stops as soon as milk is removed","C":"Diarrhea driven only by a nonabsorbable salt","D":"Secretory diarrhea","E":"Diarrhea that ends when the osmotic load is withdrawn"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Secretory diarrhea is active crypt secretion and persists during fasting, whereas osmotic diarrhea stops when the unabsorbed solute is removed. An unabsorbed sugar is an osmotic load, and that diarrhea abates with fasting. Diarrhea that stops when milk is removed is osmotic, from unabsorbed lactose. A nonabsorbable salt holds water in the lumen and the diarrhea ends when the salt is stopped. Withdrawal of an osmotic load is what ends osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed sugar is an osmotic load, and that diarrhea abates with fasting","B":"Diarrhea that stops when milk is removed is osmotic, from unabsorbed lactose","C":"A nonabsorbable salt holds water in the lumen and the diarrhea ends when the salt is stopped","E":"Withdrawal of an osmotic load is what ends osmotic diarrhea, not secretory diarrhea"}},{"id":"sdl28_q33","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old boy has watery diarrhea and gas after apple juice. The stools stop when juice is withheld, and he has no fever. He is well hydrated and the abdomen is soft and nontender. Stool sodium is 30 mEq/L and stool potassium is 20 mEq/L, with a measured stool osmolality of 320 mOsm/kg and a gap well above 50 mOsm/kg. Serum sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Which mechanism best fits these stools?","choices":{"A":"An unabsorbed solute holding water in the lumen","B":"Toxin-locked opening of crypt chloride channels that continues during a fast","C":"Blood and leukocytes pouring from an invaded mucosa","D":"Failure to make chylomicrons","E":"Portal hypertension with protein-losing enteropathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"A stool osmotic gap above 50 mOsm/kg, with diarrhea that stops when the solute is removed, is osmotic diarrhea. Toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting and leaves a low, not a high, osmotic gap. Inflamed, invaded mucosa produces blood and fecal leukocytes, which this child does not have. Failure of chylomicron assembly causes fat malabsorption, not a sugar-related osmotic gap. Portal hypertension is not the cause of a high stool osmotic gap that ends when juice is stopped.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Toxin-driven crypt secretion persists during fasting and leaves a low, not a high, osmotic gap","C":"Inflamed, invaded mucosa produces blood and fecal leukocytes, which this child does not have","D":"Failure of chylomicron assembly causes fat malabsorption, not a sugar-related osmotic gap","E":"Portal hypertension is not the cause of a high stool osmotic gap that ends when juice is stopped"}},{"id":"sdl28_q34","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has passed more than 8 L of rice-water stool in a day, and the diarrhea continues though he has taken nothing by mouth. He is weak, the pulse is 120/min, and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 134 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L), potassium is 2.9 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), and bicarbonate is 14 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L). The stool gap is small. Which cells are pouring chloride into the lumen?","choices":{"A":"Villus-tip cells that only absorb","B":"Parietal cells","C":"Crypt epithelial cells","D":"Hepatocytes","E":"Pancreatic islet alpha cells"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Secretory diarrhea is excessive chloride and water secretion by crypt epithelial cells, and it continues during fasting. Villus epithelial cells are the absorptive population, not the source of this secretion. Parietal cells secrete gastric acid, not the chloride flood of this watery diarrhea. Hepatocytes are not the crypt cells secreting chloride into the gut lumen. Islet alpha cells secrete glucagon and do not secrete intestinal chloride.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Villus epithelial cells are the absorptive population, not the source of this secretion","B":"Parietal cells secrete gastric acid, not the chloride flood of this watery diarrhea","D":"Hepatocytes are not the crypt cells secreting chloride into the gut lumen","E":"Islet alpha cells secrete glucagon and do not secrete intestinal chloride"}},{"id":"trial_sdl28_q07","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate osmotic and secretory diarrhea based on their underlying mechanisms, clinical characteristics, and effects on intestinal fluid and electrolyte balance.","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has large-volume watery diarrhea that continues while she fasts. She is not taking laxatives, has no fever, and the stool is not bloody. She is weak, the pulse rises when she stands, and the abdomen is soft. Serum potassium is 2.7 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L), serum bicarbonate is 15 mEq/L (reference range: 22–28 mEq/L), stool sodium is 100 mEq/L, stool potassium is 40 mEq/L, and stool osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg. Which process most likely accounts for her diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Unabsorbed luminal solute retaining water","B":"Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar","C":"Increased crypt chloride secretion","D":"Bile acid loss impairing fat absorption","E":"Mucosal inflammation with exudation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The stool osmotic gap is 290 minus 2 times (100 + 40), or 10 mOsm/kg, a low gap, so this is secretory diarrhea from increased crypt chloride secretion. An unabsorbed luminal solute would leave a gap above 50 mOsm/kg, and that diarrhea stops when intake stops. Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar produces gas and acidic stool of moderate volume, and it ends when the sugar is stopped. Bile acid loss that impairs fat absorption produces steatorrhea, and her stool is watery. Mucosal inflammation with exudation brings fever, blood, or fecal leukocytes, and she has none of these.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An unabsorbed luminal solute would leave a gap above 50 mOsm/kg, and that diarrhea stops when intake stops","B":"Colonic fermentation of undigested sugar produces gas and acidic stool of moderate volume, and it ends when the sugar is stopped","D":"Bile acid loss that impairs fat absorption produces steatorrhea, and her stool is watery","E":"Mucosal inflammation with exudation brings fever, blood, or fecal leukocytes, and she has none of these"}}]},{"sdlNumber":29,"title":"SDL 29 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Obstructions, Ischemia, and Malabsorption","questions":[{"id":"sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"Besides hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus, which causes make up most of the remaining mechanical obstructions?","choices":{"A":"Gallstones and bezoars","B":"Ileus and pseudo-obstruction","C":"Swallowed foreign bodies","D":"Meckel diverticula","E":"Tumors and infarctions"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins lists tumors, infarctions, and other causes of strictures such as Crohn disease as the remainder after the four major mechanical causes. Gallstones and bezoars can obstruct the bowel but are not the group Robbins names for the remainder. Ileus and pseudo-obstruction are failures of motility without a mechanical blockage. Swallowed foreign bodies are not among the causes Robbins lists for most remaining obstructions. Meckel diverticula can serve as a lead point for intussusception, which is already one of the four. Sources: Robbins ch 17, intestinal obstruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus cause most mechanical obstruction, and tumors, infarction, and strictures cause most of the rest (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: New obstruction in an older adult without prior surgery or hernia should raise concern for a tumor.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gallstones and bezoars can obstruct the bowel","B":"Ileus and pseudo-obstruction are failures of motility","C":"Swallowed foreign bodies are not among the","D":"Meckel diverticula can serve as a lead"}},{"id":"sdl29_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide?","choices":{"A":"Adhesions","B":"Volvulus","C":"Tumors","D":"Hernias","E":"Strictures"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hernias are the most frequent cause of intestinal obstruction worldwide and the third most common in the United States. Inguinal hernias, with narrow orifices and large sacs, are most often involved. Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide. Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction. Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left after hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus. Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon cause compared with hernias, which lead worldwide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hernias). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: femoral hernias protrude below the inguinal ligament and carry a greater risk of strangulation than inguinal hernias (First Aid Gastrointestinal, hernias). COMLEX: acquired hernias are found in up to 5% of the population (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adhesions are the leading cause of obstruction in the United States and developed nations, not worldwide.","B":"Volvulus is rare and occurs most often in the sigmoid colon, so it is not the most frequent cause of obstruction.","C":"Tumors fall in the minority of mechanical obstructions left after hernias, adhesions, intussusception, and volvulus.","E":"Strictures, such as those of Crohn disease, are an uncommon cause compared with hernias, which lead worldwide."}},{"id":"sdl29_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the segment of bowel that telescopes into the distal bowel in intussusception called?","choices":{"A":"Intussusceptum","B":"Intussuscipiens","C":"Hernia sac","D":"Mesenteric root","E":"Closed loop"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In intussusception, a segment constricted by a peristaltic wave (the intussusceptum) telescopes into the immediately distal segment (the intussuscipiens). The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment. A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping. The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes. The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal obstruction, Fig. 17.25). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intussusception most commonly occurs at the ileocecal junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception). COMLEX: ultrasound or CT of intussusception may show a target sign (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"The hernia sac is the serosa-lined peritoneal pouch that protrudes through an abdominal wall defect, not a telescoping bowel segment.","E":"A closed loop is formed by fibrous adhesions that trap sliding viscera, an internal hernia mechanism rather than telescoping.","D":"The mesentery is pulled along behind the telescoping intussusceptum; it is not the segment that telescopes.","B":"The intussuscipiens is the distal receiving segment."}},{"id":"sdl29_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"What causes most acute arterial obstruction of the mesenteric circulation?","choices":{"A":"Systemic vasculitis","B":"Mesenteric venous clot","C":"Tumor compression","D":"Thrombosis or embolism","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In a large majority of cases, acute mesenteric obstruction is caused by thrombosis or embolism. Severe atherosclerosis is the main risk for thrombosis, and emboli come mostly from aortic atheromas or cardiac mural thrombi. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction. Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction. Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism. Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: risk factors for acute mesenteric ischemia include atrial fibrillation, peripheral arterial disease, recent MI, and CHF (First Aid Gastrointestinal, acute mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: mesenteric venous thrombosis is an uncommon cause of ischemic bowel disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is a venous cause tied to hypercoagulable states, cirrhosis, and neoplasms, not acute arterial obstruction.","E":"Vasoconstrictive drugs such as cocaine cause nonocclusive hypoperfusion rather than arterial obstruction.","A":"Vasculitides are a less common cause of mesenteric arterial thrombosis than atherosclerotic thrombosis or embolism.","C":"Invasive neoplasms are a cause of mesenteric venous thrombosis, whereas acute arterial obstruction is mostly thrombosis or embolism."}},{"id":"sdl29_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal component is most vulnerable to ischemic injury?","choices":{"A":"Surface epithelium","B":"Crypt stem cells","C":"Basal crypt cells","D":"Muscularis propria","E":"Myenteric neurons"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Capillaries run from crypt to surface before a hairpin turn, so the surface epithelium is most vulnerable while crypt stem cells are protected. Surface atrophy with normal or hyperproliferative crypts is the signature of ischemia. The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion. Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the crypts before turning at the surface. Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becoming hyperproliferative in ischemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ischemic bowel disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia causes postprandial epigastric pain, food aversion, and weight loss (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: chronic intestinal ischemia is accompanied by fibrous scarring of the lamina propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The muscularis propria is injured only in transmural infarction, typically from acute major vascular occlusion.","B":"Crypt stem cells are protected because capillaries reach the crypts before turning at the surface.","C":"Basal crypts receive blood first and are spared, often becoming hyperproliferative in ischemia."}},{"id":"sdl29_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":1,"stem":"Generalized hypotension most often causes ischemic injury at which colonic site?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal junction","B":"Hepatic flexure","C":"Splenic flexure","D":"Mid transverse colon","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Watershed zones at the end of arterial territories are most vulnerable, and the splenic flexure, where the superior and inferior mesenteric circulations terminate, is the main one. The ileocecal junction is well supplied by the ileocolic branch of the superior mesenteric artery. The hepatic flexure lies within the superior mesenteric territory rather than at a watershed. The mid transverse colon is supplied by the middle colic artery and is not a named watershed. The ascending colon lies within the superior mesenteric territory and is not a watershed zone. Sources: Robbins ch 17, ischemic bowel disease pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Colonic ischemia favors watershed zones such as the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid (Robbins ch 17; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Crampy left-sided pain then hematochezia in an older adult after hypotension suggests ischemic colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The ileocecal junction is well supplied by","B":"The hepatic flexure lies within the superior","D":"The mid transverse colon is supplied by","E":"The ascending colon lies within the superior"}},{"id":"sdl29_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Where is angiodysplasia most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum region","B":"Cecum or right colon","C":"Proximal jejunal loops","D":"Sigmoid colon segment","E":"Lower rectum and anus"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is most common in the cecum or right colon and usually comes to attention after the sixth decade. The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the large diameter and high wall tension that drive this lesion. It arises from distention-related occlusion of submucosal colonic veins, so the stomach, proximal jejunum, and rectum and anus are outside that segment. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia presents with hematochezia from tortuous dilated vessels (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia bleeding ranges from chronic and intermittent to acute and massive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"The narrower sigmoid colon lacks the large diameter and high wall tension that drive this lesion.","A":"The stomach is not the site of this colonic venous lesion.","C":"The proximal jejunum is not a high-tension colonic segment.","E":"The rectum and anus are not the high-tension segment."}},{"id":"sdl29_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which test confirms angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Barium swallow","B":"Stool culture","C":"Plain radiograph","D":"Angiography","E":"Breath testing"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Visceral or aortic angiography (with colonoscopy) is the diagnostic test for angiodysplasia. The lesion consists of malformed, dilated, thin-walled submucosal and mucosal vessels. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (angiodysplasia); Robbins Ch. 17 (Angiodysplasia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is more common in older patients (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: angiodysplasia has a prevalence of less than 1% in adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl29_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":1,"stem":"Which cardiac valve lesion is classically associated with bleeding from angiodysplasia?","choices":{"A":"Mitral stenosis","B":"Mitral valve prolapse","C":"Aortic stenosis","D":"Tricuspid regurgitation","E":"Pulmonic stenosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Angiodysplasia is associated with end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis. Mitral stenosis is a rheumatic valve lesion not listed among the associations of angiodysplasia. Mitral valve prolapse is not among the conditions linked to angiodysplasia. Tricuspid regurgitation is not described as an association of angiodysplasia. Pulmonic stenosis is a congenital lesion with no listed link to angiodysplasia. Sources: First Aid 2025 GI, angiodysplasia; Robbins ch 17, angiodysplasia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Angiodysplasia is linked to end-stage renal disease, von Willebrand disease, and aortic stenosis and is confirmed by angiography (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Recurrent painless hematochezia in an older patient with a systolic ejection murmur suggests this pairing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mitral stenosis is a rheumatic valve lesion","B":"Mitral valve prolapse is not among the","D":"Tricuspid regurgitation is not described as an","E":"Pulmonic stenosis is a congenital lesion with"}},{"id":"sdl29_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"How does a CFTR defect lead to exocrine pancreatic insufficiency in cystic fibrosis?","choices":{"A":"Autoantibodies destroy acinar cells","B":"Trypsinogen gains resistance to lysis","C":"The duodenum stops releasing secretin","D":"Gallstones block the ampulla","E":"Inspissated secretions plug ducts"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Faulty CFTR reduces bicarbonate, sodium, and water secretion, so secretions dehydrate and form intraductal concretions that obstruct the ducts, causing low-grade chronic pancreatitis, autodigestion, and eventual exocrine insufficiency. Autoantibodies against acinar cells describe autoimmune pancreatitis, not cystic fibrosis. Trypsinogen that resists inactivation is the mechanism of hereditary pancreatitis from PRSS1 mutations. Secretin release is not the defect; the ducts cannot secrete fluid in response to it. Gallstones blocking the ampulla cause acute gallstone pancreatitis, not the ductal plugging of cystic fibrosis. Sources: Robbins ch 17, malabsorption and cystic fibrosis; Robbins ch 19, pancreatitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In cystic fibrosis, dehydrated secretions plug pancreatic ducts and cause exocrine insufficiency, impairing the intraluminal phase of absorption (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Treat steatorrhea in cystic fibrosis with pancreatic enzyme replacement and fat-soluble vitamins.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Autoantibodies against acinar cells describe autoimmune pancreatitis,","B":"Trypsinogen that resists inactivation is the mechanism","C":"Secretin release is not the defect; the","D":"Gallstones blocking the ampulla cause acute gallstone"}},{"id":"sdl29_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"IgG antigliadin antibodies","B":"IgA anti-TG2 antibodies","C":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies","D":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","E":"Perinuclear ANCA antibodies"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The most sensitive noninvasive test for celiac disease is IgA against tissue transglutaminase 2. IgG anti-TG2 can be used in patients with IgA deficiency. IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies. Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease. Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac serology also includes anti-endomysial and anti-deamidated gliadin peptide antibodies (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease shows an abnormal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgA antibodies to tissue transglutaminase (TG2) are the most sensitive serologic test for celiac disease, ahead of antigliadin antibodies.","C":"Antibodies to parietal cell H+,K+-ATPase and intrinsic factor mark autoimmune atrophic gastritis, not celiac disease.","D":"Antimitochondrial antibody against PDC-E2 is the characteristic finding in primary biliary cholangitis, not celiac disease. p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test.","E":"p-ANCA is found in up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients and is not a celiac test."}},{"id":"sdl29_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the most common cancer in people with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Gastric diffuse large B-cell lymphoma tumor","B":"Distal esophageal Barrett adenocarcinoma","C":"Enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma of the liver","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma, an aggressive lymphoma of intraepithelial T cells. Small intestinal adenocarcinoma is also more frequent. The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma. The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma. Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatitis B and C infection (70% to 85% of cases worldwide), not by celiac disease. Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers increased in celiac disease (T-cell lymphoma and small intestinal adenocarcinoma). Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Celiac disease). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with dermatitis herpetiformis and decreased bone density (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: a gluten-free diet may reduce celiac complications including anemia, female infertility, and osteoporosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The most common cancer in celiac disease is a T-cell lymphoma of the intestine (EATL), not a gastric B-cell lymphoma.","B":"The cancers increased in celiac disease are enteropathy-associated T-cell lymphoma (most common) and small intestinal adenocarcinoma, not Barrett adenocarcinoma.","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is driven mainly by chronic hepatitis B and C infection (70% to 85% of cases worldwide), not by celiac disease.","E":"Pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma is not among the cancers increased in celiac disease (T-cell lymphoma and small intestinal adenocarcinoma)."}},{"id":"sdl29_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Mutation of which gene causes IPEX?","choices":{"A":"CFTR","B":"MYO5B","C":"HLA-DQ2","D":"RET","E":"FOXP3"},"correct":"E","explanation":"IPEX (immune dysregulation, polyendocrinopathy, enteropathy, X-linkage) is caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked FOXP3 gene. CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency. HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases. MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX. Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microvillus inclusion disease is caused by MYO5B mutations and presents with intractable diarrhea in early infancy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: autoimmune enteropathy is treated with immunosuppressants such as cyclosporine or with stem cell transplantation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR mutation causes cystic fibrosis, impairing chloride and bicarbonate transport and leading to pancreatic insufficiency.","C":"HLA-DQ2 is the susceptibility allele of celiac disease, present in about 90% of cases.","B":"MYO5B mutations cause microvillus inclusion disease, not IPEX.","D":"Loss-of-function RET mutations cause Hirschsprung disease by halting neural crest migration, not the Treg failure of IPEX."}},{"id":"sdl29_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"Which defective molecule causes abetalipoproteinemia?","choices":{"A":"Cystic fibrosis transmembrane regulator","B":"Microsomal triglyceride transfer protein","C":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter protein","D":"Low-density lipoprotein receptor protein","E":"Apolipoprotein E lipid carrier protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Abetalipoproteinemia is an autosomal recessive defect in microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTP), which transfers lipids to nascent apolipoprotein B in the ER. CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly. The LDL receptor is not the defect. Apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule. SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galactose uptake, not a lipoprotein assembly protein. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Environmental enteric dysfunction; Autoimmune enteropathy; Lactase deficiency; Abetalipoproteinemia). Aligned with SDL 29 lecture notes (2026-10-31).","boardPrep":"Step 1: abetalipoproteinemia causes acanthocytes on the peripheral smear (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia presents in infancy with failure to thrive, diarrhea, and steatorrhea (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"CFTR dysfunction causes cystic fibrosis, whose malabsorption comes from pancreatic insufficiency rather than failed lipoprotein assembly.","D":"The LDL receptor is not the defect.","E":"Apolipoprotein E is not the missing molecule.","C":"SGLT1 is the Na+-glucose cotransporter for glucose and galactose uptake, not a lipoprotein assembly protein."}},{"id":"sdl29_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":1,"stem":"In regions where environmental enteric dysfunction is endemic, which observation suggests an underlying defect in mucosal immune function?","choices":{"A":"High oral vaccine failure rates","B":"Common selective IgA deficiency","C":"Absent Peyer patches at birth","D":"Positive anti-TG2 serology","E":"Rapid cure with oral antibiotics"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Robbins notes that relatively high oral vaccine failure rates in regions where environmental enteric dysfunction is endemic point to an underlying defect in mucosal immune function. Selective IgA deficiency is not described as a feature of environmental enteric dysfunction. Absent Peyer patches at birth are not reported in this acquired disorder of early childhood. Anti-TG2 serology is a marker of celiac disease, and no accepted laboratory test diagnoses environmental enteric dysfunction. Oral antibiotics and nutritional supplements do not reverse or prevent environmental enteric dysfunction. Sources: Robbins ch 17, environmental enteric dysfunction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Environmental enteric dysfunction follows chronic fecal pathogen exposure, has no single causal organism, and is linked to oral vaccine failure (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Prevention relies on sanitation and clean water, since antibiotics and supplements do not reverse it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Selective IgA deficiency is not described as","C":"Absent Peyer patches at birth are not","D":"Anti-TG2 serology is a marker of celiac","E":"Oral antibiotics and nutritional supplements do not"}},{"id":"sdl29_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-month-old boy has intermittent screaming episodes with his legs drawn up, beginning a week after a viral illness. Between episodes he is comfortable. A sausage-shaped mass is felt on the right, and there is no hernia. The stool looks like red currant jelly. What most likely served as the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"Intraluminal adenocarcinoma","B":"Inspissated meconium plug","C":"Fibrous adhesion band","D":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","E":"Incarcerated hernia sac"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Intussusception after a viral illness uses hyperplastic Peyer patches as the lead point that lets ileum telescope into colon. Intermittent pain, a right-sided sausage mass, and currant-jelly stool are that event in a toddler. An intraluminal adenocarcinoma is an adult lead point and is rare at 14 months. Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with a sweat-chloride defect, not a toddler after a virus. A fibrous adhesion follows surgery, which this child has not had. There is no hernia sac on examination to serve as the lead point.","boardPrep":"Step 1: in idiopathic intussusception of young children, contrast or air enemas are both diagnostic and therapeutic (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Meckel diverticulum and bowel wall hematoma from IgA vasculitis are pediatric intussusception lead points (First Aid Gastrointestinal, intussusception).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An intraluminal adenocarcinoma is an adult lead point and","B":"Inspissated meconium plugs the ileum of a newborn with","C":"A fibrous adhesion follows surgery, which this child has not","E":"There is no hernia sac on examination to serve"}},{"id":"sdl29_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman has abdominal pain, distention, and vomiting. She had a hysterectomy years ago and has no external hernia. The abdomen is distended and tympanitic, with high-pitched bowel sounds and no peritonitis. An abdominal radiograph shows air-fluid levels in the small bowel and little colonic gas. What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon","B":"Postoperative fibrous bands","C":"Ileocecal segment telescoping","D":"Inguinal sac herniation of bowel","E":"Gallstone lodged at the valve"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Prior pelvic surgery leaves fibrous bands that obstruct the small bowel. Distention, vomiting, high-pitched sounds, and air-fluid levels without a hernia are that mechanical block. Twisting of the sigmoid colon would dilate the colon, and the gas here is in the small bowel. Ileocecal telescoping is a toddler pattern with currant-jelly stool, not a postoperative adult picture. There is no inguinal hernia on examination. A gallstone at the ileocecal valve is uncommon and is not linked to a prior hysterectomy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: small bowel obstruction is managed with GI decompression, volume resuscitation, and bowel rest (First Aid Gastrointestinal, small bowel obstruction). COMLEX: adhesions can follow peritoneal inflammation such as endometriosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Twisting of the sigmoid colon would dilate the colon,","C":"Ileocecal telescoping is a toddler pattern with currant-jelly stool,","D":"There is no inguinal hernia on","E":"A gallstone at the ileocecal valve is uncommon and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":2,"stem":"An 82-year-old man in a nursing home has sudden abdominal distention. He has had chronic constipation and has never had abdominal surgery. The abdomen is massively tympanitic and nontender, with high-pitched bowel sounds. A plain film shows a coffee-bean sign. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Midgut volvulus","B":"Cecal carcinoma","C":"Sigmoid volvulus","D":"Colonic ileus","E":"Ischemic colitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A coffee-bean shadow in an elderly constipated man is sigmoid volvulus. Massive tympany without peritonitis fits a twist of the redundant sigmoid. Midgut volvulus is a neonatal emergency with bilious vomiting, not a coffee-bean film in an older adult. A cecal carcinoma would obstruct more gradually and would not draw a coffee-bean loop. Colonic ileus dilates the colon without the coffee-bean twist. Ischemic colitis causes pain and bloody diarrhea, not this closed-loop gas pattern.","boardPrep":"Step 1: gastric volvulus in adults is more common with paraesophageal hernia (First Aid Gastrointestinal, volvulus). COMLEX: because it is rare, volvulus can be overlooked clinically with catastrophic results (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 1 — midgut rotation and malrotation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Midgut volvulus is a neonatal emergency with bilious vomiting,","B":"A cecal carcinoma would obstruct more gradually and would","D":"Colonic ileus dilates the colon without the coffee-bean","E":"Ischemic colitis causes pain and bloody diarrhea, not this"}},{"id":"sdl29_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has sudden severe abdominal pain. He has atrial fibrillation and stopped anticoagulation after a recent fall, and there is no hernia. The abdomen is soft, the pain is far worse than the tenderness, and the pulse is 118/min. Serum lactate is 5.2 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). What is the most likely cause?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of the SMA","B":"Thrombosis of a mesenteric vein","C":"Twisting of the sigmoid mesentery","D":"Vasculitis of small serosal arteries","E":"Rupture of a cecal vascular ectasia"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Atrial fibrillation throws an embolus into the superior mesenteric artery, producing pain out of proportion to a soft abdomen. A lactate of 5.2 mmol/L shows the bowel is already ischemic. Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually slower and is linked to hypercoagulable states, not a cardiac embolus. Sigmoid twisting causes distention and a coffee-bean film, not this soft abdomen with a high lactate. Small-vessel vasculitis is not the acute event in new atrial fibrillation. A cecal vascular ectasia bleeds into the lumen and does not cause this ischemic pain.","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute arterial infarction produces a sharp demarcation between normal and ischemic bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: coagulative necrosis of the muscularis propria develops within 1 to 4 days of infarction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mesenteric venous thrombosis is usually slower and is linked","C":"Sigmoid twisting causes distention and a coffee-bean film, not","D":"Small-vessel vasculitis is not the acute event in new atrial","E":"A cecal vascular ectasia bleeds into the lumen and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 76-year-old woman has crampy left lower abdominal pain and then bloody diarrhea while recovering from septic shock. She has no history of chronic colitis. The left abdomen is tender without peritonitis, and the pulse is 104/min. The leukocyte count is 14,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). A colonic biopsy is taken. Which finding best supports ischemia?","choices":{"A":"Granulomas scattered in lamina propria","B":"Crypt abscesses in diffuse colitis","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes","D":"Upper mucosa lost but crypts spared","E":"Thickened subepithelial collagen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ischemia strips the surface mucosa and spares the crypt bases, which is the biopsy pattern after shock. Left-sided cramps and bloody diarrhea in that setting match a watershed injury. Scattered granulomas suggest a transmural inflammatory disease, not shock-related ischemia. Crypt abscesses throughout a diffuse colitis are the pattern of idiopathic colitis, not a short ischemic segment. Villous blunting with lymphocytes is a small-bowel pattern and is not this colonic injury. A thickened subepithelial collagen band causes watery diarrhea and is not produced by a shock episode.","boardPrep":"Step 1: colonic ischemia commonly occurs at the splenic flexure and rectosigmoid junction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, colonic ischemia). COMLEX: neutrophils are recruited into ischemic mucosa within hours of reperfusion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — large-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Scattered granulomas suggest a transmural inflammatory disease, not shock-related","B":"Crypt abscesses throughout a diffuse colitis are the pattern","C":"Villous blunting with lymphocytes is a small-bowel pattern and","E":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band causes watery diarrhea and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with widespread atherosclerosis has ischemic injury confined to the right colon. The right side of the abdomen is tender. CT shows right-colon wall thickening, and the inferior mesenteric artery is patent. Why does this location carry a worse prognosis?","choices":{"A":"The right colon wall is especially thin","B":"The right colon lacks a submucosal layer","C":"The right colon has no collateral arcades","D":"Bacteria are far denser in the right colon","E":"Its artery also feeds the small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The right colon is supplied by the same artery that feeds the small intestine, so ischemia there often means a long segment of bowel is threatened. Wall thickening limited to the right side is more ominous than a short left-sided watershed injury. Wall thickness is not what makes right-sided ischemia more dangerous. The right colon has a submucosa, like the rest of the colon. The right colon does have collateral arcades, and the danger is the shared trunk with the small bowel. Bacterial density is not the reason this location has a worse prognosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic mesenteric ischemia most commonly involves the SMA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, chronic mesenteric ischemia). COMLEX: COPD and symptoms lasting more than 2 weeks are poor prognostic signs in colonic ischemia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wall thickness is not what makes right-sided ischemia more","B":"The right colon has a submucosa, like the rest","C":"The right colon does have collateral arcades, and the","D":"Bacterial density is not the reason this location has"}},{"id":"sdl29_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 74-year-old woman has recurrent painless hematochezia and fatigue. She has a harsh systolic murmur of aortic stenosis and is not taking anticoagulants. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Colonoscopy shows small flat red mucosal lesions. What will histology most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Glands showing high-grade dysplasia","B":"Granulation tissue in an ulcer bed","C":"Ectatic nests of thin-walled vessels","D":"Mucosal outpouching through the muscle","E":"Noncaseating granulomas in the mucosa"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The flat red lesions are nests of ectatic thin-walled vessels in the mucosa. Aortic stenosis, painless bleeding, and iron deficiency are the setting in which these angiodysplasias are found. High-grade dysplasia is a neoplastic glandular change and is not the histology of these flat red spots. Granulation tissue lines an ulcer and is not the lesion seen here. A mucosal outpouching through muscle is a diverticulum, not a flat red spot. Noncaseating granulomas are an inflammatory finding and do not form these vascular lesions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: angiodysplasia usually comes to clinical attention after the sixth decade (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia is a neoplastic glandular change and is","B":"Granulation tissue lines an ulcer and is not the","D":"A mucosal outpouching through muscle is a diverticulum, not","E":"Noncaseating granulomas are an inflammatory finding and do not"}},{"id":"sdl29_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 79-year-old man has bleeding from colonic vascular ectasias clustered in the cecum. He has aortic stenosis. Hemoglobin is 9.2 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which mechanical factor is thought to explain that typical location?","choices":{"A":"Lowest blood flow at a watershed zone","B":"Thinnest muscularis of any colonic part","C":"Highest bacterial count in the colon lumen","D":"Largest diameter gives most wall tension","E":"Most lymphoid tissue in the large bowel"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The cecum has the largest diameter, so wall tension is highest there and the submucosal veins are repeatedly distended. Bleeding vascular ectasias cluster at that wide segment. The cecum is not a watershed between the two mesenteric arteries. A thinner muscularis is not the mechanical explanation given for this site. The highest bacterial count is not why the vessels ectasia in the cecum. Lymphoid tissue is richer in the ileum and appendix than in the cecum as an explanation for these vessels.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia in older patients is associated with aortic stenosis (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: the pathogenesis of angiodysplasia has been attributed to mechanical and congenital factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility and haustral contractions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The cecum is not a watershed between the two mesenteric","B":"A thinner muscularis is not the mechanical explanation given","C":"The highest bacterial count is not why the vessels","E":"Lymphoid tissue is richer in the ileum and appendix"}},{"id":"sdl29_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":2,"stem":"A 71-year-old man has recurrent hematochezia from colonic vascular ectasias. He has no liver disease and is not taking anticoagulants. The abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which condition is classically associated with this lesion?","choices":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus","B":"End-stage renal disease","C":"Primary hypothyroidism","D":"Gouty arthritis history","E":"Chronic obstructive lung"},"correct":"B","explanation":"End-stage renal disease is classically associated with bleeding colonic vascular ectasias. Recurrent painless hematochezia with a normal abdominal examination is the presentation that prompts that association. Type 2 diabetes is not the classic systemic association of these vascular lesions. Primary hypothyroidism does not cluster with colonic vascular ectasia. Gout is not the associated condition. Chronic lung disease is not the classic association.","boardPrep":"Step 1: angiodysplasia is confirmed by angiography (First Aid Gastrointestinal, angiodysplasia). COMLEX: normal gut distention may intermittently occlude submucosal veins crossing the muscularis propria (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Type 2 diabetes is not the classic systemic association","C":"Primary hypothyroidism does not cluster with colonic vascular","D":"Gout is not the associated condition","E":"Chronic lung disease is not the classic"}},{"id":"sdl29_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has bloating, fatigue, and an itchy blistering rash on her elbows. She eats wheat daily, and a cousin has type 1 diabetes. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 130 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. A biopsy of the second part of the duodenum is taken. What will it most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lymphocytosis with villous atrophy","B":"Vacuolated enterocytes filled with lipid","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina","D":"Transmural granulomas with deep fissuring","E":"Normal villi without inflammatory cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Dermatitis herpetiformis with a high anti-tissue transglutaminase titer is celiac disease, and the duodenum shows lymphocytes in the epithelium with villous atrophy. The elbow rash and iron deficiency are the clinical companions of that biopsy. Lipid-filled enterocytes are the picture when chylomicrons cannot be assembled, which does not cause this rash. Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina propria are a different storage disease. Transmural granulomas and fissures are a chronic inflammatory disease of the ileum, not this duodenal rash illness. Normal villi would not accompany this serology and rash.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac disease is associated with HLA-DQ2 and HLA-DQ8 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, celiac disease). COMLEX: celiac disease in adults most commonly presents between ages 30 and 60 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lipid-filled enterocytes are the picture when chylomicrons cannot be","C":"Foamy macrophages packed in the lamina propria are a","D":"Transmural granulomas and fissures are a chronic inflammatory disease","E":"Normal villi would not accompany this serology and"}},{"id":"sdl29_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has chronic diarrhea, and gluten-sensitive enteropathy is suspected. He eats wheat daily. He is thin and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 11.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). HLA-DQ typing is ordered. How is this genetic result most useful?","choices":{"A":"Positive result confirms the diagnosis","B":"Result predicts the lymphoma risk","C":"Result sets the diet duration needed","D":"Result replaces the need for biopsy","E":"Negative result argues against it"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A negative HLA-DQ result argues against gluten-sensitive enteropathy, because almost every patient carries DQ2 or DQ8. The test is ordered to help exclude the disease, not to confirm it. A positive result is common in healthy people and does not confirm the diagnosis. The typing does not predict the risk of lymphoma. The result does not set how long the diet must be continued. A genetic result does not replace the biopsy when the diagnosis is still in doubt.","boardPrep":"Step 1: celiac concordance in monozygotic twins is about 75% (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: celiac disease is associated with type 1 diabetes, thyroiditis, Sjögren syndrome, and IgA nephropathy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A positive result is common in healthy people and","B":"The typing does not predict the risk of","C":"The result does not set how long the diet","D":"A genetic result does not replace the biopsy when"}},{"id":"sdl29_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has bulky greasy stools and poor weight gain. She has recurrent respiratory infections and has stopped her enzyme capsules. She is thin and the abdomen is soft. Sweat chloride is 80 mmol/L (reference range: <30 mmol/L), and fecal elastase is 40 µg/g (reference range: >200 µg/g). Which phase of nutrient absorption is primarily impaired?","choices":{"A":"Brush-border digestion","B":"Intraluminal digestion","C":"Transepithelial transport","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport","E":"Mucosal nutrient uptake"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Missing pancreatic enzymes impair intraluminal digestion, which is why the stools are greasy when she stops the capsules. A sweat chloride of 80 mmol/L identifies the disease that destroys the exocrine pancreas. Brush-border digestion is intact, and the enzymes missing are secreted into the lumen. Transepithelial transport works once nutrients are digested. Lymphatic lipid transport is intact in this enzyme deficiency. The mucosa can still take up nutrients that have been digested in the lumen.","boardPrep":"Step 1: pancreatic insufficiency gives a normal d-xylose test (First Aid Gastrointestinal, pancreatic insufficiency). COMLEX: malabsorption can disturb intraluminal digestion, terminal digestion, transepithelial transport, or lymphatic transport (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 14 → Obj 2 — pancreatic bicarbonate secretion and CFTR","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Brush-border digestion is intact, and the enzymes missing are","C":"Transepithelial transport works once nutrients are","D":"Lymphatic lipid transport is intact in this enzyme","E":"The mucosa can still take up nutrients that have"}},{"id":"sdl29_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 6-month-old girl has steatorrhea and poor growth. She was born at term, and there is no jaundice. She has edema and a soft abdomen. The blood smear shows acanthocytes, plasma lacks apoB-containing lipoproteins, and triglycerides are 10 mg/dL (reference range: 30–150 mg/dL). What will a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Lipid-vacuolated enterocytes","B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes","C":"Neutrophils within the crypts","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages","E":"Absent ganglion cell clusters"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Without apoB, enterocytes cannot export chylomicrons and fill with lipid vacuoles. Acanthocytes and absent apoB-containing lipoproteins are the blood findings that match those vacuolated cells. Flattened villi with lymphocytes are the gluten-sensitive pattern and do not remove apoB. Neutrophils in the crypts are an infectious or idiopathic colitis finding. PAS-positive foamy macrophages are a different infiltration and are not the enterocyte lesion here. Ganglion cells are present, and this is not an aganglionic segment.","boardPrep":"Step 1: failure to absorb essential fatty acids in abetalipoproteinemia causes fat-soluble vitamin deficiencies (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: abetalipoproteinemia is autosomal recessive (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flattened villi with lymphocytes are the gluten-sensitive pattern and","C":"Neutrophils in the crypts are an infectious or idiopathic colitis","D":"PAS-positive foamy macrophages are a different infiltration and are","E":"Ganglion cells are present, and this is not an aganglionic"}},{"id":"sdl29_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has severe persistent diarrhea, type 1 diabetes, and eczema. A maternal uncle died in infancy with the same picture. He is small, with an eczematous rash and a distended abdomen. Glucose is 280 mg/dL (reference range: 70–99 mg/dL). Which cell type is defective?","choices":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8+ T cells","B":"Mature B lymphocytes","C":"CD4+ regulatory T cells","D":"Intestinal goblet cells","E":"Small-bowel enterocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"This X-linked pattern of diarrhea, diabetes, and eczema is loss of CD4-positive regulatory T cells. The affected uncle and the early autoimmune injury point to that missing regulator. Intraepithelial CD8-positive T cells are increased in gluten-sensitive enteropathy, which is not this multisystem illness. Mature B cells are not the lineage lost in this regulatory-cell disease. Goblet cells are present, and mucus production is not the defective step. Enterocytes are not the cell type whose loss produces diabetes and eczema together.","boardPrep":"Step 1: autoantibodies to enterocytes and goblet cells are common in autoimmune enteropathy (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: intraepithelial lymphocytes in autoimmune enteropathy increase less than in celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intraepithelial CD8-positive T cells are increased in gluten-sensitive enteropathy,","B":"Mature B cells are not the lineage lost in","D":"Goblet cells are present, and mucus production is not","E":"Enterocytes are not the cell type whose loss produces"}},{"id":"sdl29_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl from a rural community with poor sanitation has stunted growth and chronic malabsorption. She eats wheat, antibiotics have not helped, and no single pathogen is found. She is small, the abdomen is distended, and there is no rash. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL), and IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 2 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Classic gluten-sensitive enteropathy","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy","E":"Environmental enteric dysfunction"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Chronic exposure to fecal contamination with stunting, malabsorption, and a negative celiac serology is environmental enteric dysfunction. Wheat in the diet and a normal anti-tissue transglutaminase titer argue against gluten-sensitive disease. Gluten-sensitive enteropathy would raise anti-tissue transglutaminase, which is normal here. Microvillus inclusion disease presents in early infancy with intractable diarrhea, not this community pattern. Congenital lipoprotein deficiency shows acanthocytes and absent apoB, which are not described. Inherited autoimmune enteropathy is a genetic multisystem illness and is not tied to poor sanitation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: biopsies in environmental enteric dysfunction show more immune cell infiltration and less villous atrophy than advanced celiac disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: stunting from environmental enteric dysfunction may be accompanied by uncorrectable cognitive deficits (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy would raise anti-tissue transglutaminase, which is normal","B":"Microvillus inclusion disease presents in early infancy with intractable","C":"Congenital lipoprotein deficiency shows acanthocytes and absent apoB, which","D":"Inherited autoimmune enteropathy is a genetic multisystem illness and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 74-year-old man has 10 hours of vomiting and a tender, tense, irreducible bulge in the right groin. He has had no prior bowel resection. The pulse is 110/min and the abdomen is distended. At surgery a loop of ileum in the hernia sac is swollen, dusky, and congested. Serum lactate is 3.1 mmol/L (reference range: 0.5–2.2 mmol/L). Which event most likely started the damage?","choices":{"A":"Embolic occlusion of an ileal artery","B":"Compressed venous outflow at the neck","C":"Reperfusion injury after reduction","D":"Twisting of bowel around an adhesion","E":"A hyperplastic lymphoid tissue lead point"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The narrow neck compresses venous outflow first, so the loop swells and becomes dusky before the artery is occluded. An irreducible groin bulge with a congested loop is that sequence. An arterial embolus would infarct bowel in the mesentery, not a loop trapped in the groin. Reperfusion injury follows reduction and is not the event that first damaged the trapped loop. Twisting around an adhesion is an internal closed loop and is not this groin hernia. Hyperplastic lymphoid tissue is a toddler lead point and is not inside this hernia sac.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In a hernia, pressure at the neck impairs venous drainage, and the resulting edema leads to incarceration and then strangulation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: A tender, irreducible hernia is a surgical emergency because strangulation can follow.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 2 — physiologic herniation, omphalocele, and gastroschisis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An arterial embolus would infarct bowel in the mesentery,","C":"Reperfusion injury follows reduction and is not the event","D":"Twisting around an adhesion is an internal closed loop","E":"Hyperplastic lymphoid tissue is a toddler lead point and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the cause, morphology, associated demographics, and clinical features of ischemic bowel diseases (acute mesenteric ischemia, colonic ischemia).","batch":3,"stem":"A 77-year-old woman has crampy left-sided pain and bloody diarrhea one day after cardiac surgery with several hours of hypotension. She has no history of colitis. The pulse is 104/min and blood pressure is 96/58 mm Hg. CT shows wall thickening only at the splenic flexure, and both the superior and inferior mesenteric arteries are patent. Why was this segment most vulnerable?","choices":{"A":"Its mucosa lacks a muscularis mucosae","B":"Its wall is thinnest where diameter is largest","C":"It lies where two arterial territories end","D":"It receives a single end artery from the aorta","E":"Its veins are compressed by the spleen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The splenic flexure lies where the two mesenteric arterial territories end, so it is the first to suffer when pressure falls. Patent main arteries and a short segment of wall thickening after hypotension mark that watershed. The mucosa has a muscularis mucosae, and that is not why this segment is vulnerable. Wall thickness and diameter are not the reason the flexure is the watershed. It does not receive a single end artery directly from the aorta. The splenic vein does not compress this segment of colon.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypoperfusion injures watershed zones, chiefly the splenic flexure and the rectosigmoid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Left-sided pain with bloody stools after hypotension suggests ischemic colitis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 1 → Obj 1 — gut tube divisions and their blood supply","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The mucosa has a muscularis mucosae, and that is","B":"Wall thickness and diameter are not the reason the","D":"It does not receive a single end artery directly","E":"The splenic vein does not compress this segment of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the manifestation (tortuous dilation of vessels), most common location (cecum, right-sided colon), associated age, diagnostic testing (angiography), and associated conditions (ESRD, von Willebrand disease, aortic stenosis) of angiodysplasia.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old man has recurrent painless maroon stools and fatigue. He has no liver disease and is not taking anticoagulants. There is a harsh crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the right upper sternal border that radiates to the carotids. Hemoglobin is 8.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Colonoscopy shows small, flat, cherry-red vessels in the cecum. Which mechanism links the heart disease to the bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Raised portal pressure dilating veins","B":"Emboli from the valve infarcting mucosa","C":"Inherited weakness of capillary walls","D":"Shear loss of von Willebrand factor","E":"Platelet consumption on the stenotic valve"},"correct":"D","explanation":"High shear across the stenotic aortic valve cleaves von Willebrand factor, so the cecal vascular ectasias bleed. The radiating murmur and iron deficiency are that association. Portal pressure is not raised, and there is no liver disease to dilate veins. Emboli from the valve would infarct mucosa rather than cause recurrent painless bleeding from flat vessels. The vessel walls are not congenitally weak as the link to the murmur. Platelets are not consumed on the valve as the mechanism of this bleeding.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heyde syndrome links aortic stenosis to bleeding angiodysplasia through shear-induced loss of von Willebrand factor (Bootcamp Gastroenterology). COMLEX: Older patients with recurrent painless lower GI bleeding and aortic stenosis should be evaluated for angiodysplasia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 2 → Obj 1 — the four layers of the GI wall","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal pressure is not raised, and there is no","B":"Emboli from the valve would infarct mucosa rather than","C":"The vessel walls are not congenitally weak as the","E":"Platelets are not consumed on the valve as the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the pathogenesis of general malabsorption, the relationship between cystic fibrosis and pancreatic insufficiency, and the morphology, serum markers, genetics, and potential complications (malignancy) of Celiac disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman has bloating, loose stools, and fatigue. She eats wheat daily, and a sister has thyroid disease. She is pale and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 10.0 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL), IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is 120 U/mL (reference range: <4 U/mL), and anti-endomysial antibody is positive. The duodenum shows villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and intraepithelial lymphocytes, and HLA typing shows DQ2. Which step lets dietary gluten activate her CD4-positive T cells?","choices":{"A":"Gliadin binding to class I on enterocytes","B":"Direct toxicity of gliadin to enzymes","C":"IgE cross-linking on mucosal mast cells","D":"Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon","E":"Deamidation of gliadin peptides by TG2"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tissue transglutaminase deamidates gliadin peptides so they bind HLA-DQ2 and activate CD4-positive T cells. The high antibody titer, endomysial antibody, and flat villi are the result of that step. Gliadin is not presented on class I molecules to these CD4-positive cells. Gliadin is not directly toxic to brush-border enzymes as the activating step. IgE on mast cells is not how gluten drives this T-cell injury. Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon is not the step that activates duodenal T cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: In celiac disease, TG2 deamidates gliadin so it binds HLA-DQ2 or DQ8 and activates CD4+ T cells, and IgA anti-TG2 is the most sensitive serologic test (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: A strict gluten-free diet is the treatment, and persistent symptoms on the diet warrant evaluation for complications.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gliadin is not presented on class I molecules to","B":"Gliadin is not directly toxic to brush-border enzymes as","C":"IgE on mast cells is not how gluten drives","D":"Bacterial cleavage of gluten in the colon is not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man from southern China has bloating and gas after milk, present since adolescence. He has no weight loss. The abdomen is soft and mildly distended. Hemoglobin is 14.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and the symptoms resolve when he avoids milk. What will a duodenal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flattened villi","B":"Lipid vacuoles","C":"Unremarkable mucosa","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Foamy macrophages"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult-type lactase nonpersistence leaves the mucosa unremarkable, because the missing enzyme is not visible as villous injury. Gas after milk since adolescence, with a normal hemoglobin and relief on avoidance, is that isolated enzyme pattern. Flattened villi would mean a diffuse mucosal injury, which this milk-only symptom is not. Lipid vacuoles are the apoB-deficiency lesion and are unrelated to milk sugar. Crypt abscesses are an inflammatory colitis finding and are not expected here. Foamy macrophages are a storage disease and do not follow milk ingestion.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the lactose hydrogen breath test is positive if breath hydrogen rises more than 20 ppm over baseline (First Aid Gastrointestinal, lactose intolerance). COMLEX: congenital lactose intolerance is a rare autosomal recessive disorder (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Flattened villi would mean a diffuse mucosal injury, which","B":"Lipid vacuoles are the apoB-deficiency lesion and are unrelated","D":"Crypt abscesses are an inflammatory colitis finding and are","E":"Foamy macrophages are a storage disease and do not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl29_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the four major causes of intestinal obstruction (hernias, adhesions, volvulus, and intussusception), including their associated demographics, etiologies, and location (sigmoid colon, intussusceptum).","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has two months of colicky periumbilical pain, vomiting, and a 5 kg weight loss. She has had several weeks of stools that test positive for occult blood. The abdomen is distended, bowel sounds are high-pitched, and there is no hernia. CT shows a segment of ileum telescoped into the adjacent distal ileum, and hemoglobin is 10.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–15.5 g/dL). Which finding at surgery is most likely at the leading edge?","choices":{"A":"An intraluminal tumor","B":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches","C":"A congenital malrotation band","D":"Inspissated intestinal stool","E":"A postoperative fibrous adhesion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Occult blood, weight loss, and anemia in a 58-year-old with ileal intussusception mean an intraluminal tumor sits at the leading edge. Hyperplastic Peyer patches are the lead point of intussusception in toddlers after a viral illness. A congenital malrotation band obstructs the duodenum of a neonate. Inspissated intestinal stool plugs the ileum of a newborn with a sweat-chloride defect. A postoperative fibrous adhesion is an extraluminal band that kinks bowel rather than telescoping one segment into the next.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Childhood intussusception is usually idiopathic with lymphoid hyperplasia as the lead point, whereas adult cases usually have a tumor at the leading edge (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Adult intussusception needs surgical exploration because a neoplasm is likely.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 2 — segmentation versus peristalsis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Hyperplastic Peyer patches are the lead point of intussusception in toddlers after a viral illness","C":"A congenital malrotation band obstructs the duodenum of a neonate","D":"Inspissated intestinal stool plugs the ileum of a newborn with a sweat-chloride defect","E":"A postoperative fibrous adhesion is an extraluminal band that kinks bowel rather than telescoping one segment into the next"}},{"id":"grid_sdl29_q01","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Compare the various malabsorption syndromes (EED, IPEX, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia) by their pathogenesis, associations, morphology, and genetic components.","batch":4,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has chronic diarrhea, bulky greasy stools, and poor growth. He lives in a rural village with poor sanitation, failed to respond to two doses of an oral vaccine, and eats a mixed diet that includes wheat. There is no family history of gastrointestinal disease. He is small for his age, the abdomen is distended, and there is no rash. Hemoglobin is 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.0–14.0 g/dL). Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Fecal fat","Villus height","Anti-TG2 IgA","Serum apoB"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↓","↑","↔"],"B":["↑","↔","↔","↔"],"C":["↑","↔","↔","↓"],"D":["↑","↓","↔","↔"],"E":["↔","↔","↔","↔"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is correct. Environmental enteric dysfunction (tropical sprue) follows chronic exposure to fecal pathogens where sanitation is poor; the notes list stunting and high oral vaccine failure rates, and describe pronounced lamina propria immune cell infiltration with villous atrophy that is less advanced than in celiac disease. Loss of absorptive surface causes malabsorption with steatorrhea, and the disorder is non-genetic and not driven by gluten, so celiac serology is negative. Choice A (↑ fecal fat, ↓ villus height, ↑ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is celiac disease, in which IgA anti-tissue transglutaminase is the most sensitive serum marker. Choice B (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, as in cystic fibrosis, where intraluminal digestion fails but the mucosa is intact. Choice C (↑ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↓ serum apoB) is abetalipoproteinemia, in which absent apoB-containing lipoproteins leave enterocytes unable to export chylomicrons, causing steatorrhea in infancy with acanthocytes on the smear. Choice E (↔ fecal fat, ↔ villus height, ↔ anti-TG2 IgA, ↔ serum apoB) is lactase deficiency, an osmotic diarrhea from unabsorbed lactose without fat malabsorption. Sources: Exam 2 lecture notes (SDL 29, sections 4 and 5: general malabsorption, cystic fibrosis, celiac disease, EED, lactose intolerance, abetalipoproteinemia); Robbins Ch. 17 (malabsorption and diarrhea). The ↑ fecal fat for environmental enteric dysfunction is inferred from loss of absorptive surface, and intact villi in the pancreatic, abetalipoproteinemia, and lactase disorders are inferred because the sources describe no villous injury in them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: villous atrophy with negative celiac serology in a child from an area of poor sanitation points to environmental enteric dysfunction (Exam 2 notes, SDL 29). COMLEX: steatorrhea with normal villi suggests a luminal (pancreatic) or export (abetalipoproteinemia) defect rather than mucosal loss.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 27 Obj 2 — micelle formation and chylomicron export","isHighYield":true}]},{"sdlNumber":30,"title":"SDL 30 — Review","questions":[]}]},{"examNumber":3,"sdls":[{"sdlNumber":31,"title":"SDL 31 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Infectious Enterocolitis","questions":[{"id":"sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which bacterium is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries?","choices":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae","B":"Salmonella typhi","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica","D":"Campylobacter jejuni","E":"Shigella dysenteriae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common bacterial enteric pathogen in high-resource countries and an important cause of traveler's diarrhea and food poisoning, most often from improperly cooked chicken. Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries. Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large. Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter. Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Campylobacter are comma-shaped, flagellated, gram-negative organisms diagnosed mainly by stool culture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection can be followed by reactive arthritis, mainly in HLA-B27–positive patients (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Vibrio cholerae is endemic in India and Africa, not the leading bacterial enteric pathogen in high-income countries.","B":"Salmonella typhi is endemic in countries such as India, Mexico, and the Philippines and mainly affects travelers, not high-income populations at large.","C":"Yersinia enterocolitica causes clustered winter cases in northern and central Europe but is far less common than Campylobacter.","E":"Shigella is highly endemic in under-resourced countries."}},{"id":"sdl31_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"What is the known reservoir of Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Poultry","B":"Humans","C":"Shellfish","D":"Cattle","E":"Swine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Humans are the known reservoir of Shigella. Up to 10^9 organisms per gram of stool and an infective dose of several hundred organisms make fecal-oral spread by water and food very efficient. Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir. Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella. Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir. Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella are gram-negative, unencapsulated, nonmotile facultative anaerobes closely related to enteroinvasive E. coli (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in the United States, children in daycare, migrant workers, travelers, and nursing home residents are most affected (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Poultry is a reservoir for Campylobacter and nontyphoid Salmonella, whereas humans are the only known Shigella reservoir.","C":"Shellfish and plankton are the reservoir of Vibrio cholerae, not Shigella.","D":"Cattle are the reservoir of EHEC O157:H7, whereas Shigella has no animal reservoir.","E":"Pigs are a reservoir for Yersinia and Campylobacter, but Shigella infects only humans."}},{"id":"sdl31_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which intestinal sites does Yersinia infection preferentially involve?","choices":{"A":"Rectum, sigmoid, and left colon","B":"Stomach, duodenum, and jejunum","C":"Ileum, appendix, and right colon","D":"Esophagus, stomach, and duodenum","E":"Jejunum, transverse colon, and rectum"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Yersinia infections preferentially involve the ileum, appendix, and right colon. Organisms proliferate in lymphoid tissue, causing Peyer patch and regional node hyperplasia. The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella. Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel. The esophagus and stomach are not sites of this infection. It does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia infections are linked to pork, raw milk, and contaminated water and tend to cluster in winter (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Peyer patch and lymphatic involvement can mimic acute appendicitis in teenagers and young adults (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The rectum, sigmoid, and left colon are the favored sites of Shigella.","B":"Yersinia spares the stomach and proximal small bowel.","D":"The esophagus and stomach are not sites of this infection.","E":"It does not favor the jejunum, transverse colon, or rectum."}},{"id":"sdl31_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group is the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli (ETEC) are the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea and spread via contaminated food or water. In under-resourced countries, children younger than 2 years are particularly susceptible. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea. Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ETEC are noninvasive and produce heat-labile and heat-stable toxins (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: like cholera, ETEC infection produces mild histologic changes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes sporadic and outbreak bloody diarrhea from undercooked beef and HUS, not traveler's diarrhea.","C":"Enteroinvasive E. coli causes Shigella-like dysentery in young children in under-resourced countries, not traveler's diarrhea.","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli causes endemic infant diarrhea and nursery outbreaks in children under 2, not traveler's diarrhea.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea via biofilm, but ETEC remains the principal cause of traveler's diarrhea."}},{"id":"sdl31_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"Which E. coli group is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and invades epithelial cells without producing toxins?","choices":{"A":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteroinvasive E. coli (EIEC) resemble Shigella bacteriologically. They do not produce toxins but invade epithelial cells and cause a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader. Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC infections are most common among young children in under-resourced countries (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EIEC are transmitted via food, water, or person-to-person contact (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli produces Shiga-like toxin and is not the toxin-free, Shigella-like invader.","C":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli is noninvasive and acts through heat-labile and heat-stable toxins.","D":"Enteropathogenic E. coli attaches and effaces microvilli without invading epithelial cells.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilms on the mucosa rather than invading epithelial cells."}},{"id":"sdl31_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"What does the heat-stable toxin of ETEC bind on enterocytes?","choices":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase","B":"GM1 ganglioside receptor","C":"Rho family GTPases","D":"Guanylate cyclase","E":"Beta-1 integrin receptors"},"correct":"D","explanation":"ETEC heat-stable toxin (ST) binds guanylate cyclase and raises intracellular cGMP; its effects on epithelial ion transport resemble those of the heat-labile toxin. Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin. GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin. Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase. Beta-1 integrins are bound by Yersinia adhesins during M-cell invasion, not by ETEC heat-stable toxin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin resembles cholera toxin and activates adenylate cyclase, raising cAMP and chloride secretion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC are noninvasive, so mucosal biopsies show mild change (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Adenylate cyclase activation and raised cAMP is the action of the heat-labile toxin, not the heat-stable toxin.","B":"GM1 ganglioside is the binding receptor for cholera-like heat-labile toxin.","C":"Rho GTPases are targets of C. difficile toxins, not ETEC heat-stable toxin, which raises cGMP via guanylate cyclase.","E":"Beta-1 integrins are bound by Yersinia adhesins during M-cell invasion, not by ETEC heat-stable toxin."}},{"id":"sdl31_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":5,"stem":"What type of organism is Tropheryma whipplei?","choices":{"A":"Gram-negative curved spirochete","B":"Gram-positive actinomycete","C":"Acid-fast mycobacterial rod","D":"Flagellated intestinal protozoan","E":"Gram-negative comma-shaped rod"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Whipple's original postmortem found foamy macrophages filled with gram-positive microbes; the organism was identified as an actinomycete in 1992 and named Tropheryma whipplei. T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete. T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia. A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei. The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain positively, while T. whipplei does not. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Tropheryma whipplei is an intracellular gram-positive organism (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: intact rod-shaped bacilli can be seen within macrophages by electron microscopy (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"T. whipplei is not a spirochete; it is a gram-positive actinomycete.","D":"T. whipplei is a bacterium (actinomycete), not a flagellated protozoan like Giardia.","E":"A gram-negative comma-shaped rod describes Vibrio or Campylobacter, not the gram-positive actinomycete T. whipplei.","C":"The acid-fast stain separates the two: mycobacteria stain positively, while T. whipplei does not."}},{"id":"sdl31_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which stain result best separates Whipple disease from intestinal mycobacterial infection?","choices":{"A":"Positive mucicarmine stain","B":"Positive Gram stain result","C":"Negative acid-fast stain","D":"Positive Congo red stain","E":"Negative Giemsa stain result"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Both Whipple disease and intestinal mycobacterial infection show foamy macrophages with PAS-positive organisms. Mycobacteria are acid-fast positive, while T. whipplei is not. Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection. Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria. Giemsa stains organisms such as H. pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, trypanosomes, and Plasmodium; Whipple disease is separated by PAS positivity and mycobacteria by acid-fast staining. Robbins notes that Whipple and mycobacterial organisms are both PAS-positive, and the acid-fast stain separates them because mycobacteria stain while T. whipplei does not; a Gram stain is not the discriminator given. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease shows PAS-positive foamy macrophages in the intestinal lamina propria (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: mycobacterial infection can mimic Whipple disease, particularly in an immunocompromised host (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucicarmine highlights capsules such as Cryptococcus and does not separate Whipple disease from mycobacterial infection.","D":"Congo red stains amyloid, not intracellular bacteria, so it cannot distinguish Whipple disease from mycobacteria.","E":"Giemsa stains organisms such as H. pylori, Chlamydia, Borrelia, Rickettsia, trypanosomes, and Plasmodium; Whipple disease is separated by PAS positivity and mycobacteria by acid-fast staining.","B":"Robbins notes that Whipple and mycobacterial organisms are both PAS-positive, and the acid-fast stain separates them because mycobacteria stain while T. whipplei does not; a Gram stain is not the discriminator given."}},{"id":"sdl31_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which demographic group is Whipple disease most common in?","choices":{"A":"East Asian women","B":"West African children","C":"Native American teens","D":"South Asian older women","E":"Northern European men"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Whipple disease is most common in males of Northern European descent, particularly farmers and others with occupational exposure to soil or animals. East Asian women, West African children, Native American teens, and South Asian older women are outside that group. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease is most common in older males (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"East Asian women are outside the described group.","B":"West African children are outside the described group.","C":"Native American teenagers are outside the described group.","D":"South Asian older women are outside the described group."}},{"id":"sdl31_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which virus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide as rotavirus vaccine use has grown?","choices":{"A":"Norovirus","B":"Rotavirus","C":"Adenovirus","D":"Astrovirus","E":"Sapovirus"},"correct":"A","explanation":"With increasing rotavirus vaccine use, norovirus has become the most common cause of diarrhea worldwide, responsible for about 20% of acute gastroenteritis. Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide. Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide. First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not astrovirus. First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not sapovirus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus is an icosahedral virus with a single-stranded RNA genome (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infectious particles can be shed before symptoms and for up to 8 weeks afterward in healthy individuals (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Rotavirus incidence has fallen with vaccination, which is exactly why norovirus now leads as the cause of diarrhea worldwide.","C":"Adenovirus causes endemic pediatric diarrhea and opportunistic infection but is not the leading cause worldwide.","D":"First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not astrovirus.","E":"First Aid names norovirus the most common cause of viral diarrhea in developed countries as rotavirus falls with vaccination, not sapovirus."}},{"id":"sdl31_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Which cells does rotavirus selectively infect and destroy?","choices":{"A":"Colonic crypt stem cells","B":"Gastric parietal cells","C":"Paneth cells at crypt bases","D":"Microfold cells of Peyer patches","E":"Mature villus enterocytes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes, and immature cells repopulate the villi, so absorption falls and net secretion plus an osmotic component produce watery diarrhea. Colonic crypt stem cells are not the target; rotavirus attacks the small intestine. Gastric parietal cells are not infected by rotavirus, which causes enteritis rather than gastritis. Paneth cells at crypt bases secrete antimicrobial products and are not the cells rotavirus destroys. Microfold cells of Peyer patches are the entry route for organisms such as Salmonella, not rotavirus targets. Sources: Robbins ch 17, rotavirus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rotavirus destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes and causes malabsorptive and secretory diarrhea (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Oral rehydration is the mainstay, and vaccination prevents severe infant disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colonic crypt stem cells are not the","B":"Gastric parietal cells are not infected by","C":"Paneth cells at crypt bases secrete antimicrobial","D":"Microfold cells of Peyer patches are the"}},{"id":"sdl31_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":1,"stem":"Enteric adenovirus is a common cause of diarrhea in children. Which adult group does it also commonly affect?","choices":{"A":"Healthy pregnant women","B":"Cruise ship passengers","C":"Young adult travelers","D":"Immunocompromised adults","E":"Healthy daycare center staff"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes adenovirus as a common cause of pediatric diarrhea that also affects older immunocompromised patients, in whom biopsies often show villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia. Healthy pregnant women are not described as a group commonly affected by enteric adenovirus. Cruise ship passengers are the classic victims of norovirus outbreaks rather than adenovirus. Young adult travelers most often get traveler's diarrhea from enterotoxigenic E. coli. Healthy daycare center staff are linked to rotavirus outbreaks in daycare settings more than to adenovirus. Sources: Robbins ch 17, viral gastroenteritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enteric adenovirus causes pediatric diarrhea and disease in immunocompromised adults, with villous atrophy and crypt hyperplasia (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Consider adenovirus in a transplant recipient with prolonged diarrhea and vomiting.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Healthy pregnant women are not described as","B":"Cruise ship passengers are the classic victims","C":"Young adult travelers most often get traveler's","E":"Healthy daycare center staff are linked to"}},{"id":"sdl31_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"How is giardiasis usually documented?","choices":{"A":"Modified acid-fast stool stain","B":"Colonic biopsy for trophozoites","C":"Stool cyst immunofluorescence","D":"Serum antiparasite IgG titers","E":"Stool culture on selective agar"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Giardia infection is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples. Oral antimicrobial therapy is effective, but recurrence is common. A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia. Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented. Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar. Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples, not by serology. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia trophozoites in duodenal biopsies are pear-shaped with two equally sized nuclei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: infants, travelers, and immunocompromised patients have increased risk of severe giardiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A modified acid-fast stain detects Cryptosporidium oocysts, not Giardia.","B":"Giardia trophozoites attach to the duodenal brush border, so colonic biopsy is not how giardiasis is documented.","E":"Giardia is a protozoan diagnosed by stool cyst immunofluorescence, not by bacterial culture on selective agar.","D":"Giardiasis is usually documented by immunofluorescent detection of cysts in stool samples, not by serology."}},{"id":"sdl31_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis?","choices":{"A":"Oral vancomycin","B":"Albendazole","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Nitazoxanide","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica lacks mitochondria and Krebs cycle enzymes and depends on fermentation. Metronidazole inhibits pyruvate oxidoreductase, an enzyme required for fermentation, and is the most effective treatment for systemic disease. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis. Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica. Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: E. histolytica cysts have a chitin wall that resists gastric acid (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: amebiasis most often affects the cecum and ascending colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not amebiasis.","B":"Albendazole is an antihelminthic for worms, not a treatment for the protozoan Entamoeba.","C":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the protozoan Entamoeba histolytica.","D":"Nitazoxanide is used for Cryptosporidium and Giardia."}},{"id":"sdl31_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":1,"stem":"What property lets Cryptosporidium oocysts persist in treated but unfiltered water?","choices":{"A":"Chlorine resistance","B":"Heat resistance","C":"Chitin acid shield","D":"Surface antigen variation","E":"Bile acid tolerance"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Cryptosporidium oocysts are resistant to chlorine and persist in treated but unfiltered water; contaminated drinking water is the most common means of transmission. A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine. Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment. Heat resistance does not explain this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diagnosis of cryptosporidiosis is based on finding oocysts in the stool (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: the 1993 Milwaukee outbreak from inadequate water purification affected more than 400,000 people (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"A chitin wall gives Entamoeba cysts acid resistance, but Cryptosporidium persists in water because its oocysts resist chlorine.","D":"Surface antigen variation is how Giardia evades immunity, not how Cryptosporidium survives water treatment.","B":"Heat resistance does not explain this."}},{"id":"sdl31_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops ascending flaccid leg weakness and absent reflexes 2 weeks after diarrhea from undercooked chicken. What process most likely links the two illnesses?","choices":{"A":"Molecular mimicry of gangliosides","B":"Direct invasion of nerve roots","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release","D":"Immune complexes in muscle fibers","E":"HLA-B27 linked joint inflammation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Guillain-Barré syndrome after Campylobacter infection reflects molecular mimicry: antibodies to C. jejuni lipopolysaccharide cross-react with peripheral and central nervous system gangliosides. It is not HLA-linked. Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides. Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness. Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy. Robbins notes that Campylobacter reactive arthritis occurs mainly with HLA-B27. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: up to 40% of Guillain-Barré cases have documented Campylobacter infection in the prior 2 weeks (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Campylobacter infection is associated with postinfectious irritable bowel syndrome (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Campylobacter does not invade nerve roots; Guillain-Barré follows antibody cross-reaction with gangliosides.","C":"Toxin block of acetylcholine release is botulism, which causes descending paralysis, not post-Campylobacter ascending weakness.","D":"Immune complexes in muscle do not explain areflexic ascending weakness, which is a ganglioside-mimicry neuropathy.","E":"Robbins notes that Campylobacter reactive arthritis occurs mainly with HLA-B27."}},{"id":"sdl31_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":5,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman returns from the Philippines with fever, abdominal pain, and small salmon-colored macules on her chest. Blood cultures grow gram-negative rods. What will her terminal ileum most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures","D":"Raised Peyer patches with oval ulcers","E":"Flattened villi with lymphocytosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Typhoid fever (S. typhi) invades small intestinal M cells. Peyer patches in the terminal ileum enlarge into plateau-like elevations, and mucosal damage creates oval ulcers oriented along the ileal axis that may perforate. Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid. Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis. Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever. Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Typhoid fever). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rose spots on the chest and abdomen are a feature of typhoid fever (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: gallbladder colonization with S. typhi may be associated with gallstones and a chronic carrier state (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Flask-shaped ulcers with a narrow neck are typical of Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not typhoid.","B":"Gray pseudomembranes with volcano lesions are C. difficile colitis, not typhoid ileitis.","C":"Transmural granulomas with fissures suggest Crohn disease, not acute typhoid fever.","E":"Flattened villi with intraepithelial lymphocytosis is celiac disease, not Peyer patch hyperplasia from typhoid."}},{"id":"sdl31_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in daycare has fever, cramps, and bloody diarrhea, and stool culture grows Shigella sonnei. Which treatment should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Oral rehydration","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics","C":"Antimotility agents","D":"Acetaminophen","E":"Intravenous saline"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antidiarrheal medications can prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance, so they are contraindicated. Antibiotic treatment shortens both the clinical course and the duration of bacterial shedding. Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld. Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate. Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated. Robbins states that antidiarrheal medications prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance and are therefore contraindicated, which is a warning about antimotility agents, not acetaminophen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Infectious enterocolitis, Table 17.7) (Shigellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Shigella infections are most prominent in the left colon, and M-cell tropism can produce aphthous ulcers resembling Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Shigella dysenteriae serotype 1 releases Shiga toxin, which inhibits protein synthesis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 2 — antidiarrheal drug classes and their adverse effects","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral rehydration is safe, standard supportive care for dysentery and should not be withheld.","B":"Culture-guided antibiotics shorten the course and shedding of Shigella, so they are appropriate.","E":"Intravenous saline is appropriate rehydration for a dehydrated child and is not contraindicated.","D":"Robbins states that antidiarrheal medications prolong symptoms and delay Shigella clearance and are therefore contraindicated, which is a warning about antimotility agents, not acetaminophen."}},{"id":"sdl31_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has bloody diarrhea after a cookout with undercooked hamburgers. A week later she is pale, platelets are 40,000/µL (reference range 150,000–450,000/µL), and creatinine is rising. Which bacterial product most directly causes these complications?","choices":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin","B":"Heat-stable toxin","C":"Dispersin protein","D":"Shiga-like toxin","E":"Lipopolysaccharide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"EHEC O157:H7 from undercooked ground beef produces Shiga-like toxins and is more likely than non-O157 serotypes to cause bloody diarrhea, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and ischemic colitis. Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea. Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea. Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein that aids adherence. Lipopolysaccharide is not that toxin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC lesions show an ischemia-like morphology with surface atrophy and erosion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: antibiotic treatment can release Shiga-like toxins and raise hemolytic uremic syndrome risk, especially in children (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Heat-labile toxin is an ETEC product that raises cAMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS.","B":"Heat-stable toxin is an ETEC product that raises cGMP and causes watery diarrhea, not HUS.","C":"Dispersin is an enteroaggregative E. coli surface protein that aids adherence.","E":"Lipopolysaccharide is not the toxin that produces this syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl31_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-month-old boy in a crowded urban daycare has 5 days of watery diarrhea. Research biopsy shows bacteria tightly attached to enterocyte apical membranes with local effacement of microvilli. Which group is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Enteropathogenic E. coli (EPEC) produce attaching and effacing lesions: bacteria attach tightly to small intestinal enterocyte apical membranes and cause local effacement of microvilli. EPEC are a major cause of diarrhea in children under 2 years. Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing. Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: assays for the bacterial proteins that insert into enterocyte membranes allow molecular diagnosis of EPEC (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: EPEC are prevalent worldwide and cause endemic diarrhea and outbreaks (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — brush border of small intestinal villi","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts through toxins without attaching and effacing microvilli.","C":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli can form A/E lesions but affects the colon with bloody diarrhea, not small-intestinal watery diarrhea in a nursery.","D":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades colonic epithelium and causes dysentery rather than attaching and effacing.","E":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms biofilm in the colon rather than effacing small-intestinal microvilli."}},{"id":"sdl31_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 28-year-old man develops watery diarrhea 3 days after arriving in Mexico. Stool testing identifies an E. coli strain producing heat-labile and heat-stable toxins. What would a small-bowel biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses","B":"Ischemic surface erosion","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers","D":"Foamy lamina macrophages","E":"Minimal mucosal changes"},"correct":"E","explanation":"ETEC are noninvasive and act through toxins that raise cAMP and cGMP. Like cholera, ETEC infection results in mild histologic changes. Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change. Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea. Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the ETEC heat-labile toxin activates adenylate cyclase, while the heat-stable toxin raises cGMP (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: ETEC spread through contaminated food or water (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Neutrophilic crypt abscesses imply invasive colitis, but ETEC is noninvasive and leaves little histologic change.","B":"Ischemic surface erosion fits EHEC Shiga-like toxin injury, not toxin-mediated ETEC secretory diarrhea.","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica in the colon, not ETEC.","D":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not ETEC."}},{"id":"sdl31_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old man who farms in rural Wisconsin has diarrhea, weight loss, joint pain, and memory problems. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. What will special staining most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Acid-fast bacilli in macrophages","B":"PAS-positive lysosomal granules","C":"Congo red apple-green deposits","D":"Prussian blue iron granules","E":"Mucicarmine-staining capsules"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The macrophages of Whipple disease contain PAS-positive, diastase-resistant granules representing partially digested bacteria within lysosomes. T. whipplei is acid-fast negative, unlike mycobacteria. Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease. Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages. Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive. Robbins notes mycobacteria stain positively with the acid-fast stain while T. whipplei does not, which is how the stain separates the two foamy macrophage infiltrates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: cardiac symptoms, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms are common in Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the usual triad is diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Congo red apple-green birefringence identifies amyloid, not the bacteria-laden macrophages of Whipple disease.","D":"Prussian blue stains iron, such as in hemochromatosis, not T. whipplei within macrophages.","E":"Mucicarmine stains capsules such as Cryptococcus, whereas Whipple macrophages are PAS-positive.","A":"Robbins notes mycobacteria stain positively with the acid-fast stain while T. whipplei does not, which is how the stain separates the two foamy macrophage infiltrates."}},{"id":"sdl31_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man working on a dairy farm has steatorrhea, mesenteric lymphadenopathy, and arthralgias. Duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria filled with foamy macrophages, and tissue PCR detects Tropheryma whipplei. What best explains his malabsorption?","choices":{"A":"Impaired lymph drainage","B":"Brush-border enzyme loss","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency","D":"Bile salt deconjugation","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In Whipple disease, bacteria-laden macrophages accumulate in the small intestinal lamina propria and mesenteric lymph nodes and obstruct lymphatics. The malabsorptive diarrhea results from impaired lymph drainage. Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction. Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease. Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction. Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea and steatorrhea occur later in the course of Whipple disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Whipple disease). COMLEX: the rarity of infection despite many healthy carriers suggests genetic risk factors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — lipid absorption and chylomicron formation","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Brush-border enzyme loss fits celiac disease or viral villous damage, but Whipple malabsorption comes from lymphatic obstruction.","C":"Pancreatic enzyme deficiency causes maldigestion in pancreatic disease, not in Whipple disease.","D":"Bile salt deconjugation results from bacterial overgrowth, not from macrophage-mediated lymphatic obstruction.","E":"Toxin-driven chloride secretion is the mechanism of cholera or ETEC, not Whipple disease."}},{"id":"sdl31_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old man has had migratory joint pain for 2 years and now has diarrhea, weight loss, and a new heart murmur. Biopsy shows foamy macrophages expanding duodenal villi. What will endoscopy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions","C":"Yellow-white mucosal plaques","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dense macrophage infiltrates expand villi and give a shaggy surface. Lymphatic dilation and mucosal lipid deposition produce endoscopically visible white to yellow plaques. Macrophages can also accumulate in cardiac valves, synovium, and brain. Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease. Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease. Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease. Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the shaggy mucosal surface reflects villous expansion by dense macrophage infiltrates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: extraintestinal symptoms such as arthritis may precede malabsorption by months or years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gray adherent pseudomembranes are C. difficile colitis, not Whipple disease.","B":"Cobblestoning with skip lesions is Crohn disease, not Whipple disease.","D":"Flask-shaped undermined ulcers are Entamoeba histolytica, not Whipple disease.","E":"Scalloped duodenal folds suggest celiac disease, whereas Whipple disease shows yellow-white plaques."}},{"id":"sdl31_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman on a cruise ship develops vomiting and watery diarrhea during a shipwide outbreak and recovers in 2 days. Her 70-year-old husband, equally exposed, never becomes ill, and his saliva lacks blood group antigens. Which trait most likely protects him?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 positive genotype","B":"Heterozygous CFTR mutation","C":"Sickle hemoglobin trait carrier","D":"HLA-DQ2 haplotype expression","E":"Homozygous FUT2 inactivation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Mutations inactivating FUT2 prevent fucosylation of blood group antigens that serve as norovirus binding sites. About 20% of people of European and African descent carry this genotype. HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance. Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance. HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor. Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 inactivation (nonsecretor status), which removes the blood group antigens the virus binds; CFTR heterozygosity does not do this. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Norovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: norovirus outbreaks spread in schools, hospitals, nursing homes, and cruise ships through droplets, surfaces, and fomites (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: an infective dose of fewer than 20 viral particles makes prevention difficult (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 3 — small-intestine epithelial cell types","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HLA-B27 predisposes to reactive arthritis after Shigella and other enteric infections, not norovirus resistance.","C":"Sickle cell trait is linked to Salmonella osteomyelitis risk, not norovirus resistance.","D":"HLA-DQ2 is the celiac disease susceptibility haplotype, not a norovirus resistance factor.","B":"Natural resistance to norovirus comes from homozygous FUT2 inactivation (nonsecretor status), which removes the blood group antigens the virus binds; CFTR heterozygosity does not do this."}},{"id":"sdl31_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":5,"stem":"A 14-month-old unvaccinated boy has vomiting and watery diarrhea during a daycare outbreak. The virus has a segmented, double-stranded RNA genome. Which viral factor may mediate the enterocyte damage?","choices":{"A":"Hemagglutinin-neuraminidase","B":"Nonstructural protein 4","C":"Reverse transcriptase","D":"Spike glycoprotein","E":"Envelope fusion protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rotavirus selectively infects and destroys mature small intestinal enterocytes; damage may be mediated by nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis. Immature cells then repopulate the villus surface. Hemagglutinin and neuraminidase are envelope antigens of influenza and parainfluenza viruses; rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus. Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses and hepadnavirus (HBV), not to rotavirus, a double-stranded RNA reovirus. Rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus, so it has no envelope fusion protein. Robbins attributes rotavirus enterocyte damage to nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis, not to a spike glycoprotein. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Rotavirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is about 10 viral particles (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: rotavirus vaccines are incompletely effective in under-resourced regions, possibly due to malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Hemagglutinin and neuraminidase are envelope antigens of influenza and parainfluenza viruses; rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus.","C":"Reverse transcriptase belongs to retroviruses and hepadnavirus (HBV), not to rotavirus, a double-stranded RNA reovirus.","E":"Rotavirus is a nonenveloped reovirus, so it has no envelope fusion protein.","D":"Robbins attributes rotavirus enterocyte damage to nonstructural protein 4 (NSP4), which can induce apoptosis, not to a spike glycoprotein."}},{"id":"sdl31_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man on immunosuppression after a heart transplant has 6 days of diarrhea, vomiting, and fever. Stool PCR detects adenovirus. What would a small intestinal biopsy most likely show?","choices":{"A":"Prominent intranuclear viral inclusions","B":"Foamy macrophages in lamina propria","C":"Villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adenovirus biopsies can show epithelial degeneration but more often show nonspecific villous atrophy and compensatory crypt hyperplasia. Viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis. Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus. Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus. Robbins states that adenovirus small intestinal biopsies more often show nonspecific villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and that viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Viral gastroenteritis) (Adenovirus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenovirus is a common cause of pediatric diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: adenovirus symptoms generally resolve within 10 days (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 2 — villi and microvilli as absorptive surface area","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not adenovirus enteritis.","D":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites are amebiasis, not adenovirus.","E":"Transmural granulomas with fissures are Crohn disease, not adenovirus.","A":"Robbins states that adenovirus small intestinal biopsies more often show nonspecific villous atrophy with crypt hyperplasia and that viral nuclear inclusions are uncommon."}},{"id":"sdl31_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Mexico had bloody diarrhea a month ago and now has fever and right upper quadrant pain. CT shows a 9-cm liver abscess, and colon biopsy showed organisms ingesting red cells. How did the parasite most likely reach the liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent through bile ducts","B":"Lymphatic spread to liver","C":"Direct transmural extension","D":"Portal venous embolization","E":"Hepatic artery seeding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Entamoeba histolytica invades crypts and burrows into the lamina propria, forming flask-shaped ulcers. Parasites penetrate splanchnic vessels and embolize to the liver, producing abscesses in about 40% of patients with amebic dysentery. Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein. Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics. Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization. Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Entamoeba histolytica). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: amebic liver abscesses can exceed 10 cm and have a shaggy fibrinous lining with scant marginal inflammation (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: metronidazole is the most effective treatment for systemic amebiasis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Amebae do not ascend the bile ducts; they invade colonic splanchnic vessels and embolize through the portal vein.","B":"Entamoeba reaches the liver through the portal venous system, not lymphatics.","C":"Direct transmural extension from the colon does not explain a liver abscess, which arises from portal embolization.","E":"Colonic venous blood drains to the liver through the portal vein, not the hepatic artery."}},{"id":"sdl31_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":5,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman who drank stream water while camping has had intermittent bloating and greasy diarrhea for 4 months. Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped organisms with two nuclei on the brush border. Which parasite feature best explains her months of symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Invasion into the lamina propria","B":"Chitin wall resisting acid","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites","E":"Variant surface protein switching"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Giardia evade immune clearance through continuous modification of their major surface antigen, variant surface protein, and can persist for months or years with intermittent symptoms. They do not invade. Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border. A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection. Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia. Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Giardia lamblia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Giardia decrease brush-border enzyme expression and cause microvillous damage and epithelial apoptosis (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Giardia cysts resist chlorine, so infection is endemic in unfiltered public water supplies (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 26 → Obj 1 — carbohydrate digestion by amylase and brush-border enzymes","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Giardia does not invade the lamina propria; it stays attached to the brush border.","B":"A chitin acid-resistant wall describes Entamoeba cysts and does not explain prolonged Giardia infection.","C":"Intracellular vacuole residence describes Cryptosporidium, not Giardia.","D":"Red cell ingestion by trophozoites is diagnostic of Entamoeba histolytica, not Giardia."}},{"id":"sdl31_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":5,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with advanced HIV has weeks of profuse watery diarrhea. Ileal biopsy shows small blue spheres that appear to sit on the brush border, and stool shows oocysts. Where does the organism actually reside?","choices":{"A":"Free in lumen over villi","B":"Vacuole within enterocytes","C":"Inside lamina propria macrophages","D":"Within submucosal lymphatics","E":"Inside flask-shaped ulcers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cryptosporidium sporozoites attach to the brush border and induce enterocytes to engulf them into an endocytic vacuole, so the intracellular parasite appears to sit on the apical membrane. Sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and tight junction leak cause watery diarrhea. Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen. Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium. Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium. Robbins explains that Cryptosporidium organisms only appear to sit on the brush border and are actually enveloped by a thin layer of host cell cytoplasm, not within submucosal lymphatics. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Parasitic enterocolitis) (Cryptosporidium). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 31 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cryptosporidium causes sodium malabsorption, chloride secretion, and increased tight junction permeability, producing watery diarrhea (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: in immunodeficient hosts, organisms can involve the biliary and respiratory tracts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 1 — intestinal fluid and electrolyte absorption","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cryptosporidium is not free in the lumen.","C":"Organisms inside lamina propria macrophages describe Whipple disease or MAC, not Cryptosporidium.","E":"Flask-shaped ulcers belong to Entamoeba histolytica, not Cryptosporidium.","D":"Robbins explains that Cryptosporidium organisms only appear to sit on the brush border and are actually enveloped by a thin layer of host cell cytoplasm, not within submucosal lymphatics."}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy in Germany has 2 days of fever and right lower quadrant pain after eating undercooked pork. At laparoscopy the appendix is mildly inflamed, and the mesenteric lymph nodes and terminal ileal lymphoid tissue are enlarged. Culture on selective agar grows a gram-negative rod. Which coexisting condition would most increase his risk of sepsis from this organism?","choices":{"A":"Iron-deficiency anemia","B":"Hemochromatosis","C":"Lactase nonpersistence","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency","E":"Primary hypothyroidism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pork, northern European setting, and pseudoappendicitis with mesenteric adenitis point to Yersinia enterocolitica. A pathogenicity island gives Yersinia iron capture systems, and iron enhances its virulence and dissemination, so people with increased nonheme iron, as in hemochromatosis, are at greater risk of sepsis. Iron deficiency lowers available iron, which would not enhance this organism's virulence. Lactase nonpersistence causes osmotic symptoms with milk and does not affect bacterial invasion. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy but is not a risk factor for this sepsis. Hypothyroidism slows motility but is not linked to dissemination of this organism. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Yersinia).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Yersinia invades M cells, localizes to the ileum, appendix, and right colon, and is more virulent in iron overload (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Yersinia mesenteric adenitis can mimic appendicitis in teenagers and young adults.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Iron deficiency lowers available iron, which would not","C":"Lactase nonpersistence causes osmotic symptoms with milk and","D":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes anemia and neuropathy but","E":"Hypothyroidism slows motility but is not linked to"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy in a rural, under-resourced community has fever, cramps, and small-volume bloody stools with mucus. Stool culture grows E. coli that lacks Shiga-like toxin, heat-labile toxin, and heat-stable toxin genes. Colon biopsy shows a nonspecific acute self-limited colitis. Which pathogenic feature does this strain share with Shigella?","choices":{"A":"Effacement of small bowel microvilli","B":"Toxin-driven cAMP rise in enterocytes","C":"Aggregative fimbriae aided by dispersin","D":"Invasion of colonic epithelial cells","E":"Shiga toxin injury to glomerular vessels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"A toxin-negative E. coli causing bloody colitis in a young child in an under-resourced setting is enteroinvasive E. coli. EIEC is bacteriologically similar to Shigella and, without producing toxins, invades epithelial cells to cause acute self-limited colitis. Effacement of microvilli in attaching and effacing lesions defines enteropathogenic E. coli. A toxin-driven rise in cAMP is the mechanism of the heat-labile toxin of enterotoxigenic E. coli. Adherence fimbriae aided by dispersin define enteroaggregative E. coli. Shiga-like toxin genes are absent from this strain, so it cannot cause toxin-mediated injury. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Escherichia coli, Table 17.7).","boardPrep":"Step 1: EIEC resembles Shigella and invades colonic epithelium without toxins, whereas ETEC, EPEC, EHEC, and EAEC act through toxins or adherence (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Bloody diarrhea in a child prompts testing for Shiga toxin before choosing therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 2 — ischemic bowel disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Effacement of microvilli in attaching and effacing lesions","B":"A toxin-driven rise in cAMP is the mechanism","C":"Adherence fimbriae aided by dispersin define enteroaggregative E.","E":"Shiga-like toxin genes are absent from this strain,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man has diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias, and duodenal biopsy shows lamina propria packed with distended foamy macrophages. Besides the gut and lymph nodes, where else can these macrophages accumulate?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle","B":"Thyroid gland","C":"Cardiac valves","D":"Renal glomeruli","E":"Adrenal cortex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diarrhea, weight loss, and arthralgias with foamy macrophages filling the lamina propria indicate Whipple disease. Robbins describes accumulation of bacteria-laden macrophages in mesenteric lymph nodes, synovial membranes, cardiac valves, brain, and other sites. Skeletal muscle is not among the sites Robbins lists for these macrophages. The thyroid gland is not a described site of macrophage accumulation in Whipple disease. Renal glomeruli are not among the extraintestinal sites, which center on joints, heart, and brain. The adrenal cortex is not a recognized site of these macrophages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Whipple disease).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Whipple disease fills the lamina propria with PAS-positive foamy macrophages and can involve joints, heart valves, and brain (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Cardiac, joint, and neurologic symptoms in a patient with malabsorption should prompt consideration of Whipple disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Skeletal muscle is not among the sites","B":"The thyroid gland is not a described","D":"Renal glomeruli are not among the extraintestinal","E":"The adrenal cortex is not a recognized"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-month-old girl develops vomiting and watery diarrhea from rotavirus, but her exclusively breastfed 3-month-old brother stays well. What most likely protects the infant?","choices":{"A":"Immature villus receptors","B":"Higher gastric acidity","C":"Lower infective inoculum","D":"Faster enterocyte turnover","E":"Antibodies in breast milk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Robbins notes that children between 6 and 24 months are most vulnerable to rotavirus, probably because antibodies in breast milk protect during the first 6 months of life. The breastfed 3-month-old shares the household exposure but receives this passive protection. Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies, not to immature receptors or faster enterocyte turnover. Higher gastric acidity is not the protective factor Robbins describes for young infants. The minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is only about 10 particles, so household exposure easily exceeds it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Rotavirus).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rotavirus mainly affects children 6 to 24 months old, probably because breast milk antibodies protect younger infants, and its infective inoculum is very small (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Breastfeeding and rotavirus vaccination both reduce severe rotavirus disease in infants.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies,","B":"Higher gastric acidity is not the protective factor","C":"The minimal infective inoculum of rotavirus is only","D":"Robbins attributes early protection to breast milk antibodies,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman who recently moved from Colombia has 3 weeks of bloody diarrhea and lower abdominal pain. Colon biopsy shows flask-shaped ulcers containing trophozoites with ingested red blood cells. Which mechanism of action characterizes the first-line drug for her invasive disease?","choices":{"A":"Binding to the 50S ribosomal subunit","B":"Inhibition of DNA gyrase supercoiling","C":"Blockade of cell wall peptidoglycan","D":"Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis","E":"Toxic free radicals damaging DNA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Flask-shaped ulcers with trophozoites that have eaten red cells identify Entamoeba histolytica, and invasive amebiasis is treated with metronidazole. Metronidazole forms toxic free radical metabolites inside the organism that damage DNA, and paromomycin is added for asymptomatic cyst passers. Binding to the 50S subunit describes macrolides and clindamycin, which are not first-line for amebiasis. Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the action of fluoroquinolones. Blockade of peptidoglycan synthesis is the action of β-lactams, and amebae have no bacterial cell wall. Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis is the action of azole antifungals. Sources: First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Entamoeba histolytica; Metronidazole).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Entamoeba histolytica causes flask-shaped ulcers and liver abscesses and is treated with metronidazole, with paromomycin for cyst passers (First Aid 2025 Microbiology). COMLEX: Amebic colitis should be considered in bloody diarrhea after residence in endemic regions.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — ascending cholangitis, liver abscess, and sepsis in bacterial liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Binding to the 50S subunit describes macrolides and","B":"Inhibition of DNA gyrase is the action of","C":"Blockade of peptidoglycan synthesis is the action of","D":"Inhibition of ergosterol synthesis is the action of"}},{"id":"trial_sdl31_q06","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":3,"stem":"Eight people develop fever, cramps, and diarrhea a day after a potluck where egg salad sat unrefrigerated for hours. A 60-year-old woman who takes omeprazole daily for reflux ate a single bite yet became the most ill. Stool cultures grow a motile, H₂S-producing, non-lactose-fermenting gram-negative rod. Which factor best explains her illness after such a small exposure?","choices":{"A":"A preformed toxin ingested with the food","B":"Omeprazole-induced loss of mucosal IgA","C":"Low acid lowered the needed inoculum","D":"Raised gastric pH activating pepsinogen","E":"Omeprazole-induced loss of Th1 immunity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eggs left unrefrigerated and a motile, H₂S-producing gram-negative rod point to nontyphoidal Salmonella. Very few organisms are needed to cause infection, and the absence of gastric acid, as with acid-suppressive therapy, reduces the required inoculum further. Preformed toxin illness causes rapid vomiting without fever and would not grow an invasive rod in stool. Omeprazole lowers gastric acid and is not described as suppressing mucosal IgA. Pepsinogen is activated by low pH, so a raised pH would reduce pepsin activity. Defects in Th1 or Th17 immunity raise the risk of disseminated disease, but omeprazole does not cause them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Salmonella); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Salmonella).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Nontyphoidal Salmonella needs a small inoculum, which is lowered further by achlorhydria or acid suppression, and antibiotics are generally avoided because they prolong carriage (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: Patients on long-term proton pump inhibitors are more susceptible to enteric infections.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Preformed toxin illness causes rapid vomiting without fever","B":"Omeprazole lowers gastric acid and is not described","D":"Pepsinogen is activated by low pH, so a","E":"Defects in Th1 or Th17 immunity raise the"}},{"id":"grid_sdl31_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":4,"stem":"A 6-year-old girl has 3 days of crampy abdominal pain and frequent stools that began 2 days after she ate a hamburger at a county fair. Temperature is 37.2°C (99.0°F). Five days later she is pale and has petechiae and decreased urine output; a peripheral smear shows schistocytes, and the platelet count is 42,000/mm³ (reference range: 150,000–400,000/mm³). Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Bloody diarrhea","Epithelial invasion","Main site"]},"choices":{"A":["Absent","Present","Small bowel"],"B":["Present","Absent","Colon"],"C":["Absent","Absent","Small bowel"],"D":["Present","Present","Small bowel"],"E":["Absent","Absent","Colon"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is correct. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli O157:H7 comes from cattle and is spread by undercooked ground beef; the notes list severe bloody diarrhea, localization to the colon, and hemolytic uremic syndrome as its key complication. It injures the colon through Shiga-like toxin rather than by invading the epithelium. Choice A (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Yersinia enterocolitica, which invades the ileum and its lymphoid tissue and causes fever, pain, and diarrhea with mesenteric adenitis that can mimic appendicitis. Choice C (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, small bowel) is Vibrio cholerae, which stays in the small-intestinal lumen and causes rice-water stool. Choice D (bloody diarrhea present, epithelial invasion present, small bowel) is Salmonella typhi, which invades Peyer patches in the terminal ileum and causes enteric fever with rose spots. Choice E (bloody diarrhea absent, epithelial invasion absent, colon) is Clostridioides difficile, which causes toxin-mediated watery diarrhea after antibiotics. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 31, sections 1 and 2: gastroenteritis-causing bacteria and E. coli subgroups); Robbins Ch. 17 (Table 17.7, infectious enterocolitis). The absent invasion for EHEC and C. difficile and the invasive, usually nonbloody ileal course of Yersinia are from Robbins, since the notes do not grade them.","boardPrep":"Step 1: EHEC O157:H7 releases Shiga-like toxin, does not ferment sorbitol, and causes HUS; antibiotics can increase toxin release (Exam 3 notes, SDL 31). COMLEX: bloody diarrhea after undercooked beef followed by anemia, thrombocytopenia, and renal failure is HUS.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 31 Obj 1 — bloody versus watery bacterial diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl31_q31","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Which E. coli group forms a biofilm and causes prolonged nonbloody diarrhea, especially in immunodeficient people?","choices":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli","B":"Enteroinvasive E. coli","C":"Enteropathogenic E. coli","D":"Enteroaggregative E. coli","E":"The Shiga-toxin strain that causes hemolytic uremic syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enteroaggregative E. coli forms a biofilm and causes nonbloody watery diarrhea that can become chronic, especially in immunodeficient hosts. Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome, not a nonbloody biofilm illness. Enteroinvasive E. coli invades the colon and causes dysentery with fever. Enteropathogenic E. coli makes attaching-and-effacing lesions in the small intestine of infants, not this biofilm picture. The Shiga-toxin strain is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Enterohemorrhagic E. coli causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome, not a nonbloody biofilm illness","B":"Enteroinvasive E. coli invades the colon and causes dysentery with fever","C":"Enteropathogenic E. coli makes attaching-and-effacing lesions in the small intestine of infants, not this biofilm picture","E":"The Shiga-toxin strain is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea and hemolytic uremic syndrome"}},{"id":"sdl31_q32","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing E. coli by their symptoms, reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":1,"stem":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli produces its watery diarrhea in which part of the bowel?","choices":{"A":"Stomach","B":"Left colon only","C":"Rectum only","D":"Appendix","E":"Small intestine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Enterotoxigenic E. coli acts in the small intestine, where its toxins drive watery diarrhea. The stomach is not the segment where enterotoxigenic E. coli causes traveler's diarrhea. Left-colon invasion is the pattern of Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli, not enterotoxigenic E. coli. The rectum is not the site of enterotoxigenic E. coli toxin action. The appendix is not where enterotoxigenic E. coli localizes.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The stomach is not the segment where enterotoxigenic E. coli causes traveler's diarrhea","B":"Left-colon invasion is the pattern of Shigella and enteroinvasive E. coli, not enterotoxigenic E. coli","C":"The rectum is not the site of enterotoxigenic E. coli toxin action","D":"The appendix is not where enterotoxigenic E. coli localizes"}},{"id":"sdl31_q33","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the symptoms (CAN - cardiac, arthralgias, neurologic), PAS staining pattern (positive), demographic, and morphology (foamy macrophages) of Whipple disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which set of extraintestinal problems is grouped with Whipple disease?","choices":{"A":"Cardiac, joint, and neurologic disease","B":"Only a lung cavity with acid-fast bacilli","C":"Flask-shaped colonic ulcers and a liver abscess","D":"Migrating skin tracks after beach exposure","E":"Bloody diarrhea limited to the left colon"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Whipple disease is remembered for cardiac involvement, arthralgias, and neurologic symptoms, along with malabsorptive diarrhea and weight loss. Acid-fast bacilli in a lung cavity point to mycobacteria, and Whipple macrophages are not acid-fast. Flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess are the invasive ameba pattern. Migrating skin tracks are cutaneous larva migrans, not Whipple disease. Bloody left-colon diarrhea is dysentery, not the Whipple pattern.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Acid-fast bacilli in a lung cavity point to mycobacteria, and Whipple macrophages are not acid-fast","C":"Flask-shaped ulcers and a liver abscess are the invasive ameba pattern","D":"Migrating skin tracks are cutaneous larva migrans, not Whipple disease","E":"Bloody left-colon diarrhea is dysentery, not the Whipple pattern"}},{"id":"sdl31_q34","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate gastroenteritis causing bacteria by their symptoms (bloody vs water diarrhea), reservoir and transmission route, epidemiology and geography, shape, localization, and complications (Table 17.7).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old man develops watery diarrhea, cramps, and fever on day 6 of broad-spectrum antibiotics in the hospital. He is tender over the colon without peritonitis. The leukocyte count is 18,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL) and albumin is 2.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Stool is not grossly bloody. Which complication is this overgrowth particularly known for?","choices":{"A":"Toxic megacolon","B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome","C":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome from undercooked beef","D":"A liver abscess full of anchovy-paste material","E":"Rose spots and ileal perforation from a human-only bloodstream infection"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Clostridioides difficile overgrowth after antibiotics can progress to toxic megacolon, perforation, and a high chance of recurrence. Guillain-Barré syndrome is the post-infectious complication of Campylobacter, not this antibiotic-associated colitis. Hemolytic uremic syndrome after undercooked beef is the enterohemorrhagic E. coli pattern. An anchovy-paste liver abscess follows invasive amebiasis. Rose spots and ileal perforation are typhoid fever, which is not triggered by a few days of broad-spectrum antibiotics.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Guillain-Barré syndrome is the post-infectious complication of Campylobacter, not this antibiotic-associated colitis","C":"Hemolytic uremic syndrome after undercooked beef is the enterohemorrhagic E. coli pattern","D":"An anchovy-paste liver abscess follows invasive amebiasis","E":"Rose spots and ileal perforation are typhoid fever, which is not triggered by a few days of broad-spectrum antibiotics"}},{"id":"sdl31_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the symptoms, reservoir, transmission routes, and epidemiology of common viral causes of gastroenteritis (norovirus, rotavirus, adenovirus).","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old nursing student develops vomiting and watery diarrhea one day after a single sick classmate used the same bathroom. Dozens of students are ill within 48 hours, including some who never shared a meal. She recovers in two days. Sodium is 137 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and she stays afebrile after the first day. Which property best explains an outbreak this explosive?","choices":{"A":"A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that infects only mature enterocytes","B":"An extremely small infective dose with spread on surfaces and fomites","C":"Acid-fast oocysts that must be swallowed in thousands","D":"Invasion of Peyer patches with rose spots","E":"A toxin that is made only in undercooked beef"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Norovirus spreads by the fecal-oral route, droplets, and fomites, and fewer than 20 particles are enough to infect. A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that hits mature enterocytes describes rotavirus, not this fomite outbreak. Acid-fast oocysts are Cryptosporidium, and this illness did not require a huge swallowed dose. Peyer-patch invasion with rose spots is typhoid fever. A beef-associated toxin is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea rather than a two-day vomiting outbreak.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A segmented double-stranded RNA genome that hits mature enterocytes describes rotavirus, not this fomite outbreak","C":"Acid-fast oocysts are Cryptosporidium, and this illness did not require a huge swallowed dose","D":"Peyer-patch invasion with rose spots is typhoid fever","E":"A beef-associated toxin is enterohemorrhagic E. coli, which causes bloody diarrhea rather than a two-day vomiting outbreak"}},{"id":"sdl31_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old immunocompetent girl has five days of watery diarrhea after swimming in a community pool. She is drinking and has no blood in the stool. Temperature is 37.4°C and the abdomen is soft. Sodium is 136 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and creatinine is 0.5 mg/dL (reference range: 0.3–0.7 mg/dL). Stool shows acid-fast oocysts. Which drug is appropriate for this host?","choices":{"A":"Albendazole","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Nitazoxanide","D":"Praziquantel","E":"A luminal agent used only after metronidazole for amebic abscess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In an immunocompetent host, nitazoxanide is the treatment for cryptosporidiosis, along with fluid replacement. Albendazole is a benzimidazole used for intestinal nematodes and tissue cestodes, not this coccidian. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca. Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes and is not the treatment for these acid-fast oocysts. A luminal amebicide after metronidazole is the sequence for invasive amebiasis, not for cryptosporidiosis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole is a benzimidazole used for intestinal nematodes and tissue cestodes, not this coccidian","B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca","D":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes and is not the treatment for these acid-fast oocysts","E":"A luminal amebicide after metronidazole is the sequence for invasive amebiasis, not for cryptosporidiosis"}},{"id":"sdl31_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the associated disease, transmission route, diagnostic method, and treatment of enterocolitis causing parasites (Giardia lamblia, Entamoeba histolytica, Cryptosporidium).","batch":2,"stem":"A 27-year-old man has had greasy stools and bloating for two months after drinking from a stream. He has no fever and no blood in the stool. The abdomen is soft. Sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 4.0 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Duodenal biopsy shows pear-shaped, binucleate organisms on the brush border. Which drug treats this infection?","choices":{"A":"Tinidazole","B":"Albendazole as a microtubule inhibitor for roundworms","C":"Praziquantel","D":"Ivermectin","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Giardiasis is treated with metronidazole, tinidazole, or nitazoxanide. Albendazole's microtubule block is aimed at nematodes, not this flagellate. Praziquantel is for tapeworms and flukes. Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes and is not the treatment for this brush-border flagellate. Pyrantel pamoate is an over-the-counter option for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Albendazole's microtubule block is aimed at nematodes, not this flagellate","C":"Praziquantel is for tapeworms and flukes","D":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes and is not the treatment for this brush-border flagellate","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is an over-the-counter option for pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm"}}]},{"sdlNumber":32,"title":"SDL 32 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: IBS, IBD, Diverticular Disease, and Polyps","questions":[{"id":"sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What do endoscopic and microscopic evaluations show in irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Normal mucosal findings","B":"Patchy crypt abscesses","C":"Thickened collagen band","D":"Diffuse villous blunting","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Despite real symptoms, endoscopic and microscopic evaluations are normal in IBS, so the diagnosis rests on clinical symptoms and functional testing. Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal. A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal. Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS. Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS has no structural abnormalities and is most common in middle-aged females (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS is divided into diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, and mixed subtypes (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Patchy crypt abscesses indicate inflammatory bowel disease, which must be excluded because IBS biopsies are normal.","C":"A thickened subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, not IBS, whose mucosa is normal.","D":"Diffuse villous blunting suggests celiac disease, an organic cause excluded before diagnosing IBS.","E":"Scattered aphthous ulcers point to Crohn disease, whereas IBS shows no mucosal abnormality."}},{"id":"sdl32_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":5,"stem":"Which pair of conditions is associated with irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sacroiliitis and uveitis","B":"Gallstones and kidney stones","C":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis","D":"Fibromyalgia and depression","E":"Pyoderma and episcleritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The Elentra objective lists fibromyalgia and mood disorders as IBS associations, and First Aid notes IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and mood disorders such as anxiety and depression. The distractors are extraintestinal or metabolic complications of inflammatory bowel disease. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (FA) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS may be associated with fibromyalgia and with anxiety or depression (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: candidate IBS genes include serotonin reuptake transporters and cannabinoid receptors (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"FA"},{"id":"sdl32_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the first-line treatment for irritable bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Oral glucocorticoid course","B":"Lifestyle and diet changes","C":"Long-term opioid therapy","D":"Empiric antibiotic course","E":"Elective subtotal colectomy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The Elentra objective and First Aid both give lifestyle modification and dietary changes as first-line IBS treatment. Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease, and colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease. Long-term opioids and empiric antibiotics are not first-line. Sources: SDL 32 Elentra Objective 1; First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: first-line IBS treatment is lifestyle modification and dietary change (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: 5-HT3 receptor antagonists are effective in many cases of diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Long-term opioids are not IBS therapy.","D":"Empiric antibiotics are not first-line therapy.","A":"Glucocorticoids target inflammatory bowel disease.","E":"Colectomy is not indicated for a disorder without structural disease."}},{"id":"sdl32_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Which gene encoding an intracellular sensor of bacterial muramyl dipeptide is most strongly associated with Crohn disease?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"CFTR","C":"NOD2","D":"HFE","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"C","explanation":"NOD2 encodes an intracellular sensor of muramyl dipeptide and is the gene most strongly associated with Crohn disease in European populations, though risk alleles occur in about 30% of patients. NOD2 variants are protective in ulcerative colitis. APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease. CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2. HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease. KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ATG16L1 and IRGM, which act in autophagosome formation, are additional Crohn disease risk genes (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: very early onset IBD can be monogenic, most often from IL10 or IL-10 receptor mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations drive familial adenomatous polyposis and sporadic colon cancer, not Crohn disease.","B":"CFTR mutations cause cystic fibrosis, not Crohn disease, which is linked to the bacterial sensor NOD2.","D":"HFE mutations cause hereditary hemochromatosis, not Crohn disease.","E":"KRAS is an oncogene in colorectal adenoma progression, not a sensor of muramyl dipeptide."}},{"id":"sdl32_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"Which form of microscopic colitis shows a strong association with celiac disease?","choices":{"A":"Collagenous colitis","B":"Diversion colitis","C":"Ischemic colitis","D":"Ulcerative proctitis","E":"Lymphocytic colitis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphocytic colitis is strongly associated with celiac disease and autoimmune diseases including Graves disease, rheumatoid arthritis, and autoimmune gastritis. Its collagen layer is of normal thickness, and intraepithelial lymphocytes often exceed 20 per 100 colonocytes. Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band. Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis. Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis. Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon segment, shows prominent mucosal lymphoid follicles, and resolves when fecal flow is restored. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis is most common in older females and has a normal endoscopic appearance (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: both forms of microscopic colitis cause chronic, nonbloody, watery diarrhea without weight loss (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Collagenous colitis is defined by a thick subepithelial collagen band.","C":"Ischemic colitis is a vascular injury with abnormal endoscopy, not a form of microscopic colitis.","D":"Ulcerative proctitis is a limited form of ulcerative colitis with visible mucosal disease, not microscopic colitis.","B":"Diversion colitis arises in a surgically defunctioned colon segment, shows prominent mucosal lymphoid follicles, and resolves when fecal flow is restored."}},{"id":"sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"Which antibodies are often present in Crohn disease but typically absent in ulcerative colitis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Saccharomyces antibodies","B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease patients often have anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies, which are typically absent in ulcerative colitis. In contrast, up to 75% of ulcerative colitis patients have p-ANCA. Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease. Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease. Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease may be positive for anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae antibodies (ASCA) (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: fecal calprotectin helps distinguish IBD activity from noninflammatory disease such as IBS (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Antimitochondrial antibodies mark primary biliary cholangitis, not Crohn disease.","C":"Anti-smooth muscle antibodies mark autoimmune hepatitis, not Crohn disease.","D":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase IgA is the serologic test for celiac disease, not Crohn disease.","E":"Anti-parietal cell antibodies mark autoimmune gastritis, not Crohn disease."}},{"id":"sdl32_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colonic wall do sigmoid diverticula form?","choices":{"A":"Bases of colonic crypts","B":"Lymphoid aggregate sites","C":"Vasa recta entry points","D":"Mesenteric fat attachments","E":"Haustral fold crests"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Focal gaps in the inner circular muscle occur where nerves and arterial vasa recta penetrate it. In the colon these gaps are unreinforced, so raised intraluminal pressure herniates mucosa through them. Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate. Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle. Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle. Robbins places colonic diverticula at unreinforced sites where nerves and vasa recta cross the inner circular muscle layer. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: false diverticula occur especially where vasa recta penetrate the muscularis externa (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticula of the GI tract). COMLEX: colonic diverticula are 0.5 to 1 cm flask-like outpouchings that are uniformly spaced (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticula are not at crypt bases; they herniate through muscle gaps where vasa recta penetrate.","B":"Lymphoid aggregates are not the weak points; diverticula form at vasa recta entry gaps in the circular muscle.","E":"Haustral fold crests are not the weak points; diverticula herniate where vasa recta penetrate the inner circular muscle.","D":"Robbins places colonic diverticula at unreinforced sites where nerves and vasa recta cross the inner circular muscle layer."}},{"id":"sdl32_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern promotes sigmoid diverticular disease by raising intraluminal pressure?","choices":{"A":"High intake of dairy foods","B":"Frequent spicy food intake","C":"Heavy daily caffeine intake","D":"Gluten-containing grains","E":"Low fiber, low stool bulk"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Low-fiber diets reduce stool bulk, so the sigmoid colon generates exaggerated peristaltic contractions and high intraluminal pressure that push mucosa through weak points where vasa recta cross the muscle. A high intake of dairy foods is not the dietary factor Robbins or First Aid ties to diverticula. Frequent spicy food intake is not linked to diverticula in these sources. Heavy daily caffeine intake is not described as a cause of colonic diverticula. Gluten-containing grains trigger celiac disease in susceptible people, not diverticular disease. Sources: Robbins ch 17, sigmoid diverticular disease; First Aid 2025 GI, diverticulosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-fiber diets raise sigmoid intraluminal pressure and drive pseudodiverticula at vasa recta entry points (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Fiber supplementation may ease symptoms of diverticular disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A high intake of dairy foods is","B":"Frequent spicy food intake is not linked","C":"Heavy daily caffeine intake is not described","D":"Gluten-containing grains trigger celiac disease in susceptible"}},{"id":"sdl32_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the sex ratio of Meckel diverticula?","choices":{"A":"Twice as common in males","B":"Twice as common in females","C":"Equal in males and females","D":"Four times more in females","E":"Ten times more in males"},"correct":"A","explanation":"By the rule of 2s, Meckel diverticula have a male-to-female ratio of 2:1, occur in about 2% of people, lie within 2 feet of the ileocecal valve, are about 2 inches long, and are most often symptomatic by age 2. Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s. Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance. Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong. A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a Meckel diverticulum extends from the antimesenteric side of the ileum and may contain ectopic pancreatic tissue (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: about 4% of Meckel diverticula ever become symptomatic (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Meckel diverticula are twice as common in males, not females, per the rule of 2s.","C":"Meckel diverticula are not equal by sex; the rule of 2s gives a 2:1 male predominance.","D":"Meckel diverticula favor males 2:1, so a fourfold female excess is wrong.","E":"A tenfold male excess overstates the 2:1 male-to-female ratio of Meckel diverticula."}},{"id":"sdl32_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"Where in the colon are hyperplastic polyps most commonly found?","choices":{"A":"Right colon","B":"Transverse colon","C":"Cecum and appendix","D":"Left colon","E":"Terminal ileum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are most common in the left colon, are typically less than 5 mm, and are frequently multiple in the sigmoid colon and rectum. The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon. The transverse colon is not the typical site. Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix. The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are the most common colonic polyps and are predominantly located in the rectosigmoid region (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colonic hyperplastic polyps are typically discovered in the sixth and seventh decades (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The right colon is the usual site of sessile serrated polyps, whereas hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon.","B":"The transverse colon is not the typical site; hyperplastic polyps cluster.","C":"Hyperplastic polyps favor the left colon, sigmoid, and rectum rather than the cecum and appendix.","E":"The terminal ileum is small bowel, while hyperplastic polyps are colonic lesions mostly of the left colon."}},{"id":"sdl32_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the proposed pathogenesis of colonic hyperplastic polyps?","choices":{"A":"Biallelic APC gene inactivation","B":"Germline STK11 gene mutation","C":"Repeated mucosal prolapse injury","D":"Mismatch repair gene silencing","E":"Delayed epithelial shedding"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps are thought to result from decreased epithelial turnover and delayed shedding of surface epithelial cells, leading to a piling up of goblet and absorptive cells. Mucosal prolapse drives solitary rectal ulcer polyps instead. Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding. Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps. Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp. Robbins attributes repeated cycles of injury and healing with mucosal prolapse to inflammatory polyps of solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, not hyperplastic polyps. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperplastic polyps are composed of mature goblet and absorptive cells with a serrated surface architecture (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: epithelial hyperplasia can occur as a nonspecific reaction overlying any mass and may signal an adjacent lesion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Biallelic APC inactivation starts adenomas, but hyperplastic polyps are nonneoplastic and come from delayed epithelial shedding.","B":"Germline STK11 mutation causes Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not hyperplastic polyps.","D":"Mismatch repair silencing drives microsatellite-unstable neoplasia, not the nonneoplastic hyperplastic polyp.","C":"Robbins attributes repeated cycles of injury and healing with mucosal prolapse to inflammatory polyps of solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, not hyperplastic polyps."}},{"id":"sdl32_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":1,"stem":"What underlying abnormality causes solitary rectal ulcer syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Prior pelvic radiation for carcinoma","B":"Poor anorectal sphincter relaxation","C":"Hypoperfusion of the rectal watershed","D":"Recurrent enteric bacterial infection","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa in the rectum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Impaired relaxation of the anorectal sphincter creates a sharp angle at the anterior rectal shelf, causing recurrent abrasion and ulceration of the overlying mucosa. Entrapment of the resulting polyp in the fecal stream leads to mucosal prolapse. Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation. Recurrent infection is not the cause. Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer. Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and recurrent abrasion of the anterior rectal shelf, not from radiation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer syndrome presents with rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and an inflammatory lesion of the anterior rectal wall (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: inflammatory polyps form through repeated cycles of injury and healing (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Hypoperfusion of a watershed causes ischemic colitis, not solitary rectal ulcer syndrome, which comes from poor sphincter relaxation.","D":"Recurrent infection is not the cause.","E":"Ectopic gastric mucosa is the ulcer-causing feature of Meckel diverticulum, not of solitary rectal ulcer.","A":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome comes from impaired anorectal sphincter relaxation and recurrent abrasion of the anterior rectal shelf, not from radiation."}},{"id":"sdl32_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":5,"stem":"Which gene is the most common mutation identified in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"KRAS","C":"MUTYH","D":"SMAD4","E":"PTEN"},"correct":"D","explanation":"SMAD4, which encodes a signaling intermediate in the TGF-beta pathway, is the most common mutation in autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. BMPR1A mutations account for other cases, and together they explain fewer than half of patients. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis. KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis. MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis is an autosomal dominant syndrome of numerous hamartomatous polyps in the colon, stomach, and small bowel (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: patients with SMAD4 mutations often have both juvenile polyposis and hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, not juvenile polyposis.","B":"KRAS is an oncogene in sporadic adenomas, not the germline cause of juvenile polyposis.","C":"MUTYH mutations cause an autosomal recessive adenomatous polyposis, not autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis.","E":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden syndrome hamartomas, whereas juvenile polyposis most often involves SMAD4."}},{"id":"sdl32_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Where are the polyps of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome most common?","choices":{"A":"Gastric antrum","B":"Small intestine","C":"Rectosigmoid colon","D":"Distal esophagus","E":"Ascending colon"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are most common in the small intestine, although they may occur in the stomach and colon and rarely in the bladder and lungs. Grossly they are large, pedunculated, and lobulated. Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine. Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine. The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine. The ascending colon is a less common site. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome features numerous hamartomatous polyps throughout the GI tract with hyperpigmented macules (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome presents at a median age of 11 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps can occur in the stomach but are most common in the small intestine.","C":"Peutz-Jeghers polyps are less common in the colon than in the small intestine.","D":"The distal esophagus is not a typical site of Peutz-Jeghers polyps, which favor the small intestine.","E":"The ascending colon is a less common site."}},{"id":"sdl32_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which pathway does the Peutz-Jeghers tumor suppressor STK11 regulate?","choices":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling","B":"Wnt signaling","C":"AMPK signaling","D":"DNA mismatch repair","E":"Hedgehog signaling"},"correct":"C","explanation":"STK11 is a tumor suppressor that regulates AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) pathways, which control cell polarization and brake growth and anabolic metabolism. Germline heterozygous loss-of-function STK11 mutations occur in about half of familial cases. BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway. Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers. DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK. Hedgehog signaling is the pathway activated in medulloblastoma, not the AMPK pathway that STK11 regulates. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: the second normal STK11 copy is often lost through somatic mutation in Peutz-Jeghers-associated cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"BMP receptor signaling is the TGF-beta pathway mutated in juvenile polyposis (BMPR1A), not the STK11 pathway.","B":"Wnt signaling is disrupted by APC loss in adenomas, not by STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers.","D":"DNA mismatch repair defects cause Lynch syndrome, not Peutz-Jeghers, whose STK11 regulates AMPK.","E":"Hedgehog signaling is the pathway activated in medulloblastoma, not the AMPK pathway that STK11 regulates."}},{"id":"sdl32_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman has crampy lower abdominal pain on about 5 days each month for 6 months, with bloating and alternating loose and hard stools. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal. Which additional feature best supports the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools","B":"Weight loss over 10%","C":"Elevated stool calprotectin","D":"Pain eased by defecation","E":"Symptom onset after age 60"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins lists IBS criteria as abdominal pain or discomfort at least 3 days per month over 3 months with improvement after defecation and a change in stool frequency or form, after other causes are excluded. An elevated fecal calprotectin points toward organic disease. Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS. Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS. Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome); First Aid Gastrointestinal (Irritable bowel syndrome, Inflammatory bowel diseases). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is recurrent abdominal pain with at least 2 of: related to defecation, change in stool frequency, change in stool form, so under FA no single feature is mandatory (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: IBS prevalence in high-income countries is about 5% to 10%, peaking between 20 and 40 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 4 — colonic motility, haustral contractions, and mass movements","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Nocturnal bloody stools are an alarm feature of organic disease such as IBD, not support for IBS.","B":"Weight loss over 10% suggests organic disease that must be excluded, not IBS.","E":"Onset after age 60 raises concern for organic disease, whereas IBS typically begins in younger adults."}},{"id":"sdl32_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":5,"stem":"A 34-year-old man has 2 years of urgent loose stools and intermittent abdominal cramping. Colonoscopy is normal, and he does not have celiac disease. A test shows excess fecal bile acids. Which mechanism best explains this subtype of his disorder?","choices":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury","B":"Subepithelial collagen band","C":"Bile acid malabsorption","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excess bile acid synthesis or bile acid malabsorption is one identified cause of diarrhea-predominant IBS, likely through effects of bile acids on intestinal motility and epithelial ion transport. Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded. A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS. Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS. Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: diarrhea-predominant IBS shows increased colonic contractions and transit rates (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: opioids and anticholinergic psychoactive drugs are also used for diarrhea-predominant IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Gluten-induced villous injury is celiac disease, which has already been excluded.","B":"A subepithelial collagen band is collagenous colitis, which a normal colonoscopy with biopsy would reveal, not IBS.","D":"Bacterial invasion of crypts would cause inflammatory colitis with abnormal biopsies, not IBS.","E":"Granulomatous ileal disease is Crohn disease, which would be visible on colonoscopy, unlike IBS."}},{"id":"sdl32_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman returns from Mexico after a week of fever and watery diarrhea. Six months later she has recurring cramps, bloating, and variable stool form with normal colonoscopy and biopsies. Which mechanism is proposed for this onset pattern?","choices":{"A":"Shift in the gut microbiome","B":"Antibodies to enteric neurons","C":"Persistent mucosal granulomas","D":"Chronic bacterial tissue invasion","E":"Subepithelial collagen deposits"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Some IBS patients relate onset to a bout of infectious gastroenteritis, suggesting that immune activation or a shift in the gut microbiome triggers disease. Granulomas, invasion, and collagen deposition would produce abnormal biopsies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is chronic and may be diarrhea-predominant, constipation-predominant, or mixed (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Irritable bowel syndrome). COMLEX: defective brain-gut axis signaling is proposed in IBS (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has 8 months of right lower quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a draining perianal tract. Resection of a strictured terminal ileum shows a thick rubbery wall with deep fissures. Which additional gross finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Continuous disease of mucosa","B":"Thin wall with smooth serosa","C":"Superficial broad ulcers","D":"Flat atrophic mucosal surface","E":"Creeping fat over the serosa"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In transmural Crohn disease, mesenteric adipose tissue frequently extends over the serosal surface as creeping fat. Wall thickening from edema, fibrosis, and muscular hypertrophy drives strictures. Continuous mucosal disease with a normal serosa fits ulcerative colitis. A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat. Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers. A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa. Robbins describes ulcerative colitis as continuous disease without skip lesions, while Crohn disease can be discontinuous with skip lesions. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease shows cobblestone mucosa, creeping fat, and a string sign on small bowel follow-through (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: disease often recurs at the anastomosis, and up to 40% need more resection within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A thin wall with smooth serosa contradicts Crohn disease, whose transmural inflammation thickens the wall and draws creeping fat.","C":"Superficial broad-based ulcers are typical of ulcerative colitis, whereas Crohn disease has deep linear fissuring ulcers.","D":"A flat atrophic surface does not fit Crohn disease, which shows cobblestoning from deep ulcers between spared mucosa.","A":"Robbins describes ulcerative colitis as continuous disease without skip lesions, while Crohn disease can be discontinuous with skip lesions."}},{"id":"sdl32_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"A 30-year-old man with newly diagnosed ulcerative colitis has pruritus and an alkaline phosphatase of 420 U/L (reference range 44–147 U/L). MRCP shows beaded intrahepatic and extrahepatic bile ducts. When should colonoscopic dysplasia surveillance begin?","choices":{"A":"About 8 years after diagnosis","B":"At the time of diagnosis","C":"Beginning at 50 years of age","D":"After the first bloody relapse","E":"Upon confirmation of pancolitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Patients with IBD typically enter surveillance about 8 years after diagnosis. The major exception is IBD with primary sclerosing cholangitis, which carries greater cancer risk, so surveillance starts at diagnosis. Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis. Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent. Robbins notes that surveillance usually begins about 8 years after IBD diagnosis, but patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis are enrolled at diagnosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease) (Colitis-associated neoplasia). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is associated with primary sclerosing cholangitis and MPO-ANCA/p-ANCA (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: colitis-associated cancer risk rises sharply 8 to 10 years after onset (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 5 — primary sclerosing cholangitis and cholestatic disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Age 50 is the average-risk screening start, but PSC-associated IBD requires surveillance from diagnosis.","D":"Surveillance does not wait for a relapse; PSC with IBD warrants surveillance from diagnosis.","E":"Surveillance in PSC-associated colitis begins at diagnosis regardless of disease extent.","A":"Robbins notes that surveillance usually begins about 8 years after IBD diagnosis, but patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis are enrolled at diagnosis."}},{"id":"sdl32_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman has 4 months of watery, nonbloody diarrhea without weight loss. Colonoscopy is normal, but biopsies show more than 20 intraepithelial lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes with a collagen layer of normal thickness. Her medications are listed. Which is most likely contributing?","choices":{"A":"Metformin","B":"Lisinopril","C":"Levothyroxine","D":"Naproxen","E":"Amlodipine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"These biopsies show lymphocytic microscopic colitis. Similar histologic changes can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors. Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors; metformin is not among the drugs implicated. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Microscopic colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: microscopic colitis histology shows lamina propria lymphocytes with intraepithelial lymphocytosis or a thickened subepithelial collagen band (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Microscopic colitis). COMLEX: collagenous colitis has a subepithelial collagen layer thicker than 10 µm (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 4 — large-intestine lamina propria and the collagen layer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Microscopic colitis changes like these can be induced by NSAIDs and possibly proton pump inhibitors; metformin is not among the drugs implicated.","B":"Lisinopril is not part of that answer.","C":"Levothyroxine is not part of that answer.","E":"Amlodipine is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl32_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old man had left lower quadrant pain and fever with a WBC of 16,000/µL (reference range 4,500–11,000/µL) 2 months ago. He now has recurrent urinary tract infections and air in his urine. Which complication best explains his current findings?","choices":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding","B":"Sigmoid volvulus","C":"Ischemic stricture","D":"Free perforation","E":"Colovesical fistula"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inflammation in sigmoid diverticula can extend through the thin wall to cause serositis, abscesses, sinus tracts, and perforation. A tract into the bladder produces pneumaturia and recurrent infection. Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections. Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine. An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia. Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulitis complications include abscess, colovesical fistula with pneumaturia, obstruction, and perforation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulitis). COMLEX: surgery is generally reserved for severe or recurrent diverticulitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diverticular bleeding causes hematochezia, not pneumaturia and recurrent urinary infections.","B":"Sigmoid volvulus causes obstruction and distention, not air in the urine.","C":"An ischemic stricture causes obstruction, not a tract into the bladder producing pneumaturia.","D":"Free perforation causes acute peritonitis, not the chronic pneumaturia of a colovesical fistula."}},{"id":"sdl32_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old woman with a lifelong low-fiber diet has dozens of sigmoid outpouchings on colonoscopy, but small bowel imaging shows no outpouchings. Which structural difference best explains the sparing of her small bowel?","choices":{"A":"Lower intraluminal small bowel pressure","B":"Absence of penetrating vasa recta","C":"Denser submucosal lymphoid tissue","D":"Continuous longitudinal muscle coat","E":"Thicker muscularis mucosae layer"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Both bowels have gaps in the inner circular muscle where nerves and vasa recta penetrate. In the small intestine these gaps are reinforced by a continuous outer longitudinal layer, whereas in the colon that layer is gathered into taeniae coli. Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps. Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing. The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps; the continuous outer longitudinal layer does. The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitudinal muscle layer reinforces the gaps where vasa recta penetrate; lymphoid density is not the reason. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Sigmoid diverticular disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diverticulosis is caused by intraluminal pressure and focal weakness in the colonic wall (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Diverticulosis). COMLEX: hypertrophy of the inner circular muscle is commonly associated with diverticulosis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Small bowel pressure is not the key difference; the small bowel's continuous longitudinal coat reinforces the muscle gaps.","B":"Vasa recta penetrate both small bowel and colon, so their absence does not explain small bowel sparing.","E":"The muscularis mucosae is not what reinforces the gaps.","C":"The small bowel is spared because a continuous outer longitudinal muscle layer reinforces the gaps where vasa recta penetrate; lymphoid density is not the reason."}},{"id":"sdl32_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy has episodes of screaming with legs drawn up, vomiting, and a stool of blood and mucus. A sausage-shaped right abdominal mass is palpated. At surgery, a congenital ileal pouch lined by gastric mucosa is found at the leading point. Which complication of this pouch explains his presentation?","choices":{"A":"Diverticulitis","B":"Intussusception","C":"Small bowel volvulus","D":"Peptic perforation","E":"Adhesive obstruction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A Meckel diverticulum can act as a lead point for intussusception and can also cause hematochezia, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum. Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis. Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction. Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis. Adhesive obstruction follows surgery. Sources: First Aid Gastrointestinal (Meckel diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum can cause painless hematochezia, RLQ pain, intussusception, volvulus, or obstruction near the terminal ileum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Meckel diverticulum). COMLEX: symptomatic Meckel cases can mimic acute appendicitis or obstruction (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 21 → Obj 3 — vitelline duct remnants and Meckel diverticulum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Meckel diverticulitis mimics appendicitis.","C":"Volvulus causes twisting with obstruction.","D":"Peptic perforation from ectopic gastric mucosa causes peritonitis.","E":"Adhesive obstruction follows surgery."}},{"id":"sdl32_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 64-year-old man has several small smooth colonic polyps. Histology shows mature goblet and absorptive cells with a saw-tooth outline limited to the upper third of the crypts. Which premalignant lesion must be distinguished from these?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Juvenile hamartoma","C":"Sessile serrated polyp","D":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","E":"Lymphoid aggregate polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, but they must be distinguished from sessile serrated polyps, which are histologically similar and premalignant. A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp. A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic. An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic. First Aid classifies serrated polyps as neoplastic lesions with CpG island methylator phenotype that must be separated from hyperplastic polyps, unlike a lymphoid aggregate polyp. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps show a saw-tooth crypt pattern, BRAF mutations, and CpG island methylation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: hyperplastic polyps may occur singly but are frequently multiple (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A tubular adenoma is dysplastic from the start and does not mimic the saw-tooth pattern of a hyperplastic polyp.","B":"A juvenile hamartoma has cystic mucin-filled glands, not serrated crypt tops, and is not the key mimic.","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp is nonneoplastic regenerating mucosa, not a premalignant serrated mimic.","E":"First Aid classifies serrated polyps as neoplastic lesions with CpG island methylator phenotype that must be separated from hyperplastic polyps, unlike a lymphoid aggregate polyp."}},{"id":"sdl32_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with 15 years of ulcerative pancolitis has colonoscopy showing many mucosal projections, some joined at their tips into bridges. Biopsies show no dysplasia. How do these projections form?","choices":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth","B":"Clonal adenomatous epithelial growth","C":"Submucosal lymphoid hyperplasia","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands","E":"Regrowth of mucosa between ulcers"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In ulcerative colitis, isolated islands of regenerating mucosa bulge into the lumen to create pseudopolyps, and their tips may fuse into mucosal bridges. Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa. Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps. Robbins explains that pseudopolyps are isolated islands of regenerating mucosa whose tips may fuse into mucosal bridges, not lymphoid hyperplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Inflammatory bowel disease); Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: inflammatory pseudopolyps are due to mucosal erosion in inflammatory bowel disease (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: pseudopolyps are marked in ulcerative colitis and moderate in Crohn disease (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hamartomatous stromal overgrowth describes Peutz-Jeghers or juvenile polyps, not UC pseudopolyps.","B":"Clonal adenomatous growth would be dysplastic, but these biopsies show none; pseudopolyps are regenerating mucosa.","D":"Mucin retention in cystic glands describes juvenile retention polyps, not UC pseudopolyps.","C":"Robbins explains that pseudopolyps are isolated islands of regenerating mucosa whose tips may fuse into mucosal bridges, not lymphoid hyperplasia."}},{"id":"sdl32_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has rectal bleeding, mucus discharge, and chronic straining. A polypoid lesion is on the anterior rectal wall. Biopsy shows erosion and epithelial hyperplasia. Which additional histologic feature indicates mucosal prolapse?","choices":{"A":"Lamina propria smooth muscle","B":"Noncaseating submucosal granulomas","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen","D":"Apoptotic bodies in crypt bases","E":"Foamy macrophages in lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Solitary rectal ulcer syndrome polyps show mixed inflammation, erosion, and epithelial hyperplasia with prolapse-induced fibromuscular hyperplasia of the lamina propria. Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse. Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse. Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse. Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibromuscular hyperplasia (smooth muscle) in the lamina propria, not by apoptotic crypt bodies. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic and inflammatory polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary rectal ulcer polyps are an example of an inflammatory lesion (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: granulation tissue-like capillary proliferation in the lamina propria reflects repeated erosion (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Noncaseating granulomas suggest Crohn disease, not mucosal prolapse.","C":"Thickened subepithelial collagen is collagenous colitis, not prolapse.","E":"Foamy lamina propria macrophages are Whipple disease, not mucosal prolapse.","D":"Prolapse in solitary rectal ulcer syndrome is marked by fibromuscular hyperplasia (smooth muscle) in the lamina propria, not by apoptotic crypt bodies."}},{"id":"sdl32_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has painless rectal bleeding, and a red mass prolapses through the anus. The resected pedunculated polyp is smooth, with cystic glands filled with mucin and inflammatory debris in an expanded lamina propria. He has no family history of polyps. What is the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory pseudopolyp","B":"Juvenile retention polyp","C":"Tubulovillous adenoma","D":"Sessile hyperplastic polyp","E":"Submucosal leiomyoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps, also called retention polyps, are usually solitary, often rectal, and occur mostly before age 5. They show cystic mucin-filled glands in inflamed lamina propria and can prolapse through the anal sphincter. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks. A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old. A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands. First Aid classifies juvenile polyps as hamartomatous polyps of growths of normal colonic tissue with distorted architecture, not a submucosal leiomyoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: solitary hamartomatous polyps do not have significant risk of transformation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: dysplasia is rare in sporadic juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises in chronic IBD, which this child lacks.","C":"A tubulovillous adenoma is a dysplastic lesion of adults, not a cystic mucin-filled polyp in a 4-year-old.","D":"A hyperplastic polyp is small with serrated crypt tops, not cystic mucin-filled glands.","E":"First Aid classifies juvenile polyps as hamartomatous polyps of growths of normal colonic tissue with distorted architecture, not a submucosal leiomyoma."}},{"id":"sdl32_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman has dozens of hamartomatous polyps in the colon, recurrent nosebleeds, lip telangiectasias, and a pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. Her father had similar polyps. Which malignancy is she at greatest risk of developing by her mid-40s?","choices":{"A":"Medulloblastoma","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma","C":"Colonic adenocarcinoma","D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic islet tumor"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with dysplasia in polyps and separate adenomas, and 30% to 50% of patients develop colonic adenocarcinoma by age 45. Pulmonary arteriovenous malformations are recognized extraintestinal features. Hepatocellular carcinoma, pancreatic islet tumors, medulloblastoma, and papillary thyroid carcinoma are not that cancer risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: juvenile polyposis syndrome is associated with increased risk of colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colectomy may be needed to limit chronic hemorrhage from ulcerated juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is not a juvenile polyposis risk.","E":"Pancreatic islet tumors are not juvenile polyposis features.","A":"Medulloblastoma is not the cancer linked to this syndrome.","B":"Papillary thyroid carcinoma is not the cancer linked to this syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl32_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":5,"stem":"An 11-year-old boy has dark blue-brown macules on his lips, nostrils, and palms, and imaging shows multiple GI polyps. A resected polyp has an arborizing network of smooth muscle and normal epithelium. Which neoplasm warrants surveillance starting at birth?","choices":{"A":"Sex cord stromal tumor","B":"Gastric adenocarcinoma","C":"Small intestinal carcinoma","D":"Pancreatic carcinoma","E":"Lung adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries a lifetime cancer risk near 40%. Surveillance begins at birth for testicular sex cord tumors, in late childhood for gastric and small intestinal cancers, and in the second and third decades for colon, pancreatic, breast, lung, ovarian, and uterine cancers. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hamartomatous polyps). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome carries increased risk of breast and GI cancers, including colorectal, gastric, small bowel, and pancreatic (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: smooth muscle mixed with lamina propria distinguishes Peutz-Jeghers polyps from juvenile polyps (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with fibromyalgia has 8 months of recurrent lower abdominal pain that eases after defecation, bloating that worsens through the day, and alternating hard and loose stools. CBC, tissue transglutaminase IgA, and colonoscopy with biopsies are normal. She wants to avoid medication. Which dietary change most directly targets the mechanism of her bloating?","choices":{"A":"Strict gluten avoidance to let villi recover","B":"Limiting poorly absorbed short carbohydrates","C":"High-protein meals to slow colonic transit","D":"Low-fat meals to reduce bile acid secretion","E":"Prolonged fasting to rest the colonic mucosa"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Recurrent pain relieved by defecation with altered stool form, normal colonoscopy and biopsies, and an associated fibromyalgia point to irritable bowel syndrome, where poorly absorbed short-chain carbohydrates reach the colon, undergo bacterial fermentation, and produce the gas that drives bloating, so limiting them is a first-line diet change. Strict gluten avoidance treats celiac disease, which her normal transglutaminase serology and normal biopsies argue against. High-protein meals are not an IBS therapy and do not address colonic fermentation of carbohydrates. Low-fat meals target bile acid or fat malabsorption, which is not the mechanism of bloating in IBS. Prolonged fasting is not a recommended IBS measure because the mucosa is structurally normal in this disorder. Sources: Course master PDF (Irritable Bowel Syndrome); Robbins Ch. 17 (Irritable bowel syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBS is a clinical diagnosis of pain related to defecation with altered bowel habits and normal mucosa; fermentation of poorly absorbed short-chain carbohydrates causes gas and bloating (course master PDF, IBS). COMLEX: start with diet and lifestyle changes and screen for fibromyalgia, depression, and anxiety.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — pharmacologic therapy of IBS by symptom pattern","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Strict gluten avoidance treats celiac disease, which","C":"High-protein meals are not an IBS therapy","D":"Low-fat meals target bile acid or fat","E":"Prolonged fasting is not a recommended IBS"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 27-year-old man develops bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus. Colonoscopy shows continuous mucosal inflammation from the distal colon upward. Which recent habit change most likely preceded his onset?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarette smoking","B":"Starting oral contraceptives","C":"Starting daily NSAID use","D":"Adopting a high fiber diet","E":"Beginning heavy alcohol use"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Unlike Crohn disease, onset of ulcerative colitis can be associated with smoking cessation, and smoking may give partial symptomatic relief. Smoking is a strong risk factor for Crohn disease. NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not produce continuous grossly visible inflammation. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ulcerative colitis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ulcerative colitis is treated with 5-aminosalicylic acid preparations, 6-mercaptopurine, infliximab, or colectomy (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: most ulcerative colitis patients have at least one relapse within 10 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 38 → Obj 3 — drug therapy of ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"NSAIDs are a trigger for microscopic colitis, which does not produce continuous grossly visible inflammation."}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathogenesis, symptoms, complications (diverticulitis, abscess, fistula, obstruction, perforation) and demographics of Meckel diverticulum and sigmoid diverticular disease.","batch":3,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy has passed large painless maroon stools for 2 days. He is pale, and his abdomen is soft and nontender. Hemoglobin is 7.9 g/dL (reference range: 10.5–13.5 g/dL). Upper endoscopy and colonoscopy are normal, and a technetium-99m pertechnetate scan shows a focus of uptake in the right lower quadrant. Which mechanism best explains his bleeding?","choices":{"A":"Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein","B":"Telescoping of ileum with venous infarction","C":"Erosion of a vasa recta at a colonic outpouching","D":"Acid from ectopic mucosa ulcerating nearby ileum","E":"Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Painless brisk bleeding in a toddler with a right lower quadrant focus on a pertechnetate scan indicates a Meckel diverticulum containing ectopic gastric mucosa, which secretes acid that ulcerates the adjacent ileal mucosa and bleeds. Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein describes variceal or hemorrhoidal bleeding, and the pertechnetate scan marks gastric mucosa, not veins. Telescoping of ileum is intussusception, which causes colicky pain, vomiting, and currant jelly stool rather than painless bleeding in a calm child. Erosion of a vasa recta at a colonic outpouching is sigmoid diverticular bleeding in older adults, and his colonoscopy was normal. Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen describes appendicitis, which presents with pain and fever rather than painless hemorrhage. Sources: Course master PDF (Meckel Diverticulum); Robbins Ch. 17 (Meckel diverticulum).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Meckel diverticulum is a true diverticulum from a persistent vitelline duct; ectopic gastric mucosa secretes acid that ulcerates nearby ileum, and a pertechnetate scan detects it (course master PDF, Meckel). COMLEX: painless rectal bleeding in a young child calls for a Meckel scan and surgical resection if symptomatic.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 34 → Obj 5 — high-fat, low-fiber diets in nutritional disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rupture of a dilated submucosal vein describes","B":"Telescoping of ileum is intussusception, which causes","C":"Erosion of a vasa recta at a","E":"Mucosal inflammation from an obstructed lumen describes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps by their symptoms, anatomical location, malignant potential, morphology, and causes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old man has fatigue and 5 kg of weight loss. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) with low ferritin. Colonoscopy shows a 4 cm ulcerated, friable sigmoid mass. Superficial biopsies from the edge of the mass show mature goblet and absorptive cells with serration limited to the upper crypts and no dysplasia. What is the best interpretation of these biopsies?","choices":{"A":"A benign hyperplastic polyp needing no follow-up","B":"A sessile serrated lesion that explains the mass","C":"Reactive change bordering an unsampled lesion","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp from chronic colitis","E":"A juvenile hamartoma with cystic dilated glands"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hyperplastic epithelium can form as a nonspecific reaction next to or over any mass, so bland serrated epithelium from the rim of an ulcerated mass in a man with weight loss and iron deficiency is a clue to an adjacent lesion that has not been sampled and needs deeper biopsies. A benign hyperplastic polyp has no malignant potential, but it would not explain a 4 cm ulcerated mass with anemia and weight loss. A sessile serrated lesion shows serration down to the crypt base with lateral crypt growth, which these upper-crypt changes lack. An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises from cycles of injury and healing in colitis, and he has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. A juvenile hamartoma is a childhood lesion with cystic glands and inflamed stroma, not mature serrated epithelium. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hyperplastic polyps; Sessile serrated polyps).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hyperplastic polyps show serration confined to the upper crypt and have no malignant potential, but hyperplasia also occurs as a reaction beside a mass (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: a biopsy that does not explain the endoscopic lesion should prompt repeat, deeper sampling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A benign hyperplastic polyp has no malignant","B":"A sessile serrated lesion shows serration down","D":"An inflammatory pseudopolyp arises from cycles of","E":"A juvenile hamartoma is a childhood lesion"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 15-year-old girl has had recurrent episodes of colicky abdominal pain; the last one was a jejunal intussusception, and the resected lead point was a hamartomatous polyp. She has dark blue-brown macules on her lips, buccal mucosa, and palms. Her father had similar spots. For which malignancy should she have surveillance as an adult?","choices":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma","B":"Cerebellar medulloblastoma","C":"Abdominal desmoid tumor","D":"Clear cell renal carcinoma","E":"Pancreatic adenocarcinoma"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Hamartomatous small-bowel polyps causing intussusception plus mucocutaneous pigmentation that involves the buccal mucosa in an autosomal dominant pattern indicate Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, which carries a markedly increased lifetime cancer risk that includes pancreatic adenocarcinoma, along with colon, breast, lung, and gynecologic cancers. Hepatocellular carcinoma is linked to cirrhosis and chronic viral hepatitis, not to hamartomatous polyposis. Cerebellar medulloblastoma is the CNS tumor of Turcot syndrome, a variant of adenomatous polyposis or Lynch syndrome. Abdominal desmoid tumor is an extracolonic lesion of Gardner syndrome, a form of familial adenomatous polyposis. Clear cell renal carcinoma is associated with von Hippel-Lindau disease rather than Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Peutz-Jeghers syndrome); First Aid 2025 GI (hereditary polyposis syndromes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is autosomal dominant STK11 loss with hamartomatous polyps, buccal pigmentation, intussusception, and increased breast, GI, and pancreatic cancer risk (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: arrange lifelong multi-organ cancer surveillance and screen first-degree relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 2 — familial adenomatous polyposis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hepatocellular carcinoma is linked to cirrhosis and","B":"Cerebellar medulloblastoma is the CNS tumor of","C":"Abdominal desmoid tumor is an extracolonic lesion","D":"Clear cell renal carcinoma is associated with"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":5,"stem":"A 22-year-old man with Crohn disease has resection of a fibrotic terminal ileal stricture. The surgeon plans to remove as little bowel as possible. Which outcome makes this bowel preservation important?","choices":{"A":"Postoperative toxic megacolon","B":"Loss of colonic water absorption","C":"Adhesive small bowel obstruction","D":"Recurrence at the anastomosis","E":"Postsurgical bile reflux gastritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Crohn disease often recurs at the site of anastomosis, and up to 40% of patients need additional resections within 10 years, so bowel preservation is an important part of surgical planning. Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and is unrelated to how much ileum is preserved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Crohn disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 32 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Crohn disease is treated with glucocorticoids, azathioprine, antibiotics, and biologics such as infliximab and adalimumab (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Inflammatory bowel diseases). COMLEX: extensive small bowel Crohn disease can cause hypoalbuminemia and malabsorption of vitamin B12 and bile salts (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Toxic megacolon is a complication of ulcerative colitis and is unrelated to how much ileum is preserved."}},{"id":"grid_sdl32_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":4,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman who has never smoked has had 3 months of recurrent bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and lower abdominal cramps that ease after defecation. Stool studies for enteric pathogens are negative. Colonoscopy is performed, and biopsies of the involved segment are examined. Which of the following sets of findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["Gross lesions","Trans­mural","Collagen band","Many IELs"]},"choices":{"A":["+","+","−","−"],"B":["−","−","+","+"],"C":["+","−","−","−"],"D":["−","−","−","+"],"E":["−","−","−","−"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (+ gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is correct. Ulcerative colitis begins in the rectum and extends proximally without skip lesions; nonsmokers are at higher risk, and attacks of bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus, tenesmus, and cramps relieved by defecation are typical. The mucosa is diffusely granular and friable with broad-based ulcers and pseudopolyps, but inflammation is limited to the mucosa and superficial submucosa, with neutrophilic crypt abscesses and crypt distortion. Choice A (+ gross lesions, + transmural, − collagen band, − many IELs) is Crohn disease, with transmural inflammation, skip lesions, cobblestoning, strictures, and fistulae, usually in the terminal ileum and favored by smoking. Choice B (− gross lesions, − transmural, + collagen band, + many IELs) is collagenous colitis, a microscopic colitis of older women with a normal-looking colon and a dense subepithelial collagen band >10 µm. Choice D (− gross lesions, − transmural, − collagen band, + many IELs) is lymphocytic colitis, with normal collagen thickness but >20 intraepithelial T lymphocytes per 100 colonocytes; both microscopic colitides cause nonbloody watery diarrhea. Choice E (all −) is irritable bowel syndrome, in which endoscopy and biopsies are characteristically normal. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 32, sections 1–2: IBS, Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis); Robbins Ch. 17 (inflammatory bowel disease) [Robbins]. The absence of an intraepithelial lymphocytosis and collagen band in Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis is inferred, as the notes list these features only for microscopic colitis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: continuous rectal-up mucosal inflammation in a nonsmoker with bloody diarrhea is ulcerative colitis; transmural disease with skip lesions is Crohn (Exam 3 notes, SDL 32). COMLEX: chronic watery diarrhea in an older woman with a normal colonoscopy calls for biopsy to find microscopic colitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 38 — IBD drugs (5-ASA, budesonide, anti-TNF)","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl32_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":1,"stem":"A clinical diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome requires abdominal pain on at least how many days, and over what span?","choices":{"A":"One day in a year","B":"Daily pain for one week only","C":"Pain only at night for ten years","D":"At least 3 days per month over 3 months","E":"A single episode of bloody diarrhea"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Irritable bowel syndrome requires pain or discomfort on at least 3 days per month over a 3-month period, plus two of the stool-related features, after organic disease is excluded. A single day in a year does not meet the duration required for this diagnosis. One week of daily pain is too short for the 3-month requirement. Nocturnal pain for years is a warning of organic disease, not the stated threshold. A single episode of bloody diarrhea is an organic alarm, not the symptom count for irritable bowel syndrome.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A single day in a year does not meet the duration required for this diagnosis","B":"One week of daily pain is too short for the 3-month requirement","C":"Nocturnal pain for years is a warning of organic disease, not the stated threshold","E":"A single episode of bloody diarrhea is an organic alarm, not the symptom count for irritable bowel syndrome"}},{"id":"sdl32_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the diagnostic criteria, associations (fibromyalgia, mood disorders), and first line treatments (lifestyle/diet modifications) for irritable bowel syndrome.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has had loose urgent stools and crampy lower abdominal pain for eight months. Colonoscopy and biopsies are normal, celiac serology is negative, and the pain eases a little after she stools. She is afebrile. Hemoglobin is 13.4 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 1 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Diet changes have failed. Which drug class both eases the visceral pain and slows colonic transit?","choices":{"A":"A low-dose tricyclic antidepressant","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Linaclotide","D":"Polyethylene glycol","E":"Infliximab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"For diarrhea-predominant irritable bowel syndrome, a low-dose tricyclic such as amitriptyline reduces visceral pain and slows colonic transit. Lubiprostone opens chloride channels and is used when constipation predominates. Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C agonist for constipation-predominant disease. Polyethylene glycol is an osmotic laxative and would not be chosen to slow transit. Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease, and this mucosa is normal.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone opens chloride channels and is used when constipation predominates","C":"Linaclotide is a guanylate cyclase-C agonist for constipation-predominant disease","D":"Polyethylene glycol is an osmotic laxative and would not be chosen to slow transit","E":"Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease, and this mucosa is normal"}},{"id":"sdl32_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Crohn disease is driven mainly by which T-cell pattern?","choices":{"A":"Th1 and Th17 activation","B":"A mucosal Th2-like response","C":"Isolated IgE against a food antigen","D":"Only regulatory T cells with no effector response","E":"A pure B-cell response without T cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Crohn disease is an immune dysregulation with Th1 and Th17 activation. A mucosal Th2-like response is the pattern described for ulcerative colitis. IgE against a food antigen is allergy, not the Crohn T-cell pattern. Loss of regulation may contribute, but the effector pattern named for Crohn disease is Th1 and Th17. Crohn disease is not a pure B-cell illness without T cells.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A mucosal Th2-like response is the pattern described for ulcerative colitis","C":"IgE against a food antigen is allergy, not the Crohn T-cell pattern","D":"Loss of regulation may contribute, but the effector pattern named for Crohn disease is Th1 and Th17","E":"Crohn disease is not a pure B-cell illness without T cells"}},{"id":"sdl32_q34","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":1,"stem":"Collagenous colitis is identified by which finding under the surface epithelium?","choices":{"A":"Caseating granulomas through the full wall","B":"Flask-shaped ulcers","C":"A normal collagen layer with no lymphocytes","D":"Hundreds of adenomas","E":"A dense collagen band thicker than 10 µm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Collagenous colitis has a dense subepithelial collagen band thicker than 10 µm, with a normal endoscopic appearance. Caseating granulomas are not the lesion of collagenous colitis, and Crohn granulomas are noncaseating. Flask-shaped ulcers are amebic. A normal collagen thickness is the contrast with lymphocytic colitis, which is defined by intraepithelial lymphocytes instead. Hundreds of adenomas are polyposis, not microscopic colitis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Caseating granulomas are not the lesion of collagenous colitis, and Crohn granulomas are noncaseating","B":"Flask-shaped ulcers are amebic","C":"A normal collagen thickness is the contrast with lymphocytic colitis, which is defined by intraepithelial lymphocytes instead","D":"Hundreds of adenomas are polyposis, not microscopic colitis"}},{"id":"sdl32_q35","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had bloody diarrhea with stringy mucus for four months. Colonoscopy shows continuous granular mucosa from the rectum proximally, without skip areas, and biopsies stay in the mucosa. He does not smoke. Hemoglobin is 11.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and albumin is 3.4 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Medicines have failed. Which operation can cure the colonic disease?","choices":{"A":"A short stricturoplasty that must be repeated at the old join","B":"Colectomy","C":"Removal of only the terminal ileum","D":"Drainage of a perianal fistula with no bowel resection","E":"Ligation of the superior mesenteric artery"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colectomy cures ulcerative colitis, whose inflammation is limited to the colon and rectum. Repeated resection at an anastomosis is the surgical problem in Crohn disease, which colectomy does not cure. The terminal ileum is the usual Crohn site, and this continuous rectal-based colitis spares that pattern. A perianal fistula is a Crohn complication, not the lesion that colectomy is used to remove here. Ligating the superior mesenteric artery would infarct midgut and is not treatment of this colitis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repeated resection at an anastomosis is the surgical problem in Crohn disease, which colectomy does not cure","C":"The terminal ileum is the usual Crohn site, and this continuous rectal-based colitis spares that pattern","D":"A perianal fistula is a Crohn complication, not the lesion that colectomy is used to remove here","E":"Ligating the superior mesenteric artery would infarct midgut and is not treatment of this colitis"}},{"id":"trial_sdl32_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Differentiate the various inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn disease, ulcerative colitis, microscopic colitis) by their location, epidemiology, pathogenesis, morphology, complications, symptoms, and treatment.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man has right-lower-quadrant pain, nonbloody diarrhea, and a perianal fistula. He smokes a pack a day. Resection shows skip lesions, creeping fat, and a noncaseating granuloma. Hemoglobin is 12.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and C-reactive protein is 28 mg/L (reference range: <10 mg/L). Which exposure is most likely to keep this disease active?","choices":{"A":"Quitting cigarettes","B":"A high-fiber breakfast","C":"Oral iron for a low ferritin","D":"Continued cigarette smoking","E":"Drinking pasteurized milk"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Cigarette smoking is a strong risk factor that worsens Crohn disease activity. Stopping cigarettes is not what worsens Crohn disease; smoking itself does. Fiber is a diet measure in irritable bowel syndrome and is not the exposure that drives this transmural disease. Oral iron treats deficiency and is not the exposure that keeps Crohn disease active. Pasteurized milk is not the risk that worsens Crohn disease.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Stopping cigarettes is not what worsens Crohn disease; smoking itself does","B":"Fiber is a diet measure in irritable bowel syndrome and is not the exposure that drives this transmural disease","C":"Oral iron treats deficiency and is not the exposure that keeps Crohn disease active","E":"Pasteurized milk is not the risk that worsens Crohn disease"}},{"id":"sdl32_q36","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"A single juvenile polyp in a toddler is usually found in which site?","choices":{"A":"The stomach, by the dozens","B":"The rectum","C":"The esophagus","D":"The gallbladder","E":"The pancreas"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sporadic juvenile polyps are solitary rectal lesions in children younger than 5 years and have no malignant potential. Dozens of polyps throughout the gut are juvenile polyposis, not the usual solitary toddler polyp. The esophagus is not the usual site of a sporadic juvenile polyp. The gallbladder does not harbor juvenile retention polyps. The pancreas is not the site of a solitary juvenile polyp.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dozens of polyps throughout the gut are juvenile polyposis, not the usual solitary toddler polyp","C":"The esophagus is not the usual site of a sporadic juvenile polyp","D":"The gallbladder does not harbor juvenile retention polyps","E":"The pancreas is not the site of a solitary juvenile polyp"}},{"id":"sdl32_q37","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand hamartomatous polyps based on their morphology, mutated genes and pathways involved, and their associated syndromes (juvenile polyposis and Peutz-Jeghers syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 14-year-old boy has crampy pain, and surgery reduces a small-bowel intussusception led by a lobulated polyp. The polyp shows an arborizing network of smooth muscle invested by normal epithelium. He has no rectal bleeding and no telangiectasias. Hemoglobin is 13.0 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which skin finding belongs with this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Dark blue-brown macules on the lips and palms","B":"Flaky-paint desquamation from protein deficiency","C":"Rose spots on the chest","D":"A serpiginous track on the foot","E":"Bronze skin from iron overload"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome pairs hamartomatous polyps that have arborizing smooth muscle with dark blue-brown macules on the lips, nostrils, buccal mucosa, and palms. Flaky-paint skin is kwashiorkor, not this hamartoma syndrome. Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever. A serpiginous foot track is cutaneous larva migrans. Bronze skin is the pigment of hereditary hemochromatosis.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Flaky-paint skin is kwashiorkor, not this hamartoma syndrome","C":"Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever","D":"A serpiginous foot track is cutaneous larva migrans","E":"Bronze skin is the pigment of hereditary hemochromatosis"}}]},{"sdlNumber":33,"title":"SDL 33 — Pathology - Small Intestine & Colon: Malignant Lesions, Anal Canal, and Appendix","questions":[{"id":"sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which histologic hallmark defines colorectal adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Crypt base serration","B":"Mucosal prolapse fibrosis","C":"Epithelial dysplasia","D":"Granulomatous inflammation","E":"Hamartomatous smooth muscle"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The hallmark of colorectal adenomas is epithelial dysplasia, seen as nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and stratification, often with prominent nucleoli and fewer goblet cells. Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps. Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas. Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma. Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps are neoplastic and arise via the chromosomal instability pathway with APC and KRAS mutations (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: colorectal adenomas range from 0.3 to 10 cm and have a velvet or raspberry-like surface (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serration is the architecture of hyperplastic and sessile serrated polyps.","B":"Prolapse-induced fibromuscular change marks solitary rectal ulcer polyps, not adenomas.","D":"Granulomatous inflammation suggests Crohn disease, not an adenoma.","E":"Arborizing smooth muscle defines Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas."}},{"id":"sdl33_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which colorectal polyp type carries the greatest malignant potential?","choices":{"A":"Tubular adenoma","B":"Villous adenoma","C":"Hyperplastic polyp","D":"Hamartomatous polyp","E":"Inflammatory polyp"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Villous adenomas harbor cancer more often than tubular adenomas, partly because they tend to be larger, and size remains the strongest predictor of malignancy. Tubular adenomas are usually small and have less malignant potential than villous adenomas. Hyperplastic polyps are generally nonneoplastic, small, and found in the rectosigmoid. A solitary hamartomatous polyp has no significant risk of transformation. Inflammatory polyps form from mucosal erosion and regrowth in IBD and are not neoplastic. Sources: Robbins ch 17, colonic adenomas; First Aid 2025 GI, colonic polyps.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Villous histology carries more malignant potential than tubular, and size is the key predictor (Robbins ch 17; First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Large villous adenomas warrant complete removal and closer surveillance.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tubular adenomas are usually small and have","C":"Hyperplastic polyps are generally nonneoplastic, small, and","D":"A solitary hamartomatous polyp has no significant","E":"Inflammatory polyps form from mucosal erosion and"}},{"id":"sdl33_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":5,"stem":"Why does intramucosal carcinoma within an adenoma carry little metastatic potential?","choices":{"A":"Absent mucosal lymphatics","B":"Intact muscularis propria","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Functional lymphatic channels are absent in the colonic mucosa, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential and complete polypectomy is generally curative. Invasion beyond the muscularis mucosae carries a risk of spread. The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomas are usually asymptomatic but may present with occult bleeding (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: invasion into the submucosal stalk of a pedunculated polyp constitutes invasive adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"The muscularis propria is not the barrier; lack of mucosal lymphatics limits spread, and invasion past the muscularis mucosae already carries risk.","C":"Low tumor mitotic activity is not part of that answer.","D":"Peritumoral lymphocyte barrier is not part of that answer.","E":"Rapid surface cell shedding is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl33_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"How many adenomas are needed for a diagnosis of classic familial adenomatous polyposis?","choices":{"A":"At least 10","B":"At least 25","C":"At least 100","D":"At least 50","E":"At least 1000"},"correct":"C","explanation":"At least 100 polyps are necessary for a diagnosis of classic FAP, though thousands may be present. Apart from their number, FAP polyps look like sporadic adenomas. Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100. Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100. Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP. Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP polyps arise after puberty, are pancolonic, and involve the rectum (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: about 75% of FAP cases are inherited and the remainder arise from de novo mutations (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ten adenomas is far below the FAP threshold of at least 100.","B":"Twenty-five adenomas is below the classic FAP threshold of at least 100.","D":"Fifty adenomas falls short of the at-least-100 threshold for classic FAP.","E":"Thousands may be present, but the diagnostic threshold is at least 100, not 1000."}},{"id":"sdl33_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"What does the protein product of the gene mutated in familial adenomatous polyposis normally do?","choices":{"A":"Repairs base mismatches","B":"Activates RAS signaling","C":"Inhibits mTOR signaling","D":"Blocks TGF beta signals","E":"Degrades beta catenin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"APC is a key negative regulator of Wnt signaling. It binds and promotes degradation of beta catenin, so its loss lets beta catenin enter the nucleus and drive MYC and cyclin D1 transcription. Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC. APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence. Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC. APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FAP is an autosomal dominant mutation of the APC tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q21-q22 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancers without APC mutations often carry beta catenin mutations that resist APC-dependent degradation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Repairing base mismatches is the job of mismatch repair genes such as MSH2 and MLH1, not APC.","B":"APC does not activate RAS; it degrades beta-catenin, while KRAS activation is a separate later step in the adenoma-carcinoma sequence.","C":"Inhibiting mTOR via AMPK is the role of STK11 in Peutz-Jeghers, not APC.","D":"APC does not block TGF-beta; TGF-beta pathway genes such as SMAD4 are lost later in the sequence or in juvenile polyposis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":1,"stem":"Which extraintestinal manifestation of familial adenomatous polyposis can be detected at birth?","choices":{"A":"Multiple mandibular osteomas","B":"Abdominal wall desmoid tumors","C":"Multiple epidermoid skin cysts","D":"Retinal pigment hypertrophy","E":"Impacted supernumerary teeth"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Congenital hypertrophy of the retinal pigment epithelium can be detected at birth and may be an adjunct to early FAP screening. Osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental anomalies appear later in variants such as Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gardner syndrome adds osseous and soft tissue tumors and impacted or supernumerary teeth to FAP (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: specific APC mutations partly explain FAP variants (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which genes are mutated in most patients with Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"APC or CTNNB1","B":"BRCA1 or BRCA2","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A","D":"KRAS or BRAF","E":"MSH2 or MLH1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"There are at least five mismatch repair genes, but most HNPCC patients have mutations in MSH2 or MLH1. Patients inherit one mutant and one normal allele. APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes. SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome. KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome. Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mismatch repair genes MSH2 or MLH1; BRCA1 and BRCA2 are not the genes involved. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome is an autosomal dominant mismatch repair defect with about 80% progression to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: the human genome has about 50,000 to 100,000 microsatellites (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC mutations cause FAP, not Lynch syndrome, which involves mismatch repair genes.","C":"SMAD4 or BMPR1A mutations cause juvenile polyposis, not Lynch syndrome.","D":"KRAS and BRAF are somatic oncogene mutations in sporadic tumors, not the germline defect in Lynch syndrome.","B":"Most patients with Lynch syndrome carry mutations in the mismatch repair genes MSH2 or MLH1; BRCA1 and BRCA2 are not the genes involved."}},{"id":"sdl33_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"How do colon cancers in hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer typically differ from sporadic colon cancers?","choices":{"A":"They arise among hundreds of adenomas","B":"They appear at younger ages","C":"They are mostly left-sided tumors","D":"They show chromosomal instability","E":"They carry germline APC mutations"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Colon cancers in Lynch syndrome tend to occur at younger ages than sporadic cancers and are often in the right colon, driven by mismatch repair loss and microsatellite instability. Hundreds of adenomas define familial adenomatous polyposis, whereas Lynch syndrome is nonpolyposis. Lynch syndrome cancers favor the proximal, right colon rather than the left side. Chromosomal instability marks the APC pathway, while Lynch tumors show microsatellite instability. Germline APC mutations cause familial adenomatous polyposis, while Lynch syndrome involves MSH2 or MLH1. Sources: Robbins ch 17, hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome cancers arise younger, favor the right colon, and show microsatellite instability (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Colon cancer before 50 or a family with endometrial cancer prompts mismatch repair testing.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hundreds of adenomas define familial adenomatous polyposis,","C":"Lynch syndrome cancers favor the proximal, right","D":"Chromosomal instability marks the APC pathway, while","E":"Germline APC mutations cause familial adenomatous polyposis,"}},{"id":"sdl33_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":1,"stem":"Which colonic region is characteristically involved in Lynch syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Sigmoid colon","B":"Rectal ampulla","C":"Proximal colon","D":"Splenic flexure","E":"Descending colon"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The objective and First Aid place Lynch syndrome in the proximal colon, and Robbins notes HNPCC colon cancers are often in the right colon. The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site. The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon. The splenic flexure is not characteristic. The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon. Sources: SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3; Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the proximal colon is involved in Lynch syndrome (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: microsatellite unstable and CIMP tumors are frequently located in the right colon (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The sigmoid colon is a left-sided site.","B":"The rectum is distal, whereas Lynch syndrome cancers favor the cecum and ascending colon.","D":"The splenic flexure is not characteristic.","E":"The descending colon is left-sided, whereas Lynch syndrome favors the right colon."}},{"id":"sdl33_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"In the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, which gene is mutated in most colon cancers but uncommonly in adenomas?","choices":{"A":"APC","B":"BRAF","C":"MLH1","D":"TP53","E":"KRAS"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TP53 is mutated in most colon cancers but uncommonly in adenomas, so its loss is a late event that marks progression to carcinoma in the chromosomal instability pathway. APC loss is the early first hit that starts adenoma formation, so it is already present in adenomas. BRAF mutation belongs to the serrated, microsatellite unstable pathway, whose tumors usually keep intact TP53. MLH1 loss drives mismatch repair deficiency and is not the late step of the APC pathway. KRAS mutations are already present in many adenomas larger than 1 cm, so they are not rare in adenomas. Sources: Robbins ch 17, colonic adenocarcinoma pathogenesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classic sequence is APC loss, then KRAS activation, SMAD2 and SMAD4 loss, and TP53 loss at the transition to carcinoma (Robbins ch 17). COMLEX: Removing adenomas before late genetic hits is why screening colonoscopy prevents cancer.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"APC loss is the early first hit","B":"BRAF mutation belongs to the serrated, microsatellite","C":"MLH1 loss drives mismatch repair deficiency and","E":"KRAS mutations are already present in many"}},{"id":"sdl33_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"Which dietary pattern is most closely associated with increased colorectal cancer risk?","choices":{"A":"Low vegetable fiber diet","B":"High citrus fruit intake","C":"Low red meat consumption","D":"High fish oil consumption","E":"Low refined sugar intake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Low intake of unabsorbable vegetable fiber with high intake of refined carbohydrates and fat is most closely tied to colorectal cancer. Reduced stool bulk may prolong mucosal contact with bacterial oxidative byproducts. Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk. Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is. Robbins names low unabsorbable vegetable fiber and high refined carbohydrate and fat intake as the dietary factors most tied to colorectal cancer, not citrus fruit intake. First Aid lists a diet of processed meat with low fiber among colorectal cancer risk factors, not high fish oil consumption. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include adenomatous and serrated polyps, IBD, tobacco use, and processed meat with low fiber (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high fat intake raises hepatic bile acid synthesis, and bacteria can convert bile acids into carcinogens (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Low red meat intake is not a risk; high animal fat intake raises colorectal cancer risk.","E":"Low refined sugar intake is not a risk; high refined carbohydrate intake is.","B":"Robbins names low unabsorbable vegetable fiber and high refined carbohydrate and fat intake as the dietary factors most tied to colorectal cancer, not citrus fruit intake.","D":"First Aid lists a diet of processed meat with low fiber among colorectal cancer risk factors, not high fish oil consumption."}},{"id":"sdl33_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":5,"stem":"Which organ is the most common site of distant metastasis from colon cancer?","choices":{"A":"Lung","B":"Liver","C":"Bone","D":"Brain","E":"Ovary"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Because the colon drains through the portal circulation, the liver is the most common site of distant metastases. Regional nodes, lungs, and bones can also be involved. Robbins states that, because of portal drainage, the liver is the most common site of distant colon cancer metastasis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rectosigmoid is the most common colorectal cancer site, followed by ascending and descending colon (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: the rectum does not drain via the portal circulation, so metastatic anal carcinomas often circumvent the portal route (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Lung is not part of that answer.","C":"Bone is not part of that answer.","D":"Brain is not part of that answer.","E":"Robbins states that, because of portal drainage, the liver is the most common site of distant colon cancer metastasis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Deep tenderness two-thirds of the way from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine is known by what name?","choices":{"A":"Rovsing sign","B":"Psoas sign","C":"Murphy sign","D":"McBurney sign","E":"Obturator sign"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The McBurney sign is deep tenderness two-thirds of the distance from the umbilicus to the right anterior superior iliac spine, a classic finding in acute appendicitis. Rovsing sign is severe RLQ pain on palpation of the LLQ, not tenderness at the RLQ point itself. Psoas sign is lower abdominal pain made worse by hip extension, not point tenderness on deep palpation. Murphy sign is inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation in cholecystitis, not an RLQ appendiceal finding. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: acute appendicitis may also elicit psoas, obturator, and Rovsing signs (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: acute appendicitis has a lifetime risk of 7% and affects males slightly more often (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rovsing sign is severe RLQ pain on palpation of the LLQ, not tenderness at the RLQ point itself.","B":"Psoas sign is lower abdominal pain made worse by hip extension, not point tenderness on deep palpation.","C":"Murphy sign is inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation in cholecystitis, not an RLQ appendiceal finding."}},{"id":"sdl33_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"Which is the most common tumor of the appendix?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous cystadenoma","B":"Signet ring carcinoma","C":"Burkitt lymphoma","D":"Gastrointestinal stromal tumor","E":"Neuroendocrine tumor"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The well-differentiated neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumor is the most common appendiceal tumor. It is usually incidental, forms a bulbous swelling at the distal tip, and rarely spreads. Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common. A gastrointestinal stromal tumor is not this lesion. Signet-ring cell carcinoma is the diffuse pattern of gastric adenocarcinoma. Burkitt lymphoma is not an appendiceal tumor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs such as octreotide are used to treat carcinoid tumors (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Octreotide). COMLEX: conventional adenomas and adenocarcinomas of the appendix can cause obstruction that mimics acute appendicitis (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mucinous neoplasms occur in the appendix but are less common.","D":"A gastrointestinal stromal tumor is not this appendiceal lesion.","B":"Signet-ring cell carcinoma is the diffuse pattern of gastric adenocarcinoma.","C":"Burkitt lymphoma is not an appendiceal tumor."}},{"id":"sdl33_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":1,"stem":"What is the major risk factor for HPV-associated squamous cell carcinoma of the anal canal?","choices":{"A":"Receptive anal intercourse","B":"Chronic laxative overuse","C":"Portal venous hypertension","D":"Low dietary fiber intake","E":"Untreated ulcerative colitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anal squamous cell carcinoma is frequently associated with high-risk HPV strains, and the major risk factor for these HPV-associated lesions is anal sex. Low-risk HPV strains cause condyloma acuminatum. Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma. Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma. Chronic anthraquinone laxative use causes melanosis coli, a brown pigmentation of the colon, and epidemiologic studies do not link it to colorectal cancer; it is not a risk factor for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Anal canal). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: squamous cell carcinoma arises below the pectinate line, while adenocarcinoma arises above it (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: basaloid anal squamous cell carcinoma has been called cloacogenic carcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Portal hypertension predisposes to hemorrhoids, not HPV-related anal squamous carcinoma.","D":"Low fiber intake is a colorectal adenocarcinoma risk, not a risk for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma.","E":"Ulcerative colitis raises colonic adenocarcinoma risk, not HPV-associated anal squamous carcinoma.","B":"Chronic anthraquinone laxative use causes melanosis coli, a brown pigmentation of the colon, and epidemiologic studies do not link it to colorectal cancer; it is not a risk factor for HPV-driven anal squamous carcinoma."}},{"id":"sdl33_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman has a 3 cm sessile colonic polyp with long, slender surface projections on biopsy. Her daughter asks what best predicts whether such a growth harbors cancer. Which feature is most important?","choices":{"A":"Goblet cell number","B":"Degree of inflammation","C":"Lesion diameter","D":"Tubular gland pattern","E":"Patient sex and age"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Size is the most important characteristic correlating with malignancy in an adenoma. Villous architecture correlates with cancer partly because villous adenomas are larger, and architectural category has little significance in isolation. Inflammation, goblet cell number, and patient sex and age are not that predictor. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tubular adenomas have less malignant potential than villous adenomas (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: tubular adenomas tend to be small pedunculated polyps of rounded glands (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 3 — gastric polyps and precursor epithelial lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Architectural pattern has little significance on its own.","B":"Inflammation is not a predictor of cancer in an adenoma.","A":"Goblet cell number does not track with cancer in an adenoma.","E":"Patient sex and age do not track with cancer in an adenoma."}},{"id":"sdl33_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":5,"stem":"A 59-year-old man has three colonic adenomas removed. One shows high-grade dysplasia and the others show low-grade dysplasia. How does the high-grade lesion affect cancer risk in his remaining polyps?","choices":{"A":"Higher risk in other polyps","B":"Unchanged risk in other polyps","C":"Field cancerization of colon","D":"Germline repair defect implied","E":"Doubled risk in other polyps"},"correct":"B","explanation":"High-grade dysplasia is a risk factor for cancer in that individual polyp but does not increase cancer risk in other polyps within that patient. High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps. High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: adenomatous polyps progress through an adenoma-carcinoma sequence with APC loss followed by KRAS mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: high-grade dysplasia raises cancer risk within the individual polyp (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 3 — chronic reflux injury and Barrett dysplasia","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"High-grade dysplasia raises risk only in that polyp, not in his other polyps.","E":"High-grade dysplasia does not double risk in other polyps; it affects only the polyp containing it."}},{"id":"sdl33_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman has small adenomas removed at each of several surveillance endoscopies. She asks why repeated procedures lower her colon cancer risk. Which explanation is best?","choices":{"A":"Ablation of mucosal lymphatics","B":"Reversal of APC mutations","C":"Induction of mucosal immunity","D":"Removal of precursor polyps","E":"Shrinkage of hyperplastic polyps"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Adenomas are precursors of colorectal adenocarcinoma, and removed polyps do not progress to adenocarcinoma, so repeated surveillance is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Most adenomas do not progress, and no tool identifies those that will. Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress. Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations; it removes precursor polyps. Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk. First Aid describes adenomatous polyps as neoplastic precursors that progress via the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, so removing them lowers risk rather than inducing mucosal immunity. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: average-risk screening begins at age 45 (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: adenomas develop in about 30% of Western adults by age 60 years (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Polypectomy does not ablate lymphatics; it lowers risk by removing adenomas before they progress.","B":"Endoscopy cannot reverse APC mutations.","E":"Hyperplastic polyps have no malignant potential, so shrinking them would not lower cancer risk.","C":"First Aid describes adenomatous polyps as neoplastic precursors that progress via the adenoma-carcinoma sequence, so removing them lowers risk rather than inducing mucosal immunity."}},{"id":"sdl33_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman with familial adenomatous polyposis had a prophylactic colectomy at age 20. Which site most needs continued endoscopic surveillance for adenomas?","choices":{"A":"Ampulla of Vater","B":"Distal esophagus","C":"Gallbladder fundus","D":"Pancreatic tail","E":"Hepatic hilum"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Colectomy prevents colorectal cancer in FAP, but patients remain at risk elsewhere. The ampulla of Vater and the stomach are common extracolonic sites of adenomas and, if untreated, cancer. The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site. The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site. The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site. The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: without prophylactic colectomy, 100% of FAP patients progress to colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: untreated FAP leads to colorectal adenocarcinoma often before age 30 and in nearly all untreated patients by age 50 (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 4 — location-dependent presentation of pancreatic head and periampullary tumors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The distal esophagus is not an FAP adenoma site.","C":"The gallbladder is not a recognized FAP adenoma site.","D":"The pancreatic tail is not an FAP adenoma site.","E":"The hepatic hilum is not an FAP adenoma site."}},{"id":"sdl33_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":5,"stem":"A 42-year-old man has about 30 colonic adenomas plus several serrated and hyperplastic polyps. His parents have normal screening exams, but his brother has a similar polyp burden. APC testing is negative. Which gene is most likely mutated?","choices":{"A":"STK11","B":"SMAD4","C":"PTEN","D":"MSH6","E":"MUTYH"},"correct":"E","explanation":"MUTYH-associated polyposis is an autosomal recessive base-excision repair disorder in patients without APC loss. It has fewer than 100 polyps that appear later, with frequent sessile serrated and hyperplastic polyps. STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps. SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis. PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance. MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MUTYH-associated polyposis is also associated with duodenal adenomas and ovarian and bladder cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: colon cancer development in MUTYH-associated polyposis is delayed relative to FAP (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"STK11 mutations cause Peutz-Jeghers hamartomas, not adenomas with serrated polyps.","B":"SMAD4 mutations cause autosomal dominant juvenile polyposis, not recessive adenomatous polyposis.","C":"PTEN mutations cause Cowden hamartomas, not adenomas with recessive inheritance.","D":"MSH6 is a Lynch syndrome gene, which causes few polyps with dominant inheritance, not about 30 adenomas in siblings."}},{"id":"sdl33_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 19-year-old woman with numerous colonic adenomas also has firm bony masses of the skull and a fibrous mass in the abdominal wall. Which variant best describes her disease?","choices":{"A":"Turcot syndrome","B":"Cowden syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Gardner syndrome","E":"Li Fraumeni syndrome"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Gardner syndrome is an APC-related FAP variant with multiple adenomas plus osteomas, thyroid and desmoid tumors, and skin cysts. Turcot syndrome adds CNS tumors such as medulloblastoma. Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids. Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids. Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a broad tumor spectrum led by sarcomas, breast cancers, leukemias, and brain tumors, not adenomas with osteomas. Robbins lists Turcot syndrome as an APC variant with multiple adenomas plus medulloblastoma or glioblastoma, whereas osteomas and desmoid tumors define Gardner syndrome. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis) (Table 17.10). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Turcot syndrome is FAP or Lynch syndrome plus a malignant CNS tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioma (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Polyposis syndromes). COMLEX: attenuated FAP is also caused by APC mutation (Robbins Ch. 17, Table 17.10).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 5 — hamartomatous polyposis syndromes","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cowden syndrome is a PTEN hamartoma syndrome, not an APC variant with osteomas and desmoids.","C":"Juvenile polyposis features hamartomatous polyps, not adenomas with osteomas and desmoids.","E":"Li-Fraumeni syndrome is an inherited TP53 mutation with a broad tumor spectrum led by sarcomas, breast cancers, leukemias, and brain tumors, not adenomas with osteomas.","A":"Robbins lists Turcot syndrome as an APC variant with multiple adenomas plus medulloblastoma or glioblastoma, whereas osteomas and desmoid tumors define Gardner syndrome."}},{"id":"sdl33_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has endometrial carcinoma. Her mother had colon cancer at 45, and tumor staining shows loss of a mismatch repair protein. Which mutational mechanism most likely drives her tumors?","choices":{"A":"Chromosomal instability","B":"Telomere attrition crisis","C":"Oncogene amplification","D":"Whole chromosome aneuploidy","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In HNPCC, loss of the second mismatch repair allele raises mutation rates up to 1000-fold, mostly in short repeats called microsatellites. Chromosomal instability is the hallmark of the APC/beta catenin pathway. Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss. Oncogene amplification, such as HER2 in breast carcinoma, is not the defect produced by mismatch repair loss. Telomere attrition leaves naked chromosome ends that fuse in bridge-fusion-breakage cycles, not the repeat-sequence mutations of mismatch repair loss. Robbins calls chromosomal instability the hallmark of the APC/beta-catenin pathway, whereas mismatch repair deficiency produces microsatellite instability. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome mismatch repair defects cause subsequent microsatellite instability (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: MSI can target TGFBR2 and BAX, promoting growth and survival of abnormal clones (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 39 → Obj 1 — chemotherapy classes for colorectal cancer","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Whole-chromosome aneuploidy reflects chromosomal instability of the APC pathway, not mismatch repair loss.","C":"Oncogene amplification, such as HER2 in breast carcinoma, is not the defect produced by mismatch repair loss.","B":"Telomere attrition leaves naked chromosome ends that fuse in bridge-fusion-breakage cycles, not the repeat-sequence mutations of mismatch repair loss.","A":"Robbins calls chromosomal instability the hallmark of the APC/beta-catenin pathway, whereas mismatch repair deficiency produces microsatellite instability."}},{"id":"sdl33_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 78-year-old woman with no family history of cancer has a mucinous large bowel adenocarcinoma with peritumoral lymphocytes and mismatch repair deficiency. Which finding best supports a sporadic origin rather than a hereditary syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Mucinous differentiation","B":"Advanced patient age","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes","D":"BRAF activating mutation","E":"Repair protein loss"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sporadic mismatch repair–deficient cancers often arise through the CpG island hypermethylation pathway, which silences MLH1. The combination of MSI, BRAF mutation, and methylation is the signature of this pathway. Mucinous change, lymphocytes, and right-sided location occur in both. Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: serrated polyps follow the CIMP pathway, which may silence MLH1 and lead to BRAF mutation (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colonic polyps). COMLEX: KRAS and TP53 are typically intact in CIMP cancers (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 4 — hyperplastic and inflammatory intestinal polyps","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Repair protein loss occurs in both Lynch and sporadic MSI tumors, so it cannot distinguish them.","A":"Mucinous change occurs in both.","C":"Peritumoral lymphocytes do not separate sporadic from Lynch tumors."}},{"id":"sdl33_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman's brother had colon cancer at 38 and her mother had endometrial cancer at 50. Genetic testing confirms a germline mismatch repair mutation. Which other cancer is she at increased risk for?","choices":{"A":"Ovarian carcinoma","B":"Breast carcinoma","C":"Thyroid carcinoma","D":"Lung carcinoma","E":"Cervical carcinoma"},"correct":"A","explanation":"HNPCC clusters cancers of the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin. The objective names endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers. Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium, ovary, stomach, and urinary tract; breast is not among them. The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin; lung is not on the list. The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium and ovary, but not the cervix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer); SDL 33 Elentra Objective 3. Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the Lynch syndrome mnemonic CEOS stands for colon, endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lynch syndrome). COMLEX: identifying germline mismatch repair mutations matters for genetic counseling and second malignancy risk (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Thyroid cancer is linked to Gardner syndrome, not Lynch syndrome.","B":"Breast is not among them.","D":"The cancers that cluster in Lynch syndrome include the colorectum, endometrium, stomach, ovary, ureters, brain, small bowel, hepatobiliary tract, pancreas, and skin; lung is not on the list.","E":"The cancer sites of Lynch syndrome include the endometrium and ovary, but not the cervix."}},{"id":"sdl33_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman has fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference 12 to 16), MCV is 72 fL (reference 80 to 100), and ferritin is 8 ng/mL (reference 15 to 150). She denies visible bleeding. Which next step is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy","B":"Colonoscopy referral","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis","D":"Empiric oral iron","E":"Serum B12 level"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers often present with fatigue and weakness from iron deficiency anemia. Iron deficiency anemia in an older male or postmenopausal female is GI cancer until proven otherwise. Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy. Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead. Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise. B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia in males older than 50 and postmenopausal females raises suspicion for colorectal cancer (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: proximal colon tumors grow as polypoid masses that rarely obstruct (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Bone marrow biopsy is unnecessary when low ferritin already confirms iron deficiency, whose source must be sought by colonoscopy.","C":"Hemoglobin electrophoresis evaluates thalassemia, but a low ferritin confirms iron deficiency instead.","D":"Empiric oral iron without colonoscopy risks missing GI cancer, which iron deficiency in an older woman implies until proven otherwise.","E":"B12 deficiency causes macrocytic anemia, whereas her microcytic, low-ferritin anemia points to GI blood loss."}},{"id":"sdl33_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man has months of narrowing stool caliber, crampy left lower quadrant pain, and bloating. Imaging shows a short constricting sigmoid mass. Which gross appearance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sessile serrated plaque","B":"Large pedunculated villous polyp","C":"Diffuse linitis plastica","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa","E":"Annular napkin-ring lesion"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Distal colon carcinomas tend to be annular napkin-ring constrictions that narrow the lumen, sometimes to obstruction. Left-sided tumors produce visible bleeding, bowel habit change, and left lower quadrant cramping. A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass. Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting. Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools. Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse infiltrative gastric cancer, not a short constricting sigmoid mass. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: left-sided colorectal cancer is associated with hematochezia and obstruction (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: invasive colon cancer elicits a desmoplastic response responsible for its firm consistency (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A sessile serrated plaque is a right-sided precursor lesion, not a constricting sigmoid mass.","B":"Large polypoid masses are typical of the right colon, whereas left-sided cancers are annular and constricting.","D":"Flat atrophic mucosa does not produce a constricting mass or narrowed stools.","C":"Linitis plastica is the rigid leather bottle wall of diffuse infiltrative gastric cancer, not a short constricting sigmoid mass."}},{"id":"sdl33_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":5,"stem":"A 55-year-old man takes daily low-dose aspirin after a myocardial infarction and asks whether it affects his colon cancer risk. Which mechanism most likely explains a protective effect?","choices":{"A":"Lipoxygenase inhibition","B":"Thromboxane receptor blockade","C":"Cyclooxygenase 2 inhibition","D":"Platelet P2Y12 blockade","E":"Phospholipase A2 inhibition"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aspirin and other NSAIDs are protective, likely through inhibition of COX-2, which is highly expressed in 90% of colorectal carcinomas and 40% to 90% of adenomas. COX-2 drives prostaglandin E2, which promotes epithelial proliferation. Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon cancer by inhibiting COX-2, which is overexpressed in most colorectal carcinomas and drives PGE2-mediated proliferation; lipoxygenase is not the target. Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase rather than blocking the thromboxane receptor. Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine, not aspirin. Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through annexins (lipocortins), not by aspirin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenocarcinoma). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 33 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: overexpression of COX-2 has been linked to colorectal cancer, and NSAIDs may be chemopreventive (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: some NSAIDs cause polyp regression in FAP patients whose rectum was left in place (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 2 — NSAIDs and H. pylori in peptic ulcer disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Aspirin and NSAIDs are thought to protect against colon cancer by inhibiting COX-2, which is overexpressed in most colorectal carcinomas and drives PGE2-mediated proliferation; lipoxygenase is not the target.","B":"Aspirin lowers thromboxane A2 synthesis by irreversibly acetylating cyclooxygenase rather than blocking the thromboxane receptor.","D":"Irreversible P2Y12 ADP receptor blockade is the mechanism of clopidogrel, prasugrel, and ticlopidine, not aspirin.","E":"Phospholipase A2 is inhibited by corticosteroids through annexins (lipocortins), not by aspirin."}},{"id":"sdl33_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has periumbilical pain that moved to the right lower quadrant, then nausea and low-grade fever. His resected appendix is examined. Which histologic finding is required for the diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Muscularis propria neutrophils","B":"Mucosal neutrophils with ulcers","C":"Lymphoid follicle hyperplasia","D":"Submucosal eosinophil clusters","E":"Granulomas in lamina propria"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are not specific. Diagnosis of acute appendicitis requires neutrophilic infiltration of the muscularis propria. Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding. Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis. Submucosal eosinophils are not the criterion. Robbins notes mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are often present but are not specific markers of acute appendicitis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: appendicitis can be due to fecalith obstruction in adults or lymphoid hyperplasia in children (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Appendicitis). COMLEX: complications of appendicitis include pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, abscess, and bacteremia (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Lymphoid hyperplasia can obstruct the appendix in young patients, but it is a cause rather than the diagnostic finding.","E":"Granulomas in the lamina propria suggest Crohn disease or infection, not acute appendicitis.","D":"Submucosal eosinophils are not the criterion.","B":"Robbins notes mucosal neutrophils and focal superficial ulceration are often present but are not specific markers of acute appendicitis."}},{"id":"sdl33_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with chronic constipation and straining notices bright red blood on toilet tissue. Anoscopy shows dilated anal venous cushions. Which condition must still be excluded?","choices":{"A":"Anal fistula","B":"Pilonidal cyst","C":"Colon carcinoma","D":"Perianal abscess","E":"Rectal prolapse"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hemorrhoids present with pain and bright red blood on toilet tissue, but colon cancer can coexist and must be excluded. Straining at defecation and venous stasis of pregnancy are the main predisposing influences. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hemorrhoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: internal hemorrhoids receive visceral innervation and are not painful (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Pectinate line). COMLEX: hemorrhoidal bleeding can be treated by sclerotherapy, rubber band ligation, or infrared coagulation (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has progressive abdominal distention. Surgery finds tenacious, semisolid mucin filling the peritoneal cavity and a ruptured, mucin-distended appendix with pushing tumor margins. Which primary lesion is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Appendiceal carcinoid tumor","B":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasm","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma","E":"Gastric signet ring cell carcinoma"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Appendiceal mucinous neoplasms occur in adults, often in the sixth decade, with pushing margins and extracellular mucin that can rupture the appendix and fill the peritoneum as pseudomyxoma peritonei. An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads. Ovarian serous carcinoma, peritoneal mesothelioma, and gastric signet-ring carcinoma are not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Appendix). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peritoneal spread of appendiceal mucinous neoplasms can produce pseudomyxoma peritonei (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: GNAS, encoding Gαs, is frequently mutated in appendiceal mucinous neoplasms and is thought to drive excess mucin production (Robbins Ch. 17).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 3 — mucinous pancreatic cystic neoplasms","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An appendiceal carcinoid is a small solid tip mass that rarely spreads, not a mucin-distended ruptured appendix.","E":"Gastric signet-ring carcinoma is not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix.","C":"Ovarian serous carcinoma is not a ruptured mucin-distended appendix.","D":"Peritoneal mesothelioma is not the mucin-producing source of this picture."}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":3,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has a 2.5 cm pedunculated sigmoid polyp removed whole by snare. Pathology shows a tubulovillous adenoma with a focus of malignant glands that have breached the muscularis mucosae and invaded the submucosa of the stalk. The resection margin is clear. Why does this finding change his management compared with carcinoma confined to the mucosa?","choices":{"A":"The stalk lacks blood vessels that tumor could reach","B":"Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a hamartoma","C":"Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the lamina propria","D":"Submucosa has lymphatics, so nodal spread is now possible","E":"Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no dysplasia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The colonic mucosa lacks functional lymphatic channels, so intramucosal carcinoma has little or no metastatic potential, but once malignant cells cross the muscularis mucosae into the submucosal stalk they are invasive adenocarcinoma with access to lymphatics, so nodal metastasis becomes possible and further staging is needed. The stalk lacks blood vessels is false, because the submucosa of the stalk carries both vessels and lymphatics. Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a hamartoma is wrong, since the lesion is a dysplastic adenoma that progressed to carcinoma. Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the lamina propria inverts the key fact that colonic mucosa has no functional lymphatics. Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no dysplasia is incorrect, because all adenomas are defined by epithelial dysplasia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Adenomas; Intramucosal carcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Intramucosal carcinoma rarely metastasizes because colonic mucosa lacks lymphatics, whereas invasion into the submucosal stalk is invasive cancer (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: a malignant polyp with submucosal invasion needs margin review and staging rather than polypectomy follow-up.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The stalk lacks blood vessels is false,","B":"Submucosal invasion means the polyp was a","C":"Mucosal carcinoma spreads by lymphatics in the","E":"Stalk invasion means the adenoma had no"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":3,"stem":"A 16-year-old girl whose father had a colectomy at 30 has hundreds of small adenomas carpeting her colon and rectum. Genetic testing shows a germline truncating mutation in a tumor suppressor gene on chromosome 5q. Immunostaining of her adenoma epithelium is performed. Which finding is most expected in the adenoma cells?","choices":{"A":"Loss of mismatch repair protein staining","B":"Nuclear accumulation of beta-catenin","C":"Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin","D":"Loss of SMAD4 from stromal cells","E":"Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hundreds of adenomas in a teenager with an affected parent and a germline 5q mutation is familial adenomatous polyposis; loss of the second APC allele in each adenoma prevents beta-catenin destruction, so beta-catenin accumulates and moves to the nucleus to drive Wnt target genes such as MYC and cyclin D1. Loss of mismatch repair protein staining is the signature of Lynch syndrome tumors, not APC-driven adenomas. Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin is not the APC defect; the hallmark is free beta-catenin entering the nucleus. Loss of SMAD4 is the lesion of juvenile polyposis, a hamartomatous syndrome. Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation is the mechanism of sporadic microsatellite-unstable cancers, not FAP. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Familial adenomatous polyposis; APC/beta-catenin pathway); First Aid 2025 GI (FAP).","boardPrep":"Step 1: In FAP, germline APC loss plus a second hit lets beta-catenin escape degradation and activate Wnt targets in the nucleus (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: pancolonic adenomas in a teenager call for prophylactic colectomy and genetic counseling of relatives.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 3 — colonic epithelial renewal and stem cells","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Loss of mismatch repair protein staining is","C":"Overexpression of membrane E-cadherin is not the","D":"Loss of SMAD4 is the lesion of","E":"Absent MLH1 from promoter methylation is the"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old man has a right-sided colon adenocarcinoma. His mother had endometrial cancer at 48. He carries a germline MSH2 mutation. Immunohistochemistry shows absent MSH2 staining in tumor cells, but normal colonic mucosa from the same specimen stains normally for MSH2. Which event best explains the difference between the two tissues?","choices":{"A":"Gain of extra copies of the KRAS oncogene","B":"Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy","C":"Chromosome 5q loss in normal colonic crypts","D":"Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium","E":"Loss of the remaining normal MSH2 allele"},"correct":"E","explanation":"A right-sided colon cancer in a young man with a germline mismatch repair mutation and a relative with endometrial cancer is Lynch syndrome; every cell carries one mutant MSH2 allele, but normal mucosa still makes protein from the normal allele, and the tumor lost staining because that second allele was inactivated by mutation or epigenetic silencing. Gain of extra copies of the KRAS oncogene would not remove MSH2 protein from tumor cells. Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy is not how Lynch tumors lose function, and imprinting would affect normal mucosa equally. Chromosome 5q loss relates to APC, and loss in normal crypts would not explain absent tumor MSH2 staining. Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium is opposite to tumor biology and unrelated to mismatch repair loss. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome patients inherit one mutant mismatch repair allele; tumors arise after the second copy is lost, producing microsatellite instability (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: young right-sided colon cancer with endometrial cancer in the family warrants mismatch repair testing and cascade screening.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 9 → Obj 4 — gastric adenocarcinoma risk factors and precursors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gain of extra copies of the KRAS","B":"Epigenetic imprinting of the paternal MSH2 copy","C":"Chromosome 5q loss relates to APC, and","D":"Silencing of telomerase in tumor epithelium is"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and fatigue and a new diastolic murmur. Three blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in chains that are nonhemolytic and identified as a gut-colonizing streptococcus formerly named for cattle. Echocardiography shows an aortic valve vegetation. Besides antibiotics, which evaluation is most important?","choices":{"A":"Dental panoramic radiography","B":"Urine culture and renal ultrasound","C":"Colonoscopy of the entire colon","D":"Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis","E":"Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Endocarditis from Streptococcus gallolyticus, formerly S bovis, is associated with an increased incidence of colon cancer, so the patient needs colonoscopy to look for an occult colonic adenocarcinoma. Dental panoramic radiography fits viridans streptococcal endocarditis after dental procedures, not a gut-colonizing streptococcus. Urine culture and renal ultrasound look for a genitourinary source, which is more relevant to enterococcal infection. Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis does not apply to this man and is not linked to this organism. Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer does not address the colonic neoplasm that this bacteremia signals. Sources: First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Streptococcus gallolyticus) and GI (colorectal cancer presentation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: S gallolyticus bacteremia or endocarditis is associated with colorectal cancer (First Aid, Streptococcus gallolyticus). COMLEX: after treating the endocarditis, arrange colonoscopy even in a patient without GI symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross features of the large intestine, including teniae coli","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dental panoramic radiography fits viridans streptococcal endocarditis","B":"Urine culture and renal ultrasound look for","D":"Transvaginal ultrasound of the pelvis does not","E":"Upper endoscopy for peptic ulcer does not"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the symptoms, risk factors, and diagnostic criteria associated with appendicitis (McBurney sign) as well as symptoms and risk factors of hemorrhoids and tumors of the anal canal and appendix.","batch":3,"stem":"A 20-year-old man had a perforated appendix removed 1 week ago. He now has spiking fevers, rigors, and right upper quadrant tenderness. WBC is 21,000/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). CT shows several small abscesses scattered within the right lobe of the liver. By which route did infection most likely reach his liver?","choices":{"A":"Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts","B":"Through mesenteric veins into the portal vein","C":"Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct","D":"Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery","E":"Direct extension through the subphrenic space"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Blood from the appendix drains through tributaries of the superior mesenteric vein into the portal vein, so bacteria and infected thrombus from a perforated appendix travel straight to the liver, which is why Robbins lists pylephlebitis, portal venous thrombosis, and liver abscess as complications of appendicitis. Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts is ascending cholangitis, which follows biliary obstruction rather than appendiceal perforation. Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct empties into the systemic veins and does not carry bowel bacteria directly to the liver. Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery requires systemic bacteremia first, whereas the portal veins deliver appendiceal bacteria to the liver directly. Direct extension through the subphrenic space would form a collection above the liver rather than abscesses scattered within it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Acute appendicitis complications); course master PDF (hepatic portal vein formation).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Appendicitis complications include pylephlebitis, portal vein thrombosis, liver abscess, and bacteremia, reflecting portal venous drainage of the bowel (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: fever and RUQ pain after perforated appendicitis call for imaging of the portal vein and liver.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 17 → Obj 1 — circulatory disorders of the liver","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ascent from the duodenum up the bile ducts is","C":"Lymphatic spread through the thoracic duct empties","D":"Arterial seeding from the hepatic artery requires","E":"Direct extension through the subphrenic space would"}},{"id":"trial_sdl33_q06","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":3,"stem":"A 70-year-old man has a sigmoid adenocarcinoma resected. The pathology report lists a 5 cm moderately differentiated tumor with mucin production, invasion through the muscularis propria into pericolic fat, and metastatic tumor in 4 of 18 regional lymph nodes. No distant metastases are found. Which reported feature most worsens his prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters","B":"Moderate histologic differentiation","C":"Production of extracellular mucin","D":"Metastasis to regional lymph nodes","E":"Tumor location in the sigmoid colon"},"correct":"D","explanation":"For colorectal adenocarcinoma the two most important prognostic factors are depth of invasion and the presence of lymph node metastases, so among the reported features his regional nodal metastases most worsen his outlook. Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters matters less than depth of invasion and nodal status in staging. Moderate histologic differentiation has less prognostic weight than invasion depth and nodal spread. Production of extracellular mucin is a histologic feature that is not one of the two leading prognostic factors. Tumor location in the sigmoid colon does not outweigh nodal metastasis in predicting survival. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Colonic adenocarcinoma, prognosis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Depth of invasion and lymph node metastasis are the two most important prognostic factors in colorectal adenocarcinoma (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: node-positive colon cancer is stage III and is an indication for adjuvant chemotherapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 5 — large-intestine lymphatic drainage and adenoma spread","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tumor diameter of 5 centimeters matters less","B":"Moderate histologic differentiation has less prognostic weight","C":"Production of extracellular mucin is a histologic","E":"Tumor location in the sigmoid colon does"}},{"id":"grid_sdl33_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the associated mutation, complication, associated conditions (endometrial, ovarian, and skin cancers), and location (proximal colon) of hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (aka Lynch syndrome).","batch":4,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman has 2 months of fatigue. Hemoglobin is 9.8 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with microcytic indices. Colonoscopy shows a mass, and biopsy shows a mucinous adenocarcinoma with signet-ring cells and dense intraepithelial lymphocytes. Her mother had endometrial cancer at age 46, and a maternal uncle had colon cancer at age 44. She has no history of inflammatory bowel disease. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the disorder underlying this patient's cancer?","grid":{"headers":["Inheritance","Microsatellite status","Usual location"]},"choices":{"A":["Autosomal dominant","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"B":["Autosomal dominant","Stable","Pancolonic"],"C":["Sporadic","MSI-high","Proximal colon"],"D":["Sporadic","Stable","Distal colon"],"E":["Acquired (colitis)","Stable","Pancolonic"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (autosomal dominant, MSI-high, proximal colon) is correct. Hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (Lynch syndrome) is autosomal dominant and caused by germline loss-of-function mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes, most often MSH2 or MLH1; loss of the second allele causes microsatellite instability. The notes place these cancers predominantly in the proximal (right) colon at young ages, describe mucinous and signet-ring differentiation with intraepithelial lymphocytes, and list endometrial cancer as the most common extraintestinal malignancy in women. Choice B (autosomal dominant, stable, pancolonic) is familial adenomatous polyposis, an APC germline disorder with at least 100 adenomas throughout the colon beginning in adolescence and progressing through the chromosomally unstable APC/beta-catenin pathway. Choice C (sporadic, MSI-high, proximal colon) is the sporadic CIMP (serrated) pathway, in which CpG island hypermethylation silences MLH1 and BRAF is activated, arising from sessile serrated lesions without a family pattern. Choice D (sporadic, stable, distal colon) is sporadic adenocarcinoma through the classic APC, KRAS, SMAD4, TP53 sequence with chromosomal instability, which accounts for about 80% of sporadic cases. Choice E (acquired from colitis, stable, pancolonic) is colitis-associated cancer arising in long-standing ulcerative pancolitis, an acquired risk factor. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 33, sections 2–4: FAP, Lynch syndrome, colorectal cancer pathways and risk factors); Robbins Ch. 17 (colonic neoplasia). The proximal location of CIMP tumors, the distal predominance of CIN-pathway sporadic cancers, and the microsatellite-stable, pancolonic profile of colitis-associated cancer are from Robbins and are not stated in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lynch syndrome = germline mismatch repair defect (MLH1, MSH2) → MSI-high, right-sided colon cancer before 50, plus endometrial and ovarian cancer (Exam 3 notes, SDL 33). COMLEX: sporadic MSI-high cancer from MLH1 promoter hypermethylation with BRAF mutation is the main look-alike.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 2 — FAP genetics and pancolonic polyposis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl33_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nuclear change is part of the epithelial dysplasia in a colorectal adenoma?","choices":{"A":"Nuclear pseudostratification","B":"A normal basal nucleus with abundant mucin in every cell","C":"Caseating necrosis in the nucleus","D":"Viral inclusions that fill the nucleus","E":"Loss of the nucleus in a mature squamous cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Adenomatous dysplasia shows nuclear hyperchromasia, elongation, and pseudostratification, with fewer goblet cells. A quiet basal nucleus and retained mucin describe normal colonocytes, and dysplasia reduces goblet cells. Caseating necrosis is a granulomatous pattern, not the nuclear change of adenoma. Viral nuclear inclusions are not the dysplasia of an adenoma. Loss of the nucleus in a squamous cell is not colonic adenomatous dysplasia.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A quiet basal nucleus and retained mucin describe normal colonocytes, and dysplasia reduces goblet cells","C":"Caseating necrosis is a granulomatous pattern, not the nuclear change of adenoma","D":"Viral nuclear inclusions are not the dysplasia of an adenoma","E":"Loss of the nucleus in a squamous cell is not colonic adenomatous dysplasia"}},{"id":"sdl33_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Recognize the morphology, risk factors, and complications of colorectal adenomas and understand the relationship between size and malignancy.","batch":2,"stem":"A 66-year-old man has fatigue. A 4 cm sessile sigmoid polyp is removed and shows villous architecture with dysplasia but no invasion through the muscularis mucosae. He takes no anticoagulant. Hemoglobin is 10.4 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), mean corpuscular volume is 74 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and ferritin is 9 ng/mL (reference range: 24–336 ng/mL). Which complication of the polyp itself best explains the anemia?","choices":{"A":"Metastasis to the brain","B":"A fistula into the bladder","C":"Chronic occult bleeding from the polyp surface","D":"Hemolysis from a vitamin E deficiency","E":"Hookworm infection of the polyp"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Large adenomas can ooze enough occult blood to cause iron deficiency, even before they invade. A noninvasive adenoma confined to the mucosa has essentially no metastatic potential. A bladder fistula is a complication of diverticulitis, not of a mucosal adenoma. Vitamin E deficiency can shorten red-cell survival, but this low ferritin and low mean corpuscular volume are iron loss. Hookworms cause iron deficiency from the small-bowel mucosa, not from living in a polyp.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A noninvasive adenoma confined to the mucosa has essentially no metastatic potential","B":"A bladder fistula is a complication of diverticulitis, not of a mucosal adenoma","D":"Vitamin E deficiency can shorten red-cell survival, but this low ferritin and low mean corpuscular volume are iron loss","E":"Hookworms cause iron deficiency from the small-bowel mucosa, not from living in a polyp"}},{"id":"sdl33_q33","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the genetics, diagnostic criteria, polyp location (pancolonic), timing (teenager), morphology, and complications of familial adenomatous polyposis.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old boy has hundreds of colorectal adenomas, and his father had a colectomy at 28. He now has headaches and ataxia. There is no jaw mass and no skin cysts. Hemoglobin is 13.8 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). MRI shows a mass in the cerebellum. Which variant pairs this polyposis with a brain tumor?","choices":{"A":"Gardner syndrome","B":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","C":"Juvenile polyposis","D":"Lynch syndrome with no excess polyps","E":"Turcot syndrome"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Turcot syndrome is familial adenomatous polyposis plus a central nervous system tumor such as medulloblastoma or glioblastoma. Gardner syndrome adds osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental abnormalities, not a cerebellar tumor. Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is hamartomas and mucocutaneous macules, with a different gene. Juvenile polyposis is hamartomatous polyps from SMAD4 or BMPR1A, not carpeting adenomas with a brain tumor. Lynch syndrome is mismatch-repair cancer with few polyps, not hundreds of adenomas.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gardner syndrome adds osteomas, desmoids, skin cysts, and dental abnormalities, not a cerebellar tumor","B":"Peutz-Jeghers syndrome is hamartomas and mucocutaneous macules, with a different gene","C":"Juvenile polyposis is hamartomatous polyps from SMAD4 or BMPR1A, not carpeting adenomas with a brain tumor","D":"Lynch syndrome is mismatch-repair cancer with few polyps, not hundreds of adenomas"}},{"id":"sdl33_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":1,"stem":"A carcinoma in the cecum usually grows in which shape?","choices":{"A":"A short napkin-ring stricture","B":"An exophytic polypoid mass","C":"A flat ulcer confined to the anus","D":"A linitis plastica of the whole stomach","E":"A pedunculated worm ball"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Right-sided colon cancers grow as exophytic polypoid masses in a wide lumen and often do not obstruct early. A napkin-ring stricture is the usual shape on the left, where the lumen is narrower. A flat anal ulcer is not the growth pattern of a cecal adenocarcinoma. Linitis plastica is a diffuse gastric carcinoma, not a cecal mass. A worm ball is a complication of heavy Ascaris infection, not a carcinoma.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A napkin-ring stricture is the usual shape on the left, where the lumen is narrower","C":"A flat anal ulcer is not the growth pattern of a cecal adenocarcinoma","D":"Linitis plastica is a diffuse gastric carcinoma, not a cecal mass","E":"A worm ball is a complication of heavy Ascaris infection, not a carcinoma"}},{"id":"sdl33_q35","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Understand the morphology, pathogenesis, epidemiology, risk factors, symptoms, and diagnosis (iron deficiency anemia), of adenocarcinoma of the colon (colorectal cancer).","batch":2,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman has a cancer of the ascending colon. Her mother had endometrial cancer at 48, and a brother had a ureteral cancer. The tumor is mucinous, with many lymphocytes in the epithelium, and testing shows high microsatellite instability. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and mean corpuscular volume is 76 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Which pathway produced this cancer?","choices":{"A":"Defective DNA mismatch repair","B":"Early loss of TP53 as the first event, with stable microsatellites","C":"A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas","D":"Only a dietary vitamin C deficiency","E":"Cholera toxin locking a G protein on"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The microsatellite-instability pathway is driven by loss of DNA mismatch repair, most often MSH2 or MLH1. TP53 loss is a late step of the chromosomal-instability pathway, and this tumor is microsatellite-unstable. A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas is familial adenomatous polyposis, not a mismatch-repair cancer. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not generate microsatellite instability. Cholera toxin drives secretory diarrhea and does not cause this colon cancer.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"TP53 loss is a late step of the chromosomal-instability pathway, and this tumor is microsatellite-unstable","C":"A germline APC mutation with thousands of adenomas is familial adenomatous polyposis, not a mismatch-repair cancer","D":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy and does not generate microsatellite instability","E":"Cholera toxin drives secretory diarrhea and does not cause this colon cancer"}}]},{"sdlNumber":34,"title":"SDL 34 — Nutritional Diseases","questions":[{"id":"sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most severely in kwashiorkor?","choices":{"A":"Somatic protein compartment","B":"Bone mineral compartment","C":"Plasma lipid compartment","D":"Intracellular water compartment","E":"Visceral protein compartment"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor results when protein deprivation is relatively greater than the reduction in total calories, and it causes marked loss of the visceral protein compartment, chiefly the liver, with resulting hypoalbuminemia. Marasmus predominantly depletes the somatic compartment of skeletal muscle. Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver. Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment. Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment. Robbins notes the somatic protein compartment is depleted more severely in marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Protein Energy Malnutrition). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the kwashiorkor mnemonic MEALS lists malnutrition, edema, anemia, liver malfunction, and skin lesions (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: serum albumin and transferrin reflect the visceral protein compartment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Bone mineral is not the compartment targeted in kwashiorkor, which depletes visceral protein, chiefly the liver.","C":"Plasma lipid is not a protein compartment.","D":"Intracellular water is not the depleted compartment.","A":"Robbins notes the somatic protein compartment is depleted more severely in marasmus."}},{"id":"sdl34_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"Marasmus is defined when a child's weight falls to about what percentage of normal for sex, height, and age?","choices":{"A":"About 30 percent","B":"About 60 percent","C":"About 45 percent","D":"About 75 percent","E":"About 90 percent"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Robbins defines marasmus as weight falling to about 60% of normal for sex, height, and age, with growth retardation and loss of muscle and subcutaneous fat. In kwashiorkor, weight is typically 60% to 80% of normal, and the true loss is masked by edema. About 30 percent is far below the threshold. About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight. About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold. About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: absence of edema distinguishes marasmus from kwashiorkor (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: in marasmus the head appears large relative to the emaciated body (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"About 30 percent is far below the threshold.","C":"About 45 percent is below the defining threshold of about 60% of normal weight.","D":"About 75 percent falls within the kwashiorkor range of 60% to 80%, not the marasmus threshold.","E":"About 90 percent is near normal weight and does not define marasmus."}},{"id":"sdl34_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"The enlarged fatty liver in kwashiorkor results mainly from which process?","choices":{"A":"Increased delivery of dietary fat","B":"Hepatic venous congestion","C":"Hepatocyte iron accumulation","D":"Reduced apolipoprotein synthesis","E":"Excess hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In kwashiorkor the liver is enlarged and fatty because synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins is reduced, so triglyceride is not exported efficiently. The diet is typically carbohydrate based rather than fat rich. Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins. Excess glycogen is not the problem; triglyceride accumulates because apolipoprotein synthesis falls. Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, the mottled nutmeg liver, not a diffusely fatty liver. Robbins attributes the enlarged fatty liver of kwashiorkor to reduced synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins, not increased dietary fat delivery. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: First Aid attributes the fatty change in kwashiorkor to decreased apolipoprotein synthesis (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: superimposed fatty change can occur in marasmus as well, but it is characteristic of kwashiorkor (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Iron accumulation is not the cause; the fatty liver comes from failure to make lipoprotein carrier proteins.","E":"Excess glycogen is not the problem.","B":"Hepatic venous congestion produces centrilobular hemorrhagic necrosis, the mottled nutmeg liver, not a diffusely fatty liver.","A":"Robbins attributes the enlarged fatty liver of kwashiorkor to reduced synthesis of the carrier protein components of lipoproteins, not increased dietary fat delivery."}},{"id":"sdl34_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"Acute vitamin A toxicity produces headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that can mimic which condition?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial meningitis","B":"Pseudotumor cerebri","C":"Migraine with aura","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute toxicity from a large dose of vitamin A causes headache, vomiting, and blurred vision that may be confused with a brain tumor, known as pseudotumor cerebri. Bacterial meningitis is an infection. Migraine is a primary headache, not raised intracranial pressure. Subarachnoid hemorrhage is a sudden worst headache with blood in the CSF. Hypertensive encephalopathy needs severe hypertension with end-organ damage. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Toxicity). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin A excess causes idiopathic intracranial hypertension, and isotretinoin is teratogenic (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: retinoic acid stimulates osteoclast production and activity, so chronic excess raises fracture risk (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Migraine is a primary headache: unilateral, pulsating, 4-72 hours, with nausea and photophobia and sometimes an aura; it is not a state of raised intracranial pressure.","D":"Subarachnoid hemorrhage is a sudden \"worst headache of my life\" from a ruptured aneurysm or AVM, with bloody or xanthochromic CSF; vitamin A excess instead raises ICP without bleeding.","E":"Hypertensive encephalopathy needs severe hypertension (>=180/120 mm Hg) with acute end-organ damage; vitamin A toxicity raises ICP without hypertension.","A":"Bacterial meningitis is an infectious cause of headache, not isolated raised intracranial pressure from vitamin A."}},{"id":"sdl34_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin K is required for hepatic carboxylation of which group of clotting factors?","choices":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII","B":"Factors VIII, IX, XI, and XII","C":"Factors II, VII, IX, and X","D":"Factors II, V, VIII, and X","E":"Factors III, VII, XI, and XIII"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of procoagulant factors II, VII, IX, and X and of proteins C and S. Deficiency produces a bleeding diathesis, especially in fat malabsorption and after broad antibiotic courses. Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; the dependent factors are II, VII, IX, and X. Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is. Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X. Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase and lowers functional factor levels (First Aid Hematology, Warfarin). COMLEX: vitamin K is fat soluble, so biliary and pancreatic disease reduce its absorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Factors I, V, VIII, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent.","B":"Factors VIII, XI, and XII are not vitamin K dependent; only IX of this set is.","D":"Factors V and VIII are not vitamin K dependent, so this set is wrong despite including II and X.","E":"Factors III, XI, and XIII are not vitamin K dependent; only VII of this set is."}},{"id":"sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"Which factor stimulates renal 1-alpha hydroxylase to increase active vitamin D production?","choices":{"A":"Parathyroid hormone","B":"Fibroblast growth factor 23","C":"Serum hypercalcemia","D":"Serum hyperphosphatemia","E":"Calcitriol excess"},"correct":"A","explanation":"After hepatic 25-hydroxylation, the kidney converts 25-hydroxyvitamin D to the active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D. Renal 1-alpha hydroxylase is stimulated by PTH and hypophosphatemia and inhibited by 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D and FGF23. Hypocalcemia acts indirectly by raising PTH. Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH. Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does. FGF23, made by osteoblasts and osteocytes, inhibits renal 1-alpha hydroxylase expression rather than stimulating it. 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D itself inhibits 1-alpha hydroxylase expression, so excess calcitriol shuts the enzyme down. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Metabolism). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: chronic kidney disease reduces renal activation of vitamin D (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: 25-hydroxyvitamin D is the major circulating form and the usual marker of vitamin D status (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Hypercalcemia suppresses PTH and therefore 1-alpha hydroxylase; it is hypocalcemia that raises PTH.","D":"Hyperphosphatemia does not stimulate the enzyme; hypophosphatemia does.","B":"FGF23, made by osteoblasts and osteocytes, inhibits renal 1-alpha hydroxylase expression rather than stimulating it.","E":"1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D itself inhibits 1-alpha hydroxylase expression, so excess calcitriol shuts the enzyme down."}},{"id":"sdl34_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Riboflavin functions in the body after conversion to which coenzyme?","choices":{"A":"Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate","D":"Flavin adenine dinucleotide","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Riboflavin is converted to flavin mononucleotide and flavin adenine dinucleotide, which serve as cofactors for many enzymes in intermediary metabolism. Deficiency causes cheilosis, stomatitis, glossitis, dermatitis, and corneal vascularization. NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin. Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin. Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin. Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: riboflavin deficiency causes cheilosis and corneal vascularization, the two Cs of B2 (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B2). COMLEX: niacin forms the nicotinamide coenzymes, while thiamine forms the pyrophosphate (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"NAD is the coenzyme derived from niacin, not riboflavin.","B":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the active form of vitamin B1, not riboflavin.","C":"Pyridoxal phosphate is the active form of vitamin B6, not riboflavin.","E":"Tetrahydrofolic acid is derived from folate, not riboflavin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Copper is an essential component of which enzyme needed for collagen cross-linking?","choices":{"A":"Lysyl oxidase","B":"Glutathione peroxidase","C":"Carbonic anhydrase","D":"Xanthine oxidase","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Copper is a component of cytochrome c oxidase, dopamine beta hydroxylase, tyrosinase, lysyl oxidase, and other enzymes. Deficiency causes muscle weakness, neurologic defects, and abnormal collagen cross-linking, and it may follow excess zinc intake. Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking. Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase. Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid and is the target of allopurinol, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme. Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Menkes disease impairs copper transport and lysyl oxidase activity (First Aid Biochemistry, Menkes disease). COMLEX: glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not copper (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Glutathione peroxidase is a selenium-containing antioxidant enzyme, not the copper enzyme of collagen cross-linking.","C":"Carbonic anhydrase is a zinc enzyme, not copper-dependent lysyl oxidase.","D":"Xanthine oxidase converts hypoxanthine and xanthine to uric acid and is the target of allopurinol, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme.","E":"Alcohol dehydrogenase oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, not a collagen cross-linking enzyme."}},{"id":"sdl34_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":1,"stem":"Selenium deficiency is the cause of which endemic cardiomyopathy?","choices":{"A":"Wet beriberi","B":"Chagas disease","C":"Keshan disease","D":"Kawasaki disease","E":"Loeffler endocarditis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Selenium is a component of glutathione peroxidase, an antioxidant enzyme. Deficiency causes myopathy and congestive cardiomyopathy known as Keshan disease, described in regions with selenium-poor soil. Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency. Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi parasitizing myofibers, not a selenium deficiency. Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that damages coronary arteries, not a nutritional cardiomyopathy. Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven by eosinophil products in hypereosinophilia, not selenium deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glutathione peroxidase is a free radical scavenging enzyme (First Aid Pathology, Free radical injury). COMLEX: the antioxidant role of selenium overlaps functionally with vitamin E (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wet beriberi is high-output heart failure from thiamine deficiency, not selenium deficiency.","B":"Chagas disease is a myocarditis caused by Trypanosoma cruzi parasitizing myofibers, not a selenium deficiency.","D":"Kawasaki disease is an arteritis of young children that damages coronary arteries, not a nutritional cardiomyopathy.","E":"Loeffler endomyocarditis is endomyocardial fibrosis driven by eosinophil products in hypereosinophilia, not selenium deficiency."}},{"id":"sdl34_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"The central processing system that integrates peripheral signals of energy balance lies mainly in which hypothalamic nucleus?","choices":{"A":"Supraoptic nucleus","B":"Suprachiasmatic nucleus","C":"Preoptic nucleus","D":"Arcuate nucleus","E":"Mammillary nucleus"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Robbins describes energy balance as an afferent system, a central processing system in the hypothalamus, and an efferent system. Within the arcuate nucleus, POMC and CART neurons promote catabolism and NPY and AgRP neurons promote anabolism. Supraoptic nuclei control renal water excretion by releasing ADH from the posterior pituitary, not energy balance. The suprachiasmatic nucleus above the optic chiasm is the circadian master clock, not the energy-balance integrator. The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus regulates body temperature, not energy stores. The mammillary bodies partly control feeding reflexes such as lip licking and swallowing, whereas arcuate neurons integrate energy-store signals. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Regulation of Energy Balance). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a ventromedial hypothalamic lesion causes hyperphagia and obesity (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: arcuate NPY and AgRP neurons also inhibit neighboring POMC neurons (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Supraoptic nuclei control renal water excretion by releasing ADH from the posterior pituitary, not energy balance.","B":"The suprachiasmatic nucleus above the optic chiasm is the circadian master clock, not the energy-balance integrator.","C":"The preoptic area of the anterior hypothalamus regulates body temperature, not energy stores.","E":"The mammillary bodies partly control feeding reflexes such as lip licking and swallowing, whereas arcuate neurons integrate energy-store signals."}},{"id":"sdl34_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Peptide YY and glucagon-like peptide 1 are secreted by which intestinal cell type?","choices":{"A":"K cells","B":"I cells","C":"S cells","D":"G cells","E":"L cells"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Peptide YY and GLP-1 are secreted by L cells in the distal ileum and colon. GIP comes from K cells in the duodenum and jejunum. These gut signals act on the hypothalamus and pancreas after a meal. I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GLP-1. K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa secrete GIP, not peptide YY or GLP-1. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GIP and GLP-1 are the incretin hormones of the small intestine (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Gastrointestinal regulatory substances). COMLEX: incretins act on pancreatic beta cells after enteral glucose (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"I cells of the duodenum and jejunum secrete cholecystokinin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","C":"S cells of the duodenum secrete secretin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","D":"G cells of the stomach secrete gastrin, not peptide YY or GLP-1.","A":"K cells of the duodenal, jejunal, and ileal mucosa secrete GIP, not peptide YY or GLP-1."}},{"id":"sdl34_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":5,"stem":"Binding of GLP-1 to its receptor on pancreatic beta cells increases which second messenger to promote insulin release?","choices":{"A":"Cyclic AMP","B":"Cyclic GMP","C":"Inositol trisphosphate","D":"Diacylglycerol","E":"Nitric oxide"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Incretin receptors on beta cells are G protein coupled receptors that activate adenylate cyclase. The resulting rise in cyclic AMP enhances glucose-stimulated insulin secretion. Cyclic GMP is the messenger of nitric oxide signaling through guanylyl cyclase, not of the incretin receptor that activates adenylate cyclase. Nitric oxide is a diffusible gas that signals through guanylyl cyclase; it is not the second messenger raised by GLP-1 receptor activation. Inositol trisphosphate comes from phospholipase C cleavage of PIP2 and mobilizes stored calcium, not the GLP-1 messenger. Diacylglycerol is the other phospholipase C product of PIP2 breakdown, not the GLP-1 messenger. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: the incretin effect explains why oral glucose releases more insulin than IV glucose (First Aid Endocrine, Insulin). COMLEX: GIP and GLP-1 are the two principal incretins (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Cyclic GMP is the messenger of nitric oxide signaling through guanylyl cyclase, not of the incretin receptor that activates adenylate cyclase.","E":"Nitric oxide is a diffusible gas that signals through guanylyl cyclase; it is not the second messenger raised by GLP-1 receptor activation.","C":"Inositol trisphosphate comes from phospholipase C cleavage of PIP2 and mobilizes stored calcium, not the GLP-1 messenger.","D":"Diacylglycerol is the other phospholipase C product of PIP2 breakdown, not the GLP-1 messenger."}},{"id":"sdl34_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Why does obesity predispose to cholesterol gallstones?","choices":{"A":"It cuts hepatic cholesterol synthesis","B":"It raises biliary cholesterol output","C":"It speeds gallbladder emptying","D":"It increases biliary bile salt pools","E":"It promotes red cell hemolysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Obesity increases total body cholesterol and cholesterol turnover, raising biliary excretion of cholesterol and making bile supersaturated with it. Cutting hepatic cholesterol synthesis would lower biliary cholesterol and protect against stones. Faster gallbladder emptying would oppose stone formation, since hypomotility is a contributor. Larger bile salt pools would keep more cholesterol in micelles and oppose crystal formation. Red cell hemolysis leads to black pigment stones, not obesity-related cholesterol stones. Sources: Robbins ch 9, obesity; Robbins ch 18, gallbladder, risk factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity raises biliary cholesterol excretion and is a major risk factor for cholesterol stones (Robbins ch 9). COMLEX: Rapid weight loss after bariatric surgery also raises risk, and ursodiol can be used for prevention.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 18 → Obj 2 — biochemical pathogenesis of cholesterol gallstones","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cutting hepatic cholesterol synthesis would lower biliary","C":"Faster gallbladder emptying would oppose stone formation,","D":"Larger bile salt pools would keep more","E":"Red cell hemolysis leads to black pigment"}},{"id":"sdl34_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Obesity raises the risk of which type of esophageal cancer?","choices":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma","B":"Small cell carcinoma","C":"Adenocarcinoma","D":"Leiomyosarcoma","E":"Mucosal melanoma"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma of the lower third is associated with chronic GERD, Barrett esophagus, obesity, and smoking, and Robbins lists esophageal carcinoma among obesity-related cancers. Squamous cell carcinoma is tied to alcohol, smoking, hot liquids, and achalasia rather than obesity. Small cell carcinoma is a rare esophageal tumor and is not among the obesity-associated types. Leiomyosarcoma is a rare smooth muscle tumor with no described link to obesity. Mucosal melanoma is rare in the esophagus and is not an obesity-associated cancer. Sources: First Aid 2025 GI, esophageal cancer; Robbins ch 9, obesity and cancer.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Obesity, GERD, and Barrett esophagus predispose to distal esophageal adenocarcinoma (First Aid 2025 GI). COMLEX: Progressive dysphagia to solids in an obese patient with long-standing reflux warrants endoscopy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Squamous cell carcinoma is tied to alcohol,","B":"Small cell carcinoma is a rare esophageal","D":"Leiomyosarcoma is a rare smooth muscle tumor","E":"Mucosal melanoma is rare in the esophagus"}},{"id":"sdl34_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"Obesity raises free estrogen in part by reducing hepatic synthesis of which protein?","choices":{"A":"Thyroxine binding globulin","B":"Corticosteroid binding globulin","C":"Vitamin D binding protein","D":"Retinol binding protein","E":"Sex hormone binding globulin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Obesity increases estrogen synthesis from androgens in adipose tissue and reduces hepatic synthesis of sex hormone binding globulin, raising free estrogen. This mechanism helps explain the higher risk of endometrial and breast cancer. Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen. Thyroxine-binding globulin carries most plasma T4 and T3, not estrogen. Corticosteroid-binding globulin (transcortin) binds most plasma cortisol, not estrogen. Vitamin D-binding protein carries vitamin D to the liver; it does not regulate free estrogen. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: aromatase in adipose tissue converts androgens to estrogen (First Aid Reproductive, Estrogen). COMLEX: hyperinsulinemia also suppresses sex hormone binding globulin (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Retinol binding protein carries vitamin A from the liver and does not regulate free estrogen.","A":"Thyroxine-binding globulin carries most plasma T4 and T3, not estrogen.","B":"Corticosteroid-binding globulin (transcortin) binds most plasma cortisol, not estrogen.","C":"Vitamin D-binding protein carries vitamin D to the liver; it does not regulate free estrogen."}},{"id":"sdl34_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 2-year-old boy in a refugee camp was weaned after his sister was born and now eats mainly maize gruel. He is apathetic and has generalized pitting edema, peeling hyperpigmented skin patches, pale banded hair, and hepatomegaly. Serum albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.5 g/dL). Which change most directly produces his edema?","choices":{"A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure","B":"Increased endothelial permeability","C":"Obstructed lymphatic drainage","D":"Decreased plasma oncotic pressure","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention"},"correct":"D","explanation":"This child has kwashiorkor. Loss of visceral protein lowers serum albumin, which reduces plasma oncotic pressure and allows fluid to leave capillaries, producing generalized edema. The flaky paint dermatosis and banded hair are characteristic accompanying findings. Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: a small child with a swollen belly and peeling skin is the classic kwashiorkor picture (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: kwashiorkor is also seen with protein-losing enteropathy, nephrotic syndrome, and burns (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Obstructed lymphatics cause lymphedema, whereas kwashiorkor edema results from hypoalbuminemia.","A":"Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure is not part of that answer.","B":"Increased endothelial permeability is not part of that answer.","E":"Increased interstitial sodium retention is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl34_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had little food of any kind for months. She is emaciated with wasted limbs, no subcutaneous fat, and a head that looks large for her body. There is no edema. Her weight is 55% of expected for age. Which hormonal change most likely contributes to the loss of her fat stores?","choices":{"A":"Increased cortisol secretion","B":"Increased leptin secretion","C":"Increased insulin secretion","D":"Decreased glucagon secretion","E":"Decreased epinephrine secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This child has marasmus. Low leptin levels stimulate the hypothalamic pituitary adrenal axis, and the resulting high cortisol contributes to lipolysis. Fat and muscle are mobilized as energy sources. Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise. Glucagon secretion rises, not falls, when blood glucose drops in starvation. Hypoglycemia stimulates sympathetic outflow and adrenal epinephrine release, so epinephrine rises rather than falls. Robbins explains that low leptin in marasmus may stimulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis to produce high cortisol that contributes to lipolysis, not increased insulin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Marasmus). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: marasmus results from overall calorie deficiency rather than isolated protein lack (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus also causes immune deficiency, especially of T cell mediated immunity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin falls, not rises, in marasmus as fat stores shrink, and low leptin drives the cortisol rise.","D":"Glucagon secretion rises, not falls, when blood glucose drops in starvation.","E":"Hypoglycemia stimulates sympathetic outflow and adrenal epinephrine release, so epinephrine rises rather than falls.","C":"Robbins explains that low leptin in marasmus may stimulate the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis to produce high cortisol that contributes to lipolysis, not increased insulin."}},{"id":"sdl34_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":5,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy is treated for protein malnutrition with generalized edema, skin desquamation, and a fatty liver. After starting full-strength milk-based feeds he develops bloating and watery diarrhea. Stool is acidic and positive for reducing substances. Which mechanism most likely explains his diarrhea?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency","B":"Mucosal disaccharidase loss","C":"Cow milk protein allergy","D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, small bowel crypt mitoses decrease, causing mucosal atrophy, loss of villi and microvilli, and secondary disaccharidase deficiency. Infants may therefore respond poorly to full-strength milk diets until the mucosa recovers. Acidic stool with reducing substances points to undigested sugar. Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances. Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea. Pancreatic lipase deficiency and cow milk protein allergy are different problems. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: lactase deficiency gives osmotic diarrhea with low stool pH (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Lactose intolerance). COMLEX: the mucosal changes of kwashiorkor reverse with treatment (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"D":"Ileal bile acid malabsorption would not produce acidic stool with reducing substances.","E":"Bacterial enterotoxin secretion causes secretory diarrhea.","A":"Pancreatic lipase deficiency is a different cause of fat malabsorption.","C":"Cow milk protein allergy is an immune reaction to milk protein."}},{"id":"sdl34_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with Crohn disease had 120 cm of distal ileum resected 3 years ago. She has greasy stools and now has trouble seeing when driving at dusk. Examination shows dry conjunctivae with small gray foamy plaques. The deficient vitamin normally serves which function?","choices":{"A":"Cofactor for gamma carboxylation","B":"Membrane lipid antioxidant","C":"Regulator of calcium absorption","D":"Component of visual pigment","E":"Cofactor for collagen hydroxylation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin A deficiency develops in fat malabsorption such as Crohn disease because absorption requires bile and lipid digestion. Retinal combines with opsin to form rhodopsin, so night blindness appears first, followed by xerophthalmia and Bitot spots. Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness. Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia. Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness. Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin A, Deficiency). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bitot spots and night blindness indicate vitamin A deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin A). COMLEX: vitamin A deficiency also causes squamous metaplasia of airway and urinary epithelium (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — terminal ileal disease and bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma carboxylation is the role of vitamin K, whose deficiency causes bleeding, not night blindness.","B":"Membrane antioxidant protection is vitamin E, whose deficiency causes ataxia and hemolysis, not xerophthalmia.","C":"Regulating calcium absorption is vitamin D, whose deficiency causes osteomalacia, not night blindness.","E":"Collagen hydroxylation needs vitamin C, whose deficiency causes scurvy, not Bitot spots."}},{"id":"sdl34_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 17-year-old girl with cystic fibrosis rarely takes her pancreatic enzymes. She has a wide-based gait, absent ankle reflexes, and loss of vibration and position sense. Hemoglobin is 10.1 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) with MCV 88 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL). Serum methylmalonic acid is normal. Which process most likely underlies her findings?","choices":{"A":"Myelin methylation failure","B":"Clotting factor undercarboxylation","C":"Membrane lipid peroxidation","D":"Collagen hydroxylation failure","E":"Rhodopsin regeneration failure"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Vitamin E is a fat-soluble antioxidant that protects membrane lipids from free radical peroxidation. Deficiency, seen with fat malabsorption such as pancreatic insufficiency, causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia. A normal methylmalonic acid argues against B12 deficiency. Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration. Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia. Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia. Robbins lists vitamin E as the major free radical scavenging antioxidant whose deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia, not a myelin methylation defect. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin E). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin E deficiency mimics B12 neuropathy without megaloblastosis or raised methylmalonic acid (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin E). COMLEX: vitamin E prevents oxidation of unsaturated fats in cell membranes (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Undercarboxylated clotting factors cause bleeding from vitamin K deficiency, not spinocerebellar degeneration.","D":"Failed collagen hydroxylation is scurvy, which causes bleeding gums and petechiae, not ataxia.","E":"Failed rhodopsin regeneration is vitamin A deficiency, which causes night blindness, not ataxia.","A":"Robbins lists vitamin E as the major free radical scavenging antioxidant whose deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and hemolytic anemia, not a myelin methylation defect."}},{"id":"sdl34_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with poorly controlled celiac disease has diffuse bone pain and proximal muscle weakness. Serum calcium is 8.6 mg/dL (reference range: 8.4–10.2 mg/dL), phosphorus 2.1 mg/dL (reference range: 3.0–4.5 mg/dL), alkaline phosphatase 210 U/L (reference range: 25–100 U/L), and PTH 98 pg/mL (reference range: 10–65 pg/mL). A bone biopsy would most likely show which finding?","choices":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization","B":"Mosaic pattern of lamellar bone","C":"Marrow fibrosis with brown tumors","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae","E":"Thickened seams of unmineralized osteoid"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin D malabsorption leads to secondary hyperparathyroidism, which restores calcium toward normal but leaves phosphate low. Impaired mineralization in the adult produces osteomalacia, in which excess unmineralized osteoid accumulates on bone surfaces. Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget disease, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia. Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, an infarction of bone, not defective mineralization. Robbins describes osteomalacia as excess persistent, inadequately mineralized osteoid seen as a thickened matrix layer, not marrow fibrosis with brown tumors. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Deficiency States). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: osteomalacia shows low calcium or phosphate with high PTH and alkaline phosphatase (First Aid Musculoskeletal, Osteomalacia). COMLEX: the same defect in children causes rickets with growth plate deformities (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Thin trabeculae with normal mineralization describe osteoporosis, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia.","B":"A mosaic pattern of lamellar bone is the hallmark of Paget disease, not vitamin D-deficient osteomalacia.","D":"Necrotic trabeculae with empty lacunae mark osteonecrosis, an infarction of bone, not defective mineralization.","C":"Robbins describes osteomalacia as excess persistent, inadequately mineralized osteoid seen as a thickened matrix layer, not marrow fibrosis with brown tumors."}},{"id":"sdl34_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with alcohol use disorder is admitted with confusion and receives an IV dextrose infusion. Hours later he has nystagmus, bilateral lateral gaze palsy, and a wide-based gait. Serum lactate is 42 mg/dL (reference range: 4.5–19.8 mg/dL). Reduced activity of which enzyme most directly explains his lactate rise?","choices":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase","C":"Pyruvate dehydrogenase","D":"Fructose bisphosphatase","E":"Phosphofructokinase"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Thiamine pyrophosphate is the coenzyme for oxidative decarboxylation reactions, including pyruvate dehydrogenase. Without it, pyruvate is shunted to lactate, and a glucose load worsens the deficit. Ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and confusion indicate Wernicke encephalopathy. Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate. Pyruvate carboxylase is a biotin-containing carboxylase that adds CO2 to pyruvate to form oxaloacetate; it is not thiamine-dependent. Phosphofructokinase-1 is the allosteric rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis, not a thiamine-dependent oxidative decarboxylation. Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase is a gluconeogenic enzyme, not a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme like pyruvate dehydrogenase. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.9, Vitamins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Thiamine).","boardPrep":"Step 1: give thiamine before dextrose in alcohol use disorder to avoid precipitating Wernicke encephalopathy (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B1). COMLEX: thiamine deficiency also causes dry and wet beriberi (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Lactate dehydrogenase is not thiamine-dependent and actually forms the excess lactate.","B":"Pyruvate carboxylase is a biotin-containing carboxylase that adds CO2 to pyruvate to form oxaloacetate; it is not thiamine-dependent.","E":"Phosphofructokinase-1 is the allosteric rate-limiting enzyme of glycolysis, not a thiamine-dependent oxidative decarboxylation.","D":"Fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase is a gluconeogenic enzyme, not a thiamine pyrophosphate-dependent enzyme like pyruvate dehydrogenase."}},{"id":"sdl34_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman has a metastatic midgut neuroendocrine tumor with episodic flushing and diarrhea. Over months she develops a rough dermatitis on sun-exposed skin of her neck and forearms and new forgetfulness. Diversion of which amino acid most likely explains her new findings?","choices":{"A":"Tyrosine","B":"Histidine","C":"Methionine","D":"Phenylalanine","E":"Tryptophan"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Niacin can be synthesized from tryptophan. Carcinoid tumors divert tryptophan toward serotonin, reducing niacin synthesis and producing pellagra with dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia. Tyrosine is the precursor converted through dopa to the catecholamines, not the substrate diverted to serotonin. Histidine is decarboxylated to histamine, not diverted to serotonin. Phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase, not used to make serotonin or niacin. Methionine supplies the sulfur for cysteine synthesis; niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, not methionine. Sources: First Aid Biochemistry (Vitamin B3: niacin from tryptophan; carcinoid syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pellagra occurs with carcinoid syndrome, Hartnup disease, and isoniazid use (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin B3). COMLEX: niacin forms NAD and NADP for redox reactions (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Tyrosine is the precursor converted through dopa to the catecholamines, not the substrate diverted to serotonin.","B":"Histidine is decarboxylated to histamine, not diverted to serotonin.","D":"Phenylalanine is hydroxylated to tyrosine by phenylalanine hydroxylase, not used to make serotonin or niacin.","C":"Methionine supplies the sulfur for cysteine synthesis; niacin is synthesized from tryptophan, not methionine."}},{"id":"sdl34_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has received home parenteral nutrition for 6 months using a trace element mix that omitted zinc, with other trace elements supplied after extensive small bowel resection. He now has a red scaly rash around his mouth, nose, and anus, worsening diarrhea, poor wound healing, and loss of taste. Which additional finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Impaired night vision","B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Zinc deficiency from zinc-free parenteral nutrition causes a periorificial rash like acrodermatitis enteropathica, anorexia, diarrhea, poor wound healing, and impaired night vision. Zinc is a component of many enzymes, principally oxidases. Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency. Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Table 9.10, Trace Elements). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: zinc deficiency causes delayed wound healing, dysgeusia, and hypogonadism (First Aid Biochemistry, Zinc). COMLEX: acrodermatitis enteropathica is the inherited form of zinc malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Perifollicular hemorrhages indicate vitamin C deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","C":"Ring sideroblastic anemia indicates copper deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","D":"Goiter with hypothyroidism indicates iodine deficiency, not zinc deficiency.","E":"Dilated cardiomyopathy indicates selenium deficiency (Keshan disease), not zinc deficiency."}},{"id":"sdl34_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old girl has had insatiable hunger and severe obesity since infancy. Serum leptin is undetectable, and sequencing shows a homozygous mutation in the leptin gene. She begins daily recombinant leptin injections. The expected decrease in her food intake is mediated mainly by which hypothalamic effect?","choices":{"A":"Activation of NPY and AgRP neurons","B":"Activation of lateral orexin neurons","C":"Activation of lateral MCH neurons","D":"Inhibition of paraventricular TRH neurons","E":"Activation of POMC and CART neurons"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leptin acts in the arcuate nucleus to stimulate POMC and CART neurons, which release alpha MSH to act on melanocortin receptors and decrease food intake. It also inhibits NPY and AgRP neurons that drive feeding. Orexins A and B are orexigenic and stimulate feeding, so their activation would not reduce intake. Melanin-concentrating hormone is orexigenic and increases feeding, so activating MCH neurons would not reduce intake. Leptin inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, the appetite gas pedal, so activating them would increase rather than reduce food intake. Second-order neurons downstream of POMC/CART produce TRH, BDNF, and CRH to reduce food intake, so inhibiting TRH neurons would oppose leptin's effect. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin is secreted by adipose tissue and decreases appetite (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: mutations of leptin or its receptor cause massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Orexins A and B are orexigenic and stimulate feeding, so their activation would not reduce intake.","C":"Melanin-concentrating hormone is orexigenic and increases feeding, so activating MCH neurons would not reduce intake.","A":"Leptin inhibits NPY/AgRP neurons, the appetite gas pedal, so activating them would increase rather than reduce food intake.","D":"Second-order neurons downstream of POMC/CART produce TRH, BDNF, and CRH to reduce food intake, so inhibiting TRH neurons would oppose leptin's effect."}},{"id":"sdl34_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with a BMI of 44 undergoes Roux-en-Y gastric bypass. Six months later she reports much less hunger before meals than she had with prior diets. A blunted rise of which hormone most likely contributes?","choices":{"A":"Leptin","B":"Peptide YY","C":"Ghrelin","D":"Insulin","E":"Cholecystokinin"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ghrelin is an orexigenic gut hormone that increases food intake. It is produced mainly in the stomach, rises before meals, and acts on NPY and AgRP neurons. Its preprandial rise is blunted after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, which may contribute to weight loss. Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass. Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger. Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger. Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppresses further feeding, so it does not drive pre-meal hunger as fasting ghrelin does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ghrelin is increased in Prader-Willi syndrome and with sleep deprivation (First Aid Endocrine, Appetite regulation). COMLEX: ghrelin levels are typically lower in obese individuals (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Leptin is a long-term fat-mass signal that suppresses appetite, not the premeal hunger hormone blunted by bypass.","B":"Peptide YY rises after meals and reduces appetite, so a blunted rise would not lessen hunger.","D":"Insulin suppresses appetite, so a blunted insulin rise would not explain reduced hunger.","E":"Cholecystokinin is released after food ingestion and suppresses further feeding, so it does not drive pre-meal hunger as fasting ghrelin does."}},{"id":"sdl34_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with type 2 diabetes and a BMI of 34 starts a weekly injected drug that mimics a hormone of ileal and colonic endocrine cells. Over 6 months his hemoglobin A1c improves and he loses 9 kg. Which action of the drug most likely accounts for his weight loss?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption","B":"Central suppression of food intake","C":"Increased brown fat thermogenesis","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion","E":"Decreased hepatic glycogen storage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"GLP-1 from intestinal L cells is an incretin that augments insulin release, and it also acts on the hypothalamus to decrease food intake and slows gastric emptying. GLP-1 receptor agonists produce weight loss mainly through reduced appetite. Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the GI lipase inhibitor orlistat, not of a GLP-1 mimetic. Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors, not of a GLP-1 receptor agonist. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Gut Hormones). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Costanzo Ch. 8 (Hunger and Satiety).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 agonists decrease appetite and slow gastric emptying (First Aid Endocrine, Diabetes mellitus therapy). COMLEX: lipase inhibitors reduce fat absorption, a different weight loss mechanism (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 4 — oral glucose, incretins, and insulin release","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibition of intestinal fat absorption is the action of the GI lipase inhibitor orlistat, not of a GLP-1 mimetic.","D":"Increased renal glucose excretion is the action of SGLT2 inhibitors, not of a GLP-1 receptor agonist."}},{"id":"sdl34_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"A 49-year-old man with a BMI of 37 has acanthosis nigricans and a waist circumference of 118 cm. Fasting glucose is 118 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL), triglycerides 260 mg/dL (reference range: <150 mg/dL), and HDL 32 mg/dL (reference range: 40–60 mg/dL). Which mediator, released after inflammasome activation by excess free fatty acids, most likely drives his insulin resistance?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 10","B":"Interleukin 4","C":"Transforming growth factor beta","D":"Interleukin 1","E":"Interferon alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In obesity, excess free fatty acids activate the inflammasome, which releases interleukin 1 and promotes insulin resistance. IL-10 and TGF-beta limit immune responses. IL-4 drives allergic IgE switching. Interferon alpha is a type I interferon made in viral infection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: metabolic syndrome combines central obesity, hypertension, hyperglycemia, and dyslipidemia (First Aid Endocrine, Metabolic syndrome). COMLEX: obesity is associated with high triglycerides and low HDL (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that serves mainly to limit and terminate immune responses, not to drive obesity-associated inflammation.","B":"IL-4 is a signature Th2 cytokine that drives IgE isotype switching in allergic responses.","C":"TGF-beta is an anti-inflammatory and fibrogenic cytokine that limits immune responses.","E":"Interferon alpha is a type I interferon made in viral infection."}},{"id":"sdl34_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":5,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with a BMI of 38 and type 2 diabetes drinks alcohol rarely. ALT is 78 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L) and AST is 52 U/L (reference range: 12–38 U/L). Ultrasound shows a diffusely echogenic liver, and viral and autoimmune studies are negative. A reduced serum level of which adipose hormone most likely contributes to her liver disease?","choices":{"A":"Adiponectin","B":"Leptin","C":"Resistin","D":"Angiotensinogen","E":"Testosterone"},"correct":"A","explanation":"This is metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease. Adiponectin, which is reduced in obesity, increases fatty acid oxidation and insulin sensitivity in liver and muscle. Its decline contributes to insulin resistance, type 2 diabetes, and hepatic steatosis. Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Adiponectin; Obesity, Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MASLD is the hepatic manifestation of metabolic syndrome, with ALT usually above AST (First Aid Gastrointestinal, MASLD). COMLEX: obesity related steatosis may progress to steatohepatitis and fibrosis (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"Leptin is elevated, not reduced, in obesity because of central leptin resistance.","C":"Resistin is not part of that answer.","D":"Angiotensinogen is not part of that answer.","E":"Testosterone is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl34_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with a BMI of 39 and fasting hyperinsulinemia is diagnosed with sigmoid adenocarcinoma. His physician explains that high insulin raises free levels of a growth factor that stimulates tumor cell proliferation. Through which signaling route does this growth factor mainly act?","choices":{"A":"Wnt and catenin pathways","B":"RAS and PI3K pathways","C":"Notch and Hedgehog pathways","D":"TGF beta and SMAD pathways","E":"JAK and STAT pathways"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hyperinsulinemia raises free insulin-like growth factor 1, a mitogen whose receptor activates the RAS and PI3K AKT pathways and promotes growth of tumor cells. This is one proposed link between obesity and colorectal and other cancers. Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1. TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route. JAK/STAT signaling is activated by JAK2 point mutations in myeloproliferative neoplasms, not the main IGF-1 receptor route. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Obesity and Cancer). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: colorectal cancer risk factors include obesity, red meat, and a low fiber diet (First Aid Gastrointestinal, Colorectal cancer). COMLEX: excess adipose estrogen production is another obesity cancer link (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma risk factors","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Wnt and beta-catenin signaling is the APC pathway, not the target of insulin-like growth factor 1.","D":"TGF-beta and SMAD signaling is a growth-inhibitory pathway lost in colon cancer, not the IGF-1 mitogenic route.","E":"JAK/STAT signaling is activated by JAK2 point mutations in myeloproliferative neoplasms, not the main IGF-1 receptor route."}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":3,"stem":"A 14-month-old girl in the United States has been fed only a rice beverage since weaning. She has generalized edema, flaky skin, and a fatty liver. Which tissue is relatively spared?","choices":{"A":"Subcutaneous fat stores","B":"Hepatic protein stores","C":"Plasma albumin pool","D":"Thymic lymphoid tissue","E":"Intestinal villous mucosa"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In kwashiorkor, the visceral compartment is markedly depleted while loss of subcutaneous fat and muscle mass is relatively mild and masked by edema. Thymic and lymphoid atrophy and small bowel villous loss are prominent. Robbins notes cases in developed countries from milk replacement with rice-based beverages. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Kwashiorkor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: kwashiorkor follows a diet deficient in protein despite adequate calories (First Aid Biochemistry, Malnutrition). COMLEX: marasmus depletes subcutaneous fat and muscle more severely (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 2 — chylomicron formation and lipoprotein transport","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 68-year-old man has taken no food by mouth for 2 weeks while receiving broad-spectrum antibiotics. He now oozes from IV sites. PT is 24 s (reference range: 11–15 s). He takes no anticoagulants. What is the main cause?","choices":{"A":"Increased hepatic vitamin breakdown","B":"Increased renal vitamin loss","C":"Blocked epoxide reductase activity","D":"Loss of gut bacterial synthesis","E":"Impaired platelet granule release"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Vitamin K comes from the diet and from intestinal bacteria. Without oral intake and with gut flora suppressed by antibiotics, vitamin K dependent factors fall and the PT rises. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin K). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Guyton Ch. 72 (Vitamin K).","boardPrep":"Step 1: vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT and aPTT with a normal bleeding time (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin K). COMLEX: vitamin K is a cofactor for hepatic carboxylation of clotting factors (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":3,"stem":"A 72-year-old widower eats mostly tea and toast. He has bleeding gums, corkscrew hairs, and perifollicular hemorrhages. The deficient vitamin is a cofactor for which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Gamma glutamyl carboxylase","B":"Prolyl hydroxylase","C":"Pyruvate carboxylase","D":"Glutathione peroxidase","E":"Transketolase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin C activates prolyl and lysyl hydroxylases needed for collagen hydroxylation. Deficiency, seen in older adults with poor diets, causes scurvy with gum bleeding and perifollicular hemorrhages. Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as its cofactor. Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin C). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: scurvy causes swollen gums, bruising, and corkscrew hair (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin C). COMLEX: vitamin C excess can cause oxalate stones and iron overload (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and terminal ileal disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gamma-glutamyl carboxylase in the liver uses vitamin K as its cofactor.","D":"Glutathione peroxidase depends on selenium, not vitamin C."}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a BMI of 41 has a high serum leptin level. She remains hungry despite large fat stores. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Hypothalamic leptin resistance","B":"Rapid renal leptin clearance","C":"Circulating antileptin antibodies","D":"Reduced adipocyte leptin release","E":"Excess hepatic leptin uptake"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Leptin is secreted in proportion to fat stores, so obese people usually have high leptin. Most are resistant to its central effects, so the satiety signal fails despite abundant hormone. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Leptin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: leptin acts on the ventromedial hypothalamus to promote satiety (First Aid Neurology, Hypothalamus). COMLEX: leptin gene mutations are a rare cause of massive obesity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 5 → Obj 6 — orexigenic and anorexigenic pathways","isHighYield":true},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":3,"stem":"A 41-year-old woman with a BMI of 46 had a Roux-en-Y gastric bypass and has lost 30 kg over 5 months. She now has episodes of postprandial right upper quadrant pain. Ultrasound shows multiple mobile gallstones. Bilirubin, hemoglobin, and reticulocyte count are normal, and she has no history of biliary infection. Which mechanism best explains her stones?","choices":{"A":"Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis","B":"Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile","C":"Cholesterol-supersaturated bile with stasis","D":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption from bypass","E":"Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Obesity and rapid weight reduction are risk factors for cholesterol gallstones, which form when bile becomes supersaturated with cholesterol beyond the solubilizing capacity of bile salts and lecithin and the gallbladder empties poorly, letting crystals nucleate and grow. Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis produces pigment stones, and her hemoglobin and reticulocyte count are normal. Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile forms pigment stones in biliary infection, which she does not have. Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs with ileal disease or resection, and gastric bypass leaves the ileum in continuity. Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile would lower stone risk rather than raise it. Sources: Robbins Ch. 18 (Cholelithiasis, risk factors and pathogenesis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cholesterol stones need supersaturated bile, gallbladder hypomotility, crystal nucleation, and mucus hypersecretion; obesity and rapid weight loss are risk factors (Robbins Ch. 18). COMLEX: counsel patients after bariatric surgery about symptomatic gallstones during rapid weight loss.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 16 → Obj 1 — MASLD versus alcohol-associated liver disease","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Excess unconjugated bilirubin from hemolysis produces pigment","B":"Bacterial beta-glucuronidase acting in bile forms pigment","D":"Loss of ileal bile salt reabsorption occurs","E":"Reduced hepatic secretion of cholesterol into bile"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":5,"stem":"A 10-month-old breastfed infant living in a northern city has soft skull bones and bowed legs. Which chest finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Sternal pectus excavatum","B":"Costochondral beading","C":"Clavicular callus formation","D":"Inferior rib notching","E":"Scapular winging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Vitamin D deficiency in children causes rickets. Overgrowth of cartilage at costochondral junctions produces the rachitic rosary, along with craniotabes and leg bowing. Breastfed infants with little sun exposure are at risk. Sources: Robbins Ch. 9 (Vitamin D, Rickets). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 34 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: rickets shows rachitic rosary, bowed legs, and craniotabes (First Aid Biochemistry, Vitamin D). COMLEX: the chest may also show a pigeon breast deformity (Robbins Ch. 9).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — absorption of fat-soluble vitamins, iron, and calcium","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"grid_sdl34_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Correlate deficiencies of water-soluble vitamins (B-complex and C) and essential trace minerals with their biochemical functions, characteristic clinical manifestations, and associated gastrointestinal disorders.","batch":4,"stem":"A 52-year-old man who drinks 10 beers daily and eats little besides crackers has fatigue and a sore, smooth red tongue. Neurologic examination, including vibration and position sense, is normal. Hemoglobin is 9.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), and a peripheral smear shows hypersegmented neutrophils. Which of the following sets of laboratory findings is most likely in this patient?","grid":{"headers":["MCV","Serum methylmalonic acid","Serum homocysteine"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↑"],"B":["↓","↔","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↑"],"D":["↔","↑","↑"],"E":["↑","↔","↑"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↑ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is correct. Poor intake with heavy alcohol use causes folate deficiency; the notes describe folate as essential for one-carbon transfers in purine and thymidylate synthesis, so deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia with hypersegmented neutrophils but no neurologic deficits. Without methyl-tetrahydrofolate, homocysteine is not remethylated and rises, while methylmalonyl-CoA mutase, which needs B12 but not folate, keeps working. Choice A (↑ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B12 deficiency, as after ileal resection or in atrophic gastritis, which also causes subacute combined degeneration with loss of vibration and position sense. Choice B (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↔ serum homocysteine) is iron deficiency, a microcytic anemia. Choice C (↓ MCV, ↔ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is vitamin B6 deficiency, which causes a microcytic sideroblastic anemia and impairs the B6-dependent conversion of homocysteine to cystathionine. Choice D (↔ MCV, ↑ serum methylmalonic acid, ↑ serum homocysteine) is the anemia of chronic kidney disease, a normocytic anemia from low erythropoietin in which reduced renal clearance raises both metabolites. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 34, section on water-soluble vitamins: B12, folate); First Aid 2025 Biochemistry (folate, B12, and B6 deficiency; methylmalonic acid and homocysteine) and Hematology (microcytic and macrocytic anemias). The laboratory arrows are from First Aid because the notes do not list them; the raised metabolites in chronic kidney disease are from general clinical chemistry and are inferred.","boardPrep":"Step 1: folate and B12 deficiency both raise homocysteine, but only B12 deficiency raises methylmalonic acid and causes neurologic deficits (First Aid 2025 Biochemistry). COMLEX: replacing folate in B12 deficiency can correct the anemia while the neurologic damage progresses.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 3 Obj 2 — intrinsic factor and B12 absorption","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl34_q31","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Which protein compartment is depleted most in marasmus?","choices":{"A":"Visceral protein, with muscle spared","B":"Somatic protein in skeletal muscle","C":"Only plasma immunoglobulins, with a fatty liver","D":"Surfactant protein in the lung","E":"Collagen in bone, with edema as the main sign"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Marasmus burns the somatic protein compartment in skeletal muscle, while the visceral compartment is relatively spared. Visceral protein loss with relative sparing of muscle is the kwashiorkor pattern. A fatty liver with visceral protein loss is kwashiorkor, not marasmus. Lung surfactant is not the compartment marasmus consumes. Edema is absent in marasmus, and bone collagen is not the compartment that is mobilized.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Visceral protein loss with relative sparing of muscle is the kwashiorkor pattern","C":"A fatty liver with visceral protein loss is kwashiorkor, not marasmus","D":"Lung surfactant is not the compartment marasmus consumes","E":"Edema is absent in marasmus, and bone collagen is not the compartment that is mobilized"}},{"id":"sdl34_q32","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":1,"stem":"Serum albumin in marasmus is usually which of the following?","choices":{"A":"Extremely low, and the cause of severe edema","B":"Undetectable in every case","C":"High, from dehydration only","D":"Normal or only slightly reduced","E":"Replaced by a monoclonal spike"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albumin stays normal or only slightly reduced in marasmus because visceral protein synthesis is relatively preserved. Very low albumin with edema is the kwashiorkor laboratory picture. Albumin is not undetectable in marasmus. Marasmus does not raise albumin; it leaves it near normal. A monoclonal spike is a plasma-cell disorder, not marasmus.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Very low albumin with edema is the kwashiorkor laboratory picture","B":"Albumin is not undetectable in marasmus","C":"Marasmus does not raise albumin; it leaves it near normal","E":"A monoclonal spike is a plasma-cell disorder, not marasmus"}},{"id":"sdl34_q33","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"An 18-month-old boy was weaned onto maize gruel when his sister was born. He is apathetic, with swelling of the face and legs, sparse reddish hair, and patches of peeling hyperpigmented skin. The abdomen is protuberant and the liver is enlarged. Albumin is 1.8 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL) and glucose is 70 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Which skin change is characteristic?","choices":{"A":"Rose spots on the trunk","B":"Dark macules on the lips","C":"Bronze skin with diabetes","D":"A serpiginous track on the sole","E":"Flaky-paint desquamation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Kwashiorkor produces flaky-paint skin, with zones of hyperpigmentation, peeling, and hypopigmentation, plus edema and a fatty liver. Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever. Dark lip macules are the pigment of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome. Bronze skin with diabetes suggests hereditary hemochromatosis. A serpiginous sole track is cutaneous larva migrans.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Rose spots are the rash of typhoid fever","B":"Dark lip macules are the pigment of Peutz-Jeghers syndrome","C":"Bronze skin with diabetes suggests hereditary hemochromatosis","D":"A serpiginous sole track is cutaneous larva migrans"}},{"id":"sdl34_q34","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate marasmus and kwashiorkor based on their underlying nutritional deficiencies, affected protein compartments, clinical manifestations, and associated laboratory findings.","batch":2,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl in a drought region has had almost no food for months. Her limbs are wasted, subcutaneous fat is gone, the head looks large for the body, and there is no edema. Weight is far below the standard for her age. She has oral thrush. Albumin is 3.6 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL) and glucose is 68 mg/dL (reference range: 70–100 mg/dL). Which immune problem fits this state?","choices":{"A":"Selective IgA deficiency as the cause of the wasting","B":"A high eosinophil count from a luminal tapeworm","C":"T-cell immune deficiency","D":"Autoantibodies against parietal cells","E":"Complement consumption from a liver abscess"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Marasmus includes T-cell immune deficiency, so these children develop infections while albumin stays near normal and edema is absent. Selective IgA deficiency is not the mechanism of this calorie-starved state. A luminal tapeworm does not produce this total loss of fat and muscle with thrush. Parietal-cell antibodies cause pernicious anemia, not this picture of starvation. Complement consumption from an amebic abscess is a different illness.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Selective IgA deficiency is not the mechanism of this calorie-starved state","B":"A luminal tapeworm does not produce this total loss of fat and muscle with thrush","D":"Parietal-cell antibodies cause pernicious anemia, not this picture of starvation","E":"Complement consumption from an amebic abscess is a different illness"}},{"id":"sdl34_q35","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Vitamin A is stored in which hepatic cell?","choices":{"A":"Stellate cell","B":"Kupffer cell","C":"Cholangiocyte","D":"Endothelial cell of the portal vein","E":"Gallbladder epithelial cell"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Vitamin A is stored in hepatic stellate cells and travels in blood bound to retinol-binding protein. Kupffer cells are the sinusoidal macrophages and are not the vitamin A store. Cholangiocytes line bile ducts and do not store vitamin A. Portal-vein endothelium is not the vitamin A reservoir. Gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile and does not store vitamin A.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Kupffer cells are the sinusoidal macrophages and are not the vitamin A store","C":"Cholangiocytes line bile ducts and do not store vitamin A","D":"Portal-vein endothelium is not the vitamin A reservoir","E":"Gallbladder epithelium concentrates bile and does not store vitamin A"}},{"id":"sdl34_q36","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":1,"stem":"Active vitamin D increases intestinal calcium absorption by inducing which enterocyte protein?","choices":{"A":"Intrinsic factor","B":"Apolipoprotein B","C":"Calbindin","D":"Trypsinogen","E":"Pepsinogen"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Active 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D induces calbindin, which ferries calcium across the enterocyte. Intrinsic factor is made by gastric parietal cells for vitamin B12, not by vitamin D in the enterocyte. Apolipoprotein B is required to export chylomicrons, not to absorb calcium. Trypsinogen is a pancreatic zymogen. Pepsinogen is the gastric protease precursor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Intrinsic factor is made by gastric parietal cells for vitamin B12, not by vitamin D in the enterocyte","B":"Apolipoprotein B is required to export chylomicrons, not to absorb calcium","D":"Trypsinogen is a pancreatic zymogen","E":"Pepsinogen is the gastric protease precursor"}},{"id":"trial_sdl34_q07","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain the biochemical functions, metabolism, and clinical manifestations of deficiencies in fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), particularly in the context of gastrointestinal malabsorption.","batch":3,"stem":"A 9-year-old boy with long-standing cholestasis has an unsteady gait and absent ankle reflexes. He rarely takes the prescribed enzymes. Hemoglobin has fallen to 10.2 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL), bilirubin is 2.4 mg/dL (reference range: 0.1–1.0 mg/dL), and the smear shows no schistocytes from a microangiopathy. The neurologic findings fit a fat-soluble vitamin problem. Which blood consequence does that same deficiency produce?","choices":{"A":"Iron-deficiency microcytosis from hookworms","B":"A high neutrophil count from an abscess","C":"Sickle cells","D":"Megaloblastic anemia from folate lack, with no nerve damage","E":"Hemolysis from a shortened red-cell life span"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Vitamin E deficiency causes spinocerebellar degeneration and also shortens red-cell survival, leading to hemolytic anemia. Hookworm iron deficiency is a separate cause of anemia and does not produce this ataxia. Neutrophilia from an abscess is not the hematologic result of vitamin E deficiency. Sickle cells are a hemoglobinopathy, not vitamin E deficiency. Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia without the spinal-cord picture, and vitamin E deficiency does damage nerves.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworm iron deficiency is a separate cause of anemia and does not produce this ataxia","B":"Neutrophilia from an abscess is not the hematologic result of vitamin E deficiency","C":"Sickle cells are a hemoglobinopathy, not vitamin E deficiency","D":"Folate deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia without the spinal-cord picture, and vitamin E deficiency does damage nerves"}},{"id":"sdl34_q37","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the hormonal and hypothalamic mechanisms regulating appetite, satiety, and energy homeostasis, including the roles of leptin, ghrelin, GLP-1, and hypothalamic neuropeptides.","batch":1,"stem":"Agouti-related peptide increases food intake by blocking which receptor?","choices":{"A":"The insulin receptor on muscle","B":"The epidermal growth factor receptor","C":"The vitamin D receptor","D":"The melanocortin-4 receptor","E":"The bile-salt export pump"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Agouti-related peptide is a competitive antagonist at the melanocortin-4 receptor, which removes the satiety signal of alpha-melanocyte-stimulating hormone. The insulin receptor is not the target Agouti-related peptide blocks to drive hunger. The epidermal growth factor receptor is a growth-factor target, not this appetite receptor. The vitamin D receptor mediates calbindin induction, not the orexigenic block. The bile-salt export pump moves bile acids out of hepatocytes and is not an appetite receptor.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The insulin receptor is not the target Agouti-related peptide blocks to drive hunger","B":"The epidermal growth factor receptor is a growth-factor target, not this appetite receptor","C":"The vitamin D receptor mediates calbindin induction, not the orexigenic block","E":"The bile-salt export pump moves bile acids out of hepatocytes and is not an appetite receptor"}},{"id":"sdl34_q38","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":1,"stem":"Intestinal-type gastric cancer is linked to which dietary exposure?","choices":{"A":"Salted, smoked, or preserved foods","B":"A diet of only fresh fruit","C":"Parenteral vitamin B12","D":"Pasteurized milk alone","E":"A gluten-free diet"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Intestinal-type gastric adenocarcinoma is linked to dietary N-nitroso compounds and benzo[a]pyrene in salted, smoked, or preserved foods. Fresh fruit is not the exposure linked to intestinal-type gastric cancer. Parenteral vitamin B12 treats deficiency and is not a gastric carcinogen. Pasteurized milk is not the preserved-food exposure named for this cancer. Avoiding gluten treats celiac disease and is not the risk named for intestinal-type gastric cancer.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Fresh fruit is not the exposure linked to intestinal-type gastric cancer","C":"Parenteral vitamin B12 treats deficiency and is not a gastric carcinogen","D":"Pasteurized milk is not the preserved-food exposure named for this cancer","E":"Avoiding gluten treats celiac disease and is not the risk named for intestinal-type gastric cancer"}},{"id":"sdl34_q39","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with a body mass index of 36 has taken a combined oral contraceptive for eight years. She now has episodic right-upper-quadrant pain after fatty meals. She drinks little alcohol. The abdomen is soft and she is not jaundiced. Alkaline phosphatase is 110 U/L (reference range: 40–129 U/L) and alanine aminotransferase is 28 U/L (reference range: 10–40 U/L). Ultrasound shows gallbladder stones that shadow. Estrogen promoted the stones by stimulating which hepatic enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Alcohol dehydrogenase","B":"Pepsin","C":"Enterokinase","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"HMG-CoA reductase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Estrogen stimulates hepatic HMG-CoA reductase and increases biliary cholesterol secretion, which favors cholesterol stones. Alcohol dehydrogenase metabolizes ethanol and is not the estrogen target that raises biliary cholesterol. Pepsin is the gastric protease. Enterokinase activates trypsinogen. Thymidylate synthase is the target of fluoropyrimidines, not of estrogen in the liver.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Alcohol dehydrogenase metabolizes ethanol and is not the estrogen target that raises biliary cholesterol","B":"Pepsin is the gastric protease","C":"Enterokinase activates trypsinogen","D":"Thymidylate synthase is the target of fluoropyrimidines, not of estrogen in the liver"}},{"id":"sdl34_q40","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Analyze the pathophysiologic consequences of obesity and dietary factors, including insulin resistance, metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease, cholesterol gallstones, and gastrointestinal malignancies.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man with a body mass index of 41 has had heartburn for years. Endoscopy shows salmon-colored mucosa in the distal esophagus and a mass at the gastroesophageal junction. He does not smoke. Hemoglobin is 11.6 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL). Which mechanical effect of his central fat started this sequence?","choices":{"A":"Portal-vein thrombosis","B":"Higher intra-abdominal pressure with reflux","C":"Occlusion of the inferior mesenteric artery","D":"A volvulus around the superior mesenteric artery","E":"Failure of the anal membrane to open"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Central obesity raises intra-abdominal pressure, which promotes reflux and Barrett esophagus and thereby esophageal adenocarcinoma. Portal-vein thrombosis causes portal hypertension and is not the mechanical start of this reflux sequence. Inferior mesenteric occlusion causes hindgut ischemia, not Barrett esophagus. Midgut volvulus is an embryologic accident, not the result of central fat. Failure of the anal membrane to break down causes imperforate anus.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Portal-vein thrombosis causes portal hypertension and is not the mechanical start of this reflux sequence","C":"Inferior mesenteric occlusion causes hindgut ischemia, not Barrett esophagus","D":"Midgut volvulus is an embryologic accident, not the result of central fat","E":"Failure of the anal membrane to break down causes imperforate anus"}}]},{"sdlNumber":35,"title":"SDL 35 — Intestinal Helminths","questions":[{"id":"sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do Strongyloides stercoralis larvae enter the human host?","choices":{"A":"Penetration of intact skin","B":"Ingestion of eggs in soil","C":"Bite of a sandfly vector","D":"Inhalation of soil dust","E":"Ingestion of raw fish meat"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Strongyloides worms live in soil, and humans are infected when larvae penetrate the skin. The larvae travel in the circulation to the lungs, move up the trachea, and are swallowed. Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinworm are acquired, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin. Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not Strongyloides. Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin from soil. First Aid groups Strongyloides with hookworms as cutaneous-route nematodes: larvae in soil penetrate the skin, they are not inhaled. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides, Ancylostoma, and Necator enter through the skin, while Enterobius, Ascaris, Toxocara, Trichinella, and Trichuris are ingested (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides occurs sporadically in the United States, including in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ingesting eggs from soil is how Ascaris, Trichuris, and pinworm are acquired, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin.","E":"Raw fish transmits the tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum, not Strongyloides.","C":"Sandfly bites transmit Leishmania, not Strongyloides, whose larvae penetrate skin from soil.","D":"First Aid groups Strongyloides with hookworms as cutaneous-route nematodes: larvae in soil penetrate the skin, they are not inhaled."}},{"id":"sdl35_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"How do humans acquire Ascaris lumbricoides?","choices":{"A":"Ingestion of metacercariae on plants","B":"Ingestion of eggs passed in stool","C":"Ingestion of cysts in pond water","D":"Bites of infected deer flies","E":"Inhalation of larvae in soil dust"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs from soil contaminated with human stool (A. lumbricoides) or pig stool (A. suum). Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as Fasciola, not Ascaris. Cysts in water transmit protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not Ascaris. Deer and horse flies transmit Loa loa, not Ascaris. Larvae in soil (Strongyloides, hookworms) enter by penetrating skin; Ascaris is not inhaled. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris is spread fecal-orally, and its knobby-coated oval eggs are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: about a billion people are estimated to be infected with Ascaris (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit liver flukes such as Fasciola, not Ascaris.","C":"Cysts in water transmit protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not Ascaris.","D":"Deer and horse flies transmit Loa loa, not Ascaris.","E":"Larvae in soil (Strongyloides, hookworms) enter by penetrating skin; Ascaris is not inhaled."}},{"id":"sdl35_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":1,"stem":"Humans acquire the adult intestinal form of Taenia solium by ingesting what?","choices":{"A":"Eggs in fecally soiled water","B":"Metacercariae on water plants","C":"Cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Cercariae in fresh lake water","E":"Eggs in undercooked raw beef meat"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Ingested cysticerci in undercooked pork attach to the intestinal wall and develop into adult tapeworms several meters long. This route completes the life cycle without causing disseminated cysticercosis. Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not the adult intestinal tapeworm. Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia solium. Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomiasis, not taeniasis. Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. solium, and eggs are not the infective form in meat. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium intestinal tapeworm comes from encysted larvae in pork, and cysticercosis comes from eggs in food contaminated with human feces (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: tapeworm proglottids contain both male and female reproductive organs (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ingesting T. solium eggs produces tissue cysticercosis, not the adult intestinal tapeworm.","B":"Metacercariae on water plants transmit flukes, not Taenia solium.","D":"Cercariae in fresh water penetrate skin to cause schistosomiasis, not taeniasis.","E":"Raw beef transmits Taenia saginata via cysticerci, not T. solium, and eggs are not the infective form in meat."}},{"id":"sdl35_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"For most intestinal helminths, which factor determines the adult worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Number of infective exposures","B":"Rate of adult worm replication","C":"Host gastric acid output","D":"Duration of fecal egg shedding","E":"Peak blood eosinophil count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Most intestinal helminths do not multiply to new adults inside the human host, so each adult worm reflects a separately acquired infective egg or larva. Strongyloides is the key exception. Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden depends on the number of exposures. Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine it, since eggs must mature outside the host. Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of adult worms. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides hyperinfection reflects accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris symptoms vary with the burden of worms, and light infections are often asymptomatic (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Adult worms do not replicate in the human host, so burden depends on the number of exposures.","D":"Egg shedding reflects existing burden but does not determine it, since eggs must mature outside the host.","E":"Eosinophil count reflects tissue invasion, not the number of adult worms."}},{"id":"sdl35_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"In strongyloidiasis, what allows the worm burden to rise without new exposure from soil?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms dividing by fission","B":"Eggs hatching in moist soil","C":"Larvae maturing within the host","D":"Cysts forming in skeletal muscle","E":"Proglottids budding off the scolex"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Most Strongyloides larvae pass in stool, but some develop into infective forms within the host, leading to autoinfection. Females in the small intestinal mucosa produce eggs by parthenogenesis. Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burden rises through autoinfection by larvae maturing in the host. Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly what autoinfection bypasses. Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongyloides autoinfection. Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform Strongyloides larvae are seen in feces (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: immunocompetent hosts with Strongyloides are often asymptomatic but may have diarrhea, bloating, or malabsorption (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Helminth adults do not divide by fission; Strongyloides burden rises through autoinfection by larvae maturing in the host.","B":"Eggs hatching in soil require new exposure, which is exactly what autoinfection bypasses.","D":"Cysts in skeletal muscle describe Trichinella, not Strongyloides autoinfection.","E":"Proglottids budding from a scolex describe tapeworms, not Strongyloides."}},{"id":"sdl35_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug class both suppresses eosinophil function and stimulates female Strongyloides to produce more infective larvae?","choices":{"A":"Beta blockers","B":"Antihistamines","C":"Statin agents","D":"Loop diuretic agents","E":"Corticosteroids"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils and other immune cells that accumulate in response to infection, and they stimulate female worms to increase larva production. Prolonged therapy can lead to fatal hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: ivermectin or bendazoles treat Strongyloides (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: organ transplantation and lymphoma also increase the risk of hyperinfection (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Beta blockers are a different drug class.","B":"Antihistamines are a different drug class.","C":"Statin agents are a different drug class.","D":"Loop diuretic agents are a different drug class."}},{"id":"sdl35_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"How do hookworms cause anemia?","choices":{"A":"Hemolysis of circulating red cells","B":"Blood loss from the intestinal wall","C":"Marrow invasion by migrating larvae","D":"Competition for dietary cobalamin","E":"Malabsorption of dietary folate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ancylostoma and Necator attach to the intestinal wall and suck blood, causing chronic blood loss and microcytic anemia. Hookworms do not hemolyze circulating red cells. Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothrium latum. Folate deficiency gives a megaloblastic anemia. Migrating larvae do not invade the marrow. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: iron deficiency anemia is the classic parasite hint for Ancylostoma and Necator (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae penetrate skin after walking barefoot on contaminated soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Hookworms do not hemolyze circulating red cells.","D":"Competition for cobalamin is the mechanism of Diphyllobothrium latum, not hookworm.","E":"Folate deficiency gives a megaloblastic anemia, not a microcytic one.","C":"Migrating larvae do not invade the marrow in this infection."}},{"id":"sdl35_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"Which nutrient deficiency results from Diphyllobothrium latum infection?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Folic acid","C":"Thiamine","D":"Vitamin B12","E":"Ascorbic acid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for vitamin B12 in the intestine, causing megaloblastic anemia. Robbins notes that in humans it lives only in the gut and does not form cysticerci. Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium. Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vitamin B12 and can cause megaloblastic anemia; vitamin B6 is not affected. Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder. Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy (a collagen hydroxylation defect). Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diphyllobothrium latum is acquired by ingesting larvae in raw freshwater fish (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: the beef tapeworm and fish tapeworm are acquired by eating undercooked meat or fish (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Folate is not consumed by Diphyllobothrium.","A":"Diphyllobothrium latum competes with the host for dietary vitamin B12 and can cause megaloblastic anemia; vitamin B6 is not affected.","C":"Thiamine deficiency is typical of alcohol use disorder.","E":"Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy (a collagen hydroxylation defect)."}},{"id":"sdl35_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":5,"stem":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can mechanically obstruct which structure?","choices":{"A":"Ileocecal valve","B":"Pyloric sphincter","C":"Lower esophageal sphincter","D":"Internal anal sphincter","E":"Duodenojejunal flexure"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Heavy Ascaris burdens can cause abdominal obstruction requiring surgery, including obstruction of the ileocecal valve and the appendix. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may also cause biliary obstruction and intestinal perforation (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult Ascaris worms are 15 to 35 cm long and live mainly in the jejunum (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":5,"stem":"Which Th2 cytokine stimulates eosinophils in helminth infections?","choices":{"A":"Interleukin 2","B":"Interleukin 12","C":"Interleukin 17","D":"Interferon gamma","E":"Interleukin 5"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Invasive helminths stimulate a Th2 response. IL-5 stimulates eosinophils, and IL-4 drives IgE production. IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response to helminths. Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit Th2 responses with IL-5. IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell proliferation, not the cytokine that stimulates eosinophils. IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and monocytes, not the Th2 eosinophil stimulator. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Filariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils produce major basic protein and respond to IL-5 (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: Th2 responses increase intestinal contractility, which expels adult Trichinella worms in animal models (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"B":"IL-12 drives Th1 responses, not the Th2 eosinophil response to helminths.","D":"Interferon gamma is a Th1 cytokine, whereas helminths elicit Th2 responses with IL-5.","A":"IL-2 is the T-cell growth factor that drives T-cell proliferation, not the cytokine that stimulates eosinophils.","C":"IL-17 is the Th17 cytokine that recruits neutrophils and monocytes, not the Th2 eosinophil stimulator."}},{"id":"sdl35_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"When is peripheral eosinophilia most expected during a helminth infection?","choices":{"A":"When adults reach peak fecundity","B":"When eggs pass in the stool","C":"When worms attach by a scolex","D":"When larvae invade host tissues","E":"When adults reside in the cecum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Eosinophilia is driven by Th2 responses to parasites that migrate through or encyst in tissue. Trichinella larvae invading muscle cause marked eosinophilia, and Ascaris larvae in the lungs cause eosinophilic pneumonitis. Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia, which requires tissue invasion. Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause systemic eosinophilia. Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal tapeworms do not cause eosinophilia. Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not cause eosinophilia. Sources: Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly); Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis); Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites lead the PACMAN mnemonic for causes of eosinophilia (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: granulomas around schistosome eggs contain eosinophils, which are distinctive for helminth infections (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Peak fecundity of luminal adults does not drive eosinophilia, which requires tissue invasion.","B":"Eggs in stool reflect luminal adults, which do not cause systemic eosinophilia.","C":"Scolex attachment is luminal tapeworm behavior, and luminal tapeworms do not cause eosinophilia.","E":"Adults in the cecum, like pinworms, stay luminal and do not cause eosinophilia."}},{"id":"sdl35_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":5,"stem":"Which eosinophil granule product is toxic to helminths?","choices":{"A":"Cathelicidin peptide","B":"Major basic protein","C":"Serum amyloid A","D":"Lysozyme enzyme","E":"Lactoferrin protein"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophils defend against helminths and release major basic protein, a helminthotoxin, along with eosinophil cationic protein and eosinophil-derived neurotoxin. Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whereas major basic protein is the eosinophil product toxic to helminths. Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by IL-1 and TNF, not an eosinophil granule product. Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil helminthotoxin major basic protein. Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eosinophil granules. Sources: First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: eosinophils have bilobate nuclei and large uniform eosinophilic granules (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: corticosteroids inhibit eosinophils that accumulate in tissues in response to Strongyloides (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Cathelicidins are antimicrobial proteins of neutrophils, whereas major basic protein is the eosinophil product toxic to helminths.","C":"Serum amyloid A is a hepatic acute-phase protein induced by IL-1 and TNF, not an eosinophil granule product.","D":"Lysozyme is a neutrophil granule enzyme, not the eosinophil helminthotoxin major basic protein.","E":"Lactoferrin is found in neutrophil specific granules, not eosinophil granules."}},{"id":"sdl35_q25","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"How does pyrantel pamoate eliminate susceptible intestinal nematodes?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of tubulin polymerization","B":"Increased tegument calcium permeability","C":"Neuromuscular blockade of worms","D":"Inhibition of parasite folate synthesis","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pyrantel is a poorly absorbed neuromuscular blocking agent that releases acetylcholine and inhibits cholinesterase, paralyzing luminal worms, which are then expelled. Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimidazoles such as albendazole, not pyrantel. Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of praziquantel, not pyrantel. Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed action of niclosamide against tapeworms. Inhibiting folate synthesis (dihydrofolate reductase) is the action of antiprotozoals such as pyrimethamine, not pyrantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Pyrantel Pamoate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pyrantel pamoate is listed with ivermectin, mebendazole, and diethylcarbamazine as antihelminthic therapy (First Aid Microbiology (Antihelminthic therapy)). COMLEX: pyrantel is ineffective against Trichuris and Strongyloides infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Inhibiting tubulin polymerization is the action of benzimidazoles such as albendazole, not pyrantel.","B":"Increasing tegument calcium permeability is the action of praziquantel, not pyrantel.","E":"Uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation is the presumed action of niclosamide against tapeworms.","D":"Inhibiting folate synthesis (dihydrofolate reductase) is the action of antiprotozoals such as pyrimethamine, not pyrantel."}},{"id":"sdl35_q26","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"What shape do Enterobius vermicularis eggs have on microscopic examination?","choices":{"A":"Barrel shape with polar plugs","B":"Oval with one flattened side","C":"Round with a thick knobby shell","D":"Oval with a large lateral spine","E":"Operculated with a terminal knob"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm eggs have a characteristic elongated oval shape with one of the long sides of the oval flattened. Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinworm. Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascaris, not pinworm. Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni, not pinworm. Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, not pinworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm causes anal pruritus worse at night and is most common in children aged 5 to 10 (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: adult pinworms live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are Trichuris, not pinworm.","C":"Round eggs with a thick knobby (mammillated) shell are Ascaris, not pinworm.","D":"Oval eggs with a large lateral spine are Schistosoma mansoni, not pinworm.","E":"Operculated eggs with a terminal knob are Diphyllobothrium, not pinworm."}},{"id":"sdl35_q27","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice treats intestinal infection with the fish tapeworm Diphyllobothrium latum?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Diethylcarbamazine","D":"Pyrantel pamoate","E":"Mebendazole"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Katzung lists praziquantel or niclosamide as the drugs of choice for Diphyllobothrium latum and Taenia saginata, which live only in the gut lumen. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca, not tapeworms. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestinal tapeworms. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not tapeworms. Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1); Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tapeworms such as Diphyllobothrium latum are covered with the cestodes (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: a single 2-g dose of niclosamide cures more than 85% of Diphyllobothrium latum infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides and Onchocerca, not tapeworms.","C":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not intestinal tapeworms.","D":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as pinworm, Ascaris, and hookworm, not tapeworms.","E":"Mebendazole is first-line for intestinal roundworms."}},{"id":"sdl35_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old pig farmer passes a 25-cm worm in his stool. He recalls a dry cough with fever several weeks earlier. After the eggs were ingested, which route did the larvae take before maturing in his intestine?","choices":{"A":"Portal flow to the hepatic lobules","B":"Lymphatic flow to the dermis","C":"Direct growth in the jejunal lumen","D":"Lung passage then reswallowing","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ingested Ascaris eggs release larvae in the small intestine. The larvae penetrate the cecal mucosa, travel to the lungs in blood or lymph, mature for 10 to 14 days, and are coughed up and swallowed. Ascaris suum is acquired from pig stool. Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they migrate to the lungs and are reswallowed, unlike Trichuris. Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris life cycle; larvae migrate through the lungs. Ascaris larvae migrate to the alveoli (Loeffler syndrome), not to the dermis; skin is the entry route of hookworm and Strongyloides larvae, not of Ascaris. First Aid describes Ascaris larvae migrating to the alveoli to cause Loeffler syndrome, the pulmonary passage before maturation in the gut, not portal flow to the hepatic lobules. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: migration of Ascaris larvae to alveoli causes Löffler syndrome with pulmonary eosinophilia (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 1 — duodenum, jejunum, and ileum histology","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Ascaris larvae do not mature directly in the lumen; they migrate to the lungs and are reswallowed, unlike Trichuris.","E":"Bile flow back into the duodenum is not part of the Ascaris life cycle; larvae migrate through the lungs.","B":"Ascaris larvae migrate to the alveoli (Loeffler syndrome), not to the dermis; skin is the entry route of hookworm and Strongyloides larvae, not of Ascaris.","A":"First Aid describes Ascaris larvae migrating to the alveoli to cause Loeffler syndrome, the pulmonary passage before maturation in the gut, not portal flow to the hepatic lobules."}},{"id":"sdl35_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old vegetarian woman who emigrated from rural Guatemala has new seizures. MRI shows multiple small cystic brain lesions. How did she most likely acquire the infection?","choices":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork","B":"Metacercariae on watercress","C":"Eggs from dog feces","D":"Cercariae in lake water","E":"Eggs from human feces"},"correct":"E","explanation":"When humans ingest Taenia solium eggs in food or water contaminated with human feces, oncospheres penetrate the gut wall and spread hematogenously to form cysticerci, including in the brain. Adult tapeworms do not form by this route. Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tapeworm, not brain cysticerci, and she is vegetarian. Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticercosis. Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, whereas T. solium eggs come from human feces. Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocysticercosis. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: brain cysts with seizures is the parasite hint for neurocysticercosis (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: viable cysticerci evade immunity with taeniaestatin and paramyosin (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Larvae in undercooked pork produce the adult intestinal tapeworm, not brain cysticerci, and she is vegetarian.","B":"Metacercariae on watercress transmit Fasciola, not cysticercosis.","C":"Eggs from dog feces cause echinococcal hydatid cysts, whereas T. solium eggs come from human feces.","D":"Cercariae in lake water cause schistosomiasis, not neurocysticercosis."}},{"id":"sdl35_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":5,"stem":"A 26-year-old aid worker waded in a freshwater lake in East Africa. Months later she has hepatomegaly and eggs in her stool. Which mechanism allowed the parasite to enter her body?","choices":{"A":"Larval proteolytic enzymes","B":"Ingestion of snail tissue","C":"Bite of an infected black fly","D":"Inhalation of aerosolized eggs","E":"Ingestion of contaminated watercress"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Schistosome cercariae swim in fresh water and penetrate human skin with proteolytic enzymes that degrade the keratinized layer, causing minimal skin reaction. S. mansoni affects mainly the liver and gut. Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, whereas schistosome cercariae penetrate skin. Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving rivers, not schistosomes, whose cercariae penetrate skin in fresh water. Snails are only the intermediate host that releases cercariae into fresh water; humans are infected through skin, not by eating snails. Schistosoma cercariae released by snails penetrate the skin in fresh water; schistosome eggs are not inhaled. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Schistosomiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: portal hypertension is the parasite hint for Schistosoma mansoni and S. japonicum (First Aid Microbiology (Trematodes); Parasite hints). COMLEX: females produce hundreds to thousands of eggs per day (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 8 → Obj 4 — portal hypertension and portosystemic varices","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"E":"Contaminated watercress transmits Fasciola metacercariae, whereas schistosome cercariae penetrate skin.","C":"Black flies transmit Onchocerca volvulus near fast-moving rivers, not schistosomes, whose cercariae penetrate skin in fresh water.","B":"Snails are only the intermediate host that releases cercariae into fresh water; humans are infected through skin, not by eating snails.","D":"Schistosoma cercariae released by snails penetrate the skin in fresh water; schistosome eggs are not inhaled."}},{"id":"sdl35_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy has nocturnal perianal itching that returns weeks after one course of treatment. He often sucks his thumb, and no one else at home is affected. What best explains his reinfection?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated drinking water","B":"Hand-to-mouth egg transfer","C":"Adult worm division in cecum","D":"Insect vector bite","E":"Eating undercooked meat"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Pinworm females migrate out of the anus at night to deposit eggs on perianal skin. The eggs become infectious within about 6 hours and spread from the hands or bed linens, allowing self-reinfection. Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re-ingesting eggs. Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pinworm. Insect bites transmit filarial worms such as Loa loa, Onchocerca, and Wuchereria; pinworm spreads fecal-oral. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is spread fecal-orally and diagnosed with the tape test (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm is the most common helminthic infection in the United States (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Adult worms do not divide; pinworm reinfection comes from re-ingesting eggs.","E":"Undercooked meat transmits tapeworms and Trichinella, not pinworm.","D":"Insect bites transmit filarial worms such as Loa loa, Onchocerca, and Wuchereria; pinworm spreads fecal-oral."}},{"id":"sdl35_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":5,"stem":"A 62-year-old man from rural Kentucky takes high-dose prednisone after a lung transplant. He develops diffuse pulmonary infiltrates and Escherichia coli bacteremia, and larvae are found in his sputum. What is the source of the bacteremia?","choices":{"A":"Contaminated central venous line","B":"Aspiration of gastric contents","C":"Larval damage to the bowel wall","D":"Infected urinary catheter","E":"Obstructed common bile duct"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In Strongyloides hyperinfection, larvae invade the intestinal wall, and intestinal bacteria enter the blood through the damaged wall, causing sepsis. Worms of all stages may be found in the lungs and sputum. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides causes pulmonary symptoms such as dry cough and hemoptysis (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hyperinfection larvae invade the colonic submucosa, lymphatics, and blood vessels (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins"},{"id":"sdl35_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":5,"stem":"A 51-year-old man from Jamaica with no history of immunosuppressive drug use develops overwhelming strongyloidiasis with larvae throughout his body. Which underlying infection most likely predisposed him?","choices":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C virus","B":"Latent Epstein-Barr virus","C":"High-risk papillomavirus","D":"Reactivated human cytomegalovirus","E":"Human T-lymphotropic virus 1"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Human T-lymphotropic virus type I infection predisposes to very high Strongyloides worm burdens through uncontrolled autoinfection, leading to fatal disease. Chronic hepatitis C (with hepatitis B) virus infection is a cause of hepatocellular carcinoma, not the infection that predisposes to Strongyloides hyperinfection. Epstein-Barr virus is implicated in Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and B-cell lymphomas in T-cell immunosuppression, not in Strongyloides hyperinfection. High-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18 cause squamous cell carcinomas of the cervix, anogenital region, and head and neck, not overwhelming strongyloidiasis. Latent cytomegalovirus reactivates when cellular immunity is depressed and then causes systemic CMV disease; it is not the classic predisposition to Strongyloides hyperinfection. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: hyperinfection syndrome results from accelerated autoinfection in the immunocompromised (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical South America, sub-Saharan Africa, and Southeast Asia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"A":"Chronic hepatitis C (with hepatitis B) virus infection is a cause of hepatocellular carcinoma, not the infection that predisposes to Strongyloides hyperinfection.","B":"Epstein-Barr virus is implicated in Burkitt lymphoma, nasopharyngeal carcinoma, and B-cell lymphomas in T-cell immunosuppression, not in Strongyloides hyperinfection.","C":"High-risk HPV types such as 16 and 18 cause squamous cell carcinomas of the cervix, anogenital region, and head and neck, not overwhelming strongyloidiasis.","D":"Latent cytomegalovirus reactivates when cellular immunity is depressed and then causes systemic CMV disease; it is not the classic predisposition to Strongyloides hyperinfection."}},{"id":"sdl35_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 9-year-old girl in rural Colombia passed a long roundworm last month. She now has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, and imaging shows a worm in the common bile duct. Which complication has developed?","choices":{"A":"Acute pancreatitis","B":"Perforated duodenal ulcer","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia","D":"Splenic infarction","E":"Ureteral colic"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ascaris worms that enter the biliary tree can cause acute pancreatitis and cholangitis. A perforated duodenal ulcer shows free air under the diaphragm and may refer pain to the shoulder. Acute mesenteric ischemia is usually embolic SMA occlusion with pain out of proportion to the exam in patients with atrial fibrillation or vascular disease, not a child with a biliary worm. An obstructing ureteral stone causes unilateral flank tenderness and colicky pain radiating to the groin with hematuria, not epigastric pain radiating to the back. First Aid lists biliary obstruction among Ascaris lumbricoides complications, so a worm in the common bile duct with epigastric pain radiating to the back fits pancreatitis rather than splenic infarction. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris may cause biliary obstruction and can emerge from the nose or mouth (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Ascaris infections are diagnosed by eggs in the stool or passed adult worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 19 → Obj 2 — acute and chronic pancreatitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"A perforated duodenal ulcer shows free air under the diaphragm and may refer pain to the shoulder.","C":"Acute mesenteric ischemia is usually embolic SMA occlusion with pain out of proportion to the exam in patients with atrial fibrillation or vascular disease, not a child with a biliary worm.","E":"An obstructing ureteral stone causes unilateral flank tenderness and colicky pain radiating to the groin with hematuria, not epigastric pain radiating to the back.","D":"Splenic infarction is not the pain pattern of a biliary worm."}},{"id":"sdl35_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia who walks barefoot has fatigue and pallor. Hemoglobin is 8.2 g/dL (reference 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and MCV is 68 fL (reference 77–95 fL). Stool shows nematode eggs. Which additional laboratory finding is expected?","choices":{"A":"Low serum folate","B":"Low serum ferritin","C":"High indirect bilirubin","D":"High methylmalonic acid","E":"Positive Coombs test"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hookworms suck blood from the intestinal wall, and chronic blood loss causes iron deficiency with microcytic anemia and depleted iron stores. High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anemia comes from chronic blood loss. High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fish tapeworm, not the microcytic anemia of hookworm. A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookworm blood loss. Folate deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia, whereas this low-MCV anemia points to iron loss. Sources: First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes) (Robbins Ch. 8 does not cover hookworm; Elentra objective framing). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: hookworm can also cause cutaneous larva migrans, a pruritic serpiginous rash (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: hookworm larvae enter through skin after walking barefoot on contaminated beach or soil (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of iron, calcium, and fat-soluble vitamins","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"High indirect bilirubin implies hemolysis, but hookworm anemia comes from chronic blood loss.","D":"High methylmalonic acid indicates B12 deficiency, as with fish tapeworm, not the microcytic anemia of hookworm.","E":"A positive Coombs test indicates immune hemolysis, not hookworm blood loss.","A":"Folate deficiency causes a megaloblastic anemia, whereas this low-MCV anemia points to iron loss."}},{"id":"sdl35_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl with nightly perianal itching develops vulvar itching and discharge. A tape test is positive. What mechanism explains her new symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Eggs carried in the bloodstream","B":"Larvae entering through the skin","C":"Bacteria spreading from the bladder","D":"Worms moving into the vagina","E":"Cysts forming in the vulvar tissue"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Enterobius adults live in the cecum and appendix, and females migrate out of the anus at night. Worms moving into the vagina can cause vulvovaginitis. Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae entering through skin. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is most common in children aged 5 to 10 years (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: very high pinworm burdens rarely cause rectal prolapse (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 5 — appendicitis and anal canal lesions","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Pinworm is acquired by ingesting eggs, not by larvae entering through skin."}},{"id":"sdl35_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":5,"stem":"A 44-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He has fever, myalgias, and periorbital edema, and the eosinophil fraction is 32% (reference range: 1–4%). Where are the parasites now located?","choices":{"A":"Skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hepatic sinusoids","C":"Lumen of the cecum","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts","E":"Renal tubular cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Trichinella larvae disseminate hematogenously and penetrate striated muscle cells, causing fever, myalgias, marked eosinophilia, and periorbital edema. The infected muscle cell gains a collagenous capsule and new blood vessels. Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 weeks the larvae have encysted in skeletal muscle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Trichinellosis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Robbins) — detail not in SDL 35 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle after undercooked pork ingestion (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: humans are dead-end hosts for Trichinella (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Robbins","whyNot":{"C":"Trichinella adults live briefly in the intestine, but by 3 weeks the larvae have encysted in skeletal muscle.","B":"Hepatic sinusoids are not part of that answer.","D":"Intrahepatic bile ducts are not part of that answer.","E":"Renal tubular cells are not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl35_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy has dry cough, wheezing, fever, and transient pulmonary infiltrates with eosinophilia. Two stool examinations show no eggs, but 2 months later he passes a large roundworm. Why were the early stool tests negative?","choices":{"A":"Eggs were destroyed by acid","B":"Larvae had not yet matured","C":"Worms were in the bile duct","D":"Infection was in the skin","E":"Eggs were shed in urine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"The initial pulmonary stage of ascariasis causes Loeffler syndrome, a self-limited eosinophilic pneumonitis. Stool examination may not reveal the parasites if they have not yet matured in the intestine. Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin phase. Eggs in urine point to Schistosoma haematobium (hematuria, bladder cancer); Ascaris eggs pass in feces. First Aid describes Ascaris larvae migrating to the alveoli to cause Loeffler syndrome before adults mature in the gut, so negative early stools reflect immature larvae rather than acid destruction of eggs. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Löffler syndrome is pulmonary eosinophilia from Ascaris larvae in the alveoli (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: heavy Ascaris burdens can cause malnutrition (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"D":"Ascaris is acquired by ingesting eggs, so it has no skin phase.","E":"Eggs in urine point to Schistosoma haematobium (hematuria, bladder cancer); Ascaris eggs pass in feces.","A":"First Aid describes Ascaris larvae migrating to the alveoli to cause Loeffler syndrome before adults mature in the gut, so negative early stools reflect immature larvae rather than acid destruction of eggs."}},{"id":"sdl35_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old Ethiopian man who eats raw beef passes motile flat segments in his stool. The eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What best explains the normal eosinophil count?","choices":{"A":"Steroid suppression of eosinophils","B":"Concurrent bacterial sepsis","C":"Adult worm confined to gut lumen","D":"Eosinophils trapped in spleen","E":"Low parasite egg output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"In humans, Taenia saginata lives only in the gut lumen and does not form tissue cysticerci, so it lacks the tissue phase that drives strong Th2 eosinophilic responses. He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a luminal adult worm without tissue invasion. Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections); Elentra objective framing (Robbins does not state this directly). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: parasites are a classic cause of eosinophilia in the PACMAN mnemonic (First Aid Hematology (Eosinophils)). COMLEX: tapeworm mature distal proglottids contain many eggs and detach into the feces (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 5 — small-intestine GALT and secretory IgA","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"He has no steroid exposure; his normal count reflects a luminal adult worm without tissue invasion.","E":"Egg output does not drive eosinophilia; tissue invasion does"}},{"id":"sdl35_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old girl has 10 days of perianal itching that wakes her at night. A morning tape test is positive for pinworm eggs. Her CBC is normal, with hemoglobin 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which treatment plan is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Mebendazole now and at 2 weeks","B":"Niclosamide as one single dose","C":"Oral ivermectin daily for 2 days","D":"Oral metronidazole for 7 days","E":"Oral vancomycin given for 10 days"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Tape test eggs flattened on one side confirm Enterobius. Katzung gives mebendazole 100 mg once for pinworm, repeated at 2 weeks, because reinfection from newly hatched worms is common. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm. Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinworm, which is treated with mebendazole or albendazole. Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not pinworm. Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Enterobiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: pinworm eggs are visualized via the tape test and treated with bendazoles or pyrantel pamoate (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: pinworm eggs mature rapidly and become infectious in about 6 hours (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not pinworm.","C":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for Strongyloides, not pinworm, which is treated with mebendazole or albendazole.","D":"Metronidazole treats protozoa such as Giardia and Entamoeba, not pinworm.","E":"Oral vancomycin treats C. difficile colitis, not helminths."}},{"id":"sdl35_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from rural Kentucky has 3 months of intermittent diarrhea and bloating. His eosinophils are 14% (reference range: 1–3%). Stool microscopy shows motile larvae; no ova are seen. Which mechanism explains the action of the first-line drug for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Blocked tubulin polymerization in cells","B":"Intensified GABA signaling in nerves","C":"Increased calcium influx in tegument","D":"Blocked folate synthesis in cytoplasm","E":"Inhibited cell wall synthesis in worms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Larvae rather than ova in stool indicate Strongyloides stercoralis, whose larvae are mostly passed in the stool. Its drug of choice, ivermectin, paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission in peripheral nerves. Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechanism, not that of ivermectin, the Strongyloides drug of choice. Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action against flukes and tapeworms. Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacterial dihydropteroate synthase, not the action of an antihelminthic. Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglycan, a polymer unique to bacteria and absent from worms. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Strongyloidiasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: rhabditiform larvae of Strongyloides are seen in feces under the microscope (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: Strongyloides is endemic in tropical regions and occurs sporadically in Appalachia (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking tubulin polymerization is the benzimidazole mechanism, not that of ivermectin, the Strongyloides drug of choice.","C":"Calcium influx in the tegument is praziquantel's action against flukes and tapeworms.","D":"Blocking folate synthesis is the sulfonamide action on bacterial dihydropteroate synthase, not the action of an antihelminthic.","E":"Cell wall synthesis inhibition targets bacterial peptidoglycan, a polymer unique to bacteria and absent from worms."}},{"id":"sdl35_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy who recently moved from rural Bangladesh passes a 20-cm pink worm from his rectum. Stool examination shows numerous eggs. His hemoglobin is 11.9 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which drug is first-line for this infection?","choices":{"A":"Niclosamide","B":"Diethylcarbamazine","C":"Albendazole","D":"Doxycycline","E":"Metronidazole"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Adult worms 15 to 35 cm long passed from the rectum, with eggs in stool, diagnose Ascaris lumbricoides. Katzung lists albendazole, pyrantel pamoate, or mebendazole as the drugs of choice. Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris. Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris. Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms. Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont of filarial parasites, so it is not a treatment for an intestinal roundworm. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Ascariasis); Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ascaris has knobby-coated oval eggs in feces and is treated with bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Nematodes)). COMLEX: each adult female Ascaris releases about 200,000 eggs per day into the stool (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Niclosamide treats tapeworms, not the roundworm Ascaris.","B":"Diethylcarbamazine treats filarial infections, not Ascaris.","E":"Metronidazole treats protozoa, not intestinal roundworms.","D":"Doxycycline acts indirectly by killing Wolbachia, a symbiont of filarial parasites, so it is not a treatment for an intestinal roundworm."}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":3,"stem":"A 52-year-old man from northern Minnesota has fatigue, a smooth tongue, and numb feet. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL), MCV is 118 fL (reference range: 80–100 fL), and serum B12 is low. Stool examination shows operculated eggs and flat segments. He makes his own cured lake fish. How did he acquire the parasite?","choices":{"A":"Eating embryonated eggs on unwashed produce","B":"Eating metacercariae on raw water plants","C":"Eating cysticerci in undercooked pork","D":"Skin entry by larvae from damp soil","E":"Eating larvae in raw freshwater fish"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Megaloblastic anemia with low B12, operculated eggs, and tapeworm segments in a man who eats home-cured lake fish indicate Diphyllobothrium latum, which humans acquire by eating larvae encysted in raw or undercooked freshwater fish; the adult worm then competes for dietary B12. Eating embryonated eggs on unwashed produce is how Ascaris and whipworm are acquired. Eating metacercariae on raw water plants transmits Fasciola, a liver fluke that does not cause B12 deficiency. Eating cysticerci in undercooked pork gives the pork tapeworm, which is not linked to fish or B12 loss. Skin entry by larvae from damp soil is the route for hookworm and Strongyloides. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Intestinal cestodes); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Cestodes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D latum is acquired from larvae in raw freshwater fish and causes megaloblastic anemia by competing for B12 (First Aid, Cestodes). COMLEX: ask about raw fish in a patient with B12 deficiency and treat with praziquantel.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 4 — vitamin B12 absorption and the terminal ileum","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Eating embryonated eggs on unwashed produce is","B":"Eating metacercariae on raw water plants transmits","C":"Eating cysticerci in undercooked pork gives the","D":"Skin entry by larvae from damp soil"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q02","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":3,"stem":"A 10-year-old boy moved from rural Honduras to Chicago 2 years ago. Stool examination on arrival showed many barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs, but he was lost to follow-up and never treated. He has had no travel or soil exposure since. Repeat stool examination now shows only rare eggs. Which best explains the fall in his worm burden?","choices":{"A":"Larvae have matured inside him to replace adults","B":"Eggs hatched in his colon and reinfected him","C":"Eggs must mature in soil, so adults go unreplaced","D":"Adult worms divided asexually inside the cecum","E":"His colon converted adult worms into dormant cysts"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs are whipworm, a soil-transmitted helminth whose eggs must mature outside the host before they are infective, so without new exposure each adult worm that dies is not replaced and the burden falls; Strongyloides differs because it can autoinfect. Larvae have matured inside him to replace adults describes Strongyloides autoinfection, which whipworm cannot do. Eggs hatched in his colon and reinfected him also describes autoinfection, which applies to Strongyloides and not to whipworm. Adult worms divided asexually inside the cecum is wrong because helminths do not multiply by division within the host. His colon converted adult worms into dormant cysts is not part of the whipworm life cycle. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Ascaris; Strongyloides; Trichuris); Bootcamp Microbiology (Trichuris).","boardPrep":"Step 1: For most intestinal helminths the worm burden reflects the number of infective exposures, because a stage outside the host is required, whereas Strongyloides can autoinfect (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: a falling egg count after leaving an endemic area fits loss of re-exposure, but treatment is still indicated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and the anal sphincters","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Larvae have matured inside him to replace","B":"Eggs hatched in his colon and reinfected","D":"Adult worms divided asexually inside the cecum","E":"His colon converted adult worms into dormant"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q03","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 5-year-old boy in rural Southeast Asia has months of bloody mucoid stools, tenesmus, and poor growth. During straining the rectal mucosa prolapses, and small thread-like worms are seen attached to it. Hemoglobin is 8.4 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–13.5 g/dL) with an MCV of 66 fL (reference range: 75–87 fL). Which mechanism best explains his anemia?","choices":{"A":"Worm uptake of dietary vitamin B12 in gut","B":"Hemolysis caused by worm-derived toxins","C":"Blood sucked from the duodenal mucosa","D":"Blood loss from inflamed colonic mucosa","E":"Bone marrow invasion by migrating larvae"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bloody mucoid diarrhea, tenesmus, and rectal prolapse with thread-like worms on the rectal mucosa in a young child is heavy whipworm infection, and the adults in the colon cause mucosal inflammation and bleeding that lead to microcytic iron deficiency anemia. Worm uptake of dietary vitamin B12 in gut is the fish tapeworm mechanism and would cause a macrocytic anemia. Hemolysis caused by worm-derived toxins is not how intestinal nematodes cause anemia, and his indices show iron deficiency. Blood sucked from the duodenal mucosa is hookworm, which does not cause dysentery and rectal prolapse. Bone marrow invasion by migrating larvae does not occur with intestinal nematodes. Sources: Bootcamp Microbiology (Trichuris); Robbins Ch. 17 (Trichuris trichiura); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Nematodes).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Heavy Trichuris infection causes bloody diarrhea, rectal prolapse in children, and iron deficiency anemia (First Aid, Nematodes). COMLEX: treat with a benzimidazole and replete iron.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 24 → Obj 1 — gross and mucosal features of the large intestine","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Worm uptake of dietary vitamin B12 in","B":"Hemolysis caused by worm-derived toxins is not","C":"Blood sucked from the duodenal mucosa is","E":"Bone marrow invasion by migrating larvae does"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q04","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":3,"stem":"A 3-year-old girl with a habit of eating soil from a yard shared with a new puppy has fever, wheezing, and an enlarged tender liver. WBC is 24,000/µL (reference range: 5,500–15,500/µL) with 42% eosinophils (reference range: 1–4%). Three stool examinations show no eggs or larvae. Which feature of her infection best explains the marked eosinophilia?","choices":{"A":"Adult worms laying eggs in the gut lumen","B":"Bacterial endotoxin from damaged bowel","C":"Viral coinfection of the liver","D":"Allergy to proteins in dog dander","E":"Larvae migrating through her tissues"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pica with puppy exposure, hepatomegaly, wheezing, and marked eosinophilia with negative stool studies is visceral larva migrans from Toxocara; eosinophilia is driven by larvae invading and migrating through host tissues such as liver and lung, not by worms in the gut. Adult worms laying eggs in the gut lumen would produce eggs in stool and little eosinophilia, since luminal worms do not invade tissue. Bacterial endotoxin from damaged bowel causes neutrophilia rather than eosinophilia. Viral coinfection of the liver causes lymphocytosis, not a 42 percent eosinophil count. Allergy to proteins in dog dander would not explain fever, hepatomegaly, and this degree of eosinophilia. Sources: Robbins Ch. 17 (Strongyloides; Intestinal cestodes); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Tissue nematodes, Toxocara canis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peripheral eosinophilia in helminth infection is expected when larvae invade host tissues and is minimal when worms stay in the lumen (Robbins Ch. 17). COMLEX: marked eosinophilia with negative stool studies suggests a tissue-migrating helminth such as Toxocara.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 23 → Obj 4 — small-intestine epithelial renewal","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Adult worms laying eggs in the gut","B":"Bacterial endotoxin from damaged bowel causes neutrophilia","C":"Viral coinfection of the liver causes lymphocytosis,","D":"Allergy to proteins in dog dander would"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q05","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Differentiate the diagnostic findings and appropriate pharmacologic treatments of major intestinal helminth infections, including identification of eggs, larvae, or adult worms and the mechanisms of action of commonly used anthelmintic agents.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old man who grew up farming beside irrigation canals in Egypt has hepatosplenomegaly, esophageal varices, and intermittent bloody diarrhea. Liver enzymes are near normal. Stool examination shows large eggs with a prominent lateral spine. He is given a single-day oral drug of choice. How does that drug kill the parasite?","choices":{"A":"Binds beta-tubulin to block microtubules","B":"Raises worm membrane calcium permeability","C":"Enhances inhibitory signaling in worm nerves","D":"Depolarizes worm nicotinic receptors","E":"Kills endosymbiotic Wolbachia bacteria"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Freshwater exposure, portal hypertension with near-normal liver enzymes, and eggs with a lateral spine indicate Schistosoma mansoni, and the drug of choice for all schistosomes is praziquantel, which increases worm membrane permeability to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death. Binds beta-tubulin to block microtubules describes the benzimidazoles such as albendazole, which are not used for schistosomiasis. Enhances inhibitory signaling in worm nerves is the action of ivermectin, which intensifies GABA-mediated transmission and is used for Strongyloides and onchocerciasis. Depolarizes worm nicotinic receptors is the action of pyrantel pamoate, used for luminal nematodes such as pinworm. Kills endosymbiotic Wolbachia bacteria is how doxycycline acts against filarial worms. Sources: First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Trematodes, Schistosoma); Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel).","boardPrep":"Step 1: S mansoni eggs have a lateral spine and cause periportal fibrosis with portal hypertension; praziquantel raises worm calcium permeability (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: ask about freshwater exposure in patients with unexplained portal hypertension from endemic areas.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — pharmacology and clinical uses of antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Binds beta-tubulin to block microtubules describes the","C":"Enhances inhibitory signaling in worm nerves is","D":"Depolarizes worm nicotinic receptors is the action","E":"Kills endosymbiotic Wolbachia bacteria is how doxycycline"}},{"id":"trial_sdl35_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had cystic brain lesions without symptoms for years. He now has new seizures, and imaging shows edema surrounding one lesion. What best explains the change?","choices":{"A":"Adult worm growth in the brain","B":"Cyst rupture into the bloodstream","C":"New egg release within the cyst","D":"Inflammation around a dying cyst","E":"Bacterial abscess within the cyst"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Viable Taenia solium cysts often do not cause symptoms and evade immunity, but an inflammatory response develops when cysticerci die and degenerate. Sources: Robbins Ch. 8 (Cysticercosis and tapeworm infections). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole (First Aid Microbiology (Cestodes)). COMLEX: larval cysts in tissues outside the intestine do not develop into mature worms (Robbins Ch. 8).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — choosing among antihelminthic drugs","isHighYield":true},{"id":"grid_sdl35_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":4,"stem":"A 58-year-old man who emigrated from rural Southeast Asia 30 years ago is treated with high-dose prednisone for a flare of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Two weeks later he has abdominal pain, diarrhea, cough, and wheezing, followed by fever and hypotension; blood cultures grow Escherichia coli. Microscopy of stool and sputum shows motile larvae. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the organism causing this patient's illness?","grid":{"headers":["Entry route","Autoinfection","Eosinophilia"]},"choices":{"A":["Egg ingestion","No","Present"],"B":["Skin penetration","No","Present"],"C":["Skin penetration","Yes","Present"],"D":["Egg ingestion","Yes","Absent"],"E":["Larvae in fish","No","Absent"]},"correct":"C","explanation":"Choice C (skin penetration, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia present) is correct. Strongyloides stercoralis filariform larvae in soil penetrate intact skin and migrate through the lungs to the gut. The notes describe rhabditiform larvae that molt into filariform larvae inside the host and re-penetrate the bowel wall or perianal skin, so infection can persist for decades and become hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis after corticosteroids; tissue migration causes eosinophilia, though steroids can blunt it. Choice A (egg ingestion, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is Ascaris lumbricoides, whose eggs hatch in the small intestine and whose larvae migrate through the lungs, causing transient eosinophilic pneumonitis, but which needs soil maturation and cannot reinfect the host. Choice B (skin penetration, no autoinfection, eosinophilia present) is hookworm (Necator, Ancylostoma), which enters through skin and migrates through the lungs but requires external development. Choice D (egg ingestion, autoinfection yes, eosinophilia absent) is Enterobius vermicularis, which reinfects by hand-to-mouth transfer of perianal eggs but stays in the lumen. Choice E (larvae in fish, no autoinfection, eosinophilia absent) is Diphyllobothrium latum, acquired from undercooked freshwater fish and confined to the lumen, where it competes for vitamin B12. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 35, sections 1, 2, and 4: transmission, autoinfection, and eosinophilia); Robbins Ch. 8 (helminths). Suppression of eosinophilia by corticosteroids is from general clinical knowledge and is not in the notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is the intestinal nematode that autoinfects, persists for decades, and causes hyperinfection with gram-negative sepsis on corticosteroids; stool shows larvae, not eggs, and ivermectin is the drug of choice (Exam 3 notes, SDL 35). COMLEX: screen and treat Strongyloides before steroids in patients from endemic areas.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — choosing ivermectin versus albendazole versus praziquantel","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl35_q31","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Identify clinical manifestations associated with specific intestinal helminths and describe mechanisms of disease including nutrient competition, mechanical obstruction, and mucosal damage.","batch":1,"stem":"A heavy Trichuris burden in a child can prolapse which structure?","choices":{"A":"The stomach into the chest","B":"The rectum","C":"The gallbladder","D":"The urinary bladder through the umbilicus","E":"The cerebellum"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heavy Trichuris infection causes mucosal bleeding, dysentery, and rectal prolapse in children. Gastric herniation into the chest is a hiatal hernia, not a whipworm complication. The gallbladder is not prolapsed by whipworm. A urachal problem can present at the umbilicus, and Trichuris does not do that. The cerebellum is not prolapsed by an intestinal whipworm.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Gastric herniation into the chest is a hiatal hernia, not a whipworm complication","C":"The gallbladder is not prolapsed by whipworm","D":"A urachal problem can present at the umbilicus, and Trichuris does not do that","E":"The cerebellum is not prolapsed by an intestinal whipworm"}},{"id":"sdl35_q32","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibody class works with eosinophils against tissue helminths?","choices":{"A":"IgE","B":"Secretory IgA alone, with no eosinophils","C":"IgM against a blood-group antigen","D":"IgG that only neutralizes a virus","E":"None, because eosinophils ignore antibody"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Eosinophils, coordinated by IgE, release toxic granule proteins against helminths that invade tissue. Secretory IgA guards the lumen and is not the antibody paired with eosinophils in the notes. IgM against a blood-group antigen is an alloantibody, not the helminth response. Virus-neutralizing IgG is a different host defense. Eosinophils are coordinated with antibody in this response, rather than ignoring it.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Secretory IgA guards the lumen and is not the antibody paired with eosinophils in the notes","C":"IgM against a blood-group antigen is an alloantibody, not the helminth response","D":"Virus-neutralizing IgG is a different host defense","E":"Eosinophils are coordinated with antibody in this response, rather than ignoring it"}},{"id":"sdl35_q33","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":1,"stem":"An itchy serpiginous track in the skin, with eosinophilia, after bare skin contacted contaminated sand is which process?","choices":{"A":"A luminal beef tapeworm with no tissue phase","B":"Pinworm eggs on the perianal skin only","C":"Cutaneous larva migrans","D":"Peutz-Jeghers macules","E":"Rose spots of typhoid"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Animal hookworm larvae that wander in skin cause cutaneous larva migrans, a tissue-invasive infection that raises eosinophils. An adult beef tapeworm stays in the lumen and does not make a migrating skin track. Pinworm eggs cause perianal itch without a serpiginous skin track or tissue eosinophilia. Peutz-Jeghers macules are flat pigment, not a moving track. Rose spots are the macular rash of typhoid fever.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"An adult beef tapeworm stays in the lumen and does not make a migrating skin track","B":"Pinworm eggs cause perianal itch without a serpiginous skin track or tissue eosinophilia","D":"Peutz-Jeghers macules are flat pigment, not a moving track","E":"Rose spots are the macular rash of typhoid fever"}},{"id":"sdl35_q34","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Explain the role of eosinophilia in helminth infections and describe when eosinophilia is expected based on tissue invasion.","batch":2,"stem":"A 46-year-old man who left rural Southeast Asia 20 years ago has intermittent hives and a new cough. He has not walked barefoot in soil for years and takes no new drug. The eosinophil fraction is 18% (reference range: 1–4%), and sputum shows larvae. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L). Why is the eosinophil count high?","choices":{"A":"An adult beef tapeworm is confined to the lumen","B":"Autoinfective larvae are invading tissue","C":"Pinworm eggs were swallowed once and never migrated","D":"A cholesterol stone is irritating the gallbladder","E":"Vitamin B12 deficiency alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Strongyloides autoinfection sends larvae back through tissue, and that tissue phase produces a marked eosinophilia. A luminal beef tapeworm does not raise the eosinophil count. Pinworm stays in the lumen, and a single swallowed dose does not cause this larval eosinophilia years later. Gallbladder irritation from a stone does not put larvae in the sputum or raise eosinophils this way. Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia, not larval eosinophilia.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"A luminal beef tapeworm does not raise the eosinophil count","C":"Pinworm stays in the lumen, and a single swallowed dose does not cause this larval eosinophilia years later","D":"Gallbladder irritation from a stone does not put larvae in the sputum or raise eosinophils this way","E":"Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anemia, not larval eosinophilia"}},{"id":"sdl35_q35","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the modes of transmission and life cycle entry points of major intestinal helminths, including ingestion of eggs or encysted larvae, and skin penetration by larvae.","batch":2,"stem":"A 7-year-old boy in a rural village has bloody mucoid stools and tenesmus. He never had a cough or a transient lung infiltrate. Stool shows barrel-shaped eggs with polar plugs. Hemoglobin is 9.4 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Why was there no lung phase?","choices":{"A":"The larvae encysted in pork and were swallowed as cysticerci","B":"The larvae matured in the intestine without a tissue migration","C":"Filariform larvae penetrated his skin and then crossed the alveoli","D":"Eggs hatched in the duodenum, and larvae then traveled through the lungs before returning to the gut","E":"Cercariae drilled through his skin in fresh water"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Trichuris larvae hatch and mature in the intestine and do not migrate through the lungs. Cysticerci in pork are how the intestinal adult of Taenia solium is acquired, and those eggs are not barrel-shaped. Skin penetration followed by a lung crossing is the route of hookworm and Strongyloides. A lung journey after hatching in the gut is the Ascaris cycle, which this boy did not have. Cercarial skin penetration is the schistosome route, not the source of barrel-shaped eggs.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cysticerci in pork are how the intestinal adult of Taenia solium is acquired, and those eggs are not barrel-shaped","C":"Skin penetration followed by a lung crossing is the route of hookworm and Strongyloides","D":"A lung journey after hatching in the gut is the Ascaris cycle, which this boy did not have","E":"Cercarial skin penetration is the schistosome route, not the source of barrel-shaped eggs"}},{"id":"sdl35_q36","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"An 8-year-old boy in a crowded household has abdominal pain, and stool shows dwarf-tapeworm eggs. No one has served him raw beef, pork, or fish, and the worm count is rising anyway. He is afebrile and the abdomen is soft, without a mass. Hemoglobin is 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL) and the eosinophil fraction is 3% (reference range: 1–4%). What lets the burden increase inside him?","choices":{"A":"Eggs can hatch in his own intestine and finish a cycle there","B":"Each adult worm buds hundreds of new adults every night","C":"He must be reinfected from soil every day or the count cannot rise","D":"The worm only multiplies inside a mosquito","E":"Freezing the meat would have increased the count"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hymenolepis nana eggs can hatch in the intestinal lumen and complete a cycle in the same host, so the burden can rise without a new external exposure. Intestinal helminths do not bud new adult worms every night; one egg ordinarily becomes one adult. Soil reinfection is required for worms that cannot autoinfect, which is the opposite of this dwarf tapeworm. A mosquito is not the host in which this tapeworm multiplies. Freezing meat is not what increases a dwarf-tapeworm burden.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Intestinal helminths do not bud new adult worms every night; one egg ordinarily becomes one adult","C":"Soil reinfection is required for worms that cannot autoinfect, which is the opposite of this dwarf tapeworm","D":"A mosquito is not the host in which this tapeworm multiplies","E":"Freezing meat is not what increases a dwarf-tapeworm burden"}},{"id":"sdl35_q37","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Explain how the worm burden in helminth infections is determined and differentiate species capable of autoinfection from those that are not.","batch":2,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has had intermittent abdominal pain for years. She left an endemic region a decade ago and has not contacted contaminated soil since. Larvae are found on the perianal skin, and none were acquired from the ground. Eosinophils are 12% (reference range: 1–4%) and hemoglobin is 12.2 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL). Which route is maintaining the infection?","choices":{"A":"Swallowing embryonated eggs from raw vegetables each week","B":"Eating cysticerci in undercooked pork","C":"A mosquito bite that injects new larvae","D":"Drinking copepods in well water","E":"Larvae penetrating her perianal skin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Strongyloides filariform larvae can penetrate perianal skin and re-enter the circulation, which maintains the infection without new soil exposure. Weekly swallowing of eggs from vegetables is a new external exposure, which she has not had. Cysticerci in pork produce an intestinal tapeworm and do not leave larvae on the perianal skin. A mosquito is not the source of these perianal larvae. Copepods in well water transmit other parasites and are not this perianal cycle.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Weekly swallowing of eggs from vegetables is a new external exposure, which she has not had","B":"Cysticerci in pork produce an intestinal tapeworm and do not leave larvae on the perianal skin","C":"A mosquito is not the source of these perianal larvae","D":"Copepods in well water transmit other parasites and are not this perianal cycle"}}]},{"sdlNumber":36,"title":"SDL 36 — Pharmacology of Antihelminths","questions":[{"id":"sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Benzimidazoles such as mebendazole are thought to kill nematodes by inhibiting what?","choices":{"A":"Ergosterol synthesis","B":"Folate synthesis","C":"Peptidoglycan synthesis","D":"Chitin synthesis","E":"Microtubule synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Benzimidazoles, including mebendazole and albendazole, are thought to act against nematodes by inhibiting microtubule synthesis. Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifungals such as amphotericin B, not by benzimidazoles. Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target of sulfonamides, not of benzimidazoles. Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synthesis beta-lactam antibiotics block; it is not a target of antihelminthics. Benzimidazoles are thought to act by inhibiting microtubule synthesis, not chitin synthesis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: mebendazole binds tubulin and is listed with the microtubule-acting drugs (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: less than 10% of an oral mebendazole dose is absorbed (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Ergosterol is the fungal membrane sterol targeted by antifungals such as amphotericin B, not by benzimidazoles.","B":"Folate synthesis via dihydropteroate synthase is the target of sulfonamides, not of benzimidazoles.","C":"Peptidoglycan is the bacterial cell wall polymer whose synthesis beta-lactam antibiotics block; it is not a target of antihelminthics.","D":"Benzimidazoles are thought to act by inhibiting microtubule synthesis, not chitin synthesis."}},{"id":"sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Which antibiotic kills adult filarial worms indirectly by eliminating their Wolbachia bacterial symbionts?","choices":{"A":"Doxycycline","B":"Azithromycin","C":"Ciprofloxacin","D":"Vancomycin","E":"Clindamycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Doxycycline has macrofilaricidal activity against Wuchereria bancrofti and activity against onchocerciasis because it kills Wolbachia, an intracellular bacterial symbiont of filarial parasites. Azithromycin is a macrolide that binds the 50S ribosomal subunit. Ciprofloxacin is a quinolone that inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall synthesis in gram-positive bacteria. Clindamycin is a lincosamide that shares the 50S ribosomal binding site with macrolides. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Doxycycline). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tetracyclines cause tooth discoloration and are generally avoided in pregnancy and in children, except doxycycline (First Aid Antimicrobials — Tetracyclines). COMLEX: Katzung reports better activity against adult W bancrofti than any other available drug (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Azithromycin is a macrolide that binds the 50S ribosomal subunit.","C":"Ciprofloxacin is a quinolone that inhibits DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV.","D":"Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala to block cell wall synthesis in gram-positive bacteria.","E":"Clindamycin is a lincosamide that shares the 50S ribosomal binding site with macrolides."}},{"id":"sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":1,"stem":"Praziquantel paralyzes trematodes and cestodes by increasing their membrane permeability to which ion?","choices":{"A":"Chloride","B":"Sodium","C":"Calcium","D":"Potassium","E":"Magnesium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, causing paralysis, dislodgement, and death of the worm. Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated channels is ivermectin's mechanism, not praziquantel's. Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, not sodium, causing paralysis and dislodgement. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not potassium. Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not magnesium. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats Schistosoma, Clonorchis, and Taenia infections (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: praziquantel tablets are swallowed without chewing because the bitter taste can induce retching (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Increased chloride signaling via GABA or glutamate-gated channels is ivermectin's mechanism, not praziquantel's.","B":"Praziquantel increases the permeability of trematode and cestode cell membranes to calcium, not sodium, causing paralysis and dislodgement.","D":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not potassium.","E":"Praziquantel increases trematode and cestode membrane permeability to calcium, not magnesium."}},{"id":"sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"Which drug of choice is used for lymphatic filariasis from Wuchereria bancrofti and for loiasis?","choices":{"A":"Diethylcarbamazine","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Pyrantel pamoate","D":"Piperazine citrate","E":"Niclosamide tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Diethylcarbamazine is a drug of choice for W bancrofti, Brugia, and Loa loa because of its efficacy and lack of serious toxicity; it immobilizes microfilariae and exposes them to host defenses. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, not filariasis. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris and pinworm, not filariae. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections and has no role in filariasis or loiasis. Piperazine is an alternative drug for ascariasis only, not a treatment for filariasis or loiasis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa (deer fly) and Wuchereria bancrofti (mosquito) are both treated with diethylcarbamazine (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: the plasma half-life of diethylcarbamazine is longer in alkaline urine (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, not filariasis.","C":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal nematodes such as Ascaris and pinworm, not filariae.","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworm infections and has no role in filariasis or loiasis.","D":"Piperazine is an alternative drug for ascariasis only, not a treatment for filariasis or loiasis."}},{"id":"sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For neurocysticercosis, why is albendazole now generally preferred over praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Avoids need for corticosteroids","B":"Single oral dose regimen","C":"No liver toxicity with long use","D":"Better subarachnoid penetration","E":"Proven safety in early pregnancy"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Albendazole is preferred for neurocysticercosis because of a shorter course, lower cost, improved penetration into the subarachnoid space, and higher drug levels with corticosteroids. Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actually rise when steroids are co-administered. Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-dose regimen. Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires liver monitoring. Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis is treated with albendazole, while intestinal taeniasis is treated with praziquantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: corticosteroids are usually given with the antihelminthic to limit inflammation from dying organisms (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Albendazole does not avoid corticosteroids; its levels actually rise when steroids are co-administered.","B":"Albendazole is preferred for a shorter course, not a single-dose regimen.","C":"Long-term albendazole can cause hepatotoxicity and requires liver monitoring.","E":"Albendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy."}},{"id":"sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":1,"stem":"For uncomplicated strongyloidiasis, which drug is given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Ivermectin","C":"Niclosamide","D":"Oxamniquine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis, given as 200 mcg/kg once daily for 2 days; thiabendazole is an alternative but is more toxic. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms. Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm. Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms. Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosoma mansoni. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin; Thiabendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Strongyloides is treated with ivermectin or bendazoles (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: in disseminated disease in immunosuppressed patients, monthly suppressive therapy may help (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not Strongyloides.","E":"Pyrantel pamoate is for Ascaris and pinworm.","C":"Niclosamide is a second-line drug for tapeworms.","D":"Oxamniquine is an alternative to praziquantel for Schistosoma mansoni, not a treatment for strongyloidiasis."}},{"id":"sdl36_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old sheep farmer with a large hepatic hydatid cyst starts several months of albendazole before surgery. Which monitoring is advised during this long course?","choices":{"A":"Blood counts and liver tests","B":"Serum creatinine and urinalysis","C":"Serial ECG for QT changes","D":"Audiometry and visual fields","E":"Serum magnesium and phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Long-term albendazole for hydatid disease is usually well tolerated but can cause raised liver enzymes and pancytopenia, so blood counts and liver function are monitored. Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not the kidneys, so creatinine and urinalysis are not the advised monitoring. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Echinococcus hydatid cysts are treated with albendazole, with surgery for some cysts (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: albendazole should be avoided in patients with cirrhosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 5 — drug and toxin induced liver injury","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Long-term albendazole threatens the liver and marrow, not the kidneys, so creatinine and urinalysis are not the advised monitoring.","C":"Serial ECGs for QT change are not the advised monitoring.","E":"Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts and liver function, not serum magnesium and phosphate.","D":"Long-term albendazole calls for monitoring of blood counts and liver function, not audiometry and visual fields."}},{"id":"sdl36_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":5,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman at 8 weeks' gestation has perianal itching, and a tape test shows pinworm eggs. Her physician defers mebendazole. What property of mebendazole explains this?","choices":{"A":"Causes neonatal gray syndrome","B":"Inhibits fetal folate uptake","C":"Causes fetal tooth staining","D":"Teratogenic in animal studies","E":"Causes fetal renal tubule damage"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mebendazole is teratogenic in animals and is therefore contraindicated in pregnancy. Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern, not a mebendazole one. Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumulation in infants, not from mebendazole. Mebendazole is deferred in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because it blocks fetal folate uptake. Mebendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because of fetal renal tubule damage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Mebendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 36 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Enterobius is treated with bendazoles or pyrantel (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: for pinworm, mebendazole 100 mg is given once and repeated at 2 weeks (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Fetal tooth staining is a doxycycline (tetracycline) concern, not a mebendazole one.","A":"Neonatal gray syndrome results from chloramphenicol accumulation in infants, not from mebendazole.","B":"Mebendazole is deferred in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because it blocks fetal folate uptake.","E":"Mebendazole is contraindicated in pregnancy because it is teratogenic in animals, not because of fetal renal tubule damage."}},{"id":"sdl36_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man from Ghana with onchocerciasis receives a single oral dose of the drug of choice, a macrocyclic lactone. Concurrent use of which drug class should be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors","B":"Thiazide diuretics","C":"Beta-blockers","D":"ACE inhibitors","E":"Benzodiazepines"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Ivermectin paralyzes nematodes by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so it is best not combined with other GABA-enhancing drugs such as barbiturates, benzodiazepines, or valproic acid. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Onchocerca volvulus causes black skin nodules and river blindness (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: treatment reactions in onchocerciasis come mainly from killing microfilariae and peak on day 2 (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Proton pump inhibitors are not part of that answer.","B":"Thiazide diuretics are not part of that answer.","C":"Beta-blockers are not part of that answer.","D":"ACE inhibitors are not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl36_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old man from Cameroon has a worm crossing his conjunctiva and a very high Loa loa microfilarial load, well above the 2500/mL high-risk level. Which initial approach is safest?","choices":{"A":"Immediate full-dose diethylcarbamazine","B":"Single-dose praziquantel","C":"Slower-acting albendazole first","D":"Topical thiabendazole cream","E":"Weekly oral niclosamide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"For heavy Loa loa burdens (more than 2500 parasites/mL), Katzung lists apheresis or therapy with albendazole, which is slower acting and better tolerated, before definitive filaricidal treatment. Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy and retinal hemorrhage, so burden is lowered first. Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in loiasis. Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and larva migrans, not loiasis. Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorbed from the gut, with no activity against Loa loa microfilariae. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate; Ivermectin). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Loa loa causes skin swelling and a worm in the conjunctiva (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: reactions to dying microfilariae are occasionally severe in L loa infections (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Full-dose DEC in heavy Loa loa loads risks encephalopathy and retinal hemorrhage, so burden is lowered first.","B":"Praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms and has no role in loiasis.","D":"Thiabendazole is an alternative for strongyloidiasis and larva migrans, not loiasis.","E":"Niclosamide is a second-line tapeworm drug, minimally absorbed from the gut, with no activity against Loa loa microfilariae."}},{"id":"sdl36_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman with symptomatic parenchymal neurocysticercosis is taking dexamethasone for cerebral edema. What effect does dexamethasone have on praziquantel?","choices":{"A":"Reduced plasma drug levels","B":"Increased plasma drug levels","C":"Prolonged elimination half-life","D":"Greater CSF drug penetration","E":"Faster cyst calcification"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Corticosteroids can decrease plasma praziquantel levels by up to 50%, whereas albendazole levels increase, one reason albendazole is preferred when steroids are needed. Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is albendazole levels that rise. Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an effect of dexamethasone on praziquantel. Corticosteroids lower the plasma level of praziquantel by up to 50% rather than prolonging its half-life. Dexamethasone is given with praziquantel to blunt the inflammatory response to dying cysts, and it lowers praziquantel plasma levels; it does not speed cyst calcification. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Albendazole). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Taenia solium cysticercosis follows ingestion of eggs in fecally contaminated food (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: some workers caution against praziquantel in spinal neurocysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Dexamethasone lowers praziquantel levels by up to 50%; it is albendazole levels that rise.","D":"Superior CSF penetration is an albendazole advantage, not an effect of dexamethasone on praziquantel.","C":"Corticosteroids lower the plasma level of praziquantel by up to 50% rather than prolonging its half-life.","E":"Dexamethasone is given with praziquantel to blunt the inflammatory response to dying cysts, and it lowers praziquantel plasma levels; it does not speed cyst calcification."}},{"id":"sdl36_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man from Peru has right upper quadrant pain, eosinophilia, and Fasciola hepatica eggs after eating watercress. Why is praziquantel a poor choice for him?","choices":{"A":"Excessive toxicity in hepatic disease","B":"Lack of activity in this infection","C":"Poor oral bioavailability in adults","D":"Need for prolonged IV dosing","E":"High rates of liver fluke relapse"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Praziquantel treats most trematodes but was not effective for fascioliasis even at high doses; triclabendazole is the drug of choice, with bithionol as an alternative. Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem in fascioliasis is Fasciola resistance. Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis because Fasciola is resistant. Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatosplenic stage of advanced schistosomiasis; its problem in fascioliasis is lack of efficacy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel; Triclabendazole; Bithionol). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: praziquantel treats trematodes such as Schistosoma and Clonorchis (First Aid Microbiology (Antiparasitic drugs / helminth tables)). COMLEX: triclabendazole may prolong the QT interval (Katzung Ch. 53).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Praziquantel has about 80% oral bioavailability; its problem in fascioliasis is Fasciola resistance.","D":"Praziquantel is given orally; it fails in fascioliasis because Fasciola is resistant.","A":"Praziquantel is generally well tolerated even in the hepatosplenic stage of advanced schistosomiasis; its problem in fascioliasis is lack of efficacy."}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 30-year-old man from coastal India has progressive swelling of his right leg. A night blood smear shows sheathed microfilariae. He starts the drug of choice for lymphatic filariasis. On the first day he develops fever, headache, a papular rash, and painful lymphangitis of the leg, and his eosinophil count rises. Which best explains this reaction?","choices":{"A":"IgE allergy to the drug molecule","B":"Direct liver toxicity of the drug","C":"Bacterial infection of the swollen leg","D":"Cholinergic excess from the drug","E":"Host reaction to dying parasites"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lymphedema with microfilariae on a night smear is lymphatic filariasis treated with diethylcarbamazine, and fever, rash, lymphangitis, and rising eosinophils soon after the first dose result from release of proteins from dying microfilariae and adult worms, which is why antihistamines or corticosteroids may be given early in therapy. IgE allergy to the drug molecule would not explain lymphangitis localized to the area of the worms or the rising eosinophil count. Direct liver toxicity of the drug is not a recognized reaction to diethylcarbamazine and would not cause rash and lymphangitis. Bacterial infection of the swollen leg could cause lymphangitis but not timed onset after the first dose with eosinophilia. Cholinergic excess from the drug is a feature of cholinesterase inhibitors, not of this antifilarial agent. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Diethylcarbamazine citrate).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Diethylcarbamazine is the drug of choice for lymphatic filariasis and loiasis, and its adverse effects come largely from dying parasites (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: reactions are worse with heavy microfilarial loads, so screen Loa loa burden before treatment.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"IgE allergy to the drug molecule would","B":"Direct liver toxicity of the drug is","C":"Bacterial infection of the swollen leg could","D":"Cholinergic excess from the drug is a"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman from Jamaica with epilepsy controlled on valproate and clonazepam is scheduled to start high-dose prednisone. Her eosinophils are 12% (reference range: 1–4%), and stool shows rhabditiform larvae. Her physician plans the drug of choice for this infection. Why must her antiseizure regimen be considered first?","choices":{"A":"Both inhibit worm and human microtubule assembly","B":"Both enhance gamma-aminobutyric acid signaling","C":"Both strongly induce hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes","D":"Both block dopamine D2 receptors in the gut","E":"Both raise worm membrane calcium permeability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Eosinophilia with rhabditiform larvae is Strongyloides, which must be treated with ivermectin before immunosuppression; ivermectin paralyzes the worm by intensifying GABA-mediated transmission, so concurrent use with GABA-enhancing drugs such as benzodiazepines, barbiturates, and valproate is best avoided. Both inhibit worm and human microtubule assembly describes benzimidazoles, and her antiseizure drugs do not act on tubulin. Both strongly induce hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes is not the relevant concern, since neither clonazepam nor ivermectin is a strong inducer. Both block dopamine D2 receptors in the gut describes metoclopramide, not these drugs. Both raise worm membrane calcium permeability describes praziquantel, which is not used for Strongyloides. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Ivermectin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Ivermectin is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis and onchocerciasis and acts by intensifying GABA-mediated signaling in the parasite (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: treat Strongyloides before steroids and review GABA-enhancing medicines.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — worm burden and autoinfection","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Both inhibit worm and human microtubule assembly","C":"Both strongly induce hepatic CYP3A4 enzymes is","D":"Both block dopamine D2 receptors in the","E":"Both raise worm membrane calcium permeability describes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old man from rural Peru has new seizures. MRI shows several viable parenchymal brain cysts with scolices. Fundoscopy reveals a mobile cyst in the vitreous of the left eye. His physician avoids praziquantel. What is the main reason?","choices":{"A":"It cannot reach therapeutic levels in the brain","B":"It has no activity against tapeworm larvae","C":"Killing the eye cyst may cause lasting damage","D":"It must be given by daily IV infusion","E":"It causes optic neuritis through direct toxicity"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Brain cysts with scolices plus a vitreous cyst are neurocysticercosis with ocular cysticercosis, and praziquantel is contraindicated in ocular disease because destruction of the parasite inside the eye can provoke inflammation that causes irreparable damage. It cannot reach therapeutic levels in the brain is false, since praziquantel enters cerebrospinal fluid and is an alternative for neurocysticercosis. It has no activity against tapeworm larvae is wrong, because praziquantel is active against cestodes including cysticerci. It must be given by daily IV infusion is false, because praziquantel is taken by mouth. It causes optic neuritis through direct toxicity is not a described adverse effect of praziquantel. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Praziquantel, pharmacokinetics, adverse reactions and contraindications).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Praziquantel raises cestode and trematode calcium permeability and is contraindicated in ocular cysticercosis (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: examine the fundus before antiparasitic therapy for neurocysticercosis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 4 — eosinophilia and tissue invasion in helminth infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It cannot reach therapeutic levels in the","B":"It has no activity against tapeworm larvae","D":"It must be given by daily IV","E":"It causes optic neuritis through direct"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 47-year-old hunter ate undercooked bear meat 3 weeks ago. He now has fever, periorbital edema, severe muscle pain and weakness, and splinter hemorrhages. Creatine kinase is 6,800 U/L (reference range: 40–200 U/L), and eosinophils are 28% (reference range: 1–4%). Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel plus a corticosteroid","B":"Ivermectin plus a corticosteroid","C":"Pyrantel pamoate plus a corticosteroid","D":"Albendazole plus a corticosteroid","E":"Diethylcarbamazine plus a corticosteroid"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fever, periorbital edema, myositis with high creatine kinase, and eosinophilia after eating undercooked game meat indicate trichinosis, which is treated with albendazole or mebendazole, adding a corticosteroid for severe infection to control the inflammatory response to larvae in muscle. Praziquantel plus a corticosteroid is wrong because praziquantel treats flukes and tapeworms, not the tissue nematode Trichinella. Ivermectin plus a corticosteroid is not preferred, since ivermectin is the choice for Strongyloides and onchocerciasis rather than trichinosis. Pyrantel pamoate plus a corticosteroid fails because pyrantel acts on luminal worms and not on migrating or encysted tissue larvae. Diethylcarbamazine plus a corticosteroid fits lymphatic filariasis and loiasis, not trichinosis. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1; Mebendazole and Albendazole clinical uses); First Aid 2025 Microbiology (Trichinella spiralis).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Trichinella larvae encyst in striated muscle and cause myositis, periorbital edema, and eosinophilia; treat with a benzimidazole plus corticosteroids if severe (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: ask about undercooked pork or game meat in patients with fever, myalgia, and eosinophilia.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 15 → Obj 3 — hydatid cysts and other parasitic liver infections","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Praziquantel plus a corticosteroid is wrong because","B":"Ivermectin plus a corticosteroid is not preferred,","C":"Pyrantel pamoate plus a corticosteroid fails because","E":"Diethylcarbamazine plus a corticosteroid fits lymphatic filariasis"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old sheep farmer has right upper quadrant fullness. CT shows a 9 cm liver cyst containing daughter cysts with a calcified rim. Surgery is planned, and an oral benzimidazole will be given for weeks before and after the operation. Why is albendazole chosen over mebendazole for this patient?","choices":{"A":"It is not absorbed and stays in the bowel","B":"It kills worms by blocking calcium entry","C":"Its active metabolite reaches tissue cysts","D":"It has no effect on the liver or blood counts","E":"It is the safer choice during pregnancy"},"correct":"C","explanation":"A liver cyst with daughter cysts in a sheep farmer is hydatid disease, and albendazole is the medical treatment of choice because it is converted to a sulfoxide that distributes to tissues and enters hydatid cysts, especially when taken with a fatty meal, whereas less than 10 percent of oral mebendazole is absorbed. It is not absorbed and stays in the bowel describes mebendazole and would make a drug useless for a liver cyst. It kills worms by blocking calcium entry is not the benzimidazole mechanism, which is inhibition of microtubule synthesis. It has no effect on the liver or blood counts is false, because long courses require monitoring of liver enzymes and blood counts. It is the safer choice during pregnancy is wrong, since albendazole safety in pregnancy has not been established. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Albendazole; Mebendazole).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Albendazole is erratically absorbed, increased by fatty meals, and its sulfoxide enters bile, cerebrospinal fluid, and hydatid cysts (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: give albendazole around hydatid cyst surgery and monitor liver enzymes and blood counts.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 3 — clinical manifestations of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is not absorbed and stays in","B":"It kills worms by blocking calcium entry","D":"It has no effect on the liver","E":"It is the safer choice during pregnancy"}},{"id":"trial_sdl36_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old man from southern China often eats raw freshwater fish. He has intermittent right upper quadrant pain and mild jaundice. Ultrasound shows dilated intrahepatic bile ducts without stones, eosinophils are 9% (reference range: 1–4%), and stool shows small operculated eggs. Which drug is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Praziquantel","B":"Triclabendazole","C":"Ivermectin","D":"Diethylcarbamazine","E":"Pyrantel pamoate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Raw freshwater fish, dilated intrahepatic ducts, eosinophilia, and small operculated eggs in stool from a man raised in southern China indicate the Chinese liver fluke Clonorchis sinensis, which is treated with praziquantel. Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for Fasciola hepatica, a liver fluke acquired from watercress rather than fish. Ivermectin targets Strongyloides and onchocerciasis and is not used for liver flukes. Diethylcarbamazine is used for lymphatic filariasis and loiasis. Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal roundworms such as pinworm and Ascaris, not flukes. Sources: Katzung Ch. 53 (Table 53-1; Praziquantel clinical uses); First Aid 2025 Oncology (Clonorchis and cholangiocarcinoma).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Praziquantel treats Clonorchis and most other trematode and cestode infections, while Fasciola requires triclabendazole (Katzung Ch. 53). COMLEX: chronic Clonorchis infection raises cholangiocarcinoma risk, so treat and follow biliary symptoms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — transmission and entry routes of intestinal helminths","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Triclabendazole is the drug of choice for","C":"Ivermectin targets Strongyloides and onchocerciasis and is","D":"Diethylcarbamazine is used for lymphatic filariasis and","E":"Pyrantel pamoate treats luminal roundworms such as"}},{"id":"grid_sdl36_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Appreciate when one antihelminthic medication may be chosen over another in a clinical scenario.","batch":4,"stem":"A 27-year-old man who emigrated from a rural area of Central America has a first generalized seizure. Brain CT shows multiple small cystic lesions, several containing an eccentric scolex. An ophthalmologic examination shows no ocular cysts. Dexamethasone is started, and an antiparasitic drug is chosen that gives a shorter course, reaches the subarachnoid space well, and is not weakened by the corticosteroid. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the antiparasitic drug chosen?","grid":{"headers":["Molecular action","Level on steroids","Site of action"]},"choices":{"A":["↑ Ca²⁺ permeability","↓","Tissue and lumen"],"B":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Tissue and lumen"],"C":["Binds β-tubulin","↔","Gut lumen"],"D":["Opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻","↔","Tissue and lumen"],"E":["Binds β-tubulin","↑","Gut lumen"]},"correct":"B","explanation":"Choice B (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, tissue and lumen) is correct. Albendazole is preferred over praziquantel for neurocysticercosis. The notes describe a benzimidazole that binds parasite β-tubulin to block microtubule synthesis and is converted to albendazole sulfoxide, which distributes widely into tissues, CSF, bile, and hydatid cysts. The course is shorter and cheaper, CSF penetration is better, and levels of the active metabolite rise when corticosteroids are added. Choice A (raises Ca²⁺ permeability, level falls on steroids, tissue and lumen) is praziquantel, which causes Ca²⁺ influx and paralysis of cestodes and trematodes, but dexamethasone lowers its plasma level by up to 50%. Choice C (binds β-tubulin, no steroid effect, gut lumen) is mebendazole, a benzimidazole with less than 10% systemic bioavailability that acts in the gut lumen against intestinal roundworms. Choice D (opens GABA/glutamate Cl⁻ channels, no steroid effect, tissue and lumen) is ivermectin, which paralyzes nematodes and arthropods and is the drug of choice for strongyloidiasis and onchocerciasis, not cysticercosis. Choice E (binds β-tubulin, level rises on steroids, gut lumen) matches no agent in the notes; it pairs the corticosteroid interaction of albendazole with the luminal action of mebendazole, but a luminal drug could not treat brain cysts. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 36, sections 1 and 2: albendazole, mebendazole, ivermectin, praziquantel, and drug choice in neurocysticercosis); Katzung Ch. 53 (antihelminthic drugs). The unchanged corticosteroid effect on mebendazole and ivermectin levels is inferred because the notes describe no such interaction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: neurocysticercosis → albendazole (β-tubulin) plus corticosteroids; steroids raise albendazole sulfoxide but lower praziquantel levels by up to 50% (Exam 3 notes, SDL 36). COMLEX: exclude ocular cysticercosis before therapy, because dying cysts in the eye cause irreversible damage.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Taenia solium cysticercosis from egg ingestion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl36_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, clinical uses and adverse reactions for the following medications used in the treatment of helminthic infections: albendazole, bithionol, diethylcarbamazine, doxycycline, ivermectin, mebendazole, moxidectin, praziquantal and thiabendazole.","batch":2,"stem":"A 20-month-old girl has perianal itching, and a morning tape test shows pinworm eggs. She is otherwise well. Weight is at the 40th percentile and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 12.0 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Why is mebendazole used cautiously at this age?","choices":{"A":"Rare convulsions have been reported under 2 years of age","B":"It stains the teeth the way tetracycline does","C":"It is the drug of choice for river blindness at any age","D":"It must be given intravenously with forced hydration","E":"It only kills flukes and cannot touch pinworm"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Mebendazole is used cautiously in children younger than 2 years because rare convulsions have occurred. Tooth staining is a tetracycline effect, and the notes assign that warning to doxycycline. River blindness is treated with ivermectin or moxidectin, not with mebendazole as the drug of choice. Forced intravenous hydration is a cisplatin precaution, not a mebendazole one. Mebendazole is a standard drug for pinworm and other intestinal roundworms.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Tooth staining is a tetracycline effect, and the notes assign that warning to doxycycline","C":"River blindness is treated with ivermectin or moxidectin, not with mebendazole as the drug of choice","D":"Forced intravenous hydration is a cisplatin precaution, not a mebendazole one","E":"Mebendazole is a standard drug for pinworm and other intestinal roundworms"}}]},{"sdlNumber":37,"title":"SDL 37 — Pharmacology of GI motility, IBS, and short-bowel syndrome","questions":[{"id":"sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Metoclopramide speeds gastric emptying and raises lower esophageal sphincter pressure mainly by what action?","choices":{"A":"Muscarinic M3 receptor agonism","B":"Dopamine D2 receptor blockade","C":"Serotonin 5-HT3 receptor blockade","D":"Histamine H2 receptor blockade","E":"Mu opioid receptor agonism"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist; blocking inhibitory dopamine receptors in the gut raises LES pressure, enhances esophageal peristalsis and speeds gastric emptying, with little effect on small bowel or colon. Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not metoclopramide's. 5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoclopramide's prokinetic effect. Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather than speeding gastric emptying. H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell acid secretion and does not speed gastric emptying. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: metoclopramide is a D2 antagonist used for diabetic gastroparesis and persistent GERD (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: metoclopramide also blocks D2 receptors in the area postrema chemoreceptor trigger zone (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct muscarinic agonism is bethanechol's mechanism, not metoclopramide's.","C":"5-HT3 blockade is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D, not metoclopramide's prokinetic effect.","E":"Mu opioid agonism, as with loperamide, slows transit rather than speeding gastric emptying.","D":"H2 receptor blockade competitively inhibits parietal cell acid secretion and does not speed gastric emptying."}},{"id":"sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Which agent given intravenously is used to treat acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie syndrome)?","choices":{"A":"Atropine","B":"Octreotide","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Neostigmine","E":"Dicyclomine"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine at enteric synapses; a 2 mg IV dose rapidly evacuates colonic gas and stool in acute pseudo-obstruction. Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would worsen colonic pseudo-obstruction. The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidiarrheal effect, the opposite of what colonic pseudo-obstruction needs. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no effect on gastric motility and may slow colonic transit, so it would not relieve pseudo-obstruction. Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses inhibits motility, so it does not decompress a dilated colon. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: neostigmine is a quaternary (charged) AChE inhibitor that does not enter the CNS and treats postoperative ileus and urinary retention (First Aid Autonomic Drugs (cholinomimetic agents)). COMLEX: cholinergic effects of the IV dose include salivation, vomiting and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Dicyclomine is an antispasmodic for IBS cramping and would worsen colonic pseudo-obstruction.","A":"The anticholinergic properties of atropine add to its antidiarrheal effect, the opposite of what colonic pseudo-obstruction needs.","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic that has no effect on gastric motility and may slow colonic transit, so it would not relieve pseudo-obstruction.","B":"Octreotide inhibits intestinal secretion and at higher doses inhibits motility, so it does not decompress a dilated colon."}},{"id":"sdl37_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":5,"stem":"Why does domperidone cause extrapyramidal effects far less often than metoclopramide?","choices":{"A":"Poor blood-brain barrier entry","B":"Faster renal clearance of the drug","C":"Weaker binding at gut D2 receptors","D":"Added serotonin 5-HT4 agonism","E":"Greater pituitary prolactin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Domperidone shares the D2-blocking mechanism but crosses the blood-brain barrier poorly, so central extrapyramidal effects are rare. Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic mechanism as metoclopramide, so weaker gut binding is not the reason. Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CNS sparing reflects poor blood-brain barrier entry. Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not explain fewer extrapyramidal effects. Katzung explains that domperidone rarely causes extrapyramidal effects because it does not cross the blood-brain barrier to a significant degree. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: centrally acting D2 blockers cause parkinsonism and acute dystonia as drug-induced movement disorders (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone still acts at the area postrema, which lies outside the barrier (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Domperidone has the same peripheral D2-blocking prokinetic mechanism as metoclopramide, so weaker gut binding is not the reason.","D":"Domperidone is a D2 antagonist without 5-HT4 agonism; its CNS sparing reflects poor blood-brain barrier entry.","E":"Domperidone still causes hyperprolactinemia, which does not explain fewer extrapyramidal effects.","B":"Katzung explains that domperidone rarely causes extrapyramidal effects because it does not cross the blood-brain barrier to a significant degree."}},{"id":"sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Alosetron use for diarrhea-predominant IBS is restricted mainly because of what serious adverse effect?","choices":{"A":"Torsades de pointes","B":"Ischemic colitis","C":"Agranulocytosis","D":"QT shortening","E":"Hepatic necrosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist, causes constipation in up to 30% and ischemic colitis in up to 3 per 1000 patients, so it is restricted to women with severe IBS with diarrhea. Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-HT4 agonists like cisapride, not alosetron. Agranulocytosis is the classic serious toxicity of clozapine, not of the 5-HT3 antagonist alosetron. Hepatic necrosis is reported with drugs such as colchicine. First-generation 5-HT3 antagonists can prolong, not shorten, the QT interval (most with dolasetron). Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-HT3 antagonists such as ondansetron are antiemetics whose adverse effects include headache and constipation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on extrinsic afferents that carry visceral pain signals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Torsades and cardiac toxicity were the problem with older 5-HT4 agonists like cisapride, not alosetron.","C":"Agranulocytosis is the classic serious toxicity of clozapine, not of the 5-HT3 antagonist alosetron.","E":"Hepatic necrosis is reported with drugs such as colchicine.","D":"First-generation 5-HT3 antagonists can prolong, not shorten, the QT interval (most with dolasetron)."}},{"id":"sdl37_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Lubiprostone relieves constipation-predominant IBS by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"ClC-2 chloride channel activation","B":"Mu opioid receptor activation in gut","C":"Na+/K+ ATPase pump inhibition","D":"Bulk retention of stool water","E":"5-HT3 receptor inhibition on afferents"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lubiprostone, a prostanoic acid derivative, activates type 2 chloride channels (ClC-2) on the apical enterocyte membrane, increasing chloride-rich fluid secretion into the lumen. Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and treats diarrhea, not constipation. Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psyllium, not lubiprostone. 5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and causes constipation. Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition is the action of digoxin on the heart, not of lubiprostone. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Chloride Channel Activator). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: lubiprostone appears in the anticonstipation table with diarrhea and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: lubiprostone is approved for women with IBS-C and avoided in women who may become pregnant (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Mu opioid activation, as with loperamide, slows transit and treats diarrhea, not constipation.","D":"Bulk retention of stool water describes fiber such as psyllium, not lubiprostone.","E":"5-HT3 inhibition is alosetron's mechanism for IBS-D and causes constipation.","C":"Na+/K+-ATPase inhibition is the action of digoxin on the heart, not of lubiprostone."}},{"id":"sdl37_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":1,"stem":"Eluxadoline for diarrhea-predominant IBS should be avoided in a patient with which history?","choices":{"A":"Prior appendectomy","B":"Hypothyroidism","C":"Prior pancreatitis","D":"Recurrent gout attacks","E":"Migraine headaches"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Eluxadoline, a mu-opioid agonist, can cause sphincter of Oddi spasm with pancreatitis, so it is avoided with prior pancreatitis, alcoholism or sphincter of Oddi disease. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripherally acting mu agonists slow gut motility with low addiction potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a lower 75 mg dose is used after cholecystectomy because sphincter of Oddi complications are more common (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide is an analog of which gut hormone?","choices":{"A":"Glucagon-like peptide 1","B":"Glucose insulinotropic peptide","C":"Cholecystokinin","D":"Glucagon-like peptide 2","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog used for short-bowel syndrome; GLP-2 is an intestinotrophic peptide released from L cells. GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-lowering drugs for diabetes. Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder emptying, not the peptide mimicked by teduglutide. GIP is an incretin secreted by duodenal and jejunal K cells that stimulates insulin secretion; teduglutide mimics GLP-2, not GIP. Pancreatic polypeptide is a pancreatic peptide that inhibits pancreatic HCO3- and enzyme secretion; teduglutide mimics GLP-2. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GLP-1 analogs such as exenatide are incretin drugs, unlike the intestinotrophic analog used for short gut (First Aid Endocrine Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide binds receptors on enteric neurons and endocrine cells (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"GLP-1 receptor agonists such as exenatide are glucose-lowering drugs for diabetes.","C":"Cholecystokinin is the hormone that stimulates gallbladder emptying, not the peptide mimicked by teduglutide.","B":"GIP is an incretin secreted by duodenal and jejunal K cells that stimulates insulin secretion; teduglutide mimics GLP-2, not GIP.","E":"Pancreatic polypeptide is a pancreatic peptide that inhibits pancreatic HCO3- and enzyme secretion; teduglutide mimics GLP-2."}},{"id":"sdl37_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"By which route is teduglutide given for short-bowel syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Weekly intravenous infusion pump","B":"Twice-daily oral tablet","C":"Monthly intramuscular depot","D":"Daily rectal suppository","E":"Daily subcutaneous injection"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Teduglutide is given at 0.05 mg/kg once daily by subcutaneous injection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: teduglutide is a GLP-2 analog given by subcutaneous injection (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: teduglutide dosing is weight based at 0.05 mg/kg per day (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":1,"stem":"Teduglutide helps patients with short-bowel syndrome mainly by promoting what?","choices":{"A":"Faster emptying of the stomach","B":"Slower colonic transit","C":"Mucosal epithelial growth","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output","E":"Hepatic bile acid output"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Through trophic hormones, teduglutide stimulates mucosal epithelial growth of the remaining bowel and enhances fluid absorption. Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than speeding it. Slower colonic transit is how the mu-opioid agonist eluxadoline treats diarrhea. Pancreatic enzyme output is not the target; teduglutide releases trophic hormones that grow the remaining mucosa and enhance fluid absorption. Bile acid output is not the target; teduglutide releases trophic hormones that grow the remaining mucosa and enhance fluid absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: short-bowel syndrome causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluid after extensive resection (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: teduglutide acts on enteric neurons and endocrine cells to adapt the residual gut (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Teduglutide actually delays gastric emptying rather than speeding it.","B":"Slower colonic transit is how the mu-opioid agonist eluxadoline treats diarrhea.","D":"Pancreatic enzyme output is not the target.","E":"Bile acid output is not the target."}},{"id":"sdl37_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 68-year-old woman with diabetic gastroparesis has taken metoclopramide for 2 years. She now has repetitive lip smacking and tongue thrusting that persist after the drug is stopped. Which adverse effect is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute dystonic reaction","B":"Akathisia restlessness","C":"Serotonin syndrome","D":"Tardive dyskinesia","E":"Essential tremor"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Long-term metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia that may be irreversible, so prolonged use is avoided, especially in older adults. Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy. Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent orofacial movements of tardive dyskinesia. Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs, not persistent orofacial movements after long-term D2 blockade. Essential tremor is a familial postural tremor of the hands, head, or voice, not the persistent orofacial movements of long-term D2 blockade. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: delayed orofacial choreiform movements follow long-term D2 blockade by antipsychotics or prokinetics (First Aid Pharmacology (drug adverse effects)). COMLEX: tardive movements are more likely with long-term use in older patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 6 → Obj 4 — CCK and GIP slow gastric emptying","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Acute dystonia occurs early in therapy.","B":"Akathisia is subjective restlessness, not the persistent orofacial movements of tardive dyskinesia.","C":"Serotonin syndrome is an acute hyperthermic syndrome from serotonergic drugs such as SSRIs, not persistent orofacial movements after long-term D2 blockade.","E":"Essential tremor is a familial postural tremor of the hands, head, or voice, not the persistent orofacial movements of long-term D2 blockade."}},{"id":"sdl37_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman takes metoclopramide for postsurgical gastroparesis. Months later she has milky nipple discharge and missed periods. Which laboratory finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum prolactin","B":"High serum cortisol","C":"High serum prolactin","D":"Low free thyroxine level","E":"High serum calcium"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Dopamine tonically inhibits prolactin release; D2 blockade raises prolactin and can cause galactorrhea, amenorrhea and gynecomastia. Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhea, not lowering it. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Dopamine Receptor Antagonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: dopamine antagonists are a drug cause of hyperprolactinemia with galactorrhea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: domperidone is used off-label for postpartum lactation through the same pituitary effect (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Metoclopramide's D2 blockade raises prolactin, causing galactorrhea, not lowering it.","B":"High serum cortisol is not part of that answer.","D":"Low free thyroxine level is not part of that answer.","E":"High serum calcium is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl37_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":5,"stem":"A 50-year-old man with diabetic gastroparesis improves on oral erythromycin, which induces fasting migrating motor complexes. After several weeks the benefit fades despite the same dose. What best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Gastric acid breakdown of the drug","B":"Rapid development of tolerance","C":"Induced CYP3A4 metabolism","D":"Selection of resistant flora","E":"Worsening autonomic damage"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and initiates the migrating motor complex, which normally recurs about every 90 minutes during fasting; tolerance develops rapidly, limiting long-term use. Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a metabolite complex that inactivates the enzyme. Oral erythromycin works at first in gastroparesis, so acid breakdown does not explain fading benefit. Erythromycin speeds emptying by directly stimulating motilin receptors on gut smooth muscle, so bacterial resistance does not affect this prokinetic action. Fading benefit at the same dose fits the rapid tolerance that limits chronic erythromycin, not progressive autonomic damage. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Macrolides). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 37 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 and cause QT prolongation as notable interactions (First Aid Antimicrobials (macrolides)). COMLEX: a single IV macrolide dose of 3 mg/kg can clear the stomach of blood before upper endoscopy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 3 — migrating motor complex during fasting","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Erythromycin inhibits rather than induces CYP3A, forming a metabolite complex that inactivates the enzyme.","A":"Oral erythromycin works at first in gastroparesis, so acid breakdown does not explain fading benefit.","D":"Erythromycin speeds emptying by directly stimulating motilin receptors on gut smooth muscle, so bacterial resistance does not affect this prokinetic action.","E":"Fading benefit at the same dose fits the rapid tolerance that limits chronic erythromycin, not progressive autonomic damage."}},{"id":"sdl37_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 30-year-old woman has IBS with diarrhea: frequent loose stools and urgency but mild pain. She wants an inexpensive over-the-counter medicine. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Lubiprostone","C":"Psyllium","D":"Bisacodyl","E":"Neomycin"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Loperamide, a peripherally acting mu-opioid agonist, reduces stool frequency and urgency in IBS with diarrhea; it does not relieve abdominal pain. Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator for IBS-C and would worsen diarrhea. Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would worsen diarrhea. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial digestion of fiber can increase bloating and flatus. Neomycin is a poorly absorbed oral aminoglycoside used before bowel surgery or in hepatic encephalopathy, not an IBS-D remedy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: antidiarrheal mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and cause constipation and nausea (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: diphenoxylate is combined with atropine to discourage overdosage (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lubiprostone is a prescription chloride channel activator for IBS-C and would worsen diarrhea.","D":"Bisacodyl is a stimulant laxative for constipation and would worsen diarrhea.","C":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, and colonic bacterial digestion of fiber can increase bloating and flatus.","E":"Neomycin is a poorly absorbed oral aminoglycoside used before bowel surgery or in hepatic encephalopathy, not an IBS-D remedy."}},{"id":"sdl37_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 40-year-old man has IBS with constipation and bloating. Fiber and over-the-counter laxatives have not helped. He wants a capsule taken each morning that raises enterocyte cGMP. Which drug fits?","choices":{"A":"Lubiprostone","B":"Linaclotide","C":"Alosetron","D":"Eluxadoline","E":"Hyoscyamine"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Linaclotide (290 mcg daily) activates guanylyl cyclase C, raising cGMP, which opens CFTR and increases chloride and bicarbonate secretion; lubiprostone is approved in women. Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would worsen constipation. Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits diarrhea, not constipation. Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does not raise cGMP. Lubiprostone activates the ClC-2 chloride channel rather than raising cGMP, and it is approved for IBS-C only in women. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Guanylate Cyclase C Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: GC-C agonists such as plecanatide cause diarrhea, bloating and flatulence (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: GC-C agonists are contraindicated in children because of dehydration risk (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 3 — CFTR and intestinal chloride secretion","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Alosetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist for IBS-D in women and would worsen constipation.","D":"Eluxadoline treats IBS-D and is constipating, so it suits diarrhea, not constipation.","E":"Hyoscyamine is an antispasmodic for acute cramping and does not raise cGMP.","A":"Lubiprostone activates the ClC-2 chloride channel rather than raising cGMP, and it is approved for IBS-C only in women."}},{"id":"sdl37_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":2,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has mixed-type IBS, alternating stool form with chronic abdominal pain as her main complaint. Which nightly drug best targets the pain?","choices":{"A":"High-dose bismuth salts","B":"Daily lactulose syrup by mouth","C":"Oral metoclopramide","D":"Low-dose amitriptyline","E":"Weekly erythromycin"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants alter central processing of visceral afferent input and help chronic IBS pain; their anticholinergic effect also slows stools. Katzung does not separate mixed-type therapy, so this item is objectives-first. Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bacteria ferment into flatus and cramps, so it does not target IBS pain. Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatment for chronic visceral pain. Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and acute or traveler's diarrhea, not as chronic pain therapy for IBS. Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastroparesis, and tolerance develops rapidly; it does not relieve IBS pain. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs Used in the Treatment of Irritable Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: tricyclic antidepressants cause anticholinergic, sedative and cardiac conduction effects (First Aid Psychiatry Pharmacology). COMLEX: desipramine is dosed at 10 to 50 mg per day for chronic IBS pain (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lactulose is a nonabsorbable sugar laxative that colonic bacteria ferment into flatus and cramps, so it does not target IBS pain.","C":"Metoclopramide is a prokinetic and antiemetic, not a treatment for chronic visceral pain.","A":"Bismuth subsalicylate is used short term for dyspepsia and acute or traveler's diarrhea, not as chronic pain therapy for IBS.","E":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors to treat gastroparesis, and tolerance develops rapidly; it does not relieve IBS pain."}},{"id":"sdl37_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 45-year-old man had massive small-bowel resection after a volvulus and needs home intravenous nutrition 6 nights a week. Teduglutide is started. Which outcome best shows that it is working?","choices":{"A":"Higher fasting glucose","B":"Faster gastric emptying","C":"Lower serum prolactin level","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin","E":"Reduced infusion frequency"},"correct":"E","explanation":"In trials, 54% of patients given teduglutide cut parenteral support by at least 1 day per week, versus 23% given placebo. Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral support is the measure of benefit. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: extensive resection removes absorptive surface and causes malabsorption of nutrients and fluids (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: patients with under 200 cm of small intestine usually need partial or complete parenteral support (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 4 — pathogenesis of general malabsorption","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Teduglutide delays gastric emptying, and reduced parenteral support is the measure of benefit.","A":"Higher fasting glucose is not part of that answer.","C":"Lower serum prolactin level is not part of that answer.","D":"Rise in serum bilirubin is not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl37_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome is being considered for teduglutide. Which screening is most important because of a known drug risk?","choices":{"A":"Echocardiography for valves","B":"Bone density scanning","C":"Colonoscopy for neoplasia","D":"Audiometry for hearing","E":"Slit-lamp eye examination"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Teduglutide is trophic to the mucosa and may raise the risk of colorectal and small-bowel neoplasia, so colonoscopy is done before and during therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: trophic stimulation of intestinal epithelium raises concern for adenomatous growth (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: GLP-2 analog therapy carries a neoplasia warning for colon and small bowel (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":2,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with short-bowel syndrome after Crohn resections starts teduglutide. Its effect on the remaining mucosa is mediated downstream by release of which hormone?","choices":{"A":"Insulin-like growth factor","B":"Histamine from mast cells","C":"Somatostatin from D cells","D":"Substance P from neurons","E":"Gastrin from antral G cells"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells and stimulates release of trophic hormones, including insulin-like growth factor, that drive epithelial growth and fluid absorption. Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid secretion, not the trophic response of small-bowel mucosa to teduglutide. Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal secretion and hormone release rather than driving mucosal growth. Histamine from ECL cells mediates gastrin-driven acid secretion via parietal H2 receptors, not GLP-2 mucosal growth. Teduglutide acts through trophic hormones such as insulin-like growth factor, not substance P, to drive mucosal epithelial growth. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-Like Peptide 2 Analog for Short-Bowel Syndrome). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: VIP from enteric neurons increases intestinal water and electrolyte secretion (First Aid Gastrointestinal Physiology (GI regulatory substances)). COMLEX: octreotide may be used for high-output diarrhea in short-bowel syndrome (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Gastrin from antral G cells stimulates parietal cell acid secretion, not the trophic response of small-bowel mucosa to teduglutide.","C":"Somatostatin, mimicked by octreotide, inhibits intestinal secretion and hormone release rather than driving mucosal growth.","B":"Histamine from ECL cells mediates gastrin-driven acid secretion via parietal H2 receptors, not GLP-2 mucosal growth.","D":"Teduglutide acts through trophic hormones such as insulin-like growth factor, not substance P, to drive mucosal epithelial growth."}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old man is 3 days out from hip replacement. His abdomen is markedly distended and tympanic, and CT shows a cecum dilated to 11 cm with no mechanical obstruction. He receives a single IV drug and within minutes passes large amounts of flatus and stool. He then has heavy salivation, and his heart rate falls from 88 to 44/min. Which mechanism explains both effects?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors","B":"Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors","C":"Activation of motilin receptors","D":"Peripheral mu-opioid receptor blockade","E":"Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction after surgery is treated with IV neostigmine, which inhibits acetylcholinesterase so acetylcholine accumulates at muscarinic receptors in the gut, producing colonic evacuation, and in the heart and salivary glands, producing bradycardia and salivation; atropine should be available. Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors is the action of metoclopramide, which does not speed colonic motility. Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is the action of prucalopride, an oral agent without prompt cholinergic toxicity. Activation of motilin receptors is how erythromycin speeds gastric emptying and would not explain salivation and bradycardia. Peripheral mu-opioid receptor blockade relieves opioid constipation but does not cause cholinergic effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Cholinomimetic agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IV neostigmine, a cholinesterase inhibitor, treats acute colonic pseudo-obstruction but causes salivation, vomiting, diarrhea, and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: give it with cardiac monitoring and atropine at the bedside.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors is the","B":"Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is the","C":"Activation of motilin receptors is how erythromycin","D":"Peripheral mu-opioid receptor blockade relieves opioid constipation"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman has chronic constipation with slow colonic transit on a marker study and no obstruction on colonoscopy. Her physician prescribes metoclopramide before meals. After 6 weeks her stool frequency is unchanged, although her early satiety has improved. Which best explains the lack of effect on her constipation?","choices":{"A":"It acts mainly as a colonic chloride secretagogue","B":"Its prokinetic effect is limited to the upper gut","C":"It blocks enteric acetylcholine release in the colon","D":"Tolerance to its effect develops within days","E":"It is destroyed by colonic bacteria before acting"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Metoclopramide blocks dopamine D2 receptors that normally inhibit cholinergic smooth muscle stimulation, which raises esophageal peristalsis and sphincter pressure and speeds gastric emptying but has no effect on small intestinal or colonic motility, so her satiety improved while her slow-transit constipation did not. It acts mainly as a colonic chloride secretagogue describes lubiprostone or linaclotide, not a dopamine antagonist. It blocks enteric acetylcholine release in the colon is the opposite of its action, which removes dopaminergic inhibition of cholinergic stimulation. Tolerance to its effect develops within days is the problem with erythromycin, and her gastric symptoms did respond. It is destroyed by colonic bacteria before acting is not a property of metoclopramide, which is absorbed systemically. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Metoclopramide and domperidone).","boardPrep":"Step 1: D2 antagonists such as metoclopramide enhance esophageal and gastric motility but not small bowel or colonic motility (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: for slow-transit constipation choose laxatives, secretagogues, or a 5-HT4 agonist instead.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It acts mainly as a colonic chloride","C":"It blocks enteric acetylcholine release in the","D":"Tolerance to its effect develops within days","E":"It is destroyed by colonic bacteria before"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has IBS with constipation and daily cramping pain. Her physician adds a low nightly dose of amitriptyline for the pain. Three weeks later the pain is better, but she has fewer and harder stools and a dry mouth. Which property of the drug best explains the change in her bowel habit?","choices":{"A":"Serotonin 5-HT4 receptor agonism","B":"Guanylate cyclase C activation","C":"Mu-opioid receptor agonism in gut","D":"Blockade of muscarinic receptors","E":"Increased colonic chloride secretion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Low-dose tricyclic antidepressants ease IBS pain by altering central processing of visceral afferent signals, but their anticholinergic properties reduce motility and stool frequency, which can help diarrhea-predominant disease and worsen constipation, as her dry mouth also suggests. Serotonin 5-HT4 receptor agonism would speed colonic transit and relieve constipation. Guanylate cyclase C activation is the action of linaclotide, which increases secretion and loosens stool. Mu-opioid receptor agonism in gut describes loperamide or eluxadoline, not a tricyclic antidepressant. Increased colonic chloride secretion would loosen stools rather than harden them. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Drugs used in the treatment of irritable bowel syndrome).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Low-dose tricyclics reduce IBS pain via central visceral processing, and their anticholinergic effect slows the gut (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: tricyclics suit pain-predominant IBS with diarrhea better than IBS with constipation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 1 — irritable bowel syndrome diagnosis and first-line treatment","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Serotonin 5-HT4 receptor agonism would speed colonic","B":"Guanylate cyclase C activation is the action","C":"Mu-opioid receptor agonism in gut describes loperamide","E":"Increased colonic chloride secretion would loosen stools"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":3,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman has severe diarrhea-predominant IBS with urgency and lower abdominal pain despite loperamide. She enrolls in a restricted prescribing program and starts a tablet twice daily. Her pain, urgency, and stool frequency improve, but she is warned to stop at once for constipation or rectal bleeding. How does this drug relieve her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Activates the ClC-2 chloride channel","B":"Blocks serotonin 5-HT3 receptors","C":"Activates guanylate cyclase C","D":"Stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors","E":"Blocks peripheral mu-opioid receptors"},"correct":"B","explanation":"A drug restricted to women with severe diarrhea-predominant IBS, with warnings about constipation and ischemic colitis, is alosetron, a 5-HT3 antagonist that blocks visceral afferent pain signaling and inhibits enteric cholinergic neurons to slow colonic transit. Activates the ClC-2 chloride channel is lubiprostone, which treats constipation rather than diarrhea. Activates guanylate cyclase C is linaclotide, which increases fluid secretion and would worsen diarrhea. Stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is prucalopride, a prokinetic for constipation. Blocks peripheral mu-opioid receptors would speed transit and treat opioid constipation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Serotonin 5-HT3-receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Alosetron blocks 5-HT3 receptors on visceral afferents and enteric neurons, slowing the left colon, and its use is limited by ischemic colitis and severe constipation (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: reserve it for women with severe refractory IBS with diarrhea who understand the risks.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Activates the ClC-2 chloride channel is lubiprostone,","C":"Activates guanylate cyclase C is linaclotide, which","D":"Stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is prucalopride, a","E":"Blocks peripheral mu-opioid receptors would speed transit"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q05","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":3,"stem":"A 78-year-old man is 3 days out from hip replacement surgery. His abdomen is markedly distended and tympanitic, and CT shows a dilated cecum and colon with no mechanical obstruction. His electrolytes have been corrected. He is given an intravenous drug on a cardiac monitor, and within minutes he passes flatus and stool. His heart rate falls from 82/min to 48/min, and he begins to salivate heavily. Which action of the drug explains both the colonic response and the side effects?","choices":{"A":"Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors","B":"Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors","C":"Activation of motilin receptors","D":"Inhibition of acetylcholinesterase","E":"Activation of colonic ClC-2 channels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Acute colonic distention without mechanical obstruction in a hospitalized patient is acute colonic pseudo-obstruction, or Ogilvie syndrome, which is treated with intravenous neostigmine. Neostigmine inhibits acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine accumulates and enhances gastric, small intestine, and colonic emptying, while the same excess acetylcholine causes salivation, nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, and bradycardia. Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors is the action of metoclopramide, which has no effect on colonic motility. Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is the action of prucalopride, an oral agent for chronic constipation, and it would not explain the salivation and bradycardia. Activation of motilin receptors is how erythromycin stimulates gastric emptying, not a treatment for colonic pseudo-obstruction. Activation of colonic ClC-2 channels is lubiprostone's mechanism, which works by increasing intestinal fluid secretion rather than by cholinergic stimulation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (cholinomimetic agents; metoclopramide and domperidone; serotonin 5-HT4-receptor agonists; macrolides; chloride channel activator).","boardPrep":"Step 1: intravenous neostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, treats acute colonic pseudo-obstruction, and its cholinergic effects include salivation and bradycardia (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: an older postoperative patient with massive colonic distention and no mechanical obstruction needs heart-rate monitoring during neostigmine therapy.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 4 → Obj 5 — enteric nervous system control of motility","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blockade of dopamine D2 receptors is the","B":"Agonism at serotonin 5-HT4 receptors is the","C":"Activation of motilin receptors is how erythromycin","E":"Activation of colonic ClC-2 channels is lubiprostone's"}},{"id":"trial_sdl37_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Explain the mechanism of action and clinical use of teduglutide in the management of short-bowel syndrome","batch":3,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman with short-bowel syndrome after multiple Crohn resections needs parenteral fluids 5 nights a week. She starts a daily subcutaneous analog of a peptide that ileal L cells secrete along with GLP-1, and over the next months her stoma output falls and she needs fewer infusions. Which mechanism best explains the lower stoma output?","choices":{"A":"Blocking CFTR chloride channels","B":"Activating mu-opioid receptors","C":"Enhanced intestinal fluid absorption","D":"Binding bile acids in the lumen","E":"Somatostatin inhibition of secretion"},"correct":"C","explanation":"An L-cell peptide analog co-secreted with GLP-1 and used daily for short-bowel syndrome is the GLP-2 analog teduglutide, whose release of trophic hormones from enteric neurons and endocrine cells enhances fluid absorption across the remaining mucosa. Blocking CFTR chloride channels is the action of crofelemer, an oral agent for HIV drug-related diarrhea. Activating mu-opioid receptors describes loperamide, an oral antidiarrheal that slows transit. Binding bile acids in the lumen describes cholestyramine, an oral resin for bile acid diarrhea. Somatostatin inhibition of secretion is how octreotide works, not a GLP-2 analog. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucagon-like peptide 2 analog for short-bowel syndrome; Antidiarrheal agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Teduglutide binds enteric neurons and endocrine cells, releasing trophic hormones that grow mucosa and enhance fluid absorption (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: success is measured by fewer days of parenteral support.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking CFTR chloride channels is the action","B":"Activating mu-opioid receptors describes loperamide, an oral","D":"Binding bile acids in the lumen describes","E":"Somatostatin inhibition of secretion is how octreotide"}},{"id":"grid_sdl37_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare and contrast the pharmacologic therapies for irritable bowel syndrome (IBS) based on the predominant symptom pattern, including IBS with diarrhea, IBS with constipation, and mixed-type IBS.","batch":4,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman has had 3 years of urgent loose stools with crampy lower abdominal pain relieved by defecation. Colonoscopy and celiac serologies are normal. Loperamide and dicyclomine did not control her symptoms. She is enrolled in a restricted prescribing program and begins a new drug. Six weeks later she has sudden left-sided abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea, and colonoscopy shows segmental mucosal edema and ulceration consistent with ischemic colitis. Which of the following sets of effects best characterizes the drug she was given?","grid":{"headers":["Colonic transit","Intestinal fluid secretion","Visceral pain"]},"choices":{"A":["↑","↑","↓"],"B":["↑","↑","↔"],"C":["↓","↔","↔"],"D":["↑","↔","↔"],"E":["↓","↔","↓"]},"correct":"E","explanation":"Choice E (↓ colonic transit, ↔ intestinal fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is correct. Alosetron is a selective 5-HT3 receptor antagonist approved only for women with severe diarrhea-predominant IBS refractory to conventional therapy. The notes describe blockade of 5-HT3 receptors on enteric cholinergic neurons, which slows colonic transit, and on visceral sensory afferents, which reduces pain, cramps, and urgency; severe constipation and ischemic colitis carry a black box warning and require a REMS program. Choice A (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↓ visceral pain) is linaclotide, a guanylyl cyclase-C agonist that raises cGMP and activates CFTR, accelerates colonic transit, and blunts visceral nociception in IBS-C. Choice B (↑ colonic transit, ↑ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is lubiprostone, which activates ClC-2 chloride channels to drive fluid secretion in IBS-C. Choice C (↓ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is loperamide, a mu-opioid agonist that slows transit and increases fluid reabsorption but does not relieve visceral pain. Choice D (↑ colonic transit, ↔ fluid secretion, ↔ visceral pain) is prucalopride, a 5-HT4 agonist that triggers high-amplitude propagating contractions in chronic constipation. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 37, sections 1 and 2: prokinetic agents and IBS therapy by subtype); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBS). The unchanged secretion for alosetron, loperamide, and prucalopride and the unchanged pain for lubiprostone and prucalopride are inferred because the notes describe no such effect.","boardPrep":"Step 1: alosetron (5-HT3 antagonist) for severe IBS-D in women; black box warning for ischemic colitis and severe constipation (Exam 3 notes, SDL 37). COMLEX: IBS-C secretagogues (linaclotide, lubiprostone) cause diarrhea; IBS-D agents (alosetron, eluxadoline, loperamide) cause constipation.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 3 — CFTR-mediated Cl⁻ and water secretion","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl37_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":1,"stem":"Bethanechol stimulates gastrointestinal smooth muscle by which action?","choices":{"A":"Blocking the dopamine D2 receptor","B":"Agonism at the muscarinic receptor","C":"Inhibiting acetylcholinesterase","D":"Opening the motilin receptor","E":"Blocking the 5-HT3 receptor"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Bethanechol is a direct muscarinic agonist that contracts gastrointestinal smooth muscle. Dopamine D2 blockade is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide and domperidone. Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is the action of neostigmine, which raises endogenous acetylcholine. Motilin-receptor stimulation is the action of erythromycin. 5-HT3 blockade is the antidiarrheal action of alosetron, not a prokinetic muscarinic effect.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dopamine D2 blockade is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide and domperidone","C":"Acetylcholinesterase inhibition is the action of neostigmine, which raises endogenous acetylcholine","D":"Motilin-receptor stimulation is the action of erythromycin","E":"5-HT3 blockade is the antidiarrheal action of alosetron, not a prokinetic muscarinic effect"}},{"id":"sdl37_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Describe the mechanisms of action, clinical uses, and important adverse effects of medications (prokinetic and cholinomimetic agents) used to stimulate gastrointestinal motility.","batch":2,"stem":"A 58-year-old man vomits blood an hour after the onset of hematemesis. He is tachycardic, and the abdomen is soft. Hemoglobin is 9.1 g/dL (reference range: 13.5–17.5 g/dL) and the urea nitrogen is 32 mg/dL (reference range: 7–20 mg/dL). The stomach is full of clot, and endoscopy is planned urgently. Which drug is given intravenously beforehand to clear blood by triggering migrating motor complexes?","choices":{"A":"Octreotide as a somatostatin analog only","B":"Infliximab","C":"Sulfasalazine","D":"Lactulose","E":"Erythromycin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Intravenous erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and is used to clear blood clot from the stomach before emergency endoscopy. Octreotide lowers splanchnic flow in variceal bleeding and is not the motilin agonist used to empty clot. Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease. Sulfasalazine is a 5-aminosalicylate prodrug for colitis. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative used for constipation and hepatic encephalopathy.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Octreotide lowers splanchnic flow in variceal bleeding and is not the motilin agonist used to empty clot","B":"Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease","C":"Sulfasalazine is a 5-aminosalicylate prodrug for colitis","D":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative used for constipation and hepatic encephalopathy"}}]},{"sdlNumber":38,"title":"SDL 38 — Pharmacology of laxatives, antidiarrheals, and IBD","questions":[{"id":"sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Docusate softens stool mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Lowering surface tension","B":"Drawing water in by osmosis","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel","E":"Opening apical ion channels"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Docusate is a stool surfactant that lets water and lipids penetrate the stool; it is often given to hospitalized patients to minimize straining. Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxatives such as PEG and lactulose, not docusate. Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not docusate. Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as psyllium, not the surfactant docusate. Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as lubiprostone and linaclotide, not docusate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: emollient laxatives are surfactants that promote water entry into stool (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: glycerin suppositories are another stool surfactant option given rectally (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Drawing water in by osmosis is the action of osmotic laxatives such as PEG and lactulose, not docusate.","C":"Stimulating enteric neurons is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, not docusate.","D":"Forming a bulky fiber gel describes bulk laxatives such as psyllium, not the surfactant docusate.","E":"Opening apical ion channels describes secretagogues such as lubiprostone and linaclotide, not docusate."}},{"id":"sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":5,"stem":"A woman has used an anthraquinone laxative daily for years. Which colonoscopy finding is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Pseudomembranes","B":"Cobblestone mucosa","C":"Melanosis coli","D":"Crypt abscesses","E":"Angiodysplasia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Aloe, senna and cascara are hydrolyzed in the colon; chronic use causes brown colonic pigmentation called melanosis coli, without a proven link to colorectal cancer. Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic anthraquinone use. Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxative use. Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative colitis, not anthraquinone pigmentation. Chronic anthraquinone use causes brown pigmentation of the colon (melanosis coli), not angiodysplasia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anthraquinone Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives act by enteric nerve stimulation and colonic contraction, with diarrhea as an adverse effect (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: oral anthraquinones act in 6 to 12 hours and rectal doses within 2 hours (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pseudomembranes indicate C. difficile colitis, not chronic anthraquinone use.","B":"Cobblestone mucosa is a feature of Crohn disease, not laxative use.","D":"Crypt abscesses indicate active colitis such as ulcerative colitis, not anthraquinone pigmentation.","E":"Chronic anthraquinone use causes brown pigmentation of the colon (melanosis coli), not angiodysplasia."}},{"id":"sdl38_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term use of mineral oil as a lubricant laxative can cause which nutritional problem?","choices":{"A":"Copper deficiency anemia","B":"Iron overload in the liver","C":"Zinc deficiency rash","D":"Fat-soluble vitamin loss","E":"Excess vitamin C levels"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and retards water absorption; long-term use impairs absorption of vitamins A, D, E and K, and aspiration can cause lipid pneumonitis. Mineral oil lowers absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K); it does not raise water-soluble vitamin C levels. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Stool Surfactant Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K depends on bile emulsification, and mineral oil intake can cause their deficiency (First Aid Biochemistry, fat-soluble vitamins). COMLEX: mineral oil is used for fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 5 — intestinal absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and calcium","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Copper deficiency is not its nutritional risk.","B":"Mineral oil reduces absorption of fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K rather than causing hepatic iron overload.","C":"Zinc deficiency is not its nutritional risk.","E":"Mineral oil lowers absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K); it does not raise water-soluble vitamin C levels."}},{"id":"sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Why do diphenoxylate tablets contain a small amount of atropine?","choices":{"A":"Enhancing pain relief","B":"Preventing nausea","C":"Discouraging abuse","D":"Delaying tolerance","E":"Speeding absorption"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Diphenoxylate is a prescription opioid agonist with CNS effects and dependence at higher doses; 0.025 mg atropine is added to discourage overdosage and may add to the antidiarrheal action. It is not added for pain relief, nausea prevention, delayed tolerance, or faster absorption. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid Agonists). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: diphenoxylate is a mu agonist that slows gut motility with constipation and nausea as adverse effects (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: opioids slow colonic transit by inhibiting presynaptic cholinergic nerves in the enteric plexuses (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate has no analgesic properties at standard doses.","B":"The atropine dose is not an antiemetic.","D":"The atropine is not added to delay opioid tolerance.","E":"The atropine is not there to speed absorption."}},{"id":"sdl38_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":5,"stem":"Long-term somatostatin-analog therapy for acromegaly most commonly causes which problem?","choices":{"A":"Kidney calcium oxalate stones","B":"Severe symptomatic hypercalcemia","C":"Sustained sinus tachycardia","D":"Hyperthyroid storm crisis","E":"Gallbladder sludge or stones"},"correct":"E","explanation":"By inhibiting gallbladder contractility and altering fat absorption, long-term octreotide causes sludge or gallstones in over 50% of patients; it can also cause steatorrhea, hyperglycemia, hypothyroidism and bradycardia. Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia. Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid storm. Kidney stones are not a listed octreotide effect. Hypercalcemia is not a listed octreotide effect; its endocrine effects are hyper- or hypoglycemia and hypothyroidism. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: somatostatin analogs raise cholelithiasis risk through CCK inhibition (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: a monthly intramuscular depot form of the somatostatin analog is available (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"C":"Octreotide causes bradycardia, not tachycardia.","D":"Octreotide is associated with hypothyroidism, not thyroid storm.","A":"Kidney stones are not a listed octreotide effect.","B":"Hypercalcemia is not a listed octreotide effect; its endocrine effects are hyper- or hypoglycemia and hypothyroidism."}},{"id":"sdl38_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Colestipol for chronic diarrhea should be taken at least 2 hours apart from other medicines. Why?","choices":{"A":"It induces hepatic CYP3A4","B":"It binds many oral drugs","C":"It raises gastric pH","D":"It blocks P-glycoprotein","E":"It speeds gastric emptying"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cholestyramine and colestipol bind many drugs and reduce their absorption, so they are separated from other drugs by 2 hours; colesevelam has little effect on drug absorption. Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not induce hepatic CYP3A4. Raising intragastric pH is how antacids alter drug dissolution, whereas colestipol is a resin that binds drugs. Enhanced gastric emptying is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide and domperidone, not colestipol. Katzung states that cholestyramine and colestipol bind a number of drugs and reduce their absorption, so they should not be given within 2 hours of other drugs. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins cause GI upset and decreased absorption of other drugs (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin adverse effects include bloating, flatulence, constipation and fecal impaction (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Colestipol is a resin that is not absorbed, so it does not induce hepatic CYP3A4.","C":"Raising intragastric pH is how antacids alter drug dissolution, whereas colestipol is a resin that binds drugs.","E":"Enhanced gastric emptying is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide and domperidone, not colestipol.","D":"Katzung states that cholestyramine and colestipol bind a number of drugs and reduce their absorption, so they should not be given within 2 hours of other drugs."}},{"id":"sdl38_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":5,"stem":"Sulfasalazine releases active 5-aminosalicylic acid in the colon through which process?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial azoreductase cleavage","B":"Hepatic CYP450 oxidation of it","C":"Gastric acid hydrolysis of the bond","D":"Pancreatic esterase cleavage","E":"pH-dependent resin release"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine by an azo bond; colonic bacteria cleave it with azoreductase, releasing 5-ASA in the terminal ileum and colon. pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, whereas sulfasalazine is an azo compound cleaved by colonic bacteria. Sulfasalazine is not activated by hepatic CYP450; its azo bond is cleaved by bacterial azoreductase in the terminal ileum and colon. Gastric acid does not split the azo bond; the azo structure keeps the parent drug intact and poorly absorbed until colonic bacteria cleave it. Pancreatic esterases do not release 5-ASA from sulfasalazine; resident colonic bacteria cleave its azo (N=N) bond. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine combines antibacterial sulfapyridine with anti-inflammatory 5-ASA and is activated by colonic bacteria (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: mesalamine products instead use timed-release granules or pH-sensitive coatings (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"E":"pH-dependent release describes delayed-release mesalamine, whereas sulfasalazine is an azo compound cleaved by colonic bacteria.","B":"Sulfasalazine is not activated by hepatic CYP450.","C":"Gastric acid does not split the azo bond; the azo structure keeps the parent drug intact and poorly absorbed until colonic bacteria cleave it.","D":"Pancreatic esterases do not release 5-ASA from sulfasalazine; resident colonic bacteria cleave its azo (N=N) bond."}},{"id":"sdl38_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":5,"stem":"A patient starting sulfasalazine for ulcerative colitis should take which daily supplement?","choices":{"A":"Vitamin B6","B":"Vitamin D3","C":"Iron sulfate","D":"Folic acid","E":"Zinc salts"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Sulfasalazine impairs folate absorption and processing, so 1 mg/d folic acid is recommended. Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent its neuropathy, not with sulfasalazine. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: sulfasalazine adverse effects include malaise, nausea, sulfonamide toxicity and reversible oligospermia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: slow acetylators of sulfapyridine have more frequent and severe adverse effects (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6) is given with isoniazid to prevent its neuropathy, not with sulfasalazine.","B":"Vitamin D3 is not part of that answer.","C":"Iron sulfate is not part of that answer.","E":"Zinc salts are not part of that answer."}},{"id":"sdl38_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":5,"stem":"Before starting azathioprine for Crohn disease, which test helps predict severe marrow toxicity?","choices":{"A":"G6PD enzyme activity","B":"TPMT enzyme activity","C":"HLA-B*5701 typing","D":"Serum ferritin level","E":"CYP2C19 genotype"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Thiopurine methyltransferase catabolizes 6-MP; activity is low in 11% and absent in 0.3% of people, raising active thioguanine nucleotides and marrow suppression risk. G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not thiopurine marrow toxicity. HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hypersensitivity, not before azathioprine. CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, not thiopurine toxicity. Serum ferritin reflects stored body iron, not the TPMT catabolism of thiopurines that predicts marrow toxicity. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine is an antimetabolite precursor of 6-mercaptopurine that causes pancytopenia (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: complete blood counts and liver tests are monitored in every patient on purine analogs (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"G6PD activity predicts oxidant hemolysis of red cells, not thiopurine marrow toxicity.","C":"HLA-B*5701 screening is done before abacavir to prevent hypersensitivity, not before azathioprine.","E":"CYP2C19 genotype predicts poor activation of clopidogrel, not thiopurine toxicity.","D":"Serum ferritin reflects stored body iron, not the TPMT catabolism of thiopurines that predicts marrow toxicity."}},{"id":"sdl38_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":5,"stem":"A 74-year-old man with stage 4 chronic kidney disease has taken milk of magnesia nightly for months. He now has weakness, absent reflexes and bradycardia. Which disturbance is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Hypercalcemia","B":"Hypokalemia","C":"Hypermagnesemia","D":"Hyperphosphatemia","E":"Hypophosphatemia"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Magnesium hydroxide should not be used for long periods in renal insufficiency because of the risk of hypermagnesemia. Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium phosphate preparations. Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, not of magnesium hydroxide. Hypercalcemia is the risk of excess calcium carbonate taken with dairy products (milk-alkali syndrome), not of magnesium hydroxide. Phosphate disturbances belong to sodium phosphate preparations, which cause hyperphosphatemia. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: magnesium-containing laxatives provide an osmotic load and can cause diarrhea and dehydration (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: the colon neither concentrates nor dilutes fecal fluid, so nonabsorbed solute obligates stool water (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"B":"Hypokalemia is listed among the electrolyte risks of sodium phosphate preparations.","D":"Hyperphosphatemia is a risk of sodium phosphate purgatives, not of magnesium hydroxide.","A":"Hypercalcemia is the risk of excess calcium carbonate taken with dairy products (milk-alkali syndrome), not of magnesium hydroxide.","E":"Phosphate disturbances belong to sodium phosphate preparations, which cause hyperphosphatemia."}},{"id":"sdl38_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":5,"stem":"A frail 79-year-old woman with mild renal insufficiency takes sodium phosphate tablets for colonoscopy preparation. A week later her creatinine has doubled. Biopsy would most likely show which change?","choices":{"A":"Urate nephropathy","B":"Nephrocalcinosis","C":"Oxalate nephropathy","D":"Myoglobin casts","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sodium phosphate can cause hyperphosphatemia and hypocalcemia, and acute renal failure from tubular calcium phosphate deposition (nephrocalcinosis); it is avoided in frail, elderly or renal patients. Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collecting ducts, typical of treated leukemia or lymphoma, not phosphate preparations. Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glycol poisoning, not sodium phosphate exposure. Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones proteins in multiple myeloma, not bowel preparation. Sodium phosphate injures the kidney through tubular deposition of calcium phosphate (nephrocalcinosis), not myoglobin casts. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic Laxatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: osmotic agents draw water into the lumen and can be misused by patients with bulimia (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: balanced isotonic lavage solutions avoid intravascular fluid and electrolyte shifts during preparation (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Urate nephropathy is uric acid crystal obstruction of collecting ducts, typical of treated leukemia or lymphoma, not phosphate preparations.","C":"Oxalate crystals in the tubules are typical of ethylene glycol poisoning, not sodium phosphate exposure.","E":"Light-chain cast nephropathy results from Bence-Jones proteins in multiple myeloma, not bowel preparation.","D":"Sodium phosphate injures the kidney through tubular deposition of calcium phosphate (nephrocalcinosis), not myoglobin casts."}},{"id":"sdl38_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"An 83-year-old bed-bound nursing home resident has had no bowel movement for 4 days. Staff want a stimulant laxative that works within an hour. Which is most appropriate?","choices":{"A":"Oral senna tablets","B":"Oral psyllium powder","C":"Oral docusate capsule","D":"Bisacodyl suppository","E":"Oral lactulose syrup"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bisacodyl, a diphenylmethane stimulant, works in 30 to 60 minutes rectally versus 6 to 10 hours orally, with minimal systemic absorption. Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an hour like rectal bisacodyl. Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts slowly. Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not a fast-acting stimulant. Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does not act within an hour. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Diphenylmethane Derivatives). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: stimulant laxatives cause colonic contraction through enteric nerve stimulation (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: long-term stimulant use is probably safe and may be needed in neurologically impaired patients (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oral senna is a stimulant but acts over hours, not within an hour like rectal bisacodyl.","B":"Psyllium is a bulk-forming fiber, not a stimulant, and acts slowly.","C":"Docusate is a stool softener used to prevent straining, not a fast-acting stimulant.","E":"Lactulose is an osmotic laxative, not a stimulant, and does not act within an hour."}},{"id":"sdl38_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":5,"stem":"A 41-year-old man had 90 cm of terminal ileum resected for Crohn disease. He now has watery postprandial diarrhea without steatorrhea. Which drug best treats it?","choices":{"A":"Cholestyramine","B":"Pancrelipase","C":"Ondansetron","D":"Diphenoxylate","E":"Metronidazole tablets"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Ileal disease or resection causes malabsorbed bile salts that trigger colonic secretory diarrhea; bile salt-binding resins reduce it, though they can worsen fat malabsorption if the bile pool is depleted. Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bind the malabsorbed bile acids driving this secretory diarrhea. Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, not bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherapy and postoperative emesis and does not bind bile acids. Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive protozoa, which do not cause bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Bile Salt-Binding Resins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 38 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bile acid resins prevent intestinal reabsorption of bile acids, prompting the liver to use cholesterol to make more (First Aid Cardiovascular Pharmacology, lipid-lowering agents). COMLEX: resin powders are taken one to three times daily before meals (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"D":"Diphenoxylate slows transit nonspecifically but does not bind the malabsorbed bile acids driving this secretory diarrhea.","B":"Pancrelipase replaces enzymes for exocrine pancreatic insufficiency with steatorrhea, not bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection.","C":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist antiemetic for chemotherapy and postoperative emesis and does not bind bile acids.","E":"Metronidazole targets anaerobic bacteria and sensitive protozoa, which do not cause bile acid diarrhea after ileal resection."}},{"id":"sdl38_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with metastatic carcinoid tumor has flushing, wheezing and voluminous watery diarrhea. Her liver lesions cannot be removed surgically. Which drug best controls her symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Diphenoxylate","B":"Octreotide","C":"Bismuth","D":"Lubiprostone","E":"Prednisone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Octreotide inhibits hormone secretion by carcinoid and VIPoma tumors, reducing secretory diarrhea and flushing, and may slow tumor progression. Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormone secretion or control carcinoid flushing. Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and would worsen diarrhea. Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infectious diarrhea and does not inhibit carcinoid hormone secretion. Katzung states that for advanced carcinoid tumors that cannot be resected, octreotide decreases secretory diarrhea and systemic symptoms such as flushing and wheezing by inhibiting hormonal secretion. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: carcinoid syndrome is diagnosed by urinary 5-HIAA and treated with a somatostatin analog (First Aid Endocrine, carcinoid syndrome). COMLEX: higher subcutaneous doses of 100 to 250 mcg inhibit motility in dumping or vagotomy diarrhea (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Diphenoxylate slows transit but does not inhibit tumor hormone secretion or control carcinoid flushing.","D":"Lubiprostone stimulates intestinal chloride secretion and would worsen diarrhea.","C":"Bismuth subsalicylate reduces stool frequency in acute infectious diarrhea and does not inhibit carcinoid hormone secretion.","E":"Katzung states that for advanced carcinoid tumors that cannot be resected, octreotide decreases secretory diarrhea and systemic symptoms such as flushing and wheezing by inhibiting hormonal secretion."}},{"id":"sdl38_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has bloody diarrhea, a temperature of 39.4°C and abdominal tenderness after eating undercooked chicken. Why should loperamide be avoided?","choices":{"A":"Risk of central opioid dependence","B":"Risk of respiratory depression in adults","C":"Risk of severe serotonin syndrome","D":"Risk of worsened underlying illness","E":"Risk of tardive movement disorder"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Antidiarrheals are safe for mild to moderate acute diarrhea but should not be used with bloody diarrhea, high fever or systemic toxicity because they may worsen the underlying condition. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so central dependence is not the concern. Loperamide does not cross the blood-brain barrier, so respiratory depression is not the concern. Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions such as SSRIs with MAOIs, not from loperamide. Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blockade by drugs such as metoclopramide, not opioid antidiarrheals like loperamide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Antidiarrheal Agents). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: peripheral mu agonists have poor CNS penetration and low addictive potential (First Aid Gastrointestinal Pharmacology). COMLEX: antidiarrheals are stopped when diarrhea worsens despite therapy (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 31 → Obj 1 — bacterial enterocolitis, including Yersinia mimicking appendicitis","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Central dependence is not the concern.","B":"Respiratory depression is not the concern.","C":"Serotonin syndrome arises from serotonergic interactions such as SSRIs with MAOIs, not from loperamide.","E":"Tardive dyskinesia follows prolonged central dopamine blockade by drugs such as metoclopramide, not opioid antidiarrheals like loperamide."}},{"id":"sdl38_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with Crohn disease is maintained on azathioprine. She is started on allopurinol for gout and 3 weeks later has a white cell count of 1,100/µL (reference range: 4,500–11,000/µL). What explains this?","choices":{"A":"Reduced renal drug excretion","B":"Increased drug absorption","C":"Reduced 6-MP breakdown","D":"Allopurinol marrow allergy","E":"Folate antagonism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase catabolism of the purine analogs, increasing active 6-thioguanine nucleotides and causing severe leukopenia. Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase breakdown of azathioprine. Renal excretion clears only small amounts of unchanged azathioprine and 6-MP; most of the drug is inactivated by xanthine oxidase to 6-thiouric acid, the step allopurinol blocks. Azathioprine is already well absorbed from the gut; allopurinol raises its toxicity by blocking xanthine oxidase catabolism of 6-MP. This is predictable dose-related toxicity, not allergy: with allopurinol the azathioprine dose must be cut to one-fourth to one-third to prevent excess marrow suppression. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Purine Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 6-MP is degraded by xanthine oxidase, so its toxicity rises with allopurinol (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: febuxostat raises toxicity the same way, and purine-analog benefit in IBD has a median delay of 17 weeks because thioguanine nucleotides accumulate slowly (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"E":"Folate antagonism is a sulfasalazine concern, whereas allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase breakdown of azathioprine.","A":"Renal excretion clears only small amounts of unchanged azathioprine and 6-MP; most of the drug is inactivated by xanthine oxidase to 6-thiouric acid, the step allopurinol blocks.","B":"Azathioprine is already well absorbed from the gut; allopurinol raises its toxicity by blocking xanthine oxidase catabolism of 6-MP.","D":"This is predictable dose-related toxicity, not allergy: with allopurinol the azathioprine dose must be cut to one-fourth to one-third to prevent excess marrow suppression."}},{"id":"sdl38_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 31-year-old man with fistulizing Crohn disease who emigrated from the Philippines will begin infliximab. Which test must be done first?","choices":{"A":"Latent tuberculosis test","B":"Serum ceruloplasmin level","C":"Hepatitis A antibody titer","D":"Stool culture panel","E":"Repeat colonic biopsy"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anti-TNF therapy suppresses the TH1 response; all patients need a tuberculin skin test or interferon gamma release assay first, and positive patients get prophylaxis before therapy. Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B mutation and is not a screen required before anti-TNF therapy. Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepatitis A, alongside the mandatory tuberculosis screen. Katzung states that all patients must undergo tuberculin skin testing or an interferon gamma release assay before anti-TNF therapy because latent tuberculosis can reactivate and disseminate, not a stool culture panel. Katzung states that all patients must undergo tuberculin skin testing or an interferon gamma release assay before anti-TNF therapy because latent tuberculosis can reactivate and disseminate, not a repeat colonic biopsy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-Tumor Necrosis Factor Therapy). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF is important in granuloma formation and stabilization, and anti-TNF drugs can cause drug-induced lupus (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: serious infection risk rises markedly with concurrent corticosteroids (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Serum ceruloplasmin is low in Wilson disease from ATP7B mutation and is not a screen required before anti-TNF therapy.","C":"Anti-TNF therapy risks reactivation of hepatitis B, not hepatitis A, alongside the mandatory tuberculosis screen.","D":"Katzung states that all patients must undergo tuberculin skin testing or an interferon gamma release assay before anti-TNF therapy because latent tuberculosis can reactivate and disseminate, not a stool culture panel.","E":"Katzung states that all patients must undergo tuberculin skin testing or an interferon gamma release assay before anti-TNF therapy because latent tuberculosis can reactivate and disseminate, not a repeat colonic biopsy."}},{"id":"sdl38_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old woman has mild to moderate Crohn disease of the ileum and ascending colon. She wants a glucocorticoid with fewer systemic adverse effects. Which is best?","choices":{"A":"Oral prednisone at high dose","B":"Hydrocortisone retention enema","C":"IV methylprednisolone","D":"Oral dexamethasone daily","E":"Controlled-release budesonide"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Budesonide has high glucocorticoid receptor affinity but extensive CYP3A4 first-pass metabolism; the distal ileum and colon release form treats mild to moderate ileocolonic Crohn disease with less systemic toxicity. High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the systemic adverse effects she wants to avoid. Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot reach the ileum and ascending colon. IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease, not a low-systemic option for mild disease. Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone, so it does not limit systemic effects. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Glucocorticoids). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: glucocorticoids inactivate NF-kB and inhibit synthesis of almost all cytokines (First Aid Immunology (immunosuppressants and therapeutic antibodies)). COMLEX: potent CYP3A4 inhibitors raise plasma levels of the controlled-release steroid severalfold (Katzung Ch. 62).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"High-dose oral prednisone is effective but causes the systemic adverse effects she wants to avoid.","B":"Hydrocortisone enemas treat distal colonic flares and cannot reach the ileum and ascending colon.","C":"IV methylprednisolone is systemic therapy for severe disease, not a low-systemic option for mild disease.","D":"Dexamethasone is a potent systemic glucocorticoid with about 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone, so it does not limit systemic effects."}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 61-year-old man with alcohol-related cirrhosis is brought in confused after 5 days of constipation. He has asterixis. He is given an oral syrup of a nonabsorbable disaccharide titrated to 2 to 3 soft stools a day, with cramps and flatus, and his mental status clears over 2 days. Which mechanism best explains his neurologic improvement?","choices":{"A":"Direct stimulation of enteric nerve plexuses","B":"Surfactant softening of the stool mass","C":"Colonic acids trapping ammonia as ammonium","D":"Blockade of peripheral mu-opioid receptors","E":"Coating of colonic mucosa against toxins"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Confusion and asterixis in cirrhosis triggered by constipation is hepatic encephalopathy, and lactulose is fermented by colonic bacteria to acids such as lactic and acetic acid that trap ammonia as ammonium for excretion, while its osmotic effect and the fermentation also explain his stools, cramps, and flatus. Direct stimulation of enteric nerve plexuses is the action of stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl, which do not lower ammonia. Surfactant softening of the stool mass describes docusate and has no effect on ammonia handling. Blockade of peripheral mu-opioid receptors treats opioid constipation and is not how this syrup works. Coating of colonic mucosa against toxins describes bismuth, not a nonabsorbable sugar. Sources: First Aid 2025 GI (Laxatives; Hepatic encephalopathy); Katzung Ch. 62 (Osmotic laxatives).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lactulose is an osmotic laxative that colonic bacteria ferment to acids, trapping NH4+ in the colon for excretion in hepatic encephalopathy (First Aid, Laxatives). COMLEX: constipation is a common trigger of encephalopathy in cirrhosis, so titrate lactulose to soft stools.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 5 — osmotic versus secretory diarrhea","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Direct stimulation of enteric nerve plexuses is","B":"Surfactant softening of the stool mass describes","D":"Blockade of peripheral mu-opioid receptors treats opioid","E":"Coating of colonic mucosa against toxins describes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":3,"stem":"A 67-year-old woman with metastatic breast cancer takes scheduled oral morphine for bone pain. She has hard stools every 5 days despite senna and polyethylene glycol. She starts a subcutaneous drug every other day and has a bowel movement within hours, yet her pain control is unchanged. Which property of this drug explains the preserved analgesia?","choices":{"A":"It is a partial agonist at central mu receptors","B":"It acts by drawing water into the colon","C":"It activates guanylate cyclase in the gut","D":"It stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors","E":"It barely crosses the blood-brain barrier"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Opioid-induced constipation refractory to laxatives in a palliative care patient is treated with methylnaltrexone, a peripheral mu-opioid antagonist that blocks intestinal mu receptors but does not readily cross the blood-brain barrier, so central analgesia is preserved. It is a partial agonist at central mu receptors would itself act in the brain and could alter analgesia. It acts by drawing water into the colon describes osmotic laxatives, which had already failed. It activates guanylate cyclase in the gut describes linaclotide and does not explain an opioid-specific response. It stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors describes prucalopride, an oral prokinetic. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Opioid receptor antagonists).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Peripheral mu antagonists such as methylnaltrexone, naloxegol, and naldemedine relieve opioid constipation without blocking central analgesia because they do not readily cross the blood-brain barrier (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: consider them when laxatives fail in patients who need continued opioids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 25 → Obj 5 — defecation reflex and anal sphincter control","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is a partial agonist at central","B":"It acts by drawing water into the","C":"It activates guanylate cyclase in the gut","D":"It stimulates serotonin 5-HT4 receptors describes prucalopride,"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q03","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man develops watery diarrhea on a trip to Mexico and takes large doses of an over-the-counter pink liquid. His stools improve, but he presents worried because his stools and tongue have turned black and his ears are ringing. Hemoglobin is normal and stool guaiac is negative. How did this preparation reduce his stool liquidity?","choices":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the colonic lumen","B":"Inhibiting gut prostaglandin and chloride secretion","C":"Activating enteric mu-opioid receptors to slow transit","D":"Inhibiting release of gut hormones from D cells","E":"Blocking muscarinic receptors on intestinal smooth muscle"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Black stool and tongue with normal hemoglobin and a negative guaiac, plus tinnitus after large doses, point to bismuth subsalicylate, whose salicylate component reduces stool frequency and liquidity by inhibiting intestinal prostaglandin and chloride secretion, while bismuth also binds enterotoxins. Binding bile acids in the colonic lumen describes cholestyramine and related resins. Activating enteric mu-opioid receptors to slow transit is the action of loperamide, which does not blacken stool. Inhibiting release of gut hormones from D cells misstates octreotide, which mimics the D-cell hormone somatostatin. Blocking muscarinic receptors on intestinal smooth muscle describes antispasmodics such as dicyclomine. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Colloidal bismuth compounds).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bismuth subsalicylate treats traveler diarrhea; salicylate inhibits intestinal prostaglandin and chloride secretion, and bismuth causes harmless black stool and tongue (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: high doses can cause salicylate toxicity, and black stool from bismuth can mimic GI bleeding.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 27 → Obj 3 — enterohepatic circulation and terminal ileal bile salt loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Binding bile acids in the colonic lumen","C":"Activating enteric mu-opioid receptors to slow transit","D":"Inhibiting release of gut hormones from D","E":"Blocking muscarinic receptors on intestinal smooth muscle"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":3,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman has 4 L per day of watery diarrhea that persists while fasting. Potassium is 2.6 mEq/L (reference range: 3.5–5.0 mEq/L). CT shows a mass in the pancreatic tail with multiple liver metastases that cannot be resected. She starts subcutaneous injections of a synthetic octapeptide, and her stool volume falls sharply. Which mechanism best explains the response?","choices":{"A":"Binding of luminal bile acids","B":"Opioid slowing of colonic transit","C":"Blocking muscarinic receptors on muscle","D":"Inhibiting tumor hormone release","E":"Coating of the intestinal mucosa"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Fasting secretory diarrhea with hypokalemia and a pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor suggests a VIPoma, and octreotide, a somatostatin analog, decreases secretory diarrhea in unresectable carcinoid and VIP-secreting tumors by inhibiting their hormone secretion and by reducing intestinal fluid secretion. Binding of luminal bile acids describes resins for bile acid diarrhea, which is not secretory diarrhea from a tumor. Opioid slowing of colonic transit is how loperamide works and would not stop tumor hormone release. Blocking muscarinic receptors on muscle is the antispasmodic mechanism, which does little for secretory diarrhea. Coating of the intestinal mucosa describes bismuth compounds, not a somatostatin analog. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Octreotide).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Octreotide inhibits secretion of gastrin, VIP, serotonin, and other hormones and reduces intestinal fluid secretion, controlling diarrhea from carcinoid and VIPoma (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: long-term use can cause gallbladder sludge and stones and glucose changes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Binding of luminal bile acids describes resins","B":"Opioid slowing of colonic transit is how","C":"Blocking muscarinic receptors on muscle is the","E":"Coating of the intestinal mucosa describes bismuth"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q05","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 29-year-old man has had ulcerative colitis in remission for 2 years on a drug that colonic bacteria split by azo-bond cleavage. He has nausea, headache, and malaise, and semen analysis for infertility shows oligospermia. He is a slow acetylator. Which change would most likely relieve these effects while keeping colonic anti-inflammatory therapy?","choices":{"A":"Switch to a mesalamine preparation","B":"Add oral folic acid supplementation","C":"Switch to long-term oral prednisone","D":"Add daily loperamide to his regimen","E":"Add allopurinol to the current dose"},"correct":"A","explanation":"An azo-bonded drug cleaved by colonic bacteria in ulcerative colitis is sulfasalazine, and most of its adverse effects, including nausea, headache, malaise, and reversible oligospermia, come from the absorbed sulfapyridine carrier and are worse in slow acetylators, so switching to a mesalamine preparation keeps 5-ASA delivery without sulfapyridine. Add oral folic acid supplementation addresses impaired folate absorption with sulfasalazine but does not remove sulfapyridine toxicity. Switch to long-term oral prednisone is inappropriate because glucocorticoids are not effective maintenance therapy and cause serious long-term toxicity. Add daily loperamide to his regimen treats diarrhea, not drug toxicity, and he is in remission. Add allopurinol to the current dose does nothing for sulfapyridine toxicity and has no role in colitis therapy. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Aminosalicylates; Glucocorticoids).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfasalazine links 5-ASA to sulfapyridine; adverse effects, including reversible oligospermia, are mainly due to sulfapyridine and are worse in slow acetylators (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: patients intolerant of sulfasalazine can usually take mesalamine.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Add oral folic acid supplementation addresses impaired","C":"Switch to long-term oral prednisone is inappropriate","D":"Add daily loperamide to his regimen treats","E":"Add allopurinol to the current dose does"}},{"id":"trial_sdl38_q06","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":3,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with moderate to severe ulcerative colitis responded to IV infusions of a chimeric anti-TNF antibody but took them only during flares, months apart. At her latest infusion she had fever, urticaria, and chest tightness, and her colitis no longer responds. Which strategy would most likely have prevented this problem?","choices":{"A":"Giving the infusions more slowly each time","B":"Switching to oral sulfasalazine maintenance","C":"Adding loperamide during flares of colitis","D":"Lowering the dose at each later infusion","E":"Scheduled dosing with a thiopurine added"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Loss of response with infusion reactions to infliximab points to antibodies against the drug, which are much more likely with episodic therapy than with regularly scheduled infusions and less likely when an immunomodulator such as 6-MP, azathioprine, or methotrexate is given at the same time. Giving the infusions more slowly each time can lessen acute reactions but does not stop antibody formation or restore response. Switching to oral sulfasalazine maintenance does not address antidrug antibodies and is weaker for moderate to severe disease. Adding loperamide during flares of colitis treats symptoms only and is risky in severe colitis. Lowering the dose at each later infusion would not prevent antibodies and could hasten loss of response. Sources: Katzung Ch. 62 (Anti-TNF therapy; Purine analogs).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antibodies to anti-TNF agents cause loss of response and infusion reactions; they are more common with episodic dosing and reduced by concomitant immunomodulators (Katzung Ch. 62). COMLEX: give anti-TNF therapy on a schedule, often with a thiopurine, after screening for latent TB.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 32 → Obj 2 — differentiating the inflammatory bowel diseases","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Giving the infusions more slowly each time","B":"Switching to oral sulfasalazine maintenance does not","C":"Adding loperamide during flares of colitis treats","D":"Lowering the dose at each later infusion"}},{"id":"grid_sdl38_q01","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":4,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has moderate-to-severe ileocolonic Crohn disease. She has needed 3 courses of prednisone in the past year and has a flare each time the dose is tapered. Her gastroenterologist starts an oral steroid-sparing drug and explains that full benefit may take several months, and that an injectable biologic might later be combined with it partly to prevent antibodies against the biologic. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug started?","grid":{"headers":["Disease activity treated","Maintains remission","Marrow suppression"]},"choices":{"A":["Mild–moderate","Yes","Yes"],"B":["Moderate–severe","No","No"],"C":["Moderate–severe","Yes","No"],"D":["Moderate–severe","Yes","Yes"],"E":["Mild–moderate","No","No"]},"correct":"D","explanation":"Choice D (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, marrow suppression yes) is correct. Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine are prodrugs converted to 6-thioguanine nucleotides that inhibit purine synthesis and promote T-cell apoptosis. The notes describe their use to induce and maintain remission in moderate-to-severe UC and Crohn disease, allowing steroids to be reduced, with onset delayed by up to 17 weeks and frequent combination with anti-TNF biologics to suppress anti-drug antibodies. Bone marrow suppression (leukopenia) is the key toxicity and becomes life-threatening with allopurinol. Choice A (mild–moderate disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression yes) is sulfasalazine, first-line for inducing and maintaining remission in mild-to-moderate UC, whose sulfonamide component causes myelosuppression. Choice B (moderate–severe disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is systemic prednisone, which induces rapid remission but does not maintain it and causes adrenal suppression, osteoporosis, and hyperglycemia. Choice C (moderate–severe disease, maintains remission, myelosuppression no) is an anti-TNF antibody such as infliximab, given by infusion or injection for refractory disease; its risks are infusion reactions and latent tuberculosis reactivation. Choice E (mild–moderate disease, no maintenance, myelosuppression no) is controlled-release oral budesonide, used for mild-to-moderate ileal and ascending colon Crohn disease with fewer systemic effects, but it is a glucocorticoid and does not maintain remission. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 38, section 3: aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, anti-TNF therapy); Katzung Ch. 62 (drugs used in IBD). The absence of myelosuppression for glucocorticoids and anti-TNF agents is inferred because the notes do not list it.","boardPrep":"Step 1: azathioprine/6-MP → 6-thioguanine nucleotides; steroid-sparing maintenance in IBD; leukopenia, hepatotoxicity, pancreatitis; severe leukopenia with allopurinol (xanthine oxidase) (Exam 3 notes, SDL 38). COMLEX: glucocorticoids induce but never maintain IBD remission.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 32 Obj 3 — Crohn disease versus ulcerative colitis","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl38_q19","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":1,"stem":"Senna and bisacodyl produce a bowel movement by which action?","choices":{"A":"Stimulating enteric nerves and colonic secretion","B":"Lowering the surface tension of stool water","C":"Coating stool so the colon cannot reabsorb water","D":"Binding bile acids in the ileum","E":"Blocking the dopamine D2 receptor"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Stimulant laxatives such as senna and bisacodyl directly stimulate the enteric nervous system and promote colonic fluid secretion. Lowering surface tension is the surfactant action of docusate. Coating stool to slow water reabsorption is the lubricant action of mineral oil. Binding bile acids is the action of cholestyramine and the other resins. Dopamine D2 blockade is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Lowering surface tension is the surfactant action of docusate","C":"Coating stool to slow water reabsorption is the lubricant action of mineral oil","D":"Binding bile acids is the action of cholestyramine and the other resins","E":"Dopamine D2 blockade is the prokinetic action of metoclopramide"}},{"id":"sdl38_q20","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 72-year-old man is two days after a myocardial infarction. He has not stooled, and his physicians want to avoid straining. The abdomen is soft. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which agent softens stool for this purpose?","choices":{"A":"A large dose of magnesium citrate as a purge","B":"Sodium phosphate tablets","C":"Docusate","D":"Castor oil in a purgative dose","E":"Erythromycin to empty the stomach"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Docusate is used to soften stool and limit straining in hospitalized, surgical, and cardiac patients. Magnesium citrate is a high-dose osmotic purge for a full evacuation, not a gentle softener after a myocardial infarction. Sodium phosphate is a purgative used for bowel preparation, not a daily softener to avoid straining. A purgative dose of castor oil is not the stool softener chosen to minimize cardiac strain. Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and is not a stool softener.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Magnesium citrate is a high-dose osmotic purge for a full evacuation, not a gentle softener after a myocardial infarction","B":"Sodium phosphate is a purgative used for bowel preparation, not a daily softener to avoid straining","D":"A purgative dose of castor oil is not the stool softener chosen to minimize cardiac strain","E":"Erythromycin stimulates motilin receptors and is not a stool softener"}},{"id":"sdl38_q21","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the mechanisms of action of medications used to treat constipation, including stool softeners, osmotic laxatives, and stimulant laxatives, and explain their clinical applications.","batch":2,"stem":"A 4-year-old boy has a large, hard fecal mass and overflow soiling. He has been holding stool because defecation hurts, and there is no fever. The abdomen is soft, with stool palpable in the left colon. Sodium is 138 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and hemoglobin is 12.4 g/dL (reference range: 11.5–15.5 g/dL). Which agent is used in children to lubricate an impaction?","choices":{"A":"Infliximab","B":"Mineral oil","C":"Metoclopramide","D":"Octreotide","E":"Albendazole"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Mineral oil coats stool and is used to treat or prevent fecal impaction in young children and debilitated adults. Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 blocker used for gastroparesis, and it does not lubricate an impaction. Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for secretory diarrhea and variceal bleeding. Albendazole is an anthelmintic and does not lubricate stool.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Infliximab is an anti-TNF antibody for inflammatory bowel disease","C":"Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 blocker used for gastroparesis, and it does not lubricate an impaction","D":"Octreotide is a somatostatin analog for secretory diarrhea and variceal bleeding","E":"Albendazole is an anthelmintic and does not lubricate stool"}},{"id":"sdl38_q22","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":1,"stem":"Long-term octreotide causes steatorrhea by suppressing which secretion?","choices":{"A":"Pancreatic exocrine secretion","B":"Gastric intrinsic factor only, with fat absorption intact","C":"Salivary amylase","D":"Erythropoietin","E":"Thyroid-stimulating hormone from the enterocyte"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Octreotide impairs pancreatic exocrine secretion, so long-term use causes steatorrhea and loss of fat-soluble vitamins. Intrinsic factor is for vitamin B12, and loss of it does not by itself cause steatorrhea. Salivary amylase digests starch and is not the secretion whose loss causes steatorrhea on octreotide. Erythropoietin is a renal hormone and is not the secretion octreotide suppresses to cause fat malabsorption. Enterocytes do not secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Intrinsic factor is for vitamin B12, and loss of it does not by itself cause steatorrhea","C":"Salivary amylase digests starch and is not the secretion whose loss causes steatorrhea on octreotide","D":"Erythropoietin is a renal hormone and is not the secretion octreotide suppresses to cause fat malabsorption","E":"Enterocytes do not secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone"}},{"id":"sdl38_q23","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Compare the mechanisms of action, therapeutic uses, and adverse effects of major classes of antidiarrheal medications, including opioid agonists, bile acid–binding resins, and octreotide, and identify clinical scenarios in which each is indicated.","batch":2,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman had 70 cm of terminal ileum removed and then had watery diarrhea after meals. Cholestyramine stopped the watery stools, but she now has greasy, floating stools she did not have before the resin. She is afebrile. Sodium is 139 mEq/L (reference range: 135–145 mEq/L) and albumin is 3.7 g/dL (reference range: 3.5–5.0 g/dL). Why did the greasy stools appear?","choices":{"A":"The resin increased pancreatic lipase above normal","B":"The resin infected the colon with an invasive ameba","C":"The resin further depleted the bile acids needed to form micelles","D":"The resin blocked vitamin B12 receptors in the stomach","E":"The resin stimulated motilin and emptied the stomach too fast"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Cholestyramine binds bile acids and can worsen fat malabsorption when the circulating bile acid pool is already depleted. The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase. The resin does not introduce amebae. Vitamin B12 receptors are ileal, and the resin does not block a gastric receptor for B12. Motilin stimulation is the action of erythromycin, not of a bile acid resin.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"The resin does not increase pancreatic lipase","B":"The resin does not introduce amebae","D":"Vitamin B12 receptors are ileal, and the resin does not block a gastric receptor for B12","E":"Motilin stimulation is the action of erythromycin, not of a bile acid resin"}},{"id":"sdl38_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Mesalamine dampens mucosal inflammation partly by inhibiting which transcription factor?","choices":{"A":"The RET receptor on neural crest cells","B":"NF-κB","C":"The melanocortin-4 receptor","D":"RNA polymerase of gut bacteria only","E":"Thymidylate synthase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"5-Aminosalicylates inhibit NF-κB, modulate eicosanoids, and scavenge reactive oxygen species in the mucosa. RET is the receptor neural-crest cells use while populating the bowel, and it is not the 5-aminosalicylate target. The melanocortin-4 receptor is an appetite receptor blocked by Agouti-related peptide. Bacterial RNA polymerase is the target of rifaximin, not of mesalamine. Thymidylate synthase is the target of the fluoropyrimidine metabolite FdUMP.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"RET is the receptor neural-crest cells use while populating the bowel, and it is not the 5-aminosalicylate target","C":"The melanocortin-4 receptor is an appetite receptor blocked by Agouti-related peptide","D":"Bacterial RNA polymerase is the target of rifaximin, not of mesalamine","E":"Thymidylate synthase is the target of the fluoropyrimidine metabolite FdUMP"}},{"id":"sdl38_q25","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Why is a systemic glucocorticoid a poor drug for keeping ulcerative colitis in remission?","choices":{"A":"It cannot induce remission in a flare","B":"Colonic bacteria destroy it before it acts","C":"It only works on tapeworms","D":"It permanently replaces the need for any other drug after one dose","E":"It does not maintain remission"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Glucocorticoids induce remission in moderate to severe ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease, but they are not effective for maintaining remission. Glucocorticoids are used precisely because they induce remission quickly in a flare. Colonic bacteria cleave the azo bond of sulfasalazine, not the reason steroids fail as maintenance. Anthelmintic activity is irrelevant to glucocorticoid use in colitis. One dose does not remove the need for maintenance therapy.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Glucocorticoids are used precisely because they induce remission quickly in a flare","B":"Colonic bacteria cleave the azo bond of sulfasalazine, not the reason steroids fail as maintenance","C":"Anthelmintic activity is irrelevant to glucocorticoid use in colitis","D":"One dose does not remove the need for maintenance therapy"}},{"id":"sdl38_q26","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Compare the major pharmacologic therapies used to treat ulcerative colitis and Crohn’s disease, including aminosalicylates, glucocorticoids, purine analogs, and anti-tumor necrosis factor therapy with emphasis on their mechanisms of action, therapeutic roles, and key adverse effects.","batch":1,"stem":"Azathioprine may take how long before its full immunosuppressive effect appears?","choices":{"A":"Up to about 17 weeks","B":"A few minutes, like intravenous neostigmine","C":"Only during the infusion of a platinum drug","D":"It never acts, because it is not absorbed","E":"One hour, the same timetable as a bisacodyl suppository"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine have a delayed onset, up to 17 weeks, before the full effect. Intravenous neostigmine evacuates the colon within minutes and is not a thiopurine. Platinum infusions are chemotherapy, not the timetable of azathioprine. Azathioprine is absorbed and converted to active thioguanine nucleotides. A bisacodyl suppository is a stimulant laxative that acts within about an hour, not an immunosuppressant.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Intravenous neostigmine evacuates the colon within minutes and is not a thiopurine","C":"Platinum infusions are chemotherapy, not the timetable of azathioprine","D":"Azathioprine is absorbed and converted to active thioguanine nucleotides","E":"A bisacodyl suppository is a stimulant laxative that acts within about an hour, not an immunosuppressant"}}]},{"sdlNumber":39,"title":"SDL 39 — Pharmacology - chemo for colorectal cancer (CRC)","questions":[{"id":"sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP exerts its main cytotoxic effect by binding which enzyme?","choices":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase","C":"DNA polymerase alpha","D":"Thymidylate synthase","E":"Thymidine kinase enzyme"},"correct":"D","explanation":"FdUMP forms a covalent ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking de novo thymidylate synthesis (\"thymineless death\"); FUTP and FdUTP are also incorporated into RNA and DNA, and the drug acts in S phase. Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate. Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not FdUMP. Thymidine kinase is not its target. DNA polymerase alpha is competitively inhibited by cytarabine and gemcitabine triphosphates, not by FdUMP. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU is a pyrimidine analog bioactivated to 5-FdUMP, lowering dTMP and DNA synthesis, and its effects are enhanced by adding leucovorin (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: 5-FU is given IV because it is broken down rapidly in the GI tract, and its plasma half-life is about 10 to 15 minutes (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Dihydrofolate reductase is inhibited by methotrexate.","B":"Ribonucleotide reductase is the target of hydroxyurea, not FdUMP.","E":"Thymidine kinase is not its target.","C":"DNA polymerase alpha is competitively inhibited by cytarabine and gemcitabine triphosphates, not by FdUMP."}},{"id":"sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"A 58-year-old man receiving FOLFOX for stage III colon cancer notices tingling in his fingers and throat when he holds a chilled drink during the infusion. Which toxicity is this?","choices":{"A":"Acute sensory neurotoxicity","B":"Cumulative motor axonopathy","C":"Cryoglobulin vasculitis flare","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany episode","E":"Cerebellar ataxia toxicity"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The platinum agent in FOLFOX causes dose-limiting peripheral sensory neuropathy in two forms: an acute form triggered and worsened by cold, and a chronic cumulative dose-dependent form that is more readily reversible than cisplatin neuropathy. The chronic form is sensory, not a motor axonopathy. A cryoglobulin flare, hypocalcemic tetany, and cerebellar ataxia are different problems. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, are cell cycle nonspecific, and cause peripheral neuropathy, nephrotoxicity, and ototoxicity (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: FOLFOX is the most widely used first-line regimen for metastatic colorectal cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"The cumulative form of this drug is sensory, not a motor axonopathy.","C":"A cryoglobulin vasculitis flare is a different illness.","E":"Cerebellar ataxia is not the pattern of this neuropathy.","D":"Hypocalcemic tetany is not this cold-triggered tingling."}},{"id":"sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":1,"stem":"Before cetuximab is added for metastatic colorectal cancer, the tumor must show which molecular feature?","choices":{"A":"HER2 gene amplification","B":"Wild-type KRAS and NRAS","C":"High EGFR protein expression","D":"BRAF V600E mutation","E":"Microsatellite instability"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 antibody against the extracellular domain of EGFR; its efficacy is restricted to tumors with wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), because a mutant RAS signals downstream of the blocked receptor. HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cetuximab. Microsatellite instability-high status selects metastatic colorectal cancer for checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, not for cetuximab. Katzung states that cetuximab efficacy is restricted to patients whose tumors express wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), not to high EGFR protein expression. First Aid lists cetuximab as an EGFR antibody for metastatic colorectal cancer with wild-type RAS, so the selection criterion is RAS status rather than a BRAF V600E mutation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies cause rash, elevated LFTs, and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: because cetuximab is IgG1, part of its antitumor effect may be immune mediated (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"HER2 amplification selects patients for trastuzumab, not cetuximab.","E":"Microsatellite instability-high status selects metastatic colorectal cancer for checkpoint inhibitors such as pembrolizumab, not for cetuximab.","C":"Katzung states that cetuximab efficacy is restricted to patients whose tumors express wild-type RAS (KRAS and NRAS), not to high EGFR protein expression.","D":"First Aid lists cetuximab as an EGFR antibody for metastatic colorectal cancer with wild-type RAS, so the selection criterion is RAS status rather than a BRAF V600E mutation."}},{"id":"sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which orally available prodrug is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase within tumor tissue?","choices":{"A":"Gemcitabine","B":"Cytarabine","C":"Methotrexate","D":"Hydroxyurea","E":"Capecitabine"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Capecitabine has 70% to 80% oral bioavailability; liver carboxylesterase and cytidine deaminase form 5'-deoxy-5-fluorouridine, which thymidine phosphorylase (high in colorectal tumors) converts to 5-FU. It is paired with oxaliplatin as CAPOX. Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is not converted to 5-FU. Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug. Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycytidine kinase, not a prodrug converted to 5-FU. Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that inhibits DNA polymerase, not a 5-FU prodrug. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Capecitabine). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine is the prodrug of 5-FU listed with the pyrimidine antimetabolites (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: CAPOX is used first line in metastatic disease and as adjuvant therapy for stage III colon cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"C":"Methotrexate is an antifolate that inhibits dihydrofolate reductase and is not converted to 5-FU.","D":"Hydroxyurea inhibits ribonucleotide reductase and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug.","A":"Gemcitabine is a deoxycytidine analog activated by deoxycytidine kinase, not a prodrug converted to 5-FU.","B":"Cytarabine is a cytidine analog converted to ara-CTP that inhibits DNA polymerase, not a 5-FU prodrug."}},{"id":"sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which DNA cross-linking agent has myelosuppression, with much less renal and GI toxicity than cisplatin, as its main dose-limiting effect?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Carboplatin","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Busulfan"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Carboplatin shares the mechanism, resistance, and spectrum of cisplatin, but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression with significantly less renal and GI toxicity, so it is easier to give and has replaced cisplatin in many regimens. Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and emesis, with minimal myelosuppression. Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive toxicities are alopecia and hemorrhagic cystitis, preventable with hydration. Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation, pulmonary fibrosis and adrenal insufficiency. Ifosfamide is an alkylating agent closely related to cyclophosphamide, notable for hemorrhagic cystitis, not a carboplatin-like platinum analog. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds cross-link DNA, and cisplatin nephrotoxicity can be prevented with amifostine (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: platinum agents bind mainly the N7 position of guanine and work in all cell cycle phases (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin's dose-limiting toxicities are nephrotoxicity and emesis, with minimal myelosuppression.","D":"Cyclophosphamide is an alkylating agent whose distinctive toxicities are alopecia and hemorrhagic cystitis, preventable with hydration.","E":"Busulfan, used for CML, is associated with skin pigmentation, pulmonary fibrosis and adrenal insufficiency.","B":"Ifosfamide is an alkylating agent closely related to cyclophosphamide, notable for hemorrhagic cystitis, not a carboplatin-like platinum analog."}},{"id":"sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":1,"stem":"Which anti-EGFR antibody is a fully human IgG2 with fewer infusion reactions?","choices":{"A":"Panitumumab","B":"Cetuximab","C":"Necitumumab","D":"Trastuzumab","E":"Pertuzumab"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Panitumumab is a fully human IgG2 anti-EGFR antibody, so it is not expected to have immune-mediated effects, and infusion reactions are much less common than with chimeric cetuximab; acneiform rash is a main adverse effect. Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe infusion reactions. Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an IgG2. Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR. Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, not EGFR. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the -umab ending marks a human monoclonal antibody and -ximab a chimeric human-mouse one (First Aid Pharmacology, biologic agents). COMLEX: necitumumab is an IgG1 anti-EGFR antibody used for squamous NSCLC and carries thromboembolic risk (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Cetuximab is a chimeric IgG1 with a higher risk of severe infusion reactions.","C":"Necitumumab is a fully human IgG1 for squamous NSCLC, not an IgG2.","D":"Trastuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2, not EGFR.","E":"Pertuzumab is a humanized IgG1 against HER2 subdomain II, not EGFR."}},{"id":"sdl39_q07","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with stage III colon cancer gets her first cycle of an IV fluoropyrimidine with leucovorin and a platinum agent. Within a week she has severe mouth ulcers, profuse diarrhea, and confusion; absolute neutrophil count is 300/µL (reference 1,500–8,000/µL). Deficiency of which enzyme best explains this?","choices":{"A":"Thiopurine methyltransferase","B":"Dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase","C":"Uridine glucuronosyltransferase","D":"Hypoxanthine phosphoribosyl transferase","E":"Cytidine deaminase enzyme"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Most of an administered 5-FU dose is catabolized by DPD. Partial or complete DPD deficiency, an autosomal recessive trait seen in up to 5% of cancer patients (Katzung Ch. 54 elsewhere cites up to 10% of the North American population), causes severe toxicity with the triad of myelosuppression, GI toxicity (diarrhea, mucositis), and neurotoxicity. TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicity. UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of the fluoropyrimidine with oxaliplatin. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase activates 6-mercaptopurine to its nucleotide; it does not catabolize 5-FU. Cytidine deaminase is a hepatic activation step for capecitabine (converting it toward 5-FU), not the enzyme that catabolizes 5-FU. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: 5-FU causes myelosuppression and is used for colon and pancreatic cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: not every DPD-deficient patient presents with this classic triad (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"TPMT deficiency raises thiopurine toxicity, not 5-FU toxicity.","C":"UGT1A1 deficiency increases irinotecan toxicity, not that of the fluoropyrimidine with oxaliplatin.","D":"Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyl transferase activates 6-mercaptopurine to its nucleotide; it does not catabolize 5-FU.","E":"Cytidine deaminase is a hepatic activation step for capecitabine (converting it toward 5-FU), not the enzyme that catabolizes 5-FU."}},{"id":"sdl39_q08","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":5,"stem":"A 66-year-old man receives FOLFIRI for metastatic colon cancer. Two hours into the infusion he has abdominal cramps, watery stools, sweating, and salivation. Which drug best treats this early reaction?","choices":{"A":"Loperamide","B":"Octreotide","C":"Atropine","D":"Ondansetron","E":"Metoclopramide"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Irinotecan causes two forms of diarrhea: an early form within 24 hours that is a cholinergic event effectively treated with atropine, and a late form 2 to 10 days later that may cause severe electrolyte imbalance and dehydration. Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this early cholinergic reaction responds to atropine. Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted to vagally mediated and chemotherapy emesis; it does not block muscarinic receptors. Octreotide treats diarrhea from vagotomy, dumping, short-bowel syndrome, or AIDS, not the acute cholinergic syndrome of irinotecan. Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 antagonist prokinetic that would not reverse cholinergic cramping and salivation. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Camptothecins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary). Textbook enrichment (Katzung) — detail not in SDL 39 lecture notes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: irinotecan and topotecan block topoisomerase I and cause severe myelosuppression and diarrhea (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: irinotecan and its metabolite are cleared mainly in bile and feces, so liver dysfunction requires a dose reduction (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — secretory diarrhea and cAMP-mediated electrolyte loss","isHighYield":true,"enrichmentSource":"Katzung","whyNot":{"A":"Loperamide treats the late irinotecan diarrhea, whereas this early cholinergic reaction responds to atropine.","D":"Ondansetron is a 5-HT3 antagonist whose action is restricted to vagally mediated and chemotherapy emesis; it does not block muscarinic receptors.","B":"Octreotide treats diarrhea from vagotomy, dumping, short-bowel syndrome, or AIDS, not the acute cholinergic syndrome of irinotecan.","E":"Metoclopramide is a dopamine D2 antagonist prokinetic that would not reverse cholinergic cramping and salivation."}},{"id":"sdl39_q09","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic nonseminomatous testicular cancer receives cisplatin, bleomycin, and a semisynthetic mayapple-root derivative. The derivative acts mainly by which mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Topoisomerase II blockade","B":"Topoisomerase I blockade","C":"Microtubule stabilization","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking","E":"DNA polymerase blockade"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Etoposide, a semisynthetic podophyllotoxin from the mayapple root, complexes with topoisomerase II and DNA, preventing religation of double-strand breaks and impairing DNA synthesis. It is active in germ cell and lung cancers; renal dysfunction needs dose reduction. Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and topotecan, not etoposide. Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the mayapple-derived etoposide. Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, already in the regimen, not etoposide. DNA polymerase blockade is the action of cytarabine triphosphate (Ara-CTP), not of the mayapple derivative etoposide. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epipodophyllotoxins). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: topoisomerase II inhibitors cause myelosuppression and alopecia and treat testicular and small cell lung cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: topoisomerase inhibitors arrest cells in the S and G2 phases (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Topoisomerase I blockade is the mechanism of irinotecan and topotecan, not etoposide.","C":"Microtubule stabilization is the action of taxanes, not the mayapple-derived etoposide.","D":"Guanine N7 cross-linking is the mechanism of cisplatin, already in the regimen, not etoposide.","E":"DNA polymerase blockade is the action of cytarabine triphosphate (Ara-CTP), not of the mayapple derivative etoposide."}},{"id":"sdl39_q10","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman with metastatic colorectal cancer is starting second-line therapy. Her oncologist adds to FOLFIRI a humanized antibody that binds circulating VEGF-A. Which drug was added?","choices":{"A":"Cetuximab","B":"Necitumumab","C":"Trastuzumab","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Rituximab"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Bevacizumab binds all forms of VEGF-A and keeps it from reaching VEGF receptors; it combines with 5-FU-, irinotecan-, and oxaliplatin-based therapy in metastatic colorectal cancer; unlike the anti-EGFR antibodies, it targets the ligand rather than EGFR. Hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, GI perforation, and proteinuria are key risks. Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humanized anti-VEGF-A antibody. Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC, not VEGF-A. Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A. Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lymphoma, not an anti-VEGF-A antibody. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: the anti-VEGF antibody also treats renal cell carcinoma, NSCLC, and angioproliferative retinopathy, with hemorrhage and blood clots as adverse effects (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: it does not appear to worsen the usual toxicities of cytotoxic chemotherapy (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 1 — colorectal adenomas and malignant potential","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cetuximab is a chimeric antibody against EGFR, not a humanized anti-VEGF-A antibody.","B":"Necitumumab targets EGFR and is approved for squamous NSCLC, not VEGF-A.","C":"Trastuzumab targets HER2, not circulating VEGF-A.","E":"Rituximab is an anti-CD20 antibody used with CHOP for lymphoma, not an anti-VEGF-A antibody."}},{"id":"sdl39_q11","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old man has metastatic colon cancer whose cells lack mismatch repair function and resist the older platinum drugs in culture. Which DNA cross-linking agent is least affected by this resistance?","choices":{"A":"Cisplatin","B":"Ifosfamide","C":"Busulfan","D":"Cyclophosphamide","E":"Oxaliplatin"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Tumors resistant to cisplatin or carboplatin through mismatch repair defects are not cross-resistant to oxaliplatin, which is used with 5-FU and leucovorin (FOLFOX) in metastatic and adjuvant colorectal cancer. Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch repair-deficient tumors resist. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum Analogs). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: platinum compounds treat GI, testicular, bladder, ovarian, and lung tumors (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: oxaliplatin regimens are also active in pancreatic, gastroesophageal, and hepatocellular cancer (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 3 — hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer and extracolonic cancers","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cisplatin is one of the older platinum drugs that mismatch repair-deficient tumors resist.","B":"Ifosfamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent.","C":"Busulfan is an alkylating agent used for chronic myeloid leukemia.","D":"Cyclophosphamide is a bis(chloroethyl)amine alkylating agent."}},{"id":"sdl39_q12","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":2,"stem":"A 57-year-old man on an anti-EGFR antibody plus FOLFOX for metastatic colorectal cancer develops an acneiform facial rash, muscle cramps, and tremor. Which lab abnormality is most likely?","choices":{"A":"Low serum magnesium","B":"High serum potassium","C":"Low serum sodium","D":"High serum calcium","E":"Low serum phosphate"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypomagnesemia and acneiform skin rash are the two main adverse effects shared by cetuximab and panitumumab; neuromuscular irritability such as cramps and tremor fits low magnesium. The other options are not that answer. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Epidermal Growth Factor Receptor Inhibitors). Lecture notes reviewed 2026-10-31 (notes primary).","boardPrep":"Step 1: anti-EGFR antibodies treat metastatic colorectal cancer and head and neck cancer (First Aid Hematology and Oncology Pharmacology). COMLEX: cetuximab given with radiation for head and neck cancer carries a small (1%) sudden-death risk and a black-box warning (Katzung Ch. 54).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"High serum potassium is not one of the two main effects.","C":"Low serum sodium is not one of the two main effects.","D":"High serum calcium is not one of the two main effects.","E":"Low serum phosphate is not one of the two main effects."}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with metastatic colon cancer receives 5-fluorouracil. In a later cycle her oncologist adds leucovorin, the reduced folate also used to rescue normal cells after high-dose methotrexate. Her tumor shrinks more, but she also develops worse mucositis and diarrhea. Which mechanism explains both changes?","choices":{"A":"Added folate locks FdUMP onto thymidylate synthase","B":"Leucovorin inhibits dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase in the liver","C":"Leucovorin is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase","D":"Folate blocks dihydrofolate reductase in dividing cells","E":"Leucovorin cross-links guanine bases in tumor DNA"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The 5-FU metabolite FdUMP inhibits thymidylate synthase by forming a covalent ternary complex with the enzyme and the reduced folate 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, so supplying a reduced folate as leucovorin enhances and prolongs that inhibition, deepening thymineless death in both tumor and rapidly dividing mucosa. Leucovorin inhibits dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase is false; DPD deficiency raises 5-FU toxicity, but leucovorin does not inhibit DPD. Leucovorin is converted to 5-FU by thymidine phosphorylase confuses it with capecitabine, whose last activation step uses that enzyme. Folate blocks dihydrofolate reductase in dividing cells describes methotrexate, whose effects leucovorin reverses. Leucovorin cross-links guanine bases in tumor DNA describes platinum analogs, not a folate. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines; Methotrexate); First Aid 2025 Oncology (Leucovorin).","boardPrep":"Step 1: FdUMP forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and 5,10-methylene THF, and leucovorin enhances 5-FU while rescuing cells from methotrexate (Katzung Ch. 54; First Aid). COMLEX: FOLFOX and FOLFIRI include leucovorin, so expect more mucositis and diarrhea than with 5-FU without it.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"B":"Leucovorin inhibits dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase is false; DPD","C":"Leucovorin is converted to 5-FU by thymidine","D":"Folate blocks dihydrofolate reductase in dividing cells","E":"Leucovorin cross-links guanine bases in tumor DNA"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 62-year-old man with metastatic colorectal cancer receives FOLFOX plus an infused antibody every 2 weeks. Over 2 months his blood pressure rises to 166/102 mm Hg and urinalysis shows 3+ protein. He then has sudden severe abdominal pain, and an upright radiograph shows free air under the diaphragm. Which mechanism of the antibody best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"Blocking epidermal growth factor receptor signaling","B":"Inhibiting topoisomerase I in dividing cells","C":"Neutralizing a vascular growth factor ligand","D":"Stabilizing microtubules in tumor cells","E":"Blocking PD-1 on cytotoxic T lymphocytes"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypertension, proteinuria, and gastrointestinal perforation are the signature toxicities of bevacizumab, a humanized antibody that binds VEGF-A and prevents it from engaging its receptors, impairing the vascular support needed by tumors and by healing tissue. Blocking epidermal growth factor receptor signaling is the action of cetuximab and panitumumab, which cause acneiform rash, diarrhea, and hypomagnesemia rather than hypertension and perforation. Inhibiting topoisomerase I in dividing cells describes irinotecan, a small molecule that causes diarrhea and neutropenia. Stabilizing microtubules in tumor cells describes taxanes, which are not part of this colorectal regimen. Blocking PD-1 on cytotoxic T lymphocytes causes immune-related adverse effects such as colitis, not this triad. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular endothelial growth factor inhibitors); First Aid 2025 Oncology (Bevacizumab).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Bevacizumab binds VEGF-A; its safety concerns are hypertension, arterial thromboembolism, wound-healing problems, GI perforation, and proteinuria (Katzung Ch. 54). COMLEX: check blood pressure and urine protein at each cycle and evaluate new abdominal pain urgently.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Blocking epidermal growth factor receptor signaling is","B":"Inhibiting topoisomerase I in dividing cells describes","D":"Stabilizing microtubules in tumor cells describes taxanes,","E":"Blocking PD-1 on cytotoxic T lymphocytes causes"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":3,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with metastatic testicular cancer receives a DNA cross-linking regimen that requires vigorous IV hydration. After 2 cycles he has tinnitus and high-frequency hearing loss, his creatinine has risen from 0.9 to 1.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.7–1.3 mg/dL), and magnesium is low. Which agent given with his chemotherapy is used to reduce this renal toxicity?","choices":{"A":"Mesna","B":"Dexrazoxane","C":"Leucovorin","D":"Amifostine","E":"Allopurinol"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ototoxicity and nephrotoxicity in a man with testicular cancer on a cross-linking regimen requiring hydration point to cisplatin, and its nephrotoxicity can be reduced with the free radical scavenger amifostine in addition to vigorous hydration. Mesna binds acrolein to prevent hemorrhagic cystitis from cyclophosphamide or ifosfamide. Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that protects against anthracycline cardiotoxicity. Leucovorin rescues normal cells from methotrexate and enhances 5-FU. Allopurinol prevents uric acid nephropathy from tumor lysis, not direct platinum tubular injury. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum analogs); First Aid 2025 Oncology (Platinum compounds; cytoprotective agents).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Cisplatin causes nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, and neuropathy; nephrotoxicity is reduced by hydration and amifostine (First Aid, Platinum compounds). COMLEX: check creatinine, magnesium, and hearing before each cycle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Mesna binds acrolein to prevent hemorrhagic cystitis","B":"Dexrazoxane is an iron chelator that protects","C":"Leucovorin rescues normal cells from methotrexate and","E":"Allopurinol prevents uric acid nephropathy from tumor"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 66-year-old woman with ovarian cancer needs a platinum-based regimen. She has chronic kidney disease and baseline hearing loss, so her oncologist substitutes a second-generation platinum analog that does not need vigorous IV hydration. Which toxicity will most likely limit dosing of the substituted drug?","choices":{"A":"Cold-triggered throat dysesthesia","B":"Severe acute renal tubular injury","C":"Hemorrhagic cystitis","D":"Dilated cardiomyopathy","E":"Bone marrow suppression"},"correct":"E","explanation":"The second-generation platinum analog chosen to avoid cisplatin kidney injury and the need for hydration is carboplatin, whose main dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression, with much less renal and gastrointestinal toxicity than cisplatin. Cold-triggered throat dysesthesia is the acute neurotoxicity of oxaliplatin, the third-generation analog. Severe acute renal tubular injury is the cisplatin toxicity that this substitution was meant to avoid. Hemorrhagic cystitis is caused by cyclophosphamide and ifosfamide, not platinum analogs. Dilated cardiomyopathy is associated with anthracyclines and trastuzumab rather than carboplatin. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Platinum analogs; Table 54-2).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Carboplatin shares cisplatin's mechanism but its dose-limiting toxicity is myelosuppression, while oxaliplatin causes cold-triggered neuropathy (Katzung Ch. 54). COMLEX: monitor blood counts, especially platelets, before each carboplatin cycle.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Cold-triggered throat dysesthesia is the acute neurotoxicity","B":"Severe acute renal tubular injury is the","C":"Hemorrhagic cystitis is caused by cyclophosphamide and","D":"Dilated cardiomyopathy is associated with anthracyclines and"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 69-year-old man with advanced hepatocellular carcinoma takes warfarin for atrial fibrillation, with a stable INR of 2.5. He starts an oral small-molecule inhibitor of several receptor tyrosine kinases, including VEGF receptors. Three weeks later his INR is 5.2 and he has gum bleeding. Which property of the new drug best explains this?","choices":{"A":"It binds a circulating angiogenic ligand","B":"It competes for hepatic CYP3A4 clearance","C":"It is cleared by renal tubular secretion","D":"It induces antibodies against warfarin","E":"It inhibits vitamin K absorption in gut"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Unlike antibody growth factor inhibitors, small-molecule kinase inhibitors such as sorafenib are metabolized by hepatic CYP3A4 and so interact with other drugs handled by that system, especially warfarin, and they also carry bleeding risk, explaining his rising INR and gum bleeding. It binds a circulating angiogenic ligand describes bevacizumab, an antibody to VEGF-A that is not metabolized by CYP enzymes. It is cleared by renal tubular secretion is incorrect; these agents are eliminated mainly by the liver into feces. It induces antibodies against warfarin is not a known mechanism and would lower rather than raise the INR. It inhibits vitamin K absorption in gut is not a described property of tyrosine kinase inhibitors. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Vascular endothelial growth factor; sorafenib, sunitinib, pazopanib).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Oral multikinase inhibitors are CYP3A4 substrates with interactions involving warfarin and grapefruit, and they cause hypertension and bleeding (Katzung Ch. 54). COMLEX: monitor INR closely and review interacting drugs when an oral kinase inhibitor is started.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It binds a circulating angiogenic ligand describes","C":"It is cleared by renal tubular secretion","D":"It induces antibodies against warfarin is","E":"It inhibits vitamin K absorption in"}},{"id":"trial_sdl39_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":3,"stem":"A 64-year-old woman's metastatic colorectal cancer has progressed on 5-FU, oxaliplatin, and irinotecan regimens. She starts an oral tablet containing a fluorinated thymidine analog plus a second component, and 3 weeks later her absolute neutrophil count is 600/µL (reference range: 1,500–8,000/µL). What does the second component of the tablet do?","choices":{"A":"It is activated to 5-FU by tumor thymidine phosphorylase","B":"It binds a growth factor receptor on tumor cells","C":"It supplies a reduced folate that stabilizes FdUMP binding","D":"It blocks thymidine phosphorylase from degrading it","E":"It inhibits topoisomerase I to add a second DNA lesion"},"correct":"D","explanation":"An oral fluoropyrimidine combination used after 5-FU failure with neutropenia as its main toxicity is TAS-102, in which trifluridine is the active nucleoside analog and tipiracil inhibits thymidine phosphorylase, the enzyme that degrades trifluridine, so higher trifluridine levels reach tumor DNA. It is activated to 5-FU by tumor thymidine phosphorylase describes capecitabine, which depends on that enzyme rather than blocking it. It binds a growth factor receptor on tumor cells describes cetuximab or panitumumab, which are antibodies, not tablet components. It supplies a reduced folate that stabilizes FdUMP binding describes leucovorin, which is given with IV 5-FU. It inhibits topoisomerase I to add a second DNA lesion describes irinotecan, which already failed and is not in this tablet. Sources: Katzung Ch. 54 (Fluoropyrimidines; TAS-102).","boardPrep":"Step 1: TAS-102 combines trifluridine with tipiracil, a thymidine phosphorylase inhibitor, retains activity in 5-FU-resistant tumors, and is limited by neutropenia (Katzung Ch. 54). COMLEX: check blood counts before each cycle in chemorefractory colorectal cancer on TAS-102.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 33 → Obj 4 — colorectal adenocarcinoma","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"It is activated to 5-FU by tumor","B":"It binds a growth factor receptor on","C":"It supplies a reduced folate that stabilizes","E":"It inhibits topoisomerase I to add a"}},{"id":"grid_sdl39_q01","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the various medication names within each class and appreciate the important differences with each medication within these classes.","batch":4,"stem":"A 63-year-old man with resected stage III colon cancer begins adjuvant chemotherapy with intravenous oxaliplatin every 3 weeks plus a tablet he takes twice daily. Six weeks later the skin of his palms and soles is red, painful, and peeling. He has no mouth ulcers, and his neutrophil count is within the reference range. Which of the following sets of features best characterizes the oral drug most likely responsible?","grid":{"headers":["Primary action","Route","Key toxicity"]},"choices":{"A":["TS inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"B":["TS inhibition","IV","Mucositis, cytopenias"],"C":["Incorporation into DNA","Oral","Neutropenia"],"D":["HER2 kinase inhibition","Oral","Hand-foot syndrome"],"E":["DNA cross-linking","IV","Sensory neuropathy"]},"correct":"A","explanation":"Choice A (TS inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is correct. Capecitabine is an oral fluoropyrimidine carbamate prodrug converted to 5-FU in the tumor; the last step uses thymidine phosphorylase, which is overexpressed in solid tumors. Its metabolite FdUMP forms a ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and methylenetetrahydrofolate, blocking thymidylate synthesis. The notes say it causes less myelosuppression and mucositis than intravenous 5-FU but a markedly higher incidence of hand-foot syndrome, and it pairs with oxaliplatin in CAPOX. Choice B (TS inhibition, IV, mucositis and cytopenias) is 5-fluorouracil, which must be given intravenously because DPD degrades it rapidly and which causes high rates of mucositis, diarrhea, and myelosuppression. Choice C (incorporation into DNA, oral, neutropenia) is TAS-102: trifluridine is incorporated into DNA, tipiracil blocks its breakdown, and neutropenia rather than hand-foot syndrome is dose-limiting. Choice D (HER2 kinase inhibition, oral, hand-foot syndrome) is an oral HER2 tyrosine kinase inhibitor such as lapatinib or tucatinib, which causes severe diarrhea and hand-foot syndrome but is not a CRC adjuvant fluoropyrimidine. Choice E (DNA cross-linking, IV, sensory neuropathy) is oxaliplatin, the intravenous partner drug, whose dose-limiting toxicity is peripheral sensory neuropathy with cold-induced dysesthesia, not palmar-plantar erythrodysesthesia. Sources: Exam 3 lecture notes (SDL 39, sections 1 and 2: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, growth factor receptor inhibitors, and intra-class distinctions); Katzung Ch. 54 (cancer chemotherapy).","boardPrep":"Step 1: capecitabine = oral 5-FU prodrug activated by thymidine phosphorylase in tumor; more hand-foot syndrome, less myelosuppression and mucositis than IV 5-FU; DPD deficiency causes severe toxicity with either drug (Exam 3 notes, SDL 39). COMLEX: oxaliplatin → cold-induced neuropathy; TAS-102 → neutropenia.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 33 Obj 3 — colorectal cancer pathways and staging context","isHighYield":true},{"id":"sdl39_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the basic pharmacology, mechanisms of action, metabolism, clinical applications and adverse effects for each of the following classes of chemotherapy: fluoropyrimidines, platinum analogs, epidophyllotoxins and growth factor receptor inhibitors.","batch":2,"stem":"A 63-year-old woman is starting adjuvant treatment after resection of a colon tumor and wants an oral fluoropyrimidine rather than a continuous intravenous infusion. She has normal renal function and no heart disease. The abdomen is soft, the incision is healed, and there is no neuropathy. Hemoglobin is 12.6 g/dL (reference range: 12.0–16.0 g/dL) and creatinine is 0.8 mg/dL (reference range: 0.6–1.2 mg/dL). Which drug is a prodrug converted to 5-fluorouracil inside the tumor by thymidine phosphorylase?","choices":{"A":"Oxaliplatin","B":"Capecitabine","C":"Irinotecan","D":"Bevacizumab","E":"Cetuximab"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Capecitabine is an oral fluoropyrimidine that is converted to 5-fluorouracil in the tumor, in a sequence that ends with thymidine phosphorylase. Oxaliplatin is a platinum analog that cross-links DNA and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug. Irinotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor and is not activated by thymidine phosphorylase to 5-fluorouracil. Bevacizumab binds VEGF-A and is not a fluoropyrimidine. Cetuximab binds EGFR and is not converted to 5-fluorouracil.","isHighYield":true,"whyNot":{"A":"Oxaliplatin is a platinum analog that cross-links DNA and is not a fluoropyrimidine prodrug","C":"Irinotecan is a topoisomerase I inhibitor and is not activated by thymidine phosphorylase to 5-fluorouracil","D":"Bevacizumab binds VEGF-A and is not a fluoropyrimidine","E":"Cetuximab binds EGFR and is not converted to 5-fluorouracil"}}]},{"sdlNumber":40,"title":"SDL 40 — Review","questions":[]}]}]};